inl note_of_bend_backend_7 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 7u32, "2026-10-16T10:03Z", "main-thread", "06:03 main: pack-1r installed both modes and blessed (single-flight-20261016-053218, 6660 rows, DISAGREE 0; review = Kotlin probe single-flight-20261016-050109, Swift 050650, Bend 051447 against the pack-1q bless: 0 changes on listed rows). Union matches over several scrutinees (union structural equality) as nested matches, mismatched tags to the fallback; Bend 364 -> 388 (wgsl_nullary_union, wgsl_payload_union + 22 rows that already agreed). Open: typecheck_long_let_body disagrees (exit 1, unlisted).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_8 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 8u32, "2026-10-16T10:08Z", "main-thread", "06:08 main: typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 chained inl ~tN lets, C exit 236) exits 1 on Bend after 5.3 s (probe single-flight-20261016-051447): looks like a Bend toolchain depth limit on the generated let chain rather than wrong arithmetic; not listed. Next: read the Bend stderr for that row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_9 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 9u32, "2026-10-16T10:30Z", "main-thread", "06:30 main: typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 chained lets) Bend exit 1 is the Bun/JSC default stack limit (bend2: the machine stack overflowed), not codegen. bun.exe reserves 18 MB; BUN_JSC_maxPerThreadStackUsage=16777216 gives exit 236 = C. Harness fix staged as tmp/pack-s/bend-stack.ps1 for pack-1s, then list the row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_10 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 10u32, "2026-10-16T11:39Z", "main-thread", "07:39 main: coverage census from probe single-flight-20261016-051447, rows where C runs but Bend does not compile: 101. Macros 37 (11 are samples/core/sm.spi byte_to_i32's Bend macro arm; value macros staged as tmp/pack-u/bend-macros.ps1, unit macros stay unsupported), f64 19, i64 13, u64 8, mutable-layout defaults 7, BackendSwitch without a Bend key 13. bend2's numeric primitives are U32, F32 and Nat only (comp.ts ops table): i64/u64 could be emulated on Nat mod 2^64 or U32 pairs (Lua spiral_w64 precedent); f64 has no primitive.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_11 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 11u32, "2026-10-16T11:57Z", "main-thread", "07:57 main: pack-1s installed both modes (07:23/07:31) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-073131, 6,685 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1r bless 053218 overridden by probes 062511 (Kotlin), 061551 (Swift), 064814 (Python); the one extra row, Bend typecheck_long_let_body, exits 236 like C. ts_int_wrap Python Known -> agree.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_12 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 12u32, "2026-10-16T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main: pack-1u installed both modes (09:16/09:21) and blessed: single-flight-20261016-092123, 6,742 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells against the pack-1s bless 073131 overridden by probes 081908 (Kotlin), 082312 (Swift), 082906 (Bend), 084211 (Python). Harness Kotlin 444, Swift 443, Bend 401, Python 422.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_13 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 13u32, "2026-10-16T18:05Z", "main-thread", "14:05 main: next grain recorded, not started: i64/u64 (22 samples, 3 C-only) need a pair representation; bend2 has U32/F32/Nat only and Nat is Peano (BendTT 1n+add), so pairs of U32 like Lua's spiral_w64: add/sub with carry, 64-bit mul via 16-bit limbs, long division, signed compare, shifts, decimal show, conversions. f64 (19 samples) has no primitive at all.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_14 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 14u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: Bend 2 u64 prototype (U32 & U32 pairs: add with carry, 32x32 to 64 multiply via 16-bit limbs) passes 4 BigInteger checks (job tmp/night/bend64). Syntax facts: pairs pass linearly, destructure with (+h,+l), match only on variables, shift counts are Nat.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_15 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 15u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread lab cleanup: the u64-as-U32-pairs prototype (w64_add with carry, w64_mul32 via 16-bit limbs, w64_mul, 4 self-checks exiting 0) moved from lab/bend-backend/main.bend to apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend. Its final home is the compiler's bendPrelude (emitted helpers, the way luaW64Helpers serves Lua) when the i64/u64 grain is done; that grain then deletes the lane file.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_16 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 16u32, "2026-10-19T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:19 lap 1 grain: today's pack 5 review (single-flight-20261019-072348): Bend 400 rows agree with the C oracle plus 1 Known (bounds), 0 disagree; the 10-14 next step (add the 20 new agreeing rows to the Bend list) is long done. Left: i64/u64 (the w64 U32-pair prototype in tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend moves into the bendPrelude) and floats (Bend 2 has F32 only, no formatter) Next: list the matrix samples whose Bend row is missing only for i64/u64 so the w64 grain has a target count")
inl note_of_bend_backend_17 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 17u32, "2026-10-19T12:59Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:59 lap 2 grain: target count for the w64 grain: of Bend's 130 missing C samples only 2 are blocked by i64/u64 alone (other tags: string 68, macro 45, array 41, f64 31, union 28), so i64 support unlocks rows mainly together with strings and f64, not by itself Next: count Bend-missing samples whose only blockers are i64 and string (Bend 2 strings exist), to see if strings are the better next grain")
inl note_of_bend_backend_18 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 18u32, "2026-10-19T18:14Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:14 lap 4 grain: blocker combinations for the 130 Bend-missing C samples (accept 125623): none 17, string+macro 15, string 15, f64 11, i64+string 6; of the 17 untagged ones 12 are C compile-error negatives (no Bend value) and only 5 run: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending, frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred (exit 0), package_default_int_i64 (exit 7), zig_exit_wide (exit 305, exit-width semantics), mega_omniledger runtime_native; the string-only 15 are mostly negatives too, so strings are not the cheap win either Next: probe the two frontier_hopacsota samples on Bend (a -Probe Bend row restricted to them), then add them to the Bend list if they agree")
inl note_of_bend_backend_19 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 19u32, "2026-10-19T18:47Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:47 lap 5 grain: probe of the untagged Bend-missing samples on the installed compiler: frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending and frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred compile and run on bend2 with exit 0 like C; package_default_int_i64 needs i64 (the w64 grain), zig_exit_wide exits 49 vs C 305 (bun truncates to 8 bits, the same exit-width class as the Linux F# CI row); staged via the generic lab/BACKEND-COVERAGE-GAPS/add-to-list.py Bend <samples> (dry run clean) Next: apply with the pack after 8b, review, accept")
inl note_of_bend_backend_20 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 20u32, "2026-10-19T21:20Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:20 main: pack 9 ACCEPTED 17:20 (single-flight-20261019-161549: 7,057 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs the Release candidate review 155055); Bend gained frontier_hopacsota_mutual_pending and frontier_hopacsota_recursion_inferred, both agreeing with C.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_21 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 21u32, "2026-10-20T03:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:41 main: estimate corrected for WSJF: 97 percent overstated the rest; what remains is i64/u64 (move the w64 U32-pair prototype from tmp/lanes/bend/w64.bend into the bend prelude, unlocking about 21 rows) and f64 (Bend 2 has F32 only: emulate or keep Known); progress 85, estimate 2,400 min total, so about 6 h remain.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_22 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 22u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "main", "18:46 w64 prelude extended and proven on Bend 2 (lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64.bend = the tmp/lanes prototype's add/mul32/mul/eq + new w64-ext): w64_not, w64_neg (two's complement), w64_sub, w64_ult and w64_slt (sign bit flipped with xor 0x80000000), w64_shl, w64_lshr and w64_ashr for n in 0..63 (n = 0, below 32 and 32+ branches), w64_or. 10 new checks (neg 1, sub across the word boundary, unsigned and signed compares both ways, shl by 32 and across the carry, lshr across words, ashr of the sign bit by 63 and of a positive value) all pass: exit 0 in about 3 s. Bend 2 lesson: match may only scrutinize a parameter, so every condition goes through a w64_pick or w64_pick_bool helper. Still missing before the codegen switch: div/rem (signed and unsigned, toward zero), conversions to and from i32/u32/f32, and printing (decimal of a U32 pair). Next: those, then codegenBend emits i64/u64 as U32 & U32 pairs plus this prelude when a program uses a 64-bit op (the Lua spiral_w64 pattern), unlocking about 21 rows.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_23 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 23u32, "2026-10-21T22:50Z", "main", "18:50 w64 division proven on Bend 2 (lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-div.bend.txt, tested by w64-div-test.bend): w64_udivmod is restoring shift-and-subtract over 64 bits in ONE self-recursive def (w64_udiv_rec) with a Nat fuel counter (64n; Bend 2's termination checker needs a structurally shrinking argument and rejects a U32 counter and mutual recursion), plus w64_udiv/urem and signed w64_sdiv/srem (toward zero, like C: sign of the quotient = xor of the operand signs, remainder takes the dividend's sign). 8 checks pass in 1.3 s: 100/7 = 14 r 2; 2^32/3 = 1431655765 r 1; -7/2 = -3; -7%2 = -1; 7/-2 = -3; (2^64-1)/1. Bend 2 rules learned (for the codegen): match and destructuring only on parameters; + (reusable) parameters must be Data, so pairs are passed as their U32 halves; arguments are eager, so recursion branches only through a match on a parameter; scalar bindings (+x = expr) are fine. Still missing: conversions (i32/u32/f32 <-> pair) and printing; then the codegenBend switch.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_24 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 24u32, "2026-10-21T22:54Z", "main", "18:54 w64 prelude complete in Bend 2 and proven: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-prelude.bend.txt (one file for codegenBend to embed when a program uses a 64-bit op, like Lua's spiral_w64). Contents: add, mul32, mul, eq, not, neg, sub, ult/slt, shl/lshr/ashr (0..63), or/and, udivmod/udiv/urem/sdiv/srem (toward zero), of_u32/of_i32 (zero and sign extension), show_u/show_s (decimal; Nat-fueled single recursion with a stop flag). Tests: 4 prototype checks + 10 (w64.bend) + 8 division (w64-div-test.bend) + 7 conversion/printing (w64-show-test.bend: 0, 2^32, 2^64-1, -5, INT64_MIN, i32 sign extension, u32 zero extension), all exit 0. Remaining for the about 21 rows: codegenBend maps Int64T/UInt64T to U32 & U32 pairs (type at 166769, zero value at 166913, literals as (hi, lo)) and routes TyOp arithmetic, compare, shift, conv and show for 64-bit operands to the w64_* calls, and float conversions (F32 only on Bend 2; f64 stays Known). That is the next compile-side slice.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_25 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 25u32, "2026-10-21T22:59Z", "main", "18:59 codegen slice staged: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-codegen.ps1 (9 anchors, dry run matches each once; shared codegenBend section, so pair-drift is clean). Int64T/UInt64T -> (U32 & U32) (high, low); zero ({0 : U32}, {0 : U32}); LitInt64/LitUInt64 split into words; TyConv 64<->64 identity, 64<-i32/i8/i16 w64_of_i32, 64<-u32/u8/u16/char w64_of_u32, 32<-64 w64_lo; Printf through w64_show_s/u; TyOp Add/Sub/Mult/Div/Mod/LT/GT/LTE/GTE/EQ/NEQ/And/Or/Xor/Complement/Shl/Shr/Neg go to the w64_* calls (signed by Int64T); the 43-def prelude (lab w64-prelude.bend.txt, whole-file Bend typecheck plus 32 checks green) is embedded as bendW64Prelude and appended only when the emitted defs call w64_, like spiral_narrow_i8. Not covered: 64-bit float conversions (F32 only on Bend 2) and 64-bit array lengths, which stay unsupported errors. Gate in PACK-AFTER-NIGHT: add the i64/u64 samples to the matrix Bend list (about 21 rows per the 10-19 23:41 note), each must agree with C, and every other row must be unchanged (programs without 64-bit ops emit byte-identical Bend).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_26 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 26u32, "2026-10-21T23:00Z", "main", "19:00 matrix side staged: lab/BEND-BACKEND/w64-matrix.ps1 adds the 15 i64/u64 samples with a C output that the Bend list lacks (array_i64, array_i64_at1, array_u64, closure_pair_i64, heap_i64_real, heap_i64_text, heap_u64_text, i64_scalar, lua_wide_integers, mut_i64_text, native_closure_union_rec, portable_console_lines, printf_portable, ts_int_wrap, union_i64_text). Excluded by kind: the 3 abi_external_*i64 C-shim samples and rust_static_closure_chain. Proven on a copy: Bend list 405 -> 420, no other key changed. In the pack, any of the 15 that still hits an unsupported shape (heap_i64_real needs float conversion, for example) goes to Known with its reason, not silently dropped.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_27 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 27u32, "2026-10-21T23:11Z", "main", "19:11 dropped samples/heap_i64_real from w64-matrix.ps1: it pairs an i64 with an f64 (1.5f64), and Bend 2 has F32 only, so it would fail on f64, not on the w64 path; it stays off the Bend list with that reason. 14 candidates remain (printf_portable and portable_console_lines also print an i64 via %d and console.write_line, which the patch routes to w64_show_s).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_28 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 28u32, "2026-10-22T18:09Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:09 main: lab drained. w64-codegen.ps1 and w64-matrix.ps1 landed with PACK-AFTER-NIGHT: bendW64Prelude is in the core, and every definition line of the 5 lab prelude parts is found there (w64-ext's 13 missing lines are only its inline check harness). In the 07:53 review run, 11 of the 14 new Bend rows agree with C, and the other 3 are Known with reasons: lua_wide_integers (f64, Bend 2 has F32 only), portable_console_lines (unit macros), union_i64_text (bend2 SOME PROOFS FAIL: expected Data, observed Type). The 32 prelude checks (w64-*-test.bend) are deleted, since the matrix rows run the same helpers against C. Still open for Done: unit macros, and the union_i64_text codegen gap (a union case carrying i64 text, where the payload type is emitted where Bend expects data).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_29 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 29u32, "2026-10-22T18:10Z", "main", "2026-10-22 14:10 main: union_i64_text hypothesis, read from samples/union_i64_text/main.bend (no run yet): the union is emitted as type U0 is Data: U0_Both{f0: (U32 & U32), f1: String}. Every other w64 use is a local or a parameter (heap_i64_text, mut_i64_text and closure_pair_i64 agree), so the one new shape is a sigma pair type as a field of a Data constructor, which matches bend2's \"expected Data, observed Type\". Test after the block, about 5 s of bend2: a 6-line file with type T is Data: T_A{f0: (U32 & U32)}, then the same file with the field split as T_A{f0_hi: U32, f0_lo: U32}. Rule: if the first fails and the second passes, codegenBend flattens each 64-bit union field into _hi/_lo U32 fields, packing at construction (via a let destructure) and repacking on unbox, and union_i64_text leaves Known. If both pass, the cause is elsewhere: bisect the file by deleting defs.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_30 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 30u32, "2026-10-22T19:06Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:06 main: union_i64_text hypothesis CONFIRMED with bend2, about 5 s each (scratch bend-sigma-field): type T is Data: T_A{f0: (U32 & U32), f1: String} fails exactly as the sample does (expected Data, observed Type); with the field split into f0_hi/f0_lo U32 it typechecks; is Type passes the declaration but IO.pure then rejects the value. The flattened shape the codegen will emit (w64_hi/w64_lo at the box, +v_hi, +v_lo binders, the pair rebuilt as the arm's first do statement) runs to exit 42 = 1 + 41 as predicted. Patch lab/BEND-BACKEND/union-w64.ps1 (6 anchors: declaration, unbox binders and rebuild, ruled-out arity, box, union default value, prelude w64_hi/w64_lo) rides PACK-NEXT-3, rule (f) in PACK-NEXT-2 note 9. Unit macros remain the other open row.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_31 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 31u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "main", "2026-10-22 15:48 main: Bend prelude on demand, staged in PACK-NEXT-4 (%TEMP%\\pack-next4\\bend-prelude-on-demand.ps1, 3 anchors on the next3 core). Every main.bend started with the same 183-line bendPrelude (33 defs, 12 of them @unsafe), 26.7% of all Bend output, and bend2 typechecks each def on every row. bendUsedPrelude keeps only the defs the program reaches, transitively; a decorator line stays attached to its def; the w64 and narrowing helpers already were conditional. A Python mirror (scratch bend_prelude_mirror.py) over the 417 samples: 286,189 -> 234,399 lines (18.1% fewer); most programs drop 20-26 of the 33 defs (the f32 conversions in 416 files, i32 show in 415, string slicing in 409). The pack review checks mechanically that each changed main.bend equals the old one minus prelude defs, and native results must match next3 row for row. The Bend median run is 810 ms, so the typecheck saving is measured there: native_ms of Bend rows next4 vs next3.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_32 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 32u32, "2026-10-25T13:27Z", "main", "10-25 09:27 main groom: the last noted step SHIPPED. bendUsedPrelude (prelude on demand, from PACK-NEXT-4) is in the installed tree core and has passed every accepted review since (latest 5j accept, DISAGREE 0). The item stays open for the rest of its Feature scope (the not-supported list in tmp/AGENTS.md: unit macros and the rest).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_33 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 33u32, "2026-10-30T01:59Z", "main", "10-29 21:59 main, groom (WIP rule, as in the 10-23 and 10-27 grooms): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 92%. No note since 10-25 and no live job; main carries 69 Actives, which is not real WIP. Resume when picked; if its pass already shipped in an installed core, close it Done with that evidence instead. Compiler/lib work: per lane-roles, the team that needs it (likely the compiler team, git-a6) resumes it.")
inl note_of_bend_backend_34 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 34u32, "2026-10-31T20:02Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:01 compiler GROOM (user direction via /btw, 10-31 15:4x): the self-hosted rewrite (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL) starts only after EVERY backend is SOTA: no memory problems, no Rc slowdowns, no union hacks, no recursion limits, no missing arithmetic; GADT bugs and conversion gaps are 'unacceptable by now'; edition 3 finishes first; Gleam is a top test target (abyssal codebases) with F#, Rust and Wasm; Cells as fast as F# projects. This item is now on that gate (priority -> P0, status -> Planned).")
inl note_of_bend_backend_35 () = Note ("BEND-BACKEND", 35u32, "2026-11-06T12:30Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: every examples-suite sample with a faithful Bend mapping agrees with the C oracle (DISAGREE 0), and the rest are listed in the matrix Known bounds with a reason (f64, i64/u8, raw C macros). Next step: rerun the coverage probe (test.ps1 -Suite examples -Native -Probe Bend) on pack52 and diff against the 10-08 list.")
inl compiler_upstream_onion () = Task ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", "Move the compiler into the upstream fork's repo with our changes as onion layers over mrakgr's files (PR-ready), one file per backend, splitter adapted for parallel builds; 2026-10-08 first upstream-ready patch: CodegenC (user globals reach C output; (void)x in empty decref bodies, 211 -Wunused-parameter -> 0 apart from one stale output), made line-local so scripts/upstream.py exports it cleanly (4 hunks): python scripts/upstream.py export deps/The-Spiral-Language HEAD <out> --since HEAD writes The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs; opening the PR on the fork is the user's call; 18:2x readable-names + C patch material now durable at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\upstream-pr\\names-and-c (CodegenC, CodegenFsharp, CodegenPython, CodegenUtils); both CodegenPython conflicts resolved by intent (fork already carries the string option source name in join_point_method); upstream.py export fix: an addition after the last line of a section goes to the end of the target file (it was spliced between codegen_env and its members); generatedMethodName uses System.Char (CodegenUtils opens only System.Text); verified by a differential build of the fork (unpatched vs patched, external includes restored from history): 0 patch-only errors, with a canary type error proving fsc reaches the codegen files past the unrelated lib/fsharp/Common.fs errors; 21:1x CodegenC now emits the backend standard includes before user globals (Seq.append globals env.globals |> Seq.distinct): user globals using bool/size_t no longer precede <stdbool.h>; dev review: only the include order changed in the 2 samples with C globals (Lean exit=-1 rows were load kills, parity alone); deployed dev32 both cores; patch material CodegenC.fs refreshed (export --since 98492a63, since the 18:34 commit moved HEAD past the names work); 19:4x two fresh candidate exports exist for the next curation pass, both exit 0 against the fork clone at polyglot/deps/The-Spiral-Language: names-and-c-194526 (export --since HEAD, so only tonight's uncommitted edits, 45634 bytes, carries the closure void-cast fix at lines 616-617 but not the committed globals fix) and names-and-c-full-194526 (export --since 98492a63, the documented PR base, 46185 bytes, carries all three: the void-cast fix, the Seq.append globals env.globals fix from C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED and generatedClosureName from the readable-names work); both report conflicts the fork has since moved into (14 and 20 hunks, mostly CodegenCpp plus 2-3 CodegenC hunks in the closure and string-compare areas), so the curated 10-08 names-and-c material stays canonical and opening the PR remains the user's call; 2026-10-12 split: non-defacing changes go upstream as a PR series (test-block parser skip, canonical package dirs, DeadPureLetElision, hermetic BackendSwitch, the new backends, bug fixes), each with its sample rows; edition-3 syntax stays fork-only (SPIRAL3-EDITIONS); 08:13 input from the water-oil re-shape probe: to make shared Graph/ServerUtils/WDiff upstream-shaped, hopac's supervisor must stop requiring interned path ids in shared code (42 compile errors when ServerUtils takes single-flight's text); plan: hopac keeps ids in its own layer and converts at the boundary of the shared functions; 2026-10-13 22:59 the spiral-split gear splitter (scripts/gear-dev.ps1, bench-split.ps1, build-splitter.ps1, tmp/splitter, lanes/splitter) was removed at the user's request: idle since early October (234 runs Sep 26-Oct 1, then 21), built for gpt5parallel's 159k lines; use build.ps1 -Configuration Debug -CoreSource <copy> for candidates; 2026-10-13 23:41 user decision: after hopacsota promotion (HOPACSOTA-P6); the splitter it mentions was deleted 2026-10-13", Epic, P1, D8, 10u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P6,SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL,UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA,CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE", "compiler,upstream,main,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_upstream_onion () = Planning ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", Minutes 3000u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:35Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:35 touch: upstream.py status vs the fork: 37,259 differing lines, 31,923 of them in Utils (shared infra), then CodegenCpp 404/253, CodegenC 400/128, SignalRSupervisor 357/248, BlockParsing 315/164; Parser/LineParsers/BlockSplitting near zero")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: core now 185,945 lines with 14 #if pairs (was 186,572 / 16 before the 10-15 unifications). Each pair unified removes a section the upstream sync would otherwise need twice. Next grain: rerun upstream.py status to see whether Utils is still 31.9k of the 37.3k differing lines.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: found and fixed a tool bug. upstream.py still looked for '#if SPIRAL_CORE_HOPAC', but the core was renamed to SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL (spiral d94dc380, 10-13). Since then sections() saw no pairs and read gpt5parallel's side as single-flight text, so status/import/export were wrong. The 10-14 status (37,259 lines, Utils 31,923) was that artefact. Fixed the marker (scripts/upstream.py lines 13, 68; LF). Real status against spiral/deps/The-Spiral-Language: 5,543 differing lines; largest CodegenCpp 404+253, CodegenC 400+128, SignalRSupervisor 355+248, BlockParsing 370+171, Prepass 226+181; Utils only 19+17. The onion is ~7x smaller than recorded.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night drift (upstream.py status, 27 mapped sections): 6,014 differing lines (ours 4,089, upstream-only 1,925); 5,543 on 10-16.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:44Z", "main-thread", "15:44 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: migrating the compiler from spiral/apps/compiler into the upstream fork stays here as the backlog home. Candidate split PRs named by the user: the sorted-names indexing change (the user's own, separate PR), the de-duplication of generated names that came from the single-file build (first done in the old dib notebooks, kept when hopac/gpt5parallel dropped the dib), union/nominal private constructors (SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS, Done since pack-1e), and Export (ours: no Export op exists upstream; history is squashed so the author is unknown). StringSlice is upstream's own op (BlockParsing.fs, CodegenC.fs), not ours. For hopacsota the end state is a neat structure in the fork, ready for review, not commit; the split into PR branches is decided later. Pause before any repeated destructive git operations by the agent until that flow is stable.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 6u32, "2026-10-18T05:47Z", "main-thread", "01:47 main-thread, from the user's forked study session (software history of the last 50 years): do not move the whole spiral repo into the fork. Move the language (apps/compiler: both cores, editions, formatter, harness, samples, oracle tables in matrix.psd1; and lib/spiral as the standard library) into i574n/The-Spiral-Language, keeping our changes as layers over mrakgr's files for PR-ability; keep the ecosystem in spiral (cells, the spiral CLI, wasm, lisp, zed, CI glue for dice/alphabet/polyglot), the way tibia and eoie already live apart. Rationale: the active fork that keeps history becomes upstream (EGCS to GCC, Neovim, LibreOffice, MariaDB; history-dropping forks like XEmacs drifted for good), and language repos hold language plus stdlib only (CPython, Go, rustc with cargo outside). Fork last merged mrakgr/master 2026-10-04. Timing: not now (history rewrite; destructive git paused until hopacsota stabilizes). Safe first step: a one-way sync keeping the fork's compiler source identical to apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs before anything moves.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 7u32, "2026-10-19T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:19 lap 1 grain: the bite-sized upstream post series is this epic's first layer in practice: UPSTREAM-POST-1-VANILLA (base branch host_cpp_and_cuda_backend + corebase as core + 5 proven fixes, make-post.ps1, upstream.py export --since/--until) then UPSTREAM-POST-2-C-SELF-TAIL; each post is one PR-shaped onion layer over mrakgr's files Next: record the post series as deps once post 1 lands in the fork (user's commit)")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 8u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:21 lap 2 grain: the core switch (CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE) lands today 11:45-13:30 with eoie, tibia and x lanes aligned (dry runs: eoie 0 source edits, tibia 44, x 0), which makes the fork's core = post 1's corebase-as-core layer in the tree; recorded as a dep Next: -")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 9u32, "2026-10-19T18:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:41 lap 4 grain: drift measured with tmp/scripts/upstream.py status against the fork clone (deps/The-Spiral-Language HEAD, the core v1 commit not yet committed there): 6,377 differing lines over the upstream-mapped sections; largest: Infer 422+238, CodegenC 413+140, CodegenCpp 404+253, BlockParsing 385+173, SignalRSupervisor 356+248, CodegenUtils 354+26, WDiff 341+90, PartEval 334+85, Prepass 294+199; smallest real layers: RefCounting 5+6, BlockSplitting 6+7, VSCTypes 7+0, ParserCombinators 0 (identical); the 30 new backends live in our own sections and are outside this count Next: first onion layer candidates are the near-zero sections (RefCounting, BlockSplitting, VSCTypes): export each as its own layer with upstream.py export --since, and check the fork file round-trips")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 10u32, "2026-10-31T16:16Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 12:15 compiler: scripts/upstream.py is the last tooling .py in apps/compiler (user: no .py in the repos). PORT PLAN, not done today because it's 1-2 h and the tool has been idle since 10-29: its status counts and its 3-way patch transfer (import/export) depend on difflib.SequenceMatcher's exact matching blocks (Ratcliff-Obershelp longest-match recursion with autojunk=False, not LCS/Myers), so 'git diff' can't replace it without changing every number. Port SequenceMatcher.get_matching_blocks/get_opcodes faithfully (C# via Add-Type inside a .ps1 for speed on the 30k-line Utils section, or an F# .fsx run by the cache's dotnet fsi), then A/B: 'status <fork> <ref>' per-section numbers byte-identical, 'export <fork> <base> <out>' trees identical, on 2-3 fork refs. Also simplify sections(): after PACK-10 there are no SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL pairs, so the pair-skipping state machine goes.")
inl note_of_compiler_upstream_onion_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", 11u32, "2026-10-31T17:20Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 13:20 compiler: upstream.py PORTED and RETIRED (n10's plan done today). apps/compiler/scripts/upstream.ps1 = the same three commands (status <fork> [ref] / import <fork> <old> <new> / export <fork> <base> <out> [-Since <ref>] [-Until <ref>], plus -Core): difflib.SequenceMatcher ported exactly to C# via Add-Type (longest-match recursion, no junk, LIFO queue, adjacent-block merge, the same opcodes), the rest in PowerShell with python's whitespace set for strip(). A/B on identical inputs: status at fork HEAD 28/28 lines identical (total 6,943) and at 71fbce46 identical (7,548); export <fork> 71fbce46: 27/27 files byte-identical; import 71fbce46->HEAD on two core copies: 189/189 log lines and the resulting core byte-identical; export HEAD -Since 33ee6412 -Until cec8840c: 13/13 files and the logs (conflicts included) identical. Two port bugs were caught by the A/B and fixed before the final runs: git output read through a StreamReader dropped the fork files' BOM (python keeps it), and a wrapped array piped into ForEach-Object was joined with spaces. tmp/README.md's Upstream block now shows the pwsh commands (and -Since). upstream.py archived as %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-131843. Speed: status ~170 s vs python ~97 s on the loaded box (the PowerShell glue, not the matcher), acceptable for a by-hand tool.")
inl hopac_speed () = Task ("HOPAC-SPEED", "Measure hopac vs single-flight (real-work rows 3-22x slower, fresh process per job), split startup from evaluation, profile and iterate to single-flight speed; stats 2026-10-08 09:5x (probe.ps1, fresh process per compile, F# backend, mean of runs 2-3, net = total - frontier_hello baseline): startup sf 1.0s / hopac 1.45s; net eval brzozowski 0.65s / 0.8s (1.2x), zeta 0.95 / 1.4 (1.5x), omniledger 2.65 / 5.0 (1.9x), lean_cic 1.8 / 5.5 (3.1x), spiral_proves 1.5 / 4.65 (3.1x); the suite-level 126x median compares warm single-flight workers with fresh hopac processes and overstates the gap; next: profile lean_cic; profile lean_cic hopac (probe -Profile 12, SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=1), innermost compiler frames: term 36%, installBridge 7.4% (thread-local tail-result bridge save/restore per declared JP step), ty_core_impl 6.8%, buildFreshApplyState 3.2%, DiagSidecar.emitReentryKeySample 2.6% (runs even when quiet: ConditionalWeakTable + 2 AddOrUpdate per re-entry; counts may feed hot-key fallback, check before gating), DiagJson runner/loop/emitRecord 5.5% (background thread, CPU not wall), EvalWorklist replay tick 2.3%; candidates: gate sidecar sampling when quiet if counts are diagnostic-only, make the bridge scope a struct/stack instead of ThreadLocal round trips; each change needs an A/B probe on lean_cic/spiral_proves; 11:3x A/B (tmp/hopac-speed/ab-threadlocal.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, runs 2-3): the 4 hot-path JP slots AsyncLocal -> ThreadLocal gave identical outputs on 4 samples but lean_cic 6.15/6.50 -> 6.15/6.20 s, spiral_proves 6.10/6.15 -> 5.95/6.10 s, brzozowski and hello within noise (A alone varies 5% between rounds): no proven gain, reverted; the installBridge 7.4% is not the AsyncLocal write cost; next: allocation profile (gc-verbose) of term on lean_cic; 11:4x runtime knobs on the unchanged binary (ab-runtime*.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, baseline noise about 0.3 s): the shipped config (server GC, concurrent, TieredPGO off) is already best: gen0 64 MB +10%, gen0 8 MB within noise, TieredCompilation off +15%, workstation GC +20-25% on lean_cic/spiral_proves (the hopac core allocates from several threads); GC tuning is ruled out; remaining levers: startup JIT (ReadyToRun, hello is 1.6 s of mostly startup) and algorithmic work in term; 11:4x gen0: test.ps1 sets DOTNET_GCgen0size=256 MB for every run; fresh-process probes (ab-gen0.ps1) show it 4-13% slower than the runtime default on lean_cic/spiral_proves in BOTH cores; but an A-B-A single-flight suite (aba-gen0-suite.ps1, warm workers, 236 C rows) shows no difference (summed 62.5 / 67.6 default / 67.4 s); test.ps1 left unchanged: switching it needs an A-B-A hopac -Suite all (fresh process per job) showing the gain; ReadyToRun is already applied by build.ps1 since 2026-10-05; 12:3x hopac harness default now warm-recycle with deferral (HOPAC-WARM-SERVER): suite 2.35x faster at identical rows; 2026-10-12: hermetic BackendSwitch validation made the hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validating every arm, per-build type cache); candidate: type-check other arms without evaluating their join points; ladders 2026-10-12: hopac 67 min vs single-flight 27-35 min on -Suite all -Native; user decision 2026-10-12 19:05: hopac remains the intended default once it matches single-flight (raised to P0 with HOPAC-WATER-OIL); user decision 2026-10-12 19:18: water-oil first, then speed: every speed fix after unification is written once; 06:22 suite wall times tonight (same machine, varying load from parallel lanes): single-flight 19-23 min per -Suite all -Native; hopac 64, 41, 47, 48 and 29 min (pack-0005, least contended: hopac 29 vs single-flight 23); user decision 2026-10-13 07:08: hopac becomes the default core when its -Suite all -Native wall time is within 5% of single-flight on an idle machine with identical results (tonight's least-contended run: hopac 29 min vs single-flight 23 min); 2026-10-13 16:07 main, early estimate from today's suites (paired ok rows, compile_ms; the two runs had different machine load, so an idle measurement follows tonight): contracts hopac/sf sum ratio 15.2 (median 8.5, p90 50.6), examples 5.1 (median 7.1, p90 19.3), frontier 2.4, mega 1.8; worst samples are the mega roots on native backends (runtime_bench sf 7.8 s vs hopac 249 s, indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 8.6 vs 173 s); the user's switch rule (hopac default at equal suite time, within 5% idle) is far from met: hopac stays the candidate lane; 2026-10-13 18:36 main, interleaved A/B measurement (hopac_speed_ab.ps1 in main's job tmp: per sample, single-flight and hopac Release alternate in fresh --batch processes, 2 rounds, best of each, C backend; background load hits both modes alike): 14 frontier+mega samples sum sf 56.3 s vs hopac 149.0 s = 2.65x, median 1.83x; fixed startup overhead ~1.4 s (frontier_hello 2.5 vs 3.9 s); the gap grows with evaluation size: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench 5.3 vs 36.6 s (6.9x), mega_zeta 4.8x, omniledger 3.4x; next target: profile runtime_bench on hopac (probe.ps1 -Profile) for the evaluation hot path, and the startup cost separately; switch rule (equal suite time within 5%) far from met; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, sampled profile (probe.ps1 runtime_bench -Mode hopac -Backend C -Profile 30; logs in spiral-bin/probes/mega_brzozowski_derivatives__runtime_bench): innermost compiler frames ~37% evaluation (term 12.0, body.Invoke 23.1, peval 1.7) vs ~30% join-point coordination bookkeeping (jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 9.1, jpTryReserveDeclaredBodyLane 4.3, jpNoteDeclaredResumeEvidence 3.2, jpSetCurrentDeclaredSafeContinuationAtDepth 3.1, jpMaybePruneDeclaredResumeSnapshots 3.1, jpPersistDeclaredBodyCheckpoint 2.4, observer lane 1.5, continuation ordinal 1.3, retire snapshot 1.2) and ~8% diagnostics/sampling (DiagJson runner+loop 6.2, JpNativeThreadCpuClock 1.9; probe.ps1 sets SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET: yes); first target: the declared-body resume-evidence/checkpoint machinery (race-34 replay store) on the hot path - make it lazy or batch it, measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, caveat on the profile above: the shares are thread-stack samples, not CPU time; DiagJson.runner/loop is the progress HUD ticker, which in quiet mode only sleeps in 25 ms slices, so its 6.2% is a sleeping thread; re-run the breakdown on the evaluator thread only (python scripts/profile-thread.py <run.speedscope.json> [thread]) before optimizing the join-point bookkeeping; 2026-10-13 18:39 main, evaluator-thread CPU profile (profile-thread.py run1.speedscope.json, busiest thread 29.1 s busy, inclusive): jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 22.4-23.3%, buildFreshApplyState 17.8%, jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation 7.6% (the rest is evaluation: term/apply/if); ~half of hopac's evaluator CPU is per-step replay/resume bookkeeping single-flight does not have; next: make jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation and the let-continuation store lazy (record only when a suspension can actually resume) and reuse apply state instead of buildFreshApplyState per apply, then re-measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x, runtime_bench 6.9x); 2026-10-13 20:02 main, next-session plan from reading jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation (spiral_compiler.fs ~145856, 23% of hopac evaluator CPU, 2 callers): per evaluator step it interns shapeText (OperationalReasonIdOps.create, a string hash per step) although only 1 in 1024 steps uses the counted shape, does a ConcurrentDictionary GetOrAdd for the job profile, takes a lock to write five last-seen fields, and polls DiagJson.terminalFailureExitRequestedNow; behaviour-neutral fixes: keep the raw shape text and intern only on sampled/read paths, cache the job profile in the owner's AsyncLocal slot instead of GetOrAdd per step, replace the per-step lock with owner-local fields published at sample points; then buildFreshApplyState (17.8%) and jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation (7.6%); measure each with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x overall, runtime_bench 6.9x) and keep hopac's full suite at 0 behaviour differences; 2026-10-13 22:47 naming: the core this item calls hopac is now gpt5parallel (user decision; SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL, -Mode gpt5parallel, lanes/gpt5parallel); the id keeps HOPAC until eoie can rename ids (EOIE-AGILE-RENAME-ID); the planned rewrite is HOPACSOTA; scope now: the measured baseline (2.65x overall, 6.9x runtime_bench, ~half of evaluator CPU in replay bookkeeping) is hopacsota's target to beat, rather than a patch list for gpt5parallel; closed 2026-10-13 23:34 as superseded by hopacsota (user decision): goal inherited by HOPACSOTA-P3 (speed) and Phase 1/2 speed gates", Epic, P0, D7, 100u32, Done, "HOPAC-WATER-OIL", "compiler,hopac,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopac_speed () = Planning ("HOPAC-SPEED", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl portable_primitives () = Task ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", "Replace C-only macros with portable ops: StringConcat, Floor/Ceil/Round/Atan2, string search/case, a char model, Zig/Lean/Bend arms in lib backend switches; 2026-10-08 13:1x lib/spiral math (edited in math.livemd via mix spiral.edit, math.spi re-exported and compared): floor/ceil/atan2/round gain C (__builtin_* with _Generic for f32/f64), Cpp (<cmath>), Delphi (Int/Frac/Ord, ArcTan2) and TypeScript arms; Lua and Gleam round fixed (gave 2 for 2.7 and 4 for 3.2; now the shared round_half_to_even); lib backend.spi dispatches C and Delphi (before: no lib function could reach a C arm); new sample portable_math_rounding: 10 backends, 9 native runs exit 0, all agree with the C oracle; 13:0x accepted the lib-using samples (30 rows; review and accept runs identical): portable_math_rounding new on 10 backends; format_any x2 C/Delphi now fail at the C/Pascal compiler with backend_switch_has_no_<backend>_arm_in_lib_spiral (BackendSwitch evaluates every arm, so a lib fallback cannot raise a Spiral error for one backend only, same as the Zig/Lean fallbacks); 7 F#/Rust/Zig rows changed only in variable numbering (proved by normalizing v<n>); next: console write_line/write for C and Delphi via the portable !!!!Printf with a typecase, which needs one agreed bool/float text across backends (F# True vs Rust true); 17:1x lib console (console.livemd via spiral.edit, console.spi re-exported): write_line/write gain C arms (printf via real typecase helpers write_line_c/write_c: string %s, i32, i64, u32, u64; floats and bools stay out: no shortest round-trip float text in C, F#/Python print True) and Delphi arms (Writeln/Write); TypeScript write_line moved from console.log to process.stdout.write(String(x)+newline): the matrix runs TS in a Worker where console.log and process.stdout.write reorder, and console.log printed i64 BigInt as -7n; new sample portable_console_lines: 8 native backends, stdout identical to C; lib samples re-reviewed (zig_lib_console changed only in numbering); 19:5x lib convert (convert.livemd): convert_i32_base gains C (strtol) and Delphi (FPC radix prefixes ##36/##37/##38, base 2/8/10/16) arms; convert_i32 gains C/Delphi/TypeScript typed dispatch (string -> base 10, float -> round half even via nearbyint/Round, int -> cast); try_convert from string gains C/Delphi/TypeScript/Lua arms through parsed_integer (strict whole-text validation, never-throwing value expression, target range check via integer_fits) and Gleam trims like Rust; Lua previously returned the unparsed string, TypeScript had no convert_i32; lib console Gleam write_line/write print ints via int.to_string (io.println took only String); new sample portable_convert_parse: 7 native backends print 255/11/-42/123/none/none/7 like C; portable_console_lines now has a Gleam row; 19:1x triaged what is left: the lib backend_switch fallback in lib/spiral/backend.spi already carries Zig and Lean arms (they only compileError or name the missing arm for non-unit types) and math (5/5/5), console (3/3/3) and optionm (5/5/5) are fully covered, but the switchable backends run_target_args' and the backend union itself know only Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Cuda, Cpp, Rust and TypeScript, so a Zig or Lean arm there needs a design call on extending a union that every consumer matches exhaustively; the lib files with not one zig or lean arm are date_time (28 switches), listm (10), resultm (10), seq (9), trace (9), runtime (4), async (3), crypto (3), env (3) and the single-switch common, guid, platform, reflection and testing, while base (23 switches, 5/5), am (13, 3/3) and sm (27, 12/11) are partly covered; nothing is blocked on this today because the matrix matrix runs only the C and Gleam backends (the 19:0x restore rung: 610 rows = 483 C + 127 Gleam, no Zig or Lean row exists to fail) and a new arm cannot be validated without a sample, which tonight's scope forbids; StringConcat, string search and case ops and a char model remain compiler-op additions that need every backend and both cores", Feature, P0, D6, 100u32, Done, "DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", "team:compiler,compiler,lib,portability,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
