inl note_of_lean_backend_32 () = Note ("LEAN-BACKEND", 32u32, "2026-11-06T12:30Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: Lean 4 matrix rows agree with C on stack layouts, closures and unions (the title's list) with the remaining shapes as Known bounds; the Lean oracle stays at 364/364. Next step: run the Lean probe on the examples suite and list the first missing shape.")
inl kit_regen_drift () = Task ("KIT-REGEN-DRIFT", "eoie follow-ups from the 2026-10-12 dogfood lane: the current compiler renames generated items and adds impl Default, so every regenerated .rs churns (a full regeneration touched 94 files and left eoie_cli at exactly the 1000-line gate); split eoie_cli before the next regeneration; regression tests for the new receipts (eoie_contracts is at 999 lines); move the remaining rust_global receipt call sites to granular Spiral; 2026-10-12: `agile check <root>` without --compiler stops with \"state drift requires build-time Spiral attestation\" instead of finding the deployed compiler (spiral-bin single-flight SpiralCompiler.dll); with --compiler it passes; default to the deployed compiler or name it in the error; 2026-10-12 eoie lane: not started (gate waits 40-68 min); measured eoie_cli regenerates to eoie.rs 921 lines (+5); crates near the 1000-line gate: agile_task_listing ~956, agile_planning_migration ~988, eoie_contracts 999 (no room: new tests went to agile_planning_rollup/tests); 2026-10-13 17:37 Kino lane: (1) agile check without --compiler now finds the deployed compiler: compiler discovery order is argument, EOIE_SPIRAL_COMPILE, config, PATH, then deployed (SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, else <SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR or LOCALAPPDATA/spiral-bin or ~/.cache/spiral-bin>/bin/single-flight/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0/SpiralCompiler.dll); the build-time attestation error names that path; verified on a copy of this backlog: agile check ok source=deployed build_attested=2 in 7 s; agile_cache contract tests isolate it (SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR=fixture) and assert the error names the deployed path; (2) regeneration workspaces leaked: 99 staged copies, 15.8 GB under spiral-bin/eoie-regeneration; test-regeneration.ps1 now removes its workspace after a successful run (-KeepStaged keeps it; test-dev-workflow passes it) and prunes workspaces older than 2 days at start (failed runs stay for diagnosis until then); first run freed 13 GB (99 -> 34 dirs); test-dev-workflow contracts pass; (3) the gate splits (eoie_cli 528, eoie_contracts 587, eoie_agile_state 510) leave room before the next full regeneration; the generated-name churn itself is compiler-side (bootstrap_policy.rs regenerates with a changed allow list and wasm32 main); 2026-10-14 01:17 receipt regression tests (uncommitted): src/eoie_contracts/tests/proxy_receipts.rs, 5 tests that run the CLI (EOIE_BIN_UNDER_TEST or target eoie) in a temp root with a space and a non-ASCII character and compare whole receipt lines exactly, with only elapsed_ms/serial_ms/parallel_ms/workers normalized to N and the root to <root>: fs-read, fs-list, fs-write (create and --create-or-replace), fs-copy, fs-remove, fs-remove-tree, text-replace check/apply/--all and the --expect mismatch (exit 2, quoted corrected command), fs-search, parallel-search (hits, truncated=true found_at_least), fs-slice, fs-context, hash and hash-tree (digest on stdout, receipt apart), parallel-manifest, command-output ok and child-failed (child exit propagated); cargo test -p eoie-contracts --test proxy_receipts: 5 passed against eoie.exe; still open: the agile set receipt is covered only by planning_cli show; the receipt call sites are still whole Rust functions inside rust_global blobs (parallel_search_contract/search_terminal.spi 20, proxy_file_receipt users, legacy_operations_entry), which needs a rewrite (EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE), not a move; new test files may need census/evidence renewal (compiler-contracts/renew-strict-evidence.ps1); 2026-10-14 02:34 drift measured without touching the tree (job scratch eoie-lane/regen-drift.ps1: compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 -KeepStaged, never -Publish, outputs compared byte for byte with git show HEAD:<target>, staged work removed): compiler snapshot 555BB9B47239 (spiral-bin single-flight SpiralCompiler.dll), 128/128 owners emitted (median 2.7 s, slowest 5.7 s), 44 same as HEAD and 84 differ (3 of them have dirty sources from the other lane: eoie_proxy_edit_contracts, proxy_failure_classification); the 84 diffs add 3,573 and remove 3,446 lines; 14 owners gain 42 impl Default blocks and drop 681 lines naming numbered variants (US0_0 style), and the rest are other generated-name or layout changes (largest: agile_task_creation +198/-153, handoff_state_projection_domain +189/-190, canonical_plan_ir_domain +188/-189, state_receipt_codec_core +155/-156); a second run for determinism is running; 2026-10-14 02:57 regeneration is deterministic: a second staged run with the same compiler snapshot (555BB9B47239) produced byte-identical outputs for all 128 owners (0 of 128 SHA-256 differ), again 84 differ from HEAD and 44 match; a diff sample (handoff_state_projection_domain) shows only intentional codegen changes: methodN functions get readable names (method4 -> starts_with_loop_4, method1 -> codec_empty_1), empty Rc<str> literals become thread_local cached clones, the allow list drops unreachable_patterns, and a stale comment line goes; so the churn is one-time, not ongoing: the fix is one published regeneration, committed, after the main thread's codegen cleanup lands (pwsh compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 -Test -Publish, then a second test-regeneration.ps1 run must report 0 changed owners); 2026-10-14 03:12 the regenerated staged workspace compiles: cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked -j 4 (scratch target dir, through the heavy gate) exit 0 in 161 s with no errors; staged copies and the scratch target removed; source-topology on the live tree with the new test file: topology_limits_ok=true (crate max 995 lines, rust_global_site_count 261); left: publish+commit the one-time regeneration after main's codegen cleanup (and with the other lane's eoie_proxy_edit_contracts/proxy_failure_classification edits settled), confirm a follow-up regeneration changes 0 owners; the agile set receipt still lacks a CLI test (needs the agile_cache fixture with package.spiproj and a fixture compiler); moving receipt call sites out of rust_global is a rewrite of whole Rust functions (EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE), not part of this item; 2026-10-14 03:39 agile set receipt covered too (uncommitted): src/eoie_contracts/tests/agile_receipts.rs builds a backlog with agile init + agile add in a temp root (space and non-ASCII), asserts the whole set receipt line (eoie agile set ok id=ID-A progress=40 status=Active validation=runtime-attested compiler=not-required, byte counts and elapsed normalized), that show reflects it, and that progress 140, status Sleeping and an unknown id are rejected with their messages while state/agile.spi stays byte-identical; cargo test -p eoie-contracts --test agile_receipts --test proxy_receipts: 6 passed; source-topology still topology_limits_ok=true; 2026-10-14 03:44 correction to the 02:5x conclusion: determinism was shown for one compiler snapshot only, and every Rust codegen change in the compiler produces another wave like this one, so the churn is per compiler change, not one-time: regenerate and commit eoie whenever the compiler's Rust output changes (a step after each compiler bless), then confirm a second regeneration changes 0 owners; the receipt tests were checked against the deployed eoie.exe, not a build of the current tree, so rerun them with build.ps1 -Test once the other lane's edits land; its pending change only lets '~' stay unquoted after the first character in corrected commands (proxy_failure_classification proxy_words), which does not touch the pinned lines (the pinned root is quoted for its space either way); 2026-10-14 04:26 review fix: proxy_receipts.rs and agile_receipts.rs set EOIE_LEASE_POLICY=renewing and EOIE_CONFIG_HOME=<fixture root> on every command, so a machine without the renewing lease-policy file (CI) cannot lease-block the mutations; 6/6 pass with the shell's EOIE_LEASE_POLICY unset; 2026-10-14 06:01 the drift found in eoie_contracts is also a source drift: contracts.rs had been hand-patched (a743a92) with lease-test isolation its source contracts_entry/harness.spi lacked, so regeneration silently removed it; now in the source, regenerated, dev.ps1 passes", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_regen_drift () = Planning ("KIT-REGEN-DRIFT", Minutes 300u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl spiral3_editions () = Task ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "Spiral 3 as an edition of the fork, both editions in one compiler (user 2026-10-12: mrakgr has not touched the language for over a year): edition per package in package.spiproj (Rust-edition style, mixed-edition imports allowed), lexical only: the tokenizer var table (spiral_compiler.fs ~43072, words -> TokKeyword Spec*) gets an edition-3 table mapping symbol keywords to the same Spec tokens and the operator lexer consults it first, so parser/typecheck/peval/backends stay shared; start from geandra_v0_4.py FORWARD (inl :=, let :~, rec ^, union :|, match ?=, nominal :!, prototype :?, instance :+, fun \\, if ?, else |:, ...) and audit collisions per edition (=> already a match arm); keep dot record access, 2-space indent as the edition-3 formatter default (parser is column based, verify); test blocks need an edition-3 spelling; gate: spiral convert --edition 3|2 round-trips every sample and lib .spi byte for byte and both editions compile to identical residuals; 2026-10-12 first collision audit (regex lexer over 1,739 .spi in spiral lib + samples, dice/lib, eoie/src; script tmp/edition3/collisions.py): 25 of the 29 FORWARD symbols never occur as operators today (free: !+ !- &= -| :! :+ :< := :== :? :| :~ ;; ?& ?: ?= \\* \\? \\| ^ |: |? ~> ~~ ~~>); => occurs 10,932 times as the match/lambda arrow (reusing it for then/with is a parser-context question, not a lexer clash); a real clash: & is pattern conjunction (`{ level } & trace_state`, 4 uses) so `and` needs another symbol in edition 3; the ? (60) and \\ (41) hits look like $'...' macro-string escapes the regex does not model, so redo the audit with the compiler tokenizer (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY) before deciding; 2026-10-12 18:25 main, lexical-only check (reference converter tmp/edition3/lexical.py: Spiral-style tokens = words / maximal punctuation runs / strings+comments skipped; forward = keyword words -> symbols, reverse = symbol runs -> words; exact round trip per file over apps/compiler/tmp/samples + lib/spiral, 1,269 files): with the geandra_v0_4 FORWARD table 665/1,269 round-trip; drift is dominated by => (already the match-arm/lambda arrow in 602 files, and it also stands for both then and with, so reversing needs parser context, not lexing) and & for and (pattern conjunction, plus && glue); proposed table change: then -> ?>, with -> ?|, and -> :& (all three never appear as operators in the corpus) gives 1,262/1,269; the last 7 involve fun -> \\ next to the existing !\\ inline-code operator and $'...' strings with escaped quotes that the reference converter does not skip, to be settled by the real lexer; the geandra prototype itself only round-trips 26/820 samples (whole-program parser subset: rejects nominal/union and operators like +. =. $ !!!!), so its 116 tests are frontend acceptance cases, not a converter; correction: the note above is from 18:06, not 18:25; decided by the user 2026-10-12 18:44: table changes accepted (then ?>, with ?|, and :&) and edition-3 symbols lexed in every file (edition3_symbol_token, both cores, one shared Tokenize section); a per-package edition flag only when a real conflict needs it (upstream '#' escape); 18:45 first real-compiler check (single-flight Debug, 820-sample corpus, each sample compiled as written and converted to edition 3): 590 compared before the Debug batch hit a stack overflow; 249 ok in both with byte-identical C, 340 failed identically in both (C-backend contract rejections, intended type errors), 1 difference: closure_pair_bool, where !-, glued the comma into the run '!-,'; fixed in the shared Tokenize section (a run that is a symbol followed only by commas gives the commas back); rebuild + recheck queued; 19:00 recheck with the comma fix (single-flight Debug 18:47): 590 of 820 samples compared, 250 byte-identical C in both editions (closure_pair_bool included), 340 failed identically, 0 status or text differences; the Debug batch again died with a stack overflow (0xC00000FD) around sample 591, so the last 230 need a Release build run; check script tmp/edition3/build-and-check.ps1 + check.ps1, corpus tmp/edition3/corpus; user direction 2026-10-12 19:14: edition 3 prioritizes X compatibility (a source must survive being posted to X unchanged); '#': edition 2 = upstream (literal '#' in macro strings), edition 3 never uses '#' before a letter (no hashtags), so it needs its own escape spelling; this is the first real reason for the per-package edition flag; 20:03 edition-3 lexing verified harmless for edition 2 by single-flight-20261012-193841 (-Suite all -Native, Release built 19:36 from the source with the unified Tokenize section, edition-3 symbols + comma fix, WGSL nullary unions and the FOR-D offside fix): 3,799 jobs, baseline parity 3,793 / new 6 / missing+regressed 0 / residual-differs 0, native 2,775 ran, 11 build-fail, oracle agree 2,307, DISAGREE 0, 23 min: every existing row is byte-identical to the accepted oracle; 22:46 edition-3 lexing Release check (single-flight 22:26 DLL): 743 of 820 corpus samples: 363 identical C, 379 fail identically in both editions, 0 C text differences; the batch died at runaway_inline_match_recursion (e2 timeout, exit 3); the remaining 155 jobs (tmp/edition3/corpus/jobs-remaining.tsv) rerun after the InlineDepth guard; 00:07 edition-3 lexing check COMPLETE on Release: all 820 corpus samples: 438 identical C, 381 fail identically in both editions, 0 C text differences; the only status difference is runaway_inline_match_recursion (e2 timed out on the old 22:26 DLL, e3 stopped with EJP0011 on the 00:04 DLL with the InlineDepth guard), a DLL difference, not lexing; 2026-10-13 12:35 main: concrete first use: upstream programs (Spiral-s-ML-Library head) need prefix * = RawLayoutIndex (var or parenthesised term), while the fork's lib needs * = user ~* over the application chain; the parser plumbing (RawLayoutIndex through Infer/Prepass, ETY-ExpectedLayout) is in both cores, only the surface case needs the edition switch; Kino already reads edition: 3 from package.spiproj for X-compat; 2026-10-13 18:05 main: first switch landed and accepted (pack 3): DefaultEnv.edition (SpiralEdition = UpstreamEdition2 | ForkEdition3), --edition 2|3 on the host / SPIRAL_EDITION, default 3; edition 2 parses prefix * as upstream's layout index; lib/spiral unaffected (lib smoke green both cores); next candidates for the switch: '#' inside macro strings, edition3_symbol_token keywords", Epic, P0, D7, 80u32, Active, "SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", "compiler,edition,syntax,main,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral3_editions () = Planning ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:06Z", "lane-a", "08:06 lane-a: real-tokenizer converter (spiral tmp/formatter/Convert.fsx): user corpus 86 files to edition 3, 5,567 lines, 86/86 same parse; 1 gap: '!-;' lexes as one run, patch lane-a\\patches\\edition3-semicolon-giveback.ps1 (TrimEnd , and ;).")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T15:16Z", "lane-a", "11:16 lane-a: semicolon give-back patch verified on an isolated Debug build: '{ verbose = !-; strict = !+ }' fails to parse on the 04:35 core, compiles on the patched core to the same C as its edition-2 twin (9A1D33CBCAD1). Ready for main's pack.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 3u32, "2026-10-14T17:22Z", "lane-a", "13:22 lane-a: real-tokenizer converter over current lib (77), apps (14), eoie src (482): 21,156 lines to edition 3, 0 rejected, every file parses to the same blocks and round-trips 2->3->2 byte for byte (the '!-;' case did not occur outside the user snapshot).")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 4u32, "2026-10-14T21:09Z", "lane-a", "Semicolon give-back patch installed in pack-1e (e3 '!-;' lexes as '!-' then ';', twin of e2 residual 9A1D33CBCAD1); converter round trip lib/apps 91/91 re-verified 16:30.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 5u32, "2026-10-15T02:04Z", "lane-a", "22:04 measured in spiral_compiler.fs: the edition-3 symbol table (edition3_symbol_token) is consulted by the tokenizer in both editions, and DefaultEnv.edition is read in one place only (the parser's '*' layout index, edition 2 only). So mixed-edition imports already work except for '*'; the package.spiproj 'edition' field (pack-1e) sets SPIRAL_EDITION process-wide from the entry's package, not per package. Next step for true per-package editions: carry the edition per package into BlockParsingEnv (only '*' needs it today)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 6u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: measured: the edition setting only changes how the parser treats '*'; edition-3 symbols tokenize in both editions. EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE gives byte-identical edition-2/3 Rust and C.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night trace: four independent edition readers: compiler config (CLI --edition or SPIRAL_EDITION only), package schema edition (applied in the package default env used by file_default_env), the formatter editionOf line scan and x-compat xCompatPackageEditionThree. The positional host path never calls file_default_env, so a package edition has no effect there. Fix: one resolver (nearest package.spiproj, then env, then 3) used by all four.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:19 styles-survey: closest established terms for typefeatureset (from knowledge, no web search). No single standard term covers all five. Candidates: System F-omega (HKTs, polymorphism, existentials by encoding; no GADTs or type classes); System FC (GHC Core, Sulzmann/Chakravarty/Peyton Jones/Donnelly 2007: F-omega + type-equality coercions, built to host GADTs, existentials, HKTs and type classes via dictionaries) = best fit for the first four; prototypes alone = type classes / ad hoc polymorphism (Wadler-Blott 1989); typecase and union_to_record reflection = intensional type analysis (Harper-Morrisett 1995); inl/real = a two-level language (Nielson-Nielson) / multi-stage programming with partial evaluation (Jones-Gomard-Sestoft); type-directed partial evaluation (Danvy 1996) is a specific NbE technique, not a fit; type-level programming is the practice, not the feature set. What is distinctive in Spiral: the typing features are resolved by the partial evaluator at compile time, not by a typechecker over a core calculus, so the honest gloss is System FC-style type features plus intensional type analysis, resolved by staged partial evaluation. Suggestion: keep typefeatureset as the house word and define it once in docs with that gloss; the shortest accurate phrase for outsiders is staged type-level programming.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "styles-survey", "15:20 styles-survey correction to note 8: Spiral does typecheck top-down code (GADTs, HKTs, prototypes, forall are checked there); what the partial evaluator resolves is the reflection part (typecase, union_to_record, record folds in real) and the specialization of inl code. So the gloss is: a System FC-style typed surface plus intensional type analysis in a two-level (staged) language.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 10u32, "2026-10-17T19:43Z", "main-thread", "15:43 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: when edition 3 is ready, migrate package.spiproj autonomously to the chosen manifest (spiral.toml was the last pick), supporting both workspace layouts during the transition. Also research whether edition 3 can absorb the need for .spir files (the real dialect) by drawing on current languages with benchmarks (staged/comptime designs: Zig comptime, Mojo parameters, Lean 4 elaboration, Scala 3 inline/macros), so real-mode code lives in ordinary modules.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:21 lap 1 grain: fresh evidence for the edition-3 candidate '#' inside macro strings: on 2026-10-19 a Gleam tuple pattern Ok(#(a, _)) inside a lib $'...' arm failed to parse (# is MTermInline, a splice starter with  ! @), and the x lane hit the same wall; the gotcha list already records Pascal #36 and Gleam ##(a, b) escapes, so # as a splice costs every backend whose syntax uses # (Gleam tuples, Pascal chars, C preprocessor, Rust attributes) Next: count # occurrences inside lib/sample macro text that are escapes (##) vs splices to size an edition-3 change (e.g. #{...} only)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: sized the # question on lib: inside $'...' and $\"...\" macro text, 17 ## escapes vs 65 single # splices across lib/spiral, so # as a splice is used 4x more than escaped; an edition-3 change should narrow the splice syntax (for example #{expr}) rather than drop it Next: count the 65 single # uses by following character to see whether #( or #name dominates")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T14:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:43 lap 3 grain: exact macro rule from the tokenizer (spiral_compiler.fs 43394-43443, macro_body): after any of the four starters (backtick type, ! term, @ type literal, # inline term) a ( opens an expression splice, the same starter twice is the literal char, an identifier is a variable splice, and anything else is a parse error; so Gleam's tuple syntax #( collides by construction with the # expression form (and Rust's format!( with !( ), and my lap-2 regex counts (17 ## vs 65 #) were unreliable because they also matched raw strings; an edition-3 fix that removes both collisions is a splice form no target language uses, for example #{expr} and !{expr}, with bare #( and !( left as literal text Next: check how many lib and eoie macro texts use #( or !( as splices today (they would need rewriting under that edition-3 rule), using the tokenizer's own TokensCli output rather than a regex")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:21 lap 4 grain: macro splice usage measured (macro-text lines in lib/spiral and eoie/src): #( expression splices 11 lines in lib (base 2, convert 2, lustre 1, listm 6) and 0 in eoie; bare !( splices 50 lines in 18 lib files and 148 in 57 eoie files; so an edition that frees #( for Gleam tuple text costs 11 lib lines (mechanical #( -> !( or a named splice), while touching !( would cost about 200 lines Next: edition 3 rule proposal: drop # as an expression starter inside macro text (keep ## as a literal for compatibility), migrate the 11 lib lines with a rule script, and let Gleam #( pass through")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 15u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: finish edition 3 and migrate everything to symbols (EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION), getting closer to x sim compatibility, then split everything to 280 (EDITION3-280-SPLIT): standardization is near; the name stays edition 3 (LANGUAGE-NAME closed); raised to P0.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 16u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 main: back to P1. I had raised it to P0 at 22:3x from the user's also-need list; the user's order is XSD first, then full hopac SOTA, so it waits behind those.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 17u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-20T03:25Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 35%.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 18u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: migrate everything destructively once edition 3's symbol version is ready, with backups, always forward when not drifting (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 19u32, "2026-10-24T19:05Z", "main", "main 15:1x (user 15:0x: the compiler must compile projects mixing edition-2 and edition-3 packages, because core is hosted upstream). Checked in the core: already per package. SpiProj schema reads edition: 2|3 per package.spiproj (schema ~178537/178661); file_default_env (~182996) resolves each file's owning package and package_default_env applies its edition, so the edition-2 parse rule (prefix * = RawLayoutIndex, ~45256) follows the package, not the process (--edition / SPIRAL_EDITION is only the default). The lexer applies edition3_symbol_token in every file by the 10-12 18:44 decision (per-package lexing only on a real conflict); conflict check today with the compiler's own tokens CLI over the 18 core files: 0 tokens spelled as an edition-3 symbol (positive control: a scratch file with := ? ?> |: gives keyword tokens). So an edition-3 project over the upstream edition-2 core needs no compiler change; the converter must skip edition-2 packages (FormatterConvert already reads editionOf per file), and the core stays words. A lexer flag becomes necessary only if upstream code starts using one of the symbol runs as an operator.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 20u32, "2026-10-30T02:04Z", "main", "10-29 22:04 main, groom: RESUMABLE. Every dep is Done (SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY), so nothing blocks it; it's Paused only for want of a picker (P1, 35%). Pick-up: the team that needs it (lane-roles), after the pack-7 install.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 21u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "main", "10-30 10:12 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R2/R4: deps Done, owner team compiler ('agile next --team compiler' hands it out), assignee agent (nothing in it is the user's). Resume when that team picks it up; the pack-7 install it waited on is done.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 22u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:1x: the user settled on 'spiral (i574n dialect)' for now (10-31 14:39); DIALECT-IDENTITY tracks the manifest split dialect = i574n / edition = <year>. The browser research's 24-month roadmap (DIRECTION-1031 note) sets edition 4 at T8 as shallow changes only, with automatic migration of the whole suite.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 23u32, "2026-11-10T23:45Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:1x compiler (groom, picked up; the 10-30 notes said only a picker was missing): edition 3 is in daily use (every sample and lib file written tonight is edition 3; the host takes --edition 2|3 / SPIRAL_EDITION), so 35% is stale. The epics gate is runnable today: the converter exists as the compiler subcommand format-corpus convert (scripts/formatter/format.ps1 convert), which tallies per file round-trip-differs and, with parse verification on, parse-same / parse-differs / parse-original-has-errors. QUEUED on europa (~/remote-stage/ed3/g.sh, waits for load < 1.5, one job): corpus.ps1 over the mirror (lib, apps, samples; 1342 tracked sample .spi), then format.ps1 convert with a per-file listing, installed pack89. Next step: read ed3/g.log at \"ed3 done\"; every round-trip-differs or parse-differs row becomes a filed item, and the epic closes when the tally is clean.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_24 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 24u32, "2026-11-11T00:32Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:3x compiler: GATE RESULT (europa ed3, installed pack89, format-corpus convert over 1610 corpus rows): apps 88 files: 892 lines converted, 0 rejected, round-trip-identical 16, round-trip-differs 72, parse-same 32; lib 173 files: 7 lines converted, round-trip-differs 173, parse-same 2; samples 1349 files: 17 lines converted, round-trip-identical 3, round-trip-differs 1346, parse-same 9. So the converter rejects nothing, but its round trip back is NOT identical on almost every file, including files where it converted 0-1 lines, which points at a systematic difference (e.g. formatting or trivia on the way back) rather than per-construct failures. Listing at europa ~/remote-stage/ed3/convert-list.tsv. Next step: diff one round-trip-differs file that had 0 converted lines to find the systematic change, fix it, rerun the gate.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_25 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 25u32, "2026-11-11T00:33Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:3x compiler: CAUSE of the 1591 round-trip-differs: a harness bug, not the converter. runConvert always does symbols -> words and compares the words text to the original, which is a round trip only for an EDITION-2 source; corpus.ps1 tags every row edition 3 (and the corpus is edition 3), so it compared edition-2 output to edition-3 input. Fix drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL3-EDITIONS\\1-convert-round-trip-per-edition.kitdo: for an edition-3 source the round trip is words, then re-lex as edition 2, then symbols, compared to the original; edition-2 sources keep the old direction. pack95 = pack89 + this (core e382cc82), queued on europa (one job, load gate): build, then the same convert over ~/remote-stage/ed3/corpus.tsv. Next step: read ~/remote-stage/pack95/g.log at \"p95 done\"; any remaining round-trip-differs is a real converter gap to file.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_26 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 26u32, "2026-11-11T00:43Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:4x compiler: GATE RERUN with the harness fix (europa, pack95 = pack89 + 1-convert-round-trip-per-edition, built in 80 s): round-trip-identical 1567 of 1610 (apps 56/88, lib 171/173, samples 1340/1349), 0 lines rejected either way. The 43 differing files: most apps rows are scripts/formatter/synthetic fixtures that are odd on purpose (crlf_lone_cr, no_trailing_newline, offgrid_call_closer, head_as_written, ...), which the round trip need not preserve; the real candidates are lib/spiral/bytes.spi, lib/spiral/keccak.spi, cells templates/new_project PROJECT_NAME.spi, 2 export-check-rust fixtures and 9 samples. Listing: europa ~/remote-stage/pack95/convert-list.tsv. Next step: diff bytes.spis round trip to classify the residue (converter gap vs trivia), file each class, and land the harness fix with pack95s program.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_27 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 27u32, "2026-11-11T00:43Z", "compiler", "DoD: both editions in one compiler (done: edition per package, the --edition 2|3 host switch); the converter round-trips the whole corpus in each editions own direction with 0 rejected lines, and every remaining round-trip difference is either a deliberately odd formatter fixture or a filed item; the harness fix (pack95 program) landed. TypeFeatures: none (lexical edition layer; parser, typechecker and backends shared).")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_28 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 28u32, "2026-11-11T00:43Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:4x compiler: correction to the next step: format-corpus convert only writes the forward symbol text (--out), not the round-trip text, so diffing bytes.spi needs a small authoring step first: a --round-trip-out dir option in runConvert that writes roundTrip next to symbolText for differing files. That is the next step (authoring, light), then the diff and classification.")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_29 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 29u32, "2026-11-11T00:45Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:4x compiler: DRAFTED drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL3-EDITIONS\\2-convert-round-trip-out.kitdo (3 replacements, stacks on program 1): runConvert takes --round-trip-out <dir> and writes the round-trip text of every differing file there (positional already skips unknown --key value pairs). pack95b = pack95 + this, queued on europa: rebuild, rerun the convert with --round-trip-out, then diff lib/spiral/bytes.spi and keccak.spi against their round trips. Next step: classify the diff at \"p95b done\".")
inl note_of_spiral3_editions_30 () = Note ("SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", 30u32, "2026-11-11T00:59Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: RESIDUE CLASSIFIED (europa pack95b, --round-trip-out wrote the 43 differing files). lib/spiral/bytes.spi and keccak.spi differ ONLY by module access respelling: the source still has the pre-dcolon form sm.length / listm.fold / sm.slice, and the round trip writes the canonical sm::length / listm::fold. So the converter is right and the source is not canonical edition 3: that residue belongs to DCOLON-MIGRATION-LINT (respell drift that escaped into lib). Expect the same class in most of the 9 samples and the apps rows; the formatter synthetic fixtures are odd on purpose. Round-trip files: europa ~/remote-stage/pack95b/rt. Next step: land the two harness programs (1-convert-round-trip-per-edition, 2-convert-round-trip-out) with the next compiler pack, and hand the respell residue to DCOLON-MIGRATION-LINT.")
inl edition3_golden_sample () = Task ("EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE", "The chat's worked program (canonical_form_certificate/main.spi at 31d26ec4, the geandra_v0_4.py fixture: unions, nominals, prototypes/instances, lambdas, records, GADT signatures, existentials) as the first edition-3 golden test: same residual as its edition-2 original on C and Rust; the Python prototype's 116 tests become acceptance cases for the real compiler instead of a separate implementation", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "edition,samples", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_edition3_golden_sample () = Planning ("EDITION3-GOLDEN-SAMPLE", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl test_run_report () = Task ("TEST-RUN-REPORT", "The test command reports discovered/executed/passed/failed/skipped/unsupported per file and in total; a deliberately failing test block fails the test command while an ordinary build stays green (the compiler skips blocks, so a green build says nothing about tests); from the 2026-10-12 review of dc7b955c; done 2026-10-12 (Kino lane, unstaged in apps/kino): Notebook.evaluate/2 in-memory entry point with per-test verdicts and a report (discovered/executed/passed/failed/skipped/unsupported/filtered/not_run); run_notebook prints one `spiral-tests file {json}` line per source file (a .spi with real tests also gets its _real.spir line) plus `spiral-tests total {json}`; non-zero exit on any failed test or cell error; lib/spiral/build.ps1 prints per-notebook and total counts and the failed test names from those lines; Kino mix test 151/151", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tests,kino", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_run_report () = Planning ("TEST-RUN-REPORT", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl test_identity_i18n () = Task ("TEST-IDENTITY-I18N", "Test names are derived through test_name_set (ASCII letters, digits, _), so Korean/Chinese/Hindi headings collapse to generic names: separate a stable test identity, a localized title, and a backend-safe symbol (three fields, not one string); done 2026-10-12: report rows carry \"symbol\" (ASCII backend-safe, e.g. test_u441_...) and \"title\" (the Unicode heading); a Unicode --test сложение passed through pwsh -> mix -> erl in a real run; lib .spi files stay byte-identical through the round trip", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tests,i18n", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_identity_i18n () = Planning ("TEST-IDENTITY-I18N", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl source_boundary_authority () = Task ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", "Kino finds test blocks with line heuristics (spi_walk/header_of: column-0 test header, 4-space body) while the compiler skips them in BlockParsing: make the compiler the one authority on source boundaries (expose test-block spans/bodies from its tokenizer), reuse it in Kino, the formatter and the notebook renderer; add tests for multiline strings, (* *) comments, malformed headers, tabs; record the indentation convention per edition (4-space extraction is a compatibility rule, not the edition-3 rule); 2026-10-14 01:47 lane 1: compiler truth extracted without touching the compiler (an fsx over a scratch copy of SpiralCompilerCore.dll: wdiff_tokenizer_all, then state.blocks whose first non-comment token is TokVar test, body = the block's lines after the header with trailing comment/blank lines dropped, since comment tokens never start or end a block in block_at); Kino truth = Document.parse_spi + Document.tests; corpus (582 .spi in spiral lib+apps, eoie src, dice, alphabet, polyglot lib): before and after 373 compiler test blocks = 373 Kino test cells, 0 count and 0 body disagreements (2 name rows differ only because the extractor reads keyword names test forall / test exists as non-TokVar); 11-file edge suite (col-0 doc and line comments inside a body, test b = 1, test c without =, test<TAB>d =, unknown flag, test i=, col-0 continuation of a triple-quoted string and of a (* *) comment, tab-indented body, words starting with test): before 9 of 10 test-bearing files disagreed (5 tests the compiler skips that Kino silently compiled as code, 4 bodies cut short); after 10/10 counts agree, the comment cases are identical, the 5 malformed headers are now failing tests (body failwith 'malformed test header: <line>' : (), a run panics with that text) instead of silent code; left 2 disagreements that need the compiler's tokenizer (col-0 continuation lines of a multi-line triple-quoted string or (* *) comment inside a test body; a line-level approximation broke parsing.spi, which has triple quotes inside a dollar-quote macro); Kino change (uncommitted, proven in a private copy first: document_test 16/16 incl. 3 new tests, notebook_report_test 23/23): apps/kino/spi/document.spi spi_walk keeps a column-0 comment line (// or (* ) in a test when an indented line follows before the next column-0 statement (resumes_test), and test_like lines that header_of rejects become Malformed test cells; for the main thread (no spiral_compiler.fs edit made): (1) one authority: a host CLI mode, e.g. SpiralCompiler.dll --test-blocks <file.spi> printing name, header line, body first/last line from wdiff_tokenizer_all + state.blocks + blockParsingIsTest (exactly what the probe does, so it can live in host Program.fs over existing public functions), then Kino's spi_walk takes spans from it and the 2 remaining cases close; (2) blockParsingParse skips every block that starts with test (blockParsingIsTest, spiral_compiler.fs ~45394) without checking the header, so a malformed header silently disappears from compilation; it should return a parse error (e.g. ExpectedTestHeader) unless the block is test <ident> [static|print_code|ignore|no_agree 'reason'|build_only] [fails ...] = with an indented body, the same grammar as Kino's header_of/modes_valid", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,kino,tests", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_source_boundary_authority () = Planning ("SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl multilingual_presentation () = Task ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", "One resolved program, several human presentations (Korean/Chinese/Hindi/Russian/Arabic and the original names): binding-aware alias tables that rename bindings only (never literals, JSON keys, FFI/external protocol names), checked by identical compile results; a pinned UAX #31 identifier profile with NFC for names only (string contents preserved); agents propose names and docs, tooling verifies; prototype mapping exists for the certificate sample (66 spellings, 4 presentations round-trip); 2026-10-14 02:32 lane 3 typed model (not compiled; %USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\multilingual\\model.spi, 255 lines of Spiral unions/records + operation types), built from the geandra_v0_4.py prototype (per-locale bijective alias tables, NFC/identifier check, constructor marker for scripts without case, structural-AST equality after each translation): bindings are keyed by canonical name + kind (term, type, nominal, union case, record field, module, type variable, pattern variable, prototype, test name) + definition site, each with an exposure (internal or reaching JSON keys, foreign symbols, external protocol names, macro text, generated public names) and the fixed spellings that are never aliased (string and macro text, keywords per edition, symbols used as keys); alias tables per language profile (direction, letter case, constructor marking, Edition2Words or Edition3Symbols keywords, UAX #31 profile with NFC) are verified against one program digest; conflicts are a union: two aliases for one binding, one alias for two bindings in scope, shadowing and capture, keyword or edition-3-symbol clashes, non-NFC/non-UAX31, alias on a fixed spelling, unmarked constructor, unknown binding, table for another program; presentation maps presented spans to canonical source ranges, measures width in East Asian display columns for the formatter's 120 rule and reports overflow lines; edits map back to canonical patches or alias renames, and are rejected when the structure or residual hash changes; Zed/Kino/terminal/X surfaces carry display-only bidi isolates and a hover with the canonical name; 2026-10-14 02:34 lane 3 correction: model.spi is now 267 lines (not 255) after adding a surface_warning union on the presentation (bidi controls stripped, grapheme count differs from columns, hover unavailable, alias not renderable: the X-compat warnings), CanonicalNeedsAlphaRename (binding_key * edition) in round_trip_error for a canonical name that is a keyword in the other edition (the prototype's 'requires alpha-renaming before Spiral export'), and a separate resolve_failure for resolve_program; record-key decision: record field symbols are aliasable bindings (RecordFieldSymbol) unless a symbol reaches output as a key (SymbolLiteralUsedAsKey / ExposedBinding with JsonKey), which stays a fixed spelling", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "TEST-IDENTITY-I18N,SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "i18n,edition,agents,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_multilingual_presentation () = Planning ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", Minutes 3000u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_1 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:46Z", "lane-a", "08:46 lane-a: width check for the formatter's 120 rule: 302 corpus lines are non-ASCII, 260 have display columns != UTF-16 length (lib 237), 0 change the over-120 verdict today. Decision: measure in East Asian display columns so presentations get the same limit. measure\\display-width.txt")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_2 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:49Z", "main-thread", "06:49 groom: unchanged; the formatter's 120-column rule still counts code points, not display width (302 non-ASCII corpus lines). P3.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_3 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom correction: the formatter does use display width where it matters. Layout.fsx fits (248) and Check.fsx (106) call Lexed.displayWidth (text elements). Only the over-120 tallies in Format.fsx (236, 247) use String.Length (UTF-16 units), so the reported counts can over-count non-ASCII lines. Next grain: switch those two tallies to displayWidth (lane A's files).")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_4 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 precision: String.Length differs from display width in both directions. Wide CJK characters count 1 but display 2, so it under-counts; combining sequences count several but display 1, so it over-counts. The tallies are approximate for non-ASCII lines; the layout itself is not affected.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_5 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:53Z", "main-thread", "06:53 groom: done for the tallies. Format.fsx over120-out, over120-out-outside-fences and the over120-left listing now use Lexed.displayWidth instead of String.Length (3 edits, LF). Smoke run on lib/spiral/sm'.spi (format, no --split-literals): exit 0 in 45 s, same counts as before on ASCII lines (3 long literals > 120, which --split-literals handles). So layout, checker and tallies all measure display width now. Remaining scope of this item: presentation of non-Latin identifiers and docs.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_6 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 6u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17 scope for edition 3: symbols plus presentations in each language's native writing, battle-testing edition 3: English (default), Portuguese, Chinese, Korean in hangul, Russian, Arabic, Spanish, French, Hindi, German, Japanese, Italian, Polish, Turkish. Goal: the canon programming term per function, code piece and notebook block in each culture's own developer jargon, not a literal gloss. Spend tokens once: a content-addressed translation cache keyed by (source text hash, language, translator id), so a name is translated once and every later run is a cache hit, like the compile memo. Benchmark the translators before choosing: a weak model, a SOTA model, and a dedicated MT system, scored by back-translation agreement, identifier validity (UAX 31 + NFC) and spot checks.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_7 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 7u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread: English stays the default, and the translations feed back into picking it (user flagged this may be a stretch). A deterministic rule that fits the cache: for each candidate English name, take every cached round trip (English -> language -> English); the score is the number of languages whose round trip returns the same name. The highest score wins, ties go to the shorter name and then lexicographic order. Same cache, same choice; a new translator id invalidates only its own rows. Feeds LIB-CANON-NAMES.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_8 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 8u32, "2026-10-19T12:39Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:39 helper-lane lap 1: LAB-IN-REPO fix: the 10-14 typed model existed only in job tmp (%USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\multilingual\\model.spi, 268 lines, LF, comment-free: nominals for names/tags/digests, source_range, binding keys by kind, exposure, alias tables, conflict and round-trip unions). Copied it into the repo lab as spiral/lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/model.spi so it survives job-tmp cleanup and stays reviewable; the title's job-tmp path is now historical (original left in place). Deps unchanged: TEST-IDENTITY-I18N and SPIRAL3-EDITIONS.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_9 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 9u32, "2026-10-19T13:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:25 helper-lane lap 2: the rescued 10-14 model still type-checks on today's compiler: lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/model.spi as a module under a trivial probe_main.spi (try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy) compiles with exit 0, so its 268 lines of unions/records (binding keys, exposures, alias tables, conflicts, round-trip errors, surface warnings) are valid Spiral against the samples core after two weeks of compiler packs. That makes it usable as the typed spec when TEST-IDENTITY-I18N and SPIRAL3-EDITIONS open.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_10 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 10u32, "2026-10-19T14:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:57 helper-lane lap 3: first data instance of the model: lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/certificate_aliases.spi gives samples/contract_end_to_end_execution_certificate four alias tables (Korean, Chinese, Russian, Arabic) over its four user bindings, keyed by exact byte ranges in main.spi (certificate type 618-629, Certificate case 665-676, certify 924-931, render 1227-1233). Each table carries its language_profile: Korean, Chinese and Arabic are ScriptWithoutCase, so their constructors use ConstructorPrefixMark and the type and the case can share a spelling (인증서, 证书, شهادة) while Russian keeps UppercaseInitial (сертификат / Сертификат); Arabic is RightToLeft. Origin is AgentProposed, so check_alias_table must still verify them. Probe (try.ps1 C with model.spi + the table module, bounded): compiles, exit 16 = 4 tables x 4 entries, so non-Latin localized_name literals pass through the compiler and C intact.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_11 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 11u32, "2026-10-19T15:37Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:37 helper-lane lap 4: first check_alias_table rule as code, lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/check_rules.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): two entries of one table that share a spelling are an OneAliasForTwoBindingsInScope conflict only when their bindings live in the same Spiral namespace (terms: term bindings, union case constructors, pattern variables, fields, prototypes; types: type, nominal and type-variable bindings; modules; test names). That is why the certificate tables' shared type/case spelling (인증서 for both certificate and Certificate) is legal: the type and the constructor are in different namespaces, so constructor marking is a readability rule, not a uniqueness one. Expected exit 1: the four certificate tables give 0 conflicts, and a Korean table that spells certify and render both 표시하다 gives exactly 1.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_12 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: check_rules.spi exits 1 as predicted - the four certificate tables have no same-namespace spelling conflict, and the Korean table spelling certify and render alike yields exactly one OneAliasForTwoBindingsInScope.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_13 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): check_rules.spi unrun (jobs held) -> next: run after main's go | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): check_rules PASS (1) -> next: AliasIsKeyword rule | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: check_rules.spi 1 -> next: next rule")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_14 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 14u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_multilingual_presentation_15 () = Note ("MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION", 15u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION folder (5 files: certificate_aliases.spi, certificate_probe.spi, check_rules.spi, model.spi, probe_main.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
