inl kino_default_target () = Task ("KINO-DEFAULT-TARGET", "2026-10-13 07:28 an untargeted test block or a file's 'test x static' prelude compiles Rust (Builders.default = blank(:rust)); user: 'rust is slow to compile, we need a reason for having such cells'; make the default target configurable per package or file (apps/tibia wants c) and stop compiling the static prelude cell natively; serves: fast Tibia iterations; 2026-10-13 08:45 done: untargeted tests use the ///> lines of the file's static prelude test (///> c above test x static =), else SPIRAL_KINO_DEFAULT_TARGET / default_target:, else Rust host; static preludes are never compiled or run (row status static, own count, not executed); real run: default_fixture with ///> c prelude ran its test on c (passed), prelude static; tests in notebook_report_test.exs, notebook_test.exs updated; README", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,tibia,performance", "current", "current")
inl kino_one_front_end_per_test () = Task ("KINO-ONE-FRONT-END-PER-TEST", "2026-10-13 07:28 measured on apps/tibia (per-target ms): rust avg 10.5 s (max 22.5), c 8.2 s, wgsl 7.5 s, lua 6.4 s per test; C's time is mostly the Spiral compile, not gcc, and each extra ///> target repeats the whole front end and partial evaluation; partial-evaluate once per test and emit every listed backend from that result, so an extra target costs only its codegen and native build; serves: multi-target tests (c oracle + wgsl GPU + lua live) without multiplying compile time; 2026-10-13 09:51 Kino lane measurement (apps/tibia damage.spi, 3 c tests + 1 wgsl, warm daemon, memo off): every unit-ending test compiled twice (attempt 1 failed main : i32 = <unit>, then the 0i32 retry; daemon log error+change per test); fix: scoped test bodies ending in an _assert call emit spiral_kino_test () then 0i32, so one compile; per-test 11.8-15.7 s -> 5.6-8.7 s, Kino compile phase 3.4-6.0 s -> 2.8-3.0 s, server compile ~0.7-0.9 s per test; 2026-10-13 12:24 final (damage.spi, 3 c + 1 wgsl, warm, memo off, pool default): per test 3.9-4.0 s (from 11.8-15.7 s this morning), whole file 15 s wall (from 40 s); phases now compile 0.8-2.0 s, queue 0.4-2.0 s, target (gcc -O2 + run) 0.9-1.3 s, mount 8-20 ms, cleanup 3-28 ms, so Kino's own overhead is gone; a second ///> target of the same test is already a front-end hit ('compile unchanged' in the daemon log: the server reuses parse/typecheck for the unchanged file and only partial-evaluates and generates for the new backend, ~1-1.6 s), and partial evaluation is backend-specific, so no further sharing is possible there", Feature, P2, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,compiler,tests,performance", "current", "current")
inl tibia_cooldown_per_spell () = Task ("TIBIA-COOLDOWN-PER-SPELL", "2026-10-13 08:03 found by the new GADT readiness test (apps/tibia/readiness.spi): cooldowns.spi kept one shared spell_ready_at, so any spell's own cooldown blocked every other spell (a heal right after a strike was refused); Canary keeps the own cooldown per spell id (spells.cpp: CONDITION_SPELLCOOLDOWN with m_spellId) and group cooldowns per group; fixed: spell carries spell_id, timers hold strike/light_healing/haste own slots plus the three group slots; the old test own_cooldown_gates_every_group pinned the wrong belief and became own_cooldown_gates_only_the_same_spell_like_canary; sims re-pinned: standing scripted seed 7 now 0 kills / 10 escapes (its earlier kill came from the bug), hunter_survives_and_kills restated for the chasing cavebot, chasing 357 vs standing 27 kills, pack CurrentFirst 360 vs ReachFirst 59", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,fidelity,cooldowns", "current", "current")
inl tibia_typed_domain () = Task ("TIBIA-TYPED-DOMAIN", "2026-10-13 08:07 user priority: domain modeling with GADTs, existentials and HKTs instead of the PhantomData simulation used in the Rust apps; first four apps/tibia modules, each with negative tests (programs that must not compile) and C + WGSL rows: quantity.spi (phantom units: amount hitpoints/milliseconds/tiles/gold_coins; hp + ms and gold-as-health fail), readiness.spi (GADT witnesses: ready attack_group / ready healing_group, gates open only when the group and spell are ready, Strike needs an attack proof; its first run exposed TIBIA-COOLDOWN-PER-SPELL), catalog.spi (existential spawn catalog: kind k GADT over troll/goblin/cyclops tags, entry = exists k. spawn k, a troll spawn cannot hold a cyclops), views.spi (HKT: prototype mapped f with instances one and lanes, decide_all written once over f : * -> *, a view without mapped is rejected); next: wire them into the simulators (mixed packs from the catalog, strikes gated by readiness proofs, units through hunt state), then the lanes view for batched GPU evaluation; limitation: constructors are public, so a witness can be forged by calling the constructor directly (needs module-private constructors); 2026-10-13 08:13 wired in: pack_sim now runs a camp of three kinds (existential catalog entries unpacked by catalog.entry_creature; the all-troll numbers are unchanged, a mixed troll/goblin/troll camp gets 357 kills over 20 seeds), strikes require a readiness.ready attack_group proof (a bare timestamp is rejected), the player's health is quantity.amount hitpoints through the whole state; finding: record update is structural, so {p with player_health = quantity.ms 150} type-checks and makes a new record type; the unit error only appears where a pack is expected (respawn_player), so state that must keep its field types should be a nominal, not a type alias; 2026-10-13 08:52 vocations.spi: spell v GADT indexed by vocation tags (knight, paladin, sorcerer, druid); single-vocation spells are typed by their vocation (WoundCleansing, Berserk : spell knight; DivineHealing : spell paladin), shared spells need a healer v proof (LightHealing, IntenseHealing), and no knight healer exists, so a knight casting exura is a compile error; data from Canary's spell scripts (words, level, mana, cooldowns, group); found: the simulators' knight was casting Light Healing, which knights cannot cast; it now casts Wound Cleansing (same 1 s / 1 s cooldowns, own cooldown slot WoundCleansingSpell), all pinned numbers unchanged; package order matters (a module sees only earlier modules), so vocations sits before readiness; 2026-10-13 16:58 afternoon additions, all bit-exact refactors or new rules with negative tests: readiness gains an action-slot witness (ready action_slot from the 250 ms parity gate; potions and retreat steps need it; an attack proof cannot pay for one); the objective is a unit-indexed GADT (Kills :: objective kill_score, Profit/ProfitKeepingKills/CheapClearWithin :: objective gold_coins, measured returns amount unit; kills_and_gold_do_not_add fails to compile); network weights are addressed through feature/output unions and a hidden nominal (an output passed as a feature fails); terrain is a layout union with a pure integer blocked function (the scanned arena spot as row masks, so WGSL works); combat has a typed gear record from Canary's formula; views gains a trio container and a counted HKT prototype (instances for one, lanes and trio), and the pack now holds its foes as views.trio with attacker counts and occupancy as counted folds; quantity gains scaled; gold and potion costs are amount gold_coins; next: module-private constructors (SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS) so witnesses cannot be forged; 2026-10-14 07:35 GPU: new notebook apps/tibia/gpu_sim.spi pairs C and WGSL runs of the multi-creature simulator: 10 ticks, a full 3000-tick troll hunt and 20 arena-spot cyclops clears are bit-exact on WGSL (4/4 green); throughput waits on WGSL-PARALLEL-LANES (single-thread dispatch today); 2026-10-14 07:44 the benchmark protocol is now type-checked: hunting_pack gains role-indexed seed blocks (nominal training, held_out; union seed_block role = TrainingBlock :: seed_range -> seed_block training | HeldOutBlock :: seed_range -> seed_block held_out) and typed entry points (cavebot_heal_tuned_on, rules_evolved_on, tactic_evolved_on, worth_on_training take only training blocks; judged_on, clears_judged_on only held-out blocks; ledger_on either); every benchmark test in pack_sim migrated (no raw seed ranges left), all 20 tests green with unchanged pins; tuning on held-out seeds and judging on training seeds are compile-time failures (two negative tests)", Feature, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,modeling,gadt,existential,hkt", "current", "current")
inl spiral_private_constructors () = Task ("SPIRAL-PRIVATE-CONSTRUCTORS", "2026-10-13 08:13 GADT witnesses in apps/tibia (readiness.spi: ready attack_group / ready healing_group) are a convention because any module can call the constructor (AttackReady (quantity.ms 0)) and forge a proof; the mega samples hit the same wall (negative_forge_constructive_minimality_witness compiles once names are visible: 'a nominal constructor is public'); add module-private constructors (or a sealed union whose cases are constructible only inside its module) so a witness can only come from its checking function; serves: typed domain modeling that replaces Rust PhantomData patterns", Feature, P2, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,language,modeling,gadt", "current", "current")
inl c_self_tail_loop_heap () = Task ("C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP-HEAP", "C self-tail loop for methods with refcounted parameters: pack-1 C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP converts only heap-free self-tail methods; a loop closing over a heap value (tibia neural tactic holding the weights array, 3000 ticks) stays recursive, the incref/decref around the call also blocks gcc's sibcall, and it overflows the 1 MB Windows stack (0xC00000FD) even at -O2; extend the conversion by emitting the incref/decref the call would have done before continue; filed 2026-10-13 09:10 from the tibia lane; 2026-10-13 11:57 pack 2 (11:00:55 Release) fixes hunt_sim's loops; the kiting-network simulation still overflows (repro %USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\ac4b4e98\\tmp\\c_network_loop, loop17: self call inside an if whose result fills a tail let, heap v0 decref'd in the final statement); main's pack-3 candidate targets it; 2026-10-13 12:35 main: landed in pack 2 (direct self call: the dropped return's refcount ops run before continue) and fixed in pack 2c after the tibia lane hit a regression (the hook leaked into lazily generated nested methods: USDecref0(&(v16)) in irwin_hall_twelve; now scoped to direct self calls and cleared per method); pack 3 candidate generalizes tail position: kept tail lets at any depth compose their refcount ops before continue (tibia c_network_loop loop17: overflow -> C=Rust=1); probes -O0/-O2 = Rust on both cores; regression sample c_self_tail_loop_heap; 2026-10-13 18:05 main: blessed with packs 3+4: single-flight-20261013-174154 reproduces reviewed single-flight-20261013-153507 on all 3872 rows (0 differences), DISAGREE 0; hopac-20261013-161426 DISAGREE 0 (15 load timeouts rerun ok in isolation)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,c-backend,performance", "current", "current")
inl kino_rust_flags_parity () = Task ("KINO-RUST-FLAGS-PARITY", "Kino builds Rust as plain rustc --edition 2021 (debug) while the harness uses -C opt-level=2; a 7600-simulation tibia training test hits Kino's 300 s timeout in debug Rust; align Rust flags with the harness the way KINO-C-FLAGS-PARITY did for C; filed 2026-10-13 09:10 from the tibia lane; 2026-10-13 09:51 done: Spiral.Kino.rustc_args = harness's Rust row flags (+1.99.0 when rustc is rustup's proxy, -C opt-level=2 -A warnings --edition 2024); unit test in kino_test.exs; target_audit rust_host ok; README; Tibia lane confirmed the 960-simulation test runs in 3.5 min instead of the 300 s timeout", Story, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,rust-backend,performance", "current", "current")
inl c_array_create_init () = Task ("C-ARRAY-CREATE-INIT", "!!!!ArrayCreate on C returns uninitialized memory while Rust fills Default, so a program that reads before writing differs across backends; decide one semantics for every backend (zero/default fill, measured cost) and add a regression sample; found 2026-10-13 09:10 by the tibia lane; 2026-10-13 15:07 main, decided by measurement (experiment-until-sota): C ArrayCreate always zero-fills (init_at_zero true; upstream zero-filled only heap-element arrays); cost best-of-7 malloc+fill: 1e3 0.002 ms all variants, 1e6 2.32 -> 2.58 ms (memset) / 2.41 (calloc), 1e8 281 -> 334 (+19%) / 324 (+15%); kept memset because the allocator is configurable ({malloc}), calloc would bypass a custom one; sample array_create_reads_zero (old array filled with 7 then freed, new array read before write): pack 3 C -O0 = 705 (stale 7), pack 4 C -O0/-O2 = Rust = 5; in pack 4 with C-US-REFC-NOOP, corpus green on both cores; 2026-10-13 18:05 main: blessed with packs 3+4: single-flight-20261013-174154 reproduces reviewed single-flight-20261013-153507 on all 3872 rows (0 differences), DISAGREE 0; hopac-20261013-161426 DISAGREE 0 (15 load timeouts rerun ok in isolation)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,c-backend,semantic-parity", "current", "current")
inl c_deep_decref () = Task ("C-DEEP-DECREF", "C heap-union teardown recursed once per node (UHDecref -> UHDecrefBody -> UHDecref of the tail, free after the call), so dropping a 1,000,000-node list overflowed the 1 MB Windows stack even at -O2 (Rust survives only through its 1 GB main thread); found 2026-10-13 09:16 by main while probing C-SELF-TAIL-LOOP-HEAP; fix in pack 2: heap unions with a self-typed field get an iterative UHDecref (loop while refc drops to 0, other fields decref'd normally, last self-typed field becomes next); trees still recurse by depth only; Cpp/Cuda generators have the same shape (check next); 2026-10-13 12:35 main: in pack 2 (iterative UHDecref for heap unions with a self-typed field); sample c_deep_list_decref (1,000,000 nodes, C=Rust=4); 13 suite C residuals changed by it, all oracle-agree; 2026-10-13 14:04 main: blessed with pack 2c (single-flight-20261013-133955 reproduces reviewed single-flight-20261013-112227 row for row except the 4 rewritten samples; hopac-20261013-115605 DISAGREE 0; 3856 rows)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,c-backend,memory", "current", "current")
inl wgsl_unit_loop_break () = Task ("WGSL-UNIT-LOOP-BREAK", "2026-10-13 09:28 codegenWgsl: a unit-returning self-tail loop (let rec fill (i : i32) : () = if i < count then (set x i v; fill (i + 1))) compiles to loop { if (cond) { ...; continue; } else { } } with no break/return on the fallthrough path, so the shader never ends (spiral-wgsl hangs, Kino cell timeout 300 s); C exits 0; repro %USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\ac4b4e98\\tmp\\wgsl_net (probe.spi, fn sp_fill_0); blocks apps/tibia network.spi on the GPU (network_decides_on_gpu); serves: GPU training of the neural policy; 2026-10-13 09:29 main: reproduced on tibia wgsl_net (sp_fill_0: loop { if (c) { ..; continue; } else { } }); fix in the pack-2 candidate (tmp/unify/wgsl_unit_loop_exit.py): inside a WGSL self loop every non-continue unit tail path emits return; (empty return data, unit tail return', tail while); verification + regression sample with pack 2; 2026-10-13 11:57 verified by the reporter on the 11:00:55 Release DLL (pack 2): network.spi network_decides_on_gpu un-ignored and passing, hunt_sim golden_seed_7_on_gpu matches the CPU; 2026-10-13 12:35 main: in pack 2 Release since 11:00; tibia lane verified (network_decides_on_gpu un-ignored and passing); sample wgsl_unit_self_loop (WGSL=C=7); 2026-10-13 14:04 main: blessed with pack 2c (single-flight-20261013-133955 reproduces reviewed single-flight-20261013-112227 row for row except the 4 rewritten samples; hopac-20261013-115605 DISAGREE 0; 3856 rows)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "wgsl,compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_test_timeout () = Task ("KINO-TEST-TIMEOUT", "2026-10-13 09:28 '///- --timeout <ms>' extends a notebook cell's 300 s budget, but a test block ignores it: apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi's neuroevolution test (with the line above it) still died at 300 s; honour the directive (or a test-header option) for test blocks; serves: training and evaluation tests; 2026-10-13 09:44 RETRACTED by the reporter: Kino documents that --timeout is read only from the FIRST ///- line (apps/kino/README.md line 72); pack_sim.spi had it on a second line; not a bug (fix: ///- --package . --timeout <ms>); 2026-10-14 08:31 REOPENED with evidence: apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi's first line is '///- --package . --timeout 1800000' and two test blocks were still killed at 'Spiral cell timed out after 300s' (2026-10-14 08:00 and 08:06 runs); so the file-level --timeout does not reach test blocks in .spi notebooks; yesterday's retraction was wrong; the slowness itself was app-side (an inl lookup inflating generated C to 89 MB) and is fixed, so this is now P2 hygiene", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,tests,tibia", "current", "current")
inl kino_prefix_module_unbound () = Task ("KINO-PREFIX-MODULE-UNBOUND", "Kino: in a ///- --package . notebook, a package module whose name is a prefix of the notebook file stem is unbound in test programs (apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi with module pack, listed last: open pack -> ETY-UnboundModule; renamed hunting_pack passes); ruled out by the tibia lane: daemon cache, mounts, the compiler (hand-built cell package resolves open pack), core/testing bindings; likely a starts_with on the notebook stem in test-plan or module selection; filed 2026-10-13 09:37 from the tibia lane (workaround: hunting_pack); 2026-10-13 09:55 Kino lane: not reproducible on current Kino (3 real runs pass: minimal pack + pack_sim fixture; module added to the project after a first run; a copy of apps/tibia with hunting_pack renamed back to pack, listed last, golden_seed_7_troll_pack on c passes with open pack); no starts_with on the notebook stem exists in Kino's plan, mounts or memo; a fake-compile regression test (module named like a prefix of the stem stays opened in the test program) is in notebook_report_test.exs; needs the exact failing command/state from the tibia lane (likely the pre-08:30 Kino, a second ///- line, or a stale mount)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,modules", "current", "current")
inl kino_accept_slot_swap () = Task ("KINO-ACCEPT-SLOT-SWAP", "Kino --accept swapped two actual values between asserts and then looped 25 rounds: evolved |> _assert_eq 948i32 and cavebot |> _assert_eq 1099i32 with real values evolved=1099, cavebot=995 became 995/1099; likely matches the failing actual (1099) to a literal already present in the file instead of to the assert that failed; repro: ac4b4e98/tmp/accept_swap_repro_before.spi over apps/tibia/pack_sim.spi in a scratch copy, test cyclops_pack_tuned_cavebot_beats_cold_started_evolution_on_fifty_held_out_seeds, run record ac4b4e98/tmp/runs/repin.ipynb; also cost: accept fixes one assert per round and reruns the whole test (rounds x test time), fix all failing asserts of one run per round; filed 2026-10-13 10:41 from the tibia lane; 2026-10-13 10:56 done by the Kino lane: root cause: C prints the failing __assert_eq line first (builder message) and again after the passing lines, and the old position count stopped at the first mismatch, so it saw 0 earlier passes and picked the first equal literal; now the failing assert is the (k+1)-th _assert_eq written in the block where k = all passing __assert_eq lines in the output, and its literal must be E or the test is refused (never another equal literal); regression test with the repro's exact output in accept_test.exs; one assert per round stays (an assert aborts the program, so later failures are unknown until the rerun)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,testing", "current", "current")
inl spiral_record_update_expr () = Task ("SPIRAL-RECORD-UPDATE-EXPR", "2026-10-13 12:07 record update accepts only a variable before 'with': {(offense_against kind) with level = c.hunter_level} fails with 'Expected: lowercase variable' (hunting_pack.hunter_of), forcing a throwaway inl; allow an expression (parenthesized or an application) as the update base; serves: domain code that refines computed records", TaskKind, P4, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,syntax,tibia", "current", "current")
inl c_us_refc_noop () = Task ("C-US-REFC-NOOP", "C stack unions whose cases hold nothing refcounted emitted USIncref/USDecref calls with empty switch bodies; they bloat the code and keep calls out of tail position (tibia lane suggestion (a), 2026-10-13 11:51); pack 4 emits none for them: tibia c_network_loop 411 -> 0 calls (702,896 -> 695,145 bytes), c_loop_repro 29 -> 0; corpus green on both cores 2026-10-13 15:07; 2026-10-13 18:05 main: blessed with packs 3+4: single-flight-20261013-174154 reproduces reviewed single-flight-20261013-153507 on all 3872 rows (0 differences), DISAGREE 0; hopac-20261013-161426 DISAGREE 0 (15 load timeouts rerun ok in isolation)", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,c-backend,performance", "current", "current")
inl tibia_retreat_target_spread () = Task ("TIBIA-RETREAT-TARGET-SPREAD", "2026-10-13 20:00 live failure mode of the retreat-at-2 policy (1 of 17 v6 runs, arena-logs/20261013-194904-cyclops-spiral-retreat-7.log): after each retreat step ReachFirst switched to whichever cyclops was now adjacent, damage spread over all three (86% -> 48% -> 60%), no kill in 200 s; the simulator never shows it (ReachFirst retreat: 886 potions, 0 deaths, 89.0 s per 100 held-out clears); a NearbyCurrentFirst order (keep the current target within 2 tiles) collapsed like CurrentFirst in the simulator (468 s, 4807 potions) because a target two tiles away can be unreachable, so it was removed; next: find why live switching spreads damage when simulated switching does not (target choice among equally reachable cyclops, weaker_or_nearer uses health percent the live client rounds), and pin it with a simulator test before changing the live probe (2026-10-15: actually a floor change: the retreat step climbed to floor 6 and every cyclops lost the knight)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,policy,arena", "current", "current")
inl kit_one_call () = Task ("KIT-ONE-CALL", "eoie was meant to be one-call shots for everything, patch batches included (user, 2026-10-13 22:02): a verb needing a plan file written first, or separate check/rehearse/apply calls, is drift; today patch apply|check|rehearse|gated <root> <plan.spi>, proxy fs-actions/fs-batch-write <preview|apply> <root> <plan.spi> and batch-plan <root> <spec> ... are all file-then-call, and that shape was in the first substantive commit (9a5c34c 2026-10-04, extracted from the bundle), so the requirement never reached eoie's README, command spec or the agents' rules; single-edit verbs (text-replace, fs-write, command-output) are already one call; decide later between a restart and a retrofit (inline plan text or stdin in the same call, apply that validates and rehearses atomically, one receipt); 2026-10-14 03:56 lane 3: eoie do <root> '<program>' designed against all 33 cases of spec/one-call.md (%USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\eoie-do\\cases.md): EBNF surface grammar, each case written as its AST value and the receipt or error that yields its expected output; atomicity: the program is cut into segments at external effects (and at a read that depends on a staged write), every edit no earlier barrier can touch is preflighted at call start (anchor counts, expect files/replacements, root, before-hash) so a failed check writes nothing (cases 2, 26), later edits are checked at their segment, each commit re-hashes, writes temp siblings and renames in sorted order with rollback; dry-run stages and previews only; 2026-10-14 03:58 lane 3: agile do <root> '<program>' designed too (add/edit/set/note/notes from-title/rename/show/list/plan typed as agile_statement effect receipt; one read, in-memory apply in program order, validation of every statement, one atomic write guarded by StateChangedSinceRead; 8 cases A1-A8 from tonight's history, A1 replaces the 3-call title-append+set+show habit); 2026-10-14 04:04 lane 3 revision: a statement can pass while the call fails (receipt_verdict prototype: run exit n, wait/job timeouts are receipts with exit 3 that keep what they saw; call status and exit fold over verdicts and errors, error_tallies for failed statements), file handles (files ... as x binds handle file_kind, reused as a target), COMMAND is a word or quoted string; deviation stated: writes commit at barriers, so checks after a barrier cannot gate earlier writes (case 24 needs its edit on disk before the build); agile cases A1-A8 now run on synthetic DEMO-* fixtures with outputs computed from them (gap 2026-10-19: no job stop verb; a mistaken job can only be stopped by hand through its supervisor process tree)", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-JOBS,KIT-READ-QUERY,KIT-EDIT-BATCH,KIT-TABLE-QUERY,KIT-REPO-STATE,KIT-AGENT-GUIDE,KIT-IDIOMATIC-CORE,KIT-ONE-CALL-SPEC,KIT-AGILE-BODY,PS1-TO-SPIRAL", "kit,design,cli,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_one_call () = Planning ("KIT-ONE-CALL", Minutes 6135u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_one_call_1 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:23Z", "lane-a", "08:23 lane-a: surface count from eoie help: 5 verbs, agile 19 subverbs, 70 proxy capabilities; file-then-call entry points left: patch apply/check/rehearse/gated, proxy fs-batch-write/fs-actions (plan.spi), batch-plan/command-capture-matrix (spec file), bundle retention-plan/apply = 10.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_2 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: eoie help on 10-15: 6 verbs (status, patch, agile, bundle, proxy, help), 17 distinct agile subverbs, 70 proxy capabilities (unchanged since 10-14 08:23). No one-call verb landed yet; tonight's agent work still went through pwsh for reads, edits and job control (e.g. the renewal and harness runs). Still the root of the EOIE-READ-QUERY/EDIT-BATCH/JOBS/TABLE-QUERY/REPO-STATE cluster.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_3 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 provenance (see BUNDLE-WISDOM note 3): the rule is older than commit 9a5c34c. Downloads\\espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml (2026-01-08) [eoie] parallelism: 'if you feel you would run 3+ commands in a row, try to collapse them into 1'; batch 'patch, rebuild and bench in a single call'. Same lineage: bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml (01-05) allowed a 2-stage pipe with persisted intermediates.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_4 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 4u32, "2026-10-18T17:01Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 13:01 eoie lane handoff from the main lane: (a) every verb should take its operands from a file or stdin (--from FILE or -) so arguments with double quotes survive PowerShell native-argument splitting; seen twice today (a backlog title rename had to go through Python subprocess lists, and my own notes avoid quote characters for this reason); (b) agile check prints lease and catalog lines on every call, too noisy for one-call use: quiet by default, details behind a flag. Not eoie lane: scripts/publish.ps1, .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml with actions.lock, /dist/ in .gitignore belong to the main lane TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO work; coordinate before touching them")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_5 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 5u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 landed (finding a): eoie --args-from <file|-> relaunches eoie with one argument per line (CR stripped, remaining argv appended), routed before every verb in eoie_cli_model.argument_file (argument_source union, argument_text nominal); quotes and commas survive; e2e from file and stdin; Spiral test 1/1")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_6 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 6u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie lane, backlog refactor (user: spend 10 minutes refactoring the eoie backlog): the agile model has no parent field, and 11 items listed this epic as a dependency, so none of them could ever be ready while the epic is open: EOIE-JOBS (the only P0) never appeared in agile next and the epic itself scored 'unblocks=2324'. Convention now: an epic depends on its children (it is done when they are), children never depend on their epic. This epic's deps are its 11 children (EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-READ-QUERY, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, EOIE-REPO-STATE, EOIE-AGENT-GUIDE, EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC, EOIE-AGILE-BODY, EOIE-ZED-SWEEP, PS1-TO-SPIRAL). Also reversed or dropped: EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE now waits on EOIE-STYLE-CHECKPOINT (the rewrite follows the style decision, not the reverse); EOIE-SPIRAL-TESTS and EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS no longer wait on unrelated items. State backup before the edit: scratchpad eoie-backups/agile.spi.*.before-refactor.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_7 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 7u32, "2026-10-21T11:07Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:07 eoie team, day-run rules until 21:00 (user 07:05: decide everything myself, by tests, benchmarks and scratch source backups; check every hour that no lane waits on a hook). R1 release order: r26d installs -> runner jobs-home fix (-CleanOld) -> snapshot -> r28 (14 slices, 60-min test budget). R2 a failed release gets diagnosed, fixed and relaunched at once; a slice that fails its notebooks twice is pulled (backed up in scratch) and the rest ship. R3 before any release, a slice's notebook files run on the scratch tree when the dev driver can filter files; previews alone missed test-block type errors three times. R4 next work = eoie-team items by WSJF; a design trade-off is built both ways and decided by measured lines/ms, ties to the smaller, reversible one. R5 every generated file <= 1000 lines; no new rust_global. R6 git stays the user's (no stage/commit). R7 hourly hook check at :13 (cron), noted on AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_8 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 8u32, "2026-10-23T06:46Z", "tibia-lane", "Use case from tibia-lane (2026-10-23 02:4x): appending a sentence to an existing note takes three calls today (agile show, cut out the note text, agile note-edit with the full text), while edit already has title-append. Tonight the cut went wrong once: a regex on the plain show output swallowed the two following notes, and note-edit wrote all three into one note. It was caught from the byte count (1100 to 3439) and repaired with a second note-edit. show --json would have made the cut safe, but it is still read-modify-write. Requested one-call shape: agile note-edit <root> <id> <n> --append <text> (or a note-append verb), appending with one space. Expected: text_bytes grows by exactly the appended length plus one, and no other note changes.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_9 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 9u32, "2026-10-23T18:29Z", "eoie", "14:29 eoie: tibia's 06:46 use case drafted (scratch note-append/apply-note-append.ps1, release M): agile note-edit <root> <id> <n> <text> [--as <author>] [--append]. --append joins the text onto the stored note with one space in the same locked write, so adding a sentence is one call instead of show, cut and note-edit. A typed note_change (ReplaceText | AppendText) plus an order-free option fold replace the positional --as check; the author rule is unchanged; the touched integer parse moves to lib convert.try_convert; agile_subcommand's note-edit shape shows [--append]. CLI test in notes_cli: append a sentence, show reads 'first finding. second sentence.', and an unknown option leaves state unchanged. All 7 M slices apply on a fresh post-L tree.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_10 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 10u32, "2026-10-26T00:14Z", "eoie", "20:13 eoie: notes_cli test note_edit_append_adds_a_sentence_to_the_stored_note fails on the release candidate: note-edit's usage advertises --append and --as, but its route refuses 7 operands (root id number text --append --as author). Same class as the show arity gap (EOIE-SHOW-NOTE-FILTER note of the same time); fix after KIT-RENAME, tracked meanwhile as a known failure in the release notebook gate.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_11 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 11u32, "2026-10-26T06:41Z", "eoie", "02:40 eoie: route fix PROVEN on the worker node. Draft kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\route-arity.do: AgileShow and AgileNoteEdit route through agile_with_root, so each verb's own parser validates its options (show refuses with 'options are --json, --last <n> or --grep <word>'; note-edit prints its full usage). Candidate: renamed tree plus the kit_named length fix plus this draft; kit_adoption and kit_agile_mutation regenerated; debug build; this box's compiler (core CDD68DC3EE829A67); SPIRAL_LIB from a synced spiral mirror. Strict verdict (executed above 0, passed equal to executed, unsupported 0; positive control kit_lcov executed 2, passed 2): 10/10 agile and notes notebooks pass, notes_cli included, so show_reads_the_newest_or_matching_notes_with_their_numbers and note_edit_append_adds_a_sentence_to_the_stored_note both pass. The help shape string is deliberately unchanged: spec/one-call.md line 296 searches for the literal shape, and README and the subcommand test pin it, so the shape text is its own coordinated change. Lands right after KIT-RENAME; then both rows leave the release known-failures table.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_12 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 12u32, "2026-10-29T23:23Z", "main", "10-29 19:23 main, fill-all-task-fields: estimate set to 6135 min by the epic rule (the sum of each open child's estimate x (100 - progress)%, all children estimated): EOIE-JOBS 18, EOIE-READ-QUERY 54, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH 45, EOIE-IDIOMATIC-CORE 1800, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC 78, EOIE-ZED-SWEEP 60, PS1-TO-SPIRAL 4080; the other 4 children are Done. Re-derive it when a child's estimate or progress moves. Epic progress stays at its owner's value.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_13 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 13u32, "2026-11-01T13:15Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: dropped EOIE-ZED-SWEEP from this umbrella's deps. It is Paused indefinitely (resume trigger: Zed's threads.db changes; unchanged since 10-12), and an umbrella waiting on an indefinite pause can never close. It stays related: when Zed is used again the sweep adds adoption evidence here.")
inl note_of_kit_one_call_14 () = Note ("KIT-ONE-CALL", 14u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-ONE-CALL (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_read_query () = Task ("KIT-READ-QUERY", "one-call read and search for every agent: files, line ranges, globs, regex with context, many files at once, rayon-parallel, build/deps/target and junction cycles skipped by default, bounded output, --json; replaces ~22.3k file reads and ~13.2k searches (Get-Content/ReadAllText/Select-String/Get-ChildItem and native Read/Grep/Glob/read_file/grep); 48% of shell calls chain 3+ operations, so read, search, filter and count must compose inside one call; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D5, 70u32, Planned, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_read_query () = Planning ("KIT-READ-QUERY", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_read_query_1 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 1u32, "2026-10-14T10:54Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 06:54 reprioritized P0 -> P1 with EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC: the formatter comes before further eoie work, and the restart style waits for EOIE-STYLE-CHECKPOINT")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_2 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 2u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: measured rust_global in eoie/src (392 hits): parallel-search 4.3 s (re-runs serial 3.5 s for parity), fs-search 0.8 s; both print whole lines unbounded (hits of 4 KB+). Spec cases 3,7,8,10,18 = 3,507 recorded calls, all red.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_3 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: unchanged; tonight's reads were Grep/Read tools plus pwsh Select-String. No new measurement.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_4 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:55Z", "main-thread", "06:55 groom: related tool friction found while grooming: PowerShell splits a note argument with embedded double quotes (eoie reported 6 operands). A read/query verb that takes its arguments from a file or stdin avoids that class.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_5 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:23 from the bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 8): treat EPIPE (output piped into head) as a normal stop, not a panic; one root sentinel '.' accepted by every read/search/list/manifest verb; native per-file line counts (the most repeated gap, wc in about 15 rounds); one source classifier (own/generated/vendor/reference/evidence), because source-stats counted evidence .spi as own code and disagreed with source-topology on crate max; bounded tail and numbered slices; help schemas generated from the dispatch registry.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_6 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 6u32, "2026-10-18T15:38Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:38 main-thread evidence: editing an agile title that contains double quotes (KINO-SOTA) from PowerShell splits the argument; the 21-title rename went through Python subprocess argument lists instead. Arguments from a file or stdin (--from <file>/-) would remove the whole class for every verb.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_7 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 7u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "eoie read/lines/grep e2e (lab/EOIE-READ-QUERY/read-e2e.ps1) 5/5 on build 1 (19:12), including the receipt line and the missing-file failure; receipt crate tests green in the build 1 suite (eoie_receipt 1, read_query model 1).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_8 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 8u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane groom: most of this item exists (read <path>[:from-to] [--max-lines], lines <glob>, grep <root> <needle> [--glob --context --max --ignore-case], plus eoie do search/read/files/head/tail), rayon-parallel, build/deps/target skipped, bounded output; the 0% here was stale. Missing was --json: grep ignored it and read took it for a path. Now in source: grep and read --json, by splitting the two print-everything host blobs into thin host loaders plus Spiral renderers (search.spi, slice.spi); the text form must stay byte-identical, checked by a parity step in the next release run (old vs new eoie on 4 grep and 4 read queries) and notebook src/eoie_read_query/grep_cli.spi.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_9 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 9u32, "2026-10-21T01:38Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:38 eoie lane, status catch-up: grep and read moved from rust_global into Spiral with --json (release 19, 10-20, parity-checked against the old text output on 8 real queries); remaining from the title: globs and many-file reads in one call, regex context options as typed queries.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_10 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 10u32, "2026-10-21T11:28Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:28 eoie team: the quoted-argument friction in notes 4 and 6 is covered by 'eoie --args-from <file|->' (eoie_cli_model/argument_file.spi: one argument per line, relaunch without a shell, so quotes survive). Checked live just now: a plain 'agile note' with embedded double quotes failed with 6 operands, and the same note through --args-from landed. Remaining scope from the title: globs and many-file reads in one call.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_11 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 11u32, "2026-10-21T13:56Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:56 eoie team: globs in read drafted for r30 (scratch apply-read-globs.ps1, 3 base-hash guards). lines already expanded a one-level glob inside its print blob; that expansion is now one glob_paths primitive in eoie_read_query/model.spi (same rule: one * in the last path segment, files only, sorted, a plain path passes through) used by both verbs. read src/x/*.spi:1-20 reads every match with the same range in one call; a glob matching nothing reports one unreadable entry (exit 2), like a missing file. lines' blob now only reads the paths it is given, so its output must stay byte-identical: the r30 release job adds a lines parity step (old vs new binary on a glob, plain paths and misses). CLI test read_and_lines_expand_a_glob_in_one_call in grep_cli.spi. Recursive ** globs are in the same slice as their own primitive (walks below the ** prefix, skips target, .git and node_modules, matches the file name against the mask after **, sorted, / separators), chosen in Spiral by the pattern shape so the flat rule lines relied on stays as it was; the CLI test covers notes/**/*.spi. The title scope is then complete once r30 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_12 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 12u32, "2026-10-21T22:33Z", "eoie-lane", "18:33 eoie team: r30's read globs are in the r30-r33 release (queued for a heavy slot, install ~19:55). r35 adds two fixes found by dogfooding today (EOIE-GREP-FILE-ROOT): grep on a single file (a file root searched 0 files with exit 0) and read <file>:-N for the last N lines (':-3' silently printed the whole file). read_query.rs is 993 generated lines after both: the next change to this crate splits it first. After r30 installs: progress 80; Done when r35 ships and the one-call guide points agents at read/grep instead of Get-Content/Select-String (still the largest habits: 664 and 595 calls in the eoie lane on 10-21).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_13 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 13u32, "2026-10-21T22:46Z", "eoie-lane", "18:46 eoie team: drafted the read_query split for r36 (scratch apply-grep-split.ps1, needs r35): grep moves into a new crate eoie_grep_query (search.spi moved whole; grep.spi takes search_options, search_receipt and the grep branch from read.spi; usage text still comes from eoie_read_query.model through the package import), VerbGrep routes to eoie_grep_run, read_query keeps read/lines (slice.spi takes json_text and stdout_text). Workspace member, eoie-cli dependency and a Cargo.lock entry inserted in sorted order. This makes room for the next read/grep features (e.g. grep --regex; the needle is literal today) without crossing the 1000-line gate. Preview chained (r36-early on top of the r35 check tree).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_14 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 14u32, "2026-10-21T23:07Z", "eoie-lane", "19:07 eoie team: r36 slice 2 drafted (scratch apply-grep-regex.ps1, after the split): eoie grep <root> <needle> --regex. A needle_kind union (LiteralNeedle | CaselessNeedle | PatternNeedle caseless) picks a line_matcher primitive (an Arc<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool + Send + Sync> for the rayon walk); hits_found takes the matcher instead of needle + ignore_case, so the matching rule lives in Spiral and the blob only applies it. An invalid pattern is refused before the walk ('grep: invalid --regex pattern: ...', exit 2). Usage updated in read_query and the command spec; grep_cli test for alternation and a bad pattern; regex dependency + lock entry for eoie-grep-query. Preview and notebook typecheck chained.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_15 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 15u32, "2026-10-21T23:09Z", "eoie-lane", "19:09 r36 slice 2 checked: preview exit 0 (grep_query.rs 473, read_query.rs 632, command_spec.rs 962), read_query model and command spec notebooks pass; grep_cli's new regex test fails only at runtime against the pre-r36 debug binary, like the other new CLI tests.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_16 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 16u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): READ-1 (root sentinel, absolute roots), READ-2 (count-only, resume, and an absence receipt with a positive control, per grep-brace-glob-silent), READ-3 (EPIPE), FS-8 (non-UTF-8 paths and lossy reads), CENSUS-1 (all-language census with origin classes). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_17 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 17u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 180m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D5 at 65%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_18 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 18u32, "2026-10-27T16:04Z", "kit", "12:0x kit: pendencies checked on the installed kit.exe. READ-1 holds for grep: a '.' root and an absolute root both search (files 6 searched, 2 matched). READ-3 was still open: 'kit grep <src> fn' piped into a reader that closes after one line panicked with 'failed printing to stdout: The pipe is being closed. (os error 232)'. Fix: kit_cli/main.spi installs one panic hook before run that ends the process with exit 0 on a stdout-printing panic and prints any other panic as before (clean/kit-rename/cli-closed-pipe.do, one host expression; kit.rs 971, +1). Proven in a harness with the same print pattern: without the hook the identical panic reproduces (positive control); with it, stderr is empty and the exit is clean. Queued as next-train step 5d. Left here: READ-2 (budget-aware search: depth, count-only, absence receipt), FS-8 (non-UTF-8 paths), CENSUS-1, plus --limit/--offset pagination from READ-3's text.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_19 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 19u32, "2026-10-27T16:05Z", "kit", "12:0x kit: READ-2 re-measured. Count-only already works as 'grep <root> <needle> --max 0': only the receipt prints, with full counts ('files 6 searched 2 matched matches 5 shown 0'). The absence receipt exists: an absent needle reports 'files 6 searched 0 matched matches 0', and the files-searched count is the positive control that the walk ran (with the existing literal-needle hint against the silent-glob trap). Still missing from READ-2: --depth, and shallow listing by name for very large trees.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_20 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 20u32, "2026-10-27T21:25Z", "kit", "kit 17:2x, where --depth (READ-2) has to go: the walk lives in parallel_search_contract/search_terminal.spi, a large legacy raw-Rust terminal with three hand-written fs::read_dir recursions (lines ~77, ~147, ~246). Under the AGENTS contract, touching it means migrating it, so --depth is a slice of its own: move the walk into the rust_std_fs boundary as one primitive (a bounded directory listing with a depth, reparse points skipped, as tonight's scratch-archive fix does) and keep the search logic in Spiral. Then a depth test plus a spec case. Shallow listing by name for very large trees falls out of the same primitive (depth 1). Not started; ranked after TRAIN's post-install work.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_21 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 21u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit; next step per its last note: READ-2 --depth as its own slice, moving the walk from search_terminal.spi's three raw fs::read_dir recursions into one rust_std_fs bounded-listing primitive.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_22 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 22u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_23 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 23u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-READ-QUERY (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_24 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 24u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries FS-8 (Byte-safe paths and content); READ-2 (Budget-aware search and listing); CENSUS-1 (All-language census). Why: 10-24 note #16: non-UTF-8 paths, count-only/resume/absence receipt, all-language census with origin classes (READ-1 and READ-3 now ship, see Done rows)")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_25 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 25u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. FS-8 · Byte-safe paths and content · P2 · ota, ge2e · Open / Legacy archives produce non-UTF-8 file names (`Atualizações` in CP850) that `find` printed lossily and `slice` could not reopen; logs with one invalid byte could not be read at all. Keep raw-byte path identity with a stable token and an optional ASCII alias, and add `--encoding utf8-lossy|binary|hex` to reads with a receipt of substitutions; strict UTF-8 stays the default. Replacements need a byte-level mode too: `text-replace` refused a TSV that was not valid UTF-8. || READ-2 · Budget-aware search and listing · P1 · ota, erp, ge2e, sbin · Partial / Broad search over million-line trees or corpora of 338k files hit the caller timeout before returning anything. Add filename/suffix/glob filters, max depth, shallow listing by name, summary-only counts, repeatable needles in one call, result limits, partial results that persist, resume by shard, predicates over the header and body of many files, a list of files changed in the last N minutes, and a receipt that records patterns proven absent. `fs-list`'s second argument looks like a filter but is a path; add `--match/--glob`. *Now:* `eoie grep` (parallel, skips target/.git/deps, `--max`, `--context`) and `do search ... first n per file` exist; there is no resume, count-only mode or absence receipt. || CENSUS-1 · All-language census · P0 · node, ota, sbin, erp, ge2e, brz, scp · Open / `source-stats` counts Rust and Spiral; every project still counted C/C++, C#, F#, TypeScript, Pascal, SQL, PowerShell, shell and Python externally (it returned zero for a 170k-line F# compiler). Report per language with owned / generated / supplied / vendor origin, comment-only lines, planned vs implemented, scoped to a subtree, written to a receipt (`--out`), and diffed against a baseline bundle. Never assume every directory named `target` is build output.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_26 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 26u32, "2026-11-09T08:51Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 04:5x local rig, FS-8 first slice (disclosure, unprobed, rides TRAIN43): measured on installed kit 5B9082C1: kit read of a file with one invalid UTF-8 byte (ba 0xE7 o) prints line 2 as ba U+FFFD o with exit 0 and NO receipt of the substitution; do search reads it the same way. Change in src/rig_read_query/slice.spi, pure Spiral (the census ratchet refuses new host blocks in this legacy crate): replaced_in counts U+FFFD per loaded line, the header gains ; N invalid UTF-8 sequences shown as U+FFFD and --json gains replaced:N, both only when N > 0, so existing receipts are unchanged. Caveat: a file that already holds U+FFFD counts it too (wording says shown as, not replaced). Still open from FS-8: --encoding utf8-lossy|binary|hex, raw-byte path identity, byte-level replace; READ-2 (count-only, resume, absence receipt) and CENSUS-1 untouched.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_27 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 27u32, "2026-11-09T10:00Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:0x local rig, READ-2 checked on installed kit 5B9082C1: the absence receipt EXISTS (do search with no match: ok files 1 searched 0 matched matches 0), count-only and paths-only exist in the do grammar (paths, first n per file, context). Gap left: a glob that matches NO files is indistinguishable in exit code from a clean miss (search nothere in *.md on a root with no .md: ok files 0 searched, exit 0), so a mistyped glob reads as not found. Proposal: files 0 searched -> a distinct receipt word (no-files) and exit 3, documented in the do grammar; behavior change, so it goes in with a spec case. Resume (READ-2) is covered by the resume verb (spec case 54).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_28 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 28u32, "2026-11-09T10:02Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:1x local rig, READ-2 zero-file gap, COMPATIBLE half written (rides TRAIN43): src/rig_do_read/run.spi receipt_counts appends \"  no file matched the glob\" when a program searched and 0 files matched its glob; exit stays 0 so scripts that search to confirm absence keep working (an exit-code change needs a spec case and the user's view). CLI test a_search_whose_glob_matches_no_file_says_so in src/rig_do_edit/edit_cli.spi (beside the other do tests): a miss in an existing file keeps today's receipt, a glob with no files carries the new words. do_read.rs was 967 generated lines; this adds one branch.")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_29 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 29u32, "2026-11-09T10:59Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "DoD: every read/search reports what it could not do (invalid UTF-8 shown as U+FFFD counted in the header, a glob with no files named in the receipt), reads offer --encoding utf8-lossy|binary|hex with a substitution receipt, and byte-level replace exists for non-UTF-8 tables. TypeFeatures: a read_encoding union (Strict, Lossy, Binary, Hex) and a read_disclosure record (replaced, unmatched_globs).")
inl note_of_kit_read_query_30 () = Note ("KIT-READ-QUERY", 30u32, "2026-11-09T14:28Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 10:4x local rig, TYPED MODEL BUILT (unstaged, unprobed; in the post-TRAIN42e probe batch): src/rig_read_query/encoding.spi, registered after model in package.spiproj. read_encoding union Strict | Lossy | Binary | Hex with encoding_of (utf8/strict, utf8-lossy, binary, hex; an unknown word is refused, never guessed); read_disclosure record { replaced; unmatched_globs } with disclosure_notes naming every gap (\"N invalid UTF-8 sequences shown as U+FFFD\", \"no file matched the glob G\"); strict_refusal (Strict + replaced > 0 refuses and names the three other encodings); hex_row offset bytes via lib crypto.hex_byte. NEXT STEP after the probe: route --encoding through the do read grammar and the read verb (slice.spi already counts U+FFFD for the Lossy disclosure), then byte-level replace for non-UTF-8 tables.")
inl kit_edit_batch () = Task ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", "one-call multi-file edit: inline anchored replacements with expected counts, whole-file writes and apply_patch-style hunks, validated, rehearsed and applied atomically with before/after hashes in one receipt, LF preserved; replaces ~16.1k edits/writes (.Replace+WriteAllText, python re.sub, Edit/apply_patch/search_replace/write) and today's compiler pack scripts; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_edit_batch () = Planning ("KIT-EDIT-BATCH", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl kit_jobs () = Task ("KIT-JOBS", "one-call build/test/run with receipts plus a job registry: background runs with wall-clock timeout, completion notification instead of polling, process listing scoped to the calling session with CPU deltas, and stop by job id; replaces ~10k build/test/wait/process calls (build.ps1, test.ps1, monitor.ps1 752 calls, Start-Sleep, Get-CimInstance, Stop-Process) and would have prevented the 14 h runaway searches of 2026-10-13; source: transcript sweep 2026-10-13 22:13 over ~45k tool calls (claude 41.6k, codex 2.0k, opencode 1.1k, grok 0.2k; zed 8 threads parsed, tool shape not yet recognized); report in main's job tmp/sweep/intent-report.txt", Story, P0, D6, 85u32, Planned, "", "kit,one-call,adoption,wipe-redo,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_jobs () = Planning ("KIT-JOBS", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_jobs_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:25Z", "lane-a", "08:25 lane-a: spec coverage map: cases 6,13,14,15,16,24,30,33 = 1,069 recorded calls, all red. Today's evidence: heavy-gate jobs queue silently (Invoke-Heavy waited 5+ min with only a heartbeat); a job registry should show queue position.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 2u32, "2026-10-14T17:08Z", "lane-a", "13:08 lane-a: today's heavy-gate waits for lane A's small jobs (seconds to acquire): 965, 72, 5003, 987, 1092, 950, 93, 177, 245, 338; the 83-min wait gave only a 5-min heartbeat. A job registry should show queue position, holders and an ETA.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 3u32, "2026-10-15T10:50Z", "main-thread", "06:50 groom: evidence from tonight: every heavy job went through the job-local pwsh heavy.ps1 gate (Invoke-Heavy) with notifier-driven waits. That is exactly the slot/queue/memory-admission job model this verb should own. Raised P1 -> P0 inside the cluster because it is used most.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night use case: about 15 heavy jobs ran through tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy (2 slots, memory admission only); each needed by hand: a log path, a wall-clock bound, the exit code and the result lines pulled from the log, the run dir, and chaining (build then suite); one inventory walk hung 85 min unnoticed because nothing watched CPU against output. A job registry should record command, slot, start, log, exit and a liveness rule (output silence vs CPU) per job.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T11:29Z", "main-thread", "07:29 main-thread day: interim receipts outside eoie: tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 Invoke-Heavy and new Invoke-Timed append every run to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv (queued/start/end, wait_s, run_s, slot, label, outcome with exit code, free MB before/after, command). apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/cost-report.ps1 [-Runs N -Top N -Notebooks paths] summarizes ledger, harness runs, cost per backend, a signal table (rows changed over the last 5 full runs) and Kino per-target ms from ipynb metadata.spiral_test.targets. First readings: full harness ~26 min wall / 95 native min summed, Lean 26 of it; ~70 native min per full run re-verify rows unchanged across 5 runs; Kino sm' target minutes: gleam 8.6, fsharp 8.1, cuda 5.0 of ~30. This is the receipt schema the eoie job registry should own.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:25 from the bundles (spiral-bin alpha417, brzozowski alpha54, 2026-06..09; full list in BUNDLE-WISDOM note 5): the job registry should (1) write the job record before spawn; (2) own the process group and reap descendants on timeout (an orphan compiler facade ate 3.8 cores and faked regressions); (3) re-read the child's completion receipt before reporting timed_out; (4) type outcomes as semantic_failure|time_budget|yield|environment_failure; (5) declare expected exits per job (rc5 negatives, diff rc1, installer rc75 = resumable); (6) on a first semantic failure cancel undispatched work and record the frontier, retry only the timed-out cells; (7) keep the stop/status path out of a saturated worker pool; (8) judge liveness by material progress, not heartbeat churn; (9) store argv, the exact tool paths and the first diagnostic from both streams.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:28Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-17 16:28 process hygiene belongs here: find processes by command-line pattern with their trees, stop them, check listening ports, and retire known daemons by name; agents repeated these by hand after every test run.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T22:21Z", "main-thread", "18:21 main-thread handoff for the next (overnight) run, in order: (1) bless single-flight so the 6 new negative samples (reactors_negative_port_type, conversation_negative_*, routes_negative_*) get baseline rows; (2) add -Oracle C,Zig to test.ps1 and run the first Zig bless (CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE); (3) formatter distance over all repos, with tracked-but-missing files skipped first (SPIRAL-FORMATTER notes 60-61); (4) agile todos --apply (TODO-AS-SOURCE). All today's changes are unstaged in spiral, eoie, x, i574n.github, polyglot, dice and alphabet; the user commits.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:04Z", "main-thread", "19:04 main-thread handoff item 1 done: blessed single-flight-20261017-182920 (6,939 rows) after review single-flight-20261017-182437 (6,915 parity + exactly the 24 new rows of the 6 negative samples, DISAGREE 0); compare-runs review vs bless: 0 differing cells.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 10u32, "2026-10-18T19:53Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 15:53 eoie lane slice 1 landed: new granular crate eoie_jobs (model.spi job_command and job_state unions + record classification, host.spi thin primitives with nominal child_process/spawn_outcome, jobs.spi supervisor as a tail-recursive Spiral loop). Verbs routed in eoie_cli_model before the legacy catalog: job start <label> <timeout-ms> <program> [args] (detached supervisor: CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB|DETACHED_PROCESS|CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP with a fallback without breakaway, so the session reaper cannot kill it), job status|wait <id> <max-ms>|list [n]|tail <id> [lines]. Registry under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs (EOIE_JOBS_HOME overrides): label, argv, cwd, timeout_ms, started_ms, pid, heartbeat_ms every second, exit (code, ended_ms, finished|timed-out|spawn-failed). wait exits with the job code, 124 timed-out, 125 spawn failure or lost (heartbeat older than 15 s), 3 still running. E2E: exit 7 finished, 1.5 s timeout killed at 1639 ms, missing program spawn-failed, list newest first; Spiral tests 2/2. 686 generated lines (inl primitives turned let to pass the gate). Next: heavy-gate integration and completion notification")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_11 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 11u32, "2026-10-18T20:28Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 16:28 slice 2: job start --heavy runs the program inside spiral's Invoke-Heavy through a generated job-dir wrapper (heavy.ps1 from EOIE_HEAVY_GATE or a spiral checkout above the cwd; args read from argv.inner, no quoting), so gated runs also survive the reaper; e2e: ledger row e2e-heavy (eoie job ...) ok. Supervisor now runs from a copy under <jobs>/supervisor/eoie-<size>-<mtime>.exe so a running job never locks target/debug/eoie.exe against cargo builds (the main lane adopted job start for its pack reviews after the reaper killed a run at 15:35). job list/status only count job-* folders")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_12 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 12u32, "2026-10-18T22:08Z", "claude", "2026-10-18 18:08 eoie lane dogfood: the session's low-memory reaper killed my Invoke-Heavy background shell while it waited at the gate (18:04, nothing had run). Restarted as eoie job start --heavy from the supervisor copy: the starting shell hung on the inherited stdout pipe even with *> file (PowerShell reads native output through a pipe and writes the file itself), so *> is not a workaround; TaskStop on that shell left supervisor 99576 running with a live heartbeat, then job wait from the copy picked it up. Fix in the pending build: job start clears HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT on stdin/stdout/stderr (SetHandleInformation in an unsafe extern block, Rust 2024) before spawning; proof is lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 (captured pipes must close within 10 s).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_13 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 13u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "Pipe hang root cause (verified 19:05): eoie --args-from relaunches itself with Stdio::inherit; Rust duplicates the std handles as inheritable for the child, so the child also inherits the caller's ORIGINAL pipe handles as extra inheritable handles, which then leak into the supervisor even after it clears its own std handles. Fix: argument_file.run_from clears HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT on the std handles at CLI start (one place, covers every relaunch); supervisor_launched no longer needs its own copy. Direct job start from a captured tool shell already returns at once with the current build. lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 recheck pending on build 3.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_14 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 14u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "Timeout now stops the whole tree (main lane report 2: the 2 h timeout killed only the pwsh wrapper, review-pack3 + test.ps1 + dotnet/erl survived and the heavy slot was released under them). The supervisor assigns the child to a Windows job object (BREAKAWAY_OK so nested eoie job start stays independent), TerminateJobObject on timeout, waits up to 3 s and counts ActiveProcesses; unix uses process_group(0) + kill -KILL -pgid + a /proc pgrp count. Exit detail and job status/wait/list show detail=wall-clock timeout; process tree stopped | N processes left alive (tree_stop union + test). Live timeout e2e pending on build 3.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_15 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 15u32, "2026-10-18T23:35Z", "claude", "ps/kill (eoie_process_table): lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-e2e.ps1 3/4 on build 1, the miss was the test expecting 2 tree processes while pwsh brings conhost (3); test fixed to tree: N. Also fixed: full sysinfo refresh made ps/kill take 2-3 s (now targeted ProcessRefreshKind), and kill of a missing pid counted as stopped; now a stop_result union (NoSuchProcess | StoppedOf stopped,total) makes missing or partial stops failures, receipt stopped N  failed F.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_16 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 16u32, "2026-10-18T23:55Z", "claude", "Verified on build 3 (19:55): lab/EOIE-JOBS/inherit-pipe-check.ps1 PASS (captured pipes close after 306 ms); lab/EOIE-JOBS/timeout-tree-check.ps1 2/2 (pwsh job with a grandchild, 3 s timeout: exit 124, detail=wall-clock timeout; process tree stopped, zero survivors); lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-e2e.ps1 5/5; ps 226 ms (was 2.2 s). eoie --help now prints every verb line (help sat at usage index 5 behind repo, so read/table/do/ps/job never printed and help errors quoted repo usage); new export eoie_command_more_usages.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_17 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T00:24Z", "claude", "Dogfood find (20:12): eoie kill <supervisor> --tree on a running build job reported stopped 2 of 8 and exit 1, yet all 8 processes were gone. Children that died with their parent made sysinfo kill() return false. Build 5b counts by survivors after a 200 ms recheck instead (stopped = total - still alive). ps-e2e 5/5 on 5b. Open: that kill took 5.7 s on a cargo/rustc tree (163 ms on small trees); not profiled yet.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_18 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T01:01Z", "claude", "kill speed (21:01, build 8b): sysinfo 0.38 Windows Process::kill spawns taskkill.exe /F per process (~0.7 s each; 8-process tree 5.7 s). process_stopped now calls OpenProcess(PROCESS_TERMINATE) + TerminateProcess directly (sysinfo kill only off Windows); a 3-process tree stops in 394 ms including the 200 ms survivor recheck. All EOIE-JOBS lab checks green on 8b: inherit-pipe PASS (249 ms), timeout-tree 2/2, ps-e2e 5/5.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_19 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T07:31Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 03:31 eoie lane: job children were visible black windows. The supervisor runs detached (no console), so each console program it spawned got a new window. Fix: CREATE_NO_WINDOW on the job child plus env_remove of NoDefaultCurrentDirectoryInExePath (cmd /c x.cmd in the job cwd). Proof: a pwsh child under the fixed supervisor has MainWindowHandle 0 (live, 03:30); lab/EOIE-JOBS/hidden-window-check.ps1 checks it. New exports for eoie do: eoie_job_launched (label, timeout, argv, cwd), eoie_job_settled (summary + pid + timeout_ms), eoie_job_output, eoie_job_labeled; job_started now wraps a job_launched with a launch union (CLI messages unchanged). eoie_jobs at 938 lines. Release blocked since 10-18: the topology gate refused publishing because agile_task_notes (1034) and eoie_coverage_prune (1072) were over 1000; note-edit moved to agile_note_edit (538, notes back to 623) and the lcov parser to eoie_lcov (309, coverage.rs 459).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_20 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 20u32, "2026-10-19T08:24Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 04:24 eoie lane, follow-up: supervisor copies are always named eoie-<len>-<stamp>.exe, so a test binary that starts a job (tonight's probe) puts a fake eoie in the shared folder, and lanes that pick the newest copy got one without ps. Fix: name the copy after current_exe's file stem. Also job wait's relaunch keeps the original process alive to pass on the exit code, so src\\target\\debug\\eoie.exe stays locked anyway; build scripts now move the exe aside before cargo (renaming a running exe works on Windows).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_21 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 21u32, "2026-10-19T09:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 05:40 eoie lane: release eoie.exe installed 05:39:54 (sha256 608C85E2...), the first since 10-17. Job eoie-release-install-6: regen published, debug build, Spiral tests (8 notebooks incl. eoie-jobs, eoie-lcov, eoie-do-write/git/jobs/lab), one-call 34/34, read e2e 5/5, ps e2e 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, release build, topology gate passed; the installed binary re-ran hidden window 2/2 and one-call 34/34. Lessons: Spiral test blocks compile with plain rustc (no crate deps), so a test must not call a regex/serde primitive; build.ps1 throws, so a job script that calls it ends there unless wrapped.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_22 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 22u32, "2026-10-19T10:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 06:20 eoie lane: final eoie.exe for the night installed 06:19:33 (sha256 18C9ECA1...); regen, debug build, Spiral tests, one-call 34/34, read e2e 5/5, ps e2e 5/5, hidden window 2/2 and timeout tree 2/2 passed before publishing, and the installed binary passed 34/34 and 2/2. Supervisor copies are named <exe stem>-<len>-<stamp> (real builds keep the eoie- prefix), and making a new copy removes same-stem copies more than a day old; copies in use survive because Windows refuses to delete a running exe. The supervisor folder held 15 copies (~400 MB) at 06:15.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_23 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 23u32, "2026-10-19T12:11Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 08:11 eoie lane (user: 'there are eoie processes running for hours, fix this'): job wait outlived its caller. When the reaper killed a waiting shell, 'eoie job wait <id> 7200000' and its relaunched supervisor copy kept polling for up to two hours with nobody listening. Fix: the wait loop (CLI job wait and eoie do job wait) stops when the shell that started it is gone. parent_pid from a Toolhelp snapshot (parent_id on Unix), process_alive via OpenProcess(SYNCHRONIZE)/WaitForSingleObject (access denied counts as alive), and the relaunch passes the shell pid down as EOIE_JOB_WATCH. lab/EOIE-JOBS/orphan-wait-check.ps1 kills only the waiting shell: the 06:19 release leaves 2 waiters alive (fails 1/2), the fix passes 2/2. Installed in eoie.exe 08:10:44 (sha256 36C3CD19...) after the full gate (spec 34/34, read 5/5, ps 5/5, hidden window 2/2, timeout tree 2/2, orphan wait 2/2, re-run on the installed binary). eoie_jobs 965 lines. Supervisors still live as long as their job, by design.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_24 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 24u32, "2026-10-19T16:57Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 12:57 Publish-EoieBinary failed with access denied while another lane's eoie job wait ran from the installed eoie.exe (the original process stays alive to forward the exit code). Fix in compiler-contracts/workspace.ps1: on Windows the locked exe is renamed into %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\retired (a running exe can be renamed, not overwritten), the new one moves in, and on failure the old exe is restored; retired copies are deleted on the next publish once they are unlocked. Verified live: published=True while x's wait held the old exe.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_25 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 25u32, "2026-10-20T02:44Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 22:43 main-lane friction (also on EOIE-ONE-CALL): no job stop verb; a mistaken job can only be stopped by walking its supervisor tree by hand. Design for the next eoie run: job stop <id> reuses the timeout path already in eoie_jobs (jobs.spi line 124: tree_stop_of plus timeout_detail), adds a JobStopped arm to the exit union in model.spi (word stopped, its own exit code), writes the exit file once, and refuses an id with no job folder, a job that already has an exit file, or a supervisor PID whose command line is not this eoie job. Test: Spiral notebook starts a sleeping child job, stops it, checks status stopped and that no process of the tree survives; mutant check. Priority: first item next eoie run, not tonight (time box ended 21:00).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_26 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 26u32, "2026-10-20T11:09Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 07:09 eoie lane: job stop <id> in source, release job eoie-release-17 running. The verb writes a stop request file into the job folder; the supervisor checks it every 200 ms tick and stops its own Job Object tree like a timeout (exit 143, state stopped, detail 'stopped on request; process tree stopped'), so no caller ever kills a PID. Refuses unknown ids and settled jobs; fails after 15 s when the supervisor predates the verb. 1k gate: eoie_jobs reached 1226 lines (the halt reason became a runtime union, so 7-arm state matches got inlined); halt terms now map in one match, state_word/exit/settled are let functions, and stop+tail moved to a new crate eoie_job_control (eoie_jobs 965, control 441). Spiral notebook src/eoie_jobs/stop_cli.spi: a parent+child sleeper stops with both heartbeats frozen.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_27 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 27u32, "2026-10-20T18:00Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 14:00 eoie lane, three findings from today's queueing, fixed in source for release 20: (1) a --heavy job's timeout counted time spent queued at the window or the heavy gate (a 15 min quick compile timed out at 13:46 without ever getting a slot while x-round19 and redo-apply held both); the supervisor now pushes its deadline by the queued time on each heartbeat until stdout shows 'Invoke-Heavy: acquired slot' (gate_queued shared with job_summary; windows_cli covers it with a 3 s timeout queued 4 s behind a live window); (2) job wait <id> without max-ms waits until the job settles (was max 0, main's EOIE-JOB-STOP note 7); (3) a quick single-owner compile does not need a heavy slot: window request compile <min> --wait + direct run + release shares the box with heavy builds and still respects live-gui/compiler-install windows (used for the experiments crates at 13:56-14:00 while both heavy slots were held for hours).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_28 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 28u32, "2026-10-21T00:34Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:34 eoie lane, release 22 installed 20:32 (user 18:45-19:15: dangling processes; keep eoie and erl processes in their parent's tree; stop blocking on job wait): job objects are kill-on-close with no breakaway (stop_cli: killing only the supervisor stops child and grandchild); job wait no longer relaunches from the supervisor copy (orphan e2e asserts one eoie per waiter); new 'job run [--heavy]' supervises in the caller's process and exits with the job's code, meant to run as an agent background task so the completion arrives as a notification; --heavy supervisors wait for their window and heavy-gate slot themselves (eoie_job_gate: ticket {UtcNow.Ticks:D20}-{supervisor pid}.t, first live ticket + mutex probe, then SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET for Invoke-Heavy, main's contract), so a queued job is one 6 MB process: verified live at 20:34 (supervisor only, jobs/<id>/window waiting behind a compiler-install, stop during the wait removes the window). The eoie do glue exports moved to eoie_job_control (eoie_jobs 920 lines).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_29 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 29u32, "2026-10-21T00:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:46 eoie lane: ticket handoff proven end to end (main, 20:46): two 'eoie job run --heavy' jobs (sefaz-family-gates3, formatter-c9) adopted their supervisor tickets: jobs/<id>/window 'granted ... waited_ms=0', jobs/<id>/slot 'ready ticket=00639281403243756206-53768.t waited_ms=0', Invoke-Heavy 'acquired slot 1 after 0 s', tickets removed from the queue, holder-1.h/holder-2.h naming both jobs.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_30 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 30u32, "2026-10-21T01:23Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:23 eoie lane: release 23 installed (one-call 34/34, orphan 3/3): lost jobs settle on first observation and orphaned job windows drop with their dead supervisor; checked on main's two reaped 'job run --heavy' jobs of 20:46: both now read state=lost exit=125 with the detail and a recorded exit file, elapsed frozen at 334 s and 332 s; job run's usage, help and README warn that an attached job ends with its caller.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_31 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 31u32, "2026-10-21T11:32Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 07:32 eoie team: drafted job chaining (scratch apply-job-after.ps1, after r28 slice 2): job start/run [--after <id>|--after-ok <id>] [--heavy|--compile] ... The supervisor waits for the named job to settle before its window gate and spawn, beating its own heartbeat and honoring job stop, so the timeout starts when work starts. --after-ok skips with a new typed state JobSkipped (word skipped, exit 125, detail '--after-ok: <id> ended <state> exit <n>'). Status shows queued while waiting, and start validates that the named job exists. Replaces the hand-written follow-on scripts (follow-r26b..e) that release chaining needed tonight. Notebook: stop_cli after_waits_for_the_named_job_and_after_ok_skips_when_it_failed (rustc exiter fixture). Preview queued behind tibia's live block.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_32 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 32u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): job start --after/--after-ok (eoie_job_after), the run summary printed by eoie_jobs (launch.rs 1044 -> 737), and job_state as Live/Settled.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_33 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 33u32, "2026-10-21T20:59Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:59 eoie team, progress review: shipped since 75%: --after/--after-ok chaining, the run summary, job_state Live/Settled, --compile; r33 adds the --max-private-mb memory cap; left: session-scoped process listing with CPU deltas (eoie machine watch part 1 covers the machine-wide view).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_34 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 34u32, "2026-10-21T21:34Z", "eoie-lane", "17:34 eoie team: last item drafted for r35 (scratch apply-ps-cores.ps1, dry-run clean): eoie ps gains a cores column (500 ms sample) and --mine (descendants of the nearest claude/codex ancestor; the chain is pwsh -> pwsh shim -> cmd -> claude.exe). The process_table Rust blob goes away: the listing is granular Spiral over eoie_machine_watch's process rows (ps_scope union EveryProcess|SessionTree|NoSession). Found by dogfooding: eoie ps could not tell a busy scan from a stuck one (no CPU column), and 'stop only own processes' needs a session scope.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_35 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 35u32, "2026-10-21T22:00Z", "eoie-lane", "18:00 papercut: 'job list [count]' shows the newest 20 jobs, so a queued or running job started earlier (tonight: the r30-r33 release, queued 35 min for a heavy slot) drops out once a few check jobs finish after it; I had to ask job status by id. Rule for the fix: unsettled jobs (queued, running) always print, before the newest settled ones that fill the count.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_36 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 36u32, "2026-10-22T03:32Z", "eoie", "23:32 user 23:2x: 'heal what's causing eoie to generate all these dangling processes without parent stealing cpu'. Measured: the parentless processes are eoie job supervisors (eoie-<size>-<stamp>.exe job supervise), detached on purpose so jobs outlive their caller; at 23:28 there were 10 (2 running jobs, 7 queued tibia jobs, 1 just ended); 0 processes alive under finished/stopped jobs, no Cells daemon or compiler server orphaned. The waste is in the queue: each waiting supervisor wakes every 2 s (window gate) or 3 s (slot loop), rewrites heartbeat_ms and status files, lists the queue dir and probes every ticket's PID (O(n^2) in waiters): 80-110 ms CPU per 20 s each, 2.7% of a core for 10. Worse, job_window_gate takes the heavy-build window BEFORE queuing for the slot, so a queued job holds a counting window and can block other lanes' window requests (tibia's report). Fix apply-gate-queue.ps1 for release C: slot first, window granted fresh at slot time, waits back off to a 10 s cap (heartbeat silence limit is 15 s). Next: the supervisor's own run loop and window run clients get the same backoff; eoie ps --tree shows supervisors as job roots so detached is not mistaken for dangling.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_37 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 37u32, "2026-10-22T10:54Z", "eoie", "06:54 feature from tonight's ABC tail: it queued from 03:42 behind two other lanes' heavy jobs, and the rule 'hold a heavy slot by 04:00 or stop' (EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN R14) had to be enforced by hand with a cron and a job stop. As a job option: eoie job start --heavy ... --slot-by HH:MM makes the supervisor give up its queue ticket and window at that time if no slot was acquired (state=expired-in-queue, its own exit code, no process tree started), and --done-by HH:MM stops a started job at a hard deadline (the R12 rule). Same shape as the release-verb deadlines in EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN note 45; the job registry is the right home since every lane's heavy work goes through it. Needs a compile.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_38 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 38u32, "2026-10-22T11:42Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-22 07:42 --slot-within <minutes> drafted as an anchored slice (scratch apply-slot-within.ps1): launch parses it after --max-private-mb, rejects 0 and plain starts, writes slot_by_ms; slot_loop's new branch removes the ticket and exits 75 'no heavy slot within --slot-within; the job never started'; windowed_gate caps its limit at slot_by_ms. Dry-run on a src copy applies cleanly and composes with F. Lands with release F (size check: launch.rs 891 before).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_39 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 39u32, "2026-10-22T13:10Z", "eoie-lane", "09:10 eoie team: the second half of the 06:54 request is drafted as --done-within <minutes> (apply-done-within.ps1, in F after slot-within; prelaunch-check clean on F+G+E2). It is minutes-based like --slot-within, so there is no clock parsing; it replaces the requested --done-by HH:MM. It is a hard wall-clock cap from submission that is never stretched by queue time, unlike timeout-ms since r20. Launch writes done_by_ms; slot_by_ms becomes the earlier of slot-within and done-within, so a job still queued at the cap expires in the queue (expired-in-queue, 75); supervised takes a hard deadline and a started job past it halts as timed-out (124). Allowed on plain jobs too. The receipt shows done_within_min. stop_cli test a_job_past_its_done_within_minutes_stops_as_timed_out (~60 s) plus the zero refusal. Also from this pass: queue deadlines (--slot-within and the 24 h window limit) settle as a typed JobExpiredInQueue (state=expired-in-queue exit=75) instead of finished exit=75, as the request asked.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_40 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 40u32, "2026-10-22T20:24Z", "eoie-lane", "16:24 lab drain rule, written before F lands (memory lab-in-repo: delete lab copies only after landing): scratch drain-after-F.ps1 removes a lab copy only when (1) no tree lease is live, (2) the installed eoie's 'machine watch' prints the '== eoie jobs running' section (F installed), and (3) at most 3 of the lab file's non-blank lines are missing from its src counterpart (src supersedes it). Measured now: lab/EOIE-MACHINE-WATCH (machine-watch/eoie_machine_watch: model identical; host, run, Cargo.toml and spiproj have src-side additions or 1-2 changed lines; machine-watch-2/jobs_watch.spi identical) and lab/EOIE-JOBS/ps-cores (listing.spi and ps_cli.spi identical to src/eoie_process_table). EOIE-JOBS' five .ps1 behavior checks stay until each is a CLI notebook case.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_41 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 41u32, "2026-10-23T00:07Z", "eoie-lane", "20:07 kill under the job host (found in F4's candidate stop_cli, fixed in the tree, verified by job-1792712662424 and then F5): kill counted survivors by presence in a process snapshot 200 ms after TerminateProcess, and a supervisor whose handle the host still holds (500 ms sweep) stays listed as a zombie, so kill reported 'stopped 0 of 1'. Kill now waits up to 2 s on each pid's handle (SYNCHRONIZE) and counts a signaled one as stopped. This also corrects the 08:10 DH diagnosis of the same stop_cli failure (blamed then on the host-start gap). EOIE-JOBS' install probes ride with F5.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_42 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 42u32, "2026-10-23T03:16Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:16 x (dogfood): the installed eoie.exe (10-21 15:38) silently mis-parses 'job start --after <id> --heavy --done-within 459 <label> <ms> <program>...'. It took '--done-within' as the label and '459' as timeout_ms, and accepted the job (exit 0, label=--done-within, timeout_ms=459). With the flag before --heavy, the program became '--heavy' and the job only failed at spawn. The 10-22 source (launch.spi:92) parses --done-within, so a release fixes the flag itself. The remaining bug is generic: a label or program that starts with '--' should be rejected at submit ('unknown option --x'), not queued. A silently mis-parsed heavy job holds a queue position and then wastes a slot. Two mis-parsed jobs of mine were stopped before they ran.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_43 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 43u32, "2026-10-23T03:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:25 x (dogfood): 'job start --after <id> --heavy' took a heavy-build window at submit (23:16) although its --after job was still queued, so the window sat idle for what would have been hours. It also overtook tibia's older waiting live-gui request (23:13). tibia is filing that admission order gap. Suggested here: a job waiting on --after should request its window only once the dependency has settled. Workaround used: a plain job submits the heavy job only once no live-gui request is waiting.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_44 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 44u32, "2026-10-23T03:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:37 x (dogfood): a job started with 'eoie job start' from INSIDE another eoie job dies when that outer job ends. My plain waiter job submitted a heavy job and exited, and the new job went to state=lost after 209 ms ('the supervisor stopped beating'). It follows from the documented kill-on-close job object (everything a job started ends with it), but nothing warns at submit time and the receipt still says 'job start ok'. Suggested: when job start runs inside a job (an EOIE job env marker or a parent supervisor in the tree), hand the new supervisor to the job host outside the caller's job object, or refuse with 'nested job start: the new job would end with job <id>'. Workaround: submit from the agent shell.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_45 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 45u32, "2026-10-23T07:06Z", "eoie", "03:06 eoie: answers to x's three notes (23:16-23:37). (1) Options mis-parsed as label/program: release K turns job_started's positional chain into a typed order-free fold; adding there the refusal of a label or program that starts with '--' ('unknown option'), as x asks. (2) A --after job taking its window at submit: in the tree (ships with F) supervise_job waits on --after before any gate, and the gate takes the slot before the window, so a dependent job holds no window while it waits. (3) A nested job start dying with its outer job: with the host installed, supervisors are the host's children, outside the caller's job, but only if the host itself broke away. It didn't, because eoie's containers lack BREAKAWAY_OK; fixed for GE2 (EOIE-JOB-HOST note 22), with nested-host-check as GE2's probe. Until GE2 installs, submit from an agent shell, as x does.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_46 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 46u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-JOBS slot-within): PASS. exit 2 eoie error: job --slot-within applies to --heavy or --compile jobs, which wait for a slot or window")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_47 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 47u32, "2026-10-23T09:11Z", "eoie-lane", "05:11 install probe (EOIE-JOBS done-within): PASS. eoie job start ok id=job-1792746541275-done-probe supervisor_pid=126080 timeout_ms=60000 gate=plain label=done-probe done_within_min=1 | eoie job wait ok id=job-1792746541275-done-probe state=finished exit=0 elapsed_ms=338 stdout_bytes=0 stderr_bytes=0 label=done-probe")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_48 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 48u32, "2026-10-24T02:32Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:32 main: gap from eoie's 22:33 process trace: job start has no way to request a completion notification, so main's two --after chains (17 queued, 2 running) had no waiter until main armed background 'job wait' calls on each tail. The item's title promises completion notification; wanted: job start --notify (or a chain-tail waiter the job host owns) that pings the caller's session when a chain settles or a link is skipped (exit 125).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_49 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 49u32, "2026-10-24T02:35Z", "eoie", "22:35 doctor check proposal from the 22:14-22:45 audit: 'eoie job doctor' lists unwatched jobs, meaning live jobs with no waiter (job wait/run process) on the job itself or on any successor reachable through after links. It prints the sample time, because a snapshot reported 19 minutes late misled two teams tonight. x's case shows why chains count: a waiter on its recheck covered the gate. Pairs with main's note here on completion notification (job start --notify or a host-owned chain waiter).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_50 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 50u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "eoie", "23:08 groom: progress 95 -> 85, because completion notification (in the title) is still missing. Constraint: eoie can't create a session's notifying background task; only the session can (a background 'eoie job wait' or 'job run'). Design within that: (1) job start prints the exact waiter command for the chain tail in its receipt ('watch: eoie job wait <tail-id>'), the tail found through after links; (2) job doctor's unwatched check (EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR note 6) catches the rest; (3) with EOIE-JOB-OWNER-PARENT the host knows its owner session, so a host-owned chain waiter becomes possible once a session-side inbox exists. Milestones: receipt hint, doctor check, owner-aware waiter.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_51 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 51u32, "2026-10-24T04:57Z", "eoie", "00:57 milestone 1 (receipt hint) dropped by rule: job start's receipt is a parsed contract. Lanes extract id=, label= and take the last line ('Select-Object -Last 1'), so either form of hint breaks someone: appended watch= breaks greedy label=(.+)$ parses, and a second line breaks -Last 1 consumers (several of my own scripts). The unwatched-jobs check in job doctor (EOIE-JOBS-DOCTOR note 6) delivers the same signal without touching the receipt; that's milestone 2 and it rides with doctor (stage M ships doctor itself).")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_52 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 52u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 120m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 85%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_53 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 53u32, "2026-10-28T10:29Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_54 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 54u32, "2026-10-31T21:25Z", "kit", "10-31 17:2x kit (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK repair): Paused -> Planned. The 10-28 pause was for silence only ('Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued'), the pattern the user rejected today: a pause needs a reason AND a resume condition. Its recorded next step still stands: check whether the remaining milestone shipped (now against the installed E14F1F0B, not TRAIN2c), close it with that evidence if so, otherwise size the remaining slice. Back in 'agile next' as ready work.")
inl note_of_kit_jobs_55 () = Note ("KIT-JOBS", 55u32, "2026-11-04T15:50Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:50 tibia (cross-team pull, the recorded next step: check + size): NOT SHIPPED in the installed kit (5B9082C1). 'kit job doctor' exists and audits processes, job files and after-chains (finding union in kit/src/kit_jobs_doctor_model/model.spi:40-47: IdleOrphanHost, OrphanCompilerServer, SettledButSupervised, WaitOnSettled, WaitOnMissing, ChainToMissing, HostEnvironmentDiffers). Waiters are checked only for jobs that already settled or vanished; there is no finding for a LIVE supervised job that nobody waits on, which is milestone 2 (n51). KIT_JOB_WATCH (control.rs watched_caller) only names the pid a 'job wait' stays tied to; it is not a completion notification. REMAINING SLICE, sized: in process_findings (model.spi:95-110) collect the job ids of JobWaiter processes, and for each JobSupervisor job whose standing is JobLive and whose id is not among them add a new finding UnwatchedLive : u64 * string (supervisor pid, job id); render it in the doctor CLI; one test block with a live supervised job and no waiter (one finding) and a counter-example with a waiter (none). About 20-30 lines in one module, D2, about 45 min, team kit. Field evidence for why it matters: on 11-04 the node's job start reported 'supervisor never recorded its pid' three times while the jobs ran (KIT-JOB-START-SATURATED), and only a separate waiter showed they were alive.")
