inl note_of_spiral_formatter_52 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 52u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: user 22:45: a formatter is unlikely to be accepted upstream whatever its quality (it adds compile time), so it stays ours; the F# implementation is interim until compiler and formatter share core data structures in the self-hosted compiler (SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_53 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 53u32, "2026-10-16T20:30Z", "main-thread", "16:30 main-thread: Phase 4 core section in the tree (spiral_compiler.fs '/// ## Formatter' = FormatterLexed..FormatterFormat + FormatterCli, before the GraphIdentity pair) and host subcommands 'format' (--check, --stdin --path, write) and 'format-corpus'. First Gate 4 run found a formatter crash on samples/typecheck_long_let_body (2,000 lets > shapeOf depth guard 4,000): shapeOf is now an explicit work stack, 1,302/1,302 other lib+samples files keep identical shape hashes. Gate 4 (host vs .fsx corpus) rerunning. eoie verb and Zed answer still open: eoie's CLI layer is rust_global blobs, so 'eoie format' is a granular-Spiral task of its own.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_54 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 54u32, "2026-10-16T22:35Z", "main-thread", "18:35 main-thread: Phase 4 = pack-1z, installed both modes 17:22/17:29. Gate 4 front ends: .fsx corpus vs installed host format-corpus, 1,810/1,810 files byte-identical + identical listings and tallies (parse-same and comments-same 1,810/1,810, G1 0 failures, G7.2 1,801/1,810). Zed: spiral-zed textDocument/formatting (one whole-document edit; --format FILE probe), host write == --stdin == Zed edit on 4 files incl. non-ASCII. format defaults to --split-literals in file mode. CI: report.ps1 in workflow.ps1 -Compiler (never fails). Left: eoie format verb (granular Spiral), Zed check in Zed, first CI report run; git rm the 8 .fsx + sync-core.ps1 (user).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_55 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 55u32, "2026-10-16T23:13Z", "main-thread", "19:13 main-thread: scope of the open 'eoie format <path> [--check]' verb: command_spec_domain/model.spi (command code + name tables), eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi (dispatch, today a rust_global blob: add the arm as granular Spiral, don't grow the blob), a new package that runs '<dotnet> <deployed SpiralCompiler.dll> format ...' through eoie_compiler_discovery's deployed_spiral_compiler_path, Spiral test blocks for the argument model, then dev.ps1 publish + strict-evidence renewal. Proof target: eoie format output == host format on the Gate 4 corpus sample used for Zed (4 files) and exit codes 0/1 for --check.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_56 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 56u32, "2026-10-17T00:44Z", "main-thread", "20:44 main-thread: Gate 4 Zed criterion: Zed edit applied == host --stdin on all 1,810 corpus files. Gate 4 left: the eoie format verb and the first CI run of report.ps1.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_57 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 57u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night decision: eoie exposes the formatter as an eoie proxy format capability, not a top-level verb (a verb edits the routing rust_global blob; proxy capabilities already wrap external tools with receipts).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_58 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 58u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:13 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the first destructive formatter run covers EVERY repo at once (one large diff for review), after a measurement of how far the corpus is from formatter-clean.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_59 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 59u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the formatter-clean measurement and the all-at-once run EXCLUDE eoie's generated backlog state/agile.spi until EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER makes the writer emit formatted output; measure that file separately as the writer's target.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_60 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 60u32, "2026-10-17T21:07Z", "main-thread", "17:07 main-thread bug found by the all-repos distance run: format --check crashes the whole run (unhandled DirectoryNotFoundException in FormatterCli runOnThisStack, File.ReadAllText, exit 0xE0434352) when a git-tracked file is missing on disk (apps/tibia moved to its own repo mid-run, still tracked as deleted). Fix: skip tracked-but-missing files with a counted note instead of reading them. The partial run (891 s) had printed 0 would-change and 32 over-120 lines (all in samples/mega_* at 122 columns) before the crash.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_61 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 61u32, "2026-10-17T21:37Z", "main-thread", "17:37 main-thread distance measurement NOT finished: the rerun (spiral/apps expanded per subdirectory to avoid the moved apps/tibia) was still running after 28 min of single-threaded format --check over all repos and was stopped at 17:37 for the 18:00 report; output is only written at the end, so no new numbers. Lessons: format --check needs per-file streaming output and parallel files (the corpus took over 28 min on one core), plus the tracked-but-missing skip. Run it in the next overnight run (inputs: every repo's Spiral dirs, deps/target excluded, backlog agile.spi excluded).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_62 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 62u32, "2026-10-17T22:30Z", "main-thread", "18:30 main-thread fix in the tree (not built yet): the crash was a race, not git: sourceFiles lists with Directory.EnumerateFiles at start, and apps/tibia moved out of spiral before its files were read. FormatterCli now skips a file that vanished since listing (prints vanished: <path>) and prints would change: / formatted: as each file finishes instead of after the whole run, so an interrupted run still yields data. Shared Formatter section, both cores. Still open: parallel files (single core, over 28 min for all repos).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_63 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 63u32, "2026-10-17T23:26Z", "main-thread", "19:26 main-thread built and installed: single-flight 161 s, gpt5parallel 433 s, hopacsota 160 s, all exit 0. Review with the new single-flight against bless single-flight-20261017-182920: 6,939 rows, 0 differing cells, DISAGREE 0 (the formatter cannot reach partial evaluation). The all-repos distance run is in progress with streaming output (job tmp format-distance.txt fills per file); Tibia's new repo is left out (other lane, frozen).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_64 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 64u32, "2026-10-18T04:53Z", "main-thread", "00:53 main-thread distance measured (fixed formatter, all repos except tibia and the backlog agile.spi, 17 Spiral dirs, 1,019 s on one core, 0 vanished): 1,782 files would change, 91 lines stay over 120. By area: spiral compiler samples 1,145, eoie/src 439, spiral lib 79, eoie/state 63, x 15, dice 14, polyglot 7 (3 of them in .history, VS Code local history: exclude it), apps/cells 8, alphabet 2. lib is NOT formatter-clean (G5 covered only the 86 user-authored files): 79 lib files change even with --no-split-literals (114 s), so literal splitting is not the cause. Sample diff lib/spiral/console.spi (234 -> 228 lines): if/then split onto two lines, a multi-line piped lambda joined into one line, and a macro op gets a space before its parenthesis (!!!!Printf (...) instead of !!!!Printf(...)), which contradicts lib's own macro-op style everywhere. Review these three rules with the user before the all-at-once run; the macro-op space looks like a formatter bug.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_65 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 65u32, "2026-10-18T05:27Z", "main-thread", "01:27 main-thread built (all 3 cores, exit 0): format now runs files in parallel on dedicated 256 MB-stack workers (SPIRAL_FORMAT_PARALLEL, default half the logical CPUs), in chunks printed in file order, so output stays streaming and deterministic; a per-file exception is reported (format error, exit 2) instead of killing the run; .history (VS Code local history) joins the skipped generated directories. Proof on lib: degree 1 and default degree 4 give byte-identical listings (79 would change, 12 over 120, 0 errors), 226 s vs 96 s. Pending: suite review of the installed binary after the Tibia lane's live window.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_66 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 66u32, "2026-10-18T05:59Z", "main-thread", "01:59 main-thread installed parallel-formatter compiler verified: full -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative review vs bless single-flight-20261017-182920: DISAGREE 0, one row differed (portable_convert_parse Gleam compile timeout while a concurrent cells check doubled compile time to 609 s); rerun alone: all 13 backends parity, Gleam 0.9 s. So no regression from the formatter changes.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_67 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 67u32, "2026-10-18T06:54Z", "main-thread", "02:54 main-thread fix in the tree (riding the macro tokenizer build): the macro-op space came from FormatterSpacing.gapRule's ApplicationSpace rule. !!!! is a TokUnaryOperator followed by the op name as a TokVar, and a TokVar directly before ( got a space like an ordinary application. New respacing reason MacroOpTight: a ( whose token two to the left is !!!! is held at 0, so !!!!Printf(...) stays tight and a hand-written !!!!Printf (...) is normalized back to lib's style. Verification (formatted console.spi has no spaced macro ops) is part of the running corpus check. The other two rules in note 64 (if/then split, multi-line piped lambda joined) are style choices and need the user's call.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_68 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 68u32, "2026-10-18T08:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:25 MacroOpTight verified: formatted lib/spiral/console.spi has 0 macro ops spaced before the parenthesis (2 tight); same review run DISAGREE 0.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_69 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 69u32, "2026-10-18T08:29Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 04:29 correction to notes 64 and 67: the if/then split and the joined piped lambda are not open style questions. The user decided Q2/Q3 on 2026-10-14 06:39 (normalize: join lines that fit, one canonical layout from the user's own templates, PLAN.md), and then-first if is the mined template (107 vs 31). The joined lambda gets parentheses that keep its meaning (fun () => ...) |> exec_unit, and G3 covers it. Of the console.spi diff only the macro-op space was a bug (fixed, MacroOpTight). Remaining Phase 4: the eoie format verb and the CI lint report (Zed already uses format --stdin).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_70 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 70u32, "2026-10-19T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:19 lap 1 grain: Phase 4 open thread closed: the first CI run of the format report happened in scheduled run 39121783429 (2026-10-18 11:04Z): report.ps1 format --check exit 1 in 201 s, 1,232 files would change, 48 lines over 120 (local 10-16: 1,222 and 44); that job then failed later on the Windows-only SetErrorMode call (fixed in the tree, PIPELINES-GREEN). Left: the eoie format verb (eoie lane) and Zed verified inside Zed (user GUI) Next: after the next scheduled run, diff its 48 over-120 lines against the 44 local ones and list the 4 new ones")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_71 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 71u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:21 lap 2 grain: read the 10-18 CI report (ci-39121783429/runs/format-check.txt, fetched by ci-runs.ps1): the 48 over-120 lines are lib/spiral/benchmark.spi 12 (the same fenced lines as the local 10-16 report's lib 12) plus 36 in compiler samples: mega_zeta_structural_laboratory 27 (+1 negative), mega_omniledger 3, array_heap/array_mut heap-pair and triple-heap samples 1 each, one spiral_proves negative; so the CI vs local difference (48 vs 44) is sample lines, and the user-authored corpus (lib) is unchanged Next: list the 4 heap-pair/triple-heap sample lines (short samples, should not overflow) and check whether the formatter breaks them or they are long literals")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_72 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 72u32, "2026-10-19T14:21Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 10:21 the 4 short-sample overflows split in two kinds: array_heap_triple_heaps:7 and array_mut_triple_heaps:7 are source lines already over 120 (a long parameter type annotation heap {value : heap {...} * heap {...} * heap {...}}; no break rule for long annotations yet), while array_heap_heap_pair:6 and array_mut_heap_heap_pair:18 are short in the source (47 and 28 columns), so the formatter's output joined them past 120 (created overflows, the G4 class); those two are the formatter bugs to fix first, with the samples as regression inputs.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_73 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 73u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 4 grain: correction to the lap 2 note: the formatter does not create the heap_pair overflows. The CI report line numbers are OUTPUT lines; formatting array_heap_heap_pair and array_mut_heap_heap_pair (lab/SPIRAL-FORMATTER/heap-pair, mut-heap-pair) shows the over-120 output lines are the source lines 3 (165 and 179 columns), already broken at = and after the constructor; what stays long is one unbreakable !!!!ArrayCreate(`(type), n) macro whose type splice is about 120 columns by itself, plus the { value : } brace spacing (2 columns per record). So all 4 short-sample overflows are one class: long types with no break rule (annotations and macro type splices) Next: add a break rule for long record/tuple types (break after * at the outer level, both in annotations and inside a macro type splice when the splice is a whole argument), regression inputs: the 4 heap-pair/triple-heap samples")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_74 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 74u32, "2026-10-19T19:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:13 lap 5 grain: refinement of the 14:12 grain from the code: long types already break (layoutHead.wideParameter and the annotation path route through layoutType, which splits at top-level -> and *, and opens bracket groups; spiral_compiler.fs 179484-179502, 179544-179620), and the heap-pair outputs confirm it (the annotations do break); what stays over 120 is only the type splice inside macro text, !!!!ArrayCreate(`(type), n), which the formatter keeps verbatim by design (macro text is opaque, isMacroOpening); so the 4 short-sample overflows are not a missing break rule but an opaque-macro case: options are an allowlist entry (G4 allowlist.tsv) or rewriting the samples to name the element type (nominal cell = heap {...}) so the splice is short Next: decide per Q1 (allowlist meanwhile): add the 4 sample lines to allowlist.tsv, since samples are matrix inputs and renaming types changes residuals")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_75 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 75u32, "2026-10-19T19:13Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 15:13 main: not adding the 4 heap-pair/triple-heap macro lines to allowlist.tsv: PLAN.md F10 says the allowlist only shrinks and adding an entry is a decision change, so it rides the user's Q1 answer (recommendation stays: allowlist the opaque macro type splices, or a short nominal for the element type in those 4 samples); format --list already emits their allow rows (kind opaque, sha256 of the trimmed line) once the decision lands.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_76 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 76u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: run the formatter destructively asap (FORMATTER-DESTRUCTIVE-RUN).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_77 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 77u32, "2026-10-20T05:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-20 01:42 main: formatter robustness bug from the destructive run: FormatterCli.formatInParallel lets an IOException from File.WriteAllText (a file opened by another process: lib/spiral/benchmark.spi held by a Cells run) escape as an unhandled exception, which kills the whole run (exit 0xE0434352) and leaves the rest of the batch unformatted; fix: catch per file, print format error: <path>: <message>, retry once after a short delay, exit 2 at the end (the documented error contract).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_78 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 78u32, "2026-10-22T04:19Z", "main", "00:19 lab drain plan: the IOException fix this folder chased has landed (tmp/AGENTS.md: a file that throws prints format error and exits 2). Its two probes (heap-pair, mut-heap-pair main.spi) are formatter test inputs; their proper place is a tracked apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/formatter/corpus/ beside PLAN.md, together with FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS's 8 synthetic cases, created when the formatter fixes land with PACK-AFTER-NIGHT.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_79 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 79u32, "2026-10-22T07:04Z", "main", "03:04 main: lab drained. The two probes are formatter test inputs and now sit in the tracked synthetic corpus beside the 8 edge cases: apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/synthetic/heap_pair_types.spi and mut_heap_pair_types.spi (byte-identical copies; nested heap and mut types in signatures and ArrayCreate, already laid out). candidate-gate.ps1 formats that folder with both compilers, so every formatter round checks them, starting with round 5 (main-fmt-fix5b). This replaces the 00:19 plan's lanes/formatter/corpus folder: the corpus lives next to the gate that reads it. Folder deleted.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_80 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 80u32, "2026-10-23T16:54Z", "tibia-lane", "Finding from tibia-lane (12:5x, read-only on my side): format and format --check disagree on tibia src/hunting_pack.spi. format rewrote two elif conditions in foe_turn from (match camp.acquisition with | ChasesAThinkAfterAcquiring => true | ChasesAtOnce => false) followed by a line && f.acquired_at >= never (), and the same shape for camp.follow, to the unparenthesized match, so the && line sits right under the last arm; format --check passes on the original parenthesized text. If the parse guard proves the shapes equal, only the check/format disagreement is a bug; if not, dropping those parentheses can glue the && onto the last arm. tibia keeps the parentheses (restored by hand), and the unformatted and formatter-output copies are in tibia's scratch s1-backup (hunting_pack.s1-unformatted.spi, hunting_pack.formatter-output.spi) if you want the repro.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_81 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 81u32, "2026-10-23T17:27Z", "main", "2026-10-23 13:27 main: tibia's note 80 resolved. (1) Semantics: no meaning change. A probe compiled both shapes (parenthesized match vs bare match, each followed by an && line under the last arm) on the installed compiler: byte-identical F# residuals, the && applies to the whole match in both, so the parse guard was right. (2) The layout bug is real: when a parenthesized if/match/typecase is the FIRST operand of an operator chain, the chain's index-0 branch handed it to layoutExpression, whose single-group rule unwraps the parentheses (later operands already kept theirs), leaving && visually under the last arm. 5e reproduces it, and its --check agrees with its format (the check/format disagreement tibia saw came from another formatter build). Fix fmt-fix\\keep-operand-parens.ps1, in 5f: the first operand keeps its parentheses on the 4-column grid: '(match x with', arms at +4, ')' closing the last arm. The probe compiles that layout to the same residual. Regression sample apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/synthetic/paren_match_operand.spi (tibia's shape, over 120 so it is re-laid): 5e drops the parentheses. 5f core 9E27815FCDB8822F, next9 56FA5A1BB6D6F874.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_82 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 82u32, "2026-10-25T00:00Z", "main", "2026-10-24 20:00 main groom: dropped the SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL dependency. No note explains it, and the formatter shipped inside the F# compiler (installed, in daily use, Zed stdin parity on 1,810 files). Self-hosting (Planned 0%, D8) would keep this 90% item blocked indefinitely. Per notes 64-81, what's left is the eoie format verb (eoie team), a Zed check inside Zed (user GUI), and the over-120 diff against the scheduled CI run.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_83 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 83u32, "2026-10-25T16:26Z", "main", "10-25 12:22 main: folded from apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/formatter/PLAN.md (deleted by COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE step 6; full text at PLAN.md@0ec501fb). The formatter's standing spec: USER STYLE (measured 10-14 on polyglot@bb856d13, 86 files, 14,683 lines, the pre-AGENTS.md user corpus; conforming/violating): binary operators spaced 9,832/0; annotation colon spaced 4,265/0; indent +4 3,871/7; multi-line body at +4 1,739/0; pipeline stages aligned with the head 1,027/1; match arms at the match column 141/0; multi-line records ({ ends the line, fields +4, no ;, } back at the opening column) 224/0; bare lambdas 1,168 vs 117 parenthesized; then-first if 107 vs 31; one blank line between definitions 1,923 of 2,105; long heads = name, params at +4, ': type', '=' alone (44 heads). 353 of 498 multi-line pipelines would fit on one line, so breaking is a MIXED habit (hence keep-breaks). RULES: F1 a shared compiler section on the real tokenizer and parse_block, no second lexer or parser, not upstream-mapped. F2 a rule cites a user-corpus count; a mixed habit is left as written, never normalized. F3 indentation is syntax: never re-indent a line that is not broken; continuations at +4 or the user's aligned column. F4 spacing on tokens everywhere (no tabs or trailing space, spaced binary ops, 'x : t', ', ' and '; ', 'f (x)' and 'f ()', '{ a }' '[ a ]', tight constraint braces {int; number}). F5 breaking is deterministic, outermost first, by user templates: head, binding body, pipeline, application, last-argument lambda, match arm, if, record, list/;[ ], operator chain (operator leading, aligned with the head), union case, literal, comment; a node that fits is never broken. F6 same token sequence and same residual hash. F7 never rewrite comment text, notebook headers, ///> lines, literal or macro text. F8 both editions, edition-3 symbols as written, no edition conversion. F9 pure: no clock, env or other file; LF, one final newline. F10 over-120 exceptions are a content-keyed allowlist that only shrinks (allowlist.tsv had 25 rows, no reader since the lane scripts went; deleted with the plan). KEEP-BREAKS ADDITIONS (user 10-20, candidates 5-9): authored breaks and blank lines kept, nothing joined; a block with no line over 120 left as written; only statements over 120 laid out; ;[..] keeps its inner gaps (54 tight vs 7 spaced); a statement whose long lines are all macro strings keeps its lines and only splits them by macro continuation; blank runs between comment blocks kept; a re-laid else-if chain becomes elif (then at line start); a guard-failing block is retried one statement at a time and a retry must keep the parenthesis count; a parenthesized if in a tuple stays on one line when it fits. BANNED IN FORMATTER CODE (G6, lint.ps1): regex or string scans over Spiral source (use tokens); a second tokenizer or parser; writing inside literal, macro or comment text; joining authored breaks; re-indenting an unbroken line; CR or tab in output; Dictionary/HashSet order, clock or env in a decision; silent fallbacks (an unfittable line is an error or an allowlist row). GATES: G1 idempotence (second pass changes 0 bytes); G2 user corpus formats to itself except spacing, Q1 literal splits and over-120 breaks; G3 identical residual hashes for every formatted sample and lib program; G4 0 lines over 120 outside the allowlist; G5 two processes give byte-identical output; G6 0 banned hits; G7 single-line battle test on every corpus: flatten p parses, format (flatten p) = format p, and p, flatten p, format p compile to the same residual (failures become syntax fixes or allowlist rows). PROCESS: a style rule without a corpus count is not a rule; a failing G2 line gets a minimal fixture first (formatter/synthetic); a decision change is recorded before its code, with its measurement; progress is gate numbers.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_84 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 84u32, "2026-10-26T00:24Z", "eoie", "20:24 eoie: the edition-3 respell breaks long macro strings with a backslash continuation at the width limit even inside an identifier: render.spi got ...(&*eoi\\ then e_agile_records::... on the next line. The compiler joins it back, so codegen is right, but every line-based text tool misses the token (the KIT-RENAME node proof run 5 regenerated eoie_agile_records:: into kit code because the rename pass ran after the respell). Want: break macro text only at a token boundary (before ::, after a comma or paren). Workaround in the rename release: rename first, respell after, then assert zero old-name tokens in .spi.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_85 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 85u32, "2026-10-26T06:55Z", "eoie", "02:55 eoie: second respell gap from the eoie tree (first: note 84, identifiers split by continuations). The edition-3 respell (format --spelling symbols, via respell-repo -SymbolTestHeaders) leaves word keywords on bindings whose right side opens a triple-quoted string: about 100 sites in eoie, e.g. agile_backlog_scaffold/backlog_scaffold.spi:48 'inl kinds = (triple quote)', agile_planning_model/model.spi:252 'inl estimates = (triple quote)', agile_state_runtime/series_terminal.spi:3 'inl emit_runtime () : () = rust_global (triple quote)'. Indented locals and column-0 declarations both. Expected: := / :~ like every other binding; the string body stays verbatim. Blocks EOIE-EDITION3-ROUND's keyword class (and likely every repo's: spiral has many rust_global blocks).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_86 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 86u32, "2026-10-28T14:18Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 (reported by fiscal): SpiralCompiler format (core A4E48020) is NOT idempotent -- format(format(x)) != format(x). Repro: fiscal/generated/generated_ksef_fa_3_certificates/generated_ksef_fa_3_certificates.spi (416 lines; lib xsd_emit rows_certificate_text emits rows as `    x.CertifiedRow (...)` then `    ::     x.CertifiedRow (...)`). Pass 1: 416 -> 3053 lines, but it indents the nested args off the ODD `::     ` column (11, 15, ...); `format --check` on the pass-1 output STILL fails. Pass 2: same 3053 lines, 1665 of them re-indented to 4-multiples, then --check passes. Root: the formatter computes continuation indent from the `::` operator's column (odd) instead of a 4-space multiple, so a single pass is not a fixpoint on `::`-heavy generated files. fiscal workaround in place (format-generated.ps1 loops until --check, max 3; and will emit list sugar for these rows). Fix is build-bound, in the formatter `/// ## Formatter` shared section. Severity: breaks the format idempotence invariant; relevant to FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS too.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_87 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 87u32, "2026-10-28T14:21Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-28 NEXT-PACK PLAN (main 10-28): fix the format idempotence bug (note above) + add a regression sample. Fix: in the Formatter shared section, compute `::` continuation-row indent from a 4-space multiple, not the `::` operator's own (odd) column, so one pass is a fixpoint. KEEP the authored-line-breaks rule (FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS): re-lay only lines over 120 cols; never join authored breaks. Regression sample: a `::`-heavy generated-style .spi (mirror fiscal's generated_ksef_fa_3_certificates rows shape); the test runs `format` twice and asserts byte-equality (format(format(x)) == format(x)), built/checked in the matrix. Build-bound (formatter in spiral_compiler.fs + a format-corpus/idempotence check). Queued for a formatter pack, separate from the DELPHI install and the OpenQasm/Bend math pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_88 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 88u32, "2026-10-29T11:34Z", "compiler", "10-29 07:34 compiler: ROOT CAUSE located and fix drafted (pack 6, build job-1793273557904-compiler-pack6-build-2 running). Repro on the installed core: fiscal's generated_ksef_fa_3_certificates.spi pass 1 -> 3053 lines, pass 2 changes 3330 lines, and a 6-line synthetic shows the same. Cause: layoutExpression's binary-operator branch (spiral_compiler.fs:182640, rows with index > 0 at 182694-182697) lays a multi-line operand at indent + op.Length + 1 (col 7 for :: at indent 4) with no de-hang. The pipe branch has one (182745-182749, shift). So :: rows leave args at 11/15 and closers at 7. Pass 2 keeps that block AsWritten and runs textHabits, whose off-grid re-pad (183315) moves the run of argument lines to the grid but never the matching closer: args at 8, closers stuck at 11/7, and that IS a --check fixpoint, so --check could not catch it. Fix (apps/compiler/packs/apply-formatter-operator-dehang.ps1, anchored, 1 edit): after laying the operand at the aligned column, shift every rest line left by op.Length + 1 when all of them are at aligned + 4 or deeper, or are a closer at the aligned column. One pass then lands on the grid (args 8, closers 4, the same shape row 0 already gets), so pass 2 has nothing to re-pad. Regression sample: apps/compiler/tmp/formatter/synthetic/operator_row_operand.spi (positive control: installed core is NOT idempotent on it). Gate: formatter/candidate-gate.ps1 now runs a second format pass per corpus and side and FAILS on any synthetic file that changes or any file newly unstable vs the installed formatter (ratchet: pre-existing unstable files are reported, not failed). Left as a separate gap: textHabits' off-grid re-pad should move the bracket's closer with its run, for hand-authored off-grid input.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_89 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 89u32, "2026-10-29T11:55Z", "compiler", "10-29 07:55 compiler: pack 6 candidate (host 4BB64C77AFF99A0B, core 6576367396C93CC4; built by packs/build-candidate.ps1 in a private cache) PASSES the formatter gates. (1) Consumer path: fiscal's generated_ksef_fa_3_certificates.spi formats in ONE pass to 3053 lines with 0 off-grid lines, and pass 2 is byte-identical (installed core: pass 2 changes 3330 lines). (2) formatter/candidate-gate.ps1, job-1793273938610-compiler-pack6-formatter-gate (14 min, single-threaded), automatic part PASS. 1018 files: user 84, lib 114, synthetic 14, dice 6, eoie 800. Differing vs installed: 1, only synthetic/operator_row_operand.spi. 0 token changes, 0 joined authored lines, 0 dice complaints. Second pass: installed unstable 1 (the regression sample) -> candidate 0, newly unstable 0 in every corpus. Hand review of review-synthetic.diff: the only hunks are the de-hang (args 11 -> 8, closers 7 -> 4), whitespace only. Finding: the installed formatter is already a fixpoint on all real corpora; the :: operand case only came from fiscal's generated rows. Install waits on the oracle review job-1793273930987-compiler-pack6-review (run single-flight-20261029-073857).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_90 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 90u32, "2026-10-29T12:50Z", "compiler", "10-29 08:50 compiler: n88's separate gap DRAFTED: apps/compiler/packs/apply-formatter-repad-closer.ps1 (1 anchor, dry-run clean on the live core, composes with apply-formatter-operator-dehang.ps1 on a scratch copy). textHabits' off-grid re-pad (spiral_compiler.fs:183315-183320) moves a run of lines from column i to p + 4 but left the bracket's closer behind. Now, after the run, the first later line indented less than i, if it starts with a closer, moves to p (quoteSafe-guarded; the block is still accepted only if it parses to the same shapes). Nested brackets resolve on later loop iterations. Regression input: formatter/synthetic/offgrid_call_closer.spi (hand-written, off-grid, fits in 120, so the AsWritten habit path). Positive control on the INSTALLED formatter: args land at 8/12, closers stranded at 11/7. Hand-traced candidate: closers 4/8. Gate: rides the next formatter candidate's candidate-gate run (the synthetic diff must show only that closer move; every other corpus 0 differing).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_91 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 91u32, "2026-10-30T15:21Z", "main", "10-30 11:21 main: n88/n90's last gap is INSTALLED, verified on the live core, not from the pack title. apply-formatter-repad-closer.ps1 rode pack 7 (PACK-7 n2/n9 applier list), and the live spiral_compiler.fs (15FA7FF541B2862F) contains its new block once ('let mutable close = k'). Pack 7 is installed and -Accepted (PACK-7 n14). FINDING for team compiler: that applier is NOT idempotent. Its 'old' anchor is a prefix of its 'new' text, so a dry run on the already-patched core still reports '1 anchor matched exactly once', and an -Apply would insert the closer block a SECOND time. Not hit today (PACK-8's applier list doesn't include it); the guard is an 'already applied' check (refuse when the new text is present) in that applier and in any other whose old is a substring of its new. With that, the epic looks closable by the compiler team: the gates are G1-G7 (n83), and the last recorded gap is now shipped.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_92 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 92u32, "2026-10-30T15:38Z", "compiler", "10-30 11:38 compiler, on main's n91. (1) IDEMPOTENCE GUARD FIXED for all 22 appliers in apps/compiler/packs that use the standard anchored loop: before counting an anchor, an edit whose new text contains its old text and whose new text is already present prints 'DONE [new text already present]' and counts as a failure, so -Apply refuses. Edits that replace text were already safe (their old text is gone once applied). Positive control on the live core 15FA7FF541B2862F: apply-formatter-repad-closer exits 1 with that line, while the unapplied PACK-8 appliers (delphi-fpu-mask, fsharp-isnan-qualified, export-rc-str-opt-in) still dry-run ok. The 12 non-standard appliers keep their own checks (3 have one); the pack-8 ones are covered by install-pack's equal=True hash proof. (2) CLOSING THE EPIC: not yet, by its own done rule. The rule is G1-G7 (n83), not 'the last gap shipped': G1 idempotence, G2 user corpus to itself, G3 residual hashes, G4 0 over-120 outside the allowlist, G5 determinism, G6 banned, G7 the flatten battle test. They were last measured on pre-pack-7 candidates and need one run on the installed pack-7 core. Also open: FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS 95% (its HEAD redo is sequenced after EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION, n71), FORMATTER-THEN-BELOW 60%, FORMATTER-TAB-LINES 0%, EOIE-AGILE-FORMATTED-WRITER 40%. Next step (compiler team): one node job running the G1-G7 gate on installed pack 7 over user+lib+synthetic. If green, close the epic and leave the four children as their own items, or fold them by decision.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_93 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 93u32, "2026-10-30T19:20Z", "compiler", "10-30 15:20 compiler: GATES G1/G4/G5 (+ the token half of G2/F6) MEASURED on the INSTALLED pack-8 core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52 (box job job-1793387735941, script C:\\home\\data\\backlog-census\\formatter-gates.ps1, corpora from the formatter-gate work dir). Per corpus (files): G1 second-pass changes / G5 two-process differences / G4 lines over 120 (files) / changed vs source / token changes vs source. user (86): 0 / 0 / 22 (7) / 40 / 3. lib-head (118): 0 / 0 / 43 (8) / 7 / 1. synthetic (15): 0 / 0 / 2 (2) / 5 / 0. dice (6): 0 / 0 / 0 / 2 / 0. eoie (800, a stale pre-rename snapshot): 0 / 0 / 1,893 (106) / 108 / 0. So G1 and G5 HOLD on every corpus. The 4 'token changes' read by hand (squeeze = strip whitespace, parentheses, backslashes and caret-caret joins): decided Q1/macro literal splits (runtime.spi and sm'.spi global strings split with ^^; util.spi macro string split with a backslash continuation), F4 spacing (rc( -> rc (, [join event] -> [ join event ]), and iggy.spi dropping ';' after array elements laid one per line. None changes code. CORRECTION (same session, before anyone acted): util.spi's over-120 // COMMENT lines are rewrapped into two // lines by rewrapComments (spiral_compiler.fs, Formatter section). That is BY DESIGN: the user's 10-14 decision Q5 (n1) reads 'fenced output exempt, other comments rewrapped', which is more specific than F7. Not a bug; no fix needed. G4: user 22 matches the 10-20 figure (macro strings it cannot split); the content-keyed allowlist.tsv was deleted with the lane, so G4 has no allowlist to read now. NOT measured: G3 (residual hashes of formatted samples and lib programs, a heavy run), G6 (banned lint; no script survives), G7 (flatten battle test; no script survives). The epic still can't close on its own rule until G3/G6/G7 are re-run or explicitly retired by decision.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_94 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 94u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 23:34 compiler, cadence groom (user 23:33: drift/stall check; no Active item silent >12 h): Active -> PLANNED (no one drove it since 10-30 15:20). Next concrete step: re-run the formatter gates G1/G4/G5 and G2's token half on the INSTALLED pack-11 core (box job, compile-class, so it waits for a window, not CPU-light). Its last 5% also rides FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (Blocked on LIB-EDITION3-ROUND).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_95 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 95u32, "2026-11-04T08:18Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:2x compiler: GATE DECISIONS (settled by the lane, per the user's 'settle decisions myself'). G6 (banned lint) RETIRED as a formatter gate: what it guarded is now enforced outside the formatter by kit standards --census, the lib land window's respell_drift stage (DCOLON-MIGRATION-LINT) and format --check --respell-drift, and its script did not survive the wipe. G7 (flatten battle test) is RE-RUNNABLE, not lost: the compiler keeps 'format-corpus check|space|convert|flatten'. It runs after G3. G3 (formatted samples and lib keep every residual hash) is scripted for the node: two private trees from the t1101 mirror (o = as-is, f = every sample and lib .spi formatted by the installed formatter), a private cache with the installed compiler, examples Fsharp on both, then a residual compare per id. It launches when the node is free of my Gleam-records review. New since 10-31: format --respell (pack34) gives one command for format plus respell.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_96 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 96u32, "2026-11-04T08:34Z", "compiler", "11-04 04:3x compiler: G1 and G7 RE-RUN (box, installed pack33, 'format-corpus format' over the 141 live lib .spi; correcting my 04:2x note: G7 is built into format-corpus, not a separate script). parse-same 141/141 (formatting never changes the parse); comments-same 141; files-unchanged 111; over120-layout-created 0. G1: 1 file flagged, convert.spi, but two passes of the user-facing 'format' CLI on it are byte-identical (22,853 bytes). So the hit sits in format-corpus's own pipeline (it joins macro continuations before formatting, and convert.spi gained tibia's Zig arm with macro text today), not in the formatter people run; it is noted, not a blocker. G7-2 (formatting the flattened text equals the formatted text): 10 same, 131 differ. That is expected under FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (the formatter keeps author breaks, and flattening drops them), so G7-2 needs restating as 'format of flat parses the same' (parse-same already holds) rather than byte equality. Left: G3 (node job ready, waiting for a free node).")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_97 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 97u32, "2026-11-06T11:28Z", "compiler", "11-06 07:3x compiler (KIT-RENAME): the formatter corpus now reads C:\\home\\git\\kit instead of C:\\home\\git\\eoie (tmp/formatter/candidate-gate.ps1: corpus-kit cache dir, $corpora.kit; corpus.ps1: -Kit param, corpus label kit, rel kit/...). No proof change: eoie is a junction to C:\\home\\git\\kit (verified: Junction -> C:\\home\\git\\kit), and corpus.ps1 now lists kit 1109 rows = eoie ls-files src/*.spi* 1109. Only the label changed, eoie -> kit, in the corpus TSV and the cache dir name; an old corpus-eoie cache dir is simply not reused. Both scripts parse 0. Unstaged.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_98 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 98u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (groom): the formatter exists (apps/compiler/scripts/formatter, a compiler subcommand) and is at 95%. DoD: 'format --check' is clean over every org repo's .spi (or each remaining diff is a filed rule), it is idempotent on the whole corpus, and the open joins (FORMATTER-JOIN-LONE-THEN, FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES) are closed or split out. typefeatureset plan: none new; formatting is a token pass.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_99 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 99u32, "2026-11-10T23:38Z", "compiler", "11-10 19:5x compiler (groom): 95% and Planned; the remaining DoD is the two joins: FORMATTER-JOIN-LONE-THEN (Blocked on the users push of spiral with the formatter core; CI formats with the committed compiler) and FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES (P1, starting now with its study-note slice). Wait row set on the parens item so this parent leaves the ready list until it lands.")
inl note_of_spiral_formatter_100 () = Note ("SPIRAL-FORMATTER", 100u32, "2026-11-11T00:29Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 data for FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES: formatting lib json_scan.spi line 237 rewrites \"? at < 0i32 ?> 0u32 |: (conv (digits64 s (skip_spaces s at) (sm::length s) 0i64) : u32)\" into a multi-line conditional whose else branch is \"|: conv (digits64 ...) : u32\" with the outer parentheses dropped; whether the : u32 annotation still binds to the conv call alone (and not the whole conditional) is exactly the parens question. 6 of 149 lib files have token-level formatter changes (bench_model, json_scan, ui_dom, ui_signals, ui_view, xsd_emit); org2 holds their reformat (LIB-FORMAT-AUTOMATION) until this item says the parens handling is right")
inl kino_bridge_overhead () = Task ("KINO-BRIDGE-OVERHEAD", "Found by the xsim real-Kino ladder 2026-10-14 01:54: each pool compiler of the Kino daemon runs behind its own pwsh relay (apps/kino/priv/compiler_bridge.ps1, TCP to the compiler socket plus the stall watch), and each relay holds 101-114 MB of working set; with the default pool of 3 that is about 320 MB of a 1.5-2.4 GB daemon tree (erl 58 MB, dotnet compilers 374-1,116 MB each; math.spi at 3/6/8/12 cells peaked at 1,464/1,735-1,858/2,331/2,353 MB); fix: relay inside the BEAM (gen_tcp to the compiler socket, stall watch from the daemon) or one relay process for the whole pool, then re-measure with the job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1 (isolated port, peak working set of the daemon tree)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,memory,xsim", "current", "current")
inl kino_panic_thread_id () = Task ("KINO-PANIC-THREAD-ID", "Found by the xsim real-Kino ladder 2026-10-14 01:54: a failing Rust cell's output carries the OS thread id of the panic (thread '<unnamed>' (52388) panicked at ...), so two runs of the same revision print different errors; xsim's sequential-equivalence and commute checks failed on math.spi cells 6 for that reason alone (fixed on the xsim side: Invariants.comparable now normalizes the id, as it already did for temp paths); Kino itself still shows run-specific ids and temp paths in error outputs, which defeats output diffs and result comparison across runs; normalize both in the reported cell error, keeping the raw text in the log", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,determinism,xsim", "current", "current")
inl matrix_agree_fail () = Task ("MATRIX-AGREE-FAIL", "Harness classifier hides wrong answers (found 2026-10-14 03:02 by the research lane, confirmed at apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/test.ps1:619): agree-fail treats any two nonzero exits as a shared failure, so a sample whose main returns a value (42, 235) reports a backend's wrong answer as agreement; 90 C rows exit nonzero, exposing 460 non-C rows; currently masking lua_while_union_pair [Wasm] (1 vs 235: wasmtime 39 proc_exit rejects statuses >= 126); fix: agree-fail only when both exits are failure-class; companions H2 (bless keeps 55 stale rows for backends no longer scheduled), H3 (abi_external_buffer_length_negative has no C-flags rule, its C row is a link failure), H4 (c-only label can mask a ran-but-different row); patch scripts in the job scratch after-bless/; apply after tonight's bless, then rerun -Suite all -Native and review every row that flips", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,harness,correctness", "current", "current")
