inl kit_memory_bootstrap () = Task ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", "Swap to bootstrap (needs the user's go): each client keeps one bootstrap entry ('memory lives in eoie: run eoie memory recall; tutorial at eoie/spec/...') and eoie in git becomes the synced source for the laptop and the work machine; a pull plus 'eoie memory export --client <c>' sets up a new machine. Rollback: export renders the full Claude memory dir again", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-MEMORY-FEATURES", "kit,memory,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_memory_bootstrap () = Planning ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_memory_bootstrap_1 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 prior art in bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\bundle_eoie_v20260723-alpha70462.zip state\\bootstrap.spi (Sep 2026): eoie's own bootstrap as typed Spiral. bootstrap_transition Boot -> FallbackSelected -> CandidateBuilt -> CandidateVerified -> Promoted; an execution_route chain Candidate -> RecentFallback -> ArchiveFallback -> HostEscapeRejected (host escape is a typed refusal, not an option); promotion only with typecheck AND e2e AND cold differential; rules such as CompilerPinnedByIdentity, FallbackBeforeHostEscape, GeneratedRustIsMirror, CrateUnderBudget. A ready shape for the bootstrap entry and for 'eoie memory export' rollback.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_bootstrap_2 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:53Z", "eoie-lane", "17:53 same grooming rule: the swap reads typed memory features, which do not exist yet, and its title says it needs the user's go. It depends on EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES and stays Planned until then (the go stays the user's call; nothing here asks for it).")
inl note_of_kit_memory_bootstrap_3 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 3u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 240m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 0%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_bootstrap_4 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 4u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByTest. QUESTION: Does the user give the go to swap each client's memory to a single bootstrap entry backed by kit? RULE (written before acting): The local swap is allowed iff (1) import->render is byte-identical for 100% of the Claude memory files plus MEMORY.md, (2) `kit memory recall` returns every slug, and (3) a fresh session that has only the bootstrap recalls profile-wipe-2026-10-28. Only the cross-machine sync (a git push of kit) is the user's step. BACKUP/UNDO: Full memory dir archived with scratch-archive.ps1 before the swap. Undo = `kit memory export --client claude` re-renders it. OWNER: kit. NEXT: Stays Planned behind EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES / EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_kit_memory_bootstrap_5 () = Note ("KIT-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 5u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_union_reflection () = Task ("KIT-UNION-REFLECTION", "Drift from the GPT era (user 2026-10-17: eoie maps unions to numbers; with reflection and the GADT/prototype feature set we could do it in fewer lines): measured 2026-10-17 across 493 eoie .spi files, 16 modules use the pattern: 67 pipe_index/pipe_member/pipe_lookup calls, 79 '| Nu64 =>' arms and 22 literal 'a|b|c|...' name tables. Example: agile subcommands live in 5 hand-synced tables (the union in agile_subcommand_domain, its name string, the u64 index match, agile_full_usage_error's usage text, agile_operand_shapes in eoie_agile_mutation, plus the pipe list in agile_entry); adding a verb means editing all of them. Target: one union per concept; case names by reflection (lib reflection, with case enumeration added to the lib/compiler if it can't enumerate nullary cases at compile time yet); parsing by a fold over the cases; usage and operand shapes in one exhaustive spec : case -> {name; shape; summary} prototype match the compiler checks for completeness. The numeric layer goes away. Proof: eoie tests unchanged, generated crates stay under the 1k gate, LOC before/after per module. Blocks new verbs such as agile links (EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,drift,reflection,gadt", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_union_reflection () = Planning ("KIT-UNION-REFLECTION", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl dib_modernize () = Task ("DIB-MODERNIZE", "Retire every .dib remnant straight to .spi test blocks, skipping the .livemd stage (user 2026-10-17: the livemd phase that lasted until test blocks was never enough). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 .dib files remain in our repos, 14 files mention them. Kino's F# route (fsi_chain.ex and fsi_session.ex resolve #!import x.dib from the dotnet-repl era; ~15 tests build .dib notebook fixtures); polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd imports lib/fsharp/Notebooks.dib and Testing.dib, which no longer exist (dead); history mentions in apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md and eoie ingest_frontier's port-now classifier. Delete the route and fixtures, or port what's still live", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,polyglot,migration,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dib_modernize () = Planning ("DIB-MODERNIZE", Minutes 105u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dib_modernize_1 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at spiral/apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/fsi_chain.ex:184 (at the .dib import route); eoie agile todos harvests it with id DIB-MODERNIZE.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_2 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:41Z", "main-thread", "14:41 main-thread scope measured. Kino .dib surface: Document.parse_dib is the default parser for any unknown notebook extension (notebook.ex parse), cli_export falls back to the old dib-export CLI subcommand, the domain exports parse_dib (spi/document.spi and main.spi), directives.spi has a dib error kind, and fsi_chain.ex resolves #!import x.dib (TODO block placed there). Tests: 21 use .dib fixtures (notebook_test 13, display 3, fsi_session 3, notebook_report 1, document 1); 15 are Spiral notebook-runner behaviours and 6 exercise the F# FSI route. Many test the notebook-to-.spi export feature itself (spi is written only when the cells pass). So the modernization is architectural: .spi is the only source (test blocks are the cells), notebooks are output only (ipynb/html), and the .dib/.livemd parsers and the interactive F# kernel retire together (also the bold-native-goals direction). Order: (1) port the 15 Spiral runner tests to .spi fixtures with test blocks; (2) drop parse_dib and dib-export; (3) decide the FSI kernel (polyglot Perf.livemd and the i574n.github workflow livemd still have cells: port them to .spi or scripts); (4) drop parse_livemd. Each step is proven by the Kino suite (~2.5 min).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_3 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:21Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:21 helper-lane lap 1: re-measured against today's tree: Kino now lives at spiral/apps/cells (apps/kino is gone; the TODO block is in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/fsi_chain.ex). .dib surface: notebook.ex:464 still falls back to Document.parse_dib for any unknown extension; mentions per file document.ex 3, domain.ex 2, fsi_chain.ex 7, fsi_session.ex 2, notebook.ex 2, spi/document.spi 1, spi/main.spi 1. Tests touching .dib: notebook_test 12 (spiral cells accumulate..., a failing cell still writes the ipynb..., an fsharp notebook runs through the host callback, host cells share the job slots, rust contract flags reach the target, a package missing beside the notebook ..., the spiral lib package is mounted once, a //> _ cell is not compiled..., cells compile together, a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, --real cells become the main_real module, at most four cells run at a time), fsi_session 7 lines, display 1, document 1, notebook_report 1. Step 1 (port the Spiral-runner tests to .spi fixtures) therefore covers 10 of the 12 notebook_test cases; the fsharp host-callback and host-slots tests belong to step 3 (FSI kernel).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_4 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:11Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:11 helper-lane lap 2: first port drafted: lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/accumulate_test_port.exs is the .spi version of notebook_test.exs's first case (spiral cells accumulate, tests keep opens, and spi is written only when they pass). Mapping: the #!spiral cells become a .spi with a test demo static prelude (open testing), a plain definition (inl value) and a targeted test block value_is_one; the assertions that survive are the semantic ones (the prelude alone is never compiled; the test program carries both the prelude's open and the earlier definition; an .ipynb report is written), while the spi-export assertions (demo.spi written only when cells pass, without opens or calls) disappear because the .spi is now the source. It reuses the test module's own helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). Not wired into the suite yet.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_5 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T14:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:28 helper-lane lap 3: second case mapped: notebook_test.exs:80 (a failing cell still writes the ipynb and does not write spi) needs no new .spi test, because notebook_report_test.exs:30 (a .spi run reports each test block and a failing block fails the run) already asserts the same semantics on a .spi: error message =~ exited 1, the .ipynb is written, its metadata counts failed 1. The spi-not-written half disappears with .dib (the .spi is the source). The only assertion it lacks is the failing cell's output ename SpiralError; that 4-line assertion is staged as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/failing_case_assertion.exs, to append after notebook_report_test.exs line 60 when the ports are wired (unverified whether .spi runs set ename, so it rides the same queued Cells-suite run as the accumulate port). Ports needed so far: 1 new test (accumulate) + 1 assertion; 8 of the 10 Spiral-runner .dib cases remain to map.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_6 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:32 helper-lane lap 4: ported cases 3 and 4 of notebook_test.exs as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/flags_package_ports.exs (not wired, same helpers as the file: run_value, rustc_ok): (3) rust contract flags reach the target (notebook_test 218) becomes a .spi whose ///> rust -cd near-token line sits on a test block; the target callback must see tool rust, contract empty, deps near-token at *, and a source without ///> lines that carries both definitions; the old two-cell split (the flagless prior cell compiled alone) has no .spi counterpart, so that assertion drops. (4) a package missing beside the notebook (notebook_test 272) is the same test on note.spi with ///- --package vendor/pkg as its header: the mounted package.spiproj must list pkg-. Both keep only behaviour the .spi path has. Mapped so far: 4 of the 10 Spiral-runner cases (1 new test + 1 assertion + these 2).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_7 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane lap 5: mapped the remaining six Spiral-runner .dib cases, so step 1's plan is complete: (5) the spiral lib package is mounted once (308) - same test on a .spi with ///- --package <lib>, the mounted spiproj must list spiral- exactly once; (6) a //> _ cell is not compiled and still exported (336) - .spi form is a block with ///> _ above it; keeps the not-compiled assertion, drops the export assertion (the .spi is the source); (7) cells compile together (370) - .spi with two test blocks, asserting their compiles overlap; (8) a --test static cell's definitions reach later cells, a --test cell's do not (404) - already .spi-native as test x static = versus test x =, port 1:1; (9) --real cells become main_real (443) - .spi with a .spir sibling or ///- --real blocks, keeping the main_real*- spiproj and the no-real-in-top-down assertions; (10) at most four cells run at a time (508) - ten test blocks in one .spi, same 2..4 concurrency bound. Totals: 2 cases need no port (2 is covered, 6 loses its export half), 8 become .spi tests (1, 3, 4 drafted in lab; 5, 7, 8, 9, 10 mechanical). The F# host-callback and host-slots cases stay for step 3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_8 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:52Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:52 helper-lane: wrote ports 5, 6, 7, 8 and 10 as lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/remaining_ports.exs (lib mounted once, ///> _ block not compiled, blocks compile together with overlapping compiles, static block definitions reach later blocks while plain ones do not, at most four blocks at a time), using only the notebook_test.exs helpers (compile, rustc_ok, run_value, calls?, lines). With the accumulate port, the ename assertion and the flags/package ports, everything but the --real case (9, needs a .spir sibling fixture) is drafted. Unrun: they wait for one coordinated Cells change with lane 2 and a mix test.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_9 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 9u32, "2026-10-23T13:41Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:41 main: step 1 mapping complete. All 10 Spiral-runner .dib cases of notebook_test.exs now have a .spi counterpart in lab/DIB-MODERNIZE: accumulate_test_port.exs (case 1), failing_case_assertion.exs (case 2, an assertion appended to notebook_report_test.exs), flags_package_ports.exs (cases 3-4), and remaining_ports.exs (cases 5-9 plus the last one). Added today: 'spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module', the port of notebook_test.exs:443 (--real cells). real.spi holds twice (real twice_real), real_real.spir holds twice_real and its real test block, and it keeps the original's assertions (no real definition in a top-down program, main_real*- before main in package.spiproj, the real test compiled as real spiral_cells_real (), a real test's code never reaching a later block), with 2 runs instead of 3 because only test blocks compile in a .spi. The file parses (Code.string_to_quoted!). Remaining: wire the ports into notebook_test.exs in place of the 10 .dib cases and run the Cells suite in a gap after cells-pack lands. Its suite verdict stays separate, so a port failure can't hold the pack's other steps. Then step 2 (drop parse_dib and dib-export), and steps 3-4 (FSI kernel, parse_livemd).")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_10 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 10u32, "2026-10-23T13:43Z", "main", "2026-10-23 09:43 main: the wiring is staged and queued. lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/wire-ports.ps1 (for a Cells copy) retires the 10 .dib cases by exact test name (424 lines), appends the 9 port tests before the module end, and inserts failing_case_assertion after notebook_report_test.exs line 60. On a scratch copy both files parse and notebook_test.exs has 13 tests: the 4 left for steps 3-4 (livemd accepted, spi: false, fsharp host callback, host slots) plus the 9 ports. lab/DIB-MODERNIZE/suite.ps1 runs mix test on just those two files in a fresh New-CellsRoot copy as job main-dib-suite (job-1792762997519, after cells-pack, before hopacsota). Rule: if every test passes, wire-ports applies to the tree in the next apps/cells landing; any failing port is fixed in lab and rerun, never landed red.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_11 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 11u32, "2026-10-23T14:31Z", "main", "2026-10-23 10:31 main: step 2's exact surface, read from today's tree (Grep over apps/cells *.ex and *.spi). notebook.ex:487 parses any unknown extension with Document.parse_dib; this becomes an error naming .spi and .ipynb. notebook.ex:349 falls back to the 'dib-export' CLI subcommand, which goes. document.ex:6-8 parse_dib and domain.ex:42-44 parse_dib go. spi/document.spi:106 inl parse_dib and spi/main.spi:11 export 'parse_dib' go, which regenerates the Gleam domain module spiral_cells@domain, so this step is a compile plus the Cells suite. fsi_chain.ex:184-187 (the #!import x.dib route) and :244 (.dib -> .livemd resolution), with fsi_session.ex's .dib docs, belong to step 3 (the F# kernel), not step 2. Order unchanged: main-dib-suite proves the 9 ports and the assertion; then step 2 as one Cells patch with its suite; then step 3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_12 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 12u32, "2026-10-23T22:16Z", "main", "2026-10-23 18:16 main: main-dib-suite (job-1792766412909) had 2 red tests in the ports but exited 0, because suite.ps1 dropped mix's exit code and filtered out the summary. suite.ps1 now writes the full log to spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\dib-suite.log and exits with mix's code. Red 1, 'spi: test blocks compile together': a port bug. The compile stub tagged sides with source =~ 'left ()', and the right block's program also holds 'inl left () = 1i32', so both blocks reported :left. It now uses calls?/2, which matches a whole trimmed line. Red 2, 'spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module': length(runs) == 2 failed, and the filtered output gave no count. The full log from main-dib-suite-2 (job-1792793766788, queued after cells-pack-2) decides the cause.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_13 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 13u32, "2026-10-24T00:17Z", "main", "2026-10-23 20:17 main: status fixed from 0% Planned to Active 60 by the rule 'implemented and staged in a pack or queued gate, awaiting review'. Where: ports wired (lab/DIB-MODERNIZE), red 1 fixed, rerun main-dib-suite-2 in chain A.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_14 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 14u32, "2026-10-24T03:08Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main-dib-suite-2 finished at 21:29 with suite exit 1 (log spiral-bin\\cells-suite\\dib-suite.log, root cells-dib-root). Its remaining_ports rewrite of notebook_test.exs and notebook_report_test.exs ran; read the log for which tests stayed red before the next attempt.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_15 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 15u32, "2026-10-24T03:54Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:54 main: dib-suite-2's exit 1 was environmental, not a test result. mix could not build because MIX_BUILD_PATH (spiral-bin\\mix-builds\\dib-suite) held a priv symlink to the old pre-nesting copy root (cells-dib-root\\apps\\cells\\priv, gone), so File.cp_r failed on tlds.txt before any test ran. Fixed: cells-root.ps1 gains Remove-DanglingBuildLinks (removes only links whose target is gone), and suite.ps1 calls it after setting MIX_BUILD_PATH. Requeued as main-dib-suite-3.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_16 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 16u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 6 18:26 (subagent): re-read: remaining_ports.exs staged; lands in apps/cells/test -> next: lane 2 applies, then mix test | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: remaining_ports.exs staged; Cells is now helper lane 1's -> next: apply in a Cells window, mix test")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_17 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 17u32, "2026-10-24T14:40Z", "main", "main 10:4x: main-dib-suite-3 (07:12) ran for real after the dangling-link fix: 45/46 passed. The one red is the ported case \"spi: a _real.spir sibling becomes the main_real module\" (notebook_test.exs as rewritten by remaining_ports in the cells-dib-root copy): assert length(runs) == 2 got 3, so the tree now makes a third run (likely an extra compile or target run for the main_real sibling) that the port does not expect. Next: read which three runs the tree makes for that case (tree notebook_test has the case; compare its assertion) and update the port, then requeue the suite.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_18 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 18u32, "2026-10-24T16:53Z", "main", "main 12:5x: main-dib-suite-4 (11:56) is green: 46 passed, after aligning remaining_ports.exs's _real.spir case with the tree's own .dib case (3 runs: definitions, the real test, the top-down test). The ports are proven in the cells-dib-root copy; next is landing them into apps/cells/test (they replace the .dib cases), which is a Cells test edit gated by the tree suite.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_19 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 19u32, "2026-10-24T16:54Z", "main", "main 12:5x: ports wired into the tree (lab wire-ports.ps1 -Cells apps/cells -Apply): 10 .dib cases retired from notebook_test.exs and the accumulate/flags/remaining ports appended; notebook_report_test.exs gets the failing-case assertion after line 60. Backups (hash C84F76ECCC73 / 142AB84C7B6F) in the session scratchpad dib-land-backup. Tree check queued: main-serial-dib-ports-tree (run_suite on both files); on any red, restore both files from the backups.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_20 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 20u32, "2026-10-24T21:14Z", "main", "main 17:1x: lab drain done under the lab rule (land first, then remove). The ports are wired into apps/cells/test (tree check green 13:58, uncommitted for the user). Reference search by stem with ripgrep over spiral, eoie src, x, docs, fiscal scripts, cells-pack and the session scratch (the earlier Get-ChildItem walk hung 2 h and was stopped). Hits: only the lab folder itself (the positive control), backlog notes, old scratch reports, and the finished 10-23 cells-pack chain; no live code. The folder was untracked, so it was moved, not deleted: %TEMP%\\lab-attic\\DIB-MODERNIZE-20261024-1713 (6 files, hashes verified). spiral/lab/DIB-MODERNIZE no longer exists.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_21 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 21u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_22 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 22u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 105m (D-base rule: base(D4) x (1 - 60%)). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_23 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 23u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_24 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 24u32, "2026-11-03T08:58Z", "main", "main 11-03 04:3x SCOPE: polyglot's .dib remnants are read from the local copy (polyglot left the org 11-03). They are absorbed only when they carry something no spiral/i574n.github file has; the rest are ruled out with POLYGLOT-RETIRE.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_25 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 25u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: 0 .dib files left in the own repos; each former notebook is a .spi with test blocks that run. Next step: git ls-files '*.dib' across the 11 repos.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_26 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 26u32, "2026-11-06T13:10Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DONE, proof: git ls-files '*.dib' returns 0 files in all 11 own repos (spiral, kit, x, fiscal, regex, html, dice, alphabet, blog, tibia, i574n.github); the notebooks live as .spi with test blocks (physics.spi ran 43/43 today). The only .dib left is in polyglot's history (the plot reference page CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT compares against), which is outside the org.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_27 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 27u32, "2026-11-06T13:13Z", "main", "main 11-06 12:0x CORRECTION to my 11:5x DONE note: kit's done gate refused it (exit 2), correctly. A source marker still names this item: spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/fsi_chain.ex:185 says about 15 Cells tests build .dib fixtures to delete or port. So: 0 tracked .dib files in the 11 repos (proven), but the .dib code path and its fixture tests remain in Cells (owner x). Next step: x decides delete vs port for the fsi_chain .dib tests; the item closes when the marker goes. Stays Planned at 60.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_28 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 28u32, "2026-11-10T09:02Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:1x (help pool, org item) the last remnant is GONE: the fsi_chain.ex marker (the reason mains 11-06 close was refused) is removed with the routes it named. spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/fsi_chain.ex: #!import now takes .fs/.fsx only, resolved against the notebooks directory; the .dib and .livemd import routes, expand_notebook (notebook-in-notebook expansion), resolve_livemd (.dib -> .livemd fallback) and the nesting guard are deleted, and an imported notebook is refused as unsupported (alias Document dropped). Checked first: no own-repo file imports a notebook (the only #!import of .livemd/.dib is in polyglot, outside the org), and the Cells-vs-dib bench does not need it (Cells runs the .spi notebooks; dotnet repl runs the recreated .dib). Tests PORTED, not dropped (test/spiral/cells/fsi_session_test.exs): the import test now proves .fsx resolution plus refusal of both nb.dib and nb.livemd; the session test splits the old helpers.dib into three top-level cells (#!import first.fsx, type First_US0 = US0, #!import second.fsx), which keeps its point: each cell is its own fsi submission, so the shadowed US0 stays usable. proof: box mix test fsi_session_test + document_test = 30 passed, 0 skipped (dotnet present), no compile warnings; no DIB-MODERNIZE marker left in any source (rg). All unstaged.")
inl kino_rename () = Task ("KINO-RENAME", "Rename the Kino project folder and code to something generic (user 2026-10-17 dislikes the kino name), timed with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR: the supervision part rewritten in Spiral compiling to Gleam 1.19 (the new compiler) removes Elixir from the repo, and that native core should give the name. Parallel or performance-heavy parts (rayon-style) compile to Rust. Precedent: the X client started as apps/spindle and became x/apps/client (xc); no memory recorded that rename, and backlog items X-CLIENT-THREADS and X-CLIENT-AGENT-JSON still say spindle", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,naming", "current", "current")
inl lib_usage_diagnostics () = Task ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", "Tooling to diagnose lib/spiral usage across our repos: which lib functions and test cells are never reached from any app, sample or repo; duplicated and generated code (the CLI lane found that ~9k of spiral.rs's 24k lines come from inlining the same parser 3 times); and expansion hot spots per generated crate, so the 1k-line packaging limit stays useful. Part of the branded tool suite (with the formatter); reuse and grow lib instead of per-app copies", Feature, P1, D5, 50u32, Planned, "LIB-USAGE-VERB", "lib,tooling,dogfood,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_usage_diagnostics () = Planning ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", Minutes 450u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:39Z", "main-thread", "14:39 main-thread first census (text-based, job tmp lib-usage.ps1 and lib-usage.tsv, 96 s over every tracked .spi/.spir in spiral/eoie/polyglot/dice/alphabet/x): lib/spiral has 1,361 top-level inl/let definitions in 70 modules. 41 are never referenced anywhere, 20 of them FParsec-style trailing-underscore variants in fparsec.spi (p_string_, many1_satisfy_, sep_by1_, not_followed_by_ ...: strong deletion candidates), plus seq 5, testing 4 and listm' 3. 671 are used only inside their own file (candidates for private helpers or inlining). Limit of the text method: backend.run_target shows as unused although it is used everywhere, because it comes into scope by a route a grep can't see. So the real tool must ask the compiler's name resolution (e.g. a --references mode next to --source-blocks that emits definition -> use sites per package) and report per repo, per package and per lib cell; later, generated-code duplication per crate for the 1k gate.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: inlining sprawl is wanted where it buys speed, so the tool must not just count expanded lines. Classify each expansion as hot or cold and measure it: an A/B per backend (inl vs let for the same definition) through the harness benchmark rows. Measured today: eoie agile_subcommand_shape, a reflection fold over 18 cases, was an inl called from 7 usage-error sites, and agile_mutation.rs went 614 -> 1567 lines. That is cold code (error paths), so the copies gain nothing at run time and cost rustc time and the 1k gate; as a let it is emitted once. Hot loops are the opposite case: specialization removes dispatch and allocation. Report per crate: expansion factor, hot or cold by benchmark evidence, and a suggested inl/let flip. Longer term the compiler could outline cold paths itself (like Rust's cold attribute).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_3 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 3u32, "2026-10-18T12:19Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:19 main-thread: (1) text census v2 in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1: resolves package.spiproj flat modules (backend-, base-, common-, trace-), open lines and qualified module.name uses, separates test-block uses, verdicts unused/tests-only/file-local/lib-only/used-outside (running). (2) compiler-backed references, in the tree, not built: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT=<file> makes prepass (shared section) record def rows at FInl/FRecInl and use rows whenever a RawV resolves outside local scope, linked by E object identity, flushed at process exit; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 aggregates them. Plan: a compile-only harness run with the variable set (no -ReuseCompile) gives every sample's real resolution, then diff the two censuses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_4 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 4u32, "2026-10-18T13:16Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:16 main-thread text census v2 (lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/census.ps1, 781 s at BelowNormal over 1,985 tracked .spi/.spir in spiral, eoie, polyglot, dice, alphabet, x, tibia): 1,408 lib definitions in 80 modules: used-outside 404, lib-only 254, file-local 401, tests-only 118, unused 231. Flat modules, open lines and qualified uses resolved, so run_target is used-outside (95 lib uses, 2 outside), which v1 called unused. Unused by module: async 25, rust 22, threading 19, runtime 19, leptos 18, sm' 17, file_system 10, listm' 8. Spot check of 5 async ones (base_let', block_on_futures_lite, block_on_async_std, block_on_tokio_send, stream_collect_tokio): each occurs only in its own header and definition across all 7 repos, so they are real. Fixes made on the way: ordinal (case-sensitive) indexes, operator counts from a per-file token table. Next: the compiler-backed census (qualified uses recorded since the 08:5x source) to cross-check, then deletion candidates per module.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_5 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 5u32, "2026-10-18T14:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:21 SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT census over the full compile-only suite (668 s): 1,422 lib defs; unused 639, lib-only 400, file-local 376, used-outside 7. The used-outside count of 7 conflicts with the text census v2 (404). Suspected cause: use rows carry tree-copy sample paths that the aggregator classifies as lib or file-local. Check the path normalization in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 before trusting the unused column.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_6 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 6u32, "2026-10-18T14:27Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 10:31 used-outside=7 explained, and it's not a path bug. Of 38,282 use rows, only 19 come from outside lib and target lib/spiral. The compile-only samples import their own samples/core (10.7k rows) and the VS Code Plugin core in deps (7.8k), not lib/spiral. So this suite is the wrong corpus for outside use. Next run: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over the consumers (Cells gate on lib, blog engine, eoie, apps/*), then aggregate. Second gap: 7,016 outside use rows have no target. Those are names reached through open (create/set/index after open am) and nominal constructors (cell_array, f64_array), which PrepassReferences does not attribute yet. Until it covers open-resolved names and nominal defs, the unused column overcounts.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_7 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 7u32, "2026-10-18T14:28Z", "main-thread", "Correction: note 6 was written at 10:27, not 10:31 (the eoie stamp 14:27Z is the right one).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_8 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 8u32, "2026-10-18T14:32Z", "main-thread", "10:32 patch plan for the reference recorder's open/nominal gaps (spiral_compiler.fs; not applied, no build today). (1) Local open: prepassModule_open (~52893) folds the opened module's members into env.term.env. RawV (~53148) records a use only when the name is absent from env.term.env, so every name reached through a local open is dropped as if it were a local. Fix: record whenever definitions.TryGetValue e succeeds, and keep the env.term.env guard only for unresolved rows (locals are EV values and are never in definitions). (2) Unattributed defs: FNominal/FNominalRec (~53314) and FPrototype (~53337) add terms without PrepassReferences.define, so constructors such as cell_array/f64_array and prototype names stay unresolved; call define on each nominal constructor's term and on the prototype's EForall. (3) Still unexplained: top-level open am uses of create/set in samples main.spi, unresolved although 226 other rows resolve to deps core/am. Check whether package re-prepass rebuilds E (identity loss) by dumping both sides on one sample. Verify with: SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT over one sample that uses a local open plus a nominal, expect 0 unresolved rows for those names; then rerun the consumer corpus census.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_9 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 9u32, "2026-10-18T14:34Z", "main-thread", "10:34 plan steps (1) and (2) applied in the tree, unbuilt: PrepassReferences.used_unless_local (RawV records a use when its E is a known def, even if the name is in env.term.env, which covers local open), and nominal_rec plus FPrototype call PrepassReferences.define (constructors at the nominal's name range). Changes only the recorder, gated on SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT. Verify in the next pack: one sample with a local open and a nominal, then the consumer corpus census. Step (3), identity loss on top-level open am, is still open.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_10 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 10u32, "2026-10-18T14:35Z", "main-thread", "10:35 step (3) resolved, and it's not identity loss. The unresolved create/set/index uses in samples main.spi (package core-) are prototypes: samples/core/operators.spi defines them as prototype plus instance (instance create array = ...). Before today's edit FPrototype never called define, so the in-tree FPrototype define covers them. Expected after the next build: the unresolved outside rows (7,016) drop to roughly the builtin and type-level names.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_11 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 11u32, "2026-10-18T14:39Z", "main-thread", "10:39 lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 now prints the unresolved use rows grouped by name. Today's run: 11,966 rows over 1,886 names. The top names are module heads (sm' 520, optionm' 292, leptos 284, resultm 264, am' 216, sm 181, listm 172) and union constructors (Nil 474, Cons 375, None 211, Some 180, Ok 144). Constructors are covered by the in-tree nominal define. Module heads get a new in-tree recorder rule: used_unless_local skips EModule values, since the member is already recorded through module_member. This is also unbuilt. Next build check: rerun references.ps1 and expect the top unresolved names to be builtins and type-level names only.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_12 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 12u32, "2026-10-18T15:42Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 11:42 main-thread: the recorder edits from note 9 onwards (used_unless_local, define on nominal constructors and prototypes, skipping EModule heads) were pushed by the user at 15:29Z in f5e487e0; spiral CI compiles them now, the first build of them anywhere. New in the tree since then, unbuilt: the ProcessExit flush retries File.AppendAllLines up to 100 times at 20 ms on IOException, because parallel compiler processes appending to one SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT file could lose a process's rows. New lab script lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 runs the consumer corpus: the Cells --gate over the 40 lib/spiral .spi files that have test blocks plus the 9 blog engine modules, in chunks of 20 paths (mix.bat command-line limit), with SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process so every compiler inherits the variable, memo off, a temp MIX_BUILD_PATH. It throws if no rows were recorded (compiler without the recorder), then runs references.ps1. Run it after the next install that contains the recorder.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_13 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 13u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: the recorder edits (used_unless_local, nominal constructors and prototypes, module heads skipped, and the ProcessExit flush retry) were built and installed with compiler pack 4 (2026-10-18 22:55 build, accepted 10-18 single-flight-20261018-234436) and ride pack 5; lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/references.ps1 turns SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT into the census Next: HEAVY QUEUE: one census over the Cells lib build with SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT set, to list never-reached lib definitions")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_14 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 14u32, "2026-10-19T12:58Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 08:58 lap 2 grain: verified the installed compiler carries the recorder (UTF-16 literal SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT found by byte search in spiral-bin/bin/single-flight/SpiralCompiler/Release/net11.0/SpiralCompilerCore.dll, built 10-18 22:57; note: a naive Unicode.GetString search misses it at an odd byte offset). The HEAVY QUEUE row for this item is concretely pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 (lib files with tests + blog engine + x test files, chunks of 20, process compiler, memo off). Edited the script to throw right after the first chunk when no reference rows were recorded, instead of after the full corpus, so a recorder-less compiler costs one chunk of the heavy slot, not the whole run. Next: after the run, read consumer-census.tsv and list never-reached lib definitions per file")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_15 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 15u32, "2026-10-19T13:43Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:43 lap 3 grain: references.ps1 now prints the census goal directly: never-reached lib definitions grouped per module, most first, with their names in line order (cut at 160 chars), right after the verdict tally, so the queued consumer-census run ends with the list this item asks for. Checked on the 10-18 census file (pre-fix recorder, lib-only corpus, so only indicative): unused 639 of 1,422 definitions, top modules leptos 46, runtime 45, parsing 41, async 40, sm' 36, physics 30, rust 30, am' 30; real-dialect defs are recorded (sm'_real show_real lib-only 3, format_real 2 + 1), so .spir modules are not falsely unused. Next: after the heavy census, compare unused per module against the 10-18 numbers and turn the top modules' unused names into deletion candidates (dangling check by stem across repos first)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_16 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:09 lap 5 grain: first deletion candidates checked by stem across every repo under C:\\home\\git (spi, spir, livemd, dib): of the 17 sm' names the 10-18 text census called unused, the definitions index_of_char, is_white_space, builder_append_line, char_is_alphanumeric, os_string_as_ref, base64_decode, utf8_decode occur only at their own header and definition in sm'.spi (plus the matching /// headers in sm'_real.spir); format_custom' and encoding_1252' are already gone; the rest are operator names (~#, ~##, ++\\\\#...) that need the compiler-backed census. Not deleted: lib is frozen in the core switch window, and the switch may move sm' itself. The queued census row is made concrete: pwsh lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/consumer-census.ps1 after the switch accept (counts on the old core would be stale at once).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_17 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:28 lap 6 grain: stem check extended to runtime and file_system (the 10-18 census's unused lists): all 29 names occur only in their own lib module across every repo under C:\\home\\git; the only outside hits are look-alikes with their own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy nominal process_exit_code, tictactoe 2023 lib_spi copy of path_display). Candidates: runtime 19 = the clap builder leftovers (arg_overrides_with, arg_num_args, arg_value_name, value_parser_path_buf, value_parser_expr, matches_get_occurrences, matches_values_of) and the .NET Process wrapper family (new_process, process_start, process_exit_code, process_has_exited, process_kill, process_begin_error_read_line, process_begin_output_read_line, process_add_output_data_received, process_add_error_data_received, process_wait_for_exit_async, event_data) plus execute_retry; file_system 10 = file_stream, directory_info_exists, directory_info_creation_time, file_move, path_buf_from, path_buf_strip_prefix, path_display, path_buf_is_symlink, get_directory_name, init_trace_file. With lap 5's 7 sm' names: 36 verified deletion candidates, all waiting on the switch all-clear (lib frozen) and the census to confirm no route the text scan misses.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_18 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 18u32, "2026-10-19T15:42Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:42 progress bookkeeping: the recorder is built and installed, the consumer census and references.ps1 are ready, and 36 deletion candidates are verified by stem; left: the compiler-backed census after the switch, then the deletions. Progress 40.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_19 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 19u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain: stem check on async + threading (40 plain names from the 10-18 unused list). 38 occur only in their own module across every repo; the futuresales livemd hit is Elixir code, not Spiral. Two are false positives of the text census: cancellation_token (runtime.spi 198, 210, 835, 1115; networking.spi 150) and mutex_guard_ref_mut (runtime.spi 918-1005) are used, most likely in type position, where a term and a type share one name and the text scan counts only one of the two. That is one more case for the compiler-backed census. Running candidate total: 7 sm' + 29 runtime/file_system + 38 async/threading = 74 definitions with no use anywhere, the two false positives removed.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_20 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:19Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:19 lap 8 grain: post-switch re-check of the 36 sm', runtime and file_system candidates on today's migrated lib: each still occurs exactly at its header and definition (sm' also its _real.spir header), and no .spi/.spir/.livemd under C:\\home\\git gained a use; the only outside hits are own definitions (eoie bootstrap_policy and proxy_failure_classification process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi copies) and backlog text. So the switch did not change the candidate list. Next: stage lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default) for the 36, applied after the compiler-backed census confirms")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_21 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 21u32, "2026-10-19T16:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:44 lap drive8 grain: deletion staging for the 36 verified candidates: lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS/delete-unused.py (dry run by default). Per name it finds the single /// ### header, requires the next line to be that definition, takes the indented body plus trailing blank lines, and re-checks that the name occurs exactly twice in its file; --apply writes only if every name passes (all or nothing, LF kept). Dry run on today's lib: 36 definitions, 209 lines (sm' 57, runtime 95, file_system 57), 0 problems; spot-checked builder_append_line 775-794 ends before builder_replace. Apply order: compiler-backed census confirms, evidence note, --apply, then lib Cells --gate over sm', runtime, file_system plus an apps/spiral compile (heavy queue)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_22 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 22u32, "2026-10-19T16:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:46 lap 9 grain: delete-unused.py now covers all 74 verified candidates: added the 38 async and threading names (census.tsv verdict unused, minus the false positives cancellation_token and mutex_guard_ref_mut and the 4 primed names the stem check did not cover). One had no /// ### header (async.spi default_cancellation_token right after cancellation_token), so the script now also takes a lone bare definition and expects 1 occurrence instead of 2. Dry run: 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_23 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 23u32, "2026-10-19T17:23Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:23 lap 10 grain: post-switch recheck plus a by-stem dangling check across every sibling repo (dangling-check-by-stem rule): delete-unused.py dry run still 74 definitions, 415 lines, 0 problems; a word-bounded search for all 74 names over C:\\home\\git\\*\\**\\*.spi,spir,livemd finds hits outside lib in only 4 files, all local homonyms, not lib consumers: eoie bootstrap_policy/outcome.spi and proxy_failure_classification define their own process_exit_code, tictactoe lib_spi/rust/util.spi:527 defines its own path_display (its own lib copy), and a futuresales livemd output line mentions an Elixir start_child. So the text census and the cross-repo stem check agree. Next: compiler-backed confirmation (consumer-census.ps1, heavy, queued), evidence note, apply, lib Cells --gate + apps/spiral compile")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_24 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 24u32, "2026-10-19T17:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:44 lap 11 grain: joined delete-unused.py's 74 (module, name) pairs with the compiler-backed references-census.tsv of 10-18 (SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT rows through references.ps1): all 74 are present and all 74 have verdict unused with SameFile 0, Lib 0, Outside 0. With today's text dry run and the cross-repo stem search (0 consumers) that is three independent views agreeing; the one gap is that the compiler census predates the core switch and covers the builds it was fed, so consumer-census.ps1 (lib test files + blog engine + apps, heavy, queued) remains the post-switch confirmation before --apply")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_25 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 25u32, "2026-10-19T22:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:48 lap 19 grain: consumer-census.ps1's corpus (lib tests, blog/engine, x) leaves out dice, alphabet and polyglot, which main migrated to core v1 at 18:3x; a whole-word text scan of their 43 .spi/.spir/.livemd files (deps/target/dist skipped) finds 0 uses of any of delete-unused.py's 74 candidates, bare or qualified, so those three repos add no keeper. Next: the compiler-backed consumer-census run (a full lib gate, main's queue), then delete-unused.py --apply")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_26 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 26u32, "2026-10-21T22:22Z", "x", "2026-10-21 18:22 x: re-checked x against delete-unused.py's 74 candidates on tonight's tree (after the post_id slice and glyphs.split_all): a whole-word scan of x/apps (36 .spi/.spir/.livemd files, target/_build/deps skipped), bare or qualified, finds 0 uses. x adds no keeper; the 10-19 census still holds for x.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_27 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 27u32, "2026-10-21T23:07Z", "main", "19:07 text census extended to the repos the 10-19 scan left out (ripgrep over .spi/.spir/.livemd, ignored dirs skipped, whole-word against all 74 delete-unused.py candidates): tibia 0 hits; spiral/apps 0 hits; eoie 6 hits, all of process_exit_code in bootstrap_policy/outcome.spi and proxy_failure_classification/failure_classification.spi, which is eoie's own 'nominal process_exit_code = i32' (its packages use |core- and its own contracts, never lib/spiral's runtime.process_exit_code), so not a keeper. With dice, alphabet, polyglot (10-19) and x (10-21 18:22, by x) every repo is now covered and none keeps any of the 74. Remaining before delete-unused.py --apply: the compiler-backed consumer census (a full lib gate), and the user's OK, since --apply deletes definitions from tracked lib files.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_28 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 28u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 1 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS until the item lands.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_29 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 29u32, "2026-10-22T06:35Z", "main", "02:35 main: lab drained into tooling, the item's own aim ('part of the branded tool suite'). In apps/compiler/tmp/scripts: lib-usage-census.ps1, lib-usage-consumers.ps1 (calls lib-usage-references.ps1; spiral root resolved from its new depth) and lib-usage-references.ps1, all with default outputs now in the temp dir instead of beside the script, so a run never writes into the repo. lib-usage-delete-unused.py finds lib/spiral at parents[4] and still writes only with --apply after the census confirmation; parse checks are clean and the paths resolve. The 10-18 census.tsv and references-census.tsv (outputs) moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS (sha256 checked). Folder deleted. Still open as before: the compiler-backed consumer census (a full lib gate), then the user's OK for --apply (it deletes definitions from tracked lib files; text census 0 keepers across every repo, notes 18:22 and 19:07).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_30 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 30u32, "2026-10-22T09:59Z", "main", "2026-10-22 05:59 main: deciding --apply myself (memory decide-autonomously, 10-21: rule-based deletions are mine, the user commits). The text census is done in every repo with 0 keepers (notes of 10-21 18:22 and 19:07). Rule fixed now: apply to the tree only if (a) the dry run on the current tree reports 0 problems, (b) on a lib copy with the 74 definitions removed, the Cells gates of the 5 touched files show no new failing row against tonight's tree baselines (sm' 45 of 45; file_system 18 of 18; runtime 6 of 7 with one flaky TypeScript row; threading 1 of 1; async 0 of 0), and (c) a full lib sweep of the copy (every lib file with tests, apps/cells/spi/lib_sweep.ps1) shows no failure that the tree does not also show, run after the pack's heavy jobs. Then land through lib_land.ps1 with its tree-hash guard. (a) PASSED: 433 lines in 74 definitions, 0 problems. The copy is %TEMP%\\lib-unused, applied with a copy of the script pointed at it: sm' 2050 -> 1993, runtime 1265 -> 1170, file_system 1346 -> 1289, threading 462 -> 372, async 501 -> 367 lines. (b) is queued behind tibia's block as job-1792663132617-main-unused-gates.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_31 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 31u32, "2026-10-22T10:11Z", "main", "2026-10-22 06:11 main: the queued gate job was granted a compile window in the gap between tibia's PR-21 and D21 live-gui windows (about 06:00-06:10), so it ran into tibia's block. Stopped it and released its window by hand (job stop left it held); no gate had finished, and tokens.spi and the lib copies are unchanged. Requeue only after tibia's explicit 'block end' (memory live-runs-alone, updated).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_32 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 32u32, "2026-10-23T00:26Z", "main", "2026-10-22 20:26 main: the combined lib gate (job-1792713100977) went red on a bug in the removal script, not in the lib. delete-unused-copy.py matched definitions with '{name}\\b', and since ' is a non-word character, 'nominal file_stream''' counted as file_stream's definition. It deleted the helper nominal and kept the unused 'inl file_stream', so file_system.spi:60 had an unbound type 'file_stream''', and the package-wide type check failed every test in the gated files (26 of 26 in the first). Fixed: the definition boundary is (?![\\w']), like the use counter's. A '/// ### name' section may hold primed helper nominals ahead of the definition; the section goes whole only when the helper has no use outside it. On the tree the dry run removes lines 60-71 (header, nominal file_stream', inl file_stream), with 0 problems over 74 definitions (442 lines). The combined gates were requeued as job-1792715175438, with the retype census after them.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_33 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 33u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 10:32 (subagent2): waiting: consumer census queued -> next: unused defs per module vs 10-18 | lap 12 14:06 (subagent2): re-dry-run after base.spi edit (14:04, +50/-2): still 74 defs/415 lines/0 problems; none of the 74 names in base.spi's added lines -> next: consumer-census.ps1 post-switch (heavy queue), then evidence note + apply | lap 13 14:31 (subagent2): consumer-census precondition checked: the installed Release SpiralCompilerCore.dll (13:56, after the pack 8 rollback) contains the SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT recorder (source line 52934), so the queued census will record rows on the first chunk -> next: consumer-census.ps1 (heavy queue), then apply | lap 14 14:59 (subagent2): post-apply gate list fixed: the 74 deletions touch 5 lib files (async 21, runtime 19, threading 17, file_system 10, sm' 7), all with test blocks, so the proof after --apply is one mix spiral.notebook --gate over those 5 plus an apps/spiral build (lib consumers per the corpus rule) -> next: consumer-census (queue), evidence note, apply, 5-file gate | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged (74 defs, 5 files); waits on consumer-census -> next: main's queue | lap 21 19:18 (subagent2): after helper lane 1's uncommitted lib edits: delete-unused.py dry run still 74 definitions / 415 lines / 0 problems, and a whole-word scan of lib finds no use of any candidate (1 hit is the header of return_await', a different name) -> next: consumer-census (main's queue)")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_34 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 34u32, "2026-10-27T08:54Z", "main", "10-27 04:54 main: 10-27 04:54 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The tools are IN THE TREE: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/lib-usage-references.ps1 (48 lines, 10-22; turns SPIRAL_REFERENCES_OUT rows into a per-definition census) and lib-usage-census.ps1 (109 lines, 10-25). The 10-19 lap was 'waiting: consumer census queue'. Per tmp/AGENTS.md the census needs the CONSUMER builds (Cells over lib, apps, eoie/kit, blog), because matrix samples import their own core. Next: one run over those consumers on a node, and record never-reached lib definitions as candidates (not deletions).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_35 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 35u32, "2026-10-30T14:14Z", "main", "10-30 10:14 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R3: Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. No note since 10-27 and no live job, so it isn't real WIP. Nothing blocks it; the concrete next step is in the previous note (owner main). Resume by picking it, never by re-verifying.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_36 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 36u32, "2026-10-31T20:57Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_37 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 37u32, "2026-11-01T22:55Z", "main", "main lib-reshape 11-01 19:0x TEXT CENSUS on today's :: lib (typed rows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-RESHAPE\\usage_census.spi; 4,056 top-level definitions incl. per-backend duplicates, 11 repos, eoie skipped as a kit junction). Verdicts: UsedOutside 850, LibOnly 532, FileLocal 2,428, Unused 246. KNOWN LIMITS (why the compiler-backed LIB-USAGE-VERB is still needed): operators are not tokenized (operator definitions show as Unused: sm'_operators 10/10, base 13, parsing 8 are mostly operators), and bare names of flat modules overcount (trace/common 'main', base 'append'). FINDINGS, ranked for keep/test/drop: (1) modules with ZERO uses outside lib: physics (208 defs, 185 file-local: a sample, not a lib module; move to a sample or an app), xpath_eval 146, channel 20, otp_supervisor 20, xsd_constraints 19, cli_flags 20, decimal 22, lustre 22, float_text 15, erlang_map 10, erlang_text 4, store 6, util 2; xpath_eval/xsd_constraints are reached by fiscal through xpath/xsd (check with the compiler census before any drop). (2) Unused names per module (non-operator, real candidates): rust 25 (rc/ref_cell/box_pin family), leptos 24 (route/action/memo/resource family), am' 11 (vec_for_each'', vec_chunks, slice'', iter_collect'', map'), threading 8 (rw_lock family, concurrent_stack), async 7, networking 6 (tcp client/listener), listm' 6 (replicate, map4, item_, index_of_ ...). (3) Where lib is load-bearing: fiscal is the biggest consumer by count (xml::xml_node 52,416, element_children 21,856 via generated XSD bindings), sm'::length (fiscal 1,208, kit 87, x 53), base pair/unit/use, common::return (kit 1,219). Tests to add first go where outside uses are high and the module has no :# rows (next census column). (4) par' had 0 outside uses: renamed to parallel in LIB-RESHAPE R1a rather than dropped (it's the only portable fan-out helper).")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_38 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 38u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: a tool lists lib functions and test cells reached from no app, sample or repo, and duplicated code, as a regenerated report. Next step: check whether the cells mount dependency data can drive it.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_39 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 39u32, "2026-11-07T22:16Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. TypeFeatures: the report as typed rows { definition; module; reached_from : list consumer } with consumer a union (App | Sample | Repo of string), fed by kit's repo scope once it maps lib modules to consumers (PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS gap found 11-07).")
inl kit_state_scale () = Task ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", "eoie backlog state bloat is intended (user 2026-10-17): state/agile.spi is now 1.15 MB and grows with every note; keep it as a realistic append-heavy log to simulate an Apache Iggy-style stream and measure how the compiler scales on real log data (parse/typecheck time of state/agile.spi per MB). Track the growth curve; refactor only if a measured threshold breaks a workflow", Story, P1, D4, 35u32, Planned, "", "kit,scale,iggy,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_state_scale () = Planning ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", Minutes 195u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_1 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (1/3) state/agile.spi measured read-only at 14:41 (before the IGGY-EVAL notes): 1,203,199 bytes, 1,588 lines, longest line 18,177 chars (the EOIE-DOGFOOD title). Rows: 337 Task (556,734 bytes, avg 1.65 KB), 1,040 Note (623,301 bytes, avg 600 B), 170 Planning; the rest is ~40 lines of union schema. Notes per UTC day: 10-14 306, 10-15 363, 10-16 118, 10-17 253 so far. Size over git commits: 10-08 43 KB, 10-09 53 KB, 10-12 163-180 KB, 10-13 313-416 KB, 10-14 686 KB, 10-15 791 KB, 10-17 06:53 996 KB, 13:00 1,091 KB, 14:45 1,213 KB: about 150-270 KB/day lately, so ~6-8 MB in a month if linear. Write path (eoie src/agile_note_model/model.spi): a note is not appended at EOF but spliced after the task's last note (notes_insertion), the whole text is re-read, compared to the original and replaced atomically (state_written; StateChangedSinceRead = optimistic concurrency, nothing written, caller retries); note counts and insertion points come from full-text scans. So every write is O(file) read + scan + rewrite. Receipts today: note writes 8-10 ms at 1.2 MB, show 4 ms: cheap now. Already broken threshold: the file is ~66k tokens, over the agent Read tool's 25k-token limit, so agents cannot read it whole (eoie agile show is the only path). typecheck_receipts.spi last attested agile.spi at 994,972 bytes.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_2 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (2/3) What to measure (no runs done by this lane). M1 compiler: wall time and peak RSS of the installed compiler's parse + typecheck of agile.spi (eoie agile check --compiler and the compiler's own phase timings), cold (session torn down, per BENCH-FRESH-PAIR) and warm, over the 11 git snapshots 43 KB..1.09 MB plus the live file, plus synthetic 2x/4x/8x copies (bindings renamed, same row mix). Fit time = a * MB^b: b > 1.2 flags superlinear behaviour; split the cost by driver (top-level binding count ~1,550, longest line 18 K chars, total string literal bytes) with single-variable synthetic files. M2 eoie verbs: elapsed_ms of note, set, show, list --json per snapshot (all receipts already print it), p50/p95 over a day of real use. M3 growth: bytes/day and notes/day from note timestamps (a one-line census; the ledger could log it daily). M4 contention: count StateChangedSinceRead rejections; today they are not recorded anywhere, so the note/set receipt should log retries.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_3 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (3/3) Thresholds that would warrant an Iggy-style segment layout (any one, measured twice): compiler check of state over 5 s warm or 15 s cold, or b over 1.3 at 4x size; peak RSS over 1.5 GB for the check; an agile write verb p95 over 100 ms; file over ~8 MB (about a month at the current rate) or a line over 64 KB; more than 1 StateChangedSinceRead retry per hour across lanes. Layout then: state/agile/ with an active segment for current task rows (projection) and append-only note segments sealed at ~1 MB or per month (notes appended at EOF, never spliced), each sealed segment immutable with its own typecheck receipt (typecheck_receipts.spi already keys modules by bytes + hash, so sealed segments are typechecked once and skipped), a small offset index per segment (item id -> note seqs) so show reads one index instead of scanning, and readers keeping their own offsets. To keep the scale signal after a split, freeze the monolithic file at each threshold crossing as a benchmark corpus, so compiler scaling stays measurable on real log data, which is the point of this item.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_4 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: measure first (11 git snapshots + 2x/4x/8x copies, warm/cold typecheck time and memory) and switch to the segment layout only when a threshold breaks (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, worse-than-linear growth); the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_5 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 5u32, "2026-10-21T00:02Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:02 eoie lane: first measured break. state/agile.spi is 2.62 MB (1.15 MB on 10-17, about +0.45 MB/day) with 3,458 top-level definitions, and 'eoie agile check' fails for every lane: the compiler's --check of state/prompt.spi overflows the stack (0xC00000FD in bundle_blocks/fold_offset_term) because each module parses on a pool thread (main 20:0x, COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK: parse on a 512 MB thread). Per the user's 10-17 decision the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus, and this break is that corpus doing its job, so the compiler fix comes first and eoie keeps the layout; main suggested splitting agile.spi into modules (by item range, or open vs Done). That stays the fallback: switch to a segment layout if check breaks again after the parse-stack fix, or when a 10-17 threshold trips (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, about 12 days at today's rate). Writes are unaffected.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_6 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 6u32, "2026-10-21T00:46Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:46 eoie lane: the check break is healed by the compiler (COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK installed, single-flight 546B267E40B5A0C1); eoie agile check exits 0 on the shared backlog again (main 20:5x). Single-file layout kept per the 10-17 decision.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_7 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T13:08Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 09:08 eoie team, growth check against the 10-17 thresholds: state/agile.spi is 2,991,721 bytes (2.62 MB at 10-20 20:02, so about +0.7 MB/day lately, up from ~0.45). Write verbs measured today: note 32-83 ms, set 148-373 ms (set includes the runtime attestation), add 26-83 ms; the 100 ms p95 threshold is about plain writes, and set's attestation stays under 0.4 s. No threshold broken. At this rate the 8 MB threshold arrives around 10-28; recheck then, or sooner if a write verb's p95 passes 100 ms. agile next took 2.7 s on the installed release 23; re-measure after r26 (read-once) installs.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_8 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T09:30Z", "eoie", "05:30 growth check: state/agile.spi is 3,788,562 bytes (2,991,721 at 10-21 09:08, so about +0.8 MB/day; tonight 02:30-05:30 alone added 139 KB from about 50 notes, the heaviest night so far). Write verbs measured tonight from their own receipts: note 24-131 ms (most 50-90), add 133-188 ms, set 205-504 ms (set includes the runtime attestation), all on a 3.6-3.8 MB state file; note latency has not grown with the ~25% size increase since 10-21.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_9 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 9u32, "2026-10-22T21:46Z", "eoie-lane", "17:46 measured against the thresholds of note 3 (iggy-research, written 10-17, before this measurement). File: 4,384,569 bytes (3.6x the 10-17 size), 5,545 lines, longest line 18,201 chars, no line over 64 KB, so the size thresholds (8 MB, 64 KB line) hold. Write latency from today's eoie-lane receipts (elapsed_ms): about 37 agile note/add writes, p50 ~74 ms, p95 ~163 ms; 7 agile set writes at 260-494 ms. Each write rewrites the whole file (bytes_before/after ~4.3 MB), and set also runs its validation. The 'agile write verb p95 over 100 ms' threshold is crossed, measured across a whole day of receipts, so by note 3 the segment layout is now warranted. Next step (design, no compile): a measured breakdown of a write (read/parse, validate, rewrite) on a copy of the state, then the active-segment layout from note 3 (state/agile/ with an active segment plus sealed segments), so a note appends to the active segment instead of rewriting 4.3 MB. Status moves to Active; the rewrite itself is a compile item.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_10 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 10u32, "2026-10-22T21:48Z", "eoie-lane", "17:48 write breakdown on a copy of state (4.3 MB; the live state untouched; the copy is deleted afterwards): agile show (read) p50 40 / p95 51 ms; agile note (append) p50 71 / p95 112 ms; agile set p50 283 / p95 290 ms (n = 10, 10, 5). So a note's whole-file rewrite costs ~30 ms over a read, and set spends ~210 ms more than note, which is its validation (receipts say validation=runtime-attested), not file size. Consequence for the layout decision: segmenting state cuts the ~30 ms rewrite plus part of the 40 ms parse, but set's cost is in what it validates. The next measurement is to profile set's validation before choosing the segment layout. Method: repeat each verb n times on a copy and read elapsed_ms from the receipts; eoie has no verb for that yet (EOIE-BENCH-VERB), so the script was not kept.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_11 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 11u32, "2026-10-22T21:51Z", "eoie-lane", "17:51 scaling measured (installed release binary, copies only): with the same schema and one Task row, a 3.4 KB state gives set 29-49 ms and note 2-4 ms; the 4.3 MB copy gives set 279-325 ms and note 60-86 ms in the same run. So both write verbs scale with file size: set ~ 27 ms fixed + ~65 ms/MB, note ~ 17 ms/MB. This corrects note 10's guess that set's extra time is validation: reading the code, set runs typed per-line export calls on all 5,545 lines, copies and rewrites the file, reads it back and compares, then the attest step snapshots and hashes the state, and every step is per-byte or per-line. Decision by the 10-17 threshold (p95 over 100 ms, crossed): the segment layout is justified, and the measured target is writes against a small active segment, set ~30 ms and note single-digit ms.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_12 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 12u32, "2026-10-22T21:56Z", "eoie-lane", "17:56 layout model drafted, not yet compiled (scratch f5/src/agile_state_segment_model; previews wait until F ends, since no compile of mine runs beside F). Task and Planning rows stay in the task module; notes append to an active segment module notes_<n>, which seals at 256 KiB, and the next note opens notes_<n+1>. write_target is TaskModule | ActiveSegment n | SealedThenNew (n, n+1); rewritten_bytes states the cost model: a note rewrites only the active segment (~250 KB instead of 4.3 MB), a task edit only the task module (~1.7 MB today). From the measured slopes (note ~17 ms/MB, set ~65 ms/MB + 27 ms), that predicts a note at ~7 ms and a set at ~140 ms, under the 100 ms p95 for notes and halving set. A further split of the task module (Done tasks sealed) is the next lever for set. Tests are written; Cells preview after F; standards 0 findings.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_13 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 13u32, "2026-10-22T21:57Z", "eoie-lane", "17:57 composition of the 4.39 MB state (task row plus its notes, by task status): Active 127 tasks 1.94 MB (44%), Done 320 tasks 1.69 MB (38%), Planned 65 tasks 0.43 MB, Paused 22 tasks 0.26 MB, Blocked 5 tasks 0.05 MB; Planning rows 27 KB; schema 1.5 KB; no orphan notes. Sealing Done tasks with their notes into a sealed module takes 38% off what an Active task's set rewrites (with the note segments, set's rewrite drops from ~4.3 MB to the ~1.7 MB of Active tasks plus their recent notes). Notes of Active tasks are the largest share, so the note segments carry most of the gain.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_14 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 14u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 195m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 20%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_15 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 15u32, "2026-10-26T05:34Z", "eoie", "01:34 eoie: root cause of agile list taking 6-8 s, which is not the parse. A list costs about 10-20 ms per printed row at every filter (17 rows 314 ms, 125 rows 2.5 s, 792 rows 7.8 s). Each row calls agile_planning_fields::kit_agile_planning_columns_near with the whole state text, and the generated export takes v0 as a borrowed str and converts it with Rc::from(v0), so every call copies the whole 6.6 MB state: about 5 GB of allocation and copying per full list. The planning lookup itself is bounded (planning_offset_near searches only up to the next Task row). 228 exported kit functions take a borrowed str and copy it into an Rc. Fixes, by blast radius: (1) eoie: build a planning index once per call inside task_listing (like note_index_of) and look rows up in it, so the state crosses no crate boundary per row; (2) compiler: exports could take an Rc so a cross-crate string argument costs a refcount bump (asked of the compiler lane). Either one removes the quadratic term. This also moves EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG phase 1: the memo may be unnecessary for list once (1) lands; measure R1 after (1).")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_16 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 16u32, "2026-10-26T06:17Z", "eoie", "02:16 eoie: node proof of the list planning index (scratch kit-rename\\list-planning-index.do; regen agile_task_listing, debug build, run against a copy of the live 7.0 MB backlog). Output is byte-identical to the unfixed candidate on all four views (table 136,766 chars, --json 844,019, Active, --open --load light), with elapsed fields stripped. Timing in debug builds on a node at 90%+ CPU was mixed: json 35.0 s to 12.5 s, table 17.9 s to 16.6 s, Active 10.4 s to 13.2 s, light 17.9 s to 15.8 s. So the per-row 6.6 MB copy dominates the json path, but in debug the table path has other per-row costs. Correction to note 15: the copy explains json, not yet table. Rule for the verdict, written now: release builds of both binaries, 5 runs each on a quiet machine, median; accept when table and json both improve and outputs stay identical. Next: profile the table path's per-row work (table_line and the sort) in a release build.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_17 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 17u32, "2026-10-26T06:45Z", "eoie", "02:45 eoie: reading the list path after the node proof (note 16). (1) Table and json rows each made exactly one whole-state copy per row (planning_columns_near or planning_json_near), and the index fix removes both. So json saving 22.5 s while table saved 1.3 s cannot both come from the copy: that run timed wall clock, in debug builds, on a node at 100% CPU (the Active view even got slower with less work), so it is noise, not evidence. Its only solid result is the byte-identical output. (2) In debug, parsing every task row (task_row_at for about 800 tasks, about 300 ms total in release here: Blocked list 314 ms) costs seconds at any filter, so debug wall time hides per-row effects. (3) A second, smaller quadratic term: rows_from builds the sorted row_list by insertion into a linked list (row_inserted, about n^2/2 = 314,000 steps for 792 rows, each allocating a node). Cheap per step, but quadratic, and it grows with the backlog; replace it with collect-then-sort (a lib sort on a list) when the index fix lands. Measurement rule unchanged (written in note 16): release builds of both binaries, 5 runs each, median of the binary's own elapsed_ms, on a quiet machine.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_18 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 18u32, "2026-10-27T17:44Z", "kit", "kit (fc1568a6), 13:4x: the 8 MB threshold of the 10-17 decision broke as a hard failure. At 13:31, state/agile.spi reached 8,390,714 bytes, 2,106 over the 8 MiB read guard in agile_state_runtime (agile_read_regular_text_limited at 8*1024*1024, five call sites), so every agile write (note, set, add, edit) failed for every lane. Reads still worked. Fiscal reported it; main measured that notes are 7.53 MB of the 8.39 (Active 4.47, Done 1.99, Planned 0.54, Blocked 0.32, Paused 0.22), with growth of 0.5-1.0 MB/day. Emergency unblock at 13:39, data only, no release: the 2,543 notes of the 403 Done items (1,991,308 bytes) moved verbatim and in order to docs/backlog/archive/done-notes.spi, still as inl note_of_* lines, so they merge back unchanged. Before writing, the script proved kept + archived lines = the original, in order, and that every moved note belongs to a Done item. It wrote a hashed backup of the full pre-trim file, which also keeps the 8.39 MB compiler-scaling corpus: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\state-backups\\agile-20261027-133935-09DB8912A567.spi. Script: scratch clean/agile-trim/done-notes-trim.ps1. Results: state 6,399,406 bytes; show, list Done (403) and a note write all ok; agile check ok on a frozen copy (build_attested=3; live attestation kept retrying while lanes replayed their held writes). Cost: agile show on a Done item no longer lists its archived notes. Headroom is ~1.9 MB, about 2 days. By the 10-17 rule the segment layout is now due (notes in their own append-only segments, the 8 MiB guard per file), and the 10-22 model draft (agile_state_segment_model) is the starting point. The Done-notes archive stays as a periodic trim until then.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_19 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 19u32, "2026-10-27T17:53Z", "kit", "kit 13:5x, correcting note 18: 'agile note' never failed. Its path reads the state without the 8 MiB guard, and on a copy of the 8.39 MB pre-trim file a note still writes (ok, 1.1 s). The guarded writers failed: 'agile set' (reproduced: 'regular file exceeds limit ... limit=8388608'), and by the code add and edit. Bridge staged for TRAIN as next-train step 5g (scratch clean/agile-trim/agile-guard-64.do): the guard goes 8 -> 64 MiB at its 8 sites in agile_state_runtime, and the lease's attestation reader goes 8 -> 64 MiB per file with a 128 MiB total. post-install-TRAIN runs agile-guard-check.ps1, a set plus a note on that 8.39 MB copy, which fails on today's binary through the guard error.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_20 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 20u32, "2026-10-28T11:54Z", "eoie", "07:5x kit: segment-layout design, written now so it's ready before a threshold breaks. State today: state/agile.spi 6.95 MB, archive/done-notes.spi 1.99 MB. The guard is 64 MiB per file since TRAIN2c (10-28 05:25); growth is about 0.5-1.0 MB/day of notes. Layers: (1) Done notes leave the state through 'agile seal' (ships in TRAIN3), which moves every Done item's notes to archive/done-notes.spi, byte-identical to the 10-27 hand trim. Running it at each day's first groom keeps the state at roughly open items plus their notes. (2) If the open-item notes alone grow past the rule below, notes move to monthly segments, state/notes-YYYY-MM.spi, each holding the Note rows written that month. state/agile.spi keeps Task rows and planning fields; readers (show, list --notes counts, next) merge the segments by item id, the same way show already merges the archive (note 1 of KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE). Writes append only to the current month's file, so a note write touches a small file and a 1 MB parse instead of the whole log. Pre-registered trigger, decided before any measurement: start (2) when state/agile.spi passes 24 MiB after a seal, or when an 'agile note' write on the installed release binary takes over 2 s median of 5 on the review box. Until then, (1) only. Next step: a daily seal after TRAIN3 installs, plus a size and timing line added to the morning groom.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_21 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 21u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 35%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_22 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 22u32, "2026-11-01T13:17Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: the 'append-only note segments' step this item planned is IMPLEMENTED and node-verified (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG: state/notes_<yyyymm>.spi, writer gated by KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1, note 287-306 ms vs ~2,550 ms whole-file on a copy, readers merge segments), shipping with TRAIN7. So EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG's dep on this item is satisfied in substance. TENSION FOR THE USER to see in one place: the 10-17 decision 'state bloat is intended' (a realistic append-heavy log for measuring compiler scale) now meets measured costs: agile.spi passed 10 MB on 11-01 08:03 and a whole-file rename ran 421 s and lost the write race twice (KIT-SCALE n2, STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA L2, recurring since 10-25). Segments keep every note (the measurement data survives) while removing the whole-file rewrite from the hot path, so they serve both goals; a seal (KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE) still waits on this item. NEXT: after TRAIN7, record the compiler-scale measurement this item wanted on the segmented state, then decide here whether the measurement is done.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_23 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 23u32, "2026-11-05T05:52Z", "tibia", "11-05 01:52 tibia (READY light item; data point only): GROWTH CURVE. state/agile.spi is 13.73 MB with 13,734 Note rows (10-17 1.15 MB; 10-28 6.95 MB; 11-01 past 10 MB), so it grew about 0.9 MB/day over the last 4 days. archive/done-notes.spi is still 1.90 MB, unchanged since 10-27 13:39, so no seal has run since the hand trim. There are NO notes_<yyyymm>.spi segment files, and KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS is unset in lane shells, so the segment writer (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG, TRAIN7) is not on and every note still rewrites the whole file. WRITE COST seen by tibia tonight, from kit's own elapsed_ms on 12 'agile note' calls 00:52-01:49 local: 1493-4626 ms (typical 1.5-2.8 s), plus 2 CAS-race-free sets at 835-1290 ms; bytes_before/after show other lanes writing between my reads. That is about 6-15x the 287-306 ms the segment writer measured on a copy. The 64 MiB guard is about 55 days away at this rate. A workflow-breaking threshold is not hit yet, but turning segments on (env default or flag) is the measured lever.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_24 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 24u32, "2026-11-06T11:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-STATE-SCALE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_25 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 25u32, "2026-11-09T05:16Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 01:3x rig MEASURED (the item's own rule: refactor only when a measured threshold breaks a workflow). state/agile.spi 9.87 MiB (1.15 MB on 10-17, 9x in 3 weeks); notes per day 408-883 (11-06: 883, 11-07: 798, 11-08: 730). Read times on the box, TRAIN41 kit, quiet-ish box: agile list all --json 6.4 s, agile next --team rig --open 6 8.4 s, agile show <one id> 4.2 s (every read parses the whole state). THRESHOLD NEAR: the autonomous stop gate gives its list and next calls 15 s total in parallel threads; at 8.4 s plus load it can overrun and offer an EMPTY list (seen once in tonight's scratch gate test, run 1). Writes queue behind agile.spi.lock (tonight one set waited about 3 min behind other lanes). Options in order of cost: (1) a seal now (move Done items' notes to archive, as the 11-07 seal did) and a scheduled seal when next passes 7 s; (2) a read index (per-item byte offsets) so show and next stop parsing the whole file; (3) split state per team. DoD: next under 5 s at the current note rate, measured. Raised P3 to P1 because the gate is a live consumer. Next step: (1), measure next before and after. TypeFeatures: a state_cost record (bytes, parse_ms, next_ms) logged per seal so the curve is data.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_26 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 26u32, "2026-11-09T05:21Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 01:4x rig OPTION (1) DONE: agile seal moved 635 notes of Done items to the archive (sealed=635 new_in_archive=635, 10347212 -> 9768892 bytes, 40 s under the lock; archive now: done-notes.spi 10,177,175 B 01:19). agile next --team rig went 8.4 s -> 7.1 s cold, 4.8 s warm. agile check could not confirm: it timed out twice on the compiler typecheck of state/views.spi (332 bytes, untouched since 11-07) with the box at 100 pct CPU from other lanes, a load timeout, not a seal fault; next and list parse the sealed state cleanly. PENDING: agile check rerun on a quiet box. The gain is small (6 pct of bytes), so option (2), a per-item read index, is what gets next under 5 s cold; scheduled seal when next passes 7 s still applies.")
inl note_of_kit_state_scale_27 () = Note ("KIT-STATE-SCALE", 27u32, "2026-11-09T06:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:5x local rig: the PENDING agile check after tonight's seal ran on a quieter box: kit agile check ok files=7 (build_attested 6, compiler available, source deployed), exit 0 in 29 s; so the sealed state (635 notes moved to the archive) is valid; the earlier two timeouts were load (box at 100 pct), not the seal. Only warnings: two new rig items lacked load/assignee, fixed.")
inl iggy_eval () = Task ("IGGY-EVAL", "Evaluate integrating Apache Iggy (persistent message streaming) in our projects: typed GADT/existential messages with reflection-derived codecs could make multidimensional sagas (compensations indexed by state at the type level); candidates: eoie job registry and receipts (EOIE-JOBS), the matrix run ledger, Kino cell events, xsim. Local workaround and evaluation only (no upstream contact)", Story, P3, D5, 100u32, Done, "", "iggy,messaging,research", "current", "current")
inl ci_offload () = Task ("CI-OFFLOAD", "Use the GitHub Actions minutes freed by dropping Fable (builds no longer need them, even on the NEAR projects) for heavy processing that spares the laptop: full matrix suites with cached compile-memo/native-memo, benchmarks, Kino lib suites, bundle mining. Results come back as artifacts the local tools read (run ledger rows, results.tsv). Learn first from the recent Anthropic Scion case and the DeltaDB release (research notes on this item)", Story, P1, D5, 75u32, Active, "", "ci,benchmark,offload,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_offload () = Planning ("CI-OFFLOAD", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_offload_1 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread research 2026-10-17. Anthropic Scion (engineering post 2026-10-13; thenewstack.io/anthropic-scion-git-replacement): Anthropic moved its monorepo off Git to an in-house system after Claude pushed merges from ~2,500/day (May) to 30,000+/day (Sept); its CPU and memory needs are about 200x below the Git mirroring it replaced; a team of ~7 built it in ~5 months, and it is not released. Zed DeltaDB (zed.dev/blog/delta-public-beta, Delta public beta 2026-09-16): extends Git's content-based versioning with incremental deltas and stores the agent conversations and instructions behind each change, while a commit stays the checkpoint you push, pull and build. Lessons for us: (1) agent-scale work needs content-addressed, incremental state; our compile/native memos and reproducible builds are the same idea applied to the harness, and CI can run them cheaply on cached memos; (2) link work history to the conversations behind it (DeltaDB): eoie notes with --as lanes already do this per item, and EOIE-MEMORY plus commit links would make it per change; (3) offload heavy runs to CI only where the result is content-keyed and replayable locally.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_2 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 2u32, "2026-10-18T12:26Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 08:26 main-thread first slice: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ci-runs.ps1 [-RunId N] downloads the compiler job's compiler-oracle artifact (EXPECTED.tsv + runs) of the newest completed schedule/workflow_dispatch run into <cache>/runs/ci-<id>, where compare-runs.ps1 can diff a Linux CI run against a local one. Tried on 39121783429: it fetched the formatter report and the per-worker compile results; that run died before results.tsv (SetErrorMode, fixed in the tree), so the first full comparison waits on the next green nightly.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_3 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: first slice ci-runs.ps1 is in apps/compiler/tmp/scripts; blocked on a scheduled run that reaches the oracle upload: none fired on 10-19 by 12:2xZ and the 10-18 one died at SetErrorMode (fixed in HEAD 668568ac) Next: after the 10-20 scheduled run, ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against today's pack 5 run (Linux vs Windows diff)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_4 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:00 lap 2 grain: schedule reliability measured from gh run list -R i574n/spiral --workflow gh-pages.yml: cron is 17 6 * * * (06:17Z) and the compiler job runs only on schedule or workflow_dispatch; the 10-18 schedule run started 11:04Z (4h47m late) and the 10-19 one has not started at 12:59Z (6h42m late, maybe dropped: GitHub sheds cron runs under load). The last 12 runs hold exactly one schedule run (39121783429, failed at SetErrorMode, artifact compiler-oracle 239 KB without results.tsv). So the offload path cannot rely on cron alone: a workflow_dispatch after each push the user wants scored (user-triggered; agents never trigger runs) or a second cron hour would make the Linux comparison predictable. Decision recorded for the user: none needed from agents; ci-runs.ps1 already accepts workflow_dispatch runs. Next: when any schedule or dispatch run with results.tsv exists, run ci-runs.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1 against single-flight-20261019-072348")
inl note_of_ci_offload_5 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:45 lap 3: FIRST LINUX-VS-WINDOWS COMPARISON DONE. The 10-19 schedule run fired at 13:15Z (6h58m late), compiler job 39258592236 ran -Suite all -Native on HEAD 668568ac and uploaded compiler-oracle with results.tsv (7,007 rows); ci-runs.ps1 -RunId 39258592236 fetched it into spiral-bin/runs/ci-39258592236, and compare-runs.ps1 against the accepted pack 5 run single-flight-20261019-080524 gives 12,720 differing cells. Classified: (1) 12,4xx are no-toolchain on the runner (Kotlin 452, Swift 451, Lua 448, Lean 447, Wasm 443, Delphi 406, Bend 401, Wgsl 50, Calyx 28): the CI job installs only BEAM, bun, zig and python. (2) Linux exit-code semantics: zig_exit_wide exits 49 (305 mod 256) on C/Rust/Zig/Gleam/Python while F# reports 305, so the only DISAGREE is F#; C abort() is 134 (SIGABRT) where Windows gives 3, so managed_string_invalid_utf8_slice and abi_external_buffer_length_negative turn agree-fail on 5 backends. (3) Rust build-fail on 5 array_* rows: rustup raced installing +1.99.0 under -Parallel 2 (could not rename downloaded component). (4) mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# (root and all_terminals) exits 0 on Linux but 1 on Windows. (5) RESIDUAL differences between platforms: Swift 75 and Kotlin 75 (closure_pair*, c_self_tail_loop_heap, c_deep_list_decref ...), Ebpf 17 (ebpf_bacc, facc, nest3, nested, t2, t3, tacc, tworec ...), OpenQasm 6 (ebpf_t3, tacc, native_tail_recursion, tail_loop, ts_tail_loop, python_self_tail_call_depth): the same compiler source emits different text on Linux, which contradicts byte-reproducible output and needs its own item. Fix applied for (3): scripts/workflow.ps1 -Compiler now reads the matrix rustc toolchain from test.ps1 (regex on the Rust Invoke-Native line; finds 1.99.0 at HEAD and nightly-2026-10-18 in the tree) and runs rustup toolchain install once before test.ps1 (parse-checked; needs the user's push). (2) and (5) are test.ps1 / compiler matters for main. Next: open the residual-divergence question by diffing one Ebpf output (ebpf_tacc) between platforms once the artifact carries outputs, or regenerate it on WSL when WSL is allowed")
inl note_of_ci_offload_6 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:14 lap 4 grain: made the residual divergence diagnosable. The compiler job writes every output next to its sample (and accepts), so the runner's checkout already holds the Linux outputs; the artifact never carried them. gh-pages.yml compiler job: new always() step git diff --no-color -- apps/compiler/tmp/samples > \\/samples-output.diff, and the compiler-oracle artifact now uploads that file (YAML parse-checked; a run: step, so actions.lock is unaffected). ci-runs.ps1 prints the number of committed outputs that differ on the runner, grouped by extension, when the diff is present. Together they show the exact Swift/Kotlin/Ebpf/OpenQasm text that differs between Linux and Windows (75/75/17/6 residual hashes on run 39258592236). Needs the user's push; the next schedule or dispatch run carries it. Next: read the first samples-output.diff and classify each divergence (platform-dependent codegen vs path or newline leaks)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_7 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:12 lap 5 grain: narrowed the Linux-vs-Windows residual divergence by joining CI run 39258592236 against single-flight-20261019-080524 on rows that compile ok on both sides: Ebpf 17, Kotlin 75, OpenQasm 6, Swift 75, and the Swift and Kotlin sets are IDENTICAL (closure_pair*, method_pair*, tuple_pair, nested_tuple, record_value, tail loops, managed_string_*, native_string_utf8_*_source, wgsl_record_loop ...), while C, Rust, Zig, F#, Gleam, Python agree on the same samples. Ruled out: the compiler source (tree spiral_compiler.fs equals 668568ac, the commit CI built; sample .spi inputs unmodified) and CRLF (spiral_compiler.fs and the committed outputs hold 0 CR bytes, so multi-line literals cannot carry a CR). Suspects: the four divergent codegens are the newest, adjacent ones (codegenKotlin 170288, codegenSwift 171050, codegenOpenQasm 171797, codegenEbpf 172097) and share local name tables, so the cause is in shared helpers used only there (e.g. culture-dependent text or ordering; ICU on Linux vs NLS on Windows). The uncommitted samples-output.diff artifact step (lap 4, needs the user's push) shows the exact text. Next: read that diff and check the shared helpers of the four codegens.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_8 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:29Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:29 lap 6 grain: the Linux-vs-Windows triage is one call now: lab/CI-OFFLOAD/classify.ps1 -Local <run> -Ci <ci-run dir> joins results.tsv rows and classifies each differing row (no-toolchain-on-runner, toolchain-install-race, residual-only, oracle-only, exit-mod-256, abort-exit-134-vs-3, behavior) with per-backend counts and lists the unclassified ones. On run 39258592236 vs single-flight-20261019-080524: 3,171 differing rows = no-toolchain 3,126 (the Kotlin/Swift residual divergences hide inside these rows because their compile still runs), residual-only 23 (Ebpf 17, OpenQasm 6), oracle-only 6, exit-mod-256 5, abort 134-vs-3 2, toolchain race 1, behavior 8. The 8: 4 Rust array_* build-fail (the same rustup race, detail truncated before could not rename), mega_omniledger_erp_kernel F# exit 1 local vs 0 Linux (x2), and NEW: native_cube_flush_delay_direct and native_cube_monotonic_delay_direct are C build-fail (Known) on Windows but build and exit 42 on Linux, so their Known entries are Windows-only facts and should carry that scope. Promote classify.ps1 next to ci-runs.ps1 once the user's push lands the diff artifact.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_9 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:46 lap 7 grain, refines lap 6's NEW item: the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C Known entries already say why (POSIX-only residual, poll.h and clock_gettime; native C runs only on Linux), and the Linux run confirms it: both build and exit 42 there. So the matrix is right, and the gap is that a Known entry cannot name a platform: on Linux these rows ran and agreed, yet the Known entry still matches by id and backend whenever such a row is DISAGREE or build-fail, so a Linux regression on them would be masked. Proposal for main (test.ps1/matrix are main's): an optional Platform key on Known entries (Windows or Linux, compared with IsWindows) next to the existing Oracle scope, and Platform = 'Windows' on these two. classify.ps1 then reports them under their real verdict on Linux.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_10 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:20Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:20 lap 8 grain: lap 5 suspects checked by reading codegenKotlin and codegenSwift (spiral_compiler.fs 170352-171849): every float and int literal goes through ToString with InvariantCulture (R for floats), generated method and closure names are ASCII-only (generatedMethodName/generatedClosureName 161549), method and closure tags come from Dictionary insertion order (dict.Count), and there is no sort, culture compare or Environment.NewLine in those codegens; the only StartsWith calls take ASCII prefixes. So culture-dependent text is ruled out for the Kotlin/Swift residual divergence. Next: the samples-output.diff artifact after the user's push; if it shows name or order changes, look at closureBindingNames and the structural method/closure keys")
inl note_of_ci_offload_11 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:47 lap 9 grain: the Platform proposal from lap 7 is staged as lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform.py for main (test.ps1 and matrix.psd1 are main's files): Get-ScopedKnown (test.ps1:1002-1006) is the only place Known entries are filtered (both oracles go through it), so one Where-Object clause adds an optional Platform key (Windows or Linux, from IsWindows) next to the Oracle scope, and the two native_cube_*_delay_direct C entries (matrix.psd1:29-30) get Platform = 'Windows'. Dry run: 3 anchors match once, LF. Also checked: no new spiral CI run since 39258592236 (13:15Z schedule on 668568ac), so classify.ps1's split stands")
inl note_of_ci_offload_12 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:24 lap 10 grain: classify.ps1 rerun of CI 39258592236 against the pack-7 review single-flight-20261019-124451: 4 of the 9 behavior rows were the Rust toolchain race in another spelling (rustup: missing manifest in toolchain 1.99.0 ... installation was interrupted, array_cell and 3 more array samples), so the toolchain-install-race class now matches that text too (5 Rust rows). Behavior left: 5 rows = the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows (known-platform.py, dry run still 3 anchors once), plus mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and its all_terminals on Fsharp (local exit 1, CI exit 0; no C or Zig row, so no oracle catches it) and zig_lib_console Fsharp (residual and stdout differ). No newer CI run since 39258592236. Next: main applies known-platform.py with a pack; someone checks why the local F# mega row exits 1 (fsi stderr in the review's row detail)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_13 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: explained the 2 F# mega behavior rows (mega_omniledger_erp_kernel and all_terminals, local exit 1 vs CI exit 0): ran the committed main.fsx with the matrix's exit wrapper in dotnet fsi (one process, scratch copy): Win32Exception, An error occurred trying to start process /bin/sh, at main.fsx line 14, i.e. the sample's own F# macros (main.spi:8042, 8202, 8389: external-recovery, fenced-lock and cross-process programs) spawn /bin/sh, which exists only on the Linux runner. So these rows are platform-bound like the 2 native_cube_*_delay_direct C rows: a Platform = Linux scope (known-platform.py's mechanism) or a Windows-scoped Known entry for the 2 F# rows would make local and CI agree; no compiler bug. Behavior left unexplained: zig_lib_console F# only")
inl note_of_ci_offload_14 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:07 lap 12: known-platform.py is applied (test.ps1 Platform filter, 2 cube C rows Windows-scoped). Staged lab/CI-OFFLOAD/known-platform-fsharp.py: the existing all_terminals Fsharp Known entry gets Platform = Windows (its F# macros spawn POSIX programs); dry run matches 1 anchor; queue row for main")
inl note_of_ci_offload_15 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 15u32, "2026-10-19T18:32Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:32 lap 13: all 5 behaviour rows of CI 39258592236 against the pack 7 accept are now explained: 2 cube C rows (Windows-scoped in the tree), 2 F# mega rows (POSIX programs; known-platform-fsharp.py staged) and zig_lib_console Fsharp, whose committed main.fsx printed a bool through _.ToString() (True) while the uncommitted compiler emits an explicit true/false (git diff main.fsx line 9), so that row resolves with the push. Next: classify the first CI run after the push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_16 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 16u32, "2026-10-19T18:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:44 correction to the 14:07 and 14:32 notes: test.ps1 applies a Known entry only to a DISAGREE or build-fail row (test.ps1:1033-1036), and the two F# mega rows have no oracle row, so a Platform scope on all_terminals would change nothing; known-platform-fsharp.py is withdrawn (queue row marked) and deleted. And the cube C and F# mega rows keep differing field by field after the push, so behaviour 0 was wrong. Fix in the lab tool instead: classify.ps1 reads lab/CI-OFFLOAD/platform-bound.tsv (4 id/backend rows with reasons) and classes them platform-bound; against the pack 7 accept it now reports platform-bound 4 and behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console, which the push resolves)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_17 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 17u32, "2026-10-19T22:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:50 lap 19 grain: no push since 02:48 (latest CI is still the 09:15 schedule run 39258592236), so classify.ps1 was rerun against the newest accepted baseline, pack 9 (single-flight-20261019-161549), instead of pack 7: 4,433 of 7,007 CI rows differ and all are classed: no-toolchain-on-runner 3,126, oracle-only 1,168, residual-only 122, exit-mod-256 5, toolchain-install-race 5, platform-bound 4, abort-exit-134-vs-3 2, behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console Fsharp, which the push resolves). So packs 8b and 9 add no CI-visible behaviour change. Next: classify the first CI run after the user's push, expecting behaviour 0")
inl note_of_ci_offload_18 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 18u32, "2026-10-19T23:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:28 lap 21 grain, what to offload first, measured from today's heavy-gate ledger (spiral-bin/ledger/runs.tsv, 584 jobs, 22.3 h of run time, 3.2 h of queue wait): Kino/Cells notebook runs 6.8 h (297 jobs; pack_sim.spi alone 41 min in 20 runs, date_time.spi 41 min in 7), eoie release pipelines 5.2 h (106 jobs, several 26-45 min installs), suite accepts 4.2 h (7 runs of 43-64 min), reviews 1.7 h, censuses and sweeps 1.7 h, probes and candidate builds 0.9 h. An accept is the best first candidate on paper (deterministic, must reproduce a review row for row) but CI lacks 7 native toolchains (3,126 no-toolchain rows in the last classify), so a CI accept cannot replace a local one; Kino notebook gates are the larger and more portable share (C, Rust, Gleam, F#, Zig all exist on the runner), so the first offload should be a Cells lib gate job whose metrics TSV comes back as an artifact. Next: draft that job's inputs and artifact shape (no workflow edits or triggers from this lane)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_19 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 19u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:45 cells-gate-ci.ps1 checked locally (bounded job job-1792457040116 through lab cells-gate-ci-local.ps1, which only adds the heavy gate, a private MIX_BUILD_PATH and SPIRAL_CC): -Match routes.spi gated 1 file in 57 s and wrote the artifact folder as designed, cells-gate.tsv (routes.spi 1 discovered, 1 executed, 1 passed, 0 failed), metrics-0/tests.tsv and gate.log. So the CI step script works end to end on one file; a CI job only needs the compiler build that workflow.ps1 -Compiler already does, then this script over all 40 files and an upload of the folder. Next: the user wires the job (workflow edits and actions.lock are theirs)")
inl note_of_ci_offload_20 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 20u32, "2026-10-21T23:08Z", "main", "19:08 drafted the workflow job for the user to wire in (no workflow edit or trigger from this lane): lab/CI-OFFLOAD/cells-gate-job.yml.txt is a cells-gate job for spiral's gh-pages.yml. It runs on ubuntu-24.04 on workflow_dispatch and the existing 06:17 schedule, so it never adds time to a push. It reuses the build job's pinned actions (checkout v6.1.0, setup-rust v1.3.0 nightly-2026-10-18, setup-beam v1.24.1 OTP 29 / Elixir 1.20 / Gleam 1.19.2, setup-dotnet v5.4.0, setup-zig v2.2.1), then runs workflow.ps1 -Compiler (the compiler build only), cells-gate-ci.ps1 into runner.temp/cells-gate, and uploads that folder as the cells-gate artifact even on failure. No new uses: entries, so actions.lock needs no regeneration (memory actions-lock-on-workflow-edits). The local side reads it through ci-runs.ps1-style download into spiral-bin/runs. Blocked only on the user pasting it under jobs: and committing.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_21 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 21u32, "2026-10-22T06:30Z", "main", "02:30 main: lab drained and the job wired, uncommitted, for the user. The cells-gate job is now in spiral/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml between compiler and push: ubuntu-24.04, workflow_dispatch and schedule only (never adds time to a push), the build job's pinned actions (no new uses:; spiral has no actions.lock), workflow.ps1 -Compiler, then apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 -Out runner.temp/cells-gate, with the artifact uploaded even on failure. YAML parses with jobs build, compiler, cells-gate, push. Promoted: cells-gate-ci.ps1 is now apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 (root path fixed for that depth); classify.ps1 is now apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/ci-classify.ps1, reading its platform-bound list from apps/compiler/tmp/tests/platform-bound.tsv (beside matrix.psd1). Applied before, verified: known-platform.py (test.ps1 filters Known rows by Platform; matrix.psd1 has the 2 Windows-scoped rows). Dropped: cells-gate-ci-local.ps1 (local runs use lib_gate.ps1). Next: the user commits; the first scheduled run's cells-gate artifact feeds the local tools.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_22 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 22u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 13b 14:44 (subagent2): CORRECTION: known-platform-fsharp.py withdrawn (Known only touches DISAGREE/build-fail rows, test.ps1:1033-1036; the F# mega rows are no-oracle, so the scope changes nothing) and the 14:32 expect-behaviour-0 claim was wrong (cube and F# rows keep differing by field). Fix in my own tool: classify.ps1 + lab platform-bound.tsv (4 rows: 2 cube C, mega root + all_terminals F#) -> class platform-bound; rerun vs pack 7: behaviour 1 (zig_lib_console, resolves with the push) -> next: classify the first post-push CI run: expect behaviour 0 with platform-bound 4 | lap 14 15:00 (subagent2): no newer spiral CI run than 39258592236 (gh run list: 13:15Z schedule, failure), so classify has nothing new to read -> next: first post-push run: classify, expect platform-bound 4 + behaviour 0 | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged; classify ready with platform-bound -> next: first post-push CI run | lap 21-drive 19:30 (subagent2): drafted lab/CI-OFFLOAD/cells-gate-ci.ps1: the step a CI Cells job would run (tracked lib .spi with test blocks, 40 today, chunks of 20, --metrics per chunk, cells-gate.tsv summary parsed from the spiral-tests file reports, gate.log) with a -Match name filter instead of an array parameter; parse clean, not run yet -> next: a local bounded run on one small file, then the user wires a job (no workflow edits from this lane) | lap 23-drive 20:45 (subagent2): cells-gate-ci.ps1 works end to end locally on routes.spi (57 s; cells-gate.tsv 1/1 passed, metrics-0, gate.log); set 40 -> next: user wires the CI job")
inl note_of_ci_offload_23 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 23u32, "2026-10-26T00:57Z", "main", "10-25 20:57 main: the CI cells-gate job is a SILENT FALSE GREEN. It is committed (HEAD gh-pages.yml has the cells-gate job), and the scheduled runs since report it 'success': run 40189052944 (10-25 11:29Z), job 121155939544, log line 8,096: 'cells gate: 66 files, 0 reports in 2 s'. 66 lib notebooks cannot run in 2 s; mix spiral.notebook evidently failed at once, and ci_gate.ps1 never read mix's exit code and treated 0 reports as a normal result. The 436-byte artifact is the empty cells-gate.tsv plus a gate.log holding the real reason (not downloaded: the log gets the reason from now on). So no CI Cells result has ever reached the local tools; this item's 70% overstated that half. Fixed in apps/cells/spi/ci_gate.ps1 (unstaged, for the user's commit; parse 0 errors, LF): (1) each mix chunk's exit code is read, and a failing chunk with no 'spiral-tests file' report prints its last 30 lines into the job log; (2) the summary line now says how many tests executed; (3) 0 executed throws 'cells gate is VOID', so the job goes red. Positive control of (3)'s arithmetic: an empty summary gives executed 0 (throws), one report with 3 executed gives 3 (passes). The same class as the node gate's GREEN-by-omission this morning (lib gate kit VOID rule). Next: the first scheduled run after the user commits prints mix's failure; fix that cause (likely a missing deps.get/compile step or the Gleam domain build in the job).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_24 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 24u32, "2026-10-26T00:59Z", "main", "10-25 20:59 main: cause found by reading the job, before any new run. ci_gate.ps1 called 'mix spiral.notebook' directly, skipping everything run_notebook.ps1 does before mix: local.hex, local.rebar and deps.get when deps/kino is missing (a fresh runner has none), plus SPIRAL_DOTNET, SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, DOTNET_ROOT and the core package dir. So mix died at once, every chunk. Fixed: ci_gate.ps1 now runs each chunk through run_notebook.ps1 (same arguments; it forwards them to mix spiral.notebook, and its deps/kino check is relative to apps/cells, where ci_gate already is), which is also the path the local gates use. Separate finding: the job runs 'workflow.ps1 -Compiler', which ends in test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Accept, so the cells-gate job repeats the whole compiler matrix (about 16 min of its 16) before the gate; it needs only the compiler build. Left as is for now (CI minutes are the spare resource here); a build-only entry is a workflow.ps1 change to decide with the next workflow edit. Local check queued for the block-end batch (main-ci-gate-local-check: ci_gate.ps1 -Match ^array_dim\\.spi$ must exit 0 with more than 0 tests executed). ci_gate.ps1 diff: 11 insertions, 3 deletions, unstaged for the user.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_25 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 25u32, "2026-10-26T04:23Z", "main", "10-26 00:23 main (user 10-26 00:0x: use the node's resources so it isn't left idle): at 00:07 the node sat at 18% CPU / 7.6 GB free while main had ~20 jobs queued behind the two local heavy slots. Moved there, read-only, scripts pushed to the node's %TEMP% under the same folder names, so they run unchanged: caret-fold-probe, the registry planner probes, the dir-tree root-cut proof then the cli-args proof (--after), and the Cells console-heal verify (its compiler probe calls rustc and zig directly, so the stale node spiral.exe doesn't matter; it now runs mix deps.get when a copy lacks deps/kino). spiral was synced plainly (node idle at that moment); dir-tree-html went to mirror main-dtc (a job had appeared), with the proofs' repo path rewritten for the node copy only. Kept local on purpose: the blog split (writes the tree), the zed fixture verify (needs this box's dist/spiral-zed.exe), and every Cells/lib gate, because node rust Cells verdicts aren't trusted while SPIRAL_EXE points at the 2025-03-13 spiral.exe. Queued main-node-spiral-exe-rebuild (cargo build --release in the node's spiral workspace, after the registry probe). Then one lib gate there as a trial; if its rows match a local gate of the same slice, main's queued lib gates (json-canonical, new-vec, convert-zig, zig-index-of-3, test-interleave, x-cells-unicode, sm-heal-blocks, console-trace-blocks) move to the node, with lands still local. Tooling: scratchpad node-push.ps1 (scp a script folder to the node), node-watch.ps1 (wait and tail node jobs). Fixed remote.ps1's spurious MIRROR-DEPS-MISSING for non-spiral mirrors.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_26 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 26u32, "2026-10-26T04:35Z", "main", "10-26 00:35 main: the ci_gate.ps1 fix is verified locally (main-ci-gate-local-check, 00:19): -Match ^array_dim\\.spi$ through run_notebook.ps1 gives 1 report and 1 test executed, exit 0. Before the fix, the CI job ran nothing and still passed. The fix waits on the user's commit, then the first scheduled run is read.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_27 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 27u32, "2026-10-26T05:41Z", "main", "10-26 01:41 main: node lib-gate trial is unblocked and prepared. The node's spiral.exe was rebuilt at 00:55 (D96F204D; tibia's run after the compiler lane installed perl), and SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR is persisted there. Trial (scratchpad node-gate-trial.ps1; it launches only when the node is at or under 70% CPU, since node jobs start at once): re-gate LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH on the node through the same kit. Pass rule, written before the run: GREEN with the local gate's exact rows (baseline red 4, candidate reds not red in the baseline 0, same failing test names). Then main's queued lib gates (json-canonical, new-vec, convert-zig, zig-index-of-3, test-interleave, x-cells-unicode, sm-heal-blocks, console-trace-blocks) move to the node, with lands staying local. Held at 01:39: node at 100% CPU (kit reruns, x).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_28 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 28u32, "2026-10-26T08:31Z", "main", "10-26 04:31 main: node lib-gate trial (node job-1793000465578, LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH-NODE, the same kit and apply script as the local 10-25 21:56 gate) did NOT pass its pre-written rule. Its verdict is GREEN with candidate-only reds 0, which matches local, but the baseline is red 6 vs local 4. Compared by name from the local raw table (spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\LIB-FS-LINK-LENGTH\\baseline-file_system.spi-20261025-2156.txt.tests.tsv): both sides fail file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths (gleam), find_parent (fsharp), find_parent_2 (rust) and trace_file (fsharp). Only the node fails get_full_path_3 and get_full_path_4, path-environment tests (the node's profile is C:\\Users\\alias, and its cwd and drive layout differ). Corrected 04:3x: an earlier version of this note guessed the extras by elimination and named the wrong pair. Why the rule matters: the gate compares candidate against its own baseline, so a node gate is blind on those 2 tests. Decision, by the rule: main's lib gates stay local. Next, CPU-light: read get_full_path_3/4 for the assumption that breaks on the node, then make either the tests portable or the node layout match, and rerun the trial.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_29 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 29u32, "2026-10-26T08:43Z", "main", "10-26 04:43 main: shared lib gate kit change (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, parse-checked): the verdict file keeps its first line 'VERDICT stamp' and now adds, for every file whose candidate shows a red, a 'candidate red <file>: test/target, ...' line and a 'baseline red <file>: ...' line. Every verdict reader found (lib-gate-kit and kit-land-merge land.ps1, lib-edition3-symbols ready.ps1, pack-spelling) reads only the first line, so lands are unaffected. Why: the node trial compared by count and had to recover the local names from the raw tests.tsv. Running gates read the old script at start; the change applies to gates that start later. The node copy is refreshed by node-gate-trial.ps1's push on the next trial.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_30 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 30u32, "2026-10-26T08:45Z", "main", "10-26 04:45 main, corrected 04:5x: the node-only reds get_full_path_3/4 are NOT about paths. The node's baseline notebook records the outcome of both cells as SpiralError 'could not start the compiler on port 13905: :lock_timeout', after about 242 s of compile phase on a node at 100% CPU. That's compiler-start lock contention under load. The three path hypotheses ruled out above (junction, drive-root cwd, case) were the wrong frame; reading the cell outputs was the right first step. Two consequences. (1) The shared gate kit retried a candidate red once only for 'cell timed out after'. It now also retries on 'could not start the compiler on port N: :lock_timeout' (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, parse-checked), the same class of load failure. (2) The trial's rule correctly refused, because a baseline red from load makes those tests blind. Rerun the trial when the node is under 70% CPU; with the names now in the verdict file, compare them name by name.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_31 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 31u32, "2026-10-26T08:58Z", "main", "10-26 04:58 main: the 4 reds shared by both machines in file_system.spi's baseline, read from the local notebook outputs. find_parent_2 (rust, a shipped backend) panics with \"No parent for dir 'workspace' at '...\\lib-gate-kit\\work\\<Name>\\base\\lib\\spiral' (until 'c:\\')\". It walks up from the cwd for a 'workspace' dir and a README.md and expects the parent to be named 'spiral', which the tree has (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace + README.md) and the gate's copies did not. So every lib gate was blind on it, a staging artifact rather than a lib bug. Fix in the shared kit (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\gate.ps1, backup gate.ps1.bak-markers, parse-checked): the copies now stage at work\\<Name>\\spiral\\lib\\spiral and work\\<Name>\\base\\spiral\\lib\\spiral, and each 'spiral' gets an empty workspace dir and a README.md, mirroring the tree. Cells' lib resolution (first ancestor holding lib/spiral) still picks the copy. The touched-file scan is unaffected because the markers sit outside lib. The check: the next file_system.spi gate's verdict lines should no longer list find_parent_2 as a baseline red. The other three (file_exists_on_gleam_tells_files_from_missing_paths: gleam.toml not in the Gleam run's cwd; find_parent and trace_file: dotnet fsi exit 1 on the F# target, which has had no consumer since Fable) are open. The F# pair are candidates for dropping their ///> fsharp target lines, by the F#-is-gone rule.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_32 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 32u32, "2026-10-26T09:27Z", "main", "10-26 05:27 main: file_system.spi's F# baseline reds: find_parent (///> fsharp) runs the same workspace/README.md parent lookup as find_parent_2, so its dotnet fsi exit 1 is very likely the same gate-staging artifact the 04:5x marker fix addresses (n31). Only trace_file (///> fsharp) has an unknown cause. Rule: don't drop either F# target line until the next file_system.spi gate, whose verdict file now lists failing names, shows which reds survive the staging fix. A red that survives on the F# target alone is then a candidate for dropping its ///> fsharp line, by the F#-is-gone rule (no consumer since Fable).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_33 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 33u32, "2026-10-26T09:46Z", "main", "10-26 05:46 main: shared land kit guard (%TEMP%\\lib-gate-kit\\land.ps1, parse-checked): right after the apply script runs on the stage, New-Stage re-hashes every target file it staged (the \\ map) and refuses, with nothing committed, if any changed. That's the gap x found at 05:2x: an apply script that ignores -Lib and writes the live tree landed outside the stage/commit, so outside the Global\\spiral-lib-land mutex, and the job then reported failure on a land that had happened. Positive control (scratchpad land-wrote-target-test.ps1, on a scratch lib copy): a probe that appends to the target's array_dim.spi directly -> land exit 1 with the guard message -> GREEN. A false alarm needs another land to commit during those seconds; it fails loudly and commits nothing. Respell exclusions: fiscal released xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run and xml (all landed through the kit); still held by hand in %TEMP%\\respell-exclusions-manual.tsv: xsd_emit, xsd_regex, xsd, xsd_resolve, json_schema, xsd_constraints, xsd_certificate. schematron_run.spi's one Cons site joins the next list-sugar batch.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_34 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 34u32, "2026-10-26T09:59Z", "main", "10-26 05:59 main: land kit: the reader-gap and live-block waits are now a parameter, -WaitMinutes, default 600 (it was a hard-coded 180). The fs re-land (GRANULAR-HEAL n29) staged correctly and then gave up after 180 min, because the kit release train read the live lib continuously, although its job had a 12 h timeout. With 600 a land outlasts a long release train and fails only inside the job's own lifetime. The throw messages state the actual wait. Parse-checked; applies to lands that start from now on.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_35 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 35u32, "2026-10-29T06:40Z", "main", "10-29 02:4x main: ROOT CAUSE of the Linux-vs-Windows Kotlin/Swift/Ebpf/OpenQasm residual divergence (n6-n10), read from CI run 40691709422's samples-output.diff (cec8840c, the same commit the local tree holds): 112 outputs, 872 changed lines, and every changed line is a renamed temporary, spiral_tupleN (864 lines) or tN_k (8). After normalizing both counters, the CI and committed texts are IDENTICAL. The local number is always higher, by a per-site offset (7 x142, 9 x54, 5 x51, 6 x32, ...). Mechanism: Kotlin (spiral_compiler.fs:172667, 172712), Swift (173445, 173490), the eBPF profile (174011) and 174305/174337 name temporaries by s.text.Length, the character offset in the body's StringBuilder. The shared helper at 162111, `let line x (s : string) = x.text.Append(' ', x.indent).AppendLine s`, appends Environment.NewLine, which is CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux. Every backend's final text is then normalized with .Replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\"), so the visible lines match while each name counts one extra character per preceding line on Windows. Lap 8 ruled out Environment.NewLine by grep, but AppendLine uses it implicitly. FIX drafted (untracked, packs convention): apps/compiler/packs/apply-codegen-lf-lines.ps1 -Core <file> [-Apply] changes the helper to .Append(s).Append('\\n'); dry run on the live core: 1/1 anchor matches exactly once. The other four AppendLine helpers (175376, 176715, 177901, 178392) serve backends that name nothing by length (their .text.Length uses only compare against an earlier length), so they are left alone. PREDICTION, which is the proof after an install: a Windows -Suite all run with the fixed core rewrites exactly those 112 committed outputs, and `git diff samples` equals the reverse of CI's samples-output.diff, so the committed outputs then match CI. Same family as COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_36 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 36u32, "2026-10-29T13:21Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:21 compiler: PACK 6 REVIEW VOID, and n35's LF prediction was only PARTIALLY met by apply-codegen-lf-lines. Review job-1793273930987-compiler-pack6-review (candidate host 4BB64C77AFF99A0B / core 6576367396C93CC4, run single-flight-20261029-073857) hit its own 90-min cap during the native phase (machine shared with TRAIN3), so there is no verdict. samples/ was restored to clean afterwards (2 stray .tmp files from the kill removed; the candidate's sample diff is kept in scratch). The compile phase had finished, so the outputs were complete: (a) Delphi: 411 .pas changed, every changed line the NOREGVAR directive. (b) LF: exactly CI 40691709422's 112 files were rewritten (0 missing, 0 extra), but all 112 still differ from CI side b by ONE in every length-derived name (closure_pair main.kt: HEAD spiral_tuple194, candidate 188, CI 187). ROOT CAUSE: codegenKotlin/Swift/OpenQasm/Ebpf start each function or closure body StringBuilder with text.AppendLine(header), the same builder whose Length names spiral_tupleN/tN_k, so one CRLF per body remained on Windows. FIX: apps/compiler/packs/apply-codegen-lf-headers.ps1 (text.AppendLine(X) |> ignore -> text.Append(X).Append('\\n'), scoped to those 4 codegens with exact counts Kotlin 10, Swift 15, OpenQasm 2, Ebpf 2; Lean also names by length but CI showed no Lean drift, so it is left alone). Composes with apply-codegen-lf-lines (30 changed lines). PLAN (agreed with main): pack 6b = pack 6 + lf-headers, building now (job-1793279975270-compiler-pack6b-build, heavy slot 1). Then a targeted proof (compile the 112 samples to scratch and byte-compare with CI side b; expect 112/112 equal). Then the full -Suite all -Native review with a 4 h cap, including main's C-shim fix. Install after kit's TRAIN3 publishes.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_37 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 37u32, "2026-10-29T13:38Z", "compiler", "10-29 09:38 compiler: n35's PREDICTION MET with pack 6b. Candidate host B615E6BB7F6D392C / core 31CC96520D6B7269 (pack 6 + apply-codegen-lf-headers.ps1; job-1793279975270-compiler-pack6b-build, 338 s). Targeted proof: the 112 samples of CI 40691709422's samples-output.diff (52 .kt, 52 .swift, 8 .ebpf) compiled with the candidate into scratch, never the tree, and byte-compared with CI side b (HEAD files extracted by git archive, CI diff applied): EQUAL 112/112. Positive control: the same script on the pack-6 candidate (lf-lines only) reported DIFFERS on all 4 sampled files (the off-by-one). So Windows now emits the Kotlin/Swift/eBPF residuals CI emits on Linux. Full review requeued with a 4 h cap: job-1793281021284-compiler-pack6b-review (--heavy slot 1, samples/ clean before, includes main's C-shim fix).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_38 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 38u32, "2026-10-29T16:17Z", "compiler", "10-29 12:17 compiler: PACK 6b VERDICT CLEAN, handed to main for install. Candidate host B615E6BB7F6D392C / core 31CC96520D6B7269. Run single-flight-20261029-120916 (12:09-12:16, exit 0; the lib landings changed the memo key, so all 7,367 rows were recompiled and rerun on one lib): 6,842 parity + 525 parity-residual-differs, DISAGREE 0, no REGRESSED or NATIVE-DIFF, native 6,846 ran, oracle agree 6,118, build-fail 9 = the baseline's 9. The 525 are exactly the prediction: Delphi 413 (the NOREGVAR directive only), Kotlin 52 + Swift 52 + eBPF 8 = this item's 112, byte-equal to CI 40691709422's Linux outputs. The first run (single-flight-20261029-093803) had 74 compile timeouts + 2 Zig build-fails + 1 Wgsl timeout under 100% load, all on byte-unchanged programs; all pass here.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_39 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 39u32, "2026-10-29T16:24Z", "main", "10-29 12:2x main: PACK-6b INSTALLED and verified live (installed-is-done). Appliers: delphi64-noregvar, codegen-lf-lines, codegen-lf-headers and formatter-operator-dehang (core), rust-emit-unit-arg (host). git-a6's verdict was CLEAN (run single-flight-20261029-120916: all 7,367 rows on one post-land lib, DISAGREE 0, no REGRESSED or NATIVE-DIFF; 525 predicted residuals = Delphi 413 NOREGVAR + Kotlin/Swift/eBPF 112 byte-equal to CI 40691709422; dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C] exit 0). Tool: the new apps/compiler/packs/install-pack.ps1 (unstaged). Its dry run showed today's live core 9FF1B7088BDCAB6F and host B24847FCB88A1BE6 plus the appliers reproduce the reviewed candidate source byte for byte (core 6DD9AB3B728D1BA0, host F55C9572CBC90869), with nothing using the installed compiler. -Apply (job-1793290813517, 129 s) backed up to C:\\home\\data\\compiler-installs\\pack6b-20261029-122022, wrote the candidate sources to the live tree, and built single-flight. Installed SpiralCompilerCore.dll 31CC96520D6B7269 = the candidate. The host SpiralCompiler.dll is 9895730DAE807B70 vs the candidate's B615E6BB7F6D392C: same source, so host build non-determinism. Proven by output: installed and candidate compile dynamic_array_reserve_zero [C], closure_pair [Rust], [Delphi] and [Kotlin] byte-identically; the installed Delphi output carries the NOREGVAR directive and equals the committed residual; the installed Kotlin output has 0 CR bytes. Hopacsota mode was not rebuilt (non-default, not reviewed). samples/ holds the 525 residuals, which are now the installed outputs. Next: git-a6's node ship, then RUNNER-STALE, then COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE, then pack 7.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_40 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 40u32, "2026-10-29T16:34Z", "main", "10-29 12:4x main: pack-6b RUNTIME-TARGET finding (git-a6). The installed 6b runtimeconfig requires .NET runtime 11.0.0-rc.3 (build.ps1 picked the newest SDK; after the wipe the bundled toolchain had only rc.3), while C:\\Program Files\\dotnet has rc.1, so a bare PATH 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll' fails ('You must install or update .NET'). Main's live verification ran through the bundled rc.3 dotnet and missed it. Impact census: none in-repo. Cells (toolchain.ex:31: opts > SPIRAL_DOTNET > bundled > PATH), lib.ps1 BuildSpiral, kit's regen (two pack-6b publishes OK) and the Zed server all use the bundled rc.3. The NODE (rc.1/rc.2 only) refused the ship smoke safely. DECISION (main + git-a6): no rollback and no rebuild; 6b stays on the box. git-a6 added the rc.1 SDK beside rc.3 in the box's bundled toolchain, so pack 7 onward targets rc.1 again (the pre-wipe baseline), and installs the rc.3 runtime additively on the node to ship 6b there. install-pack.ps1 HARDENED: (a) it backs up the whole installed bin folder (today's 2-DLL backup could not have restored the runtimeconfig and deps); (b) it fails unless the installed compiler launches under the SYSTEM dotnet. The positive control shows today's 6b install fails (b).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_41 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 41u32, "2026-10-31T22:56Z", "main", "10-31 18:56 main, pre-night groom: Active -> PLANNED (not Paused: no note in over 48 h and no live job, so it isn't work in progress, but nothing blocks it; it stays in agile next at its priority and progress for the night run to pull).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_42 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 42u32, "2026-11-01T14:39Z", "compiler", "11-01 10:38 compiler, R5: child SPIRAL-CI-COMPILER-BINARIES (6 RIDs win/linux/osx x64/arm64 as workflow artifacts with a sha manifest, fetched by core hash) uses the freed minutes; mac runners also unlock Swift/Kotlin backend checks later.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_43 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 43u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: the Scion/DeltaDB research on this item (n1) now also feeds KIT-DEFINITION (what kit is); no new research needed there.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_44 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 44u32, "2026-11-02T14:07Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:07 x: GIT CONDITION RE-CHECKED from the tree (groom of user waits). A read-only survey at 09:4x shows every active repo 0 commits ahead of origin: the user committed and pushed alphabet, blog, dice, dir-tree-html, kit, fiscal, html, polyglot, regex, spiral, tibia and x in the last 1-3 h. i574n.github.io is the exception (3 unstaged files, last commit 2 years ago). So this item's user step is probably MET. What is left is deterministic and belongs to the owner, not the user: confirm the step's files are in HEAD (git log -- <paths>) and that CI on HEAD is green (gh run list --commit <HEAD>), then close it. Only a red or missing file sends it back to the user queue. Pages enablement (HTML, REGEX-BENCH-PAGE) is a real user step, since it is a public repo setting.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_45 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 45u32, "2026-11-05T22:48Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:5x MEASURED (gh api organizations/i574n/settings/billing/usage): November so far the org ran 3,807 Linux + 4,633 Windows Actions minutes and 83 storage units, net $0. The org is on the free plan with 15 PUBLIC repos and 0 private, and public repos get standard runners free and unmetered. So Actions capacity is not the limit (the personal account fc1943s used 9 minutes, dir-tree-html + polyglot). The real limit is ACCESS: Actions runs only pushed commits (workflow_dispatch/schedule run HEAD), and pushing is the user's. So (a) committed-state heavy work (scheduled suites, benches, cells-gate on HEAD) can grow freely on Actions, e.g. more matrix rows or more schedule slots, at no cost; (b) unstaged-tree work needs a codespace (quota-limited, ~31 of 120 core-h used this month; today's pre-push rehearsal, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS n14) or own machines. Concrete next for (a): add Windows to the scheduled cells-gate (Linux only today), since Windows minutes are free too.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_46 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 46u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: the heavy jobs that fit on Actions (public repos, free minutes) run there instead of the box or node, each with its runtime recorded. Next step: list today's box-heavy jobs that could move (the cells-gate and compiler suite already run on schedule).")
inl note_of_ci_offload_47 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 47u32, "2026-11-06T15:11Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:1x measured: kit jobs on the box today = 69 min (TRAIN33, its resume, backups); the box's steady 100% CPU is lane-driven compiles and gates plus the agent CLIs (RESOURCE-REPORTS 15:1x). Today main moved all its own Linux proofs off the box onto codespaces (9 runs: physics x4, fork x2, blog, dice x2+), at no Actions or box cost beyond the 120 core-hour codespace quota (about 10 core-hours used today). Offload candidates for the other lanes: Linux proofs and cells gates of lib or Cells drafts, via the codespace driver (scratch plots/cs pattern, to become a kit verb under CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS). DoD stays: heavy jobs that fit run on Actions/codespaces with runtime recorded.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_48 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 48u32, "2026-11-09T05:05Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 01:1x ACTIONS INVENTORY since 11-07 (gh run list, wall minutes = updatedAt - createdAt; the DoD wants runtimes recorded): spiral gh-pages 8 runs (push + schedule) 443 min, 2 green; fiscal gh-pages 3 runs 555 min, 0 green; dice 6 runs 226 min, 4 green; x ci 4 runs 117 min, 0 green; alphabet 4 runs 101 min, all green; tibia 2 runs 46 min, 1 green; regex 3 runs 43 min, all green; html 3 runs 36 min, all green; blog 3 runs 28 min, 2 green; pages-build-deployment is ~0 min everywhere. TOTAL ~1,600 runner-min in 2 days, all push/schedule; nothing is offloaded on demand yet. FINDING (worth more than the inventory): CI is RED on pushed code in 3 repos, sent to the owners: fiscal (family-gates.ps1:4 ' is missing', both OSes, all 3 pushes), x (workflow.ps1 build failed, last 3 pushes), spiral (2/8). OFFLOAD DIRECTION under the user's rule (one CI job per repo calling the entry script, no YAML sharding): only triggers of EXISTING workflows (workflow_dispatch / re-run) count, so the candidates are proofs that are exactly a repo's own workflow (an html, regex or blog proof = its gh-pages run), not new jobs. Next step: check which workflows accept workflow_dispatch, and route 'full workflow of repo X at HEAD' proofs there instead of ganymede when ganymede is saturated (it is at load ~17 tonight). Progress 60 -> 65.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_49 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 49u32, "2026-11-09T05:06Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 01:1x DISPATCH CHECK: among org repos only blog, rig (kit), spiral and x have workflow_dispatch in their gh-pages/ci workflow; html, regex, fiscal, tibia, dice and alphabet do not. HARD LIMIT: a dispatched run builds the committed HEAD, and lanes never commit, so Actions can offload only proofs of ALREADY-PUSHED code (re-run spiral's full suite or cells-gate at HEAD, re-check a red CI after a fix lands), never a lane's unstaged work. That's why nodes and codespaces stay the main offload for in-flight proofs. Next step: (a) when a lane wants a full-repo proof at HEAD, dispatch that repo's workflow (gh workflow run gh-pages.yml -R i574n/<repo>) instead of using a node; (b) adding workflow_dispatch to the 6 repos without it is a 1-line YAML change per repo, allowed under the one-job rule (a trigger, not a new job), but each changes a workflow, so it needs the actions.lock check and the user's push.")
inl note_of_ci_offload_50 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 50u32, "2026-11-09T07:39Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 04:1x STEP (b) FOR ORG-OWNED REPOS: workflow_dispatch added to html and regex. Source of truth edited: i574n.github/scripts/pins/workflows.spi dotnet_pages triggers += Dispatch (dotnet_pages serves only html + regex). The two generated gh-pages.yml files got the identical one-line trigger by hand (box at 100% CPU, so no pins.ps1 compile now); no uses: line changed, so actions.lock needs no regen. Behaviour: a dispatched run builds HEAD and, like push, its push job deploys gh-pages from HEAD (pages_push_job None has no event gate; that is unchanged and equivalent to the last push). fiscal, tibia, dice, alphabet are other lanes' generators/repos: left to them. Proof pending: pins.ps1 check on a quiet box or node must report 0 drift for html + regex; the user's push then makes gh workflow run gh-pages.yml -R i574n/html|regex usable. All unstaged.")
inl todo_as_source () = Task ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", "Destructive migration, to try autonomously with backups (user 2026-10-17): spread // TODO: blocks through the code on purpose as the source of truth for work, multi-line and holding Spiral code (the 120-column formatter allows dense blocks), harvested by eoie agile todos instead of a separate backlog file. Iterate on copies with periodic backups in tmp until it's state of the art. Open design points: how a TODO block links to an eoie item ID, how status and progress live in the block, the formatter's handling of them, and the no-comments rule (TODO blocks become an allowed marker kind)", Epic, P1, D7, 50u32, Planned, "", "kit,todo,migration,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_todo_as_source () = Planning ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", Minutes 1200u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_todo_as_source_1 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "main-thread", "14:20 main-thread dry run (non-destructive; backlog state backed up to job tmp todo-source/agile.spi.20261017-142038.bak; mapping in todo-source/mapping.tsv): of 332 items (162 open), only 32 open items cite a source file that exists, spread over 22 files, and 8 of those point at lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, which is prose. The other 130 open items (epics, research, cross-cutting work) have no code home. A full move into code TODO blocks would orphan ~80% of the open backlog, so the measured design is a hybrid. Code-anchored tasks become typed top-level todo blocks, parsed like test blocks (the compiler already skips top-level 'test' blocks in both BlockParsing sections, so a 'todo ID P1 D3 = <multi-line Spiral>' block can hold typecheckable Spiral and costs nothing in a build); everything else stays in eoie state. eoie agile todos then harvests both into one view, and the block's ID keys its notes. Next step, autonomous with backups: the compiler/formatter accepts todo blocks (one regression sample), then the 32 code-anchored items move into their files, rolled back by restoring the backup.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_2 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:31Z", "main-thread", "14:31 main-thread: step 1 landed in eoie and is published. eoie agile todos now harvests TODO blocks. A full-line TODO comment absorbs the following comment lines whose leader starts at the same column, keeping their indentation, so multi-line Spiral survives; a blank line, code, a directive or another TODO ends the block. TODO(ID): yields an id field in --json (only when present), and json_escaped now also escapes CR and LF. Lives in src/agile_todo_model/model.spi (new test todo_blocks_keep_their_spiral_lines_and_ids; 6/6 model and 3/3 todo_cli tests pass). The first build broke the 1k crate gate (todo_lines.rs 685 -> 1,024 lines: json_escaped was inlined 3x in finding_json and comment_starts_line twice). Making json_escaped, todo_id, finding_json, comment_starts_line and todo_free let join points brought it to 846. Release built, topology checks passed, eoie.exe published. End to end on a fixture: the block prints with its Spiral lines, and JSON carries id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS. Next: join harvested IDs with backlog status in agile todos, then move the 32 code-anchored open items into blocks (backup in job tmp todo-source/).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_3 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: first migration slice. Three items with an exact code home now have TODO(ID) blocks: EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi, target shape in Spiral), EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS (eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi) and DIB-MODERNIZE (kino fsi_chain.ex). Backups are in job tmp todo-source/*.bak. The generated .rs is unchanged (comments). Finding: the automatic file mapping is weak (titles cite files in passing, e.g. SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM -> scripts/build.ps1), so placement needs per-item judgment; items in the Tibia lane and design-prose targets were left alone. Usage note: agile todos takes the backlog root first, then the paths.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_4 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:19Z", "main-thread", "15:19 main-thread next grain, designed not built: agile todos joins each TODO(ID) block with the backlog row of that ID (the scan already gets the backlog root as its first operand) and prints the status after the id, plus tally stale=N (block whose item is Done) and unknown=M (no such item). Placement: a new small crate agile_todo_status (status lookup by the Task anchor + agile_task_text tuple_field 6, a typed todo_status union Open | Stale | Unknown), because agile_todo_lines is at 846 of the 1000-line gate and agile_todo_scan at 678. JSON adds a status field next to id.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_5 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 15:5x: tag format is // TODO: ID text (tag right after the colon, IDE TODO patterns respected; the ID is the leading upper-case token with a dash; TIBIA, HOPACSOTA and BLOG are the only dashless IDs, never used as tags). Done today: agile todos joins each tagged block with its backlog row and reports stale (item Done) and unknown (no item) blocks, via a new census export eoie_agile_task_status.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_6 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: tags also accept a single upper-case word (TODO: TIBIA ...), so dashless IDs work. Stale tags leave the code by a deterministic rule (Jira-style state change + comment): agile todos --apply removes a block whose item is Done and archives its text as a note on that item; an unknown tag creates the item from the block's first line instead of being deleted; open tags stay. Without --apply it only reports, exit 0.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_7 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: an item created by agile todos --apply for an unknown tag gets triage defaults: Story, P4, D3, Planned, tags todo,triage, title cut from the block's first line to 3-7 words; the TODO stays in code as an open tag.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_8 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done (unstaged): tag format // TODO: ID text, with a single upper-case word accepted too; todo_id lives once in agile_todo_lines and is exported (eoie_agile_todo_id) so agile_todo_scan stays under the 1k gate (it hit 1,079 when the parser was copied into it); the status join prints stale/unknown lines and stale=N unknown=M; both existing tags migrated (agile_mutation EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS, cells fsi_chain DIB-MODERNIZE). Release build: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Next: agile todos --apply (remove Done blocks into notes, create triage items for unknown tags).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_9 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T23:28Z", "main-thread", "19:28 main-thread --apply deferred on evidence: the repos hold 2 tags today, both open, so it would do nothing yet. Design ready: agile_todo_scan is at 971 of the 1,000-line gate, so the standing join (status lookup, stale/unknown lines, tallies) moves into a new crate agile_todo_standing that scan calls once with (backlog text, rendered findings, apply flag, root). Apply only touches blocks whose TODO line starts with a comment (a trailing tag after code is reported, never cut); the block extent is the finding's line plus its continuation lines up to the next path:N prefix. Stale: cut the lines, then add a note via a new export of agile_task_notes note_added (author todos-apply, body = path:line + the block). Unknown: create the item via a new export of agile_task_creation agile_add with the triage defaults (Story, P4, D3, tags todo,triage, title = text after the id cut to 7 words). Build when the first stale or unknown tag appears.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_10 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 10u32, "2026-10-18T13:08Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:08 main-thread migration slice: 3 open items now live as TODO blocks at their code sites, harvested by eoie agile todos (stale 0, unknown 0): FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL above lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir format_real, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE above lib/spiral/date_time.spi date_time_switch, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR at the top of apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/runner.ex (its first strangler slice). With DIB-MODERNIZE and EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS that is 5 tags across the repos; --apply becomes useful once some of them close.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_11 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 11u32, "2026-10-19T12:30Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:30 lap 1 grain: harvest health: eoie agile todos over spiral/lib and apps/cells/lib finds 4 TODO blocks (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE in fsi_chain.ex:184, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR in runner.ex:1), stale 0, unknown 0; note: todos with only the backlog root scans 4 files and finds 0, so callers must pass code paths Next: move one more open item to its code site (KINO-HOT-PATHS above memo.ex's key hashing)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_12 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 12u32, "2026-10-19T13:01Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:01 lap 2 grain: KINO-HOT-PATHS now lives as a TODO block at its code site, above compiler_binaries_print in apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex:145 (comment only, 2 lines under 120 columns, carrying the measured cost and the cache plan). eoie agile todos over spiral/lib + apps/cells/lib: files=141 todos=5 stale=0 unknown=0 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, DIB-MODERNIZE, KINO-HOT-PATHS, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR). Side finding: runner.ex:1's block line is longer than 120 columns; the formatter does not wrap comments, so the 120-column rule for TODO blocks is by hand. Next: move one more item whose work is a single code site (candidate: SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS at spiral_compiler.fs UnboundTypeName, about line 50208)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_13 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 13u32, "2026-10-19T13:46Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:46 lap 3 grain: RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE now has a TODO block at its single remaining code site, dice/contract/build.ps1:50 above the cargo +nightly-2024-07-14 wasm build (2 lines of 117 columns: why the pin exists and the lift being probed); eoie agile todos over dice/contract: todos=1 stale=0 unknown=0. First TODO block outside spiral, so a harvest over all items needs the dice root too: callers pass code roots explicitly (lap 1 note), and the set to pass is now spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib and dice/contract. SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS was skipped on purpose: its site is spiral_compiler.fs, and a comment there forces a compiler rebuild while main packs builds. Next: when CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES settles, move CELLS-GATE's failed_targets plan to summary_lines in notebook.ex")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_14 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 14u32, "2026-10-19T14:27Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:27 lap 4 grain: SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT moved to its code site, dice/scripts/init.ps1 above the polyglot link (2 lines, 117 and 120 columns: what still routes through polyglot and the prepared rewrite). eoie agile todos over dice/scripts + dice/contract: todos=2, stale=0, unknown=0; rewrite-deps.ps1's anchors still match (dry run unchanged at 22 files), since the block sits above, not on, the anchored line. Harvest roots in use now: spiral/lib, spiral/apps/cells/lib, dice/scripts, dice/contract. Open design point answered by practice: blocks for items whose fix is a pending lab patch go on the line the patch will change (here the link line the rewrite keeps and the deps paths it rewrites), so applying the patch is also the moment to cut the block. Next: CELLS-GATE and CELLS-REPIN are unblocked now that CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES is Done (helper lane 1, 100), but their patches are queued; no block until they land")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_15 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 15u32, "2026-10-19T14:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:52 lap 5 grain, one open design point answered by experiment (the formatter's handling of TODO blocks): SpiralCompiler format on a copy of lib date_time.spi re-wraps comments to 120 columns, and the DATE-TIME-PORTABLE block's first line is 125 columns, so it came out as // TODO: ... calendar / // fields and format / //     in plain Spiral...: the wrapped tail becomes an unindented comment line between the head and its indented continuation, which breaks the block shape the harvest relies on (head line plus indented continuation lines). The second lib block (sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL) has the same risk if over 120. Rule for TODO blocks, now with a reason: every line at most 120 columns, so the formatter leaves the block alone (my memo.ex, dice init and dice contract blocks are 113-120). Fix for the two lib blocks: rewrap them to 120 by hand in the next lib edit window (lib is frozen for the core switch until main's all-clear), or teach the formatter to treat // TODO: blocks as unwrappable (compiler lane, SPIRAL-FORMATTER). Next: after the all-clear, rewrap the two lib blocks and recheck the harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_16 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 16u32, "2026-10-19T15:30Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:30 lap 6 grain, two more design points answered from the harvest itself: (1) link = the first token after TODO: is the eoie item ID, and eoie agile todos checks it against the backlog (stale=0 unknown=0 over cells, dice scripts and contract, lib: 7 blocks); (2) status and progress stay in the item, not in the block: the block holds where and what, and it is rewritten when the facts at that site change. Did that for dice/contract/build.ps1's RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE block (now 3 lines, each at most 120 columns, harvest shows it whole): it records today's result (nightly-2026-10-18 + build-std + mvp builds once rust-lld gets --allow-undefined or near-sys >= 0.2.14 is locked). Open: the formatter wrap (two lib blocks, after the all-clear) and the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_17 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 17u32, "2026-10-19T15:47Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:47 lap 7 grain, a full census of the migration: eoie agile todos over spiral, dice, alphabet, blog, dir-tree-html and polyglot (10,677 files, 2,690 scanned, 5.5 s) finds 12 TODO blocks, stale 0, unknown 0, so every block links to a live item. Width check: 3 blocks have lines over 120 columns. runner.ex (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, Elixir: mix format never rewraps comments, so harmless) and the 2 known lib blocks (date_time.spi DATE-TIME-PORTABLE, sm'_real.spir FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), which are the only ones the Spiral formatter would break. So the post-switch rewrap list stays at exactly those 2. The harvest also reports skipped generated:5,619 and syntax:2,363 files, which keeps generated outputs from adding false TODOs.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_18 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 18u32, "2026-10-19T16:22Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:22 lap 8 grain: rewrapped the two lib TODO blocks the formatter would break: date_time.spi:107 (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE) and sm'_real.spir:591 (FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL), each now 3 lines of at most 120 columns, same words, LF kept, comment-only hunks (the loop.while' hunks in sm'_real.spir are the switch migration, not mine). eoie agile todos over lib: todos=2 stale 0 unknown 0, both harvested whole. Helper lane 1: lib/spiral/date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir touched (comments only). Left: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_19 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 19u32, "2026-10-19T16:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:48 lap 9 grain: the no-comments rule wording for TODO blocks was drifted at its source: the memory rule (no-comments-self-explanatory) told writers to link with TODO(BACKLOG-ID):, but eoie's harvester reads the ID only as the token right after TODO: (agile_todo_model/model.spi:328-339 todo_id: colon at marker+4, then a capitalized token closed by a space), so the documented spelling would give unlinked TODOs. All 12 blocks in the repos already use the harvested form. The rule text now says: // TODO: BACKLOG-ID what is left, continuation lines //     ..., each at most 120 columns, TODO(ID): yields no ID. Left in the epic: confirm the formatter keeps a rewrapped block byte-identical")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_20 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 20u32, "2026-10-19T16:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:50 lap 9b grain: formatter design point answered by running it (installed Release, format --stdin --path, two short processes): both rewrapped TODO blocks come back byte-identical (3 lines each in date_time.spi and sm'_real.spir), so a block of at most 120 columns is stable under the formatter. format --check still reports both files as would change, but for pre-existing code lines (sm'_real.spir over-120 at 178, 205 ...), the known lib drift (79 lib files), not the TODO blocks. All four open design points of this epic are now answered (link = first token after TODO:, status in the item, formatter-stable at 120, the rule wording)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_21 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 21u32, "2026-10-19T17:25Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:25 lap 10 grain: post-switch harvest of spiral (eoie agile todos, 3.3 s): 10 blocks, stale 0, unknown 0. The 5 ID-linked blocks all point at open items (DATE-TIME-PORTABLE Active 50%, DIB-MODERNIZE Planned, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL Planned, KINO-HOT-PATHS Active, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR Active), so no block outlived its item. The other 5 are upstream core text in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1 (TODO: This..., TODO: Put...; listm.spi:120, serializerm/utils.spir x3), counted as ID-less; they leave the harvest when those lab copies are promoted or deleted after the switch. Next: per-repo migration step 1 = a harvest rule check that a block whose item is Done is reported (stale today means unknown ID only?)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_22 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 22u32, "2026-10-19T17:45Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:45 lap 11 grain: the stale rule answered by a probe (scratch file, two blocks): eoie agile todos reports stale (item is Done) for a block naming CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE (Done) and unknown (no backlog item) for NO-SUCH-ITEM-XYZ, counts stale=1 unknown=1. So stale means exactly the Done case and the harvest already enforces that a block dies with its item; today's stale=0 over spiral means no block outlived its item. All design points of the epic are now answered by experiment; what remains is the migration itself, which needs the user's go (destructive by the epic's own definition)")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_23 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 23u32, "2026-10-19T22:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:52 lap 19 grain: harvest after main's core-v1 migration of dice, alphabet and polyglot: spiral 10 blocks, dice 2, alphabet 0, polyglot 0, stale 0 and unknown 0 in all four. Spiral's 5 ID-less upstream blocks still live in lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/candidate-v0 and candidate-v1; CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE is Done now, so deleting those two lab copies (lab rule: promote or delete when Done) would drop them from the harvest, a call for main. Dice's SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT block (init.ps1:22) is replaced by rewrite-deps.ps1's own text when that lands. Next: the per-repo migration, on the user's go")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_24 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 24u32, "2026-10-19T23:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:31 lap 21 grain, migration prototype on a copy (the epic allows autonomous tries on copies): lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE/migrate-prototype.py writes 5 TODO blocks for compiler-side items (CALYX-BACKEND, OPENQASM3-BACKEND, EBPF-C-PROFILE, GLEAM-JS-TARGET-GAPS, LEAN-BACKEND) at their codegen sites, each at most 120 columns, into a copy only (it refuses the tree file and a copy that already has them). Harvesting the copy found 0 blocks: eoie agile todos skips files over 1 MiB as large (agile_todo_model/model.spi:59 largest_scanned_bytes 1048576, :234 SkipLarge), and spiral_compiler.fs is 11 MB, which is also the large:1 in every spiral harvest so far. So the single largest code site cannot hold TODO blocks today; the migration needs either a size exemption or a bounded scan for that file in eoie (an eoie-lane change, recorded here for main to route), or compiler TODOs living next to the tests instead. Next: route the size limit to the eoie lane; then rerun the prototype harvest")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_25 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 25u32, "2026-10-20T00:00Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 20:00 the 1 MiB harvest limit was sent to the eoie lane (git-0e) as main asked: exempt the compiler path, raise the limit, or scan large files line by line")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_26 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 26u32, "2026-10-20T00:45Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 20:45 eoie-lane (request from helper lane 2 via main): agile todos skipped spiral_compiler.fs (11 MB) as large because agile_todo_model largest_scanned_bytes was 1 MiB; raised to 32 MiB (whole-file read with NUL check stays cheap at that size), boundary test block added (11 MB scanned, 32 MiB+1 skipped), the old 2 MB large probe moved to 40 MB; model.spi 7/7. Ships with release job-1792457142086 (the 20:10 run regenerated the owner on pack 11; its test stage failed only on a flaky port fixed since).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_27 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 27u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: review how the spread TODO comments pay off and what more eoie can leverage (TODO-SOURCE-PAYOFF-REVIEW).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_28 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 28u32, "2026-10-20T02:49Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-19 22:49 eoie lane queue for next run, from main's review (TODO-SOURCE-PAYOFF-REVIEW notes 2-3), in order: (1) todos --json emits key todos twice, list then count (todo_scan.spi 170-177); (2) single-word IDs only if they exist in the backlog, harvest only TODOs that start a comment (spiral_compiler.fs TODO in commented-out code reads as item I), before any --apply; (3) harvest roots stored in the backlog; (4) agile show lists live code sites; (5) agile check TODO rules; (6) Done refuses or folds blocks; (7) todos --record auto-notes; (8) --tagged/--id filters; (9) README still says 1 MiB cap (now 32 MiB). Queued after job stop (EOIE-JOBS note 25).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_29 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 29u32, "2026-10-20T04:44Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 00:44 eoie lane, main's review items (note 28) in source, awaiting the full regen and release: (1) --json count key is todo_count; (2) a TODO counts only where it starts a comment (no earlier leader outside strings on the line), and a single-word ID only when the backlog declares Task (WORD,; dashed IDs always; (3) agile roots <root> [add|drop <path>...] keeps HarvestRoot rows under a harvest_root union in state/agile.spi and a bare agile todos <root> scans them; (6) agile set <id> <p> Done refuses and lists the blocks still naming the item (quiet eoie_agile_todo_sites export from the scan crate, no --apply cut yet); (8) --tagged and --id <ID>; (9) README 32 MiB plus the join rules. Decision on (4): no sites in agile show, because a show would pay the 10 s harvest; agile todos <root> --id <ID> over the stored roots is the one-call site lookup that notes should cite instead of line numbers. Left: (5) check rules and (7) --record auto-notes, which need recorded sites first.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_30 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 30u32, "2026-10-20T12:20Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 08:20 eoie lane: (7) agile todos <root> --record in source (crate agile_todo_record, 861 lines): harvests tagged blocks over the stored roots, diffs them with TodoSite (ID, path:line) rows from the previous record, writes one note per task whose blocks appeared, moved or vanished (author todos), and rewrites the rows in one guarded write; a vanished block now leaves a note instead of going unnoticed (the EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION case). (5) agile todos prints wide=N and a line per TODO block over 120 columns in a Spiral file. Notebooks ledger.spi and record_cli.spi. Remaining from note 28: (5) check rules beyond width.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_31 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 31u32, "2026-10-24T02:25Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main grooming: Paused by rule (Active, no note since 2026-10-20T12:20Z, no child item noted in 3 days). Resume when picked; progress kept at 50%.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_32 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 32u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 12 14:10 (subagent2): sibling harvest: dice 2 blocks (contract/build.ps1:50 RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE, scripts/init.ps1:22 SPIRAL-INDEPENDENT, both open), polyglot 0, alphabet 0; stale 0 unknown 0 in all three -> next: migration needs the user's go | lap 13 14:32 (subagent2): harvest coverage completed for the non-lane repos: i574n.github, blog, dir-tree-html 0 blocks each (with lap 12: dice 2, polyglot 0, alphabet 0; spiral 10); every linked block names an open item -> next: migration on the user's go | lap 14 15:00 (subagent2): blocked on the user's go for the migration (all design points answered; harvest stale 0 unknown 0 across 7 repos) -> next: user's go | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged -> next: user's go | lap 21-drive 19:31 (subagent2): FINDING via a copy-only prototype (migrate-prototype.py, 5 compiler TODO blocks): eoie agile todos skips files over 1 MiB (model.spi:59), so spiral_compiler.fs (11 MB) can never hold harvested TODO blocks; eoie-lane change needed -> next: main routes the size limit to the eoie lane")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_33 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 33u32, "2026-10-25T12:14Z", "eoie", "08:14 eoie: the user's 10-25 08:0x request revisits this epic. EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO (phase 1: per-repo agile files with CLI aggregation) is the step before it, and its pre-registered benchmark includes layout C (per-repo files plus TODO blocks) to judge this epic's migration on token, latency and contention numbers rather than design alone.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_34 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 34u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'TODO-AS-SOURCE\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_35 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 35u32, "2026-10-31T20:23Z", "kit", "10-31 16:2x kit (same repair): Paused-by-rule -> Planned. This is a deliberate destructive migration (the user, 10-17: try autonomously with backups), not abandoned work; silence was the only reason it was paused. It needs a fresh decision on scope when picked: which repos, with the backups and dry-run that EOIE-AGILE TODO markers now support (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_36 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 36u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/TODO-AS-SOURCE folder (1 files: migrate-prototype.py) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'TODO-AS-SOURCE/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_37 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 37u32, "2026-11-02T16:01Z", "main", "main 11-02: the scope decision this epic was waiting on now has a measurement plan, TODO-VS-AGILE-BENCH (pre-registered metrics and decision rule; run on the node). Parent of KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS and TODO-VS-AGILE-BENCH, named here because children no longer depend on parent epics.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_38 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 38u32, "2026-11-09T19:20Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig: linked KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS as this epic's child (Parent row), per main's 11-02 note naming it; TODO-VS-AGILE-BENCH (team:org) is the other named child and is left for org to link, since it is their item. With a child row the gate no longer offers this 1200-minute epic as ready work: the work is in the children.")
inl disk_cleanup () = Task ("DISK-CLEANUP", "Periodic disk cleanup for this machine: measure build outputs (spiral-bin bin/obj/runs/scratch/native, compile-memo, cargo target dirs, Kino builds, job tmp caches, Downloads bundles) and prune what's provably regenerable on a schedule, with a report of what was freed. The memo stores already prune themselves (compile 2 days, native 14 days)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "disk,ops,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_disk_cleanup () = Planning ("DISK-CLEANUP", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl bundle_wisdom () = Task ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", "Wisdom mined from the 2026 zip bundles and bootstrap_llm files (user 2026-10-17)", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,history", "current", "current")
inl kit_lease () = Task ("KIT-LEASE", "From the January 2026 bootstrap bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 4): one time-box authority for agile begin/lease; a write class allowed during WRAP so closeout writes are never blocked; lease rollover on every new user turn (a stale lease survived into the next turn 5+ times); start-at for resumed chats; status and mutation must agree", Story, P2, D4, 45u32, Active, "", "kit,agile,lease,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_lease () = Planning ("KIT-LEASE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_lease_1 () = Note ("KIT-LEASE", 1u32, "2026-10-22T09:29Z", "eoie", "05:29 link, not a duplicate: tonight added a second authority, the TREE lease (a release reading eoie holds it; the PreToolUse hook blocks writes under the leased root; D turns it into a typed tree-read window class with eoie window claim). This item's time-box lease (agile begin/lease) stays separate, but its 'a write class allowed during WRAP' point applies to both: the backlog lives outside eoie (i574n.github), so agile closeout writes are never blocked by an eoie tree lease. Keep that property when the tree lease moves into eoie (no backlog root under a leased tree).")
inl note_of_kit_lease_2 () = Note ("KIT-LEASE", 2u32, "2026-10-22T21:37Z", "eoie-lane", "17:37 pure model drafted and tested (scratch f5/src/agile_time_box_model, HostFree + LibReferenced), covering the bundle points against today's agile_lease_terms, which derives running/wrapping/expired from two booleans. (1) One time authority: phase_at (box, now) computes the phase from started/budget/wrap. (2) A write class for WRAP: write_class CodeWrite | CloseoutWrite | ReadOnly. Closeout writes are never refused, even after expiry; code writes need Running, and a refusal names the phase. (3) Rollover: 'rolled box turn_started' restarts the clock on a new user turn and never moves it back, the fix for the stale lease that survived into the next turn 5+ times. (4) Start-at: box_start StartNow | StartAt mark for resumed chats. (5) Status and mutation agree by construction: both go through 'with_phase forall result.', so they cannot read different clocks. 3 test blocks; Cells 3/3 pass on the first compile; standards 0 findings after re-laying one line. Next (compile): agile_lease_terms reads its phase through this model, and agile begin/lease write a box row with its start mark.")
inl note_of_kit_lease_3 () = Note ("KIT-LEASE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-1, LEASE-2, LEASE-3, LEASE-5, LEASE-6, LEASE-8 (one lease authority, adopt a clock, configurable budgets, a lease for any workspace, pause). Their 85-minute container parts are parked with EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA.. Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_lease_4 () = Note ("KIT-LEASE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T23:44Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 180m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D4 at 25%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
