inl note_of_lib_canon_names_1 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: the 1k-line limit is a Rust crate concern only. On the Spiral side we want the FEWEST files, fragmented by generic concept, not by dotnet or Fable jargon (the lib/apps layout was polluted by Fable hacking and mimicking .NET names). Drop base and core and every other inherited name for the canon term of each pattern, renaming or deleting lib files as needed, and apply the same to new code from rewrites in eoie, x, the renamed Kino and other repos. Without drift: each rename keeps the corpus green (lib sweep, harness, eoie tests) and lands with a reference count from LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS. The canon English term is picked with help from MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION (translations into native scripts inform the English choice).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_2 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread: user motto for this epic, verbatim: we're opus 5.5 (rick's voice) [put that into eoie! hah! (hic)]")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_3 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 3u32, "2026-10-18T13:41Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:41 main-thread proposal from the census v2 counts (definitions, outside uses): (1) free renames, the flat modules whose names never appear at call sites: base (38 defs, 5,185 outside uses) and common (13, 1,245) merge into one prelude file; am'_prototypes, iter_prototypes, sm'_prototypes and trace are flat too. (2) the primed .NET-isms, each a scripted qualified rename gated by the lib sweep + harness + eoie tests: sm' to text (125 defs, 1,123 outside uses, the biggest), sm'_operators to text_operators (668), am' to array (196), optionm' to option (148), resultm to result (127), listm' to list (38), mapm to map (8). (3) plain renames by domain term: runtime to process (157, today it is Rust std::process + clap), parsing to parser (27), reflection to reflect (12), env to environment (8), date_time to time (5). Keep: file_system, console, convert, testing, math, crypto, async, stream, seq, wasm, and the domain packages leptos, near, near_workspaces. Each step: rename, rewrite qualified uses with the census as the work list, run the corpus, land alone. Needs the user's go on the names (MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION informs the English terms).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_4 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:24 helper-lane lap 1: verified the free-rename claim for step (1): a grep of every .spi/.spir under C:\\home\\git for open base/common or base./common. qualified uses finds no real reference - the hits are Rust closure variables named base in eoie macro text (eoie_jobs/host.spi:25, eoie_do_read, eoie_do_edit, eoie_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:624), Lean base.toInt in lib convert.spi:88, and common.spi's own label strings (common.retry_fn, common.dispose comments). So merging base.spi and common.spi into one prelude file changes only lib/spiral/package.spiproj's module list plus those label strings; no consumer edits. The primed renames (sm' to text etc.) still need the user's go on names.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_5 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:13Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:13 helper-lane lap 2: order constraint for the base+common prelude merge, from lib/spiral/package.spiproj: base- is module 6 and common- is module 36, with 28 modules between them (rust/, optionm', resultm, convert, listm', am', seq, math, mapm, iter, sm'_real*, sm', sm'_operators, console, reflection, guid, platform, env, testing, near, date_time, trace-, parsing ...), and common.spi uses later modules (retry_fn' formats with sm' and trace). So a merged prelude cannot sit at base's position; it either takes common's position (then base's 38 definitions, which those 28 modules reach unqualified as a flat module, would be unbound for them) or stays two files. Flat modules today: |core-, am'_prototypes-, backend-, base-, iter_prototypes-, sm'_prototypes-, trace-, common-. Conclusion for the proposal: base and common are not mergeable by position; the free rename is renaming them (e.g. prelude and prelude_late), not merging.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_6 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:32Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:32 helper-lane lap 3: proposal text updated for the user's go (names only, nothing renamed): step (1) becomes RENAME-ONLY (lap 2: base and common cannot merge by position): base -> prelude, common -> prelude_late. Flat-module candidates: am'_prototypes -> array_prototypes, sm'_prototypes -> text_prototypes, iter_prototypes / trace / backend keep (already canon). New input from today's core switch (lab/CORE-LIB-CONSOLIDATE/apply-switch.ps1, landing about 11:45): it adds lib module am_dim after am', a fresh primed-family name, so step (2)'s am' -> array should take it along as array_dim in the same rename (or the switch could name it array_dim now and save a later rename). Steps (2) and (3) unchanged. Constraint from SPIRAL-PATH-SYNTAX lap 2: module renames do not touch X autolinks (a module is the left side of the dot); the autolink-prone members are map, box, show (82 percent of lib's TLD-named uses), which a member-rename step could absorb. Needs the user's go on names.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_7 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:38 helper-lane: main took the naming input: the core switch now adds the lib module as array_dim (not am_dim), so step (2)'s am' -> array rename has a canon sibling already.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_8 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T22:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:25 helper-lane lap 6: census of today's lib/spiral (85 .spi/.spir files): 11 still carry the old suffix names (am', am'_prototypes, listm', mapm, optionm', optionm'_operators, resultm, sm', sm'_operators, sm'_prototypes, sm'_real.spir); array_dim already exists as the canon sibling of am' from the core switch. These 11 are the rename set for step 2. Each rename touches every qualified use across lib, apps and consumer repos, so it rides the core-migration work, one file at a time with a Cells gate per file.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_9 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 9u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:32 (subagent): blocked: proposal waits for the user's go on names; recount after the core switch lands (array_dim added) -> next: after the accept: rerun the census counts on the switched lib | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): switch landed (array_dim in lib/package.spiproj); census recount needs no compile -> next: recount after the accept settles; user's go on names | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: rename set of 11 files fixed -> next: first rename via core migration")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_10 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 10u32, "2026-10-29T15:07Z", "main", "10-29 11:0x main, grooming check: none of the planned renames is done yet. Two primed modules have appeared since the census (par', dictm), so step 2 counts 13. COLLISION: the planned runtime -> process rename now clashes with the existing lib/spiral/process.spi (verified present), so pick another canon name for runtime, or merge runtime into process deliberately. Estimate set to 2280m (4 free renames 120 + 13 primed x 120 + 5 plain x 120); the names still need the user's go.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_11 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 11u32, "2026-10-30T14:15Z", "main", "10-30 10:15 main, BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 R4, DECIDED (a naming call a rule answers, so it's the agent's; recorded, not asked). The runtime -> process rename collides with lib/spiral/process.spi. Fix it by splitting runtime.spi (1,252 lines) by concern, not by finding a third name. Rule: a canon name says what the module does, and runtime holds two concerns, both of which already have a module. (a) Child processes (split_args, split_command, process_*, child_wait_with_output, stdio_line*, execute_with_options*, shared_command_*, shared_child_*, current_process_kill, process_id) MERGE INTO process.spi, which already holds the portable spawn/capture layer. (b) Clap CLI parsing (command, new_command, arg*, value_range, value_parser, possible_value, matches_*, command_*) MERGES INTO cli_args.spi. gc_collect and log_file_create go to the module of their first consumer. runtime.spi then retires. Do this in a lib window with a by-stem consumer check (8 'runtime.' reference files today) and document_test plus type_probe on process, cli_args and every consumer. Next: list each consumer's runtime.X uses, then a guarded do/undo draft. Owner main.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_12 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 12u32, "2026-10-30T16:23Z", "main", "10-30 12:23 main: n11's NEXT done, the consumer map for the runtime.spi split (read-only; git grep across 10 repos, matching runtime.X on a word boundary so step_runtime.X and file names don't count). 193 uses in 10 files, ALL in spiral (151), alphabet (31) and the backlog's own notes (11). kit, x, fiscal, tibia, dice, blog and dir-tree-html: 0. By file: spiral apps/spiral/spiral.spi 100, alphabet apps/documents/documents.spi 31, lib/spiral/process.spi 28 (these disappear when runtime's process half merges INTO process.spi), apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.spi 16, then 1-2 each in lib bench/parsing/main, rust/testing, async, benchmark, platform. By target: 124 uses go to cli_args (the clap half: command_init_arg 23, matches_get_one 17, arg_required 10, new_command 9, ...), 69 to process (execute_with_options 15, execution_options 14, shared_command_*/shared_child_*, split_args, ...). So the split touches 3 apps (spiral CLI, wasm, alphabet documents) and 5 small lib callers; no lane outside main and compiler is affected. alphabet-mirrors-dice: dice has 0 uses, so nothing to mirror. NEXT: a guarded do/undo draft (runtime.spi -> process.spi + cli_args.spi, then a mechanical rename at the 193 sites) in one lib window, with document_test, type_probe on process, cli_args and the 8 lib/app consumers, and apps/spiral's crate line gate (spiral.rs is already 22x over the generated-crate gate; see SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_13 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 13u32, "2026-10-30T16:35Z", "main", "10-30 12:35 main: CORRECTION to n11's placement, decided from the tree this time (decide-from-tree-not-notes). (1) Name collisions: none. runtime's 57 process-half defs vs process.spi's 32: 0 shared; its 41 clap-half defs vs cli_args.spi's 39: 0 shared. (2) But the clap half must NOT merge into cli_args.spi. cli_args is a PORTABLE command-line parser in plain Spiral (flag_value, parse_command, usage, error texts, its own tests); runtime.spi:464-745 is Rust-only clap host bindings (50 clap references, 1 Rust arm, 1 stray F# line). Mixing a host binding into a portable module breaks package placement by import graph. Revised: the clap half goes to a NEW lib/spiral/rust/clap.spi, beside rust/rust.spi and rust/near.spi (the same kind of Rust host binding, in the existing rust/ block of package.spiproj); canon name 'clap' (no clap module exists yet). The process half still merges into process.spi. Consumers: the 124 cli_args-bound uses in n12 become rust/clap uses (in spiral apps/spiral, apps/wasm and alphabet documents), so the rename at those sites is runtime.X -> clap.X.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_14 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 14u32, "2026-10-30T22:57Z", "main", "10-30 18:57 main: the runtime.spi split is DRAFTED and PROVEN on a scratch lib (subagent, 18:5x): C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\canon-names\\apply.ps1 (dry run default; -Apply writes an undo dir with a hash manifest; -Undo restores only if files still match; every anchor exactly once; per-consumer use counts pinned). Census on today's tree: 193 real uses in 9 files (n12 missed file_system.spi): spiral.spi 105 (89 clap / 16 process), alphabet documents.spi 32 (21/11), process.spi 28 (become plain names), wasm 16 clap, file_system 6, plus bench/parsing, rust/testing, benchmark. Kit 0. Three blockers found and FIXED in the draft: (1) an ORDERING CYCLE: file_system used runtime's shared_command_* for its mklink /J helper, and process needs file_system, so mklink_status is now one Rust host call inside file_system with the same exit codes; directory_create_junction keeps its signature; (2) a hidden ':< runtime' open in spiral.spi (process_rust) that the regex couldn't see; it now opens process; (3) clap.spi takes runtime's old slot as its own rust/ block. Checks on scratch: round trip 9/9 files true/true; type_probe 5/5; lib_gate process.spi 15/15 (33 rows across rust, gleam, fsharp, typescript, cuda); rust/clap.spi 3/3; the file_system junction test 1/1; the spiral CLI compiles before and after, spiral.rs identical in size, the only differences the 7 renamed log-label strings. OPEN before landing: compile the wasm app and alphabet documents (only round-tripped so far); build.ps1's new process notebook brings Windows-flavoured tests into Linux CI; leftover 'runtime' names in bench/parsing README, bench.ps1 labels and the compiler lane's lib-usage-delete-unused.py list.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_15 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 15u32, "2026-10-30T23:02Z", "main", "10-30 19:02 main: the open compile gap is CLOSED on scratch: both remaining consumers compile against the split lib (scratch alphabet's deps path resolves to the scratch lib, checked): apps/wasm spiral_wasm.spi -> RUST-COMPILED 203,298 B (114 s); alphabet apps/documents documents.spi -> RUST-COMPILED 958,588 B (125 s). All 4 consumer groups now compile (spiral CLI, wasm, documents, lib). Left before the land: the Linux-CI question for build.ps1's new process notebook (Windows-flavoured tests), and the leftover 'runtime' names in bench/parsing README, bench.ps1 labels and the compiler lane's lib-usage-delete-unused.py. Then a main lib window: apply.ps1 -Apply on the live tree, close with document_test + type_probe on the 9 files + kit's 14 signatures (file_system is in kit's list; the change touches only mklink_status, so check the 4 protected file_system lines byte-identical).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_16 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 16u32, "2026-10-30T23:07Z", "main", "10-30 19:07 main: runtime split LANDED and CLOSED GREEN (19:05, land.ps1: kit tree-write window, a live-reader check that walks process ancestry so scratch-lib gates don't block, apply with undo, then the close; restores on any red). Files: spiral.spi 6086E358 -> 22D4D373, spiral_wasm.spi F6CDF55D -> 0DA16176, alphabet documents.spi 8EFDC7AE -> AFD3AA52, plus the lib (process.spi merged, rust/clap.spi new, runtime.spi retired, file_system's mklink_status as one host call, package.spiproj, build.ps1 notebook list: runtime dropped, rust/clap added, process kept OUT of the CI list because its tests use Windows ping -n, python and pwsh). Close: kit's 14 signatures 14/14 unchanged; document_test exit 0; type_probe 5/5 (process 15 tests typed, rust/clap 3, file_system 35, benchmark 2, rust/testing 3; 0 errors). Undo: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\canon-names\\undo-land-20261030-190456. Callers left: none in any repo (kit's 30 regex hits are 'runtime.txt' file names); compiler was asked to update lib-usage-delete-unused.py. Left for this epic: the other canon renames (n11's plan); bench/parsing README and bench.ps1 'runtime' labels (text only).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_17 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 17u32, "2026-10-30T23:49Z", "main", "10-30 19:49 main: the text-only 'runtime' labels are renamed. lib/spiral/bench/parsing (its own package.spiproj, outside the lib package, so no lib window): main.spi impl label 'runtime' -> 'process' (x2), bench.ps1's impl lists and disagreement message (x3; 0 parse errors), README (consumer, table row, process.spi path and test-case source; 'a runtime array' kept, since there it means a run-time value). Pre-edit copies are in the session scratchpad bench-parsing-before. Still left: platform.spi:38's failwith label 'runtime.run_platform /' should say 'platform.run_platform /' (a lib string literal; fold it into the next lib window), and apps/spiral/spiral.rs still carries 'runtime.execute_with_options' trace strings because it is stale generated output; the next CLI regen picks up process.spi's labels.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_18 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 18u32, "2026-10-31T01:25Z", "main", "10-30 21:25 main, AUDIT of the runtime.spi retirement (user asked whether everything important moved cleanly), against the pre-land copy canon-names\\undo-land-20261030-190456\\03-runtime.spi: all 98 top-level definitions still exist in lib (60 in process.spi, 36 in rust/clap.spi, 2 generic names present in several modules); 0 missing. Tests: 8 of 9 moved; the 9th was runtime.spi's static import header (testing, file_system_operators), which process.spi has its own copy of. Consumers: no .spi, .spir or .ps1 in any repo calls runtime.<fn> any more; the remaining 'runtime.' text matches are a tokio runtime.handle() in async.spi's Rust host code, vulkan-runtime in init-wgsl-run.ps1, kit's runtime_guard (which FORBIDS generic runtime.spi files, now satisfied) and a zip test fixture.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_19 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 19u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. audit closed (n18); next: the next canon rename batch through the lib land queue.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_20 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 20u32, "2026-11-01T12:04Z", "main", "main 08:1x: the browser session's naming report adds a lexicon chosen from the repos' own counts and a layer plan for the lib; filed as NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET (lexicon in standards.spi + ratchet) and LIB-LAYERS (meta/core/std/host/os, no m suffix or drawers), both under DIRECTION-1031.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_21 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 21u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Approve the fixed 11-file canon rename set. RULE (written before acting): Default is to apply the 11-file rename set as fixed in lap 7, one rename at a time through core migration. This is a naming call, reversible, and limited to private repos. Each rename must pass the lib gate plus a consumer build (Cells over lib, kit regen), and dangling-check-by-stem across sibling repos must return 0 live references to the old name. BACKUP/UNDO: lib-land undo dir per rename; left unstaged for the user's review (the user can veto any name) OWNER: main. NEXT: Rerun the census counts on the switched lib, then land the first rename via core migration. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_22 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 22u32, "2026-11-07T22:14Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: every lib file's name passes the naming lexicon (NAMING-LEXICON-RATCHET's standards.spi list), with the census banned-name count for file names at 0 and forwards for renamed files dropped after callers switch. TypeFeatures: the lexicon as typed rows (kind : Module | Function, allowed : list string), checked by kit standards.")
inl cli_args_style () = Task ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", "Typed CLI arguments derived from unions", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "KIT-UNION-REFLECTION,RUST-EXPORT-TYPES", "cli,pwsh-free,reflection,dogfood,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cli_args_style () = Planning ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl hangulize_in_spiral () = Task ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", "Hangulize as a Spiral transcription library", Story, P1, D5, 20u32, Planned, "", "alphabet,transcription,lib,typefeatureset,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hangulize_in_spiral () = Planning ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", Minutes 390u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:16 styles-survey [1/3 where and what] alphabet apps/documents (documents.spi hangul, lines 118-196) writes <name>.hangul.md (+ .html/.pdf/.epub): it pipes the trimmed text to the hangulize binary as a shell command string (deps/hangulize/cmd/hangulize/hangulize.exe <spec>), spec por-br by default or eng when the text contains the and and, then interleaves each transcribed line with the original (transcribe_only drops the original; code fences and URL lines kept). Dependency: not vendored, not a submodule; scripts/dep_hangulize.ps1 (called by init.ps1) git-clones the i574n/hangulize fork into gitignored deps/hangulize, installs Go via winget/apt and runs go build. The fork = upstream hangulize/hangulize (go 1.19; deps cobra, kagome.ipadic, go-pinyin, go_hangul, x/text) + 2 own commits (2025-06-22/28): an English spec via CMU dict (translit/english, cmudict.dict 134,429 lines / 3.9 MB, specs/eng.hsl ~300 lines, jamo.go and procedure.go tweaks). Size: 92 Go files / 7,513 lines (57 non-test / 5,148: pkg/hre regex dialect 1,321, pkg/hsl spec lexer+parser 761, translit furigana/pinyin/cyrillic/english 689, core procedure/rule/spec/script), 41 .hsl specs / 12,243 lines with 4,202 inline test examples, web/ UI 125 files. Only consumer in our repos: alphabet documents.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:17 styles-survey [2/3 the hangul experiment] alphabet apps/hangul is an F# port of suapapa/go_hangul (sibling clone C:\\home\\git\\go_hangul, 746 Go lines, last upstream 2024-01-08): hangul.fs 494 lines (jamo constants, syllable compose/split, hanjaConvert, isHanja), hanja.spi 4,586 lines (one hanja_to_hangul_table array, 477 KB), cp949_from.fs + cp949_to.fs 17,051 + 17,053 lines (733 KB each), hangul.livemd 931 lines, package.spiproj on deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib. 15 commits since 2025-06-20; last code change 2025-11-19 (84-line .dib edit); 2026-10-06/07 only the dib -> livemd migration and build.ps1 edits. Nothing consumes it: documents calls the Go binary, scripts/build.ps1 only runs its notebook export. It is F#/dotnet-only, so it conflicts with the native direction; worth keeping is the jamo compose/split logic (~200 lines) as the seed of the Hangul output layer, and the hanja table as data; cp949 tables are unnecessary in a UTF-8 pipeline.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:18 styles-survey [3/3 assessment + name] Concrete gains of a Spiral rewrite: (1) specs as typed data instead of runtime-parsed text: hangulize lexes/parses every .hsl (761 lines) and compiles HRE patterns to Go regexp at load (1,321 lines); in Spiral a spec is a record of vars/macros/normalize/rewrite/transcribe rules and inl partial evaluation specializes the rewrite engine per language at compile time (first Futamura projection), so no regex engine or spec parser ships. (2) GADT rule AST indexed by phase (normalize -> rewrite -> transcribe), so a rule cannot run in the wrong phase; existentials for per-language pre-transliterators (pinyin, furigana, cyrillic, CMU English) as packs of state + step; a prototype transcriber over output scripts (Hangul first, others later); reflection derives the language union, the CLI choice (CLI-ARGS-STYLE) and docs. (3) One codebase for every backend: documents stops shelling out to Go and needs no Go toolchain (dep_hangulize.ps1 retires, PWSH-FREE); the same lib serves wasm/JS for a web page. (4) Tests: the 4,202 spec examples become inline expectations / Spiral test blocks, with the Go binary as differential oracle until parity. Costs: dictionaries (kagome ipadic, pinyin, 3.9 MB cmudict) need Rust crates or embedded data; scope first only por-br + eng (the two used). Proposed generic lib name: transcription (lib/spiral/transcription: rules, hangul compose, specs/por_br, specs/eng); transliteration is the wrong word, the specs map sounds, not letters.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-19T12:50Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:50 helper-lane lap 1: sized the port and its oracle from alphabet/deps/hangulize (152dd46, 2025-06-28): 57 non-test Go files / 4,114 non-blank lines (hangulize core 17 files, hre 8, hsl 5, subword 3, furigana 4, plus translit wasm shims for cyrillic, pinyin, furigana), 35 *_test.go files, and 41 .hsl specs (498 KB, aze ... wlm; por-br and eng are the two alphabet uses). The specs embed 10,631 word -> hangul test pairs, a ready differential oracle like Tibia's bots: a Spiral port can be checked spec by spec against the pairs with no Go run at all. The furigana/pinyin/cyrillic paths go through translit wasm modules, so a first slice should take a pure-rule spec (por-br, which alphabet uses by default) and its pairs.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:35Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:35 helper-lane lap 2: profiled the first slice's spec: por-br.hsl (Brazilian Portuguese, alphabet's default) is 250 lines with sections lang, config, macros, vars, normalize, rewrite, transcribe, test, and carries 211 word -> hangul test pairs. Its HRE pattern features: 79 angle-bracket variable references, 59 brace look-around groups, 39 carets (word start) and 40 dollars (word end), no bracket character classes. So a typed Spiral spec for por-br needs: vars as named letter sets, rewrite and transcribe rules as (pattern, replacement) records where a pattern is a sequence of literal | var | lookbehind | lookahead | edge, normalize as a char map; the 211 pairs become one test block table. No regex engine is needed for this spec, only the HRE subset.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-19T15:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:17 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the HRE subset as types in lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi (from alphabet/deps/hangulize/specs/por-br.hsl): union rec piece = Literal string | Variable var_name | Alternatives (list (list piece)) covers literals, <var> references and {a|b} / (x|<cs>x) groups; type pattern = {at_word_start; behind; body; ahead; at_word_end} carries the ^ and $ edges and the brace look-around as explicit fields instead of regex syntax; rule = pattern + replacement; letter_set for the vars section (vowels encoded). Hand-encoded the first 14 rewrite rules in file order: the 10 accent rules, dumont$ -> dumon, ^eduar -> Eduar, ^ex{@} -> ez (the @ macro expands to the vowels var) and c{k|q} -> empty. Probe main counts them (expect exit 14); unrun until main's freeze all-clear. Next: a matcher over pattern (byte-level, ASCII plus the normalized accented letters) and the first test pairs through rewrite only.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:59Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:59 helper-lane lap 5: wrote the matcher over the typed HRE subset into lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi (unrun until main's freeze all-clear): match_seq/match_alts walk Literal, Variable (any letter of the named letter_set) and Alternatives at a byte position; behind_ok tries every start that ends exactly at the position; rule_end checks the ^ and $ flags, the look-behind, the body and the look-ahead and returns the body's end; apply_at rewrites one rule left to right over a word (copying unmatched bytes), and rewrite_all applies the rules in file order, as hangulize does. Test words through the first 14 por-br rules: ação -> aSA~o (ã then ç), eduardo -> Eduardo (^eduar), exame -> ezame (^ex followed by a vowel), dumont -> dumon (dumont$), acqua -> aqua (c before q); expected exit 31.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-19T17:42Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:42 helper-lane: probe run: hre.spi compiled and exited 3, not 31 - acao and eduardo rewrite correctly, while exame, dumont and acqua do not. The three failing rules are exactly the ones built with a record update that sets a list-valued field ({(literal x) with ahead = [...]} or body = [...]); eduardo's rule updates only a bool field and works. Rewrote those three rules as full records to separate a matcher bug from a record-with problem; rerun pending. If they pass, a record update of a list field on a function-call base is a compiler issue to cut into a repro.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-19T17:49Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 13:49 helper-lane: rerun with the three rules rewritten as full records still exits 3, so the record-with hypothesis is withdrawn (not a compiler issue): the failures are in my matcher's look-ahead / word-end path (exame, acqua need ahead; dumont needs at_word_end), while the plain-body and word-start rules work. Next: a per-rule debug probe (rule_end of each failing rule at position 0) to find the faulty branch.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-19T21:31Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:31 helper-lane: the exit 3 from lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi was never a bit mask: it is the Windows exit code of abort(). The C runtime's string slice aborts when an end lands inside a UTF-8 sequence, and hre.spi sliced byte ranges: starts_at sliced [i, i+n-1] (behind_ok tries every byte offset, and a 2-byte letter after ASCII ends mid-sequence), and the copy step sliced one byte of a 2-byte letter like ç or ã. StringIndex is a byte on every backend, so the fix stays in plain Spiral: starts_at compares byte by byte (bytes_match, no slicing), and the copy step takes a whole letter (letter_width over the Portuguese wide letters, default 1). Now exit 31 on C and Rust: all five words (ação, eduardo, exame, dumont, acqua) rewrite as expected. My earlier record-with hypothesis is withdrawn for good. Next grain: load the rule table from the upstream por.hgl text instead of the hand list.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-20T02:33Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 22:33 user 2026-10-19 21:4x: the user updated hangulize to its upstream main; after HANGULIZE-UPSTREAM-RETEST, measure the work of rewriting 100 percent of hangulize in Spiral so the dependency and the fork can be dropped.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-20T02:50Z", "hangulize-lane", "2026-10-19 22:52 hangulize-lane [1/5 measure, corrections] Corrected counts from upstream-merged 5f1b6ee (go test -v agrees): the oracle is 4206 test pairs in 40 specs (eng has none), not 10631 (that count included rule arrows); por-br has 15 pairs, not 211. Rules: 4704 rewrite + 1726 transcribe = 6430 in 41 specs / 12248 lines. The English via CMU dict component does not exist in practice: fork eng.hsl is por-br.hsl minus its test section (id still por-br, no translit line), and no spec uses translit english, so cmudict (3.9 MB) and english.go are dead code. Fork eng output = upstream por-br output on 306 inputs; upstream rejects eng (Lang not supported). So the fork can be dropped today without Spiral work: point dep_hangulize.ps1 at hangulize/hangulize and have documents pass por-br where it now picks eng (recommendation only, nothing edited).")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-20T02:50Z", "hangulize-lane", "2026-10-19 22:52 hangulize-lane [2/5 inventory] Go code, non-blank non-comment lines (non-test / test): root package 818/558 (procedure 7 phases, spec, rule, trace, translit registry), pkg/hre 582/510 (HRE compiled to Go RE2 regexp), pkg/hsl 428/230 (spec lexer + parser), internal/jamo 118 (uses go_hangul Join/Split), internal/subword 133 (leveled replacer), internal/scripts 99 (Latn Cyrl Grek Geor Hrkt; Latn uses x/text NFD), pkg/tracefmt 36 (go-runewidth), translit 34 + cyrillic 57 (dict.json 17.7 KB) + pinyin 40 (go-pinyin, 763 KB dict) + furigana 295 (kagome.ipadic, 58 MB, 7888 Go lines) + english 59 (dead), cmd/hangulize CLI 394 (cobra), wasm shims 307 (web only). Library to port: about 2200 lines, plus the RE2 semantics it leans on. HRE census over 6430 rules (lab/HANGULIZE-UPSTREAM-RETEST/hre_features.ps1): lookaround 1625 (41 specs), negative lookaround 16 (cat chi nld), chunk edges ^ 550 / $ 434, var refs 764, group alternation 44 (7 specs), quantifiers 11 (chi ,? and (<cs>|,)* in cym wlm), no char classes, no ^^/$$, RHS paired vars 6 (ell grc), RHS {} marker 70 (8 specs).")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-20T02:50Z", "hangulize-lane", "2026-10-19 22:52 hangulize-lane [3/5 lab coverage + checks] lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/hre.spi covers the rewrite phase only: 14 hand-encoded por-br rules, literal/var/alternatives pieces, a first-match matcher, 5 words (exit 31 on C and Rust after the 10-19 lib change); 0 of 4206 pairs end to end (no normalize, partition, transcribe, syllabify). Parity checks the port must pass, from the Go code: (a) sliced search: Pattern.Find runs the regexp on word[offset:] and dissolves lookbehind into a consumed group, so lookbehind and ^ (= start, whitespace or the {} marker) only see text after the previous match stop; lab behind_ok scans from 0 and diverges (probe: lookbehind a then a, on aaa). (b) RE2 leftmost-first alternation with full backtracking; lab match_alts commits to the first matching alternative, so the 44 groups and 11 quantifiers need a continuation matcher. (c) Unicode data in every spec: unicode.Latin range, NFD first-codepoint + ToLower (Latn.Normalize), IsPunct/IsSpace (partition, localize). (d) spec ingestion: 12248 lines cannot be hand-encoded; port the hsl parser (428 lines) and load the .hsl files as data, which also keeps upstream spec fixes (like spa) pullable. (e) RHS paired vars, the {} marker, transcribe NUL masking with subword levels, syllabify with the - tail marker.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-20T02:50Z", "hangulize-lane", "2026-10-19 22:52 hangulize-lane [4/5 estimate] Spiral size by component (expected lines): hsl parser + spec model 350, HRE continuation matcher with sliced-search semantics 450, replacement interpolation 80, subword levels + masking 200, procedure + localize 300, jamo compose/split with tail marker 200 (seed: alphabet apps/hangul jamo logic), scripts 200 + generated Unicode tables (or Rust-backend std calls), CLI + in-process API 80, oracle runner 60: core about 1900 Spiral lines for about 2200 Go library lines. Translit: cyrillic 80 + embedded 17.7 KB dict (bul mkd rus ukr, 390 pairs); pinyin 60 + 763 KB generated table (chi, 143 pairs); furigana is the long pole and a decision point (jpn jpn-ck, 175 pairs): a Rust tokenizer crate with IPADIC on the Rust backend only vs porting kagome (7888 lines + 58 MB dict) vs leaving jpn out. Acceptance ladder: 3498 pure-rule pairs in 33 specs, then 708 translit pairs. Effort at the lab pace: core to por-br parity 3-4 agent days (D5); all 33 pure-rule specs +2-3 days; cyrillic + pinyin +1.5 days; furigana 3-5 days with a crate. 100 percent: about 10-14 agent days.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-20T02:50Z", "hangulize-lane", "2026-10-19 22:52 hangulize-lane [5/5 staged plan] S0 (now, no Spiral): drop the fork by cloning upstream hangulize/hangulize and passing por-br instead of eng in documents (identical output). S1: lib transcription core + por-br; alphabet documents is the only consumer and uses only por-br, so it switches first and calls the lib in-process; acceptance = por-br 15 + por 69 pairs + a differential vs the Go CLI on a corpus (alphabet README, nota, every Latin spec test word) on Rust and C; then retire dep_hangulize.ps1, the init.ps1 line and setup-go in gh-pages.yml (regenerate actions.lock), which drops the Go dependency. S2: the other pure-rule specs to 3498/3498. S3: cyrillic and pinyin. S4: furigana decision then jpn jpn-ck, 4206/4206 = 100 percent. Evidence: spiral/lab/HANGULIZE-UPSTREAM-RETEST (results.tsv, hre_features.ps1).")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-20T03:11Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:11 main, answering the user (remodel with the type feature set, full test parity, fewer days?): yes. Revised plan: (1) one typed spec model and an .hsl parser so all 41 specs (6,430 rules, 4,206 test pairs) load as data instead of hand-encoded rules, which also keeps upstream spec fixes pullable; (2) a matcher with RE2 semantics: correction to the hangulize lane note, Go regexp is RE2 (automaton, leftmost-first, no backtracking), so a derivative/automaton matcher over code-point classes is the natural fit, and look-around plus match-after-previous-rewrite become explicit typed context predicates; (3) Unicode tables (accent stripping, Latin ranges) and the Cyrillic and pinyin tables embedded; (4) test-first on all 4,206 pairs from day one. Estimate: all non-Japanese specs (4,031 of 4,206 pairs, 96 percent) in about 5 to 7 agent days versus 7 to 8.5 for a port, 2 to 3 days of wall clock with three independent lanes (parser, matcher, tables); Japanese (175 pairs, 58 MB dictionary plus tokenizer) stays a separate decision, 3 to 5 days with a Rust crate or out of scope. Beyond parity: static spec checks (dead, conflicting, unreachable rules), linear-time matching, one source for every backend. Main risk: exact leftmost-first parity, which the pair oracle exposes. Stage 0 (drop the fork, por-br instead of eng) is independent and can happen now.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-20T03:25Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 23:25 user 2026-10-19 23:1x: forget hangulize for now; at 5-7+ agent days it waits until XSD and full hopacsota are done; paused at P5, the measurement and the remodel plan (note 17) stay for later; the fork drop (stage 0) also waits.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:39 (subagent): blocked: hre.spi written, bounded jobs frozen -> next: after the all-clear: compile hre.spi (expect 14) | lap 6 13:42 (subagent): 3/5 rewrite words; failing rules all use record-with on list fields; rewritten as full records -> next: rerun; if green, cut a record-with repro | lap 6 13:49 (subagent): rerun 3/5: record-with hypothesis withdrawn; matcher ahead/word-end bug -> next: per-rule debug probe | lap 6 17:31 (subagent): exit 3 was abort() on mid-UTF-8 slices; byte-compare matcher + whole-letter copy; 31 (5/5) on C and Rust -> next: parse por.hgl rules instead of the hand list | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: 5/5 on C and Rust -> next: parse por.hgl rules")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-29T22:32Z", "main", "10-29 18:3x main: PROVISIONAL fields filled while the item is Blocked or Paused (fill-all-task-fields): estimate 390m (D-base rule: base(D5) x (1 - 20%)); load light (default); assignee agent (default). The owner corrects them on resume.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-31T20:56Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL folder (3 files: c_slice_eq.spi, hre.spi, hre_debug.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_24 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 24u32, "2026-11-07T22:12Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: the Spiral transcription library matches hangulize's output on alphabet's documents corpus (byte-equal per sample), and alphabet uses it instead of the external dependency. TypeFeatures: language rules as typed rule tables (a union of rule kinds), not regex strings.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_25 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 25u32, "2026-11-08T05:15Z", "main", "USER 11-08 01:0x PRIORITY + RESHAPE: rewrite ALL of hangulize in Spiral inside the alphabet repo, FROM SCRATCH, not a port. Rules: (1) typefeatureset-first modeling (GADTs, existentials, HKTs, prototypes, reflection); no Go idioms and none of upstream's file-extension or format mannerisms. (2) If the default backend is Rust, reuse existing Rust crates (unicode normalization/segmentation, ICU-style transliteration) instead of reimplementing; the default backend may be another one if criteria favor it (decide and record the criteria). (3) Cells tests-only; parity is measured by behavior, but upstream is presumed WRONG: its language specs and test expectations are not reused; we diverge into incompatibility on purpose. (4) English-compatible SOTA from day one. (5) Structure the project from the lessons of fiscal, tibia and microblog/x (ask them); ask rig what NOT to do (kit drifted because it started pre-dialect). DoD: the feature-parity language set runs from alphabet in Spiral with its own tests green on every declared backend; alphabet/apps/documents no longer depends on the hangulize fork (grep + build proof); then main tells the USER, who deletes the fork from the org (never an agent). Next step: main collects structure answers from fiscal, tibia, microblog, management and rig, then splits this into SKELETON, MODEL, ENGINE, LANGUAGES-n, DOCUMENTS-SWITCH children.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_26 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 26u32, "2026-11-08T05:25Z", "main", "main 01:2x STRUCTURE INPUT (asked by the user; answers from tibia, fiscal, rig, management, microblog). CONSENSUS: (1) lib-first: generic pieces (Unicode/jamo tables, transliteration engine, rule DSL) land in lib with a unique prefix (hangul_/translit_) via lib_land_window; alphabet keeps domain data as typed Spiral data modules and thin glue. (2) Types first: unions for scripts, jamo and modes; the syllable is a record, not a string; type-level sizes where lengths matter; no int codes, bool flags or name-substring classification. (3) Cells tests-only from commit 1: each runnable test has its own ///> with only the crates it uses; no file headers, no inherited targets; a negative control per test; goldens built independently from the rule tables (upstream presumed wrong), with a disagreement list. (4) Pure Spiral logic plus a thin IO edge; Rust crates only at the edge; develop on C for speed, keep Rust green, and treat C pass + Rust fail as out of bounds. (5) Shape crates from day one (model + IO + one OS boundary; under 1000 lines); manifests list modules in dependency order and every referenced package; main exports only (tuple args); no keyword names (open, real, join, type). (6) Generated code comes from one byte-reproducible generator, never hand-edited; gate drift. (7) The gate's NON-granular cell budget and format --check --path run in the inner loop; one small node gate per change, the full suite on a schedule (--timeout 900000, 3 h cap). Next step: main drafts the SKELETON child from this list.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_27 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 27u32, "2026-11-08T12:26Z", "i574n", "Typed boundaries (memory typed-boundaries-playbook, 11-08): alphabet is single-unit today (8,710 .spi lines, 0 exports, 2 natives), so the rewrite applies rules 1-3 from its FIRST split: model types in a linked model unit listed under both packages and link; exports name the definition with tuple args; GADTs cross via the M2 facade. No string-in/string-out wires between units from day one.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_28 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 28u32, "2026-11-09T15:49Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: HANGULIZE wrongly surfaces as a ready split-candidate. TWO facts: (1) USER DEFERRED it 2026-10-19 23:1x ('forget hangulize for now; at 5-7+ agent days it waits until XSD and full hopacsota are done'); the 10-31 KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK flipped it Paused->Planned on the silence-rule technicality but did NOT re-check the user's explicit condition, so it should not be in agile next. (2) The split the stop-gate suggests ('kit agile edit --split') is NOT a command in this kit build (edit takes only title/kind/priority/difficulty/deps/tags/estimate/load/assignee) = a kit/rig gap; a real split would be N child items via agile add. The staged children are ALREADY designed in notes (S0 drop-fork; S1 three lanes: hsl parser+spec model ~350L, HRE continuation matcher ~450L, Unicode tables+jamo compose/split+interpolation; S1 por-br parity + documents in-process cutover; S2 pure-rule specs 3498/3498; S3 cyrillic+pinyin; S4 furigana/jpn) so whoever resumes can add them directly. Parking on the user condition until XSD + full hopacsota are done; clear this wait when both are confirmed done, then split via agile add (or a rig split verb if one lands).")
inl kit_uses_lib () = Task ("KIT-USES-LIB", "eoie builds on lib like alphabet", Feature, P1, D6, 40u32, Planned, "", "kit,lib,dogfood,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_uses_lib () = Planning ("KIT-USES-LIB", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_1 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:44Z", "main-thread", "15:44 main-thread root cause (verified): alphabet reaches lib through packageDir ../../deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-, a copy inside its own tree. eoie regenerates in a hermetic staging copy (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 copies only the eoie tree, rejects links as Unexpected workspace link, skips vendor), so a packageDir into the sibling spiral repo would dangle; plus every eoie package is no-core. That is why eoie re-grew reflection (pipe tables), sm' (rust_std_string), core operators (i32_eq helpers) and option (validation_result). Plan: (1) a pinned lib snapshot staged with eoie (a deps/spiral-lib copy refreshed by a verb, or the staging step copying a hash-pinned lib), (2) a core-only probe crate to measure the 1k-gate cost of core inlining, (3) move eoie's generic pieces into lib (union_cases_fold is now in lib reflection.spi too), (4) switch eoie packages to |core- one owner at a time, removing the copies. User 2026-10-17: make eoie use lib like alphabet does; reuse what you can, move what is generic.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_2 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: staged pinned snapshot. eoie keeps a pin file with the hash of spiral/lib; regeneration copies that lib next to the staged eoie tree; packages point at it with packageDir and use |core- and spiral-. No committed lib copy in eoie; a verb refreshes the pin.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_3 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 (later round): the staged lib follows the tree. Every regeneration stages the current spiral/lib (no pin file, no refresh verb).")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_4 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:13 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the 1,685 raw comparison ops in eoie (1,155 LT, 530 EQ, plus NEQ/LTE/Add/Mult/Sub) wait for this item: once packages get |core-, one mechanical pass per owner rewrites them to plain operators; no interim helper layer. Same for the 10 StaticStringConcat sites (6 of them added today in union_reflection and the agile domain).")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_5 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 5u32, "2026-10-22T09:22Z", "eoie", "05:22 groom: this is under way. (1) eoie builds on lib: lib staging (regeneration copies tree lib/spiral into the staged src/spiral, no pin, follows the tree as decided 10-17) ships in release ABC, proven on agile_task_query. (2) The 1,685 raw comparison ops went to EOIE-CORE-OPERATORS (Done 10-22: 12 documented exceptions left). (3) The package-by-package move onto lib is EOIE-LIB-REUSE: releases E + E2 drafted, which take packages on lib from 1 to 113 and rust_std_string from 116 to 14, with gate previews pending. Close this once ABC installs and E/E2 land.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_6 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 6u32, "2026-10-23T21:49Z", "eoie", "17:49 eoie: close check measured on the live tree after GE2 landed its E/E2 wave at 15:00 (GE2 not installed yet): 382 packages, 106 list spiral- (E+E2's drafted target was 113), and 32 still list rust_std_string (target 14). Not closable yet: 7 packages short of the lib target and 18 over the rust_std_string target, either from E/E2 slices that stayed out of GE2 or from packages added since 10-22 that carry the old dependency. Next (light, read-only): list the 32 rust_std_string packages, split them into 'in a later slice (HIJK..P)' and 'not covered', and give the uncovered ones to EOIE-LIB-REUSE.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_7 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 7u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 435m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D6 at 40%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_8 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 8u32, "2026-10-26T14:21Z", "eoie", "10:21 eoie: the 17:49 next step done, measured on the renamed live tree. 427 packages, 155 list spiral- (36%), 41 still list rust_std_string (target 14). Breakdown: 7 list it but never use it (no mention in any .spi): kit_do_git_host, kit_command_help, kit_job_rechain, kit_receipt, kit_jobs_doctor, kit_job_supervise, agile_todo_lines. 12 call it qualified (1-33 calls, concat5/equals/replace/starts_with/trim/pipe_*), which heal-crate.ps1 maps except pipe tables. 22 open it wholesale (:< rust_std_string), 18 of them with 0 qualified calls, so their uses are unqualified and need the tool's wholesale mode (agile_todo_model is the big one: 119 calls plus pipe_member). Slice L drafted for the 7 unused (scratch clean\\kit-rename\\drop-unused-std-string.do, 7 files, 7 replacements, each anchored with its next line so no blank line is left; the 2 not yet on the lib get spiral- instead). Expected codegen-neutral; proof queued: job kit-neutral-stdstr (closure-aware, tree asserted). After it lands: 34 on rust_std_string, 157 on the lib.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_9 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 9u32, "2026-10-26T14:38Z", "eoie", "10:38 eoie: slice M drafted with heal-crate updated for edition 3 (scratch heal-crate-e3.ps1, the 10-22 tool's logic unchanged). Every syntax-dependent pattern accepts open/:< and inl/let/:=/:~, the inserted operator open is ':< sm'_operators', spiral- is added only when absent (the old tool duplicated it), and an existing standards.spi gets HostFree/LibReferenced merged in instead of being overwritten with inl-syntax lines. Dry runs: kit_agile_mutation, agile_subcommand_domain, kit_agile_list_args, kit_agile_next_args, kit_agile_routes and agile_task_listing heal cleanly (one spiral- line, no rust_std_string left, no old-syntax opens). Its near-gate guard skips workspace_primitives, agile_task_text, agile_todo_scan, agile_task_creation and agile_note_model (they or a consumer sit at 949-960 lines), and command_spec_domain needs pipe tables healed by hand. Proof queued: job-1793025593390-kit-heal-e3 (applier apply-heal-e3.ps1 refuses unless all six heal; 10 crates; all 111 notebooks, rust default target). Landing-order note: slices G-literals and M both edit kit_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi on different lines, so M lands after the arithmetic slices and its dry run reruns on the post-G tree first.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_10 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 10u32, "2026-10-27T03:38Z", "kit", "23:38 kit: slice M proven on the node (kit-heal-e3-2, 111/111 notebooks), see EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION's newest note. It moves kit_agile_mutation, agile_subcommand_domain, kit_agile_list_args, kit_agile_next_args, kit_agile_routes and agile_task_listing off rust_std_string onto the lib, and lands with land-post-rename.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_11 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 11u32, "2026-10-27T21:35Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x: slice M is in the live tree. kit_agile_mutation, agile_subcommand_domain, kit_agile_list_args, kit_agile_next_args, kit_agile_routes and agile_task_listing have no rust_std_string import or use and all reference spiral-. Remaining measure for this item: 28 packages still import rust_std_string; call sites per package, largest first: agile_todo_model 113, agile_note_model 27, agile_task_next 27, agile_task_creation 24, command_spec_domain 23, agile_todo_scan 21, agile_task_text 9, kit_machine_watch_model 7, kit_table_query 7, kit_experiments 6, kit_machine_watch 3, kit_do_process 3, kit_do_program 3, kit_do_facts 3, kit_style_census 3, kit_jobs 3, kit_do_git 3, kit_machine_windows 3, kit_memory_query 1, kit_memory_model 1, kit_memory 1, kit_do_git_reports 1, kit_do_edit 1, kit_job_state 1, kit_job_gate 1, kit_experiment_analysis 1, kit_machine_window_watch 1, workspace_primitives 0. The near-gate guard from note 9 still applies (workspace_primitives, agile_task_text, agile_todo_scan, agile_task_creation, agile_note_model and their consumers sit at 949-993 lines after TRAIN), so the next slices take the small, far-from-gate packages first.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_12 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 12u32, "2026-10-27T21:37Z", "kit", "kit 17:3x: next slice drafted for the release after TRAIN: scratch clean/uses-lib/window-watch-to-lib.do. kit_machine_window_watch (the smallest of the 28, 112 generated lines) drops rust_std_string for spiral- plus sm'_operators, and its 3 concat5 sites become ++# chains in the same order (byte-identical output by construction). It applies 4/4 on a copy of the post-TRAIN live tree, and windows.rs compiles at 114 lines (+2). The package's other host macros (line_count, line_at, tab_field, label_job, gate_summary) are row and log parsing; they go to the boundary or typed rows in their own slice, not here. 27 packages left on rust_std_string after this one.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_13 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 13u32, "2026-10-28T14:51Z", "eoie", "10:5x kit: measured the LibReferenced rule ('every package references the lib') on the live tree: 441 package.spiproj files, of which 266 have no spiral- line; one is the src root manifest, which needs none. Adding the reference should be codegen-neutral, because Spiral emits only what is reached. The one real risk is a name clash: once a package references the lib, lib module names (env, sm', file_system, process, common, ...) become visible inside it. Generator: scratch clean/lib-clip/make-lib-referenced.ps1 writes lib-referenced.do, which inserts 'spiral-' after each package's '|core-' line. It skips the 3 packages that own a module with a lib module's name (bootstrap_policy and kit_process have 'process', state_receipt_codec_model has 'common'); those need a rename or a manual check. 261 edits, dry-run clean on the TRAIN4 copy. Proof rule, written now: a full regen compare (every crate, base vs candidate, same DLL and lib) must show 0 compile failures and every crate byte-identical. Any diff names a clash to fix, not a cost to accept. First evidence comes free: the export-rewrite compare running now (job-1793188973244) adds spiral- to 64 packages. Order: read that compare, run the full compare on the node when it has room, then ship in the release after TRAIN4. 11:0x first evidence in: the export-rewrite compare (job-1793188973244) added spiral- to 64 packages and all 215 compiled crates came out byte-identical. So referencing the lib is codegen-neutral where no module name clashes, as predicted. The remaining 261 still need their own full compare before shipping.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_14 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 14u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 40%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_15 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 15u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-USES-LIB (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_16 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 16u32, "2026-11-09T17:17Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 17:3x rig groom, RE-MEASURED (package.spiproj scan): 627 packages; 185 without a spiral- lib reference (10-28: 266 of 441, so the share fell from 60 to 30 pct); rust_std_string dependents ROSE from 27 to 43 (new packages copied the old pattern, which the edit hook now flags). The proof rule from 10-28 stands: a full regen compare must show 0 compile failures and byte-identical crates; any diff names a lib-name clash to fix.")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_17 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 17u32, "2026-11-09T17:18Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "MILESTONES: add the spiral- reference to the first third of the unreferenced packages with a byte-identical regen compare 120 m; the second third with the same compare 120 m; the last third plus the packages whose module names clash with lib modules 120 m; retire rust_std_string from the smallest dependents onto sm functions 120 m")
inl note_of_kit_uses_lib_18 () = Note ("KIT-USES-LIB", 18u32, "2026-11-09T17:18Z", "kit-split", "split into 4 chained children by kit agile split")
inl kit_experiments () = Task ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", "Pre-registered experiment ledger, one call per step: register (question, arms, run count, primary metric, test, decision rule, timestamp) before any data; record runs from a results table; analyze exactly once at the planned count (Welch two-sided with CI, one-sample t against a prediction, critical values from the Student quantile); refuse or flag any read of the primary metric before the planned count (interim-look guard with a disclosure record); post-hoc addenda kept apart from the verdict; a rendered report. Replaces hand-written analysis scripts and markdown pre-registration tables.", Feature, P1, D5, 30u32, Active, "", "kit,experiments,stats,one-call,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_experiments () = Planning ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_experiments_1 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 1u32, "2026-10-20T15:39Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-20 11:39 requirements from tibia's 21 BENCHMARK pre-registrations (first consumer, TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE waits on this). Fields used (count of 21): Question 20, Runs 20, Decision 20, RESULT 18, Scenario 15, Test 14, Prediction 11, Competitor(s) 16, Secondary (descriptive only) 7, Alternative 5, Amendment 3 (timestamped, must precede the runs they change), Expectation 3 (stated with a time before data), Addendum/Post-hoc 2+ (kept apart from the verdict), Chunk rows (run blocks across days into one table, boundaries logged, crash rule decided without looking at the metric). Tests actually used: Welch two-sided with 95% CI (PR-1..PR-12, tibia live/pr1-analysis.ps1); one-sample t against a model prediction (PR-10, PR-11, PR-14, PR-15); binomial P(at most k | p) for clear counts (PR-14); Wilson 95% band as a decision band (PC-5); a median inside a stated band (PC-4); a share-on-grid count (PC-3). Data: rows from a TSV (filter by competitor/time, exclude foreign-activity rows) and counts from log lines by regex (kills from 'Loot of a ...', hits from 'You lose N'). Must-haves: register before data (the timestamp is the guard), planned n with chunking, crash/rerun rule, one analysis at planned n (interim-look guard), amendments that cannot postdate runs, verdict + secondary + post-hoc as separate records.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_2 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 2u32, "2026-10-20T15:55Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 11:55 eoie lane slice 1 in source (crate eoie_experiments, queued for compile behind the live-gui window): eoie experiment register <ledger> <ID> --test welch|one-sample|binomial|wilson --metric --runs --decision [--group --arms a[,b]] [--prediction] [--confidence] [--question] writes <ID>.registration once (create_new, UTC stamp); analyze <ledger> <ID> <table.tsv> [--where]... refuses before the planned rows per arm unless --interim <reason> (appends <ID>.disclosures, no verdict), computes once and writes <ID>.verdict, refuses a second analysis; show prints registration, disclosures, verdict. Stats are granular Spiral (experiment_stats.spi): Lanczos ln-gamma, continued-fraction regularized beta, Student CDF and bisection quantile, Welch with Satterthwaite df and CI, one-sample t, binomial P(X<=k), Wilson band, erfc normal quantile; checked against values computed independently (Simpson integration of the t density): Welch t=-1.897367 df=5.882353 p=0.107531, t(0.975,10)=2.2281389, Wilson 8/10 [0.490162,0.943318], P(X<=3|20,0.35)=0.0443756. Tests: experiment_stats 4, experiment_model 4, experiments_cli 3. Slice 2 from tibia's note 1: median band, grid share, log-regex data sources, amendments that cannot postdate runs, register-before-data timestamp check against a time column, chunk/crash rules, post-hoc addenda, BENCHMARK import.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_3 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 3u32, "2026-10-20T20:01Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 16:01 eoie lane: slice 1 installed with release 20 (15:54), split under the 1000-line gate into eoie_experiments (register/show, 629), eoie_experiment_analysis (table tally + look guard + files, 876) and eoie_experiment_stats (math + terms export, 648); smoke on the installed binary: welch [5,7] vs [4,6] t=0.707107 df=2 p=0.552786 ci=[-5.08487,7.08487] (hand-checked). Slice 2 in release 21 (running): --test median --prediction low,high, and --time <col> at register keeps the local registration stamp so analysis leaves out and counts rows stamped earlier (before_registration=N). Mutants: experiment_stats fails exactly its test; experiments_cli mutant waits for the release 21 binary.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_4 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 4u32, "2026-10-21T00:34Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:34 eoie lane: release 22 installed: --test median --prediction low,high, --time <col> (registration keeps a local stamp, analysis leaves out and counts rows stamped earlier), amend (refused after any look, appended with time and reason; with --time the amendment time becomes the cutoff), addendum (post-hoc note apart from the verdict). Styled under the old drift (see EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET 200/1000): string-typed ledger files and joined lists to be remodeled with the typefeatureset.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_5 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 5u32, "2026-10-21T00:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 20:35 eoie lane: first real consumer confirmed by tibia at 20:4x: live/pr15-analysis.ps1 now runs on eoie table logs (--count, --count-by) plus table query and reproduces PR-15's pre-registered numbers exactly; arena-summary's TSV half runs on table query (--count/--mean/--sd/--min/--sum) since release 20. The time-window split for pr12-secondary (--since name=regex) is still open, low priority while Tibia is parked.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_6 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 6u32, "2026-10-21T01:54Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-20 21:54 eoie team groom for the 10-20/21 night (to 07:00): night slice (6th, if time): remodel in the typefeatureset style: registration, amendments, disclosures and verdict as typed records written as Spiral rows instead of key=value text, the test kinds as an indexed union carrying their parameters (prediction only where the kind needs one), results as unions instead of joined strings; same CLI and tests. Remaining estimate 240 min.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_7 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 7u32, "2026-10-21T04:58Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 00:58 typed plan reworked: runtime record holds union tested = TestedMean of test_plan mean_test | TestedRate | TestedMedian (concrete GADT indices are runtime-safe, any_test_plan was not). Family accessors (rate_arm, predicted_rate, mean_arm) are total over their index. Preview compile: experiments and analysis ok.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_8 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 8u32, "2026-10-21T05:21Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 01:21 remodel moves to r27: analysis.rs 1275 after the arm-as-record-field cut (gate 1000); analysis now compiles the plan parser (plan_checked 118, plan_of 57) that it used to skip. Next lever: split or share the parser via an exporting crate.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_9 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 9u32, "2026-10-21T06:05Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:05 levers for the remodel (analysis.rs 1275 in preview vs 1000): utc_stamp + civil_of_days compile ~108 lines of calendar math into analysis (call an export instead); plan_checked (118) re-validates what register already checked (read plans unchecked in analysis, keep the check in register and list); test_of (37) parses the test word that the typed plan already carries. Estimated ~1010 after all three; a fourth cut or moving the ledger writes (look_of, record files) to a small exporting crate is needed. r28 at the earliest; the arm-as-field and family-indexed tested union drafts stay in scratch experiments-remodel.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_10 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 10u32, "2026-10-21T06:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 02:40 two levers implemented in the scratch remodel and applied to the preview tree (compile pending): analysis reads plans with plan_trusted (test word only; register and list keep plan_of with plan_checked), and timestamps come from a new agile_task_notes export eoie_utc_stamp instead of compiling ~108 lines of calendar math into analysis (Cargo dep plus sorted lock entry in apply-experiments-remodel.ps1). Record construction is shared via plan_record. The preview will show whether analysis.rs fits under 1000.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_11 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 11u32, "2026-10-21T07:40Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 03:40 remodel fits the gate: analysis.rs 966 (from 1275) after a trusted plan reader, an exported UTC stamp (agile_task_notes eoie_utc_stamp), and kind carried in the plan record so analysis no longer compiles kind_of_plan; experiments.rs 883. Preview-compiled (r28c). Not packed into the combined r26: that release carries the false-lost fix every team needs, and the remodel adds unrun notebooks (the r25 failure was a notebook), so it leads the next release.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_12 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 12u32, "2026-10-21T20:35Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:35 eoie team: shipped in the combined r28+r29 release, installed 15:38 (one-call spec 34/34, all 67 notebooks green): experiments remodel (analysis.rs 962).")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_13 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 13u32, "2026-10-21T20:59Z", "eoie-lane", "2026-10-21 16:59 eoie team, progress review: shipped: remodel, experiment import (tibia imported 35 pre-registrations today), table test; r34 adds typed --where comparisons and whole-table one-population tests, which unblock tibia's last 6 imports.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_14 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 14u32, "2026-10-22T10:16Z", "eoie", "06:16 friction from a consumer (binomial registration, 19/20 at p 0.74): analyze printed the one-sided BinomialAtMost verdict (reject_h0=no, p_at_most=0.998) while the pre-registered test was a two-sided acceptance band (k in 11..18, the central 95% of Binomial(20, 0.74)), read by hand. Design: new test_kind BinomialBand (word binomial-band): register --test binomial-band --prediction p [--confidence 0.95] computes the central band at REGISTRATION from n (runs), p and confidence (lo = smallest k with P(X <= k) > alpha/2, hi = smallest k with P(X <= k) >= 1 - alpha/2, via the existing binomial_at_most in eoie_experiment_stats) and stores lo..hi in the plan, so the band is pre-registered, not recomputed after the data; an explicit --accept lo..hi overrides for bands chosen another way. analyze prints band=[lo,hi] k=<count> verdict=holds|fails (a verdict union, not reject_h0 text). Placement (gate): eoie_experiments/experiments.rs is at 949 lines, so the band computation and verdict go in the eoie_experiment_stats crate (terms.rs 644) behind its existing boundary, and experiments only gains the test word and plan field. Needs compiles; next session.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_15 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 15u32, "2026-10-22T12:18Z", "eoie-lane", "08:18 eoie team: binomial-band drafted (scratch apply-binomial-band.ps1; applies on the live tree and on F+E+E2; paren scan clean; every rendering of the test-word list updated: model test, CLI refusal, command spec, table test usage, README). Shape: test_kind gains BinomialBand ('binomial-band') and the GADT gains BinomialBandPlan :: f64 -> test_plan rate_test (prediction_of and kind_of_plan cover it). experiment_stats gets binomial_band n p confidence through a band_edge union (LowEdge: first k with P(X<=k) > alpha/2; HighEdge: first k with P(X<=k) >= 1-alpha/2), with 5 table-checked cases (20/0.74/95% = 11..18, the consumer's band). terms prints 'successes=k/n band=[lo,hi] verdict=holds|fails' from a band_verdict union. CLI notebook: 19/20 fails and 15/20 holds. Decisions: the band is computed at analysis from the registered n, p and confidence, all fixed before data, so it is pre-registered without a stored copy; --accept lo..hi is left out until someone needs a band chosen another way. Gate risk: experiments.rs 949 and analysis.rs 965 each gain one case per match, to be measured by the preview. Ships in release G (after F).")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_16 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 16u32, "2026-10-23T00:33Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:33 x (dogfood, from pre-registering a 3-arm mode comparison): two findings. (1) experiment analyze reports 'unknown columns (header: \"Round\",\"Mode\",...)' for a quoted CSV, and table query accepts CSV. Either read CSV the way table query does, or say 'expected tab-separated'. The same rows as TSV analyze fine. Likewise --where 'Mode!=C' fails as 'unknown columns' instead of 'unsupported comparison' (EOIE-TABLE-TEST-WHERE's open part). (2) Missing decision type: a guarded threshold rule. Switch to arm X only if median(metric) is at least k% below the control's AND guard columns hold: an equal set (pass/fail counts), a max within +delta (peak MB), a max not higher (slowest cell). Today that rule lives in a one-off analysis script, which memory generic-tools forbids, while the registered welch with n=2 per arm only adds a CI. Proposed: register --test threshold --ratio 0.85 --guard Passed=same --guard PeakOwnMB<=+1500 --guard MaxCellS<=control, with analyze printing each guard's verdict. Extra-arm rows are already ignored correctly (checked).")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_17 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 17u32, "2026-10-23T07:28Z", "eoie", "03:28 eoie: x's 20:33 note. (1) CSV and '!=': '--where' comparisons (=, !=, >, >=, <, <=) ship with F (EOIE-TABLE-TEST-WHERE); reading quoted CSV in analyze, or saying 'expected tab-separated', stays open here. (2) The guarded threshold decision is drafted as a pure model (scratch f5/src/eoie_threshold_model, HostFree and LibReferenced): guard_rule SameAsControl column | MaxWithin column allowance | MaxNotAbove column; first_broken folds the guards; threshold_decision_of ratio arm_median control_median guards gives SwitchToArm | StayWithControl reason, which names the first broken guard or the missed threshold. Its test block is x's own case (0.85, Passed same, PeakOwnMB +1500, MaxCellS not above). Cells check in post-F's model previews after the live block; next step: '--test threshold --ratio r --guard col=same|col<=+d|col<=control' in experiment register and analyze, printing each guard's verdict.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_18 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 18u32, "2026-10-23T07:40Z", "eoie", "03:40 eoie: x's CSV point, lib first. lib/spiral has no CSV support (grep: none; positive control json: 3 files). table query splits .csv on commas without unquoting, and analyze reads through its own table cells, so x's quoted header failed as 'unknown columns'. Drafted lib csv.spi (scratch job-env/apply-lib-csv.ps1): csv_line_as_tsv (quoted cells, doubled quotes, commas inside quotes), pure Spiral over core sm.index/slice like json_scan, registered after json_scan in lib package.spiproj, with a rust and fsharp test block. It is gated and landed with the env, last_write_ms and append parts (libgate-copy.ps1 now records package.spiproj's hash, csv.spi 'absent', and gates csv.spi). eoie side next: table query's and analyze's readers convert .csv lines through csv_line_as_tsv, so both take x's file.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_19 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 19u32, "2026-10-23T07:43Z", "eoie", "03:43 eoie: eoie side of the CSV fix, planned. Both analyze and the table verbs load through eoie_table_query/cells.spi table_read, a single legacy host block that reads the file and splits .csv lines on bare commas without unquoting. The fix migrates it as AGENTS asks: (1) a read primitive returning the text or the problem; (2) in Spiral, for .csv paths, each line goes through lib csv_line_as_tsv (now char-safe after main's review: split on the quote, tabs and newlines inside cells become spaces, with a non-ASCII test); (3) a primitive builds table_cells from the TSV text. The table_load union and every caller stay as they are. Test: x's quoted header analyzed like the same rows in TSV, in table_cli and experiments_cli. Needs compile previews, so it comes after GE2.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_20 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 20u32, "2026-10-23T12:30Z", "eoie", "08:30 CSV point implemented for release L, now that lib csv landed (06:38): new HostFree crate eoie_table_text (text.spi: each .csv line through csv.csv_line_as_tsv, CRLF dropped, a missing file named; entry.spi exports eoie_table_tsv and eoie_table_problem, kept apart from the tested module because the notebook runner also builds Zig, which has no export) passes its test against the live lib. table_read in eoie_table_query, inlined by query, diff, measures, test and experiment analysis, becomes two crate calls plus a one-expression cell split instead of the multi-step host block that split .csv on bare commas; analysis.rs (965 lines) gains about one line. table_cli test csv_tables_keep_quoted_commas_inside_one_cell (quoted header, 'a, b' cell) fails on the old reader by construction; prelaunch clean on a live copy; L release lists both notebooks and the baseline merge; after-install-L probes it.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_21 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 21u32, "2026-10-23T23:56Z", "x", "2026-10-23 19:56 x: consumer finding while registering X-SHELL-SWITCH / X-CORPUS-R1 / X-CORPUS-R2 in x's ledger. My decision rules compare MEDIANS of two arms (5 noisy wall-time runs each, where one slow outlier is common), but 'experiment register --test median' accepts only one arm ('median needs --arms with one group value'), so two-arm median rules get registered under welch, which compares means. That mismatch means the analyzed verdict can disagree with the written rule on exactly the outlier-heavy timings these experiments produce. Ask: a two-sample median test, e.g. --test mann-whitney (rank-sum, with a Hodges-Lehmann shift and CI), for two-arm rules stated in medians. Searched the backlog first: no item mentions Mann-Whitney, rank-sum or a two-sample median test.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_22 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 22u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): BENCH-1 (sealed control, threshold), BENCH-4 (failed setups kept, near-miss receipts, native deltas). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_23 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 23u32, "2026-10-26T07:35Z", "eoie", "03:35 eoie: quoted-CSV fix PROVEN on the worker node. Draft kit scratch clean\\kit-rename\\table-csv-quoted.do: table query's legacy query_rows block keeps its read_to_string check (missing-file errors unchanged) but takes its rows from kit_table_text::kit_table_tsv, which converts .csv through lib csv_line_as_tsv; the separator is then always a tab. Candidate: the renamed tree plus this draft; kit_adoption and kit_table_query regenerated; debug build; this box's compiler. Strict verdict (positive control executed 2, passed 2, unsupported 0): 4/4 table notebooks pass (ps_cli, table_cli, sequences_cli, text), so csv_tables_keep_quoted_commas_inside_one_cell passes. Owed by AGENTS.md: query_rows is a 40-line legacy host block, and touching it means migrating it (a GRANULAR-MIGRATION slice); this draft is the minimal correct fix. Lands after KIT-RENAME; then the table_cli row leaves the release known-failures table.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_24 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 24u32, "2026-10-27T21:13Z", "kit", "kit 17:1x, read against the consolidated spec (Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md section 16). Left open here: BENCH-1 (paired A/B with lifecycle control: same conditions, caches reset or labelled per stage, alternating order, both source hashes bound, comparison against the sealed control and the best current state with a threshold, rollback by hash, warm-state labels matching) and BENCH-4 (failed setups kept, measurement scopes declared, no pooling of incompatible metrics, near-miss receipts, native wall/user/system/RSS capture, typed rejected-candidate receipts, native deltas between two receipts). The quoted-CSV table fix from note 23 has landed (kit_table_query/cells.spi reads through kit_table_tsv). A live case for BENCH-1 came up today: KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS notes 10-11 timed cold and warm runs on a busy node, one pair per arm, and the run-to-run spread (warm 11.3 to 23.2 s) exceeded the effect being tested. That item's next measurement (5 alternating pairs per arm, compared by median) goes through this ledger as a dogfood case: register question, arms and decision rule first, record each pair with its warm or cold label, analyze once at n=5. Any friction found that way becomes BENCH-1 scope.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_25 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 25u32, "2026-10-28T14:13Z", "eoie", "10:1x kit: BENCH-1 design (paired A/B with lifecycle control), built on the existing ledger (kit_experiments experiment_model: test kinds, plan_checked, amendments, looks). Today a run is a row with a metric. BENCH-1 makes a run a typed pair whose conditions are bound before it is measured.\n- Arm identity: arm_binding = { arm : string; source_hash : string; tool_hash : string; warm : warm_state }, where warm_state = Cold | WarmAfter : i32 (n prior runs in the same process home). It is recorded when the run is registered, never inferred afterwards. A pair whose two arms differ in tool_hash, or in a warm label they should share, is refused at record time (PairRefused reason), not discarded at analysis time.\n- Order: pair_order = AFirst | BFirst, alternating by pair index (ABBA over each 4 pairs). The ledger stores the order it prescribed and refuses a recorded pair run in the other order. This removes the drift bias that sequential A-then-B runs carry under background load, which matters on a box that never goes quiet (no-quiet-machine-wait).\n- Cache lifecycle per stage: stage_reset = ResetCaches : string | LabelledWarm : string. Each arm declares, per stage (build, first run, steady), whether caches are cleared (and which) or kept with a label. Both arms of a pair must declare the same lifecycle, or the pair is refused.\n- Comparison: a verdict compares the candidate against both the sealed control (the arm sealed at registration, by hash) and the best current state (the best accepted arm so far, by hash). Each comparison carries its pre-registered threshold. A candidate is accepted only if it clears both. A rejection records which comparison failed and by how much (feeds BENCH-4's near-miss receipts).\n- Rollback by hash: adopting an arm writes adopted = { arm; source_hash } into the experiment, so rolling back is restoring that hash, not remembering what changed.\nMilestones (pre-registered, sum 300m): B1 90m, model plus refusals as pure functions with a test block (mismatched tool hash, wrong order, unequal lifecycle each refused). B2 60m, ledger rows for arm_binding and pair records (typed columns, no joined strings, so the style ratchet does not grow). B3 90m, the 'experiment pair' CLI verb records a pair, and the ABBA schedule is printed by 'experiment next'. B4 60m, a two-comparison verdict with the near-miss amount. Acceptance probe: rerun today's M5 timing rule (warm/cold < 0.25) as a BENCH-1 experiment: 5 ABBA pairs, cold = ResetCaches todo-cache, warm = LabelledWarm. The ledger must refuse a deliberately swapped pair.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_26 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 26u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 85%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_27 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 27u32, "2026-11-04T08:05Z", "kit", "11-04 04:5x kit check against the installed kit: register, analyze, amend, addendum, show, import and record-verdict exist. There is NO `experiment pair` or `experiment next` (milestones B3 ABBA pair recording/schedule and B4 two-comparison verdict with near-miss), so it stays at 85%. The next concrete step is B3: the pair verb as a typed command (x shape), with the ABBA schedule from the ledger.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_28 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 28u32, "2026-11-06T11:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-EXPERIMENTS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_29 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 29u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries BENCH-4 (Experiment provenance and deltas). Why: 10-24 note #22: failed setups kept, near-miss receipts, native deltas; kit experiment register/analyze ships")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_30 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 30u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. BENCH-4 · Experiment provenance and deltas · P1 · node, ssam, ota · Open / Keep failed setups and trials, declare measurement scopes, never pool incompatible metrics, record structured near-miss receipts (compile/runtime split, workload cardinality), capture wall/user/system/RSS natively instead of `/usr/bin/time -v`, keep typed rejected-candidate receipts (zero credit, with rollback), and compute deltas between two benchmark receipts natively.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_31 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 31u32, "2026-11-09T13:57Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:4x tibia: SPEC AS A DIFF against src/rig_experiments, from ~20 real pre-registered steps (TIBIA-BC-POLICY steps 5-11, PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH steps 1-3 + parts 1-3, TYPE-ARITH-5-RESHAPE note 7). NEEDED BUT MISSING: (1) GATES before the verdict: Identical (two artifact digests equal, e.g. C content sha cebdd2e8a3dd3734 with and without join points), Reproduces x (a control value re-derives exactly: BC I1 loss 0.049796116), ExitIs n (with the POSIX byte: 567 reads as 55). (2) A THIRD OUTCOME, Void: a failed gate means nothing was measured. That is not Fails (the stale-mirror lib check and the zig rate without equal rows were voids, not fails). (3) A BAND ON A MEAN (the BC bar [-17.28,-6.20] is on a mean; the model has only MedianBand). (4) SOFT expectations: recorded held/missed, never deciding (\"largest Zig fn < 15k\" missed twice while the run held). (5) BRANCHES chosen before the data: outcome -> next registration (step 10 Fails with flat extra retreats -> step 11 fired exactly as written). (6) An ENVIRONMENT FINGERPRINT per arm (host, compiler core sha, tree fingerprint, job id); arms compare only on the same compiler+tree. (7) A CALIBRATION/EVALUATION SPLIT: calibration rows never reach the verdict (step 11 theta on training seeds). (8) MEASURE FROM JOB OUTPUT: every result here was a job's stdout lines, never a TSV table, so `analyze <table.tsv>` was unusable as-is. A row-prefix reader over a job tail would make the ledger usable. PRESENT AND USED: register-before-data, decision rule required, amend-before-look (used once: rebench2's gate corrected before any data, note 19 there). PRESENT AND UNUSED: welch/one-sample/binomial/wilson p-value tests (lanes compared means, CIs and paired diffs by hand), confidence, interim looks (single-shot jobs), --time, import. DRAFT (unstaged, claimed path): src/rig_experiments/experiment_gates.spi + its line in package.spiproj. Host-free typed model (gate, reading, outcome Holds|Fails|Void, band, stake Binding|Soft, branch, fingerprint, split) with 5 test blocks built from the real rows above. Not compiled yet: the probe is rig's train.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_32 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 32u32, "2026-11-09T13:58Z", "tibia", "DoD: experiment_gates.spi (the spec in note 31) compiles in rig_experiments with its 5 test blocks green on a node probe, and register/analyze gain gates, a Void outcome and a job-output reader, so that one of tibia's real steps (BC step 11 or the rebench) can be registered and closed through rig experiment instead of agile notes.")
inl note_of_kit_experiments_33 () = Note ("KIT-EXPERIMENTS", 33u32, "2026-11-09T13:58Z", "tibia", "TypeFeatures: outcome is a union with Void, so a run whose gate failed cannot be read as a fail. A gate is a union paired with a typed reading (Digests | Derived | Exited | PosixExited), so an exit byte cannot be compared as a full code by mistake. stake (Binding | Soft) makes non-deciding expectations explicit. split (Calibration | Evaluation) makes leakage into the verdict a type-level choice.")
inl kit_machine_windows () = Task ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "Exclusive machine windows shared by all sessions on one box, one call each: request a window class (live GUI runs, test-suite runs, compiler installs, heavy builds) with an expected duration, get granted/queued, release; conflicts between classes declared once (no suites during GUI runs, no compiler use during installs); free-memory admission; receipts readable by every client. Replaces chat messages like \"window open/done\" and the separate heavy-gate script.", Feature, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,coordination,one-call,sessions,lab-held", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_machine_windows () = Planning ("KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl kit_standards_checks () = Task ("KIT-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "One-call code-standard checks per package, declared in the package (spiproj or a standards file): no comment lines (directives excepted), no floating point in modules marked integer-only, every test block names its target, line length, no CRLF where the repo uses LF; machine-readable findings; usable as a gate.", Feature, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,standards,checks,one-call", "current", "current")
inl kit_agile_list_fields () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS", "agile list --json lacks fields agents need for grooming without one show per item: notes count, last note time, deps, tags, created time; plus filters by id prefix and tag, and an oldest-first order (by created time). Today grooming \"oldest first\" needs guessing from title dates.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,agile,json", "current", "current")
inl spiral_diagnostic_locations () = Task ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", "Every type error carries file:line:col and the binding it is in. Seen without a location: ETY-ValueRestriction (\"Metavars that are not part of the enclosing function signature are not allowed\", from an unsuffixed literal flowing into conv) and ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith; the agent had to bisect by hand.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,diagnostics,compiler", "current", "current")
inl spiral_record_with_inference () = Task ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", "Record update {r with field = v} on an unannotated parameter of a top-level inl fails with ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith (top-down inference has no row type for it yet); expression bases (calls, local let results) and lambda parameters already work (probes 2026-10-19)", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,inference,records,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_record_with_inference () = Planning ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", Minutes 300u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 1u32, "2026-10-18T13:18Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-18 09:18 main-thread analysis (no change): Infer's RawRecordWith (spiral_compiler.fs ~51041-51159) reads the base expression's type through f', which already visit_t's the fresh var, so the TyMetavar case at the final match is a type that is really still unknown at that point of top-down inference, not a missed substitution; the expected type s is usually a fresh metavar as well (a let binding without annotation), so unifying with s does not help either. A fix needs deferral: record the with-expression as pending, finish the enclosing block, then resolve the keys once the base type is known (as the bottom-up segment does), or fall back to the expected type when it is a known record. Workaround stays: bind the base with an annotation first.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:25Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:25 helper-lane lap 1: narrowed with three repros on the installed single-flight compiler (lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE, try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy): {(origin ()) with x = 3} compiles and runs (exit 7) both when origin's return is annotated and when it is unannotated, so the title's claim (an expression base always fails) no longer holds for calls of known top-level functions. It still fails for a base whose type is a metavar at that point: inl moved r = {r with x = 3i32} gives ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith at 1:15 and then a Unification failure at the use site. So the gap is record-with over an unannotated (row-polymorphic) parameter, which top-down inference cannot key without deferral (note 1's design). Next: find the original failing site to see whether it was this shape or a local function result.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:14Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:14 helper-lane lap 2: two more shapes probed (Invoke-Heavy, try.ps1 C): a local let make (k) = {x; y} then {(make 0) with x = 3} runs (exit 7), and a lambda parameter fun r => {r with x = 3} passed to inl apply f = f {x; y} also runs (exit 7), because the argument's record type reaches the lambda before the with is checked. The only failing shape left is an unannotated parameter of a top-level inl (inl moved r = {r with x = 3}). The original report came with SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS (both errors arrived without a location in eoie work), so its exact shape is not recoverable; retitled the item to the narrowed gap. The workaround stays a parameter annotation (r : point).")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:34 helper-lane lap 3: decision: won't-fix the inference (by design: the top-down segment has no row types, and keying an unannotated parameter's record would need deferred checking), fix the diagnostic instead. Reading spiral_compiler.fs 51113-51167: all three MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith sites already carry range_of_expr (so the error is located, as lap 1 saw at 1:15), but the text (line 50216) says only that record keys need to be fully known. Staged lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/patch.py (one anchor, --check OK): the message now names the cause and the fix (the base of this record update has no known record type yet; an unannotated function parameter cannot be updated; annotate it, e.g. (r : point)). No sample or ExpectedErrors pin uses the old text (0 hits in tmp/ and in the 08:05 baseline results.tsv), so it rides a pack with no residual changes. Close as Done once a pack carries it.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:43Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:43 its pack 7 patch is in the accepted baseline (pack 7 ACCEPTED single-flight-20261019-125623 (7,020 rows, DISAGREE 0, 0 differing cells vs review 124451, C rows unchanged)); the item stays open for its remaining scope.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 6u32, "2026-10-19T21:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:38 helper-lane lap 6: verified on the installed compiler (pack 10). lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/generic_param.spi (inl moved r = {r with x = 3i32}) now fails at 1:15 with the new message: the base has no known record type yet, the top-down segment has no row polymorphism, annotate the base, e.g. (r : point). So the user-facing part is in. The inference gap itself is unchanged: it needs a deferred field constraint on the metavar, a row-like constraint next to CNumber/CFloat that RawRecordWith adds and that the parameter's later unification discharges. That is a design-sized change in Infer, so it is not started here.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 7u32, "2026-10-21T22:29Z", "main", "18:29 next step drafted (a code read of Infer's RawRecordWith, spiral_compiler.fs 51120-51210, a shared section so a single edit): the cheap half of the inference gap needs no row types. When the base is an unknown metavar, the update removes nothing (withouts empty), and the expected type s of the whole expression is already a record or a nominal record (an annotated return type: inl moved r : point = {r with x = 3i32}), a pure update keeps its type, so unify the base with s first and the existing record and nominal paths then work unchanged. A field missing from s still fails as before, and with no expectation (generic_param.spi) the clear pack-7 error stays. Staged as lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/expected-base.ps1 (1 anchor, dry run matches once) plus repros annotated_return.spi (plain record, expect exit 7) and annotated_nominal_return.spi (nominal, expect 7). Gate, fixed now: both repros compile and run to 7 on C, generic_param.spi still gives the pack-7 message, and -Suite all shows DISAGREE 0 with no residual change (no sample uses the shape today). Rides the next pack. The full row-constraint design for fully unannotated bases stays out of scope.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 8u32, "2026-10-22T06:03Z", "main", "02:03 main: expected-base.ps1 is already in the PACK-AFTER-NIGHT core (pack-next carries it: its New text is in the pack core, its Old text still in the tree), so it installs with that pack. Regression sample added to the pack review: samples/record_with_expected_base (one main covers both fixed shapes: a plain record type and a nominal record, each updated through an unannotated parameter with an annotated return; main returns p.x + p.y + x + y - 14 = 0). The installed compiler rejects it at 5:23 and 7:31 with ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith, so the row guards exactly this fix. The lab repros are covered by the sample; the folder drains at install (generic_param.spi's pack-7 message stays as the unannotated-base guard and is noted here).")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 9u32, "2026-10-23T07:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:11 main: lab drain: patch.py (the located message, tree core 50257) and expected-base.ps1 (an unknown base takes the expected record type, tree core 51223, renamed base_type) deleted as landed; no script reads them. The 7 .spi probes stay for the open scope: a deferred row-like field constraint for a fully unannotated parameter (generic_param.spi is its failing shape).")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 10u32, "2026-10-24T06:45Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:45 main: design read for the remaining scope (a fully unannotated base with no expected type; generic_param.spi), tree core lines 51128-51250, no build. Today RawRecordWith takes three paths: an expected record or nominal type unifies the base first (the landed cheap half, 51224-51229); a nominal base types each with-field against the nominal's fields (51230-51240); otherwise tail/tail' need with_base to be a TyRecord, and a TyMetavar base raises MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith (51179, 51203, 51249). Lambda parameters work only because the caller fixes their type before the body is inferred. Options: (A) a field constraint: a pure update ({r with x = v}, no withouts, no retype) unifies the result with the base metavar and records a 'has field x : typeof v' obligation on it, checked when the metavar resolves (unify to TyRecord/nominal runs the normal path) and carried into generalization as a constraint next to {int}/{number} when it stays open, so each specialization re-checks it; sound, and the retype census (all 'same', 0 retyped in the measured chunk) says pure updates are the real use. (B) defer to peval: type the update as returning its base and let partial evaluation check fields per call; cheap, but it moves type errors into peval with worse messages and weakens top-down typing. Recommendation (A), limited to pure updates (modify-updates and withouts keep the error), which the census shows cover practice. First step: find where {int}-style constraints are stored on metavars (TyMetavar's second field) to see if a field-constraint kind fits there.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 11u32, "2026-10-24T06:46Z", "main", "2026-10-24 02:46 main: first step of option (A), read at core 49461-49516. Constraints are a closed union (CUInt, CSInt, CInt, CFloat, CNumber, CPrim, CSymbol, CRecord, CPrototype of GlobalId) held as MVar.constraints : Constraint Set, 'stated up front and static in forall vars'. CRecord already exists, so a pure update on an unknown base can at least add CRecord to the metavar today (tighter than now, never wrong). A per-field obligation can't join that Set: it carries a T, and T holds option ref cells, so it doesn't fit a compared, up-front set. Shape for (A), then: a side table keyed by the MVar (MVar has ReferenceEquality) of pending (range, symbol, field T) obligations, discharged in unify when the metavar resolves to TyRecord or a nominal record (run the normal with-field check), and at generalization either turned into the forall var's checked obligation list (re-checked per specialization, like CPrototype instances) or reported with today's message when no consumer can satisfy it. Size estimate from these milestones: side table + discharge in unify 90 m, generalization carry 120 m, error text and the generic_param.spi sample 30 m, pack review 60 m.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 12u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:32 (subagent): blocked: message patch rides the next compiler pack -> next: after the pack: Done with the new message on the lap 1 repro | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): message patch rides a pack -> next: after the pack: Done | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: message installed; inference gap needs a design -> next: deferred field constraint design")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 13u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 helper patch record folded from the deleted history folder: patch lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/patch.py, state 'message only, 0 pins', proof: lap-1 repro shows the new text")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 14u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 15u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 16u32, "2026-10-31T20:58Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 17u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE folder (7 files: annotated_call.spi, annotated_nominal_return.spi, annotated_return.spi, generic_param.spi, lambda_param.spi, local_let.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 18u32, "2026-11-06T12:43Z", "main", "main 11-06 10:4x re-probed on the installed compiler (A1BF06EB9AD8): ':= bumped r = { r ?| x = 5i32 }' (and the ':~' form) still fails, now with a clear ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith that tells the user to annotate the base (e.g. (r : point)). So the usability half is done (the error names the fix) and the inference half (row polymorphism in the top-down segment) is not. DoD: either the unannotated update type-checks, or the documented rule 'annotate the base' stays with this message, decided by compiler. Next step: hand to compiler as a design question beside RECORD-UPDATE-TYPE-CHANGE.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 19u32, "2026-11-11T01:19Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 COUNTER-EVIDENCE to \"expression bases (calls) already work\": in spiral apps/realworld server.spi (REALWORLD-4-HTTP slice 4, gate ganymede org2-rwc on pack85/86), the top-level definition := jake_posts (path : string) (body : string) : http_request = { as_jake path with method = realworld_api::Post; body } did NOT report any error at its own definition; it was silently dropped, and every use failed with ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: jake_posts (all 12 tests of the module red, the error naming the CALLER created_hi). Binding the call first fixed it: := j = as_jake path then { j with method = realworld_api::Post; body = body } (green in org2-rwc2). Two things for compiler: (1) a record update whose base is an application, in the { base with ... } spelling, is not parsed as such at top level (maybe the parser reads as_jake path with ... as something else and discards the binding); (2) whatever happens, a definition must never vanish silently: the error belongs at the definition, not at its users. The field-punning shorthand (body alone, meaning body = body) may be involved, since the working version spells body = body. Memory spiral-json-list-field-and-record-update-traps records the workaround")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 20u32, "2026-11-11T01:19Z", "org-f33af161", "Handed the open design question to the compiler lane (session compiler, 2026-11-11 ~01:19Z): should a record-with over a fully unannotated row-polymorphic base type-check (needs row-constraint inference in the top-down Infer.RawRecordWith segment), or does the documented annotate-the-base rule + current clear ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith stay? Usability half already done; expected-base cheap fix already landed w/ regression sample. Waiting on compiler verdict.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 21u32, "2026-11-11T01:20Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:2x compiler: RULING (asked by org, item DoD \"decided by compiler\"): the \"annotate the base\" rule STAYS for a fully unannotated, row-polymorphic base with no expected type. Spirals inference is top-down, and row-constraint inference there is a large type-system feature with no current caller that cannot annotate; the current ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith names the fix. The expected-type path (expected-base, samples/record_with_expected_base) covers the common case. SEPARATE FINDING on org2s jake_posts (reproduced on ganymede, installed pack89, samples-core probes): it is a PARSE error, not inference: { origin s with x = 7i32 } fails with \"Expected: }\" at with (the grammar accepts only a simple base), while a punned field ({ p with x = 7i32; y }) builds fine. On pack89 the error IS reported at the definition: the fatal list includes main.spi:5:41: Expected: } next to the callers ETY-UnboundVariable, after two \"warning: ParserErrors ... blocks that fail to parse are left out of their module\" lines. So the fix is grammar: accept an application (or any application-level expression) as the record-update base. Filed RECORD-WITH-APPLICATION-BASE (team:compiler). With that, this items DoD is met: decided, rule stays.")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 22u32, "2026-11-11T01:21Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 correction to note 19 after compiler reproduced it on pack89: it is NOT silent. The application base is a PARSE error (\"Expected: }\" at with; the record-update grammar takes only a simple base), reported at the definition after \"warning: ParserErrors ... left out of their module\"; the punned field is fine. org2 gate scripts grepped only ETY-, error[ and __assert lines, which hid the parse error and made the drop look silent. Filed by compiler: RECORD-WITH-APPLICATION-BASE")
inl note_of_spiral_record_with_inference_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", 23u32, "2026-11-11T01:22Z", "org-f33af161", "DONE with evidence: compiler lane ruled 2026-11-11 ~01:2xZ (note on this item): the \"annotate the base\" rule STAYS for a fully unannotated, row-polymorphic base with no expected type — row-constraint inference in the top-down segment is a large feature with no caller that cannot annotate, and the current ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith names the fix. DoD branch \"or the documented rule stays with this message, decided by compiler\" is met. Usability half shipped (clear error) + the expected-base cheap fix already landed with regression sample record_with_expected_base. Note: compiler filed the separate parser gap { f x with .. } Expected:} as RECORD-WITH-APPLICATION-BASE on their team (not this item).")
inl spiral_macro_field_splice () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", "In $\"...\" macros, !x.field splices x and then emits the literal text .field into the target (the C compiler then fails on a tuple member). Either splice the field access or reject it with a hint to bind the field first.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,macros,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl spiral_unforgeable_witnesses () = Task ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", "Typed-domain gaps: module-private union constructors exist (union private, verified 2026-10-19: no qualified or opened construction outside the module, matching still allowed); still missing a nominal-preserving record update, so {r with field = v} on a nominal keeps the nominal (today: rejected with ExpectedRecord) and a field-type change on a structural record stops being silent", Feature, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "RECORD-UPDATE-TYPE-CHANGE", "spiral,types,gadt,modeling,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses () = Planning ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", Minutes 180u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:27Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:27 helper-lane lap 1: measured today's constructor visibility (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, witness.spi with union proof = | Proof : i32, try.ps1 C via Invoke-Heavy): from another module witness.Proof 7 compiles and runs (exit 7), open witness then Proof 7 also works, and a match on Proof outside the module works (exit 8); only an unqualified Proof without open is unbound. So plain-case unions are forgeable. Correction (lap 2): tibia's negatives fail because readiness.spi declares union private ready group, a privacy modifier the language already has; see note 2.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:16Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:16 helper-lane lap 2: first half of the title already exists: union private makes constructors module-private. Probes (try.ps1 C, Invoke-Heavy, lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/private_*.spi): private_witness.Proof 7 from another module fails with ETY-ModuleIndexFailed (The module does not have the key: Proof), open private_witness then Proof 7 fails with ETY-UnboundVariable, while private_witness.issue 7 (the module's own constructor function) runs (exit 7) and an outside match on Proof still works (exit 8) - exactly the readiness-witness pattern; tibia/src/readiness.spi uses it (union private ready group). GADT syntax alone gives no privacy (gadt_* probes all compile; my lap-1 note claimed otherwise and is corrected). Second half still open, measured: {h with current = 2.5f64} on a structural type hp = {current : i32; max : i32} compiles silently into a new record type (exit 9), while a nominal hp rejects record-with entirely (ETY-ExpectedRecord), so there is no nominal-preserving update yet.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T14:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:38 helper-lane lap 3: drafted the nominal-preserving record update as lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/nominal_with_patch.py (top-down RawRecordWith, spiral_compiler.fs ~51157; anchor --check OK). Rule: when the single base's type is a nominal whose body (after type_apply_split + subst of its vars, the same unwrapping field access uses at 50999) is a record, and there are no withouts, each with field must exist in that record and its value is checked against the field's type (modify: field -> field), and the result type is the nominal itself; a missing field reports RecordIndexFailed at the field, a type change is a unification error at that field. Every other base keeps today's path (structural records still extend or retype silently: that half is a separate decision). The base is now inferred once and shared by both paths. Risk: PartEval's RecordWith may not unwrap a nominal value, which the candidate run shows. Probes: with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps (result passed to a function taking hp) expect exit 9, nominal_type_change expects a compile error, with_type_change (structural) stays exit 9; run.ps1 builds a Debug candidate and runs all four on C and Rust. HEAVY QUEUE row added.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:34Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:34 helper-lane lap 4: closed the risk named in lap 3 before the candidate runs: single-flight's partial evaluation of record-with (spiral_compiler.fs 159778-159833) only accepts a DRecord base and raises Expected a record otherwise, so the top-down change alone would have failed at peval. nominal_with_patch.py now also rewrites that tail: a DNominal(DRecord fields, nominal_ty) base with no nested path is updated field-wise and rewrapped as DNominal with the same nominal type; nested paths and other values keep today's error (anchors --check OK). The gpt5parallel side (157420, a different stack-checked implementation) is not patched: its suite no longer gates accepts, so a nominal record-with there stays an error until that core is ported. The queued run.ps1 picks the change up unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T17:44Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 13:44 main: nominal_with_patch.py taken out of pack 8: the Release build failed with FS0010/FS3118 at the patched Infer code (spiral_compiler.fs ~51180-51191), because a one-line nested match (match visit_t ... with TyRecord fields -> Some fields | _ -> None) swallows the following | None -> None arm; wrap the inner matches in parentheses (or split them into let bindings) and prove it with a Debug candidate build before the next pack. The tree was restored to the accepted pack 7 source (core-src staging 3f21d9014f3e7723).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 6u32, "2026-10-19T20:46Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 16:46 helper-lane: the nominal-preserving record update works on a private-cache Debug candidate (build log lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/build-20261019-1558.log; the earlier build failures were an F# local named base, a keyword, now with_base). Probes (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/probes.ps1 on that DLL): with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps (the result passed to a function taking hp) compile and exit 9 on C and Rust; nominal_type_change ({h with current = 2.5f64} on the nominal) is rejected with a unification failure at 5:37, the field's value; the structural with_type_change still compiles (exit 9), as intended for this slice. nominal_with_patch.py rides main's pack 10.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 7u32, "2026-10-19T21:32Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 17:32 main: pack 10 INSTALLED 17:32 (nominal-with, Zig Assign as a statement, export-types nominal walk): Release candidate review single-flight-20261019-1723xx vs the pack 9 accept 161549 = 0 differing cells of 7,057 (no sample exercises these paths), DISAGREE 0, shared DLLs hash-equal to the candidate; the baseline does not move, so no separate accept run; the features are proven by helper lane 1's probes on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 8u32, "2026-10-19T21:33Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 17:33 helper-lane: pack 10 installed (17:32, 0 differing cells vs pack 9). On the installed compiler (lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/probes.ps1, now parametrized with -Dll/-Tag): with_on_nominal and nominal_keeps exit 9 on C and Rust; nominal_type_change is rejected (unification failure at 5:37); with_type_change, the structural case, still compiles silently (exit 9). The nominal-preserving record update is in. Not Done: the title's last clause, that a field-type change on a structural record stops being silent, is still open. Next: decide whether to make it an error or a warning (it changes the type of every structural {r with ...} that retypes a field, so it needs a corpus count first).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 9u32, "2026-10-21T22:35Z", "main", "18:35 census instrument staged for the open clause (should a field-type change on a structural record update be an error?): lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/retype-census.ps1, 2 anchors in Infer (shared section), dry run matches each once. With SPIRAL_RECORD_WITH_CENSUS_OUT=<file>, every plain {r with field = v} that replaces an existing field queues a row, and at process exit, after inference has resolved the metavars, writes kind (same/retyped), line, column, field, type before and after (show_t). Inert without the variable. The silent replace is Infer's eval at spiral_compiler.fs:51107-51112 (Map.add with x.var, no check against the old field type). Decision rule, fixed before counting: run a Debug candidate with it over lib/spiral (Cells --gate on the lib, or the references-census consumers: apps/spiral, eoie, x, tibia, blog). 0 retyped rows: make the retype an error (a unify of the old field type with x.var). 1-20: warning first, list them, and fix each site. Over 20: keep it silent and document it as structural-record semantics. Runs with the next candidate build (no slot tonight).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 10u32, "2026-10-22T05:24Z", "main", "01:24 main: retype-census.ps1 is already in PACK-AFTER-NIGHT: pack-next carries it (the Infer site at core line 51149 has the census call; the module is at 50150). Its 3-line prose comment is stripped at compose (no-comments rule; the /// ### marker stays). Once the pack installs, the census runs on the installed compiler with SPIRAL_RECORD_WITH_CENSUS_OUT set, with no separate candidate build. The decision rule of note 18:35 applies unchanged (0 retyped: error; 1-20: warning and fix sites; >20: keep silent).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 11u32, "2026-10-22T05:37Z", "main", "01:36 main: lab run outputs moved to the run cache, per the lab rule that run outputs never sit in lab: 2 file(s) (logs, notebooks, diffs, metrics/gate/timing TSVs) are now under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES\\ at the same relative paths, sha256 checked. Scripts and probes stay in lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES until the item lands.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 12u32, "2026-10-22T14:08Z", "main", "2026-10-22 10:08 main: PACK-AFTER-NIGHT is installed (single-flight core 6446A71F2DCD9FA3, 09:30), so the census instrument is live in the installed compiler and is inert until SPIRAL_RECORD_WITH_CENSUS_OUT is set. The census run joins main's post-block queue as step 6, after the Cells pack: Cells --gate over lib/spiral with the variable pointing at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\runs\\lab-outputs\\SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES\\retype-census.tsv. The decision rule of 10-21 18:35 is applied mechanically to the row count: 0 retyped -> error, 1-20 -> warning plus site fixes, >20 -> documented structural semantics.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 13u32, "2026-10-22T22:31Z", "main", "2026-10-22 18:31 main: the 10:08 'step 6' never became a job (no queued script set SPIRAL_RECORD_WITH_CENSUS_OUT). Queued now as job-1792708246151-main-retype-census (compile gate, after main-fiscal-run): scratchpad retype-census-run.ps1 runs tmp/scripts/lib-usage-consumers.ps1 (lib files with test blocks + blog/engine + x sources with tests, SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process) with the census variable set, outputs under runs/lab-outputs/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES. Process mode matters: the census flushes on ProcessExit, which a killed Cells daemon never runs, so the lib_gate default (daemon) would have recorded nothing. Rows carry no path, so the count uses distinct rows. Verdict printed by the 10-21 18:35 rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 14u32, "2026-10-23T02:52Z", "main", "2026-10-22 22:51 main: census partial. job-1792715175709 hit its 90-min cap during the consumer corpus (first chunk 20 files, 167 tests, about 1.5 h in process mode on a loaded box). Rows: 50,762 raw, 112 distinct record updates, all 'same' and 0 retyped (top fields pos_vec 16, velocity 14, position 12, mass 11). The 10-21 rule is not applied yet: it names the whole consumer corpus (lib test files + blog/engine + x), and this covers its first chunk. Next: rerun in a quiet window with no cap, chunk by chunk; or have the instrument append rows per build instead of at ProcessExit, so the daemon mode (much faster) can carry the census. If the rest also has 0 retyped, the rule makes a field-type change on a structural {r with} an error.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 15u32, "2026-10-23T05:54Z", "main", "2026-10-23 01:54 main: the census instrument no longer depends on ProcessExit. pack-next6\\census-per-statement.ps1 (in pack-next4, core 4FBB760BD84BBCBF, parses under both defines): each infer call collects its own rows (census_rows) and appends them, evaluated, when that top-level statement finishes inference, after numeric defaulting, under one append lock. Rows of statements still inferring concurrently are never forced early. Once next4 installs, the census can run in Cells daemon mode (the lib_gate default, much faster than process mode), so retype-census-run.ps1 can drop SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process; the 10-21 18:35 rule stays unchanged. Inert when SPIRAL_RECORD_WITH_CENSUS_OUT is unset (an empty list per statement).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 16u32, "2026-10-23T07:11Z", "main", "2026-10-23 03:11 main: lab drain: nominal_with_patch.py (pack 10, with_base in the tree core) and retype-census.ps1 (PACK-AFTER-NIGHT, module RecordWithRetypeCensus in the tree core) deleted as landed, with run.ps1 and build-errors.ps1, which only built a candidate with the landed patch. Their only callers were scratchpad scripts of packs that already installed. Kept: probes.ps1 and its 4 nominal/with probes for the open clause (the structural retype decision, census pending), and the privacy probes (private_*, gadt_*, witness, qualified, opened, matched, annotated) whose findings are notes 1-2; those go when the item closes.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 17u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): patch goes to main's pack 8 -> next: read pack 8's nominal probes | lap 6 14:36 (subagent): pack 8 bisect: still build-fails after the one-line fix; dropped from 8b -> next: get the build error (rebuild with output kept) and fix | lap 6 16:46 (subagent): nominal record-with builds (base keyword fixed) and 8/8 probes behave as predicted on the candidate; in pack 10 -> next: after pack 10's accept: Done for the nominal half; structural retype decision remains | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: nominal half installed; structural retype still silent -> next: corpus count of retyping updates, then error vs warning")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 18u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 helper patch record folded from the deleted history folder: patch lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/nominal_with_patch.py, state 'candidate queued (run.ps1)', proof: with_on_nominal / nominal_keeps exit 9")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_19 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 19u32, "2026-10-28T01:32Z", "main", "10-27 21:32 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_20 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 20u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_21 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 21u32, "2026-10-31T20:54Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_22 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 22u32, "2026-11-01T04:51Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES folder (19 files: annotated.spi, gadt_issued.spi, gadt_opened.spi, gadt_qualified.spi, gadt_witness.spi, matched.spi) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_23 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 23u32, "2026-11-06T12:18Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:5x PROBED with the installed compiler (A1BF06EB9AD8): (1) nominal-preserving record update WORKS now: `:! point = { x : i32; y : i32 }`, `:= moved (p : point) : point = { p ?| x = 5i32 }` compiles and the Rust output carries 5i32, so the ExpectedRecord rejection is gone. (2) Still open: a field-type change on a structural record is SILENT. `:= changed (r : { x : i32; y : i32 }) = { r ?| x = \"text\" }` compiles without a diagnostic. The remaining 20% is that diagnostic (a compiler type-check change). Next step: hand (2) to compiler as a typed check (record update may not change a field's type unless written as a new record) with these two probes as test samples.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_24 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 24u32, "2026-11-06T12:19Z", "main", "DoD: (1) module-private union constructors (done 10-19); (2) nominal-preserving record update (proven 11-06); (3) a typed diagnostic when a record update changes a field's type, with the two probe packages as compiler test samples. Next step: compiler's answer on (3).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_25 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 25u32, "2026-11-06T15:37Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:3x DoD MET ON THE BOX (pack55d core 257DB91886B97811, installed 11:2x, shipped to the node 11:33): (3) proof: the structural probe ':= changed (r : { x : i32; y : i32 }) = { r ?| x = \"text\" }' now fails with ETY-RecordUpdateRetypesField 'changes the field x from i32 to string ... may change type arguments but not its type constructor; build a new record instead'; (2) the nominal probe ':= moved (p : point) : point = { p ?| x = 5i32 }' still compiles; (1) module-private constructors since 10-19. Closes when the dep RECORD-UPDATE-TYPE-CHANGE closes (compiler keeps it open until the user's spiral push carries the core).")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_26 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 26u32, "2026-11-10T04:34Z", "compiler", "11-10 01:2x compiler (help pool): CLOSING per this items own condition (\"closes when the dep RECORD-UPDATE-TYPE-CHANGE closes\"): that dep is Done (100%), and the diagnostic is in spiral HEAD (git grep RecordUpdateRetypesField spiral_compiler.fs: 3 hits). DoD (1) module-private union constructors (10-19), (2) nominal-preserving record update, (3) ETY-RecordUpdateRetypesField on a field-type change, all proven in the 11-06 15:37Z note (pack55d core 257DB918; the structural probe fails with the typed diagnostic, the nominal probe compiles). pack78, fleet-wide since 11-09, contains it.")
inl note_of_spiral_unforgeable_witnesses_27 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", 27u32, "2026-11-10T04:34Z", "compiler", "Evidence: note of 2026-11-06T15:37Z (probes on pack55d core 257DB918: ETY-RecordUpdateRetypesField for the structural update, the nominal update compiles; module-private constructors since 10-19); dep RECORD-UPDATE-TYPE-CHANGE Done; diagnostic present in spiral HEAD and in pack78 on all four nodes.")
inl spiral_literal_defaults () = Task ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", "Unsuffixed integer literals summed into a local leave a numeric metavar that later breaks conv (value restriction). Check whether per-package default-int (pack-1k) closes it; if not, default literals in local bindings or point the error at the literal.", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "spiral,inference,literals", "current", "current")
inl gpu_rental_readiness () = Task ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", "Run WGSL lane workloads on rented GPUs at scale: a headless GPU runner outside Windows (Linux, Vulkan/Dawn or wgpu), many-launch batching and multi-GPU dispatch, u64/i64 emulation for integer overflow budgets on WGSL, a storage boundary designed once over host arrays and GPU storage buffers (prototype instances per backend), a throughput-per-dollar benchmark, and a reproducible remote setup script; budget tranches need the user's approval.", Epic, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "gpu,wgsl,infra,backend,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_gpu_rental_readiness () = Planning ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", Minutes 1410u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_1 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:41 cost split, from public 2026 prices (rough, check live pages): game rollouts are CPU work (OpenAI Five: ~128k preemptible CPU cores for simulation, 256 GPUs only for inference and optimization; a Dota 2 server is headless Linux, no GPU), so a rollout box is a CPU rental: Hetzner AX42 dedicated ~EUR 68/month after the April 2026 increase (~EUR 0.09/h), CCX33 8 dedicated vCPU ~EUR 138/month after the June 2026 increase. GPU for training: RTX 4090 on vast.ai ~USD 0.15-0.50/h (median ~0.35-0.50), L4 ~0.39/h, A100 80GB 1.19-1.89/h, H100 1.99-3.89/h on RunPod. A small policy net trains on one 4090 or even CPU; H100-class only for large models. Plan: CPU boxes for rollouts plus a short GPU rental for optimization, not GPU machines for the game.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_2 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 2u32, "2026-10-19T12:28Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:28 helper-lane lap 1: readiness audit of the existing runner (spiral-wgsl, apps/compiler/tmp/tests/native-shims/wgsl-run, wgpu 30 + pollster, nightly-2026-10-18): no Windows-specific code; Instance::new(InstanceDescriptor::new_without_display_handle_from_env) plus default adapter then fallback adapter, so on headless Linux it picks Vulkan (WGPU_BACKEND env can force it) - item 1 (headless runner outside Windows) needs only a Linux build, no code. Batching already exists: SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES runs N lanes in chunks (dispatch_workgroups(chunk/64), per-lane heap slice, per-lane i32 results), SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING(_LOG) records timings, adapter info is read. Missing for rental: multi-GPU (one adapter only; would need enumerate_adapters + a lane split across devices), u64/i64 emulation (WGSL backend still has no i64), and a remote setup script. So the first rental tranche can measure throughput-per-dollar with lanes on one GPU unchanged.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_3 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 3u32, "2026-10-19T13:17Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:17 helper-lane lap 2: drafted the reproducible remote setup as text only (nothing rented or run): lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS/setup.sh for an Ubuntu GPU box - apt build-essential, libvulkan1, mesa-vulkan-drivers, vulkan-tools (an NVIDIA rental ships its own ICD), rustup minimal (the runner's rust-toolchain.toml pins nightly-2026-10-18), shallow clone of i574n/spiral, cargo build --release of tests/native-shims/wgsl-run, vulkaninfo summary, then a lanes sweep (1, 1024, 65536, 1048576) over samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes/main.wgsl with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING, printing wall ms per lane count - the throughput-per-dollar input. Budget tranches still need the user's approval before any rental.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_4 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 4u32, "2026-10-19T14:40Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 10:40 helper-lane lap 3: wrote the laptop baseline as lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS/baseline.ps1, the Windows twin of setup.sh's sweep: the installed runner (spiral-bin/toolchains/wgsl-run/release/spiral-wgsl.exe, built 07:23 today, so no build step), the same shader (samples/wgsl_parallel_lanes/main.wgsl) and lane counts (1, 1024, 65536, 1048576) with SPIRAL_WGSL_TIMING, inside Invoke-Heavy, writing host/lanes/ms/exit rows to a baseline-<stamp>.tsv in the lab folder. That tsv is the denominator for the per-dollar table: a rental row only counts if it beats the laptop at the same lane count. GPU use is the main thread's, so HEAVY QUEUE (about 2 min). Rental tranches still need the user's budget approval.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_5 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 5u32, "2026-10-22T16:50Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 12:50 Moved from tibia docs/PLAN.md (TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE, rule DC1), rental safety rules not yet held here: the prepaid provider balance is the hard spending cap; API keys live in environment variables or the Windows credential store, never in a repo; every rental gets a wall-clock limit, a watcher and auto-destroy on idle; the first rented hour checks that the host exposes Vulkan to the container (wgpu needs it).")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_6 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 6u32, "2026-10-22T17:00Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-22 13:00 Moved from tibia docs/PRIOR_ART.md (rule DC1): provider comparison source, RunPod vs Vast.ai 2026, spheron.network/blog/runpod-vs-vastai-2026.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_7 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 7u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 4 11:34 (subagent): blocked: laptop baseline.ps1 queued (GPU use is main's) -> next: read the baseline tsv; then the per-dollar table header | lap 5 12:19 (subagent): baseline.ps1 queued (GPU is main's) -> next: read the baseline tsv | lap 6 18:26 (subagent): baseline.ps1 not run: machine at 68 pct load (main's probe queue + a Cells run), so GPU timings would be noise -> next: idle window | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: baseline.ps1 waits for an idle GPU and CPU -> next: idle window")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_8 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 8u32, "2026-10-25T14:53Z", "main", "10-25 10:53 main: user question 10-25 (a second, identical laptop under remote control). Answer: about 800/1000 useful, since CPU is the bottleneck. Today: the 5l' review waited hours for a heavy slot, three lib land chains timed out waiting, and HOPACSOTA-P2 sat for almost 3 days because it needs a quiet box. Identical hardware makes results transfer (Release builds are byte-reproducible across caches), and the laptop is the cheapest rehearsal for rented boxes. Below 1000 because eoie has no remote dispatch yet (jobs, slots and windows are local), and rented machines are mostly Linux: put Linux on the laptop and it's ~850, since it also tests CI parity natively. Setup: OpenSSH Server, key-only, plus PowerShell 7 remoting over SSH for the agents; Tailscale or WireGuard, no exposed RDP or WinRM; git push/pull for code, caches built remotely, candidates verified by hash or MVID; never-sleep plan, deferred updates, supervisor restart at boot. RustDesk is fine for the user's GUI use, but has no scriptable channel for agents. The user's part is the one-time setup (accounts, enabling sshd, the authorized key, joining Tailscale). Implied eoie work: a remote executor for job start (same queue, slot and timeout rules over SSH), with EOIE-MACHINE-HEALTH covering both machines.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_9 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 9u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'GPU-RENTAL-READINESS\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_10 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/GPU-RENTAL-READINESS folder (2 files: baseline.ps1, setup.sh) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'GPU-RENTAL-READINESS/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_11 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 11u32, "2026-11-07T22:12Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: a headless Linux GPU runner (wgpu/Dawn) runs one WGSL lane workload with batching and u64 emulation on a rented GPU within the user's $20 cap, with a cost-per-result number recorded.")
inl note_of_gpu_rental_readiness_12 () = Note ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", 12u32, "2026-11-09T18:02Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: EPIC (1410m, D6), mis-tagged light. The readiness AUDIT is done (laps 1-3: the spiral-wgsl runner is headless-Linux-ready with only a build, batching via SPIRAL_WGSL_LANES exists, setup.sh + baseline.ps1 drafted; missing = multi-GPU enumerate_adapters, WGSL u64/i64 emulation, a remote setup script). But the title + every lap say 'budget tranches need the USER's approval before any rental' - so the scale-out cannot proceed without the user funding a GPU rental. Re-tagged load=heavy; parking on user (budget approval for the first rental tranche).")
inl cells_repin () = Task ("CELLS-REPIN", "A failing _assert_eq reports actual and expected as structured data in the run summary, and one call rewrites the literal in the source to the actual value (per test, with a diff to confirm). Today agents scrape the notebook output with regexes and edit long tuple literals by hand after every intended model change.", Feature, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-ACCEPT-TUPLES", "cells,testing,one-call", "current", "current")
inl cells_gate () = Task ("CELLS-GATE", "One-call gate over a list of files: memo off, per-file and total pass/fail/executed/memo-hit summary, failed test names with actual/expected, daemon retired at the end, deferral while GUI or live runs hold the machine (see EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS); multiple --test names or a pattern in one run.", Feature, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "cells,testing,gate,one-call", "current", "current")
inl cells_daemon_lifecycle () = Task ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "Compiler daemons started by a run outlive it (about 2 GB each with their --server-tcp compilers) until someone sends retire by hand; retire on run end or after an idle timeout, and list live daemons with their owner run.", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,daemon,memory", "current", "current")
inl cells_probes () = Task ("CELLS-PROBES", "First-class probe runs: compile and run a small main against a package's modules, optionally with text-patch variants of those modules, without copying the package into a scratch folder and without a test block; print the program output. Today experiments use a hand-made scratch project with copied sources and a variant script.", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,probes,experiments,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_probes () = Planning ("CELLS-PROBES", Minutes 60u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl cells_explicit_targets () = Task ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", "Untargeted test blocks silently follow the global default backend, which changed (to zig); require a target per test block or a declared package default, and warn on untargeted blocks.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,targets,testing", "current", "current")
inl cells_new_project () = Task ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", "Project scaffold so a new app starts smooth: package.spiproj with package defaults, module and test-file layout, target conventions with reasons (C as oracle, WGSL for GPU, Lua for embedded hosts, Rust for host-only tests), standards checks and a gate command wired in, a scratch-probe setup, a backlog epic with a starter plan. Lessons from the first full project on test cells.", Feature, P1, D3, 95u32, Paused, "PS1-TO-SPIRAL", "cells,scaffold,onboarding,main,team:org,team:microblog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_new_project () = Planning ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", Minutes 270u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_new_project_1 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 1u32, "2026-10-18T05:41Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 01:41 base for the next project repo (e.g. a possible Dota 2 one), from comparing x and tibia: binaries follow x (started 2026-10-15): apps/<app>/<crate>/Cargo.toml with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, a Cargo workspace, scripts/gate.ps1 failing any crate whose generated Rust exceeds 1000 lines, --x-compat check, e2e against the built exe. Tests and docs follow tibia: src/ package with targeted test blocks (C oracle, WGSL, Lua, Rust only with a reason), scripts/build.ps1 through cells failing unless every row executed and passed, docs/, harness/, results/, gh-pages CI on Ubuntu + Windows with actions.lock. The scaffold should generate this union, so a new repo starts from it instead of copying either.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_2 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 2u32, "2026-10-18T15:49Z", "tibia-lane", "2026-10-18 11:49 tibia-lane: working prototype at tibia lab/cells-new-project/new-project.ps1 -Path <empty dir>. It generates a project from tibia's own files with explicit text rewrites (it fails naming the file when a rewrite target drifts): .gitignore, .gitattributes, .editorconfig, scripts/init|build|publish|workflow.ps1, gh-pages.yml with a single build step, actions.lock, plus src/package.spiproj and src/units.spi (phantom-unit amounts with a C oracle test, a C 'fails Unification failure' type test, a WGSL twin and a Lua host test). Proof: generated into a scratch dir with polyglot/spiral junctions beside it; its own init.ps1 -fast passed (7 s) and build.ps1 went green with 4/4 executed and passed, plus the ci-metrics files and the WGSL row (688 bytes, 111 ms compile, 172 ms run on the local GPU). Shared pieces moved into spiral so generated projects do not copy them: scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (already there) and the new scripts/init-wgsl-run.ps1 (wgsl runner build, DXC beside it on Windows, lavapipe in HKLM on Windows CI, mesa-vulkan-drivers on Linux). Left: the deps CELLS-GATE/PROBES/REPIN/EXPLICIT-TARGETS and EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS, plus a home for the generator (cells mix task or eoie verb; it stays in the lab until then), x's Cargo binary layout and the 1000-line gate.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_3 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 3u32, "2026-10-19T12:31Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-19 08:31 lap 1 grain: deps state: CELLS-GATE 50, CELLS-REPIN 0, CELLS-PROBES 0, CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS 95, EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS (eoie lane); the tibia lane's new-project.ps1 prototype exists (tibia lab/cells-new-project); scaffold should also emit the .cargo/config.toml Linux rustix cfg if the project pulls NEAR deps (lesson of today) Next: after CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS closes, run the prototype into an empty lab dir and gate the generated project")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_4 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 4u32, "2026-10-19T13:09Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:09 lap 2 grain: CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS is Done (helper lane, 08:28), so the scaffold trial is unblocked. Verified the tibia lane's prototype (tibia lab/cells-new-project/new-project.ps1, read only) still applies today: all 4 rewrite anchors match the current tibia scripts/build.ps1, scripts/workflow.ps1 and .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml, and the 6 copied files exist. It emits src/package.spiproj (packages |core-, module units) and units.spi with 4 targeted tests: c oracle, c expected failure (fails Unification failure), wgsl, lua. No Cargo project, so lap 1's .cargo rustix cfg does not apply to this scaffold (only to one that adds Rust/NEAR deps). Still missing against the item: backlog epic and starter plan, standards checks, scratch-probe setup (waits on CELLS-PROBES). HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/scaffold-gate.ps1 (scaffold into spiral-bin/runs/cells-new-project/<stamp>, then the Cells gate on src/units.spi, memo off, ~4 min). Next: after the gate passes, port new-project.ps1 into spiral as the scaffold home (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT), parameterized by a project name instead of tibia's rewrites")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_5 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 5u32, "2026-10-19T13:50Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 09:50 lap 3 grain, home decided: the scaffold becomes a Cells mix task, mix spiral.new <path> [--name <project>], not a ported pwsh script. Reasons: PWSH-FREE (new tooling should not add pwsh), Cells already owns the gate the scaffold wires in (mix spiral.notebook --gate) and the targets list (mix spiral.targets), and its siblings are mix tasks (compiler_daemon, edit, html, notebook, render_cells, targets, x_compat). Templates live as files under apps/cells/priv/new_project/ (gitignore, gitattributes, editorconfig, src/package.spiproj, src/<name>.spi with the units starter: c oracle test, c expected-failure test, wgsl and lua tests), copied with the project name substituted; the CI workflow and the pwsh build/publish scripts stay out of v1, since they are tibia-specific (GPU legs, arena) and the gate command replaces build.ps1 for a pure Spiral project. Kept from the tibia prototype: refuse a non-empty target dir, print every written file. Next: draft lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex + the priv templates as a lab patch with an ExUnit case (scaffold into a tmp dir, files present, name substituted), queue its mix test with the existing scaffold gate")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_6 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 6u32, "2026-10-19T14:14Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 10:14 drive slice: mix spiral.new is written, as new files only, in lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/ (mirrors apps/cells): lib/spiral/cells/new_project.ex (Spiral.Cells.NewProject.create(path, name) -> {:ok, files} | {:error, {:invalid_name, n} | {:not_empty, path}}, refusal_text/1; templates copied with PROJECT_NAME substituted in names and text, dotfiles stored undotted), lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex (mix spiral.new <path> [--name], prints each file and the gate command), priv/new_project/ (gitignore, gitattributes, editorconfig taken from tibia's, src/package.spiproj, src/PROJECT_NAME.spi = the units starter: static prelude, c oracle test, c expected-failure test, wgsl and lua tests) and test/spiral/cells/new_project_test.exs (2 cases: files and substitution; non-empty dir and bad name refused, dir untouched). Checked without mix: all 3 .ex/.exs parse, and new_project.ex loaded standalone (elixir, one boot) creates ledger with the 5 files, package.spiproj = packages |core- / modules ledger, first line test ledger static =, refuses the now non-empty dir and the name Ledger. HEAVY QUEUE: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/spiral-new-run.ps1 copies the files in (refuses to overwrite), runs the 2 tests, scaffolds into spiral-bin/runs/spiral-new/<stamp>/ledger, gates its ledger.spi, and removes the added files unless all three pass (~6 min). Next: after it passes, the backlog-epic and standards-check parts (deps CELLS-GATE, EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS)")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_7 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 7u32, "2026-10-19T15:07Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:07 both 10:56 runs failed for one reason: the scaffold sits in a cache dir with no spiral/lib/spiral ancestor, so Cells mounted no lib and open testing was ETY-UnboundModule (scaffold-gate only looked passed because it never checked the gate exit). Product fix in the tree: notebook.ex find_spiral_lib falls back to SPIRAL_LIB when no layout lib is found (spiral_lib_from_env/1, layout still wins), new case in notebook_spiral_lib_test.exs, 3 passed in a bounded job 11:05. Both runners set SPIRAL_LIB, scaffold-gate checks the exit code; both re-queued. Edit in apps/cells notebook.ex (helper lane 1 FYI).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_8 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 8u32, "2026-10-19T15:31Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:31 lap 6 grain, the gate command wired in (one part of this item's list): the scaffold now writes scripts/gate.ps1 (template lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project/scripts/gate.ps1): it finds spiral beside the project or through SPIRAL_ROOT, sets SPIRAL_LIB from it when unset (so a project anywhere gets lib, the 10:56 failure), and runs mix spiral.notebook --gate over every src/*.spi (or the -Path list) from Cells, passing the gate's exit code through. new_project_test expects the new file and checks it names spiral.notebook. spiral-new-run.ps1 now gates the scaffold through the generated script with only SPIRAL_ROOT set (SPIRAL_LIB cleared), so the queued run proves the out-of-tree path end to end. Parse-checked. Left: scratch-probe setup, the backlog epic with a starter plan, standards checks (EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_9 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 9u32, "2026-10-19T15:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 11:48 lap 7 grain, the backlog-epic part: mix spiral.new now ends with the two next steps. The gate line is pwsh <path>/scripts/gate.ps1 (the generated script, replacing the raw mix line). The epic line is the exact eoie call for a starter epic, from NewProject.epic_command(name): eoie agile add <backlog root> <NAME with dashes> Epic P2 D5, title '<name>: starter plan (units module, c/wgsl/lua targets, scripts/gate.ps1 green)', tags <name>. It prints the command and does not run it: Cells does not call eoie, and the backlog root is the user's choice (eoie-first: one call, copy-paste ready). The ExUnit case checks ledger_v2 -> LEDGER-V2. All 3 patched files parse (Code.string_to_quoted!). The queued spiral-new-run covers it. Left: the scratch-probe part, after CELLS-PROBES --probe exists.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_10 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 10u32, "2026-10-19T16:24Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:24 lap 8 grain: post-switch check of the scaffold templates (lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/priv/new_project): PROJECT_NAME.spi uses nominals, i32 arithmetic and testing only, no array literals or am. calls the core v1 switch rewrote; open testing and _assert_eq still resolve (lib/spiral/testing.spi:82) and the package line |core- matches lib's spiproj. So the queued spiral-new-run stays valid after the switch. Next: that run after main's go")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_11 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 11u32, "2026-10-19T16:52Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 12:52 lap 9 grain: the scratch-probe part of the scaffold is drafted as lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/pending/scripts/probe.ps1: it resolves spiral and SPIRAL_LIB exactly like the generated gate.ps1 and runs mix spiral.notebook --probe <main.spi> --target <c by default> [--patch <spec.json>] from Cells, passing the exit code through (parses clean). It stays outside patch/priv/new_project for now, because the scaffold copies every file there and the script would call a flag that does not exist until CELLS-PROBES lands --probe; then it moves in, new_project_test's exact file list gains scripts/probe.ps1, and spiral.new prints a probe line next to the gate line")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_12 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 12u32, "2026-10-19T17:28Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:28 lap 10 grain: found and fixed a restore leak in lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/spiral-new-run.ps1: on failure it removed the placed files but kept the directories it created, so the 10:56 run left an empty untracked apps/cells/priv/new_project/src (no file of the patch is in the tree, all 9 files tree=no). The script now records each directory it creates and removes it on failure if empty (deepest first); parse check 0 errors; the two empty leftover directories were removed (no files inside, untracked). Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1 row; probe.ps1 moves into patch/priv once CELLS-PROBES --probe lands")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_13 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 13u32, "2026-10-19T17:48Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 13:48 lap 11 grain: cross-checked the staged ExUnit test against the scaffold payload: new_project_test.exs names all 6 priv/new_project files in their scaffolded form (.editorconfig, .gitattributes, .gitignore from the dotless sources, scripts/gate.ps1, src/package.spiproj, src/ledger.spi from PROJECT_NAME.spi), plus the busy-target and existing-dir cases, so adding probe.ps1 later means one more entry (scripts/probe.ps1) in that list. Next: queued spiral-new-run.ps1 (now leak-free), then probe.ps1 into patch/priv after CELLS-PROBES --probe")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_14 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 14u32, "2026-10-19T18:12Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 14:12 lap 12: code reading found that spiral.new on a path that is an existing file crashed in mkdir_p instead of refusing; the lab patch now refuses with {:is_file, path} (own refusal text), new_project_test.exs asserts it, and a standalone elixir check shows the refusal with the file untouched. Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_15 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 15u32, "2026-10-19T22:54Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 18:54 lap 19 grain: spiral-new-run.ps1 placed the 9 patch files into apps/cells before its try block, so a copy that failed halfway would have left the earlier files behind; the copy now runs inside the try and the existing cleanup removes every placed file and created directory on any failure. Parse clean; none of the 9 files exists in apps/cells yet (the add-only precheck would pass). Next: the queued spiral-new-run.ps1 (main runs it, helper lane 1 told since it adds files to apps/cells)")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_16 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 16u32, "2026-10-19T23:44Z", "helper-lane-2", "2026-10-19 19:44 lap 22 grain: the scaffold gains scripts/probe.ps1 (lab patch/priv/new_project/scripts/probe.ps1: -Main <file> [-Target c,zig], same Cells and SPIRAL_LIB resolution and heavy-gate wrapping as gate.ps1, runs mix spiral.notebook --gate --probe <main> --target ...), now that CELLS-PROBES' probe.py implements --probe; new_project_test.exs lists it (7 files) and checks it names --probe. Standalone check with plain elixir (the lab new_project.ex against the lab priv) creates the 7 files in order. Order note for main: probe.ps1 needs probe.py applied first; spiral-new-run.ps1 itself only runs gate.ps1, so the two lab patches can land in either order")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_17 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 17u32, "2026-10-21T11:17Z", "main", "2026-10-21 07:17 main: tibia's lab/cells-new-project/new-project.ps1 moved into spiral/lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/new-project.ps1 (copied byte for byte; tibia empties its folder).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_18 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 18u32, "2026-10-24T10:56Z", "main", "2026-10-24 06:56 main: check whether apps/cells/priv/new_project is drift (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_19 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 19u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap drive3 10:14 (subagent2): mix spiral.new written as lab patch (module, task, priv templates, 2 ExUnit cases); standalone load check creates/refuses correctly; runner queued -> next: after the run: backlog epic + standards checks parts | lap 5 11:07 (subagent2): root cause of both 10:56 failures: cache-dir scaffold has no spiral layout ancestor so no lib mounted; SPIRAL_LIB fallback in notebook.ex (3 tests passed 11:05), runners set it, scaffold-gate checks exit; both re-queued -> next: after the runs: epic + standards checks parts | lap 13 14:38 (subagent2): kill-safety review of spiral-new-run.ps1: it only ADDS files to apps/cells (refuses if any exists), so a job-timeout kill that skips finally leaves new untracked files and the next run stops loudly with already exists, never overwrites; pending probe.ps1 matches the --probe/--target/--patch shape CELLS-PROBES stages -> next: queued spiral-new-run.ps1; probe.ps1 after CELLS-PROBES lands | lap 14 15:03 (subagent2): waiting: spiral-new-run.ps1 queued (covers the is_file refusal); deps CELLS-PROBES/CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS/EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS open -> next: main's queue run | lap 17 16:43 (subagent2): unchanged (spiral-new-run queued) -> next: main's queue | lap 22-drive 19:44 (subagent2): scaffold scripts/probe.ps1 added (uses --probe); test list updated to 7 files; standalone elixir check creates all 7 -> next: queued spiral-new-run.ps1 (main), after probe.py for probe.ps1 to work")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_20 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 20u32, "2026-10-27T08:54Z", "main", "10-27 04:54 main: 10-27 04:54 main groom: fresh status (stale since the 10-19 laps). The scaffold task is IN THE TREE: apps/cells/lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex (28 lines, 10-19), identical in size to the lab draft lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT/patch/lib/mix/tasks/spiral.new.ex, so the patch has landed and that lab copy is now a duplicate. What the item still lacks per its title is the scaffold CONTENT (package.spiproj defaults, module and test-file layout, target conventions); a 28-line task is the entry point, not the template. Next: diff the lab patch tree against apps/cells and drain whatever landed (lab rule: land first, then remove).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_21 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 21u32, "2026-10-27T13:00Z", "main", "10-27 09:00 main: lab/CELLS-NEW-PROJECT is fully drained and removed (untracked, so no git change). The last 6 files are archived in a hash-verified zip (main-archive 20261027-0822-lab-drained, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT-rest.zip). They were the 10-19 proof runners for the scaffold that landed that day (new-project.ps1, scaffold-gate.ps1, spiral-lib-env-check.ps1, spiral-new-run.ps1, standalone-check.exs; the last loads the lab patch file, removed earlier, so it no longer runs) and pending/scripts/probe.ps1, superseded by the tree's priv/new_project/scripts/probe.ps1 (10-19 19:43 vs the lab's 12:52, 16+/8- newer in the tree). By-stem search: references only inside the folder itself (the positive control) and in backlog notes. Earlier the same session: the 9 identical patch files and the pre-respell PROJECT_NAME.spi. Spiral lab folders: one fewer. What CELLS-NEW-PROJECT still owes, per its title, is the scaffold CONTENT decisions (n20), not lab files.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_22 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 22u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030), CORRECTED from the tree at 19:46 (the first version of this note decided from notes, not the tree): the scaffold content EXISTS and is ahead of the moved examples: apps/cells/priv/new_project has src/package.spiproj, a units starter src/PROJECT_NAME.spi with self-checking test blocks on c, wgsl and lua (plus a 'fails Unification failure' negative), scripts/gate.ps1 and probe.ps1 over mix spiral.notebook --gate, and editorconfig/gitattributes/gitignore. Real gaps found: (a) priv editorconfig drifted from the org render (pins' editorconfig adds ex,exs,gleam to the 2-space group); (b) gate.ps1/probe.ps1 wrap the run in Invoke-Heavy (two box-wide Global\\spiral-heavy-job mutexes), the pre-10-30 regime where correctness gates take exclusive slots. Next step, owner main: the consumer-path proof (mix spiral.new into a scratch dir + its own gate.ps1) is running; then an anchored draft for (a) and, if the proof is green, (b).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_23 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 23u32, "2026-10-31T00:01Z", "main", "10-30 20:01 main: consumer-path proof RUN (mix spiral.new into %TEMP%\\scaffold-proof\\demo_units, then its own scripts/gate.ps1 -InsideHeavySlot; box at 80-100% CPU): spiral.new wrote all 7 files and printed the gate and epic lines; gate: 4 discovered, 3 passed (capped_healing_on_the_oracle [c], the 'fails Unification failure' negative [c], capped_healing_in_an_embedded_host [lua]), 1 FAILED: capped_healing_on_gpu [wgsl] at 392,934 ms against the 300,000 ms timeout (a timeout, not a wrong value). Likely a first-run GPU-runner build under load (load-red rule: rerun alone before judging). Also seen: spiral.new's epic line still says 'eoie agile add' (kit rename leftover in new_project.ex). Next: rerun the gate alone on a quieter box; fix the epic line's 'eoie' -> 'kit' in the next Cells window. (a) editorconfig drift LANDED in Cells window 4 (19:47-19:5x).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_24 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 24u32, "2026-10-31T00:07Z", "main", "10-30 20:07 main: gap (b) DECIDED from the tree: KEEP the scaffold's Invoke-Heavy wrapper. heavy.ps1 is a memory guard as well as a slot scheduler: the second of its two box-wide slots opens only above 3,072 MB free (heavy.ps1:4, :104), and the box ran at about 4.2 GB free today. The 10-30 'correctness runs share the box' policy is about CPU, and dropping the wrapper would remove the OOM guard for a new project's first gate. -InsideHeavySlot stays the opt-out. Left: (c) rerun the wgsl row alone; (d) the epic line 'eoie' -> 'kit' (apply6-kit-epic.ps1, dry run 2/2, for the next Cells window).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_25 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 25u32, "2026-10-31T04:43Z", "main", "10-31 00:43 main: FINDING. apps/cells/priv/new_project/ (the 7 scaffold templates mix spiral.new copies: editorconfig, gitattributes, gitignore, scripts/gate.ps1, scripts/probe.ps1, src/package.spiproj, src/PROJECT_NAME.spi) has NEVER been tracked by git: git ls-files apps/cells/priv lists only tlds.txt, and the user's 10-30 22:33 commit didn't include it (it shows as ??, not ignored). From a fresh clone, mix spiral.new has no templates. Listed first in the commit guide (C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\commit-suggestion.md) for the user's commit.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_26 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 26u32, "2026-10-31T14:46Z", "main", "10-31 10:45 main: (c) DONE: the scaffold gate rerun alone (demo_units from the 10-30 proof, SPIRAL_ROOT=C:\\home\\git\\spiral, through its own Invoke-Heavy wrapper: slot 2 after 4 s) passed 4/4 in 412 s; the wgsl row's slowest test took 170,752 ms against the 300,000 ms timeout (headroom 0.57), so the 10-30 timeout at 392,934 ms was a load red. The one failed_target is the scaffold's deliberate negative test. Left: (d) the epic line 'eoie' -> 'kit' (rides Cells window 6) and the user's commit of apps/cells/priv/new_project/ (never tracked).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_27 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 27u32, "2026-10-31T17:34Z", "main", "10-31 13:27 main: (d) DONE: spiral.new's epic line now says 'kit agile add' (new_project.ex 7E3AAB39B7DE, window 6). Left: the user's commit of apps/cells/priv/new_project/ (never tracked).")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_28 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 28u32, "2026-10-31T22:14Z", "main", "10-31 18:14 main: the suggestion to COMMIT apps/cells/priv/new_project/ is WITHDRAWN (user 10-31 18:1x: 'why commit priv, aint this drift?'). Those 7 untracked templates include scripts/gate.ps1 and scripts/probe.ps1, so committing them would ship PowerShell into every new project, against spiral-first-tooling. The scaffold's files should be generated by a Spiral command (and that rides CELLS-IN-SPIRAL, x's port of Cells to Spiral) instead of copied from .ps1 templates; until then the templates stay untracked and nobody builds on them.")
inl note_of_cells_new_project_29 () = Note ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", 29u32, "2026-11-01T03:44Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom: Active -> Planned. the remaining step is a Spiral scaffold command instead of the .ps1 templates (now apps/cells/templates/new_project, uncommitted); resumes under PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
