inl note_of_float_text_canonical_31 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 31u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 50%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_32 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 32u32, "2026-10-31T03:21Z", "main", "10-30 23:21 main: lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL is ARCHIVED (user, 10-30 22:5x: the lab dir didn't work and drifted; drafts don't live outside the repo). Archive 20261030-232045 under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive. Restore this folder with: pwsh i574n.github/scripts/scratch-archive.ps1 -Root C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -Only 'FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL\\*' -RestoreTo C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\lab. Resume this item's work in its real place in the repo, not in lab.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_33 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 33u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_34 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 34u32, "2026-11-01T04:50Z", "main", "main 00:5x LAB CODE IS SAFE (user 11-01 00:2x: 'check for all lab references in history... make sure we didn't lose that code... to repurpose them or groom their tasks'). This item's lab/FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL folder (2 files: delphi_float.pas, delphi_float_b.pas) is archived in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045 (blob-verified). Restore it into its proper repo place with: pwsh C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github\\scripts\\scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore '%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261030-232045\\manifest.tsv' -RestoreTo <repo dir> -Only 'FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL/*'. Next step for the owner at its next pull: restore the useful files in place as incomplete drafts (Cells heals them in place), or record why it starts over.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_35 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 35u32, "2026-11-02T14:01Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Which canonical float text layout every backend's format_real emits (option A/B/C). RULE (written before acting): Default is option B, Python repr: shortest round-trip digits, fixed notation for decimal exponents in [-4, 16), and 'e+XX'/'e-XX' otherwise, with '.0' kept on integral values. Why it is the safe pick: it is a published, testable spec (CPython float_repr); Rust, TS and Python already sit closest to it; lap 6/7 measured 9/10 Delphi values matching, with only the ffExponent guard for -2.5 left. It is reversible per backend arm. The cross-backend self-checking sample then gates it: every backend prints byte-identical text for the pinned value set (including 100.0, 1e10, 1e16, 1e-5, -2.5, NaN, inf) against a Python repr oracle. BACKUP/UNDO: Each per-arm lib change lands through lib-land with an undo dir, and the old format_real arm stays recoverable from that dir. Changes are left unstaged. OWNER: main. NEXT: Write the Delphi ffExponent guard first, then one lib change per backend arm, then the self-checking sample. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_36 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 36u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: one canonical float text across backends (the C oracle's), with every backend's float tests agreeing; x landed lib float_text on 11-05. Next step: count the backends still disagreeing on float text in the matrix.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_37 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 37u32, "2026-11-07T22:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. TypeFeatures: float text as one lib function (float_text, landed 11-05) that every backend calls, so the C-oracle layout is a single definition, not per-backend printf choices.")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_38 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 38u32, "2026-11-11T00:18Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 NEXT STEP DONE (counted in spiral apps/compiler/tmp/tests/matrix.psd1; rows carry stdout plus an oracle verdict against the C oracle, per scripts/ci-classify.ps1 and compare-runs.ps1, so a green float row means identical float TEXT). Result: the Known section (lines 32-58) has NO float-text entries; its only float entries are value limits, not text (Bend lua_wide_integers: Bend 2 has F32 only; Gleam native_float_infinity: BEAM floats cannot be infinite or NaN). Float-sample coverage per backend section: Zig 27, Lean 26, Wasm 25, Swift 25, Python 25, Lua 25, Kotlin 25, Gleam 25, TypeScript 25 (all judged against the C oracle text); Bend 13, OpenQasm 10, Wgsl 3, Ebpf 2 run fewer because of float TYPE support, not text. So the count of backends still disagreeing on float text in the matrix is ZERO as recorded: since x landed lib float_text (11-05) every backend that runs float samples matches the C layout. Remaining to close the DoD: (1) confirm the newest matrix run is green on those rows (a run, not a read: the last train report), and (2) check the backends NOT in the matrix float rows (F#, Python outside the matrix, the WGSL/eBPF cases) call float_text rather than their own printf. TYPEFEATURES: one lib float_text definition, as noted. Next step: read the latest train matrix report for any red float row; if none, close")
inl note_of_float_text_canonical_39 () = Note ("FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL", 39u32, "2026-11-11T00:18Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2: no matrix report is stored on the box (kit reports holds only resource reports); trains run the matrix on the nodes. Next step: after rig TRAIN44 runs its node half (matrix included), read its report for any red float row (samples/*f64*, *float*, *f32* on Zig, Lean, Wasm, Swift, Python, Lua, Kotlin, Gleam, TypeScript); none red = close this item")
