inl near_contract_crates () = Task ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", "Give the NEAR contracts x's shape: dice_contract.rs (178 KB) and chat_contract.rs (83 KB) are single generated files; split each into library crates under the 1,000-line gate linked by one cdylib contract crate ([package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, scripts/gate.ps1), mirrored in alphabet", Story, P1, D3, 10u32, Planned, "-", "near,dice,polyglot,crate-gate,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_near_contract_crates () = Planning ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", Minutes 660u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_1 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-19T12:57Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 08:57 helper-lane lap 1: sized against x's shape: dice/contract/dice_contract.spi is 230 non-blank Spiral lines and generates dice_contract.rs 174 KB / 3,396 lines / 187 fns (a 15x expansion, because the source opens the dice package via ///- --package ../dice and partial evaluation inlines that library into the contract); polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.spi 190 lines -> chat_contract.rs 81 KB / 1,374 lines / 65 fns. So under the 1,000-line gate dice needs at least 4 crates and chat 2, and the split cannot be done by cutting the contract file: it needs Export boundaries in the dice package itself (x's pattern: per-crate .spi with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output and path dependencies, e.g. xc_args -> xc_checks). The natural first cut is dice's randomness core (already generated separately as dice/lib/dice.rs) as a library crate the contract links.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_2 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-19T13:38Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 09:38 helper-lane lap 2: picked the first exported crate from what the contract reaches: dice_contract.spi calls exactly four dice package functions (dice.create_sequential_roller, dice.roll_progressively, dice.roll_within_bounds, dice.rotate_numbers), and its 187 generated functions are 82 numbered methods, 10 tag impls (before the lazy-tag change reached this file), ~60 closures and a handful of named ones - nearly all of it inlined dice-package code. So a dice_core crate exporting those four functions (Export with a scalar/array signature) would carry most of the 3,396 lines, leaving the contract crate as the NEAR glue (state, borsh, the 6 exported methods). Caveat from CLI-ARGS-STYLE's lap-2 probe: Export signatures must stay scalar or array, because exported union types are private and per-crate numbered.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_3 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-19T15:24Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:24 helper-lane lap 3: checked the four functions' boundary types (dice/lib/dice.spi and the call sites in dice_contract.spi 127-169): none crosses a crate boundary as written - rotate_numbers maps a stream (lazy, closure-backed) to a stream, create_sequential_roller returns a roller closure, roll_progressively takes that closure, and roll_within_bounds takes a list and returns an option (a union, private and per-crate numbered under today's Rust Export, see RUST-EXPORT-TYPES). So the cut must be at the contract's use level, not per function: dice_core exports two scalar/array functions - roll_from_hash (hash : vec u8) (max : u64) : u64, composing rotate_numbers 6, stream.to_list, create_sequential_roller and roll_progressively inside the crate (contract lines 120-149), and roll_within_bounds_of (max : u64) (rolls : vec u8) : u64 with 0 for none (valid results are 1..max). The contract crate keeps state, borsh, keccak512 and the 6 NEAR methods. Once RUST-EXPORT-TYPES lands, the option can cross as itself.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_4 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-19T15:44Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 11:44 helper-lane lap 4: wrote the dice_core export module as lab/NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES/dice_core.spi (not compiled yet: it must sit beside dice/contract to resolve ///- --package ../dice, and bounded jobs are frozen): roll_from_hash max hash composes rotate_numbers 6, the sequential roller and roll_progressively exactly as dice_contract.spi lines 120-149 do, and roll_within_bounds_of returns the option's value or 0; main exports both as dice_core_roll_from_hash and dice_core_roll_within_bounds_of with u64 * am'.vec u8 -> u64 signatures, the scalar/array shape today's Rust Export carries (the dice_contract Export lines 246-249 use the same vec argument type). Next: compile it in a dice copy, then the contract crate depending on it.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_5 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-19T22:22Z", "helper-lane", "2026-10-19 18:22 helper-lane lap 6: blocked on the same thing as NEAR-MOCKED-UNIT-TESTS: today's run shows dice_contract.spi no longer type-checks on the current tree (117:12, an array literal piped into am'.to_vec, array vs array_base since core v1). dice_core.spi cannot be compiled beside it until dice or lib am' is adapted (reported to main).")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_6 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-10-22T08:09Z", "main", "04:09 main: the 10-19 blocker is gone. dice/contract/dice_contract.spi type-checks and compiles to Rust on today's installed compiler (180 KB, exit 0; compiled into TEMP, and dice's tracked dice_contract.rs is unchanged). The 117:12 array vs array_base error was fixed in dice by the '|> am'.base' after the ;[ ... ] literal (commit 4218032, 10-19). So the crate split (x's shape: library crates under the 1,000-line gate plus one cdylib) can start; the next step is measuring how the 180 KB output divides by function (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split would do it at compile time).")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_7 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-10-22T08:10Z", "main", "04:10 main: measured on today's generated dice_contract.rs (3,106 lines, 129 top-level fns; largest closure7 451, method34 313, method29 237, closure76 231, closure4 197 lines). GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES' Rust split (simulate.ps1, limit 1000) gives a 126-line root plus parts of 895/980/873/244 lines: every file under the gate, with no hand-split crates. Not rustc-checked, because the contract needs near-sdk; the two std-only samples compiled and behaved identically (GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES note 7). So the compiler-side split may replace the hand-made crate split for this item; decide when that pack lands.")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_8 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 8u32, "2026-10-24T11:49Z", "main", "2026-10-19 laps folded from the deleted history ledger (docs/agents/history/2026-10-19-backlog-laps, HISTORY-BACKLOG-LAPS-DRIFT): lap 5 12:19 (subagent): dice_core.spi uncompiled (jobs held) -> next: compile in a dice copy after main's go | lap 7 19:54 (subagent): re-read: dice contract compiles again (7/7 after main's migration) -> next: compile dice_core.spi beside the contract")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_9 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 9u32, "2026-10-27T08:20Z", "main", "10-27 04:20 main: 10-27 04:19 main groom: fresh measurement (the last real note was the 10-19 lap fold; on the stale list in EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS n5). Neither contract is split yet. dice/contract/dice_contract.rs is 169 KB / 3,058 lines in one generated file (3.1x the 1,000-line crate gate; the title's 178 KB has shrunk only from incidental heals). polyglot/apps/chat/contract/chat_contract.rs is 83 KB / 1,387 lines (1.4x; regenerated 10-27 by tibia's generate_cid heal). Each folder has 2 Cargo.toml, the contract plus its NEAR sandbox-test crate, not a library split. The x shape (library crates joined by exports) is the remaining work for both. chat_contract is the smaller and the nearer first target. Constraints to carry: the NEAR wasm still builds with the pinned nightly-2024-07-14 (dice build.ps1:50 TODO, RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE), and the sandbox tests still need WSL (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE n10).")
inl note_of_near_contract_crates_10 () = Note ("NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES", 10u32, "2026-10-29T15:07Z", "main", "10-29 11:0x main, grooming check: GENERATED-OUTPUT-MODULES is marked not delivered, so the contracts need a hand split (chat: 2 crates; dice_core + contract; more dice crates; then a gate and wasm proof; estimate 660m). The alphabet mirror the title asks for looks UNNECESSARY: alphabet has no NEAR contract (NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE n11), so alphabet-mirrors-dice does not apply to this item.")
