inl note_of_fiscal_generic_107 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 107u32, "2026-10-28T01:14Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 21:14 fiscal: first set-proof launch (20:43) FAILED on mirror setup, not code: (1) a FRESH mirror has no spiral/apps/cells/deps, so every lib gate died on 'kino ... run mix deps.get' (0 rows); (2) the node's TLS is refused by www.facturae.gob.es ('ProtocolVersion'), so setb's init stopped. Fixed in the launcher: the node copies integ's cells deps into a fresh mirror; the stage carries every local corpus folder except DFe.NET (cloned) and eu (en16931 is a junction into integ). Mirrors removed (junctions unlinked first); relaunched ~21:05.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_108 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 108u32, "2026-10-28T01:21Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 21:21 fiscal: set-proof RELAUNCHED with the setup fixes verified on the node (cells deps kino present, facturae staged, integ untouched): job-1793150142816-fiscal-set-seta and job-1793150535504-fiscal-set-setb. A background monitor flags 45 min of silence. After the verdict: remove mirrors seta/setb (unlink junctions first).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_109 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 109u32, "2026-10-28T01:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 21:44 fiscal: FIXED in the tree (init.ps1): the corpus DFe.NET clone was UNPINNED (--depth 1 at HEAD), so a fresh node got upstream's 2026-08-31 schema update (2cc2da0b: drops evCancPrestDesacordo.xsd, adds evCancVincPgto/evVincPgto, 591 files) and fiscal's pattern oracle broke on a missing file. The review box's 580 files are blob-identical to 56ad28209d (2026-08-27, the commit before it); init now checks that commit out and throws unless 580 files result. Verified from scratch: 580/580 identical. Moving to the newer schemas is a separate, deliberate step.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_110 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 110u32, "2026-10-28T01:46Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 21:46 fiscal: set-proof jobs NOW: job-1793150142816-fiscal-set-seta (running since 21:13; xml.spi lib gate HOLDS, 14 rows) and job-1793152028390-fiscal-set-setb (relaunched 21:4x with the box's DFe.NET staged; earlier setb ids are void). Monitor exits on a non-zero end or 60 min of silence. After the verdicts: remove mirrors seta/setb (unlink junctions first).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_111 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 111u32, "2026-10-28T02:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 22:53 fiscal: HOLD: kit's TRAIN2 window (queued ~23:07, ETA ~05:00). No lib/spiral writes until kit says 'TRAIN2 window closed'; any lib fix from the set-proof verdicts waits for it (fiscal-tree fixes may proceed).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_112 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 112u32, "2026-10-28T03:23Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-27 23:23 fiscal: set-proof seta lib gates: HOLD xml 14, xsd_emit 17, json_schema 5, xsd_regex 8; RED xpath_eval (real bug; fix held, proof job-1793157301295-fiscal-fix-cefix) and schematron_run (both cells killed by the 300 s per-cell budget, 302 s; no collision markers). xpath_eval's cells ran 400-800 s without a kill, so the budget covers only a phase: it may be a hang, not load. Experiment job-1793157873831-fiscal-fix-cefix2 runs schematron_run with the xpath fix and --timeout 1200000 on both cells: pass = budget only, still killed = a hang to find. set-proof's expected name lets_bind_... corrected to lets_and_includes_assemble_the_schema.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_113 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 113u32, "2026-10-28T04:12Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 00:12 fiscal: HANDOFF: 3 held programs PROVEN on the node (compiler 4FD712E9E357): xpath_eval all 4 tests HOLD (job-1793159840957) and schematron_run both tests HOLD with all 3 applied (job-1793159847486, 470/477 s of 900 s). Land after kit's 'TRAIN2 window closed', in order, via lib-guarded-do: fiscal/lib-programs/xpath-call-errors.kit, xpath-sequences.kit, schematron-run-timeouts.kit; then re-apply must refuse, type-check the touched modules on a core-linked copy, and delete fiscal/lib-programs. Proof mirrors cefix/cefix3 removed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_114 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 114u32, "2026-10-28T04:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 00:39 fiscal: set-proof setb found a GENERATOR bug in today's real-document check: generated_cl_envio_dte_v10_writes_its_example fails to TYPE-CHECK (ETY-ValueRestriction at the emitted 'inl count = sm'.obj_to_string (listm.length failures)': listm.length is generic in its result, so the count's type stays a metavar). Both templates (XML generator.spi:539, JSON :572) emit it, so EVERY unit with real documents fails the same way tonight (UBL x6 in seta; HU, GR, CL, GST in setb): those reds are this bug, not the documents. FIXED in the tree: (listm.length failures : i32); the generator type-checks. Proof that the EMITTED code compiles: the next set-proof.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_115 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 115u32, "2026-10-28T04:44Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 00:44 fiscal: job-1793162726520-fiscal-set-setc LAUNCHED: only the real-document units (generated_ubl_*, hu_nav, gr_mydata, cl_envio_dte, in_gst), with the generator fix confirmed in the mirror (listm.length failures : i32). PASS = each unit's writes_its_example / reads_its_documents green. Running alongside: seta (job-1793150142816) and setb (job-1793152028390). After all verdicts: remove mirrors seta/setb/setc (unlink junctions first).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_116 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 116u32, "2026-10-28T05:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 01:57 fiscal: set-proof seta VERDICT (job-1793150142816, exit 1): 14 broken claims, all attributed: 2 lib gates (xpath_eval bug, schematron_run budget; both fixed + proven, held for TRAIN2), 6 UBL units with ETY-ValueRestriction from the document-check template (fixed in the tree; rerun in setc), and 6 example-namespace checks void because those units never wrote an example. Green: lib gates xml 14, xsd_emit 17, json_schema 5, xsd_regex 8; generator 21/21; 5 NF-e units. Mirror seta removed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_117 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 117u32, "2026-10-28T06:57Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 02:57 fiscal: setb VERDICT (job-1793152028390, exit 1): 59 units, 55 all-green, 86 tests; 4 broken claims = exactly the 4 document units on the pre-fix generator template (cl_envio, gr, hu, in_gst; ETY-ValueRestriction). Every non-document family passes on the landed lib (CFDI, CR x9, CT-e/MDF-e/GTV-e, PT SAF-T, ...). setc (fixed generator) already shows cl_envio, hu_nav, in_gst, ubl_applicationresponse, ubl_creditnote GREEN with their real documents. Mirror setb removed (junctions unlinked first).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_118 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 118u32, "2026-10-28T09:04Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 05:04 fiscal: setc VERDICT (job-1793162726520, exit 1): 13 units, 12 all-green, 16 tests; the 1 broken claim is generated_gr_mydata_2_0_2 on its pre-fix BOM corpus. With the generator fix, EVERY other family with real documents round-trips unchanged on the node: UBL Invoice 155, CreditNote 21, DebitNote 18, ApplicationResponse 14, DespatchAdvice 5, ReceiptAdvice 4; HU NAV 30; CL EnvioDTE 1; IN GST 1 (JSON). Mirror setc removed. Greece-only rerun setd launched with the BOM-free corpus. Night summary: 3 lib programs held (proven) for TRAIN2; generator + init fixes in the fiscal tree.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_119 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 119u32, "2026-10-28T09:32Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-28 05:32 fiscal: LANDED 05:28:30-36 after TRAIN2 closed, via lib-guarded-do under Global\\spiral-lib-land, no exemptions: xpath-call-errors, xpath-sequences, schematron-run-timeouts. Verified: each re-apply refuses; live = the node-proven drafts (xpath_eval 866CA0F7 = the proven file); xpath, xpath_eval, schematron, schematron_run + 2 probes type-check on a core-linked copy; fiscal generator + corpus_checks type-check. New hashes xpath B44F8524, xpath_eval 866CA0F7, schematron_run 29D02EA9. fiscal/lib-programs deleted.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_120 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 120u32, "2026-10-29T19:18Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 15:18 fiscal: STALE-PIN RESTAMP: the 10-27/28 set-proof verdicts seta (job-1793150142816), setb (job-1793152028390) and setc (job-1793162726520), and the notes citing them (FISCAL-GENERIC n110-n118, XSD-CHOICE-REPEATED-READER n6-n7, FISCAL-PEPPOL-BIS3 n21-n25, FISCAL-IN-GST-EINVOICE n8-n10, FISCAL-CL-DTE n7, FISCAL-HU-NAV n6, FISCAL-GR-MYDATA n6, FISCAL-TR-UBLTR n12, FISCAL-CO-DIAN n9, FISCAL-SA-ZATCA n10, FISCAL-MY-MYINVOIS n5, FISCAL-RO-CIUS n5, FISCAL-PE-SUNAT n5), ran recgroups\\set-proof.ps1, which pins the 10-26 stage spiral-bin-1026-0819 (host 4FD712E9E357, core 022C3338096B4051), so they predate pack 6b and are STALE. 10-29 work re-proved the lib on 05E94769 (A4E48020 stage) and, from 13:4x, on pack 6b (stage spiral-bin-1029-6b, host 9895730DAE80, core 31CC96520D6B7269, copied from the box's installed single-flight build and hash-checked on the node). Each 10-29 verdict note names its core. The real-document round trips cited in those notes get re-run on 6b with the next full fiscal set-proof. Also: sefaz/scripts/candidate-gate.ps1:18 now calls apps/compiler/scripts/memory-cap.ps1 (COMPILER-TMP-RETIRE moved it; sefaz is not a git repo).")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_121 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 121u32, "2026-10-30T02:02Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-29 22:02 fiscal: GROOM, inconsistency flagged (value unchanged): 8 country stories list FISCAL-GENERIC as a dependency (IN-GST 70, CL-DTE 80, ES-FACTURAE 70, HU-NAV 85, GR-MYDATA 85, CR-HACIENDA 60, PT-SAFT 80, CII-PROFILES 0; mean 66%) while this Feature reads 10%. If GENERIC is their umbrella, its progress should roll up from them, and the edge should be parent/child, not a prerequisite. If it's a real prerequisite, those 8 shouldn't be at 60-85% before it. Needs a decision on the relation; no rule found, so not re-valued.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_122 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 122u32, "2026-10-30T14:16Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-30 10:16 fiscal: GROOM, ROLL-UP applied (agreed with main 10-30): progress = mean of the 8 country stories that list this Feature (IN-GST 70, CL-DTE 95, ES-FACTURAE 70, HU-NAV 85, GR-MYDATA 85, CR-HACIENDA 60, PT-SAFT 80, CII-PROFILES 0) = 68.1, so 10 -> 68. Recomputed at each groom, never hand-valued.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_123 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 123u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-31 23:40 fiscal: ROLL-UP recomputed (groom 10-31 night, same rule as 10-30: mean of the 8 country stories): IN-GST 70, CL-DTE 100 (closed tonight), ES-FACTURAE 70, HU-NAV 85, GR-MYDATA 85, CR-HACIENDA 85, PT-SAFT 80, CII-PROFILES 0 = 71.9 -> 72.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_124 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 124u32, "2026-11-01T10:53Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 06:55 fiscal: NODE SET-ALL VERDICT (job-1793483703385-fiscal-p8-t1031-set-all, 12.9 h, mirror t1031, pack 8): every unit claim HOLDS except 2 BROKEN, and neither is a defect. (1) generator \"0 failed\": generates_every_family_version is VOID, killed at the 1,400 s Cells cell cap under node contention (\"Spiral cell timed out after 1400s\"); the queued e3 regen gates the generator with a 14,400,000 ms header cap. (2) generated_ubl_invoice_2_1: t1031's xsd_emit is AE0A917F, older than the restriction fix 3872CC94, so this is the known currencyID drift; the same unit passed with 0 drift on t1031u (XSD-RESTRICTION-ATTRIBUTE-OVERRIDE closed 03:10). Also HOLDS: all other UBL business documents; 38 regenerated files keep validated nominals private; no UBL example puts a child element in the wrong namespace.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_125 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 125u32, "2026-11-01T12:39Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 08:41 fiscal: HYGIENE (groom round 4). Box: no leftover fiscal processes (no respell/format/metrics/scratch-gate process alive); background waiters kept on purpose: the e3 regen waiter and the CI push watcher. Node jobs: 1 running (e3 regen), the rest finished; none stale. Node disk left by finished fiscal jobs (14.3 GB free): mirrors t1031 0.62 GB (set-all done) and t1031u 0.18 GB (UBL zero-drift done), stage folders canton-gate, json-gate, fiscal-cc-gate, fiscal-ubl-gate(-u) (< 0.05 GB). CLEANUP RULE: after the e3 regen and the bring-home, unlink each mirror's junctions first (fiscal\\deps\\spiral, apps\\cells\\deps), then remove t1031, t1031u and the stage folders; keep t1101 until its units are committed.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_126 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 126u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-01 10:55 fiscal: SCOPE vs FISCAL-GENERIC-PROVABLE (groom round 5, to avoid a silent overlap): FISCAL-GENERIC (72%) is the COVERAGE track, i.e. more countries' schema systems generated and gated (children: CR, PT, MY, ES, PE, PINT, CII ...). FISCAL-GENERIC-PROVABLE is the ARCHITECTURE track: no per-country code (families as manifest data), reuse instead of re-emission, one generic law suite. They meet in FISCAL-FAMILY-MANIFEST: from then on a new country is added under FISCAL-GENERIC as a manifest row, not Spiral code. This item's % is raised by coverage only, not by architecture work.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_127 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 127u32, "2026-11-02T14:41Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 10:41 deps: dropped FISCAL-APP. It is the parent epic (the Brazil SEFAZ app), not a prerequisite: this item is 72% done while FISCAL-APP is open. Parent: FISCAL-APP. Also removed the duplicate team:fiscal tag.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_128 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 128u32, "2026-11-04T07:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-11-04 03:54 fiscal: PROGRESS SINCE 11-02 (children, all with proofs on their items): (1) a new family from a catalog row alone -- CII D16B, 0 generator lines, its 15 CEN examples read back (FISCAL-CII-PROFILES); counter (1) is 18/18 families from rows (FISCAL-GENERIC-PROVABLE n4); (2) one catalog fix unlocked 4 non-Brazilian families at once: the W3C xmldsig (not SEFAZ's rsa-sha1-fixed copy) for Facturae, VeriFactu, FatturaPA and Costa Rica; with the wildcard-sequence fold, Facturae's 10 real signed documents read back (FISCAL-ES-FACTURAE); (3) UBL profiles as a type (EN16931, PEPPOL BIS 3, SUNAT 2.0, MyInvois 1.0/1.1 grounded in the corpus marks; profiles.spi); (4) Peru as typed catalogs (01/05/07/09/10/16, IGV affectation indexed by group) with two rules held over the 58 SUNAT homologation cases (FISCAL-PE-SUNAT); (5) generic readers report errors with paths instead of raising (XSD-DECODE-RESULT, 70%: Facturae + NF-e proven on generated code). Remaining for this feature: the decode regen + land, Factur-X profile samples (user-gated download), and the per-country read-back tally at the next full regen.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_129 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 129u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "fiscal", "Groom 11-06: children carry the work (CANON-MODEL, CII-PROFILES, MYINVOIS-SIGNATURE-TWIN); next step = close MyInvois twin, then CII profile lattice.")
inl note_of_fiscal_generic_130 () = Note ("FISCAL-GENERIC", 130u32, "2026-11-07T15:45Z", "fiscal", "DoD: every non-Brazilian family in families.spi generates, reads back its corpus documents, and validates its examples, with 0 per-country generator lines; carried by the children (CANON-MODEL, CII-PROFILES, MYINVOIS-SIGNATURE-TWIN, LAW-MISSING-REPORTED, MODELING-CEILING).\nTYPEFEATURES: GADTs indexed by version and profile, existentials for extensions, typed regime witnesses (families_of), validated nominals for facets.")
inl x_precommit_speed () = Task ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", "x: shorten the pre-commit (scripts/build.ps1, ~53-75 min locally, one heavy slot) by measuring the Cells modes that x's Invoke-SpiralTests forces since x's first commit (56ea8c9, no reason recorded): SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER=process (a fresh dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll per compile) and SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0 (a BEAM boot per notebook), notebooks one at a time", Story, P2, D3, 70u32, Planned, "", "team:microblog,microblog,cells,ci,speed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_precommit_speed () = Planning ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", Minutes 180u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_1 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-10-22T11:59Z", "x", "2026-10-22 07:59 x: filed from a ledger and notebook-metadata breakdown (no compiler). Crate compiles 411 s, cargo release 246 s, notebooks ~2,950 s (sum of medians, 296 ledger rows). Per target over 126 tests: gleam 3,278 s (median 42 s), rust 2,931 s, zig 1,649 s; all 193 target runs were memo misses after the 00:37 compiler install. DECISION RULE, written before measuring: after tibia's live block ends, in one heavy job on a quiet box, run the same 4 notebooks (protocol, cli, splitter, transport: a mix of slow and fast) with SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 in 3 modes, in rotating order, twice each: A process+warm0 (today), B daemon compiler+warm0, C daemon+warm1. Record the wall per mode, the pass counts, and the peak private memory of dotnet and erl (100 ms sampler). Pick the mode with the lowest median wall if pass counts are identical and peak memory is at most A's + 1.5 GB. B or C must beat A by at least 15% to replace it, else keep A. Ties go to the mode with fewer shared daemons (A, then B). Apply the result to Invoke-SpiralTests only (CI runs the same script); note the reason in the item, not in the script.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_2 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-10-22T12:01Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:01 x: experiment drafted and parse-checked, not run: scratchpad precommit_modes.ps1 (session scratch; %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\b7595ac3-963d-4714-be58-0267aa270947\\scratchpad). The 4 notebooks run x 3 modes x 2 rounds, rotating A,B,C / B,C,A. It sets SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 and a private SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_PORT=13977 (the notebook warm daemon and the compiler daemon share this setting), so other teams' daemons on 13905 are never touched, and SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN=5. Each mode logs wall, pass/fail counts, and peak private MB of dotnet/erl started after the experiment began. Between modes it stops only listeners on its own port started after the experiment began, plus their children. Run it as one eoie heavy job (one heavy job, ~40-60 min) after tibia's block end, then apply the rule in note 1.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_3 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-10-22T12:03Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:03 x: second finding (read-only). A cell runs its targets one after another, and a target's ms includes its own Spiral compile. protocol's first test cell took 178 s = rust 52 + gleam 65 + zig 60. The generated Gleam per test is small (347-776 lines in cells-gleam-warm), so the cost is mostly the per-target compile: in process mode, a cold dotnet start per test x target, each type-checking the whole --package . client package. Cells' 300 s timeout applies per cell, i.e. to the sum of three targets, which is why contention killed runs 1 and 3 (exsss, schedule). A mode that cuts per-target compile time also widens that margin. The experiment now logs the slowest cell per mode; as a tie-break, the faster mode must not raise the slowest cell.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_4 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-10-22T12:06Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:06 x: third finding, from CI (gh run 39367603402, read-only). On ubuntu-24.04 the whole workflow.ps1 step took 508 s: 16 crate compiles at 2 s each, cargo release 36 s, 11 notebooks in 4.5 min (a 17-test notebook in 37 s), the sim in 1 min. Locally, run 3 took 13-101 s per crate for the same BuildSpiral in process mode. A cold compiler process does a crate in 2 s on a free CPU, so dotnet startup is not the main local cost; contention is: heavy jobs run BelowNormal while other teams' compile windows run Normal (heavy-gate lessons, 10-21: about 0.15 cores). The notebook counts are not comparable: HEAD's notebooks were mostly zig-only, and the worktree tests more gleam/rust. Prediction, written before measuring: on a quiet box mode B saves less than the 15% bar, and the main local lever is running the pre-commit when no Normal-priority compile is active. The experiment's rule stands. Also record 'eoie window list' at the start and end of each mode, so a mode measured beside a compile window is visible and rerun.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_5 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-10-22T12:07Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:07 x: correction to note 4: contention is a hypothesis, not a finding. I checked it on the ledger: each x notebook's run_s split by how many other ledger runs (>= 60 s) overlapped it. The busy/quiet median ratios scatter from 0.3 to 3.1 with no consistent direction (splitter 3.1, posts 2.7, prelude 2.0; wire 0.3, invariants 0.4, personas 0.4). Two confounders make this test weak: notebook contents and targets changed over 10-16..10-22, so early 'quiet' rows are different work; and the ledger has no compile-window rows, the Normal-priority load suspected. The CI fact stands (2 s per crate on ubuntu vs 13-101 s locally in run 3), but I don't know whether local BuildSpiral uses the same compile route as CI. Both modes and contention stay open; the experiment answers the mode question, and its compile-window column shows contention per run. Prediction in note 4 withdrawn: no prediction on mode B.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_6 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-10-22T12:11Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:11 x: probe (scratchpad crate_priority_probe.ps1, compile window, temp output, all 4 outputs byte-identical to the tree's args.rs): one BuildSpiral-equivalent compile of args.spi, alternating priority, with 4 windows held. Normal: 10.7 s and 11.3 s wall, 21.5/22.5 s CPU. BelowNormal: 24.9 s and 73.2 s wall, 22.1/25.5 s CPU. (1) Priority is real: BelowNormal heavy jobs pay 2.3-6.5x wall for the same CPU while Normal compile windows run, which explains much of run 3's 13-101 s per crate (contention hypothesis of notes 4-5 confirmed for crate compiles). (2) Independently, the local compile does ~22 CPU-s per crate where CI's took 1-2 s wall (10-20 compiler, HEAD sources). So either today's compiler or today's x sources make a crate compile ~10x more work. Next (CPU-light first): compare args.spi at HEAD vs worktree; then, as a compile, time HEAD's args.spi on today's compiler. Process note: I ran this probe while my own run 5 had just started (state=running, printed and ignored), against the 10-21 rule; ~90 CPU-s taken from run 5.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_7 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-10-22T12:13Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:12 x: correction to note 6 (2): no compiler regression. The local history of the same crate compile (RUST-COMPILED x/apps/client/args in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\eoie\\jobs logs, 30 x jobs) shows 5-11 s on 10-19..10-21 (x-chain3..23, x-postid-round 5 s, x-split-fix3 11 s), then 61/143/101 s in the 10-22 night pre-commits (heavy, BelowNormal, busy night), 16 s in run 4 and 32 s in run 5. The probe's 11 s at Normal had 4 windows held. So a quiet local compile is ~5-6 s against CI's 1-2 s (a machine factor of about 3-5x), and the 10-20x outliers come from contention at BelowNormal. Conclusion for the item: the main local lever is when the pre-commit runs (no Normal compile windows) or its priority, not the Cells mode. The mode experiment still runs once, after tibia's block, to settle B/C; I expect a small effect there.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_8 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 8u32, "2026-10-22T12:14Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:14 x: second lever, x-owned and drafted, not applied (Cells reads the live tree while run 5 runs). A .spi file's first ///- line takes --timeout <ms>, which budgets the whole file and every test cell without its own (Cells README ~380). Cells parses the line itself (Domain.prepare returns timeout, packages and print_code), so it sits beside --package. Slowest cell per notebook from the saved metadata (mostly under run 3's contention): scenario 227 s, personas 213, protocol 201, exsss 199, world 178, paging 176 (all over half of 300 s); invariants 134 and the rest under 100. RULE, written before applying: every notebook whose slowest recorded cell is at least 150 s gets '///- --package . --timeout 600000' on line 1 (today these six). Cost: a real hang fails after 10 min instead of 5, inside a job that already has a 2 h limit. Apply after run 5 finishes, outside tibia's block, verify in the next full pre-commit, and revisit the list from metadata after each full run (add at >= 150 s, drop below 100 s twice).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_9 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 9u32, "2026-10-22T12:15Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:15 x: postability check of note 8's line (no compile: xc post-thread --dry-run on temp copies, xc built 06:42). All six files keep their post count (scenario 55, personas 53, protocol 41, exsss 30, world 115, paging 13) with 0 cells over one post. The compiler's --x-compat check runs in the next full pre-commit.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_10 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 10u32, "2026-10-22T12:24Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:24 x: memo prediction from my morning analysis FALSIFIED. Run 5's prelude.spi missed the memo for all 3 tests (memo=miss, memo_hits 0), although run 3 passed the same notebook at ~06:44. Unchanged since 06:40: the single-flight compiler bin, Cells beams and lib, every x client .spi. Changed: spiral/lib/spiral xml.spi, xsd.spi, xsd_regex.spi and xsd_resolve.spi at 07:16, none of which x imports. Memo.key is meant to cover only reached sources (SourceBlocks bounds, needing the compiler's source-blocks capability). It falls back to the whole package tree when bounds are :unavailable, and keys also include Keyword.take(opts, @tool_opts). Static reading can't decide which applies; it needs a run with memo diagnostics (Cells, main's code). Watching the rest of run 5 to see whether every notebook misses or only some.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_11 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 11u32, "2026-10-22T12:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:26 x: memo miss narrowed (file timestamps only, no decoding). Of the 168 cells-results entries touched since 08:21, 144 were hits (CreationTime before 08:21, almost all 10-22 02h, i.e. cached source-block bounds; fetch touches on hit) and 24 are new. So bounds were available and the whole-tree fallback did not cause prelude's miss. Tool opts are only explicit options (Toolchain reads env fallbacks separately), so they are constant too. Remaining suspect: the package print (package_prints / reached_package_print) for x's dependency on spiral/lib/spiral changes when a file the program does not reach changes (xml.spi, xsd*.spi at 07:16). If so, every lib edit by any team invalidates every x memo entry. Handing to main with this note; settling it needs Memo.key inputs logged for one test across two runs.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_12 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 12u32, "2026-10-22T12:27Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:27 x: memo miss ROOT CAUSE (static, spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells/memo.ex). reached_package_print narrows the program's OWN package to reached blocks, but its tuple includes index.deps (line 390), built at line 332 as tree_print(dep.target, bounds) = package_print (lines 537-549): a hash of content_print for EVERY module file of the dependency plus its dependencies. x's apps/client/package.spiproj declares 'spiral-' (spiral/lib/spiral). So any edit to any lib/spiral module invalidates every x memo entry, even one x never opens (07:16 xml.spi/xsd*.spi; XSD work is the current P1 and edits lib/spiral constantly). That is why x's 193/193 and run 5's misses happen with an unchanged compiler, Cells and x. Fix direction (Cells, main's code): fingerprint each dependency by the blocks the program reaches (recurse reached_package_print into deps) instead of the whole tree. Expected effect for x: notebooks unchanged between runs hit the memo, and run time drops toward the edited notebooks only.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_13 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 13u32, "2026-10-22T12:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:28 x: status Planned 0% -> Active 40%. Done: time breakdown (notes 1-3), priority probe and history (6-7), timeout lever drafted and postability-checked (8-9), memo root cause handed to main (10-12). Left: (a) apply the --timeout line to the six notebooks after run 5; (b) the Cells-mode experiment after tibia's block, rule in note 1; (c) once main fixes the dependency fingerprint, measure a rerun with unchanged notebooks (expect memo hits). Ranking by expected gain: (c) > (a) (prevents whole-run loss) > (b) (predicted small).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_14 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 14u32, "2026-10-22T12:31Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:31 x: main accepted the memo finding (their 07:3x xml/xsd landings for the fiscal split invalidated every entry of packages that declare spiral-). Main takes the fix in the Cells pack as its own patch: fingerprint each dependency by the blocks the program reaches, recursing the reached walk into deps, with a test where an unopened lib module changes and the entry still hits. Until then, expect misses after any lib landing; main batches theirs. Step (c) of note 13 waits on that patch. Schedule: after run 5, PACK-AFTER-NIGHT installs (exclusive, ~20 min), then tibia's live block (~2.5 h): the mode experiment (b) runs after that, about 12:30-13:00 at the earliest.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_15 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 15u32, "2026-10-22T12:48Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:48 x: slowest cell per slow notebook, run 5 (quiet box: only this job held a slot, no compile windows) vs run 3 (contended): exsss 118 vs 199 s, protocol 98 vs 201, personas 76 vs 213, scenario 47 vs 227, world 85 vs 178, paging 124 vs 176. Quiet cells run at 0.2-0.7x of contended ones (median ~0.45x), and every quiet cell sits below half of the 300 s limit. So the timeout failures (runs 1 and 3) are a contention effect, consistent with notes 6-7. The --timeout rule (note 8) keys on contended measurements on purpose and stands: that is the condition it protects against.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_16 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 16u32, "2026-10-22T12:52Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:52 x: invariants.spi's slowest cell took 144 s in run 5 on a quiet box (134 s on 10-21). That is under the note 8 threshold (>= 150 s), so by the rule as written it stays off the --timeout list. At the contended ratio of note 15 (~0.45x quiet/contended) it would land near 300 s. Flagged as the first candidate for the post-run revisit: if any full run records it at >= 150 s, it joins the list mechanically.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_17 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 17u32, "2026-10-22T13:23Z", "x", "2026-10-22 09:23 x: the eoie team designed EOIE-JOB-QUIET-START for the release after E2. 'job start --heavy --quiet [--quiet-hold 30] [--quiet-wait 10]' waits for held compile windows to end, then holds a 'quiet' window that delays new compile requests for quiet-hold minutes. The eoie team will measure the slowest cell inside vs after the hold on x's next two pre-commits, and add BelowNormal for compiles beside a quiet window if cells after the hold still pass 200 s. Plan for x: once the release is installed, start every full pre-commit with --quiet --quiet-hold 60 (a full run is ~60 min alone) and record the slowest cell per run in this item.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_18 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 18u32, "2026-10-22T13:24Z", "x", "2026-10-22 09:24 x: (a) APPLIED after run 5 ended (text only, no compile, allowed during tibia's block). Line 1 of scenario, personas, protocol, exsss, world and paging is now '///- --package . --timeout 600000', via scratchpad apply_timeout.ps1, which checks the exact old line first. git: 6 files, 1 line each. Verification is the next full pre-commit after the block (Cells reads it, x-compat and postable unchanged per note 9). Uncommitted, for the user.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_19 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 19u32, "2026-10-22T15:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 11:26 x: tibia's block ended 11:25 (exit 0). Order swapped from note 14's plan, by the 'start the most important job first after a block' rule: the full pre-commit runs first (job-1792682760624-x-precommit-day6), because it verifies the --timeout lines now in the tree the user will commit. The mode experiment (predicted small gain) runs after it. Box at start: 0 windows, 5.7 GB free; main's PACK-AFTER-NIGHT install is done, so memo misses are expected again.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_20 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 20u32, "2026-10-22T16:44Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:44 x: run 6 (job-1792682760624-x-precommit-day6, 11:25-12:42, exit 1) never reached the notebooks. Passed: crate gate 16/16 (view 987 on the newly installed compiler), all-files gate 106/106, x-compat 0 in 36 files (so the --timeout lines pass the compiler's postability check). The cargo release build took 41m 27s (3m 41s quiet). Then e2e failed at startup: 'the X API stub did not start'. e2e.ps1 gave the Python stub 15 s to write its port file, while BelowNormal processes got a few CPU-s per minute. That is environment, not code (the same e2e passed in run 5 and on CI). Fix in x (uncommitted): the stub gets 60 s (the per-step timeout), and the error names which case happened (exited with code N vs no port within 60 s). RULE for run 7, written before starting: start the full pre-commit only when 'eoie window list' shows no compile window held and at most one other heavy job; the check-in looks at :17/:47. The mode experiment follows run 7.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_21 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 21u32, "2026-10-22T16:46Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:46 x: e2e fix verified without a heavy job. e2e.ps1 run directly against run 6's fresh release xc.exe (built 12:41 by PACK-AFTER-NIGHT's compiler), with the work dir in TEMP, removed afterwards: the stub started, 170 requests, 31 tutorial steps + 5 extra cases, exit 0 in 103 s on a still-busy box. So run 6's failure was only the 15 s startup deadline, and the code paths up to e2e pass on the new compiler. Run 7 still has to cover postable, the 36 notebooks (the --timeout lines) and the sim.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_22 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 22u32, "2026-10-22T16:47Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:47 x: postable stage verified the same way (build.ps1's loop, run 6's xc.exe): 1644 posts for 36 files, 0 cells over one post, identical to run 5. With the --timeout lines in place, the stages up to and including postable have now passed on the new compiler. Run 7 only adds the 36 Cells notebooks and the sim.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_23 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 23u32, "2026-10-22T16:51Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:51 x: run 7 started by note 20's rule (job-1792687896348-x-precommit-day7): at 12:51 no compile window was held and the only other heavy job was main's pack review. Its tree includes main's additive lib/spiral landing of 12:51 (xpath, schematron, xsd_emit, decimal, ...; package.spiproj F5C5A2460C0E331B), so memo misses are expected again and the spiral- type-check is a little larger.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_24 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 24u32, "2026-10-22T17:46Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:46 x: run 7 (job-1792687896348-x-precommit-day7, 12:51-13:46) stopped by me on tibia's request. tibia's live-gui block (duo smoke + PR-24, ~65 min, start 13:45 on main's go) was queued behind my heavy-build window, its runner waits only 15 min, and main and eoie had already stopped their jobs. By then it had passed: 16 crate compiles on the 12:51 lib (frames 480 s at the worst contention, free memory 2.7 GB), crate gate 16/16, all-files gate 106, x-compat 0 in 36 files, cargo release build 19m 58s. Not reached: e2e, postable (both passed at 12:45 on run 6's binary), the 36 Cells notebooks and the sim, which run 8 covers. Run 8 starts by note 20's rule after tibia's 'block end' (~14:50).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_25 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 25u32, "2026-10-22T17:48Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:48 x: run 8 covers only what is still unverified on the current tree, to save ~40 contended minutes. The tree has not changed since these passed: crate compiles, both gates, x-compat and cargo release (run 7, 13:46, on main's 12:51 lib); e2e 31+5 and postable 1644/0 (12:45, run 6's binary); actions-lock (every run). Runner: scratchpad run8_notebooks_sim.ps1. It reads the 36-module notebook list from apps/client/build.ps1 (so the two can't drift), calls common.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests on it, then apps/sim/build.ps1 through Invoke-Block. Rule: if anything in x changes before run 8, a full scripts/build.ps1 run replaces it.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_26 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 26u32, "2026-10-22T17:52Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:52 x: run 8 runner reviewed against the full pre-commit (read-only). In a full run, the notebooks start after BuildSpiral has called Ensure-SpiralCompiler in the same process, so they inherit DOTNET_ROOT, SPIRAL_WORKSPACE_ROOT, SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR and CARGO_TERM_COLOR, with cwd apps/client. run8_notebooks_sim.ps1 skipped the crate compiles and so started without them. run_notebook.ps1 resolves the same dll and package dir from the same bundle env.ps1, but DOTNET_ROOT would come from the user env. Fix (scratch runner only): dot-source lib.ps1, Set-Location apps/client, call Ensure-SpiralCompiler (no compile: the single-flight dll exists) and set the package dir the way BuildSpiral does, then print all three. Parse-checked. The run 8 start rule is unchanged (note 20).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_27 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 27u32, "2026-10-22T17:52Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:52 x: note 8's revisit rule, made exact BEFORE run 8 measures anything. (1) Run 8's notebook stage counts as a full run for the revisit: it is the same Invoke-SpiralTests call over the same 36 modules. (2) The 150 s add threshold came from contended timings. Note 15 measured quiet/contended at a median of ~0.45x, so a quiet cell of 135 s or more (300 s x 0.45) predicts more than 300 s under contention. Thresholds: a run is CONTENDED if any watcher sample (eoie window list, every 5 min during the notebook stage) shows a compile window held, otherwise QUIET. A notebook joins the --timeout list at >= 150 s in a contended run or >= 135 s in a quiet run. It leaves the list after two runs of either kind below 100 s (100 s quiet is ~220 s contended, still under 300 s). Run 5 counts as the first quiet run, so after run 8 scenario (47 s), personas (76) and world (85) leave the list if they stay below 100 s, and invariants (144 s quiet in run 5) joins if run 8 is quiet and it measures >= 135 s, or contended and >= 150 s. Any change from this rule is a 1-line text edit that x-compat and postable already cover (note 9), and it is verified in the next run.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_28 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 28u32, "2026-10-22T17:53Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:53 x: correction to note 27. It counted run 5 for removals but not for additions, which is asymmetric. Run 5 counts for both: invariants (144 s in a quiet run, >= 135 s) joins the --timeout list now, by rule. The 1-line edit waits until run 8 ends, so the tree run 8 tests stays the one runs 5-7 verified (note 25), and every list change (invariants in, any of scenario/personas/world out) lands in one edit after run 8, verified by the next full pre-commit.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_29 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 29u32, "2026-10-22T17:56Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:56 x: run 8 tooling ready, all CPU-light, nothing in x changed. (1) run5_slowest.json (scratch): run 5's slowest cell per notebook, saved before run 8 overwrites the outputs. Top: invariants 144, paging 124, glyphs 122, exsss 118, protocol 98, world 85, schedule 81, personas 76. protocol (98 s) is also a removal candidate, which note 27 missed. (2) timeout_revisit.ps1 applies notes 27-28 from run5_slowest.json plus run 8's notebook outputs. It handles both line-1 forms (13 x '--package .', 17 x '--package ..', 6 timed), refuses outputs older than run 8's start, and is a dry run unless -Apply. Self-test with run 5's data in place of run 8's gives invariants join; scenario, personas, world, protocol leave; exsss, paging keep. (3) run8_watch.ps1 samples 'eoie window list' every 5 min into a log. It classifies the run as quiet or contended (any compile window held), exits 3 at once if a live-gui or compiler-install request appears (the stop-my-job rule), and otherwise exits at job end with the job's result lines. Tested on run 7's stopped job. Check-in cron replaced (16e19579) with the full sequence.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_30 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 30u32, "2026-10-22T17:58Z", "x", "2026-10-22 13:58 x: precommit_modes.ps1 reviewed before its first run (read-only checks against Cells). Confirmed: an unset SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER selects the shared daemon (cells.ex shared_compiler?, notebook.ex shared_boot?), and the env vars below never fill opts, so they don't force process mode. 'spiral-tests total' is printed by notebook.ex. Fixed: (1) the same environment gap as run 8 (note 26): it now sets up the compiler environment like BuildSpiral, without compiling, so all modes run in a real pre-commit's environment; (2) the summary's 'median' of 2 rounds took the lower value, and it now takes the mean of the middle pair, as note 1's rule intends; (3) Stop-OwnDaemons also requires the listener to be erl, beam.smp or dotnet, besides the private port and start-after-experiment checks. Parse-checked.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_31 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 31u32, "2026-10-22T18:08Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:08 x: note 25's 'if x changes, a full run replaces run 8' is narrowed to inputs of the stages run 8 runs. apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1 changed (-OutputFormat Text, X-DONE note of 2026-10-22 14:08), but the Cells notebooks and the sim don't read it. So run 8 stays as planned, and e2e gets a direct rerun on run 7's release xc.exe after the block, the same check that verified the stub fix at 12:45 (note 22).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_32 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 32u32, "2026-10-22T18:19Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:19 x: RULE before run 8, from a CI-parity check (CELLS-MEMO-TOOL-IDENTITY). x CI runs Cells' ///> zig cells on zig 0.17.0 (setup-zig pin, the same in every sibling). Every local run so far, run 5 included, used zig-dev 0.18.0-dev.146 through the scoop shim. Run 5 already passed these exact notebooks on zig-dev, so run 8 sets SPIRAL_ZIG=%USERPROFILE%\\scoop\\apps\\zig\\current\\zig.exe (0.17.0, CI's) and SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 (the memo key ignores the Zig, so a hit would be a false pass). The two runs then cover both Zigs. A zig failure in run 8 is a CI blocker for X-DONE, not a flake, and gets fixed before the commit. Timing caveat for the note 27 revisit: 0.17.0's global cache is cold, which can only make cells slower, so it can only push a notebook toward joining (the safe side).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_33 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 33u32, "2026-10-22T18:21Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:21 x: note 32's CI-parity rule extended to Rust cells. Cells takes rustc from SPIRAL_RUSTC or PATH (the rustup shim), and its VM's cwd is spiral/apps/cells, where no toolchain file exists, so local ///> rust cells run rustup's default nightly-2025-05-09 (rustc 1.88.0-nightly). x CI runs 'rustup default nightly-2026-10-18' (seen in run 39367603402's log), so CI's Rust cells use 1.101.0-nightly 2026-10-17, x's rust-toolchain.toml. Run 8 sets SPIRAL_RUSTC to that toolchain's rustc.exe under RUSTUP_HOME and refuses to start on any other version. Run 5 covered the local default and run 8 covers CI's. Gleam 1.19.2 and Elixir 1.20 match CI, and OTP is 29 on both. Parse-checked, version guards tested.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_34 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 34u32, "2026-10-22T18:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:34 x: CI parity moved into x itself (notes 32-33 had it in the run 8 runner only). Rule written before the edit: apply if 'rustup which rustc' in the x root resolves x's rust-toolchain.toml rustc (nightly-2026-10-18, which CI also makes default). scripts/common.ps1 now has Get-XRustc and Get-XCiZigPin (reads setup-zig's version from ci.yml). Invoke-SpiralTests sets SPIRAL_RUSTC to Get-XRustc unless it is already set, and restores it afterwards like the Cells mode vars. It prints 'Cells rustc ..., zig ...' and, when the zig in use differs from ci.yml's pin, one line saying to set SPIRAL_ZIG to test what CI runs (a note, not a skip or a failure). Tested with Invoke-Block stubbed, no notebook run: without SPIRAL_ZIG it prints rustc 1.101.0-nightly 2026-10-17 and the zig-dev mismatch line; with SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0 there is no mismatch line; SPIRAL_RUSTC and SPIRAL_CELLS_COMPILER are restored. A parse error ('$zigPin:' read as a scoped variable) was caught by that test and fixed to '${zigPin}'. Run 8 no longer sets SPIRAL_RUSTC itself, so it exercises this path; it still guards the version via Get-XRustc and still sets SPIRAL_ZIG to 0.17.0. README scripts row updated. common.ps1 is an input of run 8's stages, and run 8 is its verification.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_35 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 35u32, "2026-10-22T18:39Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:39 x: note 34 amended for template users (X-REPO-TEMPLATE: new Spiral repos copy scripts/). Invoke-SpiralTests no longer requires a zig or a setup-zig pin. The zig comes from SPIRAL_ZIG, else the first zig application on PATH; when there is none it prints \"zig none\". Get-XCiZigPin returns nothing when ci.yml has no setup-zig, and the mismatch line is then skipped. Only Invoke-SpiralTests uses them; workflow.ps1 -Xc (the xc-windows job, no setup-zig) never calls it. Retested with Invoke-Block stubbed: the PATH zig-dev case notes the mismatch, SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0 is quiet, the no-zig case prints \"zig none\" plus the note, and a preset SPIRAL_RUSTC is kept.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_36 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 36u32, "2026-10-22T18:43Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:43 x: order agreed with main (main offered x the first heavy slot after tibia's block, because run 7 was preempted). At block end: e2e check (light), then run 8 heavy. Then main's PACK-NEXT-3 review (heavy, ~90 min), then main's Cells baseline and pack. The mode experiment (low priority, predicted small gain) goes after main's review. I asked main to hold compile windows and lib/spiral landings during run 8's notebooks. If main replies that a lib gate can't wait, note 20's rule still decides when run 8 starts, and run 8 is classified contended.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_37 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 37u32, "2026-10-22T18:51Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:51 x: early, compile-free signal for note 32 (zig 0.17.0 vs zig-dev). zig ast-check (parse only) on the 38 generated main.zig files Cells left in %TEMP%\\spiral_cells from 10-21 (the same Zig backend, not necessarily x's cells): 38/38 pass on 0.17.0 and 38/38 on 0.18.0-dev.146. So the backend's syntax is accepted by CI's zig. Whether its std API calls exist in 0.17.0 only shows at compile time, which run 8 checks. ~150 s wall for 76 invocations, 30 MB, ~3 CPU-s, safe beside the live block.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_38 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 38u32, "2026-10-22T18:53Z", "x", "2026-10-22 14:53 x: Get-XCiZigPin now also finds the pin when other keys come before version: under setup-zig's with:, and when the version is quoted (CRLF or LF). Tested on 4 YAML samples (plain, other key first, quoted+CRLF, no setup-zig = none) and on x's ci.yml (0.17.0). Before, any other key first silently skipped the comparison. Self-review of today's x diffs (e2e.ps1, common.ps1, apps/sim/build.ps1): nothing else to fix.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_39 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 39u32, "2026-10-22T19:03Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:03 x: run 8 coordination after tibia's block end (14:57). Main holds compile windows and lib landings (14:43). tibia stopped the 6 queued jobs of its chain; only tibia-cells-package-lines runs (started 14:57, 12 modules, est. 10-25 min). eoie stopped release F 22 s in (15:03, nothing lost) and holds it. All three wait for my 'run 8 end'. e2e passed at 15:00 (X-DONE note 51). Background waiter run8_when_clear.ps1 runs run8_start.ps1 -E2eDone every 30 s and queues run 8 as soon as note 20's rule passes (no compile/live-gui/compiler-install window, at most one other heavy job).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_40 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 40u32, "2026-10-22T19:06Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:06 x: field evidence for note 12's root cause. tibia-cells-package-lines (14:57-15:0x, after main's 12:51 lib landing) hit the memo on every test (blessings 3/3, coins 5/5, cooldowns 7/7, damage 4/4, experience 5/5, geometry 16/16). Its notebook packages (tibia apps/arena, apps/predict: packageDir ../.., packages core- + src-) do not declare spiral-. x's apps/client declares spiral- (lib-first rule) and has missed on every test since any lib edit. Same Cells, same compiler: the only difference is the declared lib dependency, which memo.ex hashes whole.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_41 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 41u32, "2026-10-22T19:11Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:11 x: run 8 STARTED 15:10:54 (job-1792696254468-x-precommit-day8, heavy slot 1), queued by run8_when_clear.ps1 within 30 s of tibia-cells-package-lines releasing its window (no window held, 5 GB free). Its first lines confirm the setup: 36 notebooks read from apps/client/build.ps1; common.ps1 reports 'Cells rustc 1.101.0-nightly (2026-10-17), zig 0.17.0' with no mismatch line, so CI's tools are in use via the new Get-XRustc path and SPIRAL_ZIG; memo off. Watcher b9r6bf5by samples windows every 5 min. Main, tibia (6 jobs) and eoie (release F) hold until 'run 8 end'. Expected ~60-70 min.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_42 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 42u32, "2026-10-22T19:14Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:14 x: mode experiment rescheduled. Order after run 8: main's PACK-NEXT-3 review (heavy, ~90 min, ~16:20-17:50), then tibia's live block PR-25 (~18:00, ~65 min, announced by tibia at 15:1x). The experiment (~57 min: 4 notebooks x 3 modes x 2 rounds, ~570 s per mode-round from run 5's times) cannot fit between them, so it goes after PR-25's 'block end' (~19:05), under note 20's rule, and ends ~20:05, before the 21:00 deadline. If PR-25 slips past 19:50, it moves to the next session and the note 1 rule is unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_43 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 43u32, "2026-10-22T19:15Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:15 x: run 8, first notebook. prelude passed 3/3 on zig 0.17.0 (CI's), the first direct evidence that the Zig backend's output builds and runs on CI's zig. Each zig cell took 210-212 s (run 5 on zig-dev, warm: prelude 86 s in total). The three cells ran in parallel, each paying the cold 0.17.0 std build at once (0.17.0's global cache had never been used here). 212 s is inside the 300 s cell limit with ~30% left. The following notebooks should run warm. By note 27's rule (quiet run, >= 135 s) prelude will join the --timeout list. That is the cold-cache artifact note 32 anticipated (it only pushes toward joining, the safe side), and the rule is applied as written. CI starts cold on every runner, but CI's prelude took 20 s in total at HEAD, so its cold std build is far below 300 s.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_44 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 44u32, "2026-10-22T19:21Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:21 x: RUN 8 FAILED 15:18 (exit 1, 7 min). prelude passed 3/3 on zig 0.17.0. exsss failed 5/5 on its rust target with 'error: multiple input filenames provided (first two filenames are +nightly-2026-10-18 and ...main.rs)'. ROOT CAUSE IS MY NOTE 34 CHANGE, not Rust. Cells (cells.ex rustc_args) adds '+nightly-2026-10-18' whenever rustup_proxy?(rustc) holds, i.e. the path contains '/.cargo/bin/' or '/rustup/'. SPIRAL_RUSTC = Get-XRustc pointed at scoop's ...\\persist\\rustup\\.rustup\\toolchains\\nightly-2026-10-18...\\rustc.exe, a DIRECT rustc whose path contains '/rustup/'. Cells took it for the proxy, and rustc read '+nightly...' as a file. RETRACTION of note 33's premise: local Cells Rust cells were ALREADY on CI's toolchain. PATH rustc is rustup's proxy (scoop\\apps\\rustup\\current\\.cargo\\bin\\rustc.exe), so Cells pins +nightly-2026-10-18 itself, the same as CI (~/.cargo/bin/rustc). My check ran plain 'rustc --version' in apps/cells (2025-05-09 default), without the + argument Cells adds. REVERTED in x: Get-XRustc and SPIRAL_RUSTC in scripts/common.ps1, the line in apps/sim/build.ps1, and the README wording. The zig half stays (pin note in common.ps1, SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0 in run 8), and that part was a real gap. Run 8's script now asserts that PATH rustc is a rustup proxy. All parse-checked. RETRY RULE: rerun run 8 unchanged otherwise (SPIRAL_ZIG 0.17.0, memo off) after main's PACK-NEXT-3 review and tibia's PR-25 block end (~19:05), under note 20's rule. The mode experiment no longer fits before 21:00 and moves to the next session (note 1's rule unchanged).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_45 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 45u32, "2026-10-22T19:23Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:23 x: RULES for run 8's retry, written before acting. (1) First a real-cell smoke test: exsss.spi alone (the notebook that failed; rust+gleam targets) via run_notebook in a 15-min compile window, with SPIRAL_ZIG=0.17.0, SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0, SPIRAL_RUSTC unset, process mode. Main's review compares rows, not timings, so a short compile beside it is acceptable. The retry is queued only if exsss passes 5/5. (2) The retry (x-precommit-day8b, the same run8_notebooks_sim.ps1) is queued with --after job-1792696765489-main-pack-next3-review, so it starts when main's review ends (~16:50). Main re-chains its jobs behind it, and tibia holds its queue behind whatever job is running. Amendment to note 20 for this retry only: if a tibia compile window (e.g. M1, up to 90 min) is held at that moment, the retry starts anyway and is classified contended (note 27's 150 s threshold), because waiting up to 90 min would risk the 21:00 deadline. (3) The mode experiment runs after the retry only if it can end by 20:45, else next session.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_46 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 46u32, "2026-10-22T19:27Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:27 x: exsss smoke test STOPPED by me at 15:26 under memory pressure, no result. Its compile window was granted at 15:24 with 2,249 MB free beside main's heavy review and two compile windows (tibia-typed-pins, eoie-preview-ge2h-crates). Free memory fell to 1,410 MB, then 458 MB, within 2 min. The smoke test was the newest load and mine, so I killed its tree (pwsh exsss_smoke.ps1, pid 10328, verified by command line; 2 children) to protect main's review. Free memory recovered to 2,493 MB, and no orphan erl/dotnet/zig/rustc from it remains. RULE for the rerun: start the smoke test only when free memory is >= 3,500 MB and at most one other compile window is held (checked right before; eoie's admission check looks only at the moment of the request). Then the retry as in note 45.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_47 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 47u32, "2026-10-22T19:28Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:28 x: retry order revised (supersedes note 45 (2)). eoie put release F into heavy slot 2 at ~15:30 (est. end 17:30-17:45, it messages 'F end'). Starting the retry after main's review (~16:50) would put two heavy jobs side by side, and that load pushed free memory to 458 MB at 15:26. RULE: the retry starts after eoie's 'F end', under note 20's rule (main's review done, no compile window held, or note 45's contended exception for a running tibia job), so ~17:45-18:50. Main, tibia and eoie were told; each holds from my 'retry start' message to 'run 8 end'. The smoke test runs before it via smoke_when_ready.ps1 (free >= 3,500 MB, at most 1 compile window, checked every 60 s). Mode experiment: only if it can end by 20:45.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_48 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 48u32, "2026-10-22T19:32Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:32 x: smoke test PASSED 5/5 (15:28-15:31, compile window, 5.2 GB free at start, >= 3.7 GB throughout). exsss.spi, the notebook run 8 failed, with SPIRAL_RUSTC unset (Cells' rustup proxy pins +nightly-2026-10-18), zig 0.17.0, memo off, process mode. Every test ran both targets: rust 20-37 s and gleam 37-55 s per test. This confirms that note 44's revert fixes run 8's failure and that x's rust and gleam cells pass on CI's toolchains. The retry (note 47) is unblocked from x's side; it waits for eoie's 'F end' and main's Cells baseline end (~17:50).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_49 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 49u32, "2026-10-22T19:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:34 x: retry automation (background bww4i02yi, retry_when_ready.ps1). Every 60 s it starts the retry (x-precommit-day8b) when no heavy-build window is held (main's review, main's Cells baseline and eoie's F all done), no live-gui/compiler-install window is held, and free memory is >= 3,500 MB. A held compile window does not block it (note 45 exception; it logs them and the run is classified contended). It launches 'eoie job start' via Start-Process with file redirects, so the supervisor inherits file handles, not the tool pipe (memory eoie-job-start-pipe). Its deadline is 19:40, because a ~65-min run must end by 21:00. On start it prints the job id: then I start run8_watch.ps1 and send 'retry start' + id to main (who re-chains its Cells pack --after it), tibia and eoie.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_50 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 50u32, "2026-10-22T19:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:34 x: progress 55% -> 70%. Done: time breakdown, priority probe, memo root cause handed to main (fix pending in the Cells pack), the --timeout lever applied plus its revisit rule and tooling, CI-vs-local speed measured (~5x), zig 0.17.0 parity check plus the common.ps1 pin note, and the exsss smoke test on CI's tools. Left: (a) run 8's retry (auto-start after the heavy slots clear, ~17:50) and the --timeout revisit it feeds; (b) the mode experiment (note 1 rule), after the retry if it can end by 20:45, else next session; (c) a memo rerun after main's dependency-fingerprint fix.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_51 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 51u32, "2026-10-22T19:36Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:36 x: modes_decide.ps1 (scratch) applies note 1's rule, plus note 3's slowest-cell tie-break and note 4's contention flag, to precommit_modes.ps1's modes.csv, so the decision is mechanical: B or C must have A's pass/fail counts, peak <= A + 1500 MB, median wall (mean of the middle pair) at least 15% below A's, and a slowest cell no higher than A's. Among eligible modes the lowest median wins, with ties to B (fewer daemons); otherwise keep A. Rounds held beside a compile window are flagged for a rerun. Tested on 3 synthetic CSVs, all as expected: B 20% faster -> B; C fastest but +1600 MB and B only 10% faster -> A; B fails a test and C is 16% faster -> C.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_52 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 52u32, "2026-10-22T19:36Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:36 x: RULE for the mode experiment today, written now. Main's Cells pack is chained --after the retry, so it takes a heavy slot when the retry ends. The experiment (~57 min of Cells) beside main's pack would be two Cells-sized heavy jobs, the 15:26 memory squeeze. So it starts today only if, when the retry ends, no other heavy job is held or queued to start, free memory is >= 3,500 MB, and it can end by 20:45. Otherwise it moves to the next session with note 1's rule and modes_decide.ps1 unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_53 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 53u32, "2026-10-22T19:38Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:38 x: new lever (d), a HYPOTHESIS, not measured. Invoke-SpiralTests runs the 36 notebooks strictly one at a time (run 5: 08:22-09:15, 53 min). In run 5, 12 notebooks had a cell >= 60 s, while 16 (mostly the per-crate notebooks: thread 12 s, frames 14, users 17, simframes 17, token 18, net 18, xc 19, transport 19, ...) never had a cell over 30 s. Their wall is probably the fixed per-notebook cost (a BEAM boot with SPIRAL_CELLS_WARM=0, plus a cold dotnet compile per target in process mode), not cell work, so running them 2-3 at a time could cut wall time with little CPU contention per cell. Risk: memory, ~1-1.8 GB per process-mode notebook (15:26 squeeze). RULE, set before measuring (next session, one heavy job, quiet box, >= 6 GB free at start): run the 16 small notebooks at concurrency 1, 2 and 3, twice each in rotating order, recording wall, pass counts and peak own private MB. Adopt the highest concurrency that cuts the small-notebook wall by >= 25% vs 1, with identical pass counts, peak <= 1-at-a-time + 2,000 MB and no cell over 150 s. The 12 large notebooks stay serial either way. It combines with the mode experiment: test (b) first, then (d) on the winning mode.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_54 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 54u32, "2026-10-22T19:39Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:39 x: lever (d) bounded from run 5's own data (output write-time gaps, no compiler). The 16 small notebooks (slowest cell < 30 s) took 560 s of the 53-min stage (median wall 35 s; fixed overhead, i.e. wall minus slowest cell, ~13-20 s each). Running them 2 at a time saves at most ~4.5 min (~9% of the notebook stage), 3 at a time ~6 min. The other 20 notebooks hold ~44 min. So (d) ranks last: behind (c) the memo fix (unchanged notebooks would skip entirely) and (b) the mode experiment (it targets the per-target compile cost inside every cell). Note 53's rule stands, but the experiment runs only after (b) and (c) have landed, if the pre-commit is still over ~30 min.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_55 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 55u32, "2026-10-22T19:41Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:41 x: MEASURED, no compiler: where cell time goes. Summing phases_ms over run 5's 169 test cells in 34 notebooks (8,230 cell-seconds; cells of a notebook overlap, so wall was 53 min): compile 7,999 s (98%), target 156 s (2%), mount 12 s, memo lookups 9 s, cleanup 5 s. In cells.ex run_builder, compile_ms times compile(...), the SpiralCompiler call that turns the cell into target source; the native build+run (rustc, gleam build, zig run) is the separate target phase. So nearly all notebook time is the Spiral compile per cell x target, each type-checking the whole --package . client package plus spiral-. RE-RANKING: (b) the mode experiment (B/C keep a warm compiler daemon instead of a cold dotnet process per target) attacks that 98% directly and moves to first among the x-side levers, with (c) the memo fix (skips unchanged cells entirely) alongside; (d) parallelism stays last. Note 1's rule is unchanged (no prediction is made); this only orders the experiments.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_56 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 56u32, "2026-10-22T19:42Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:42 x: compile cost per target from run 5's phases_ms (quiet box), grouped by a cell's target mix: zig 44 cells, median 35.5 s per target (14-66); gleam+rust (with crate deps) 36 cells, 32.8 s; rust -d serde_json 22 cells, 25.8 s; gleam+rust 12 cells, 48.6 s; rust 10 cells, 32.4 s; gleam+rust+zig 7 cells, 30.6 s. The backend barely matters, so the cost is in the shared front end. A whole x crate (BuildSpiral, one entry file) took 5-11 s on a quiet box (note 7), so one test cell's compile is 3-6x a whole crate's. HYPOTHESIS: package scope. Each cell compiles under '///- --package .' (the whole client package: all 36 notebooks' modules plus spiral-), and a crate build reaches only its entry. If the compiler type-checks the whole package rather than the reached blocks, a warm daemon (mode B/C) helps only by caching that work; narrowing scope (the compiler checking reached blocks only, or per-notebook packages) would cut it at the source. Test after the mode experiment: compile one prelude test cell with --package . vs a package holding only prelude, same compiler, 3 runs each.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_57 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 57u32, "2026-10-22T19:43Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:43 x: note 56's scope hypothesis WEAKENED (static check). The crate entries also declare the client package (args.spi line 1: '///- --package ..'), and the client package is 36 files / 5,897 lines plus spiral- (60 files / 23,808 lines). If BuildSpiral compiles args.spi in that package context, a whole crate (5-11 s) and a test cell (26-49 s per target) see the same scope, and the 3-6x gap comes from how Cells compiles. Candidates: the mount (Cells compiles in a mounted copy, which may defeat the compiler's on-disk caches), the generated per-cell program (cell + opens + test harness), or the process-mode cold start per target. Revised test, 3 runs each, same compiler, quiet box: (1) BuildSpiral of args.spi as the crate build does; (2) the same args.spi through Cells as a single cell; (3) (2) in mode B (daemon). (1) vs (2) isolates the Cells overhead, (2) vs (3) the cold start.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_58 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 58u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:55 x: lever (e) CONFIRMED by main's code read: CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE (cells.ex compile/10 always started bare, and the line-622 branch recompiled with 0i32). The fix is staged as Cells pack step 14: a --test cell with a generated main compiles once, 0i32-ended; non-timeout errors fall back to the old path. It rides main's Cells pack run (after my retry), and main measures it from compile_ms against the baseline. Expected for x: the compile phase (98% of cell time) roughly halves for every test cell. x's runtime count (one prelude cell, expecting 2 compiles before the fix) still runs as an independent 'before' figure. Ranking now: (e) double-compile fix (staged) > (c) memo fix > (b) mode experiment > (d) parallelism.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_59 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 59u32, "2026-10-22T19:57Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:57 x: note 57 narrowed (static). Cells' process-mode one_shot_compile runs 'dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --backend X main.spi out', the same invocation as lib.ps1 BuildSpiral, and x has a single package.spiproj (apps/client), which both resolve. With the double compile fixed (CELLS-UNIT-DOUBLE-COMPILE), the remaining per-target gap (a cell's single compile ~13-25 s vs a crate's 5-11 s) can only come from the mounted package the cell compiles in (Mounts: link vs shadow copies, fresh paths that defeat the compiler's on-disk caches) or from the cell program itself. Arms (1) vs (2) of note 57 isolate it. Re-measure after main's pack, since step 14 changes the baseline.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_60 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 60u32, "2026-10-22T19:58Z", "x", "2026-10-22 15:58 x: mount ruled out as a cause of the per-cell compile gap (static plus run 5 data). Mounts.ensure keys each mount dir by its entries and reuses it once a 'ready' file exists. Packages are placed as junction links (or shadow dirs) at stable paths, and run 5 spent only 12 s in total in the mount phase. What remains for note 57/59: the generated cell program in the package context, e.g. the compiler type-checking every module of the mounted client package for a cell vs only what a crate entry reaches. Arms (1) vs (2) of note 57 decide it, after main's pack.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_61 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 61u32, "2026-10-22T20:46Z", "x", "2026-10-22 16:46 x: retry timing revised with main. Main's review has a matrix stage (BelowNormal, 20 s row timeouts) ~17:15-18:15. eoie's F ends inside it (~17:45). A retry beside the matrix would cost both runs (x's 300 s cells under main's workers, main's rows under x's Cells load). retry_when_ready.ps1 already waits until no heavy-build window is held, so it starts after main's review closes its window (~18:15) and ends ~19:20. The 19:40 start deadline still holds: past it, the retry moves to the next session.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_62 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 62u32, "2026-10-22T21:08Z", "x", "2026-10-22 17:08 x: eoie's release F ended early at 17:09 (a missing Cargo dependency), freeing heavy slot 2. eoie holds F until x's 'retry end' and moves it to tomorrow if that comes after ~18:45. The retry still waits for main's review (matrix stage to ~18:15, at main's request), so it ends ~19:20 and F most likely moves to tomorrow. eoie was told so it doesn't hold the slot empty for nothing; a short F retry ending by ~18:15 doesn't delay x, since retry_when_ready waits for zero heavy jobs anyway.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_63 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 63u32, "2026-10-22T21:14Z", "x", "2026-10-22 17:14 x: RETRY STARTED 17:14 (job-1792703637403-x-precommit-day8b, heavy slot 1), queued by retry_when_ready.ps1 within 60 s of the heavy slots emptying. Main stopped its next3 review early (a compiler crash in the or-join patch; it recomposes as next3b), and eoie's F failed at 17:09 and moved to tomorrow. 5.6 GB was free. One compile window was held at start (tibia-agent-m1, ~10 min left), so the run counts as contended for note 27's thresholds unless the watcher's samples show otherwise. Watcher b6yi99v0u. Main chains next3b, the Cells pack and its compile chain --after this job; tibia holds its queue and eoie holds heavy work until 'retry end' (~18:20).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_64 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 64u32, "2026-10-22T21:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 17:25 x: RETRY FAILED 17:23 (job-1792703637403, exit 1, 9.5 min). prelude 0/3: all 3 zig cells were killed at Cells' 300 s per-cell limit, slowest 342 s. The same cells passed in ~211 s on a cold cache at 15:15. Cause: contention. tibia-f34-rerun (a Cells build check) and tibia-agent-m1 started at 17:14, seconds before the retry (the waiter logged 2 compile windows at start), so two Cells runs shared the box. Prelude's Spiral compile phase took ~4.5 min (dotnet 17:17:21 -> zig 17:21:58). Per heavy-gate-lessons, a timeout-killed cell is not a red result. Rerun count today: none. Main's chain (next3b, Cells pack) was queued --after this job and now holds the slots, and eoie relaunched F at 17:24, so no quiet slot is left before 21:00. Next session: rerun after main's Cells pack lands (the double-compile fix should halve the compile phase). note 27's rule APPLIED to prelude: it qualifies both quiet (212 s >= 135) and contended (342 s >= 150), so line 1 is now '///- --package . --timeout 600000'. xc post-thread --dry-run (offline): 24 posts, 0 cells over one post. tibia offered to stop a just-started Cells job of its own when x's run starts next time.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_65 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 65u32, "2026-10-22T21:26Z", "x", "2026-10-22 17:26 x: note 45's exception RETIRED. It let the retry start with compile windows held and classified it as contended, and that start (beside tibia's just-started Cells check) cost the run: prelude's cells went past 300 s. The 'contended' thresholds of note 27 only make sense for runs that survive. A full x notebook run starts only when no other Cells-running job is active (heavy or compile window), back to note 20's strict rule. tibia offered to stop a just-started Cells job of its own when x's run starts.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_66 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 66u32, "2026-10-22T22:02Z", "x", "2026-10-22 18:02 x: no x run window left today (checked at 18:03). Slot 1: main-pack-next3b-review, 38 min in, ~18:55 end. Slot 2: eoie-release-F, 37 min in, ~19:00 end. Then main's Cells pack (a Cells job, which must not overlap an x Cells run per note 65). Also held: tibia-agent-m1 (C, single core, not Cells). A ~65-min full run would have to start by ~19:50. Plan for next session: start the full notebooks + sim run on CI's zig 0.17.0 once main's Cells pack (double-compile fix) has landed and no Cells job is active, then the --timeout revisit and the mode experiment.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_67 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 67u32, "2026-10-22T23:52Z", "x", "2026-10-22 19:52 x: note 57's arms (1) vs (2) are scripted for next session, not run (scratch compile_arms.ps1, parse-checked). Rounds x {crate: lib.ps1 BuildSpiral of apps/client/args/args.spi; cells: run_notebook on the same file, process mode, memo off}. It reports wall time, plus for cells the summed compile phase, the number of compiles and the mean per compile. args.spi has 4 test cells, all rust (///> rust), so per-cell compile vs one crate build isolates Cells' overhead per compile. Run it only when no other Cells job is active (note 65). Run it before AND after main's Cells pack: before, the double compile shows as ~2x per cell; after, the remaining gap is what notes 57-60 ask about.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_68 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 68u32, "2026-10-22T23:53Z", "x", "2026-10-22 19:53 x: the next full run's runner (scratch run8_notebooks_sim.ps1) now ends its notebook stage with timeout_revisit.ps1 as a DRY RUN (-Run8 quiet, since note 65 lets a full run start only with no other Cells job), so the --timeout decisions print in the job log. Applying them stays a separate, checked step (-Apply), followed by the postability dry-run. Parse-checked.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_69 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 69u32, "2026-10-23T00:08Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:08 x: compile_arms.ps1 (scratchpad, 3-arm test of notes 57-60) checked against real artifacts without a compiler: the Cells ipynb writes metadata.phases_ms as '\"compile\":N' with no space (exsss smoke: 6 compile cells, 45-54 s each), so the script's regex sums the right field. args.spi's entry matches its Cargo.toml (entry args.spi, output args.rs), the notebook has only rust cells, so the Cells arm is per-target rust compile against the crate arm's one Rust compile. BuildSpiral returns only true/false. Next session: start it with 'eoie job start --heavy' and its output in a file, only with no other Cells job active (note 65), after main's Cells pack lands, so the double compile is already gone from the Cells arm.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_70 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 70u32, "2026-10-23T00:12Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:12 x: handoff. The 'scratch' scripts named in notes 26-69 live in this session's scratchpad, which a new session does not see as its own: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\b7595ac3-963d-4714-be58-0267aa270947\\scratchpad. Files: run8_notebooks_sim.ps1, timeout_revisit.ps1, run5_slowest.json, compile_arms.ps1, precommit_modes.ps1, modes_decide.ps1, smoke_when_ready.ps1. Next session's order (note 66): wait for main-cells-pack (running now, heavy-build window) to land; start run8_notebooks_sim.ps1 via 'eoie job start --heavy' with output to a file, only with no other Cells job active (note 65); then timeout_revisit.ps1 -Apply plus the postability dry run; then compile_arms.ps1; then precommit_modes.ps1 and modes_decide.ps1. Copy them into the new session's scratchpad first; they are reference drafts, not repo files.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_71 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 71u32, "2026-10-23T00:13Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:13 x: correction to note 70. A session scratchpad is not a handoff place (memory lab-in-repo), so the 8 drafts were copied to %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next: run8_notebooks_sim, timeout_revisit (+ run5_slowest.json), compile_arms, precommit_modes, modes_decide, smoke_when_ready, plus autonomous-gate.proposed.ps1 (AUTONOMOUS-STOP-GATE note 40). All parse clean and find each other and their outputs through PSScriptRoot only, so they run from there unchanged. Use that folder next session, not note 70's path.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_72 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 72u32, "2026-10-23T00:33Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:33 x: the mode experiment is pre-registered in eoie, before any data: ledger C:\\home\\git\\x\\target\\ledger (tibia's target/ledger convention), X-PRECOMMIT-MODES-B and -C, welch on WallS, group Mode, arms A,B and A,C, 2 runs per arm, with modes_decide's full rule as the decision text (registered 00:31Z). precommit_modes.ps1 (in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next) now also writes modes.tsv, because analyze rejects CSV, and prints both analyze commands. modes_decide.ps1 stays only until eoie has a guarded threshold test (requested in EOIE-EXPERIMENTS); then it is deleted.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_73 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 73u32, "2026-10-23T00:34Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:34 x: note 57's arm test is pre-registered before any data: X-PRECOMMIT-ARMS in C:\\home\\git\\x\\target\\ledger (welch on PerCompileS, group Arm, arms crate,cells, 3 runs each, registered 00:34Z). Rule: the gap is real if the cells mean is >= 1.5x the crate mean and the 95% CI excludes 0. Then file a Cells item on the cell program's scope; otherwise close the question. Run only after main's Cells pack is installed, with no other Cells job active. Fixed in compile_arms.ps1 before it ever ran: crate rows had 3 columns and cells rows 6, and Export-Csv takes its columns from the first (crate) row, so the cells' CompileS and per-compile figures would have been dropped silently. Both arms now emit the same 6 columns plus arms.tsv for analyze.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_74 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 74u32, "2026-10-23T00:45Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:45 x: timeout_revisit.ps1 fixed before its first run. Notes 27-28's 'leaves after two runs below 100 s' is hysteresis, so both runs must come after the notebook joined. The script counted run 5 plus the next run, which is right for the six notebooks that joined at run 5. Prelude joined at the 17:23 retry (342 s contended), though, and its run 5 slowest of 41 s came before that, so one quiet next run would have dropped it again. A joinedAfterRun5 table (prelude = 342) now keeps prelude listed through the next run. It can leave only after that run and one more both stay below 100 s; the revisit after that uses the next run's figures as its baseline.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_75 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 75u32, "2026-10-23T00:49Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:49 x: precommit_modes.ps1's memory guard fixed before its first run. Get-OwnPrivateMB summed every dotnet/erl/beam process started after the experiment began, so other teams' jobs (main's queue runs all night) would have counted as the arm's memory, and the '<= A + 1500 MB' guard of X-PRECOMMIT-MODES-B/C would have judged noise. It now counts only processes descended from the script, or from the compiler daemon listening on the experiment's port, by walking .Parent from a by-name Get-Process. A sample takes 24 ms; the daemon PID is refreshed every 15th sample (Get-NetTCPConnection: 7.5 s cold, 1.3 s warm). A full WMI enumeration would have cost 0.8-5.3 s per one-second sample. Tested: parent links resolve to this shell's PID; parse clean.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_76 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 76u32, "2026-10-23T00:52Z", "x", "2026-10-22 20:52 x: last handoff script fixed (smoke_when_ready.ps1 in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next). It ended with 'exit 0' whatever the window run returned, so a failed smoke looked like success to its watcher. It now exits with the run's code, and its timeout message uses -Minutes instead of a fixed 80. Its default target exsss_smoke.ps1 was missing from the handoff folder and is now copied; it finds its output through PSScriptRoot. All 4 runner scripts in the folder have had a data-flow read (notes 73-76).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_77 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 77u32, "2026-10-23T03:10Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:10 x: rules for the night run (user: iterate until 07:00), written before anything starts. N1: submit one heavy job 'x-run8-night' now, so it holds a FIFO queue position. It runs %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next\\night_run.ps1 with --done-within until 06:55, so a job still queued then expires. N2: inside the job, start the notebooks only when no foreign Cells work is running (no process outside my tree whose command line names run_notebook.ps1, apps\\cells or a Cells mix test), checking every 60 s for up to 30 min. If still busy, exit 75 (deferred) and resubmit once if it is before 05:40. N3: never stop another team's job on my own judgment. Main offered to stop their queued jobs for x, but hopacsota is a top user priority (memory priority-order), so I only ask main whether they want x ahead of main-hopacsota-promotion, and they decide. N4: results. All 36 notebooks + sim green on zig 0.17.0 closes X-DONE item 4 (CI's zig proven locally). A notebook failure gets triaged: zig-version-only failures (passes on 0.18-dev in run 5) go to an item for main. Contention timeouts are reported as such and are no verdict. timeout_revisit runs dry, and -Apply follows only for a run classified quiet: no foreign Cells work and at most 1 compile window during it. N5: experiments ARMS and MODES only after main-cells-pack has finished, and only if they fit before 06:55.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_78 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 78u32, "2026-10-23T03:16Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:16 x: N1 amended. The installed eoie lacks --done-within (EOIE-JOBS note), so job-1792725371444-x-run8-night is queued heavy with --after main-cells-pack and no time cap. I stop it myself if it is still queued at 06:55. Also in the night runner before it ran: Invoke-Block calls 'exit' on failure in a non-interactive host (core.ps1:100), so per-notebook try/catch would never fire. run8 now calls the same run_notebook.ps1 with the same env as Invoke-SpiralTests and judges each notebook by its exit code, so one failure no longer hides the other 35. The sim runs regardless, and the revisit only on a clean notebook stage. Tibia queued a live block (up to 180 min, gives up at 04:15), and jobs requested after it wait for it, so tonight's run may not fit before 07:00.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_79 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 79u32, "2026-10-23T03:25Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:25 x: N1 amended again. The --after job held a heavy-build window idle and overtook tibia's older live-gui request, so it was stopped at 23:22 (it never started). Now a plain job, x-night-submitter (job-1792725861222), submits 'x-run8-night' (heavy, no --after) once no live-gui request is waiting, giving up at 05:40. N2's start guard makes the run wait for any foreign Cells runner, main-cells-pack included, so the old 'after cells-pack' intent still holds for Cells contention. N5 is unchanged: the experiments still wait for cells-pack.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_80 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 80u32, "2026-10-23T03:37Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:37 x: the submitter fired at 23:36, when tibia released their live-gui request for eoie's F7, but the heavy job it started was lost after 209 ms: a job started inside an eoie job dies with it (EOIE-JOBS note). Resubmitted directly from my shell at 23:37 as job-1792726645820-x-run8-night (heavy, queued behind eoie-release-F7). Main had stopped their queued jobs (hopacsota-promotion, cells-pack, next4-review) by then, so N5's experiments have no cells-pack to wait for tonight and are off.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_81 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 81u32, "2026-10-23T03:44Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:44 x: N2 applied early. x-run8-night got heavy slot 1 at 23:37:49 and its guard waited on one foreign Cells runner: eoie-release-F6's run_notebook.ps1 (PID 124640, started 22:08). That runner's tree used 0.1 CPU-s in 10 s (one zig child), so it holds its slot but adds no CPU contention. Still, x's guard would have kept heavy slot 1 idle for up to 30 min while main-pack-next5c-review waited with its window held, and free memory was 1.6 GB. So I stopped the job at 23:43, inside N2's intent (defer rather than hold a slot), before any notebook ran. Next start by the same rules: once F6 has settled, no live-gui request is waiting and free memory is >= 3.5 GB. The resubmission comes from my shell at a check-in, never from inside a job. eoie was told the runner looks idle.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_82 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 82u32, "2026-10-23T03:47Z", "x", "2026-10-22 23:47 x: correction to note 81. F6's runner was slow, not idle (eoie, 23:4x): each cell compiles in the shared Cells compiler daemon (its dotnet processes, outside the runner's tree), and the daemon keeps retiring itself at its 4096 MB free-memory floor. My 10 s sample covered only the runner tree, so 'adds no CPU contention' was wrong; the deferral stands on N2 itself. Schedule from eoie: F6 ends at its 4 h limit around 00:47, then F7 (Cells tests, release build, install) runs until about 03:30-04:00, then tibia's live block, up to 180 min. N6, written now: submit x-run8-night only after F7 has settled, no live-gui request is waiting, at least 3.5 GB is free and it is before 05:25 (65 min of run + sim + slack before 06:55). Otherwise there is no run tonight, and it moves to the next session. A notebook run that is cut short proves nothing.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_83 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 83u32, "2026-10-23T04:16Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:16 x: N6 amended before any outcome. At 00:16 eoie-release-F7 had left the queue and eoie-release-F8 was waiting in its place (same role: Cells tests + release). N6 named F7 by id, but its intent was 'after eoie's release chain', so the watcher now waits until no eoie-release-* job is queued or running, besides no live-gui waiting, >= 3.5 GB free and before 05:25.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_84 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 84u32, "2026-10-23T04:55Z", "x", "2026-10-23 00:55 x: the background watcher round was reaped by Claude Code's low-memory guard while the session was idle (free memory now 3352 MB). The harness says not to restart it unasked, so N6 is now checked in the foreground at each :17/:47 check-in. A check that finds all four conditions true (no eoie-release job, no live-gui waiting, >= 3.5 GB free, before 05:25) submits x-run8-night from the shell; otherwise nothing is submitted, and after 05:25 the run moves to the next session.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_85 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 85u32, "2026-10-23T06:51Z", "x", "2026-10-23 02:51 x: no x-run8-night tonight, by N6. Tibia's live block started at 02:50 and runs until about 05:00-05:45 (no compiles or heavy jobs of any size during it), and eoie's release F9 waits to run right after it. So no eoie-release-free window opens before N6's 05:25 latest start. Nothing x started tonight ran a notebook. The full run on CI's zig 0.17.0 (all 36 notebooks + sim) moves to the next session, using the runners in %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\x-precommit-next (night_run.ps1 guard, run8_notebooks_sim.ps1 per-notebook verdicts) and the pre-registered experiments in x/target/ledger.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_86 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 86u32, "2026-10-23T13:57Z", "x", "2026-10-23 09:57 x: N7, written before any outcome; it replaces last night's N6, which never fired. Submit x-run8 (run8_notebooks_sim.ps1 via night_run.ps1's guard: all 36 notebooks + sim on CI's zig 0.17.0, SPIRAL_ZIG pinned, memo off) as ONE eoie heavy job when ALL of these hold at a check-in: (1) tibia has sent 'block end' for today's PC-9/PR-26 block; (2) my after_block jobs (x-world\\after_block.ps1: the resume, sim, lib and tree_memory checks) have settled; (3) no eoie-release-* job is queued or running; (4) at least 3.5 GB free; (5) it is before 16:30, so 65 min of run + sim + slack finish before 18:00. If (5) fails, there is no run today, and it moves again. A notebook run that is cut short proves nothing, so it is never started late. The run reads x's tree as staged (read-only) and writes only target/ledger, as before.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_87 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 87u32, "2026-10-23T21:31Z", "x", "2026-10-23 17:31 x: data point from landing trial-4 (x's real build.ps1 -fast 1 on a %TEMP% copy, with peer Cells chains and main's gates running, so contended, not a baseline). Per-module Spiral to Rust times: args 70 s, assemble 191 s, checks 512 s (285 generated lines, so it isn't size-bound and looks like contention or compiler-server wait), digest 273 s. The run timed out at 60 min before fetch finished. Takeaway for this item: under contention the serial per-module BuildSpiral dominates; measure uncontended before ranking options (pre-registered rule still applies).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_88 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 88u32, "2026-10-23T22:54Z", "x", "2026-10-23 18:54 x: N8, written before any outcome; it replaces N7, whose 16:30 start limit passed with no window (tibia's 18:03 live block, eoie-release-GE2 holding a heavy slot until ~21:48). Submit x-run8 (run8_notebooks_sim.ps1 via night_run.ps1's guard: all 36 notebooks + sim on CI's zig 0.17.0, SPIRAL_ZIG pinned, memo off) as ONE eoie heavy job at the first check-in of a later session where ALL hold: (1) no announced live block is open (the last tibia block got its 'block end'); (2) no eoie-release-* job queued or running; (3) at least 3.5 GB free; (4) the session's stop time is at least 90 min away (65 min run + sim + slack); (5) x's own after-block chains (after_block2.ps1, after_block_builds.ps1) have settled, since they read the same scratch trees. Tonight cannot meet (2) and (4) before 21:00, so nothing is submitted this session. Unchanged: verdicts per notebook; results go to this item and the ledger experiments in x/target/ledger.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_89 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 89u32, "2026-10-24T03:40Z", "x", "23:40 x: N8 armed for tonight, applied mechanically. decide_run8.ps1 (in %TEMP%\\x-precommit-next) runs as job x-run8-decide after x-deps-builds, which covers (5). It waits on foreign Cells runners without holding a slot, then checks (1) no live-gui window held or waiting, (2) no eoie-release-* job queued or running (it waits on one), (3) at least 3584 MB free, (4) at least 90 min to the 07:00 stop. Only then does it run night_run.ps1 -GuardMinutes 5 as ONE heavy job (eoie job run --heavy x-run8). (3) or (1) failing means deferred (exit 75), and a later check-in requeues it while (4) still holds. Predicates tested with a near stop time; it exited 75 at (4) and printed (3)=1710 MB.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_90 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 90u32, "2026-10-24T05:08Z", "x", "10-24 01:08 x: N8 (5) fix. x-run8-decide was chained only after x-deps-builds, so it started while x's other check chains were still pending, against (5). Stopped it (it was waiting on (2): eoie-release-GE3 is running) and requeued it as x-run8-decide2 --after chain21c (job-1792817007173-x-chain21c), x's longest chain. (4) still refuses it if that settles less than 90 min before 07:00.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_91 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 91u32, "2026-10-27T12:16Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:14 x: measured breakdown in X-VERIFY-TIMING (done): the corpus ~10 min per sim build, notebooks ~20 min client plus the sim's, regen ~5 min, then cargo and e2e. The quickest lever left that needs no tool work: notebook files are independent, so x/scripts/verify.ps1 could run them across N processes (Invoke-SpiralTests per file in parallel jobs, then Assert-SpiralTotals over all outputs).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_92 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 92u32, "2026-10-27T12:59Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:59 x: RULE for the parallel-notebook lever, written before any measurement. Candidate P: x/scripts/verify.ps1 -ParallelNotebooks N builds with -fast 1, then runs each notebook file (the module lists parsed from apps/client/build.ps1 and apps/sim/build.ps1, which stay the source of truth) in up to N processes, and asserts every spiral-tests total with Assert-SpiralTotals. Baseline S: the same tree's sequential notebooks inside build.ps1. Measure on the node at a comparable load (note its cpu% at start), N = 3, notebook wall time only. ADOPT P as verify's default when its wall time is <= 0.6 x S's AND every file's passed/executed equals S's AND no Cells run is killed by the memory guard; otherwise keep S and record why. Never raise N after a miss without a new rule.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_93 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 93u32, "2026-10-27T13:00Z", "x", "2026-10-27 09:00 x: candidate P implemented in the tree (x/scripts/verify.ps1, unstaged): -ParallelNotebooks N builds the client with -fast 1, then runs the 41 client notebooks (parsed from apps/client/build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests list, checked: 41 found) in up to N pwsh processes, throws unless every module printed a spiral-tests total, and asserts them with Assert-SpiralTotals. The sim stays sequential, because its -fast also skips the real-Cells executor and thread_run checks, which are not notebooks. Off by default; measured per the rule above at the next verify, against verify16b's sequential client notebooks as S.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_94 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 94u32, "2026-10-27T16:57Z", "x", "2026-10-27 12:57 x: METHOD revised before any measurement (no number taken yet; the 0.6 bar is unchanged). Per the user's 10-27 rule (no waiting for a quiet machine; benches must hold up under load), the \"comparable load\" baseline from another run is dropped. New method, A/B in ONE loaded run: verify.ps1 -ParallelNotebooks 3 -BenchRounds 3 builds the client once (-fast 1), then runs R rounds over the same 41 client notebooks, each round both arms in alternating order (round 1 S then P, round 2 P then S, round 3 S then P). S = the same per-file harness at throttle 1; P = throttle 3, so harness overhead cancels and only parallelism differs. SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 so no round replays memoized cells (memo_hits is part of every total, so any replay would also show up as a totals mismatch). Node cpu% is printed at each arm's start, reported beside the number. ADOPT P as verify's default iff the median over rounds of P/S wall time <= 0.6 AND every arm's sorted spiral-tests totals are identical to round 1 S's AND no arm throws. Otherwise keep sequential and record the ratios.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_95 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 95u32, "2026-10-27T17:48Z", "x", "2026-10-27 13:48 x: bench runs so far measured nothing. bench1 was stopped because my Assert-SpiralTotals rejected blank lines (fixed: AllowEmptyString). bench2's client build -fast 1 ran the e2e against a README still holding the links.txt placeholder. The bench no longer builds at all: Cells compiles every notebook cell itself, and the build was not part of either arm, so the rule's arms are unchanged (same 41 notebooks, throttle 1 vs 3, alternating, memo off). Running as job-1793123365418 beside verify20 (job-1793123162251); per the user's rule the load is reported, not avoided.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_96 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 96u32, "2026-10-27T19:23Z", "x", "2026-10-27 15:23 x: bench3 (job-1793123365418) gave one data point and found a real failure. (1) Sequential arm, memo OFF, node under load: 41 notebooks in 4695 s (78 min), against about 20 min of notebook time in a normal verify with memo on, so the memo already removes most of it and parallelism can only shave the cold remainder. (2) It then threw on token/token's renewal_replaces_and_appends_keys (Rust), a test that passed in every memo-on verify: the test expects the appended X_OAUTH2_REFRESH_TOKEN line without \\r while line_end appends \\r when the file uses CRLF, and the code is right. Suspected stale memo; to be proven by verify20's memo field for that test (job-1793123162251). Test corrected (and \\r made visible as <CR> in the comparison); proof memo-off as job-1793129063208. The rule stays as registered (3 rounds, median <= 0.6); at about 6 h for 3 rounds memo-off, the bench reruns as a long node job after the token proof, on a re-synced mirror.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_97 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 97u32, "2026-10-28T00:15Z", "x", "2026-10-27 20:15 x: REPORTING caveat written before round 3's result (the decision rule is unchanged): any round whose two arms started more than 40 cpu points apart is flagged load-confounded in the report (round 2 already is: 100% vs 37%). If the median round itself is flagged, the verdict is reported as provisional and the bench is rerun once at a steadier load before verify's default changes. Reason: main's 20:15 review; Job Object CPU cannot replace wall time here, since parallelism saves wall time, not CPU.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_98 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 98u32, "2026-10-28T01:05Z", "x", "2026-10-27 21:05 x: load note for round 3, per fiscal (20:4x): fiscal launched set-proof (fiscal-set-seta and fiscal-set-setb, 2 Cells jobs each) on the node at about 20:43, during bench4's round 3, beside x's verify22, defuse and lib-gate jobs. Round 3's arms are reported with that load beside them.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_99 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 99u32, "2026-10-28T01:45Z", "x", "2026-10-27 21:45 x: VERDICT by the note 94 rule: NOT ADOPTED, verify keeps sequential notebooks (its default already). bench4 (job-1793129519981, 22396 s): round 1 S 4835 s / P 2297 s = 0.475 (cpu 100/100 at start); round 2 P 2143 / S 3040 = 0.705 (90/37: load-confounded per note 97); round 3 S 5027 / P 5035 = 1.00 (88 at start, with fiscal's set-proof on the node from 20:43), and round 3's P arm THREW: 9 tests in defuse, splitter and reading hit the 300 s cell budget. Two clauses fail (an arm threw; median 0.705 > 0.6). Per the note 97 caveat this is a no under heavy load, not proof that parallelism can't help: rounds 2 and 3 are confounded. Lesson applied: rust+gleam notebooks need the 900 s budget under load, so glyphs, reading, splitter, tlds, paging and protocol now carry --timeout 900000 like defuse. A rerun only makes sense once those budgets are in.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_100 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 100u32, "2026-10-28T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-28 06:29 x groom: PAUSED. The registered bench gave its verdict (note 99: not adopted under load). Next step, only when the node has a steadier hour: rerun -BenchRounds 3 with the 900 s cell budgets now in. Nothing queued.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_101 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 101u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "x", "2026-10-30T02:00Z groom: blocker restated. n100's 'only when the node has a steadier hour' contradicts the standing no-quiet-machine-wait rule (queues keep boxes full; make benches robust under load). Paused -> Planned 70: the next run is -BenchRounds 3 with the 900 s cell budgets AND an in-job A/A row (S vs S) per the A/B-needs-in-job-A/A rule, so load confounding is measured inside the job instead of waited out. Queue it after verify33 and x-s3/S1b free the node's x slots.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_102 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 102u32, "2026-10-30T02:43Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:5x x: in-job A/A built into the bench (x/scripts/verify.ps1 Invoke-NotebookBench, live and unstaged). Each round is now a sandwich, sequential / parallel / sequential: ratio = P / mean(S1, S2) and A/A = S2 / S1. Any round with |A/A - 1| > 0.15 VOIDs the verdict ('load drift exceeds the effect') instead of reporting it. Adoption rule unchanged (median ratio <= 0.6). Logic checked against a mocked runner via an AST extract: stable 100/50/102 x3 gives median 0.495 with A/A 1.02 (adopt line); drifting 100/50/130 x3 gives VOID (3/3 rounds out of band), while the old rule would have read its 0.435 as a pass. Next run: -BenchRounds 3 -ParallelNotebooks 4 on the node after verify33 frees x's slot.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_103 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 103u32, "2026-10-30T03:55Z", "x", "2026-10-29 23:5x x: PRE-REGISTERED run shape. launch-x-bench-aa.ps1 (C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\launchers; fresh mirror xbench, pack 7 core 9C899B30495F7DC5, 8 h cap): verify.ps1 -BenchRounds 2 -ParallelNotebooks 4 with the sandwich arms S/P/S. 2 rounds, not 3: bench4's 2 arms x 3 rounds took 22,396 s, so 3 arms x 3 rounds would be about 9.3 h, past the cap. With 2 rounds the reported 'median' is the larger ratio (index floor(2/2) = 1), the conservative side. Rule unchanged: adopt if that ratio <= 0.6 and both rounds' A/A are within 0.85-1.15, else VOID (drift) or NOT ADOPTED. Launches when verify33 ends and the Cells window is closed. posts.spi carries --timeout 900000 in the live tree, so no 300 s cell kills.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_104 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 104u32, "2026-10-30T10:31Z", "x", "2026-10-30 06:31 x: LAUNCHED as pre-registered (n103): x-bench-aa job-1793356269763 (xbench mirror, pack 7 F2F6F4CB5A18, -BenchRounds 2 -ParallelNotebooks 4, sandwich S/P/S with in-job A/A). Its first lines confirm posts.spi carries --timeout 900000. Planned -> Active. Verdict by n101/n103's rule when it ends (about 6 h).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_105 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 105u32, "2026-10-30T14:57Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:5x x: x-bench-aa VERDICT by n102/n103's rule: VOID (load drift). Round 1: S1 5,361 s (cpu 42% at start), P(4) 4,247 s (cpu 100%), and S2, started 09:11:25 node time, was still running at 10:55:38, so S2 >= 6,253 s and A/A >= 1.166 > 1.15. Any out-of-band round VOIDs the bench, and ADOPT was already out of reach after P (ratio <= 0.6 needs S2 >= 8,795, i.e. A/A 1.64). Round 1 settled the verdict, so I STOPPED my own job (job stop, exit 143, process tree stopped; it wrote only to its xbench mirror) to free about 4.5 h of node CPU for the 4 other lanes' jobs. Rule deviation, stated: round 2 never ran; it could not change the outcome. LESSON: 90-minute arms on a node that runs 4-5 lanes' jobs drift more than the effect being measured, so the sandwich catches it but cannot fix it. Next design (to pre-register before any run): short arms (a fixed 8-notebook subset of the 43, about 10-15 min per arm) in ABBA order, 6 rounds, verdict on the median ratio with the same A/A band. Shorter arms shrink the drift window. Active -> Planned 70; owner x.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_106 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 106u32, "2026-10-30T14:57Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:0x x: enabler for n105's short-arm design, live and unstaged in x/scripts/verify.ps1 (parse-checked): -BenchRounds now takes -Notebooks as the benched subset (the bench branch runs before the plain -Notebooks branch; without -Notebooks it benches the full build.ps1 list as before). ABBA dropped from the design: the S/P/S sandwich is already symmetric, so linear drift cancels in P / mean(S1, S2); the lever is arm length. Open before pre-registering: per-notebook sequential durations, to pick a subset whose parallel tail resembles the full 43's (a uniform subset would overstate the speedup). The bench prints only arm totals, so the first step is a duration census (one -Notebooks run that prints each file's spiral-tests line, or the slowest_ms fields of a verify log).")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_107 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 107u32, "2026-10-30T14:58Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:0x x: partial duration census from the stopped bench's S2 arm (xbench target\\build\\client\\notebooks .ipynb write times, sequential, so the gaps are per-notebook durations under load), in build.ps1 order: prelude ~?, paging 234 s, glyphs 338, tlds 277, defuse 434, protocol 619, splitter 784, posts 260, reading 224, cli 336, fields 143, oauth1 124, wire 407, transport 123; the 15th was still running 31 min later when the job was stopped. S2 averaged about 300 s per notebook against S1's 125 s (5,361 s / 43), so node load rose about 2.4x between arms. That is the drift VOID quantified, not noise at the band edge. Census for the other 28 notebooks still needed before choosing a subset.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_108 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 108u32, "2026-10-30T15:54Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:5x x: FULL DURATION CENSUS (verify33's sequential client build, xv30 target\\build\\client\\notebooks, .ipynb write gaps, 10-30 00:40-02:47, node under load): giants paging 1,293 s and glyphs 1,451 s (early in the run, likely the cold Rust/Cells start); mid tlds 432, defuse 435, protocol 330, splitter 596, posts 324, reading 232, cli 358, wire 304; small fields 43, oauth1 39, transport 44; the 29 crate notebooks 14-112 s (median about 58). prelude's own time is not measurable (no prior write). The sum without prelude is about 7,600 s, so the parallelism-4 lower bound is max(glyphs 1,451, 7,600 / 4) = about 1,900 s, a ratio floor near 0.25. x-bench-aa's 0.79 was contention, not the schedule. PRE-REGISTERED short-arm run (x-bench-short, after pack 8 reaches the node and x has a slot): verify.ps1 -BenchRounds 4 -ParallelNotebooks 4 -Notebooks posts,reading,locate/locate,entities/entities,weights/weights,thread/thread,write/write,xc/xc. That is 2 mid + 6 crates, about 980 s sequential, so each S/P/S round is about 40 min and the drift window is a fifth of x-bench-aa's. Giants are excluded because they are first-run effects, which a rerun inside the same job does not repeat. Rule unchanged: ADOPT if the median ratio <= 0.6 and every round's A/A is within 0.85-1.15; any round outside the band = VOID; otherwise NOT ADOPTED. With 4 rounds the median is the 3rd-smallest ratio (index 2), the conservative side.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_109 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 109u32, "2026-11-01T06:42Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:42 x: CI evidence for this item (moved here from X-CI-NOTEBOOK-SPEED, a duplicate I filed before searching). The last green CI run (41240026259, ubuntu, 61 min in workflow.ps1): client notebooks 20:47 -> 21:11 (~24 min), sim notebooks 21:17 -> 21:41 (~24 min). So ~48 of 61 CI minutes are the same serial Cells notebooks this item measures locally. CI-side per-notebook gaps: runner_checks 178 s, scheduler 137 s, protocol 133 s, oauth1 131 s, defuse 106 s. A lever adopted here shortens CI by the same share. GitHub's 4 cores make the parallelism-4 floor (n of 10-30 11:5x, ratio ~0.25) reachable there too.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_110 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 110u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:36 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x. Estimate 90 -> 180: the census is done, but each lever needs a CI or node run of ~60 min, and n of 10-30 measured the levers one at a time. Load heavy stays (a full notebook run per arm). P2 is justified: about 48 of 61 CI minutes (n, 11-01) and 53-75 local minutes per pre-commit.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): one adopted lever lowers TOTAL notebook time by >= 30% against the same commit's baseline, measured in ONE job with an in-job A/A (memory ab-needs-in-job-aa), with every notebook still passing on 2 consecutive runs. Artifact: arm totals and the per-notebook table on this item, plus the lever's diff in x/scripts/common.ps1 Invoke-SpiralTests.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: lever (a), parallel notebooks. verify.ps1 already has -ParallelNotebooks N and Invoke-ParallelNotebooks. Run 'verify.ps1 -Part client -ParallelNotebooks 4' against a sequential arm on the node, with the S/P/S sandwich from n of 10-30 11:0x.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: n/a for product types (build tooling, PowerShell glue). If the bench grows into a kit verb it becomes Spiral, with ':| arm = Sequential | Parallel : i32' and a typed duration row, no string-parsed timings.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_111 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 111u32, "2026-11-04T01:12Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:11 x: ORPHAN SWEEP (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, x's corner: open x items with no note in 48 h). Owner x, heavy (one node run per lever). Next step unchanged: one lever per run, when the node queue is free of x proofs.")
inl note_of_x_precommit_speed_112 () = Note ("X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED", 112u32, "2026-11-10T12:06Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 08:2x slice-on-demand check: NOT sliced, because the next step is a node RUN (no authoring step to draft). Structured wait row added so the slice prompt stops offering it: when:quiet-node-slot (ganymede or moon after TRAIN43f).")
