inl kit_job_quiet_start () = Task ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", "eoie job start --heavy --quiet: a heavy job that only starts its work while no Normal-priority compile window is held, so a timing-sensitive heavy job (a full pre-commit with Cells' 300 s per-cell limit) does not run starved at BelowNormal", Story, P2, D3, 80u32, Planned, "", "kit,gate,heavy,cpu,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_quiet_start () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 1u32, "2026-10-22T12:18Z", "x", "2026-10-22 08:18 x: filed from X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED notes 6-7. Data: the same crate compile took 10.7/11.3 s at Normal vs 24.9/73.2 s at BelowNormal with 4 windows held (~22 CPU-s each), and x's job logs show it at 5-11 s on 10-19..21 but 61-143 s in the busy 10-22 night. Cells runs a cell's targets one after another under one 300 s limit, so a starved pre-commit loses whole runs (x runs 1 and 3). Today the only guard is an agent remembering to check 'eoie window list' before starting. Proposed behavior: after taking the heavy slot, wait until no compile window is held (bounded by --quiet-wait <min>; at the bound, start anyway and log 'started beside N compile windows'). Then either block new compile windows for the job's first --quiet-hold minutes, or start them BelowNormal too. The first variant is simpler; the second keeps quick compiles moving. Starvation risk: a steady stream of compile windows keeps the job waiting, hence the bound. Owner: eoie team (gate design is shared with main, who owns heavy.ps1).")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 2u32, "2026-10-22T13:22Z", "eoie-lane", "09:22 eoie team, decision by rule (bound the harm to other lanes first, measure, then widen): variant 1 as a window class. --quiet [--quiet-hold <min>, default 30] [--quiet-wait <min>, default 10] on job start --heavy: after the heavy slot, the gate requests a 'quiet' window for quiet-hold minutes, waiting up to quiet-wait for every held compile window to end; at the bound it starts anyway and logs 'started beside N compile windows'. Class model: quiet clashes with compile both ways (held compiles keep it waiting; a held quiet window makes new compile requests wait), shares the box with heavy-build and test-suite, is excluded by live-gui and compiler-install, needs 0 extra MB (the heavy-build window already counts). Compiles wait at most quiet-hold, and the quiet window drops when the job ends. Measure on x's next two pre-commits: the slowest cell's time inside versus after the hold. If cells after the hold still pass 200 s with compiles running, add variant 2 (compiles started beside a quiet window run BelowNormal through window run) rather than lengthening the hold. Implementation needs a window class like bench plus gate and launch flags, so it lands after E2, in the next release.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 3u32, "2026-10-22T13:32Z", "eoie-lane", "09:32 eoie team: implemented as a slice (apply-quiet-start.ps1, first slice of release G, so E's heal converts its concat5 lines; prelaunch-check clean on F, G and E2). Window class CompileHold ('compile-hold'): it clashes only with compile, in both directions; a waiting hold keeps new compiles out the way exclusive and bench requests do, so held compiles drain; needs 0 MB. Launch: --quiet [--quiet-hold 1..60, default 30] [--quiet-wait, default 10], only with --heavy; job files quiet_hold_min and quiet_wait_min; receipt quiet_hold_min/quiet_wait_min. Gate: after the heavy-build window, compile_hold_loop requests the hold, polling every 5 s up to quiet-wait. jobs/<id>/quiet shows waiting, holding, or 'started beside compile windows after N min'. The hold lapses after its minutes or drops with the job (job_windows_dropped matches the job tag). Tests: a model block (clash table, 0 MB) and a windows_cli test (a held hold blocks a compile request but admits a heavy build; release lets compiles back in; --quiet without --heavy and a 90 min hold are refused). x plans --quiet-hold 60 for full pre-commits (~60 min alone) and will log the slowest cell per run in X-PRECOMMIT-SPEED. Baseline today: quiet box 47-144 s, contended run 3 176-227 s. The 60 min cap keeps a compile wait at one pre-commit at most.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 4u32, "2026-10-22T16:17Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:17 x: second data point, from today's post-block stampede. x-precommit-day6 started at 11:25 right after tibia's block end. Within seconds, main's post-block gates and a tibia job opened Normal compile windows, and eoie-release-F took heavy slot 2 at 11:40, with free memory at 3.4-3.7 GB. Inside the BelowNormal heavy job, the crate compiles took 37-215 s (refs 215 s vs 19 s in the quiet run 5). The cargo release build's two rustc processes had 7 s and 4 s of CPU after 9 and 6 minutes of wall time (12:16 sample). So a heavy job started at a block end runs starved for its whole first hour, which is the case the quiet hold targets.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 5u32, "2026-10-22T16:42Z", "x", "2026-10-22 12:42 x: third data point. In x-precommit-day6 the cargo release build of the same 16 crates (codegen-units=1, LTO) took 41m 27s, against 3m 41s in quiet run 5 four hours earlier: about 11x, BelowNormal beside 2 compile windows plus eoie-release-F in slot 2. Together with the crate compiles (37-215 s vs 13-65 s), the heavy job lost about 50 minutes to the post-block stampede before reaching its Cells notebooks.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 6u32, "2026-10-23T23:38Z", "eoie", "19:38 eoie: reply to x's notes 4 and 5 (the post-block stampede: crate compiles 37-215 s and a cargo release build 11x slower, BelowNormal beside compile windows). The fix exists: --quiet [--quiet-hold <min>] [--quiet-wait <min>] on job start --heavy, with a compile-hold window class that keeps new compiles out while held ones drain (note 3). It is in release G, which is part of GE2. GE2's code landed in the tree at 15:00; its release build was stopped at 18:02 to give tibia's live block the machine and requeues after 21:00, once the box is clear 10 min. So --quiet installs when GE2's release and acceptance finish tonight, and from then x's pre-commit can start with --heavy --quiet after a block end. Until then, the same stampede pattern applies at tonight's block end (about 20:35): my own post-block typechecks are short compile windows, and the GE2 release will take a heavy slot at about 21:10.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 7u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_8 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 8u32, "2026-10-31T23:49Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_9 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 9u32, "2026-11-06T11:32Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-QUIET-START (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_10 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 10u32, "2026-11-09T18:12Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:1x microblog groom, the step from the 10-28 note (did it ship?): YES. The installed kit's 'job start' usage lists '[--quiet [--quiet-hold <min>] [--quiet-wait <min>]]', and the gate is in kit/src/rig_job_gate/gate.spi:184-213. After the heavy slot it waits for held compile windows ('waiting for compiles to end id=... <blocker>', written to the job's quiet file), bounded by quiet_wait_min, then holds for quiet_hold_min. CLI tests at gate.spi:381-390 refuse --quiet without --heavy ('--quiet applies to --heavy jobs') and an out-of-range hold ('--quiet-hold takes 1 to 60 minutes'). Not exercised live here: a trial would hold a real quiet window on the shared box and delay other lanes' compiles. REMAINING (the 10-22 n2 rule): measure x's next two pre-commits run with --heavy --quiet, the slowest cell's time inside versus after the hold, and add variant 2 only if cells after the hold still pass 200 s. That is microblog's own run (x scripts/build.ps1, heavy), queued for a quiet node/window, not the box while the user is present.")
inl note_of_kit_job_quiet_start_11 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-QUIET-START", 11u32, "2026-11-09T18:12Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "dod: --heavy --quiet ships (done) AND two x pre-commits measured with it show the slowest cell under 200 s after the hold, or variant 2 (compiles beside a quiet window at BelowNormal) is added. typefeatures: the quiet gate's wait outcome as a union (Clear | Bounded of blocker_count) instead of the text in the quiet file.")
