inl kit_tree_read_record () = Task ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", "One record for tree leases: release and regeneration scripts hold a tree-read window (eoie window run/request) instead of writing tree-leases/*.lease files; the PreToolUse hook and eoie's write verbs read the window store; the .lease directory goes away", TaskKind, P1, D3, 65u32, Planned, "KIT-MACHINE-WINDOWS,KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,lease,windows,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_tree_read_record () = Planning ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_1 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 1u32, "2026-10-22T19:48Z", "eoie-lane", "15:48 rule written before any probe: the cutover (scripts plus hook, no compile) is applied after F installs only if all of these hold on the installed binary, using a scratch folder as the leased root, so no one else's paths are touched: (1) 'window request tree-read 5 --label <scratch>' ok; (2) 'window list' shows it as tree-read with the label; (3) the drafted hook blocks an Edit inside <scratch> and allows one outside; (4) after 'window release' it allows the inside edit; (5) 'window run tree-read 30 --label <scratch> -- <long child>' with only that window-run pid (mine) killed: the next 'window list' prunes it (ProcessHolder orphan), so the hook allows; (6) hook p50 over 10 calls is at most 1.5x the current lease-file hook's p50 on the same inputs. If any fails, the .lease files stay and the failing case is noted here. Known gap found in reading: eoie_window_claim's claim_of reads stored_rows rather than live_rows, so 'window claim' still refuses for an orphaned window until it expires; the hook therefore reads 'window list' (which prunes), and the claim fix (claim over live_rows with an empty kept list) is a slice for the next compile release.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_2 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 2u32, "2026-10-22T19:55Z", "eoie-lane", "15:55 drafted in scratch tree-read/ (synced into the lab night folder after F's lease ends): (a) apply-tree-read-scripts.ps1 (no compile): workspace.ps1 gains Start-EoieTreeRead, which starts 'eoie window run tree-read 600 --label <root> -- pwsh -Command Wait-Process -Id <caller pid>', so the window ends with its caller, and if the holder itself is killed, the window-run pid dies and live_rows prunes it as an orphan ProcessHolder. test-regeneration -Publish calls it instead of writing regeneration-<pid>.lease. Dry run on copies: workspace.ps1 +14 lines, test-regeneration.ps1 5 lines -> 1, both parse. (b) the hook draft eoie-tree-lease.ps1: Edit/Write runs 'window claim <full path>'; on refusal it runs 'window list' (prunes) and claims again. Commands with a write verb parse 'window list' tree-read rows (label = root). A binary without claim (exit 2) allows. (c) apply-claim-live-rows.ps1 (compile): claim over live_rows. Anchor present in both the live tree and the GE2+H tree, so it is chained into apply-rH, and eoie_window_claim is added to H's regen filter. Left for the cutover: release-build-job-* call Start-EoieTreeRead instead of writing release-<pid>.lease; the rule's 6 probes after F installs. A trade-off to measure (case 6): the window label must be the bare root for claim to match, so a refusal names the window id, not the holder text ('eoie release F').")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_3 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 3u32, "2026-10-22T20:34Z", "eoie-lane", "16:34 the claim slice now carries its test: windows_cli gains a_tree_read_window_whose_holder_died_refuses_nothing (a '-run' tree-read row with a dead pid in the fixture store; the claim prints 'edit allowed' and the row is pruned). The row is built from Spiral strings, with no new host macro. It applies on both the live tree and the GE2+H tree. Release H's regen filter adds eoie_machine_windows, and its candidate notebooks add windows_cli.spi (4 instead of 3). job-script-check is ok.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_4 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 4u32, "2026-10-22T20:40Z", "eoie-lane", "16:40 placement for the write-verb half (eoie's own writes honoring the lease), read from the code: 'do edit' writes only through host.spi committed (one transactional host blob over the pending writes), and 'do write' through appended_to, copied_bytes and moved. A per-path claim check belongs in those write points, but do_edit.rs is at 976 lines (981 after GE2), and 'touching a legacy function migrates it' turns committed into a granular rewrite. A root-level check in eoie_do's dispatcher would be wrong the other way: a do root of C:\\work would refuse edits to a sibling repo while one tree under it is leased. Decision: the write-verb half waits for do_edit's boundary migration (EOIE-HEAL-BOUNDARY), which moves committed into the OS boundary package. There the claim export (a path-taking variant of eoie_window_claim_run) is one call per staged path. The hook half (scripts plus hook) does not depend on it.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_5 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 5u32, "2026-10-22T21:29Z", "eoie-lane", "17:29 the six probes of note 1 are scripted (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1; it parses), one call after F installs. EOIE_WINDOWS_HOME points at a scratch store, so the machine's shared window store is untouched, and the leased root is a scratch folder with a space in its name. Case 5 kills only the window-run process the probe itself started. Case 6 compares 10-call medians of the drafted hook and the installed lease-file hook on the same outside-path Edit payload.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_6 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 6u32, "2026-10-22T21:43Z", "eoie-lane", "17:43 baseline for probe 6, measured while F's real lease is live: the installed lease-file hook on an outside-path Edit has p50 2,003 ms over 10 calls (min 1,780, max 2,617). Almost all of it is pwsh startup, so the bar for the drafted hook is p50 <= 3,005 ms. An inside-tree Edit (eoie/README.md) is refused, exit 2. 'window list' costs ~760 ms, but the drafted hook calls it only on a claim refusal or for commands that contain a write verb.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_7 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 7u32, "2026-10-23T20:54Z", "eoie", "16:54 eoie: the six probes (scratch tree-read/probes.ps1) ran on the installed eoie, 6/6 PASS: request tree-read; list shows it with the leased path; the drafted hook blocks an inside Edit (exit 2) and allows outside (0); a release frees the inside (0); a killed window-run holder is pruned (inside 2 while held, 0 after the kill); hook p50 5,504 ms vs the current lease-file hook's 5,598 ms on a loaded box (bar 1.5x). Order for the swap, set now so protection never lapses: (1) release and regen scripts hold a tree-read window in addition to writing their .lease (both records for one release); (2) once one release ran with both, swap ~/.claude/hooks/eoie-tree-lease.ps1 to the window-reading draft, since every session on the box shares that hook; (3) the next release drops the .lease write and the tree-leases folder. Step 1 goes into the release scripts after GE2's train settles, not into a running release.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_8 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 8u32, "2026-10-23T22:28Z", "eoie", "18:28 eoie: step 1 drafted as scratch tree-read/apply-tree-read-step1.ps1 (dry run on copies of launch-O and launch-Q: rewritten, then idempotent). Each launch's release job start becomes 'job start --heavy <label> <ms> eoie window run tree-read <ms/60000+1> --label C:\\home\\git\\eoie -- pwsh -File release-build-job-X.ps1'. The window lives exactly as long as the release (window run's child; probe 5 showed a dead holder is pruned), and the exit code passes through. Not applied to tonight's train, by the 18:5x rule (train scripts stay frozen while it runs). Apply after P's acceptance, then step 2 (hook swap) after one release ran with both records.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_9 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 9u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-7 (source freeze on paths while a reader such as a Cells run or a release regen is in flight). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_10 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 10u32, "2026-10-24T23:43Z", "eoie", "19:43 Provisional estimate 60m from the grooming rule (EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS note): D3 at 50%. Replace it with milestone sizing.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_11 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 11u32, "2026-10-25T23:22Z", "eoie", "19:22 kit: writer half, requested by main (lib lands starve: CLI-ARGS-STYLE gated GREEN at 15:18, then its land waited 180 min for two clear 20 s samples with no Cells run and gave up; 4-5 lanes gate lib through Cells all day; detail on LIB-GATE-KIT-STAGED-LAND n12-n13). Design, ships after KIT-RENAME (feature freeze):\n(1) New window class tree-write PATH. A path conflicts with a held or waiting tree-read on that path or any path under or above it.\n(2) Writer preference: while a tree-write waits, new tree-read claims on a conflicting path queue behind it. This is the existing 'an older exclusive waiter keeps newcomers out' rule applied to the tree classes. Existing tree-read leases drain; then the write is granted, the land commits its staged files (seconds) and releases; then the queued reads are granted in order.\n(3) Readers declare themselves: lanes' Cells gate runners claim tree-read on lib for the length of their run (window run tree-read lib -- ...). land.ps1 requests tree-write instead of polling erl processes.\n(4) Bounds so nothing starves: a tree-write waits at most its --wait, and it states the reads it is waiting on by holder and age. A tree-read holder past its declared minutes shows as overdue in window list, so a stuck reader is visible rather than silently blocking a land.\n(5) Tests, pre-registered: a writer waiting behind two reads is granted when both release, and a third read claimed meanwhile waits behind the writer; a writer never waits on a read claimed after it; non-conflicting paths (lib vs eoie src) never block each other.\nPriority: the first post-rename feature slot, ahead of the splits, since every lane's lib lands depend on it.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_12 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 12u32, "2026-10-26T05:54Z", "eoie", "01:54 eoie: input for the tree-write window class, from main (10-26 01:3x). Two of main's lib lands raced at 22:11:30: both staged from the same file and committed inside one 2-second reader gap; each drift check passed because the other had not written yet, so the second write erased the first (file_system.spi lost fs-link-length). Main's interim fix: the lib gate's land.ps1 takes a machine-wide named mutex, Global\\spiral-lib-land, across drift check plus commit; a waiter rechecks and restages; a race test shows both lands survive. Design consequence: tree-write serializes writers by construction (writers queue on the window), which subsumes this mutex. Until it lands, any kit lib-landing tool takes the same mutex name. The window's write must also span check-then-commit as one critical section, not just the commit, since the race is between two checks.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_13 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 13u32, "2026-10-26T07:47Z", "eoie", "03:47 eoie: lease leak found during the rename. The renamed compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 takes its tree lease in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases (its state path renamed with everything else) and does not remove it when the regen ends: regeneration-19520.lease (FASTRENAME, 22:27) and regeneration-73716.lease (RESUME, 00:59) were both still there at 03:4x, and neither holder pid is running. Readers that judge leases by pid liveness are unaffected, but anything that treats a lease file as held is not: kit-state-move refused the move on them until it learned to check the holder pid (tests 12/12, a live lease still refuses). Two follow-ups: (1) the regen must remove its lease in a finally block, success or failure; (2) until the state move, the old eoie lease hook cannot see leases written under kit, so lease readers should look in both folders during the transition.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_14 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 14u32, "2026-10-28T13:15Z", "eoie", "09:1x kit: note 13's follow-ups are both done, checked against the tree. (1) compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 removes its lease on every path: a trap at line 29 removes it on a terminating error, and line 246 removes it at the end of a normal run. The leases live now (regeneration-74020, release-95544, release-junction-95544, all 08:58) belong to TRAIN3, job-1793192300314, which is running. (2) The transition to both lease folders is moot: the kit rename is final, and everything reads %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases. What remains on this item is the writer half from note 11 (a tree-write window class with writer preference), which would replace main's Global\\spiral-lib-land mutex (note 12) and the reader polling in lib-guarded-do.ps1.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_15 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 15u32, "2026-10-28T13:19Z", "eoie", "09:2x kit: the writer half from note 11 is drafted (scratch clean/tree-write/tree-write.do, 25 replacements in 5 files). It applies to a copy of the TRAIN4 tree, and none of its own lines are over 120 columns. Not compiled yet.\n- window_class gains TreeWrite (\"tree-write\"). It is lease_only like TreeRead, so it never clashes with loads (compile, heavy-build, live-gui), and needed_mb is 0.\n- A path-aware rule comes first in blocks: tree_clash (two tree classes, at least one of them a writer) plus path_overlaps on the labels (both lowercased, backslashes turned into slashes, and a trailing slash so lib2 is not under lib). A held tree window of either kind blocks the request. A waiting tree-write blocks a newer request (writer preference, same shape as the existing older-exclusive-waiter rule). Two readers never block each other.\n- The request's label (the path, as tree-read rows already carry their root) now flows through verdict_text, kit_window_verdict, verdict_terms, first_blocker, row_blocks and blocks. Callers: windows.spi and kit_job_gate pass ask.label; kit_window_query's check and class_admitted pass \"\" (no tree class asks through them).\n- Test block a_tree_write_waits_for_readers_and_keeps_new_readers_out, 8 expectations: a held read below the path keeps a write waiting; readers share; an older waiting write keeps a newer reader out; an older reader is not overtaken; one writer at a time; disjoint paths and a sibling with a shared prefix never clash; a tree write is a lease, not a load.\nUsage once shipped: a land runs 'kit window run tree-write <lib path> 10 --label <lib path> --wait 30 -- <check+commit>'. That replaces main's Global\\spiral-lib-land mutex (note 12) and spans check plus commit as one critical section. Gate runners claim tree-read on lib for the length of their run. Next: compile on the node with the next draft bundle; then the landers (lib-guarded-do.ps1 and the lib gate's land.ps1) switch from reader polling to the window.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_16 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 16u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 65%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_17 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 17u32, "2026-10-31T14:22Z", "kit", "10-31 10:2x kit: state after tonight. The window side is SHIPPED on the installed E14F1F0B ('kit window' lists classes ... compiler-install, tree-read and has 'window claim <path>'), and main's lib_land_window.ps1 already takes a 'window request tree-write' around a lib land. NOT cut over: test-regeneration.ps1 still writes .lease files, now narrowed to the selected packages, their transitive deps, compiler-contracts and the root files (KIT-ZERO-DOWNTIME-RELEASE n7), and snapshot trains lease nothing. One DESIGN CONSTRAINT to settle before the hook moves to the window store: the PreToolUse hook runs on EVERY tool call of EVERY lane. Today a cmd 'if exist %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases\\*.lease' prefilter keeps it at about 0 ms when nothing is leased, while a kit.exe call costs 3-6 s wall on the loaded box (measured 10-31: agile next 2.5 s by kit's clock against 6.9 s wall). So the hook must read the window store's files directly, with an equally cheap existence prefilter, or the cutover makes every lane slower. The scripts' side is then a small change: wrap the regen's publish in 'kit window run tree-read <path> ...' per leased root.")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_18 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 18u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TREE-READ-RECORD (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_19 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 19u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries LEASE-7 (Source freeze). Why: 10-24 note #9: source freeze as a tree-read window; window claim <path> ships the refusal half")
inl note_of_kit_tree_read_record_20 () = Note ("KIT-TREE-READ-RECORD", 20u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. LEASE-7 · Source freeze · P2 · scp · Open / `source-freeze <paths...>` records digests and refuses later mutation of those paths, while state and report files stay writable until release.")
