inl kit_backend_map () = Task ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", "eoie: map eoie's components to Gleam or Rust by BACKEND-BALANCE's rule. Its job host, machine windows and process watching are the strongest BEAM candidates in any repo; its CLI verbs stay Rust", Story, P0, D5, 15u32, Planned, "", "kit,backends,team:rig,gauge", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_backend_map () = Planning ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_1 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 1u32, "2026-10-23T11:01Z", "x", "2026-10-23 07:01 x (filed at the user's request, 2026-10-23: 'tibia and eoie we haven't thought about what could be gained there'; the eoie team decides). Survey: 378 packages, all Rust (269 ///> rust targets, no gleam). First classification by the rule. RUST, keep: (1) every CLI verb (agile, do, read, grep, table, experiment, standards). Per-command use, measured 45-160 ms for 'agile status' vs a 1.1-2.6 s BEAM cold start (R2). (2) Scans and bulk work (grep/read search, style census, coverage, bundle zip/deflate, table query) stay Rust (R1, R3). Their rayon use moves from host-code blobs (eoie_do_read/host.spi:36, eoie_grep_query/search.spi:51, eoie_bundle_retention/retention_terminal.spi:7) to LIB-PAR-MAP, which also serves eoie's own style contract. GLEAM candidates: (3) the job host and its supervisors (eoie_jobs, EOIE-JOB-HOST), machine windows and slot arbitration (eoie_machine_windows), machine watch, process observation and streaming (eoie_machine_watch, eoie_process_observation, eoie_process_streaming). These meet G1, G2, G3, G4 and G5. OTP supervision trees answer tonight's failure modes directly: supervisors lost with their caller, nested job starts dying with an outer job (GE2), windows held by jobs still waiting on --after, restarts after a crash. Hot code reload (G5) would let a release install a new host without stopping running jobs, which today's releases avoid by waiting for or stopping jobs. The Rust CLI keeps talking to the host over its existing socket, so commands stay fast. KEEP: agile state as files (it must work with no service running). Next step: a measured prototype of the window/slot arbiter as a gleam_otp actor (LIB-OTP-ACTORS) behind the existing protocol, comparing admission latency, memory and crash recovery with today's host.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_2 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 2u32, "2026-10-23T12:01Z", "eoie", "08:01 eoie team decision rule, written before any prototype. Baseline measured now (fixture windows home, installed binary, 5 runs): a waiting window request learns of a release by polling, release-to-grant 1853, 1791, 71, 1750, 1680 ms (median 1750, max 1853; the 71 ms run polled right after the release). The arbiter prototype (machine windows + heavy slots as one Gleam actor behind the existing CLI, per x's 07:01 survey) is adopted only if all of these hold on the same box, measured the same way: (1) release-to-grant median <= 100 ms and max <= 250 ms; (2) the actor's idle CPU <= 0.5% of one core over 10 min and resident memory <= 60 MB; (3) every existing windows_cli and run_cli notebook passes against it unchanged; (4) a killed actor is restarted by its supervisor and the window list it rebuilds equals the files on disk. Any miss keeps the Rust file arbiter, and its polling becomes a file-change notification instead (a smaller fix for the same latency). The job host stays Rust for now: it was just fixed in Rust (hotfix HP, live parent under the user shell), and BEAM's 1.1-2.6 s cold start would sit on every first job start of a session unless the actor is already warm.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_3 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 3u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-23, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_4 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 4u32, "2026-10-31T23:48Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_5 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 5u32, "2026-11-01T13:14Z", "kit", "11-01 09:1x kit groom: raised P1 -> P0 to fix a PRIORITY INVERSION: GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT (P0, 55%) has only one part left, 'kit adopting Gleam/Rust where it pays', which is this item (tibia n-latest: resume condition = this item resumes). FACT checked today: kit has ZERO Gleam code (no .gleam files; '///> gleam' or Gleam arms appear only as backend names in kit_ci_model, kit_ci_probe, kit_exec_metrics, kit_standards), so kit has never actually deliberated a component onto BEAM. This item is also CODEBASE-DIAMOND's first real backend verdict (DIAMOND-S4). DoD (the pre-registered 10-23 rule, unchanged): a Gleam actor arbitrating machine windows + heavy slots behind the existing CLI is adopted only if, on the same box and measured the same way: release-to-grant median <= 100 ms and max <= 250 ms (baseline polling median 1,750 ms, max 1,853 ms); idle CPU <= 0.5% of a core over 10 min and RSS <= 60 MB; every windows_cli and run_cli notebook passes unchanged; a killed actor is restarted by its supervisor with the window list intact. Otherwise the verdict is Native with the measured reasons, recorded in the gauge ledger. NEXT STEP: measure today's release-to-grant again on the installed binary (5 runs, same fixture), then a Gleam package kit_window_arbiter (supervised actor holding window_row state) behind 'kit window request/release' via a local port. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: actor messages as a closed union (Request of window_class * minutes * reply | Release of window_id | Announce of announced), window state reusing window_model's types through the shared codec (no stringly protocol between CLI and actor), the arbiter's grant decision the same pure functions as kit_window_announce (start_verdict_of, yield_verdict_of), so Rust and Gleam agree by construction; the verdict itself a typed gauge row backend_answer = Beam (Supervision, ManyWaiters) | Native (reasons).")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_6 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 6u32, "2026-11-05T05:49Z", "tibia", "11-05 01:49 tibia (taking a READY light item while tibia's runs are queued; measurement only, no kit source touched): BASELINE RE-MEASURED with the installed kit (bin\\kit.exe 5B9082C1) on the box, in an isolated fixture home (KIT_WINDOWS_HOME=scratch dir, KIT_WINDOWS_FREE_MB=16000), using the 10-23 rule's shape: hold live-gui, start a second live-gui request with --wait 2, settle 3 s, release, 5 runs. Read from the fixture events.log (released ms to granted ms, so kit start-up is excluded): 454, 233, 307, 465, 434 ms, median 434, max 465, min 233. The 10-23 baseline was median 1750, max 1853, so the file arbiter's wait loop is about 4x faster now, consistent with a ~500 ms poll. It still misses the pre-registered adoption bar (median <= 100 ms, max <= 250 ms) by about 4x. CAVEAT for anyone measuring end to end: the releasing CLI process itself took 604-2235 ms on the loaded box (kit start-up), so a stopwatch around 'window release' over-reports by seconds. Use the events.log stamps. Next per the 10-23 rule: either the Gleam arbiter prototype (needs the same 4 checks) or the cheaper fix the rule names, a file-change notification instead of polling, whose target is the same <= 100/250 ms.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_7 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 7u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit: KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION turns this map into a kit verb: BACKEND-BALANCE R1-R3 and G1-G5 as typed predicates over measured facts, a decision record per component, re-run when facts change. Its first test reproduces n1 classification; its first live verdicts are the agile hub and the federation coordinator.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_8 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 8u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-BACKEND-MAP (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_backend_map_9 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-MAP", 9u32, "2026-11-09T05:56Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:4x rig: held on KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION. Mapping rig's components to Gleam or Rust is that verb's output over measured per-component facts, which do not exist yet (see its 11-09 note); a hand map now would be the guess the user asked rig to replace with deterministic deliberation (user 11-05). Releases when the deliberation verb can decide one fixture component.")
