inl kit_job_retention () = Task ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", "settled job folders are pruned after a retention period, so the jobs home and every job list stay fast as the registry grows", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,jobs,stability,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_retention () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 1u32, "2026-10-23T22:51Z", "eoie", "measured 2026-10-23 18:5x: the live jobs home holds 1,421 job folders (22.5 MB), growing about 250 a day (10-19: 254, 10-21: 461, 10-22: 303, 10-23: 235), with nothing ever pruned; job list 5 takes 281 ms today because newest_directories lists and sorts every folder, so at this rate it scans tens of thousands of folders within months. Shape: eoie job prune [--older-than <days>] (default 14) removes only folders whose exit file exists and whose exit time is older than the cutoff, never a queued, held or running job and never one named by a live after file; receipt 'eoie job prune ok kept=N removed=M bytes=B'. The job host runs the same prune once per day while idle. 14 days keeps the history that today's audits read (window-slot-order since an install, adoption over 7 days). Test: a fixture home with settled-old, settled-new, running and held jobs, where only settled-old goes.")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 2u32, "2026-10-23T22:56Z", "eoie", "18:56 eoie: drafted. The crate is scratch f6/src/eoie_job_prune: model with prune_verdict (KeepUnsettled | KeepReferenced | KeepRecent | PruneSettled), a cutoff that never underflows, days defaulting to 14, and a test of every verdict. prune.spi reads the exit file's ended field, collects the predecessors named by after files of unsettled jobs (held jobs from release P included), and deletes only PruneSettled folders through lib file_system.directory_delete; the receipt is 'kept=N removed=M older_than_days=D'. The wiring slice is scratch job-prune/apply-job-prune.ps1, built like M's job doctor: JobPrune case and word, usage, route, Cargo dependency, workspace member, a registry notebook (paren balance unchanged), and a crate Cargo.toml without the doctor's sysinfo. Dry run on the post-O tree is clean and idempotent. Typecheck after tibia's block; a release vehicle once that passes. The host's daily idle prune comes as step 2 after the verb proves itself.")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 3u32, "2026-10-23T23:51Z", "eoie", "19:51 eoie: step 2 design (host housekeeping, after the verb proves itself). The host's served loop already knows when it is idle (no live children, no held jobs, no requests) and has an idle clock. Rule: when idle for 60 s and the file shell-host/pruned (holding the last prune time) is older than 24 h, the host calls eoie_job_prune's export with the default 14 days and writes the time. A prune never touches unsettled, held or referenced jobs, so it can't race a starting job: a job without an exit file is always kept. Cost per day: one directory listing plus exit-file reads, about 0.3 s at today's 1,421 folders. Test: a fixture home with an old settled job and a stale pruned file sees the old job removed after an idle beat; a fresh pruned file sees nothing removed.")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 4u32, "2026-11-06T11:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-JOB-RETENTION (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 5u32, "2026-11-09T11:46Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 MEASURED (microblog, box, CPU-light): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs holds 862 job folders, only 5.6 MB in total. By age: <1d 152, 1-3d 38, 3-7d 294, >=7d 378; the oldest is job-1793228918503 from 10-28. Each settled folder is about 12 small files (argv, cwd, exit, heartbeat_ms, host-home, label, pid, started_ms, stderr.log, stdout.log, supervisor, timeout_ms). So the cost is NOT disk but the per-call scan: every job list/status/doctor walks 862 dirs, plus held/ and hosts/. RULE PROPOSED (rig owns the implementation): prune a folder only when it is SETTLED (an exit file exists AND its pid is not alive) AND older than 7 days by started_ms. Never touch running/queued/held jobs or any job named as --after/--after-ok by a live job, and keep the newest 200 settled jobs regardless of age, for history. Run it as a cheap pass inside job list/start (at most one prune per hour, recorded in a stamp file). Before deleting, move the folder to jobs/archive/<yyyymm>.zip, or simply delete it, since stdout/stderr already land in the job run dirs: rig decides. Expected effect today: 378 folders removed, roughly halving the scan. NEXT STEP: rig implements it in kit (Spiral) and proves it on a copied jobs dir: counts before/after, and a live/held/--after-referenced job is never removed.")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 6u32, "2026-11-09T12:37Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 08:3x microblog, node side: the wifi node's kit jobs home holds 2564 job folders, of which only 2 have no exit file (one is the live x-verify-client-r2). So the node grows about 3x faster than the box (862) and is where the per-call scan cost lands first; the prune rule (settled = exit file and dead pid, older than 7 days) applies there unchanged, and the host-side daily prune from note 3 matters more on nodes than on the box.")
inl note_of_kit_job_retention_7 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-RETENTION", 7u32, "2026-11-09T18:47Z", "tibia", "11-09 14:5x tibia (help pool): STATUS + FRESH MEASUREMENT. Not shipped: no `prune` in `kit job` usage and no *job_prune* package in src; the 10-23 crate lived in scratch f6 and was most likely lost with the 10-28 profile wipe (memory profile-wipe-2026-10-28). Box jobs home now (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs): 939 folders, 21 MB (it restarted at the wipe; 10-23 had 1421). `kit job list 5` took 3.75 s wall today versus 281 ms on 10-23 with MORE folders. The box was loaded, but a 13x slowdown at fewer folders suggests the cost is no longer just listing/sorting directories (remote placement rows are listed too). So the item's premise (folder growth) still holds at ~250/day, and job list latency needs its own profile before prune is assumed to fix it. The design in note 2 (KeepUnsettled | KeepReferenced | KeepRecent | PruneSettled, 14 days, never a job named by a live after file) is still right; the crate needs rebuilding from that note: rig's.")
inl kit_window_announce () = Task ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", "a lane announces an exclusive block in the window store (class, from, until, latest start), so heavy starts, releases and waiters see it instead of relying on a peer message", TaskKind, P0, D3, 90u32, Planned, "KIT-ENTRY-X-S2", "kit,windows,coordination,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_window_announce () = Planning ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_1 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T00:02Z", "eoie", "evidence 2026-10-23: tibia announced a 4-stage live block by message at 18:0x (latest start 20:00, end 21:00). eoie's window store knew nothing of it, so I found out only from the message that my GE2 release (heavy-build window, 228 min left) would block it; I stopped GE2 by hand at 18:02 and wrote a requeue waiter, which shipped with a dead predicate. Shape: 'eoie window announce <class> --from <HH:MM> --until <HH:MM> [--latest-start <HH:MM>] --label <text>' writes an Announced row (typed: announced class, window, latest start, owner label). window list shows it. Admission tells a new request whose declared end passes an announced exclusive window's start ('would overlap tibia block PC-10 from 18:05'). 'release yield' and 'window wait-clear' read the rows (wait until the announced window ended, not a 10-minute guess). From memory window-coordination (read queue plus announced blocks before heavy work; EOIE-MEMORY-FEATURES).")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_2 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 2u32, "2026-10-24T04:39Z", "eoie", "00:39 drafted for the next train (scratch next-train\\window-announce.do + -files.do; applies on the emulated post-R tree, 20 replacements + 4 new files). Model: window_state gains Announced ('announced' in the row file); blocks/row_blocks take the request's span (now, now+minutes); an Announced row blocks only a clashing class whose span overlaps [start, until]; Held/Waiting rules unchanged. Admission passes the span. Verb: new crate eoie_window_announce behind the window dispatcher: 'eoie window announce <class> <start-in-minutes> <minutes> --label <text>' writes a lease-held row (not pruned as orphaned, expires at until; 'window release <id>' cancels it). The shape takes minute offsets rather than HH:MM, so no local-clock parsing; latest-start isn't modeled yet. Model test: overlapping compile waits, one ending before or starting after passes, the state reads back. Typecheck queued (job-1792816660444).")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_3 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 3u32, "2026-10-24T06:13Z", "x", "10-24 02:13 x evidence (cross-team, for the owner): 02:05 main's main-cells-pack-land needed two clear 20 s samples with no Cells erl, and x's chain ran notebooks back to back, so the gap never came. Worked around it with an x holder job that keeps 2 compile windows; x's gate waits for room (at most 1 other compile window) after taking its mutex, so x's next notebook waits until main's land settles. Generic shape this suggests, without a new class: a step that needs a quiet box for seconds (an apps/cells or compiler file land) requests a short EXCLUSIVE window, like compiler-install, instead of polling for natural gaps. Running jobs finish, new heavy/compile/test windows queue behind it, and the gap opens deterministically. Possible verb: 'eoie window request quiet <seconds> --until-idle <process pattern>' (exclusive, granted when the pattern has no live process). It would replace land.ps1's '2 clear 20 s samples' loop and the per-lane holder trick.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_4 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 4u32, "2026-10-24T06:33Z", "eoie", "02:33 draft typecheck PASS (02:28, 4/4 notebooks on the post-R tree: window_model with the announced-overlap test, announce, admission, crowding). The first run's Pattern miss was two matches over window_state without an Announced arm (settling_mb, crowding); both now have one. Ready for the next train (PLAN stage S2).")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_5 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 5u32, "2026-10-28T10:30Z", "eoie", "10-28 groom (user via main): Paused. Last note 2026-10-24, nothing queued. Next step: check whether its remaining milestone shipped in TRAIN2c (installed kit.exe A5B55974, 10-28 05:25). If it did, close it with that evidence; if not, size the remaining slice from its last milestone note and resume it through agile next.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_6 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 6u32, "2026-10-29T21:46Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T21:46 kit: evidence for this item. At 17:42 -04:00 git-a6's --heavy pack-7 review (job-1793310125389) was admitted beside kit's TRAIN4d (job-1793309738622, also --heavy). That's by design: HeavyBuild doesn't clash with HeavyBuild (window_model test 'heavy builds still share the box under the slot gate'), and only LiveGui/CompilerInstall are exclusive. But both lanes had agreed in messages that the gate would order them (kit's wrong assumption). git-a6 stopped it after 61 s, with no writes. A train that wants the box to itself (release trains with timing-sensitive notebooks) needs exactly this: an announced exclusive block that other heavy starts see. Until then, heavy runs are serialized by explicit signals only (memory heavy-gate-lessons).")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_7 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 7u32, "2026-10-29T23:18Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:18 kit (main's sweep 8): the same held --heavy window (git-a6's pack-7 review, job-1793314021578, HeavyBuild, ~298 min) was read three ways within 10 minutes. main told fiscal 'no window is held' (18:46). kit told fiscal 'go' for lib lands, counting only kit's own lib readers (18:51). kit itself moved a compile to the node because the review 'holds the machine' (18:5x). main and x compiled on the box, and lib-guarded-do refused main's lib land, correctly, because the review's 13 samples read lib. The gate's actual semantics: HeavyBuild is admitted beside other heavy builds on memory alone (window_model 'heavy builds still share the box under the slot gate'); it does not reserve the box, and it says nothing about lib stability. Ask for this item: an announced window must STATE what it protects. Proposed kinds: (1) box-exclusive (no other heavy or compile starts), (2) compile-quiet (heavy may share, but no compiles: timing-sensitive), (3) lib-stable (no lib lands while it holds, as lib-guarded-do already enforces for live readers). Each is shown in 'kit window list' and checked by 'kit job start'. Interim rule (main, until the review ends ~21:20): no lib lands and no box compiles; send compiles to the node.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_8 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 8u32, "2026-10-30T03:34Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T03:34 kit: a 4th window kind from tonight. kit's sm' lib_gate over a PRIVATE lib COPY (job-1793330501262, started with --compile) took a compile window, and lib-guarded-do counted it as a live-lib reader, holding fiscal's window-5 land, although the job never reads live lib/spiral. Proposed kind 'copy-read': a compile-class job whose inputs are all private copies. It keeps memory admission, but reader guards ignore it. kit job start would take it from an explicit flag (e.g. --copy-only); auto-detection from paths is fragile. Until then, run copy-only gates on the node or outside a lib window.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_9 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 9u32, "2026-10-31T23:48Z", "kit", "10-31 19:5x kit, pause re-decision (main pre-night groom; R3 is now \"never auto-pause for silence\"): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the 10-28 silence groom (\"nothing queued\"), not by a blocker, and no note names an external condition. Back in agile next at its priority; its first step stays the one in the 10-28 note: check whether the remaining milestone shipped in a later train (installed kit 4983F9C0, TRAIN6) and close it with that evidence, else do the next milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_10 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 10u32, "2026-11-01T03:44Z", "kit", "10-31 23:5x kit: raised P1 -> P0, because it is the only dep of HEAVY-HOLD-DEADLOCK (P0, main 23:5x). What is left (checked on installed 4983F9C0): 'kit window' has request/release/list/check/claim/wait-clear/run, all for NOW; there is no future-dated block, and train holds still live in a separate file store (kit/scripts/train-hold.ps1, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\train-holds). NEXT CONCRETE STEP (CPU-light): typed model first, in the window model package: announced_window record (class, from, until, latest_start, work_minutes, holder) plus the admission rule 'a heavy start is refused if it would overlap an announced block that starts within its expected minutes'; test blocks over a fake clock; then 'kit window announce <class> <from> <minutes> --work <m>' and list shows announced blocks. Then train-hold.ps1 is DROPPED (PS1-TO-SPIRAL (A)(2)): launch-train reads 'kit window list'.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_11 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 11u32, "2026-11-01T04:53Z", "kit", "11-01 00:5x kit: typed announce model DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular HostFree+LibReferenced package src/kit_window_announce (standards 0 findings, census row added, ratchet ok), reusing window_model's window_class, clashes and minutes_left. announced = { holder; kind; from_ms; until_ms; work_minutes }; start_verdict = StartNow | WouldOverlap (holder, minutes until it starts); a heavy start of N expected minutes is held back if any clashing announced block starts before now+N and has not ended. Test a_heavy_start_waits_for_an_announced_install_it_would_overlap (30-min build vs install at minute 20 -> WouldOverlap; 15-min build, later install, ended block -> StartNow). PROOF: node job-1793508524208-kit-node-announce 1/1 pass. NEXT: store + verb ('kit window announce <class> <from> <minutes> --work <m>', list shows announced blocks, heavy request consults start_verdict_of), then drop train-hold.ps1.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_12 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 12u32, "2026-11-01T06:39Z", "kit", "11-01 02:4x kit (round 3): store format DONE in the tree (unstaged): new granular package src/kit_window_announce_rows. Announced blocks are typed Spiral rows, not tab-joined text (DATA-ROWS-TYPED): 'inl announced_<n> () = Announced (holder, class-word, from_ms u64, until_ms u64, work_minutes u64)'; announced_of reads them back in order, refusing unknown classes. Test announced_blocks_round_trip_as_typed_rows. PROOF: node job-1793514810043-kit-node-r3g pass (first try: a tuple-with-cons pattern bound as (ClassKnown kind, from) :: ...; split into nested matches). LEFT: the verb ('kit window announce' writing these rows into the window store dir; list shows them; heavy request consults start_verdict_of), then drop train-hold.ps1.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_13 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 13u32, "2026-11-01T12:34Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: 'kit window announce compiler-install 14:00 10 --work 10 --label pack12' writes one Announced row; 'kit window list' shows it as announced with minutes until start; 'kit window request heavy-build 30' at 13:40 is refused with 'would overlap compiler-install from 14:00 (compiler)' and admitted at 14:11; scripts/train-hold.ps1 deleted (archived) and launch-train reads the window store; CLI tests for all three, node green. NEXT STEP: an announced-rows file in the window store dir written via kit_window_announce_rows, and kit_window_request consulting start_verdict_of. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: announce rule reuses window_model's closed window_class union; start_verdict is a union (StartNow | WouldOverlap holder minutes), no -1; still weak: times are bare u64 ms and minutes are bare u64, and minutes_left returns 0 for 'already started'; fix with nominal epoch_ms and minutes, and a union start_offset = StartsIn minutes | AlreadyRunning instead of the 0 sentinel. Legacy window rows stay tab text only inside kit_machine_windows until DATA-ROWS-TYPED migrates them.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_14 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 14u32, "2026-11-03T02:13Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:1x VERB + REQUEST GATE DONE (unstaged, next train). `kit window announce <class> <HH:MM|+minutes> <minutes> [--work m] [--label holder]` appends one typed Announced row (kit_window_announce_rows) to <windows root>/announced.spi, numbered by counting existing rows. HH:MM is local time via kit_receipt.local_offset_minutes and rolls to tomorrow once passed. Receipt: `kit window announce ok class=<c> holder=<h> starts_in_min=<n> minutes=<m> path=<file>`. `kit window request` refuses with `would overlap a block announced by <holder> that starts in <n> min` whenever start_verdict_of says the requested work would run into a live block (ended blocks are ignored). Crate split for the cap: kit_window_announce_verb (writer, 754 lines) and kit_window_announce_check (reader + refusal, 804); kit_machine_windows routes `window announce`, and window_requested consults the check first. PROOF on europa: verb 2/2 (append numbering + start parsing incl. utc-1 rollover and bad input), check 1/1 (overlap, after-the-end, short-work-before), and live: announce +1 30 then `window request heavy-build 30` exit 2 with the reason. A 5-min compile is refused too, which is correct because it would still be running when the block starts 1 min later. LEFT for DoD: `kit window list` showing announced blocks, and launch-train reading the store so scripts/train-hold.ps1 can be archived.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_15 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 15u32, "2026-11-03T02:27Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 22:3x BLOCKED on purpose: built and proven on europa, waits for the TRAIN14 install (then the live smoke). Blocked keeps the CCO queue from handing it to other lanes; main hit a claim loop on REMOTE-EXEC-S2. Resume as Active when TRAIN14 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_16 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 16u32, "2026-11-03T08:01Z", "kit", "11-03 03:5x kit PAUSE (main: the 21:00 run was armed early by mistake; run paused). STATE: Blocked; kit window list shows announced blocks (proven on europa; windows_cli 17/19, and the 2 failures are KIT-WINDOWS-CLI-LINUX). Ships in TRAIN14c. NEXT after install: set Active; then launch-train reading the store needs a kit window check <class> --minutes N option that consults announced blocks (query.spi is legacy, so migrate it in that change). WAITS ON: TRAIN14c.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_17 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 17u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "kit", "11-03 06:1x kit: TRAIN14c INSTALLED (kit.exe 5B9082C1; 58/58 notebooks; crate gate 284/284 under 1000). Unblocked: the train it waited on has installed. Resume from the last kit note.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_18 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 18u32, "2026-11-03T13:19Z", "kit", "11-03 09:3x kit: the remaining 10% (launch-train consults announced blocks via `kit window check <class> --minutes N`) belongs INSIDE KIT-ENTRY-X-S2. The window router (kit_machine_windows window_run) is a 10-branch string dispatch (request/release/list/check/run/claim/wait-clear/announce/remote), and adding --minutes there would be new drift. ORDER: (1) a lib PascalCase -> kebab helper (sm'), needed for wait-clear and by KIT-HEAL-NAMEOF for probe_word; (2) S2 types the router as a window_verb union with reflected words; (3) check gains a typed --minutes that asks kit_window_announce_check.announced_refusal; (4) launch-train calls it before `job start --heavy`. Parked Planned until S2.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_19 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 19u32, "2026-11-04T08:03Z", "kit", "11-04 04:4x kit: still 90%. The router half of the prerequisite is done: window_run already matches a typed window_verb union (kit_machine_windows). The remaining step, `kit window check <class> --minutes N` consulted by launch-train before its box half, lands in kit_window_query, which sits at 951 generated lines, so it needs that crate's check path moved into a small linked unit first. Lower priority now that trains run node-first: only the short box release step can collide with an announced block.")
inl note_of_kit_window_announce_20 () = Note ("KIT-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE", 20u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-WINDOW-ANNOUNCE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
