inl kit_do_concurrent_edits () = Task ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "eoie do edits compose across sessions on the same file: lock + changed-since-read check + anchored re-apply, pre-image backups, and edit+write in one atomic program", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Planned, "KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,do,concurrency,wipe-redo,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits () = Planning ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_1 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:20Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:20 main (user 2026-10-23 21:1x: 'shouldn't eoie's patching capabilities allow us to edit the same file from all the other sessions?'). Code read: proxy_text_replacement/text_replacement.spi replace_in (74-87) reads the file, computes the updated text and calls workspace_primitives replace_file_checked (files.spi:19), which writes <path>.<pid>.pending, renames it and reads it back. The rename is atomic, but nothing checks that the file is unchanged since the read, so two sessions editing one file can lose an update (the later rename wins). That is the class EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES fixed for agile.spi only. Design: (1) a per-file lock (an exclusive-create <path>.eoie-lock holding pid and start time; stale if the pid is gone) held across re-read, hash compare and rename; (2) on a hash mismatch, re-apply the same anchored statements to the current text; the program's expect counts decide (same counts: merge succeeds; otherwise reject all-or-nothing and name the conflicting anchor); bounded retries; (3) a pre-image backup per touched file (<root>/.eoie/backups/<stamp>/<rel>, bounded retention), as the user expects from 'automatic backups'; (4) edit and write/copy statements in one program, applied as one transaction (x's EOIE-DO-VERB-FAMILY note 5: today 'edit and write statements cannot share one program'). Test: two processes applying disjoint anchored edits to one file 100 times concurrently keep both edits every time; overlapping anchors reject one side with a named conflict.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_2 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): LEASE-9 (owner token, compare-and-swap by hash before every write), MERGE-1 (three-way merge keeping conflicts, with a receipt). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_3 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 3u32, "2026-10-25T20:20Z", "eoie", "16:20 kit: a live occurrence on agile writes, not only do edits. At 16:18 'agile note EOIE-SUPERVISOR-NO-CONSOLE' failed with 'could not move the replacement file into place' (exit 2). That is RenameFailed from workspace_primitives/files.spi replace_file_checked, which gives up on the first failed rename. Several sessions were writing and reading the 6.6 MB shared backlog at once; on Windows a rename fails while any process has the target open. The same command succeeded on a manual retry seconds later.\n- Proposal, scoped to this item's compare-and-swap:\n  - bounded retry of the rename (for example 5 tries with growing waits, about 1 s in total), with a check before each retry that the target's hash still equals the hash read before the edit;\n  - if the hash changed, re-read and re-apply the anchored edit (this item's plan) instead of overwriting.\n  This fixes the transient reader case without making lost updates possible.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_4 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 4u32, "2026-10-26T22:33Z", "main", "10-26 18:33 main: 10-26 18:33 main: live evidence of cross-session contention on the backlog store, from today's run. At 22:01Z an agile note by main failed with 'agile note EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG rejected: could not move the replacement file into place'. A note to a different item a second later succeeded, and the retry at 22:02Z succeeded after the file grew about 1.2 KB in between (bytes_before 7,760,531 -> 7,761,768), i.e. another session's write landed first. The failure was safe (atomic replace refused, nothing lost), and a plain retry was correct only because notes are appends that commute. For in-place edits (eoie do, agile edit) a blind retry would not be safe. That is this item's case: lock + changed-since-read check + anchored apply. Suggested acceptance probe: two processes appending notes to the same root in a tight loop, then assert every note is present exactly once, with no lost update.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_5 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 5u32, "2026-10-26T22:39Z", "main", "10-26 18:39 main: 10-26 18:39 main: CORRECTION to n4, checked against the session transcript. Confirmed: at 22:01Z 'agile note EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG rejected: could not move the replacement file into place' (eoie's RenameFailed arm of replace_file_checked). In the SAME command, the next note (COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS n46) succeeded, ending at 7,760,531 bytes. The retry at 22:02Z (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG n14) started at 7,761,768 bytes, so another writer landed 1,237 bytes in between. Wrong in n4: 'a second later' (it was the same command), and 'another session's write landed first' as the cause. That write came AFTER the failure, so the cause of the rename failure is unproven. On Windows a rename onto an open file fails when any process holds it without FILE_SHARE_DELETE, so a concurrent reader (or an AV scan) is as likely as a writer. Implication for this item: a lock must cover readers' open handles too, or the replace should retry RenameFailed with backoff. The acceptance probe in n4 stands, plus one variant: a tight reader loop against a writer.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_6 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 6u32, "2026-10-28T12:18Z", "eoie", "08:1x kit: first slice drafted, for the live failure in note 3 (on Windows, 'could not move the replacement file into place' fails on the first rename while any reader has the target open). workspace_primitives/files.spi gains renamed_within: the rename is retried up to 40 times, 50 ms apart (lib threading.sleep), about 2 s at most, before RenameFailed. replace_file_checked uses it, so every agile write and do edit gets it. Draft: scratchpad clean\\agile-trim\\rename-retry.do, in the TRAIN4 build; the node regen-compare of the 65 crates reaching workspace_primitives is job-1793189767176. Next slices: the changed-since-read hash check (a compare-and-swap before the rename, LEASE-9) and the anchored re-apply on conflict.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_7 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 7u32, "2026-10-28T12:19Z", "eoie", "08:2x kit: slice 2 design (compare-and-swap for kit do), from reading the write path. kit do stages edits in pending_writes (kit_do_edit/host.spi: a Vec of (path, text)), and committed writes every temporary and then renames each one over its target. Nothing compares the target with what text_now read, so a concurrent edit by another session is silently lost. Agile writes already refuse in this case ('state/agile.spi changed since it was read'); do edits don't. Design: (1) at the first read of a path, record its byte length plus a 64-bit hash in pending, beside the staged text; (2) in committed, after writing the temporaries and before the first rename, re-read every target and compare it with the recorded pair; on any mismatch remove every temporary and return 'changed since read: <path>', so nothing is written; (3) an optional --rebase on a conflict re-runs the program's anchored edits on the fresh text, a clean re-apply because edits are anchored replacements with expected counts. Placement: kit_do_edit is at 992 of 1000 generated lines, so committed and the pending primitives move first into a new boundary crate kit_do_commit (hand-modeled unit), and the CAS goes there. Proxy text-replace (proxy_text_replacement/text_replacement.spi:85-96) needs the same check: original is already read, so it compares before replace_file_checked.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_8 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 8u32, "2026-10-28T12:21Z", "eoie", "08:2x kit: the proxy text-replace half of slice 2 is drafted (scratchpad clean\\agile-trim\\text-replace-cas.do, in the TRAIN4 build). Before replace_file_checked, apply re-reads the file and refuses with '<path> changed since it was read; nothing written, run it again' if it no longer equals the text the replacement was computed from. That leaves a race only between this check and the rename (milliseconds), compared with the whole read-compute-write span before. The crate is at 385 lines. The kit do half waits for the kit_do_commit split (note 7).")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_9 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 9u32, "2026-10-28T12:50Z", "eoie", "09:0x kit: slice 2, the kit do half, drafted as the kit_do_commit split from note 7 (scratch clean/do-commit, not compiled yet: the box is held for tibia's live window). Reason for the split: kit_do_edit's do_edit.rs is at 992 of 1000 lines. A host block costs one generated line, a Spiral function many, so a granular commit inside kit_do_edit would cross the gate. Shape, per AGENTS.md (host code only as named one-expression primitives in boundary packages):\n- kit_do_store (new Spiral package, boundary): the pending handle, now (path, staged text, base text) triples, with one-expression accessors (count, path, text, base, index of a path, push, restage) and staged, which keeps the first base when a path is staged again.\n- rust_std_fs gains write_problem (the OS error text, or empty), joined_path and hidden_sibling (do-commit-fs.do). These are unused elsewhere, so other crates' output is unchanged.\n- kit_do_commit (new crate, HostFree, LibReferenced): commit_outcome = Committed | WriteFailed (path, error) | ChangedSinceRead path | PromoteFailed target. committed writes every temporary, then checks every target against its base (rust_std_fs text_equals), then renames each one through workspace_primitives renamed_within (40 tries, 50 ms, from TRAIN4). On a write failure or a conflict every temporary is removed and nothing is written. On a rename failure the remaining temporaries are removed and every file already renamed is restored from its base text (the base is the pre-image), so the commit is all-or-nothing; PromoteFailed (target, whole) says whether every restore succeeded. Exported as kit_do_committed (pending, root) -> problem text; a host-typed argument crosses crates the way iggy_decode(Rc<RefCell<Vec<u8>>>) already does.\n- kit_do_edit (do-commit-wiring.do): host.spi drops its pending type, pending_new, the dead pending_count, staged and the committed host block; committed becomes a one-line call into kit_do_commit. run.spi stages (path, after, before), where before is the disk text whenever the path is not staged yet.\nTest blocks: a clean commit writes both files and leaves no temporary; a target edited after the read refuses with ChangedSinceRead on that path, leaves the unconflicted file untouched, keeps the other session's edit and removes every temporary; restaging keeps one entry with the first base and writes the last text; a failed promotion's restore puts the pre-image back and leaves no temporary. This closes the old gap where a rename failing midway left the earlier files renamed. On-disk backups (design step 3) are still open, for undo after a successful commit (EOIE-DO-UNDO). Apply: apply-do-commit.ps1 (dry by default; refuses unless rename-retry.do is in). Next: compile both crates and run the notebook tests on the node, check that do_edit.rs shrinks, then the acceptance probe from note 4 (two processes, disjoint anchored edits to one file, every edit kept or refused by name). 10:3x: the first node compile caught a name clash. workspace_primitives.files (opened after kit_do_store.store for renamed_within) has its own pending_path (~path : string), which shadowed the store accessor: 'Unification failure. Got string, expected pending_writes' at commit.spi:16. The store accessors are now entry_count, entry_path, entry_text, entry_base and entry_index; the rerun is job-1793198615614.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_10 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 10u32, "2026-10-31T09:59Z", "kit", "10-31 06:0x kit groom (WIP limit, dangling rule 'no Active silent > 3 d'): Active -> Planned at 40%. No note for about 3 days, so this item isn't in progress. Planned keeps it in 'agile next --team kit' as ready work (Paused would park it out of the queue). Kit had 42 Active items, with Active meaning anything ever touched rather than being worked now. The progress and notes stay; whoever pulls it next sets it Active again.")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_11 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 11u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_12 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 12u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries EDIT-8 (Hash-checked append and journal writes); LEASE-9 (Owner token against concurrent writers); MERGE-1 (Three-way merge with receipts). Why: LEASE-9/MERGE-1 mapped 10-24; do append ships without a tail-hash precondition, which is the changed-since-read check this item plans")
inl note_of_kit_do_concurrent_edits_13 () = Note ("KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", 13u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. EDIT-8 · Hash-checked append and journal writes · P0 · ota, ssam, ge2e, brz, scp · Partial / Journals and feedback logs were rewritten whole, appended through shell redirection, or simulated with `text-replace` on the known last line. Add append with an expected-tail-hash precondition and EOL preservation, plus a typed journal append for several rows. `state-append` joined several rows into one space-separated line and accepted only a parent root; reject multi-record input or add an explicit multi-row form, and say in the error which root it expects. *Now:* `do append <file> 'line'` exists, without a hash precondition. Writes are also blocked after `should_wrap` (see LEASE-4). || LEASE-9 · Owner token against concurrent writers · P0 · ota · Open / Another transaction mutated the same shared workspace mid-round: it overwrote the lease file, removed a partial crate and repointed the global EOIE entrypoint. Bind the lease to the workspace with an owner token, require compare-and-swap by hash before `text-replace`, `fs-write`, `fs-remove` and workspace edits, refuse a foreign token, and pin the EOIE binary identity for the whole transaction. || MERGE-1 · Three-way merge with receipts · P2 · ota · Open / Ancestor / current / candidate merge that preserves conflicts without mutating, with conflict count, fingerprints and owner in the receipt; per-candidate ancestor selection that fails on ties, or a declarative ancestor map; merge rehearsal of owner packets onto an integration trunk.")
inl fossil_assimilation () = Task ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", "Study Fossil SCM and list what eoie should absorb (atomic check-ins, the artifact/content-addressed store, built-in tickets and wiki, sync, timeline, undo, backups), with each feature filed as an eoie item with all fields", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "kit,research,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fossil_assimilation () = Planning ("FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl lib_generic_audit () = Task ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", "Audit lib/spiral for non-generic code: product, app, team or user specifics move out of lib (to the owning repo) or get generalized", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "-", "lib,audit,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_generic_audit () = Planning ("LIB-GENERIC-AUDIT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl kit_agile_field_completeness () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", "eoie agile flags items with unset planning fields (estimate, load, assignee, deps) and gains the detail fields tasks keep putting in notes (acceptance rule, verify command, conflict surface)", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "KIT-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS", "kit,agile,dogfood,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_field_completeness () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:44Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:44 main (user 2026-10-23 21:4x: 'we also gotta get the discipline of filling all tasks's fields, also when needing more fields for detail, requesting that also'). Today many items show 'estimate=- load=- assignee=-', and the stop gate's light-work list depends on load. Ask: (1) 'agile list --incomplete' plus an agile check that names each open item with an unset field; (2) typed fields for what notes carry today: acceptance (the pre-registered rule), verify (the command that proves it), and touches (files and modules, for concurrent-edit ordering); (3) add/set accept them, and show prints them. Done when the check reports 0 incomplete open items for team main after a backfill pass.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): AGILE-5 (progress and ratings from evidence with the denominator recorded; prompt blocks: 0-1000 ratings with deltas each round). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 3u32, "2026-10-24T02:26Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:27 main grooming census: of 276 open items, 123 lack an estimate and 111 lack a load (assignee now set on all: 208 filled with agent by rule). Per team: main 104, eoie 69, x 23, tibia 2 had at least one gap. Estimates stay unset until sized from each item's milestones (estimates-from-rules); this verb should list them per team so each lane sizes its own.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 4u32, "2026-10-24T03:09Z", "main", "2026-10-23 23:08 main grooming: main team load backfill by a rule written before applying it: heavy when the remaining work is a compiler pack gate or install (backend codegen items, PACK-*, HOPACSOTA*, compiler and syntax items), light for docs, CI config, cleanup and posts, medium for lib, Cells and Kino items needing targeted compiles. Applied to 85 main items with no load. 65 main items still lack an estimate; they are sized from milestones as each is picked up.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 5u32, "2026-10-27T07:26Z", "main", "10-27 03:26 main: 10-27 03:26 main groom (user 03:2x 'groom the backlog'): planning-field audit of team main's 159 open items. 51 had no estimate. Sized by the estimates-from-rules method (each item's own milestone and stop-rule notes, never its description; estimate = REMAINING effort). SET (5): HOPACSOTA-P6 60m (both promotion gates green; left: decide whether P3 really gates promotion, then the user's gpt5parallel call), SPIRAL-FORMATTER 240m (eoie's two respell gaps, n84 and n85), CI-K3S 300m (Dockerfile, Deployment + Service, k3s CI job from the 10-26 sketch; blocked on the user's placement call), CELLS-SECTION-TARGET-SPLIT 60m (option B GREEN on the node; left: land), RUST-FNMUT-CAPTURE-MOVE 120m (root cause is lib, one slice). NOT SIZED, by reason. (a) Blocked on unsized work: HOPACSOTA-P3, P4, P5 (PLAN.md forbids starting before P2's engine gate numbers), EDITION3-280-SPLIT (after the symbol migration), HOPACSOTA (umbrella = sum of its parts). (b) Research-scale epics: HOPACSOTA-P2, SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, BEND2-IN-SPIRAL, SPIRAL-UI, PWSH-FREE, GPU-RENTAL-READINESS, LIB-CANON-NAMES, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST. (c) Another lane sizes them: fiscal (FISCAL-APP, FISCAL-BR-DELPHI-BENCH, FISCAL-XSD-PATTERNS-LARGE, FISCAL-GENERIC); compiler (HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH, HOPACSOTA-P7, COMPILER-CROSS-MACHINE-REPRO, RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION, GADT-JOIN-POINT-GROWING-INDEX, SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES, FSX-FROM-SPI, EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION). Both lanes were asked tonight. (d) Parked by user rule: DOTA-HEADLESS-WORLD, DOTA-FIRST-SCENARIO (no Dota until the user starts it). (e) STALE: the last note is the 10-24 11:49 fold of the 10-19 lap ledger, so nothing has moved since ~10-19, and they need a fresh read before sizing: NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE, NEAR-CONTRACT-CRATES, CONTRACTS-ON-ZIG, ZIG-COVERAGE-C-ONLY, SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR, KINO-HOT-PATHS, LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS, LIB-REACTORS, CELLS-NEW-PROJECT, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS. (f) Active with recent notes but no stated remaining milestone (size at their next note): BLOG, DIB-MODERNIZE, CI-OFFLOAD, CELLS-PROBES, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, UPSTREAM-CHANGES-POST. Also closed tonight: CELLS-PACK-NEXT (Done; its pack landed 10-24, all leaves closed). Kept open at 90%+: BEND-BACKEND (not-supported list), SPIRAL-FORMATTER (respell gaps), FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS (dep COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS at 75%), MATRIX-SPLIT-PARTS-MEMO (done-check needs a matrix run).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 6u32, "2026-10-27T22:57Z", "eoie", "18:57 eoie: census on the live backlog. Of 416 open items, 47 have no estimate, 2 no load and 2 no assignee. 17 of the 47 are Epics, which stay unsized by convention (an umbrella is the sum of its parts). 30 non-Epic items have a gap. Team eoie has none: its only gaps are its Epics. Two items without load or assignee: NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER (no estimate, load or assignee) and SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES (no assignee). The rest lack only an estimate: HOPACSOTA-P2..P5, EDITION3-280-SPLIT, NEAR-*, CELLS-*, LIB-REACTORS, FLOAT-TEXT-CANONICAL, FISCAL-GENERIC, RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, DOTA-*, CODESPACES-BUTTON-ALL-REPOS and similar. Most match main's 10-27 03:26 'not sized, by reason' groups. What exists today: list --load none and --assignee none select two of the three gaps. Missing: an estimate gap filter, and a single 'unplanned' view that exempts Epics from estimate. Design for the first half: list/next take --unplanned, meaning open items missing load or assignee, or missing an estimate unless the kind is Epic. Plus a view-add default named 'unplanned', so each lane runs 'agile view <root> unplanned --team <t>'. Placement: agile_task_listing is at 978/1000 generated lines, so the predicate goes in agile_planning_filters (73 lines of Spiral, already holds plan_selected) and the listing gains only one call. The second half (typed detail fields: acceptance rule, verify command, conflict surface) needs EOIE-AGILE-TYPED-FIELDS (the dep) first. Not drafted tonight; the census is the input for it.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 7u32, "2026-10-30T23:42Z", "main", "10-30 19:4x main R3 groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030): owner kit; next step: fill the 30 non-Epic gaps its census found (NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER and SIM-GLEAM-PACKAGES first: no load/assignee).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 8u32, "2026-10-31T11:26Z", "kit", "10-31 07:2x kit groom (dedup check): part (1) of n1, 'an agile check that names each open item with an unset field', is EOIE-AGILE-CHECK-FIELDS. That item is built: agile_planning_gaps is wired into check_runtime.spi, notebook green, Blocked only on TRAIN5e's install. Once it is accepted, part (1) here is done by that item, and this item keeps only part (2), the typed detail fields acceptance/verify/touches. That makes it a separate schema change, not a duplicate, so both stay.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 10u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 11u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries AGILE-5 (Regauge from evidence). Why: 10-24 note #2: progress/ratings from evidence with denominator")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_12 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 12u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. AGILE-5 · Regauge from evidence · P1 · ota, ssam, ge2e · Open / Ratings that saturate near 1000 on a local frontier hid missing roadmap blocks. Record the denominator (whole program vs local frontier), keep old→new baseline deltas, derive axis scores from evidence receipts rather than constants in source, refuse global scores built only from lane arithmetic, and add a rating roll-forward (current→previous, zero unchanged deltas) across ledgers.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_13 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 13u32, "2026-11-09T13:10Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:1x local rig, MEASURED + NARROWED. Part 1 (flag unset planning fields) is ALREADY LIVE: agile_quality_lint/lint.spi:67 PlanningFields fails an open item unless estimate > 0, load and assignee are set and a team: tag exists; it runs for Planned/Active items. Census of the live backlog (agile list, 816 open: Planned 576, Active 129, Paused 59, Blocked 51): estimate unset 6 (MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY, MICROBLOG-CROSS-BACKEND-REUSE, FLEET-NODE-SATURATION-24-7, MICROBLOG-PROFILE-AVATARS, ...), load unset 3 (FISCAL-FACTURX-PACKAGE-REQUEST, FLEET-NODE-SATURATION-24-7, NODEBENCH-SPIRAL-REWRITE), assignee unset 11 (HTML-README-ANY-CASE, NODE-BENCH-METRIC, NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS, RIG-SELF-HEALING, ...). So completeness is about 98% and the owning lanes fix their own rows. Part 2 (detail fields that keep landing in notes), mapped to what already exists: acceptance rule = the DoD: marker (DoneDefinition check); conflict surface = kit agile claim --paths (in use, e.g. microblog on KIT-STANDARDS-ENTRY-SUBDIRS); a verify command is the ONE field with no home: proofs live in prose (proof:, exit 0) and DoneEvidence only greps for the words. NEXT STEP (the remaining 60%): a typed verify row (Verify id command) set by kit agile edit <root> <ID> --verify <command>, shown by agile show, and DoneEvidence passes when the last note quotes that command's receipt. TypeFeatureSet: verify_row record + a lint_check case VerifyMissing for code-tagged Active items.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_14 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 14u32, "2026-11-09T13:10Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: verify_row record { id; command } in its own rows module (like waits.spi), a VerifyMissing case on lint_check checked for code-tagged Active items, and edit --verify parsed into a typed edit_field case, never a string row.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_field_completeness_15 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FIELD-COMPLETENESS", 15u32, "2026-11-09T13:14Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:2x local rig, VERIFY MODEL BUILT (unstaged, unprobed; in the post-TRAIN42e probe batch): new package src/agile_task_verify (verify.spi + package.spiproj + standards.spi + Cargo.toml, not yet a workspace member). task_verify record { item; command }, rows Verify (\"ID\", \"command\") in state/verify.spi, verify_reading union Quoted | NotQuoted | NoVerify: reading_of passes when the last note quotes the command case-insensitively. It reuses agile_task_waits.waits nth_quoted, quoted and slug_of through a package reference rather than copying them. kit standards: 0 findings (HostFree, LibReferenced, width 120). NEXT STEP after the probe: the edit --verify verb (mirror waits verb: verify_written with the same changed-since-read guard and module registration), the VerifyMissing lint_check for code-tagged Active items, the agile show line, then wiring (rig_agile_mutation arm, Cargo dep, workspace member, lock) in TRAIN44.")
inl kit_do_undo () = Task ("KIT-DO-UNDO", "eoie undo/redo: the last do program can be undone and redone from its pre-images (with --dry-run), as Fossil's undo restores saved file copies oldest-first", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS", "kit,do,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_do_undo () = Planning ("KIT-DO-UNDO", Minutes 180u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_do_undo_1 () = Note ("KIT-DO-UNDO", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: undo.c saves each file's prior content (undo_maybe_save, with a size cap, UNDO_TOOBIG) and the checkout tables (undo_vfile, undo_vmerge, undo_stash) before an undoable command, then undo_all_filesystem replays the copies oldest-first; one level; --dry-run; commit clears the stack. eoie: the pre-image backups from EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS become the undo record per do program (program id, files, pre and post hashes). 'eoie undo [--dry-run]' restores the pre-images only if each file still has the post hash (otherwise it names the file that moved on); 'eoie redo' re-applies. Today x hand-wrote reverse do programs (undo-8..1) for its 21:18 revert. This makes that one call. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_do_undo_2 () = Note ("KIT-DO-UNDO", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): EDIT-12 (inverse patch by receipt), CKPT-3 (restore one file by hash), EDIT-3 rollback after a failed post-write gate. Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_do_undo_3 () = Note ("KIT-DO-UNDO", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-DO-UNDO (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_do_undo_4 () = Note ("KIT-DO-UNDO", 4u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries EDIT-3 (Compilerless multi-file transaction with rollback); EDIT-12 (Inverse patch and rollback receipts); CKPT-3 (Restore by hash). Why: 10-24 intake note #2 maps EDIT-12, CKPT-3 and EDIT-3 rollback here; create/delete/move in one program already ship (do write/move, KIT-EDIT-BATCH Done); Cargo-lockfile rollback part is bundle-era")
inl note_of_kit_do_undo_5 () = Note ("KIT-DO-UNDO", 5u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. EDIT-3 · Compilerless multi-file transaction with rollback · P0 · auth, sqlt, ota, node, ssam, brz, scp, erp, ge2e · Partial / The most repeated request: one atomic call that creates, replaces, inserts (before/after an anchor) and patches many files, with expected counts, multi-hunk edits, preserved final newlines and per-file preimage hashes. It validates everything before writing anything and rolls back after a failed gate. Plan-IR batching depended on a Spiral compiler that was often unavailable, and corrupted literals when it did run. Two further rules came from real failures: / - Idempotence is row-local. A row is 'already applied' only if its own anchor location holds the replacement, not if the replacement text exists anywhere in the file (`apply-spec` skipped three live needles this way). / - A transaction that touches a Cargo workspace refreshes the lockfile offline inside the transaction, runs the locked gate, and on failure restores files, manifests, workspace members and lockfile together. Its receipt says `trial_green`, `materialized`, `postcheck_failed`, `rollback_complete` or `rollback_partial`; rollback was partial twice in oracle-to-ansi. / A transaction also needs a write-ahead id and `patch status`, so a client disconnect mid-apply leaves a recoverable record instead of an unknown state. / *Now:* `eoie do <root> -` covers multi-file exact replacements with expected counts, validated before any write, from stdin. Still missing: creating, deleting and moving files in the same transaction, anchor inserts, and rollback after a post-write gate. || EDIT-12 · Inverse patch and rollback receipts · P2 · ssam, brz · Open / Store immutable, content-addressed inverse material for every replacement transaction, so a rejected candidate can be rolled back by receipt instead of by hand. || CKPT-3 · Restore by hash · P1 · ssam, ge2e, sbin · Open / `restore-from-seal <bundle> <relative> --expect-current-hash --expect-source-hash` and `checkpoint restore-file`, resolving the authoritative sealed bundle by content identity so a same-named directory never outranks it. Record a typed rollback receipt linking both hashes.")
inl kit_edit_intent_leases () = Task ("KIT-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", "eoie edit intents: a session announces 'editing <files> against base <hash>' and other sessions get a warning before overlapping, like Fossil's ci-lock fork guard", Story, P1, D3, 10u32, Planned, "KIT-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS,KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,coordination,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_edit_intent_leases () = Planning ("KIT-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_edit_intent_leases_1 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: xfer.c 1773-1850, 'pragma ci-lock CHECKIN-HASH CLIENT-ID' stores a ci-lock-<hash> config row with login, clientid and time; a second client committing against the same base gets 'ci-lock-fail USER TIME' and is warned it would fork; checkin.c refuses a forking commit without --allow-fork/--override-lock and rechecks before writing. eoie: 'eoie intent <files> [--base <hash>] --as <team>' writes a soft lease. do edits and agile writes print a warning (not a block) when another team holds an overlapping intent; intents expire on land or after N minutes. Tonight's duplicate Kotlin val pass and the listm' ordering conflict with x are exactly what a visible intent would have caught before drafting. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_edit_intent_leases_2 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", 2u32, "2026-10-28T12:53Z", "eoie", "08:5x kit: design, grounded in the window store (kit_machine_windows/window_model.spi, kit_window_claim/claim.spi). An edit intent is one more window kind, not a new store:\n- window_class gains EditIntent (\"edit-intent\"). It is lease_only like TreeRead, so clashes is false and it never blocks or queues anything; needed_mb 0. 'kit window list' shows intents with no new code.\n- One window per path (no joined path lists, per the style contract): label = the path or directory, as TreeRead's label is a root. window_row gains holder : string as an optional seventh tab field; row_of reads it with tab_field 6 (empty when absent), so every existing row still parses and only row_text writes the extra field.\n- 'kit intent <path>... --as <team> [--minutes N, default 120]' writes the rows; 'kit intent drop <path>... --as <team>' removes the holder's own rows; expired rows are already pruned by the store (windows_cli test 'the expired window was pruned').\n- Coverage reuses claim.spi's path-under-root rule (the same check 'window claim' applies to tree-read windows), so an intent on src/kit_do_edit covers every file below it.\n- Warning, not a block (Fossil's ci-lock warns; checkin refuses only without --allow-fork). kit do: a new crate kit_do_intents exports kit_do_intent_warnings (pending, root, as) -> lines. It takes the same pending handle as kit_do_commit (kit_do_store), lists live EditIntent rows covering each staged path whose holder differs from the program's 'as <team>' header line (a new optional program line), and prints 'warning: <path> has an edit intent by <team> until HH:mm' into the receipt before the commit. Without an 'as' line every covering intent is shown. kit_do_edit gains one host line for the call (it stays under its line gate after the kit_do_commit split frees space).\n- Fossil's base hash is not needed here: losing an update is already prevented by the compare-and-swap in kit_do_commit (note 9 on EOIE-DO-CONCURRENT-EDITS). The intent's job is earlier: telling a team before it drafts against a file someone else is editing, as with the duplicate Kotlin val pass and the listm' ordering conflict in note 1.\nAcceptance: a fixture with two EditIntent rows (one expired, one live, different holders) gives one warning line for a covered staged path, none for an uncovered path or for the holder itself; window list and claim keep passing on old 6-field rows. Order: after the kit_do_commit split lands (shared pending handle), then window_model (holder field and EditIntent), then kit intent, then kit_do_intents.")
inl note_of_kit_edit_intent_leases_3 () = Note ("KIT-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:28Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-EDIT-INTENT-LEASES (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_draft_stash () = Task ("KIT-DRAFT-STASH", "eoie drafts: named patch sets saved against a baseline (stash save/list/show/apply/pop/drop/diff), replacing ad-hoc %TEMP% apply scripts", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-DO-UNDO", "kit,do,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_draft_stash () = Planning ("KIT-DRAFT-STASH", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_draft_stash_1 () = Note ("KIT-DRAFT-STASH", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: stash.c, save/snapshot/list/show/pop/apply/goto/drop/diff/rename; a stash is a changeset relative to a baseline check-in, and pop/apply/goto/drop are undoable. eoie: 'eoie draft save <name> <do program> --base <files' hashes>' stores the program plus base hashes; show prints the planned diff against the current tree; apply runs it on a target root (scratch copy or live, with a mandatory target, as x's landing rule requires); rebase re-checks anchors on the current base; list shows drafts with stale-base flags. Tonight main and x kept about 15 drafts as hand-written apply.ps1 files with private anchor checks (lib-gaps-x, lib-unicode, nullary-mirrors...). This makes them one typed store. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_draft_stash_2 () = Note ("KIT-DRAFT-STASH", 2u32, "2026-10-24T05:50Z", "main", "2026-10-24 01:50 main: a needed verb, from tonight's miss. 'stash status <target>': for every saved draft, is it applied to this target (its new text present / an 'already applied' guard trips), applicable (anchors match once), or conflicting (anchors miss)? Main found by hand (a scratch loop dry-running ~56 pack slices against a composed core) that a composite silently lacked 7 reviewed slices (next7/next8), and that one blocked a lib item (LIB-PAR-MAP's closure factory). Drafts whose apply scripts lack an 'already applied' check show as conflicting even when included, so the stash record should keep each draft's post-image anchors to decide 'applied' positively.")
inl note_of_kit_draft_stash_3 () = Note ("KIT-DRAFT-STASH", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:28Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-DRAFT-STASH (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_timeline () = Task ("KIT-TIMELINE", "eoie timeline: one time-ordered view across teams of notes, item changes, job starts and ends, windows, lands and drafts, Fossil's primary view", Story, P2, D3, 35u32, Active, "-", "kit,coordination,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_timeline () = Planning ("KIT-TIMELINE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_timeline_1 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil puts the timeline first (fossil-v-git: 'situational awareness for the whole team'): every check-in, ticket change, wiki edit and forum post in one DAG-aware time view. eoie keeps the same events in separate places (agile notes, job dirs, window log, gate logs). 'eoie timeline [--since 2h] [--team t] [--kind job,window,note,land]' merges them. That answers 'what did the other lanes just do', which tonight took cross-session messages and reads of gate.log and job lists. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_timeline_2 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 2u32, "2026-10-31T00:36Z", "main", "10-30 20:36 main groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 group C): this item's latest note cites %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read, which did not survive the 10-28 wipe (Test-Path false). It was reference reading of the fossil sources, so re-fetch it when the item resumes; no draft work was lost.")
inl note_of_kit_timeline_3 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TIMELINE (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_timeline_4 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 4u32, "2026-11-09T19:30Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 23:5x local rig, CORE BUILT (unstaged, unprobed; in the probe batch): src/rig_timeline/timeline.spi (pure, HostFree). timeline_event NoteAdded | StatusChanged | JobStarted | JobEnded | WindowOpened | Landed | DraftWritten (the title's kinds); stamped { at; team; event } with at as UTC minute text yyyy-mm-ddThh:mmZ, the backlog note format, so streams compare as text; iso_of_ms converts job ids and exit stamps (epoch ms) with exact integer civil-date math (days-from-civil inverse, no float, leap centuries right); timeline streams teams window = every stream merged, filtered to the teams (empty = all) and the [from, until) window, in time order (stable for equal minutes). Test: the epoch, TRAIN42e's job id 1794221691845 -> 2026-11-09T10:54Z (its coordinator log says 06:5x local, -04:00), 2000-02-29, a two-stream merge inside a window, and a one-team view. NEXT: the IO half reads each stream (agile notes and history, kit job records, window and land logs) into stamped rows and prints kit timeline --since <dur> [--team].")
inl note_of_kit_timeline_5 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 5u32, "2026-11-09T21:23Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig, proof: the model passed its notebook gate (run_notebook --gate, rust target) on ganymede, outside the TRAIN42e train, which timed out on moon: rig shipped the box lib and today's 41 self-contained model packages to /root/rig-probe and gated each (nice, 2 pinned cores). The first probe was red and is fixed (unstaged): strings are not ord: comparisons now use key_of (the 12 digits of the minute stamp as i64); re-probed green. The probe wait is answered; next step is the item's IO half per its last note.")
inl note_of_kit_timeline_6 () = Note ("KIT-TIMELINE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T23:24Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 19:2x rig: parked on train:TRAIN44: the IO half reads note, job and land streams and adds kit timeline (a CLI verb); the core is probe-green.")
inl kit_typed_hooks () = Task ("KIT-TYPED-HOOKS", "eoie hooks: typed events (after-land, after-job, after-note) run configured commands in sequence, with hook list/test --dry-run, as in Fossil's hook.c", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-TIMELINE", "kit,automation,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_typed_hooks () = Planning ("KIT-TYPED-HOOKS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_typed_hooks_1 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-HOOKS", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: hook.c stores hooks as a JSON array setting {type, cmd, seq}, validates the type, substitutes %F/%R/%A, feeds after-receive hooks the received artifact list on stdin, keeps a hook-last-rcvid watermark so each event is processed once, has a hook-embargo, and offers 'hook test --dry-run'. eoie: typed events with a processed watermark, so e.g. 'after a lib land, message the teams that read lib' or 'after review-N green, queue its install' become declared hooks instead of a human relaying them. That serves the user's 'more bouncy' coordination. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_typed_hooks_2 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-HOOKS", 2u32, "2026-10-31T00:36Z", "main", "10-30 20:36 main groom (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030 group C): this item's latest note cites %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read, which did not survive the 10-28 wipe (Test-Path false). It was reference reading of the fossil sources, so re-fetch it when the item resumes; no draft work was lost.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_hooks_3 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-HOOKS", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-TYPED-HOOKS (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_agile_event_log () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", "agile state as an append-only, content-addressed event log (each add/set/edit/note an immutable event), with item rows derived by replay, so concurrent writers merge by set union like Fossil's G-Set of artifacts", Story, P0, D5, 55u32, Planned, "KIT-STATE-SCALE,LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "kit,agile,concurrency,fossil,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_event_log () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 1u32, "2026-10-24T01:53Z", "main", "2026-10-23 21:53 main (FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION). Fossil: the repository is an unordered set of immutable artifacts named by content hash (fileformat.wiki, Z checksum card); every metadata table is derived and 'contains no new information' (fossil rebuild recomputes it); sync is set union (igot/gimme), with no artifact-level conflicts (sync.wiki calls it a grow-only CRDT). eoie today rewrites agile.spi rows in place under a lock (EOIE-AGILE-ATOMIC-WRITES), and EOIE-STATE-SCALE plans append-only note segments. Extension: also make status, progress and field edits events (actor, time, item, field, old, new), with rows derived by replay. Two lanes' writes then never conflict, history answers 'who set this to 60% when', and cross-machine or CI sync becomes a union. Fossil source read: %TEMP%\\fossil-src-read (release tarball from fossil-scm.org, read only)")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 2u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "2026-10-23 22:2x main: consolidated pendencies covered here (EOIE-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, class a): AGILE-2 (every mirror and report rendered from typed state in one transaction). Spec text: Downloads/eoie-consolidated/agile_eoie.md.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 3u32, "2026-10-26T05:17Z", "eoie", "01:17 eoie: user 10-26 01:1x asked whether state as .spi has paid off, and proposed persisting a binary derivative (like Cells memoizes execution) to find new uses for iggy plus typefeatureset. Assessment from measurements.\nPaid off:\n(a) state is typed: the compiler checks the union schema, so a bad row is a type error;\n(b) a real compiler corpus: at 2.6 MB it found COMPILER-MODULE-PARSE-STACK (a stack overflow);\n(c) formatter and respell dogfooding: release byte identity runs 8 verbs on a raw and a formatted backlog.\nCosts now, measured tonight on a fully loaded box:\n- 6.66 MB, 8,758 lines (1.15 MB on 10-17);\n- list 6.2 s, next 5.7 s, show 0.19 s;\n- writes rewrite the whole file: note about 17 ms/MB, set about 27 ms + 65 ms/MB (STATE-SCALE note 12), so a set is about 0.46 s now;\n- one lock: a transient 'could not move the replacement file into place' tonight under concurrent writers;\n- the 8 MB threshold arrives in 2-3 days.\nDesign, keeping the strengths:\n1. .spi stays the canonical human-readable projection, typed and checked.\n2. A content-addressed binary derivative: a memo of the parsed typed rows keyed by the state text's sha256, like Cells memo. A read with a matching hash skips the parse, and list and next become loads. Phase 1, local, no infra.\n3. Writes become typed events (TaskAdded, FieldEdited old to new, NoteAppended, StatusMoved) on an iggy agile stream, and the .spi is materialized from them (Fossil rebuild).\n   - Writers never rewrite megabytes, and lanes and machines stop contending on one lock.\n   - Each session's consumer offset answers 'what changed since I last read', which beats show --last.\n   - History answers who set what when.\n4. typefeatureset: event constructors indexed by the item's status typestate make an illegal move unrepresentable in the log. The codec family is the same one as LIB-IGGY-CLIENT (one model, Rust and Gleam).\nDecision rules, written before any measurement:\nR1 (phase 1 memo): accept when a warm list on the live file is under 500 ms and its output is byte-identical to the parse path (the byte identity check covers it); a stale-hash read must fall back to a parse.\nR2 (phase 2 events): accept when a note write's p95 stays under 20 ms at any file size, replaying the log reproduces agile.spi byte for byte, and two machines appending concurrently converge to one identical file.\nR3: until R2 passes, the single file stays canonical, per the 10-17 decision.\nSequencing: phase 1 is the first eoie change after KIT-RENAME lands (feature freeze until then); phase 2 waits for LIB-IGGY-CLIENT M2.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 4u32, "2026-10-26T09:15Z", "eoie", "05:15 eoie: dominant-phase check before building phase 1, with the installed binary on state\\agile.spi (6.87 MB, 803 items). A filtered list still parses the whole file and counts every status, yet takes 155 ms (--prefix KIT-REN, 1 row shown) and 340 ms (Blocked, 17 rows), both from the tool's own elapsed_ms. The full list takes 5,769 ms and 6,767 ms for 803 rows. So parsing is about 150 ms and rendering is about 7 ms per row, over 95% of a full list. A parse memo (phase 1 as designed) can save at most the ~150 ms; it cannot bring a full list under R1's 500 ms. The per-row cost is where list-planning-index points: row_line looked up each row's planning entry by a linear scan, so a full list is quadratic in items. The drafted index makes it one HashMap lookup, byte-identical on the node; the release speed check is still pending. EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC (each string parameter copied at the export boundary) adds a smaller constant. Revised order, decided now by R1's own target: (1) land list-planning-index after the rename and measure a full list in release (5 runs, median); (2) build the memo only if that list still misses 500 ms with parse as the larger share. R1 is unchanged; only the order of work moves.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 5u32, "2026-10-26T12:05Z", "eoie", "08:05 eoie: step (1) of the revised order measured on the worker node (scratch clean\\respell\\node-list-speed.ps1: release builds of kit-cli before and after list-planning-index.do, a copy of the shared backlog, 803 items, 5 full-list runs each, elapsed_ms from the tool). Before: median 12,158 ms (8,519 / 13,753 / 9,879 / 12,158 / 15,276). After: median 2,118 ms (1,624 / 2,118 / 3,315 / 2,976 / 1,230), about 5.7x faster. Output byte-identical: 804 lines. The node was at 100% CPU with other lanes' jobs, so absolute numbers are inflated and noisy; the ratio is the reliable part. By R1 (warm full list under 500 ms), still not met: about 2.6 ms per row remains. Per the rule, the memo is built only if parse is now the larger share. Next measurement, set now: on the same binaries, time a filtered list (--prefix, 1 row, which still parses everything) against the full list; if parse is under half of the full list, the next step is the per-row renderer again (EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC copies, then the note index), not the memo. Script bug, fixed: Say also writes to the pipeline, so Measure-List returned an array and the final ratio line threw after every result was logged.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 6u32, "2026-10-26T12:10Z", "eoie", "08:10 eoie: parse-share measurement (same two release binaries on the node, 5 runs each, medians): before full 15,057 ms, before filtered (--prefix KIT-REN, 1 row, full parse) 1,024 ms; after full 1,877 ms, after filtered 1,756 ms. So after the index, setup (parse plus building the planning index) is about 94% of a full list, and by the rule the memo is next. Two caveats before building it: (a) the filtered list got slower with the index (1,024 to 1,756 ms), which suggests planning_index_of costs more than a hash fill should, perhaps a string copy per planning line; (b) noise is large at 100% node CPU (after filtered ranged 734 to 3,314 ms). Rule, set now: remeasure with 15 runs when the node's CPU is under 70% (read from node status first). If the filtered list after the index exceeds before-filtered by more than 20%, fix planning_index_of first, and the memo waits.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 7u32, "2026-10-26T16:14Z", "eoie", "12:14 eoie: the granular planning index (no host flow: dictm plus sm' ops, scratch list-planning-index-granular.do) measured on the node, same method (release builds, 5 runs, 803-item backlog copy, node at 100% CPU). Output identical, 804 lines. Full list before 12,402 ms, after 6,764 ms (1.83x); filtered list after 7,612 ms, more than the full list, so building the index now dominates. The host-flow draft gave 1,877 / 1,756 ms in the 10:0x run. By the granular discipline (style holds at any cost), the granular index is the one that lands; the host-flow draft is withdrawn. Its cost is lib performance, to fix in lib so every user gains. Suspects, to measure in order: (1) sm'.lines over 6.8 MB (one Rc<str> per line, ~60k lines); (2) dictm.insert's unwrap_or_clone copying the whole map when the Rc is shared (quadratic); (3) per-line sm'.starts_with expansion. Measurement, set now: a lib Cells notebook timing each of the three on a 6.8 MB string, with the review box idle; the slowest goes to lib as a fix (likely a lines iterator without per-line Rc, or a dictm insert that keeps the Rc unique).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 8u32, "2026-10-26T20:50Z", "kit", "16:50 kit: the three-suspect lib measurement is done (scratch clean\\lib-perf\\index-cost.spi, Rust cells on the worker node, job-1793047551305-kit-lib-perf3, both tests pass). The first run timed out at 300 s with one end-of-test print, so the notebook now prints each phase as it ends and runs 6,000 and 12,000 backlog-shaped lines (0.55 and 1.11 MB) so scaling shows directly. Results for 6k then 12k: sm'.lines 1,065 then 3,636 ms (3.4x for 2x the input, superlinear); dictm insert fill 1 then 2 ms; starts_with marking 1 then 1 ms; and, not on the suspect list, sm'.concat_list 2,866 then 13,301 ms (4.6x, quadratic: its Rust arm is concat_right_fold, the trade-off recorded on GLEAM-STRING-LINEAR whose follow-up is a native join). Verdict by the 12:14 rule: suspect (1), sm'.lines, is the one; dictm (2) and starts_with (3) are cleared. The granular index pays for lines over the whole 6.8 MB backlog, so the fix belongs in lib (main's LIB-SM-LINES-GRANULAR, where lines' Rust arm pushes one element at a time into a vec built with am'.vec_push). Kit's side waits for that lib change: after it lands, rerun this notebook as the acceptance check (12k under 2.2x the 6k time for both lines and concat_list), then the node full-list timing.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 9u32, "2026-10-26T21:00Z", "main", "10-26 17:00 main: 10-26 16:5x main: lib sm'.lines and sm'.concat_list Rust performance, from kit's scaling data (Rust cells on the node, backlog-shaped text 6k then 12k lines, 0.55 then 1.11 MB; baseline job-1793047551305): lines 1,065 / 3,636 ms (3.4x for 2x the input, superlinear); concat_list 2,866 / 13,301 ms (4.6x, quadratic). These are the costs kit's granular planning index pays on the 6.8 MB backlog, so kit's fix waits on lib.\nCauses, by reading sm'.spi: concat_list's Rust arm is concat_right_fold, whose acc = x ++# sep ++# acc copies the whole accumulator every step (quadratic). lines' Rust arm emulates Rust's line rules (split on newline, strip a trailing CR per line, drop a final empty part) through a vec_push loop in a join point.\nSlice (TEMP lib-sm-linear/apply.ps1; anchored, 0 new lines over 120):\n- concat_list Rust: the list becomes an array (listm'.box |> listm'.to_array'), then ONE host call, Rc<str>::from((*items.borrow()).join(&*separator)) (linear).\n- lines Rust: ONE host call, text.lines().map(Rc::from).collect into the array. That is the semantics the existing test lines_follow_rust_line_rules pins ('a\\r\\n\\nb\\n' gives 3 lines).\n- Other backends unchanged.\nAcceptance rule (kit's, adopted before the run): for both functions the 12k time is under 2.2x the 6k time, on kit's own notebook (index-cost.spi, phase lines in the .ipynb). A and B run in the SAME node job for equal load (job-1793048449505-main-sm-linear-ab: A = kit's candlib, B = a copy with the slice), plus the lib gate's lines and concat tests on landing.\nOrdering: sm'.spi also carries main's queued pack (job-1793027588823) and Nil batch 2, so the slice lands after the pack, and lib is read by kit's FOLLOWUP until the rename release ends.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_10 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 10u32, "2026-10-26T21:07Z", "main", "10-26 17:07 main: 10-26 17:0x main: sm' linear slice A/B on the node, both sides in ONE job (job-1793048449505) for equal load, on kit's index-cost.spi. Outputs agree on both sides: equal bytes, line counts and planning counts at 6k and 12k, so the slice changed only time.\n- lines: A 1,181 / 3,956 ms; B 45 / 7 ms. Rule (12k under 2.2x the 6k time): B ratio 0.16, PASS.\n- concat_list: A 3,401 / 15,650 ms; B 3 / 57 ms. That is 275x faster at 12k, but the B ratio is 19x, so by the rule as written it FAILS. The rule is not amended after the fact.\nThe concat verdict is RED by rule. Two readings fit 3 -> 57 ms: single-digit-ms noise, or something non-linear left (for example listm'.box |> listm'.to_array' before the join). To tell them apart, a NEW measurement, pre-registered before its run: kit's notebook with three larger sizes on side B only (A is quadratic and would take hours at 192k); node job-1793048885441, 48k/96k/192k lines. Rule: for both functions, the 192k time under 2.2x the 96k time, with B's times there at least 50 ms so the ratio isn't noise; the planning asserts (640/1280/2560) keep the outputs checked. If concat fails that too, the cause is in the list-to-array step, and the fix moves the join to a host fold over the list itself.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_11 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 11u32, "2026-10-26T21:12Z", "main", "10-26 17:12 main: 10-26 17:1x main: the scaled follow-up (node job-1793048885441, side B only, outputs checked by the planning asserts 640/1280/2560, all passed), scored by n10's rule (192k under 2.2x the 96k time, both at least 50 ms):\n- concat_list: 96k 111 ms, 192k 80 ms. Ratio 0.72, PASS.\n- lines: 96k 191 ms, 192k 430 ms. Ratio 2.25, FAIL by 0.05.\nSo each function has now passed one rule and missed the other. The node is visibly noisy: concat measured 54 ms at 6k and 4 ms at 12k in this very run, an inverted size order, because the node runs other lanes' jobs. That points at single-sample noise, not at either implementation (code reading: box and to_array' are single linear passes on Rust; the join is one host call). I won't add a third ad-hoc reading. ONE tiebreaker, pre-registered now and final: node job-1793049153700, three repetitions each at 96k and 192k; for both functions the MEDIAN 192k time must be under 2.2x the MEDIAN 96k time. Its verdict decides. For scale, against the original costs: at 12k lines B is 7 ms vs 3,956 ms for lines and 4 to 57 ms vs 15,650 ms for concat. At kit's 6.8 MB backlog (about 75k lines) both fall from minutes to about 100 ms.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_12 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 12u32, "2026-10-26T21:18Z", "main", "10-26 17:18 main: 10-26 17:1x main: the FINAL pre-registered tiebreaker (n11; node job-1793049153700; median of 3 runs at 96k and 192k, side B, outputs asserted) FAILS for both functions:\n- concat_list: 96k 42/47/103 ms (median 47); 192k 129/136/222 ms (median 136). Ratio 2.89, FAIL.\n- lines: 96k 58/72/118 ms (median 72); 192k 150/231/247 ms (median 231). Ratio 3.21, FAIL.\nVerdict, as declared final in advance: the slice does NOT meet the linear-scaling acceptance rule, so it is not landed as accepted.\nWhat stands: identical outputs, and a large constant-factor win (12k lines: lines 3,956 to about 7 ms, concat 15,650 to 4-57 ms). Kit's index cost on the 6.8 MB backlog falls from minutes to about 100 ms either way. Medians strip the per-run noise, so the remaining ~3x per doubling at 9-18 MB is real, and may come from the platform rather than lib: str::lines and [Rc<str>]::join both allocate per line (one Rc<str> each), and these sizes cross cache and allocator regimes.\nNext, a diagnostic and not an acceptance test, offered to kit as the rule's owner: a native-Rust program doing exactly the same two operations at 96k and 192k on the node. If native Rust shows the same ratio, the right acceptance shape is 'within a stated factor of native' rather than 'under 2.2x', and kit decides that. If native is linear, the remaining cost is in lib's glue (for example the list box/clone before the join), to be traced phase by phase.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_13 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 13u32, "2026-10-26T21:24Z", "main", "10-26 17:24 main: 10-26 17:2x main: kit's acceptance rule (fixed by kit before any same-job native numbers existed; my standalone native run was disclosed to kit), run as written in ONE node job (job-1793049776329): side B median of 3 at 96k/192k, then native Rust (str::lines into Vec<Rc<str>>, [Rc<str>]::join) median of 3. Per function: (1) slice ratio at most 1.25x native's ratio, (2) slice 192k time at most 3x native's.\n- concat_list: B 44 / 124 ms (ratio 2.82); native 15 / 51 ms (ratio 3.40, limit 4.25), ok; 124 ms against limit 153, ok. ACCEPT.\n- lines: B 72 / 120 ms (ratio 1.67); native 54 / 132 ms (ratio 2.44, limit 3.06), ok; 120 ms against limit 396, ok. ACCEPT.\nBoth halves ACCEPTED. Native Rust itself grows 2.4-3.4x per doubling at 9-18 MB on this node (per-line Rc<str> allocation, cache regime), so the earlier 2.2x rule asked lib to beat native scaling. That is why it failed, and it's exactly what kit's rule was built to check. The verdict history stays on record (n10 RED, n11 split, n12 tiebreaker FAIL). The land is registered in the post-rename requeue (main-serial-sm-linear: kit gate on sm'.spi, which runs lines_follow_rust_line_rules and concat_list's own tests, then land), after main's sm' pack chain.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_14 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 14u32, "2026-10-26T22:02Z", "main", "10-26 18:02 main: 10-26 18:01 main: compiler base for n9-n13, from x's finding that the node's shared single-flight DLL is stale (499AFC18E49F, 10/25 15:23; this box has 4FD712E9E357, 10/26 08:19). The sm' A/B jobs did NOT use the shared DLL: kit's node-lib-perf.ps1 sets SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL to C:\\home\\remote-stage\\renameproof\\compiler\\SpiralCompiler.dll (kit's rename-proof compiler). A, B and native ran in the same job under that one compiler, so the relative accept verdict (n13) stands. The absolute timings carry that compiler's codegen. The gate on landing (main-serial-sm-linear) re-checks behavior on the installed compiler.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_15 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 15u32, "2026-10-31T19:02Z", "kit", "10-31 14:5x kit (user 14:4x: \"gotta make the writes faster so we keep growing\"): PROMOTED P1 -> P0 as the lead of the write-speed family (EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO, EOIE-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY and EOIE-STATE-SCALE feed it). Today's measurements on the installed E14F1F0B, live state/agile.spi at 8.9 MB: every note/set/edit rewrites the WHOLE file (bytes_before/after ~8.93 MB per receipt), and compare-and-swap re-reads it twice (before the write and on readback). kit's own clock gives note ~0.9 s and set ~1.1 s, and wall time is 3-6 s on a loaded box (process start plus that work); 'agile list --json' takes 13+ s. Concurrent writers now fail loudly instead of losing updates (seen live at 08:3x), which means every lane retries under load. Notes are most of the bytes, so FIRST SLICE: notes go to append-only segment files (state/notes/<yyyymm>.spi, one event per line: id, n, at, author, text), item rows stay in agile.spi, and readers merge. A note then appends a few hundred bytes instead of rewriting 9 MB, and two lanes' notes never conflict. Then status/field edits become events too. Acceptance: an agile note under 50 ms by kit's clock on today's state, 'agile show' and 'list --json' output byte-identical before and after migration, and the concurrent-writer test (two notes at once) both landing.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_16 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 16u32, "2026-10-31T19:08Z", "kit", "10-31 15:0x kit: MEASURED write cost against state size (scratch copies of today's backlog, notes trimmed proportionally, 3 notes plus 1 set each, kit's own elapsed_ms, loaded box): 1.82 MB -> note 266/328/341 ms, set 828; 5.18 MB -> note 1294/570/754, set 770; 8.78 MB (today) -> note 2027/3173/1717, set 1276. Note cost scales with file size (about 0.2-0.35 s per MB under load). Note rows are 8,617 of 10,415 lines, so item rows alone are about 1 MB. Moving notes out makes a note write a constant-size append and brings set down to the rows-only cost. CONTAINED DESIGN (reader inventory done): agile_note_model is THE note parser, used by about 15 packages (agile_note_listing, agile_note_seal, agile_note_edit, agile_task_notes, agile_task_views, agile_title_notes, agile_title_tails, agile_rename_rewrite, agile_task_rename, agile_todo_merge, agile_todo_record, kit_experiments, kit_experiment_amend, kit_experiment_import, kit_resume). Only 7 packages spell note rows directly. So: (1) agile_note_model reads notes from agile.spi rows UNION state/notes/*.spi segments, ordered by (id, n); (2) one writer primitive appends a note event to the current month's segment, with the next n per item from a small per-item counter index, and every note writer calls it; (3) agile seal and rename rewrite segments the way they rewrite rows today; (4) migration moves existing note rows into a segment in one guarded step. ROLLOUT: every lane writes this file all day, so the reader ships first (it understands both formats; one train), and writers switch in the next train. Then no lane ever runs a binary that can't read the other's notes.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_17 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 17u32, "2026-10-31T19:10Z", "kit", "10-31 15:1x kit: SIMPLIFICATION found while reading agile_note_model. Every note reader is TEXT-based: notes_text, notes_json, note_count and note_at take the whole state text and search it for the anchor 'inl note_of_<binding>_<n> () = Note (...'. So if segment files use the SAME row syntax, every reader works unchanged as soon as its loader returns agile.spi's text followed by state/notes/*.spi's text. The change shrinks to (a) one shared loader, 'state text with segments', replacing the direct reads of state/agile.spi (38 source files mention agile.spi; most are writers and tests, to be classified), and (b) one append writer for note rows. CAVEAT to settle first: 'agile check' typechecks state/ as a Spiral package (typecheck_receipts.spi), so a segment must be a module of that package which sees the task_note union declared in agile.spi. Either the segment opens the agile module, or the union moves to a small shared module both import. Prove that on a scratch backlog with 'agile check' before anything else. A segment with no module import would make check fail, not lose data, but it would block every lane's check.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_18 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 18u32, "2026-10-31T19:17Z", "kit", "10-31 15:2x kit: CAVEAT SETTLED by experiment (scratch copy of today's backlog, notes trimmed to 1.8 MB). The last note row was moved out of agile.spi into state/notes_s1.spi and the module registered in state/package.spiproj after agile. (a) The bare row fails 'agile check': 'notes_s1.spi:1:39: ETY-UnboundVariable: Unbound term variable: Note'. (b) With 'open agile' as the segment's first line, 'kit agile check' answers ok (files=5, build_attested=4; the first try hit the 30 s attestation cap under load, the second passed). (c) As expected, 'agile show' then listed 3 notes instead of 4, because today's loaders read agile.spi only; the reader change fixes exactly that. FORMAT therefore: a segment is 'open agile' + note rows in today's row syntax, registered as a state module after agile. Next concrete step: the shared loader (state text plus segments, in module order) used by every note reader, proven on this scratch layout (show returns 4 notes) before any writer appends.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_19 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 19u32, "2026-10-31T19:54Z", "kit", "10-31 15:5x kit: READER BUILT (unstaged) and WRITER SCOPED. Reader: agile_note_model gains segment_modules (the notes_* entries under 'modules:' in state/package.spiproj, in order), state_segments_text and state_text_with_notes. Test notes_in_segment_modules_read_with_the_state passes on the node (2/2): a note in notes_s1.spi counts with agile.spi's own, and only listed modules are read. agile_note_listing exports kit_agile_state_segments(state_dir). agile show (agile_task_inspection) joins it onto the live text for every note lookup (text, filtered and --json branches; the archive join stays as it was). The inspection package's host-macro count was rebaselined for that one crate-boundary call. In flight: a node candidate build (job-1793475988360) plus an end-to-end probe (state change on a scratch backlog: show must list 2 notes when the 2nd sits in a segment, and check must pass). WRITER: agile_task_notes (task_notes.rs is 975/1000) computes seq from note_count, builds note_row and rewrites the whole file with notes_appended plus state_written. The segment writer goes into a NEW crate (one export, append_note_row(state_dir, row) : outcome) so task_notes stays under the gate. It creates notes_<yyyymm>.spi with 'open agile' when missing, registers it in package.spiproj, and appends under an exclusive file lock so two lanes' appends never interleave or reuse a seq: it re-reads the segment inside the lock and recomputes seq there. GATED: the writer only runs with KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 until the reader-capable binary is installed for every lane (one train), then it becomes the default, then the migration moves old note rows out. Other readers to switch to state_text_with_notes before the default flips: agile list --json last_note (agile_task_listing), seal, memory links, kit_resume, the experiments packages.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_20 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 20u32, "2026-10-31T20:00Z", "kit", "10-31 16:0x kit: READER PROVEN END TO END on a built binary. The node candidate (job-1793475988360: mirror kit-verify, regen of agile_note_listing plus agile_task_inspection published into the mirror, cargo build kit-cli -> kit.exe 140D61F4) ran a probe on a scratch backlog: agile init, add SEG-1, two notes; then the second note's row was moved out of agile.spi into state/notes_s1.spi ('open agile' + the row) and notes_s1 registered after agile in state/package.spiproj. Results: 'agile show' lists notes (2) with both texts, 'show --json' includes the segment note, and 'agile check' answers ok (files=4, segment module typechecked). The installed binary on the same layout lists 1 note (n18's scratch run), so the reader is what changed. NEXT: the gated writer crate (n19), then switch the other readers (list --json last_note, seal, memory links, resume, experiments) to state_text_with_notes, all in the reader train before the writer becomes default. Probe note: the node's ssh shells need KIT_LEASE_POLICY=renewing too, or every write refuses with should_wrap=1.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_21 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 21u32, "2026-10-31T20:29Z", "kit", "10-31 16:3x kit: (1) The reader now covers 'agile list --json' too. agile_task_listing builds its note index from agile.spi + segments (kit_agile_state_segments; agile-note-listing added as a Cargo dep; Cargo.lock updated). A rebuilt node candidate (job-1793477340310, regen of agile_note_listing + agile_task_inspection + agile_task_listing) passed the extended probe: show lists notes (2) with the second in notes_s1.spi, show --json includes it, list --json reports \"notes\":2, and agile check ok. (2) Writer pure parts built and tested (agile_note_model 3/3 on the node): segment_name maps a UTC stamp to notes_<yyyymm>, and manifest_with_segment registers the segment in state/package.spiproj once, after agile and earlier segments (test cases: after agile, after an earlier segment, idempotent, agile last). LEFT: the IO writer crate (create the segment with 'open agile', register it, append under an exclusive lock, with seq recomputed inside the lock), gated by KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1; the remaining readers (seal, memory links, resume, experiments); then the reader train, the default flip, and the migration.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_22 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 22u32, "2026-10-31T20:50Z", "kit", "10-31 16:5x kit: WRITER BUILT AND MEASURED, and the measurement CORRECTS THE PLAN'S ORDER. New crate agile_note_segments (unstaged): note_appended(state_dir, row, stamp) registers notes_<yyyymm> in state/package.spiproj and appends the row to the segment (creating it with 'open agile'), both through compare-and-swap (workspace_primitives.files.replace_file_unchanged, ChangedSinceRead on a concurrent write). Test a_note_row_lands_in_this_months_registered_segment passes 1/1 on the node. agile note (agile_task_notes) now always counts segment notes when picking seq, and with KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 it appends to the segment instead of rewriting agile.spi. Node candidate BD0D8237 on a COPY OF TODAY'S REAL BACKLOG (agile.spi 8.85 MB): whole-file note 2519/2592/2602 ms; segment note 2698/2551/2677 ms; agile.spi grew 0 bytes; notes_202610.spi 365 bytes, registered; show lists the notes in order; agile check ok (files=5). NO SPEEDUP YET, and that's the finding: writing 9 MB was never the cost. The note command still reads and scans the whole agile.spi (records_text, task_declared, note_schema_ensured, note_count over 8.85 MB), and the n16 size curve is that read and scan. So the speedup comes from MIGRATION, moving the 8,617 existing note rows into segments: agile.spi drops to about 1 MB, and a note measured 266-341 ms at 1.82 MB. Revised order: (1) reader train (show, list --json, and the other note readers), (2) migration with the writer on by default in the same train, guarded and with a backup, (3) a later optimisation: the note path reads only rows (task lookup) plus the item's segment notes, instead of the full text.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_23 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 23u32, "2026-10-31T20:53Z", "kit", "10-31 16:5x kit: MIGRATION MEASURED, and this is the payoff. Same node candidate BD0D8237, a copy of today's real backlog, all 8,702 note rows moved into state/notes_legacy.spi ('open agile' + rows, registered after agile): agile.spi 8.85 -> 0.87 MB; notes_legacy 7.98 MB. With KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1: agile note 306/287/287 ms (2519-2698 ms before, about 8.5x faster); agile set 505 ms (1276 before); agile show 848 ms, still listing old and new notes. Reading the segments to pick seq is cheap; the cost was normalising and scanning the 9 MB rows text. PLAN CONFIRMED: the reader train, then migration plus writer-default, gives about 0.3 s notes at today's size, and the cost no longer grows with note history (rows grow per item, not per note). Migration script for the real move: the probe's split (inl note_of_* lines -> notes_legacy.spi, manifest registration), run under the tree-write window with a backup, after every lane runs a reader-capable kit.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_24 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 24u32, "2026-10-31T21:34Z", "kit", "10-31 17:3x kit: GATE FIX VERIFIED. The first wiring made agile_task_notes 1047/1000 (node regen-sizes). Now agile_note_segments.note_persisted(state_dir, state, raw, schema_ready, id, row, stamp) makes the whole decision (segment append when KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1, else notes_appended plus state_written, as before), and task_notes keeps one call. Published sizes in the node mirror: task_notes.rs 865 (was 975: the whole-file write path left the crate), note_segments.rs 748. A first attempt failed to compile on a value restriction (convert.u64 (sm'.length raw) needs 'raw : i32'), which the node regen caught before any box work. Candidate BB3616FB (all 7 reader/writer crates regenerated) on the real-backlog copy: the whole-file note grows agile.spi 363 bytes, the segment note leaves it unchanged, notes_202610.spi is registered, show lists the notes in order, agile check ok. Readers switched so far: show, list --json, kit_resume (its raw-Rust state read replaced by state_text_with_notes; resume.rs 766), agile memory links (links.rs 731; host-macro count rebaselined for the crate call). Still to switch before the reader train: seal (rewrites rows, so it needs design) and the experiments packages. agile_task_listing is at 998/1000 after its switch, with no headroom.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_25 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 25u32, "2026-11-01T12:36Z", "kit", "11-01 08:3x kit QUALITY PASS. DoD: with TRAIN7 installed and KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1, 'kit agile note' on the live backlog takes < 500 ms p50 (measured 287-306 ms on a copy vs ~2,550 ms whole-file), notes land in state/notes_<yyyymm>.spi registered in package.spiproj, and every reader (show, list, next, links, resume) lists them in order (CLI tests exist: segments.spi, notes_cli 12/12); the one-time migration moves agile.spi's note rows into segments losslessly (row count and sha256 of the note set equal before/after). NEXT STEP: install TRAIN7, then flip KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 for the fleet after one day on kit alone; the migration as 'kit agile migrate-notes <root> --dry-run'. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: segment identity as a nominal month (yyyymm) not a string; note rows typed (Note record) on both sides of the move; migration typestate (unsegmented / segmented phantom) so readers cannot be pointed at a half-migrated root.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_26 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 26u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 43 h ago by kit, progress kept at 55%. Resume from that note: segments.spi, notes_cli 12/12); the one-time migration moves agile.spi's note rows into segments losslessly (row count and sha256 of the note set equal before/after). NEXT STEP: install TRAIN7, then flip KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 for the fl")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_27 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 27u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit: now the first step of KIT-AGILE-HUB (its only dep). Still off fleet-wide: tibia measured on 11-05 no notes_<yyyymm>.spi and 1.5-4.6 s per note at 13.7 MB, and the file is 14.99 MB tonight. Order unchanged: once TRAIN33 installs, KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 for kit alone for a day, then the fleet; the migration stays lossless (row count and sha256 of the note set equal).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_28 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 28u32, "2026-11-06T11:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_29 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 29u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries AGILE-2 (Mirrors and reports update atomically). Why: 10-24 note #2: every mirror/report rendered from typed state in one transaction")
inl note_of_kit_agile_event_log_30 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", 30u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. AGILE-2 · Mirrors and reports update atomically · P0 · ge2e, ssam, ota · Open / `agile set/record` updated `state/agile.spi` but not the root mirrors (agile, ratings, handoff, current tables) in more than a dozen rounds. Add `agile sync-mirrors` / `sync-report`, one transaction that renders every declared mirror and Markdown table from typed state and current receipts, promotes only if every hash and gate passes, is allowed while wrapping, and fails on contradictions.")
inl kit_consolidated_intake () = Task ("KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", "File the 135 eoie pendencies consolidated from ten consumer projects (Downloads/eoie-consolidated) into the backlog by a prewritten rule", Epic, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "FOSSIL-ASSIMILATION", "kit,consolidated,intake", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_consolidated_intake () = Planning ("KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl kit_consol_bundle_era () = Task ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", "Parked consolidated items from the bundle and container era: archives (ARCH-1..8), bundles (BNDL-1..6, 8..11), checkpoints (CKPT-1, 2, 5), wrap-safe closeout (LEASE-4), lease timing aids (AGILE-6), flat backlog import (AGILE-1, 3), toolchain installers (TOOL-3, 5..7), Cargo registry and stable dev root (CARGO-5, 7), fixtures (FIXT-1..3, 5, 6), merge adjudication (MERGE-2, 3), evidence custody (EVID-3..6), self-bootstrap (CLI-2), fs mkdir and chmod (FS-2, FS-5), run as another user (PROC-9), manifest edits (EDIT-9), plan-IR providers (EDIT-11), portability (CENSUS-3), TSV upsert (TABLE-1), durability (HEALTH-1)", Feature, P4, D3, 0u32, Paused, "KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE", "kit,consolidated,parked,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_consol_bundle_era () = Planning ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_1 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 1u32, "2026-10-24T02:23Z", "main", "Paused: no consumer on this machine uses eoie bundles, archives or container leases (work lives in git repos the user commits). Resume when one does; then split one item per family with the doc text as its spec.")
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_2 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 2u32, "2026-11-06T11:37Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_3 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 3u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Drop (bundle era, parked here), part 1 of 4. EDIT-9 · Cargo manifest and lockfile edits as one operation · P1 · ota, node, ge2e · Open / Add a workspace member or a `[workspace.dependencies]` entry, refresh `Cargo.lock` offline, and attest that only path packages changed (no registry dependency resolved), in one call. Model 'unlock once, update lock, relock, prove vendor' as one dependency-admission transaction with rollback. Also emit a derived lockfile for newly generated standalone crates. [drop reason: vendored-offline dependency admission was container-era; Cargo.toml edits are do edit + cargo (new-crate-external-deps-cargo-only)] || EDIT-11 · Patch providers degrade predictably · P1 · ssam, brz, scp, ge2e · Open / When a compiled patch provider is needed, `check`, `rehearse` and `apply` must accept the same compiler/closure arguments; failures must point to the source location instead of 'type error somewhere in its path'; preparing a provider must not consume the caller's whole time budget; a plan must be checkable on its own without compiling unrelated package modules. Offer a compilerless exact-replace fallback automatically (see EDIT-3). [drop reason: compiled plan-IR patch providers superseded by compilerless kit do programs] || FS-5 · Permissions and ownership · P1 · sqlt, brz, ge2e · Open / Separate execute-bit repair from write-permission repair; chmod by manifest policy (0644 sources, 0755 executables); `fs-chown` / prepare a runtime directory for a uid/gid; copy trees with normalized ownership; quarantine an unreadable partial tree instead of failing. [drop reason: chown/uid and run-as-user served root containers; Windows box, proxy fs-chmod covers exec bits] || PROC-9 · Run as another user · P2 · erp, ge2e · Open / A uid/gid option in the runner and the job supervisor, for tools that refuse to run as root (local Postgres), without changing the controller's identity. [drop reason: chown/uid and run-as-user served root containers; Windows box, proxy fs-chmod covers exec bits] || ARCH-1 · Caller-set extraction budget · P0 · erp, ge2e, sbin, brz, ota · Open / The fixed 256 MiB projected-output ceiling (confirmed in source) rejected valid trusted inputs (269 MB to 497 MB) and forced Python/unzip fallbacks every round. Add `--max-bytes` (and member/count limits), streaming or staged extraction committed in batches that each fit the call budget, and a durable completion receipt. Keep the safe default. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-2 · Selective member access · P0 · erp, ota, ge2e · Open / List members from the central directory, read one member (including JSONL or TOML inside a ZIP), extract by `--member/--pattern/--prefix/--manifest` with a per-member receipt and resume, restore one member atomically to a target path, and search inside archives without full extraction. When extracting selected project roots, also pull in the transitive project references they need. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-3 · Accept normal directory entries · P2 · ota, ge2e · Open / Rehydrate rejects ZIP directory entries ending in `/` ('bundle entry is not normalized'; the check is still in `bundle_manifest.spi`), even in archives that EOIE created and verified. Accept or normalize them while keeping traversal and symlink checks. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-4 · Actionable rejections · P0 · ge2e · Open / Valid strict-USTAR overlays and `tar.gz` files were rejected at preflight with no rule named. Every rejection must name the member and the invariant. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-6 · Multi-archive batch extraction · P2 · ota, ge2e · Open / Extract many archives into bounded destinations in one call, one receipt per archive, into a grep-ready tree, merging into an existing tree only for declared paths. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-7 · Archive forensics · P2 · ge2e, ota · Open / Detect format by content rather than extension; report truncated-member offsets (expected vs available bytes) and salvage complete members; attest that an upload is complete (central directory present) before extracting; take an explicit filename-encoding option (cp850 vs cp437) and record it; restore executable bits. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines] || ARCH-8 · Legacy codecs · P2 · erp · Open / Read BZIP2-compressed ZIPs produced by older releases. *Now:* ZIP extraction accepts only stored and deflated members; `.tar.bz2` is recognized as a TAR format. [drop reason: archive/upload extraction served the browser-sandbox bundle workflow; work lives in git repos, no archive consumer on these machines]")
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_4 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 4u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Drop (bundle era, parked here), part 2 of 4. BNDL-1 · Profiles for application bundles · P0 · sqlt, ota, erp, node, scp, ge2e · Partial / Package any app root under a declared, versionless include/exclude manifest: allowed root files and crate folders, the required binary name, forbidden paths (`target/`, `.cargo`, credentials, stray `.exe`, `bin/obj`; temporary credential inputs get a declared lifetime and a cleanup receipt), a size ceiling, executable modes and the binary hash, with no EOIE layout assumptions. Keep 'self-release' (EOIE's own bundle) and 'application-release' as distinct, clearly named profiles. *Now:* `generic` / `eoie` / `auto` profiles and `create-flat <max-bytes>` exist. `bundle create <root> <output> [profile]` takes no allow/deny list or binary assertion: the generic profile packs every regular file under the root (refusing symlinks) up to a fixed 30,000,000-byte ceiling, and only the `eoie` profile prunes sidecars and transients first. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-2 · Vendored source is immutable · P0 · ota, erp · Partial / Older bundlers and `copy-tree` dropped every path segment named `build`, `builder` or `target`, and nested `.zip` fixtures, inside vendored crates (losing `vendor/cc/src/target/*.rs`), which broke checksums. Exclude only the workspace-root `target/` and declared caches, keep a negative fixture that proves nested `src/target/` survives, and run a post-pack closure gate that checks every path declared in `.cargo-checksum.json`. When a crate is lost, restore it from a local donor bundle only after its checksum manifest matches byte for byte. *Now:* a release-hygiene contract keeps a nested `src/target/` fixture during pruning, and generic `bundle create` keeps nested `src/target/` because it excludes nothing (so it also packs the root `target/` unless the caller removes it first). The checksum-closure gate does not exist. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-3 · Create, verify and rehydrate agree · P0 · ge2e, ota · Open / An archive accepted by `create-flat` and `verify-flat` was rejected by explicit `rehydrate ... generic` while `auto` accepted it. Share one preflight across these commands, report which predicate failed, and have `auto` explain its choice. A bundle that verifies must also be operational: one passed `verify-flat` with an empty `state/` and then `agile begin` failed. Normalize predecessor bundles accepted by older versions with a format-migration receipt; rehydrate historical or consumer bundles without requiring current development authority; report 'nothing to rehydrate' as success (`rehydration-status` exited 5). [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-4 · Hygiene classifies debris · P1 · ge2e, erp, node, ota · Open / Detect hidden root dotfiles, PID/session files, version-numbered root state, lease debris, unreferenced scratch, orphan package families and per-profile transient directories (`__pycache__`) before packing, and offer a safe prune. Count vendored C/C++ as vendor, not as product legacy code. Keep the minimal canonical receipts that retained commands reference. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-5 · Size, compression and forecasts · P1 · ota, scp, ge2e · Partial / Report exact archive bytes against the ceiling, with raw tree bytes as a separate metric (`bundle-size-regauge` compared the raw tree with the ZIP limit). Name the compression method in the receipt, offer store/fast/default/max levels, forecast compressed size, list top contributors and the largest regenerable artifacts, and prune transient binaries and raw queues while keeping source and receipts. Write the packing command's own output outside the tree being packed. *Now:* `create-flat` enforces a byte ceiling and deflate exists. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-6 · Closeout binds the tree · P2 · ssam, scp · Open / Bind a tree fingerprint at closeout and refuse to bundle a tree that changed afterwards without a new closeout. Fail closeout when a gating input points into a prunable cache. Make closeout resumable phase by phase (manifest refreshed, archive created, archive verified, size gate, export), resuming from the first missing receipt. Fail packaging when shipped binaries reference runtime files that cleanup removed, and keep dev-only configuration (absolute target dirs, private registries in `.cargo/config.toml`) out of the portable payload. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-8 · Release-mirror sync · P2 · ge2e · Open / Copy only changed declared paths from a dev tree into a rehydrated release mirror. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync] || BNDL-9 · Payload classes · P2 · sbin, ge2e · Partial / Declarative classes for compatibility runtimes (path, hash provenance, excluded from authority), ephemeral outputs that never enter a carrier, and external payloads restored on hydrate. *Now:* `proxy external-payload register|hydrate|dehydrate` exists; make it automatic for checksum-declared vendor fixtures. [drop reason: application bundles, hygiene, closeout and release mirrors were the bundle-carrier workflow; repos now travel by git and kit remote sync]")
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_5 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 5u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Drop (bundle era, parked here), part 3 of 4. CKPT-1 · Cheap, frequent checkpoints · P0 · ge2e, node, ssam, ota, erp · Open / The external session can end long before the lease does, so checkpoints must be cheap and early. Add a source-and-state-only checkpoint mode (about 2 seconds for a mid-size workspace, store/fast compression, incremental hashing); content-addressed delta checkpoints; promotion of a verified checkpoint to the release without recompression; a lease-bound scheduler that snapshots on a cadence and after each green gate, rotates N verified copies, measures cost, pauses during a deliberate source mutation, keeps append-only scheduler receipts and survives restarts; and a reentrant `checkpoint now` when the heartbeat is lost. The checkpoint alarm must fire independently of the foreground command (intervals stretched to 13–15 minutes while a long gate ran), and destinations must be absolute and outside the tree being packed. [drop reason: container session checkpoints; durability is git plus the node profile mirror and KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR] || CKPT-2 · Durable publication states · P0 · node, ge2e, ota · Open / Distinguish local, verified, published and independently restored checkpoints; publish to a configurable location outside the replaceable workspace and record a receipt. A local verified ZIP is not a backup. [drop reason: container session checkpoints; durability is git plus the node profile mirror and KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR] || CKPT-5 · Sharded checkpoints with atomic promotion · P2 · ota · Open / Commit computation in shards and promote once, with a configurable codec and an I/O budget separate from CPU. [drop reason: container session checkpoints; durability is git plus the node profile mirror and KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR] || AGILE-1 · Flat backlog import and sync · P0 · ota, erp, sbin, node, scp · Open / `agile set/list/record` could not see tasks kept in an existing flat TSV/Markdown backlog (`AGILE_CURRENT.tsv`, `AGILE_PRIORITY.tsv`). Import or sync a flat ledger into typed state (and back), or accept it as a backend, so non-EOIE projects don't keep two authorities. [drop reason: one kit backlog for every project; agile roots and TODO markers (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS) replace flat-ledger sync] || EVID-4 · Historical evidence namespace · P1 · ssam, brz, erp, ota · Open / A retention class for audit evidence that `status` does not flag as unexpected. Separate preserved evidence from current authority (nothing is promoted by a `_current` filename, and partial outputs never become current), keep candidate snapshots out of source counts, and declare derived-receipt directories excluded from manifests. [drop reason: receipt namespaces, authority chains and receipt compaction served bundle custody; kit receipts are one-line summaries with logs in job folders] || EVID-5 · Authority chains · P2 · ge2e, erp · Open / Typed source → build → deployment chains with cross-bound hashes before promotion; multi-file external authority packages validated as one unit; witness binding of subject + artifact + epoch; a gate that requires every ledger row to bind to a known identity or a terminal disposition (zero unbound); evidence replay through a declared producer contract, never heuristics. [drop reason: receipt namespaces, authority chains and receipt compaction served bundle custody; kit receipts are one-line summaries with logs in job folders] || EVID-6 · Compact receipts · P2 · ota, node · Open / Counts plus a bounded sample (limits on item count and item bytes, with explicit truncation fields) instead of exhaustive rows (one receipt shrank from 78.8 MB to 1.7 MB), and receipt compaction into provenance shards that never drop the only failure evidence. [drop reason: receipt namespaces, authority chains and receipt compaction served bundle custody; kit receipts are one-line summaries with logs in job folders] || TOOL-3 · Truthful installers · P1 · ota, ge2e, node · Open / An installer is successful only when the executable is discovered and `--version` runs. Preflight archive integrity (truncated xz) before spawning workers, distinguish pending from terminally failed workers, never replace an existing `.cargo/config.toml` wholesale (installers repeatedly swapped a vendored, fail-closed config and shared target for a registry default; merge or preserve the vendor authority), and optionally require a current toolchain proof before the first write in a governed workspace. [drop reason: container installers, SDK batch install, payload hydrate and workspace recipes; toolchains are pinned per machine (env check, ci parity), proxy external-payload exists] || TOOL-5 · Resumable local SDK install · P2 · ssam, sbin · Open / Install a local SDK archive in bounded, restartable batches with durable receipts and a stable cache identity. [drop reason: container installers, SDK batch install, payload hydrate and workspace recipes; toolchains are pinned per machine (env check, ci parity), proxy external-payload exists] || TOOL-6 · Content-addressed external payloads · P2 · brz · Partial / Resumable acquisition, verification before adoption, never vendored into the product. *Now:* `external-payload` covers register/hydrate. [drop reason: container installers, SDK batch install, payload hydrate and workspace recipes; toolchains are pinned per machine (env check, ci parity), proxy external-payload exists] || TOOL-7 · Workspace hydrate recipe · P1 · ge2e, ota, scp · Open / Symlink, workspace, toolchains and external payloads disappeared independently after resets. One content-addressed recipe with an idempotent `ensure` for each (toolchains, payloads, worktree from the latest verified bundle, entrypoint), returning a single health receipt and listing which surviving scratch is safe to merge. [drop reason: container installers, SDK batch install, payload hydrate and workspace recipes; toolchains are pinned per machine (env check, ci parity), proxy external-payload exists]")
inl note_of_kit_consol_bundle_era_6 () = Note ("KIT-CONSOL-BUNDLE-ERA", 6u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Drop (bundle era, parked here), part 4 of 4. CARGO-7 · Registry and offline fallbacks · P2 · sqlt, ota · Open / Prefer the vendored config automatically and emit a blocker receipt instead of attempting the network; audit offline closure without downloading, telling absent vendor sources, a stale Cargo home and a lost index cache apart; classify registry outages (HTTP 502/503) and a stale `Cargo.lock` separately from source failures (a stale lockfile was first blamed on the registry), and retry transient fetch failures with a bound; write source-replacement config with credentials redacted from receipts; probe whether an index lacks a package or is down (`external_blocked`); fall back to a standalone `rustc` test harness for dependency-free modules when the registry is unavailable; prove no credentials entered artifacts. Separately, a metadata-aware vendor slice. [drop reason: offline registry fallbacks were for a network-less container] || TABLE-1 · TSV upsert, append and dedupe · P1 · ota, ge2e, scp · Partial / Keyed atomic upsert/append with header validation, duplicate-key guard and dedupe, so ledgers are not rewritten whole and tab delimiters are never mangled by shell quoting. *Now:* `eoie table query/diff` and `do table ... count by` read; nothing writes. [drop reason: ledgers are typed .spi state, TSV ledgers are not kept in repos (derive-baselines-not-stored)] || FIXT-1 · Declarative process choreography · P2 · node, ge2e · Open / Process graphs with readiness extraction, dynamic port/endpoint binding, sensitive env references with redaction, expected exits, per-step and whole-suite deadlines, kill/reap of owned process groups, no orphans, progress receipts, a fixture-dependency manifest that reports missing prerequisites before execution, and batch probes that share one fixture startup while keeping their witnesses independent. [drop reason: product fixtures (process graphs, disposable Postgres, PTY, DB snapshot) with no consumer; do run background/until covers simple readiness] || FIXT-2 · Disposable database runtime · P2 · erp, ge2e · Open / Init/start/stop/createdb with an ephemeral port, a short socket directory (Unix socket paths cap at 107 bytes), stale PID ownership checks, a writable log location, validated `share` closure and a startup smoke before declaring ready, library paths passed only to the subprocess, and a receipt with the actual runtime, port and log. [drop reason: product fixtures (process graphs, disposable Postgres, PTY, DB snapshot) with no consumer; do run background/until covers simple readiness] || FIXT-5 · PTY smoke · P2 · sqlt · Open / Run a binary under a pseudo-terminal, send Ctrl+C, assert the exit code and terminal cleanup. [drop reason: product fixtures (process graphs, disposable Postgres, PTY, DB snapshot) with no consumer; do run background/until covers simple readiness] || FIXT-6 · Consistent database snapshot export · P2 · ge2e · Open / Same-transaction snapshot export with sharding and a count manifest, and the snapshot identity propagated through derived receipts. [drop reason: product fixtures (process graphs, disposable Postgres, PTY, DB snapshot) with no consumer; do run background/until covers simple readiness] || MERGE-2 · Owner-aware patch routing · P2 · ota · Open / Resolve the compiled owner through reexport/facade chains before patching; reject writes to sources unreachable from the module graph; emit one-to-many relocation receipts. [drop reason: lane-consolidation adjudication specific to one product's lane bundles] || MERGE-3 · Fingerprint-bound adjudication · P2 · ota · Open / Keep-current / port / relocate decisions bound to path + candidate fingerprint + current fingerprint, reopened when either side drifts; rank by symbol, call-site and API wiring, not file size; never count generated scaffolding or recovery trees as evidence. [drop reason: lane-consolidation adjudication specific to one product's lane bundles] || CLI-2 · Self-bootstrap and self-health · P0 · erp, ssam, brz, ge2e, ota, scp · Partial / Extract and install EOIE from its own bundle when no EOIE binary is installed yet, keeping the executable bit and recording a pointer next to checkpoints; never place the canonical executable inside a workspace it manages (removing an old dev tree once deleted the binary behind the entrypoint); time out a hung `status` and say whether the transport or EOIE failed. *Now:* `proxy install-self` and `self-upgrade-check` need an installed binary to run (`install-self` also returned `Not a directory` once). [drop reason: self-bootstrap from its own bundle was container-era; kit installs per machine through the release train] || HEALTH-1 · Filesystem durability checks · P2 · ge2e, ota · Open / Detect fsync EIO and zero-filled reads; choose a healthy filesystem for state, databases and build targets; verify promoted files against archive members after writing; audit installed runtimes byte-for-byte before blaming the product; keep a disk-audit/prune path that works when the volume is full. [drop reason: fsync EIO and zero-filled reads were container storage faults; machine watch is the health sweep, disk pressure is KIT-CACHE-INVENTORY]")
