inl note_of_granular_heal_98 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 98u32, "2026-11-01T14:46Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit: mechanical periodic healing of duplicated logic now has its own feeder, KIT-REUSE-METRICS (top Exact/Renamed duplicate classes per round become LIB-candidate rows).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_99 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 99u32, "2026-11-01T23:30Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 19:3x tibia share (main's 19:1x drift check): tibia src/apps/results hold 0 '!!!!Export' and 0 multi-line \\$'...' macros. They held 10 multi-line 'global \\\"\\\"\\\"' Rust blocks (about 35 lines of Win32 FFI) in apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi. All 10 are now one-line 'global' bindings, one Rust item each (type aliases, consts, one extern fn per block, the two repr(C) structs); items over 120 chars use '^^' literal continuation, so each stays one binding. 0 triple-quote blocks left; format clean; budget 0. Unstaged; proof = offscreen's Cells tests on a scoped node run (queued), then the user's next CI push.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_100 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 100u32, "2026-11-02T00:58Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:0x tibia, CORRECTION to n99: the offscreen heal as committed by the user in 1f40e7d is BROKEN. A node run of offscreen (job-1793580270446) failed 7/7 with a unification failure at offscreen.spi:130, because I wrote over-long items as 'global \\\"a\\\"' then a new line '^^ \\\"b\\\"', which parses as (global \\\"a\\\") ^^ \\\"b\\\". Fixed unstaged: each item is one literal again, and the formatter rewrote the five long ones in its canonical 'global (' / '\\\"a\\\"' / '^^ \\\"b\\\"' / ')' form. Format clean; re-test on the node queued (offscreen plus the results package). tibia CI run 41366570407 on 1f40e7d is expected RED on offscreen and on results/arena_rows (a combinator named 'then', a keyword, also fixed unstaged); the next push carries both fixes.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_101 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 101u32, "2026-11-02T01:15Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 21:1x tibia: offscreen heal PROVEN after the fix: node job-1793581185638 ran apps/offscreen/offscreen.spi 7/7 passed with the formatter's 'global ( \\\"a\\\" ^^ \\\"b\\\" )' form for the five long FFI items and one-line globals for the rest; 0 triple-quote blocks remain in tibia. Unstaged; the user's next push carries it (CI on 1f40e7d is expected red on the pre-fix form).")
inl note_of_granular_heal_102 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 102u32, "2026-11-03T08:20Z", "kit", "11-03 04:1x kit groom (user: kit keeps drifting; groom so it catches up). kit's slice is now the epic KIT-CATCH-UP: KIT-BASELINE-ONLY-SHRINKS first (kit had baselined its own new drift), then KIT-OS-SEARCH-GRANULAR, KIT-ENTRY-X-S1..S7 and the ranked EOIE-GRANULAR-MIGRATION slices. kit 11-03: 887 lint rows, 643 !!!!Export, 126 string-dispatch sites.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_103 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 103u32, "2026-11-05T12:58Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 09:1x kit: three job-family packages healed to HOST-FREE ticket/listing code (unstaged, box notebooks green, baselines merged down so they cannot regrow): kit_job_slot host-block 7 -> 1 (only the Windows heavy-mutex probe remains; a global would grow rust_global): heavy_queue via env.get_environment_variable, ticket names parsed in Spiral, tickets_sorted a list string via kit_os_files.entry_names + am'.sort instead of a newline-joined string, writes via kit_jobs helpers and lib file_system.rename, the 100 ns ticket clock as workspace_primitives.clock.dotnet_ticks (since_epoch nominal + two one-expression accessors, arithmetic in Spiral); slot.spi 4/4. kit_job_host 1 -> 0: next_request (min .start name) via entry_names + sort, own_pid via kit_os_process, held_released via host_paths.request_removed; new test the_next_request_is_the_earliest_start_file; host_run 1/1 + queue 4/4. kit_job_control 1 -> 0: newest_labeled via entry_names + label files + a typed stamp max (empty label guarded); new test; control 1/1. PATTERN (the same 3 times): host 'read_dir + filter + sort/min/max' blocks become kit_os_files.entry_names |> listm'.filter |> am'.sort. Also: two of these files lacked '///- --package .', so their tests could never run in a notebook (host_run went RED on unbound kit_jobs until the header was added). Sweep: kit_job_query/query.spi is the one remaining test file without the header.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_104 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 104u32, "2026-11-05T14:54Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 10:5x kit: two more host-string crate calls turned into TYPED LINKED calls (unstaged; box --check ok; baselines merged down, host-block 1 -> 0 each): (a) kit_job_launch -> kit_job_create: job_created now takes the 15-field tuple and is exported itself (it was a lambda wrapper); kit_job_launch lists kit_job_create under packages+link and calls kit_job_create.create.job_created (...). Box sizes: launch 710 (HEAD 715), create 379; 2 linked calls in launch.rs. (b) kit_memory -> kit_memory_query/import/save: 7 host strings became typed calls. The producers export tuple functions directly (memory_check, memory_list_run ((data, kind)), memory_recall ((data, query, limit)) take tuples themselves; import/save gained named tuple runs). Box sizes: kit_memory 761 (HEAD 758, 8 linked calls), import 773, save 943, query 999 (HEAD 995). A first cut with three extra wrapper functions put query at 1008, so the functions themselves now take the tuples. query has 1 line of headroom: the next change there must shrink it. Behavior is unchanged (the same functions, called through the link instead of a host string); the memory and job CLI checks ride TRAIN31.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_105 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 105u32, "2026-11-05T15:22Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 11:2x kit: experiment/table host-string calls healed partway (unstaged, box --check ok for all touched + kit_cli): kit_experiment_stats exports median_terms_of / experiment_terms (tuple functions, no lambda); kit_table_plain/filter/measures/diff/append export their entry functions directly with tuple parameters (measured_run wraps the one internally-called entry). kit_experiment_analysis and kit_table_test now call kit_experiment_stats.terms.* and kit_table_filter.filter.rows_kept as typed linked calls (linking kit_experiment_stats and kit_table_filter; Cargo dep and lock entries for kit-table-filter added). Box sizes: stats 782 (HEAD 795), append 946 (HEAD 959); the node probe also had analysis 932 and table_test 710. FINDING, not fixed: kit_table_query cannot link its five producers. plain/filter/measures/diff list kit_table_query as a package (they use its query/stats/cells/lines/wheres modules), so linking back is circular ('Package is circular'), and the host-string calls are what broke that cycle. The fix is moving those five modules (541 lines) into a new source package, but they hold the table host primitives (cells.spi 12 macros, query 2, stats 2), and the ratchet refuses a new package carrying host code. So it is: heal cells/query/stats first (one-expression primitives into an OS boundary module), then split. kit_table_query stays at HEAD (its host calls still resolve: tuple exports flatten to the same Rust signatures as the old lambdas). Node probe of all 17 touched crates: job-1793892017381 running.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_106 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 106u32, "2026-11-05T15:39Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 11:4x kit: job-family host-string crate calls -> typed linked calls (unstaged, box --check ok for every touched crate + kit_cli): kit_job_queue -> kit_job_slot (slot_held_step, slot_ticket_dropped) + kit_job_gate (held_window_step, held_window_dropped); kit_job_gate -> kit_job_slot (slot_gate); kit_job_supervise -> kit_machine_windows (job_windows_dropped x2), kit_job_gate (job_window_gate, job_compile_gate), kit_job_env (new named environment_refusal = snapshot_refusal o job_environment_adopted), kit_job_after (after_wait). Every producer now exports the function itself, not a lambda; no cycles (checked). Note: these one-line host-string calls were not counted as host blocks by the census, so the census does not move; what changes is that the calls are typed and checked across crates. NODE PROBES: (1) job-1793892017381 sized all 17 earlier-touched crates under 1000 (table_query 924, memory_query 999, analysis 932, ...), but its cargo half was VOID: 3 dependencies without committed generated .rs (kit_remote_activity, kit_flag_words, kit_job_orphans) are 'file not found'. (2) job-1793893123379 reruns with those 3 included plus the supervise chain; (3) job-1793892733688 probes slot/gate/queue on mirror size13.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_107 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 107u32, "2026-11-06T12:30Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: host-text drift (raw $ macros where a typed lib function exists) is 0 per the granular census in every project, or each remaining site is recorded as a real host boundary. Next step: rerun the granular census per repo and list the top 3 files.")
inl note_of_granular_heal_108 () = Note ("GRANULAR-HEAL", 108u32, "2026-11-07T15:48Z", "main", "TypeFeatures: host text is replaced by lib functions with typed signatures and backend_switch arms; drift classes are a closed union (host macro, host block, intrinsic) as in the census probes.")
inl granular_lint () = Task ("GRANULAR-LINT", "eoie lint macros <paths>: classify every host macro by GRANULAR-HEAL rule (1)-(3) (long, flow, cast, arith, block), per-repo baseline file so only NEW violations fail, JSON/TSV out; wired into the PostToolUse style check and the lib gate kit acceptance, so drift cannot re-enter", Feature, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "KIT-LINT-DOLLAR-LITERAL", "granular,macros,kit,lint,hooks,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_granular_lint () = Planning ("GRANULAR-LINT", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl dice_granular_heal () = Task ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal dice host macros to granular Spiral per GRANULAR-HEAL (79 sites, 7,187 chars; 53 arith: transaction.spi 30/2,315, dice_contract.spi 5/1,868, dice_view.spi 13/1,020, rpc.spi, nearblocks.spi); every change mirrored in alphabet", Story, P1, D4, 20u32, Planned, "", "granular,macros,dice,alphabet,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dice_granular_heal () = Planning ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T11:24Z", "main", "10-25 07:24 main: classified. The title's '53 arith' is stale. The bulk is 55 Leptos attribute props in ui/src/components (transaction 30, menu_tabs 8, dice_view 5, settings_view 5, content 4, transactions_view 2, home 1), all of the shape $'\"class=\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"\"'. The compiler already folds them into literals (main.rs: Rc::<str>::from(\"<div  class=\\\"flex flex-1 flex-col\\\">{ v54 }</div>\")), so a plain Spiral string \"class=\\\"...\\\"\" is the same input to the fold. Proof job: job-1792927268654 (heal-attrs.ps1) compiles dice_ui before and after one exact-pattern rewrite and requires a byte-identical main.rs; on any difference it restores every ui/src source. The tracked main.rs is restored either way. ui/src/model/near/rpc.spi (82 macro lines) and nearblocks.spi (33) are Rust type names (:! x' = $'crate::model::near::rpc::...') and single field reads (!\\\\(x, \"$0.field.clone()\")): one host call each, which granular style keeps. dist/ui is a published copy and out of scope. alphabet has no Leptos code, so nothing to mirror.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T15:35Z", "main", "10-25 11:35 main: pass 1 v2 RED (job-1792931138011), sources restored. With the 55 Leptos attribute macros as plain Spiral strings, the folded string literals of main.rs are NOT the same multiset, and cargo check (wasm32) fails with 293 errors. So my premise ('a plain string is the same input to the fold') is wrong for the leptos view path: the attribute text reaching view! as a host macro is not equivalent to a Spiral string literal there. Next: read the first cargo errors and the literal diff (snapshot folder %TEMP%\\dice-heal\\snapshot-*) to see what a plain string becomes, e.g. an Rc<str> variable spliced where view! needs a token. Until then the attribute sites count as required host text. The likely right shape is a lib helper (leptos.attr name value) that keeps the one macro inside lib, so call sites stop carrying escaped host text.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T15:42Z", "main", "10-25 11:42 main: v2 RED re-read, and the RED is not proven to be the heal. (1) With vN numbering normalized, all 376 view! bodies of main.rs are identical before and after; the only output change is 24 fewer Rc<str> literal lets (duplicate attribute texts: 19->7, 18->7, 2->1), so the sorted-multiset rule was too strict. (2) v2 never cargo-checked the before build, and the current compiler's before main.rs (1,690,148 B) differs from the tracked one (1,733,999 B), so the 293 errors may be the compiler's. (3) The error filter missed every line (--message-format short prefixes the path). Rule for v3, written before the run: GREEN iff the view! bodies are identical as a vN-normalized multiset AND the distinct normalized Rc literal set is equal AND the after build's cargo error set (code + message, vN-normalized, positions stripped) is a subset of the before build's set. If before itself fails cargo check, record it on a compiler item, as a dice build regression on the current compiler.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:44Z", "main", "10-25 14:44 main: v3 GREEN (job-1792943110694, by n3's pre-registered rule): all 55 Leptos attribute macros as plain Spiral strings give identical vN-normalized view! bodies, an identical distinct Rc literal set, and 0 new cargo errors. CORRECTION to v3's own printed NOTE ('the unhealed build already fails cargo check: a compiler-side dice regression'): FALSE. The 9 errors (Heap2/Heap3 lacking serde/Borsh traits, a Debug bound) come from my script, not the compiler. dice/ui/build.ps1 runs Add-RustDerives (rust_derive.ps1) on main.rs after BuildSpiral and appends 'pub mod model;', and v3 called only BuildSpiral, so both builds lacked the derives (tracked main.rs: 66 serde derives; v3's: 0). The compiler emits only #[derive(Clone)] on unions. So GREEN compares like with like and stands; there's no dice regression to report. Landing rule: apply the heal, then run dice/ui/build.ps1 itself (BuildSpiral + Add-RustDerives + the wasm build), which must exit 0; else restore. Separate drift noted: Add-RustDerives is a regex post-pass over generated Rust, where derives should be declared in Spiral (GRANULAR-HEAL scope, the same class as x's retired rust-modules.ps1).")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-25T19:16Z", "main", "10-25 15:16 main: alphabet-mirror check (memory alphabet-mirrors-dice): alphabet's tracked Spiral (git ls-files: 2 .spi files) has 0 sites of the Leptos attribute-macro pattern this heal rewrites, so the dice attribute heal has no alphabet counterpart. alphabet's own heals continue under ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL (heal3 waits on LIB-NEW-VEC-RUNTIME). The dice landing (job-1792953997664) is queued in the heavy queue.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-25T22:40Z", "main", "10-25 18:40 main: the land (job-1792953997664) is RED and restored (15 files, all equal to their pre-land hashes): with the 55 heals, BuildSpiral passed, Add-RustDerives and cargo check for wasm32 passed, then dice/ui/build.ps1 exited 2 in a later step (bun build-css or the trunk bundle). The land script's output filter dropped that step's message. Like v2, a RED without a baseline proves nothing about the heal. Rule, written before the run: an unfiltered baseline build of the UNHEALED sources is queued (main-dice-baseline-build). If it also exits 2 at the same step, the fault is dice's build or toolchain (fixed first, separately), and the land rule becomes 'the healed build's exit and step equal the baseline's, and cargo check passes'. If the baseline exits 0, the heal breaks a post-cargo step, and the full log names it.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-26T01:36Z", "main", "10-25 21:36 main: ROOT CAUSE of the heal-attrs-land RED found, and it is not the heal. The baseline build of the UNHEALED sources (main-dice-baseline-build, %TEMP%\\dice-heal\\baseline-210431.log) exits 2 at the same late step: trunk build fails with \"error: invalid value '1' for '--no-color' [possible values: true, false]\". trunk 0.21.14 binds --no-color to the NO_COLOR variable and accepts only true/false, while the agent shell exports the common NO_COLOR=1 (process env only; not user, machine or settings.json), and every job started from it inherits it. CI doesn't set it, which is why dice CI is green. Positive control in an empty folder: NO_COLOR=1 makes trunk exit 2 with that exact error; NO_COLOR=true gets past argument parsing and fails only on the missing Trunk config. Fixed in dice/ui/build.ps1:44 (unstaged): the trunk block's Invoke-Block -EnvironmentVariables now also passes NO_COLOR = 'true' when NO_COLOR is set at all, else 'false'; Invoke-Block scopes and restores it. alphabet has no trunk step, so nothing to mirror. main.rs was restored to its pre-land snapshot by the baseline script (hash True). Requeued as one serial job (main-serial-dice-heal, job-1792978550250): baseline build again, then heal-attrs-land. By the pre-registered rule the heal lands only if the healed build matches the baseline's exit and step.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-26T05:06Z", "main", "10-26 01:06 main: GREEN and LANDED (main-serial-dice-heal: baseline build 2 then heal-attrs-land 2, 00:59). With dice/ui/build.ps1's NO_COLOR normalization, the baseline build passes, and the heal turns 55 Leptos attribute macros into plain Spiral strings; dice/ui/build.ps1 (BuildSpiral, Add-RustDerives, cargo check for wasm32, trunk bundle) passes on the healed tree. The healed .spi files and the rebuilt main.rs are unstaged for the user. Mirror rule (alphabet mirrors dice): alphabet has no Leptos code (0 'leptos' in its .spi; positive control: 511 in 11 dice files), so nothing to mirror. Remaining dice drift: Add-RustDerives itself (RUST-DERIVE-INFERENCE, compiler lane, after the repro fix).")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-26T17:55Z", "main", "10-26 13:55 main: 10-26 13:5x main: one dice site the attribute heal didn't cover, found by a GRANULAR-HEAL census of main's repos (dice, alphabet, polyglot, blog, dir-tree-html; 0 host-text casts; flow/arith sites all claimed except this one). ui/src/components/menu_tabs.spi:85, the tab href: $'!url + \"\\#/\" + !id + \"\"' becomes url ++# \"#/\" ++# id. url is a Spiral string (leptos.use_url is a ++# memo), menu_tab_id returns string literals, and tab_link.render takes href : () -> string (then sm'.to_std_string). Reachable: content.spi:121 calls menu_tabs.render. Today's main.rs (00:51, staged by the user) already shows Rc::<str>::from(format!(\"{}#/{}\", v1, v2)) at 3 closures, so the compiler folds the host + chain into format!. The heal should therefore not change the output.\nDraft: TEMP dice-menu-href/apply.ps1 (one anchored edit). Proof: TEMP dice-menu-href/proof.ps1, reference and healed scratch copies running the consumer's steps (BuildSpiral, Add-RustDerives, cargo check wasm32). Rules, written before the run:\n- GREEN: both builds exit 0, the reference carries the host concat (1 site in source, at least 1 folded href in main.rs), the healed source has 0 host concat, and main.rs is byte-identical.\n- GREEN-NORMALIZED: the same, but identical only after vN normalization (n3's precedent).\n- VOID: the reference build fails, or the reference doesn't carry the site.\n- RED: anything else.\nLand: on GREEN, apply to the live dice/ui source only, since main.rs is unchanged. On GREEN-NORMALIZED, apply and run dice/ui/build.ps1 (the 01:06 landing rule). Alphabet mirror: alphabet has no menu_tabs (and no Leptos), so nothing to mirror. Queued after kit's rename finalize (the pause would stop it now).")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 20%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-11-06T21:59Z", "main", "main 11-06 18:0x SPLIT (rule: >240 min). Census of host macros ($' / $\" / !!!!, comments excluded) over dice's tracked .spi: 148 sites, the item said 79: rpc.spi 82, nearblocks.spi 33, dice_view 12, contract 7, lib 5, dice_ui 4, content 2, transactions_view/settings_view/menu_tabs 1 each. Children: DICE-GRANULAR-1-RPC (240), -2-NEARBLOCKS (120), -3-UI (120), -4-CORE (90). This parent closes when all four do.")
inl note_of_dice_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("DICE-GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-11-07T22:08Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: all four children Done (DICE-GRANULAR-1-RPC, -2-NEARBLOCKS, -3-UI, -4-CORE); the dice census of host macros over tracked .spi drops from 148 to the residual external-API set, each residual named; the dice notebooks green on the node (lib 24/24 and contract 13/13 today on pack61b). This parent only rolls up.")
inl alphabet_granular_heal () = Task ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal alphabet host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (34 sites, 1,340 chars, all in apps/documents/documents.spi), together with the dice mirror", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "granular,macros,alphabet,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_alphabet_granular_heal () = Planning ("ALPHABET-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl apps_granular_heal () = Task ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", "Heal spiral/apps host macros per GRANULAR-HEAL (63 sites: apps/spiral/spiral.spi 54/1,776, cells/spi/text.spi 5/911, cells console, wasm)", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "", "granular,macros,apps,cells,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_apps_granular_heal () = Planning ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_1 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 1u32, "2026-10-25T11:34Z", "main", "10-25 07:34 main: pass 1 queued (job-1792928007912) with a generic tool, %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\plain-macros.ps1 -Spi -Output -Name [-Livemd]. It rewrites plain macro strings ($'$\"text\"', no braces, escapes, splices or quotes) into Spiral strings, and passes only if (a) with -Livemd, the livemd exported to the spi before healing AND the healed livemd exports to the healed spi byte for byte, and (b) the compiled output is byte-identical before and after. Otherwise it restores. apps/spiral: spiral.livemd is the source of truth (build.ps1 exports it first), and both files have 86 such sites, most of them the generated .fsproj template. Its 216 macro lines also hold splice and escape sites for later passes. The tool is a candidate for an eoie verb next to GRANULAR-LINT's 'eoie lint macros' (rule: generic tools, not per-experiment scripts).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_2 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 2u32, "2026-10-25T11:46Z", "main", "10-25 07:46 main: pass 1 re-planned; the first job (job-1792928007912) was stopped while still queued, with spiral.spi untouched (216 macro lines). Finding: the .fsproj template is dead code. persist_code_project is called only by publish_code (only by publish_file, 0 callers) and persist_file (0 callers); publish_project and read_file are reached only from those. A grep of all 11 repos (excluding generated .rs/.fsx/.fs/.ipynb) finds the names only in apps/spiral/spiral.spi and .livemd, with a positive control; SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 1 (10-17) already called it dead. Step 1, job-1792928699219: drop the 6 definitions (persist_code_project..publish_file, the contiguous block before '## rust') from spiral.spi (151 lines) and spiral.livemd (256 lines); get_package_dir stays, since line 270 uses it. Spiral emits only reachable code, so the proof is byte-identical spiral.rs plus the livemd export reproducing the spi. This removes 41 macro lines. Step 2, job-1792928704408 (after-ok): heal the 59 plain macro strings left. FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER: the dotnet publish / fsproj path in apps/spiral is gone with step 1.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_3 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 3u32, "2026-10-25T12:38Z", "main", "10-25 08:38 main: FOUND a drift from the 10-24 edition-3 landing. apps/spiral/spiral.livemd is the build's source of truth (build.ps1 exports it with --export-only before compiling), but respell-repo.ps1 only touches .spi/.spir, so the livemd stayed in edition-2 words. The next apps/spiral build would have silently reverted spiral.spi to edition 2. The same shape applies to spiral/apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.livemd and polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd. Fix: %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\livemd-from-spi.ps1 regenerates each livemd from its edition-3 spi through Cells and applies only if the new livemd exports back to the spi byte for byte. apps/spiral dry run GREEN; the apply is job-1792931767062; wasm and perf dry runs are queued. Decision, by the '.spi as notebook' direction (memory self-checking-samples-direction): the regenerated notebook uses Cells' canonical cell layout (40 smart cells, was 64; headings and prose identical except one), so the hand cell boundaries aren't kept. The one cell dropped is '### escape_cell (test)', a static test cell that defines escape_cell and asserts nothing. It never reached the spi (the old export dropped it too), and a grep of all 9 repos finds it only in that livemd, so it's dead under the same rule as the publish chain. Then the dead-publish drop and the plain-string heal are requeued after it.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_4 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 4u32, "2026-10-25T15:35Z", "main", "10-25 11:35 main: step 1 GREEN (job-1792931858307): the 6 dead publish definitions are dropped from spiral.spi (151 lines) and the regenerated spiral.livemd (211 lines); the livemd export reproduces the spi byte for byte, and spiral.rs is byte-identical (B5D5A1E4A436DEDA). Step 2, the plain-string heal on the 59 remaining sites, runs next (job-1792931861319).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_5 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 5u32, "2026-10-25T17:23Z", "main", "10-25 13:23 main: step 2 (heal-plain-3, job-1792931861319) RED was the byte-identical rule, not a behaviour change. spiral.rs 1,459,426 -> 1,455,245 B. Read: the heal turns 59 plain macro pieces into Spiral strings, and the compiler then (a) shares identical literal constants within a function (the Cargo.toml writer reuses the earlier '[dependencies]' and '' bindings, so the standalone literal count drops 3 -> 2 with the same text written) and (b) folds adjacent plain pieces of ++# chains statically, so the concat trees differ in shape. With literal bindings inlined per function and vN normalized, 128 of 20,6xx lines still differ, all concat-shape. So text equality can't prove this class of heal; dice's attribute heal is the same class. Rule for heal-plain-4, written before the run: plain-macros.ps1 -RunCheck <project build.ps1>. If the output is byte-identical: GREEN as before. Otherwise GREEN iff the project's own release gate passes on the healed tree. For apps/spiral that is build.ps1's run check: --help, export, the export of every livemd compared with the previous CLI, and the rust/cuda/lua/gleam runs, which write Cargo.toml and gleam.toml through exactly the healed writers. build.ps1 ships the CLI only on pass. On any failure, the sources and spiral.rs are restored and the previous CLI stays.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_6 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 6u32, "2026-10-25T17:32Z", "main", "10-25 13:32 main: the Cells trailing-markdown fix is RED and withdrawn (job-1792933087976; snapshot restored, document.spi has no diff, keep_docs is back). Dropping keep_docs from export_text broke 3 suite tests: 'the export equals spiral export on the dice and alphabet notebooks', 'the export keeps directive lines ... follows the CLI export's markdown rules', and the _real.spir interleave round trip. So 'a trailing markdown cell after the last code cell is not part of the spi' is a rule Cells and the spiral CLI export share, not a Cells bug. Consequence for polyglot Perf.livemd (livemd-from-spi dry RED: 2 inserted lines): its trailing markdown can't be regenerated from the spi by design. The fix is in the notebook: move that trailing text above the last code cell (a heading section's prose), then regenerate. Rule for that edit: livemd-from-spi round-trips byte for byte, and the moved prose is unchanged as a line multiset.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_7 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 7u32, "2026-10-25T18:04Z", "main", "10-25 14:04 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd must NOT be regenerated from Perf.spi. It is a mixed notebook: 0 spiral fences, 5 elixir smart cells holding the Spiral code, and 13 fsharp cells (the original F# perf tests, e.g. returnLettersWithOddCountTests' solutions). The spi export carries only the Spiral cells, so livemd-from-spi would drop every F# cell (47,168 -> 30,026 bytes). Its dry run was RED only on the trailing results block, and only because it compared spi with spi. Rule added to livemd-from-spi: refuse a livemd that has any code fence other than spiral/elixir-smart-cell. Perf's fix is an in-place respell of the edition-2 words inside its Spiral smart cells (respell-repo over .livemd cell bodies), proven by: the livemd exports to an spi equal to today's edition-3 Perf.spi, and every non-Spiral cell is byte-identical.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_8 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 8u32, "2026-10-25T18:21Z", "main", "10-25 14:21 main: polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd respelled IN PLACE and landed unstaged (105+/105-, previous copy in %TEMP%\\perf-respell\\Perf.livemd.previous). Method (%TEMP%\\perf-respell\\respell-cells.ps1): export the livemd (670 lines); respell that export with the pack-spelling candidate's 'format --spelling symbols', which keeps the line count (670 = 670); locate each elixir smart cell's body (first to last non-blank line) in order in the export; put back the same range of the respelled export. 5 Spiral cells, 4 respelled; every fsharp cell, markdown line and cell frame byte-identical (checked by masking the cells). Proof: the respelled livemd exports to an spi byte-identical to the respelled export. The committed Perf.spi differs from it only by the installed formatter's doc-comment re-spacing plus the trailing results block (no code-only edits lost). Side finding for FORMATTER-KEEP-BREAKS: that formatter collapsed the column alignment inside /// markdown tables (e.g. 'abc        | abc' -> 'abc | abc') and rewrapped table rows over 120; comment interiors should be kept as written (F7).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_9 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 9u32, "2026-10-25T19:12Z", "main", "10-25 15:12 main: step 2 GREEN and LANDED (heal-plain-4, job-1792949109509): the 59 plain macro strings in apps/spiral/spiral.spi and spiral.livemd are now Spiral strings (unstaged; the livemd export reproduced the healed spi). The output changed only by literal sharing and folding (n5), so by n5's rule the release gate decided: apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check passed (--help, export, the 4-notebook export comparison with the previous CLI, and the rust/cuda/lua/gleam runs that write Cargo.toml and gleam.toml through the healed writers), and the new spiral.exe shipped to workspace/target/release. Next: the remaining host macros in apps/spiral are the manifest pieces with escaped quotes; heal them as escaped Spiral strings, by the same gate.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_10 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 10u32, "2026-10-25T19:20Z", "main", "10-25 15:20 main: step 3 queued (heal-escaped). plain-macros.ps1 gains -Mode escaped: a F#-string macro piece with escaped quotes or doubled braces and NO interpolation becomes a Spiral string (escaped quotes kept as Spiral escapes, doubled braces become single). Sites with a single brace or a ! (real interpolation, 53) are untouched; F# formats any type there while ++ needs strings, so they need typed rewrites. 41 sites in spiral.spi, the same 41 in the livemd. The rule is n5's, written before heal-plain-4: byte-identical output is GREEN; otherwise GREEN iff apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check passes on the healed tree (it ships the CLI only on pass); any failure restores the sources and spiral.rs, and the previous CLI stays.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_11 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 11u32, "2026-10-25T19:36Z", "main", "10-25 15:36 main: step 4 queued after step 3 (heal-interp, same files, so sequential). plain-macros.ps1 -Mode interp rewrites an interpolating F#-string macro piece into Spiral concatenation, e.g. name = spiral_{hash} becomes (literal ++#? hash ++# literal), with parentheses so the grouping inside the surrounding newline-join chain can't matter. Only sites whose every interpolated name is on a verified-string allowlist are rewritten (22 names, each read at its binding: string literals, path joins, read_all_text, sm'.concat, obj_to_string, from_std_string, union_to_string, crypto.hash_text, whose test asserts a string). On a dry run that is 20 of 46 interpolating sites. Excluded: deps, k, v, x, ext (a std_string), runtime_cmd and the two result tuples, since show (#?) formats non-primitive types differently from Rust Display. Same rule as n5 (the build.ps1 run check decides).")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_12 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 12u32, "2026-10-25T22:49Z", "main", "10-25 18:49 main: CI RED on the user's d095f13d push (run 40222011377, both build legs) is MINE, from the 10-25 08:4x wasm livemd regeneration (n3). apps/wasm/build.ps1 runs the notebook (run_notebook --output-path ipynb) before exporting, and that run failed: spiral_wasm.livemd:277:46 ETY-UnboundVariable run_main. Cause: the spi has ONE '///> _' (line 162) covering the ### main section (run_main and main). livemd-from-spi let Cells split the section into 2 cells, but the target line went only on the first. So in the notebook run the run_main cell is skipped and the main cell is compiled without it. The old livemd (59b477ba) had '///> _' in both cells. My proof (export round trip, byte-equal) can't see this, because export ignores cell targets. FIX in the tree, unstaged: the main cell gets '///> _' (livemd +3/-1, previous copy in %TEMP%\\granular-heal\\spiral_wasm.livemd.before-target-fix), and the spi is re-exported from it (+2 lines: '///> _' at 162 and 217). Verifying with CI's exact notebook run on the node (job-1792968520121, mirror main-wasm, type check). Rule fixes: (1) livemd-from-spi.ps1 must also run the notebook (not just export) before applying; (2) the Cells spi-to-livemd split should copy a section's ///> line into every cell it creates from that section (Cells item to file). spiral.livemd and Perf.livemd: check for the same section split.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_13 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 13u32, "2026-10-25T22:54Z", "main", "10-25 18:54 main: scope of the n12 split bug: only spiral_wasm.livemd. apps/spiral/spiral.livemd keeps its one '///> _' section (spi 1752: run_main + main) in ONE cell (livemd line 2102, which holds both), so its notebook run is unaffected; polyglot Perf.spi has no target lines. The first node check of the wasm fix (job-1792968520121) was VOID for environment reasons: the mirror lacked gitignored deps/The-Spiral-Language, so Cells' package.spiproj could not load the fork core. Junctioned from the node's main clone; rerun job-1792968763090.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_14 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 14u32, "2026-10-25T22:57Z", "main", "10-25 18:57 main: wasm CI fix PROVEN. CI's failing step (run_notebook on apps/wasm/spiral_wasm.livemd with --output-path ipynb) exits 0 on the node with the fixed tree (job-1792968763090, mirror main-wasm at this box's tree, digest FAF8B9167ABF9588, L6 core guard): no FatalError, run_main resolves. The notebook has no test blocks, so discovered 0 is expected. The fix (livemd +3/-1, spi +2) is unstaged for the user to commit; it should turn run 40222011377's red green on the next push. livemd-from-spi.ps1 now also (a) refuses a regeneration whose section has a ///> line on only some of its cells (positive control: it flags '### main' on the original bad wasm regeneration) and (b) runs the regenerated notebook as the build does, requiring exit 0 before -Apply.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_15 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 15u32, "2026-10-26T01:36Z", "main", "10-25 21:36 main: heal-escaped GREEN (main-apps-spiral-heal-escaped, finished 21:24): the escaped macro strings in apps/spiral/spiral.spi are Spiral strings, the output changed only in literal sharing and folding, and build.ps1's run check passed on rust, cuda, lua and gleam (each exits 42); RUST-OK apps/spiral, and it shipped the new spiral.exe to workspace/target/release. Healed spiral.spi is in the tree, unstaged for the user. heal-interp (the last of the three modes) is running.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_16 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 16u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "main", "10-29 22:00 main, groom (WIP rule, 3 days with no note): Active -> Paused, progress kept at 25%. Last note 2026-10-26, before the 10-28 profile wipe interrupted every lane, and no live job. Resume when picked; it is not abandoned, and it keeps its priority P1.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_17 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 17u32, "2026-10-31T20:54Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_18 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 18u32, "2026-11-06T14:27Z", "main", "main 11-06 14:1x CENSUS (kit standards --census, host-macro / host-block): apps/spiral 76 / 6 (the CLI; up from the 63 sites of the original count, as the CLI grew), apps/cells/spi 9 / 0, apps/wasm 4, apps/lisp 1, apps/kernel 0, apps/plot 0. Split plan when pulled (each 120 min or less): (1) apps/cells/spi 9 + wasm 4 + lisp 1; (2)-(4) apps/spiral in three module groups by file, each healed to lib functions, compiled with its run check on the box or a codespace, byte-compared against HEAD (memory heal-check-every-backend). Next step: list apps/spiral's 76 sites by file to cut the three groups.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_19 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 19u32, "2026-11-06T19:27Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:4x SLICE 1 started. Sites: apps/lisp 1 = FALSE POSITIVE (a \"$\" string literal in spl2spi.spi:160); apps/cells/spi main.spi !!!!Export = the export intrinsic itself (stays); cells/spi builders.spi 4 + text.spi 5 = calls into the Gleam FFI sidecar for grapheme text (builders_graphemes/first/rest/slice, kino_text_size/at/byte/lower) and need a Cells gate, so they come next. apps/wasm HEALED 3 of 4 (unstaged): the two F#-style interpolated error messages are now ++# concatenation (sm'.format retries, which prints integers with no suffix, and sm'.from_std_string error), and the Rust args macro is now am'.init_base (env.command_line_argument_count ()) env.command_line_argument (same std::env::args() underneath, offset 1 = the old skip(1)). The F# entry macro stays (an entry definition). proof so far: single-flight --backend Rust compiles (205485 B; the diff vs the pre-heal E10C2BB46AF9 is only those sites plus 2 new lib wrappers). --backend Fsharp fails in clap.spi:217 (no Fsharp arm), a path untouched by the heal, so Rust is the only buildable backend. PENDING: cargo build + the build.ps1 --help/--wasm run check on europa.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_20 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 20u32, "2026-11-06T19:28Z", "main", "DoD: every spiral/apps host-macro site is healed to lib functions, or recorded with a reason as a deliberate host binding (entry definitions, FFI sidecar calls without a lib equivalent, intrinsics); each healed app compiles on its buildable backends and passes its build/run check.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_21 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 21u32, "2026-11-06T20:03Z", "main", "main 11-06 16:1x SLICE 2 DESIGN (cells/spi sidecar sites, 9). They are NOT swappable for today's sm'. text.spi is BYTE-offset text (kino_text_size = erlang:byte_size, kino_text_at = clamped binary:part, kino_text_byte = binary:at, kino_text_lower = string:lowercase), while sm'.length/slice/index delegate to core sm, whose Gleam arms are string-level. A swap would shift Cells' parser offsets on any non-ASCII notebook. builders.spi is GRAPHEME text (string.length, pop_grapheme, string.slice). The heal: add two small lib families with an arm per backend and a non-ASCII test each, (a) bytes: byte_count, byte_part (clamped), byte_at, and (b) graphemes: grapheme_count, first_grapheme, rest_graphemes, grapheme_slice (unicode.spi already has grapheme_list). Land them via lib_land_window, then point cells/spi at them. That also removes the 'kino_text_*' names RENAME-LEFTOVERS-1106 counts. Gate: the Cells suite (cells_test + a notebook with non-ASCII text) on the node or a codespace, plus a byte-compare of the generated Gleam for unchanged call sites. Estimate 120 min.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_22 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 22u32, "2026-11-06T20:26Z", "main", "main 11-06 16:2x apps/wasm heal PROVEN on Linux (europa, pack57, the box's healed spiral_wasm.spi 0f64b01c543a): --backend Rust 205485 B; cargo +nightly-2026-10-18 build --package spiral_wasm exit 0 (only the pre-existing unused-dependency warnings); the built spiral_wasm --help exit 0 printing 'Usage: spiral_wasm [OPTIONS] --wasm <wasm>', so the lib-built args array (am'.init_base over env.command_line_argument) feeds clap as the old std::env::args().skip(1) macro did. Slice 1 is done except cells/spi, which is slice 2 (design in n21). apps/spiral's 76 sites are slices 3-5.")
inl note_of_apps_granular_heal_23 () = Note ("APPS-GRANULAR-HEAL", 23u32, "2026-11-06T23:46Z", "main", "main 11-06 19:5x apps/spiral SLICES CUT (76 host-macro sites by /// ### section): slice 3 = process_gleam 21 + get_cargo_toml_content 3 + get_empty_cargo_toml_content 2 + run 4 + get_command 1 (31); slice 4 = process_rust 19 + process_cuda 8 (27); slice 5 = process_lua 18. Nearly all are F#-style interpolated strings ($'$\"...{!x}...\"') building command lines, paths and Cargo/gleam toml text, so they heal as wasm did: ++# concatenation, with sm'::format for non-strings. The few real host calls (env, process) are recorded with a reason. Proof per slice: --backend Rust compiles, plus apps/spiral build.ps1's own run checks (spiral-check-rust/cuda/lua/gleam exit 0 and the protocol-level checks) on europa or a codespace. Next step: slice 3, process_gleam first; it builds gleam.toml, so compare the emitted toml byte for byte.")
