inl note_of_duplicate_code_tool_4 () = Note ("DUPLICATE-CODE-TOOL", 4u32, "2026-11-07T22:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. Split by kit 11-07 into DUPLICATE-CODE-SHINGLES and DUPLICATE-CODE-SEMANTIC; this parent closes when both do. DoD: both children Done, plus one periodic move of product code into lib driven by their report (HTML-MD-SVG-DEDUP's pair map and today's doc_parse move are the hand-made first instance). Next step: none here; work happens in the children. TypeFeatures: duplicate pairs as typed rows { left; right; kind : ShingleMatch | SameStructure }.")
inl erlang_process_sota () = Task ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", "Iterate lib erlang_process.spi to a SOTA state: peak granular wrapping that handles the nastiest process patterns, compatible with the future Spiral rewrite on hopac+subject+crossbeam; then the otp files with the same lessons, reaching patterns the typefeatureset allows where Elixir and Gleam cannot; future target: apache iggy-style sagas with compensation modeled on the 5 mega samples (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Epic, P1, D7, 75u32, Active, "", "lib,otp,beam,typefeatures,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_erlang_process_sota () = Planning ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_1 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-10-24T14:06Z", "main", "Probes 10-24 on the installed compiler (Gleam). A constructor-level forall existential is rejected (ETY-UnusedTypeVariable). An explicit exists r. query r * subject r type-checks but peval refuses it: runtime existentials cannot be destructured (compile-time only). What works: a static GADT query r (| Count :: () -> query i32) plus a per-case wrap into a closed runtime message union carrying typed subjects; call forall r. (q : query r) : r then fixes the reply type statically. Draft %TEMP%\\lib-erlang-process-sota (anchored by hash): receipt union (Got | TimedOut) in Spiral over host_received; receive/selector_receive -> *_within returning receipt; call_forever and call_within (argument order of otp_actor.call) composed in Spiral; GADT query test; down_reason as one accessor $'!event.reason' instead of a case macro; tests beside their code. Consumers checked: only subject/send are used outside the file (otp_actor, step_runtime, tibia arena_host). Probe compile 130 Gleam lines; gate+land queued as job main-serial-erlang-process. Next layers: backend-neutral channel prototype (hopac/crossbeam), otp files.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_2 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-10-24T15:55Z", "main", "main 12:2x: run 2 RED on monitor_sees_normal_exit only: the event was ProcessDown(..., Abnormal(Noproc)): the empty worker exited before monitor attached (a race the baseline test has too; it passed by timing). The test now hands the worker a go subject it owns and sends go only after monitor, so it is deterministic. Also folded in x's LIB-OTP-NAME erlang_process additions (name, new_name, named_subject, register on host_received_is_ok, their test). Local Gleam run of every test body (scratch gleam-run, a copy of a Cells warm project): Some 42, None, 7, 41, \"x\", None, True, Some 5, True = all expectations. Run 3 queued (main-serial-erlang-process-3).")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_3 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 3u32, "2026-10-24T16:40Z", "main", "main 12:4x: layer 1 LANDED (gate GREEN 12:36, land 12:39; live erlang_process.spi = the gated draft by hash, the formatter changed nothing). In lib now: receipt t = Got | TimedOut over host_received; receive_within / selector_receive_within; typed call_forever and call_within (otp_actor argument order) composed in Spiral; names (new_name, named_subject, register; x's LIB-OTP-NAME part); down_reason as a typed let accessor; the GADT query test (a static query r fixes the reply type, wrapped into a closed message union) and a race-free monitor test (go subject). Next layers: a backend-neutral channel prototype (Rust crossbeam/std mpsc vs Gleam subject behind one Spiral API), otp_actor on receipt, then the otp files.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_4 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 4u32, "2026-10-24T17:01Z", "main", "main 13:0x: layer 2 drafted and queued (job main-serial-channel): new lib channel.spi after otp_actor. One typed API (channel () : sender t * receiver t, send, receive_within -> erlang_process.receipt t) over backend_switch types: Gleam sender and receiver are the same process.Subject (identity retags), Rust is std::sync::mpsc Sender/Receiver; recv_timeout's Result is bridged by one-call is_ok/unwrap with the branch in Spiral, and the ms cast is conv. Probe: the same Spiral main prints (Some(42), None) on Rust and on Gleam. Parser lesson: inline $'Result<(), ...>' in an annotation and a tuple of two annotated macros both fail to parse; name the type as a nominal and bind each half. Next: a backend-neutral spawn (Rust needs a Send closure: Spiral closures are Rc) for a cross-thread test; then a select over several receivers (Gleam selector vs crossbeam select).")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_5 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 5u32, "2026-10-24T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x: layer 2 LANDED (lib channel.spi, 14:16). Layer 2b queued (job main-serial-channel-spawn): channel.spawn (work : () -> ()) is erlang_process.spawn on Gleam and threading.spawn on Rust, with test a_spawned_worker_sends_across_threads; probe: the same Spiral main prints Some(7) on Rust (an OS thread sends over mpsc) and Gleam (a BEAM process sends to the owner's subject). Next: select over several receivers (Gleam selector vs a Rust select), then otp_actor on receipt.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_6 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 6u32, "2026-10-24T20:54Z", "main", "main 16:5x: channel-spawn gate RED (15:55) by the livemd round-trip rule only: spawn.spi had no trailing newline, so the appended channel.spi ended without one, and Cells writes one. The apply now ends the file with exactly one newline; the round trip on a scratch copy is byte-identical. The zig-lua-gaps RED (16:19) was a gate artifact: async.spi counted as touched because async-roundtrip landed on the tree (16:00) while the gate's candidate still held the old copy; its own files are fine (unicode.spi round-trips; sm'.spi differs on the tree too, held). Both are requeued in one runner: main-serial-zig-channel-2 (gate, land, gate, land).")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_7 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 7u32, "2026-10-28T01:33Z", "main", "10-27 21:33 main: Groom 10-27 21:2x (WIP rule, set before applying). main's Active items with no note since 10-24 were classed by the evidence in the tree, not by age alone. (1) SHIPPED -> Done: the item's pass is in the installed tree core (apps/compiler/spiral_compiler.fs, core 8660C34A11F33259, the installed 5m' core), found by its own identifier or anchor, and an accept has passed on that core since (accept-6, run single-flight-20261027-173417, 0 blocking cells). (2) COLD -> Paused: no lane is driving it this week and nothing is queued. Its last note keeps the next step, and it resumes when a lane picks it or its condition fires. (3) KEPT Active: a lane is on it this week, or demand is waiting on it. (4) Blocked: only the user's commit or push unblocks it.\n Verdict for this item: (2) COLD, so Active -> Paused. The next step is the one in its latest note above.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_8 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 8u32, "2026-10-31T20:55Z", "main", "10-31 16:53 main, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK; rule R3 is now 'never auto-pause for silence'): Paused -> PLANNED. It was paused by the old silence/WIP rule, not by a blocker: its notes name no external condition (a 10-30 lab-archive note blocks nothing: restore the draft from the archive into the repo when picked). Back in agile next at its priority; deps, if any, gate it as usual.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_9 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 9u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: lib erlang_process.spi handles the hard process patterns (stdin close, kill tree, timeouts, large output) with a test per pattern on BEAM, compared against the Rust process lib. Next step: list today's erlang_process tests against those patterns.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_10 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 10u32, "2026-11-07T22:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. TypeFeatures: each hard process pattern (stdin close, kill tree, timeout, large output) as a union case of a pattern type, with one test per case, so a new pattern is a compile-visible addition.")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_11 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 11u32, "2026-11-11T00:02Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 NEXT STEP DONE (read lib erlang_process.spi, erlang_port.spi, process.spi). SCOPE CORRECTION: erlang_process.spi is BEAM ACTOR processes (subjects, named subjects, calls with timeout, monitors, selectors, trapped exits, groups; 13 gleam tests); the four DoD patterns are OS SUBPROCESS patterns, which on the BEAM live in erlang_port.spi (139 lines, 2 gleam tests: an_executable_port_reports_its_os_pid_and_exit, a_run_collects_its_output_until_the_exit), and the comparison lib is process.spi (1049 lines). COVERAGE per DoD pattern: (1) stdin close: NO test on either backend (process.spi has child stdin plumbing, lines 205-238, untested); (2) kill tree: process.spi a_running_program_is_killed_by_its_pid (rust + gleam) kills ONE sleeper, no grandchild, so tree semantics are untested; nothing in erlang_port; (3) timeout: process.spi a_program_past_its_deadline_is_killed (a 30 s sleep under a 400 ms deadline returns -1); nothing in erlang_port; (4) large output: NO test on either backend. TYPEFEATURES (per the 11-07 groom): a process_pattern union (StdinClosed | TreeKilled | DeadlineHit | LargeOutput) with one test per case on each backend, so a missing case is visible. The item title says erlang_process but the work is erlang_port plus process. Next step: draft the 4 erlang_port tests plus the 3 missing process.spi ones (stdin close, grandchild tree kill, about 1 MB of output) in kit drafts spiral/ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA, using python children like the existing tests")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_12 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 12u32, "2026-11-11T00:03Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 DRAFT (kit drafts spiral/ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA/erlang_port.spi, base 6a7051e2434 = box lib, now d8d8906b781, budget 0): TYPEFEATURES process_pattern union (DeadlineHit | LargeOutput) with pattern_run dispatching per case, and 2 gleam tests over erl children: a_child_past_its_quiet_budget_times_out (a 30 s sleeper under a 2 s quiet budget gives TimedOut) and a_megabyte_of_output_arrives_whole (1,000,000 bytes from lists:duplicate reassembled from port data chunks into Finished 0). KNOWN BOUNDS, documented rather than faked: (a) StdinClosed: Erlang spawn_executable ports cannot half-close stdin (port_close closes both directions and drops the remaining output), so the BEAM test for this case is \"the child exits after port_close\", which needs an OS liveness probe (kill -0 on Linux, tasklist on Windows); (b) TreeKilled: killing a process tree from the BEAM needs an OS command per platform (kill of the process group on Linux, taskkill /T on Windows) via os:cmd, matching process.spi kill_tree_of_pid. Those two become cases of the union once the probe helper exists. Next step: land the draft through lib_land_window on ganymede (after the running org2 jobs), then the 3 missing process.spi tests")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_13 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 13u32, "2026-11-11T00:14Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 RUST/GLEAM SIDE DRAFTED (kit drafts spiral/ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA/process.spi, base c4f81e674d3 = box lib, new cells in budget; the file itself carries many older over-budget cells): (1) a_megabyte_of_output_is_captured_whole (python writes exactly 1,000,000 bytes, capture_stdout_list returns all of them); (2) killing_a_tree_also_ends_the_grandchild: a python parent spawns a sleeping grandchild and logs its pid, the test checks the parent runs and the grandchild is alive, then kill_tree_of_pid on the PARENT and checks the parent ended AND the grandchild is gone (the existing kill test only ended one process). Liveness probe pid_alive is cross-platform python: os.kill(pid, 0) on POSIX, OpenProcess + GetExitCodeProcess == 259 (STILL_ACTIVE) on Windows, because os.kill(pid, 0) on Windows sends CTRL_C_EVENT instead of probing. StdinClosed has no public process.spi API to drive (child stdin plumbing at lines 205-238 is internal), recorded as a gap. Both files (erlang_port.spi and process.spi) land together through one lib_land_window on ganymede next; their tests are rust + gleam, and the tree test needs python3 on the node")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_14 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 14u32, "2026-11-11T00:41Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 GATE org2-jproc (lib_land_window -PrivateLib on the ganymede mirror, closed green, 26 passed, digest 979F10026F8B7C4D; NOT copied to the box): erlang_port 4/4 (both new BEAM tests green: the quiet-budget timeout and a 1 MB output reassembled whole). process 17 executed, 1 FAILED, 3 unsupported: killing_a_tree_also_ends_the_grandchild FAILED on rust with actual (true, true, true, true), i.e. after kill_tree_of_pid on the parent the GRANDCHILD IS STILL ALIVE. REAL BUG FOUND: on Linux both arms ran kill -9 <pid>, which signals one process; Windows uses taskkill /T, which does walk the tree. FIX drafted (process.spi e8cf4bfb3e3): posix_tree_kill collects descendants with a recursive ps -o pid= --ppid walk BEFORE killing (once the parent dies its children are reparented to init and the link is lost), then kill -9 on all of them plus the pid, shared by the rust arm (sh -c) and the gleam arm (os:cmd). The shell walk verified on ganymede by hand: a tree with 2 grandchildren fully killed, none left. The large-output test passed on rust. Next step: re-land both files through lib_land_window on ganymede with the fix; expect process 18/18 executed (the 3 unsupported are the gleam python cases)")
inl note_of_erlang_process_sota_15 () = Note ("ERLANG-PROCESS-SOTA", 15u32, "2026-11-11T01:18Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 LANDED on the box (unstaged), proof: ganymede org2-jproc2 (lib_land_window -PrivateLib closed green, 26 passed; erlang_port 4/4, process 17 executed 0 failed 3 unsupported = the gleam python cases). Box copy-land with -ProvenLog (fast checks rerun on the box, 0 respell drift): erlang_port.spi D8D8906B781D (TYPEFEATURES process_pattern union DeadlineHit | LargeOutput; 2 BEAM tests: quiet-budget timeout, 1 MB output reassembled), process.spi E8CF4BFB3E3F (a_megabyte_of_output_is_captured_whole; killing_a_tree_also_ends_the_grandchild; and the REAL FIX: kill_tree_of_pid on Linux now collects descendants with a recursive ps --ppid walk before kill -9, so grandchildren die too, on both the rust arm via sh -c and the gleam arm via os:cmd; before, Linux killed only the one pid). Undo C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261110-211639. Remaining for the DoD: StdinClosed (no public process.spi API to drive child stdin; BEAM ports cannot half-close stdin, so the BEAM case is child-exits-after-port_close with an OS liveness probe) and the BEAM side of TreeKilled")
inl mega_samples_as_models () = Task ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", "Use the compiler's 5 mega samples as the modeling reference for new code; study how the ml library graph types and constant indexes model things, and design deterministic tooling to measure F#-like slop vs strict Spiral modeling (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Story, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "MEGA-READ-LEAN-CIC,MEGA-READ-SPIRAL-PROVES,MEGA-READ-ZETA,MEGA-PROTOTYPES-FROM-REPOS", "spiral,modeling,typefeatures,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_mega_samples_as_models () = Planning ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_1 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 1u32, "2026-10-31T16:17Z", "main", "10-31 12:17 main, RESEARCH DONE (read-only survey of the 5 mega samples vs lib/spiral; counts measured by regex scripts). IDIOMS (paths under apps/compiler/tmp/samples): typestate via unit nominals indexing ops by from/to state with existential middle states (omniledger main.spi:3-11, :117-171); branded string nominals as type indexes (:13-33, :92-94); type-level naturals from nominals, binary positional in zeta :12-23 with addition as a relation GADT :50-78, Peano meta_zero/meta_succ in spiral_proves :1-16, length-indexed vectors that need no fallback arm (brzozowski :75-97); proof-carrying Accepted cert | Rejected reason decisions instead of bool (cic :53-118); existentials \\? pack/unpack; prototypes+instances for comparison returning an ordering union (brzozowski :7-24); nominal-over-tuple data, 0 record fields; 98-100% annotated signatures; runtime code fenced to mega_runtime_fail and i64 wrappers; positive checks as *_terminal () : string claims aggregated in all_terminals, negative checks as negative_* sub-packages that must fail to type-check (no test blocks at all). brzozowski is the weakest model (18% GADT cases, bool predicates with '| _ => !-'). LIB SLOP, cited: stringly dispatch json_schema.spi:170-182, xpath.spi:58-69, xsd.spi:134-140, xpath_eval.spi:26-28; -1 sentinels sm'.spi:825/830/845/881, json_scan.spi:12/49/58, xpath_eval.spi:33-34/424; bool flags xpath_eval.spi:387/408/473, convert.spi:216; sign ints xpath_eval.spi:397-402; silent catch-all listm'.spi:28 (map4 truncates unequal lists); mutation am'.spi:477-479/586-591; untyped point-free listm'.spi:20/23/46/93/101. METRICS (strip test blocks, comments and macro text first; mega vs lib): M1 return-annotation coverage 98-100% vs listm' 45%; M2 bare-parameter defs 0% vs listm' 52%; M3 GADT case share 86-100% vs date_time 0; M4 indexed-union share 54-88% vs 0-33%; M5 existentials per 1k lines 9-61 vs ~0; M6 wildcard-arm ratio 1-6% vs 12-30%; M7 stringly dispatch per 1k 0 vs xpath 124; M8 sentinel ints per 1k 0-0.4 vs ~11; M9 bool/option in signatures per def 0 vs convert 1.36; M10 mutation per 1k 0 vs listm' 31.7; M11 type defs per 100 fns 24-41 vs 2-4. Score FFI-binding modules (base, convert, file_system: 115-254 macros/1k lines) separately. ML LIBRARY is not in lib/spiral: C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library (old dialect; graph GADT ml\\layers.spi:24-55, type-level int literals corebase\\type_arith.spir:3-7). NEXT: the metrics as a repo tool (apps/cells/spi), report per lib file, then pick the worst files (xpath*, json_schema, listm') for modeling rewrites.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_2 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 2u32, "2026-10-31T16:34Z", "main", "10-31 12:34 main: METRICS TOOL in the repo: apps/cells/spi/model_metrics.ps1 -Path <files or dirs, comma list> [-Tsv out]. Strips test blocks (header + indented body), // and /// lines and macro text before counting; M1-M11 per file plus Kind = ffi when macros+intrinsics exceed 100 per 1k lines (score those separately). Validated against the survey: omniledger main.spi M1 98 / M2 0 / M3 86 / M5 14.5, zeta 99 / 0 / 90 / 60.9, listm' 45 / 52, base 83 / 15: identical. Whole lib: 110 files, 56 model + 54 ffi, saved %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\model-metrics-lib-20261031.tsv. Worst model files by a first combined score (100-M1 + M2 + M6 + M7 + 5*M8 + 10*M9): float_text 552, json_scan 441, decimal 210, sm'_operators 200, testing 190, json_schema 184, stream 167, xsd_regex 153, xpath 146, xsd 144. CAVEAT: M8 (sentinels) over-counts index-bound checks in parsers (float_text, json_scan: 'i >= 0i32'), so tighten it to -1 returns before ranking by it; M7 (stringly dispatch) agrees with the survey's reading (xpath 136, json_schema 101, xsd 81 per 1k). NEXT: tighten M8, then pick the first modeling rewrite (likely xpath/xsd dispatch on name strings -> a union).")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_3 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 3u32, "2026-10-31T16:38Z", "main", "10-31 12:38 main: M8 tightened to -1i32/-1i64 literals only (index-bound comparisons no longer count). Re-ranked model files: sm'_operators 200 (operator aliases: bare signatures are the norm there, read it per kind), json_scan 190 (its real -1 returns, the survey's :12/:49/:58), testing 190, stream 167, xpath 146 (M7 135.5), physics 127, xsd 122 (M7 81.3), json_schema 118 (M7 101.1). First rewrite candidates: xpath/xsd/json_schema name-string dispatch -> unions, json_scan -1 -> option.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_4 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. metrics ranked (n3); feeds TYPEFEATURESET work when code is touched; no separate step running.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_5 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T14:35Z", "main", "main 10:3x (user 11-01: 'the mega samples shouldn't be a sota measure yet because they don't use prototypes, that means all of that code could've been modeled even better while leveraging peak gadts/existentials/hkts/reflection ... the projects will compete with cognitive cannibalism'). So the scoreboard (TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT) must not treat the mega samples as the ceiling: prototypes become a scored dimension, and the reference model is a per-concept best example from any repo, not the mega samples as a set.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_6 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 6u32, "2026-11-06T13:09Z", "main", "main 11-06 11:5x DoD backfill (kit lint): DoD: a written guide naming which mega sample models which pattern (graph types, constraints), cited by at least 2 new modules. Next step: list the 5 mega samples with the pattern each shows best.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_7 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 7u32, "2026-11-09T11:43Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 fiscal: NEXT STEP DONE (the 5 samples, the pattern each models best, and the new modules that adopted one). Measured in spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mega_*/main.spi (lines; GADT cases; existentials \\?; foralls \\*; phantoms; prototype instances): brzozowski_derivatives 3,581 (14 / 33 / 263 / 3 / 4); lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel 9,979 (149 / 292 / 333 / 3 / 0); omniledger_erp_kernel 12,081 (88 / 173 / 250 / 9 / 0); spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency 11,835 (161 / 415 / 339 / 4 / 0); zeta_structural_laboratory 7,598 (162 / 451 / 338 / 4 / 0). GUIDE, pattern -> sample -> adopted by: (a) TYPESTATE over phantom stages + a GADT plan with an EXISTENTIAL middle stage (compose steps whose intermediate state is hidden) -> omniledger (erp_op chain) -> fiscal src/p2p.spi (purchase s, p2p_plan from to, 16 pinned negatives). (b) INDEXED AMOUNTS + an FX ROUTE GADT whose Via case hides the intermediate currency (\\? m. rate a m * rate m b) -> omniledger -> lib money.spi (Direct/Via, Ceiling rounding). (c) IDEMPOTENT EVENT LOG with typed append outcomes, membership EVIDENCE and checksummed frames -> omniledger (R6/R7/R9) -> lib event_log.spi (append_outcome, membership, audit). (d) NULLABILITY with an EVIDENCE GADT indexed by the answer, plus derivatives -> brzozowski -> lib regex_nullable/regex_derivative, now also the engine under lib xsd_regex (adapter 11-09). So >= 2 new modules cite a mega sample (p2p, money, event_log from omniledger; regex_* from brzozowski). Still open for the DoD: lean_cic, spiral_proves_spiral and zeta (the three densest in GADT cases and existentials per line) need their owners' pattern read (proof objects, typing derivations, structural laws), and per the 11-01 user note the guide must score PROTOTYPES too: every sample has 0 to 4 prototype instances, so for that dimension the best example comes from repos, not from the mega samples.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_8 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 8u32, "2026-11-09T17:00Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 17:0x SPLIT (gate: 480 m): the DoD's adoption half is met (fiscal 11:43: p2p/money/event_log from omniledger, regex_* from brzozowski = >= 2 new modules). The open half is split into 90 m children: MEGA-READ-LEAN-CIC, MEGA-READ-SPIRAL-PROVES, MEGA-READ-ZETA (team:compiler, the samples' owner) and MEGA-PROTOTYPES-FROM-REPOS (org). This parent keeps the guide assembly: 45 m.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_9 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 9u32, "2026-11-09T17:02Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 13:2x microblog, MEGA-READ-ZETA result (mega_zeta_structural_laboratory/main.spi, 7,597 lines), in the guide's form, pattern -> clearest instance -> adopter: (1) TYPE-LEVEL INDEXED DATA: phantom nominals spell a number's bits at the type level (:! positional_one, :! positional_zero_bit tail, :! positional_one_bit tail, lines 12-14), and a GADT ties the runtime value to that index (positive_positional_nat bits, 16-23: PositivePositionalZeroBit :: positive_positional_nat tail -> positive_positional_nat (positional_zero_bit tail)); positional_fourteen (44-48) shows a literal typed by its own bits. Adopter: any size or count that must match across values, e.g. x's thread frame header 'cN k/n' (k <= n) or lib fixed_views/array_dim lengths. (2) RELATIONS AS GADTs, constructors = derivation rules: positive_positional_successor input output (34-42) and positive_positional_add left right result (50 on); a value of the type IS a proof that the relation holds, checked by the type checker, not by a runtime assert. Adopter: regex equivalence steps (the lib regex_certificate pending pairs) and kit agile state transitions (Planned -> Active -> Done as a relation, so an illegal transition does not type). (3) SYNTHESIZE-AND-CERTIFY via existentials plus decisions: positive_positional_successor_synthesis input (324-329) wraps (\\? output. positive_positional_nat output * positive_positional_successor input output), i.e. the function returns the computed output together with the proof that it is the successor (synthesize_positive_positional_successor, 331-349, recursion unpacks and repacks the existential); positive_positional_order_decision left right (374-383) is a decision whose each branch carries its proof (Less/Equal/Greater). Adopter: lib comparison helpers that today return a bare ordering, and Cells' plan or verdict steps that should carry why. TypeFeatures: reads existing GADTs and existentials; adds none.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_10 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 10u32, "2026-11-09T17:04Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 13:2x microblog, MEGA-READ-LEAN-CIC result (mega_lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel/main.spi, 9,978 lines), pattern -> clearest instance -> adopter: (1) INTRINSICALLY TYPED SYNTAX: corrected_cic_term ctx sort (205-230) indexes every term by its context and sort; variables are typed de Bruijn indices (CorrectedBoundHere :: binder ctx sort -> corrected_cic_term (ctx_extend ctx sort) sort; CorrectedBoundThere weakens), and a lambda's body lives in ctx_extend ctx domain, so an ill-scoped or ill-sorted term cannot be built. CorrectedPi hides its domain with an existential. Adopter: any AST whose scoping bugs recur, e.g. Cells' plan steps over a notebook's definitions, or regex syntax trees bound to their alphabet. (2) CERTIFICATE + DECISION PAIRS over a schema index: cic_inductive_signature schema (37) with cic_strict_positivity_certificate (120) and cic_strict_positivity_decision (140); the decision returns either the certificate or a refutation, and downstream code demands the certificate type, so an unchecked signature cannot reach the kernel. Adopter: lib_land_window's proof (a land requires a proven-digest certificate value, not a log string) and x's thread_flaw/Whole reading result. (3) TYPESTATE ENVIRONMENTS: corrected_cic_meta_environment states (1648-1665), whose index is a type-level list of open/solved entries (:! corrected_cic_meta_states_open/solved ctx sort tail); solving a meta returns an environment of a DIFFERENT type, and membership witnesses (corrected_cic_meta_open_membership, 1666) prove a meta is still open before it is solved. Adopter: kit job lifecycle (queued -> running -> settled; a placed record can't be read as settled, cf. KIT-JOB-PLACED-SETTLE) and x post typestate (draft -> posted -> deleted).")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_11 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 11u32, "2026-11-09T17:06Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 13:3x microblog, MEGA-READ-SPIRAL-PROVES result (mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency/main.spi, 11,834 lines), pattern -> clearest instance -> adopter: (1) LINEARITY AS A TYPE: exact_linear_usage_vector context (117-140) marks each context entry Unused or Used at the type level; exact_linear_indexed_usage_merge context left right total (568-590) splits usage between two subterms, with rules UnusedUnused, UnusedUsed, UsedUnused and NO UsedUsed. A resource used by both halves therefore has no merge derivation and does not type. Adopter: use-once capabilities: a kit window or lease token consumed by its release, an x OAuth request nonce, an iggy connection handed to exactly one consumer. (2) STRUCTURAL RULES AS TYPED TRANSFORMS: index transport (exact_linear_indexed_term_index_transport, 647), weakening (exact_linear_indexed_outer_weakening, 1695), strengthening (exact_linear_indexed_variable_strengthening, 4935). Each is a relation between a source and a target context/usage, so a refactor that moves a term between scopes carries a proof. Adopter: Cells mount/package rewriting (a test moved into its module's scope, cf. CELLS-MODULE-SCOPE-TESTS) and lib_land_window's draft/live pairing. (3) REDUCTION AS TYPED DERIVATIONS: exact_linear_indexed_beta_step (6239), exact_linear_indexed_closed_atomic_reduction ty (8000) and exact_linear_indexed_value context usage ty (8803), small-step semantics where each step is indexed by the type it preserves (subject reduction by construction). Adopter: the x sim scheduler's event steps (each step preserves the world invariants; today checked at run time by invariants.spi).")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_12 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 12u32, "2026-11-09T17:11Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 13:3x microblog, MEGA-PROTOTYPES-FROM-REPOS result. Census (prototypes ':?/prototype' + instances ':+/instance', .spi): lib 35/143, tibia 38/146, regex 0/1, fiscal 199/58,837 (generated units, so it says nothing about style). Best 3 uses, pattern -> clearest instance -> why it beats a plain function -> adopter: (1) SHAPE-GENERIC OPERATIONS over fixed-arity views: lib fixed_views.spi 14-31, 'mapped' and 'counted' instanced for one, duo, trio, lanes and band. One name maps or tallies any fixed-width view, statically dispatched with no runtime tag; a plain function would need one name per arity or a list (losing the arity in the type). Adopter: f32_lanes/f32_kernels SIMD-width code and x's per-site posting (one op over X/Mastodon/Bluesky shapes). (2) CAPABILITIES BY INSTANCE COVERAGE: lib leptos/leptos.spi 313-361, signal_get instanced for all 8 signal kinds, signal_update/signal_set only for the writable ones (rw_signal, arc_rw_signal, write_signal, arc_write_signal). Writing through a read_signal or memo is a compile error rather than a runtime panic. A plain function family would either accept every signal or need 8 names. Adopter: kit's read-only vs mutating agile handles, and file_system read vs write handles. (3) DIRECTION-PARAMETERIZED TRAVERSALS (strategy as prototype): lib xsd_emit.spi 2311-2349, the 'reading' instance set of the emitter prototypes (derived_prefix, record_texts, choice_part, group_part, element_part, attribute_step, element_arm, group_arm, choice_text); one schema walk serves reading and writing, and a new direction is one instance set with no new walk. xml_codec.spi 126-128 shows the codec form (of_xml/to_xml per nominal). Adopter: Cells' document conversions (spi <-> livemd <-> dib) as one walk with direction instances, and x's typed_codec.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_13 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 13u32, "2026-11-09T17:17Z", "tibia", "11-09 13:2x tibia (MEGA-PROTOTYPES-FROM-REPOS): PROTOTYPE DIMENSION from the repos. Census: prototypes are declared as `:? name t : sig` and instantiated as `:+ name t = ...`; lib/spiral has 43 declarations / 143 instances; outside lib, regex 1, fiscal 11, tibia 8. BEST USES, in the guide form (pattern -> sample -> adopter): (1) PER-SUBSYSTEM PURPOSE KEYS. lib keyed_rng.spi:18 `:? purpose_code p : p -> u32` with `:== roll_key p` and `draw \\* p {purpose_code}`. 6 instances in 2 repos: tibia combat.spi:14 (clearest: combat_roll = StatsDraw|ArrowShot|ArrowFire), hunting_pack.spi:79, network.spi:220, search.spi:15, hunt_sim.spi:51; regex agreement.spi:18. Beats a function: each module owns its own purpose union, so there is no global enum that every subsystem edits, and adding a combat purpose cannot renumber search's draws (streams stay reproducible per module). Adopter: any seeded generator that today passes a raw salt, e.g. regex agreement's corpus draws already do; fiscal synthetic fixtures next. (2) CAPABILITY PER JURISDICTION. fiscal lifecycle.spi:22-24 `:? cancellation_event r : r -> event_code` (also correction_event, response_event) over the phantom tags nfe/cfdi/hacienda/dian (:! aliases, :9-13); instances :26-42 exist only where the jurisdiction supports the event. Beats a function: a match over a country enum would compile cancel-for-dian and fail at runtime; with the prototype, the missing instance is a COMPILE error. Adopter: lib's backend arms that end in a runtime `no_arm` (e.g. file_system read_text_or_empty `| _ => no_arm`): a per-backend capability prototype would turn unsupported targets into type errors. (3) LOSSLESS VS LOSSY EVIDENCE. fiscal document.spi:68-71 `:+ content exact` / `content with_losses` / `:+ losses exact = \\ _ => []` / `losses with_losses = ... lost`. Beats a function: whether a conversion lost data is in the TYPE, so `losses exact` is statically empty and callers of a with_losses value cannot ignore the loss list. Adopter: REGEX-LIB-4's regex_xsd example_of vs lib xsd_regex example_with (2 known divergences), which could return `with_losses` naming the alt-order / negated-class cases. Runner-up: tibia network.spi:262 `:? judge f` (2 instances: heal_judge, lanes pf1), a fitness function per genome type; fine but a plain function argument would do.")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_14 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 14u32, "2026-11-09T17:22Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:3x compiler: the DETERMINISTIC MEASURE already exists in the compiler: SPIRAL_FEATURES_OUT=<tsv> with --check counts typed-modeling features per module at parse time (keys feature-existential, feature-gadt-case, feature-hkt, feature-prototype, feature-instance, feature-phantom; pack75+, so pack77 on ganymede/europa). Baseline on the 5 mega samples (main module only, installed pack77, all exit 0), per 1,000 lines: zeta (7,597) existential 35.4, gadt-case 24.7, phantom 0.4 | spiral_proves (11,834) existential 25.9, gadt-case 16.1, phantom 0.3 | lean_cic (9,978) existential 17.7, gadt-case 21.2 | omniledger_erp (12,080) existential 9.4, gadt-case 13.3, phantom 0.2 | brzozowski (3,580) existential 4.5, gadt-case 3.9, hkt 1.4, instance 1.1, prototype 0.6, phantom 0.6. Reading: the megas model with GADT cases + existentials (10-60 per kloc); prototypes/instances/HKT appear only in brzozowski, so the PROTOTYPE dimension (microblogs and tibias notes) has to come from lib, not the megas. A slop score for new code = these densities vs the mega band, from one --check per module; F#-like code scores ~0 on every key. Run: SPIRAL_FEATURES_OUT=out.tsv dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --check <file.spi> (rows: path, key, count).")
inl note_of_mega_samples_as_models_15 () = Note ("MEGA-SAMPLES-AS-MODELS", 15u32, "2026-11-09T18:36Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 18:4x THE GUIDE (assembled from fiscal 11:43, microblog's three reads 17:02-17:06, and the prototype census; samples = spiral/apps/compiler/tmp/samples/mega_*/main.spi). Pattern -> clearest instance -> adopted by / should adopt:\nSTATE AND PROTOCOL\n1. Typestate over phantom stages + a GADT plan with an existential middle stage -> omniledger erp_op chain -> fiscal src/p2p.spi (purchase s, p2p_plan).\n2. Typestate environments (scope as a type) -> lean_cic 1648-1666 -> any checker with scoped bindings (Cells verdicts, tibia sim scopes).\n3. Linearity as a type (use-once resources) -> spiral_proves_spiral 117-140, 568-590 -> leases, claims, one-shot tokens (kit agile claims, REALWORLD-3 auth tokens).\nINDEXED DATA\n4. Indexed amounts + a route GADT whose Via case hides the middle currency -> omniledger -> lib money.spi (Direct/Via).\n5. Type-level indexed data (sizes/arity in the type) -> zeta 12-23 -> regex typed.spi sized_dfa (REGEX-ABSORB-DFA-CARDINALITY, tibia today), lib fin_vector.\n6. Intrinsically typed syntax (terms indexed by their type) -> lean_cic 205-230 -> REALWORLD-1 route GADT indexed by response type; SPIRAL-UI-3 view GADT by element kind.\nEVIDENCE AND DECISIONS\n7. Evidence GADT indexed by the answer (nullability) -> brzozowski -> lib regex_nullable / regex_derivative, under lib regex_xsd.\n8. Certificate + decision pairs (a verdict that carries its proof) -> lean_cic 37/120/140 -> lib regex_certificate (distinguishing words), regex witness.spi quotient_certificate.\n9. Relations as GADTs -> zeta 34-60 -> partition/equivalence models (regex minimal blocks, fiscal canon relations).\n10. Synthesize-and-certify existentials + proof-carrying decisions -> zeta 324-383 -> agile verdicts with evidence (kit gate, Cells verdicts).\n11. Structural rules / reduction as typed transforms and derivations -> spiral_proves_spiral 647/1695/4935, 6239/8000/8803 -> rewriting passes (formatter respell, regex similarity normalization).\nLOGS\n12. Idempotent event log with typed append outcomes + membership evidence + checksummed frames -> omniledger R6/R7/R9 -> lib event_log.spi.\nPROTOTYPES (from repos; the samples have 0-4 instances)\n13. Form-indexed walker: one generic walk against prototypes, one instance set per form -> lib xsd_emit.spi 2186-2212 (14 prototypes x reading/staged_reading/writing/staged_writing...) ; 14. open purpose enum -> lib keyed_rng.spi:18 purpose_code ; 15. host abstraction -> lib leptos.spi:134 signal_get.\nDoD: guide written (this note); >= 2 new modules cite a sample (p2p, money, event_log from omniledger; regex_nullable/derivative from brzozowski; typed.spi sized_dfa from zeta). Proof: the cited notes + file:lines.")
