inl note_of_zeta_py_retire_1 () = Note ("ZETA-PY-RETIRE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T12:14Z", "regex", "regex 08:1x FOLDED into ZETA-NUMERIC-CORE (user 11-02 asked for one final zeta; parent ZETA-FINAL). Research found the polyglot tests are FALSE PASSES: math.spi zeta__ (lines 255-266) returns the mpmath value, so the 12 tests never test the Spiral code, and the reflection formula uses pi^s instead of pi^(s-1). Close this item when ZETA-NUMERIC-CORE removes pyo3/mpmath.")
inl note_of_zeta_py_retire_2 () = Note ("ZETA-PY-RETIRE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:35Z", "regex", "regex 10:1x CORRECTS n1: the mpmath scaffolding is a porting harness (sys.settrace call logs + side-by-side Spiral/mpmath values), useful for the port and for golden-vector generation; see ZETA-NUMERIC-CORE n2. \"Delete the py code\" now means: pyo3/mpmath leave the library build once the Spiral value passes every oracle check; the harness survives as a CI-only oracle job.")
inl note_of_zeta_py_retire_3 () = Note ("ZETA-PY-RETIRE", 3u32, "2026-11-07T22:19Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: the Spiral zeta rewrite matches the Python implementation on its test values to a stated tolerance, and the .py code is deleted. Next step: list the Python functions still without a Spiral counterpart.")
inl backend_switch_prototypes () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", "Study replacing the record-based backend_switch (copied from an old upstream sample; union variant added later) with a prototype-based system over !!!!BackendSwitch; in the same study scan lib and products for other places prototypes/nominals model better (user 2026-10-24 06:56 review list)", Story, P1, D6, 40u32, Paused, "", "spiral,prototypes,lib,backends,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_backend_switch_prototypes () = Planning ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", Minutes 720u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 1u32, "2026-10-30T23:08Z", "main", "10-30 19:08 main: step 1 CENSUS (read-only, today's lib). Dispatch forms: the RECORD form 'backend_switch { Rust = ...; Gleam = ... }' at 243 sites; the UNION form 'run_target \\| | Rust => ...' at 132, plus run_target_args at 43; TYPE-LEVEL 'backend_switch (<record of types>)' at 45. Heaviest: sm' (33 record / 19 union / 6 args), date_time (29 / 9 / 1, plus 13 type-level), base (23 / 4), file_system (0 / 30 / 19), am' (13 / 9), near (13 / 0, plus 4 type-level), resultm (11 / 1). How the record form dispatches (backend.spi:1-42): '!!!!BackendSwitch' over the record does a compile-time record_type_try_find by backend key, and a MISSING key emits a per-backend fallback body: Nil (F#), print (Python), () (Rust 'no Rust arm' comment), None, nullptr (C/Cpp), compile_error! (Rust), @compileError (Zig), an unbound identifier (Lean, C), and throw/undefined (TypeScript). That fallback is exactly the 'Spiral passes, cargo fails' trap (memory backend-switch-idiom): a missing arm is found only by the target toolchain, never by Spiral's own type check. STUDY QUESTION for step 2: can a prototype ('prototype backend_impl t' with one instance per backend tag type) make a missing arm a SPIRAL-time error, and are the per-site generated lines no worse than the record form's? NEXT (CPU-light, then one compile): draft both forms of one small function (e.g. sm'.to_lower: record + union arms) in a scratch module, compile to Rust and F#, and compare the generated lines and the missing-arm diagnostics.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 2u32, "2026-10-30T23:12Z", "main", "10-30 19:1x main: step 2 READ (compiler + lib, before any compile). (1) The compiler op already makes a missing arm a SPIRAL-time error: spiral_compiler.fs:155517 'Cannot find the backend X in the backend switch op'. The 'Spiral passes, cargo fails' trap is introduced by the lib wrapper backend.spi:1-42, which catches the missing key with record_type_try_find and emits per-target placeholders. (2) A prototype keyed on a per-backend tag nominal cannot be built on today's ops: BackendSwitch checks every arm for the SAME type (spiral_compiler.fs:155451, 'The backend switch needs to have the same type for all of its branches'), so 'current_backend ()' returning rust_backend/fsharp_backend fails; UnsafeBackendSwitch skips that check but its own comment forbids type-level use. A prototype form therefore needs a NEW compiler op (a backend tag type) = compiler-lane work, not a lib change. (3) The fallback is load-bearing for unit-typed sites: date_time's 45 type-level calls 'backend_switch `(()) `({}) { Gleam = ... }' rely on a missing arm being a silent () (Gleam-only imports). The non-unit fallbacks split into target-compile errors (Rust compile_error!, Zig @compileError, Lean/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend/Kotlin/Swift unbound identifiers) and RUNTIME-silent ones (TypeScript throw at run time, Python None, Gleam Nil, F# (), Cpp nullptr, Lua print). REVISED STUDY: the cheap win is not prototypes but a strict lib wrapper: missing arm for t = () stays a no-op; missing arm for a non-unit t becomes real_core.error_type at Spiral time. NEXT: measure the blast radius on a scratch lib (backend.spi strict variant) by compiling lib_gate over the heavy files (sm', date_time, base, file_system, am') for every backend lib_gate runs; each new error is a reached missing arm that today fails late or silently. Prototype form is parked behind a compiler op proposal (backend tag type), to be filed for the compiler team only if the strict wrapper leaves a gap.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_3 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 3u32, "2026-10-31T00:49Z", "main", "10-30 20:49 main: strict-blast RUN 1 is VOID. The sm' base arm passed 72/72 (2,372 s), but the strict arm's 0/72 came from MY wrapper: backend.spi:66 ETY-UnboundVariable '++' (backend.spi does not import the string operators), so nothing measured a missing arm. Run 1's record is kept as backend-switch\\strict-blast-run1-void.txt. Lesson: I skipped the smoke step. Fix: the error is now the constant real_core.error_type 'backend_switch: no arm for the current backend', and the script runs type_probe on its first file against the strict lib and ABORTS if backend.spi has an error, before any 40-min gate. RELAUNCHED as kit job job-1793407396434-main-backend-switch-strict (--compile, 6 h cap; 5 files x 2 arms at ~40 min each under load) so it outlives this session's shells; results are in backend-switch\\strict-blast.txt (MISSING lists file:line of reached missing arms).")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_4 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 4u32, "2026-10-31T00:55Z", "main", "10-30 20:55 main: the job id changed. The first relaunch used kit job start --compile, which holds a compile window for the job's whole 6 h life; compile and compile-hold clash both ways, so every lane's timing slot would have waited behind it. It was stopped after 11 min (scratch-only, so safe) and restarted as job-1793408079555-main-backend-switch-strict, gate=plain, with no window. Results are still in backend-switch\\strict-blast.txt.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_5 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 5u32, "2026-10-31T01:53Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:53 compiler: for overlap only, no action asked. SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD n6-n7 drafts the reached-only variant in the core (packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1): lib's NON-unit fallback markers raise a Spiral type error with the call chain when the BUILT backend's path emits them; validation arms and unit no-ops are untouched. Your strict-blast measures the stricter every-build variant; the two answer different questions, and the guard's predict run will list the reached gaps on the matrix corpus.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_6 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 6u32, "2026-10-31T02:38Z", "main", "10-30 22:38 main: strict-blast job STOPPED (job-1793408079555, after 103 min) to cut box load; nothing beyond the smoke was recorded (strict-blast.txt). Rerun on a quieter box or on the node (scratch libs only).")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_7 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 7u32, "2026-10-31T12:45Z", "main", "10-31 08:45 main: the strict-blast draft (C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1030\\backend-switch: strict-blast.ps1, n2.txt and the two run records) is archived as %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-084402 (C:\\home\\data is not a working folder; the hook blocks new files there). The script is STALE regardless: Set-Strict anchors on backend.spi lines 7/8/63, and heading-cleanup batch 1 changed backend.spi at 06:43 (AAE30048E6C1). To rerun on the node: rebuild Set-Strict on a content anchor (the '!!!!BackendSwitch({' line and its closing '})') as a repo tool beside the other apps/cells/spi provers, not a per-experiment script.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_8 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 8u32, "2026-10-31T15:14Z", "main", "10-31 11:14 main: strict-blast REBUILT as a repo tool: apps/cells/spi/backend_switch_strict_prove.ps1 (-Files comma list, -LiveLib, -Work, -Report). Set-Strict finds the fallback switch by CONTENT (a '\\ () =>' line followed by '!!!!BackendSwitch({', closed by '})' at the same indent) and wraps it so a missing non-unit arm becomes a Spiral-time error; checked BYTE-IDENTICAL to the archived line-anchored transform on today's backend.spi (lines 8-63). No BelowNormal. Base vs strict lib_gate per file; MISSING lists the file:line of every record without an arm. QUEUED on the node: job-1793459618516-main-strict-blast, after the F# measurement (job-1793458299079), report C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\strict-blast.txt.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_9 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 9u32, "2026-10-31T17:49Z", "main", "10-31 13:49 main: strict-blast RAN on the node (job-1793459618516, report C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\strict-blast.txt) and is VOID as a missing-arm census. The strict lib type-probes clean (smoke: sm'.spi PASS, 71 tests typed), but nearly every test FAILS at run on its first target: sm' 0/72 (base arm 72/72), date_time 0/43 (43/44), base 0/25 (25/26), file_system 0/35 (33/35), am' 1/18 (18/18); type errors 0 and no 'no arm for the current backend' MISSING lines. A wrapper that only fired on missing arms would fail a few tests, not all, so the '?: t ?| | () => ... | _ => real_core.error_type' wrap fires on present arms too: the fallback block it wraps is evaluated (or its error_type is reached) whenever t is not unit, not only when the record lacks the backend key. NEXT, before any rerun: run one strict gate with -RawOut (lib_gate -RawOut) to read the cell error text, and move the strict check to where a missing key is actually known (inside record_type_try_find's not-found branch, keyed on the backend name) rather than wrapping the fallback switch by t's shape.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_10 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 10u32, "2026-10-31T18:26Z", "main", "10-31 14:26 main: the MISSING-ARM CENSUS strict-blast could not give came from FSHARP-OUTSIDE-COMPILER's add-rust node run (a '///> rust' target added to 78 F#-only tests): the compiler itself reports 'backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no Rust arm yet' for sm' format_debug / format_debug_2, optionm' of_obj, resultm from_try_keeps_values_and_catches_failures and seq new_seq. That is a direct, per-test list of records missing a Rust arm (raw output: node C:\\home\\remote\\main-manifest\\add-rust\\raw\\<file>.spi). The same method (add a target, gate, grep that message) works per backend, with no strict wrapper needed.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_11 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 11u32, "2026-10-31T18:27Z", "main", "10-31 14:27 main: correction to n10's list: optionm'.of_obj HAS a Rust arm (optionm'.spi:203, 'Some(!x.clone())'); its test starts from 'null ()', and null has no Rust form, so the 'no Rust arm' message there comes from the null helper the test calls, not from of_obj. Read each remaining entry the same way (which backend_switch actually fired) before treating it as a missing arm: the compiler message names no function, so the raw notebook's failing cell needs a look per test.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_12 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 12u32, "2026-10-31T18:28Z", "main", "10-31 14:28 main: ROOT CAUSE of the void strict-blast, read from backend.spi:1-32: the fallback switch ALREADY is strict for the native backends. When a record lacks the backend key, Rust emits compile_error!('backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no Rust arm yet') unless t is unit (backend.spi:19-23), Zig emits @compileError(...) likewise (:26-29), Lean similar; only the scripting backends get a silent placeholder. So strict-blast's extra '?: t ?|' wrapper around the whole fallback switch was redundant and broke every switch (it is not needed and should not be rerun). The census method is the one used today: add a target, gate, grep the compile_error text. Limitation: the message names no function (backend_switch has no caller identity), so each hit needs its failing cell read; the Rust compile_error points at a main.rs line whose surrounding generated code identifies the function. strict-blast tool (apps/cells/spi/backend_switch_strict_prove.ps1) is therefore obsolete; archive it with the next cleanup.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_13 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 13u32, "2026-11-01T03:40Z", "main", "main 23:4x WIP groom (user 23:3x: drift/stall, too much waiting): Active -> Planned. study concluded that the fallback is already strict for the native backends (n12); next: write up the prototype-based design as a decision note.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_14 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 14u32, "2026-11-07T22:09Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: a written decision note comparing the record-based backend_switch with a prototype-based one over !!!!BackendSwitch, on 3 real call sites (cost, error messages, generated code size), ending in adopt or reject. TypeFeatures: the backend set as a prototype instance per backend, so a missing case is a compile error.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_15 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 15u32, "2026-11-09T17:20Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: this is a 720m D6 STUDY, not a light pull. Its own step-2 read (note 2) established the prototype form CANNOT be built on today's ops: BackendSwitch requires all arms the same type (spiral_compiler.fs:155451) and UnsafeBackendSwitch forbids type-level use, so a prototype keyed on a per-backend tag nominal needs a NEW COMPILER OP (a backend-tag type) = compiler-lane work. The cheaper alt (a strict lib wrapper making a non-unit missing arm a Spiral-time error) is measured by a heavy multi-file blast gate (hours per backend), which is itself not a light-round task. Parking the titular prototype study on lane:compiler (the new backend-tag op); the strict-wrapper measurement is main's heavy-gate thread.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_16 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 16u32, "2026-11-09T17:24Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:3x compiler ANSWER to the lane:compiler wait (the new backend-tag op): REJECT the op. Reason (structural, not cost): prototype instances are resolved during type inference, and inference is backend-INDEPENDENT by design: one --check, the language server and every backend share one typed program (memory: --check = parse+infer). A prototype keyed on a backend-tag type needs the target backend known at inference time, so it would either break backend-agnostic checking (the LSP and --check would have no backend to pick an instance with) or need one inference per backend (Nx check cost, and editor diagnostics that change with the selected target). What already exists gives the typed, zero-cost part: lib backend.spi has a real union (:| backend = Gleam | Lua | ... | Ebpf, 19 cases), and run_target / run_target_args pass a LITERAL constructor per backend_switch arm (:= target = Rust), so partial evaluation picks the callers match arm at compile time with no runtime dispatch (e.g. sm.trims Rust arm, 98 -> 17 lines). Remaining gap = \"a missing case is a compile error\": callers that write | _ => opt out of it. Cheaper paths than a new op: (a) a census of run_target matches that use a wildcard (SPIRAL_FEATURES_OUT-style key, or a kit standards rule), so new code enumerates backends explicitly; (b) the strict lib wrapper main already measures in its heavy-gate thread. Clearing the lane:compiler wait; the study can close as reject-the-op + (a) or (b) at orgs choice.")
inl note_of_backend_switch_prototypes_17 () = Note ("BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES", 17u32, "2026-11-09T17:58Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: compiler ANSWERED (note 16, 17:24Z) - the backend-tag op is REJECTED: prototype instances resolve during backend-INDEPENDENT type inference (one --check / LSP / all backends share one typed program), so a backend-keyed prototype would break backend-agnostic checking or force one inference per backend. So the titular prototype-based backend_switch is NOT viable; the zero-cost typed path already exists (backend.spi union + run_target per-arm literal constructor, partial-eval-resolved). PROTOTYPE STUDY CLOSED on that verdict.\nRemaining value = closing the `| _ =>` opt-out gap. Census caveat (org measured): a raw grep of `| _ =>` in lib is NOISE (688 across 71 files, almost all normal match arms); the backend_switch subset needs per-site context (a `| _ =>` that is the fallback of a run_target/backend_switch block), so the precise wildcard census is real per-site analysis, not a grep. The actual fix (main's strict-wrapper making a non-unit missing arm a Spiral-time error) is a HEAVY multi-file blast gate (hours per backend, 5 files x every backend). Re-tagging load=heavy: the remaining work is not a light-round task. Owner org; schedule as a heavy blast or a kit-standards per-site rule.")
