inl note_of_x_hand_units_20 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 20u32, "2026-10-30T02:00Z", "x", "2026-10-30T02:00Z groom: its dep GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES has been Done since 10-28T12:52Z, so x's backlog tables listing 'blocked on GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES' were stale. This is ready heavy work (Active 60). The next slice is CPU-light drafting on the box plus one node compile window; it queues behind SIM-IN-SPIRAL's x-s3 relaunch and verify33 for x's node slot. X-PARALLEL-CENSUS stays Blocked on this item.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_21 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 21u32, "2026-10-30T02:30Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:3x x: RESUME CONDITION (n13/n18) half met. Pack 7 is installed (22:24; core 9C899B30495F7DC5) and carries apply-gleam-compact-arms (PACK-7 install line; GLEAM-COMPACT-OUTPUT n8). Re-measure launched: x-g2-events-p7 (n12's exact script, now launch-x-g2-events.ps1, freshly synced xunits mirror, compiler pinned to pack 7). PRE-REGISTERED before the result: n12 baseline 2,054 lines; n13 predicts about 1,790 with compaction alone (-12.7%). The BEAM decoder lever (fields_of_line through OTP json:decode plus an erlang_term walk, in a backend_switch so Rust is unchanged) removed about 800 pre-compact lines, about 700 after compaction. Rule: with R = the compact-only line count, if R - 700 <= 950 (R <= 1,650), draft the decoder arm and measure it. If the measured unit is <= 950, run step 2 (invariants importing events) as n11 registered. If R > 1,650, the unit stays parked until a byte-exact native encoder is shown (n13 condition 2).")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_22 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 22u32, "2026-10-30T02:41Z", "x", "2026-10-29 22:4x x: G2 re-measure on pack 7 VERDICT, by n21's rule: STAYS PARKED. x-g2-events-p7 (job-1793328085561, 84 s, fresh xunits mirror, pinned core 9C899B30495F7DC5): the events unit is 1,858 lines (n12 on 6b: 2,054; -9.5%, below n13's -12.7% estimate), 43 fns, 15 pub types; largest string_from 305 (was 347), encoded 276 (280), fields_from 249 (279), decoded 158, without_controls 143. 154 brace-bodied case arms remain (multi-statement arms, outside compact-arms' scope). R = 1,858 > 1,650, so even with the BEAM decoder lever (about -700) the unit lands near 1,160 > 950. Remaining resume condition: n13 (2), a byte-exact native encoder, or a further Gleam size lever from the compiler team. Output kept at node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\units-g2-p7\\xsim_events.gleam. The Rust half is unchanged (xc 960 accepted, n17).")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_23 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 23u32, "2026-10-30T14:12Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:2x x groom R2/R3 (BACKLOG-DANGLING-1030), after healing's all-clear: X-HAND-UNITS is flagged DEPS-DONE but stays Paused ON PURPOSE, by n21's pre-registered rule (G2 events unit 1,858 lines > 950 on pack 7), not for want of a picker. Owner: x (tag x; agile next --team x). Resume trigger: a native JSON encoder or another Gleam size lever that brings the events unit <= 950. The G1 Elixir routing draft came back from the wipe undelete intact: C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\gleam-units\\spiral-ex.do (D039DC4C74EA) + checks/flow/world/frames gleam.toml, so on resume G1 is a re-apply, not a rewrite. R1 applied: X-CLIENT, SOCIAL-PROFILES-LOCAL, X-REPO-TEMPLATE tagged user-batch, X-REPO-TEMPLATE assignee user. R3: no x Active item is silent > 3 d. R4: no x item waits on a rule-answerable user decision.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_24 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 24u32, "2026-10-30T14:19Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:5x x: NEW GLEAM SIZE LEVER (G3), x-side, not a compiler change. Reading n22's output (node units-g2-p7\\xsim_events.gleam) shows three source-level expansions in apps/sim/spi/events.spi: (1) without_controls folds over listm::init 32i32, so the partial evaluator unrolls 32 string.replace steps (143 lines); (2) := field is inlined at about 40 sites (57 quoted_23 calls in encoded_22, 276 lines); (3) := hex_at is inlined at code_at's 4 sites, about 33 lines each, inside string_from (305). Draft C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\g3\\stage\\events.spi (live FB6FBA33001DFBDB + 3 edits): controls_from (a runtime recursion over 0..31, the same escapes in the same order), :~ field, :~ hex_at. Bytes are unchanged by construction; the file's own tests (frames_encode_like_the_fixture, bodies_escape_quotes_and_lines, fixture_lines_keep_their_escapes, streams_decode_back_to_themselves) gate any land. PRE-REGISTERED before the result: launch-x-g3-events.ps1 (xunits, pack 7) measures base and g3 in the same job. Let D = (base string_from) - (g3 string_from + hex_at); the BEAM decoder lever then removes about 700 - D. Projected P = g3 lines - (700 - D). If P <= 950, draft the decoder arm (n21 step) and measure it; if P > 950, the unit stays parked, with the lever recorded.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_25 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 25u32, "2026-10-30T14:26Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:5x x: G3 VERDICT by n24's rule: STAYS PARKED. x-g3-events (job-1793370237487, 88 s, xunits, pack 7, base and g3 in one job): base events unit 1,917 lines (lib drift since n22's 1,858: +59, 56 fns vs 43); g3 1,728 (-189, -9.9%). Per function: without_controls 143 -> controls_from 104 (now an honest runtime loop; the rest is the short_escape if-chain), encoded 276 -> 201, string_from + hex_at 305 -> 219. x-compat 0 finding(s) in 29 file(s). D = 86, so the decoder lever is about 614 and P = 1,728 - 614 = 1,114 > 950. The lever stays recorded; the remaining gap is 164 lines. The next candidates (short_escape as a table slice, about -40) cannot close it alone. G3 still pays on its own (-10% events output for every consumer, Rust too), so it lands IF its tests pass. PRE-REGISTERED: x-g3-tests (launch-x-g3-tests.ps1, xunits) runs build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests on events + xsim_events/xsim_events with the g3 file. Pass = 'all 2 notebooks passed' and per-notebook test rows present. Pass -> apply to live x/apps/sim/spi/events.spi (unstaged, base hash FB6FBA33001DFBDB checked).")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_26 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 26u32, "2026-10-30T14:55Z", "x", "2026-10-30 10:5x x: G3 LANDED by n25's rule. x-g3-tests (job-1793370656771, 20 min, xunits, pack 7, build.ps1's Invoke-SpiralTests path with its own MIX_BUILD_PATH): events.spi discovered 8, executed 8, passed 8, failed 0, across its targets; 'all 2 notebooks passed'. Deviation, stated: xsim_events/xsim_events reports 0 rows because it holds only a static test marker. Its consumer check is the Gleam compile, already proven in job-1793370237487. Live x/apps/sim/spi/events.spi: base FB6FBA33001DFBDB checked, now DA2FFC3787B6E2DA (+6/-4 lines: controls_from, :~ field, :~ hex_at; unstaged). The rest of the file's diff against HEAD was already there before. Backup at C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\g3\\events.pre-g3.spi. Considered and REJECTED: the same unroll in client prelude.spi (digit_first over listm::init 10, without_digits). Only locate (804) and checks (310) emit digits_end/count_of_text, both far from the gate; the events copy is on the decode path the BEAM lever removes; and editing prelude.spi churns the sim fixtures. The unit stays parked (P = 1,114 > 950).")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_27 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 27u32, "2026-10-30T15:08Z", "x", "2026-10-30 11:2x x: resume path made concrete. Remaining gap after G3 + the BEAM decoder lever: 164 lines (P 1,114 vs 950). Read off the G3 output: x-side source levers come to about 80 lines (short_escape as a table slice about 40, typed text/number field helpers about 16, :~ parent_of about 12, a post_at helper in decoded about 8), which cannot close it alone. The compiler side has about 180 lines of binding noise (132 one-use literal/constructor lets plus 51 tail lets), filed as GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (compiler team, P2) and now a dep. RESUME RULE: when GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE is installed, re-run launch-x-g3-events.ps1 on the new pack; if P - 80 <= 950, draft the x-side levers and the BEAM decoder arm, in that order.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_28 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 28u32, "2026-10-30T16:00Z", "x", "2026-10-30 12:0x x: G4 x-side levers DRAFTED early (CPU-light; n27's gate governs measuring for the decision and landing, not drafting). C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\g4\\stage\\events.spi = live G3 (DA2FFC3787B6E2DA) + short_escape as one table slice (\"\\\\\" + slice (c-8) of \"btn_fr\", c in 8..13 except 11, so 11 still falls through to \\u000b) + :~ text_field / :~ number_field (12 field sites rewritten, 0 raw 'field k (quoted|number ...)' left) + :~ parent_of. Launchers launch-x-g4-events.ps1 (base = live G3, lever base 614) and launch-x-g4-tests.ps1. An information-only measurement runs now on PACK 8 (x-g4-events, xunits, core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52): it gives pack 8's base for the G3 unit and the real size of the x-side levers against n27's estimate of about 80. No land until GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE and n27's rule.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_29 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 29u32, "2026-10-30T16:05Z", "x", "2026-10-30 12:0x x: G4 information measurement (x-g4-events, job-1793376241654, 64 s, xunits, PACK 8 core 17DDB2DCEF2A2D52). Pack 8's base for the live G3 unit is 1,728 lines, identical to pack 7, so pack 8 does not change this unit's Gleam size. G4 = 1,682 (-46, not n27's estimated ~80): encoded 201 -> 155, controls_from 104 -> 87; the 3 new helper functions take back part of it. x-compat 0 findings in 29 files. Projected with the BEAM decoder: 1,682 - 614 = 1,068, a gap of 118. Corrected resume rule (supersedes n27's 'P - 80'): when GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE is installed, re-run launch-x-g4-events.ps1; if its projected value is <= 950, run launch-x-g4-tests.ps1, land G4, then draft the BEAM decoder arm. GLEAM-INLINE's ~180 measured lines cover the 118 gap with room to spare.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_30 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 30u32, "2026-10-30T16:37Z", "x", "2026-10-30 12:3x x: Rust-half drift, from x-jsondyn-ab's base arm (job-1793376758171, pack 8 + today's lib): xc 975 (n17 accepted 960, +15), refs 901, digest 954, users 879; all under the 1,000 gate. xc keeps 25 lines of gate headroom; recheck per regen, and split xc if any change must grow it past 1,000.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_31 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 31u32, "2026-10-31T12:29Z", "x", "2026-10-31 08:29 x: G1's Elixir routing draft is OBSOLETE: C:\\home\\data\\x-drafts\\gleam-units\\spiral-ex.do is one anchored replace in apps/sim/lib/xsim/spiral.ex (an @units map routing checked_text/commute_text/breaking_text/masked_text to :xsim_checks, world_files to :xsim_world, default :xsim_flow), and SIM-IN-SPIRAL's S5 removed spiral.ex with the whole mix project (n145). With no Elixir left, unit routing is the launcher's code path: scripts/sim-run.ps1 puts every frames-build ebin on -pa and calls one export per command. If units return, route by export placement in the Gleam packages, not by an Elixir map. The unit gleam.toml files in the same folder (checks/flow/world, frames-gleam.toml) stay with g4/jsondyn as this item's held inputs.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_32 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 32u32, "2026-10-31T13:38Z", "x", "2026-10-31 09:38 x: held drafts MOVED out of C:\\home\\data (DATA-DIR-HOME): g4 (stage\\events.spi + launch-x-g4-events.ps1 + launch-x-g4-tests.ps1), jsondyn (stage\\wire.spi + launcher) and gleam-units (unit gleam.toml files; spiral-ex.do obsolete, n31) are in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-093702. n29's RESUME RULE now starts with: scratch-archive.ps1 -Restore %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\20261031-093702\\manifest.tsv -RestoreTo <a scratch dir>, then rebase g4's stage onto the live events.spi (it was drafted on 10-30's G3 base) and re-run its launcher through node-proof.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_33 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 33u32, "2026-11-01T03:37Z", "x", "2026-10-31 23:37 x groom (user 23:33, Paused P1 re-decision): stays PAUSED. Reason: its remaining dep GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE (compiler codegen) is Planned at 0%; GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES is Done. Resume: that dep lands, OR any generated x Gleam unit comes within 100 lines of gleam-modules' 1,000-line part limit (today the sim parts pass after x-parity-typed2). On resume, n29's restore-and-rebase rule applies.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_34 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 34u32, "2026-11-01T06:46Z", "x", "2026-11-01 02:46 x: RESUME CONDITION FIRED (n33: 'any generated x Gleam unit within 100 lines of the 1,000-line part limit'). x-parity-metrics1 measured xsim_frames' largest part at 954 lines and xsim_split's at 945 (x_gleam_part_lines_max, X-RUN-METRICS). Paused -> Planned so agile next offers it. One concrete prompt: tonight's sim_serve and sim_run additions grew xsim_frames. On resume, n29's restore-and-rebase rule applies. GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT (filed 02:34) is the codegen-side alternative to hand-modeling and is weighed first, since it would move the part limit into the compiler.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_35 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 35u32, "2026-11-01T12:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 08:35 x: ITEM UPGRADE (round 4). Fields: tags gain team:x. Deps: GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES Done, GLEAM-INLINE-SINGLE-USE Planned. GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT (filed 02:34) is the compiler-side alternative and is decided FIRST (rule below). The estimate of 3000 / heavy stands for the hand-modeled path only.\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable): x_gleam_part_lines_max <= 850 for both xsim_split and xsim_frames (headroom >= 150 under the 1,000 gate; today 945 / 954), on two consecutive node-run parity runs, with sim notebooks green and parity 153/153, 0 differing. Artifact: the two runs' x_ rows on this item.\nDECISION RULE between the two paths, written now: if GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT gets an owner and an ETA within 3 days, x waits for it (it removes a text pass and helps every lane). Otherwise x takes the cheapest hand lever first: move tonight's growth (sim_serve's caller dispatch, sim_run's clock and metric rows) into their own Gleam unit by package placement (n29's restore-and-rebase), then measure.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: ask the compiler/main owner on GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT for owner + ETA (a note, no message unless they must act). Meanwhile list which functions sit in xsim_frames' two largest parts: the part files in apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/partNN.gleam from the next landed regen.\nTYPEFEATURESET PLAN: splitting must not degrade types at unit boundaries. Cross-unit calls pass the existing typed values (caller c GADT, api_world record, run_shape, metric u), NEVER a joined string or int code invented for the boundary. Gleam exports keep union and record types (GLEAM-EXPORT-TYPES), so no unit re-encodes a union as text. A unit is a Spiral package with its own spiral.toml module list (memory hand-modeled-build-units: never auto-split by line count in Spiral source). The model is eoie's sibling crates.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_36 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 36u32, "2026-11-01T14:34Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:34 x: SPLIT BENCHMARK PLAN (user 10:3x: x is big enough to generate many parts; the user suspects quality is better if the .spi files control the split, like Rust, even if iteration slows). Today: the sim compiles TWO Spiral entries to Gleam (xsim_split, xsim_frames; sim build.ps1:34-37), and gleam-modules.ps1 cuts each generated module by call-graph SCCs into parts. xsim_frames = 37 parts, largest 954 lines. So x is still on the post-hoc splitter. The 'unit' folders in spiral.toml (xsim_checks/, xsim_world/, ...) are module groups compiled INTO those two entries, not separate Gleam packages.\nWHAT IS MEASURED, on ONE real change: adding one metric row to sim_report.spi (a function plus a test, the size of tonight's raw-pair change).\n- A (post-hoc split): BuildSpiral simframes -> one .gleam -> gleam-modules.ps1 -> gleam build. Measure wall per stage.\n- B (.spi-controlled units): sim_report's group becomes its own Spiral entry compiled to its own Gleam package (hand-modeled, memory hand-modeled-build-units), so only that unit regenerates and rebuilds.\nNumbers per path, on the node, same core, 3 rounds alternating A/B, with an in-job A/A:\n(1) iteration wall seconds, change saved -> sim build green;\n(2) generated-diff churn: lines changed across generated .gleam files for this one source change;\n(3) largest generated file.\nWHY (2) carries the quality argument: gleam-modules packs SCCs in order, so one new function can shift which part holds what and rewrite many parts. Hand units keep stable boundaries.\nPRE-REGISTERED DECISION: if B's churn <= 25% of A's AND B's largest file <= 850 lines, B (the .spi-controlled split) is adopted for xsim_frames even if B's iteration is up to 1.5x slower (the user's quality preference). If B is also faster, adopt without question. If B's churn isn't lower, A stays and GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT moves the split into the compiler.\nNEXT STEP: measure A's churn from history right away (git log -p on apps/sim/frames/src/xsim_frames/ for tonight's raw-pair change: how many part files changed for a ~20-line source edit), then prototype B by moving sim_report + runner_checks into a third Gleam entry.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_37 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 37u32, "2026-11-01T14:35Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:35 x: PATH A churn, first reading from history (no build needed). Commit 48bdcb2 -> 08a49e2 changed 583 lines across 30 sim .spi files. MOST were syntax-only: the test -> :# respell touches no generated code, and Nil -> [] is sugar for the same constructor. The semantic part was ~60 lines (clock_ms, raw-pair metric rows, the lean serve launch is PowerShell). Yet the regenerated xsim_frames changed 28 of 39 .gleam files, 17,752 lines changed. That is about 300 generated lines rewritten per semantic source line, across 72% of the parts.\nREADING (to confirm with the controlled benchmark): renumbered generated names (closureNNN, started_NNN) plus gleam-modules' SCC repacking cascade across parts, so reviewing a generated diff is effectively impossible today. This is the quality cost the user suspected. It strengthens the case for B (.spi-controlled units with stable boundaries) and for READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES-style stable naming. The pre-registered decision rule (n of 10:34) is unchanged and will be decided on the controlled A/B numbers, not on this reading.")
inl note_of_x_hand_units_38 () = Note ("X-HAND-UNITS", 38u32, "2026-11-01T14:45Z", "x", "2026-11-01 10:45 x: prior art for the 10:34 benchmark plan: XSIM-GLEAM-SPLIT-BENCH (Done 10-28) asked whether xsim frames could compile in parallel like Rust crates, and closed under X-WIP-VERIFY's rule. The new A/B measures something it did not: generated-diff CHURN per source change (n of 10:35: 28 of 39 parts, 17,752 lines for ~60 semantic lines) and iteration time under .spi-controlled units. Read its notes before the prototype to reuse its measurement setup.")
