inl kit_agile_per_repo () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", "agile.spi per repo, aggregated by the CLI with writes routed to each item's home file; benchmark the token, latency and contention gains before building", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-HAND-CRATE-UNITS", "kit,agile,tokens,benchmark,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_per_repo () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-25T12:14Z", "eoie", "user 10-25 08:0x: 'eoie to support agile.spi sitting in each project's repo while the cli aggregates everything, then saves each change in its appropriate place automatically; could there be gains? one step closer to spreading tasks to their line of code as a comment, then maybe extinguishing the agile file; benchmark considering how cc spends tokens, agile.spi being giant while the binary filters it'. Today: one state/agile.spi of 6.3 MB for every repo and team. Phase 1 (this item): an agile.spi per repo (the item's repo from its team or tag), the CLI aggregates the roots it is pointed at (agile roots already exists for todos), and every write goes back to the item's home file; IDs stay globally unique. Phase 2 is the existing TODO-AS-SOURCE epic (paused at 50%, waiting on the user's go; its review found 19 blocks in 10 repos, 8 linked, 0 stale, and no work ever picked up from a block). Benchmark, pre-registered before any build: replay 12 agent operations recorded in this session's transcript (show, list --open --team, next, note, set, grep for an ID, add) against three layouts built on scratch copies: A today's single file, B per-repo files, C per-repo files plus TODO blocks. Per op, measure (1) tokens the agent receives (CLI output bytes, and for grep the matched-line bytes, because agile.spi lines run to thousands of chars), (2) CLI wall time (parse cost scales with file size), (3) write contention: concurrent writers per file (the .pending orphan on 10-24 came from contention). Hypothesis: agent tokens are bounded by CLI output, not file size, as long as agents never read or grep the file, so B's gains are latency, contention and locality (tasks commit with their code), while grep-based habits cost the most in A. Decision rule: adopt B if it cuts median CLI wall time by 30% or more, or removes cross-team writes to a shared file, without raising median agent tokens per op; C is judged separately with its own replay.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-25T12:49Z", "eoie", "08:49 eoie: layout A baseline measured on today's 6.3 MB file, read-only ops, loaded machine (scratch clean\\agile-per-repo-baseline-A.csv), as wall time / output bytes / ~tokens (bytes/4): show one item 0.4 s / 1.5 KB / 380; list --open --team eoie 3.1 s / 21 KB / 5200; list --open --load light 2.1 s / 17 KB / 4200; next 10 6.6 s / 1.6 KB / 400; list all 8.7 s / 131 KB / 32800; check 34.7 s / 0.1 KB / 25; grep an ID in agile.spi 0.3 s / 3.3 KB / 820; grep a word 0.1 s / 4.0 KB / 990. First reads, against the hypothesis: (1) targeted grep is cheap in tokens (3-4 KB; the long lines appear only when a match sits in a long note), so grep is not the token sink; (2) the token sinks are broad listings (list all 33k tokens, team list 5k), which per-repo files would not shrink, while narrower default views (open, team, top N) would; (3) wall time is where file size shows: next 6.6 s, check 35 s, both scaling with the whole file, which per-repo files would cut, as would an index. The decision rule stays as written; layout B gets built from a scratch split by team/tag for the same ops.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-25T12:58Z", "eoie", "08:58 eoie: layout B measured (scratch split: eoie team 184 items, 1.3 MB; rest 579 items, 4.8 MB; same read-only ops on the eoie root; clean\\agile-per-repo-baseline-B.csv). A vs B: list --open --team eoie 3.1 s vs 3.9 s, 21 KB both; list --load light 2.1 vs 2.9 s; next 10 6.6 vs 3.0 s; list all 8.7 vs 3.5 s and 131 KB vs 32 KB; check 34.7 vs 33.3 s; grep 0.3 vs 0.1 s, same bytes. Readings: (1) scoped queries cost the same tokens in A and B, since the CLI already filters; tokens drop only for unscoped calls (list all, -75%) because the root itself is scoped. (2) Latency is dominated by a fixed per-call cost, not file size: team lists take about 3 s at 1.3 MB and at 6.3 MB, and check spends about 33 s typechecking regardless; only whole-file walks (next, list all) halve. Decision rule applied mechanically: median wall time A 2.6 s vs B 3.0 s, so no 30% cut on latency (the clause fails, within load noise); but B removes cross-team writes to one shared file without raising median tokens per op, so B passes on the contention clause and is adopted as the target layout. Findings worth their own items, larger than the split: the fixed per-call cost (about 3 s for a list) and check's 33 s typecheck are the real latency sinks, and a default narrow view (open, team) beats any file layout on tokens. Layout C (TODO blocks) is still to replay, under TODO-AS-SOURCE.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 4u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 5u32, "2026-11-09T09:45Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:2x local rig, PHASE 1 DESIGN (dep KIT-HAND-CRATE-UNITS is Done; layout B was adopted 10-25 on the contention clause). New evidence since: state/agile.spi is now 9.6 MB / 1968 tasks (6.3 MB on 10-25), every write parses and rewrites the whole file, and parallel lanes hit the CAS refusal 'state/agile.spi changed since it was read; nothing written, run the call again' repeatedly on 11-08/09 (measured in KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH). PLAN: (1) shard by team inside the same root first: state/agile/<team>.spi, the team from the item's team: tag (untagged -> state/agile/_.spi), plus state/agile/index.spi (id -> shard) rebuilt by agile record; reads aggregate all shards (same output as today), writes lock and rewrite ONLY the item's shard, so two teams never conflict and a note costs one shard parse (largest team today is a fraction of the file). (2) agile rename/move between teams moves the record between shards in one locked step. (3) Per-repo homes come next as roots: a shard may live in its repo (agile roots add), with IDs globally unique across roots, which is the user's 10-25 ask. Migration: one-shot agile migrate-shards preview|apply, byte-identical aggregate list output before/after as its gate. DoD: list/next/show identical on the sharded root, a note on team A while team B writes never refuses, write wall time cut by the shard ratio (pre-registered: >=50% median on note/set). Next step: measure shard sizes per team from today's file (one read-only pass) to confirm the ratio. TypeFeatures: a shard_key union (TeamShard name | Unteamed) and a typed index row.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 6u32, "2026-11-09T09:45Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:2x local rig, SHARD SIZES measured (read-only pass over state/agile.spi, 1968 tasks, 9.64 MB; every Task/Planning/Note/... line attributed to its item's team: tag): org 3,230 KB (33%), rig 2,286 KB (23%), compiler 1,296 KB (13%), microblog 1,084 KB (11%), fiscal 638 KB (6%), tibia 631 KB (6%), untagged 613 KB (6%), rest under 1%. So a write would parse and rewrite at most a third of today's file (org), a median team about a ninth: the pre-registered >=50% wall-time cut on note/set is plausible for every team. Side finding for grooming: stale team tags survive the 11-08 renames (team:kit 24 KB, team:management 7 KB, team:resilience 7 KB, team:i574n 1 KB, team:eoie); they should be retagged to rig / org before sharding, or they become their own tiny shards.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 7u32, "2026-11-09T10:00Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:0x local rig: first code slice src/rig_agile_shards/shards.spi (pure, HostFree + LibReferenced, kit standards 0): shard_key union (TeamShard name | Unteamed), shard_of_tags (first team: tag wins), shard_file (state/agile/<team>.spi, _.spi), id_of_line for every record kind (Task/Planning/Note... first quoted arg), task_home + lines_of_shard keeping file order. Moon probe rig-probe1109m GREEN 2/2. Next: the migrate-shards preview (counts per shard + byte-identical aggregate check) on a scratch copy.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_per_repo_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", 8u32, "2026-11-09T12:37Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:5x local rig, org3 FLEET-HEALTH (state 9.84 MB, agile set > 120 s, gate next missing its budget): ran kit agile seal (dry-run first): sealed 409 Done items, 10.32 -> 9.96 MB (-3.5 pct; their notes were already archived), list 2.7 s after. MEASURED where the bulk is (read-only): notes are 8.1 MB = 86 pct of the file, and notes OLDER THAN 7 DAYS on still-open items are 4.5 MB = 47 pct. So the two levers, both needed: (1) an old-note seal: notes older than N days on open items move to archive/old-notes.spi, with agile show (full history), lint (DoD:/Next step:/TypeFeatures: markers may live in old notes: read the archive or carry a marker summary row) and next (last note only) reading accordingly: halves every parse; (2) the team shards designed above: each write parses 1/3 to 1/9 of what is left. Pre-registered: (1) alone must bring agile set under 15 s on the box at ~50 pct load.")
