inl kit_agile_call_latency () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", "agile calls carry a fixed ~3 s cost that file size does not explain, and agile check spends ~33 s typechecking; find and cut both", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-AGILE-PER-REPO", "kit,agile,latency,tokens,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_call_latency () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 1u32, "2026-10-25T13:00Z", "eoie", "eoie, from the EOIE-AGILE-PER-REPO benchmark (notes 2-3): team lists take ~3 s on a 1.3 MB file and on a 6.3 MB file alike, show takes 0.4-0.6 s, and check takes 33-35 s on both. So the cost is per call (startup, roots, lease or receipts checks, or a full parse that does not scale with the filtered output), not per byte. First step: time each phase of one agile list call (read, parse, filter, render) and of check (whose typecheck likely compiles state/*.spi through Spiral), then cut the dominant phase. Agents call agile many times per session, so seconds per call add up across teams.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 2u32, "2026-10-25T13:51Z", "eoie", "09:51 eoie, step 1 measured: on a one-item backlog (1.6 KB) list takes 0.25-0.39 s, show 0.34-1.2 s, next 0.3-1.3 s, and plain help 0.6-1.1 s (load noise). So there is no fixed 3 s per call. With the A/B data (team list: 121 rows shown in both layouts, about 3 s at 1.3 MB and at 6.3 MB), the cost follows rows shown, about 25 ms per row, not file size and not startup. Suspects, in order: (1) per-row cross-crate calls (agile_planning_fields eoie_agile_planning_columns_near(text, id, at) per row, each crossing with the whole text and scanning near the row); (2) per-row note-index lookups; (3) the per-row width and padding host blocks (padded, right_aligned, clipped in task_listing.spi). Next: time one row's phases on the 1.3 MB split root, then batch planning columns into one pass (one cross-crate call returning every row's columns), the likely fix.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:54Z", "eoie", "09:54 eoie code read (no CPU): the per-row path is task_listing row -> \\$'agile_planning_fields::eoie_agile_planning_columns_near(&*!text, &*!id, !at)' -> planning_near (agile_planning_model/model.spi:313) -> planning_offset_near, which is windowed (searches from the row's Task line to the next Task line only), then planning_at -> tuple_field text (found + offset) (byte_len text). Remaining suspects: (a) tuple_field gets the whole-text end as its bound and may walk far past the row; (b) each cross-crate call may rebuild an owned Rc<str> of the whole text (1.3-6.3 MB per row). Neither is provable by reading; step 2 times them on the 1.3 MB split root (one row; then 121 rows with planning columns stubbed). A batched columns export (one pass over all rows) removes both, whichever dominates.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 4u32, "2026-10-25T18:07Z", "eoie", "14:07 kit, step 2 (black box, 100% CPU, so noisy):\n- agile list on the 6.5 MB shared root:\n  - 0 rows shown (team open, load none; 128 rows go through the planning_selected cross-crate filter): 1.2 s;\n  - 16 rows: 0.5-1.2 s; 128 rows: 2.3-4.8 s; 780 rows (all): 7.0-12.7 s.\n- So it is linear, about 7.5-10 ms per shown row, not quadratic. That clears the stable-insertion sort (780 rows would cost ~37x the 128-row case, not 6x).\n- JSON costs the same as the table, which clears the padding host blocks (suspect c).\n- Writing to a file costs the same as writing to a pipe (7.0-8.3 s vs 7.3-8.2 s), which clears console/pipe writes.\n- With note 2 (the same ~3 s for 121 rows at 1.3 MB and at 6.3 MB), the per-row cost does not scale with text size, which clears the whole-text copy per call (suspect b).\n- What's left is per-row work proportional to the row itself: planning_columns/planning_json (planning_at -> tuple_field) and the note-index last_note copy.\n  planning_selected over 128 rows added almost nothing in the 0-row case, so the near lookup is cheap; the suspect is what columns/json do after it.\n- Next (needs a build, after the rename): time row_line with the planning columns stubbed out.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 5u32, "2026-10-25T20:28Z", "eoie", "16:28 kit: code read of the generated planning crate (agile_planning_fields .rs, 808 lines).\n- byte_len compiles to .len() (constant time, on an Rc clone), so length scans are cleared.\n- The export boundary copies: pub fn eoie_agile_planning_columns_near takes v0 as a str slice and calls closure5 with Rc str from(v0). Every cross-crate export that takes a string allocates and copies its argument. agile list passes the whole backlog text (6.6 MB) once per shown row (columns_near; json_near for --json), plus planning_selected_near when --load or --assignee is given. 780 rows x 6.6 MB is about 5 GB of copying per list all.\n- This conflicts with note 2's equal timings at 1.3 MB and 6.3 MB, which were taken at 100% CPU and so are noisy. Settle it with one experiment after the rename: time list all on a 1 MB and a 6.6 MB root on an idle machine. Copy-bound predicts a ~6x ratio; fixed per-row cost predicts ~1x.\n- Fix either way: one crossing per list, not per row. A batched export takes the text once and returns all rows' planning columns in one pass. Systemic note for main: string exports could take an Rc str (or the text could be held in the callee across calls), so large texts never copy at crate boundaries. That's a codegen-level change to Export, so it's main's call, not eoie's.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_call_latency_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY", 6u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-AGILE-CALL-LATENCY (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl kit_do_slop_gate () = Task ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", "eoie do refuses slop in the text a program adds (Cons/Nil for ::/[], logic in host macros, rust_std_string, blocks, comments, long lines), with an auditable accept line", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "GRANULAR-LINT,KIT-RENAME", "kit,do,granular,lint,one-call,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_do_slop_gate () = Planning ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_1 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 1u32, "2026-10-25T13:03Z", "eoie", "user 10-25 08:5x: 'the projects are already generating cons again instead of ::; another reason to incentivize patching through eoie is automatic slop detection; this might be the inception point'. Design: eoie do classifies only the text a program adds (the lint's diff mode from GRANULAR-LINT: added lines only) against a slop rule set, and refuses the program before writing, printing one row per finding (file:line, rule, text) and the rule's fix. Starting rules, each with a positive and a negative test: (1) Cons (a, b) / Nil where a :: b / [] fit (Spiral list syntax; caught in eoie's own new code today: eoie_lint_macros/lint.spi copied Cons/Nil from the legacy crate gate walker, fixed); (2) host macros carrying logic (lint macros classes: flow, cast, arith, block, long); (3) new rust_std_string uses and \\$'{ ... }' blocks (AGENTS.md); (4) comment lines; (5) lines over 120; (6) joined-string rows. Escape hatch, auditable: a program line 'accept <rule> because <reason>' lets one finding through and records it in the receipt, so no silent bypass exists. Why in the patch tool and not only a hook: it works for every client (Claude, Codex, OpenCode) and every repo, and an agent gets the verdict in the same call that writes, which is the incentive to patch through eoie. Relation: EOIE-HEAL-EDIT-HOOK (90%, Blocked) stays as the Claude-side check for raw Edit/Write; GRANULAR-LINT provides the engine; EOIE-STYLE-RATCHET's census counts become the per-repo baseline. Also: a one-off sweep counting Cons/Nil per repo, to see where it is regenerating.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_2 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 2u32, "2026-10-25T13:34Z", "eoie", "09:34 eoie sweep: Cons( / Nil occurrences in .spi files changed in the last 3 days. eoie/src: 328 files, 210 hits (eoie_repo_affected/graph.spi 54, eoie_log_sequences/sequence_model.spi 35, eoie_mirror/check.spi 20). spiral/lib: 108 files, 827 hits (xsd_emit.spi 282, xpath_eval.spi 96, xsd_regex.spi 64). spiral/apps: 49 files, 15 (compiler/spi/tokens.spi 15). The freshest large source is lib's new XSD/XPath modules, which is exactly the regeneration the user saw. Rule (1)'s fix is mechanical (a :: b, [], and :: patterns in matches), so the gate can print the rewrite with each finding. The counts become the first baseline: the gate refuses new hits, and a heal sweep shrinks the old ones.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_3 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 3u32, "2026-10-25T13:45Z", "eoie", "09:45 correction to note 2 (main): the user's rule is list sugar instead of the Cons constructor (x :: xs, [a; b], | x :: rest =>); Nil stays, so Nil is not slop and rule (1) matches 'Cons (' only. Corrected counts: spiral/lib 57 Cons( sites in 8 files (listm' 29, process 10, xsd_emit 6, file_system 4, xsd 4, dictm 2, schematron_run 1, unicode 1; main is taking them as LIB-LIST-SUGAR-REST, with xsd_emit's 6 handed to fiscal); eoie/src 113 sites in 19 files among those changed in the last 3 days (eoie_repo_affected/graph.spi 31, eoie_log_sequences/sequence_model.spi 20, eoie_mirror/check.spi 10, eoie_do_verbs/verbs.spi 8, eoie_mirror/mirror.spi 7, eoie_env_pins/pins.spi 6). The eoie sites go into a heal sweep after the rename; the gate refuses new ones from then on.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_4 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 4u32, "2026-10-25T14:27Z", "eoie", "10:27 eoie: rewriter for the heal sweep drafted (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\cons-to-sugar.ps1; paren-, bracket- and string-aware, finds Cons ( and its top-level comma, writes (head :: tail)). On a scratch copy of the three largest offenders (eoie_repo_affected, eoie_log_sequences, eoie_mirror): 57 sites rewritten in 6 files, 12 skipped (multi-line or unbalanced), with patterns such as '| (line :: rest) =>' and 'f ((x :: acc))'. Before use: drop the redundant outer parens when the site is already parenthesised (the doubled '((x :: acc))'), handle the 12 multi-line sites, then a regen plus notebooks per package. It runs as the eoie list-sugar heal right after the rename; the gate then refuses new sites.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_5 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 5u32, "2026-10-25T14:37Z", "eoie", "10:37 eoie: the list-sugar rewriter is complete on the test copy (scratch clean\\list-sugar\\cons-to-sugar.ps1 plus try-rewriter.ps1): it recurses into head and tail (nested Cons (a, Cons (b, c)) becomes a :: b :: c), follows balanced calls across lines, and drops its own parens when the site is already parenthesised. On eoie_repo_affected, eoie_log_sequences and eoie_mirror: 74 sites rewritten (nested ones included), 0 skipped, 0 doubled parens, 0 Cons ( left. Proof still owed after the rename: regen plus cargo check and the packages' notebooks (behaviour must be identical; :: is sugar for Cons). Then the whole-repo pass (113 sites in recent files, more in older ones) runs as one heal release.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_6 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 6u32, "2026-10-25T18:16Z", "eoie", "14:16 kit: whole-repo dry run of the list-sugar heal (scratch list-sugar\\whole-repo-dry.ps1, a copy of all 777 eoie .spi files, no target).\n- Result: 113 Cons ( sites in 19 files across 15 packages, all rewritten; 0 skipped, 0 left, 0 doubled parens.\n- Top packages: eoie_repo_affected 36, eoie_log_sequences 21, eoie_mirror 17, eoie_env_pins 9, eoie_do_verbs 8, eoie_table_sequence_args 6, eoie_crate_gate 5, eoie_repo_accept_review 3, then 1-2 each.\n- Two near-gate crates are touched: eoie_repo_accept_review (970) and eoie_style_census (959).\n- Rule for the heal release: :: is sugar for Cons, so each touched package's regenerated .rs must be byte-identical to its pre-heal .rs. Any diff stops the heal and is investigated, which also proves no behaviour change without notebooks.\n  If identical, the notebooks are an extra check, not the gate.\n- Lib's 57 sites go separately as a lib land (lib gate, Cells), after the eoie heal.")
inl note_of_kit_do_slop_gate_7 () = Note ("KIT-DO-SLOP-GATE", 7u32, "2026-10-25T19:10Z", "eoie", "15:10 kit: rule model drafted in landing form; not compiled yet (the tree is leased by SLATE). Scratch: clean\\slop-gate\\eoie_do_slop (slop.spi 119 lines, package.spiproj).\n- Spiral-only package on |core-, spiral- and eoie_lint_macros, with no host macros.\n  - slop_rule union: ConsConstructor | HostLogic of macro_class (reuses the lint's long/block/flow/cast/arith classes through file_violations) | StdString | CommentLine | LongLine.\n  - slop_finding record (file, line, rule, text); rule_word and rule_fix per rule.\n  - line_rules: string-aware, so a Cons ( inside a quoted string is not slop; /// directives and markers are kept.\n  - added_findings: added text only (the lint's diff mode). accepted_of parses 'accept <rule> because <reason>'; unaccepted filters findings.\n- Four test blocks: cons in code vs in strings, :: and Nil clean; comments vs directives; 120 vs 121 characters and std-string; accept lets exactly one rule through and keeps the reason.\n- Lib calls checked against existing use or lib source: sm'.trim, concat_list, format_debug (lib), listm'.filter, listm'.exists' (f then list), listm.fold/map/length/rev. sm'.replicate doesn't exist, so the draft uses a local recursive helper.\n- Wiring dependency: the accept line has to be parsed inside do programs, and that parser is eoie_do_program at 999 lines. So the gate's program-side wiring lands after the eoie_do_rules split (HAND-CRATE-UNITS note 13); eoie_do_slop itself is independent.\n- Joined-string rows (rule 6 in note 1) are not drafted: they need the census's row detector, so that stays a follow-up.\n- Land and prove after SLATE (it will be edition-3 respelled with the rest): add it to the workspace as Spiral-only, run its notebook, then the do wiring.")
