inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_1 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 1u32, "2026-10-26T13:54Z", "fiscal", "2026-10-26 09:54 fiscal: request from the field. Shadowing a name from an opened module is a recurring silent bug class: fiscal F1 'census_of' shadowed xsd_regex.census_of (an existing caller broke, far from the edit); R5's 'node_name' / 'node_text' shadowed xml's functions (latent trap); TIBIA-AGENT's 'index' counter shadowed the array index function; PACKAGE-REGISTRY's ':< materialize' resolved to a type alias. Stopgap used today: session scratchpad shadow-check/shadow_check.py <base>=<draft> ... (names the draft adds vs its base; each checked against every ':<' module's top-level definitions; 0 false positives across 10 drafts). Wanted: one eoie verb, e.g. 'eoie spiral shadows <file> [--base <file>]', exit non-zero on a clash, usable in the PostToolUse style hook. Done = the verb + a Spiral test block per clash kind (value, nominal, union case, alias).")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_2 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 2u32, "2026-10-31T15:29Z", "kit", "10-31 11:2x kit: BUILT (unstaged), shipping in TRAIN7. Verb: 'kit lint shadows <root> [<path>...] [--baseline <rows>] --opens [--lib <dir>]' adds rows 'file:line  name  open-shadow  from <module>' for a top-level definition whose name, in the same namespace (term or type), is also defined by a module the file opens with a top-level ':<'. 'main' is exempt. Module resolution: 'pkg.mod' -> <root>/pkg/mod.spi; a bare 'mod' -> the file's own folder, else <lib>/mod.spi, where lib comes from --lib or SPIRAL_LIB (no project paths baked in). Unresolved modules contribute nothing. Rows honour --baseline like the other kinds and count toward 'new', but only when --opens is given, so existing runs are unchanged. Code: pure clash logic in kit_lint_shadows_core (opened_modules, open_clashes, test a_definition_named_like_an_opened_member_is_a_finding: term and type clashes found, a different namespace and main not), plus a NEW crate kit_lint_opens (resolution and rows; test an_opened_package_module_is_resolved_and_its_names_checked, with a temp-folder fixture, a missing module and a baseline-suppressed rerun), called once from kit_lint_shadows. The first attempt put everything in the two existing crates and blew the gate (core 731 -> 1180, runner 777 -> 1037), hence the hand-modeled split. Now: core 731, kit_lint_opens 922, runner 847; regen plus cargo check pass; notebooks shadows.spi 6/6 and opens.spi 1/1. PROTOTYPE census before building (PowerShell): kit/src has 13 findings in 7 files (e.g. kit_agile_mutation and kit_agile_routes redefine i32_eq/i32_le/i32_nonnegative from agile_mutation_policy.model; kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' shadows section_scan.trace; kit_cli_model's expect shadows command_spec_domain's), lib/spiral has 5 in 1 file, and fiscal/tibia 0 (their layouts may not resolve with the src/<pkg> rule). ACCEPTANCE: release-acceptance.ps1 row 'lint shadows --opens names a shadowed opened member' (fixture pkg/a.spi opens pkg.b and redefines helper) FAILS on the installed E14F1F0B as expected and must pass on TRAIN7. Done once installed plus fiscal's original repro.")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_3 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 3u32, "2026-10-31T15:55Z", "kit", "10-31 12:0x kit: first fixes from the prototype census, ahead of the verb shipping. kit_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi and kit_agile_routes/routes.spi each carried byte-identical copies of i32_eq, i32_le and i32_nonnegative, which they also open from agile_mutation_policy.model. The local copies are removed (i32_positive, which is unique, stays). Behaviour is unchanged, and each package loses one host macro ($\"0 <= !value\"). Census: kit_agile_routes ratchets ok. kit_agile_mutation was rebaselined, and the reason was checked: plain 'git diff' compares against the INDEX, and the user's staged kit changes already raise its indented host macros 18 -> 20 against HEAD, while this edit took its top-level macros 17 -> 14 and left the indented count alone. So the rebaseline records drift already staged, plus a decrease. Verification (notebooks/regen) is queued with the respell batch until TRAIN6 frees the CPU. Remaining findings, left for review: kit_cli_model's expect (a test helper over command_spec_domain's), kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' over section_scan.trace (a test-only name), agile_task_census i32_text, agile_task_revision task_arguments_offset, and bundle_verify_contract preflight_binding/complete_binding.")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_4 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 4u32, "2026-11-03T07:30Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 64 h ago by kit, progress kept at 80%. Resume from that note: r command_spec_domain's), kit_log_counts' test fixture 'trace' over section_scan.trace (a test-only name), agile_task_census i32_text, agile_task_revision task_arguments_offset, and bundle_verify_contract preflight_binding/complete_binding.")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_5 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 5u32, "2026-11-06T11:35Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:4x kit, KIT-RENAME: renamed from EOIE-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT (item IDs follow the tool name; older notes keep the old ID as history).")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_6 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 6u32, "2026-11-09T12:47Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 08:4x microblog groom check, installed kit (11-08): the verb ships and works. Fixture (fiscal's case): xsd.spi defines census_of; user.spi opens :< xsd and redefines census_of -> 'user.spi:3 census_of open-shadow from xsd', receipt failed shadows 1 new 1; without --opens the same run is ok shadows 0, so existing runs are unchanged. CENSUS kit/src --opens --lib spiral/lib/spiral: 1190 files, 10 clashes, 17.6 s: task_census i32_text, bundle_verify_contract preflight_binding + complete_binding, agile_sprint_start started, rig_cli_model expect (x2), rig_crate_gate expect, rig_log_counts trace, rig_style_census appended, rig_window_announce_verb verb_words. GAP to DoD: shadows.spi has one open-clash test block (a_definition_named_like_an_opened_member_is_a_finding) covering a value and a union-vs-alias type clash; a nominal (:!) and a union-case clash have no block of their own, and the hook wiring is unverified. next: add the two test blocks, heal or baseline the 10 kit clashes, wire --opens into the PostToolUse style hook.")
inl note_of_kit_open_shadow_lint_7 () = Note ("KIT-OPEN-SHADOW-LINT", 7u32, "2026-11-09T18:29Z", "tibia", "11-09 14:3x tibia (help pool): FIELD RUN of the shipped verb on a whole repo. `kit lint shadows ../tibia --opens --lib ../spiral/lib/spiral` on the installed kit: 171 files in 787 ms, 1 finding: src/search.spi:30 `drawn` open-shadow from keyed_rng. True positive: search's drawn (one mutated, clamped gene) and keyed_rng's drawn (seed/tick/entity mixing) are unrelated, and search.spi opens keyed_rng, so inside the module the lib name was silently hidden. HEALED (unstaged): renamed to `mutant` (4 occurrences, search.spi only; no other module calls search::drawn); re-run: `ok files 171 shadows 0 new 0`. Name chosen for the cell budget: the 3 call sites sit in one cell (mutate), which went 267 -> 270 (limit 272 at 51 cells), where a longer name (mutated_gene) had pushed it to 288. Gate: ganymede job-1794248939769-tibia-shadow (format + --check). The verb works as described on a real repo with 0 false positives here; what is left for this item (DoD: a Spiral test block per clash kind: value, nominal, union case, alias) is rig's.")
inl rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Task ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", "lib string wrappers (trim/substring) inline per callsite in codegenRust, bloating generated Rust past the 1k gate; make hot wrappers join-point methods so each emits one shared fn called per use instead of inlining", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Paused, "RUST-INLINE-SINGLE-USE", "compiler,rust,codegen,size,lib,wipe-redo,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline () = Planning ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_1 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 1u32, "2026-10-26T15:37Z", "compiler", "filed from kit's measurement 10-26: kit_lint_macros 461 Spiral -> 2,056 Rust (gate 1,000). Mechanism (spiral_compiler.fs): the StringSlice intrinsic is ALREADY one shared fn string_slice (164434/164749); the bloat is lib WRAPPERS (trim/substring, sm'.spi:1321) being plain := functions that Spiral inlines whole (bound casts, Rc clones, empty-string thread_local) at every callsite. codegenRust already has the shared-helper mechanism: a METHOD join point emits one top-level fn method_N(...) called per use (164966, self-tail -> loop). Fix is LIB-LEVEL + no compiler build: put :~/join on the big, frequently-called wrappers so they land as methods. VERIFY the regen shows top-level fn <name>(...) {} (method, win) NOT Rc::new(move|...| ) / fn ->Rc<dyn Fn> (closure path at 165003, heavy: box + thread_local, WORSE). kit's :~ experiment grew because it was applied to 11 tiny 1-3 line helpers (inlining those is cheaper) and/or they became closures not methods. operator_between is kit-local, not lib. Distinct from EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC (that's export signatures). Reject: making trim/substring F# intrinsics (duplicates logic across ~15 backends, violates granular-style). Full analysis: scratchpad/lib-string-op-codegen-size.md.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_2 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 2u32, "2026-10-26T17:09Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (concrete lib edit for the codegen-size win, build-independent analysis of lib/spiral/sm'.spi). The bloaty string wrappers kit measured are OUTER `:=` functions that inline their whole chain per callsite:\n- trim (sm'.spi:1321): `:= trim (input:string):string = input |> trim_with []`\n- trim_with (:1289): `:= trim_with (chars)(input):string = input |> trim_start chars |> trim_end chars`\n- trim_start (:1217) / trim_end (:1253): `:=` outer; their INNER recursive loops are ALREADY `:~ ^ 루프` (so the loops already emit as shared methods -- good). Only the outer wrappers inline.\n- substring (:988): `:= substring (start:i32)(len:i32)(str:string):string = run_target \\| | Rust => \\ () => ... slice ...` (a backend_switch via run_target).\n\nEDIT (draft for kit to try, NOT yet applied -- lib is live-tree, Cells reads it, so land as a separate gated change): change the OUTER wrappers from `:=` to `:~` so each emits ONCE as a shared method called per use, instead of inlining the chain at every callsite: trim, trim_with, trim_start, trim_end, substring. All are monomorphic (string->string; substring i32->i32->string->string) and called in statement position, so they should land as METHODS (shared `fn`), not boxed closures. VERIFY on regen: `fn <name>(...)` emitted once + `<name>(...)` at each call (WIN); if you see `Rc::new(move|...)` / `fn ... -> Rc<dyn Fn>` it became a closure (misfire -- back it out). substring caveat: its body is a `run_target` backend_switch with `\\ () =>` arms; a `:~` wraps the resolved dispatch as a method -- should be fine but is the one most likely to misfire, check it first.\n\nSCOPE CAVEAT (important): sm'.spi is SHARED lib -- making these `:~` changes codegen for EVERY consumer across ALL backends, not just kit_lint_macros. So this is NOT a kit-crate-local change: it needs the full `-Suite all -Native` oracle-parity gate + a corpus check (lib + tibia + Kino compile, per lib-smoke-before-syntax) and must accept the perf trade-off (a method call vs inlining) on any HOT trim/substring use elsewhere. For cold lint/verb crates the size win dominates; for a hot inner loop the call overhead is a (tiny) regression. If the all-consumers trade-off is unwanted, the alternative is a crate-local wrapper kit marks `:~` rather than changing lib. Build-blocked (node committed ~8h to kit-release-FOLLOWUP); kit owns the call on whether the lib-wide change is worth it vs a crate-local wrapper.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_3 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 3u32, "2026-10-26T20:15Z", "compiler", "compiler 10-26 (DECISION recorded; item parked). kit decided: NO lib-wide :~ change to sm'.trim/trim_with/trim_start/trim_end/substring for kit's sake. kit measured the crate-local alternative on the node (kit-lint-wrappers2, lint4 mirror): lint's OWN trim/substring/starts_with/replace put through four local :~ wrappers -> each emitted as ONE fn called 6-25x, NOT a boxed closure (confirms this item's note-1/note-2 method-vs-closure prediction empirically). But lint.rs went 2,056 -> 2,005 lines = only 2.5%. So the structural bloat is not wrapper-inlining; kit splits the lint crate into hand-modeled build units instead (GRANULAR-LINT note 17), which is the real size fix.\n\nCompiler CONCURS with parking the lib-wide change: >=2.5% best case (crate-local ceiling; the lib change can't beat it inside one crate) does NOT justify a full -Suite all -Native oracle-parity gate + a per-consumer hot-path call-overhead trade-off on every trim/substring use across all backends. The draft (scratchpad/rswi-lib-draft.txt) is PARKED, not deleted: the lib-wide :~ edit is proven-sound and ready, but is NOT blocked by kit and should only be revived if a DIFFERENT consumer wants the size win for its own reasons and accepts the all-consumers perf trade-off. Status -> parked (resolve-as-wont-do for the lib-wide scope; crate-local/build-unit split is the chosen path). Pairs still with EXPORT-STRING-PARAM-RC only on the shared annotation mechanism, which is a separate open item.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_4 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 4u32, "2026-10-31T20:29Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 16:29 compiler, pause re-decision (KIT-PAUSE-RULE-REWORK): Paused -> PLANNED, back in agile next. It was paused by the 3-day silence rule, not by a decision; per-callsite inlining of lib string wrappers bloats Rust output; it belongs with the Rust-cost gate items (RUST-MOVE-ON-LAST-USE, RUST-MATCH-DUPLICATION).")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_5 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 5u32, "2026-11-06T21:01Z", "compiler", "11-06 17:0x compiler (re-decide): Planned -> PAUSED, by decision this time. Reason: the measured ceiling is small (>= 2.5% best case per crate, 10-27 note) against a lib-wide change that needs a full -Suite all -Native parity gate and adds call overhead to every trim/substring use on every backend, and the chosen path for crates over the 1,000-line gate (split the crate) became available TODAY: AUTO-EXPORT-1 + AUTO-EXPORT-2 shipped on pack57 to box, node and europa, so a unit with auto-export: monomorphic exports its calls with no hand-written wrappers (LINK-AUTO-EXPORT's regex rx_syntax proof is running). The parked lib draft (rswi-lib-draft.txt) stays ready. Resume when: a consumer crate still exceeds the gate after a unit split, or a lane asks for the lib-wide size win and accepts the per-call cost.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_6 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 6u32, "2026-11-08T05:42Z", "compiler", "11-08 01:4x compiler (re-decide): stays PAUSED. Reason (unchanged since 11-06): a small measured ceiling (about 2.5% per crate at best) against a lib-wide change that needs a full -Suite all -Native parity gate, while crates over the 1,000-line gate are now split through auto-export units (AE1-AE3 shipped). Resume when: a crate cannot be split below 1,000 lines by auto-export units (rig's crate gate names one), or a measured string-heavy hot path shows the wrappers above 5% of its Rust size.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_7 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 7u32, "2026-11-09T11:28Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:3x compiler (re-decide, star groom): stays PAUSED. Reason: the one crate-gate failure since 11-08 caused by string code (TRAIN42c, lib sm.trim emitting ~170 Rust lines) is addressed at the lib level by RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING (a Rust run_target arm, 98 -> 17 lines for a one-call program), without the lib-wide wrapper-inline change this item proposes. Resume when: a crate fails the 1,000-line gate on a string operation that a per-function lib arm cannot fix, or a measured string-heavy hot path shows the wrapper call overhead.")
inl note_of_rust_string_wrapper_inline_8 () = Note ("RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE", 8u32, "2026-11-10T05:08Z", "compiler", "11-10 01:1x compiler (re-decide): stays PAUSED, reason and resume condition unchanged from the 11-09 11:28Z note (RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING handles string-heavy crates at the lib level; the lib-wide wrapper inline has a ~2.5% ceiling). The resume condition is now a wait row (when:crate-gate-fails-on-string-op) instead of prose. Next step: resume only when rigs crate gate names a crate over 1,000 lines because of a string operation that per-function lowering cannot reach.")
inl kit_job_front_ticket () = Task ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", "job start --heavy --front: queue a job first in the heavy line with a ticket owned by its own supervisor", Story, P2, D3, 50u32, Planned, "KIT-RENAME,KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,jobs,slots,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_front_ticket () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 1u32, "2026-10-26T17:05Z", "kit", "13:05 kit: Found while launching kit-release-FOLLOWUP (KIT-RENAME note 44). The coordinator agreed it should go first, but job start --heavy can't do that. The supervisor's slot gate (kit_job_slot slot_gate) always stamps its ticket with the current ticks and overwrites SPIRAL_HEAVY_TICKET. A pre-made ticket owned by another live pid would be first forever and stall the box. The workaround was a plain job plus a hand-written heavy-wrapper.ps1 (scratch clean\\respell\\launch-FOLLOWUP.ps1 -Ticket), which also skips the window gate's bookkeeping. Shape: job start --heavy --front. ticket_created takes a stamp one tick before the current live head, still owned by the supervisor's pid, so the ticket dies with the job. Every other gate step is unchanged. The job's slot file and job list say 'front'. Milestones: (1) slot.spi stamp parameter, with a test block where a front ticket sorts first and a dead-owner front ticket is skipped; (2) the --front flag through job_start_args and start_shape; (3) one-call spec case + release. Rule for using it: only after the lane coordinator agrees the order, never by default.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 2u32, "2026-10-26T19:42Z", "kit", "15:42 kit: design read against the tree (no edits; the tree is held by FOLLOWUP). The options travel as positional values through four packages: kit_job_start_args start_args (a 14-tuple Export), kit_job_launch (re-splats them into kit_job_create's 14-argument Export), kit_job_create (writes job files such as slot_by_ms), then kit_job_slot/kit_job_gate read the files. The style contract says touching a legacy function means migrating it, so --front is not a one-flag change. Plan: (1) carry start_options as one typed record from start_args through launch into create (a record Export instead of 14 positionals), with a front field set by a width-1 flag FrontStart, refused unless the shape is HeavyStart; (2) create writes job file 'front'; (3) slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists, owner still the supervisor pid; (4) tests: a windows_cli fixture with two queued heavy jobs where the --front one is admitted first, and a dead-owner front ticket that gets skipped. Re-estimated from these milestones: 300m (the record migration of the four Export boundaries is most of it).")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 3u32, "2026-10-27T13:55Z", "kit", "09:5x kit, design correction before building (learned tonight from three crate splits): note 2's 'carry start_options as one typed record ... a record Export instead of 14 positionals' cannot work. A !!!!Export signature carries only FFI shapes: scalars, strings, arrays (Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>>) and tuples of them. Spiral records and unions are private generated types (US0, UH1 ...). Revised plan: inside each of start_args, launch and create, migrate the legacy positional handling to one local typed record (start_options) and keep the Export boundary as the positional tuple, widened by one bool 'front' (the boundary stays scalar, as kit's style contract asks of boundaries). create writes job file 'front'; slot_gate stamps the ticket one tick before the live head when 'front' exists; the owner is still the supervisor pid. The tests are unchanged from note 2. Current sizes (headroom for the record and one field): start_args.rs 578, launch.rs 593, create.rs 341, slot.rs 358. Starts after kit-release-SLICES lands, so the four crates rebuild once with the other staged changes.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 4u32, "2026-10-27T14:06Z", "kit", "10:1x kit: built on a probe copy and staged (clean/kit-rename/job-front-ticket, 4 files with base hashes; applied by staged-apply.ps1 after kit-release-SLICES). start_args: FrontStart is a width-1 flag '--front' that sets start_options.front (already a typed record inside the package); it is refused unless the shape is HeavyStart ('--front applies to --heavy jobs: it queues the job first in the heavy line'); the Export tuple gains one bool. launch threads front into create through both job_launched callers (launched_text passes false). create takes front and writes job file 'front' (folded into the existing write-error refusal). slot: front_name (pure) builds {head_ticks-1:020}-{pid}.t from the live head ticket, so it sorts first; one-expression primitives ticket_ticks, own_pid and ticket_written; slot_gate takes front_ticket when the job's 'front' file exists, and falls back to a normal ticket when the queue has no live head. A dead-owner front ticket is skipped by the existing first_live_ticket (pid liveness). The owner stays the supervisor pid. Test block: a_front_ticket_sorts_one_tick_before_the_head. Sizes: start_args 616 (was 578), launch 594 (593), create 356 (341), slot 448 (358). Not yet proven: the live two-job ordering (note 2's windows_cli fixture) needs the built candidate; it runs after the stage lands and the next release builds.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom (user, via main 21:54): STATE CHECK after the wipe. The source has it (kit_job_start_args/start_args.spi:28 FrontStart '--front', :179 refusal '--front applies to --heavy jobs...'; kit_job_slot/slot.spi also references it), but installed kit C528BE4D answers 'kit job start --front' with the generic job usage, not that refusal, and its usage text lists no --front. So it is in the tree, not in the shipped binary (the usage/dispatch path is not wired or not regenerated). Progress stays 50. Next train: wire it, and accept when 'kit job start --front' returns the --heavy refusal and a --heavy --front job is queued first.")
inl note_of_kit_job_front_ticket_6 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FRONT-TICKET", 6u32, "2026-11-01T03:37Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
inl rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Task ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", "Rust codegen: skip the let vN = vN.clone() for union-arm fields the arm body never reads (bind them as _ in the match pattern)", TaskKind, P3, D3, 90u32, Active, "", "compiler,rust,codegen,line-gate,x,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones () = Planning ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_1 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 1u32, "2026-10-26T20:16Z", "x", "2026-10-26 x: measured in the Bluesky-stacked write.rs: 112 of 1778 lines (6.3%) are clones of pattern fields that the arm never uses. Most come from ThreadReply's 7 fields bound in each platform arm of path_on, verb_on and decoded_on. Count: a let vN = vN.clone() whose vN appears at most three times in its fn (pattern, binding, clone). This differs from X-DONE note 16's dead literal lets (1.6%). Done when: codegenRust emits _ for unread arm fields, the corpus agrees, and x's generated line counts drop with no rustc warnings added.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_2 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 2u32, "2026-10-27T12:02Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:02 x: sized on today's split-tree client outputs (proof 7, pinned compiler 4FD712E9E357, 23 crates, 16,279 lines). The generated code has 935 match-arm re-binding clones (let vN: T = vN.clone();), but only 102 are never read in their arm (a brace-depth scan to the end of the arm), i.e. 0.6% of all lines. Worst crates: write 29 (pre-R1), chain 9, users/locate/thread 7 each. The 6.3% measured earlier was the Bluesky-stacked write before R1. For x this fix is a polish item, not a line-gate lever; P3 stands. The remaining arm clones are read; they are the &-match-then-own pattern, which only a borrow-aware codegen could drop.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_3 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 3u32, "2026-10-29T21:31Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:3x compiler: checked as a possible line-gate lever for kit's TRAIN4c red (kit_standards/standards.rs 1,024 > 1,000): that file has 12 self-rebind clones (let vN: T = vN.clone();), and only 2 are never read again in their fn. So the fix would save 2 lines there, not the 24 needed. It stays P3 polish, as x concluded in n2.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_4 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 4u32, "2026-11-04T01:12Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:11 x: ORPHAN SWEEP (MAIN-ORPHAN-SWEEP, x's corner: open x items with no note in 48 h). Owner is team:compiler (x is a consumer only); next step compiler's, per its last note (only 2 of 12 self-rebind clones were dead, a small lever).")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_5 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "compiler", "11-04 07:2x compiler GROOM: priority P1 -> P3 to match the notes (x n2: 102 dead arm clones = 0.6% of x client lines; compiler 10-29: 2 of 12 in kit_standards). Not a line-gate lever; polish only.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_6 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 6u32, "2026-11-11T00:38Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:4x compiler: DRAFTED drafts\\spiral\\RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES\\1-skip-unread-arm-fields.kitdo (5 replacements). The Rust liveness pass already computed each union arms live-in set, then merged them (unbox_arm_uses); it now also keeps each arms own set keyed by the arm body (unbox_body_uses, reference identity). Codegen binds an unread payload field as _ in the match pattern and emits no let vN = vN.clone() for it; unknown liveness keeps the old binding. pack96 = pack89 + this (core b2fe79c8), queued at the end of ganymedes chain: probe (Named of string * i32 whose arm ignores the string; Rust clones before/after, rustc run, want exit 43) and the suite vs pack89 (only Rust text may change). Next step: read ~/remote-stage/pack96/g.log at \"p96 done\".")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_7 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 7u32, "2026-11-11T00:38Z", "compiler", "DoD: union-arm fields an arm never reads are bound as _ with no clone in Rust output; the suite vs the previous pack shows 0 compile/native/exit/stdout diffs (only Rust text shrinks). TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_8 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 8u32, "2026-11-11T01:16Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:1x compiler: pack96 PROBE PASS (ganymede): the Named(string, i32) arm that ignores the string now emits \"US0::US0_Named(_, v2) => {\" with no binding line (pack89 had \"US0_Named(v1, v2)\" + \"let v1: Rc<str> = v1;\"); both runs exit 43 (want 43). Here the dropped binding was a MOVE (the match is by value), so the clone count stays 1; that clone is the string literals thread_local cache, unrelated. A by-reference match (where the arm used to emit .clone()) is the case where clones drop; the suite will show it across the corpus. Suite vs pack89 still running. Next step: \"p96 done\" verdict (0 compile/native/exit/stdout diffs required).")
inl note_of_rust_unused_pattern_clones_9 () = Note ("RUST-UNUSED-PATTERN-CLONES", 9u32, "2026-11-11T01:25Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:2x compiler: GATE GREEN (ganymede pack96 = pack89 + 1-skip-unread-arm-fields): suite vs pack89 7640 rows, 0 compile / native / exit / stdout diffs, residual changed 36 (Rust outputs where an unread union-arm field now binds as _ with no binding or clone line). Probe exit 43 on both. DoD met except installation: it ships with the next pack that carries it to the nodes. Next step: include program 1 in the next installed pack.")
