inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_1 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 1u32, "2026-10-27T10:52Z", "x", "2026-10-27 06:51 x: same census. The requests crate already owns failures for writes; reads could call one export too.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_2 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 2u32, "2026-10-27T11:17Z", "x", "2026-10-27 07:15 x: design. describe_failure is only the visible part. The base-regen census shows the whole read transport re-emitted in every read crate: describe_failure 48 lines in fetch/thread/refs/users (plus write before R1), app_reply 23 in fetch/thread/refs/users, string_slice 49-75 in each (through clipped in problem_detail), and each crate's raw_reply instance. Proposal for a measured round: extend the requests crate with xc_read(site JSON, path) -> (code, body, failure text), so a read crate keeps only its decoding and calls one host function for the HTTP, auth, classification and failure text. Expected about 150-200 lines less per read crate (fetch, thread, refs, users), and requests grows by about one read path (it already holds raw_reply, describe_failure and string_slice). Pre-register the rule before drafting: each touched crate <= 900 and none grows; requests <= 900; notebooks and e2e green through x/scripts/verify.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_3 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 3u32, "2026-10-27T12:05Z", "x", "2026-10-27 08:05 x: analysis, lower value than first thought. transport.spi already delegates status classification to one host call (xc_api::xc_classified), so a read crate repeats only app_reply (23 lines) and describe_failure (48). Moving reads into requests with a text boundary would save about 70 lines per read crate (fetch, thread, refs, users), and the crates would lose the typed failures they branch on (thread's CursorHalted via halted_in, users' NoSuchAccount). None of these crates is near the gate (users 842, refs 873, thread 727, fetch 718). Dropped to P3; revisit only if one nears 900.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_4 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 4u32, "2026-10-27T15:10Z", "x", "2026-10-27 11:09 x: groomed to Paused, so the light view lists only pullable work. Resume condition (from this item's own analysis): a touched crate's regen nears 900 generated lines, or a change has to grow it. Checked against every regen until then.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_5 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 5u32, "2026-10-29T12:57Z", "x", "2026-10-29 09:2x x: resume check against today's base regen (node job-1793276236043, installed compiler, respelled tree): refs 896, users 874, thread 708, fetch 728. refs is the 'nears 900' half of the n4 condition, but no pending change has to grow refs, and it has 54 lines of X-HAND-UNITS headroom (950) and 104 to the gate. describe_failure is 48 lines in refs (the pairwise ++# concatenation emits one format! per piece; a compiler-shape matter, not x's). Stays Paused; recheck on every regen, and resume the moment a change must grow refs.")
inl note_of_x_failure_text_dedup_6 () = Note ("X-FAILURE-TEXT-DEDUP", 6u32, "2026-11-04T01:13Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:13 x: ORPHAN SWEEP (x's corner): stays Paused. Its condition re-read on today's regen: refs is 901 (x-next1b A lines), so the 'nears 900' half holds, but no pending change grows refs, and X-JSON-COLLECT-DEDUP (Done today) showed the loops are already minimal. Owner x. Resume when a change has to grow refs or digest.")
