inl spiral_rust_net_text_guard () = Task ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", "compiler: a Rust build that reaches a lib body with no Rust-capable arm fails in Spiral with the definition's location, not later at cargo; covers both a .NET-only text body (no run_target/backend_switch) and a backend_switch missing its Rust arm (today: compile_error! in the generated .rs)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 95u32, Planned, "LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES,PACK-10", "compiler,rust,backends,lib,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard () = Planning ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-10-29T17:55Z", "main", "10-29 13:5x main: split from LIB-DOTNET-ONLY-BODIES (closed today; its n11 left \"the compile-time guard ... compiler lane\"). Two shapes reach cargo today. (1) A general function whose only body is .NET text compiles silently into Rust as F# text (n1 census: am'.map', average, distinct, skip_while, parallel_map, transpose, sm'.join'; all Rust-reachable ones now have arms). (2) A backend_switch without a Rust arm emits compile_error!(\"backend_switch: ...no Rust arm yet\"), so Spiral->Rust passes and only cargo fails. kit hit this at 13:21 in TRAIN4 (file_system.create_directory from kit_schedule), and a node \"compiles\" verdict missed it. Interim safety net: i574n.github/scripts/node-regen-compare.ps1 now lists \"candidate crates with a missing backend arm\" (kit, positive-controlled), and the backend-switch-idiom memory says to grep the generated .rs. Done when a Rust build reaching either shape exits non-zero in Spiral naming the def and its file:line, with one regression sample per shape (regression-samples-allowed), shipped in a pack.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-10-29T19:13Z", "main", "10-29 15:1x main: third shape for this guard, on TypeScript. A backend_switch without a TS arm compiles to `(() => { throw new Error(\"backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no TypeScript arm yet\") })()`, so the program builds and fails only at RUN time, and only on the path that reaches it. Found with convert.i64 / u64 / i32, which have no TS arm (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n61). The guard should report a reached missing arm at Spiral compile time on every backend, not just Rust's compile_error!.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-10-29T19:51Z", "main", "10-29 15:5x main: the convert.i32/i64/u64 TS missing arms named in n2 are filled by a lib draft (LIB-MACRO-HEAL n72, landing next window). The guard itself is still needed for the other missing arms.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-10-29T20:59Z", "compiler", "10-29 17:0x compiler: DESIGN (read-only; finishing it needs a build). Why lib cannot do this alone: !!!!BackendSwitch evaluates EVERY arm on every backend for type validation, and the validation pass (spiral_compiler.fs:153661) runs the other arms with {s with backend = <that arm's backend>; seq = a fresh ResizeArray; i = backend_switch_validation_tags () (a counter from 1<<<30)}. Inside an arm, s.backend names the arm's own backend, not the build target, so a compile-time error in lib's fallback would fire on every backend. That is why lib/spiral/backend.spi:19-60 emits TEXT instead (Rust compile_error!, Zig @compileError, TypeScript throw, bare identifiers on C/Delphi/...), and the failure only surfaces at cargo/zig/run time.\nFix shape: (1) core, both cores as a section pair: an explicit 'validating' flag in the env, set at the validation site next to the fresh seq and tag counter (plus single-flight's twin of :153661). (2) A new intrinsic, e.g. !!!!MissingBackendArm('<msg>'): under validation it returns a placeholder of the arm's type t (what the text arms do today); on the emitting path it raises a Spiral type error naming the definition and its file:line. (3) lib backend.spi's `_ =>` fallback arms (and the .NET-only general-body case this item lists) switch from compile_error!/throw text to the intrinsic. That covers Rust, TypeScript (n2) and every other backend at once.\nGate: two regression samples (a backend_switch missing the built backend's arm; a .NET-only text body reached from Rust) must fail in Spiral with file:line, while a program that only VALIDATES those arms (built for a backend that has its arm) still compiles. Order: the compiler install first, then the lib change (the old compiler does not know the intrinsic).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-10-29T21:49Z", "main", "10-29 17:5x main: MEASURED missing-arm census of the live lib (after window 2), a target list for this guard. Tool: C:\\home\\data\\main-drafts\\session-1029\\missing-arm-census.ps1; rows in missing-arm-census.tsv beside it (file, line, owner def, arms present, arms missing). 243 backend_switch blocks; missing TypeScript 144, Zig 132, Lua 106, Python 84, Fsharp 76, Gleam 65, Rust 47. Many are deliberately single-backend helpers (the am' Rust vec/slice family). The general-purpose ones are what matter. convert.try_convert, unbox, f32 and f64 have NO TypeScript arm, so convert.convert (= try_convert |> optionm.value) THROWS at run time on TS; today's i32/i64/u64 TS arms fixed only those three. Most of date_time (get_year, format, format_iso8601, min/max_value, to_calendar_utc, unix_epoch, specify_date_kind, to_universal_time, time_span, date_time_utc) is also missing on TS. Every one of those is a silent runtime throw today. With this guard each would become a compile-time error naming the def, but only if the guard ignores helpers that are intentionally single-backend, so it must fire only on a REACHED missing arm. CORRECTION 17:5x (measured right after posting): convert.convert does NOT throw on TS for an integer case. `convert.convert 5i32 : i64` compiles and prints 5 under bun, so try_convert's numeric cases resolve before its backend_switch is reached, and the census counts blocks, not reachable throws. The census is a list of CANDIDATE sites to probe per backend, not of confirmed bugs; only measured cases count as bugs (convert.i32/i64/u64 on TS were measured).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-10-31T01:47Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:47 compiler: DECISION before code. The n4 design is narrowed: no new env field and no new intrinsic are needed. The single-flight core (shared with hopacsota) already carries the exact flag n4 asked for: RustGapTrace.validating, a ThreadLocal depth that BackendSwitch raises around every non-current arm (spiral_compiler.fs:160941-160946) and that hopacsota hands to its body threads through the validating/set_validating seams (159801). lib's fallbacks already emit a fixed marker on every native/TS backend. Change, one applier (packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1, core only, lib untouched): (1) RustGapTrace gets `missing_arm`, one regex for lib's markers: `backend_switch[^\\n]*?\\bno\\s+\\w+\\s+arm` (Rust 'has no Rust arm yet' / 'no Rust arm (lib/spiral)', Zig, TypeScript incl. its line-continued text) and `backend_switch_has_no_\\w+_arm_in_lib_spiral` (Lean, C, Delphi, Wasm, Bend, Kotlin, Swift). (2) In EMacro, when validating = 0 (the build's own path) and an MText matches, raise_type_error with the macro text; the error carries s.trace, so it names the calling defs with file:line. (3) SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 keeps its survey role: it reports every reached gap and does NOT raise, so one compile still lists all of them. Scope: shape 2 (backend_switch without the build's arm) on all 12 marked backends, including the unit-typed Rust/TS fallbacks, which today compile to a silent no-op comment. Not covered: shape 1 (a general function whose only body is .NET text; there is no marker to key on), and the Gleam/Lua/Fsharp/Python/Cpp fallbacks, which emit placeholders without a marker (a lib follow-up can add one). gpt5parallel core: not touched (no RustGapTrace there). Regression samples (one per shape covered): backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (a marker on the build path -> ExpectedErrors 'no C arm' style Contains) and backend_switch_validated_gap (all 16 arms, only Bend's is a marker -> compiles everywhere the matrix builds it, since Bend is only validated). Gate (status-changing pack): paired predict run (pack9-predict method) -> residual set P must be EMPTY, and every ok->error row must have the new error text; that error set is the list of reached lib gaps, each answered by a lib arm (lib first) or accepted as a real gap before the pack lands. Corpus check (tibia + kit + Kino lib tests) before landing, because unit-typed Rust fallbacks may be reached there today.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-10-31T01:53Z", "compiler", "10-30 21:53 compiler: AMENDS n6 before any build. BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES n2 (main) shows the UNIT fallbacks are load-bearing: date_time's 45 type-level calls rely on a missing arm being a silent () (Gleam-only imports). So the guard no longer matches the unit markers ('backend_switch: no Rust arm (lib/spiral)', 'no TypeScript arm (lib/spiral)'); regex now: 'backend_switch: this lib/spiral function has no[\\s\\\\]+\\w+\\s+arm|backend_switch_has_no_\\w+_arm_in_lib_spiral' (non-unit Rust/Zig/TS text, the unbound-identifier arms, and console/convert/sm' per-type gap markers such as console.spi:33 C printf of an unsupported type). Drafts written, unstaged, not built: apps/compiler/packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 (2 anchors, dry-run 1x each on today's core), samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (+ matrix.psd1 ExpectedErrors 'reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the') and samples/backend_switch_validated_gap (16 arms, only Bend's is a marker). Relation to main's strict-blast: strict makes a missing NON-UNIT arm an error on EVERY build (validation included); this guard fires only on the arm the build actually emits, in core, lib untouched. If strict lands, this guard still covers the per-type gap markers and any switch written directly with !!!!BackendSwitch. Next: Debug candidate build in a free heavy slot (kit-train5 holds it now), then the paired predict run.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 8u32, "2026-10-31T02:19Z", "compiler", "10-30 22:19 compiler: BUILT and SMOKED. Candidate 'guard' (live core 4191A77F + packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 -> core BEFEDEC7AA8EB280, host EEBCBFF8658A1EF9 = installed host; Release, 359 s). probe.ps1: backend_switch_missing_arm_negative -> exit 5 'TracedError: This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the Rust backend ... Macro: backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral', trace innermost-first with file:line:col and caret (main.spi:1:9 and 2:5), same on C; backend_switch_validated_gap -> ok on C (133 B) and Rust (504 B). CONTROL: the installed compiler compiles the negative sample ok (exit 0, 193 B), so the error is the guard's. BLAST RADIUS, reasoned from the markers: every matched non-unit marker except TypeScript's already fails the NATIVE build today (Rust compile_error!, Zig @compileError, unbound identifiers on Lean/C/Delphi/Wasm/Bend/Kotlin/Swift, and console/convert/sm' per-type markers are the same kind), so a test or consumer that builds green today cannot reach one; the guard only moves those failures earlier. New failures are possible only where a reached marker never ran: TypeScript (run-time throw on an unexecuted path) and compile-only flows. Corpus check, therefore: lib_gate with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL=<guard> on the lib files whose tests target TypeScript (18 files, 114 TS target lines; base 20, sm' 17, parsing 17, date_time 8, reflection 7, env 6, listm' 5, seq 5, ...). main's strict-blast (BACKEND-SWITCH-PROTOTYPES) already measures a superset for sm'/date_time/base/file_system/am' (any reached non-unit missing arm on any target), so those 5 read from its strict-blast.txt instead of a second run. Queued: job-1793412988469-compiler-guard-predict (matrix predict, after PACK-9's; the matrix imports samples/core, not lib, so expect P empty and 0 status diffs).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 9u32, "2026-10-31T04:55Z", "compiler", "10-31 00:55 compiler: NOT in pack 9, and two open points before it can ship. (1) pair-drift refuses a single-flight-only PartEval change ('PartEval changed only on the single-flight side'), so the guard needs a gpt5parallel side. gpt5parallel has no RustGapTrace and runs evaluation on Hopac jobs, so a ThreadLocal depth is not reliable there. The stateless test 's.i.Value >= 1 <<< 30' (validation uses backend_switch_validation_tags) is WRONG: join point bodies reset i = ref 0 (spiral_compiler.fs:160066, 160497), so a body first evaluated inside a validation would look non-validating. The gpt5parallel side needs a real 'validating' flag on its env, set at its two validation sites (153672 replay, 155487 validation). (2) A hopacsota hazard in the single-flight side as drafted: Session.Defer's body runs with adapter.set_validating 0 (~54059). A join point body spawned DURING another backend's arm validation therefore runs as not-validating, and under the parallel policy the guard could fire on that other backend's marker. RustGapTrace.report has the same blind spot today. Fix: capture adapter.validating () at spawn time and restore THAT value in the body, not 0 (a hopacsota-layer edit; written as its own decision before code). Neither affects the single-flight results measured in n8.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_10 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 10u32, "2026-10-31T07:18Z", "compiler", "10-31 03:18 compiler: the guard's samples are OUT of the repo (user 01:5x: repo drift). Their whole content, so this note is the record: samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative/main.spi = ':= main () : i32 = /     $'backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral' : i32' with the usual 'packages: core- / modules: main' spiproj and the matrix ExpectedErrors line Contains 'reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the'. samples/backend_switch_validated_gap/main.spi = a !!!!BackendSwitch over all 16 keys returning 0i32, where only Bend's arm is $'backend_switch_has_no_Bend_arm_in_lib_spiral' : i32. Both are re-created in samples/ when the guard ships. Copies: C:\\home\\data\\rescue-2026-10-31\\spiral-packs\\missing-arm-guard.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_11 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 11u32, "2026-10-31T07:38Z", "compiler", "10-31 03:38 compiler: the gpt5parallel side COMPILES. Candidate guard2 = live core + packs/apply-missing-arm-guard.ps1 (7 edits: RustGapTrace regex + EMacro guard on single-flight; validating field, 5 carries, root 0, both validation sites +1, EMacro guard on gpt5parallel), Debug gpt5parallel build in a private cache: 'Build succeeded' (1,166 s). pair-drift on that core: 1 pair touched, 0 one-sided. Lib corpus over the 14 TS-target files (Normal priority): no line in any run matched the guard's error text; the failing tests also fail with the installed compiler and vary between runs in both directions (reflection: installed 6 failures, guard 3; listm': 'append' vs 'distinct'), i.e. load flakiness, not the guard. Ready for the pack after 9, with its two samples re-created from n10.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_12 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 12u32, "2026-10-31T11:35Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:35 compiler: STALE note fixed (groom): the 03:38 gpt5parallel side of the guard is DROPPED; after the retirement only the single-flight half exists (2 edits: RustGapTrace.missing_arm regex + the EMacro raise when validating = 0), and it ships in PACK-10. At the PACK-10 install the two samples and the ExpectedErrors line come back from n10. Open hazard: Session.Defer runs 'adapter.set_validating 0', so a deferred hopacsota body can raise the guard where single-flight would not; checked on the corpus only for single-flight.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_13 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 13u32, "2026-10-31T11:40Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 07:40 compiler: Defer HAZARD ANALYSED (read only, live core). Session.Defer (spiral_compiler.fs 54036-54069) runs every deferred body with adapter.set_validating 0 (54059), and the single-flight adapter maps that to RustGapTrace.validating (159801). Single-flight never resets the counter at a join point: a body first reached while backend_switch validation type-checks another backend's arm (the counter is raised at 160941-160946) runs with validating > 0, so the guard stays silent there. Hopacsota resets it to 0 on purpose, because under parallel the first claimer of a join-point key is racy, so inheriting the caller's context would make the result schedule-dependent. CONSEQUENCE: under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY bfs, random:<seed> or parallel (NOT the default 'inline', which never defers; 54011), a join point reached only from a non-selected arm, whose body hits a lib backend_switch with no arm for the target, raises the guard in hopacsota where single-flight accepts. FIX DESIGN (context-free, so deterministic on both engines): drop the validating test from the EMacro raise and check the OUTPUT instead: after codegen, fail iff the missing-arm marker survived into the emitted program (that is exactly 'the output would not build'). Report the macro's first source location, collected at EMacro time without raising. This is also stricter than today, since a validation-time body that does get emitted still yields compile_error! in the .rs with the current guard. NEEDS: one discriminating sample (non-selected C arm -> join point -> lib switch with no Rust arm) on a hopacsota build with the guard. Waits for the PACK-10 install (main's install-when-clear) plus a heavy window for the hopacsota candidate.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_14 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 14u32, "2026-10-31T14:06Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 10:06 compiler: CONTEXT-FREE GUARD DRAFTED (pending, PACK-11): apps/compiler/packs/apply-missing-arm-guard-output.ps1, 4 edits stacked on PACK-10's guard; every anchor 1x on PACK-10's candidate core source (core-src\\single-flight\\5b4655ec4bf5990b), scratch apply ok, DONE guard trips on re-run. (1) RustGapTrace gains missing_arm_sites, a ConcurrentDictionary from marker to call chain (the ordinally smallest chain wins, so the report doesn't depend on evaluation order), plus clear_missing_arm / note_missing_arm / missing_arm_error. (2) EMacro no longer raises: it records the site (SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 still prints every one). (3) build_many clears the sites before peval. (4) After codegen, a build is BuildFatalError exactly when a missing-arm marker is in the EMITTED code. The message keeps 'This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the <backend> backend ...' (so n10's ExpectedErrors Contains still holds) and adds 'Reached from: <chain>'. Effect: the verdict is the same on single-flight and every hopacsota policy (Defer's validating = 0 no longer matters), and a body first reached during validation but later emitted is now caught in Spiral instead of at cargo. Corpus: 0 generated outputs in any repo contain a marker (only lib backend/console/convert/sm' sources do), so no current row should flip. Proof still owed: candidate build, the two guard samples on single-flight AND SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel, and the predicted set (expected P = 0, verdict diffs only the guard samples).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_15 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 15u32, "2026-10-31T19:11Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 14:59 compiler: SHIPPED in PACK-10 (installed 14:30). Its samples are back in apps/compiler/tmp/samples with the ExpectedErrors line, accepted (PACK-10 n3): the negative errors with 'This program reaches a lib/spiral backend_switch that has no arm for the <backend> backend' on all 4 backends, validated_gap compiles. LEFT: the context-free v2 (packs/apply-missing-arm-guard-output.ps1, PACK-11), whose 4 anchors now match the live core.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_16 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 16u32, "2026-11-01T00:33Z", "compiler", "10-31 10-31 20:32 compiler: v2 SHIPPED in PACK-11 (installed 20:29): the guard now decides from the emitted output and names the call chain ('Reached from: ...'); the negative sample errors that way on all 4 backends at parity. Remaining proof for Done: the two guard samples under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel on a hopacsota build (the agreement v2 exists for).")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_17 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 17u32, "2026-11-03T07:31Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (55 h). Next: the two guard samples under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel on a hopacsota build, then Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_rust_net_text_guard_18 () = Note ("SPIRAL-RUST-NET-TEXT-GUARD", 18u32, "2026-11-11T00:29Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:3x compiler (groom): the remaining step (the two guard samples under SPIRAL_HOPACSOTA_POLICY=parallel on a hopacsota build) can ride the hopacsota build that HOPACSOTA-P3s queued ganymede run produces (~/remote-stage/hsp3/cache, hopacsota mode from pack89 + ivar + shard). Wait row on that run; then compile the two guard samples with that build and the policy set, and close if both fail in Spiral at the definitions location.")
inl kit_unbuilt_probe_crates () = Task ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", "kit: 89 src/*_probe crates are outside the Cargo workspace and no runner builds them; archive the unreached ones and drop their census rows", TaskKind, P3, D2, 45u32, Planned, "KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN", "kit,cleanup,probes,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates () = Planning ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_1 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-10-29T18:00Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T18:00 kit: measured on the TRAIN4 tree. 89 src/*_probe dirs are not members of src/Cargo.toml. kit_dev_spiral_tests/registry.spi names none of the sampled ones (filesystem_copy_policy_probe, bundle_verify_policy_probe, rust_std_fs_strict_read_probe); only standards/census.spi baselines count them. They carry most of kit's /tmp literals (Linux era; on Windows they write C:\\tmp). Plan: for each dir, grep its stem in kit and sibling repos (dangling-check-by-stem). Archive the unreached ones with scratch-archive.ps1 (verified, restorable), drop their census rows in the same change, and land it in a train after TRAIN4.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_2 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-10-29T18:27Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T18:27 kit: stem check (dangling-check-by-stem) done for all 89. Sibling repos: none in spiral (apps, lib, scripts; deps excluded). i574n.github has only backlog prose (agile.spi x5, done-notes x1). Inside kit, KEEP 2: kit_coverage_smoke (a workspace member) reads src/filesystem_batch_write_plan_probe/{main,rollback_plan}.spi and src/filesystem_action_batch_plan_probe/main.spi as fixture text. Leave kit/state data untouched (migration.spi, consolidation_template.spi and consolidation_fixtures.spi name some stems). state/migration.spi is a hashed gate input in contracts_entry and cold_proof_domain, so it is never respelled or edited by this cleanup. Archive set = the other 87. standards/census.spi drops their rows (1513 refs) in the same change, then census --ratchet re-proves. Stem list: %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--home-git\\fc1568a6-c6d6-4cdf-9135-008e972e514a\\scratchpad\\probe-stems.txt Durable copy of the stem list: C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tmp-leaves\\probe-stems.txt (the scratchpad copy does not survive this session).")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_3 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-10-29T23:20Z", "kit", "2026-10-29T23:20 kit: PLAN CORRECTION. The 89 probes are not dead weight. Many are the only compile path for a contract package. Of 45 *_contract/*_bindings packages without a Cargo.toml, 26 are imported by a workspace member (live; regen compiles their reachable parts), and 19 are imported ONLY by these probes: bootstrap_experiment_contract, cold_proof_contract, differential_catalog_bindings, differential_catalog_contract, differential_normalization_contract, dogfood_shim_contract, exact_replace_contract, family_contract_bindings, family_contract_contract, filesystem_copy_contract, filesystem_move_contract, filesystem_roundtrip_contract, hash_integrity_contract, parallel_search_contract, parallel_workstream_contract, patch_mutation_contract, patch_preflight_contract, path_tag_contract, portable_toolchain_contract. Archiving the probes alone would leave those 19 uncompiled. That is how path_tag_contract's column-0 parse error survived until today (KIT-PATH-TAG-PARSE was proven only by compiling path_tag_policy_probe with a bundle-only Cargo.toml). New rule per family: (a) if a probe-only contract is unreferenced elsewhere (dangling-check-by-stem across repos), archive the probes AND the contract together; (b) if it is still wanted, give the contract its own test block (notebook) so it compiles without the probe, then archive the probe. Probes whose contracts all have member importers can go as planned. The 2 fixture dirs read by kit_coverage_smoke stay.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_4 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-10-30T00:13Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T00:13 kit: CORRECTION to n3's count (19 -> 12), now TRANSITIVE. A breadth-first walk of package.spiproj imports from the 256 workspace members reaches 341 of 447 packages. Of the 45 *_contract/*_bindings packages without a Cargo.toml, 33 are reachable (n3 missed intermediate importers such as legacy_operations_entry, proxy_search_entry, patch_*_app, product_diff_contract) and 12 are UNREACHABLE from any crate: bootstrap_experiment_contract, cold_proof_contract, differential_catalog_bindings, differential_catalog_contract, differential_normalization_contract, family_contract_bindings, family_contract_contract, filesystem_copy_contract, filesystem_move_contract, filesystem_roundtrip_contract, parallel_workstream_contract, portable_toolchain_contract. None has a test module, and the only importers are their own *_negative_probe/*_policy_probe dirs. Cross-repo: 0 references in spiral (deps excluded; the glob is positive-controlled) and only backlog prose in i574n.github. So these 12 families (each contract plus its probes) are the archive set, to move together with scratch-archive.ps1 (verified, restorable). Every other probe stays until its contract gets its own test block. Controls: kit_standards and path_tag_contract both show reachable.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_5 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-10-30T01:58Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T01:58 kit groom (user, via main 21:54): analysis complete (n2-n4: stem check, keep the 2 kit_coverage_smoke fixture dirs, transitive set of 12 dead families); progress 0 -> 30, Planned -> Active. Estimate 60 -> 90m (12 families: archive with verification, drop their census rows, census --ratchet). Deps EOIE-RELEASE-TRAIN: it edits src, so it lands in a train after TRAIN5.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_6 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-10-30T02:17Z", "kit", "2026-10-30T02:17 kit: archive step ready. The set is C:\\home\\data\\kit-drafts\\tmp-leaves\\dead-families.txt: 27 dirs, the 12 unreachable contracts plus their 15 importing probes. Closure checks: 0 of the 15 are crates, and 0 packages import them. archive-dead-families.ps1 (same folder) is dry-run by default (set 27; census rows dropped 459 of 7380). With -Apply it refuses on a tree lease or any import from outside the set, copies each dir to kit-drafts\\archive\\<stamp>-dead-probe-families, checks the copy with per-file sha256, removes the source only after a match, and rewrites standards/census.spi without the 459 rows. After apply: 'kit standards --ratchet standards/census.spi' and a regen must stay green; ships in a train after TRAIN5. Progress 30 -> 45.")
inl note_of_kit_unbuilt_probe_crates_7 () = Note ("KIT-UNBUILT-PROBE-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T03:36Z", "kit", "10-31 23:4x kit groom (main 23:33, user: cadence/drift): Active -> PLANNED. No note in over 12 h, so nobody is working it this week; Planned keeps it in agile next at its priority (never auto-Paused, R3). First step when picked = the next step in its latest note.")
