inl note_of_cells_rust_opt_level_1 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL", 1u32, "2026-11-01T03:04Z", "kit", "10-31 23:1x kit, found during a node CPU/memory check (user: 'check if there's something abnormal draining cpu or memory in the node, fix what you find'). EVIDENCE: Cells compiles every Rust test cell with @rust_flags [\"-C\", \"opt-level=2\", ...] (spiral/apps/cells/lib/spiral/cells.ex:882). On the node, fiscal's job-1793494454747-fiscal-p8-t1031u-ubl-drift spent one cell's rustc (pid 24232, cell 1793494746767-11330: main.rs 6,898,306 bytes, generated_ubl_invoice_2_1) 4,988 CPU-seconds and 3.1 GB private memory by 23:00, still running; fiscal had raised that unit's timeout to 18,000,000 ms to let it finish. Gate cells check correctness, so most of that optimizer time buys nothing. PLAN (measure first, memory experiment-until-sota): on a quiet node window, compile 3 cells (this UBL unit, one mid-size kit CLI notebook cell, one lib cell) at opt-level 0, 1 and 2; record rustc wall time, peak memory, run time and pass/fail. RISK to test explicitly: Spiral's recursive output may rely on LLVM sibling-call optimization at opt-level 2; at opt-level 0 deep recursion can overflow the stack, so include a deep-recursion lib test (listm' folds over long lists) in the matrix. Likely outcome: opt-level=1 (or 0 plus an opt-in per-cell header like '///- --opt 2' for the few that need it). Owner: Cells (main / x per cells-in-spiral-x-leads); the cells.ex change lands through the Cells landing process, not a direct edit.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_opt_level_2 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL", 2u32, "2026-11-07T22:08Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. DoD: CPU-seconds and pass/fail of the same Rust-heavy Cells gate (fiscal ubl drift cell) at opt-level 0, 1 and 2 on the node, the chosen default set in cells.ex @rust_flags, and no test slower than its timeout. Next step: x picks the probe cell; run the three levels back to back in one node job. Coordinate with the spiral nightly 11-05 pass, which edits cells.ex:875 in the same area.")
inl note_of_cells_rust_opt_level_3 () = Note ("CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL", 3u32, "2026-11-09T16:24Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 12:2x microblog (now co-owner with org), PROBE CELLS PICKED and the RULE PRE-REGISTERED before any timing. Context from today's x data (CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS n28): rustc is 974 s of 23,111 s of x client cell time (4.2%), so for x the lever is small; the payoff is fiscal's giant cells (a 6.9 MB main.rs, 83+ CPU-min at O2). PROBES: (1) fiscal generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 test cell (heaviest); (2) x apps/client wire.spi's largest rust test cell (mid-size, 32 cells in the file); (3) lib sm'.spi's first rust test cell (small). Each cell's main.rs is captured once with Cells keep_files, then 'rustc +<pinned channel> -C opt-level=N -A warnings --edition 2024' for N = 0, 1, 2, 3 rounds each, rotated order, recording wall, CPU seconds, peak private MB, binary run time and the test verdict. RULE: adopt the lowest N whose verdict matches O2 on all 3 probes AND whose binary run time stays under 25% of the cell's timeout on the heaviest probe; tie-break on total (compile + run) CPU. A probe whose O0 run blows its timeout keeps O1 as the floor. Default stays 2 if no N < 2 passes. next: stage the capture + 3-level job for a quiet node slot (moon after the sim verify, or europa saturation with a 1536M cap, which may be too small for fiscal at O2: compare against the 3.1 GB measured 10-31).")
inl mirror_transfer_cost () = Task ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", "Cut the profile-mirror transfer cost: the archive is ~800 MB (transcripts ~360 MB) and a full tar+scp every ~50 min over the slow link takes 40-60 min and overlaps runs; and the post-scp remote verify can stall", TaskKind, P1, D2, 70u32, Blocked, "", "team:org,main,wipe,backup,durability,management", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_mirror_transfer_cost () = Planning ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_1 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T03:35Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no window): (a) measure transcript growth rate; (b) either raise the mirror due-interval to 3h (the daily restore drill + 3h freshness guard still cover it) or split transcripts into a separate less-frequent archive; (c) bound the remote-verify ssh with a 60s job timeout, treating a timeout as 'archive landed, verify deferred to the drill'. Prove on one run. Filed from PROFILE-MIRROR-TO-NODE n19/n21.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_2 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 2u32, "2026-11-01T06:22Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 02:3x DESIGN consolidation (resilience) - tonight three items converged on one \"mirror v2\" shape; recording so they are built together, not thrice. The hourly profile-mirror re-tars its whole ~800MB set every run and keeps 48 copies; it also cannot grow to cover large stores without bloating every run (see WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER n5 mirror-coverage review + KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR).\nMIRROR V2 (two lanes by data shape):\n1. HOT lane (keep the existing every-~50-min job, make it LEAN): memory, transcripts, config, kit\\jobs 4MB, kit\\windows, fiscal-undo, tibia dumps. Transfer-cost cut = stop full re-tar: the transcripts (.claude\\projects, the ~800MB bulk) are append-mostly, so send only files changed since the last run (mtime manifest) into a per-run incremental tar, or rsync -az over ssh if available on both ends. Target: each run moves MB not ~800MB.\n2. COLD lane (NEW daily job, Priority 5): large append-only / rarely-changing stores - kit\\archive 1.5GB (KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR) and MOVE kit\\backups 120MB here off the hot lane. Incremental (rsync or mtime-delta), not re-tarred.\n3. ONE-SHOT: irreplaceable one-time artifacts (rescue dumps) get a manual archive to node (rescue-2026-10-28 done 11-01), never a recurring lane.\nNEXT STEP (next hour): check rsync availability box+node (one probe); if present, both lanes become rsync -az over ssh = native incremental, simplest. Else mtime-delta tar+scp. Build COLD lane first (closes kit ask d), then convert HOT to incremental. Ties: MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST + KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR + WIPE-DAMAGE-LEDGER n5.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_3 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 3u32, "2026-11-01T12:37Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 08:3x QUALITY PASS (resilience, round-4). FIELDS: estimate 90m, load light, assignee agent, team resilience, deps KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (shares the kit dir-mirror verb).\nDEFINITION OF DONE (verifiable, with a THRESHOLD): the hourly profile mirror moves < 25 MB on a run where only memory/transcripts changed since the last run (today it re-tars ~800 MB every run); measured by the mirror log''s bytes-sent line across two consecutive runs with no large change between. Artifact: the HOT lane converted to incremental (the kit dir-mirror verb from KIT-ARCHIVE), a log showing bytes-sent dropping ~800MB -> <25MB, and kit\\backups 120MB moved to the daily COLD lane.\nNEXT CONCRETE STEP: after the kit dir-mirror verb exists (KIT-ARCHIVE), point the HOT profile-mirror at it in incremental mode and measure two runs.\nSTILL OPEN (honest, this is the round-4 under-specified item): the TYPED incremental-selection design for the HOT lane (transcripts are append-mostly but memory files are rewritten in place, so \"newer than stamp\" must distinguish appended vs rewritten to avoid re-sending whole rewritten files) is NOT yet modelled — it needs the same mem-metric-style typing as the BEAM cap but for a change_kind union (Appended | Rewritten | New | Unchanged). Until that''s designed, this item is a goal with a threshold but not a buildable plan.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_4 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:24Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 15:xx MEASURED + TYPED INCREMENTAL DESIGN (resilience, autonomous run) - closes the round-4 \"goal with a threshold but not a buildable plan\" gap.\n\nMEASURED 11-01 (grounds the threshold): the HOT-lane bulk is ~99% TRANSCRIPTS. .claude\\projects\\C--home-git\\*.jsonl = 8 files, 486.1 MB (biggest 139/82/74/68/54 MB), APPEND-ONLY (sessions only grow at the tail). Memory store = 250 files, 0.63 MB TOTAL (avg 2.6 KB), REWRITTEN in place. So the full-re-tar waste is almost entirely re-sending 486 MB of transcript whose only change each run is a few MB of appended tail; memory at 0.63 MB is negligible (send whole every run, free).\n\nTYPED change_kind design (the piece that was missing):\n- change_kind union (closed): | Appended : {from_offset : u64} | Rewritten | New | Unchanged.\n- manifest row per file (typed, from the last run): { path; size : u64; mtime : instant (unix_seconds newtype); prefix_sha256 : hash } where prefix_sha256 hashes the PREVIOUS full content.\n- classify current vs prev (pure, testable):\n  - not in prev            -> New        (send whole, then record)\n  - size==prev && mtime==prev -> Unchanged (send 0 bytes)\n  - size >= prev.size AND sha256(first prev.size bytes of current) == prev.prefix_sha256 -> Appended {from_offset = prev.size}  (send ONLY bytes [prev.size .. size])\n  - else                   -> Rewritten  (send whole)\n  The prefix-hash check is what distinguishes a true append (transcript) from an in-place rewrite (memory/config) - a growing file whose prefix changed is Rewritten, not Appended, so we never ship a corrupt tail-patch.\n- transfer by kind: Appended = byte-range tail; New/Rewritten = whole file; Unchanged = nothing. Rebuild the manifest after a successful run. verify_outcome union for the post-scp check: | Verified | DeferredToDrill : {reason} - a remote-verify that stalls is bounded by a 60s job timeout and classified DeferredToDrill (the daily restore drill re-verifies), NOT a failure (note 1c).\n\nWHY THIS HITS THE DoD: on a run where only memory + one active transcript changed, bytes-sent = (appended transcript tail, a few MB) + (0.63 MB memory) << 25 MB, vs ~486 MB today. Buildable now: it is the kit dir-mirror verb's --hot mode (append-aware change_kind) alongside --incremental (mtime-delta, kit\\archive COLD lane) and --full (snapshot, drafts). One verb, three modes, shared size+hash verify. rsync -az --append-verify would do this natively on a LINUX endpoint (the Cloud VM, CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP) - but the node's Windows-cmd rsync channel fails (protocol 12, probed 11-01), so the typed change_kind tail-send is the NODE hot-lane path; rsync stays the VM path. Ties KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (same verb), CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP (rsync endpoint), BEAM-GUARD metric_kind (same typed-domain discipline). DoD threshold + plan now both defined; build rides the dir-mirror verb landing.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_5 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 5u32, "2026-11-01T23:55Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 19:5x SILENT MIRROR FAILURE caught (resilience durability health check). The profile-mirror-to-node scheduled task reports result=0 (last run 19:50) but the node's NEWEST profile-mirror tgz is from 13:24 = ~6.2h STALE. So the task 'succeeds' while transferring NOTHING - the primary backup of the memory store + transcripts + config has been silently frozen for 6.2h (a wipe now would lose 6.2h of memory edits, incl. tonight's ~12 new/edited memories). TWO root issues: (1) VICIOUS CYCLE with node disk: the mirror keeps 48 copies x ~313MB = ~15GB on the node (hourly x 2 days). When the node filled tonight (4.4GB free), the scp of a new 313MB tgz FAILED - but profile-mirror.ps1 exits 0 / the task result stays 0, so the failure is invisible (the exact stale-reports-success class of HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD, now hitting MY OWN backup). (2) 48-copy retention is excessive: dropping to ~6-12 frees ~10GB on the node AND reduces the per-run pressure. FIXES: ran profile-mirror.ps1 manually NOW to catch up (node has 16.2GB after my cleanup, so it should land) - job b0uefnuvi. NEEDED: (a) profile-mirror.ps1 must FAIL LOUD on scp/verify failure (non-zero exit + a stale_after check so a >2h-old newest-tgz alarms), (b) cut retention to ~12. This is why a backup needs its own freshness monitor - 'the task ran' != 'the backup landed'. Ties HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD (apply its freshness reader to the mirror's own output), node disk, profile-mirror-to-node memory.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_6 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 6u32, "2026-11-02T00:38Z", "resilience", "2026-11-02 00:2x TRUE ROOT CAUSE of the 6.2h mirror freeze (resilience forensics - corrects my earlier 'scp failed/threw' guesses). It was a HUNG run, not a failed one: pid 28684, a profile-mirror.ps1 pwsh age 347min (~14:57), was still alive with a child SSH (pid 8652) stuck on the node VERIFY/PRUNE command (profile-mirror.ps1 line ~106 ssh ... pwsh -EncodedCommand). The ~14:57 run completed its scp, then the remote-verify ssh HUNG on the then-full node and NEVER returned - ssh -o ConnectTimeout=8 bounds CONNECT only, not a stuck remote command execution. The hung ssh held the pwsh alive 6h. Then profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 line 6 ('if a profile-mirror.ps1 is already running, return') silently SKIPPED every scheduled fire for 6h - the overlap-guard was blocked by the zombie. So: the backup didn't fail loud (it would have retried); it HUNG, and the overlap-guard turned one hang into a 6h freeze. FIXED NOW: killed the zombie 28684 + its ssh/conhost children -> overlap-guard slot freed, scheduled mirror unblocked (+ my manual hardened run is catching up). DURABLE FIXES landing: (1) ssh calls get -o ServerAliveInterval=15 -o ServerAliveCountMax=4 (a stuck remote command dies after ~60s of no response); (2) profile-mirror-if-due.ps1 line 6 kills a STALE (>90min) mirror process before skipping, so a future hang can't block retries. This is the sharpest lesson of the audit: a hung job is worse than a failed one - failure retries, a hang blocks silently behind the very guard meant to prevent double-runs.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_7 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 7u32, "2026-11-02T17:05Z", "resilience", "11-02 13:0x resilience: full profile mirror is CHRONICALLY stale - newest node archive 08:40 (193min/4.5h). Every run since stages the ~1.33GB payload (.claude/projects session transcripts dominate) but can't land it over the saturated link before the next run / a kill; box is 100% with TRAIN11's heavy window held ~5h more. profile-mirror-monitor alarms on it (PROFILE-MIRROR-SILENT-FAIL). MITIGATION applied now: re-protected the irreplaceable subset off-box via critical-now.tar (288 memory files + settings.json/gitconfig/ssh-config/remote-machines.json, byte-verified on node:C:/home/remote-archive/critical-now.tar) so this run's memory edits are safe while the full mirror lags. FIX = make the mirror INCREMENTAL (ship only files changed since last success by mtime) or TWO-TIER (small critical tier frequent + bulk-transcript tier infrequent); gated on a quiet window to safely rework the concurrently-edited, currently-running profile-mirror.ps1. Full mirror will catch up when TRAIN11 finishes / the link quiets.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_8 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 8u32, "2026-11-02T17:22Z", "resilience", "11-02 13:1x resilience: the hang-fix (bounded ssh/scp + cmd-makeDir) is in and correct, but testing reveals the bottleneck is BROADER than transfer: a -DryRun (STAGING ONLY, no remote) also exceeds 120s. So enumerating + copying ~28k files / ~400MB (the .claude/projects session transcripts + scratchpad + kit stores) into the snapshot is itself too slow under the 100%-CPU box. The full mirror cannot complete while the box is pegged (TRAIN11 holds the heavy window ~5h); it catches up when the box frees. This STRENGTHENS the incremental/two-tier redesign: full enumerate+copy+tar+transfer of EVERYTHING every run is unviable under load. Target design: (a) small CRITICAL tier (memory+configs, already proven via critical-now.tar, lands in seconds) on a frequent cadence; (b) bulk tier shipping only files changed since last success (mtime/robocopy-style) on a relaxed cadence. Critical data stays fresh regardless of the bulk tier's lag.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_9 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 9u32, "2026-11-02T17:25Z", "resilience", "11-02 13:2x resilience: MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST tier-1 DELIVERED + proven under load. critical-mirror.ps1 (scheduled PT15M, Priority 5) tars just the irreplaceable set - memory (288 files) + .claude/hooks + settings/gitconfig/ssh-config/remote-machines/wslconfig - ~1MB, bounded ssh/scp (cmd-makeDir, Invoke-Bounded 15s/60s), cmd forfiles retention (>2d). Test LANDED critical-20261102-132432.tar (1.02MB) on the node in seconds while the box is 100% and the big mirror can't complete - exactly the point: small critical tier is saturation-proof. So the irreplaceable data now stays fresh every 15min regardless of the big-mirror lag. Tier-2 (bulk, changed-files-only) still TODO for the full profile/transcripts; it rides the big mirror which now fail-fasts (no hang) + catches up when the box frees.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_10 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 10u32, "2026-11-02T23:42Z", "resilience", "11-02 19:40 resilience: profile-mirror RECURRENCE - stale 127min (newest 17:27) under the sustained TRAIN13 build saturation (3 build iterations, box 100pct CPU / ~1GB free for hours). Diagnosed: NOT a regression from the de-leak (node-env feeds correct NodeRoutes/NodeMirrorDir; the mirror runs + stages 1.5GB at 19:08, fails only at the 268MB scp over the saturated link; if-due exits 0 detached so the task result is meaningless - the independent monitor correctly caught it). CRITICAL data IS covered: critical-mirror (tier-1 ~1MB) still lands every 15min (newest 19:25), so memory/hooks/configs are safe; only the full-profile transcripts lag. This is exactly the transfer-cost this item targets: a full re-tar+scp cannot keep cadence when the box is saturated. Mitigation in place (critical-mirror covers the critical slice); the full fix is the incremental/changed-files mirror. Did NOT force a manual mirror (1.5GB stage + scp would worsen the crunch); it self-resolves when the box frees post-TRAIN13c.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_11 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 11u32, "2026-11-02T23:50Z", "resilience", "11-02 19:48 resilience: ROOT of the mirror lag + a reaper GAP found and the instance handled. A DETACHED profile-mirror.ps1 (pid 22128, parent dead via if-due) was stuck 57min on its 1.5GB tar (child tar.exe), thrashing under the TRAIN13c memory pressure - not landing, and holding ~1GB. Reaped it -> box free 1.8GB->2.8GB. GAP: orphan-reaper PreserveCmdMatch includes profile-mirror\\.ps1 (so a healthy mirror is never reaped), but that means a STUCK one grinds forever; the local tar is unbounded (only the ssh/scp go through Invoke-Bounded). FIX DIRECTION (defer, focused pass, no churn now): either (a) profile-mirror self-watchdog - abort if its own runtime exceeds ~20min, or (b) if-due kills any existing profile-mirror older than ~20min before launching a new one (normal run is a few min). Manual reap handled this instance; the next scheduled run retries fresh once the box frees. Ties to the incremental-mirror fix (a changed-files mirror would not stage 1.5GB each time, so it would not thrash).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_12 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 12u32, "2026-11-02T23:54Z", "resilience", "11-02 19:54 resilience: IMPLEMENTED the stuck-mirror auto-reap (not deferred - it would recur every build cycle). orphan-reaper.ps1 now sweeps pwsh running profile-mirror.ps1 > $MirrorStuckMinutes (30m, well past the ~11m its bounded ssh/scp allow, so it is thrashing in the unbounded tar) and kills it + its tar child, despite the PreserveCmdMatch that protects a HEALTHY mirror. Regex profile-mirror\\.ps1 matches the mirror but not profile-mirror-monitor.ps1. Parse-clean, comment-free, dry-run validated: 0 stuck now (reaped the live one manually), no false-positive on the monitor, still catches dangling git at the 3m short-grace. Runs every 15m via upkeep-tick, so a future stuck mirror dies within ~15m of crossing 30m - no manual reap. Unstaged. The deeper fix (incremental mirror that never stages 1.5GB) still stands on this item.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_13 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 13u32, "2026-11-03T09:56Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:0x resilience ROBUSTNESS FIX (adopted from x-dumps HURDLES n2: detached/atomic transfer so a partial scp never becomes the live backup). Found + fixed a real gap: profile-mirror.ps1 (scp :122) and critical-mirror.ps1 (scp :53) scp'd the tgz/tar STRAIGHT to the final name, so an interrupted scp left a TRUNCATED archive at the name readers glob (restore/monitor could pick up corruption). FIXED: both now scp to `<name>.part`, then `cmd /c move /Y <name>.part <name>` on success (atomic rename); on scp failure they `del` the `.part` (no accumulation on the disk-tight node). They already had ServerAliveInterval=15/CountMax=4 (hang protection) - the atomic promote was the missing half. Safe because every reader globs the final extension (profile-mirror-*.tgz / critical-*.tar), so a `.part` is invisible to retention, the monitor, and the restore drill until promoted; critical-mirror folds the move rc into $scpRc so its ok/FAIL line stays correct. Both parse-clean, UNSTAGED (scripts, user stages). Rule saved to memory verify-mirror-landing-on-node. This is a transfer-INTEGRITY fix (distinct from this item's transfer-COST/incremental work, but same scripts).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_14 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 14u32, "2026-11-03T09:59Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:00 resilience: atomic-scp fix (n13) VERIFIED working. Ran critical-mirror.ps1 manually: output \"1 file(s) moved.\" (the move /Y promoted critical-20261103-055838.tar.part -> critical-20261103-055838.tar) then \"ok: laptop ...critical-20261103-055838.tar 1.19MB sha256 ef54d44544f99f344efb7015e715ab56f75a2217d875fc05413e7cbdcf959b35\". So the atomic path works end-to-end (scp to .part, move on success, no partial at the live name, no stale .part left). profile-mirror.ps1 uses the identical pattern (will exercise on its next scheduled run). Durability hardening proven, UNSTAGED.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_15 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 15u32, "2026-11-03T10:05Z", "resilience", "11-03 06:05 resilience: added NODE-DISK monitoring to node-utilization-monitor.ps1 (adopted from x-dumps HURDLES n4 \"df in the heartbeat\"). It now ssh-queries the mirror node's C: free GB (reuses the EncodedCommand+Invoke-Bounded pattern) and ALARMS \"DISK-LOW\" when < 5GB (alongside the existing WASTE alarm; both fold into one $alarms list, exit 1 + note on low). Parse-clean, live-run confirmed: \"ok: box 64%, mirror 100%, compute 258%, node-free 10GB\". FINDING: the node (laptop) is at only 10GB free - approaching the <5GB zone where mirror scp/retention fails (the all-night node-full failure mode). Not alarming yet, but close; main's remote-archive (20GB) cleanup is the relevant reclaim. Rationale: the node is SHARED (profile-mirror tgz x~12 + critical tars + 1.65GB kit-archive seed + x-dumps backups + others), so it can fill from any lane and silently break MY mirrors; the monitor now catches it early instead of reactively. UNSTAGED. Pairs with the atomic-scp fix (n13/n14): atomic-scp prevents a corrupt partial backup; disk-low prevents no-backup-from-full-disk.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_16 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 16u32, "2026-11-03T11:29Z", "resilience", "11-03 07:2x resilience: node-disk + profile-mirror health investigation (prompted by node C: at 8-9GB, near the <5GB mirror-failure zone). FINDINGS (verify-before-asserting corrected an initial unbounded-drain alarm): (1) profile-mirror retention WORKS and is robust - box-side cmd prune (profile-mirror.ps1 L130-145: `cmd /c dir /b /o-d` + box-computed keep-set of newest-12 + daily<=14d + weekly<=56d + `del` via cmd) runs every cycle regardless of node load. The 17 tgz (~5GB) are within that tiered policy, not a leak; node free is STABLE ~9GB. (2) CLEANED an orphaned 348MB profile-mirror-20261103-063829.tgz.part (a dead prior run's incomplete transfer; the active run pid 2152 is a healthy new 07:05 run, slow-tarring 1.6GB under box 100%); del-on-scp-failure (L124) doesn't cover a run that dies after scp/before move, so orphan .parts can linger on the tight node - freed 348MB. (3) GAP: the remote-pwsh VERIFY step (sha256+entry-count match, L148-181) cold-starts pwsh on the node and SKIPS under saturation (L180), so tgz land+prune but integrity is often unconfirmed. FIX DIRECTION (same hardening as retention already got): compute the node-side hash via `certutil -hashfile <f> SHA256` over cmd (fast cold-start) instead of remote pwsh -EncodedCommand. Policy note for MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST: 12 retained x ~450MB compressed (1.66GB raw, 33897 files) = ~5GB on the node; tightening the newest-N or shrinking the archive (transcripts) both cut node pressure. No live-script edit made (box 100%, marathon depth; the certutil fix is a careful focused edit).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_17 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 17u32, "2026-11-03T18:45Z", "resilience", "11-03 14:4x resilience: MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST target IDENTIFIED (sized the profile-mirror sources). The bulk is .claude/projects = 959.6MB of 1106.8MB total (87%) = the session TRANSCRIPTS (.jsonl), with kit/backups 135MB a distant second; everything else <7MB. profile-mirror.ps1 copies $wholeTrees WHOLE (L59-60, Get-ChildItem -Recurse, no extension filter - the $sourceExtensions filter only applies to the TEMP scratch scan, L65), so all 960MB of transcripts are re-tarred + re-scp'd EVERY ~50min run even though transcripts are APPEND-ONLY (old ones immutable, only the current one grows). So ~87% of the transfer is re-copying unchanged data. DOUBLE WIN to cut it: lowers the transfer cost AND shortens the transfer so it is less exposed to the box-CPU-starvation that is failing backups (my 12:2x diagnosis). REDUCTION OPTIONS (implementation = a careful live-mirror change, deferred): (a) INCREMENTAL - tar only files changed since the last mirror (append-only delta), e.g. tar --listed-incremental or an mtime cutoff, with a periodic full base; (b) TIER - a small frequent profile mirror (everything but projects, ~150MB) + a separate less-frequent/incremental transcript archive; (c) cap transcripts to recent-N-days in the frequent mirror (but the 48-archive retention would age old ones out, so needs an immutable transcript base). The critical tier (memory/hooks/config, 1.2MB) is separately + reliably backed up by critical-mirror, so transcripts are purely a cost/reliability concern, not a critical-data one. Recommend (a) incremental as the cleanest. No live edit made (risky; focused pass).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_18 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 18u32, "2026-11-03T21:48Z", "resilience", "11-03 ~17:50 resilience MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST analysis (box-independent read of profile-mirror.ps1).\nPREMISE CONFIRMED (unlike SCRIPTS-DELEAK): the transfer is a FULL re-send every cycle. Mechanism: enumerate all $wholeTrees files incl .claude/projects transcripts (L59-61) -> copy to a snapshot dir (L86-91) -> tar -czf ONE profile-mirror-$stamp.tgz (L95) -> scp the WHOLE tarball to the node (L123, 600s timeout). No incrementality: every run re-tars + re-sends the entire transcript corpus even if a single transcript changed. Transcripts ~87% of the ~800-960MB.\nRESILIENCE ANGLE (elevates this above pure efficiency): THIS session's box-CPU-starvation made critical-mirror scp's slow/time out (real backup failures observed). An 800MB scp pushed through a starved box's throttled ssh is far likelier to time out than a small delta -> cutting transfer SIZE is a ROBUSTNESS win (the backup completes under starvation), directly mitigating the failure mode we hit.\nDESIGN (keep the current strength = point-in-time recoverability via tiered retention of full tarballs, while cutting per-run transfer):\n- RECOMMEND: delta-tar against a persisted local manifest (path -> size+mtime). Each run tars only NEW/CHANGED files -> small delta tgz; node keeps the delta chain; restore = last full baseline + replay deltas. Periodic (weekly) full re-baseline caps chain length. ~10-20x smaller per-run, snapshots preserved.\n- ALT: rsync --link-dest (incremental + snapshot via hardlinks) - but rsync is not native on the Windows node (fiddly to stand up).\n- REJECT: plain robocopy/rsync live-mirror - loses point-in-time snapshots (corruption propagates, no history to restore from).\nIMPLEMENTATION DEFERRED (deliberately): this is a CRITICAL live durability script; a wrong delta/manifest = silent incomplete mirror = data-loss. Must be built carefully + proven by a restore-drill (profile-restore-drill.ps1 exists) BEFORE replacing the working full-tar path - not a blind edit mid-run. The analysis + design here is the box-independent deliverable; the build is a careful staged task (good fit for a quiet box window with a drill gate).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_19 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 19u32, "2026-11-04T00:59Z", "resilience", "COVERAGE GAP found during the post-BSOD fragile window (20:55). The shared BACKLOG (docs/backlog/state/agile.spi + claims.spi, 14.4MB tar) is UNCOMMITTED (last repo commit 03:43) and BOX-ONLY: it lives in C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github, OUTSIDE profile-mirror/critical-mirror coverage (those are profile-only, cf backup-mirror-coverage-boundary), and the node has NO copy at C:\\home\\git\\i574n.github. It survives a CRASH (on-disk, reboot-safe - confirmed: intact through today's 19:44 BSOD) but a WIPE (cf the 10-28 profile wipe) would lose 16h+ of ALL lanes' agile work plus the BOX-BSOD-1103 incident record.\nBROADER: every C:\\home\\git repo's uncommitted lane WIP shares this wipe-exposure - git commit+push is the fleet's only off-box path for repo work, and lanes deliberately leave work unstaged (never-commit-leave-unstaged), so between user commits the WIP is box-only.\nINTERIM (done): scp'd a backlog tar to europa:~/backlog-stopgap (14.4MB, kept newest 2) - wipe-protected for now. Not a standing fix.\nFIX options: (a) a critical-mirror-style scheduled task that ships docs/backlog off-box on a cadence (it IS shared coordination state, highest-value uncovered item - treat like the profile's critical tier); (b) a periodic file-level C:\\home\\git WIP sync. Root cause is the profile-ONLY mirror boundary; the backlog is the highest-value thing it leaves uncovered.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_20 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 20u32, "2026-11-04T05:20Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:3x resilience MEASURED + REDUCTION DESIGN. Composition (raw): transcripts .claude/projects = 1042MB of 1213MB measured trees (86pct); rest small - kit\\backups 165MB, kit\\jobs 5MB, hooks/config/subagent-hold/autonomous <1MB. Transcript delta: 19 ACTIVE .jsonl = 910MB, ALL 19 show changed every 50min (mtime bumps on each append) so they re-ship WHOLE each run; only ~133MB (760 files) is stable/finished-session transcripts (also re-shipped every run). ROOT WASTE: the mirror tars+scps a FULL snapshot every ~50min, but active .jsonl are APPEND-ONLY (a 48MB transcript that grew 2MB re-ships all 48MB) and stable files re-ship unchanged. REDUCTION by impact: (1) APPEND-DELTA for active .jsonl - track a last-shipped byte offset per file, ship only bytes beyond it, append on the node; fallback to full re-ship if size shrank or a header hash changed (compaction/rewrite). Est 910MB -> tens of MB/run (~95pct cut). (2) SKIP-UNCHANGED manifest (mtime+size+sha) so stable transcripts + unchanged kit\\backups ship ONCE not every run (saves ~133MB + most of 165MB). (3) zstd instead of tar gzip (faster+smaller) if delta not adopted. Combined: each run ships a small delta, not ~1GB raw. IMPLEMENTATION: profile-mirror.ps1 is durability-critical + scheduled - do NOT patch mid-fragile-run; prototype on a scratch copy, test the append+reconstruct round-trip (ship, kill, re-ship, verify the node tgz reconstructs byte-identical) + a compaction-fallback test, THEN land in a calm window. critical-mirror already covers the small churny set (1.34MB) so profile-mirror cost IS the transcripts - the delta is the fix. Orthogonal to the C:\\home\\git backlog coverage gap (prior note).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_21 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 21u32, "2026-11-04T05:32Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:4x resilience APPEND-DELTA PROTOTYPE PROVEN (scratch, no live-script touch). mirror_delta_proto.ps1 simulates a growing .jsonl + a node copy over 5 ships. RESULTS: byte-identical reconstruction EVERY stage (sha256 src==node), INCLUDING after a compaction (file rewritten smaller -> detected by size<stored-offset -> full re-ship fallback, match=True). Mechanism: track a per-file byte offset; each run read [offset,EOF) via FileStream.Seek + append node-side; on size<offset re-ship full + reset. Saving in the (deliberately high-growth, 20pct/stage) synthetic was 31pct of naive-full; real active transcripts grow a few pct per 50min window so the real saving is ~90pct+. CORE MECHANISM DE-RISKED. REMAINING to land (careful, live durability script, calm window only): persist the offset manifest across runs (kit\\schedules\\profile-mirror-offsets.json), per-file delta + node-side append over ssh (not whole-tar), keep full-ship for small/config files + compaction fallback; prototype the ssh node-append on a SCRATCH COPY of profile-mirror.ps1, test full->kill->re-ship->verify under a simulated stall, then land. Evidence: scratch/mirror_delta_proto.ps1.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_22 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 22u32, "2026-11-04T07:55Z", "resilience", "11-04 03:5x resilience COVERAGE GAP REFINED (reassuring): the un-landed WIP in C:\\home\\git (growing as trains go red - 4-5 tonight) IS backed up off-box after all. unstaged-backup.ps1 (kit-unstaged-backup job, periodic, exit 0) captures per-repo git diff --binary (unstaged) + --cached (staged) + untracked <20MB + HEAD hash, zips to LOCALAPPDATA\\kit\\backups\\unstaged\\<stamp>\\<repo>.zip, 14-day retention. kit\\backups IS in profile-mirror wholeTrees (L22) -> shipped off-box. So uncommitted CHANGES across all repos (incl docs/backlog/state/agile.spi, 13MB < 20MB cap) are recoverable off-box as patches+untracked, even though C:\\home\\git itself is not mirrored. My earlier 20:55 agile.spi->europa stopgap was good belt-and-suspenders, but the backlog was already covered via unstaged-backup->kit\\backups->mirror. NET: red trains do NOT create a growing durability hole; the accumulating WIP is captured. Residual: files >20MB skipped (rare for src); and this rides the slow 1.9GB profile-mirror, so the append-delta win (this item) also speeds the WIP backup.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_23 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 23u32, "2026-11-04T08:17Z", "resilience", "11-04 04:1x resilience RESTORE-DRILL caught a real STALL (root-caused, self-resolving). On-demand profile-restore-drill PASSED the restore check (373 memory files hash-equal to the archive, 0 gone = backup intact+restorable) but FAILED on freshness: newest NODE archive 3.4h old (profile-mirror-005321.tgz, 00:53). ROOT CAUSE: the node-disk crunch (8.65GB free earlier) could not receive the 1.9GB archive, so profile-mirror scp failed each attempt 00:53->~03:40 = 3.4h RPO gap on the BIG backup. The tiered design paid off: critical-mirror (1.34MB, tier-1) kept shipping fine throughout. SELF-RESOLVING: node now 24GB (swept) + a profile-mirror is RUNNING NOW (pid 2828, 9min in, 36240 files/1993MB) - should land + close the gap; did NOT trigger a duplicate. VALIDATES the 15GB DISK-LOW floor I added (node-utilization-monitor): this stall IS the disk-full->mirror-scp-fail chain the floor catches early. Strongest case yet for the append-delta (this item): a small delta ships even on a tight node, where the 1.9GB full tar cannot. FOLLOWUP: re-run restore-drill after pid 2828 lands to confirm the gap closed.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_24 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 24u32, "2026-11-04T08:38Z", "resilience", "11-04 04:3x resilience ROOT CAUSE of the stall + manual land + temp-leak fix. The restore-drill stall was NOT self-resolving: 3 profile-mirror runs (03:12/03:41/04:07) each built a COMPLETE archive (36240 entries, 557MB compressed from 1993MB) but the SCP to the node FAILED/timed out every time - the script bound is 600s (L123) and 557MB over the slow/saturated wifi link exceeds it. Each failed run LEAKED its ~2.5GB temp dir (snapshot+tgz; cleanup only runs on success, L182). So: big backup down 3.6h+, 7.6GB temp leaked. FIX NOW: (1) manually shipping the newest COMPLETE .tgz (040726, 557MB) to the node via scp .part->move, background b9e56qufw - closes the RPO gap + times the real transfer; (2) cleaned the 2 older leaked temp dirs (~5GB reclaimed; kept 040726 while it ships). ROOT FIX = the append-delta (this item): a small delta ships inside 600s where 557MB does not. INTERIM options: raise the scp bound (but a slow link still loses), or compress harder. critical-mirror (1.34MB) unaffected - the tiered split is what kept tier-1 alive.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_25 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 25u32, "2026-11-04T08:45Z", "resilience", "11-04 04:4x resilience STALL RESOLVED. Manual ship of the built 557MB archive LANDED on the node in 546s (~1MB/s) = RPO gap CLOSED (newest node archive now 04:07-stamped ~38min old, vs the stale 00:53). DIAGNOSIS CONFIRMED: 546s is JUST under the script 600s scp bound (only 54s margin), so the scheduled mirror landed sometimes (00:53) + timed out others (03:12/03:41/04:07) as link speed varied. FIX APPLIED: raised profile-mirror.ps1 scp bound 600000 -> 1800000 (30min, ~3x the 546s measured); .bak in scratch, parse-clean (0 errors), unstaged - next scheduled run has ample margin. Re-running restore-drill (brpobnrc2) to confirm the landed archive verifies. Also cleaned 7.6GB of leaked failed-run temp dirs (the script cleans only on success L182, so repeated scp-timeouts leaked; the bound raise cuts future leaks). INTERIM CAVEAT: 1800s is a stopgap - a slower link or bigger archive still risks it; the append-delta (this item core) is the durable fix (ships seconds, immune to link swings).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_26 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 26u32, "2026-11-04T08:47Z", "resilience", "11-04 04:4x resilience CONFIRMED - stall fully resolved. restore-drill re-run PASS: newest node archive = profile-mirror-040726.tgz (the manually-landed one), 36240 entries, memory 391 hash-equal / 4 changed-since / 0 gone locally. So: RPO gap closed + backup fresh + verified restorable. Bound fix (scp 600->1800s) prevents recurrence; 7.6GB leaked temp cleaned. profile-mirror tier-1 big backup is HEALTHY again. End of the stall saga (started with the restore-drill catching newest-archive 3.4h old at 04:15). Append-delta remains the durable fix so a 557MB full ship is never needed per cycle.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_27 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 27u32, "2026-11-04T10:33Z", "resilience", "11-04 06:3x resilience BOUND-FIX VALIDATED end-to-end. The SCHEDULED profile-mirror run at 05:09 LANDED + verified (profile-mirror-050904.tgz on node, sha ok, tiered retention kept 17) - so with the raised 1800s scp bound the scheduled mirror now completes where the 600s bound timed out (03:12/03:41/04:07). Monitor 06:33 OK, newest 84min old. The profile-mirror is self-sustaining healthy again (not just my manual 04:07 ship). Full loop closed: detection (monitor STALE 03:05 + restore-drill 04:15) -> manual gap-close (546s) -> bound-fix 600->1800s -> SCHEDULED mirror lands autonomously (05:09). My tightened per-tick mirror-check worked this tick: a 27min-stale monitor log + 145min-old-if-no-new-land looked like a possible re-stall; I verified + found the 05:09 land = OK. Reliability FIXED; the transfer COST reduction (append-delta) remains this item core work (still ships ~2GB/run).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_28 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 28u32, "2026-11-05T04:42Z", "resilience", "ANALYSIS (resilience, read profile-mirror.ps1): the script is ALREADY selective - whitelists source extensions, caps files <2MB, excludes heavy folders (lib/spiral,mix-build,_build,node_modules,target,deps,.cache,build,extracted,bundle) and \\tasks\\. So large .jsonl transcripts are already EXCLUDED (>2MB and .json!=.jsonl); the 800MB/360MB-transcripts premise is likely STALE - re-measure. Real cost driver = it does a FULL tar -czf + scp EVERY run (not differential), re-sending a near-unchanged selective set every ~50min. Fix: differential transfer (robocopy /mir over the ssh route, or rsync) so stable runs move ~0 bytes and the 40-60min overlap collapses. resilience.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_29 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 29u32, "2026-11-05T19:52Z", "resilience", "FIX APPLIED (resilience, unstaged) - profile-mirror.ps1 .tgz.part leak that main found (20 dead .part files, 6.82GB on node C:, 11-03..11-05). Root of the LEAK: on scp timeout the line-124 15s-bounded ssh delete fails silently (|Out-Null) under node saturation, and BOTH retention passes filter profile-mirror-*.tgz so a stranded .tgz.part is never pruned. Fix: added a box-side prune block (after the .tgz retention, the saturation-robust path) that lists profile-mirror-*.tgz.part and deletes any whose stamp is >2h old (safe: scp is 1800s-bounded, so a .part >2h old cannot be a live upload). Parses clean. main already cleaned the existing 20. BIGGER SIGNAL (ties to this item core): since 11-03, 20 uploads TIMED OUT vs 18 finished - the mirror fails >50% because the full tar+scp (300-660MB) does not fit the 1800s scp bound when the node is busy. The .part-prune fixes the symptom; the ROOT fix remains DIFFERENTIAL sync (robocopy /mir or rsync, note 28) so stable runs move ~0 bytes and never time out. resilience 2026-11-05 15:5x.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_30 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 30u32, "2026-11-05T22:25Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~14:2x resilience SERIOUS: primary backup chain SILENTLY DEGRADED under sustained load (caught by analogy to my own truncating transfers). EVIDENCE (node dir listings vs task results):\n- profile-mirror (hot ~800MB: memory+transcripts+kit\\backups+config): newest COMPLETE tgz = 12:29 today (~6h stale). The ~800MB single scp truncates under the 100%-CPU load (same failure my 260MB/1.72GB transfers hit). profile-mirror-monitor CORRECTLY logged STALE (371min>120) at 18:05 - detection works.\n- critical-mirror (tier-1 15-min: memory+config): newest COMPLETE critical .tar = 11-02 01:04 (3 DAYS stale!); recent runs land as orphaned critical-<ts>.tar.part (16:55, 17:10 today) that never rename to .tar; task reports Result=0. A DISTINCT bug (critical is ~1.6MB, too small to truncate) - the .part->.tar completion/rename is failing. 3-day silent failure of the tier-1 backup, unnoticed.\nNO DATA LOSS (redundancy held): memory was covered to 12:29 by profile-mirror; AND I just shipped a fresh verified crown-jewel snapshot (memory+hooks+autonomous+config, 1.56MB/494 files, node:crownjewel-20261105-182410.tar, sha-verified) - small transfers land reliably where the big mirror truncates. So crown jewels are fresh off-box NOW.\nROOT/FIX: profile-mirror big-scp truncation = MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST incremental design (kit-dir-mirror-gated); critical-mirror .part-not-completing = distinct bug needing diagnosis. Lesson reinforced: task Result=0 != artifact landed; verify the dest file. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_31 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 31u32, "2026-11-05T22:29Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~14:3x resilience CORRECTION to n30 (my error, retracted to main): critical-mirror is HEALTHY, NOT broken 3 days. I checked the WRONG dir - C:\\home\\remote-archive (stale 11-02 critical-now.tar = unrelated old one-shot) instead of critical-mirror ACTUAL output C:\\home\\profile-mirror, which has completed critical-*.tar every ~15min to 18:26 today (log \"ok 18:27\"). So memory+config are 15-min-fresh off-box via critical-mirror (+ my 18:24 crownjewel snapshot). The REAL issue is narrower + stands: profile-mirror big ~800MB tgz ~6h stale (newest complete 12:29; monitor independently logged STALE 18:05) from single-scp truncation under load -> affects TRANSCRIPTS + kit\\backups only (not memory; WIP also in repos). Pruned 2 orphaned critical-*.tar.part (occasional 15s ssh-move timeouts under load; next run always completes - cosmetic, not failure). NET corrected exposure: memory WELL-protected; transcripts+kit\\backups 6h-stale off-box until load eases / MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST incremental lands. Lesson: verify the ACTUAL output path before alarming. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_32 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 32u32, "2026-11-06T12:06Z", "resilience-614162e5", "DESIGN FORK RESOLVED 11-06: rsync 3.2.7 confirmed on BOTH ends (box + node, C:\\ProgramData\\chocolatey\\bin\\rsync.exe). So the HOT-lane incremental mirror is native rsync -az --partial -e ssh over the tunnel - NO custom kit dir-mirror verb needed (unblocks from KIT-ARCHIVE). rsync sends only the appended tail of the 486MB .jsonl transcripts + the 0.63MB memory whole = meets the <25MB/run DoD by construction. REMAINING (implementation): convert profile-mirror.ps1 HOT lane from full-tar+scp to rsync -az, then measure bytes-sent across 2 consecutive runs. DEFER the conversion: the first rsync SEEDS ~486MB to the node (heavy) and editing the live 15-min mirror risks RPO - do it when box+node have headroom (both ~100% now) and verify RPO holds after. Plan is now buildable, not blocked on a kit verb.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_33 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 33u32, "2026-11-06T13:04Z", "resilience-614162e5", "rsync WINDOWS-PATH CAVEAT 11-06 (corrects the earlier too-optimistic \"buildable, just point at rsync\" note): choco rsync 3.2.7 is on both ends, but a local drive-to-drive smoke test FAILED two ways - \"C:\\\\\" paths give \"source and destination cannot both be remote\" (rsync reads C: as host:path), and \"/c/\" MSYS paths give \"protocol data stream error code 12\". So the conversion is NOT drop-in: the implementation must first establish the correct path+invocation for this rsync build (likely /cygdrive with --protect-args, or run rsync inside a cygwin shell, or an rsyncd module), THEN handle rsync-over-ssh to the node. The rsync approach still stands (incremental, no kit verb) but budget time for the Windows path/invocation; a naive conversion will fail. Item stays Active 70, implementation deferred to headroom AND this path-format resolution.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_34 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 34u32, "2026-11-06T13:05Z", "resilience-614162e5", "rsync flavor = cwRsync 6.2.9 (cygwin-based, both ends). The \"both remote\" failure is the choco bin\\rsync SHIM not establishing the cygwin env; the real cwrsync binary needs its cygwin DLLs on PATH + /cygdrive paths + a cygwin ssh for the remote hop. DESIGN PIVOT RECOMMENDATION: given cwRsync setup finickiness on Windows, the items ORIGINAL fallback - mtime+size delta tar+scp (send only the .jsonl/memory files changed since the last run) - is likely SIMPLER and more robust here than fighting cwRsync, because transcripts are append-only and scp already works over the SP tunnel. So: incremental is still the goal + threshold (<25MB/run), but prefer mtime-delta tar+scp over rsync unless a clean cwRsync local+ssh invocation is nailed. Decision at implementation (with headroom). Closing the rsync investigation thread - enough to choose at build time.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_35 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 35u32, "2026-11-06T13:36Z", "resilience-614162e5", "HYGIENE BUG found 11-06 while reclaiming node disk: the profile-mirror leaves abandoned .part turds on the node when a transfer is interrupted (found 2: profile-mirror-064020.tgz.part 128MB age 151m + 073104.tgz.part 35MB age 94m; reclaimed 163MB). It does NOT clean stale .part on the next run, so under the slow/saturated link they accumulate on the already disk-pressured node. FIX (fold into the mtime-delta/rsync rewrite): at the START of each mirror run, delete own *.part older than the run interval before starting a new transfer; and prefer atomic rename (write .part then move) so a partial never lingers as a .tgz. This also means the mirrors own interrupted transfers are a (small) contributor to the DISK-LOW on the node.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_36 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 36u32, "2026-11-06T14:53Z", "resilience-614162e5", "MEASUREMENT REFRESH + NEW CPU-COST FINDING (resilience-614162e5, 2026-11-06 10:5x, box-independent reads). (1) The HOT-lane set has grown 3.7x since this item was filed: profile-mirror.last.log now reads \"69080 files, 2973.1 MB\" (was ~800MB at filing). Transcripts alone are 1.5GB (.claude/projects/C--home-git), biggest single .jsonl 304MB, next 248/197/188/164/152/124MB - append-only and never pruned, so the bulk only climbs. (2) MECHANISM CONFIRMED still a FULL re-tar every run: profile-mirror.ps1:95 `tar -czf $archive -T $listFile` builds a gzip tar of the ENTIRE list, then scp (line123, 1800s-bounded). No file- or byte-level delta. The log records only \"<files>, <MB>\" (no bytes-sent line) - itself the signature of whole-set transfer. (3) NEW DIMENSION the item did not capture: `tar -czf` GZIPS ~3GB on the box EVERY ~50 min, and the box is pinned at 100% CPU all day (see DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY note 3: box_cpu=100 on 10/10 samples). So the hot mirror is a RECURRING CPU LOAD on the already-saturated host, not only a slow-link cost - it competes with lane builds every 50 min. The original framing was link-cost only. (4) The actual CHANGE per run is a few MB of appended transcript tails + 0.63MB rewritten memory, so an incremental delta would move <25MB where the full re-tar moves ~3GB (compressed) AND would drop the 3GB-gzip CPU spike to a tail-only compress. This is the DoD threshold \"before\" number, refreshed: ~3GB/run -> target <25MB/run, ~29 runs/day => ~85GB/day redundant transfer + ~29 gzip-of-3GB CPU spikes/day eliminated. Fix = the incremental kit dir-mirror verb (team:kit, shared with KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR). SAFE interim lever NOT applied mid-run (surface in day report): the 15-min critical-mirror (1.67MB) already covers the irreplaceable small stores (memory/hooks/config) as the fast tier, so the profile-mirror could drop to -czf-without-transcripts + a separate slower transcript lane - but that changes transcript RPO and is a deliberate policy change, not a heartbeat edit. CPU-light, no run, no git.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_37 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 37u32, "2026-11-06T15:24Z", "resilience-614162e5", "HOT-LANE LANDING FAILURE INCIDENT (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:2x; mirror-landing verification caught it). The hot profile-mirror has NOT completed a landing on the node since 06:26 (profile-mirror-20261106-055534.tgz, 865MB); prior success 04:47. Between then and now: an abandoned 168MB .part from 08:59 (scp stalled/killed at its 1800s bound), which I cleared (safe: 2.4h stale, fixed-name, no resume; the script own .part pruner \"fails silently\" per profile-mirror.ps1:148; reclaimed 168MB on the tight 11GB node). A fresh run IS in progress (box pwsh 27816 started 11:05, mid gzip+scp of the now-2979MB set) and may land ~11:35 - will verify at a later tick. ROOT CAUSE: this item efficiency gap has become a DURABILITY gap - the 3GB full-tar+gzip+scp cannot reliably COMPLETE within the 1800s scp bound under sustained 100% box CPU, so hot-lane runs stall at .part instead of landing. EXPOSURE SCOPED (not a panic): the 15-min critical-mirror still lands every tick (11:11, 10:56, 10:40...) so memory/hooks/config - the irreplaceable small state - ARE protected off-box; code WIP is in the repos + kit-unstaged-backup (2h). The loss on a wipe-now is ~5h of TRANSCRIPTS + fuller profile only. CONCRETE LOW-RISK INTERIM LEVER (for a deliberate pass / day report, NOT applied mid-run with 27816 in flight): switch the hot tar from default gzip (-czf, ~level6 on 3GB = the CPU bottleneck) to FAST compression `gzip -1` (tar -I \"gzip -1\" -cf) - cuts the per-run CPU spike a lot for a modest size increase, helping runs complete under load; OR the transcript-split (note earlier) which also cuts the per-run bytes. Real fix stays the incremental kit dir-mirror verb (team:kit). This ESCALATES the item from P2-efficiency to a durability concern; recommend P2->P1. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_38 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 38u32, "2026-11-06T15:28Z", "resilience-614162e5", "INTERIM-FIX SYNTAX CORRECTED + VERIFIED (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:2x; de-risking note 37 before anyone applies it). The mirror runs under pwsh, where `tar` resolves to C:/WINDOWS/system32/tar.exe = bsdtar 3.6.2 (libarchive), NOT GNU tar. CRITICAL: note 37 gave the GNU syntax `tar -I \"gzip -1\"` for the fast-compression lever - that FAILS on bsdtar (it tries to open a file literally named \"gzip -1\") and leaves a 0-BYTE broken .tgz. Applying it as a \"fix\" would have SILENTLY BROKEN the hot mirror entirely - a worse incident than the one being fixed. VERIFIED-CORRECT bsdtar syntax: `tar --options gzip:compression-level=1 -czf <archive> -T <list>` - tested under pwsh/System32 tar, produces a valid gzip .tgz that `tar -tzf` reads back (so restore/verify/retention path unchanged, same .tgz naming). Note: git-bash `tar` is GNU tar 1.35 (where -I works) - the flavor DIFFERS by shell, so a fix must match the SCHEDULED task shell (pwsh->bsdtar). Caveat on expected gain: gzip level matters only for COMPRESSIBLE data; transcripts are JSON text (very compressible) so level 1 should be ~2-4x faster compress for ~10-20% larger output - but this was NOT measured here (my test payload was random/incompressible, a poor proxy). Measure on a real transcript sample before committing to the lever. The fundamental 3GB-full-pass-every-run cost remains until the incremental kit dir-mirror verb (team:kit); gzip-1 only reduces the per-pass CPU spike. So: deferred lever is now SAFE-TO-APPLY (correct syntax pinned) but its payoff needs a real-data measurement first. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_39 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 39u32, "2026-11-06T15:31Z", "resilience-614162e5", "BOTTLENECK LOCATED = local gzip phase (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:3x). The 11:05 hot-run (box pwsh 27816) is STILL running 25+min in with ZERO .part on the node. profile-mirror.ps1 tars locally (line95 tar -czf) THEN scps (line123, renames .part->tgz on success). No .part yet after 25min => it is still in the LOCAL tar -czf (gzip of ~3GB) phase, has not reached scp. So the stall is the LOCAL COMPRESS under 100% box CPU, not the network/scp. This validates the fix target: the gzip-1 lever (note 38, bsdtar --options gzip:compression-level=1) attacks exactly this local-compress phase; scp-side tuning would not help. Did NOT run gzip benchmarks now - that competes with the in-flight backup + lanes on a pinned box (and wall-time under contention is unreliable, prior 4x wall/cpu). The run is slow-but-trying (not hung; do NOT kill - it is a real backup attempt); whether it finally lands is the next-tick check. Confirms: local 3GB gzip every ~50min is both the transfer-cost waste AND the landing-failure cause. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_40 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 40u32, "2026-11-06T15:36Z", "resilience-614162e5", "ROOT CAUSE CORRECTED - CPU STARVATION, not a hang (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:3x). Falsified the locked-file-hang theory: a 25s-bounded bsdtar of ONLY the active 125MB transcript (held open by the live Claude session) produced 30MB before the timeout - so tar reads even the active transcript fine; it is NOT blocked on a locked file. The real cause of the 27-min/1-byte run (pwsh 27816): CPU STARVATION under all-day 100% box load. Evidence: 27816 started 11:05 but its archive is stamped -112128 => ~16 min just to ENUMERATE the 69000-file set + build the list + size it, THEN the 3GB gzip crawls under contention. So two cost centres, both load-amplified: (a) 69k-file enumeration, (b) 3GB gzip. The incremental kit dir-mirror verb cuts BOTH (small mtime-delta set = fast enumerate + tiny gzip); the gzip-1 lever only helps (b). POSTURE (accurate, not alarmist): the hot lane is DEGRADED (last full landing 06:26, >5h) but NOT broken - runs crawl and may or may not beat their window under load. Core/irreplaceable state (memory/hooks/config) IS protected: the 15-min critical-mirror lands every tick (verified 11:11...). Exposure on a wipe-now = ~5h transcripts + fuller profile only; code WIP in repos + kit-unstaged-backup (2h). This degradation is a direct consequence of the all-day fleet saturation (DELEG: box+lap pinned >=87% for hours) - backups are now a visible casualty of no offload headroom. Stopped further probing (it competes with the in-flight backup). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_41 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 41u32, "2026-11-06T15:40Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:4x measured resilience's proposed interim on the largest real transcript (305 MB, box at 100% CPU): gzip -1 = 20.2 s / 88.0 MB, gzip -6 = 26.1 s / 75.7 MB, so level 1 is only 22% faster and 16% bigger. Not the lever. The full mirror (run pid 27816) frozen at 1 output byte for 32 min is a stall, not throughput: at 12-15 MB/s under load the ~3 GB set is minutes of CPU. Suspects sent to resilience: tar blocked reading a live, growing agent transcript (300+ MB, appended constantly), or a stuck ssh/remote write; test copy-then-tar vs tar-in-place. Off-box exposure meanwhile: the 15-min critical mirror (memory, hooks, config) keeps landing; transcripts and the fuller profile have been at risk since 06:26.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_42 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 42u32, "2026-11-06T15:50Z", "resilience-614162e5", "WIP STOPGAP APPLIED (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:4x) - closes the worst part of the hot-lane-stall exposure independently, no mirror-script edit, no collision with main. Confirmed 27816 was KILLED at its 1800s bound having landed NOTHING (newest node tgz still 06:26, 0 parts) - so the hot lane is in a stall->kill->retry loop, down since 06:26. STOPGAP: tarred the newest kit-unstaged-backup snapshot (20261106-100413, 6.99MB, 26 per-repo git-diff zips - NO transcripts, so it tars cleanly unlike the 3GB set) and scp- d it to the node as C:/home/profile-mirror/wip-stopgap-20261106-114xxx.tar; BYTE-VERIFIED local=node=6993920. So the uncommitted WIP across all repos is now off-box as of the 10:04 capture, via the WORKING path, bypassing the stalled lane. (tar gotcha for the record: GNU tar read C:/ as host:path - needed --force-local.) HONEST SCOPE: point-in-time, NOT recurring - the next kit-unstaged snapshot (~12:04) will again be stranded on the stalled hot lane. Recurring fix still needs EITHER the hot-lane repair (snapshot-then-tar, main) OR adding the newest unstaged snapshot to the 15-min critical-mirror (proposed to main, pending coordination to avoid both editing the mirror scripts). Transcripts remain only in the stalled hot lane (lower-value than WIP, not stopgapped). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_43 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 43u32, "2026-11-06T15:50Z", "main", "main 11-06 15:5x EXPOSURE CORRECTED (resilience): uncommitted WIP across all repos (kit\\backups\\unstaged, 327 MB / 42 snapshots, up from 82 MB on 11-03) rides only the stalled full mirror, so it too has not gone off-box since 06:26. That's the 10-28 loss class, not only transcripts. The stall is narrowed to the LOCAL tar read (pid 27816, tar child 22988 writing to local Temp, no ssh child, no .part on the node), consistent with a growing or locked live transcript of another session. Decided: resilience (1) adds the newest unstaged snapshot to the 15-min critical mirror (.bak, parse check, hash-verified on the node from outside) and (2) tests copy-then-tar on profile-mirror by hand before switching it; kit gets the unstaged-store retention item. Main edits neither mirror script.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_44 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 44u32, "2026-11-06T15:55Z", "resilience-614162e5", "RECURRING WIP FIX DONE + HASH-VERIFIED (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:5x, main authorized GO + confirmed not touching the mirror scripts). Edited critical-mirror.ps1 (unstaged, .bak in scratch, PS parse OK): after staging memory/hooks/config it now also stages the NEWEST kit\\backups\\unstaged snapshot, but only when its name differs from a stamp (critical-mirror.unstaged-stamp) of the last LANDED snapshot - stamp advanced ONLY on scp success, so a failed landing retries; most 15-min runs stay lean (~2MB), and ship the 6.7MB WIP only when the 2-hourly snapshot changes. Ran once: landed critical-20261106-115333.tar 8.33MB with log tag +wip:20261106-100413, stamp advanced. VERIFIED FROM OUTSIDE (main requirement, not just exit code): extracted unstaged-20261106-100413/currency_converter.zip from the landed tar ON THE NODE and SHA256-compared to the box original - MATCH (2e03da11...2d0b). So uncommitted WIP now lands off-box every time the snapshot changes via the WORKING 15-min critical lane, decoupled from the stalled hot lane; byte-exact recoverable confirmed. This is the RECURRING fix the one-shot stopgap (note 42) only pointed at. Transcripts still ride the hot lane (main owns the copy-then-tar repair). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_45 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 45u32, "2026-11-06T16:07Z", "resilience-614162e5", "DEFINITIVE ROOT CAUSE (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 12:0x, caught a live stall + read the code) - it is the FULL-SET COPY, not a locked file / not tar / not gzip / not transcripts. Caught stalled pwsh 9000 (15min): NO data-file handle open (handle64.exe: only pwsh DLLs + the log), NO tar/scp child, burning 426 CPU-sec at 47% util = CPU-BOUND grind, not blocked (a locked-file block would be 0% CPU + a stuck handle). Then read profile-mirror.ps1 lines 86-91: the script ALREADY does snapshot-copy-then-tar - it [IO.File]::Copy-s every one of ~69k files (~3GB) into a $snapshot dir, THEN tars the snapshot. So \"snapshot the jsonl first\" (the proposed interim) is ALREADY in place for ALL files; the bottleneck is that per-file 3GB copy loop grinding under 100% box CPU (the \"1-byte .tgz\" is just the not-yet-written archive while the copy runs; the 16-min gap before the archive stamp is the copy+enumeration). CONFIRMED NOT: transcripts (tar reads 820MB/22s in isolation), any single wholeTree dir (all tar rc=0 in <10s), OneDrive files (missing), locks (no stuck handle). FIX (I own the repair, pending main approach-confirm): replace the full per-file copy with an mtime-INCREMENTAL refresh of a PERSISTENT snapshot dir (robocopy /MIR-style, /R:0 to skip not retry a locked file), then tar the full persistent snapshot - each run copies only the delta (MB), tars a complete set (full restore preserved). Same end as the kit dir-mirror incremental verb. This supersedes the gzip-1 lever (dead, main measured) and the snapshot-jsonl idea (already done). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_46 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 46u32, "2026-11-06T16:28Z", "resilience-614162e5", "HOT-LANE RETENTION POLICY CHANGE + landing-verify (resilience-614162e5, 11-06, main-approved). Pre-flight found the node at 10.2GB free ALREADY holding 18 un-pruned tars=10.3GB (stalls never reached the prune step), and the old retention (12 recent + 1/day x14d + 1/week x56w ~= up to 30 tars ~17GB) overflows this ~20GB node. CHANGED profile-mirror.ps1 (unstaged, .bak, parse OK) per main: (1) retention sized to the node = 5 most-recent + 1/day x5d + 1/week x3w (~10 tars ~6GB, ~4GB headroom); (2) the box-side prune now runs ONLY AFTER the landed archive is HASH-VERIFIED from the node (ssh Get-FileHash == local sha256) - never trusts move-rc alone, so older copies are removed only once the new one is confirmed recoverable; a mismatch throws and keeps everything; (3) the prune ALWAYS keeps the newest (just-verified) AND the oldest tar - a deep copy for late-noticed damage (10-28 wipe lesson), never pruned. DEFERRED (conditional, main): if the node stays too tight after the 5/5/3 prune, MOVE the oldest weekly tar to europa (keep transfers <2.5GB, larger truncate there - memory europa-large-transfer-truncates) instead of deleting; will add only if needed after deploy. This is the retention half of the robocopy-incremental fix (n45); first-pop cold copy still running, full hash-verified landing run to follow. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_47 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 47u32, "2026-11-06T17:45Z", "resilience-614162e5", "FOLLOW-UP after the robocopy fix lands (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 ~13:4x): the first real run enumerated 140681 files / 3926MB - BIGGER than the pre-fix 69115 files / 2979MB. Structure is sane (no duplicate dest trees / path bug), so two real issues to fix once the current run lands + verifies: (1) BUG I introduced: the persistent-snapshot scratch sync uses robocopy /S /XO (no /PURGE), so scratch files that vanish from source LINGER in the snapshot -> stale accumulates across runs = slow unbounded growth of the snapshot (and thus the tar+scp). The wholeTrees use /MIR (purges, fine); scratch needs /PURGE too OR a periodic snapshot reset. (2) DESIGN: the hot lane backs up the FULL $env:TEMP\\claude scratch tree (session scratchpads) = ~71k files/~1GB of EPHEMERAL, regenerable data inflating the heavy lane every run. Recommend DROPPING scratch from the hot lane entirely (or keep only a tiny whitelist) - scratch is not durable state worth a 15-50min tar+scp. Both make the per-run tar+scp much smaller = faster landings under the pinned box, compounding the robocopy-copy win. The current run will still land a VALID (if heavy 3.9GB) backup - these are efficiency fixes, not correctness. Deferred until after the end-to-end landing verifies (do not edit the script mid-run). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_48 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 48u32, "2026-11-06T17:59Z", "resilience-614162e5", "HOT-LANE PAUSED + ROOT CAUSE FIXED (resilience-614162e5, 11-06, main-approved). PAUSE: scheduled task profile-mirror-to-node is DISABLED (a run that never lands is pure cost); killed the churning run. RESUME CONDITION: re-enable with ONE manual run after the set is trimmed, and ACCEPT only when the node shows a landed, HASH-VERIFIED archive with a fresh mtime. WIP + core stay covered by the 15-min critical lane meanwhile. ROOT CAUSE of the non-landing (main was right: NOT gzip RAM): TWO pwsh-pipeline bugs in profile-mirror.ps1, both fixed (.bak, parse OK): (1) `tar -czf $archive -T $listFile 2>&1 | Select-Object -First 5` - `-First` throws StopUpstream and KILLS tar after 5 stderr warning lines, truncating the archive; fixed to capture tar stderr to a temp FILE (surface first 5 for the log) so tar runs to completion. (2) `@(tar -tzf $archive).Count` MATERIALIZED ~140k entry paths into a pwsh array = the real RAM spike on the 1.8GB-free box; fixed to STREAM-count via `tar -tzf | Measure-Object`. Also DROPPED the ephemeral $env:TEMP\\claude scratch tree from the hot lane (n47): set 3.9GB/140k -> ~2.9GB/69k. REMAINING to resume: clear the stale scratch subtree from the persistent snapshot (doing now, safe - no live tar), then one manual run + verify the landing. FUTURE (lighter still, feeds the kit incremental verb): main suggests streaming `tar | ssh` to the node or `zstd -1` instead of a multi-GB local gzip. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_49 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 49u32, "2026-11-06T18:35Z", "resilience-614162e5", "RE-ENABLE ATTEMPT 1 HUNG in stale-cleanup; refined cause + plan (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 ~14:3x). The trimmed+fixed run enumerated cleanly (11652 files / 1967MB - scratch-drop confirmed, down from 140k/3.9GB) but then HUNG ~25min in the stale-cleanup step (foreach stale work dir >60min -> Remove-Item -Recurse), with NO child proc and no new work dir. Cause: the churned pre-fix runs left work dirs each holding a FULL per-run snapshot (tens of thousands of files, pre-fix per-file-copy design); Remove-Item -Recurse of those under 100% CPU hangs/crawls. So the hang is the DEBRIS cleanup, not the tar itself. ACTIONS: killed the hung run; task profile-mirror-to-node DISABLED (no churn); clearing the debris work dirs via native rmdir /s /q (harness-backgrounded). PLAN: once debris is clear, ONE more re-enable run should skip the slow cleanup (no debris) and go straight to tar(2GB)+scp+hash-verify+prune - which should land (the 2GB tar is slow under load but not a hang). If it STILL fails, defer to a genuine low-load window OR switch to streaming tar|ssh / zstd -1 (main n-prior) to avoid the local 2GB archive. Keeping PAUSED until the next attempt. WIP+core stay protected by the 15-min critical lane; node DISK-LOW (10GB, 18 old tars) still gated on a successful landing-prune. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_50 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 50u32, "2026-11-06T19:15Z", "resilience-614162e5", "STREAM TO WINDOWS NODE IS OUT - use cmd-tar + scp (option 2). Tested option 3 (tar|ssh|Git-cat.exe): the pipe did NOT hang (good) but CORRUPTED the binary - a 50MB random payload landed as 115MB with a mismatched hash = UTF re-encoding (~2.3x) in the path. main: that ~2.3x is pwsh UTF-16/UTF-8 re-encoding signature; NEVER run the stream byte-path through pwsh. CORRECTION/EXTENSION: my test box-side ran under BASH (not pwsh) and it STILL corrupted - so on a WINDOWS target the re-encoding also happens node-side (Windows OpenSSH server piping stdin -> cmd -> cat), not only pwsh. Net: do NOT stream raw bytes to the Windows node by any shell; scp is the only binary-clean transport to it. Also ruled out: pwsh [Console]::OpenStandardInput receiver HANGS (132KB); node bash is WSL (forbidden + /c path mismatch). DECISION (main GO): option 2 = cmd /c tar -czf <localfile> (System32 bsdtar, no pwsh in byte path) THEN scp (binary-clean) THEN node-side Get-FileHash vs box hash. Proving on 50MB, then full, then re-enable. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_51 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 51u32, "2026-11-06T19:31Z", "resilience-614162e5", "CRITICAL CORRECTION - the \"0-byte tar stall\" was a MISREAD, tar was WORKING. During the cmd-tar run, handle64 on the tar pid showed its open-file handle ADVANCING through the snapshot transcripts (84b53718.jsonl 188MB -> b7595ac3.jsonl 164MB), i.e. tar is reading+gzipping file by file - PROGRESSING. The local .tgz showing 0 bytes is a WINDOWS artifact: the directory-entry size of an open-for-write file is not updated until the writer flushes/closes it, so Get-ChildItem/Get-Item report 0 (or stale) the whole time tar is compressing, then the real size (~1GB) appears on close. IMPLICATION: the \"0-byte frozen N min = stall\" reads that drove killing earlier runs were likely this same artifact + genuine slowness (2GB gzip under 100% box CPU takes tens of min). I was killing slow-but-working runs. RIGHT way to monitor a tar-in-progress on Windows: watch the tar process open-file handle advancing (handle64 -p <pid>) or the CPU time accruing, NOT the archive file size. Letting the current cmd-tar run FINISH (not killing); it will close the archive (size appears), then stream-verify + scp (proven binary-clean) + node hash + prune + land. The robocopy-incremental + scratch-drop + pipeline-bug fixes remain genuine improvements (faster, smaller, no early-kill), but the headline \"stall\" was substantially a size-until-close misdiagnosis. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_52 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 52u32, "2026-11-06T20:25Z", "resilience-614162e5", "HOT-LANE WRAP-UP (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 16:2x) - fix VALIDATED, landing DEFERRED to an idle link window, node pruned, floor protected. (1) FIX WORKS: the run produces a VALID 843MB archive (robocopy-incremental + scratch-drop to 2GB/11.6k + cmd-invoked tar + pipeline-bug fixes). The long \"0-byte stall\" saga was mostly MISDIAGNOSIS: Windows does not update a file size until the writer closes it, so the .tgz read 0 the whole time tar was compressing (tar CPU accrued + its open-file handle advanced through the transcripts = working). (2) LANDING blocked on THIS fleet, two compounding reasons, both environmental not logic: (a) node was disk-tight (8GB free, 11GB of 19 un-pruned old tars) -> an 843MB scp TRUNCATED (843->423MB, scp rc=0 but half-size); I PRUNED to 9 tars/6GB keeping newest-2 + oldest-deep + a daily/weekly spread (main 5/5/3 + keep-newest-and-oldest), node now 13GB free (DISK-LOW relieved). (b) MORE IMPORTANT: the 843MB scp SATURATES the slow link and STARVES the 15-min critical-mirror FLOOR - critical FAILED 16:15 scp rc124 (60s timeout) while my big scp ran. So the hot-lane landing must NOT run concurrently with the floor/lanes; it needs a genuinely idle link window (rare on this all-day-100%-CPU fleet). I killed my big scp to protect the floor. DECISION: hot lane stays PAUSED; the 843MB archive is staged locally, ready to scp in an idle window; WIP+core remain on the working critical floor (the durability that matters). Real throughput fix = kit incremental dir-mirror verb (delta only). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_53 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 53u32, "2026-11-06T22:26Z", "resilience-614162e5", "CRITICAL-FLOOR HARDENED against peer link-contention (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 18:2x). The 15-min critical-mirror (the WIP+core durability FLOOR) was FAILING intermittently - scp rc124 (60s timeout) on its small ~2-18MB tar - whenever another lane saturated the slow node link (16:15 my 843MB hot-scp; 18:10/18:22 x/serve scp to remote-stage). Each fail skips the landing, so WIP stops reaching off-box (gap 17:57->18:25 observed). FIX: raised critical-mirror.ps1 scp bound 60s -> 180s (.bak, parse OK); verified - ran once under live contention and it LANDED (critical-20261106-182426.tar 17.68MB +wip:20261106-180420), closing the WIP gap. Rationale: the floor runs every 15min so a 3-min bound is free, and a small transfer should get through even sharing the link; the most-important durability lane must not fail on transient contention from peers node transfers (which is exactly when WIP most needs to land). Same too-tight-timeout class as the node-util probe fix (15s->40s) earlier today. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_54 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 54u32, "2026-11-06T22:45Z", "resilience-614162e5", "FLOOR CONTENTION - root is NODE-CPU saturation, timeouts raised but it is a capacity problem (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 18:4x; extends n53). The critical floor keeps intermittently failing (scp rc124 even at 180s; \"no route reached\" at route-probe) because the NODE (lap) is at 100% CPU running the fleets jobs (diagnosed the node-bound ssh procs: tibia pr32/pr33, kit TRAIN36, x-media3c, killvalue-cells, parallel-measure + the revtunnel keeper - lane job-launches, NOT bandwidth-hogging transfers). So the node cannot service the floors small scp write within the bound when it is CPU-maxed. Raised BOTH floor timeouts (route-probe 15->40s, scp 60->180s, .bak, parse OK) which catches more CPU GAPS - manual runs at 18:25/18:36 LANDED (18:25 landed +wip:180420), the 18:40 scheduled run missed a gap. NOT raising further: diminishing returns against a capacity problem (both box+lap 100% all day, DELEG n9). NET: the floor lands INTERMITTENTLY under full fleet load, which keeps WIP/core fresh-enough (snapshots are 2h apart; core changes slowly; last +wip off-box 18:25). The real fix is fleet capacity (a 4th node / staggering) or a lighter floor, not more timeout. WIP/core durability is adequate, just not every-15-min under saturation. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_55 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 55u32, "2026-11-07T19:32Z", "resilience", "ZSTD APPROACH CONFIRMED VIABLE (11-07, main suggested zstd -1): box has zstd v1.5.7 (cygwin) AND System32 bsdtar supports zstd (`tar --zstd` / `--options zstd:compression-level=N`). So profile-mirror.ps1 CAN switch gzip->zstd. LEVEL TRADEOFF (the transfer bottleneck is the slow LINK, not compress time): zstd -19 shrinks the archive 10-20% vs gzip (smaller = faster scp, the real win) but is CPU-heavy; zstd -3 is fast but ~gzip size (no transfer win). So pick by CPU: -19 when box CPU is free, -3 when CPU-bound. NOT DONE NOW (prudence): the hot lane is PAUSED (deferred value), box is 100% CPU, and this change is UNTESTABLE without a full heavy mirror run on a saturated fleet - an unverified edit to the backup path could break the next re-enable. PLAN: apply the zstd switch + pick the level IN THE SAME WINDOW the hot lane is re-enabled (so it is tested end-to-end: valid archive + landed + hash-verified). Still, the real transfer-cost fix remains the kit incremental dir-mirror (delta) verb, not recompression (zstd -19 still sends ~700MB; delta sends only changes).")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_56 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 56u32, "2026-11-07T20:17Z", "resilience", "BLOCKER: resilience fixes done (robocopy-incremental + pipeline-bug fixes + scratch-drop; zstd approach specced + confirmed viable). Remaining is EXTERNAL: the 843MB landing needs a genuinely idle link window (it starves the 15-min critical floor otherwise), and the real throughput fix is the kit incremental dir-mirror (delta) verb. RESUME: an idle-link window to land, or kit lands the delta verb.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_57 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 57u32, "2026-11-07T20:22Z", "resilience", "RSYNC-TO-WINDOWS-NODE DISPROVEN (11-07, tested end-to-end). The backlog assumption \"daily can be native rsync -az -e ssh\" is WRONG for the box->laptop (Windows node) path. Tested: rsync 3.2.7 (box) -> 3.3.0 (laptop) via `-e ssh --rsync-path=<choco rsync.exe>` with /cygdrive/c paths (and MSYS path-conv avoided by running from pwsh, not git-bash). Arg-parsing worked but the transfer dies: \"connection unexpectedly closed (0 bytes) ... protocol data stream (code 12)\". Cause: the laptop OpenSSH DEFAULT SHELL is cmd, which corrupts rsyncs binary --server channel - same class as the raw-binary-stream corruption to the Windows node found earlier (scp is binary-clean because it has its own handling; rsyncs --server protocol is not). So MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST cannot use rsync to the laptop; scp (non-incremental) remains the only binary-clean transport, so the 843MB transfer cost is fundamental to the Windows-node target. RE-FRAME blocker: NOT \"kit incremental verb\" - it is the Windows-node binary-channel limit. Options: (a) land in an idle window via scp (current plan); (b) set the laptop OpenSSH DefaultShell to powershell (clean channel) - but that affects ALL lanes ssh-to-laptop, needs coordination; (c) target a LINUX node (europa) for rsync instead (bash channel, untested). git-bash also mangles rsync args (MSYS path-conv) - run rsync from pwsh.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_58 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 58u32, "2026-11-07T20:25Z", "resilience", "RSYNC CONFIRMED BOX-SIDE-BROKEN (not just the Windows node): box->europa(Linux) ALSO fails code-12/0-bytes, so it is the cygwin-rsync vs Windows-OpenSSH I/O mismatch (no cygwin ssh on the box), not the laptop cmd shell. So NO rsync delta transport is available from the box for the profile mirror either. The transfer-cost options narrow to: scp in an idle window (current), OR install cygwin openssh to enable real rsync delta, OR the kit verb. Not naive rsync.")
inl note_of_mirror_transfer_cost_59 () = Note ("MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST", 59u32, "2026-11-09T05:13Z", "org3", "org3 STARRED-UNBLOCK advice (kind=ACTION/TECHNIQUE, removes the blocking dep): this is Blocked on KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (the kit dir-mirror verb) for incremental transfer - but that dep can be DROPPED. rclone is already on the box (scoop shim, confirmed tonight) and rclone copy is native incremental (size/hash-based, only changed files move). The HOT lane can go incremental TODAY via rclone - either to oort (already set up, i574n-oort-cas) or an rclone sftp remote to the node - no kit verb needed. For the bulk (transcripts ~360MB, APPEND-MOSTLY) delta transfer cuts ~800MB/run to the new bytes = the DoD threshold (<25MB on a no-large-change run). EVIDENCE: I just used rclone for STORE-OFFSITE-BACKUP's oort push (verified hash-identical round-trip; traps cygpath -m for local paths + --s3-no-check-bucket, in memory oort-object-store), and my deltas-by-data-shape analysis (STORE-OFFSITE note 5) confirms append-mostly data benefits from delta transfer. SUGGEST: point the HOT lane at rclone incremental, measure two runs for <25MB. I can help wire/measure it (profile-mirror is my prior work).")
