inl formatter_parens_misses () = Task ("FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES", "Measure which unnecessary parentheses the formatter still keeps (beyond trailing lambdas of LAMBDA-PARENS): atoms, applications as last argument, nested tuples, pipes; a census per shape and a formatter rule for every shape proven safe", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "LAMBDA-PARENS", "team:compiler,formatter,style,census,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_formatter_parens_misses () = Planning ("FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_formatter_parens_misses_1 () = Note ("FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:38Z", "compiler", "R5 SPEC. DOD: a table shape -> sites per repo, produced by a kit command reading the compiler token stream (paren pair whose removal leaves the same parse tree, checked by re-parsing), plus for each shape with 0 parse-tree changes over the corpus a formatter rule that removes it, proven by format-corpus round trip and an identical generated-output hash on the matrix. NEXT STEP: run the parse-tree-equality check over lib/spiral first (no compile needed: parse only, CPU-light). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: ParenShape = Atom | TailLambda | TailApply | TupleInTuple | PipeOperand | Annotated (needs parens, kept) as a union; the safe set is a value of the union, not a list of strings.")
inl note_of_formatter_parens_misses_2 () = Note ("FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES", 2u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (groom, user 11-07 via main): RAISED P2 -> P1. The user asks whether we are SOTA at detecting and removing unnecessary parentheses, possibly as an upstream PR, with our samples as the corpus, and to study Scala 2 -> 3 (optional braces, significant indentation) and similar migrations for more than parens. DoD extended: (1) the miss census over every repo's .spi plus the samples (each kept-but-unneeded paren classified by construct), (2) a short study note on Scala 3's optional-braces / indentation migration and its rewrite tool, listing rules worth taking, (3) the top misses fixed in the formatter with fixtures. next step: the census.")
inl note_of_formatter_parens_misses_3 () = Note ("FORMATTER-PARENS-MISSES", 3u32, "2026-11-10T23:40Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:0x compiler: STUDY NOTE (DoD 2). Sources: docs.scala-lang.org/scala3/guides/migration/syntax-rewriting.html (read); scalafmt changelog (scalameta.org/scalafmt/docs/changelog.html) via search; the EPFL report scala3-syntax-rewriting-TROPIN-2024.pdf was unreachable (DNS failure on infosciences.epfl.ch), so it is NOT summarized. What Scala 3 does: four style options used with -rewrite: -new-syntax / -old-syntax (control structures without / with parentheses: if (c) -> if c then, for {..} -> for .. do) and -indent / -no-indent (significant indentation vs braces). One option per compilation, and ORDER MATTERS: -new-syntax first, then -indent (indent keeps braces around an old-syntax for block). Without -rewrite, -new-syntax/-old-syntax/-no-indent turn the other style into an error (a style check), but significant indentation cannot be enforced. scalafmts RedundantParens/RedundantBraces history is a cautionary tale: changelog fixes include \"dont rewrite (()) (unit literal)\" and \"may not be redundant around infix\", and an issue where RedundantBraces removed syntactically REQUIRED parens. RULES WORTH TAKING for Spiral: (1) a removal is legal only if the re-parse is AST-equal, checked per removal, never by pattern alone; the census uses this as its oracle. (2) Parens a delimiter keyword already bounds are redundant: a whole ? cond ?> condition, a whole ?= scrutinee ?|, a macro/op argument already delimited by commas. (3) Keep-lists from the scalafmt bugs: the unit literal (), and comparisons/infix ops feeding |> (Spiral REQUIRES (a < b) |> f). (4) Staged rewrites with a fixed order and a check mode per style, like -new-syntax then -indent: an edition-2 -> 3 respell should run as ordered passes, each with its own format --check. Next step (DoD 1, the census): a formatter mode that, for every paren pair in a .spi, removes it, re-parses, and records construct + AST-equal yes/no; run it over every repo plus samples on a node and rank the kept-but-removable constructs.")
