inl kit_orphan_sim () = Task ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", "Orphan and process-tree edge cases become simulable from collected logs: the node and box record enough (process tree snapshots, parent/child edges, reap events) that kit can replay a monstrous incident and prove the job host catches it in minutes (user 11-01 round 5: main noticed node orphans too late, scaffolding still poor)", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-EXEC-S1", "kit,jobs,node,resilience,simulation,typefeatureset,team:rig,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_orphan_sim () = Planning ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_1 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:42Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (round 5, user list item 7). DO WE COLLECT LOGS THAT MAKE THIS SIMULABLE? NO. Measured: a kit job dir holds argv, cwd, pid, supervisor pid, slot, heartbeat_ms, started_ms, exit, stdout/stderr; machine-watch holds health.tsv and quiet.tsv (tab rows, DATA-ROWS-TYPED drift). Missing for replay: (a) child pids and the process tree over time (orphans are children whose job ended); (b) reap events (who killed what, when, why); (c) the node side at all from the box (ssh laptop failed to resolve moon at 10:4x, so a box-side watcher would have been blind too). PLAN: (1) typed event log, one row per observation: union proc_event = | Spawned : job_id * pid * parent_pid * image | Exited : pid * code | Snapshot : list (pid * parent_pid * image * cpu_ms * rss) | Reaped : pid * reason | Unreachable : host; rows as .spi modules (no tsv), written by the job host every heartbeat and on each job exit; (2) a pure detector orphan_verdict: given the event stream, a pid whose parent job has Exited and that is still in a later Snapshot for > N heartbeats is Orphan (job_id, pid, age); (3) a simulator that generates monstrous streams (fork bombs under a dead supervisor, pid reuse after exit, clock skew between box and node, unreachable host for 30 min, 500 orphans) and checks the detector flags each within 2 heartbeats with no false positives on pid reuse. DoD: detector + simulator tests green on the node; the job host writes proc_event rows on box and node; kit jobs orphans prints the live verdict; a replay of tonight's node incident (from main's notes) is flagged at the first heartbeat after the job exit. NEXT: the proc_event union + orphan_verdict + the pid-reuse test in a new HostFree package kit_proc_events (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: proc_event union, phantom host markers (:! Box / :! Node) on streams so box and node clocks are never compared raw, a GADT for detector states (Watching -> Suspect -> Orphan).")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_2 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 2u32, "2026-11-02T12:26Z", "resilience", "11-02 08:1x resilience (fresh box census): real taxonomy for the simulator + a detector false-positive class the plan misses. Dead-parent classes observed on the BOX (the sole accumulator; wifi + silicon-valley nodes were orphan-clean): CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL_LAUNCHER pwsh (Claude Code's own shell-tool processes), spiral-bin dotnet (unreaped Cells/compiler children, 6h-57h), gh codespace logs, ssh to codespaces, conhost pairs, ancient argless pwsh; on silicon-valley a 19h less reparented to init. KEY DETECTOR INSIGHT: shells (pwsh/conhost) are PARENTLESS BY DESIGN -- every run_in_background task, kit job, and shell-launcher has an exited parent immediately -- so orphan_verdict must NOT flag a dead-parent shell as Orphan on the parent-Exited-plus-still-in-Snapshot rule alone, or it false-positives on all live detached work. Needs class-tiered grace (compiler/remote children = minutes; shells = HOURS). Resilience's orphan-reaper.ps1 uses 60m vs 720m for exactly this. The simulator should add a legit-long-lived-detached-shell stream the detector must NOT flag.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_3 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 3u32, "2026-11-03T00:35Z", "resilience", "11-02 20:33 resilience (fresh evidence from tonight's reaper runs, for the simulator taxonomy + detector states): TWO classes the n1/n2 design misses, both observed live today.\n\n(1) THREE-TIER grace, not two. n2 said \"minutes vs hours\"; today's reaper proved a distinct SHORT tier: git/ssh/scp/gh = 3min grace (orphan-reaper ShortClasses), because they orphan on EVERY Fork-agent spawn and remote op (reaped 3 dangling git today, ppid dead, 17min old - Fork-spawned git children whose agent shell exited). So: SHORT (git/ssh/scp/gh, ~3min - net/VCS tools, orphan constantly, reap fast) < MEDIUM (dotnet/cargo/rustc/erl/beam/epmd compiler+remote children, ~60min) < LONG (pwsh/conhost shells, ~720min - parentless by design per n2). The simulator needs a fast-orphaning-VCS-tool stream reaped at SHORT grace that must NOT be conflated with the MEDIUM compiler tier.\n\n(2) NEW detector state: StuckDetached. The orphan_verdict GADT Watching->Suspect->Orphan assumes an orphan goes idle/zombie. But today a profile-mirror.ps1 (parent dead via if-due detach) was stuck 57min ACTIVELY thrashing on an unbounded tar child under memory pressure - dead-parent but CPU-BUSY, not idle. The \"still in a later Snapshot > N heartbeats\" rule flags it, but the REASON + remedy differ: Reaped reason = stuck-runtime-exceeds-bound, NOT abandoned-idle. Add to proc_event: a Reaped reason union | AbandonedIdle | StuckRuntime : {runtime_s; bound_s} | DeadParentShort. The simulator should generate a \"dead-parent-still-busy\" stream (busy tar) the detector flags via a runtime-bound rule, distinct from the idle/zombie orphan - else a legit long compile (busy, live parent) and a stuck detached run (busy, dead parent) look identical on CPU alone; the discriminator is PARENT-ALIVE x RUNTIME-BOUND, which the typed verdict must carry. Resilience's orphan-reaper already encodes all three: ShortClasses(3m)/Classes(60m)/ShellClasses(720m) + a dedicated stuck-mirror runtime sweep (>30m) - that script is the executable oracle the kit detector sim should match.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_4 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 4u32, "2026-11-04T04:53Z", "kit", "Moved here from DATA-ROWS-TYPED (closed 11-04): the machine-watch health.tsv and quiet.tsv rows under %LOCALAPPDATA% become typed Spiral rows (one record per fact, written and read through the kit_table_rows codec), since this item already simulates from those logs. Local state, so no repo census impact.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_5 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:32Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:4x resilience TAXONOMY REFINEMENT from today's incidents. NEW CLASS the n1/n2/3-tier-grace model misses: NON-KIT RESOURCE SATURATION. SystemInformer (a monitor, NOT kit-launched) held ~1.5 cores for 7.3h - INVISIBLE to the job-host: it is not a kit job/orphan, so orphan-reap + the escaped-daemon invariant never see it, yet it fed the sustained 100pct saturation preceding the 11-03 BSOD. IMPLICATION: the simulator/detector must model RESOURCE-saturation events (per-process CPU over time, ANY process incl non-kit tools), not only parent/child/reap events. DATA SOURCE now live: the per-heartbeat process-health sweep (MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE) samples top CPU consumers + classifies orphan(parent-dead)/runaway(tool >0.5 core)/kit-escape - the recordable time-series the sim can replay. CLEAN DATA POINT: today's escaped-daemon audit = 0 kit escapees (the kit-orphan class is well-controlled by the job-host; the UNcontrolled risk is non-kit tools + legit builds saturating). Recommend the sim event schema include {pid,ppid,name,lane,ws,cpu,is_kit_job,class} sampled, so it can replay a SATURATION incident (SI), not only an orphan incident. Deps REMOTE-EXEC-S1 + the sim engine are team:kit; releasing (resilience taxonomy contribution recorded).")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_6 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 6u32, "2026-11-04T14:27Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:2x data-source note: the new hourly kit-machine-watch task is accumulating a process-snapshot time-series in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\hourly.log (4 samples on schedule). Useful as replay data for this sim, BUT hourly is too COARSE to replay a FAST incident (the SI core-burn, a fork-storm, a saturation spike happen in minutes). For incident replay the sim wants finer sampling DURING an event: either a burst-mode (per-minute while rogues/hogs/load exceed a threshold) or the native health.tsv written at a tight cadence when something is detected. The hourly log gives the slow-trend baseline; the fast-incident capture is the gap.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_7 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 7u32, "2026-11-05T03:39Z", "resilience", "Captured a real typed process-tree frame (proc-frame.v1: pid,ppid,parent_live,name,session,threads,ws_mb,start,age_min; 303 procs, host box) as the concrete input a replay must consume. Key empirical finding for the simulator design: parent-dead is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT for orphan-to-reap. 18 parent-dead edges in the frame; 0 genuine runaways. They split into (a) by-design parent-exit daemons age 20-28h (csrss/wininit/winlogon via smss, igfx, explorer, rustdesk, mariadbd, SystemInformer) that a naive parent_live==false detector false-positives on and must never reap; (b) transient by-design git.exe age ~8min; (c) forming one-shots age <=4min (tail.exe, grep.exe) correctly under the 30min threshold. So the detector/replay must classify on name-allowlist x age x session-lineage, not on a parent-liveness bit alone. Frame saved to resilience scratchpad; can be promoted to a repo fixture once kit lands the detector verb (feeds MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR typed series). resilience tick1 2026-11-04 23:4x local.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_8 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 8u32, "2026-11-05T21:06Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~13:0x resilience SATURATION-INCIDENT FIXTURE + taxonomy (addresses note-6 gap: sim must replay RESOURCE-saturation, not only orphans; \"fast-incident capture is the gap\"). Captured saturation-frame.v1 live during a real 100%-CPU multi-compile starvation (resilience scratchpad saturation-frame-v1.txt; schema pid,ppid,name,cpu_pct,ws_mb,is_kit,class; 26 rows >=5% cpu; header carries cpu_total/commit/find_probe_ms=5646 as the incident signature - even a find over 6905 files times out).\nKEY TAXONOMY (reframes \"compiles dominate\" - they DO NOT):\n- DOMINANT saturators are NON-KIT: rustdesk ~476% (~4.75 cores, x-dumps-owned never-degrade) + SystemInformer (monitor, High prio) - together ~5.5 cores.\n- kit-live compiles (3x dotnet) sum only ~266% (~2.7 cores) = a MINORITY of load. The orphan/kit-escape detector would see NOTHING wrong (0 orphans, 0 escapes) yet the box is saturated - exactly note-6 point that saturation != orphan incident.\n- NEW detector-relevant class: PRIORITY-INVERSION STARVATION. SystemInformer ran at High priority (81% of one core sustained, measured clean over 3s - the 1s delta oversampled to 250%), PREEMPTING normal/below-normal work incl the Priority-7 xdumps-backlog-sao task (which was being terminated/erroring). A pure cpu_pct threshold misses this; the discriminator is PRIORITY_CLASS x cpu, so the sim schema should carry priority per row. Resilience ACTION taken: lowered SI High->BelowNormal (live, reverts on SI restart) so it yields - on the \"SI tuned to minimal CPU\" mandate; reduces the exact saturation implicated in the 11-03 pre-BSOD note.\nFixture promotable to a repo replay fixture once kit lands the detector verb. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_orphan_sim_9 () = Note ("KIT-ORPHAN-SIM", 9u32, "2026-11-09T06:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:0x local rig, three REAL cases from tonight for the replay corpus: (1) REMOTE WAITER ORPHANS: a forwarded kit job wait runs a waiter on the target; when the box-side ssh ended, the remote waiter kept polling (org2 killed one by pid on ganymede); three lanes' waiters burned about 16 pct CPU each at load 21 (backoff fix in TRAIN42; the outlive-the-session part is open). Signature: process args job wait <id>, parent a dead sshd session, age far past its ssh. (2) KILL-BY-COMMAND-LINE HITS ITS OWN SHELL: pgrep -f <pattern> | kill over ssh matched the remote bash whose own command line held the pattern, so the session died with exit 255 (twice tonight, memory remote-kill-by-cmdline-hits-own-shell); a reaper must match by executable path (awk on the args' first field), never by full-text pattern. (3) NON-KIT SATURATION: the box sat at 97 pct with no compiles; the top consumers were the Claude sessions themselves (60-140 pct each) and System, matching the 11-05 taxonomy that the dominant saturators are not kit. A sim fixture for (1): two frames, ssh alive with a waiter child, then ssh gone with the waiter re-parented; expected verdict: reap after its job settled or after a grace. Next step: those two frames as fixtures beside proc-frame.v1. Code held for TRAIN43.")
inl kit_definition () = Task ("KIT-DEFINITION", "A breakthrough one-paragraph definition of what kit is, grounded in prior art (Akita ai-memory, Zed DeltaDB, Anthropic Scion) and in what kit already does, used to cut bloat: every verb must serve it (user 11-01 round 5)", Story, P1, D4, 60u32, Active, "", "kit,research,memory,team:rig,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_definition () = Planning ("KIT-DEFINITION", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_definition_1 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit (round 5, user list item 5). BACKLOG FIRST: Scion and DeltaDB were already researched (CI-OFFLOAD n1 main-thread 10-17; EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR n9 kit2 10-25; EOIE-MEMORY n1 mentions DeltaDB). New here: Akita's project is akitaonrails/ai-memory (github.com/akitaonrails/ai-memory, MIT, Rust, launched 2026-05, v1.29.0 on 2026-08-19): a git-versioned markdown wiki of what coding agents learned, indexed with SQLite FTS5, so a task can move between Claude Code, Codex and Cursor without re-explaining (cross-provider handover); its author calls it beta. PRIOR ART IN ONE LINE EACH: ai-memory = agent memory as untyped markdown + full-text search; DeltaDB = version history that keeps the agent conversations behind each change; Scion = server-authoritative history with thin client fetch of only the needed files. WHAT KIT ALREADY IS: one typed state (agile.spi: items, notes with author lanes, planning), typed job records and receipts, content-keyed run/compile caches, a style census that refuses drift, and Claude memory files it is meant to absorb (kit-embodied-memory). DRAFT DEFINITION: kit is the typed ledger of a codebase's work: every fact an agent or person produces (task, decision, memory, job, receipt, metric) is one record of a Spiral type, content-addressed, linked to the conversation and the build that produced it, and checked by the compiler and by kit's own gates before it is trusted. ai-memory remembers text, DeltaDB remembers why, Scion fetches only what is needed; kit remembers typed facts, so memory can be refused when wrong (provenance + verification), not just searched. ANTI-BLOAT TEST: a verb that writes no typed record and checks none is not kit; it is glue. DoD: the definition lands as the first paragraph of kit AGENTS.md (user reviews wording), a verb census marks each kit verb Records|Checks|Glue, glue verbs get an archive-or-justify item; EOIE-MEMORY scope reconciled with ai-memory's handover feature (adopt cross-provider handover as a typed record, not markdown). NEXT: the verb census (kit --help walk, CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: verb_role union Records|Checks|Glue on the census rows.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_2 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 2u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit: KIT-TWO-HUBS n2 holds the prior-art lessons (Anthropic Scion, Zed DeltaDB, HTCondor, Slurm backfill, Nomad regions, Fossil, ETH SCION) and what is actually novel. The draft definition gains: federated across networks, one authority per record, provenance per change, decisions made by pre-registered typed rules.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_3 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 3u32, "2026-11-09T09:42Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:0x local rig, DRAFT DEFINITION v1 (from the verb set in command_spec_domain/direct_verbs.spi + agile subcommands, and the prior art in note 1 and KIT-TWO-HUBS n2): rig is the one-call operating layer for a fleet of coding agents. Every action an agent takes on code, machines or plans is ONE verb whose arguments, refusals and receipts are typed, so an agent never chains shell steps to do one thing, never redoes another agent's work, and always leaves a receipt a later agent or the user can check. It keeps three records, each with one writer and provenance per change: the PLAN (backlog items, typed planning, waits, claims, notes), the WORK (jobs, windows and slots placed on machines by load and memory) and the CODE'S HEALTH (standards, lint, census ratchets, units, trains). Every decision about them (where a job runs, whether an item is ready, whether a change may land) is made by a pre-registered typed rule, not by a prompt. What is new against the prior art: ai-memory and DeltaDB keep memory and history UNTYPED (markdown, conversations); Scion and HTCondor/Slurm place work but do not own the plan or the code's health; rig types all three and rules over them. VERB FIT (a verb stays if it serves one clause): one-call primitives = do, read, lines, grep, table, repo, patch, status, help, guide; plan = agile, backlog, resume, drafts, experiment, evidence, gap (pending); work = job, run, window, process, ps, kill, machine, schedule, mirror, ci, env, hook, metrics; code health = standards, lint, crate, units, heal. CANDIDATES TO EXAMINE (fit unclear, not a cut decision): proxy (70 legacy capabilities, most now have a one-call verb), bundle, story and standup (overlap agile views/cadence), adoption and memory (measure agents rather than serve them). Next step: per candidate, measure last-30-day calls from transcripts and report keep/fold/cut rows for the user. DoD: one paragraph accepted by the user, and every verb has a clause row or a decision row. TypeFeatures: a verb_role union (OneCall, Plan, Work, Health) on direct_verb, so a verb without a role does not compile.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_4 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 4u32, "2026-11-09T09:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:0x local rig, CANDIDATE USAGE (rg over ~/.claude/projects/C--home-git/**/*.jsonl, i.e. transcripts since the 10-28 profile wipe; counts are text MENTIONS of kit <verb> <word>, so tool calls plus quotes and notes, an upper bound): baselines job start 4281 (26 files), do 845 (38 files). Candidates: story|standup 348 (13 files) -> in real use, likely KEEP (check whether agile cadence/views replace them before folding); adoption|memory 131 (9 files) -> occasional; proxy 86 (9 files, 45 of them in this session's own transcript, mostly notes) -> near-dead outside a few capabilities; bundle 19 (6 files) -> near-dead. Next: split proxy mentions by capability (which of the 70 still get called) and confirm bundle's last real call, then keep/fold/cut rows for the user.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_5 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 5u32, "2026-11-09T09:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 06:1x local rig, PROXY SPLIT (same transcripts, first 100 mention lines): about 14 of the 70 capabilities appear at all: fs-remove-tree (most, the delete path), source-topology, archive-extract and zip-extract, toolchain, command-capture, fs-write, prune-build-cache, prune-release-transients, prune-uncovered, spiral-session-shutdown, incident-recovery, coverage-export, run. Reading: proxy is a grab-bag whose live part is (a) safe filesystem mutation (remove-tree, write, archive extract) and (b) maintenance (prunes, session shutdown, toolchain, source-topology). Proposal for the user (no cut without a yes): promote the live ones to typed verbs under the definition's clauses (fs ops into do write/move/remove; prunes into a maintenance verb; source-topology into units or repo), then retire the other ~56 behind a deprecation receipt for one release, measured by kit adoption.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_6 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 6u32, "2026-11-09T12:13Z", "tibia", "11-09 08:3x tibia (offered by rig; read-only analysis, no kit code touched). USAGE TABLE for the definition (keep/fold/cut rows; NO cuts without the user). METHOD: across ~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl (97 transcripts, 2.0 GB, every lane and session on the box), only lines holding a \"type\":\"tool_use\" entry (real calls, not quoted text in results), first kit.exe/rig.exe verb per line, plus that line's date; calls through a variable (& $k agile) are NOT counted, so these are LOWER bounds. COUNTS (verb: calls, last used): agile 3335 (11-09), job 1381 (11-09), do 606 (11-09), window 497 (11-09), standards 404 (11-09), story 64 (11-09), env 37 (11-09), help 26 (11-09), crate 13 (11-09), table 11 (11-01), machine 8 (11-07), repo 5 (11-08), guide 4 (11-09), backlog 4 (11-09), status 3 (11-08), process 3 (10-30), memory 3 (10-31), resume 2 (11-09), evidence 2 (10-31), ps 1 (10-30), proxy 1 (10-30); ZERO agent calls: standup, read, lines, grep, edit, kill, patch, mirror, ci, experiment, adoption, bundle. ROWS. KEEP (core, daily): agile, job, do, window, standards. KEEP (low but live): env, crate, help, machine, repo, guide, status, resume, table. FOLD (pure aliases, byte-identical output measured read-only on the live backlog: `standup status` == `agile status`, `story show` == `agile show`, 0 diff lines): story, standup, backlog -> document them as aliases of agile or drop the alias names (user decides). KEEP, script-only: bundle (0 agent calls, but compiler-contracts/renew-strict-evidence.ps1:76 bundle growth-receipt, renew-cold-rebuild.ps1:101 and test-strict-preflight.ps1:41 bundle check, last changed 11-08), proxy (plumbing: test-strict-preflight.ps1:41 proxy command-capture). CUT CANDIDATES (0 agent calls; script callers NOT yet checked one by one): read, lines, grep, edit (agents use the harness Read/Grep/Edit tools instead), kill, ps (1 call), patch, mirror, ci, adoption, process, evidence, memory. experiment = keep pending KIT-EXPERIMENTS. NEXT for this row set: grep scripts for each cut candidate (as done for bundle) before the user decides.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_7 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 7u32, "2026-11-09T12:19Z", "tibia", "11-09 08:4x tibia, follow-up to note 6: the SCRIPT-CALLER check for every cut candidate is done (one Grep over *.ps1/*.sh/*.yml/*.yaml under C:\\home\\git, matching both `kit|rig[.exe] <verb>` and `$kit|$rig|$KitBinary|$kitExe|$k <verb>`). Result: ZERO script callers for read, lines, grep, edit, kill, ps, patch, mirror, ci, adoption, process, evidence, memory. The only hit was a false positive (compiler-contracts/test-job-orphan-wait.ps1:21, the phrase \"kit process\" in a PASS message). Combined with note 6 (zero or near-zero agent calls in 2.0 GB of transcripts), these 13 verbs are the evidence-backed CUT/FOLD set for the user's decision. Caveat: Spiral sources calling them in-process were not scanned (kit's own src/ dispatch tables reference every verb by construction). Still kept: experiment (pending KIT-EXPERIMENTS). Kept as script-only: bundle, proxy. FOLD as aliases of agile: story, standup, backlog.")
inl note_of_kit_definition_8 () = Note ("KIT-DEFINITION", 8u32, "2026-11-09T18:24Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 21:1x local rig, DRAFT DEFINITION (grounded in tibia's usage table, notes 6-7; for the user's review): \"rig is the shared ground a fleet of autonomous coding agents works on: a typed backlog they claim, wait on and hand off through (agile), supervised jobs placed on a fleet of machines with receipts and time windows (job, window), one-call file programs that read and edit with proof of what changed (do), and enforced code standards that only ratchet tighter (standards). Every verb replaces a multi-step agent habit with one typed call that leaves evidence, and a verb that serves none of those four jobs does not belong.\" WHY THIS CUT: the five named verbs carry 6,223 of the ~6,400 counted agent calls (agile 3,335, job 1,381, do 606, window 497, standards 404); the definition names exactly what agents actually use. CONSEQUENCES (proposals only, the user decides): the 13 zero-caller verbs (read, lines, grep, edit, kill, ps, patch, mirror, ci, adoption, process, evidence, memory: 0 agent calls in 2.0 GB of transcripts AND 0 script callers) become fold-or-cut candidates; story, standup and backlog fold into agile as aliases; env, crate, help, machine, repo, guide, status, resume and table stay as live support verbs; experiment waits on KIT-EXPERIMENTS; bundle and proxy stay script-only. Prior art comparison (ai-memory, DeltaDB, Scion) is still owed: the draft is grounded in kit's own usage first, on purpose.")
inl kit_standards_ecosystem () = Task ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", "kit standards grows the repo-level rules of a minimally usable ecosystem and every own repo gets a root standards.spi: RepoEntry, WorkflowShape, NoUntypedRows, LibNotCopied (user 11-01 round 5, after the standards.spi payoff check)", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-STANDARDS-SPI", "kit,standards,ci,drift,typefeatureset,team:rig,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_standards_ecosystem () = Planning ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_1 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 1u32, "2026-11-01T14:43Z", "kit", "11-01 10:4x kit. Why: standards.spi pays off in kit (KIT-STANDARDS-SPI n re-measure: 2 true refusals tonight, 0 false) but only kit and tibia use it. RULES (one constructor each in the standards rule union, each a refusal with the fix printed): RepoEntry = scripts/init.ps1 and scripts/workflow.ps1 exist and are the only CI entry points; WorkflowShape = .github/workflows holds only gh-pages.yml + actions.lock and every run step calls scripts/workflow.ps1 (shared with CI-WORKFLOW-NAMES); NoUntypedRows = no tracked .tsv/.rows/.csv/.txt/.py outside an allowlist of upstream vendored dirs (DATA-ROWS-TYPED, no-py-txt-in-repos); LibNotCopied = a definition whose normalized digest equals a spiral/lib definition is refused in an app (consumes KIT-REUSE-METRICS Exact classes). ROLLOUT ORDER (by drift found): tibia, fiscal, x, spiral, then the small repos; each repo gets a root standards.spi with its exceptions written as typed rows, ratchet mode first (legacy counted, new refused). DoD: kit standards <repo> --ecosystem prints the four verdicts for every own repo; the root standards.spi lands unstaged in each; the edit hook kit-style-check.ps1 reads it; node CLI tests: one refusal and one pass per rule. NEXT: add the RepoEntry rule (simplest, no parsing) with its two tests in kit_standards (CPU-light). TYPEFEATURES: rule union with a phantom scope marker (:! RepoScope / :! PackageScope) so a repo rule cannot be applied to a package.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_2 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:01Z", "kit", "11-01 13:1x kit: NoUntypedRows gets a companion: drafts belong in the kit drafts store (KIT-DRAFTS-STORE), so the refusal message points to kit drafts put <repo> <ITEM> instead of only saying no.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_3 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 3u32, "2026-11-09T14:29Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "MILESTONES: RepoEntry rule (init.ps1 + workflow.ps1 are the only CI entries) 60 m; WorkflowShape rule (gh-pages.yml + actions.lock only, steps call workflow.ps1) 60 m; NoUntypedRows rule (tracked .tsv/.rows/.csv/.txt/.py outside a vendored allowlist) 60 m; LibNotCopied rule (digest equal to a lib definition, from KIT-REUSE-METRICS Exact classes) 90 m; root standards.spi rollout to tibia and fiscal in ratchet mode 60 m; rollout to x, spiral and the small repos 30 m.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_4 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 4u32, "2026-11-09T14:30Z", "kit-split", "split into 5 chained children by kit agile split")
inl note_of_kit_standards_ecosystem_5 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-ECOSYSTEM", 5u32, "2026-11-09T18:13Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig groom: already split into M1-M6 (M6 restored by hand after the split defect); the parent carries no work of its own, so it waits on its last child.")
