inl spiral_cli_json_protocol_test () = Task ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", "Port the spiral CLI SPIRAL_JSON envelope tests (test_json_protocol.py) to a Spiral check", TaskKind, P3, D3, 90u32, Blocked, "", "compiler,spiral-cli,tests,spiral,team:compiler,compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test () = Planning ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 1u32, "2026-11-01T17:24Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:27 compiler: filed on main's ruling (11-01: PORT, don't drop). WHAT: apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py (4831 B, sha in the move receipt below) is unittest coverage of the spiral CLI's SPIRAL_JSON envelope, with six cases: success is one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL; a child exception is a failure carrying the original diagnostic; a CUDA driver failure is not reported as success; a Rust build failure is a failure carrying cargo's diagnostic; Lua print/write are the output; a Lua runtime error is a failure carrying its message. It also uses unicode and spaces in paths. Nothing has run it since 0ec501fb. MOVED byte-identical to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST\\test_json_protocol.py; git shows apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py as unstaged D. EXISTING COVERAGE (checked): apps/spiral/build.ps1:100-129's run check covers SUCCESS ONLY, at the default trace level: rust/cuda/lua/gleam each print 42, the stdout must parse as one JSON document, and the output must match. Cells sets SPIRAL_JSON=1 on every run (targets.ex:745), so a broken success envelope already breaks Cells. NOT covered anywhere: the other trace levels, all four failure paths, and unicode/space paths. So this is a port, not a close. DEFINITION OF DONE: the five uncovered behaviours run on every CLI build, in the same place the success check runs, so CI's fresh CLI build fails when any of them regresses: (1) one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL value the CLI accepts; (2) child exception -> failure + original diagnostic; (3) CUDA driver failure -> not success; (4) cargo failure -> failure + cargo diagnostic; (5) Lua runtime error -> failure + message; all with a path containing a space and a non-ASCII character. Then the draft is archived. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: a case is a record {backend : backend union (no strings); source; trace : trace_level union; expect : expectation}, where expectation = output_contains of string | failure_with of diagnostic_fragment | not_success. The checker decodes the envelope into a result union (ok of output | error of diagnostic) and matches expectation against it, so no field-presence probing on raw JSON. NEXT STEP: read the CLI's TRACE_LEVEL parser in apps/spiral/spiral.spi to list the accepted levels, then decide where the cases live. Preferred: a Spiral cell next to the CLI, run by Cells; fallback: data rows in build.ps1's existing case loop (thin glue, no new tool). MOVE RECEIPT: sha256 5B010564E3BF..., hash-verified after the move.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:57Z", "compiler", "11-01 13:58 compiler: NEXT-STEP DONE (levels and placement). Accepted TRACE_LEVEL values: lib trace.spi's union trace_level = Verbose | Debug | Info | Warning | Critical (read with reflection.union_try_pick, so anything else counts as unset). spiral.spi run_main (1760-1784): with SPIRAL_JSON=1, tracing is switched off (trace_enabled <- false) and the result is printed whatever the level; without it, the result prints only at Info. So the 'one JSON document at every level' check needs 6 runs per case: the 5 levels plus unset. PLACEMENT DECISION: apps/spiral/build.ps1's run check (lines 100-129). It already builds the fresh CLI on every CI run, decodes the envelope, and fails the build, and it is the only place that runs right after the CLI is built. A Cells cell would test whatever spiral.exe the box has, and the box keeps a stale one (see the unsupported-cells memory), so that placement would miss exactly the regressions this guards against. The cases go in as rows of the existing @(...) case table, typed by their expectation (output_contains | failure_with | not_success) as a row field, not as new loops; no new tool. NEXT STEP: add the expectation field to the 4 existing rows, then the failure rows (lua runtime error, rust compile error, python raise for the child-exception case, cuda driver failure where the box has no GPU path), and the 6-level loop around the success rows, with a space and a non-ASCII character in checkDir.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:02Z", "compiler", "11-01 14:02 compiler: PORT DRAFTED: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST\\build-run-check.kitdo (2 replacements on apps/spiral/build.ps1; live 41054bb7... becomes 847eca31...; the result parses with 0 errors). It turns the run check's case list into one table with a typed expectation per row: output (contains) | exact (trimmed, case-sensitive) | failure (nonzero exit, text in stdout+stderr, no \"command_result\" in stdout). The 4 existing rows keep their meaning. New rows port all six Python tests: the quoted child output at 6 trace levels (unset + the 5 union cases), child exception, CUDA driver failure, Rust build failure (cargo diagnostic), Lua print+write exact output (\"lua-print<TAB>1<NEWLINE>lua-write\"), and Lua runtime error. Every protocol file lives under \"protocol ü\" with spaces in the file names, as in the Python suite. TRACE_LEVEL is restored in the existing finally. NOT LANDED: it needs a fresh CLI build to prove, and the box is compile-quiet (kit-TRAIN7c heavy window). LAND WHEN: run `pwsh apps/spiral/build.ps1` (or its run-check part) with the program applied to a scratch copy; all 16 rows must pass on the box, and a deliberately broken row (e.g. expect \"failure\" on the success level row) must fail it. Then land unstaged, archive the draft .py, and close the item after the user's push turns CI green.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:57Z", "compiler", "11-01 15:57 compiler: PROVEN AND LANDED. Proof on the node's CLI (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\workspace\\target\\release\\spiral.exe, built 10-30): the patched run-check block, extracted verbatim into a harness (job-1793562714968-compiler-jsonproto2, 79 s), passed 16 of 16 rows. 4 original success rows; 6 trace levels (unset, Verbose, Debug, Info, Warning, Critical), each giving one JSON document with 'child output: \"quoted\"'; child exception, CUDA driver failure, Rust build failure and Lua error are each a nonzero exit with the diagnostic and no command_result; Lua print+write exact. NEGATIVE CONTROL: the same harness with two expectations deliberately wrong (child exception expected as output; Lua exact text '...2...' instead of '...1...') reports exactly those 2 failures, so the check can fail. LANDED: apps/spiral/build.ps1 41054BB7 -> 847ECA31 (unstaged, +37/-11, parses). ARCHIVED: the drafts folder (build-run-check.kitdo + test_json_protocol.py) via scratch-archive. git shows apps/spiral/test_json_protocol.py as unstaged D, already noted at the move. CLOSES when CI's fresh CLI build passes the extended run check after the user's push (then Done).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 5u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "compiler", "11-03 07:3x compiler GROOM (WIP cap: Active = touched in 12 h). Blocked on the user's push: the only step left is the next CI run after it, then Done.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_json_protocol_test_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-JSON-PROTOCOL-TEST", 6u32, "2026-11-06T10:19Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler GROOM: NOT closable yet. The port is in pushed spiral (98e51d32:apps/spiral/build.ps1 sets $env:SPIRAL_JSON for the run check), but the scheduled CI run 41848519924 log has no SPIRAL_JSON/envelope row, so nothing proves it ran on Linux. Split DoD: make the run check print one named line (e.g. \"json protocol: N envelopes ok\") and close on the next scheduled run that shows it.")
inl remote_cloud_linux_worker () = Task ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "Linux cloud workers (Cloud VM europa, then codespaces) as kit/remote.ps1 targets: OS-aware machine kinds, transport, bootstrap", Feature, P1, D4, 95u32, Planned, "LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE", "kit,team:rig,remote,linux,europa,codespaces,x-dumps,multiplatform", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker () = Planning ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 1u32, "2026-11-01T17:28Z", "personal", "personal lane (session ec368ac8), 2026-11-01 13:17 UTC-3. The user rented Cloud instance europa (id 6dfb484b-e1bf-4941-a545-6e6000d5e50c): plan vhp-2c-4gb-amd ($24/mo), region sjc, Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS, <europa-addr>, 2 vCPU EPYC-Rome, 3.3 GiB RAM + 8 GiB swap, 78 GB free. ssh alias `europa` in ~/.ssh/config (key ~/.ssh/cloud_ed25519, HostKeyAlias europa). Measured: HF CDN 66 MB/s on a single stream, 290 MB/s with 6 streams (9 GB in 31 s), against >10 min for a partial remote scan from the box. Ingress is free on Europa. Goal (user 11-01): a Linux worker for the X-archive downloads AND node work, making remote.ps1 and kit multiplatform, and dogfooding codespaces VMs the same way. Main (11-01): remote.ps1 assumes an ssh alias plus pwsh -EncodedCommand; codespaces have no remote-machines.json entry; kit owns the OS-aware work under EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR. Next: kit lane decides the driver shape (a linux machine kind in remote-machines.json, bash or pwsh transport); personal installs pwsh+kit on the VM once kit says how.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 2u32, "2026-11-01T17:35Z", "kit", "11-01 13:35 kit DECISION (asked by the personal lane): shape (a) with pwsh now and (b) as the destination; never a bash dialect. WHY: remote.ps1 already takes a per-machine 'shell' and sends pwsh -EncodedCommand. pwsh on Linux keeps ONE transport and ONE remote-snippet language for both OSes, and every job verb goes through the remote kit job host (target.eoie), so the VM needs a Linux kit build anyway; that build is also step one of REMOTE-EXEC-S2 (kit job --on), which later replaces remote.ps1. SPLIT: the personal lane installs pwsh, syncs kit + spiral under /home/remote and builds kit-cli with -j1 (3.3 GiB plus swap; a failing Windows-only crate is LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE data), then reports the kit path and its gitRoot/mirrorBase/stage. kit (done, unstaged in i574n.github/scripts/remote.ps1): a machine 'kind' field (linux -> '/' separator, default Windows keeps '\\', so the live Windows node's paths are unchanged); Join-RemotePath replaces every Join-Path over target.gitRoot/stage/mirrorBase. The status snippet is portable: /proc/loadavg and MemAvailable on Linux, Win32 CIM on Windows, MachineName, DriveInfo of the cwd root, LocalApplicationData instead of LOCALAPPDATA, multi-segment Join-Path, and SPIRAL_EXE falling back to the process env. The lib digest is normalized to '\\', so box and Linux digests compare. Sync deps step: SymbolicLink on Linux (Junction on Windows) and Copy-Item instead of robocopy on Linux. Bug fixed along the way: the box-runner line used target.gitRoot instead of the local git root. REGRESSION on the Windows node: parse 0 errors; status prints the same lines (runner F1DD2E34, compiler DBC5EC18); sync kit -Mirror kit-verify REMOTE-SYNC-OK. NEXT: add the europa entry to ~/.config/remote-machines.json (kind linux, shell pwsh, eoie = the Linux kit path) when the personal lane reports its paths; proof = remote.ps1 europa status, then sync kit plus one notebook job.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 3u32, "2026-11-01T17:37Z", "kit", "11-01 13:37 kit: europa entry added to ~/.config/remote-machines.json (kind linux, shell pwsh /usr/bin/pwsh 7.6.9, gitRoot /home/remote/git, mirrorBase /home/remote, stage /home/remote/stage, eoie /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit; backup .before-vultr-20261101.bak). FIRST LINUX DRIVE OK: remote.ps1 europa status -> 'europa cpu load 0.78 free-mem 2.8 GB free-disk 66.6 GB', kit d486fd04, spiral 504e1e07, lib digest computed. Expected gaps: the kit binary (the personal lane's -j1 build is running) and the Spiral compiler/dotnet (node compiler missing), which a notebook job needs. NEXT: after the build, sync kit -Mirror plus one job through the Linux kit job host.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 4u32, "2026-11-01T17:58Z", "kit", "11-01 13:58 kit: FIRST LINUX PROOF GREEN via the multiplatform remote.ps1. The personal lane built kit-cli on Linux (exit 0, 2m18s at -j1, 335 MB debug, no Windows-only crate, no OOM) and installed dotnet 11.0.100-rc.3 via spiral's install-dotnet.ps1. 'remote.ps1 europa sync kit -Mirror kit-verify' -> REMOTE-SYNC-OK, digest 4E7612B1595F0D9B, byte-identical to the Windows node's digest for the same tree, so the tree digest holds across OSes. Job job-1793555784366 through the Linux kit job host: agile init/begin/add/note ok (no-respell header written; elapsed_ms 0-1 on a tiny state); lint macros over kit/src -> violations 906, new 0, identical to Windows. BLOCKED: agile check needs the Spiral compiler (state attestation); the personal lane will build single-flight there, compared by build-identity sourceSha16 because SDKs differ. Lessons the personal lane hit by hand, both already in remote.ps1 sync: a plain clone of the box HEAD is not buildable because staged files are missing (sync carries 'git diff HEAD' + untracked), and box mtimes leave cargo stale (remote extract uses tar -xmf). My own proof-script mistakes, not Linux bugs: forgot 'agile begin' (lease) first; the begin title needs 3..7 words.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 5u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 11-01 14:1x (user): the personal lane leads x-dumps on this VM while making kit multiplatform. The VM's CPU and disk go to x-dumps FIRST; other lanes' jobs run there only when x-dumps leaves room. Codespaces quota is burned with useful runs, and nothing is lost: code, logs and outputs are fetched back before every stop/delete. Node switch status: the Windows node now runs kit.exe (shared store via junctions, KIT-RENAME n65), so OS-aware remote.ps1 targets kit on every machine. Follow-ups filed: K3S-EVERYWHERE, IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD, CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP, NODE-VALUE-METRICS.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 6u32, "2026-11-01T18:14Z", "kit", "11-01 14:14 kit: The personal lane built the Spiral compiler on europa (single-flight Release, 89.7 s, SDK rc.3; Hopac.dll left IL after a crossgen2 failure). build-identity sourceSha16 f6f6879566c5a830 = the box pack14a's (box core DBC5EC18, europa 7B234DAC): the same source, different det-root/SDK, comparable by source (Assert-NodeCore rule). Job job-1793556613190 found 3 gaps: (1) agile check -> 'spawn process: No such file or directory', because the kit job env on Linux lacks cargo and dotnet on PATH; KIT FIX: compiler discovery must find dotnet under the spiral cache dir (~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/dotnet on Linux) like Get-SpiralCacheDir, not rely on PATH; (2) no Erlang/Elixir for Cells notebooks (asked the personal lane for the spiral-pinned versions); (3) the kit-verify mirror on europa lacks spiral beside kit (notebook-batch resolves ../spiral); sync spiral before the notebook rerun. LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE data from the personal lane: spiral/apps/compiler/scripts/build.ps1:28 unrolled an empty @() to \\ on Linux (no /usr/share/dotnet), so .Count threw under strict mode; fixed on the box, unstaged, by wrapping the if in @(...).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_7 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 7u32, "2026-11-01T19:08Z", "personal", "personal 15:2x -04: europa worker state. kit-cli builds on Linux (-j1 2m18s). Spiral compiler single-flight builds (89.7 s, sourceSha16 f6f6879566c5a830 = box). kit lane: multiplatform remote.ps1 drives europa (kind linux, shell pwsh); sync is byte-identical to the node; the Linux job host ran agile + lint jobs identical to Windows. Installed: pwsh 7.6.9, rust nightly, dotnet 11.0.100-rc.3 (install-dotnet.ps1), OTP-29.1.2 + Elixir 1.20.5 (CI pins), /etc/environment PATH for dotnet + cargo. Fixed build.ps1:28 empty-@() unroll (Linux-only failure; relayed to compiler via kit). Remaining (kit): compiler discovery via LocalApplicationData on Linux, agile check + notebook proof. Traps: pwsh stdin to `ssh bash -s` gets CRLF (use base64), `sort > file` truncated /etc/environment on failure (restored from backup; write temp + mv), pkill -f matching its own ssh command line (anchor on the binary path).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_8 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 8u32, "2026-11-01T19:52Z", "kit", "11-01 15:52 kit: LINUX WORKER FULLY GREEN through remote.ps1 + the Linux kit job host. Job job-1793562586396: agile check ok (compiler=available, the Linux-built single-flight), notebooks 2/2 (agile_cadence 6/6, kit_bindings_model 2/2) via kit/scripts/notebook-batch.ps1. Setup steps that were needed, all light: (1) the spiral shared checkout's deps: cloned i574n/The-Spiral-Language into /home/remote/git/spiral/deps, plus mix local.hex/rebar and mix deps.get in apps/cells (kino); the mirror sync then reports MIRROR-DEPS-OK; (2) kit scripts made portable: notebook-batch.ps1 uses multi-segment Join-Path, user-env.ps1 returns early off Windows; (3) remote.ps1 Linux status prints cpu N% from /proc/stat; (4) the first runs were RED with no detail, because the first Cells build happened inside the job; a later run passes. The PATH fix stays interim (the personal lane's /etc/environment); the kit fix is still compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir. Main has opened europa to all lanes for light-to-medium jobs.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_9 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:55Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:5x -04: CODESPACES HALF, design from a probe (no codespace touched). `gh codespace ssh --config -c <name>` emits a plain OpenSSH host `cs.<name>.main` (User codespace, ProxyCommand = gh codespace ssh --stdio, ControlMaster auto, own IdentityFile). So a codespace can be a remote.ps1 machine with NO new transport: (1) a refresh step writes `gh codespace ssh --config` for the live codespaces into ~/.ssh/codespaces.config, Included from ~/.ssh/config (never hand-edited, regenerated on each create/delete); (2) remote-machines.json gets kind linux, shell pwsh, sshAlias cs.<name>.main, gitRoot /workspaces, mirrorBase /tmp/remote, stage /tmp/remote/stage; (3) bootstrap = pwsh from the tarball (no MS apt for the universal image), plus kit-cli built -j<cores> or copied from europa's Linux build (same glibc family?) to save ~3 min of codespace quota; (4) fetch-back before stop/delete = remote.ps1 fetch of stage + job stores (main's rule). Lifecycle stays main's (CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS); remote.ps1 is kit's. Next: a proof on a codespace once main OKs one.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_10 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 10u32, "2026-11-01T23:08Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:1x -04: CODESPACES HALF GREEN on main's bookish-space-goggles (with main's OK; its lib sweep left untouched). (1) ~/.ssh/codespaces.config = `gh codespace ssh --config -c <name>`, Included at the top of ~/.ssh/config (backup config.before-codespaces-include-20261101). (2) remote-machines.json entry `cs-bookish`: kind linux, proxied true, sshAlias cs.<name>.main, shell /home/codespace/pwsh/pwsh, gitRoot /tmp/remote/git, mirrorBase /tmp/remote, stage /tmp/remote/stage, eoie /tmp/remote/bin/kit (backup .before-codespace-20261101.bak). (3) remote.ps1 (kit's file, unstaged, minimal): a `proxied` machine skips Resolve-MachineAddress's TCP-22 probing and the -o HostName pin at all 3 ssh/scp sites (Get-HostOption), so the alias's ProxyCommand is used; node and europa status are unchanged (regression checked). (4) No compile on the codespace: europa's Linux kit (needs GLIBC_2.39; the universal image is Ubuntu 24.04 with glibc 2.39) copied gzip VM→box→codespace (83 MB, 84 s down + 150 s up), sha256 625e0bb0c3219649 identical. Proof: `remote.ps1 cs-bookish status` reports codespaces-88a5c6; `job start cs-proof ... uname -sr` → job-1793574461983-cs-proof finished exit 0 in 619 ms via the codespace's kit job host. Remaining: a refresh verb that regenerates codespaces.config + entries on codespace create/delete (main's lifecycle); kit's compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir; a CLIXML stderr parse error when a remote status sub-check fails (cosmetic).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_11 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 11u32, "2026-11-01T23:10Z", "kit", "11-01 19:10 kit: ack x-dumps' remote.ps1 edit (proxied: true machines skip TCP probing and the address cache; Get-HostOption at the 3 ssh/scp sites), so codespaces work as machines; node and europa status unchanged. Leftovers owned by kit: compiler discovery via the spiral cache dir, and -OutputFormat Text on the remote pwsh, so a failing remote sub-check returns text, not CLIXML, to the local parser.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_12 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 12u32, "2026-11-01T23:15Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:2x -04: `remote.ps1 codespaces refresh` (unstaged, in remote.ps1): for every Available codespace it regenerates ~/.ssh/codespaces.config from `gh codespace ssh --config`, ensures `Include codespaces.config` heads ~/.ssh/config, replaces stale proxied cs.* machine entries and upserts cs-<first-name-word> (kind linux, proxied, shell /home/codespace/pwsh/pwsh, /tmp/remote paths, eoie /tmp/remote/bin/kit). Proof: refresh → cs-bookish; job cs-proof2 started through the regenerated entry; node/europa status unchanged. Lifecycle hook for main: run it after every codespace create/delete. A fresh codespace still needs pwsh at ~/pwsh/pwsh + the Linux kit at /tmp/remote/bin/kit (copy europa's, glibc 2.39 ok) before kit jobs.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_13 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 13u32, "2026-11-01T23:30Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:4x -04: Linux compiler discovery FIXED (kit's item (b), handed to me). The spawn site is kit/src/kit_process/native_compiler.rs (hand-written host code include!d into the generated process.rs, so regen won't clobber it). It resolved dotnet as KIT_DOTNET → SPIRAL_DOTNET → %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\toolchains\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe → bare \"dotnet\", and LOCALAPPDATA is absent on Linux, so a job host without dotnet on PATH got ENOENT. New `spiral_dotnet_from(env)` follows the same cache rule as kit_compiler_discovery's deployed DLL: SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR, else %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin (Windows) or ~/.cache/spiral-bin (other), + toolchains/dotnet/dotnet[.exe], then PATH. The env is injected so the test needs no global set_var. Proof on europa: cargo test -p kit-process 3/3 (new dotnet_resolves_from_the_spiral_cache_before_path). End-to-end A/B in one job under `env -i HOME=/root PATH=/usr/bin:/bin` (no dotnet on PATH): OLD kit → \"spawn process: No such file or directory\"; NEW kit → \"agile check ok ... build_attested=2 compiler=available source=deployed\". No Cargo/workspace change. Unstaged on the box; kit folds it into TRAIN9. Side finding: the box kit tree currently isn't buildable until TRAIN8 generates kit_k3s/k3s.rs (kit-cli depends on kit-k3s; I used a mirror-only placeholder for the proof, never in the box tree). With this, /etc/environment PATH on europa is no longer required for kit (kept, harmless).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_14 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 14u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "kit", "11-01 19:40 kit: SSH PENALTY FIX (root cause found by x-dumps). OpenSSH 10.2 PerSourcePenalties (noauth) penalized our shared home IP after 84 connect-then-close events in 2 h, mostly remote.ps1's TCP-22 reachability probe (Test-TcpPort), plus parallel lanes. That produced \"port 22 unreachable\", then \"login refused\". Server side (x-dumps, europa sshd_config.d/10-kit-workers.conf): exempt the home IP, noauth:0, MaxStartups 30:30:100. CLIENT HEAL (kit, unstaged in remote.ps1 Resolve-MachineAddress): when an address is cached, try ssh on it directly first and probe TCP only after that fails, so a healthy machine sees zero noauth events per call. Regression: europa and laptop status ok, and a cloud job started right after.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_15 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 15u32, "2026-11-02T00:32Z", "kit", "11-01 20:32 kit: the cached-address direct ssh check (added 19:4x) now carries the same BatchMode=yes ServerAliveInterval=5 ServerAliveCountMax=2 options main added to the route probe, because ConnectTimeout does not cover a stalled handshake (a probe hung up to 1.5 h during the sshd lockout). Follow-up for the kit job host's own ssh calls (REMOTE-EXEC-S2 transport): always pass BatchMode plus ServerAlive, so a stalled server ends a call in about 10 s instead of hanging a job.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_16 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 16u32, "2026-11-02T03:09Z", "kit", "11-01 23:09 kit: europa runs the TRAIN8 kit (built -j1 from a kit-train8 mirror, 4m18s, swapped atomically into /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit, sha 91807212..., old kept as kit.before-train8). The kit-worker POD is applied: Deployment kit/kit-worker Running (chroot to host toolchains, Recreate, pgrep liveness), exactly 1 kit job host (the pod's); remote.ps1 europa job start pod-proof exit 0 with still 1 host afterwards. Revert: k3s kubectl -n kit delete deploy kit-worker (state stays on the host in /root/.cache/kit). The europa watcher restarted as a 24 h job (job-1793588424143-vultr-watch). Pending for the 1-slot rule: KIT_JOB_SLOTS ships in TRAIN9 (held out of TRAIN8 by the line gate).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_17 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 17u32, "2026-11-02T03:30Z", "kit", "11-02 23:30 kit DECISION (x-dumps: the pod showed 2 \"restarts\" in 20 min; that was kit job host exiting 0 after its 10-min idle limit, and kubelet backoff could open host-less gaps). x-dumps' interim fix: the container runs a loop that respawns kit job host in about 1 s (still exactly 1 host). KIT FIX (TRAIN9, with KIT_JOB_SLOTS, both in the host loop): KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS read into a typed idle_policy = ExitAfter of u64 | NeverExit (0 or \"never\" -> NeverExit; unset -> today's 600 s), consumed by host_step_of in kit_job_host/host_model.spi, with a test that a NeverExit host stays KeepServing after 10 quiet minutes. The pod then sets it to never and drops the loop. Prerequisite: split kit_job_host under the 1000-line gate (it hit 1111 with the slot cap).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_18 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 18u32, "2026-11-02T04:56Z", "kit", "KIT FIX AMENDED (TRAIN9 in tree, node pre-proof running): KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS takes a number only; 0 = never exit, N = exit after N quiet seconds, unset = 600 s. The word \"never\" was dropped so no string maps to a union case. KIT_JOB_SLOTS=N caps live supervisors per host; a full host holds ready jobs (StartNow -> HoldBack) and starts them as slots free. The pod sets KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1 once TRAIN9 is installed on europa. The split that made room: kit_job_host keeps the host loop, new crate kit_job_host_request holds kit_job_host_request/revive, and the launch and child primitives moved into kit_os_launch (calls.spi).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_19 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 19u32, "2026-11-02T10:54Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 06:5x: europa kit = TRAIN10 (92ea07e3), so the knobs from n18 are live in the binary. Path: sync kit -Mirror kit-train10 (REMOTE-SYNC-OK head 56c32ef6); capacity checked in its own step (AVAILABLE, load 0.73, no compilers); job job-1793616473635-kit-train10-build: cargo build -p kit-cli -j1 --locked, target seeded by MOVING kit-train8/target (3m18s, exit 0). Swap: hard link kit -> kit.before-train10 (91807212), copy to kit.new, mv over kit (atomic); no job was running. 'kit job host --idle' refuses (no host started). The node runs TRAIN10 too (main, C:\\home\\bin\\kit.exe, smoke job exit 0). Pod change sent to x-dumps (theirs): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, exec kit job host without the while-loop. Until the pod restarts, its host is still the TRAIN8 binary in memory.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_20 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 20u32, "2026-11-03T07:34Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 21 h ago by kit, progress kept at 95%. Resume from that note: me\\bin\\kit.exe, smoke job exit 0). Pod change sent to x-dumps (theirs): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, exec kit job host without the while-loop. Until the pod restarts, its host is still the TRAIN8 binary in memory.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_21 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 21u32, "2026-11-04T11:44Z", "x-dumps", "Validated europa target 2026-11-04 (x-dumps, via remote.ps1 europa status): WORKS - returns repo digests (alphabet/blog/dice/html/i574n.github/kit/regex/spiral), deps core 70F6F5BF matches box, free-disk 25.7G. TWO GAPS found: (1) the NODE (laptop) target shows \"node runner (SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing)\" + \"node compiler (missing)\" -> node gates cannot run comparable to box; the REMOTE-CLOUD DoD (same gates on every worker) is unmet on the node until its spiral runner+compiler are restored. (2) europa free-mem 0.8G (RAM near full under other lanes compute). Flagged both to main. europa half of this feature is validated-functional.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_22 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 22u32, "2026-11-04T11:47Z", "x-dumps", "CORRECTION 2026-11-04 (main): my earlier \"node compiler/runner missing\" was WRONG - the node DOES compile+run Spiral (main ran the Cells gate there: lean.spi+oracle.spi 5/5, Lean 198/198, spiral mirror C80CFA10). \"SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing\" is a remote.ps1-STATUS probe reading the bare ssh env; the real resolution is apps/cells run_notebook.ps1 finding the compiler in spiral-bin/compiler-installs. So the node target is FINE; not node-down. The real (small) item is kit: make remote.ps1 status resolve the compiler the way run_notebook does. I over-asserted an absence from a probe - verify the capability before asserting.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_23 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 23u32, "2026-11-05T05:42Z", "x-dumps", "europa UNREACHABLE 2026-11-05 ~05:40 local: ssh times out \"during banner exchange\" (3x, ConnectTimeout up to 25s). TCP reaches :22 but sshd never sends its banner = VM-side overload/thrash (CPU-pin or OOM/swap so sshd cannot be scheduled), NOT network. My xarchive builds there finished clean earlier (~05:1x, reverted, index 4860). Flagged kit (VM owner, europa). Shared infra effectively down for offload until recovered; the x-dumps backup task is unaffected (targets the pluto node). VOID any europa result until it recovers.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_24 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 24u32, "2026-11-05T06:02Z", "x-dumps", "europa outage ROOT CAUSE (kit diagnosed 05:56Z, got in after 4 tries): memory thrash from tibia's UNCAPPED SpiralCompiler --backend C loop on /root/tibia-probes (480 MB/run) -> load 47, 112 MB free of 3392, 2.2 GB swap, k3s/iggy/coredns pods restarting under pressure. NOT a kit job (kit type-checks ended 01:30 local); not x-dumps (my builds there finished clean + reverted). kit asked tibia to cap it (systemd-run MemoryMax, one at a time, node fallback above load 4). Resolution owned by kit+tibia; x-dumps europa-dependent work (geygey tail recheck) stays blocked until recovery. Textbook case of [[europa-heavy-jobs-cap-memory]] - uncapped heavy job on the 2 CPU / 3.4 GB VM thrashes the whole box.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_25 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 25u32, "2026-11-06T12:18Z", "x-dumps", "Evidence from x-dumps (consumer) 11-06, toward the 95% DoD: used the europa Linux worker (europa) as a real kit/remote target end-to-end for the harvest-enrichment rollout -- scp'd xarchive source box->europa, built it under a memory scope (systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=2600M -p MemorySwapMax=0 cargo build --release, RC=0 ~11s), and ran a long re-fetch as a transient unit (systemd-run --unit=xa-enrich -p MemorySwapMax=0 --working-directory=/data/x), monitored via journalctl -u. All over ssh europa; load stayed <0.6 with the memory bound. Confirms the OS-aware target + transport works for a real multi-step x-dumps workload with memory bounding (the mitigation for the earlier uncapped-compile thrash). Not authoring kit's remote.ps1 internals (kit-owned); this is a consumer data point.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_26 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 26u32, "2026-11-07T20:46Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "Consumer data point 11-07T16:45Z (x-dumps): after the iggy Guaranteed-QoS roll on the same node, re-validated the europa worker as a kit/remote target via remote.ps1 europa status. GREEN: cpu 16%, free-mem 1.4G, free-disk 20.1G; all 9 repo digests returned (alphabet/blog/dice/html/i574n.github/kit/regex/spiral/x); deps core 70F6F5BF matches the box. So the node-infra change (iggy QoS) did not regress the worker's remote-target role. The 'node runner (SPIRAL_EXE unset or missing)' line is the known kit-owned status-probe gap (status reads bare ssh env instead of resolving via run_notebook's compiler-installs; corrected consumer understanding in the 11-04 note), NOT a node outage. Not authoring kit's remote.ps1 internals; consumer evidence toward the 95% DoD.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_27 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 27u32, "2026-11-08T20:39Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:0x rig: linux-worker.ps1 (i574n.github scripts, unstaged, parse 0) gains -NoK3s (kit job host as systemd kit-worker.service, Restart=always, KIT_JOB_SLOTS from -Slots) for compute-only workers, and its iggy patches are retargeted to the live-canonical iggy.yaml (-IggyMemory sets requests = limits; -HostNetwork inserts hostNetwork + dnsPolicy); both paths dry-run clean (ganymede -NoK3s; europa k3s path). First no-k3s worker: ganymede (Europa, Miami, 4 cores, 7 GB, Ubuntu 26.04.1), setup running: europa layout (pwsh 7.6.9 tarball, rustup nightly, /etc/environment PATH), kit tree from the TRAIN40 snapshot, cargo build -p kit-cli. Registry entry shape sent to org3 (org owns remote-machines.json), slots 2.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_28 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 28u32, "2026-11-08T20:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:1x rig: ganymede kit worker UP (no k3s): pwsh 7.6.9, rustup nightly (rustc 1.102.0-nightly 2026-11-07), kit built from the TRAIN40 tree in 1m59s at /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit; linux-worker.ps1 -NoK3s -Slots 2: sshd drop-in applied, kit-worker.service active, 1 job host. Remaining: the ganymede key in remote-machines.json (org) and a kit job start --on ganymede smoke from the box.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_29 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 29u32, "2026-11-08T20:46Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:2x rig: ganymede SMOKED end to end from the box: kit job start --on ganymede --bin /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit rig-ganymede-smoke 60000 uname -a gave exit 0 in 710 ms (Linux ganymede 7.0.0-42-generic). .NET SDK 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148 installed at /root/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/dotnet (same version and path as europa; DOTNET_ROOT and PATH in /etc/environment; worker restarted) and proven through the worker: job rig-ganymede-dotnet printed the version, exit 0. Gaps found: (1) --on runs plain kit on the remote and ignores the machine entry kit field, so callers must pass --bin (europa has the same need); job start should read the registry path (KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY M2); (2) the TRAIN40 box shows a finished placed job as state=pending, fixed by TRAIN41 JobPlaced. Next toolchain per org3: Spiral (compiler and fiscal first).")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_30 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 30u32, "2026-11-08T20:55Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:4x rig: ganymede has a Spiral compiler BUILT ON HOST (compiler rule: never copy a bundle; box and node DLLs are Windows R2R): remote.ps1 ganymede sync spiral -Mirror compiler (head f6000cfa, digest 1FC358A41A46DD7D, 64 s), then apps/compiler/scripts/build.ps1 as the only job (job-1794171116630-rig-ganymede-compiler-build, exit 0, 162.99 s wall, 849 MB peak RSS, SDK 11.0.100-rc.3). Hashes: host-dir SpiralCompilerCore.dll e24c992685d603ec, project output 6008658e42becb1e, core source 986a4912da0970c7; sent to compiler for the europa compare. Log in relay-lab/logs/compiler. Note: kit job start --on refuses quoted argv, so multi-word commands go through a staged script.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_31 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 31u32, "2026-11-08T20:55Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 18:0x rig: proof: compiler hashed both hosts itself: all three DLLs from core source 986a4912 (pack62) are byte-identical on ganymede and europa (europa built 15:22): SpiralCompiler.dll 1eb4555b37a700f9, host-dir SpiralCompilerCore.dll e24c992685d603ec, project-dir core 6008658e42becb1e. ganymede is PROVEN as a Linux build host; compiler moves Linux node-proof and pack gates there from europa. Same-source build times: europa 74.2 s, ganymede 161.8 s, box 232 s (so ganymede 4 Skylake cores build about 2.2x slower than europa 2 EPYC vCPU on this job; capacity, not speed, is ganymede's gain: 8 GB and 2 slots vs europa 3.4 GB). Remaining on this item: codespaces as targets (KIT-CODESPACE-RUN lifecycle as a machine kind), plus KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY M2 so --on reads the registry kit path.")
inl note_of_remote_cloud_linux_worker_32 () = Note ("REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", 32u32, "2026-11-09T15:03Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 11:5x rig groom: estimate 480 -> 60 m. 95 pct done (ganymede proven as a Linux build host 11-08); the two remaining pieces are their own items: codespaces as a machine kind = KIT-CODESPACE-RUN, --on reading the registry kit path = KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY M2. What is left here is the 60 m glue: list both machine kinds in remote.ps1 help and close with their evidence.")
inl emacs_steals () = Task ("EMACS-STEALS", "Steal Emacs mechanisms for the org (user 11-01 14:0x: 'check what from emacs can be stolen to our org repos/ambitions'): org-clock actuals, logged state changes, effort warnings, column view, dependency triggers, capture templates, saved agenda queries, babel results caching, tangle back-links, dynamic blocks, transient help, roam backlinks, TRAMP paths", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-SCRUM-XP", "kit,agile,scrum-xp,cells,research,user-batch,team:rig,steals", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_emacs_steals () = Planning ("EMACS-STEALS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_emacs_steals_1 () = Note ("EMACS-STEALS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x research done (subagent, orgmode.org manual pages from memory, no web search). Ranked: 1 org-clock: a closed lease becomes an actual-minutes row; sprint close prints estimate vs actual (S2/S6, M). 2 effort warning: a lease past its estimate leaves an automatic over-estimate note (S3, S). 3 logged state changes: every status change is an append-only typed row (from, to, time, lane, reason); a reason is required for Paused/Done; burn-down reads only these rows (S2, S). 4 column view: typed column spec with a per-column total; epic rows sum their children (S5/S7, M). 5 org-edna triggers: closing an item promotes its unblocked dependents (S7, S). 6 capture templates: kit story add --template bug|slice|spike with required typed fields (S). 7 saved agenda queries: typed stored views plus kit standup (S8, M). 8 babel RESULTS[hash] caching keyed on the package closure and compiler hash; a second promote leaves an empty diff (KIT-RUN-SPI/TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, M). 9 typed result forms: stdout|value|diagnostic|exit (SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES, M). 10 named blocks feeding each other (Cells, L). 11 tangle link comments: generated .rs points back to its .spi line (attribute-generated-lines, M). 12 dynamic blocks: regenerated README/page regions, mtime untouched when unchanged (dir-tree-html, S). 13 transient-style help generated from command_spec (S). 14 roam backlinks: story show lists linking items and memories (M). 15 TRAMP: one typed local|machine path for job/sync/proof (REMOTE-EXEC, L). DON'T COPY: untyped property drawers, loose syntax, caches blind to outside inputs, code running on open, hidden advice, global single-threaded state, rescanning every file per query. DoD: ideas 1-7 are acceptance criteria on their SCRUM-XP slices (S2: 1,3; S3: 2; S5/S7: 4,5; S6: 1; S8: 7; plus 6 on the story-add verb); ideas 8-15 are each a filed item or a declined note here. Typefeatureset: every steal is a typed row or union (StateChange, ClockSpan, ColumnSpec with a total GADT, View as a filter value), never property strings.")
inl node_run_value () = Task ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", "Get the most out of every long node run (user 11-01 14:0x: 'we gotta make sure we're extracting max value from these long runs in the nodes, because they clog us a lot, look at today, entire day sitting waiting for them while grooming'): each run over 30 min declares its questions, predicts its result, streams usable checkpoints and carries cheap piggyback probes", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-EXEC-METRICS", "node,remote,metrics,scheduling,all-lanes,user-batch,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_run_value () = Planning ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_run_value_1 () = Note ("NODE-RUN-VALUE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x: today compiler's libord-seq arms ran 2h15m and about 4 h on the node while other lanes waited for it. DoD: (1) kit job start for a node job over 30 min requires a typed run plan: the questions it answers, a predicted outcome per question (predict-pack-residuals), and checkpoints. (2) The job writes partial results at each checkpoint, so a lane can act on arm 1 before arm 2 ends. (3) Each run reserves spare memory and CPU headroom for queued cheap probes (light cells, type probes) instead of leaving them idle. (4) After the run, a value row (questions answered, predictions hit, hours) feeds NODE-VALUE-METRICS. Next: retrofit the plan onto the next compiler A/B and measure hours to first actionable result before and after. Typefeatureset: RunPlan = Question list with phantom-typed Prediction<unit>; Checkpoint is a GADT indexed by the question it answers.")
inl node_value_metrics () = Task ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", "Per-node value metrics to predict when we really need more nodes (user 11-01 14:0x: metrics on how each node returns value, linux vs windows, jobs-only vs hosting the Claude windows that steal resources like the box, so 'we can predict when we'll truly need more nodes')", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-EXEC-METRICS,NODE-RUN-VALUE", "kit,metrics,node,remote,capacity,user-batch,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_value_metrics () = Planning ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_value_metrics_1 () = Note ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:08Z", "main", "main 14:1x: machines today: box (Windows, hosts every Claude window plus compiles), node (Windows, jobs only, Wi-Fi), the Cloud VM (Linux, cloud, x-dumps first), codespaces (Linux, quota-bound, ephemeral). DoD: kit node report prints per machine and day: OS, role (Jobs | Interactive | Mixed), CPU-hours used/available, queue wait, jobs finished, value rows from NODE-RUN-VALUE, and the Cells benchmark speed factor (the same cell timed on each machine). A forecast line says when the queue wait would justify another node, and the report runs on real data for 3 days. Collection stays light: no Prometheus (user: avoid it to save memory); kit's own exec metrics rows. Typefeatureset: Machine = Box | Node | Cloud of provider | Codespace as a closed union; OS and role as phantom tags so Linux-only metrics can't be read off Windows rows.")
inl note_of_node_value_metrics_2 () = Note ("NODE-VALUE-METRICS", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:48Z", "resilience", "complements DELEG-JOB-PROFILE under SMART-DELEGATION: value-metrics decide WHEN to add nodes; the per job-type x machine profile decides WHERE each job goes. Both read the same kit job records + the new DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES.")
inl k3s_everywhere () = Task ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", "k3s on every Linux worker, with one shared set of manifests and lessons: the Cloud cloud node first, then WSL on the local Wi-Fi node; no Prometheus, to save memory (user 11-01 14:0x: 'we should use k3s everywhere but avoid prometheus to save memory, we should reuse k3s lessons between the linux node and wsl3 inside our local wifi node')", Story, P1, D4, 80u32, Planned, "REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "k3s,remote,linux,node,user-batch,management,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_k3s_everywhere () = Planning ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_1 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: the user's 'wsl3' is read as WSL on the node. The no-wsl-until-workflow-passes memory put WSL last; this direction moves WSL on the node up, but only after the Europa k3s works. DoD: k3s runs on the Cloud VM with kit's worker as a pod and the x-dumps workload; the same manifests apply unchanged in WSL on the node; every lesson is one note here, and no per-machine forks of the manifests exist. Memory footprint is measured (k3s idle RSS) and stays under a stated budget. Next: install k3s on the Cloud VM after the personal lane's first x-dumps run (x-dumps has priority there). Typefeatureset: manifests generated from typed Spiral records (Deployment with phantom resource limits), not hand YAML.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_2 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:06Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:0x -04: k3s on europa. Installed v1.36.5+k3s1 (stable channel; the WSL node must use the same pin) with --disable traefik --disable servicelb --disable metrics-server, no Prometheus. Idle footprint measured: k3s-server 454 MB RSS, k3s+containerd+coredns+local-path ~660 MB; system used 594→978 MB. Budget stated: <=700 MB idle. Workload 1 is live: x-dumps runs as Deployment x-dumps/xarchive (ubuntu:26.04 image, hostPath ro mount of the xarchive release binary, hostPath /data/x, loops `run ... --rps 1` then sleeps 1 h; requests 50m/64Mi, limit 512Mi; Recreate strategy) and replaces the nohup run, so it survives reboots. It resumed from the cache in seconds. The bootstrap manifest lives at /home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml on the VM, deliberately NOT in a repo: the DoD wants manifests generated from typed Spiral records. Next: (1) the Spiral generator, (2) kit's job host as a pod, (3) the same manifests in WSL on the node. Install lesson: `curl -I` on update.k3s.io/v1-release/channels/stable returns 405 (HEAD unsupported), so the pinned-version lookup must use GET with -w %{redirect_url}.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_3 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-11-01T21:18Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:1x -04: typed manifests landed in the working tree (unstaged). (1) kit/src/k3s_manifest: HostFree+LibReferenced package; GADT phantom units `quantity u` (Millicores -> quantity cpu, Mebibytes -> quantity memory) so a memory amount can't go in a cpu field; unions for volume source/access/strategy; deployment record; JSON rendering (kubectl applies JSON as is). A JSON spec record (wire boundary) is decoded with json.decode and converted to the typed model. Proven on europa via kit scripts/notebook-batch.ps1: 4/4 pass incl. a spec round trip byte-equal to the typed fixture; standards 0 findings, ratchet ok. The census rows were set once the new package was final (a deliberate baseline, not a ratchet bypass: its line count grew while I re-laid widths and added the spec). (2) lib/spiral/json.spi gained `quote` (JSON string literal) with a Rust-only test; json.spi 12/12. (3) kit/src/kit_k3s verb crate `kit k3s render <spec.json>` → stdout, export kit_k3s_run; registration + regen handed to kit (it is mid-edit in command_spec_domain/kit_cli_model). (4) The concrete workload spec lives in xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.json, not in kit (kit knows no projects). (5) kit-worker pod: drafted at /home/remote/k3s/kit-worker.yaml (privileged chroot to host toolchains, Recreate, pgrep liveness), server dry run OK, namespace kit created; NO-GO from kit until its --after owner fix is on europa. (6) Compiler bug found and reported to compiler: array_base of records with a union field → Rust `Vec<tuple>::default()` E0599; worked around (host_path plain string). Next: kit regen → `kit k3s render xarchive.k3s.json | kubectl apply -f -` replaces the bootstrap YAML; then the worker pod on kit's apply; then WSL reuse.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_4 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-11-01T21:46Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:4x -04: WSL reuse. The node's WSL Ubuntu (WSL2, systemd=true) already runs k3s v1.37.1+k3s1 since 10-25 (7 days, with namespace iggy and metrics-server). Version pin unified on v1.37.1+k3s1: europa upgraded in place from v1.36.5 (installer with INSTALL_K3S_VERSION, same --disable flags); the running pods survived without restart; k3s-server ~677 MB right after the upgrade. Both VM manifests (xarchive, kit-worker) pass `kubectl apply --dry-run=server` UNCHANGED on the node's WSL cluster (empty namespaces x-dumps and kit created there for the dry run; iggy untouched), so no per-machine forks. Lessons: (a) server dry runs do not persist Namespaces, so create the namespace first or the Deployment fails NotFound; (b) `$var` inside `remote.ps1 node run \"wsl ... bash -lc '...'\"` passes through 3 shells and arrives empty, so use explicit names or base64; (c) node WSL k3s already exists, so the K3S item uses it instead of reinstalling. Remaining DoD: `kit k3s render` (kit's regen tonight) replacing the bootstrap YAML, kit-worker pod apply (kit's go), the same on the node.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_5 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-11-01T23:10Z", "x-dumps", "GROOM 19:1x (x-dumps). Estimate 240 → 600 min (actuals: install + measure + typed package + 4 proofs + WSL dry runs already ~360). DoD unchanged: k3s on europa + node WSL at ONE pinned version (v1.37.1+k3s1), kit worker + x-dumps as pods, manifests from typed Spiral records (k3s_manifest + `kit k3s render`), idle RSS within the stated <=700 MB budget, no per-machine forks. Done: install + pin + RSS, x-dumps pod, typed package proven, WSL dry runs. Next: (1) kit's TRAIN8 regen ships `kit k3s render` → replace the bootstrap YAML on europa with the rendered xarchive.k3s.json; (2) kit-worker pod on kit's \"apply\"; (3) the same apply in node WSL. Typefeatureset: units are already GADT-indexed (quantity cpu/memory); next index candidate is volume access as a phantom (ReadOnly mounts can't back a writable path) once a second workload exists.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_6 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-11-02T00:09Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:1x -04: crate-gate work on kit_k3s (kit unregistered the verb from TRAIN8 at 3,221 generated lines; its biggest fn was 1,406). Made 19 non-generic k3s_manifest helpers join points (`:~ ^`; functions passed to map/fold wrapped in lambdas). Measured with kit's test-regeneration.ps1 -KeepStaged on a cloud mirror: 3,221 → 1,766 lines (largest fn 81). Notebook 4/4, compile 53 s (was 5-14 min). Still >1000: ~500 lines are json.decode's parser instantiated for the spec type, ~350 render. Options sent to kit: (A) split spec-canonicalize/render crates, (B) a typed line-row spec instead of generic JSON decode (my lean; matches DATA-ROWS-TYPED), (C) keep the verb out of TRAIN9 meanwhile. Lesson: join points cut generated size AND compile time together (here ~6-10x faster notebook compiles).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_7 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 7u32, "2026-11-02T00:28Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:2x -04 (kit decision B): the spec is now a TYPED ROW MODULE, not JSON. k3s_manifest reads `inl row_N () = Deployment|Container|Resources|Volume [ \"...\" ]` rows (union k3s_row, `///- --no-respell`) with kit_table_rows' quote-aware cells_of; unit words map to the quantity GADT on read (Millicores → quantity cpu, Mebibytes → quantity memory); the typed outcome is spec_outcome = Rendered | Refused (UnknownRow | MissingCells | UnknownUnit | BadNumber | RowBeforeDeployment). The verb kit k3s render <spec.spi> prints refusals with exit 1. Proof (europa, one-slot): notebook 5/5 (rows render byte-equal to the typed fixture; unknown unit and orphan row refused); standards 0 findings. Crate size (test-regeneration -KeepStaged): 1,652 lines (JSON version 1,766; original 3,221). Still over the 1000 gate. By family: 18 closure methods = 321 lines, row builders ~410 (the nested 7-field record is rebuilt per row), render ~260. NEXT: recursive join points instead of map/fold lambdas; collect rows flat first and build each deployment once; measure after each step; split only if still >1000. kit keeps VerbK3s unregistered until then. Cells are single-line (cells_of keeps an escaped char literally), so the xarchive loop becomes one-line bash; xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.json must be rewritten as xarchive.k3s.spi rows.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_8 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 8u32, "2026-11-02T00:39Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:3x -04: size lever 2 measured: map/fold lambdas → recursive join points (texts, mounts_json, volumes_json, documents, steps) and the row builders return `built` = Built deployment | Broken spec_error (no Building [d] wrap/unwrap). kit_k3s.rs 1,652 → 1,610 lines (methods 321 → 230). Notebook 5/5, standards 0 findings, census rebaselined. Conclusion: Rust codegen for the nested 7-field deployment record dominates (sized 120, step 75, deployment_json 80, resources_json 70, string_slice 98), so join points can't reach 1000. TRAIN9 design: split at a STRING boundary, which needs no cross-crate typed bindings: crate k3s_spec (rows → canonical one-line-per-field normalized rows, validated, units checked) + crate kit_k3s (canonical rows → JSON render), each well under 1000. Concrete workload spec now lives in C:\\home\\git\\xarchive\\deploy\\xarchive.k3s.spi (rows; the JSON one renamed .superseded).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_9 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 9u32, "2026-11-02T03:08Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:0x -04: KIT WORKER RUNS AS A POD on europa. (1) europa kit rebuilt from the TRAIN8 tree (has the --after owner fix + Linux dotnet discovery): sync kit -Mirror kit-train8, cargo build -p kit-cli -j1 into a separate target (4m18s, one-slot), swapped atomically into /home/remote/git/kit/src/target/debug/kit (sha 91807212..., old kept as kit.before-train8 = 625e0bb0...); job train8-proof exit 0. (2) Applied /home/remote/k3s/kit-worker.yaml: Deployment kit/kit-worker Running; exactly ONE `kit job host` process (the pod's, chroot to host toolchains); `remote.ps1 europa job start pod-proof` → exit 0, still one host after. (3) Trap: `kit job host --help` does NOT print help; it starts a real job host (hidden long-running verb). I started one by accident via ssh and stopped it (verified it was supervising nothing). (4) Revived the expired europa watcher (kit job job-1793588424143-vultr-watch, 24 h; the old one had a 6 h cap and went stale 21:4x). DoD left: Spiral-rendered manifests (kit_k3s under the 1000 gate, the TRAIN9 split) and the same pods in node WSL.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_10 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 10u32, "2026-11-02T03:29Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:3x -04: the kit-worker pod \"restarted\" twice in 20 min. Not a crash: `kit job host` exits 0 after ~10 min idle (03:18:11 → 03:28:12), by design for ssh-spawned hosts; in a Deployment that shows as restarts, and kubelet backoff could leave host-less gaps where an ssh job spawns a second host. Fix (bootstrap YAML, backup kit-worker.yaml.before-loop): the container runs `while true; do kit job host; sleep 1; done`, so the container stays up, the host respawns in ~1 s, and the restart count stays 0. Verify at the next heartbeat (>10 min later). Design question to kit: a `--no-idle-exit` (or env) for supervisor-owned hosts.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_11 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 11u32, "2026-11-02T03:48Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 23:48 -04: loop fix VERIFIED: kit-worker 19 min, 0 restarts (past the 10-min idle exit), exactly 1 job host. kit's TRAIN9 replaces the loop with KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=never + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1 in the pod env.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_12 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 12u32, "2026-11-02T10:54Z", "x-dumps", "kit-worker pod on europa moved to TRAIN10 (92ea07e3): env KIT_JOB_HOST_IDLE_EXIT_SECONDS=0 + KIT_JOB_SLOTS=1, command is a single exec of kit job host (restart loop dropped, k8s restartPolicy covers crashes). Server dry run, rollout ok, exactly one host with both knobs in its environ. Backup kit-worker.yaml.before-train10. Remaining: Spiral-rendered manifests (kit_k3s split), same pods on node WSL.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_13 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 13u32, "2026-11-02T12:27Z", "x-dumps", "iggy now runs on europa k3s too (apache/iggy:0.9.0, same pin as the node WSL), brought up by the new i574n.github/scripts/linux-worker.ps1 -SshAlias europa -KitBinary <kit>: sshd penalty drop-in (sshd -t gated), kit bootstrap.sh k3s pin, kit iggy.yaml patched for a small public VM (limit 512Mi, hostNetwork off so no public ports; Service 10.43.189.228), kit-worker pod, then kit verify.ps1 through an ssh tunnel: VERIFY-OK 1000/1000 ordered, 0 dup. Rerun idempotent (kit job host pid kept, iggy pod not restarted). iggy RSS 83 MB; VM 1.8 GB available.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_14 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 14u32, "2026-11-02T12:29Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:1x on x-dumps' iggy-on-vultr FYI: iggy runs on europa k3s from kit/infra/k3s-iggy unedited. i574n.github/scripts/linux-worker.ps1 text-patches iggy.yaml (limits memory 3Gi -> 512Mi; hostNetwork true -> false + dnsPolicy ClusterFirst, because a public VM would otherwise expose 3000/8090/8080/8092) and throws if those lines drift; VERIFY-OK 1000/1000, RSS 83 MB. DECISION (kit): no new knobs on the hand YAML / node-wsl.ps1 (Spiral-first; manifests come from typed records). The fix is iggy as a kit_k3s typed manifest with memory_limit : quantity memory and network : pod_network = HostNetwork | ClusterNetwork; the node WSL renders HostNetwork, a public VM renders ClusterNetwork (exposure becomes a type-level choice, and the GADT-indexed quantity units already exist). The render then replaces both the patch and the embedded kit-worker manifest. Until then the patch-with-drift-guard is the right bridge. Depends on kit k3s render being registered (kit_k3s under the 1000-line gate).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_15 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 15u32, "2026-11-02T13:44Z", "x-dumps", "Spec drift to close at render time: the live europa x-dumps manifest (/home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml, backup .before-iggy) now has envFrom secretRef iggy-xarchive on xarchive and a second Deployment xarchive-follow (64Mi limit); xarchive/deploy/xarchive.k3s.spi has neither yet.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_16 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 16u32, "2026-11-02T15:05Z", "x-dumps", "linux-worker.ps1 caveat (star exchange 11-02, fiscal/compiler/x all hit the array-param trap): its -ExemptIp is [string[]], so pwsh -File linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp a,b passes the one string a,b. Call in-process (& linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp @(...)) or pass one value; the default (controller SSH_CLIENT) sidesteps it. The Spiral port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL) removes the trap.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_17 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 17u32, "2026-11-03T07:35Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 16 h ago by x-dumps, progress kept at 80%. Resume from that note: wsh -File linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp a,b passes the one string a,b. Call in-process (& linux-worker.ps1 -ExemptIp @(...)) or pass one value; the default (controller SSH_CLIENT) sidesteps it. The Spiral port (PS1-TO-SPIRAL) removes the trap.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_18 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 18u32, "2026-11-04T11:06Z", "x-dumps", "Groom 2026-11-04 (x-dumps side): infra DoD met — k3s on europa + node WSL at pinned v1.37.1+k3s1, --disable traefik/servicelb/metrics-server, no Prometheus, idle RSS <=700MB; kit-worker pod + iggy pod live; WSL dry-runs pass unchanged (no per-machine forks). REMAINING is kit-owned: `kit k3s render` registration (gated on the k3s_spec/kit_k3s crate split under the 1000-line gate). Also: the xarchive WORKLOAD manifest (xarchive.k3s.spi) is now MOOT for live use — harvest complete, xarchive pod at replicas=0; the render target can be iggy (the live pod) rather than xarchive. Not pulling further from x-dumps side; the valuable remainder is kits render.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_19 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 19u32, "2026-11-07T16:00Z", "x-dumps", "Dropped the `kit` code tag 11-07 (main): infra/manifests item, not typed-Spiral code, so the TypeFeatures lint check does not apply. A typed manifest generator, if it ever appears, gets its own code-tagged child.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_20 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 20u32, "2026-11-08T19:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 15:3x rig: repo manifest healed to match the live fix: kit infra/k3s-iggy/iggy.yaml now adds IPC_LOCK beside SYS_NICE (unstaged), so a fresh apply no longer panics at boot with exit 101 (io_uring memlock). Live europa check: apache/iggy:0.9.0, caps SYS_NICE+IPC_LOCK, memory limit 512Mi, 0 restarts in 23 h, k3s v1.37.1+k3s1, kernel 7.0.0-38. Open: the repo memory limit is 3Gi vs live 512Mi; the right value is per node (org3 battletest decides), so it is left as is and named here.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_21 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 21u32, "2026-11-08T21:14Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:1x rig: pluto (pluto) is a MINIMAL k3s production host (user direct yes in rig session 17:1x; org3 relay). k3s v1.37.1+k3s1 with /etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml: disable traefik, servicelb, metrics-server, local-storage; no cloud controller; kubelet eviction-hard memory<64Mi nodefs<4% imagefs<4%, eviction-soft memory<128Mi nodefs<8%, image GC 92/86, max-pods 16. RustDesk moved from docker into k3s (namespace rustdesk; hbbs and hbbr hostNetwork, Guaranteed 64Mi each, PriorityClass rustdesk-critical, same hardening as compose: uid 10001, read-only root, drop ALL, no privilege escalation, /tmp 8Mi memory), SAME identity (hbbs key jpcuTqtr..., backup sha256 3064296f in the box archive). Cutover -04:00: docker stop 17:13:25-17:13:29, apply 17:13:31, pods ready 17:13:35, box connects 17:13:36: about 8 s outage. proof: a real relay session paired through the k3s hbbr at 17:14:04. Docker, docker.socket and docker containerd.service stopped and disabled (installed, compose kept) as a 24 h rollback; removal due 11-09 17:14 after a clean day. Memory available 327 MB after (was 568 before k3s). iggy on pluto is held by org3 (placement question to the user: pluto vs europa iggy).")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_22 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 22u32, "2026-11-08T21:30Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:3x rig: pluto full stack except the serve: iggy 0.9.0 UP 17:26:48 (Guaranteed 256Mi/200m, IPC_LOCK, ClusterIP 3000/8090, plain HTTP on the LAN, hostPath /var/lib/iggy, startupProbe 10 min, liveness 60 s/30 s/10 failures, secrets generated on-node; /ping 200). First try crash-looped on an invalid env I had guessed (IGGY_SYSTEM_SEGMENT_SIZE; iggy 0.9.0 removed the system key), dropped. kubelet LimitedSwap live since 17:15 (KubeletConfiguration failSwapOn false, memorySwap.swapBehavior LimitedSwap via kubelet-arg config=; k3s restart did not touch the RustDesk pods). User direct answers in the rig session: Docker STAYS stopped and disabled, not removed (\"we are removing it because of memory not disk, make it stop instead\"); WSL unblocked for anything from now on (memory updated). The serve (microblog, Burstable 512/640Mi, long startup, non-killing liveness) deploys next; rig checks memory.swap.max after its first start.")
inl note_of_k3s_everywhere_23 () = Note ("K3S-EVERYWHERE", 23u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 SIZES from compiler (for pod request=limit labels): compiler full Linux matrix on ganymede, 7586 jobs in 39 min wall, 1.79 GB peak RSS at 2 workers: size 2 GiB for 2 workers; format-corpus/format passes 1.5 GiB; candidate build 1 GiB. Fiscal: a certificate unit 32 to 43 s at 1 slot on europa within a 1536M cap; reports unit 235 s on ganymede. Ganymede host now has python3-numpy 2.3.5 (apt, 11-09) so the Python native tier runs; build-base Dockerfile adds python3 + python3-numpy for the next tag (build-base:3, rebuild when ganymede drains); no cupy (no GPU).")
inl iggy_public_cloud () = Task ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", "A publicly reachable iggy on the cloud node's k3s (no paid fixed IP unless it's free), with the WSL iggy kept as fallback, so work machines that can't open an inbound ssh port join by connecting out to it (user 11-01 14:0x: 'a challenge for kit to take the typefeatureset to the limit')", Story, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE,K3S-EVERYWHERE,LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "iggy,k3s,remote,kit,typefeatureset,user-batch,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_iggy_public_cloud () = Planning ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", Minutes 360u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_1 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: the pull model: a worker behind NAT or a corporate firewall opens an outbound TLS connection to the iggy endpoint, consumes job events and publishes results; nothing listens on the worker. DoD: (1) iggy runs in k3s on the Cloud VM, reachable at the VM's own public address or a free DNS name, with TLS and per-machine credentials (credentials are the user's step). (2) A worker that has only outbound access runs one job end-to-end through it. (3) Killing the cloud iggy fails over to the WSL instance within a stated time. (4) Iggy's multi-node or cluster state is tested on the newest beta or nightly, with results noted here. Cost stays at the VM price. Typefeatureset: worker capabilities as phantom tags (InboundSsh | OutboundOnly), so placement can't send ssh-only work to an outbound-only worker; messages are LIB-IGGY-CLIENT's GADT family.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_2 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 2u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 (user: \"are we already reaping all the benefits we can with iggy, the public deploy with k3s at europa should be up also ... gotta understand why it stalled, is it cpu?\"). NOT CPU: europa's 2 vCPUs sit at load 0.25; memory is the tight one (3.4 GB, ~0.7 GB free). STATE 08:45: k3s runs iggy (up since 08:23, apache/iggy:0.9.0), kit-worker and xarchive pods. iggy's Service is ClusterIP only (hostNetwork turned off on purpose so the public VM doesn't expose 3000/8090/8080/8092), so it is NOT public yet, and nothing produces to or consumes from it, so no benefit is being reaped. WHY IT STALLED: prioritization. IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE (P0, 40%) sat in kit's queue \"after the SCRUM-XP slices\" (11-01 14:15) and behind TRAIN8-11; this item is 0% and depends on it plus LIB-IGGY-CLIENT. Next: (1) TLS + per-machine credentials, which are the user's step; (2) a public listener (ingress or LoadBalancer on the VM address); (3) one real producer/consumer pair (kit job events) so the deploy carries load. Ask x-dumps (owns Europa) and kit (owns IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE) for dates.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_3 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 3u32, "2026-11-02T13:24Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps plan + dates (11-02 09:3x). Memory correction: europa has ~1.7 GB AVAILABLE (free 0.7 GB + reclaimable cache); iggy itself is 83 MB RSS.\n(3) Real producer/consumer, starting NOW (no user step), done 11-02 by ~11:30 -04: xarchive sends every progress / stage-summary / backoff line as a JSON event to stream x-dumps, topic harvest, over the ClusterIP (reqwest is already a dep, so no new HTTP stack). An iggy user \"xarchive\" holds only send/poll/read permissions; its secret lives in namespace x-dumps; root never leaves the iggy namespace. Consumer: a second pod running `xarchive follow`, polling the topic and writing /data/x/status.tsv (latest event per stage+account, each with its age). Heartbeats read that file instead of grepping logs. kit job events stay with kit's IGGY-MULTI-MACHINE date.\n(1) What the user must decide, nothing to type:\n (a) YES/NO to opening TLS-only ports to the internet on <europa-addr>: TCP 8090 + HTTPS 3000; QUIC and websocket stay closed. An IP allowlist would defeat the pull model (NAT workers have unknown IPs), so protection is TLS + per-machine auth.\n (b) Certificate choice. Default: our own CA (CA key in the box ~/.secrets; server cert with an IP SAN; clients pin the CA; no domain needed). Alternative: a DNS name the user controls with an A record pointing at <europa-addr>, for a public Let's Encrypt cert.\n (c) Per-machine credentials. Default: I generate one iggy user per machine (box, node, europa, each codespace), send/poll only, written to that machine's ~/.secrets/iggy.env. If the user wants to hold them in their own password manager instead, they create them.\n(2) Public listener: same day as the user's yes (~1 h). iggy env tcp.tls/http.tls enabled with cert_file/key_file (0.9.0 supports both natively, no proxy), hostPort 8090/3000 only, linux-worker.ps1 -PublicTls. Proof: the box runs kit verify.ps1 against https://<europa-addr>:3000 with the pinned CA and no tunnel, plus a TCP-TLS client connect.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_4 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 4u32, "2026-11-02T13:43Z", "x-dumps", "DONE (3) real producer/consumer on europa iggy, 11-02 09:38 -04, ahead of the 11:30 date. xarchive publishes every progress/stage/merge/backoff/fetch-error line as JSON {at_ms,line,dropped} to stream x-dumps topic harvest (reqwest, no new deps), as iggy user xarchive (read_streams/read_topics/poll/send only; create-stream gets 403; password generated on the VM into secret x-dumps/iggy-xarchive). Consumer: Deployment x-dumps/xarchive-follow (`xarchive /data/x follow`, 7.7 MB RSS) polls with consumer_id 1 + auto_commit and writes /data/x/status.tsv (latest event per stage+account with at_ms). Proof: topic messages_count 1,344 = 1,344 xarchive log lines since restart (0 dropped), 577 KB. (1)+(2) wait on the user via main; no port opened.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_5 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 5u32, "2026-11-02T16:19Z", "x-dumps", "TLS proven end-to-end INTERNALLY 11-02 (no public port opened). Own CA (ED25519) + server cert at /root/iggy-ca on europa (CA fp 27:7E:2A:20...), SANs: iggy.iggy.svc(.cluster.local), localhost, 127.0.0.1, ClusterIP, and <europa-addr> (ready for public). iggy pod serves HTTPS only (IGGY_HTTP_TLS_ENABLED/CERT_FILE/KEY_FILE from secret iggy-tls; http.tls has NO self_signed field - iggy rejects unknown env vars and crashloops, so only enabled/cert_file/key_file are valid; probes switched to scheme HTTPS). Plain HTTP refused. xarchive events.rs trusts the CA via IGGY_CA_PEM env; secret iggy-xarchive -> IGGY_URL=https://iggy.iggy.svc.cluster.local:3000 + CA. Verified: follow consumer reads over TLS (status.tsv fresh, no errors), topic messages_count climbing (4717) over https, least-priv user still 403 on create-stream. REMAINING for public: (1) USER yes to open hostPort 3000 (+8090 once TCP TLS env keys confirmed) on <europa-addr>; (2) per-machine iggy users into each box ~/.secrets. Internal secure path is complete.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_6 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 6u32, "2026-11-02T21:13Z", "x-dumps", "TCP-8090 TLS enabled + verified 11-02 (completes iggy TLS). Keys confirmed from the iggy-server binary strings (no config file ships): tcp.tls DOES have self_signed (unlike http.tls), so IGGY_TCP_TLS_ENABLED/SELF_SIGNED=false/CERT_FILE/KEY_FILE are all valid. openssl s_client to ClusterIP:8090 with --CAfile ca.crt -> subject CN=iggy.iggy.svc, Verify return code 0 (ok). HTTP 3000 still serves pong over TLS; iggy pod 0 restarts; event stream unaffected. iggy now secures BOTH protocols a pull-worker uses (HTTP 3000 + TCP 8090) with our CA. REMAINING for public: only (1) user yes to open hostPort 3000+8090 on <europa-addr> (cert already has that SAN), (2) per-machine send/poll users into each box ~/.secrets. Reproducibility gap: the TLS env is on the live deploy via set env; linux-worker.ps1 kit iggy.yaml would need a -PublicTls option to redeploy with TLS (follow-up).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_7 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 7u32, "2026-11-02T21:20Z", "x-dumps", "Reproducibility gap CLOSED 11-02: linux-worker.ps1 gained a -Tls switch (+ -TlsSanIp for the public SAN). After iggy comes up on HTTP it generates the CA+cert (reused if present), creates secret iggy-tls, kubectl set env the HTTP+TCP TLS vars, patches the probes to scheme HTTPS + mounts the cert, then verifies https 3000 + tcp 8090 s_client. Uses set env/patch (not fragile manifest string-surgery), so it is the proven live recipe made one-command. Dry-run validated on europa (DRY iggy-tls reached, composes clean); NOT re-applied to live europa (already TLS). A fresh/second iggy node (e.g. the pluto move) now brings up TLS iggy reproducibly. Spiral port tracked on PS1-TO-SPIRAL.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_8 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 8u32, "2026-11-02T21:37Z", "x-dumps", "PUBLIC LISTENER LIVE 11-02 (user authorized the port-open). iggy on europa is now reachable at <europa-addr>:3000 (HTTPS) + :8090 (TCP TLS) via hostPort (only those 2 ports; QUIC 8080 / ws 8092 stay closed). Block was ufw (active, only 22) - added ufw allow 3000/tcp + 8090/tcp (no cloud cloud firewall; k3s CNI hostPort DNAT was already in place, and forwarded-to-pod traffic bypasses the INPUT DROP anyway). EXTERNAL verify from the box: TCP 3000+8090 reachable; openssl s_client -> cert CN=iggy.iggy.svc, TLSv1.3, Verify return code 0 (ok) with our CA. NOTE: the cert is ED25519 - Windows schannel (curl/.NET) cannot handshake it, but rust/rustls clients (reqwest, the iggy crate = the real pull-workers) do; verify externally with openssl, not schannel. Protection: TLS both ports + iggy JWT auth + least-priv users. Remaining: per-machine users + a cross-region producer/consumer proof (wiring pluto worker next).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_9 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 9u32, "2026-11-02T21:49Z", "x-dumps", "CROSS-REGION PULL-WORKER PROVEN 11-02 (DoD 1+2 met). The pluto worker (Sao Paulo, pluto) now publishes to the PUBLIC iggy in San Jose over <europa-addr>:3000 (TLS, CA-verified) with a per-machine least-priv user xarchive-sao (send+poll; create-stream 403). Proof: polled the x-dumps/harvest topic, 134 pluto-origin events (/root/xdata, dropped:0) alongside 263 sjc-origin (/data/x) - both nodes producing to one iggy, outbound-only from sao. Wiring: events.rs gained IGGY_CA_FILE (CA from a path, for systemd/non-k8s); pluto unit recreated with EnvironmentFile=/root/.secrets/iggy.env (URL/user/pass/CA_FILE, pw never printed - generated on europa, relayed file-to-file). New binary ffc5e66a (backward-compat with IGGY_CA_PEM, so the sjc pod was not restarted). REMAINING: DoD(3) auto-failover to the node WSL iggy within a stated time; DoD(4) iggy multi-node/cluster on a beta; the worker-capability phantom tags (LIB-IGGY-CLIENT). Refinement: sjc+pluto share the event key \"progress snaps geygey_hadid\" so they clobber in status.tsv (raw topic distinguishes by path); add a node/root tag to the event key.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_10 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 10u32, "2026-11-02T21:57Z", "x-dumps", "Clobber refinement DONE 11-02: events.rs key() now tags the progress key with the data-root (progress <stage> <root>:<account>), so sjc (data:geygey_hadid) and pluto (root:geygey_hadid) no longer overwrite each other in status.tsv - it is now a true unified multi-node view. Only the follow consumer needed the new binary (1124d46d; producers send raw lines, unchanged), so the sjc harvester was not touched. Verified data: keys write; root: appears on pluto's next progress publish (symmetric logic, pluto publishing already proven).")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_11 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 11u32, "2026-11-02T22:42Z", "x-dumps", "DECISION (technical, decided by rule 11-02): keep the iggy cert ED25519, do NOT reissue as ECDSA now. Rule: match the cert to the production client. The real pull-workers are rust/rustls (xarchive reqwest, the iggy crate, LIB-IGGY-CLIENT = rust+gleam) which handle ED25519 fine - proven by pluto publishing cross-region and xarchive's live TLS event stream. The ONLY thing ED25519 blocks is Windows schannel/.NET clients (curl, Invoke-RestMethod), which are test tools, not the production path; verify those endpoints with openssl, not schannel. Revisit ECDSA P-256 only IF a Windows/.NET process must consume the public iggy directly (then reissue CA+cert ECDSA, update iggy-tls + IGGY_CA_PEM, restart iggy+consumers, and set linux-worker.ps1 -Tls to ECDSA). Recorded so the question is closed, not re-litigated.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_12 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 12u32, "2026-11-03T07:40Z", "x-dumps", "HOW A LANE GETS ITS OWN LEAST-PRIVILEGE IGGY USER (x-dumps 11-03). Recipe: x-dumps scratchpad iggy-box-user.sh (mirror of iggy-sao-user.sh), run ON europa as root. Steps: login as iggy (k8s secret iggy-root) -> token; POST /users with permissions.global = read_streams/read_topics/poll_messages/send_messages = true and everything else false, streams:null (send+poll only; cannot manage servers/users/streams/topics). Password is generated on europa, written umask 077 to /root/iggy-<name>.env, NEVER printed; then scp file-to-file to the target ~/.secrets/iggy.env and scp /root/iggy-ca/ca.crt to ~/.secrets/iggy-ca.crt, and shred the europa temp. Env keys: IGGY_URL=https://<europa-addr>:3000, IGGY_USERNAME, IGGY_PASSWORD, IGGY_CA_FILE. Verify: public-IP login returns a token AND POST /streams returns 403 (least-priv proven). CAVEAT: the ED25519 CA blocks Windows schannel (curl/.NET) - consume via a rustls client (xarchive/events.rs reads IGGY_CA_FILE or IGGY_CA_PEM), not Windows curl. ISSUED: xarchive-sao (pluto node), xarchive-box (this laptop, 11-03, verified login+403). PENDING: xarchive-t1101 - x-dumps has no ssh route to t1101; give the route or run iggy-box-user.sh there with username xarchive-t1101.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_13 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 13u32, "2026-11-03T07:55Z", "x-dumps", "t1101 (laptop-ts) DONE 11-03: xarchive-t1101 created and verified SERVER-SIDE (public-IP login OK, POST /streams -> 403). iggy.env (176B) + iggy-ca.crt delivered file-to-file to C:\\Users\\alias\\.secrets (scp -3 europa->node through the box), password never printed, europa temp shredded. Node-side LITERAL login is not provable on t1101: its only curl is Windows curl 8.13 (Schannel), and .NET is Schannel too - both reject the ED25519 CA (known). The node must consume via a rustls/OpenSSL client (xarchive/events.rs), same as box. All three machines now have least-priv send+poll iggy users: xarchive-sao, xarchive-box, xarchive-t1101.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_14 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 14u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x-dumps", "NODE-SIDE RUSTLS PROBE (plan for the run, per main 11-03): node-side iggy login cannot be proven via Windows curl (Schannel rejects the ED25519 CA; the node has only C:\\Windows\\System32\\curl.exe = Schannel, and .NET is Schannel too). Plan: deploy a small rustls probe (or xarchive/events.rs) to t1101 and box, do a login + poll against <europa-addr>:3000 using IGGY_CA_FILE, to prove node-side consumption end to end. Server-side proof (public login + POST /streams 403) is enough for now.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_15 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 15u32, "2026-11-03T12:39Z", "x-dumps", "STREAM/TOPIC for x lane 11-03: created stream \"x\" (id=1) + topic \"run-progress\" (id=0, 1 partition) on the public iggy (least-priv users cannot create them; root does). iggy 0.9 CreateTopic RECIPE (422 unless these are present): POST /streams/<stream>/topics with {\"name\":\"...\",\"partitions_count\":N,\"compression_algorithm\":\"none\",\"message_expiry\":0,\"max_topic_size\":0,\"replication_factor\":1} (0 => never-expire / unlimited). CreateStream is just {\"name\":\"...\"}. Existing least-priv users (xarchive-sao/-box/-t1101) have GLOBAL send+poll so they can publish/consume ANY stream incl x/run-progress without a per-stream grant. events.rs (xarchive) is a reusable single-module iggy client (Sink + publish + follow + base64, TLS via IGGY_CA_PEM/FILE) - liftable or extractable to a shared iggy_events crate.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_16 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 16u32, "2026-11-05T12:18Z", "x-dumps", "Cross-check (x-dumps, user double-check 2026-11-05 ~08:15): iggy VERIFIED publicly reachable on europa <europa-addr> - openssl s_client CONNECTED on BOTH :8090 (TCP) and :3000 (HTTP), serving TLS, cert subject CN=iggy.iggy.svc issuer \"x-dumps iggy CA\". Port 8092 (websocket) NOT publicly exposed. Client caveat unchanged: needs an ED25519-capable TLS client (rustls + iggy-ca.crt); box curl/Schannel rejects the ED25519 CA (HTTP 000) as noted 11-03. WATCH: the iggy pod has 13 restarts (last ~6h ago) from this morning tibia-induced europa memory thrash - recovered + serving now, but pod limit is 512Mi; worth confirming it survived cleanly. Reachability = GOOD.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_17 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 17u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit: this iggy becomes the cross-network bus of KIT-TWO-HUBS (both hubs dial out; work machines cannot take inbound). RISK, from x-dumps n-last: pod limit 512Mi and 13 restarts after the 11-05 europa thrashes. Burstable pods get a high oom_score_adj, so the kernel kills them before a plain process (europa-heavy-jobs-cap-memory). PROPOSAL (the pod is x-dumps own; not changed): Guaranteed QoS (requests = limits for cpu and memory) sets oom_score_adj -997, so a thrashing job dies before the bus does.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_18 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 18u32, "2026-11-07T15:53Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom markers. NEXT STEP: ask x-dumps (pod owner) to apply Guaranteed QoS (requests = limits) to the iggy pod so a thrashing europa job dies before the bus; then measure restarts over 48 h. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: already typed on the client side (iggy codec event GADT in lib iggy.spi); the QoS change is manifest data, no new model.")
inl note_of_iggy_public_cloud_19 () = Note ("IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD", 19u32, "2026-11-09T13:57Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 14:1x europa process census (read-only, from microblog's dangling sweep): iggy-server is STILL LIVE on europa (up 1d17h), exposed by /root/ockam-iggy-up.sh (90 h; ockam node iggy-edge on :4100 with tcp-outlets to the local iggy http and tcp ports; script dated 11-05). A SECOND exposure, bash /root/sjc-iggy-outlet.sh (93 h, ppid 1), runs from a file that no longer exists on disk, so it is likely superseded by the ockam script. Neither was touched. Against the user's 11-08 rule (europa = saturation only, no services) both stay only until iggy moves off europa (this item / SERVE-TO-PLUTO). Ask for rig as the iggy owner: (1) confirm whether the deleted-file sjc outlet is dead weight and retire it by exact pid; (2) name the cut-over at which iggy-server + ockam-iggy-up leave europa.")
inl cloud_node_backup () = Task ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", "Use the Cloud node's large disk as one more off-site backup copy, encrypted, assuming the VM can vanish at any time (user 11-01 14:0x: 'considering that we might lose the node at any time, but could still be useful for backups, a few gbs dont hurt')", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER", "backup,management,cloud,remote,user-batch,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cloud_node_backup () = Planning ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_1 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:09Z", "main", "main 14:1x: candidates are what has no off-box copy beyond the Wi-Fi node: the kit archive and drafts store, the wipe-recovery ledger, C:\\home\\data. DoD: a nightly encrypted push (age or similar; keys never leave the box, and nothing is uploaded unencrypted) of a resilience-chosen set to the VM; a restore drill from it is byte-exact; losing the VM costs nothing but this copy. Owner: resilience. Typefeatureset: BackupSet as a typed list of sources with phantom Encrypted/Plain tags; push accepts only Encrypted.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_2 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 2u32, "2026-11-01T18:19Z", "resilience", "2026-11-01 1x:xx GROUNDED DESIGN (resilience, builds on main n1). Owner accepted.\n\nCRITICAL OPEN QUESTION main's spec does NOT close - KEY SURVIVABILITY (surface to user): \"keys never leave the box\" is right for confidentiality, but if the decrypt key lives ONLY on the box and the BOX is wiped (exactly the disaster class this guards against - see profile-wipe-2026-10-28), the VM ciphertext is undecryptable => a WRITE-ONLY backup, useless for box-loss recovery. The key/identity must be escrowed OFF the box but NOT on the VM (that would defeat the encryption). Candidates: (a) the node - the key is tiny, store it there (already a trusted off-box location), or (b) user-held (password manager / the proton account). DECISION NEEDED from user. Until solved, this copy defends against NODE loss only, not box loss, for the encrypted set. This is the top of the user-batch for this item.\n\nENCRYPTION TOOL (grounded, probed box 11-01): age/rage ABSENT; gpg, openssl, 7z PRESENT (scoop). Use ASYMMETRIC-to-box-pubkey so the push side needs only the PUBLIC key and the secret key stays escrowed: today = `gpg -e -r <box-key>` (present now); preferred = age (download+pin per downloads-standing-ok; recipient model is cleaner, `age -r <pubkey>` / `age -d -i <identity>`). Either way the VM only ever holds ciphertext, and only the escrowed secret key decrypts. NOT symmetric-passphrase (a passphrase on the box has the same survivability problem and is weaker).\n\nTRANSPORT: VM is a LINUX worker (dep REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER), not the Wi-Fi node's cmd channel - so rsync box->Linux should work cleanly (the node rsync probe failed because the node is Windows-over-cmd; a real Linux sshd is the normal rsync case). Nightly = rsync the ciphertext blob dir (append/update only). Fallback scp per blob.\n\nBACKUP SET (sized from measured bytes 11-01, cadence by churn):\n- kit archive 1.5 GB (append-only; node copy exists) => NIGHTLY, MtimeDelta - deltas tiny.\n- drafts store 151 KB (only-copy; node copy exists) => NIGHTLY, FullSnapshot (tiny).\n- wipe-recovery ledger 148 MB (only-copy; node copy exists) => OnChange (near-static; re-push only when recovery re-triaged).\n- C:\\home\\data 575 MB but 99% ALREADY protected: rescue-2026-10-28 569 MB is the static node tar already (rescue-2026-10-28.tar, byte-verified) => ONE-TIME encrypted push, not nightly; node-checkout-only-extract 5 MB already safe (node holds its source zip) => SKIP; main-drafts 4.7 KB + churny lane drafts => NIGHTLY.\nInitial encrypted seed ~= 2.2 GB; nightly delta small. Respects x-dumps FIRST CLAIM on VM disk/CPU: schedule AFTER the x-dumps window, rate-limit rsync (--bwlimit), cap VM backup dir to an agreed quota (coordinate with personal lane, who runs the VM).\n\nDoD (byte-exact, concrete):\n- kit backup-push verb: per-source tar -> encrypt to box pubkey -> upload ONLY ciphertext (phantom Encrypted gate: push accepts no Plain path) -> append manifest {source, plaintext_sha256, cipher_sha256, bytes, uploaded_at}.\n- kit backup-drill verb (weekly, profile-restore-drill shape): pull ciphertext, decrypt with escrowed secret key, sha256(plaintext) == recorded plaintext_sha256 => ByteExact | Mismatch{local,restored} | Missing | DecryptFailed. \"Losing the VM costs nothing but this copy\" is VERIFIED by the box+node copies remaining intact and independently drill-passing without the VM.\n- Nightly push + weekly drill registration = user-batch (SCHEDULED-TASKS-CONFIRM; tag user-batch already set). I never self-register persistent machine config.\n\nTYPEFEATURESET (extends main n1):\n- BackupSet = list {source: typed source; cadence: | Nightly | OnChange | OneTime; shape: | FullSnapshot | MtimeDelta}.\n- phantom path location = | Plain | Cipher; upload : path Cipher -> result (a Plain path cannot be uploaded - compile-time guarantee nothing leaves unencrypted, satisfying main's \"push accepts only Encrypted\").\n- key as a typed Identity handle read from a box-local escrowed path, never inlined, never passed to the Cipher transport.\n- drill_result union above; manifest rows carry typed hash newtypes (plaintext vs cipher sha256 are distinct types, not swappable).\nGENERALIZES the kit dir-mirror verb (KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR): backup-push = dir-mirror + an encrypt stage + a manifest; same size/hash verify. One verb family, three transports (node Windows, VM Linux, hot profile mirror).\n\nDEPENDENCIES: REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER (VM ssh target + sshd) - already a dep; gated on it. Coordinate with PERSONAL lane for: VM backup dir, disk quota, upload window after x-dumps. Message personal lane once the VM is reachable (nothing for them to act on before REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER lands). NODE-SAFETY note (unrelated to VM, flagged by main 11-01): node now junctions kit\\{jobs,windows} -> eoie\\{jobs,windows}; unlink before any node cleanup under eoie\\ or kit\\ (captured in memory unlink-mirror-junctions-first). Ties KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR, PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT, memory backup-mirror-coverage-boundary.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_3 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x PARKED USER DECISION (22:00 batch), from resilience n2: where does the decrypt key live off the box? A key only on the box makes the VM copy useless after a box wipe, the exact disaster this item covers; on the VM it gives no confidentiality. Options: (a) the node, already a trusted off-box machine; (b) the user's password manager or Proton account. Main recommends (b), plus (a) as a second copy, so losing any one machine never loses both the data and the key. Until decided, the copy covers node loss only.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_4 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 4u32, "2026-11-02T14:32Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByTest. RULE: Default: the decrypt key lives on BOTH private machines (box and node), never in a repo, plus a monthly restore drill (the existing profile-restore-drill task). PASS iff a restore from the Europa copy decrypts with the node's key and with the box's key. A password-manager copy is optional and stays the user's. BACKUP/UNDO: The key file is copied, never moved; the old location is kept. OWNER: resilience.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_5 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 5u32, "2026-11-05T04:47Z", "resilience", "GROUNDING (resilience, europa df): europa /dev/vda2 = 94G total, 66G used, 24G FREE (74%), no ~/backups dir yet (not landing there). 24G free fits the user intent (treat VM as disposable, a few GB is fine) for a SELECTIVE ENCRYPTED backup of the hardest-to-recover data - kit archive (3.31G job evidence), .claude memory, unstaged drafts - NOT a full-box copy (box has 738G used). Design: age/gpg-encrypt a small (<5G) tar of {kit archive, memory, unstaged} and push to europa ~/backups as one more off-site copy (box + node + europa = 3 copies), pruned to 1-2 generations given the 24G shared with europa workload. CONSTRAINTS: (a) europa is x-dumps-owned for CPU - coordinate timing so a backup push does not collide with its jobs; (b) encryption key material stays on the box, never sent to europa; (c) europa load was 2.49/1.34/1.08 (2 vCPU) so gate the push on loadavg like other europa work. Pairs with KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR (node copy) - europa = the off-SITE tier. resilience.")
inl note_of_cloud_node_backup_6 () = Note ("CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP", 6u32, "2026-11-07T15:56Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP: once REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER (dep) lands a worker, add an encrypted (age) off-site copy of the critical-*.tar to europa /home/remote/stage/backup, treating the VM as ephemeral (user 11-01: a few GB do not hurt). PLANNING: P2 D2, gated behind the worker dep; no node CPU cost (encrypt on box, scp small tar).")
inl kit_typed_crates () = Task ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", "kit enforces the typefeatureset on itself: typed cross-crate bindings generated from Spiral types instead of $' string calls, and shared model packages as real dependency crates built once instead of inlined into every importer; a rewrite of kit's composition model if needed (user 11-01 18:1x)", Epic, P0, D8, 25u32, Planned, "", "kit,typefeatureset,architecture,drift,standards,team:rig,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_typed_crates () = Planning ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", Minutes 4800u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_1 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:18Z", "kit", "11-01 18:1x kit. USER (verbatim): 'we gotta study all models of project bootstrapping that we have so we help kit fix its drift. a review diagnosed \"generate typed cross-crate bindings from spiral types, instead of $' string calls\", also \"make shared model packages real dependency crates, so they're built once instead of copied into every importer\". we gotta be using the typefeatureset to the max at all times, that's kit priority number one to enforce and it needs to happen with itself too, even if we rewrite the whole project in the process, we're iterating 24h anyway'. EVIDENCE FROM TODAY (TRAIN7): kit_lint_model is inlined into 4 crates and agile_todo_model into 7; agile_todo_model's path_verdict_of_code alone generates 96 lines in each importer, because a union crossing a crate boundary is flattened to an i32 code and rebuilt by reflection (case_at/case_index); two crates went over the 1000-line gate partly from that inlining, and the hand split needed two new crates (agile_todo_standing, kit_lint_baseline) whose calls are more untyped $' strings the style hook flags. PLAN: S0 = the study of every bootstrapping/composition model across our repos (kit, spiral lib + cells scaffold, tibia, fiscal, x, dice, alphabet, polyglot), running now; S1 = a typed binding generator: from each crate's export signatures (Spiral types), emit one typed Spiral binding module per crate (the only place a $' call lives, a boundary package) plus the Rust extern side, so callers write typed calls and the hook has nothing to flag; S2 = derived codecs for unions/records/GADTs crossing crates (reflection-derived, byte-identical both sides, the iggy codec idea), replacing i32-code flattening; S3 = shared model packages become dependency crates (built once, exported through S1 bindings), with a measure of generated lines saved per importer; S4 = migrate kit crate by crate under the ratchet; S5 = a kit standards rule refusing new $'<crate>:: calls and new multi-importer inlined model packages. DoD: 0 hand-written cross-crate $' calls in kit/src; every model package imported by more than one crate is a crate; total generated lines drop measurably; the crate gate and ratchet stay green; node proof per slice. TYPEFEATURES: binding signatures as a GADT indexed by the export's type (op from to); existential packed exports in the registry; phantom crate markers so a binding can only be called from a crate that declares the dependency.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_2 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 2u32, "2026-11-01T18:44Z", "kit", "11-01 14:44 kit S0 STUDY DONE (read-only survey of 9 projects). MODELS: kit = 272 crates, each a whole-program compile of its package graph, calling each other by \\$'crate::fn' after string exports, unions flattened to i32 (kit/src/union_reflection/cases.spi:26,40); x apps/client = 29 crates with TYPED exports (export ... : () -> command_fields becomes pub fn ... -> (i32, Rc<str>, bool, ...), args.rs:520) but the caller redeclares the record; dir-tree-html = 5 crates with every \\$' stub in one module (shared.spi:102-106), the hand-made version of a binding module; tibia/fiscal = plain Spiral calls inside whole-program entries (fully typed, but kit as one program would be ~150k lines, against the 1000-line gate); dice/alphabet/polyglot/lib/Cells scaffold = single package. KIT MEASURED: 480 package dirs, 272 crates, 205 non-crate packages, 44 of them inlined into more than one crate transitively (rust_std_string 114 crates, union_reflection 95, state_receipt_codec_model 64, workspace_primitives 62, agile_mutation_policy 45, agile_task_text 42, kit_receipt 37, agile_note_model 19, ...), 33 crate packages also inlined into others (rust_std_env 79, rust_std_fs 64, kit_jobs 29); 116 packages reached by no crate; 303 hand-written \\$'<kit crate>:: sites in 91 files over 132 crates (+496 \\$'std::); 644 !!!!Export + 27 export; 150,048 generated lines; top five crates at 996-999 lines, so the gate is what forces splits. COMPILER ALREADY HAS (spiral_compiler.fs): typed Rust export signatures, records of primitives become pub struct (:14050-14094); every union reachable from an export becomes 'pub enum <name>' with tag() (:12453-12476, :14499-14506; checked by scripts/export-check-rust/union_return); '///- --import-types <crate>:<U1>,<U2>' makes a crate drop its own copy of those enums and 'use <crate>::{...}' (:12441-12451, :14507-14540; host Program.fs:1221-1235; Cells directives.spi:53); 'rust-extern: derive = ...' adds derives (:13529); the Gleam backend has the same import/export logic (:17754, :18515). UNUSED by kit, x and eoie. No type-derived codec exists (lib reflection.spi has the primitives; iggy.spi:44-64 is hand-written). DESIGN DECISION (S1-S3 reshaped): converge on x/apps/client's crate layout + typed exports + union export + --import-types: (S1) shared model packages become crates exporting typed functions, whose unions become pub enums; (S2) callers import those types with --import-types, so unions cross as real Rust enums and path_verdict_code/path_verdict_of_code/case_at round-trips disappear; (S3) the typed \\$' stubs are GENERATED into one binding module per callee crate (dir-tree-html's shared.spi pattern, automated), replacing the 303 hand-written sites; (S4) a standards rule refuses new hand-written cross-crate \\$' and new multi-importer inlined models. RISKS TO SPIKE FIRST: the struct path covers only records of primitives (unions with list/nested-record payloads may need compiler work, which goes to the compiler lane); the 'use' replacement matches union names on generated text (:14512-14514), so names that exist in more than one package must be checked. NEXT: SPIKE on agile_todo_model (inlined into 7 crates; path_verdict holds nested unions comment_syntax/skip_reason): make it a crate exporting path_verdict_of_path, make agile_todo_paths/scan/merge use --import-types agile_todo_model:path_verdict,comment_syntax,skip_reason, measure generated lines before and after and run the todo_cli notebook on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_3 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 3u32, "2026-11-01T18:55Z", "kit", "11-01 14:55 kit SPIKE GREEN (node job-1793558949388, 111 s). Change (unstaged): agile_todo_paths exports kit_agile_todo_path_verdict as 'string * bool -> path_verdict' (typed, migrated from !!!!Export to export, i32 code path removed). agile_todo_scan and agile_todo_merge carry '///- --import-types agile_todo_paths:path_verdict,comment_syntax,path_skip' and call '\\$'agile_todo_paths::kit_agile_todo_path_verdict(...)' : path_verdict' with no path_verdict_of_code decode. RESULT: regen published 3 owners and the candidate built (cargo build of kit-cli = cross-crate type check); generated lines agile_todo_paths 340 -> 324, agile_todo_scan 937 -> 812 (-125), agile_todo_merge 939 -> 815 (-124): -265 lines for ONE union crossing, and the two biggest drops come from no longer inlining union_reflection's case_at decoder. Both importers emit 'use agile_todo_paths::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict};' and drop their local enums: nested union payloads cross as real Rust enums (risk 1 cleared for unions; record payloads still untested). todo_cli 4/4 green (consumer path: stale/unknown/scan verdicts through the binary). Name-collision risk (2) not hit here; the generator must check for source-name collisions. NEXT (S1 proper): (a) a kit verb 'kit bindings <crate>' in Spiral: read the crate's export declarations (name + typed lambda signature), emit one typed binding module per callee (the only \\$' sites, plus the import-types list the importer needs), and rewrite importers' call sites to the typed functions; (b) apply to the next biggest crossings by measured payoff (union_reflection in 95 crates, state_receipt_codec_model 64): measure generated lines per crossing first; (c) retire path_verdict_code/path_verdict_of_code from agile_todo_model once the model test stops using them.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_4 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 4u32, "2026-11-01T20:08Z", "kit", "11-01 16:08 kit: S1 progress. (a) kit_bindings_model (HostFree, node 2/2 and Linux 2/2): reads both export spellings with a depth-aware scan, splits typed signatures, renders typed binding lines plus the --import-types header. (b) The kit bindings verb is wired (VerbBindings in command_spec_domain, routed in kit_cli_model, new crate kit_bindings, kit-cli dep). First node proof: notebooks green, but kit_bindings.rs was 1,899 lines (decls_from alone 399), because every := helper inlined the char-scan chain into each caller; fixed by making the 18 heavy helpers :~ (emitted once). The verb also read argv off by one (kit argv starts at the verb word); fixed. Re-proof running: job-1793563609342 (also carries the node --after owner fix). EVIDENCE for S3 from resilience (BEAM-GUARD): kit_machine_sample's exported 9-tuple process_rows_sampled is inlined into 7 importer crates (kit_machine_sample, kit_machine_watch, kit_machine_health, kit_process_table, kit_process_kill, kit_jobs_doctor, kit_jobs_doctor_servers); adding one field broke two importers with E0308 until all 7 were regenerated, which is exactly the coupling a typed record export plus --import-types removes. LESSON for the binding generator and the regex repo: in this kit dialect := inlines, so any helper over ~5 lines that several callers reach must be :~, or the 1000-line gate fails on code that looks small in Spiral.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_5 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 5u32, "2026-11-01T21:08Z", "kit", "11-01 17:08 kit: S1 milestones from node proof 3 (job-1793565238955): (1) kit bindings <crate> on agile_todo_paths reproduces the spike's hand-written binding plus import line exactly (two-line format: signature, then the indented typed call); (2) CENSUS over all 274 crates: 643 exports, 638 typed (99.2%), 5 untyped (kit_job_gate 1, kit_job_slot 1, state_receipt_codec_core 1, plus 2 false markers from test-fixture strings, now skipped); (3) shared-model-as-crate applied to itself: kit_bindings_model became a crate with two typed exports, and the verb crate kit_bindings (516 lines) calls them only through the generated-format binding module kit_bindings_model_api (host macros in the verb: 0). Open: kit_bindings_model.rs was 1,356 lines; the scanner is rewritten around ONE shared depth fold (marks: byte offsets of top-level delimiters, with all splitting done by substring) instead of five char folds, re-proving in job-1793567257674 with the dogfood check (the generated binding module must equal kit_bindings_model_api byte for byte).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_6 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 6u32, "2026-11-01T21:10Z", "kit", "11-01 17:10 kit S2 MIGRATION QUEUE (measured: 37 union-flattening sites, case_at/case_index/*_of_code, across 15 packages). Order by payoff: (1) the kit_lint family: kit_lint_classify::kit_lint_class takes place/origin as i32 and returns a class code; kit_lint_walk::kit_lint_known takes a class i32; decoders place_of_code/origin_of_code/class_of_code in kit_lint_model (inlined into 6 crates). Migration: typed exports over macro_place/macro_origin/macro_class, with --import-types kit_lint_classify:macro_place,macro_origin,macro_class in macros/scan/walk; the decoders retire. (2) agile_todo_model: path_verdict done (spike); the remaining ones are comment_syntax and syntax codes in agile_todo_lines' findings export (13 sites in the model). (3) kit_lint_shadows_core kind_of_code. (4) command_spec_domain (4), authority_state_domain (2), canonical_plan_ir_domain (1): small, after the generator writes the binding modules. S5 NOTE: the refusal of new hand-written cross-crate calls already exists in effect, because the ratchet counts host-macro per package and refuses growth (it refused the 0->2 in kit_bindings today, which pushed the call into a binding module). The remaining S5 work is to name the rule (cross-crate-call) separately from OS host macros, and to exempt generated *_api binding modules by a header marker.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_7 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 7u32, "2026-11-01T22:08Z", "kit", "11-01 18:08 kit DIRECTION CHANGE (user 17:4x via main): \"now we're inventing things like 'import-types'? damn, that strayed far hard from upstream ... we got plans to use types instead of this weird plumbing we're having to build to support the old splitting way. help us get sota!\" ACTIONS: (1) the --import-types spike is reverted in the tree (unstaged reversal of the staged spike hunks in agile_todo_paths/scan/merge; the spike files are archived at kit\\archive\\20261101-180537-typed-crates-spike; evidence kept in n3); (2) the bindings verb is unregistered from TRAIN8 and parked as module packages (Cargo.toml archived at kit\\archive\\20261101-175534-bindings-cargo); (3) no more building on --import-types. NEXT: one design agreed with the compiler lane for native typed cross-crate exports in an upstream-compatible form (no header flag), possibly fewer crates instead of typed plumbing for the split, before any more code. What stays valid from today: S0's measurements (inlined models, 303 untyped sites, 150k generated lines), the census tool idea, and the := vs :~ size lesson.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_8 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 8u32, "2026-11-01T22:16Z", "kit", "11-01 18:16 kit: DESIGN AGREED with compiler (18:1x). Native typed linking at monomorphic export boundaries, declared in package.spiproj as a new `link:` field (same syntax as packages:), the upstream-compatible form, since upstream Spiral is whole-program over the spiproj graph and has no separate compilation. Units stay hand-modeled and the 1000-line gate stays per crate. Step A (compiler, small): the importedTypeUnits map is built from the package graph, so unions declared in a link: package belong to that unit; the SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES env var / --import-types plumbing is deleted; unions stop flattening to i32 (the 37 reflection rebuilds go). Step B (compiler, medium): calls to monomorphic exports of link: packages become typed extern calls (a new TyOp carrying the signature; codegenRust emits unit::fn(args)), replacing the 303 $' host strings; model-only link: packages contribute types, not code (the rust_std_string-in-114-crates win). Compiler files it as COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS; this epic depends on it. Holes kit raised: Cargo deps generated from link: (one source of truth), payload coverage beyond records of primitives, union identity by owning package not source name, and the same unit graph for Gleam. kit's part: baseline measurements (n2), running each step's candidate over kit on the node, and the Cargo-deps generator.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_9 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:49Z", "kit", "11-01 18:49 kit: compiler Step A candidate ready (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS): DLL at node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\linked\\cache\\bin\\single-flight\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll, pinned per job with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL. An importer's package.spiproj gets `link:` (same syntax as packages:); unions declared in a linked package are matched by owning-package id and imported as use <unit>::{name}, where unit = the linked package's folder; no SPIRAL_IMPORT_TYPES. Not yet: Step B typed extern calls, Cargo deps from link:, record payloads. kit's measurement plan (after the TRAIN8 pre-proof frees kit-verify; node disk is tight, so no new mirror): agile_todo_model becomes a unit exporting kit_agile_todo_path_verdict : string * bool -> path_verdict; agile_todo_scan/merge add link: agile_todo_model; compare with today's baseline (paths 340 / scan 937 / merge 939) and the import-types spike (scan 812 / merge 815); gate todo_cli 4/4 + cargo build. rust_std_string and union_reflection are Step B measurements (no unions / decoder only).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_10 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 10u32, "2026-11-02T00:35Z", "kit", "11-01 20:35 kit: STEP A FIRST RUN over kit (node job-1793579446108): HOLE found and sent to compiler. With link: agile_todo_model in scan/merge, the importers emitted use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict, content_verdict, todo_filter} (every linked-package union they use), but the unit emits pub enum only for unions reachable from its exports, so cargo failed with E0432 (content_verdict, todo_filter). Proposed fix (2): a link: unit emits pub enum for every union it declares (the single owner of its types). Measurement pending the fix.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_11 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 11u32, "2026-11-02T00:39Z", "kit", "11-01 20:39 kit: compiler's design for the Step A second cut (20:4x). The export-reachable union set can only come from the UNIT's own compile (export is a runtime op that exists only when the unit's main is partially evaluated), so compiling a link: unit writes a typed UNIT GRAPH next to its output (package id, each export name plus monomorphic signature, and the union nominals reachable from it with concrete type args); importers read it and owner-map exactly those unions, keeping the rest local. Build order: the unit before its importers; the graph is generated, never committed. It is also kit's hole-1 artifact: the Cargo-deps generator reads the same graph (one source of truth). Scoped on COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS for the next session. Kit workaround until then: link: only packages whose cross-boundary unions are all export-reachable. kit-verify target pruned for node disk (main's ask); the Step A rerun rebuilds it.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_12 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 12u32, "2026-11-02T01:14Z", "kit", "11-01 21:14 kit: the S0 study agent (resumed by the user) extended its report. NEW FACTS: (1) --import-types came in bulk commit cec8840c (10-28, message \"tests\", 50+ files, 383 compiler lines) with no fixture, no docs and no user, implemented by regex surgery on generated text (delete enum/impl blocks, insert use) and matched by source name while the producer renames clashes to <name>_<n> (spiral_compiler.fs:12463), which supports the user's 17:4x rejection; (2) x apps/client has the same root problems, milder: a hand-mirrored 15-field tuple (xc.spi:32 parsed, copied from fields.spi:36 command_fields; a same-typed field swap compiles silently), shared modules copied per crate, one package for all 29 crates (no declared boundaries), and 48 hand-written $'xc_*:: strings; (3) x client also has the 1000-line gate (x/scripts/gate.ps1). TEMPLATE IDEAS kept as inputs: crate kinds (model/boundary/feature/bin), generated binding modules plus Cargo deps, ratchets (0 $'<crate>:: outside binding modules, 0 non-type packages copied into more than one crate, string->string exports only go down), one build script reading Cargo spiral metadata. SUPERSEDED: the study's union mechanism (--import-types) is replaced by the agreed `link:` design (n8, COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS), so the generated bindings and Cargo deps come from the unit graph, not from header flags.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_13 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 13u32, "2026-11-02T10:50Z", "main", "main 11-02 07:0x from the kit-session Explore report (aaf2a6f472dc70094). PHASES: K0 tag kit 0.x before the typed boundary. K2 promote shared models to units: state_receipt_codec_model (copied into 64 crates), workspace_primitives 62, agile_mutation_policy 45, agile_task_text 42, kit_receipt 37, agile_note_model 19, kit_do_records 13, agile_planning_model 11, agile_todo_model 7, kit_lint_model 6. K3 remove rust_std_string (114) + union_reflection (95); stop importing rust_std_env/rust_std_fs/kit_jobs/kit_machine_windows as packages. K4 delete the 37 union-flattening sites (n6), 303 hand call sites, 116 packages no crate uses. KIT-BINDINGS-INTERIM: `kit bindings` (n4-n5) covers only the period before COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS-EXTERN-CALLS (Step B); delete it when Step B lands. RECONCILE: census says 638/643 exports \"typed\", but by return type 432 return i32 (many union codes) and 146 Rc<str>; \"typed\" means annotated, not typed. Hard rule for exports: fully concrete signatures (no type params); a GADT index crosses a unit boundary only wrapped in an existential (EJP0041 advice).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_14 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 14u32, "2026-11-02T10:53Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:0x GROOM of main's n13 (accepted, with corrections). (1) RECONCILE, measured on the live tree after TRAIN10: 892 generated pub fns; return types 434 i32, 146 Rc<str>, 75 Result<(),String>, 54 u64, 23 bool. Both earlier numbers were the wrong metric: the census's 638/643 'typed' means annotated, and the 434 i32 include every verb entry's exit code, which is a legitimate i32. The metric that tracks the defect is call-side: 127 hand-written cross-crate calls typed ': i32', and 39 decoder sites (*_of_code / case_at / case_index) in 16 files (n6 counted 37; +2 since). K4's DoD uses those two counts, both going to 0 for unions (exit codes stay i32). (2) OWN REGRESSION, recorded: TRAIN10's kit_job_host host_run.spi decodes kit_job_queue::kit_job_queue_decide's i32 (0/1/else) into queue_verdict: one more union-as-code crossing, added by me. It joins the K4 list; it goes away when kit_job_queue links kit_job_host's queue_verdict (Step A) instead of returning a code. (3) K0 (tag kit 0.x) is a git write, so it's the user's action; kit prepares the tag name and commit, nothing more. (4) ORDER: KIT-LOAD-PROOF-E2E runs before K2/K3, because pruning unreached code first shrinks what K2-K4 migrate. K2 waits on Step A cut 2 (the unit graph, n12); K3's rust_std_string/union_reflection removal needs Step B (they carry functions, not just types). (5) KIT-BINDINGS-INTERIM: already parked as module packages since n7 (not registered, no crate); when Step B lands, archive kit_bindings and kit_bindings_model with scratch-archive.ps1, never delete. (6) Export rule accepted: fully concrete export signatures; a GADT index crosses a unit only inside an existential.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_15 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 15u32, "2026-11-02T12:05Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:0x STEP A SECOND CUT GREEN over kit (node job-1793620674664-kit-stepA2b, compiler linked2 DLL host 9489144496A935E6, lib json.spi 20C39CB9). agile_todo_model is a link: unit exporting kit_agile_todo_path_verdict : string * bool -> path_verdict; agile_todo_scan/merge add link: agile_todo_model and call it typed. Compiled directly in dependency order (model 6 s -> model.unit.json 878 B; scan 6 s; merge 7 s). RESULT: scan 937 -> 812, merge 939 -> 815 (= the --import-types spike's numbers, with no header flag); both import exactly use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict}; content_verdict/todo_filter stay local, so n11's E0432 is gone (0); cargo build -p kit-cli exit 0; todo_cli notebook 4/4. KIT BUILD GAPS this exposed (kit's work, before linked units enter kit's normal build): (1) test-regeneration.ps1 compiles owners in one staged workspace and copies back only .rs, so a unit's <stem>.unit.json is lost and the importer of a separately regenerated unit fails ('has no unit graph'); it must order owners by link: (units first) and keep/copy *.unit.json; (2) the Cargo [dependencies] generator reads the unit graph's links field (compiler n12); (3) *.unit.json goes in .gitignore. Also: kit_bindings_model's generator no longer emits '///- --import-types' (link_section returns the importer's 'link:\\\\n    <crate>' fragment, binding modules carry only typed lines), unblocking IMPORT-TYPES-DELETE on kit's side; node test pending.")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_16 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 16u32, "2026-11-02T12:47Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 08:4x BUILD GAP (1) FIXED + A/B-PROVEN. Decision by construction: auto-include each link: unit in the same regen run and compile it before its importers, instead of copying *.unit.json into the tree (a persisted graph can go stale when the unit changes without a regen; a same-run graph can't). compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 (unstaged): Get-ProjectSection reads packages: and link:; link: units are inputs of their importers (watched closure); selection grows by the transitive link: closure and is ordered units-first by a DFS that throws on a link: cycle; it logs 'link: units compiled first: ...'. .gitignore gains /src/**/*.unit.json. NODE A/B on the same mirror (Step A overlay, linked2 DLL), regenerating ONLY scan+merge through candidate-build: A = HEAD regen -> exit 1, both 'FatalError: The link: unit agile_todo_model has no unit graph ... Build agile_todo_model before the units that link it' (job-1793623343093); B = new regen -> '3 selected owners' (model pulled in), Published 3, candidate built, scan 812 / merge 815 with use agile_todo_model::{comment_syntax, path_skip, path_verdict}, 0 *.unit.json left in the tree, todo_cli 4/4 (job-1793623099032). Remaining gap (2): Cargo [dependencies] from the unit graph's links (today the overlay hand-adds agile-todo-model to scan/merge Cargo.toml).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_17 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 17u32, "2026-11-02T13:16Z", "main", "main 11-02 (user: \"gotta check if we fully healed from this rust_std_env, we're using lib now, gotta find all similar drift in the other lanes\"). NOT HEALED: 280 kit files still reference kit's local wrapper packages: rust_std_fs 370 uses (package 695 lines + rust_std_fs_mutation 552), rust_std_string 250 (34 lines), rust_std_env 165 (58 lines), rust_std_process 55 (124 lines). No other lane uses them (the i574n.github hits are backlog text). Phase K3 covers this and has not started; it is the concrete next step. Similar drift elsewhere = lanes redefining lib helpers under the same name (DUPLICATE-CODE-TOOL note 11-02).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_18 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 18u32, "2026-11-02T16:00Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 12:1x K3 STEP 1 (unstaged): rust_std_string's concat5 (363 call sites, the dominant use; then replace 47, contains 27) no longer is a host macro: `:~ concat5 a b c d e = a ++# b ++# c ++# d ++# e` (a join point, emitted once per crate), and the package now references spiral- (lib-referenced) and opens sm'_operators. Chosen by measurement over a per-call-site rewrite: zero call sites edited, and the per-call Rc::from([..].concat()) expansion is gone. PRE-REGISTERED RULE: accept if no crate passes 1000 and each grows <= 5%. NODE (job-1793634757738, 10 owners regenerated + built): kit_machine_windows 455 -> 458, kit_machine_watch 898 -> 888, kit_memory_query 996 -> 995, kit_do_process 946 -> 936, kit_style_census 987 -> 988; accepted. Census ratchet rust_std_string: 0 regressions. Next K3 steps: replace/contains/starts_with/ends_with/trim the same way (each delegating to lib sm'), then a Spiral (not pwsh) rename of call sites to lib names and deletion of rust_std_string. A pwsh call-site converter was tried and dropped: PowerShell tuple returns corrupted lines and it was the wrong tool class (Spiral-first).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_19 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 19u32, "2026-11-03T07:35Z", "kit", "kit groom 11-03 03:3x (WIP cap, memory wip-cap-active-means-touched): Active -> Planned, last note 16 h ago by kit, progress kept at 25%. Resume from that note: lib sm'), then a Spiral (not pwsh) rename of call sites to lib names and deletion of rust_std_string. A pwsh call-site converter was tried and dropped: PowerShell tuple returns corrupted lines and it was the wrong tool class (Spiral-first).")
inl note_of_kit_typed_crates_20 () = Note ("KIT-TYPED-CRATES", 20u32, "2026-11-03T08:50Z", "kit", "11-03 04:5x kit: now on KIT-CATCH-UP's critical path. The first granular heal (kit_grep_query found.spi, draft in the kit store) needs its own owner crate behind a typed link: boundary carrying search_hits (an array of records holding arrays). NEXT for kit: generate Cargo [dependencies] from the compiler's unit graph (compiler's note: \"Kit's Cargo generator reading the graph is kit's\"). Ask compiler about payload coverage for records holding arrays when the run starts.")
inl pages_file_viewers () = Task ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", "Every text file on a Pages site opens in the browser: dir-tree-html writes an x.ext.html viewer next to each .spi/.tsv/.toml/.ps1 (and any type Pages serves as a download), and the tree links to the viewer, keeping a small raw link (user 11-01 14:0x: clicking tsv/spi/toml/ps1 on tibia's Pages downloads the file, unlike lua/yml/txt)", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,ci,all-repos,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pages_file_viewers () = Planning ("PAGES-FILE-VIEWERS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl dir_tree_markdown_gfm () = Task ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", "dir-tree-html's README renderer handles GitHub-flavored markdown well enough for tibia's homepage: tables, ordered and nested lists, bold/italic, images, and READMEs in subfolders, until it reaches parity with GitHub's own rendering (user 11-01 14:0x: 'dir-tree-html readme.md renderer doesn't support tables, gotta use the tibia homepage as example to fix until it's sota')", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "dir-tree-html,pages,markdown,user-batch,main,team:main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dir_tree_markdown_gfm () = Planning ("DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl cells_html_parity () = Task ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", "Cells' static HTML catches up with the old dib pages and goes past them: error outputs and tracebacks, pages written for failed runs too, inline SVG/HTML/table/image outputs, per-cell duration, phases and per-target times, labelled output per backend (user 11-01 14:0x: 'our new erl based cells code gotta match the rich functionality that we had with the old dib runner')", Story, P1, D4, 55u32, Planned, "NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER", "cells,html,notebook,user-batch,microblog,team:microblog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_html_parity () = Planning ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_1 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:37Z", "main", "main 14:4x, gaps from the parity research (old: polyglot core.ps1 Invoke-Dib, dotnet repl -> ipynb -> nbconvert dark theme, Plotting.dib inline SVG, one output block per backend). The ipynb already holds everything; html.ex renders only text/plain: (1) error outputs: notebook.ex:1313 stores output_type error, html.ex:75-77 drops it; (2) an HTML page is written only when every test passes (notebook.ex:418-423), so failed runs, the ones that matter, have no page; (3) display.ex:50-61 produces image/svg+xml and display.ex:17 allows any MIME type, but html.ex:77 drops them; (4) duration_ms, phases_ms and per-target times sit in metadata (notebook.ex:1398-1407) and aren't shown; (5) no labelled output per backend; (6) markdown cells lack tables, ordered lists, bold and images (shared with DIR-TREE-MARKDOWN-GFM). Already better than the old pages: semantic Spiral coloring from the compiler tokens verb, and pass/fail badges. DoD: lib/spiral/physics.spi's page shows its plots inline like the 706e6beb~1 physics.dib.html reference (82 SVGs); a deliberately failing sample's page shows the error; every cell shows its time and per-backend status. Typefeatureset: Output = Text | Error of trace | Svg | Html | Table | Image as a GADT rendered by one exhaustive match (CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT's output union).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_2 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-11-02T12:24Z", "x", "2026-11-02 08:24 x: GROOM: this item also owns porting html.ex (231 Elixir lines) for CELLS-IN-SPIRAL DoD 1, which lists it as a dep. The parity work and the port are one slice: the typed renderer replaces html.ex, not a second Elixir pass.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_3 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:31Z", "x", "2026-11-02 18:31 x: markdown renderer port (phase A, html_markdown.spi exported as markdown_html; drafts in kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-HTML-MD; phase B html.ex 231 -> 181 drafted in \\CELLS-HTML-MD-B, Html.markdown delegates). x-cells-md1 VOID: the blocks passed 1/1 and html_test passed, but the corpus walk Path.wildcard('*/**/*.md') from the mirror root crawled every deps and _build tree and timed out at 30 min before comparing anything. x-cells-mdjson1 VOID for markdown: the doc-text Path.wildcard brace pattern raised badpattern missing_delimiter on the mirror path. Fix: the corpus is git ls-files per repo (*.md plus the /// doc text of tracked lib and apps spi), which is what 'tracked' means. Rule unchanged: blocks 1/1, parity over > 100 texts with 0 differ, html_test 0 failures. Rerun inside x-cells-mdjson2.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_4 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 4u32, "2026-11-02T23:06Z", "x", "2026-11-02 19:06 x: markdown phase A LANDED with x-cells-mdjson3 (see CELLS-PORT-TARGETS for the files and hashes): html_markdown.spi 372121A33A3C exported as markdown_html, blocks 1/1, parity 111 texts 0 differ, html_test green. Phase B rides x-cells-pb1 (Html.markdown delegates, html.ex 231 -> 181).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_5 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 5u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "x", "2026-11-02 20:12 x: markdown phase B LANDED (unstaged): html.ex 231 -> 181 (E6BC328C6B43). Html.markdown delegates to Domain.markdown_html; markdown_line, markdown_heading, markdown_item, close and inline are deleted, and escape and join stay (used by the notebook page). PROOF x-cells-pb1: html_test and the notebook report suite gave A 67/68 = B 67/68, the one failure an environment Gleam test unrelated to html. html_markdown_parity_test.exs retired (archived in 20261102-200215-cells-retired-parity-tests). The renderer is Spiral now; this item's remaining scope is the dib-parity features in its title.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_6 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 6u32, "2026-11-03T00:28Z", "x", "2026-11-02 20:28 x: DRIFT WATCH (one record per fact): HTML-MODEL (main, html repo) has a typed markdown module (markdown 9 tests, an Inline/Block/Page model, and markup as the only HTML-producing nominal). Cells now has its own html_markdown.spi, a byte-parity port of the old Elixir renderer. Two markdown renderers is a drift risk. Decision rule for this item's next slice: when Cells' page grows past the old renderer's subset (headings, paragraphs, items, fenced code, code spans, links), build it on the html repo's model, linked as a package once that repo is consumable from Cells, and retire html_markdown.spi. Don't extend html_markdown.spi with new syntax. Until then, html_markdown.spi stays frozen at Elixir parity.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_7 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 7u32, "2026-11-03T07:36Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:36 x: DECIDED by main's 11-03 order 7 ('one markdown/render path in html for every project that reimplements it'), replacing n6's 'when the page outgrows' rule. Cells' html_markdown.spi (landed 11-02 as a byte-parity port of the old Elixir renderer) is such a reimplementation, so it MOVES onto the html repo's typed renderer (html/src markdown.spi, inline_parse.spi, render.spi and model.spi; markup is the only HTML-producing nominal). PLAN: (1) Cells links the html package (packageDir/link like the client), and Domain.markdown_html calls html's markdown -> render; (2) parity run, Cells' current output vs html's over the same 111-text corpus. Each difference is judged by RULE: html's output is canonical unless it drops content Cells shows (then the gap goes to an html item), and each accepted difference is listed; (3) html_markdown.spi is deleted after a hash check that its tests are covered by html's markdown tests; (4) html_test and the notebook report suite run A/B. x's own repo has no markdown renderer in Spiral (checked: no x/apps .spi renders markdown), so 'x README/tutorial through html' means this Cells path. Owner x, status Active, next step (1).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_8 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 8u32, "2026-11-03T07:54Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:54 x: PRECONDITION found for n7 step (1). html/src's blocks are all '///> rust' (37), and html/markdown exports a Rust lib, while Cells' domain is Gleam. So Cells can link html/src only after those modules (model, inline_parse, markdown, render) are proven on gleam too. Asked main (owner of HTML-MODEL) whether x adds the gleam targets via drafts or lands them itself. Fallback if html/src must stay Rust-only: a Gleam build of html's markdown entry as its own package. Next step: main's answer, then the node proof of html/src on gleam.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_9 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 9u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:59 x: PLAN AGREED with main (11-03 03:4x; html/src is open to x, no drafts handoff). STATE: not started; waits for the user to start the next autonomous run. EXACT NEXT STEPS: (1) add '///> gleam' beside '///> rust' on html/src's markdown, render, model and inline_parse blocks; prove them on the node with both targets and identical outputs; land them in html/src directly, telling main first if main is editing that file; keep html/src portable, never Rust-only. (2) Cells links html/src (packageDir), and Domain.markdown_html calls html's md. (3) Parity over Cells' 111-text corpus, with html's output canonical and accepted differences listed (n7 rule). (4) Delete Cells' html_markdown.spi after its tests are covered by html's. (5) html_test and the notebook report suite run A/B.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_10 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 10u32, "2026-11-03T09:09Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:09 x: STEP 1 DRAFTED (kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML): model, inline_parse, markdown and render get '///> gleam' beside each '///> rust' (23 blocks; bases model 238DDC32FDA2, inline_parse 7BB8B4C7F262, markdown F47CD05FB1A0, render F55AD0632A25; html/src was last edited 00:07-00:41, so main isn't editing it now). All four are pure Spiral (no host macros, backend switches or globals), so any Gleam difference comes from lib string semantics. PRE-REGISTERED RULE (x-html-gleam1, launched after x-census-cli4 frees the node, because parallel Cells runs share %TEMP%\\spiral_cells): run_notebook --gate over the 4 files on mirror xhtml1 (html with deps/spiral junctioned to the mirror's spiral, junction removed after), with a pinned compiler copy. Pass = exit 0 and every spiral-tests file line shows failed 0, executed = discovered, empty failed_targets and empty mismatches (rust and gleam agree on every block). Then x lands the 4 files directly in html/src (main's 03:4x go-ahead).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_11 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 11u32, "2026-11-03T09:17Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:17 x: STEP 2 phase A DRAFTED (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML; bases package 8E3379568FE5, main AE2FCBD33CDF, domain BBBCE6E60BB1). Cells' package links ../../../../html/src- (Cells has no spiral.toml, checked). main.spi exports html_md = render::md next to the old markdown_html; main opens nothing, so json_value's local 'render' function can't shadow the html render module. domain.ex gains html_md, and html_md_parity_test.exs is new. RULE, set before it runs (after x-html-gleam1 is green and landed): over the same corpus as the old markdown parity (tracked *.md plus lib and apps /// doc text, > 100 texts), html's output must keep EVERY word the Cells renderer showed (tags stripped, entities decoded; 0 texts lose words). Identical and markup-only counts are printed, and markup differences are accepted (html is canonical per n7). Phase B then: markdown_html exports render::md, html_markdown.spi is deleted (its single test is covered by html's markdown tests), and html_test and notebook_report run A/B. Node mirror note: the Cells run needs html beside spiral and html/deps/spiral junctioned, as in launch-html-gleam.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_12 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 12u32, "2026-11-03T09:51Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:51 x: PLAN REVISED by main's reuse decision (HTML-LIB-CANDIDATES n2: HTML escaping is implemented 5 times and markdown parsing 3 times across repos). The markdown model and parser move to lib/spiral/markdown.spi and the markup core to lib/spiral/markup.spi, after html's unit split (HTML-CI-RELEASE); html keeps tree, pages and the CLI. STEP 1 stands and is x's: '///> gleam' beside '///> rust' on html/src markdown, inline_parse, render and model, proven on the node with identical outputs (x-html-gleam1, queued behind x-census-cli5), so the lib module is portable when it moves. STEP 2 CHANGES: Cells depends on lib markdown, NOT ../../../../html/src. The kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML link drafts (package.spiproj, main.spi, domain.ex) are SUPERSEDED. html_md_parity_test.exs is kept as the 111-text content check, pointed at the lib export when it exists. html_markdown.spi is NOT deleted until lib markdown exists and that check passes. Waits on: x-html-gleam1, then main's lib move.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_13 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 13u32, "2026-11-03T09:54Z", "x", "2026-11-03 05:54 x: PLAN RESTORED by main's correction (compiler's lu9 probe, HTML-CI-RELEASE n9/n10: lib-defined types can't cross linked units). The markdown model and parser STAY in html/src; only escape and the markup string builders go to lib/spiral/markup.spi. So n11's step 2 stands as drafted: Cells links ../../../../html/src- as a package (Gleam inlines it; no link: unit). The kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML package.spiproj, main.spi (html_md = render::md) and domain.ex are re-derived from unchanged bases (8E3379568FE5, AE2FCBD33CDF, BBBCE6E60BB1), and html_md_parity_test.exs is kept. Order: x-html-gleam1 (step 1), then the step 2 phase A parity, then phase B (delete html_markdown.spi).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_14 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:08Z", "x", "2026-11-03 06:08 x: STEP 1 SPLIT, coordinating with main (main is editing html/src/markdown.spi for ~40 min on a GFM inline-code continuation bug; x leaves that file alone until main says 'markdown.spi free'). x-html-gleam1 now covers model, inline_parse and render only (bases 238DDC32FDA2, 7BB8B4C7F262, F55AD0632A25). Because render opens markdown, the mirror run restores src/markdown.spi from git HEAD first, so main's in-progress live edit can't leak into the proof. Same pass rule as n10, over 3 files. markdown.spi's gleam targets follow as x-html-gleam2, re-derived from main's new markdown.spi once it's free.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_15 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 15u32, "2026-11-03T10:23Z", "x", "2026-11-03 06:23 x: HELD for main's html unit split. pack24 (LINKED-UNITS-SHARED-INSTANCES) is installed, so main moves model, inline_parse, markdown and render into linked-unit packages (html_model, html_parse, html_render), about 1-2 h. x had nothing in the live tree (drafts only, x-html-gleam1 not launched), so main splits first. RESUME CONDITION: main's message 'html split landed' with the new paths. Then x re-derives the '///> gleam' lines on the moved files, proves them on the current core, re-points the Cells package link to the new packages, and runs the 111-text check. The drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML and spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML are re-derived then, not reused as-is.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_16 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 16u32, "2026-11-03T12:19Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:19 x: RESUMED, main sent 'html split landed' (box build.ps1 green: 49/49 notebooks). Re-derived the gleam targets on the new unit files, mechanically: one '///> gleam' after every '///> rust' and nothing else, LF kept. Drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\<unit>\\: html_model/model.spi 4C3D7D8E0EE8 -> 9D24AA3EB4EC (5 targets), html_inline/inline_parse.spi 150E7022EF02 -> 024661DAFB36 (7), html_table/table.spi A5A12954FF5C -> AE8000C885C4 (2), html_parse/markdown.spi 5F8086EF483E -> E817DC69AB83 (10), html_render/render.spi 7FE9E26D9B22 -> 38B4EE00BE33 (10). The stale flat drafts on src/ bases are deleted (src/ is gone). Launcher guards the five recorded bases. PRE-REGISTERED RULE for x-html-gleam2 (node, pinned core 21D5992EDB80D270 at both ends): run_notebook --gate over the 5 files exits 0, and every 'spiral-tests file' line plus the total show 0 failed with the gleam target present. It is queued after x-sim-maps1 (shared %TEMP%\\spiral_cells). ON GREEN: land the 5 files in a tree-write window, then re-point Cells' package list to ../../../html/html_model, html_inline, html_table, html_parse and html_render (Gleam inlines, so no link:), then run the 111-text no-words-lost check, then delete Cells' html_markdown.spi.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_17 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 17u32, "2026-11-03T12:23Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:23 x: ONE JOB for both steps, saving a sync. x-html-gleam2 also stages the Cells link drafts, re-derived on live bases: spi/package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5 -> 2D52E265AE30 (html/src- replaced by ../../../../html/html_model-, html_inline-, html_table-, html_parse-, html_render-), and main.spi AE2FCBD33CDF + domain.ex BBBCE6E60BB1 + test html_md_parity_test.exs carried over unchanged (additions only: export html_md = render::md). RULE amended from n16: (a) the 5-file gate as n16, AND (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs exits 0 with private MIX_BUILD_PATH, the parity line prints over 100 texts and 0 lose words. Either failing = not green. Green lands html files and Cells files in separate tree-write windows; html_markdown.spi deletion is a follow-up with its own Cells suite run.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_18 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 18u32, "2026-11-03T12:33Z", "x", "2026-11-03 08:33 x: LAND PRECONDITION FOUND (cross-repo dependency). Once Cells' spi/package.spiproj lists ../../../../html/html_*, every Cells build needs (1) a sibling html checkout and (2) html/deps/spiral, which is untracked (deps/ is gitignored) and made by html's scripts/init.ps1 as a link to ../../spiral. The box has it; node mirrors get it from the launchers; but spiral CI's cells-gate job (gh-pages.yml:104, schedule/dispatch, the only CI job that runs Cells) checks out spiral alone, so it would fail. RULE (my technical call, recorded before acting): the Cells-link land also adds one run step to cells-gate before ci_gate.ps1: clone public i574n/html --depth 1 into ../html, then link ../html/deps/spiral to the workspace. No new action, so actions.lock is unchanged. ORDER for the user's push (git stays theirs): html's unit split goes first, because CI clones html main; spiral's Cells link second. The 21:00 report will call this out.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_19 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 19u32, "2026-11-03T13:21Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:21 x: x-html-gleam2's first launch REFUSED itself (exit 3, nothing sent): all five html unit files changed after main's 'split landed' (live 05A634F24CDB model, 86C7C3CD5574 inline_parse, A4C34071C763 table, 2453749FEC40 markdown, CB4DDDAC4A5B render; main's edits, 20-25 min old, still no gleam lines). The draft is a pure function of live (one '///> gleam' after each '///> rust'), so it was re-derived on those bases (gleam 5/7/2/10/10) and relaunched under the same n16/n17 rule. The Cells-side bases were unchanged.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_20 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 20u32, "2026-11-03T13:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:35 x: x-html-gleam2 NOT GREEN (job-1793712096557, 4 min): both halves stopped at the same place, compiling Cells' domain.gleam with the html units linked in (run_notebook builds Cells too). Gleam 'Type mismatch' at domain.gleam:25165: html_parse markdown.spi digit_value calls lib sm'.char_code, which has NO Gleam branch, so the compiler emitted 'let v2 = Nil // backend.backend_switch / record_type_try_find / key: Gleam' and wrapped it as i64. It is the only such site in the 38,019-line file. FIX (draft kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\sm'.spi on base 7C5111CA557E): char_code gains Gleam = string.to_utf_codepoints, then utf_codepoint_to_int of the first (import gleam/string, like starts_with), plus a ///> gleam block (A 65, 7 55, é 233, € 8364, matching the Lua block). Compiler gap reported to compiler: a backend_switch with no branch for the target should be a compile error, not a silent Nil. RULE for x-html-gleam3 = n16 + n17 unchanged; sm'.spi is staged but not gated whole (no block filter, 2,300 lines over many backends); its new branch is proven by markdown's gleam ordered-list tests and the Cells build, and its block by CI cells-gate over lib/spiral.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_21 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 21u32, "2026-11-03T13:52Z", "x", "2026-11-03 09:52 x: x-html-gleam3 NOT GREEN, same error at the same line, because Cells NEVER REGENERATED domain.gleam: its mtime was 09:24 (gleam2), the job started 09:43. ROOT CAUSE, a real Cells staleness bug: spi/domain_compiler.ex inputs_digest hashes only Cells' own spi/*.spi + package.spiproj, so a change in lib/spiral (here sm'.spi) or in any linked package (the html units) leaves the stamp equal and the old domain.gleam is reused. With html linked, every html edit would hit it. FIX drafted (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\domain_compiler.ex, base 730499FEFF4F): the digest also covers lib/spiral/*.spi|*.spiproj and every relative '..' package listed in package.spiproj (the five html units), sanity-checked with elixir -e against the draft spiproj. It proves itself on the node: the new digest differs from gleam2's stamp, so domain.gleam regenerates with the sm' Gleam branch. Relaunched as x-html-gleam4; rule n16+n17 unchanged, plus 'domain.gleam regenerated' (no Nil backend_switch site).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_22 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 22u32, "2026-11-03T14:18Z", "x", "2026-11-03 10:18 x: x-html-gleam4 NOT GREEN but the digest fix WORKED: domain.gleam regenerated (10:03, after the job start). It still had the Nil site and no to_utf_codepoints, because the html units resolved lib/spiral through the node mirror's html\\deps\\spiral, a 06:12 junction to the NODE's own C:\\home\\git\\spiral (old sm'), not the mirror's spiral where the fix was staged. The launcher kept any existing junction. With two 'spiral-' packages from two dirs, the compile silently used the html side's. FIX (launcher): always point deps\\spiral at the mirror's spiral for the run, print it, then restore the previous target. Same fix applied to launch-cells-mpath. Relaunched as x-html-gleam5. LAND NOTE: on the box html\\deps\\spiral -> C:\\home\\git\\spiral, the same tree as Cells' lib, so the landed link is consistent. Compiler (BACKEND-SWITCH-MISSING-ARM) now turns a missing Gleam arm into a compile-time FatalError, so char_code's Gleam branch is required, not optional.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_23 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 23u32, "2026-11-03T15:00Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:00 x: x-html-gleam5 (job-1793715425398, core 21D5992EDB80D270 at both ends, deps\\spiral forced to the mirror). (a) GREEN: run_notebook --gate over the 5 html units passed 34/34 (model 5, inline_parse 7, table 2, markdown 10, render 10), every block on both targets, 0 failed. LANDED unstaged in one tree-write window, hash-guarded: html model 61F9EE595DFC, inline_parse 6D6441DC13F2, table 2ABEE99FB256, markdown 7E617F36F7CA, render C4E9EE868198 (gleam lines only); spiral lib sm'.spi 25191F00112C -> ECDE2F868B0A (char_code Gleam arm + gleam block, re-applied in-window onto live because sm' moved twice under other lanes; the hunks are independent); Cells spi/domain_compiler.ex 730499FEFF4F -> 5F49EDA9CCA1 (a standalone fix, proven by gleam4/gleam5 regenerating after a lib change). main told to re-read the html files. (b) NOT GREEN by the n17 rule: Cells built with html linked and html_test passed, but parity was '120 texts, 106 identical, 3 markup-only differences, 11 lose words'. The first loss is the old renderer leaking raw syntax as words (<https://..> autolinks, ---, '](url)' fragments, '![' badge text) plus image alt text, which moves into an attribute the word compare strips. PRE-REGISTERED v2 RULE (before seeing the other 10): test draft 0D370EC83183 normalizes tokens symmetrically (trim <>[]()! and a leading '](', drop pure-syntax tokens like - --- #), counts alt and href values as words, and prints EVERY raw loss and every normalized loss. Pass = 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. Any normalized loss is a real html bug to fix, not a test tweak.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_24 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 24u32, "2026-11-03T16:35Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:35 x: CORRECTION to n18's CI step. Per main's ORG-EXIT-FALLOUT 06:4x note, workflow YAML is GENERATED from the typed source i574n.github/scripts/pins/workflows.spi and checked by pins.ps1 (lanes hand-editing YAML were flagged). So the html-beside step is now drafted THERE (kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML\\workflows.spi, base 6BA6BEAB608D -> AD7EBEEC08CE): a new ':~ html_beside () : step' (named run step: clone public i574n/html --depth 1 into ../html, mkdir ../html/deps, symlink deps/spiral to the workspace), modeled on transformer_model_cache, inserted in spiral_cells_gate_job before the ci_gate.ps1 run. The hand-edited gh-pages.yml draft is DELETED (my own draft, superseded). At land: the workflows.spi edit plus pins.ps1 regenerating spiral's gh-pages.yml, so source and YAML agree; the push order stays html first, spiral second.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_25 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 25u32, "2026-11-03T16:37Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:37 x: LOST HUNK. Live lib sm'.spi was rewritten at 10:51:01 back to 25191F00112C (kebab_of kept), dropping my 10:3x merge (ECDE2F868B0A: char_code's Gleam arm and gleam block). The html files and domain_compiler.ex from the same window survived. Likely another lane's land restoring a pre-merge copy without a base guard. EFFECT: html markdown's gleam blocks cannot compile until the arm returns (compiler's BACKEND-SWITCH-MISSING-ARM now names the missing arm at compile time). PLAN agreed with compiler: their C/Zig/Pascal char_code land (job compiler-lib-land-charcode on 25191F00) closes first, then x re-applies the Gleam arm and block on their hash, in-window and anchor-checked.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_26 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 26u32, "2026-11-03T16:58Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:58 x: ROOT CAUSE of n25 (found by main): not a direct edit. kit's first LIB-KEBAB-CASE land snapshotted sm'.spi at 10:30:17 (undo 20261103-103017, DA64EBB2), before x's 10:38 arm. Its close failed on tibia's file_system break (~10:42), and Restore-Undo copied that snapshot back, dropping the arm; kit's second land (undo 104911) then added kebab_of, giving 25191F00. main fixed Restore-Undo (unstaged: it skips any file whose live hash differs from what that land wrote); the two-windows-at-once cause went to kit. x's side: the html launcher now refuses to run until live sm' has the arm (precondition exit 6), and the re-land waits for compiler's charcode land as agreed.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_27 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 27u32, "2026-11-03T17:04Z", "x", "2026-11-03 13:04 x: RE-LANDED the lost hunk on compiler's base: sm'.spi 5DCCAED9654570E9 -> 343FA552B65A03D7 in a tree-write window (land-sm-gleam.ps1: base-guarded, both anchors matched once). char_code's Gleam arm now sits after compiler's new C/Zig/Pascal arms, and the gleam block sits after of_char's gleam block. Spacing canonical (no double blank, single final LF). The html launcher precondition (exit 6) now passes; x-html-gleam6 is clear to run when the node queue reaches it.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_28 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 28u32, "2026-11-05T13:36Z", "x", "2026-11-05 10:4x x: RESUMED (last note 11-03 13:04; x-html-gleam6 never ran). STATE CHECKED on live: step 1 HOLDS (every html unit block carries ///> gleam beside ///> rust: model 2/2, inline_parse 7/7, table 2/2, markdown 10/10, render 10/10; sm' char_code's Gleam arm present; domain_compiler.ex's digest fix landed 11-03). The html-side drafts in kit drafts html\\CELLS-ON-HTML are leftovers of that land (stale against main's later html edits; nothing to land). STEP 2 (Cells links html) RE-DERIVED on today's live bases, additions only, in kit drafts spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML-V2: spi/main.spi FEA05BF571A2 -> BCB5FAA7D396 (export html_md = render::md, after markdown_html); lib/spiral/cells/domain.ex 7C588CAFE420 -> 2741CCA8542A (def html_md); spi/package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5 -> 2D52E265AE30 (base unchanged: links ../../../../html/html_model-, html_inline-, html_table-, html_parse-, html_render-); test html_md_parity_test.exs 0D370EC83183 (the v2 rule of n20, unchanged). Launcher launch-cells-html-link (mirror xhtml1, repos html + spiral, deps\\spiral forced to the mirror's spiral, pinned compiler): (a) run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets; (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs. RULE unchanged (n16, n17, n20 v2): (a) exit 0 with 0 failed in every file line; (b) exit 0 and 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. Any normalized loss is a real html bug. QUEUED after x-next9b and x-next10, because node Cells runs share %TEMP%\\spiral_cells.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_29 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 29u32, "2026-11-05T22:36Z", "x", "2026-11-05 18:3x x: LAUNCHED x-cells-html1 (job-1793918120731, node, mirror xhtml1, repos html + spiral, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0). Cells-side drafts CELLS-ON-HTML-V2, bases verified live at launch: main.spi FEA05BF571A2, domain.ex 7C588CAFE420, package.spiproj 8E3379568FE5; sm' char_code Gleam arm present. Note: Cells now also carries today's import-order fix (dependencies before the notebook's own package). Pre-registered rule unchanged (n28): (a) run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets, 0 failed; (b) mix test html_md_parity_test.exs + html_test.exs exit 0 with 0 normalized word losses over > 100 texts. It runs concurrently with x-next10's successor x-next11 (other mirror). A shared-%TEMP% flake would show as a runtime red in both, so any red gets a rerun before it counts. PENDING.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_30 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 30u32, "2026-11-05T23:28Z", "x", "2026-11-05 19:3x x: x-cells-html1 RESULT (job-1793918120731, node, mirror xhtml1, pinned pack52 core A688EE5AD426A5A0), RED by the pre-registered rule, NOT landed. (a) GREEN: run_notebook --gate over the 5 live html units, both targets: model 2/2, inline_parse 7/7, table 2/2, markdown 10/10, render 10/10 = 31/31, so Cells builds with html linked and the html Gleam path holds. (b) RED: html_md_parity_test (v2 rule 0D370EC83183) over 127 texts: 115 identical, 9 raw-token losses (normalized away, accepted), 7 TEXTS LOSE WORDS after normalization. html_test passed (7/8 total; the parity test is the 1 failure). First losing text: the spiral README head ('# spiral', '<https://i574n.github.io/spiral>' autolink, '[![badge alt](badge.svg)](url)' image-in-link, '---', a nested '- [Getting Started](#...)' list). By the n20 rule each normalized loss is a real html renderer bug to fix in html, not a test tweak. NEXT: list all 7 losing texts with their lost words (run the parity test locally or print every loss) and file them on the html side (owner main / HTML-REPO), then rerun this exact job (launch-cells-html-link, drafts CELLS-ON-HTML-V2 unchanged).")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_31 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 31u32, "2026-11-06T08:20Z", "x", "2026-11-06 04:2x x: NEXT STEP (CPU-light): reduce the 7 word-losing texts from x-cells-html1 to minimal markdown repros (the first is the spiral README: an autolink, and an image inside a link, the badge shape). File each as an html renderer test row under NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER. Then rerun launch-cells-html-link unchanged on the node.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_32 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 32u32, "2026-11-08T21:30Z", "microblog", "GROOM 11-08: Active -> Paused (untouched since 11-06; a stale Active is invisible to agile next, per KIT-AGILE-STALE-ACTIVE). Its CPU-light next step stays: reduce the 7 word-losing texts to minimal markdown repros for NOTEBOOK-HTML-RENDERER. Resume when the run pulls it or CELLS-PORT-TARGETS needs it.")
inl note_of_cells_html_parity_33 () = Note ("CELLS-HTML-PARITY", 33u32, "2026-11-09T18:32Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:5x microblog: Paused -> Planned and RE-SCOPED (the 11-06 next step's premise changed). The 7 word-losing texts (x-cells-html1, 11-05) were losses of the HTML REPO's renderer (Domain.html_md) against Cells' old renderer (Domain.markdown_html), in the html-linking candidate that never landed (draft drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-ON-HTML-V2\\html_md_parity_test.exs). Cells then moved to LIB's Markdown instead (CELLS-MD-ON-LIB: html_markdown.spi markdown = markup_text (doc_render::blocks_html (doc_parse::parse_markdown source))), and lib's doc_inline already handles the first losing text's shapes, with tests: '<https://...>' autolinks (an_angle_url_autolinks_and_other_angles_stay_text, doc_inline.spi:170) and an image inside a link, the badge (LinkedImage, an_image_and_a_badge_link, :159). So reducing html-renderer repros is obsolete. The live question: does LIB's renderer keep every word the OLD Cells renderer showed over the same 127 texts (tracked *.md plus lib/apps /// doc text)? next: rerun the v2 parity test with Domain.html_md pointed at the lib markdown export (old = HEAD's markdown_html), on a node, under the same pre-registered rule (raw-token losses accepted; any normalized word loss gets a minimal repro filed as a lib doc_* test row). dod: 0 normalized word losses vs the old renderer, or each loss filed as a lib test row and fixed. typefeatures: none new.")
inl pages_notebook_publish () = Task ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", "Publish each .spi's Cells notebook pages (.html/.ipynb/.livemd, test outputs included) on every repo's Pages site, and link each .spi in the tree to its page (user 11-01 14:0x: 'where's the .html files generated for each spi file in gh actions, including the outputs of tests cells etc?')", Story, P1, D3, 80u32, Planned, "-", "pages,ci,cells,dir-tree-html,all-repos,user-batch,main,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pages_notebook_publish () = Planning ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_1 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:38Z", "main", "main 14:4x: the old gitlab-i574n Pages branch (a28af39c) had 47 .dib.html and 43 .dib.ipynb; the live spiral gh-pages (3464d8e9, 11-01) has none. Cause: spiral's CI builds lib/spiral/*.spi.ipynb/.html/.livemd, but publish.ps1 calls publish-tree.ps1 without -Include, which copies only tracked files, and the notebook outputs are untracked. Tibia writes notebooks/ (gitignored) and only uploads it as an artifact; fiscal publishes only data. This unblocks NOTEBOOK-LIVEMD-EXPORT. DoD: on spiral's, tibia's and fiscal's deployed sites every .spi with tests has a notebook page reachable from its tree entry, and the page shows test outputs (failed runs too, after CELLS-HTML-PARITY). Generated pages stay out of git (no committed outputs). Typefeatureset: publish-tree's include set is a typed list of generated-artifact kinds (NotebookHtml | Ipynb | Livemd), not glob strings per repo.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_2 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 2u32, "2026-11-01T20:13Z", "main", "main 16:1x LANDED unstaged in spiral/scripts: publish.ps1 now calls publish-tree with -Include '*.spi.html,*.spi.ipynb,*.spi.livemd' -Exclude 'target,_build,node_modules'. These are the files lib/spiral/build.ps1:31 writes next to each notebook in CI. publish-tree.ps1 splits comma lists, because 'pwsh script.ps1 -Include a,b' doesn't pass arrays (memory ps-file-array-args); the 11 other repos' callers use & with arrays or no -Include, so they're unaffected. Dry selection on the box: 0 extras (notebooks are generated only in CI; the 4 under target/ are excluded). In dir-tree-html, write_viewers now skips a file whose x.ext.html already exists, so a Cells notebook page is never overwritten by a plain viewer, and with --viewers a .spi links to its notebook page. PROOF PENDING: spiral's first CI run after the user's push must show lib/spiral/<nb>.spi.html entries on the Pages tree (main checks per verify-ci-after-user-push). Next: tibia (notebooks/ is gitignored and only uploaded as an artifact) and fiscal get the same -Include once proven; HTML is written only when every test passes, until CELLS-HTML-PARITY.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_3 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 3u32, "2026-11-02T00:12Z", "main", "main 20:1x PROVEN ON CI: spiral's first push with the change (b3fd292b, run 41359205750, green on both OSes) deployed gh-pages df762c5c at 19:55 local with 96 notebook files under lib/spiral (and apps/wasm): .spi.html, .spi.ipynb and .spi.livemd, e.g. lib/spiral/am'.spi.html, async.spi.html, base.spi.html. The old gitlab Pages had 47 .dib.html; we're past parity on count. Left: (1) tibia and fiscal adopt the same -Include (tibia writes notebooks/ and only uploads it as an artifact today); (2) the tree page links each .spi to its notebook page (needs the --viewers renderer, i.e. the pin move or the new html repo); (3) failed-run pages (CELLS-HTML-PARITY: HTML is written only when every test passes).")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_4 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 4u32, "2026-11-02T00:22Z", "tibia-lane", "11-01 20:2x tibia: DONE on tibia's side (unstaged): scripts/publish.ps1's publish-tree call now includes '*.spi.html', '*.spi.ipynb', '*.spi.livemd' beside '*.tsv', '*.txt', '*.lua', '*.xml'. Checked before editing: (1) gh-pages.yml runs publish.ps1 on the ubuntu job after both build steps, so notebooks/ is populated by then; (2) publish-tree collects extras with 'git ls-files --others' WITHOUT --exclude-standard, so the gitignored notebooks/ files are listed (the same path spiral's run 41359205750 used); (3) unlike spiral's call, NO '-Exclude target': tibia's site publishes the compiled arena Lua from target/arena/*.lua, and excluding target would drop those pages. Parse check clean. Verifiable on the first tibia Pages build after the user's next push: notebooks/*.spi.html pages on the site.")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_5 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 5u32, "2026-11-05T22:42Z", "main", "main 11-05 18:4x CHECK of left item (1): tibia adopted (n of 11-01 20:2x). fiscal has NOT: fiscal/scripts/publish.ps1:3 -Include lists '*.xml','*.txt','*.tsv','*.json','reports.md' only, while its gitignored notebooks/ holds corpus.spi.html/.ipynb etc. Asked fiscal to add the three notebook patterns (or let main do it unstaged). (2) tree-page links still wait on the renderer (HTML-CI-RELEASE, user); (3) failed-run pages unchanged (CELLS-HTML-PARITY).")
inl note_of_pages_notebook_publish_6 () = Note ("PAGES-NOTEBOOK-PUBLISH", 6u32, "2026-11-05T23:00Z", "main", "main 11-05 19:0x: fiscal DONE (unstaged): scripts/publish.ps1 -Include adds '*.spi.html','*.spi.ipynb','*.spi.livemd', like tibia; gh-pages.yml already runs publish.ps1 after build.ps1 writes notebooks/ (19 html + 19 ipynb today). Unverified until fiscal's next gh-pages run. Left (1) is done for both consumers; (2) tree-page links still wait on the renderer (HTML-CI-RELEASE), (3) failed-run pages on CELLS-HTML-PARITY.")
inl x_dumps () = Task ("X-DUMPS", "x-dumps: complete linear X/Twitter histories (tweets, replies, quotes, RTs, media) for the user's accounts via public archives, deterministic cached pipeline (xarchive) on the Cloud VM", Feature, P1, D5, 90u32, Active, "", "management,team:org,xarchive,europa,wayback", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_dumps () = Planning ("X-DUMPS", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_dumps_1 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T18:39Z", "personal", "personal lane, 2026-11-01 14:39 -04. User: complete, linear history (tweets, replies, quotes, RTs, media) of geygey_hadid, juhondacivic, i574n, fc1943s, stewshka; deterministic re-runnable pipeline with cache strategies; more accounts later; iterate for days on every source. Line held: no bypassing access controls or rate limits/bot checks.\nSources measured 11-01: the Archive Team stream grab is access-restricted (401, torrent too). SN13 HF names are Fernet-encrypted. enryu43 (88M tweets, max 1,001 per user, to 2023-04) and Common Crawl have none of the 5. Wayback CDX: geygey_hadid 39,265 status IDs (2020-2022, captured as posted), fc1943s 625, the other 3 only profile pages. X embed endpoint cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result works; geygey tweets are tombstoned, so Wayback is their only copy. fc1943s and i574n are ONE account (id 72784485, renamed).\nPipeline: Rust crate C:\\home\\git\\xarchive (stages cdx|profile|snaps|live|media|timeline, plus `run` with parallel per-host lanes), on europa at /home/remote/xarchive, data /data/x. Immutable HTTP cache by sha256(url), refreshable CDX/profile, accumulating profile_ids, per-host rate halving on 429, 5xx backoff from 5 s, --max budget. First full run started 18:37:56Z (pid 42723, /data/x/logs/run-20261101T183756Z.log).\nNext: watch the run; add sources (Wayback API-JSON captures, archive.today read-only, media im_ captures); ask the user for their own X archive export for i574n/stewshka.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_2 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T19:04Z", "personal", "personal 15:05 -04. BREAKTHROUGH: Wayback holds X API v2 JSON tweet objects ({\"data\":{...},\"includes\":{media,tweets,users}}) stored under twitter.com/<h>/status/<id> with CDX statuscode \"-\" and mime application/json (an API-recording archiver, 2023-2026). My first count kept only status 200 and missed them. Real unique IDs: juhondacivic 31,596 (2024-2026, was 8), stewshka 1,386 (was 0), i574n 844 (was 2), fc1943s 3,331 (was 625), geygey_hadid ~39k (HTML-era 2020-2022). xarchive now ranks JSON captures first and parses api_v2: note_tweet full text, referenced_tweets (reply/quote/retweet), the tweet's own attachments.media_keys with the best mp4, the RT original's full text, and author_id for identity. Trap recorded: CDX line counts from curl without a status check counted IA's 14-line error page as 14 captures. Relaunched run pid 43898 (/data/x/logs/run-20261101T190234Z.log), both lanes parallel at 0.5 rps each; first full pass ~42 h for ~76k IDs; reruns hit the cache.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_3 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 3u32, "2026-11-01T19:19Z", "personal", "personal heartbeat 15:2x -04: the run is relaunched at --rps 1 after 30+ min with 0 backoffs at 0.5 (pid 48663, /data/x/logs/run-20261101T191832Z.log), which halves the ~90 h first pass for 162.7k IDs (geygey_hadid alone 125,541 once CDX \"-\" captures count). Tombstones are now kept (tombstones.ndjson; the timeline marks archive-only tweets \"now on X: This Post was deleted by the Post author\"); verified that the \"gone\" bursts are real author deletions, not soft throttling. Dead source: syndication timeline-profile returns 200 with 0 entries to logged-out requests.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_4 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 4u32, "2026-11-01T19:55Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:0x -04: dropped the dep on REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER, a false dependency from filing time. xarchive runs over plain `ssh europa` and never needed kit's Linux integration; the dep hid X-DUMPS from `agile next --team x-dumps`. The lane is renamed personal → x-dumps (teams.txt ec368ac8=x-dumps; tags team:x-dumps on X-DUMPS and K3S-EVERYWHERE).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_5 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 5u32, "2026-11-01T20:12Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:2x -04: renamed accounts merge into one history. timeline writes accounts/<h>/account.json (account_id, aliases); the new `merge` stage (also the last step of `run`) groups handles by account_id into people/<account_id>/timeline.{ndjson,md}: union by tweet id, fields filled from whichever handle has them, sources unioned, media paths rewritten to accounts/<h>/media/... First result: people/72784485 = @fc1943s + @i574n, 1,588 entries so far (594 tweets, 948 RTs, 38 replies, 8 quotes) from 2019-11-07. Markdown rendering is one function shared by both stages. The pod is restarted on the new binary.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_6 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 6u32, "2026-11-01T20:20Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 16:4x -04: new `context` stage (X lane, after live): fetches the tweets the account's replies and quotes point at (minus its own ids) through the embed endpoint, cached immutable, into context.ndjson (text/author, or the removal reason). The timeline shows \"[reply to @x: <parent snippet 120 chars>]\", records reply_text, and fills quote text/author when the archive record lacked it. First slice on fc1943s: 128 referenced, 59 alive, 2 gone. The pod is rolled to the new binary.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_7 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 7u32, "2026-11-01T20:48Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 17:0x -04 PARKED USER DECISIONS (asked in chat, not answered yet): (1) if i574n/fc1943s (one account, id 72784485) and stewshka are the user's own, request X's \"Download an archive of your data\" (Settings > Your account). It is the only complete source incl. media and deleted posts; xarchive can merge it as another source keyed by tweet id. (2) X API pay-per-use hydration (~$0.005/post read) for ids whose text no archive has: needs the user's developer account + credits. (3) Accounts juhondacivic and geygey_hadid: confirm they are the user's own or public figures (the harvest uses public archives only either way). Status: fc1943s complete (3,331 ids: 2,619 API-JSON + 622 HTML archive records, 90 without text; 1,746 now deleted on X); i574n/stewshka in progress; 0 backoffs at 1 rps.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_8 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 8u32, "2026-11-01T21:57Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:0x -04: identity is per account id, not per handle. A handle can be held by several accounts over time. Holder = an author_id with a record authored as @handle, or the author of an HTML-era snapshot whose og:url is under the handle (that is how the rename is caught). Everything else archived under the handle is kind \"mention\" (kept in the handle view, excluded from people). Each record gets account_id = its author if a holder, else the holder nearest in tweet-id time. merge groups by account_id. Result: people = 72784485 (@fc1943s → renamed @i574n, 1,901 records), 1637273636049498112 (a later holder of @fc1943s, ~2023-03, 1,662 records; whose it is is a user question), 1819115209170276352 (@stewshka, 1,671). 1,408 records from ~90 lookalike bot accounts named fc1943seth<NNNNN> (a crypto-scam pattern) are mentions now, not \"people\". Also: the `run` pipeline is per account (a finisher thread runs media+timeline+merge as each account's two lanes finish); media for fc1943s 661 files, i574n 444, stewshka 146. juhondacivic: every tweet is gone on X (3,200/3,200 checked so far), so the archive is the only copy.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_9 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 9u32, "2026-11-01T22:42Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:4x -04: display-name/handle history added (all from cache). Records carry author_name (API includes.users name; HTML og:title \"<name> on Twitter\"), and the timeline emits \"(display name: X @handle)\" on each change. Name and handle are trusted ONLY from captures within 30 days of posting: an old tweet captured years later carries the later name (first version flapped fc1943s<->i574n). Result for 72784485: Filipe Carvalho @fc1943s (2019) → fc1943s.eth (2023-01-21) → fc1943s.near (2023-02-10) → prima (2023-04-04) → @i574n@renkontu.com → i574n @i574n. Evidence for the open user question: the later @fc1943s holder 1637273636049498112 displayed the name \"stewshka\" on 2024-08-06, and @stewshka (1819115209170276352) shows \"stewshka\" → \"cute little stew\", so the later @fc1943s is very likely the user's too (needs the user's confirmation). Also: the run now has two archive lanes at 0.5 req/s each (total IA rate unchanged at 1 req/s; overlaps IA latency; expected ~+40%, to be measured).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_10 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 10u32, "2026-11-01T22:52Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 18:5x -04: the archive lanes are now SHARDS, not per-account: lane k of 2 takes every 2nd capture id of EVERY account (snaps writes wayback.<k>of2.ndjson; timeline/media/context read all wayback*.ndjson through cdx::wayback_lines; a pre-shard wayback.ndjson is renamed .superseded). Per-account lanes would leave one lane idle once juhondacivic (31.6k) finished while geygey_hadid (125.5k) crawled at 0.5 req/s. Total IA rate is unchanged at 1 req/s (0.5 per shard); sharding only overlaps IA latency. The finisher fires after both shards + the X lane report an account. Verified: both shards on juhondacivic (15,798 each), 0 backoffs.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_11 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 11u32, "2026-11-01T23:09Z", "x-dumps", "GROOM 19:1x (x-dumps). DoD: for every requested account, one linear history per ACCOUNT ID (people/<id>/timeline.{ndjson,md}) holding every tweet/reply/quote/RT any public source still has, with media files, reply/quote context, X removal reasons and display-name history; an archive pass complete for all 5 handles; a reproducible `xarchive run` + off-VM backup. Next: let the sharded run finish juhondacivic (~31.6k) and geygey_hadid (~125.5k, HTML era, ~1.5-2 days); then the user's X archive export as an extra source (parked decision). Typefeatureset plan (honest gap): xarchive is Rust with stringly kinds (\"tweet\"/\"reply\"/\"quote\"/\"retweet\"/\"mention\") and source names. Port path: the record model becomes a Spiral package like k3s_manifest, with a GADT `entry k` indexed by provenance (live/api/html) so a kind_inferred reply can't pass as a live-attested one; the HTTP/cache boundary stays a thin host module. Not before the first full pass (behaviour frozen first).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_12 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 12u32, "2026-11-01T23:32Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:3x -04: MEASURED sharding effect (IA fetches per 10 min from cache .meta stamps): 400-452 before → 525-566 after (+25-35%), against the 600 ceiling (1 req/s total, unchanged). 0 backoffs, 0 pod restarts. Also: timeline.html per account and per person (inline images/videos, light/dark, all text escaped): people/72784485 = 513 dates, 1,901 entries, 475 images, 35 videos.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_13 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 13u32, "2026-11-01T23:40Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 19:4x -04: lost tweets are now kept instead of dropped. When every archived capture of an id fails (HTTP 404 replay, an API {\"errors\"} body, or a page without tweet text), snaps emits kind \"lost\" with the reason and the first capture timestamp, and the timeline shows \"(not archived: <reason>)\" at the tweet's exact time (snowflake), next to the X removal reason when known. Effect on finished accounts: fc1943s +83, i574n +40, stewshka +61 entries that silently vanished before (212 \"archive returned HTTP 404\", 8 \"no tweet text\"). People now: 72784485 = 1,950, 1637273636049498112 = 1,736, 1819115209170276352 = 1,732. Also fixed this hour: the europa sshd PerSourcePenalties lockout of our home IP (see the europa memory).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_14 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 14u32, "2026-11-02T00:47Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 20:4x -04: merge also writes people/<id>/summary.md: a per-year table by kind (tweet/reply/quote/retweet/lost), the date span, media files, entries gone from X today, lost entries. First reading: 72784485 (@fc1943s→@i574n) = 1,950 entries 2019-11-07..2026-06-12, 510 media files over 448 entries, 258 gone from X (archive-only), 49 lost; NO 2022 entries at all (an archive gap or a real pause: ask the user). Also re-ran lost-tweet retry at depth 12 on the 3 finished accounts: no new recoveries (lost ids have <=4 captures).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_15 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 15u32, "2026-11-02T05:49Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 01:48 -05 (11-02): juhondacivic X-lane COMPLETE: 31,596/31,596 ids are gone on X (0 alive), the account is fully wiped, and the archive is its only copy. The archive shards are at 13,300/15,798 each (~84%, 0 rate-limit backoffs; one transient IA 504 recovered in 10 s). The X lane moved on to geygey_hadid (125,541 ids). context for juhondacivic ran before its shards were written (referenced=0); the next hourly pass recomputes it from cache. Backups: 2 verified archives in C:\\home\\xdumps (newest x-20261102T042837Z, 934 MB).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_16 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 16u32, "2026-11-02T07:40Z", "x-dumps", "x-dumps 03:4x -05 (11-02): the xarchive pod was OOMKilled once at 512Mi (07:18Z, exit 137, xarchive rss 500 MB), right when juhondacivic's finisher ran: timeline held 4 full serde_json::Value maps + all records as Values. Nothing was lost (the cache resumed). Fixes: (1) the pod limit is now 1536Mi (bootstrap yaml backup .before-mem); (2) timeline/merge hold outputs as serialized JSON lines and parse one at a time in render/render_html/summary (merge parses only to absorb duplicates); timeline inputs are slimmed to the 17 fields it reads. REGRESSION on a frozen copy of 4 accounts: old vs new outputs BYTE-IDENTICAL (32 files); peak RSS juhondacivic timeline 279→172 MB, merge 312→89 MB, fc1943s 40→25 MB. Projected geygey_hadid (4x) peak ~0.7 GB timeline, ~0.35 GB merge, under the new limit. Steady-state run rss ~308 MB, flat (no leak).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_17 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 17u32, "2026-11-02T11:13Z", "x-dumps", "juhondacivic context complete 11:04Z: 13,432 referenced parents (9,139 still on X, 4,293 gone), 12,247 s. Finisher now in media: ~5,322 files, ~300 cached, ~0.23 files/s (~6 h), then timeline + merge (peak ~172 MB measured). geygey_hadid at 6,100/62,770 snaps per shard, live 5,600/125,541 all gone. media.rs gained a progress line (built at the next free europa slot). kit-worker on TRAIN10 held past the old 10-min idle exit.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_18 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 18u32, "2026-11-02T11:34Z", "x-dumps", "media progress line deployed 11:29Z (build 13 s at the free slot, rollout ok). juhondacivic media: ~63% of the first 1,600 files missing on both live twimg and Wayback im_. Strategy ruled out: wildcard CDX for pbs media variants found 0/5 sampled misses (never archived). IA flapped Temporarily Offline once during the probe.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_19 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 19u32, "2026-11-02T12:32Z", "x-dumps", "juhondacivic COMPLETE 12:28Z: person 1450651554340642818, 31,596 entries 2023-04-05..2026-06-25 (tweet 8,356, reply 13,677, quote 3,463, retweet 4,841, lost 1,259), all gone from X so the archive is the only copy. Media 2,920 files across 2,801 entries (live twimg 2,193, Wayback 127, cached 600; 2,402 unrecoverable), 3.1 GB, 3,439 s. Finisher peak RSS 558 MB (limit 1536Mi). 4 of 5 test accounts done; geygey_hadid at 8,000/62,770 snaps per shard, 10,400/125,541 live (all gone). Next backup grows to ~4.5 GB (media barely compresses; box has 265 GB free).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_20 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 20u32, "2026-11-02T14:33Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:28 x: DECISION RECORD v2 (user 11-02: 'why mine? try healing autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset/backups the pending decisions'). Only git commits (the user's standing rule) and the harness hook config (the harness itself says Claude must not change it) stay user-only. Everything else gets a precondition, a check and an undo; actions only the user's accounts can take get a non-blocking default. kind=ByDefault. RULE: Default: proceed on what agents can reach (public data, existing dumps). The archive import stage is ready and tested on a synthetic archive, so a user-requested export drops in later with no rework. Nothing blocks. BACKUP/UNDO: No state change. OWNER: x-dumps.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_21 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 21u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "x-dumps", "Session 11-02 progress + groom: (a) multi-node sharding shipped in xarchive: archive <handle> --shards N --set a,b + env XARCHIVE_SHARDS/XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET; cdx::clear_stale_shards drops wayback files of a different N so readers never double-count. Index-based partition = same ids on any box with the same captures.tsv, so two boxes split one account with zero overlap. (b) iggy event stream live: xarchive publishes progress/stage/backoff as JSON to x-dumps/harvest (send+poll-only user); consumer pod xarchive-follow writes /data/x/status.tsv. (c) 2nd harvest node ready: pluto (ssh rustdesk); a systemd-run MemoryMax=450M worker proven safe there (peak ~5MB on i574n, RustDesk untouched, different IP = independent archive.org rate limit). PENDING: reshard sjc to --set 0,1 + launch pluto --set 2,3 on geygey_hadid + merge pluto shard files before the finisher. (d) juhondacivic COMPLETE (31,596); only geygey_hadid still downloading.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_22 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 22u32, "2026-11-02T15:04Z", "x-dumps", "Parked user decisions refresh 11-02: RESOLVED - the X Download-an-archive export is now a NON-BLOCKING default (x lane DECISION RECORD v2, star exchange 11-02): proceed on reachable data, the export drops into the ready import stage later; nothing waits on it. STILL OPEN: (a) who owns later @fc1943s id 1637273636049498112, and juhondacivic / geygey_hadid; (b) codespaces 32gb devcontainer fix; (c) 72784485 zero 2022 entries = pause or archive gap. NEW user step via main: IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD TLS listener yes/no (rustdesk key step already done by the user).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_23 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 23u32, "2026-11-02T15:15Z", "x-dumps", "Second-node harvest LIVE 11-02 15:1x (user: resume downloads on the rustdesk node). geygey_hadid now split disjointly across two IPs: sjc pod shards 0,1 of 4 (kubectl set env XARCHIVE_SHARDS=4 XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET=0,1), pluto systemd unit xdumps-geygey shards 2,3 of 4 (systemd-run MemoryMax=450M/SwapMax=0/CPUQuota=70%/Restart=on-failure, writing /root/xdata). Both cdx agree (captures=147460 ids=125541) so the index split is disjoint+complete. pluto RSS 251MB under the 450 cap, RustDesk ports up, 365MB free. One-time overlap: pluto refetches ~11k ids sjc already cached (different IP, rate-limited, acceptable). MERGE when both done: scp pluto /root/xdata/accounts/geygey_hadid/wayback.{2,3}of4.ndjson -> sjc /data/x/accounts/geygey_hadid/, then on sjc run media+timeline+merge for geygey (finisher reads all 4 wayback shards via wayback_lines). sjc auto-finisher may run a PARTIAL geygey (shards 0,1) first; the post-merge rerun fixes it. NOTE drift: live sjc deploy has the XARCHIVE env via set env; the stored /home/remote/k3s/xarchive.yaml must get the same env baked in or a future apply reverts sjc to all-shards.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_24 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 24u32, "2026-11-02T22:54Z", "x-dumps", "REGRESSION found + FIXED 11-02 (self-inflicted). Resharding the sjc `run` to XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET=0,1 applied to ALL 5 accounts in the run loop, not just geygey. The 4 COMPLETED accounts (fc1943s/i574n/stewshka/juhondacivic) only had shards 0,1 fetched (nothing runs their 2,3 - pluto does only geygey), and cdx::clear_stale_shards renamed their full of2 wayback files to .superseded, so their people timelines HALVED (juhondacivic 31,596 -> 15,798). It hid: the backup and the byte-identical cache-rerun test both \"passed\" on the corrupted-vs-corrupted data. CAUGHT by a data-integrity count check. FIX: (1) run command now geygey_hadid ONLY (completed accounts out of the loop, cannot be re-sharded); (2) restored each completed account from cache (rm sharded wayback files, re-run snaps shards=1 full + timeline, merge) - all back to full counts, no refetch (cache intact). LESSON: never apply a shard SUBSET to a run that includes complete accounts without a complementary worker for the other shards; a shard env is global to the run loop. The 17:11Z backup has the completed accounts half (its cache is intact, so re-derivable); older backups are full.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_25 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 25u32, "2026-11-02T23:12Z", "x-dumps", "WATCH: pluto node disk. pluto has 23G disk, at 65% used / 7.8G free with its geygey shard-2,3 cache at 7.6G and only ~26% of its snaps done (still HTML-era, which caches many failed candidates per id, so cache-per-id is highest now and eases in the API era). Risk: pluto fills before geygey completes there. pluto runs `archive` (snaps only, no media), so its cache is just snapshot HTTP responses - transient, needed only during its harvest; the MERGE needs pluto's wayback.{2,3}of4.ndjson, not its cache. MITIGATION if pluto free-disk < ~2G: (a) let pluto finish its shards (writes wayback files), then its cache is disposable; or (b) stop pluto and let sjc harvest all 4 shards (slower but one disk); or (c) copy pluto's partial wayback + prune. Added `df -h /root` to the pluto heartbeat check.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_26 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 26u32, "2026-11-02T23:16Z", "x-dumps", "PIPELINE RE-RUN RUNBOOK (deterministic, the user-requested re-runnable recipe). Binary: /home/remote/xarchive/target/release/xarchive (host) or /opt/xarchive/xarchive (pod). All stages are cache-backed (sha256(url) at <root>/cache/http; Wayback id_/embed immutable, CDX/profile refreshable). CACHE STRATEGIES: default = use cache (no refetch, byte-identical rerun); --refresh = refetch CDX + profile (pick up new captures); --max N = cap network fetches per run (bounded/test). RE-RUN FULL: `xarchive <root> run <handle...> --rps R` = cdx -> 2 archive shards (snaps) + X-lane (profile/live/context) in parallel -> per-account finisher (media+timeline+merge). MULTI-NODE: `--shards N --set a,b` (+ env XARCHIVE_SHARDS/XARCHIVE_SHARD_SET) runs only those snaps shards; split one account across boxes (index partition = same ids given same captures.tsv); merge needs all wayback.*ofN copied to one box. SINGLE STAGE: `xarchive <root> <cdx|profile|snaps|live|context|media|timeline> <handle>` (snaps here = shards=1 full). FINALIZE/REPAIR an account from cache: rm its wayback.*of*.ndjson, then `snaps` + `timeline` + `merge` (rebuilds full output, no refetch). MERGE across accounts: `xarchive <root> merge` (groups by account_id into people/<id>/). EVENTS: set IGGY_URL/USERNAME/PASSWORD (+IGGY_CA_PEM or IGGY_CA_FILE) to publish progress to iggy; `xarchive <root> follow` consumes to status.tsv. GUARD: completed accounts must stay out of a sharded run (a shard env is global to the run loop).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_27 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 27u32, "2026-11-02T23:25Z", "x-dumps", "geygey completion read (11-02 19:2x): snaps sjc 16,400/31,386 (API era, api_json 6,132 = ~1 fetch/id, faster), pluto 8,500/31,385 (still HTML era, api_json 0 = multiple candidate fetches/id, slower). X-lane live 75,100/125,541. pluto looks behind but it is NOT a box-speed bottleneck - it just has not reached the cheap API-era captures yet (geygey is HTML 2020-2022 then API 2023-2026); pluto will accelerate entering the API era, so the 2-node split self-balances. Rough completion ~a day out, gated by the snaps shards; DO NOT rebalance by resharding (re-partition + cache remap is fiddly and resharding just caused a regression). Merge (geygey-merge.sh) fires at completion: scp pluto wayback.{2,3}of4 -> sjc, rerun geygey finisher over all 4 shards + the completed-account guard still green.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_28 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 28u32, "2026-11-02T23:31Z", "x-dumps", "Multi-shard merge path VALIDATED 11-02 (pre-flight for geygey). Split a completed account (stewshka) wayback.ndjson into wayback.0of2+1of2, re-ran timeline+merge in a scratch root: entry order is deterministic and ids are identical to live (no non-determinism in timeline - the earlier worry is ruled out). The only field that differed was media[] (empty in the test) because the self-test did not stage media.tsv - timeline populates local media/<id>_N paths from media.tsv, so the finisher MUST run the media stage before timeline. geygey-merge.sh already does media -> timeline -> merge, so geygey will merge with media intact. (Also confirms the restore of the completed accounts kept media: it left media.tsv untouched, so their timelines still carry media paths.)")
inl note_of_x_dumps_29 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 29u32, "2026-11-02T23:47Z", "x-dumps", "PHASE-2 AUDIO SIZING (11-02, read-only estimate from completed-account timelines). Own-text segments (tweet/reply/quote, retweets dropped), at 150 wpm x3 renditions (orig + EN/PT + KO): juhondacivic 25,496 segs / 249k words = ~83h; fc1943s+i574n (72784485) 830 segs / 15.8k words = ~5.3h; fc1943s-later 1.8h; stewshka 1.3h. TOTAL completed = 27,415 segments / 274k words = ~91h trilingual audio (~3.8 of the user 24h files), BEFORE geygey. juhondacivic dominates (prolific). Phase-2 cost implication: translation ~Opus batch $2-3 per ~24h-account-file (memory x-account-audio-project) so ~$8-12 for the completed set; TTS on a rented GPU (Chatterbox Multilingual, box/node GPUs too small). This is deliverable sizing only; Phase-2 (translate->TTS) stays gated on the user and on finishing the harvest - no Phase-2 code built. Script: scratchpad/audio-estimate.py (reads people/*/timeline.ndjson).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_30 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 30u32, "2026-11-02T23:55Z", "x-dumps", "Regression fix COMPLETED (media too) 11-02. The reshard also halved the completed accounts MEDIA: during the reshard window the hourly finisher re-ran their media stage against the halved wayback, rewriting media.tsv to reference ~half (juhondacivic 1,494 vs original 2,920). My first restore ran snaps+timeline+merge but SKIPPED media, so media stayed half. FIX: re-ran `media --max 0` (re-scan existing files on disk, no refetch - the 2,920 media files were never deleted, and the missing media's failed responses were already cached) + timeline + merge. juhondacivic media back to 2,920 (= original); all 4 accounts full. The count guard only checked ENTRIES, so it missed this - EXTENDED /data/x/check-counts.sh + expected-counts.tsv to baseline media too (31596/2920, 1736/584, 1732/764, 1950/510). LESSON: a regression can hit a derived artifact (media.tsv) that a count-of-entries guard does not see; guard every independent output. Restore of a resharded account must re-run media as well as snaps+timeline+merge.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_31 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 31u32, "2026-11-02T23:57Z", "x-dumps", "Parked question (d) 2022 gap CONFIRMED REAL, not a harvest bug (11-02). Account 72784485 (fc1943s+i574n, the user's) is fully self-consistent: 1,950 entries, kind breakdown (tweet 697, reply 125, quote 8, retweet 1,071, lost 49) sums exactly to 1,950 and matches the summary.md table row; years 2019 (7), 2020 (112), 2021 (520), [2022 = ZERO], 2023 (27), 2024 (623), 2025 (595), 2026 (66). The internal consistency means the 2022 gap is a property of the archive, not a pipeline error: either the account paused posting in 2022, or Wayback holds no 2022 captures for it. Still for the user to say which, but it is NOT a bug to chase. media=510, gone-from-x=258, lost=49.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_32 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 32u32, "2026-11-03T00:01Z", "x-dumps", "Parked question (a) identity - STRONG evidence 11-02. Account 1637273636049498112 (the later @fc1943s holder) is almost certainly the USER's own: handle author=fc1943s (1,267), display author_name=\"stewshka\" (1,188), 1,736 entries 2024-2026, and its recent own-text tweets are SPIRAL CODE - e.g. \"open sm'_operators\", \"///- --package . test thread_sample static\", pipe/operator syntax (|> && <> =>). No public figure posts the user's own language. So the user's accounts span THREE ids: 72784485 (fc1943s->i574n), 1819115209170276352 (stewshka), and 1637273636049498112 (fc1943s/stewshka, Spiral code). juhondacivic + geygey_hadid remain the PUBLIC FIGURES (the user said so). Final ownership is the user's to confirm, but the Spiral-code content is near-definitive for 1637273636049498112.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_33 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 33u32, "2026-11-03T00:12Z", "x-dumps", "Phase-2 narration format VALIDATED on real data 11-02 (read-only demo, no Phase-2 code). The harvested timeline feeds the user-described narration shape directly: date header (on change), HH:MM, original text, then [EN/PT] + [KO] translation slots - chronological, with time + date + own-text all present per record. Sample from juhondacivic confirms the content is PORTUGUESE (e.g. \"babaca fanfarrao comedia brabo\", \"sou fa dela\"), exactly the PT<->EN + KO translation case the project targets. So Phase 2 (translate the slots via Opus batch, then TTS) can consume people/<id>/timeline.ndjson as-is; no reformatting of the harvest needed. Demo script: scratchpad/narration-sample.py. Phase 2 stays gated on the user + finishing the harvest.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_34 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 34u32, "2026-11-03T08:00Z", "x-dumps", "WIND-DOWN STATE 11-03 (autonomous run NOT started; standing.txt expired; pausing per main). HARVEST is RUNNING - let it finish, do NOT kill. geygey_hadid sharded 4-way over 2 nodes: sjc pod (shards 0,1) live DONE 125,500/125,541, snaps ~98% (30,600/31,385), context done - its in-pod finisher will auto-run media+timeline+merge for shards 0,1 (partial geygey + first media download). pluto systemd unit xdumps-geygey (shards 2,3) snaps ~71% (22,200/31,385), ~2h left. The 4 completed accounts are intact and guarded (check-counts COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK). Disk 24G free. NEXT STEP (at run start, once BOTH nodes finish snaps+live): run scratchpad/geygey-merge.sh (pulls pluto shards 2,3 -> sjc, rebuilds host binary so narrate emits translate_input.ndjson + HTML-decoded text, runs media+timeline+merge+narrate over ALL accounts), then check-counts.sh must print COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK. WAITS ON: pluto snaps completion + the autonomous run starting (the cross-node merge is mine to run). Then XDUMPS-FINAL-SWEEP and XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER follow.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_35 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 35u32, "2026-11-03T10:07Z", "x-dumps", "DISK WATCH 11-03 ~06:00: geygey media download is heavier than projected - ~1.77 MB per downloaded file (video-heavy), /data free dropping ~3G per 20-min tick (22->20->17G). ~9,000 downloads remain (~16G) but only 17G free -> WILL fill the disk before media completes (~80 min to the 5G threshold). Pinged kit to prune kit-verify target (~4.9G) + git/k3s-manifest if safe. PLAN per heartbeat: (1) check df + whether kit freed space; (2) if kit freed ~10G, no action; (3) if free approaches ~6G and kit has NOT freed enough, HOLD by scaling the pod down (k3s kubectl -n x-dumps scale deploy xarchive --replicas=0) - media is resumable from the http cache on restart (cached media skip) - then resume (replicas=1) after a prune. pluto (systemd) is unaffected by a pod hold. Do NOT let the disk hit 0 (write corruption).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_36 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 36u32, "2026-11-03T10:09Z", "x-dumps", "DISK RESOLVED 11-03 ~06:10: kit freed ~10.6G (kit-verify target 6.5G + target-nodebug 1.5G + regen staging 2.3G + an A/B binary) -> /data now 27G free (was 17). New math: remaining geygey media ~16G of downloads -> lands ~11G at completion. SAFE. New shared threshold: ping kit if /data drops under 8G (kit will hold its europa builds; its next build regrows ~5G target). The later merge re-run hits the http cache (no re-download), so no second disk hit. Media at 9,200/23,682.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_37 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 37u32, "2026-11-03T10:29Z", "x-dumps", "DISK 11-03 ~06:20: drain is ALL media (cache 20->29G; kit not building, target pruned). Media alone lands ~6G free = SAFE (just needs write headroom, >0 fine). Asked kit to HOLD europa builds until I signal geygey media done (~2-3h), so no concurrent +5G build pushes /data to 0. Watch each tick that free stays >0; media 11,200/23,682, pluto snaps ~84%.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_38 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 38u32, "2026-11-03T10:35Z", "x-dumps", "DISK 11-03 ~06:30: kit re-pruned a finished build target -> /data 23G free; kit now HOLDS ALL europa builds (incl a pending TRAIN14c Linux-kit rebuild) until I signal geygey media is done. COMPLETION CHECKLIST (when geygey media + merge finish): (1) run geygey-merge.sh; (2) check-counts.sh = COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK; (3) SIGNAL kit \"geygey media done, resume europa builds\"; (4) tell x lane geygey complete with the id list (X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES). Disk safe; no action until completion.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_39 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 39u32, "2026-11-03T11:11Z", "x-dumps", "DISK CAPACITY 11-03 ~06:50: geygey media is heavy (~2.65 MB/download) and its FULL media likely exceeds the europa disk (15G free now, draining ~5G/tick, ~14G of media left -> lands near 0). Media is SECONDARY (user priority: text first; text/timeline/snaps already safe on disk). DECISIONS: (1) FIXED geygey-merge.sh: media is now best-effort (|| continue), so a disk-full/partial media run never blocks the small, important timeline/merge/narrate. (2) The sjc AUTO-finisher media is REDUNDANT (my merge redoes media over all shards), so if df < ~3G, scale the pod down (k3s kubectl -n x-dumps scale deploy xarchive --replicas=0) to stop the drain + avoid a disk-full crashloop; snaps/live/wayback are already on disk; the merge is the authoritative media run. (3) Flagged main for ~5-7G headroom (git 4.3G / k3s-manifest 2.9G reclaimable?). NET: worst case geygey media lands partial; text + narration (the audio-project input) are complete regardless. WATCH df each tick; act at ~3G.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_40 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 40u32, "2026-11-03T11:12Z", "x-dumps", "DISK UPDATE 11-03 ~07:00: main freed 2.8G (regenerable k3s-manifest cargo target) -> /data 18G free. /home/remote/git is NOT reclaimable (remote.ps1 root + the running kit job-host binary, up 16h); its other 4.1G are kit build artifacts (kit could selective-clean keeping the job-host binary, but not worth the risk now). PLAN per main: media needs ~15G, 18G free -> lands ~3G, so media LIKELY COMPLETES on its own; the ~2-3G pod scale-down (replicas=0) is the FLOOR-CAP safety only if it dips toward 2G. geygey-merge.sh already makes media best-effort so timeline/merge/narrate always finish. Watch df each tick; act only at ~3G.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_41 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 41u32, "2026-11-03T11:50Z", "x-dumps", "POD OOM + INTERVENTION 11-03 ~07:20: the sjc xarchive pod was OOMKilled (exit 137) during geygey media at its 1536Mi cgroup limit, then crash-looped (re-ran cached snaps/context, heading back to media = re-OOM). I scaled deploy xarchive to replicas=0. SAFE: shards 0,1 wayback.{0,1}of4.ndjson are on disk (14.5MB each); follow pod + iggy + kit-worker untouched; pluto (systemd, shards 2,3) still running ~90% snaps. WHY stop it: the pod finisher is (a) redundant - full geygey REQUIRES my cross-node geygey-merge.sh which re-runs media over all 4 shards on the HOST binary (no 1536Mi cgroup; europa has 4GB+swap), and (b) structurally doomed - its media->timeline->merge is one process so a media OOM kills the finisher (no timeline), whereas geygey-merge runs media as a separate best-effort step. COMPLETION PATH now = geygey-merge.sh only (run when pluto finishes snaps+live). CAUTION at merge time: media on the HOST could use >1536Mi; watch box free-mem (4GB) during media so it does not box-OOM iggy/kit-worker - if memory climbs, the media || continue still lets timeline/merge/narrate finish. Pod STAYS at replicas=0 (no pending accounts need it). LESSON: 1536Mi pod is too small for a large account finisher; use the host binary (or raise the limit) for big accounts.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_42 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 42u32, "2026-11-03T13:44Z", "x-dumps", "MERGE LAUNCHED 11-03 ~09:20: both nodes done (pluto unit inactive, wayback.{2,3}of4 written 14.5MB each; sjc shards 0,1 on disk). Running geygey-merge.sh (bo424zh0o). TRAP: `bash <script>` on the box resolves to WSL (broken, needs kernel update) and exits 1 - MUST run via git-bash: & \"%USERPROFILE%\\scoop\\apps\\git\\current\\bin\\bash.exe\" <script>. Merge uses media --max 0 (media.tsv from cache, no downloads, disk-safe) + timeline+merge+narrate over ALL accounts. On success: check-counts COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK, signal kit resume europa builds, tell x lane geygey complete (ids: 1450651554340642818, 1637273636049498112, 1819115209170276352, 72784485, + geygey_hadid people id).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_43 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 43u32, "2026-11-03T13:53Z", "x-dumps", "HARVEST COMPLETE 11-03 ~09:4x: all 5 accounts harvested, merged across shards, and narrated with the new format (translate_input.ndjson + HTML-decoded). geygey_hadid (people id 1096932140665786369): 125,528 entries 2012-2026 (tweet 34,431 / reply 36,198 / quote 15,765 / retweet 35,717 / lost 3,417), 86,394 narratable segments (85,222 unique). Other accounts unchanged. GUARD updated: check-counts.sh here-doc now baselines all 5 (geygey entries=125528 media=13520, .bak taken); re-run = COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK. Pod stays at replicas=0; kit told to resume europa builds; x told the corpus is ready at /data/x/people/<id>/ for X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES. RESIDUAL: geygey media is PARTIAL (12,209 of 47,378 cached; secondary, disk-limited) - a bounded media-fill is an optional follow-up. NEXT per user directive: XDUMPS-FINAL-SWEEP (recovery pass, low yield validated for non-notable) + XDUMPS-BACKUP-TRANSFER (now unblocked - pod idle, disk stable).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_44 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 44u32, "2026-11-03T15:38Z", "x-dumps", "MEDIA-FILL (turn-key, for when the box is free + user confirms they want more geygey media; SECONDARY). geygey media is ~12.2k of 47.4k cached. A disk-SAFE bounded fill needs NO code change - the existing --max caps network fetches: at ~2 MB/download, run `xarchive /data/x media geygey_hadid --max N` with N ~= (free_GB - 4 buffer) * 512, so it stops before filling the disk (e.g. 14G free -> N~5000 -> ~5k more media). Do it only when kit is NOT mid-build (disk contention) and ideally when /data has more headroom; then re-run timeline+merge over geygey so media.tsv reflects the new files (text/narration unchanged). Decided NOT to run it now: kit is building, disk 14G, media is the lowest priority and the text deliverable is complete+backed-up. No disk-floor code needed (--max suffices).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_45 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 45u32, "2026-11-04T04:16Z", "x-dumps", "Harvest/merge/narrate complete for all 5 (guard COMPLETED-ACCOUNTS-OK, text backup local+restore-proven). Phase shift 2026-11-04 per user: joined-timeline sim/frontend + battle-test vs real profiles + deeper recovery; translate/TTS parked.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_46 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 46u32, "2026-11-04T11:05Z", "x-dumps", "Groom 2026-11-04 (main keep-working broadcast): core DONE this run — all 5 accounts harvested/merged/narrated; recovery PASS-2 recovered fc1943s/i574n +765 & stewshka +26 (all reachable; geygey suspended + juhondacivic deletions indexed+filtered); joined world (163307 posts) built+media-complete+lost-semantics-healed; ALL data backed up local+verified; typed model verified by x & landing in x-next6; typed corpus first-cut emitted+validated. REMAINING (gated): the joined-timeline FRONTEND (x building, x-next6 route core; my GET /timeline HTTP entry + typed codec land right after x-next6) and translate/TTS (user budget). Not idle — paced by x-next6 (imminent).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_47 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 47u32, "2026-11-04T11:16Z", "x-dumps", "europa disk freed 2026-11-04 (main: node at 97%, blocking all lanes checks): pruned /data/x/cache (re-derivable http fetch cache; harvest+recovery done, backup done). Freed 16G -> europa 89%->71% (27G free, under the 85% target), fleet unblocked. The 24G curated media (accounts/*/media) is HARDLINKED into cache, so it survived the prune (confirmed intact: geygey 20G/13532 files, fc1943s 633M, juhondacivic 3.1G) and the box backup holds real bytes. /data/enryu43 (9G parquet training set) is NOT x-dumps - left it. No data lost.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_48 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 48u32, "2026-11-04T13:16Z", "x-dumps", "RECOVERY COMPLETENESS — alternative-websites check 2026-11-04 (user directive \"more checks / alternative websites\"): archive.today (archive.ph) CHECKED, 0/24 sampled lost ids have ANY capture (both twitter.com and x.com URL forms), probe validated by positive controls (jack/status/20=38 mementos, elonmusk=2006, twitter.com=4014 — so the 0s are genuine absence, not a blocked probe). Conclusion: the 4860 unrecoverable are gone from ALL viable public archives — syndication embed API (used, PASS-2 +791), Wayback CDX (mined), archive.today (empty for these accounts). Google cache dead (deprecated 2024), Nitter defunct. No further fetching technique or site yields these losses; recovery is comprehensive, not technique-limited.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_49 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 49u32, "2026-11-04T23:14Z", "x-dumps", "JOINED-TIMELINE UI DEMO LIVE + VALIDATED 2026-11-04: x built a scratch demo (x-next6/7/8/9 stack) and it serves the full 5-account joined world (114MB, 163307 posts) on localhost:58151/timeline. Validated end-to-end on REAL data: single chronological feed newest-first (2026-10-18 -> 2012), all 5 accounts interleave by date (confirmed geygey+stewshka interleaved at a deep cursor), pagination cursor (f=ids&c=offsets) works, fc1943s-by-id disambiguation works (@fc1943s · <id>), 4860 lost hidden. My GET /timeline entry + x route core + render all check out live. Perf: ~13-17s/page (rebuilds feed per request; cache/index is a prod follow-up). This is a DEMO of unproven drafts; real landing rides x-next9.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_50 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 50u32, "2026-11-05T00:59Z", "x-dumps", "x-next6b LANDED 2026-11-04 (~00:58Z): posts.spi survival model (seen_at/archival_of/surviving) is now live in apps/client/posts.spi. This RELEASES the XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI\\posts.spi freeze (5AE47672CF27) and UNBLOCKS: (a) the codec reader gate — typed_codec.spi can now compile-check against the real posts types; (b) the deferred no-comments heading-strip of posts.spi (needs its own A/B rule, coordinate with x). Next steps for the next run/user-direction; not started now (2 min to the 21:00-local wind-down, would orphan a Spiral compile).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_51 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 51u32, "2026-11-05T04:12Z", "x-dumps", "REFERENCE-GRAPH COMPLETENESS check 2026-11-05 (user directive: did we get all tweets we could). Streaming analysis on idle europa over the 5 per-account timeline.ndjson (line-by-line, memory-safe, no OOM). Of 46,449 distinct ids our posts REFERENCE (reply_to + quote.id): 44,595 not in our timelines; minus 7,178 context-cached = 37,528 referenced tweets we hold nothing for - but these are overwhelmingly THIRD-PARTY (reply parents / quote targets by accounts we do not harvest), OUT OF SCOPE for the 5-account histories. IN-SCOPE signal (referenced id authored BY one of our 5 handles via reply_to_user/quote.author): fc1943s 0, i574n 0, stewshka 0 => the 3 REACHABLE accounts are 100% self-reference-complete (zero own tweets referenced-but-missing). Only the 2 SUSPENDED accounts have gaps: juhondacivic 48 + geygey_hadid 73 = 121 ids. CROSS-CHECK: all 121 are NOT in the 4860-id unrecoverable index (0 overlap) - UNCOUNTED phantom ids, known only via the reference, never had a capture to attempt, never booked lost. Saved: europa:/data/x/joined/reference-gap-own-accounts.tsv (id, handle). These 121 are the concrete recovery frontier: candidates for a targeted by-id Wayback/syndication fetch, else add to the unrecoverable index if confirmed gone. Reachable accounts need no further recovery by this metric.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_52 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 52u32, "2026-11-05T04:18Z", "x-dumps", "RECOVERY PROBE COMPLETE 2026-11-05: direct by-id syndication fetch (cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result?id=<id>&token=x, the harvest live technique) of ALL 121 in-scope reference-gap ids -> 121/121 TOMBSTONE (X suspension/removal notice), 0 recovered, 0 other. Endpoint+network verified with known-live id 20 (HTTP 200 full JSON). So these 121 self-referenced tweets by the 2 suspended accounts are CONFIRMED GONE, not recoverable via the live/embed path (consistent with priors: juhondacivic 0-elsewhere, archive.today 0/24). COMPLETENESS CONCLUSION: the 5-account histories are as complete as any public source allows - reachable accounts 100% reference-complete, suspended accounts only reference-gaps (121) all confirmed unrecoverable. Disposition: these 121 belong in the unrecoverable index (currently omits them) -> true unrecoverable 4860+121=4981.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_53 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 53u32, "2026-11-05T04:27Z", "x-dumps", "Reference-extraction completeness VERIFIED 2026-11-05 (correctness check on the 121-gap finding): confirmed reply_to + quote.id captured ALL id-bearing references - the refs array is populated in 0 of 163307 records (empty in timeline.ndjson), and retweets carry NO original-tweet-id (only retweet_author handle), so retweet originals are not id-checkable; BUT the original text + media ARE captured inline in the retweet record (even removed/suspended retweets keep text + media files), so no content is lost there. Kinds: tweet 44357, reply 50213, quote 19239, retweet 44638, lost 4860 = 163307. Therefore the 121-id in-scope reference gap is COMPLETE, not under-counted; the completeness conclusion stands.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_54 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 54u32, "2026-11-05T05:42Z", "x-dumps", "UI-tail completeness observation 2026-11-05 (UNVERIFIED - europa down): the served timeline oldest geygey_hadid posts bottom at ~2019-09 (feed end near offset 125527, 0 past it), but the world span was recorded 2012-04-24 -> 2026-10-18. Either geygey 2012-2019 is lost-filtered (hidden from scroll) or the 2012 start is a single outlier / other account. Could NOT verify vs europa source (ssh timing out on banner exchange). Minor curiosity for geygey (suspended -> pre-2019 likely unrecoverable anyway). RECHECK when europa recovers: jq -r .utc /data/x/people/1096932140665786369/timeline.ndjson | sort | head, and the global min across people/*/timeline.ndjson.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_55 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 55u32, "2026-11-05T06:07Z", "x-dumps", "geygey tail question RESOLVED 2026-11-05 (europa recovered, load 0.39 - tibia capped its loop; verified against source). The 2012-04-24 world-span start is a SINGLE geygey_hadid post that is LOST (\"no tweet text in the archived page\") - it IS in the data and correctly in the 4860 unrecoverable index, but the UI lost-filter hides it, which is why the served tail showed 2019-09 as oldest SURVIVING. geygey year distribution: 1 (2012, lost), 16 (2019), 2773 (2020), 24584 (2021), 12237 (2022), 168 (2023), 23218 (2024), 39960 (2025), 22571 (2026) - so the 2012->2019 gap is REAL (isolated early tombstone then dormancy), NOT a missed harvest window. Global earliest non-lost = 2017 retweets by 72784485. CONCLUSION: UI tail correct, lost-filter correct, index correct, no completeness gap. Closes the earlier UNVERIFIED flag.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_56 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 56u32, "2026-11-06T10:11Z", "x-dumps", "HARVEST NUMBERS (week 10-30..11-06, dated for countability): 5 accounts harvested into one joined world = 163,306 tweets, 113.6MB joined.world.json. Media: 26GB, 18,621 files across all 5 (fc1943s 961, i574n 444, stewshka 764, juhondacivic 2920, geygey_hadid 13532). Completeness by reference-graph metric: 3 reachable accounts 100% complete (0 gaps); 121 suspended-account referenced tweets confirmed DESTROYED (syndication tombstone); unrecoverable index = 4981. Durability: 3 independent off-box backup copies, all integrity-verified (zstd -t + tar -tf). Serve: joined-timeline UI live on :8787, battle-tested (pagination 0 violations, 5-feed merge, authorship labels). Open gap found 11-06: UI did not display post media (now being fixed, see XDUMPS-TIMELINE-MEDIA).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_57 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 57u32, "2026-11-06T11:03Z", "x-dumps", "Harvest-enrichment finding+battle-test 11-06: engagement (likes/replies) IS fetchable from the syndication endpoint we already use - it was a PARSER gap, not a source gap. Extended live.rs::summarize (additive: favorite_count, conversation_count, lang, expanded urls/hashtags/mentions, author_name/avatar/verified, media_dims, possibly_sensitive, edited, quote media+likes). PROVEN on a 22-tweet fc1943s sample on the box: likes max 11456 (17/22 nonzero), replies max 140 (16/22), lang on all, author_name on all (was blank), avatars on all, urls/hashtags/mentions populated, media_dims on all. NOT recoverable (authed-API only, we lack creds): retweet_count, quote_count, view_count. Full enrichment = re-harvest live stage on europa + rethread through parse.rs/world.rs/typed codec/UI. See memory syndication-returns-engagement-and-entities.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_58 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 58u32, "2026-11-06T15:55Z", "x-dumps", "Completeness pass 11-06 (standing \"did we capture all recoverable tweets\" directive). Ran a REFERENCE-GRAPH CLOSURE check over joined.world.json (163,316 captured ids): for every captured post, checked whether its reply-parent / quoted / conversation-root ids are themselves captured, classified by owner handle. Finding: the graph is well-closed against our own accounts -- the great majority of dangling edges point at OTHER people's tweets (27.5k reply / 18.2k quote / 27.4k conv), which we correctly don't archive. But 170 DISTINCT own-account tweet ids are referenced-but-uncaptured, and NONE are in our Wayback CDX (183,154 ids) or tombstone (159,912 ids) inventories -- i.e. graph-only discoveries the crawler never saw. Likely cause: the 11-06 engagement-enrichment extended the parser to expose more reply_to/quote refs than the Nov2-4 context run had seen. Owner split: 81 geygey_hadid, 58 juhondacivic, 20 i574n, 9 stewshka, 2 fc1943s. Syndication probe (token=x endpoint): i574n/fc1943s/stewshka samples return live Tweets => RECOVERABLE; juhondacivic + geygey_hadid return TweetTombstone => gone (account/tweet deleted). RECOVERY IN PROGRESS: re-running context for fc1943s/i574n/stewshka (fetches the refs via summarize(); cache Immutable so only new ids hit network), then overlay ONLY alive own-authored records into each owner's live.ndjson (append-only by new id -- existing enriched records untouched, no replace/decay risk; timeline.rs builds posts from live+wayback only, so context alone would not add them), then timeline->merge->world. Backed up context.ndjson{.pre-refgraph} first. Count added TBD on completion. Tombstone subset (geygey 81 + juhondacivic 58) is confirmed-unrecoverable (deleted). Enriched-world transfer to box stays HELD pending x's base-media land (x confirmed not landed yet 11-06).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_59 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 59u32, "2026-11-06T16:01Z", "x-dumps", "World-correctness + frontend check 11-06 (non-competing, while context fetch runs). (1) ENRICHED EUROPA WORLD (the transfer target) is structurally clean: 5 users = fc1943s(72784485, 2717 posts), juhondacivic(31596), geygey_hadid(125528), stewshka(1732), fc1943s(1637273636049498112, 1744) -- the dup-handle fc1943s correctly preserved as two accounts keyed by id (per the known two-accounts case). Total 163,317 posts; ZERO posts with a non-ISO created_at; the second fc1943s id is an account id, not a post id (0 posts carry it as id). (2) FRONTEND: the local joined-timeline serve is browsable at http://127.0.0.1:8787/timeline (200, 50 posts/page, paginated via ?f=<acct ids>&c=<offsets>; / and api routes 401 by design -- sim API auth). It currently runs the STALE Nov-4 box world + pre-media build, so no media/engagement/dark-mode yet and page-1 bylines render oddly (old-build byline format; newest posts are that account's recent probe tweets, so newest-first merge draws 50 from one feed -- expected, not a merge bug). All resolved by the gated transfer + x's new build. (3) OFF-BOX BACKUP healthy: xdumps-backlog-sao ran 11:52 local, result 0, next 12:22. Net: transfer target verified correct; frontend directive met (browsable/paginated); only the media/dark-mode/engagement surfacing waits on x's base-media land.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_60 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 60u32, "2026-11-06T16:07Z", "x-dumps", "Media-coverage completeness check 11-06 (second dimension; non-competing jq read, europa load 0.33). Referenced media URLs in the world vs files on disk: stewshka 764/764 (100%), juhondacivic 2920/2920 (100%), geygey_hadid 13520 urls / 13532 files (full), fc1943s(merged 72784485) 1534 urls vs 1097+448=1545 files across its two account dirs (i574n+fc1943s back the same merged world user) = full. Totals 18,738 referenced urls <= 18,761 files on disk. CONCLUSION of the completeness pass: the archive is complete on both checkable dimensions -- reference-graph closure (own-account refs all captured except genuinely-gone) and media (all referenced media downloaded) -- modulo content that is actually deleted (tombstoned tweets, dead CDN urls). Remaining recoverable content = the ~31 alive own-tweets the reference graph surfaced, recovery re-run in flight (context enriched re-fetch b3ttxzekj), overlay+rebuild staged.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_61 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 61u32, "2026-11-06T16:09Z", "x-dumps", "Temporal (linear-history) completeness check 11-06. Per-account date span + distinct months: fc1943s(72784485) 2017-01..2026-06 2717 posts/63 months; juhondacivic 2023-04..2026-06 31596/28; geygey_hadid 2012-04..2026-07 125528/72; stewshka 2024-08..2026-08 1732/19; fc1943s(1637...) 2024-02..2026-10 1744/29. Long-span accounts show many zero-post months but that is consistent with (a) sparse early activity and (b) retweets carrying the ORIGINAL tweet's date (geygey's own tweets are the 2020-2022 Wayback window; 2012/2026 edges are retweet dates). Dense recent accounts (stewshka, juhondacivic, fc1943s-1637) have no suspicious gaps. No clear missing-PERIOD signal; a rigorous gap audit would cross-ref CDX date coverage, deferred as low-signal. x UPDATE: Cells unblock + events->sim_events rename LANDED on box; my 3 media bases unaffected (don't open events); base-media gate = node job x-media2 (pinned pack55d 257DB91886B97811) imminent. On its green I transfer the enriched world box-side + restart :8787. pack55d pin is a compile/gate pin, not a source reformat (drafts stay as-wrapped until user pushes spiral).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_62 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 62u32, "2026-11-06T16:14Z", "x-dumps", "ROOT CAUSE of the recoverable-but-uncaptured gap (permanent fix spec, filed from the 11-06 recovery). The reference graph surfaced ~31 alive own-account tweets that were fetched into context.ndjson but never appear as posts. WHY: xarchive/src/timeline.rs::run builds the post id set at line ~98 as let ids: BTreeSet<u64> = live.keys().chain(wayback.keys()).copied().collect(); -- context.ndjson is loaded (line ~39) but used ONLY to resolve inline reply/quote context (line ~41), never as a POST source. So any own-authored tweet that exists only as a reference TARGET (a reply-parent/quote of a captured tweet, never itself in live/wayback) is fetched by context() but dropped from the timeline/world. The overlay I ran this session is a one-off; every future harvest re-loses them. PERMANENT FIX (xarchive, careful -- touches world-build core, verify by byte-compare control vs HEAD world): in timeline.rs::run, extend ids to also include context keys whose record is OWN-authored (author_id in the account's holders set) and alive (has text, no \removed); the post-construction loop must then treat context as a third source alongside live/wayback (pick()/field() fall back to the context record for those ids), attributing kind/author via the same own path. Guard: do NOT promote NON-own context records (other people's reply-parents) -- those stay inline-only, else the world balloons with strangers' tweets. Regression sample: an account whose only copy of tweet X is a reply-parent reference -> X appears as an own post after timeline. Until fixed, the recovery overlay (/tmp/overlay_recover.sh on europa, append-only by new id) is the mechanism; re-run it after any future context refresh that surfaces new own refs. Not touching the source mid-recovery (would leave a half-verified world-build change); filed for careful implementation. See reharvest-must-merge-not-replace + the reference-graph completeness approach.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_63 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 63u32, "2026-11-06T16:17Z", "x-dumps", "RECOVERY DONE 11-06: reference-graph recovery applied + verified. The enriched context re-fetch (xa-ctx-rg, 10m40s) produced 32 alive own-authored records (all enriched, favorite_count present); the overlay appended the 31 genuinely-new ones to live.ndjson (fc1943s +1, i574n +21, stewshka +9; 1 i574n id already present, skipped), then timeline->merge->world. VERIFIED: joined world post count 163,317 -> 163,348 (+31 exact); spot-checked recovered ids 1616896499497009153 (i574n), 1907828886684983696 (stewshka), 2093465283914519007 (fc1943s) each present as 1 post with correct author + enriched fields (favorite_count, lang). Append-only, no existing record rewritten; pre-enrich backups intact. COMPLETENESS PASS CLOSED: every recoverable own-account tweet the archive can see is now captured across all 3 checkable dimensions (reference-graph closure, media coverage, temporal span); the unrecoverable remainder is geygey_hadid(81)+juhondacivic(58), confirmed deleted (TweetTombstone). The permanent pipeline fix (timeline.rs promoting own-authored context records) is specced in the prior note. Enriched world on europa now = 163,348 posts; transfers to box on x's base-media land (x-media2).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_64 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 64u32, "2026-11-06T16:24Z", "x-dumps", "Alternative-source cross-check 11-06 (founding \"more checks / alternative websites\" directive): probed the tombstoned graph-gap ids against the Wayback Machine CDX API (web.archive.org/cdx, public, rate-respecting) -- a DIFFERENT source than the syndication endpoint that returned TweetTombstone. Sampled 6 (3 juhondacivic, 3 geygey_hadid); 5 confirmed so far (6th in bg bex53c16t) return 0 CDX rows across twitter.com / mobile.twitter.com / x.com. So these deleted tweets are absent from Wayback too, not only syndication -> unrecoverable confirmed DUAL-SOURCE (syndication + Wayback), consistent with them not being in our CDX inventory (183,154 ids) earlier. No remaining source respects the no-bot-bypass line (archive.today requires defeating a Cloudflare challenge = off-limits per the standing \"no bypassing access controls/bot checks\" rule). CONCLUSION stands: the 139 tombstoned (geygey 81 + juhondacivic 58) are genuinely gone; the 31 alive were recovered (world now 163,348). The completeness directive is satisfied to the limit of permissible sources.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_65 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 65u32, "2026-11-06T16:39Z", "x-dumps", "Source-freshness check 11-06 (founding \"iterate on every source\" directive, bounded/rate-respecting): queried Wayback CDX API for each account's status pages captured SINCE our harvest (from=20261103, prefix match, collapse urlkey, across twitter.com/mobile/x.com). Result: fc1943s 0, i574n 0, stewshka 0, juhondacivic 0, geygey_hadid 0 -- ZERO new Wayback captures since Nov 3 for all 5 accounts. So the archive is CURRENT with Wayback; nothing new crawled to harvest. Combined completeness status this cycle: (1) reference graph -> 31 alive own-tweets recovered (world 163,348); (2) syndication -> 139 referenced own-ids are TweetTombstone (deleted); (3) Wayback -> those 139 absent (0 CDX rows) AND 0 new captures fleet-wide. All three permissible live sources exhausted; archive complete to the limit of accessible sources (no bot-check bypassing). RE-CHECK CADENCE: this cheap from=-filtered CDX query is the way to detect future Wayback growth without a full re-harvest; re-run periodically (the harvest only needs re-running when it returns nonzero). Still pending: the x-gated media-display transfer (world ready at 163,348, backed up on box).")
inl note_of_x_dumps_66 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 66u32, "2026-11-06T16:44Z", "x-dumps", "DATA-QUALITY FINDING 11-06 (HTML-entity double-encoding in tweet text; frontend + audio-downstream relevance). The syndication endpoint returns tweet text already HTML-ENTITY-ENCODED (< as &lt;, > as &gt;, & as &amp;, ' as &#39;). xarchive summarize() stores it verbatim via text(&v[\\\"text\\\"]); 567 of 163,348 world posts carry such entities. The joined-timeline UI then HTML-ESCAPES on render (timeline_page escape/blocks_html), so &lt; -> &amp;lt; -> the browser shows the LITERAL string \\\"&lt;\\\". Verified on the running :8787 /timeline: 72 double-encoded sequences (&amp;lt; etc.) in one page. So affected posts display garbled markup, and the downstream audio/narration project would read \\\"&lt;\\\" aloud. SCOPE: display/consumer-quality only (0.35% of posts); the stored data is faithful to what syndication returned. FIX (preferred layer = ingestion, xarchive, my domain): HTML-DECODE entities when extracting text in summarize() (and any other text()/reply/quote.text paths) so stored text is real Unicode; then the UI's single escape renders correctly and audio reads clean text. Rust: a small html-escape/entities decode (e.g. the html_escape::decode_html_entities crate, or a hand decode of the 5 named + numeric refs) applied in text(). Requires rebuild + reprocess (re-run live/context summarize -> timeline -> world, merge-overlay safe since it only rewrites the text field of existing records). ALT layer: decode at UI render (x's timeline_page) before escape -- but ingestion is cleaner (fixes all consumers once). NOT fixing now (reprocessing 163k mid-run is deliberate work, and the UI is x's gated build); specced for careful implementation, coordinate the render-vs-ingestion choice with x. Low priority vs the gated media-display land.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_67 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 67u32, "2026-11-06T16:48Z", "x-dumps", "CORRECTION to the prior HTML-entity note (layer was wrong). xarchive is NOT buggy here: it DELIBERATELY stores raw entity-encoded tweet text (faithful to syndication) and decodes PER-CONSUMER. Evidence: src/narrate.rs already has a complete decode_entities(s) (regex over &(#xHH|#NN|name); -> char; handles amp/lt/gt/quot/#39 + numeric) and the narrate/audio path calls it (narrate.rs:44) before emitting narration. So the AUDIO downstream is already correct. The gap is ONLY the UI: timeline_page renders the raw stored text straight through scape/blocks_html with NO decode step, so &lt; -> &amp;lt; -> browser shows literal \"&lt;\" (72 double-encoded seqs seen on one /timeline page; 567 posts affected). FIX (UI layer, minimal, consistent with the store-raw + decode-per-consumer design): add a decode-before-escape step in timeline_page mirroring narrate::decode_entities -- decode entities, THEN escape for HTML. This is a MEDIA-DRAFT FOLLOW-ON to do AFTER the base-media land (the drafts are frozen + x hash-checks now; do not touch them pre-land; my own drafts share this gap). ALT (bigger): decode once at ingestion in summarize() so every consumer gets clean text and narrate's decode becomes an idempotent no-op -- cleaner but changes stored-data semantics + needs reprocess; defer. Recommend the UI-layer fix. Supersedes the earlier \"fix at ingestion, xarchive\" note.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_68 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 68u32, "2026-11-06T16:57Z", "x-dumps", "DATA-INTEGRITY check 11-06 (dup detection; europa idle, non-competing with x's node build). World = 163,348 posts, 163,347 distinct ids -> exactly 1 id appears twice: 1814831297249837420. RESOLVED as NOT corruption: it is a RETWEET of @makeuokay that BOTH fc1943s accounts retweeted (world users 72784485 = renamed i574n, and 1637273636049498112 = newer fc1943s). xarchive keys a retweet record by the ORIGINAL tweet's id, so two of the user's accounts retweeting the same tweet legitimately collide on id; each record carries its own correct account_id/author_id (two real retweet events, same person's two accounts). Present in accounts/fc1943s + accounts/i574n live+wayback. Predates my recovery (the 163,317/163,316 gap existed before). So: archive is CLEAN, no duplicate/corrupt records; the lone id collision is a faithful dual-account retweet. Only consequence = mild display redundancy in the joined view (same retweet shown once per account). OPTIONAL future UI nicety: dedup cross-account identical retweets in the joined timeline (low priority; faithful as-is). No data fix needed.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_69 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 69u32, "2026-11-07T00:10Z", "x-dumps", "MEDIA-QUOTE carry-over 11-06 (gate x-media3c, STABLE pack57 8F0094967068FC85 -> compiler ruled out; the 4 reds are MY draft's Gleam-backend bugs). Base media UI stays LIVE + unaffected; this is the quote/engagement follow-on only. Draft files: media-quote-draft/ (page D089FFEA6102, serve 27CABB70AB1A, lib B4CBB6280AF5 landed). Failing timeline_serve ///> gleam tests (21/25): a_page_hides_lost_posts_but_counts_them, the_timeline_path_serves_an_html_page, the_page_ends_with_the_scroll_script, range_spec_rejects_out_of_range_and_missing. timeline_page 14/14, sim_serve 9/9.\nNARROWING: my only page-path diff vs the base timeline_serve is entry_of (base: labeled_of(plain_of,kind_of,media_of) 3-arg; mine: with_meta(post_meta_of p)(labeled_of(...,quote_media_of p)) 4-arg). So the 3 page-test regressions trace to the with_meta/quote_media threading or the new Range route; a_page_hides_lost_posts asserts (shown.text, offsets) not HTML, so check whether entry_of now perturbs the shown set / errors on a post. range_spec: logic is correct in Spiral AND consistent with the PASSING sibling range tests (parses/suffix/multirange), so count_of_text (prelude digits parser) parses fine; the Gleam got-value is needed to see what differs (i32 compare or Some/None lowering). NEXT SESSION: get expected-vs-got from x's x-media3c run log (asked x; node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\x-media3c-out is source-only). REPRO: pwsh apps/sim/build.ps1 (frames gleam build is prereq) then the timeline_serve notebook; or Invoke-SpiralTests @('timeline_serve') -- heavy (~10min/iter on loaded box), so prefer x's got-values first. Fix minimally, plain mix spiral.cell_budget --check (0 over_budget), send x new hashes. DO NOT reformat drafts until user pushes spiral w/ pack57+.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_70 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 70u32, "2026-11-07T00:23Z", "x-dumps", "MEDIA-QUOTE FIX 11-06 (derived from code, no got-value). ROOT CAUSE of the 3 page-test Gleam regressions: quote_media_of crashes on quote-LESS posts. sim_serve::field key m = field_or key (str \"\") m -> a MISSING key returns str \"\" (empty binary). quote_media_of did items_at \"media\" (field \"quote\" p); for a post with no quote, field \"quote\" = str \"\" (binary), items_at -> field_or -> fields_of -> erlang_map::map_view on a BINARY = badmap crash. entry_of calls quote_media_of per post, so every quote-less fixture post crashed the page build -> a_page_hides_lost_posts_but_counts_them, the_timeline_path_serves_an_html_page, the_page_ends_with_the_scroll_script all failed. timeline_page 14/14 passed (they pass meta explicitly, not via entry_of). int_field/field_text are safe (via id_text/text which handle binaries). FIX: quote_media_of guards ? erlang_term::is_binary q ?> [] |: ... (q = field \\\"quote\\\" p). range_spec_rejects (4th): added defensive ? header = \\\"\\\" ?> None before starts_with (the unique \\\"\\\" input; \\\"2000-3000\\\"/1000 traces to None correctly) -- if it still fails, need the Gleam got-value. NEW HASHES sent to x: timeline_serve 0CF97B67E788 (was 27CABB70AB1A), timeline_page D089FFEA6102 (unchanged), lib B4CBB6280AF5. x reruns the gate tomorrow to verify (base media stays live). LESSON: sim_serve::field on a missing key returns an empty BINARY, so items_at/any map op on an optional nested field (quote, etc.) must guard is_binary first, else badmap on BEAM/Gleam.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_71 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 71u32, "2026-11-07T21:12Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "CAPTURE-COMPLETENESS CHECKPOINT 11-07T21:1xZ (founding 'more checks on whether all recoverable tweets were captured'; from local joined.world.json). TOTAL 163,348 posts across all 5 accounts: geygey_hadid(1096932140665786369) 125,528; juhondacivic(1450651554340642818) 31,596; fc1943s(72784485) 2,738; fc1943s(1637273636049498112) 1,745; stewshka(1819115209170276352) 1,741. All 5 profiles present; the two fc1943s are distinct IDs (known dup-handle pair). SPAN 2012-04-24 -> 2026-10-18 (14y linear). Per-year: 2012=1, 2017=4, 2019=29, 2020=3337, 2021=25406, 2022=12238, 2023=218, 2024=34269, 2025=59028, 2026=28818. WATCH: 2023 dips to 218 between 2022(12k) and 2024(34k) — LIKELY REAL (the two high-volume accounts skew to 2024-2025, so their histories start later, leaving 2023 to the low-volume fc1943s accounts), but flagged per the founding directive; a per-account-per-year breakdown (needs jq, deferred off the saturated box) would confirm. No capture-gap evidence, just an uneven distribution to confirm later.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_72 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 72u32, "2026-11-07T21:16Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "DATA-SAFETY CHECKPOINT 11-07T21:2xZ (founding 'local + off-box backups'; verified both sides). LOCAL (box C:\\home\\xdumps, 67GB): joined.world.json 139.6MB current + .etf 161MB + version history (prebinfix/enriched/prev-nov4), media tars ~25GB (media-geygey ×29, media-important ×7), full x-*.tar.zst snapshots (6GB/1.3GB/934MB). OFF-BOX (europa europa /data/x): accounts=24G, 19222 media files, joined.world.json present (symlink -> /data/x/joined). 24G off-box matches ~25GB local media tars => NOT truncated (the known europa-large-transfer risk). Both locations hold the full corpus; data safety confirmed. Scheduled backups healthy: xdumps-backlog-sao result=0 (every 30m), critical-mirror result=0 (15m), kit-unstaged-backup result=0. Note: off-box is a single rented VM; box holds everything incl snapshots, so box-loss is covered by europa and europa-loss by the box. Directive satisfied.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_73 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 73u32, "2026-11-07T21:23Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "2023-DIP RESOLVED 11-07T21:2xZ (per-account-per-year, capped jq offloaded to europa — box untouched). The 2023 total of 218 = geygey 168 + juhonda 4 + fc1943s 46 (the other two accounts start 2024). MOSTLY REAL: juhonda was just starting (4 -> 9755 in 2024); fc1943s 46 fits its low baseline (2022=1, 2024=624). ONE FLAG worth a targeted re-check: geygey_hadid (the primary 125k-post account) drops 12237(2022) -> 168(2023) -> 23218(2024) — a ~98% single-year collapse bracketed by high-volume years. Either a REAL 2023 hiatus (account locked/private that year => those tweets unrecoverable, so 168 is complete-as-recoverable) OR a capture gap for geygey's 2023 window (~12-24k tweets potentially missing if geygey was active). The corpus cannot distinguish; surfacing per the founding 'all recoverable tweets' directive. Full per-account-per-year: geygey 2020=2773/2021=24584/2022=12237/2023=168/2024=23218/2025=39960/2026=22571; juhonda 2023=4/2024=9755/2025=16284/2026=5553; fc1943s(72784485) spans 2017-2026 low-volume; fc1943s#2 + stewshka start 2024. SUGGESTION: if geygey was known active in 2023, a targeted re-harvest of that window (syndication/wayback) would close it; else mark as unrecoverable-confirmed.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_74 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 74u32, "2026-11-07T21:26Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "GEYGEY 2023 GAP SHARPENED 11-07T21:2xZ (monthly breakdown, europa jq) — stronger than the earlier 'likely real'. geygey_hadid (primary, 125k posts) has a CLEAN CONTIGUOUS BLACKOUT, not a sparse year: 2022-09=259 declining (Oct27/Nov30/Dec24), 2023 H1 present-but-sparse (Jan47/Feb23/Mar28/Apr36/May24/Jun10 = all 168 of its '2023' posts), then 2023-07..2023-12 = EXACTLY ZERO (6 straight months), 2024 trickle (Jan3/Feb2/Mar0/Apr6), then 2024 H2 ramps to thousands. So a ~10-month near-blackout 2023-07->2024-04 bracketed by normal/high activity on BOTH sides (geygey spans 2012->2026, so the harvest reached far past 2023 => NOT a simple API-limit tail-truncation). TWO HYPOTHESES, ambiguous from corpus: (a) REAL dormancy/private/suspension (declining-then-dark shape fits; tweets unrecoverable, capture complete); (b) a harvest-window FAILURE for that span (recoverable, ~thousands missing if geygey was active). RECOMMENDATION (user decision; I did NOT re-harvest — harvesting is 'done'): probe a sample of geygey 2023-09..2024-02 via syndication/wayback; if tweets exist there -> targeted re-harvest of that window; if none -> mark unrecoverable-confirmed. This is the one real completeness flag from today's checks; surfacing to the user in the 02:00 report.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_75 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 75u32, "2026-11-07T21:28Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "SYSTEMIC CAPTURE GAP CONFIRMED 11-07T21:2xZ (corpus-wide, decisive). Total corpus by month: 2023-01=56,02=55,03=28,04=42,05=27,06=10, THEN 2023-07..12=ZERO, 2024-01=3,02=3,03=0,04=6, THEN 2024-05=3713,06=5994,07=6493,08=4762. A 600x discontinuity (6 posts 2024-04 -> 3713 2024-05) that CANNOT be organic. All 3 pre-2024 accounts (geygey/fc1943s/juhonda) share the exact blackout 2023-07->2024-04 and resume together in 2024-05 => NOT account dormancy; this is a HARVEST-BATCH BOUNDARY: the archive is two sources joined at ~2024-05, and the ~10-month window before it was never fetched. Also the entire pre-2024-05 history is sparse (tens/month vs thousands/month after), so pre-2024-05 may be under-captured generally. IMPACT: at the accounts' 2024 rate (~3-6k/month) the gap alone is potentially TENS OF THOUSANDS of recoverable tweets. This is the headline completeness finding (founding 'all recoverable tweets' directive). NOT re-harvesting autonomously (user: harvesting done). USER DECISION NEEDED: re-harvest the 2023-07..2024-04 window (and audit pre-2024-05 completeness) for all 5 accounts via the same method that got 2024-05+. Surfacing to main now + in the 02:00 report.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_76 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 76u32, "2026-11-07T21:41Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "CORRECTION to notes 73-75 (11-07T21:3xZ) — my 'tens of thousands of RECOVERABLE tweets missing' was OVERSTATED; here is the evidence-grounded reframing after reading the raw sources on europa /data/x/people/<id>/timeline.ndjson + status.tsv. (1) NOT a merge/join bug: geygey raw timeline = joined world EXACTLY (raw 2023=168, same hard gap 2023-07..2024-04); the gap lives in the RAW SOURCES. (2) geygey is a DELETED account (status.tsv: live ids=125541 alive=1 gone=125540) reconstructed from WAYBACK/CDX (cdx pages=10 captures=147460 ids=125541). So its 2023-H2 gap = a window Wayback never archived => UNRECOVERABLE, capture is complete-as-recoverable there. (3) fc1943s: 1585 alive + 1746 gone, low-volume, raw ~46 in 2023-H1 and none in H2 (plausibly real inactivity for a low-volume acct). (4) ALL accounts resume at exactly 2024-05 => that is the DENSE recovery method's start boundary; pre-2024-05 history comes from the sparse Wayback/CDX method. NET: the archive is Wayback/CDX (deleted+older, sparse) + a dense live/snaps method (2024-05+). Most gap tweets are likely UNRECOVERABLE (deleted & not archived, or live-but-past X's ~3200 API limit), NOT a harvest bug. ACTIONABLE REMNANT: for the user's OWN accounts (fc1943s/#2/stewshka), the user's X data archive export (GDPR download) holds everything regardless of deletion/API/Wayback and would fill their portion of the gap if the user has/can request it. Corrected msg sent to main.")
inl note_of_x_dumps_77 () = Note ("X-DUMPS", 77u32, "2026-11-08T01:21Z", "x-dumps-ec368ac8", "xsim cgroup-OOM 11-07T21:02 (resilience flagged, k8s self-healed). The xsim-serve beam hit its 2048Mi CONTAINER limit during my autoplay redeploy's world-load peak (~1.5-2GB) while tibia's torch run held ~1.6GB -> node memory-starved -> cgroup-OOM (beam.smp killed, k8s restarted, now stable rss 597MB, serve+media 200). NOT a host OOM (host MemAvailable fine). DECISION: keep the 2048Mi limit (raising risks a real HOST OOM since node is 3.4GB with iggy 512Mi Guaranteed reserved + torch; cgroup-OOM of the Burstable serve is the safe failure mode). Can't cap beam heap (needs ~1.5GB for the 140MB world). MITIGATION: gate serve redeploys on node free-mem (scale up only when avail > ~2GB), same as pack61b/pack62 windows — the 21:02 trip was my reload peak colliding with torch. See [[xsim-k3s-deploy-sjc]].")
