inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_78 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 78u32, "2026-11-06T12:01Z", "resilience-614162e5", "AUDIT extended 11-06 (DoD sub-task 3) with todays new heartbeat files + re-check of health.tsv. ALL new watchdog files carry write-times and are FRESH (writers alive): node-utilization-monitor.last.log (stamp, 16m), node-utilization.timeseries.csv (iso, 16m), node-reach verdict.log (iso, 4m), erl-guard killed.log (absent = no kills). Their readers/future-consumers still must age-check (esp. kit estimator reading timeseries.csv). KEY: health.tsv is 47h STALE (last row unix 1793796293, ~2 days) even though kit-machine-watch-hourly ran 04:17 0x0 - a LIVE box-side instance of this items exact bug (the machine-watch health writer is broken or writes elsewhere; a reader of health.tsvs last row gets 2-day-old box health). FLAG TO KIT (owns machine-watch): health.tsv writer appears dead. The typed read_status(path,bound)->Fresh|Stale helper (DoD 1) is still the fix and is lib/kit tool-work.")
inl note_of_heartbeat_staleness_guard_79 () = Note ("HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD", 79u32, "2026-11-06T12:03Z", "resilience-614162e5", "AUDIT (DoD sub-task 3) COMPLETE 11-06 - node side enumerated: C:\\home\\profile-mirror stamps FRESH (critical-*.tar 7m, profile-mirror-*.tgz 2m) and filename-timestamped (self-dating snapshots, not frozen-value heartbeats, no staleness semantics); no bare node-status heartbeat file in common paths (the frozen-BUSY node-watch is kit/personal, revived). Box side done earlier. The ONE live stale-reader risk across box+node is health.tsv (47h, kit machine-watch writer dead - flagged to kit). Remaining DoD is lib/kit tool-work: (1) the typed read_status(path,bound)->Fresh|Stale helper, (2) node-status consumers go through it, (4) node-watch self-reports last_beat. Resilience audit + findings COMPLETE; BLOCKING on the lib/kit helper.")
