inl note_of_regex_publish_2 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:26Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x LICENSE FACT (main checked 11-02): spiral's LICENSE is the GNU AGPL v3 (upstream files may carry MPL-2.0), and C:\\home\\git\\regex has NO LICENSE file. The regex license (and a generated-code exception) stays the USER's decision; the crate name is still open (\"regex\" is taken on crates.io). Launch timing from the share (4edd442c tactics): not on a Rust release day; Rust 1.100 ships 2026-11-12.")
inl note_of_regex_publish_3 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 3u32, "2026-11-02T11:28Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x SOURCE: share re-read by subagent a7601c1fd3d1a599f, verbatim at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SOTA\\sota-reread-20261102.md (C = artifact cccab1fd, E = 4edd442c, N = d05f9cdd, S = 76f3f8a7). ADDITION 20 (verbatim): 20. REGEX-PUBLISH: launch rules. Cite RE# on the first screen. Claim \"faster than regex\" only where it holds on an intersection. Don't launch on a Rust release day (1.100 ships 2026-11-12). Order: lobste.rs or r/ProgrammingLanguages first, then HN, then r/rust; one headline per post. Before launch, fix --check silently dropping blocks, the ';' bug in multi-line records, and the two legacy negatives that fail for the wrong reason. Source: E §4, §7 #1, \"Campaign tactics\".")
inl note_of_regex_publish_4 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 4u32, "2026-11-02T11:54Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x USER 11-02: licenses and repo metadata are autonomous (\"things like this should always be autonomous\"); crate name \"i don't know about the crate name yet, regex-typed or something?\". DONE: LICENSE = GNU AGPL v3 copied from spiral (unstaged in regex, plus x, html, fiscal, kit, blog, i574n.github.io, dotfiles, which had none); bench/Cargo.toml license-file = ../LICENSE; README gained a License line and the bench page link; GitHub Pages enabled (gh-pages) and the description + homepage set. CRATE NAME, crates.io checked 11-02: regex-typed, typed-regex, regex-certified, certified-regex, regex-spiral, spiral-regex, regex-nerode, regex-proof are all FREE; working candidate \"regex-typed\" (the user's suggestion), final pick at REGEX-PUBLISH time. OPEN: whether the published crate keeps AGPL (generated code embedding the lib runtime inherits it) or gets a generated-code exception; record the choice here.")
inl note_of_regex_publish_5 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 5u32, "2026-11-02T14:02Z", "x", "2026-11-02 09:59 x: DECISION RECORD (typed; groom of user waits, user 11-02: 'decisions waiting on me get met autonomously/deterministically with tests/benchs/typefeatureset modeling/backups'). kind=ByDefault. QUESTION: Choose the crate name (crates.io 'regex' is taken), the license of the generated code, and the dialect id. RULE (written before acting): Name: the first of 'spiral-regex', 'regex-spiral' that 'cargo search' shows as free. Dialect id: the crate name plus the major version. License: the same license as the spiral repo, so the org stays consistent. All three are reversible until a crates.io publish. The actual crates.io publish and any license change after publishing are a later UserBoundary. BACKUP/UNDO: Names live in README and Cargo.toml unstaged; nothing is published OWNER: main. NEXT: Record the three defaults in the item and README draft, then run the D1-D12 audit when REGEX-AGREEMENT is green. VETO: the user can reverse this at any time; the backup above is the undo.")
inl note_of_regex_publish_6 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 6u32, "2026-11-03T07:24Z", "main", "main 11-03 03:1x CRATE NAME DECIDED (user 11-03: 'couldn't regex-proof be better than regex-typed? gotta deliberate considering the new pattern that might emerge of us rewriting a lot of crates in spiral to cannibalize'). crates.io 11-03 03:1x, all 404 = free: regex-proof, regex-typed, proven-regex, regex-proven, regex-certified, spiral-regex, json-proof, toml-proof, base64-proof.\nRULE, written first: the name must be (1) a FAMILY pattern that works for every crate we rewrite; (2) name the differentiator (certificates, laws, independent oracle), not something every Rust crate has; (3) unambiguous; (4) outside the original authors' namespace, since 'regex-*' is where rust-lang publishes regex-syntax, regex-automata and regex-lite, and 'tokio-*' and 'serde-*' work the same way.\nSCORES: regex-typed fails (2), because every Rust crate is typed. regex-proof passes (1) and (2) but fails (3) ('-proof' reads as resistant-to, as in waterproof: immune to regex) and (4) (it looks like an official rust-lang sibling). proven-regex passes all four: the family is proven-<crate> (proven-json, proven-toml, proven-base64), it names the guarantee, reads as one meaning, and sorts as our own family on crates.io.\nDECISION: proven-regex, family proven-<crate>. Reversible until the first publish; nothing is published. The README guarantee line is: every claim is a typed certificate checked at build time.")
inl note_of_regex_publish_7 () = Note ("REGEX-PUBLISH", 7u32, "2026-11-07T15:53Z", "main", "DoD: regex's Pages site publishes the bench report, the notebooks and (later) the state-space video from CI on push, with the 1000-line gate green.")
