inl machine_slot_caps () = Task ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", "Per-machine concurrent-job caps enforced by kit's job host, read from remote-machines.json (europa 1, node N, Cells-heavy jobs counted separately): tibia's void gates, x's 7.7x slowdown and europa's load 104 were all over-admission", Story, P0, D3, 70u32, Active, "-", "kit,remote,node,europa,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_machine_slot_caps () = Planning ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_1 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T22:00Z", "main", "main 18:0x: kit already wrote KIT_JOB_SLOTS (typed slot_cap = Unlimited | AtMost n; queued while full; test a_slot_cap_holds_new_requests_while_the_machine_is_full). x-dumps asks that the cap come from remote-machines.json ('slots: 1' for europa) instead of /etc/environment, so one file configures every machine; tibia and x ask for a node cap on concurrent Cells jobs (daemon-start failures at 7 jobs). DoD: node and europa run the new host; a burst of 10 starts queues instead of overloading (load stays under 2x cores); per-lane fairness is shown in node job list.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_2 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 2u32, "2026-11-01T23:41Z", "main", "main 19:4x from x-dumps: europa-status said 'jobs=0 AVAILABLE' at 19:40 while a gleam build + beam.smp compiles ran (load 13.6), because the watcher counts only kit jobs and builds started via 'remote.ps1 europa run' are invisible. Heal: the status line also carries load1 and a count of compiler processes (cargo/rustc/dotnet/beam.smp/gleam), AVAILABLE only if load1 < cores and no compilers run; and lanes launch compiles only through 'job start' (kit-counted), never 'run'. Also: europa sshd PerSourcePenalties locked out our IP at 19:3x because of remote.ps1's TCP probes (exempted by x-dumps; kit has the details).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_3 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T15:00Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 groom (AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT): parent epic GATE-HEALS-1101. It was this item's only dep, which hid the item from agile next because an epic finishes after its children; parents are named in notes, deps are for real ordering only.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_4 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 4u32, "2026-11-03T05:35Z", "kit", "11-03 01:3x kit groom, design for the json source (x-dumps' ask), not started:\n- The host side is done and typed: kit_job_host host_model slot_cap = Unlimited | AtMost n, may_take/admitted, test a_slot_cap_holds_new_requests_while_the_machine_is_full. host_run.spi:111 reads KIT_JOB_SLOTS.\n- ~/.config/remote-machines.json exists only on the box (remote.ps1's -Config), so a remote job host cannot look up its own entry.\n- Shape: give each machine an optional \"slots\" (europa 1, node N) and have remote.ps1's `job start` path export KIT_JOB_SLOTS=<slots> in the remote command. One file still configures every machine, with no /etc/environment edits.\n- Cells-heavy jobs count separately: a second key \"cellsSlots\" with KIT_CELLS_SLOTS needs a second slot_cap in host_run, keyed by job class (--heavy/--compile labels).\nUSER CALL NEEDED: adding \"slots\" to ~/.config/remote-machines.json is a persistent machine-config edit; proposed values: europa 1, node 6.\nThen: DoD burst test (10 starts queue; load under 2x cores) on europa after the remote.ps1 change.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_5 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 5u32, "2026-11-03T08:01Z", "kit", "11-03 03:5x kit PAUSE (main: the 21:00 run was armed early by mistake; run paused). PLAN (waits for the run AND a direct user yes; a peer relay is not user approval for persistent machine config): .bak ~/.config/remote-machines.json; add slots (europa 1, node 6); remote.ps1 job start exports KIT_JOB_SLOTS=<slots>; hooks-test-style test of the export; then the DoD burst on europa (10 starts queue, load under 2x cores). Note: europa heavy jobs are hung by KIT-WINDOWS-CLI-LINUX until that fix ships.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_6 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 6u32, "2026-11-03T09:01Z", "kit", "11-03 05:0x kit APPLIED under the user's direct order ('config and hook changes need to be autonomous with backups etc', 04:56). ~/.config/remote-machines.json gains slots: europa 1, node 6, laptop 6 (the same machine as node); cs-reimagined is unset, so no cap. Backup ~/.config/remote-machines.json.bak-slots-20261103-0459. remote.ps1 'job' verb prefixes $env:KIT_JOB_SLOTS='<slots>' when the target has slots; backup %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\remote.ps1.before-slots-*. PROOF: remote.ps1 europa job start slot-probe printed 'slots=1' inside the europa job (job-1793696409308-slot-probe). CAVEAT: the job host reads KIT_JOB_SLOTS when it starts, so a running host keeps its old cap until restart. NEXT (DoD): restart the cloud host (or wait for the TRAIN14c twin swap), then a burst of 10 starts on europa: 1 runs, 9 queue, load stays under 2x cores. Note europa --heavy jobs hang until KIT-WINDOWS-CLI-LINUX's process_alive fix ships.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_7 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 7u32, "2026-11-03T10:34Z", "kit", "11-03 06:3x kit: the europa burst DoD and the europa Linux kit rebuild are HELD at x-dumps' request until their geygey media stage finishes (about 2-3 h; the disk margin cannot take a +5 GB cargo target). kit builds on the box and node meanwhile.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_8 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T05:57Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:1x resilience EVIDENCE for the caps (resilience owns machine-health; ties BOX-BSOD-1103 f2). Tonight saturation data supporting per-machine slot caps: (1) BOX 16GB: ~7 lanes concurrent -> 100pct CPU + the 19:44 BSOD (0x7e under sustained 100pct); an unguarded BEAM once hit 51GB. The box needs a HEAVY-job concurrency cap (build/train/native), not just a memory cap, since the BSOD was CPU-saturation-triggered - recommend box heavy-slots low (1-2), CPU-light unbounded. (2) NODE (laptop): reported saturated repeatedly tonight; it is the train host. (3) EUROPA (2 CPU): load 10-16 tonight -> --check attestation timeouts (exit 3 VOID); cap concurrent europa checks ~2 and bound each (--timeout-ms 150000). (4) RED-TRAIN SIGNAL: TRAIN17b/18/19 all red tonight under heavy concurrency (TRAIN19 fast-failed ~5min); worth checking whether resource pressure (not only code) contributes - if so, slot caps keeping a train machine below saturation would raise train success. The user standing rule (11-04 node/europa first, box last) is the MANUAL version of these caps; this item makes them enforced. Caps read per-machine from remote-machines.json keyed by load class (light/medium/heavy).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_9 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T06:28Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:2x resilience NODE DISK SWEEP + floor alert (main/x alarm: node C: 8-9GB free, Erlang disk_almost_full - a disk-exhaustion cousin of the CPU/mem caps this item tracks). SWEPT resilience share: deleted node Temp\\wsl-crashes 1.04GB (10 WSL crash dumps, disposable; newest 02:20 = still growing). Node C: 8.65 -> 14.91GB free after (node self-cleanup / peer sweeps also helped). No stale resilience stage dirs on the node. FLOOR ALERT added (main ask): node-utilization-monitor.ps1 DISK-LOW threshold 5GB -> 15GB - the node raised disk_almost_full at 8.65GB, ABOVE the old 5GB alarm, firing too late; + ssh ConnectTimeout 8 -> 12 (at 8 the node-free check silently fails under saturation = no alarm exactly when the disk fills; per connecttimeout-8-fails memory). .bak in scratch, parse-clean, unstaged. At 14.91GB the monitor now DISK-LOWs correctly (node tight: remote-stage 20GB + remote 16GB + spiral-bin 10GB). ROOT-CAUSE FOLLOWUP: wsl-crashes recurs because WSL keeps being invoked (plain bash -> broken WSL -> crash dump) despite wsl-policy Block; dumps refill - lasting fix is stopping bash->WSL fleet-wide. A per-machine DISK floor belongs alongside the CPU/mem slot caps here.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_10 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 10u32, "2026-11-04T06:31Z", "resilience", "11-04 02:3x resilience wsl-crashes containment follow-up (no speculative edit). node-temp-contain.ps1 DOES target Temp\\wsl-crashes but: (1) `-Filter *.dmp` (L8) - may MISS the actual dump filenames (I deleted the 10 files, ~1GB, in the urgent sweep WITHOUT capturing extensions, so cannot confirm now - verify-before-acting slip: next node-disk event, dir the filenames BEFORE deleting); (2) it ARCHIVES (zips to NodeArchiveDir\\wsl-crash-dumps on the SAME C:, then removes originals) rather than deleting - for intentionally-Blocked WSL the dumps are expected noise with no diagnostic value, so archiving both fails to reclaim C: and bloats the off-box mirror (KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR). HYPOTHESES for the 1GB growth: the *.dmp filter missed them, OR node-temp-contain is not scheduled/frequent enough. RECOMMENDED data-informed fix (deferred, needs the filenames): if non-.dmp, broaden the filter to all files in wsl-crashes; switch wsl-crashes from archive->DELETE (blocked-WSL dumps are disposable); confirm it is scheduled. The real root cause remains bash->WSL invocations fleet-wide. For now the 15GB floor alarm (node-utilization-monitor) will catch refill.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_11 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 11u32, "2026-11-04T09:48Z", "kit", "11-04 07:4x kit: machine-owned cap BUILT (unstaged). kit_job_host reads its slot cap from KIT_JOB_SLOTS, else from <jobs root>/slots (host_paths.machine_slots_file; host_model.cap_text_of: a set env wins, test added). So each machine carries its own cap and nothing has to be forwarded over ssh. Written: node C:\\Users\\alias\\AppData\\Local\\kit\\jobs\\slots = 6 (same as remote.ps1's KIT_JOB_SLOTS) and europa ~/.cache/kit/jobs/slots = 1; both are inert until the host change ships in a train. Proof: europa pack34 --check exit 0 (host_run + host_model test bodies); box notebook host_model.spi 8/8 pass. Remaining for DoD: Cells-heavy jobs counted separately (a heavy-slot class), and a proof that the node host holds the 7th job.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_12 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 12u32, "2026-11-04T12:04Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x resilience BOX cap spec, grounded in the 11-03 BSOD root cause (BOX-BSOD-1103). Box = 8 logical CPUs (~4 physical HT), 15.9GB RAM (only ~3.5GB free now), shared by ~8 lane sessions + desktop + an EOL Quadro K1100M GPU. The 11-03 crash was OVER-ADMISSION: concurrent heavy jobs (pwsh builds, erl/BEAM, cc1) + desktop GPU load + SystemInformer saturated the box to sustained 100pct => nvlddmkm TDR => 0x7E BSOD. RECOMMENDED box heavy-slot cap = 2, with Cells/BEAM-heavy counted separately at 1: with ~3.5GB free and heavy jobs eating GB each, more than 2 concurrent risks RAM pressure + CPU saturation; and per heavy-runs-node-first-box-last the box should fill LAST, so a LOW box cap also enforces routing heavy work to node/europa. team:kit owns actually setting it (fleet-wide policy) - recommending, not setting unilaterally. This is the STRUCTURAL BSOD-prevention complementing the detection layer (hourly kit machine watch + idle/hog alarms).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_13 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 13u32, "2026-11-04T12:07Z", "kit", "11-04 08:5x kit (resilience flagged the box as uncapped after the 11-03 BSOD): wrote the box's %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\Local\\kit\\jobs\\slots = 3. It is inert until TRAIN22 installs the host code that reads it. SEMANTICS GAP found: the cap is per job-host instance. On the box each session's jobs run under its own host home (jobs\\hosts\\<pid>-<start>), and only owner-less starts (ssh, node) share one host, so slots=3 caps EACH session at 3 live jobs, i.e. up to ~3 x lanes machine-wide. The --heavy gate is already machine-wide (one heavy job at a time). REMAINING for the DoD: a machine-wide count (sum live children across hosts\\* before admitting), then box total ~6 with heavy 1, and Cells/BEAM-heavy as its own class.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_14 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 14u32, "2026-11-04T12:20Z", "kit", "11-04 09:2x kit, main's europa finding (load 26.9 on 2 CPUs; fiscal, main and kit all compiling at once): (1) a per-host heavy cap only governs work that goes through `kit job`; lanes' europa --check / build runs are raw `ssh europa dotnet ...`, so no job-host cap sees them. Fix direction: run europa checks as `kit job start --on europa --heavy` (REMOTE-EXEC-S4 dispatch + the slots file europa=1 written today) so they queue. Until then kit self-limits: one europa compile at a time from this lane. (2) Stopped 2 orphaned sleepers of kit's own \"job kill on close\" test on europa (pids 1816162/1816163, parent 1816046 gone, alive 15.5 h). That test leaked its children on Linux, i.e. kill-on-close did not reap them there. To investigate (KIT-JOB tests on linux).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_15 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 15u32, "2026-11-04T15:02Z", "kit", "11-04 12:5x kit: since 08:5x, the machine-wide count is BUILT (unstaged). Each job host records its running count in <host home>/live, and admission adds the live counts of every other host with a live pid (new linked crate kit_job_peers, live_elsewhere_of; host_model.counted_live tests), so <jobs root>/slots now caps the MACHINE, not each session. Sizes: kit_job_host 920, kit_job_peers 294. NEXT, heavy class (main: europa 2, node by cores): the --heavy path is the window gate in kit_job_gate (gate.rs already at 943 generated lines), so a heavy-count cap belongs in kit_window_admission behind a <jobs root>/heavy-slots file with the same peer counting. Not started today because of that crate's headroom.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_16 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 16u32, "2026-11-04T17:11Z", "kit", "11-04 17:4x kit: HEAVY CLASS cap BUILT (unstaged), in kit_job_slot (not the window gate, whose gate.rs is at 943). Correction to my 12:5x plan: heavy admission is not the window gate at all (HeavyBuild windows do not clash with each other); it is the heavy-queue ticket line in kit_job_slot, where a job runs only while its ticket is the FIRST live ticket and the Windows mutex probe allows it (1 slot, a 2nd when > 3 GB free; on Linux the probe always allows, so the ticket line alone serializes there). New: <jobs root>/heavy-slots (default 1, < 1 -> 1) and slot_turn = the ticket ranks within heavy_slots among LIVE tickets (live_rank skips dead pids) && slot_free; used at both admission sites (slot_loop and slot_held_step). With no file, rank 0 = the old first-live-ticket rule, so behavior is unchanged until a machine writes the file. Effect: europa/Linux heavy-slots=2 admits 2 heavy jobs; on Windows the shared spiral-heavy-job mutexes (compiler tooling protocol) still cap at 1-2, so a node cap above 2 needs that protocol to grow, a compiler-team change, not kit's. Test a_ticket_ranks_among_the_live_tickets_ahead_of_it (dead ticket ahead not counted, missing ticket = -1). europa --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-heavy-slots-tests.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_17 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 17u32, "2026-11-05T12:02Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 08:0x kit: design check on the last DoD clause (Cells/BEAM-heavy as its own class), read against kit_job_slot/slot.spi. The heavy class is one ticket queue (heavy_queue: KIT_HEAVY_QUEUE or <LOCALAPPDATA|HOME>/spiral-bin/heavy-queue), ranked within <jobs root>/heavy-slots among live tickets AND gated by slot_free = the compiler's Global\\spiral-heavy-job mutex probe (a 2nd slot when > 3 GB free). A Cells class cannot simply be a second queue: notebook runs also compile Spiral (CompilerDaemon), so they contend for the same compiler mutex, and a separate cells-slots count would admit a notebook beside a full regen, exactly the 11-01 VOID case (one Cells job per node). Proposed shape, not built: a job class union (Plain | Heavy | Compile | Cells) in kit_job_launch_model.shapes (the StartPlain/StartHeavy/StartCompile reflection heal already exists), where Cells takes a heavy ticket (so it serializes with regens) and also counts against <jobs root>/cells-slots (default 1), so two notebook batches never overlap while plain jobs stay unbounded. kit_job_slot is legacy host-text (13 $' blocks), and touching it means migrating, so this slice is: (1) boundary module for the ticket file primitives, (2) the class union, (3) cells-slots counting. Parked until TRAIN29 installs (slot.rs size headroom unknown until then).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_18 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 18u32, "2026-11-05T12:30Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 08:5x kit: step (1) DONE in the tree (unstaged): kit_job_slot's ticket code migrated off host text. heavy_queue reads env.get_environment_variable (KIT_HEAVY_QUEUE, else LOCALAPPDATA|HOME + spiral-bin/heavy-queue via joined); ticket_pid/ticket_ticks parse in Spiral (split_list over the name minus .t; unparsable = 0, as before); ticket_created = ticket_name (workspace_primitives.clock.dotnet_ticks ()) own_pid, same {:020}-{pid}.t format the compiler tooling shares; tickets_sorted is now a list string (kit_os_files.entry_names, .t filter, am'.sort) instead of a newline-joined string, so first_live_ticket/live_rank are list matches; ticket_written/ticket_renamed use kit_jobs directory_created/text_written and lib file_system.rename. New boundary primitives only in workspace_primitives/clock.spi (since_epoch nominal, epoch_elapsed, whole_seconds, sub_second_nanos; dotnet_ticks arithmetic in Spiral). The Windows mutex probe (slot_free) stays the one legacy host block, untouched (a global would grow rust_global; baselines only shrink). Census kit_job_slot host-block 7 -> 1, host-statement 7 -> 1; ratchet ok (kit_job_slot, workspace_primitives). Box --check ok; box notebook job-1793881634465 running (old 3 tests + a_ticket_name_reads_back_its_ticks_and_pid). DESIGN CONCLUSION on the Cells class: with heavy-slots = 1 (box, node default) any --heavy job already serializes with regens through this ticket line, so a separate cells cap only matters where heavy-slots > 1, and it needs a per-ticket class marker; ticket names are shared with compiler tooling and ticket contents already mean 'owner id', so that is a protocol change, not a kit-only slice. The live gap is simpler: notebook batches start PLAIN (all of kit's today show gate=plain) and bypass the queue entirely. Cheapest fix: notebook-batch callers pass --heavy (a policy line in the gate hint, or kit job start defaulting notebook-batch.ps1 programs to heavy). Proposing that to main rather than growing the protocol.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_19 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 19u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit: KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION generalizes these counts to resource needs (p95 memory, cores, p90 wall) learned from kit archive job evidence and enforced per OS. This item stays the count cap and ships first; the job class union proposed in n-1 (Plain | Heavy | Compile | Cells) becomes the job kind key of that estimator.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_20 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 20u32, "2026-11-06T08:26Z", "fiscal", "Star exchange 11-06 (merged by fiscal): three lanes hand-rolled the same europa launch gate: tibia #1 and kit #2 (load < 4 AND MemAvailable >= cap + 600 MB, systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=.. -p MemorySwapMax=0; remote.ps1 swallows -p as -Publish, so it lives in a sh wrapper), and fiscal ~/remote-stage/fiscal-check/eff-run.sh + eff-lib-run.sh (1500 MB floor). Ask: one kit-owned europa launch verb that applies the gate and the scope, so lanes drop their wrappers. A cap above MemAvailable protects nothing (kit 17:52, tibia twice).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_21 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 21u32, "2026-11-06T08:26Z", "x", "2026-11-06 04:2x x: CROSS-LANE (star exchange 11-06): kit n2, tibia n1 and main n6 all hit europa thrash or OOM from a MemoryMax above MemAvailable. Ask kit to fold the launch gate into this item: refuse a cloud launch unless load < 4 AND MemAvailable >= cap + 600 MB, and put systemd-run inside the sh wrapper (remote.ps1 eats -p). Owner: kit.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_22 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 22u32, "2026-11-06T10:48Z", "resilience", "part of SMART-DELEGATION: remote-machines.json should carry live SPECS + headroom per machine, not only concurrency caps - box 16GB/saturated, lap 16GB/idle, europa 2c/3.4GB (mem-ceiling ~2.5GB), codespace. DELEG-ROUTER reads specs+caps from here to place jobs.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_23 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 23u32, "2026-11-06T21:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 17:1x kit: the star-exchange ask (one kit-owned europa launch gate so lanes drop their wrappers) BUILT (unstaged): new linked crate kit_job_remote_gate (gate.spi: machine_gate {max_load_x100; floor_mb; memory_max_mb}; gate_rule GADT Ungated|Gated; admission GADT Admitted|TooLoaded|TooLittleMemory|SampleUnread; gated_dispatch {admitted; line; reason}). kit job start --on <machine> now reads <jobs root>/machines/<machine>.gate (a typed MachineGate row, data in config beside <machine>.bin, kit knows no machine), samples /proc/loadavg + MemAvailable over ssh, refuses with exit 3 (kit job start busy machine=.. reason=admission: load 4.20 >= 4.00 | MemAvailable X MB < cap + floor Y MB | unread sample), and when admitted wraps the remote job start in systemd-run --scope --quiet -p MemoryMax=<cap>M -p MemorySwapMax=0 (inside the remote line, so remote.ps1 never sees -p). Ungated machines (node) are unchanged. Config written: %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/jobs/machines/europa.gate = MachineGate (400i32, 600i32, 1800i32). Test a_gated_machine_admits_under_its_limits_and_wraps_the_job_in_a_memory_scope. Node probe job-1793999589636-kit-probe1106w (gate + dispatch notebooks, sizes). Rides the train after TRAIN35; then lanes switch from their sh wrappers to kit job start --on europa.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_24 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 24u32, "2026-11-06T21:51Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 17:5x kit: probe job-1793999589636-kit-probe1106w: kit_job_remote_gate/gate.spi 1/1 and kit_job_dispatch/dispatch.spi 3/3 GREEN on the node; gate crate 822 lines, BUT kit_job_dispatch grew 958 -> 1585 generated lines, so the gate call is most likely INLINED, not linked (gated_line returns a bare record; every export that linked today returns a primitive or an array_base of records). Held out of TRAIN35 (archived in drafts/kit/TRAIN36-HOLD-1106-split-topology) and back in the tree after its snapshot. Probe kit-probe1106x greps dispatch -> gate; fix if 0: return array_base gated_dispatch (one row), the shape kit_net_topology fleet_report already crosses with.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_25 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 25u32, "2026-11-06T23:05Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 19:1x kit: SIZE FIXED: returning the gate decision as a one-row array_base gated_dispatch (instead of a bare record) makes the call LINK: kit_job_dispatch 965 generated lines (was 1585 with the inlined gate), kit_job_remote_gate 821, 2 kit_job_remote_gate:: refs in dispatch (probe job-1794004492358-kit-probe1106y). Notebooks gate 1/1, dispatch 3/3 (probe w). Ready for the next train; memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes updated (bare record returns inline).")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_26 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 26u32, "2026-11-07T15:50Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom markers. NEXT STEP: the europa launch gate (kit_job_remote_gate, proven 11-06: gate 1/1, dispatch 3/3, dispatch 965 lines, linked) rides the next kit train; after install, lanes switch their sh wrappers to kit job start --on europa and the gate reads machines/europa.gate. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: machine_gate record, gate_rule GADT (Ungated, Gated), admission GADT (Admitted, TooLoaded, TooLittleMemory, SampleUnread), decision returned as array_base gated_dispatch so the link holds.")
inl note_of_machine_slot_caps_27 () = Note ("MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS", 27u32, "2026-11-09T03:10Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:3x rig STATUS: the europa launch gate (kit_job_remote_gate, machines/vultr.gate) has been INSTALLED since TRAIN41 and earned its keep tonight: cap 1800 refused small jobs on an idle europa -> 800 at 20:01 -> 1536 at 01:2x after the pluto cutover (fiscal-eur-certs admitted and running). Slot caps: the host reads <jobs_root>/slots (KIT_JOB_SLOTS overrides); ganymede had NO slots file (its 2-slot behaviour came from defaults), now /root/.cache/kit/jobs/slots = 2 written explicitly; comet has none yet. NOT adding a ganymede.gate: a gate refuses at submit (exit 3), while org asked that load admission HOLD jobs (KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR item 5); ganymede sat at load 9-11 on 4 cores at 02:3x with 2 slots, which is fan-out per job, addressed by machine defaults.env (KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR item 4, in TRAIN42) once ganymede kit is rebuilt from that tree. Left for this item: Cells-heavy jobs counted separately, and slots read from the registry instead of a hand-written file. Next step: after TRAIN42, rebuild the ganymede kit and write its defaults.env with the org pins.")
inl proof_scope_by_imports () = Task ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", "Consumer proofs rebuild only what a change can affect: the import-graph closure of the changed files, plus input-hash skips for generators (tibia 129 min for 6 changed files of 66; fiscal 81 units serial for 90+ min; x verify ~3 h; compiler Gleam proofs 30 min vs 3 in batch mode)", Story, P0, D4, 80u32, Blocked, "", "proofs,cells,node,all-lanes,team:org,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_proof_scope_by_imports () = Planning ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_1 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 1u32, "2026-11-01T22:00Z", "main", "main 18:0x: generic part (main + kit): a typed import-graph walker over package.spiproj/spiral.toml and :< imports that returns the closure for a changed-file set (one walk, reused by GRANULAR-STATS' definition walker). Per-lane parts: tibia build.ps1 -Files closure; fiscal per-family generator tests in parallel, skipped when schema+lib+generator hashes are unchanged; x verify -Part by changed paths; compiler proof scripts use test.ps1's Gleam batch mode. DoD: tibia's keyed_rng-sized change proves in under 20 min; fiscal's unchanged families are skipped and reported as such; a full run stays available as an explicit flag.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_2 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T00:14Z", "main", "main 20:1x DESIGN of the generic part (implementation = a kit verb in Spiral, not a pwsh tool; owner kit or main after the run). 'kit proof scope <repo> [--changed <paths>|--since <rev>]' prints the minimal proof set.\nINPUT MODEL (read through kit's Spiral manifest reader, typed): package.spiproj and spiral.toml both give (a) modules in order (a module sees only EARLIER modules plus its package deps), (b) packages: deps such as '../../src-' (sibling packages), 'spiral-' (lib) and '|core-'. Example: tibia's apps/arena depends on ../../src-, so any change in src/ re-proves arena.\nCLOSURE RULE (deterministic): changed file f in package P at position i -> affected = modules of P at positions >= i, plus every package that depends on P (transitively), all modules. A lib change -> every repo package with 'spiral-', narrowed to modules that import the changed lib module when imports are explicit (:< m / open). Generated outputs (Rust crates, Gleam parts) follow their owner package.\nOUTPUT: typed rows (Package, Module, Reason = Changed | LaterInPackage | DependsOn of package | LibImport of module), printed as a table, plus '--files' for build.ps1 -Files and lib_gate -File lists.\nTYPEFEATURESET: Reason as a closed union; the package graph as a nominal over a map with a phantom 'Validated' tag (no cycles, all deps resolved) produced by one constructor, so the closure function can't run on an unvalidated graph.\nDoD for this part: on tibia, a change to src/keyed_rng-like files selects ~12 of 66 modules (tibia's estimate) and the -Files build passes the same tests as the full build on that commit; on lib, a one-module change lists only the importing consumer modules.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_3 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:29Z", "main", "main 11-02: parent epic is GATE-HEALS-1101; recorded here instead of as a dep, because a dep on an unfinished parent epic hid this item from 'agile next' (backlog-wide sweep: 67 open items depended on an unfinished Epic).")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_4 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:43Z", "main", "11-04 08:0x main DRAFTED the generic closure core as a pure Spiral module in the KIT DRAFTS STORE (not kit's tree, which is mid-TRAIN22): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS\\src\\import_scope.spi + package.spiproj. Parts: manifest_of reads package.spiproj (packageDir, packages with |core-/spiral-/../../src- normalized, nested dir/ modules flattened to engine/b in order, CRLF-safe); normal_path collapses ./.. and backslashes so every package has ONE key; package_of resolves deps (relative from the package, bare names under packageDir); scope_of pkgs changed-paths applies the n2 rule (longest-prefix package; modules from the earliest changed index; transitive dependents all modules; an unknown file such as package.spiproj means the whole package; outside paths add nothing). 6 tests on a tibia-shaped fixture (src, arena -> ../../src, lone, lib). Gate PENDING on europa. LEFT for kit: the verb (kit proof scope <repo> --changed|--since) = discover package.spiproj files under the repo + git diff names + print scope_lines; spiral.toml prelude-only manifests (lib) need modules from the file list.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_5 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:50Z", "main", "11-04 08:5x main EUROPA (spiral head 98e51d32 + draft): import_scope.spi notebook gate 6/6 passed, 0 failed, first try (manifest parse with nested modules and CRLF, path normalization, later-modules + dependents closure, leaf change stays local, lib change reaches every lib user, manifest/unknown file = whole package, outside path = nothing). The closure core is PROVEN; handed to kit for the verb wiring (n4 LEFT). Scratch copy on europa removed.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_6 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 6u32, "2026-11-05T11:09Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 07:1x kit: the verb main handed over is WIRED (unstaged): kit repo scope <root> --changed a,b[,...] (repeatable) prints one line per proof set (<package dir>: <modules to reprove>) and then 'kit repo scope ok packages=N proof_sets=M'. Exit 2 on bad words. Layout after two size rounds: NEW crate kit_proof_scope_manifests (manifest.spi = main's parser + path normalizing; manifest_walk.spi = package.spiproj walk skipping target/.git/node_modules/.cache/_build/deps/bin/obj, depth 8; exports manifests_under root -> array_base manifest_row {dir; deps; modules} and anchored_paths (root, changed)). kit_proof_scope keeps main's closure (import_scope.spi, now fed by rows) + scope_run.spi (args, print) and links the manifests crate. Routed via kit_cli_routes RepoScope. NODE: sizes manifests 761, scope 961, routes 217 (first cut 1393, then 1475 because a curried export inlined; exports must take a tuple or a single argument). cargo check -p kit-proof-scope -p kit-proof-scope-manifests exit 0. BOX: first-cut notebooks green (import_scope 6/6, scope_run 1/1, routes 1/1); re-cut set job-1793876386435 running (manifest.spi 2/2 so far). Rides TRAIN29.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_7 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 7u32, "2026-11-06T08:21Z", "main", "main 11-06 04:2x groom (untouched >12 h): BLOCKED on kit install: kit repo scope is wired but unstaged and rides TRAIN33 (launched 11-05 20:43). Resume = run kit repo scope --changed on the 11-05 lib diff once TRAIN33 installs.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_8 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 8u32, "2026-11-06T14:43Z", "main", "main 11-06 14:5x UNBLOCKED: TRAIN33 installed kit repo scope at 07:05. Probed: x --changed apps/sim/spi/sim_serve.spi -> 1 proof set, 14 modules (sim_serve, timeline_serve, sim_publish, ...); regex --changed src/syntax.spi -> 2 proof sets (src: syntax plus the 14 importers; bench: host, bench). GAP: in spiral, --changed lib/spiral/sm'.spi (relative or absolute) -> 1315 packages, 0 proof sets, though nearly every package imports sm'. Consumers reach the lib through packageDir ('spiral-' under packages:), which the scope walk doesn't follow, and lib changes are the most common case. DoD: a lib change yields the consumer proof sets across the repos (apps, samples, other repos' packages via their packageDir), and one real lib land is proven with only its scope instead of a full rebuild, timed against today's. Next step: kit extends the walk to packageDir packages (sent to kit).")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_9 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 9u32, "2026-11-06T14:51Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 11:0x kit, from main 10:5x (lib change gives proof_sets=0): ROOT CAUSE: the manifest walk (kit_proof_scope_manifests/manifest_walk.spi manifests_in) reads only package.spiproj, and lib/spiral is described by spiral.toml (packages = [...], a prelude list, no module list: every .spi file under it is a module), so the lib package never entered the graph and no consumer could be reached from a lib file. The same blind spot hides every other spiral.toml package. FIX (unstaged): manifest_file GADT (SpiprojFile | TomlFile); a directory with spiral.toml becomes a manifest row whose deps come from packages = [...] resolved through packageDir like the spiproj path, and whose modules are every .spi file under it (recursively, skipping target, .git, deps and the other skipped names); a unit test reads both quote kinds and the package dir. NEXT: node notebook + cargo check in the next probe, then dogfood kit repo scope C:\\home\\git\\spiral --changed lib/spiral/sm'.spi after the train installs it (expect proof sets for every package whose spiral- entry resolves to lib/spiral, in this repo and in repos whose packageDir points there).")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_10 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 10u32, "2026-11-06T17:43Z", "main", "main 11-06 17:4x REAL CASE for this item: fiscal's lib land at about 13:15 (xpath_eval 049AED14) closed green, because lib_land_window's consumer check only compiles apps/spiral, but it breaks alphabet/apps/documents/documents.spi:88 (ETY-TermError; bisected: committed lib + only the live xpath_eval fails, every other live lib diff passes, the pre-pack55d compiler fails the same way). A scope walk that follows packageDir into other repos' packages would have named alphabet/apps/documents as a consumer to compile. Told fiscal with the repro.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_11 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 11u32, "2026-11-06T17:57Z", "main", "main 11-06 13:5x RETRACTION of the 13:43 'REAL CASE' note: fiscal's xpath_eval land does NOT break alphabet documents. Fiscal's A/B on the box (core 257DB91886B97811) gives exit 0 and an identical 946405 B for each of: the committed lib, committed + the live xpath pair, and the whole live lib. My bisect swapped in one file at a time, so 'committed + xpath_eval only' lacked the table_lookup that xpath.spi gained in the same land. That is an unbound name the FatalError summary hid. The whole-tree red was most likely sampled mid-land (lib_land_window writes live before closing). Method fix: bisect co-landed lib files as a set (the land's draft list), and read the trace (SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE / full log), not the summary. The item's premise (a close that compiles only apps/spiral can't prove other repos' consumers) still stands, but has no live case.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_12 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 12u32, "2026-11-06T18:26Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 14:2x kit: the spiral.toml fix grew kit_proof_scope_manifests to 1103 generated lines, so it is SPLIT: new linked crate kit_proof_scope_toml (toml_package_of ((dir, text)) returns an array of {package_dir; packages; modules}, records in an array so it crosses the link; reads packageDir and packages = [...] with either quote kind, and lists every .spi under the dir as a module); manifest_walk keeps resolution (dep_name, resolved) and a manifest_file GADT (SpiprojFile | TomlFile) picks the reader. Also fixed: a binding named exists failed to parse (exists is the existential keyword). PROOF node probe job-1793982955768: toml.spi 1/1, manifest_walk.spi 1/1, kit_proof_scope_manifests 860 lines, kit_proof_scope_toml 493 lines, manifests links into toml. Ships in TRAIN35; then dogfood kit repo scope C:\\home\\git\\spiral --changed lib/spiral/sm'.spi.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_13 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 13u32, "2026-11-07T17:50Z", "main", "main 11-07 14:0x DOGFOOD of the installed `kit repo scope` (after TRAIN35/36): the walk finds packages (1321 under C:\\home\\git\\spiral, 25 s), and a changed APP file maps to its package (apps/spiral/spiral.spi -> proof_sets=1, 'C:/home/git/spiral/apps/spiral: spiral'). Every changed LIB file maps to NOTHING: lib/spiral/pickler.spi, lib/spiral/sm'.spi (kit's own example), absolute, backslash and bare-name forms all give proof_sets=0. Yet pickler.spi has known consumers (pickler_derive.spi, pickler_real.spir, apps via the spiral- package). So the reverse edge from a lib module to every package whose packageDir + packages include it is missing, and that edge is this item's core. Real stake today: spiral's scheduled cells-gate went red on a lib rename (pickler size -> count) whose .spir consumer no landing gate compiled. DoD addition: `kit repo scope <root> --changed lib/spiral/<m>.spi` lists every package that resolves <m> through packageDir (spiral- / core- includes), across repos when given C:\\home\\git. Next step (kit): add the lib-module -> consuming-package edge from manifest_walk's resolved packages list, and test it with pickler.spi (expect apps/spiral and the lib package itself at least).")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_14 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 14u32, "2026-11-08T04:29Z", "main", "main 00:2x ROOT CAUSE of the 11-07 dogfood (proof_sets=0 for lib files): the spiral.toml reader NEVER SHIPPED. Installed %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe = 11-06 07:05 (same bytes as kit/kit.exe), but kit_proof_scope_toml was written 11-06 14:2x and is still untracked in kit/src (manifest_walk.spi, Cargo.toml, package.spiproj unstaged). Proof: kit repo scope C:/home/git/spiral/lib/spiral --changed pickler.spi -> packages=0; C:/home/git/regex/src --changed dfa.spi -> packages=0 (both spiral.toml roots); C:/home/git/spiral/apps/spiral --changed spiral.spi -> packages=1 proof_sets=1 (spiproj root). The 11-07 note's 'missing reverse edge' is likely wrong: import_scope.grown/closed_over already follow deps, and apps/spiral's resolve to C:/home/git/spiral/lib/spiral, the toml row's dir. Next step (kit): ship kit_proof_scope_toml in the next train (after AGILE-WAITS-AS-DATA), then rerun the pickler.spi dogfood expecting apps/spiral + lib/spiral; only if that still gives 0, add the reverse edge. Told kit.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_15 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 15u32, "2026-11-09T04:11Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 00:2x VERIFY after TRAIN41 (installed kit 93BDD93F): the spiral.toml reader SHIPPED; the 11-08 root cause is fixed. Same probes as the 11-08 note: kit repo scope C:/home/git/regex/src --changed dfa.spi -> packages=1 proof_sets=1 (was 0); C:/home/git/spiral/lib/spiral --changed pickler.spi -> packages=2 proof_sets=2 (was 0). CROSS-REPO DoD addition MET: kit repo scope C:/home/git --changed spiral/lib/spiral/pickler.spi walks 2847 packages -> 43 proof sets across repos (x apps/sim + apps/client, blog, i574n.github scripts/pins + apps/graphs + examples/typed-units, kit infra, ...). Caveats for the next step: (a) the root C:\\home\\git also reaches non-org trees (singularity playground, the duplicate eoie checkout of rig); a scope over org repos should skip them (the fork/non-ours list); (b) precision is unverified from names: x/apps/client lists ~15 modules, and I can't tell whether each truly imports pickler (import closure) or the whole package is listed because it includes the spiral- lib. LEFT: the DoD's 'one real lib land proven with only its scope instead of a full rebuild, timed against today's' + the tibia keyed_rng-sized run. Progress 65 -> 80. Next step: on the next lib land (e.g. REGEX-LIB-3-DFA), run its consumer gate from kit repo scope's set and time it against the full consumer run.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_16 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 16u32, "2026-11-09T08:11Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 05:4x PRECISION CHECK (caveat b) FAILS SAFETY, not just precision. Installed kit (TRAIN41), kit repo scope C:/home/git/regex/src --changed <m>, compared with the TRUE import closure (fixpoint over the :< lines of all 12 regex/src files): changed dfa -> kit: dfa laws lazy minimal partial semantics table typed witness xsd; true: agreement certificate dfa lazy minimal partial semantics table typed. MISSED agreement + certificate (both open dfa directly: unsafe, their proofs would be skipped); EXTRA laws witness xsd (open only lib regex_* modules). Rule that fits all 4 probes (agreement -> all 12; certificate -> certificate..xsd; dfa -> dfa..xsd; minimal -> minimal partial semantics table typed witness xsd): kit returns the changed module plus every module whose NAME SORTS AFTER it, i.e. for spiral.toml roots it treats ALPHABETICAL order as dependency order and reads no imports. Even the declared spiral.toml order (dfa lazy minimal certificate table partial agreement semantics laws witness xsd typed) would be a safe superset; alphabetical is not. Blocker: rig fix in kit_proof_scope_toml (module order from the modules = list, or better the :< import closure as the item title says). Until then no lib land may be proven by scope alone. Reported to rig 11-09 05:4x.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_17 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 17u32, "2026-11-09T08:12Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "Blocker: rig fixes kit repo scope for spiral.toml roots (alphabetical order used as dependency order; see note 16). Next step: after the fix ships, rerun the 4 regex/src probes against the import closure (expect dfa -> agreement certificate dfa lazy minimal partial semantics table typed), then prove REGEX-LIB-3-DFA's land with its scope only.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_18 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 18u32, "2026-11-09T08:20Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 04:2x local rig, SAFETY FIX (stopgap) for org2's report: for spiral.toml roots rig_proof_scope_toml took modules from a directory listing (alphabetical), so --changed dfa.spi in regex/src skipped agreement and certificate (both open dfa). Cause: toml_package_of ignored the declared modules block. Fix in src/rig_proof_scope_toml/toml.spi: declared_modules reads the modules = triple-quoted block in declared order (dependency order, so the earliest-changed slice is a safe superset: dfa now reproves all 12 regex modules); the directory listing stays only as the fallback when no block exists (still unsafe ordering there; flagged). New test declared_modules_keep_their_dependency_order_not_the_alphabet; kit standards 0 findings; toml.rs 501 lines. Ships in TRAIN43 (TRAIN42b snapshot predates it). NEXT (the item title): the import closure from the :< opens (fixpoint), which drops laws, witness and xsd from a dfa change and covers the no-block fallback.")
inl note_of_proof_scope_by_imports_19 () = Note ("PROOF-SCOPE-BY-IMPORTS", 19u32, "2026-11-09T09:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:1x moon probe rig-probe1109j: src/rig_proof_scope_toml/toml.spi GREEN 2/2 (incl. declared_modules_keep_their_dependency_order_not_the_alphabet). Rides TRAIN43.")
inl warm_mirror_caches () = Task ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", "Node/europa mirrors stop starting cold: one shared node build cache keyed by source sha (compiler: 435-528 s cold vs 98-268 s on the box), a Cells memo store shared across mirror syncs (tibia: fresh mirrors start at memo_hits 0), one warm Cells build per mirror (tibia: lib_gate recompiled Cells' 44 .ex files per step), warm daemons in iterate mode (x-dumps: cold 5-14 min proofs)", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "-", "node,cells,cache,kit,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_warm_mirror_caches () = Planning ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_1 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 1u32, "2026-11-01T22:00Z", "main", "main 18:0x: kit owns the job/mirror plumbing; compiler shares how the box's shared cache and r2r cache are keyed. DoD: a second proof on a fresh mirror of the same tree shows memo hits and no Cells recompile, and a node candidate build reuses obj/bin across stage dirs (time measured before and after in one job).")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_2 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 2u32, "2026-11-02T15:00Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 groom (AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT): parent epic GATE-HEALS-1101. It was this item's only dep, which hid the item from agile next because an epic finishes after its children; parents are named in notes, deps are for real ordering only.")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_3 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 3u32, "2026-11-06T10:48Z", "resilience", "a delegation-cost input for DELEG-ROUTER: a node run is only worth offloading if its build cache is warm; the router should prefer a machine whose cache is warm for that source sha. part of SMART-DELEGATION.")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_4 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 4u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries CARGO-5 (Stable dev root and cache continuity). Why: shared build cache keyed by source sha so mirrors stop recompiling cold: same cache-continuity intent (was parked 10-24)")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_5 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 5u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. CARGO-5 · Stable dev root and cache continuity · P1 · ota, brz, ge2e · Open / Cargo fingerprints are path-sensitive, so rehydrating each version into a new path recompiled everything despite a shared target. Rehydrate versioned bundles into one stable development path (refreshing it only when it is older than the selected bundle) and keep versions external; take an exclusive lease on a shared target directory so concurrent gates neither contend nor leave a stale binary; validate the target's effective identity (Cargo.lock hash, logical root) before running, since a nominally shared target recompiled from scratch twice; persist a target-cache generation so a cold container can tell cache loss from source invalidation.")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_6 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 6u32, "2026-11-09T11:51Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 08:3x local rig, evidence + first measurement plan: tonight rig ran 9 single-notebook moon probes (1109h..1109q), each into a FRESH mirror name, so each synced a full spiral+kit mirror (~85 MB) and started Cells cold: elapsed 106-580 s for 1-4 notebooks under train load. PRE-REGISTERED A/B (rule written before data): after TRAIN42e lands, run the post-train probe batch (rig_code_index, rig_code_dup, rig_job_escaped, rig_agile_todo_rows, rig_machine_windows window_model) twice into ONE reused mirror name (rig-probe-warm), cold then warm, same notebooks, moon otherwise idle; report elapsed and memo_hits per run. Decision: warm <= 50 pct of cold -> rig probes always reuse one mirror name per lane (and node-proof gets a -Reuse default); otherwise the cost is not mirror warmth and this item looks at the Cells build (44 .ex recompiles) next. TypeFeatures: none yet (measurement).")
inl note_of_warm_mirror_caches_7 () = Note ("WARM-MIRROR-CACHES", 7u32, "2026-11-09T16:59Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 17:0x rig, from microblog via org2: the Cells result memo has been OFF for every notebook run since ~11-06 (notebook_cli.ex passes build_receipt nil and Memo.enabled? tested has_key?, true for nil): memo_hits 0 fleet-wide, prune never ran (33k entries vs a 5k keep on moon). Fix unstaged in spiral apps/cells. Consequences for this item: (1) every notebook and train timing since 11-06 is a COLD-memo number, so baselines are not warm ones; (2) the planned cold/warm A/B (probe batch run twice) must run AFTER the memo fix installs, and should separate the two caches it measures (the mirror's compile cache vs the Cells result memo), or it measures neither cleanly. rig's probe script sets SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO=0 explicitly, so its own numbers were cold by design.")
