inl planning_of_spiproj_retire () = Planning ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", Minutes 900u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_1 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:02Z", "kit", "Filed by kit 11-02 07:1x from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094; the user resumed it and asked that everything go to execution). USER: when spiral.toml exists, package.spiproj must be deleted; no item covered it (EDITION3-SPIRAL-TOML, Done, only made the compiler read either, toml first). Readers to switch first: (1) compiler host Program.fs:1200-1219 applyPackageDefaults reads only the nearest package.spiproj; (2) kit kit_repo_affected/graph.spi:26 and kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:401 parse packages: from spiproj (kit's part, team:kit); (3) the Cells new_project template still writes src/package.spiproj; (4) link: (COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS) must exist in spiral.toml too. Duplicates today: tibia (7 spiproj plus spiral.toml), x client (both). Use the dialect naming from birth (DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3: dialect = i574n), not 'edition 3'. DoD: all readers on spiral.toml; every non-core spiproj archived (scratch-archive, never deleted outright) per repo; upstream core keeps its own; the gate is on in kit standards.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_2 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:08Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 07:1x KIT INVENTORY (main's broadcast ask 2; no deletes). Scope today: kit/src has 492 package.spiproj and 0 spiral.toml, so the 'spiproj beside a spiral.toml' gate fires nowhere in kit yet; kit's part is to read BOTH (toml first) before any package migrates. PRODUCTION readers (must learn spiral.toml): (1) package graph: kit_repo_affected/affected.spi:13 (path) + graph.spi:26 (packages: section parse); kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:192, :235, :374, :382, :401 (packages: parse), :425 (import count), :557, :603-604 (owner discovery by spiproj presence); kit_standards/standards.spi:91,:98 (the lib-referenced rule reads spiproj); kit_product_diff/recovery_terminal.spi:24 (file-kind list). (2) BACKLOG STATE manifest, a different use: the agile backlog's own state/package.spiproj (modules: list of segment files) is read/written by agile_note_segments/segments.spi:29,59,63, agile_note_model/model.spi:280,335, agile_task_views/views.spi:46,97, agile_backlog_scaffold/backlog_scaffold.spi:19,102,113, kit_agile_lease/lease.spi:82,92 + package_inputs.spi:41; and bundle/archive policy names it (bundle_policy_domain/policy.spi:103-139, toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/receipt.spi:67-85, kit_bundle_zip/archive_terminal.spi:168). The backlog has no spiral.toml, so it's out of this item's rule until the state dir gets one; decide then whether state/ moves to spiral.toml too (one atomic migration: reader+writer+bundle policy together). FIXTURES only (move with their readers): contracts_entry/cli_driver.spi (16), kit_agile_contracts/agile_cli.spi (9), agile_task_views/views_cli.spi, kit_repo_affected/affected_cli.spi, kit_source_topology_core/terminal.spi:334,336,530,535, kit_coverage_smoke, kit_patch_mutation_contracts, kit_bundle_retention probes, kit_archive_contracts, retention_semantic_probe. Help text: command_spec_domain/direct_verbs.spi:60. Total 79 mentions in 24 files.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_3 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 3u32, "2026-11-02T11:13Z", "main", "main 11-02 GROOM ROUND RESULT (ask 2: package.spiproj readers by file:line; nothing deleted anywhere). ORDER: the compiler host goes first, because every lane's manifests are read by it. compiler host tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs hard-codes spiproj with no toml fallback at :54 openOwningProject, :162/:165 source graph owner/walk, :310 fingerprint relevance, :1248 applyPackageDefaults; already toml-first: core :33385/:34157/:34530/:29234, host :366/:754/:1372; core :33433 is the spiproj->toml migrator (reads spiproj by design). compiler scripts: try.ps1:19 (writes one), lib-usage-census.ps1:10, env.ps1:145, test.ps1:130, bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1:12, test-rust-exports.ps1:22. kit: n2 (79 mentions in 24 files; 492 spiproj, 0 spiral.toml in kit/src). CARVE-OUT: the backlog's own state/package.spiproj is a DATA manifest (5 agile packages + bundle policy read/write it): one atomic migration, out of scope until state/ has a spiral.toml. fiscal: src/generator.spi:6 (builds the text) and :37 (writes it into all 81 generated units) and :998 (test reads one); README.md:142; src/package.spiproj duplicates src/spiral.toml; generated headers say `--package .`, so Cells reads them. The generator must switch to emitting spiral.toml before fiscal can retire anything. tibia: scripts/predict.ps1:21,:22 (patch packageDir + module list), live/paths.ps1:14 (hashes .spiproj as a source), README.md:25-26; 9 spiproj beside spiral.toml. x: 0 readers in x code; apps/client and apps/sim/spi spiproj sit beside matching spiral.toml, deletable once the host is retired. x-dumps: no readers; kit/src/kit_k3s and k3s_manifest hold spiproj with no toml (rule doesn't fire). resilience: no restore/backup path reads spiproj; retirement is restore-safe.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_4 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 4u32, "2026-11-05T07:19Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:19 tibia: tibia's share DONE (unstaged): all 9 tibia package.spiproj files deleted (src, results, apps/arena, canary, host, map, offscreen, predict, suite), each beside an equal spiral.toml (normalized compare; predict's spiproj was already stale, missing gpu_plan). Proof: europa --check of the last module of every package, toml-only, 9/9 exit 0 with 0 diagnostics and a negative control (missing module, exit 5 with 2277/3113 diagnostics); node Cells build.ps1 (coins 5/5, cooldowns 8/9 with only the known node WGSL runner crash) and predict toml-only. The compiler host's relatedClosure and applyPackageDefaults still name package.spiproj, but tibia does not depend on them (diagnostics flow, edition 3 resolves). Details in TIBIA-1-0-DOD.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_5 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 5u32, "2026-11-05T10:05Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:0x compiler, HOST PART DRAFTED (main's 11-02 order: the compiler host goes first). The 4 spiproj-only sites in tmp/compiler/host/Program.fs now find the manifest toml-first through the core's spiproj_suffix (spiral.toml if present, else package.spiproj): openOwningProject (:54), the related-diagnostics closure owner and walk (:162/:165; schema already applies withDerivedModules), applyPackageDefaults (:1290; config already converts toml text). sourceGraphFingerprint (:344) now counts spiral.toml as relevant, so editing a toml manifest invalidates the compile cache (it did not before: a cache-reuse hole for toml packages). The two 'Cannot find package.spiproj' errors name both manifests. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\SPIPROJ-RETIRE\\1-host-toml-first.kitdo (7 replacements; pack44 host A1E7136E -> 7BA81BF6). Proof running (node compiler-pack45): a toml-only copy of package_default_int_i64 ('default-int = i64' in spiral.toml) compiled by the node's installed compiler vs the draft. Expect the draft to honour i64 and the control not to. PENDING.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_6 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 6u32, "2026-11-05T10:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:1x compiler, COMPILER SCRIPTS PART (unstaged): apps/compiler/scripts/env.ps1 gains Test-SpiralManifest (spiral.toml or package.spiproj in a dir) and Get-SpiralManifestText (package.spiproj read as before; for a spiral.toml it calls the compiler's --resolve-manifest, which prints the classic spiproj text form, so every existing section regex keeps working). Get-SpiralPackageDir accepts a core package with either manifest. test.ps1's 4 spiproj-only reads now go through them: families module list (:111), linked case unit discovery and packages/link sections (:127-128), frontier sample discovery (:333). Proof: both files parse 0; -Suite linked on the box, 11 rows, 11 pass (unchanged); on a toml-only package (pack45 probe: spiral.toml with default-int i64) Test-SpiralManifest True and Get-SpiralManifestText returns 'packages: |core- / default-int: i64 / modules: main', which the packages regex parses. Not changed: bench-cells-vs-matrix.ps1:12 (does not load env.ps1; it reads tests/families, still spiproj), lib-usage-census.ps1 (already toml-aware at :10), try.ps1:19 (writes a scratch spiproj on purpose), test-rust-exports.ps1:22 (copies a fixture's spiproj). The compiler's own sample and family packages can now move to spiral.toml without breaking the harness.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_7 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 7u32, "2026-11-05T10:14Z", "compiler", "11-05 06:1x compiler: HOST DRAFT PROBE = NOT DISCRIMINATING (recorded as such; the draft stays a candidate, not installed). Node compiler-pack45: draft host 200121C567FB451E (core 657DA4B1, same as pack44) vs the node's installed pack44 (host E441B6B3). Both compile the toml-only default-int = i64 package to C with int64_t, exit 0, 252 bytes. The core already reads toml package defaults itself, so applyPackageDefaults' spiproj-only lookup was not decisive here. Where the draft DOES change behaviour: (a) sourceGraphFingerprint now includes spiral.toml. The host reuses compilations only inside a long-lived process (CompilationCache(true) for the TCP server that Cells daemons use; batches with more than one job), so editing a toml manifest between two server requests can reuse a stale result on the control. (b) the related-diagnostics closure walk now finds toml packages (relevant only to the TypeErrors routing). (c) openOwningProject, which is env-gated by SPIRAL_HOST_OPEN_PROJECT. A discriminating proof needs a server session: one --server-tcp process, compile, edit spiral.toml (default-int i64 -> i32), compile again; expect the control to keep int64_t and the draft to switch. Next run, before this joins a pack. Node stage deleted.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_8 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 8u32, "2026-11-05T12:18Z", "compiler", "11-05 08:2x compiler: HOST DRAFT (pack45) SESSION PROOF, box. Bin core 657DA4B173F9D3D3 (= pack44) / host 200121C567FB451E vs control pack44 host E441B6B3. One --server-tcp per arm, compile, edit spiral.toml default-int i64 -> i32, compile again. Control: second compile 'cache-hit', i.e. spiral.toml is not in the fingerprint. Draft: 'unchanged', the cache is invalidated exactly as for package.spiproj, which gives 'unchanged' on BOTH arms in the same probe. So the draft brings toml to parity with spiproj; that was its purpose. NOT fixed by it, and pre-existing for both manifests: the recompiled output is still int64_t (fresh one-shot: int32_t), because the session never re-reads package defaults when only the manifest changes. Filed as COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST. The draft (1-host-toml-first.kitdo) is cleared to join the next pack.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_9 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 9u32, "2026-11-05T13:28Z", "compiler", "11-05 09:3x compiler: host draft 1 joins PACK49 (pack48 + this + COMPILER-SESSION-STALE-MANIFEST 1); one-shot parity vs pack48 linked 13/13, smoke 159 rows 0 diffs; installing on the box now.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_10 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 10u32, "2026-11-05T14:14Z", "compiler", "11-05 10:1x compiler: host program 1 (toml-first lookup, toml in the session fingerprint) SHIPPED in pack49 on box, node and europa.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_11 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 11u32, "2026-11-06T08:20Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:2x compiler GROOM: progress 0 -> 20. Host program 1 (toml-first lookup, toml in the session fingerprint) SHIPPED in pack49 on box, node and europa (11-05 10:1x). Next step, CPU-light: count the package.spiproj files still read by any corpus package (fiscal reports 2 references left on LIB-SPIRAL-TOML-MIGRATION).")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_12 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 12u32, "2026-11-06T10:13Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler: note for main (owner, team:main): I set progress 0 -> 20 at 04:2x from a loose --team compiler list. The fact holds (host program 1 shipped in pack49 on box, node and europa), but progress on a main item is main's call; revert it if 20 is wrong.")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_13 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 13u32, "2026-11-06T13:05Z", "compiler", "11-06 09:0x compiler: CENSUS of tracked manifests (git ls-files, box). package.spiproj vs spiral.toml: spiral 1304 (1284 are compiler samples under apps/compiler/tmp/samples, 20 elsewhere) vs 5; kit 596 vs 0; fiscal 0 vs 84 (done); x 2 vs 2; tibia 0 vs 9 (done); regex 2 vs 0; blog 1 vs 0; html 8 vs 0; dice 2 vs 0; alphabet 2 vs 0; polyglot 3 vs 0. The host reads spiral.toml first since pack49, so a migration is per repo and mechanical (manifest text -> toml); the bulk is spiral's 1284 sample manifests (compiler-owned, mechanical) and kit's 596 (kit-owned, mostly generated units, so the generator must emit toml first). NEXT STEP: split into per-owner children: SPIPROJ-SAMPLES-TOML (compiler, the 1284 samples: one converter run plus all-backend parity), SPIPROJ-KIT-TOML (kit generator), and a sweep item for the small repos (regex, blog, html, dice, alphabet, polyglot, x: 20 files).")
inl note_of_spiproj_retire_14 () = Note ("SPIPROJ-RETIRE", 14u32, "2026-11-07T22:18Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom. TypeFeatures: the manifest as one typed record read from spiral.toml by every tool; the gate refuses a package.spiproj beside a spiral.toml. Today: blog's root package.spiproj retired (converted, CI rehearsal green).")
inl x_gleam_manual_split () = Task ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", "split the x sim Gleam by hand into modeled link: units (like Rust crates) and retire x/scripts/gleam-modules.ps1, which text-splits one generated .gleam at 1000 lines after the fact", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "LINKED-UNITS-GLEAM-RECORDS", "team:microblog,microblog,gleam,linked-units,line-gate", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_gleam_manual_split () = Planning ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_1 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:02Z", "kit", "Filed by kit 11-02 07:1x from the S0 study subagent's execution packet (aaf2a6f472dc70094; the user resumed it and asked that everything go to execution). USER asked for this explicitly. Today: 45 .gleam files under apps/sim/frames/src and split/src come from the post-hoc text splitter. DoD: xsim_split/simframes are link: units with typed exports; gleam-modules.ps1 archived; the same sim e2e green; every module at most 1000 lines.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_2 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-11-02T15:30Z", "x", "2026-11-02 11:30 x: COST EVIDENCE for this item. A 17-line source cut (events.spi's copied json escape helpers replaced by json::quote, main's drift-sweep heal; proof x-sim-json2: sim build, client build and sim exit 0, 153 requests, RUST-OK 37+5) changed 2 generated lines out of 32,253 and kept the same helpers (quoted_119, plus controls_from 121 -> 120). The post-hoc text splitter still turned it into a 33-file diff (parts 5-37 and types), because one renumbered id ripples through every later part. Typed link: units would localize it to the events unit.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_3 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-11-08T12:43Z", "microblog", "Compiler answers 11-08 (user asked whether compiler-made splits are architecturally worse, and whether Gleam parallel build is benchmarked). (a) A compiler split keyed on source modules would NOT remove the ripple: peval specializes and inlines across modules into one program, and generated names (quoted_119, US7, closure numbers) come from per-compilation counters, so one early renumbering shifts every later name. Content-addressed ids would be a large codegen change and still touch every user of a changed helper. Linked units fix it structurally: separate compilations with their own name spaces, so an edit inside a unit leaves other units byte-identical unless the export changes. So this item (hand-modeled typed units, M2 layout) is the path; GLEAM-PARTS-EMIT is retired. (b) Gleam link: units work on pack61b (lgf2/lgf4/lgf6); host gap: each unit is its own Gleam package, so gleam.toml path deps are hand-written. (c) Not benchmarked: the only number is test.ps1 (40 programs as modules of ONE gleam build 5.5 s vs ~2.7 s per separate build), so per-invocation cost dominates at sim scale; source reading predicts P packages >= M modules in wall time. Compiler offered the node bench (same sim code both ways, 5 rotated rounds, A/A first).")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_4 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-11-08T12:57Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: PRE-REGISTERED RULE for the Gleam build-layout bench (microblog asked; the user asked whether Gleam's parallel build is confirmed by a benchmark). Written before any node timing.\nINPUT: the landed generated sim Gleam (47 modules, ~37k lines: frames 40 parts + types + entry, split 3 parts + types + entry), built 4 ways from the same files: M = one package with all modules; Mb = an identical copy of M (the A/A arm); P2 = today's 2 packages (frames with a path dep on split); PN = one package per module (48 packages, path deps following the import graph).\nSCENARIOS: cold (the local packages' build dirs removed, hex deps kept); entry edit (a pub fn appended to xsim_frames, which nothing imports); types edit (a pub fn appended to xsim_frames/types, which 40 modules import); no-op. 5 rounds, layout order rotated each round, one gleam build at a time, node quiet (QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST slot).\nRULE: a difference counts only if it exceeds the A/A spread (|M - Mb| / min) for that scenario. If P2 or PN beats M on cold or types-edit wall time by more than the spread, Gleam package splitting buys build parallelism, and the layout may weigh it. If neither beats M beyond the spread, splitting buys NO build speed: X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT chooses units for diff locality and the 1000-line gate only, preferring fewer packages.\nBox dry run (1 round, box loaded, NOT evidence, script proof only): every layout compiles the same 47 modules. Cold: M 17.6 s, P2 26.2 s, PN 46.3 s. Types edit: M 14.9 s, PN 44.7 s. A/A spread up to 38% under box load.\nNEXT STEP: run on the node in the booked window; the script is the compiler scratchpad gleam-shape-bench.ps1 with gsb-node-cmd.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_5 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-11-08T14:44Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: RESULT under note 4's pre-registered rule: splitting buys NO build speed. Node job-1794148762345-compiler-gleam-layout3 (gleam 1.19.2, 8 logical CPUs; precheck CPU avg 2%, 0 other pwsh jobs; 5 rounds, layout order rotated; the landed sim Gleam, 47 modules, ~37k lines). Medians in ms, wall time:\n- cold: M 3428, Mb 3561 (A/A spread 3.9%), P2 3987 (+16%, slower), PN 9890 (2.9x slower)\n- types edit (40 importers): M 3331, Mb 3386 (spread 1.7%), P2 3273 (-1.7%, within the spread, so a tie), PN 9505 (2.85x)\n- entry edit: M 684, Mb 658 (spread 3.9%), P2 671, PN 658 (all ties)\n- no-op: ~600 in every layout\nVERDICT: neither P2 nor PN beats M beyond the A/A spread in any scenario, so X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT picks units for diff locality and the 1000-line gate only, preferring fewer packages. WHY PN is slow: the gleam process itself used ~1.0 s CPU in every layout (cold M 1078 ms vs PN 1047 ms), so Gleam's type checking and codegen are the same work. The extra ~6.5 s in PN is per-package Erlang compilation outside the gleam process: about 0.14 s for each of the 48 packages. That confirms the source reading: packages build one after another, each with its own BEAM compile. The parallel part works within one package only. Scale: the whole sim builds cold in ~3.4 s, so build time isn't a layout driver here. A split into a few typed units (the M2 model unit + a few consumers) costs ~0.15 s per extra package, and diff locality pays for that. Raw TSV: compiler scratchpad gsb-node-bench.tsv. Script: gleam-shape-bench.ps1.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_6 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 6u32, "2026-11-08T15:01Z", "microblog", "GROOM (user 11-08: forecast Gleam parallel compilation from its source; keep the 1k split even if compiles get slower?). FORECAST: none on the roadmap. gleam-lang/gleam #156 Parallel compilation of Gleam (lpil, 2019: we already compile the generated Erlang in parallel) was closed NOT PLANNED on 2023-06-29 in favour of finer issues; no successor issue found. Current main: compile_dependencies (project_compiler.rs) is a sequential loop over toposorted packages; PackageModulesAnalyser::analyse (package_compiler.rs) type-checks modules in a sequential for loop; no rayon, threads or TODOs about it in either file. The nearest open item is #5653 (shared build directory for multi-package workspaces, 2026-04), which is about caching, not parallelism. Even if it landed, the bench shows gleam CPU ~1.0 s for the whole sim, so it would not move our numbers. Do not plan around it. DECISION PROPOSED: keep the manual split, because its value is the typed boundaries (M2, typefeatureset linkage), diff locality (one edit stays in its unit instead of the 33-file renumber ripple), split invariance and the 1000-line gate, and its measured cost is small at M2 granularity (2 packages +16% cold, tie on edits). Avoid one package per module (2.9x). OPEN QUESTION for compiler: can Gleam link: units be emitted as MODULES of one Gleam package (one gleam.toml, unit-prefixed module names) instead of one package per unit? That keeps every unit benefit and costs layout M (3.4 s, the fastest) instead of ~0.14 s per extra package, and removes the hand-written gleam.toml path deps.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_7 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 7u32, "2026-11-08T15:02Z", "microblog", "Compiler answer 11-08: YES, Gleam link: units can be MODULES of ONE Gleam package; the layout is the build script choice. Evidence: spiral_compiler.fs:36646, a linked call emits import {unit} and calls {unit}.{export} with no package qualifier; {unit} is the unit package dir name (spiral_compiler.fs:35555). Constraints: each unit dir name must be a valid unique Gleam module name (lowercase, underscores, no dashes); per-unit FFI files (.erl/.mjs) need unique names; generated internals (Us0, Uh0, closures) are module-scoped, so no clash; the unit graph and the Cells memo are Spiral-side, so unaffected; keep the rebuild-units-before-consumers order. Lost: Gleam-level import direction enforcement; the Spiral link: graph already fixes it and generated imports follow link:, so add a lint for hand-written Gleam/FFI only. One gleam.toml carries the union of hex deps (no hand-written path deps). PLAN SHAPE: typed link: units, emitted as src/<unit>.gleam of one package, cost = layout M (3.4 s, fastest measured). PENDING: compiler proof, lgf4 proto + app in one package on the BEAM, expect app main = 14, after pack62 installs on the box.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_8 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 8u32, "2026-11-08T15:14Z", "microblog", "SETTLED 11-08 (compiler): the one-package layout needs no extra proof. apps/compiler/scripts/test.ps1:176-193 already builds every Gleam linked row that way: each unit's generated <unit>.gleam is copied into ONE project (scratch/gleam-linked, one gleam.toml from tests/native-shims/gleam); the root unit goes in as main.gleam, the others under their unit names, plus *_ffi files; one gleam build, then gleam run -m spiral_run. linked_records, linked_union and linked_union_name_clash pass that way on every pack (pack62 linked 20/20). TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 takes the same rule (i574n tu example: one gleam.toml, tu_model/tu_io/tu_feature/tu modules). DESIGN for this item: typed link: units, emitted as src/<unit>.gleam of ONE gleam package; unique valid module names (unit dir names, lowercase/underscores) and unique FFI file names; no hand-written gleam.toml path deps; retire scripts/gleam-modules.ps1. Copy test.ps1:176-193 as the build recipe.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_9 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 9u32, "2026-11-08T15:19Z", "microblog", "Gleam compiler SOURCE audit (user 11-08: forecast from the actual gleam compiler). Partial clone of gleam-lang/gleam at a6a12021 (2026-11-07, v1.19.2). (1) Rust side: 0 uses of rayon, par_iter, crossbeam, thread::spawn or thread::scope anywhere; the only std::thread use is a sleep in hex/auth.rs. tokio (rt-multi-thread) and futures serve network work only: hex.rs, dependencies.rs, dependency_manager.rs, publish.rs, docs.rs, owner.rs, http.rs. compile_dependencies loops packages in sequence and PackageModulesAnalyser::analyse loops modules in sequence. (2) The ONLY parallelism is the Erlang side: compiler-cli/templates/gleam@@compile.erl starts one worker per BEAM scheduler to compile one package's generated Erlang. (3) History: across all commits, the only messages matching parallel/concurrent/rayon/thread are a CI concurrency limit (2025-02-26) and a spelling fix (2019); the changelogs mention concurrency once (v1.1, a bug fix for files compiled twice concurrently). (4) gh search: no PR on parallel compilation ever; the only issue is #156 (2019, closed not planned 2023). FORECAST: no work, no plan, no prep in 7 years; nothing to date a release on, so plan as if it never comes. At our scale it would not matter anyway (gleam CPU ~1.0 s for the whole sim).")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_10 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 10u32, "2026-11-08T16:08Z", "microblog", "Benefit stated by the user 11-08: less diff noise from changes. Today the post-hoc text splitter renumbers generated ids across every later part, so a 17-line source edit becomes a 33-file diff (note 2). Rust does not have this problem because its output is already cut into hand-modeled crates, each a separate compilation with its own counters. Typed Gleam units (each a separate Spiral compilation, emitted as src/<unit>.gleam) give Gleam the same locality.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_11 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 11u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: if Gleam auto splits give no gain, go back to the monolith until the manual splits land? Not measured yet: the bench compared one package of M modules vs packages, but NOT one single module (the monolith). Today's auto split already IS layout M (modules of one package, the fastest measured), and the only parallel step Gleam has (the Erlang compile) runs across modules, so a single 33k-line module would serialize it; a monolith also fails the 1000-line gate. ASK compiler: add a monolith arm (one module) to the same quiet bench. If the monolith is not slower, a temporary gate exemption for the sim is a valid choice; otherwise keep the auto parts until the manual units replace them.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_12 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 12u32, "2026-11-08T16:43Z", "microblog", "Monolith arm: compiler has arm ONE ready (one package, each package's raw single module) beside M, Mb and P2; prediction: cold about the same, but every edit recompiles the whole ~33k-line module, so edit time jumps from ~0.7 s to near cold. The raw pre-splitter output is not kept anywhere (gleam-modules.ps1:124-125 overwrites -Source in place), so microblog runs a compile-only node job (label x-gsb-mono-raw, launcher in the microblog scratchpad gsb_mono_raw.ps1) into C:\\home\\remote-stage\\gsb-mono\\ when i574n grants a slot.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_13 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 13u32, "2026-11-08T16:47Z", "microblog", "Raw pre-splitter output delivered to compiler: job-1794156339605-x-gsb-mono-raw (node, pack62 4524F0D8D0AABDEE, exit 0, 95 s, beside fiscal shards with i574n approval, compile-only, no timing value). C:\\home\\remote-stage\\gsb-mono\\frames\\xsim_frames.gleam 33,139 lines and split\\xsim_split.gleam 2,447 lines (equal to the splitter's reported input), plus the 4 *_ffi files. PENDING: compiler's quiet-slot bench ONE vs M vs Mb vs P2 decides monolith vs keep the auto parts until the manual units.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_14 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 14u32, "2026-11-08T18:10Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: MONOLITH ARM MEASURED (the user asked whether to go back to a Gleam monolith). Node job-1794160843905-compiler-gleam-mono, quiet precheck, 5 rotated rounds. ONE = the compiler's raw pre-splitter output as single modules (xsim_frames.gleam 33,139 lines + xsim_split.gleam 2,447, pack62, from microblog's job-1794156339605) in one package. Medians in ms:\n- cold: M 3366, Mb 3471 (A/A 3.1%), P2 3904, PN 9723, ONE 11464 (3.4x M)\n- types edit: M 3229, Mb 3383, P2 3445, PN 9422, ONE 11336 (3.5x)\n- entry edit: M 666, Mb 645, P2 646, PN 663, ONE 11442 (17x)\n- no-op: M 638, Mb 624, P2 641, PN 611, ONE 2722 (4.3x)\nVERDICT: the monolith is the WORST layout on every scenario, worst of all on edits. Every edit recompiles the whole 33k-line module, and Gleam's within-package parallelism has only one module to work on. Even a no-op build costs 2.7 s, because the cache check hashes the huge module. So do NOT go back to a monolith. The best measured layout is ONE PACKAGE WITH MANY MODULES (M); 2 packages cost +16% cold and tie on edits. The P2 result reproduces note 5 (+16% both times). Consistent with the user's direction: typed manual units as modules of one Gleam package. Raw TSV: compiler scratchpad gsb-mono-bench.tsv.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_15 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 15u32, "2026-11-08T18:11Z", "microblog", "DECIDED 11-08 (user question: back to the monolith?): NO. Compiler monolith arm, quiet node, 5 rounds: one raw module per package loses everywhere vs M (one package, many modules): cold 11.5 vs 3.4 s, types edit 11.3 vs 3.2, entry edit 11.4 vs 0.67 (17x), no-op 2.7 vs 0.64. Keep the post-hoc parts (they are layout M) until the typed units replace them; the target stays typed units as modules of one package. Stage dir C:\\home\\remote-stage\\gsb-mono deleted on the node.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_16 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 16u32, "2026-11-09T13:38Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 09:5x microblog, inliner audit (the CELLS-TYPED-UNITS n3 lesson applied to x): the sim's generated Gleam (frames + split, 47 part files, 37,192 lines) has NO single function over the 1000-line gate. The largest are feed_or_api_450 891 lines (frames part38), closure130 864 (part23), outcomes_from_368 589 (part17), then 539/508/438. So unlike Cells (spi_walk 1966), the sim can be split into typed modules without join points first; the two 850+ functions are the ones to watch when units are drawn, since a unit holding either has little headroom. Decision unchanged (11-08): one Gleam package, many modules.")
inl note_of_x_gleam_manual_split_17 () = Note ("X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT", 17u32, "2026-11-09T15:02Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 11:0x microblog, CPU-light design step: the UNIT MAP from the sources (apps/sim/spi package.spiproj order plus each file's top-level opens; no cycles). Layers: TEXT (client package: prelude, glyphs, protocol, splitter, defuse) <- EVENTS (exsss 123, sim_events 400, final_state 144) <- PEOPLE (personas 217, scenario 215, schedule 175) <- WORLD (world 471, remote_chunks 22) <- RULES (invariants 424, inbox 354, scheduler 370, runner_checks 218, scenario_checks 315) <- RUN (sim_args 556, sim_run 361, sim_report 151, executors 41, cells_executor 437, runner_guard 84, runner_shell 258, xsim_shell_entry 101) and SERVE (sim_serve 1043, timeline_serve 581, sim_publish 58, sim_progress 47: they open only prelude and sm'_operators, so they cut cleanly). Entries: simframes (442; opens glyphs, protocol, splitter, schedule, invariants, world, scenario, personas) -> xsim_frames; xsim_split (34; opens the TEXT layer) -> xsim_split. Candidate units = those layers, each a link: unit emitting src/<unit>.gleam in the one package. MEASUREMENT LIMIT: attributing today's 37,192 generated lines (frames + split) to source modules by function base name fails. 58% of the lines are synthetic method_N (400 fns) and closure_N (162) with no source name; named ones include sim_serve 5,644, defuse 1,482, splitter 1,146, timeline_serve 1,178, sim_events 574. So per-unit size needs one compile per candidate unit (each layer as its own entry exporting what the layer above calls), which is a node job. The largest generated fns are 891 and 864 lines (n16), so no unit is forced over the gate by a single function. next: stage a node job compiling each layer as an entry and report generated Gleam lines per unit against the 1000 gate. typefeatures: units share types through a Spiral-only types unit (events + protocol records), as in the Rust crates.")
