inl note_of_regex_bench_catalogue_1 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-CATALOGUE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T11:28Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x FILED for the user (11-02: \"have as many benchmarks as possible being run in gh actions with reports visible in dir-tree-html\"). DOD: every workload below is a typed table in bench (one union case each) run on ubuntu-24.04 AND windows-2025, A/A passing, counts equal to the regex crate, listed on the page with its own heading and a Runtime/AOT label: short anchored words (done), long texts (done), rebar-style curated equivalents (01-literal, 02-literal-alternate, 03-date with case-insensitivity, 06-cloud-flare-redos, 08-words with \\b once supported, 10-bounded-repeat), pathological (a|b)*a(a|b){n} (state cap refusal timing), (0|00)*1 ReDoS vs backtracking, the fiscal XSD pattern corpus (validation throughput per pattern), compile-time per pattern (build + minimize + certify), Windows and Linux reported separately (one machine per table). The full catalogue with sources comes from the share re-read relayed via main (drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SOTA). Windows report: upload it as a CI artifact too (today only ubuntu publishes). NEXT STEP: generalize bench workloads into a list of typed workload records (pattern re + crate pattern + input generator + expected minimal size) so adding a table is one record.")
inl note_of_regex_bench_catalogue_2 () = Note ("REGEX-BENCH-CATALOGUE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T11:28Z", "regex", "regex 07:5x SOURCE: share re-read by subagent a7601c1fd3d1a599f, verbatim at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SOTA\\sota-reread-20261102.md (C = artifact cccab1fd, E = 4edd442c, N = d05f9cdd, S = 76f3f8a7). ADDITIONS 11, 15, 22 (verbatim): 11. REGEX-BENCH-PAGE: run the M0 cut on every push. Add these arms: regex-automata dense::DFA, sparse::DFA, the hybrid lazy DFA, resharp, fancy-regex, and aho-corasick for literal sets. Add a long &[u8] haystack. DoD: the cut ratio is a CI row on both OSes, with the A/A check passing. Source: C §B.2 M0, §A.1. 15. REGEX-BENCH-PAGE: publish compile time next to run time, including the cost of Hopcroft minimization and certification. DoD: one compile-time row per workload. Source: E §4 \"Tempo de compilação\"; C §B.2 M8. 22. REGEX-BENCH-PAGE: a pinned reference table, never quoted as our own result. It holds the third-party numbers from (2), marked \"re-measure on the same machine before citing\". Source: C \"Riscos\"; E \"Caveats\". CATALOGUE (verbatim): (2) BENCHMARK CATALOGUE\n\n- rebar curated 01-literal, 02-literal-alternate: against rust/regex, regex-automata, resharp, hyperscan (Linux runner); models count and count-spans. The M0 entry, and where resharp admits regex/fancy-regex is 20-80x faster on literals. (C §B.1; E §6)\n- 06-cloud-flare-redos: same engines plus pcre2 and a backtracking arm; model count. A DFA should win here; this is the ReDoS headline. (C §B.1)\n- 03-date needs Unicode and case-insensitivity; 08-words needs a Unicode \\b. Engines and model as above; both gated on items 7-8. (C §B.1)\n- 10-bounded-repeat, 12-dictionary, 13-noseyparker, 14-quadratic: models to be confirmed (item 3). RE#'s (?i) dictionary row reads 505 vs 0.03 MiB/s; 14 is the quadratic/ReDoS case. (C §B.1; E §6)\n- Out of scope (captures): 04-ruff-noqa, 05-lexer-veryl, 07-unicode-character-data, 09-aws-keys, 11-unstructured-to-json.\n- rebar compile model: our runtime engine against all engines. Upstream: 13 engines, pcre2 1.41 first, hyperscan 582.92 last. (C §B.1)\n- Scoreboard to beat (geomean of speed ratios; upstream README is roughly from Nov 2025): hyperscan 2.37 (28 benchmarks); rust/regex 1.12.2 3.08 (38); dotnet/compiled 3.60; pcre2/jit 6.00; dotnet/nobacktrack 6.36; re2 10.39; javascript/v8 11.99; go/regexp 74.79; rust/regex/lite 156.49. In RE#'s fork, resharp scores 1.44 on 28 against rust/regex 1.61 on 38. GATE 1 is our own rank --intersection <= 2.0x. (C §A.1, §B.1)\n- Dense loop against regex-automata dense::DFA on the same pattern: the M0 cut at 3x. (C §B.2)\n- AOT category: our staged DFA against regex-cli generate serialize dense and sparse, logos and re2c. Throughput >= sparse and <= 1.1x dense; binary size <= dense; cargo build time published. (C §B.2 M6)\n- State blow-up: (a|b)*a(a|b){n} must be a typed refusal under the cap, with build time recorded; UTF-8 \\w/\\pL state counts <= 1.5x regex-automata. (C §B.2 M1, M8)\n- ReDoS workloads: (0|00)*1 against 0^n with a backtracking baseline, plus 06 and 14. (BRZOZOWSKI n5 D8; C)\n- XSD corpus: R{1,1000} and the 7 NF-e QR patterns, against lib xsd_regex and .NET. (Already in REGEX-XSD.)\n- Pre-tokenizer cl100k/o200k: against tiktoken (Python, 7-20 MiB/s), tiktoken-rs, bpe (33-64), the tiktoken crate v3 (34-88) and toktok (about 10x tiktoken-rs, about 3.5x bpe-openai). Token IDs must be identical. (C §E(i), §F.3)\n- Constrained-decoding masks: against llguidance (about 50 µs per mask at 128k, p99 0.5 ms, p100 20 ms, about 1.5 ms for a full JSON-schema mask), XGrammar (under 8 µs on half the masks, but tens to hundreds of ms when precomputation fails) and outlines-core. A trie-automaton paper reports 0.65 µs per step. On JSONSchemaBench's 11,306 schemas, GRID (an interested party) records llguidance at 83.9% and XGrammar at 90.3%. (C §E(ii))\n- eBPF/XDP: packets/s against hand-written Aya, with 100% verifier acceptance. (C §F.5)\n- Differential fuzz: 24 h of cargo-fuzz against regex with 0 divergences. (C §B.2 M8)")
