inl transformer_cpu_speed () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", "Transformer CPU speed: tok/s >= llama2.c runfast single-thread and >= its OpenMP build on the same machine, then toward llama2.mojo; measured per model size with compile time published beside run time", Feature, P1, D7, 85u32, Planned, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE,COMPILER-RUST-ARRAY-HOT-LOOP,COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS,COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN,COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS,KIT-OMP-RUNTIME-PATH", "transformer,compiler,benchmarks,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_cpu_speed () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", Minutes 1800u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). CPU failure gate (missing in the deliberation, set here): if single-thread tok/s stays under 50% of runfast after tiling + SIMD, stop and trace before any GPU work.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:43Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia: parent TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB. COMPILER-PERCEUS-FBIP dropped from deps: it is the deliberation's timebox premise (without it the timebox doubles), not a blocker, since the hot loop is staged and must not use Rc at all. Kept as prerequisites: CPU-REFERENCE, EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS and SIMD-CODEGEN.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 3u32, "2026-11-02T16:47Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST BASELINES on the node (job transformer-bench-1; i7-4810MQ, 8 logical, gcc 15.2.0, 256 greedy steps, -t 0). tok/s for run -O3 / runfast (-Ofast -march=native) / OpenMP (+ -fopenmp): stories15M 32.1 / 70.4 / 10.4; stories42M 10.3 / 28.3 / 10.3; stories110M 3.9 / 10.4 / 3.7. Greedy stdout is byte-identical across the three builds for each model (sha 15M E0B2AC33607F, 42M 4CF27F238035, 110M 566CEB7C3D63), so the oracle does not depend on build flags. CAVEAT: these are NOT clean benchmark numbers. The same -O3 15M build gave 60.7 tok/s an hour earlier, and OpenMP below single-thread means contention: the node is shared by 7 lanes, and mingw libgomp with no OMP_NUM_THREADS is suspect. Rule for this item: speed claims come only from same-machine A/B runs on a quiet box (rented GPU box, or the node during a held quiet window), run-O3 reference interleaved with ours, with thread count pinned. Model sizes and hashes: 15M 60,816,028 B CD590644...; 42M 167,020,572 B 9F65A100...; 110M 438,381,596 B 51526716.... The bench script is in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-ORACLE\\.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 4u32, "2026-11-02T20:55Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST SAME-MACHINE A/B (node, i7-4810MQ, gcc 15.2, stories15M, 256 greedy steps, rounds interleaved, node at 100% load). -O3 class: llama2.c run.c -O3 8,198/7,892/8,096 ms against Spiral C (gcc -O3 -march=native) 8,192/8,738/8,405 ms, i.e. Spiral at about 96% of run -O3. -Ofast class: runfast 3,730/3,231/3,958 ms against Spiral C with -Ofast -march=native 3,791/3,824/4,392 ms, i.e. about 91% of runfast, tokens identical. So the C backend output is already near llama2.c single-thread; the gap to runfast is about 9%, and OpenMP/Mojo-class speed needs COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS + SIMD. The Rust backend lags C by about 3.5x after pack17 (step 4 RefCell-free arrays is the next Rust lever). Script: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE\\ (transformer-ab-*.ps1).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:46Z", "tibia", "11-02 21:4x tibia, SPECIALIZATION A/B on quiet europa (stories15M, 256 steps, rotated pairs, output byte-identical to the installed build in every variant). gcc -O3 -march=native: shape-dispatched (constant trip counts) 3,351/3,372/3,301/3,306/3,216/3,149 ms (mean 3,282) vs generic 3,370/3,511/3,374/3,312/3,227/3,193 (mean 3,331): about 1.5%, inside the noise band, because without fast-math gcc still cannot reorder the float reduction. gcc -Ofast -march=native, the 4 undisturbed pairs: specialized 1,112/1,196/1,318/1,134 (mean ~1,190) vs generic 1,305/1,327/1,337/1,355 (mean ~1,331): about 11% FASTER; the last 2 pairs (1,673-1,835) ran after another lane started on europa and are excluded. Also: -Ofast alone is 2.5x faster than -O3 and still greedy-identical on stories15M. Reading: constant shapes pay where vectorization is allowed, which is the case for the SIMD codegen work (multi-accumulator / vector-type lowering is reordering by construction). Next: the typed per-model config (n8 plan) so the dispatch is not a hand-written shape list.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_6 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 6u32, "2026-11-03T09:19Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:1x tibia, QUIET RUNFAST A/B on europa (2 vCPU, load 0.38-0.59 throughout; stories15M, 256 greedy steps, wall time including model load; 6 rotated rounds of 3 arms ABC/BCA/CAB x2). llama2.c run.c fetched at the pinned commit 350e04fe (sha256 9c4f2d5c6ae0...) and built as runfast (gcc -Ofast -march=native -lm). All three arms print the same 813-byte greedy text. runfast 927/826/863/820/873/799 ms, mean 851; Spiral shape-specialized -Ofast (spec-fast, 11-02 build) 919/923/917/909/941/927, mean 923; Spiral generic -Ofast 999/1012/1025/954/1069/963, mean 1004. So Spiral C is 92% of runfast's single-thread speed when shape-specialized and 85% when generic: specialization recovers about half of the gap. DoD part 1 (>= runfast single-thread) is NOT met; the gap is 8%. NEXT, in order of expected payoff: (1) the same A/B with the ParamEscape compiler (paramesc2, pack20) on the specialized source, since borrowed params remove the per-row refcount ops in dot/matmul_rows; (2) a typed per-model config so the specialization is not a hand-written shape list (sized_array dims as type literals); (3) SIMD codegen (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN). Script: scratch ab-runfast.sh (on europa /root/paramesc-ab).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_7 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 7u32, "2026-11-03T09:25Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:2x tibia, PARAMESCAPE ON THE SPECIALIZED SOURCE, quiet-ish europa (load 0.66-0.86), 4 arms x 8 rounds as a Latin square (each arm in each position twice), stories15M 256 greedy steps. The specialized transformer.spi (scratch transformer-spec, node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\transformer-spec) compiled with the paramesc2 compiler (node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\paramesc2, the 11-02 20:11 build with the noinline fix): refc++ sites 282 -> 171; output byte-identical to spec-fast. Means: runfast 893 ms; Spiral specialized + ParamEscape -Ofast 947 ms = 94% of runfast's speed; specialized 994 ms = 90%; generic 1,058 ms = 84%. So ParamEscape is worth about 5% on top of specialization, and together they close the gap to runfast from 16% to 6%. (Absolute times sit about 5% above n6's run because the load was higher; the ratios are what count, and each round holds all arms.) NEXT: (1) when pack20 installs, rebuild from the in-tree transformer (f32_kernels in lib) to confirm the same 94% on installed bits; (2) the typed per-model config, so specialization is not a hand-written shape list; (3) SIMD or multi-accumulator reductions for the last 6% (runfast's edge is gcc vectorizing its plain-C dot loop under -Ofast).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_8 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 8u32, "2026-11-03T09:35Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:3x tibia, HYPOTHESIS REFUTED (n7 guessed runfast's last 6% = gcc vectorizing its plain-C dot loop). gcc -Ofast -march=native -fopt-info-vec-optimized on europa: our hot loops ARE vectorized, spec-pe.c:376 (dot_l10, the 288-trip dot) and :440 (dot_l12) 'loop vectorized using 32 byte vectors' (plus 16-byte epilogues), the same width as run.c's matmul (run.c:185-224). So the matmul inner loop is not the gap. Totals: run.c 66 vectorized loops, spec-pe.c 29. The missing vectorization is elsewhere (rmsnorm, softmax, RoPE, attention score/mix loops). NEXT: list the hot loops gcc reports as missed in spec-pe.c (-fopt-info-vec-missed) against run.c's vectorized ones by kernel, then a perf profile on europa to see which kernel holds the 6% before changing any codegen.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_9 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 9u32, "2026-11-03T09:36Z", "tibia", "11-03 05:4x tibia, THE LAST 6% LOCATED with perf (europa, quiet, perf record -F 4999, by symbol). spec-pe-fast: dot_l10 72.3%, dot_l12 6.5% (both vectorized, n8), axpy 2.4%, dot14 1.9%, and the CHECKPOINT LOAD: decode_floats1 1.82% plus its kernel page-fault work (asm_exc_page_fault 1.48, kernel_init_pages 0.93, rep_movs_alternative 0.86, do_anonymous_page 0.38, mod_memcg_lruvec_state 0.28), about 5.8% in all. runfast: forward 94.6%, generate 0.55%, almost no load cost, because run.c mmaps the checkpoint while ours decodes 60 MB float by float into a freshly allocated array (every page faulted in and written). So the compute is at parity and the remaining gap is the weight load, which a 256-step run amortizes poorly (stories110M and longer generations will dilute it). The FIX is TRANSFORMER-CHECKPOINT-LOAD's scope: a zero-copy load (map the file and view the float region in place; per backend: mmap on C, memmap or a borrowed slice on Rust). PREDICTION to verify: with a zero-copy load, specialized + ParamEscape reaches 98-100% of runfast on stories15M/256.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_10 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 10u32, "2026-11-03T10:41Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:4x tibia, SPEC + PARAMESCAPE + ZERO-COPY vs runfast, and the n9 PREDICTION FAILS (europa, load 0.66-0.84, 3 arms x 8 rotated rounds, stories15M 256 steps, identical output in all arms). Both Spiral arms were built by europa's current compiler from the 11-02 specialized source; the spec arm's C is 100,535 bytes, byte-for-byte the size of the paramesc2 build (n7), so this compiler evidently carries ParamEscape. spec-map adds the model_at/map_f32s load. MEANS (medians): runfast 802.5 (772.5); spec 925.9 (905.5); spec-map 885.3 (883.5). So zero-copy adds about 4.4% (926 -> 885), as expected from the load share, but spec-map is 87-91% of runfast, NOT the predicted ~100%. Runfast ran faster this round than in n7 (803 vs 893 ms mean), so n7's 94% was flattered by a slow runfast; across rounds runfast's spread is wide (743-955 ms), which caps what one 8-round square can claim. HONEST STATE: with the load gone, a ~10% compute gap to runfast remains, and it is not explained by vectorization of the dot loop (n8: both are 32-byte vectorized). NEXT: perf annotate dot_l10 against run.c's matmul inner loop (instruction mix: index arithmetic, bounds/len loads from the Array header, accumulator chains), and a larger square (16 rounds) to tighten the ratio.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_11 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 11u32, "2026-11-03T10:43Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, THE ~10% COMPUTE GAP LOCALIZED (perf annotate on europa, spec-map-fast vs runfast). Inner loops are the SAME instruction mix: ours dot_l9 (83.7% of samples) = vmovups + vmulps 0x8(%rcx,%rax) + one vaddps accumulator, add 0x20, cmp 0x480 (a constant 288-float trip count, specialization at work); runfast's matmul = vmulps + add 0x20 + cmp, also 8-wide and one accumulator. The difference is the per-row EPILOGUE: our dot is a separate noinline function (ParamEscape's noinline-on-self-loop rule, added to stop gcc losing constprop clones), so every row pays a horizontal reduction (vextractf128, vmovhlps, vshufps, 3x vaddps: about 5.5% of dot samples) plus vzeroupper (1.0%) and call/ret; runfast inlines the dot into matmul's row loop. LEVERS, cheapest first: (1) let a dot whose trip count is a constant inline into its row loop (scope the noinline rule to non-constant loops), measured against the 11-02 18% regression it was added for; (2) process 2-4 rows per call with separate accumulators, so one epilogue and one call serve several rows and the vaddps chains overlap (rows_l with a 4-row body); (3) multi-accumulator inside one dot. Expected: (1) or (2) recovers most of the 6-7%; the rest is runfast's spread.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_12 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 12u32, "2026-11-03T10:44Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, ~RUNFAST PARITY REACHED, single thread (europa, load 0.46-0.62, 3 arms x 8 rotated rounds, stories15M 256 greedy steps). Lever (1) of n11 tested by hand on the generated C: spec-map.c with its 2 '__attribute__((noinline)) float dot_l' removed by sed (0 left), gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical. MEANS: runfast 835.6 ms; spec-map with the dot INLINED 845.0 (~99% of runfast's speed, inside one square's noise); spec-map with noinline 895.5 (93%). So inlining the constant-trip dot recovers 5.6%, and the stack of measured levers (shape specialization, ParamEscape, zero-copy map_f32s load, an inlined constant-count dot) brings Spiral's C transformer to llama2.c runfast's single-thread speed on stories15M. DoD part 1 (>= runfast single-thread) is MET in a hand-patched build; to make it real: (a) the compiler scopes its noinline-on-self-loop rule to non-constant trip counts (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS n12-n13), (b) the typed per-model config replaces the hand-written shape list, (c) a 16-round square on a quiet box to tighten the ratio. Part 2 (>= the OpenMP build) needs COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS (pack19).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_13 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 13u32, "2026-11-03T10:49Z", "tibia", "11-03 06:5x tibia, TYPED PER-MODEL CONFIG PROTOTYPED (lever (b) of n12), in a scratch variant of the in-tree transformer (f32_kernels + map_f32s), built on europa. Design: ':! len n = i32' width witnesses; dot_l/rows_l take them (constant trip counts); 'shaped ka kb kc kv' derives one model's (n, d) pairs itself (dim x dim, dim x kv_dim, dim x hidden, hidden x dim, dim x vocab) and runs the matching specialized rows_l or reports !-; the model table is ONE line per checkpoint: 15M (288, 768, 32000, kv 288), 42M (512, 1376, 32000, 512), 110M (768, 2048, 32000, 768), 260K (64, 172, 512, kv 32), with f32_kernels::matmul as the fallback, so any shape outside the table stays correct. Adding a model = one line; no hand-written pair list. RESULT (stories15M, 4 arms x 8 rotated rounds, load 0.8-0.9): output byte-identical; C grows from 126 KB to 319 KB (7 dot widths, 20 row kernels); means runfast 811, hand-written spec (dot inlined) 856, typed config (dot inlined) 871, typed config (noinline) 894 ms. So the typed table matches the hand-written list within noise (+1.8%, under one round's spread). Runfast ratios swing 95-99% between 8-round squares on this shared VM; a 16-round square on a quiet box is the next measurement. PORT PLAN: move the variant into apps/transformer (cells split for the 272 budget: shaped -> two pair helpers, matmul -> one helper per model line), where the in-tree 260K oracle tests then exercise the GQA kv_dim = 32 pair; queued after the current node gates.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_14 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 14u32, "2026-11-03T10:51Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, 16-ROUND SQUARE (europa, load about 0.5-0.9, 3 arms rotated, stories15M 256 steps): runfast mean 828.8 / median 816.5 (750-934); hand-written specialized + ParamEscape + map + dot inlined mean 820.2 / median 823.0 (768-853); typed-config variant (n13, dot inlined) mean 871.9 / median 862.0 (789-970). So the HAND-WRITTEN specialized build is at llama2.c runfast parity single-thread (mean 1% faster, median 0.8% slower, tighter spread): DoD part 1 is met in a hand-patched build, pending the compiler's noinline scoping. The typed config is a REAL ~5% behind the hand-written list (the 8-round square hid it). perf: both profiles have the same shape (four row kernels about 90%), plus 1.33% in main for the typed build only: the per-call dispatch chain (every matmul walks the model table's pair checks) got inlined into the forward; the rest is likely code size (319 vs 126 KB). FIX for the port: resolve the model ONCE at load into a runtime union (| Stories15M | Stories42M | Stories110M | Stories260K | Unknown) and dispatch each matmul by a single match on that union, so a call pays one switch, not the whole table; keep the per-model line as the single place where its dims are declared.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_15 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 15u32, "2026-11-03T10:54Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, TYPED CONFIG v2 CLOSES THE 5% (n14's fix applied, scratch variant transformer-typed2.spi): the model is resolved ONCE at load into ':| known = | Stories15M | Stories42M | Stories110M | Stories260K | Unknown' (known_of cfg via 'fits (dims_*) cfg'), stored as model.k, and each matmul does one '?= k ?|' match to its model's 'shaped_by (dims_*)', else f32_kernels::matmul. Each model's dims are declared exactly once, in one dims_* function (four len witnesses) used by both known_of and the dispatch. 16-round square on europa (load ~0.2-0.5, 3 arms rotated, stories15M 256 steps, output byte-identical): runfast mean 821.8 / median 791.5; hand-written spec 835.2 / 812.0; typed v2 831.6 / 820.0. So typed v2 equals the hand-written list (within 1%) and is within about 1% of runfast on the mean (3.5% on the median), with dots inlined by hand as in n12. C size 334 KB. PORT next into apps/transformer (cell splits for the budget), gated by the in-tree 260K oracle tests, which exercise the GQA (dim 64, kv 32) pair through dims_260k.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_16 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 16u32, "2026-11-03T10:57Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:1x tibia, TYPED CONFIG v2 PORTED into apps/transformer/transformer.spi (unstaged): len witnesses, dot_l/rows_l, rows_if, shaped_square + shaped_wide + shaped, ':| known' with one dims_* per model (15M, 42M, 110M, 260K), fits/known_of, shaped_by, hit_known/hit_large, matmul_known (falls back to f32_kernels::matmul); model gains 'k : known' (k = known_of c at load); both matmul call sites go through matmul_known m::k. Cells sized for the budget (largest 261) and lines <= 120. GATE: the queued node load chain (tibia-load-chain-1) syncs spiral and runs the transformer oracle 5/5, where the 260K tests now take the dims_260k path (dim 64, hidden 172, vocab 512, kv 32: GQA). The compiler lane's scoped-noinline draft (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS 3-inline-constant-trip-loops.kitdo, pack24) un-noinlines exactly dot_l here (j >= width k folds to a literal), so the installed-bits speed check waits for that candidate DLL.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_17 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 17u32, "2026-11-03T10:59Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, PORT PRE-VERIFIED ON C (europa, the in-tree transformer.spi built with europa's compiler, gcc -Ofast -march=native -Wall: 0 errors, 0 free-nonheap warnings): stories15M 256 greedy steps byte-identical to the old arm; stories260K (pinned files fetched with sha256 -c OK against oracle.spi's pins) prints 566 bytes, the same length as llama2.c runfast on the same model, with the first 400 characters identical. So the dims_260k path (dim 64, hidden 172, vocab 512, GQA kv_dim 32) is correct on real weights. Rust and the memo-off Cells oracle remain for the node's load chain.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_18 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 18u32, "2026-11-03T11:02Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:0x tibia, COUNTER-EVIDENCE for lever (1), see COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS n14: it is MODEL-DEPENDENT. stories42M (pinned: 167,020,572 B, sha256 prefix 9f65a100; output matches runfast for 300 chars), the in-tree typed-config transformer built on europa, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, load 0.3-1.0: runfast 2,269.4 ms mean (median 2,276); dot NOINLINE (as generated) 2,320.4 (97.8% of runfast); dot INLINED (sed) 2,391.5 (94.9%). So on 42M inlining the constant-trip dot is 3.1% SLOWER, the opposite of stories15M (n12: inlining 5.6% faster). Reading: with dim 512 / hidden 1376 the per-row horizontal-sum epilogue is a smaller share of each row, so the inlined loop's cost (code size or register pressure in the row loop) dominates. So 'constant trip => no noinline' gains on small rows and loses on wide ones; a trip-count threshold (inline only below N, e.g. 384, between 15M's 288 and 42M's 512) or keeping noinline and fusing several rows per call (TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED n11 lever 2) may be the better rule. Please hold pack24's replacement until both models are measured with the candidate.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_19 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 19u32, "2026-11-03T11:07Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:1x tibia, LEVER (2) REFUTED: 4 rows per call is SLOWER. Scratch variant of the in-tree transformer: rows_l computes 4 rows per step through dot4_l (4 f32 accumulators in a quad tuple, sharing each x[j] load; remainder rows via dot_l). Built on europa (C 361 KB), output byte-identical on stories15M and stories42M. 16 rounds x 3 arms per model (load ~0.4-1.5): 15M runfast 845.7, tree (current, dot noinline) 918.0, rows4 978.0 (6.5% slower than tree); 42M runfast 2,155.5, tree 2,213.5, rows4 2,422.1 (9.4% slower). Reading: four row streams plus a tuple carried through the join point defeat the clean 8-wide single-stream loop gcc vectorizes in dot_l; the epilogue saving does not pay for that. So multi-row fusion at the Spiral source level is off; compiler's threshold rule (inline constant-trip loops only up to 384, COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS) is the lever to measure next on 15M and 42M. Also this round: tree (noinline) is 97.4% of runfast on 42M and 92.1% on 15M.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_20 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 20u32, "2026-11-03T11:23Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:2x tibia, stories110M (pinned: 438,381,596 B, sha256 prefix 51526716; output matches runfast for 300 chars), 16 rounds x 3 arms on europa: runfast 6,496.0 mean / 6,493.5 median; in-tree typed, dot noinline 6,714.2 / 6,650.0 (96.8% of runfast); dot inlined 6,755.4 / 6,731.5 (96.2%). Consistent with compiler's threshold rule (inline only trip literals up to 384): at 768/2048-wide rows noinline is slightly better. PER-MODEL SUMMARY, current tree on europa, single thread: 15M 92% (noinline) / ~99% (dot inlined); 42M 97.4-97.8% (noinline best); 110M 96.8% (noinline best). With the threshold candidate, every model should sit at its best column.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_21 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 21u32, "2026-11-03T11:33Z", "tibia", "11-03 07:3x tibia, CANDIDATE MEASURED, RECOMMEND NOT SHIPPING the (1, 384] rule. compiler's DLL (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\compiler-noinline\\..., core 867B0BD81652261F) compiled the in-tree transformer (typed per-model config, 7 dot widths) to cand.c on the node: noinline dropped on exactly the 3 dot_l at or below 384 (288, 172, 64), kept on 4 (512, 768, 1376, 2048). base.c = the same C with noinline restored on those 3 (sed), so the pair differs ONLY in the rule's effect. europa, 16 rounds x 3 arms rotated, gcc -Ofast -march=native, output byte-identical (and equal to the tree build) on both models. stories15M: runfast 807.6 mean / 798.5 median; base 864.2 / 877.5; cand 994.2 / 985.5, so cand is 15% SLOWER (its fastest run, 925, is slower than base's median). stories42M: runfast 2,102.7; base 2,175.9; cand 2,164.9 (no 42M dot affected; equal within noise). READING: the +5.6% of n13 came from a single-model specialized source; in the typed-config program (20 row kernels plus dispatch) inlining the small dots into rows_l, and those into the dispatch, costs more than the epilogue saves. Whether inlining pays depends on the surrounding code, not just the trip count, so a trip-count rule cannot get it right; keeping noinline on every Borrowed self-loop is the safe default. Current tree per model (base, noinline): 15M 93%, 42M 97%, 110M 97% of runfast.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_22 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 22u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:17 tibia GROOM: parked to Planned (no note in 12 h). State: typed transformer at 93% (15M), 96% (42M), ~98% (110M) of llama2.c runfast, single thread, 16-round europa squares. The constant-trip inline rule was measured 15% slower and not shipped (COMPILER-C-BORROWED-PARAMS 11-03). NEXT: re-run the 16-round square on a quiet europa after the next compiler pack that touches C codegen; first check europa load.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_23 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 23u32, "2026-11-04T04:21Z", "tibia", "11-04 00:21 tibia: back to Active, percent kept. User 11-04 00:2x moved the stale-Active window from 12 h to 48 h, and this item has a note within 48 h, so this morning's parking (12 h rule) is undone. The next step in the 00:2x note still holds.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_24 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 24u32, "2026-11-04T13:06Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:06 tibia: the multi-thread half of this item has a working path now: lib parallel::for_index (C: omp parallel for over 16-row chunks; compiler emits atomic refcounts when SPIRAL_THREADS is set) applied to the Q8 matmul in TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v5 gives byte-identical tokens at 1 and 2 threads. The same 16-row chunking applies to the fp32 matmul. A scaling measurement waits for a quiet multi-core machine.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_25 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 25u32, "2026-11-04T13:12Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:12 tibia, fp32 THREADED TOO (draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v6 = v5 + the shape-specialized fp32 kernels chunked: rows_l takes an end, and rows_par runs 16-row chunks through parallel::for_index; 210 omp pragmas, one per specialization). europa stories15M: fp32 output byte-identical to the fp32 C oracle at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 2, and Q8 byte-identical to runq at 1 and 2. Speed still unmeasured (needs a quiet multi-core machine; europa was at load 13 on 2 vCPUs). Landing order: lib parallel.spi (box gate queued), then transformer v3 -> v6 into the tree, then a 1/2/4-thread square against llama2.c runomp.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_26 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 26u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:40 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_27 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 27u32, "2026-11-04T15:02Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:02 tibia, WHERE THE 15M GAP IS, AND ONE REFUTED FIX: perf on europa (stories15M, OMP 1): 93% of the time is the two shape-specialized dot_l loops (84% n=288, 9% n=768); attention, softmax and allocation are under 2%. Under -Ofast -march=native our dot_l and run.c's matmul compile to the SAME inner loop: vmovups + vmulps + vaddps on ymm into ONE accumulator, so both are latency-bound on the vaddps chain, and the remaining gap is around the loop (cycles:u over 3 rounds: base 2.98e9/3.14e9/2.95e9 vs runfast 2.85e9/2.92e9/2.89e9, about 4% at load 7-11). REFUTED: 4-row blocking with one accumulator per row (drafts spiral\\TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED\\rows4 8C4BA8A3415F, and rows4b 102CF98DF645 sized for the budget). It keeps each row's summation order, so strict -O3 output is BYTE-IDENTICAL to base on 15M (813 B) and 42M (746 B), but it costs 7.95e9/8.28e9/7.78e9 cycles, 2.6x base: gcc SLP-vectorizes ACROSS the 4 rows (xmm lanes built from 4 strided loads) instead of 8-wide along each row. Without SIMD types (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN) the multi-accumulator idea cannot be expressed so gcc keeps 8-wide-per-row vectors, and splitting one row's sum would break bit parity with run.c. Next lever to test instead: the per-row overhead around the loop (dot_l is a separate constprop function called per row; run.c inlines it into the row loop).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_28 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 28u32, "2026-11-04T15:03Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:03 tibia: per-row call lever also REFUTED. The C backend emits every join point as __attribute__((noinline)), so dot_l stays a separate call per row; raising gcc's inline limit changes nothing for that reason. Rewriting only the 7 dot_l definitions to static inline (sed on the generated C, same output as base) made it SLOWER: cycles:u 3.00e9/3.16e9/3.18e9 vs base 2.90e9/2.90e9/3.00e9 in the same rounds. Those same rounds put base level with runfast (2.87e9/2.95e9/3.02e9); the round before showed about 4.6 percent. So at europa load 7 the cycle counts are noisy to about 4 percent and do not resolve the remaining gap. Only a clean, load-gated wall-clock square can (the 11-03 squares: 15M 93.3, 42M 96.4, 110M about 98 percent of runfast). The hot loop itself is already the same as run.c's (see the previous note).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_29 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 29u32, "2026-11-04T16:48Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:48 tibia: inherits from TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 (closed): typed Q8 is about 1.5x behind llama2.c runq on a clean europa square (mean 934 vs 633 ms, median 915 vs 607, stories15M, 16 rounds, 0 of 32 rows loaded). Same method as the fp32 work: perf the Q8 path to see whether rows_q8/dot_i8 or the activation quantization dominates before trying a lever.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_30 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 30u32, "2026-11-04T16:52Z", "tibia", "11-04 12:52 tibia, Q8 GAP LOCATED, AND A LEVER THAT KEEPS PARITY: perf (europa, stories15M v2 Q8, single thread): typed Q8 spends 40.5% in row_q8 and 39.6% in dot_i8, i.e. one noinline call per 64-wide group (the C backend emits every join point as __attribute__((noinline))), while runq spends 82.8% in one matmul whose group dot is inline. Experiment on the generated C (sed: dot_i8 only -> static inline). STRICT -O3 (no fast-math, no march), the only fair parity reference: base and inlined builds are both IDENTICAL to strict runq.c (724 B prefix); timing over 6 rounds at load 1.0: strict runq 741 ms, base 1033 ms, inlined 853 ms, so -17%, and the strict gap narrows from 1.39x to 1.15x. Under -Ofast the inlined build changes tokens (byte 221): once dot_i8 is inline, gcc reassociates the float group sum (val += f32(ival) * ws * xs) differently from runq -Ofast; with -fno-associative-math both base and inlined differ from runq -Ofast, so '-Ofast parity' only ever held because both sides reorder alike. Contrast: inlining the fp32 dot_l made it SLOWER (11-04 11:0x note), because there the call is paid per 288-wide row, not per 64-wide group. So a blanket noinline removal is wrong; the ask is selective (small leaf join points called inside a loop). Sent to compiler with both data points.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_31 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 31u32, "2026-11-04T17:07Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:07 tibia: measured compiler's call-site rule (C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE draft, applied as a text pass to the generated C: 29 single-site borrowed loops become static inline, multi-site dot_l36/dot_l30 keep noinline). Strict -O3: Q8 output of base, dot_i8-only and rule builds all IDENTICAL to strict runq.c; fp32 output of both inlined builds IDENTICAL to base. 6 interleaved rounds (europa busy: 20 of 36 rows above load 1.5, so relative order only): Q8 base 1152 / dot_i8-only 936 / rule 844 ms (rule about -27% vs base); fp32 base 3098 / 3049 / 3120 ms (within noise). Sent to compiler: no size term needed for these programs.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_32 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 32u32, "2026-11-04T17:25Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:25 tibia: THREAD SCALING ON EUROPA: VOID for scaling. Square (stories15M fp32, 8 rounds, -Ofast -march=native -fopenmp; waited 24 min for load 0.47, but 17 of 32 rows still ran above load 1.5): v6 (threaded rows, draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 v6) output IDENTICAL at 1 and 2 threads; v6 1 thread 971 ms, v6 2 threads 920 ms, llama2.c runfast 1 thread 842 ms, runomp 2 threads 887 ms. Even llama2.c's own OpenMP build is slower on 2 threads than its single-thread build here, so europa's 2 shared vCPUs cannot measure thread scaling for anyone. The scaling claim needs a quiet machine with real cores (the node at idle, or a rental). Relative reading only: v6 at 2 threads ~= runomp at 2 threads within this noise.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_33 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 33u32, "2026-11-04T17:37Z", "tibia", "11-04 13:37 tibia: CLEAN SQUARE for compiler's call-site inline rule (C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE): europa, strict gcc -O3, stories15M, 16 interleaved rounds per build and model, 0 of 64 rows above load 1.5. Outputs IDENTICAL base vs rule (Q8 and fp32). Q8 996 -> 798 ms (-19.9%); fp32 2807 -> 2800 ms (neutral). When it ships, typed Q8 moves from about 1.39x to about 1.1x of strict runq.c on the same machine (strict runq 741 ms in the earlier 6-round run).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_34 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 34u32, "2026-11-04T20:10Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:10 tibia: CLEAN fp32 SQUARE (europa, stories15M, 16 interleaved rounds, gcc -Ofast -march=native both, 0 of 32 rows above load 1.5): outputs IDENTICAL; typed (live apps/transformer build) mean 1010 ms vs llama2.c runfast 938 ms, so typed runs at 92.9% of runfast's speed, consistent with 11-03's 93.3%: no regression from today's landed changes, and the ~7% gap is real. Where it is NOT: the hot loop (same vmulps/vaddps chain as run.c), inlining dot_l (slower), 4-row blocking (2.6x slower). Remaining candidates are outside the dot: per-token work around the 50k row calls, the rope/softmax/attention code paths (under 2% each in perf), and allocation; a perf diff typed vs runfast by symbol on a quiet machine is the next step.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_35 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 35u32, "2026-11-04T20:13Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:13 tibia: WHERE THE fp32 GAP IS NOT (europa, load ~1.5, stories15M, same binaries as the clean square). perf stat user-mode: typed 2.685e9 cycles / 3.078e9 instructions vs runfast 2.642e9 / 3.049e9, i.e. +1.6% cycles, +1.0% instructions. /usr/bin/time over 3 runs each: sys 0.01-0.02 s for both (typed's first cold run 0.11 s, 12 major faults), minor faults ~2290 vs ~2345, user 0.81-0.86 vs 0.77-0.84 s. So it is NOT checkpoint loading or paging (my mmap guess was wrong), and the compute differs by only ~1-2%. The clean square's 7.7% wall gap (1010 vs 938 ms) is therefore mostly outside the counted user cycles: candidates are per-process start (tokenizer build, allocation before the first token) and cold-start effects that a 256-token run does not amortize. Next: time the start alone (load + tokenizer, 0 tokens) for both, then a 1024-token run, where a fixed start cost would shrink in relative terms.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_36 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 36u32, "2026-11-04T20:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:18 tibia: startup ruled out too, the other way round: typed with TRANSFORMER_DUMP_STEPS=0 (checkpoint + tokenizer + run state, no forward step) takes 11-15 ms, while runfast -n 1 (start plus one token) takes 27-32 ms (first cold run 107). So typed starts FASTER. State of the fp32 comparison: counted user-mode work differs by +1.6% cycles / +1.0% instructions, sys time and faults are equal, startup favours typed, yet the clean wall-clock square shows 7.7%. That difference is UNEXPLAINED; one untested candidate is steal time on europa's shared vCPUs, which user-mode counters do not see, but this is not shown. A machine with dedicated cores (the node when idle) would separate it; until then, report the compute gap as ~1-2% and the wall gap as 7.7% on europa.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_37 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 37u32, "2026-11-04T20:19Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:19 tibia: steal time REFUTED as the cause: /proc/stat steal ticks read around each of 12 interleaved runs (europa, load ~1.3) were 0 for every run of both programs. Same run, cold first typed run (1778 ms) excluded: typed ~1019 ms vs runfast ~953 ms, so the ~7% wall gap reproduces. Open discrepancy, stated as is: user-mode cycles differ by ~1.6% but user TIME by ~5% (0.81-0.86 vs 0.77-0.84 s), so typed spends its user time at a lower effective clock or with stalls the cycles:u counter does not separate. Not explained; the next instruments would be perf stat with stalled-cycles/cache-misses and a run on dedicated cores. Parking this thread here: no lever is indicated yet.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_38 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 38u32, "2026-11-04T20:28Z", "tibia", "11-04 16:28 tibia: last probe on europa, then parked. At load 1.17 one perf stat pair gave task-clock typed 858 ms vs runfast 852 ms (0.7% apart; cycles:u typed 2.41e9 < runfast 2.46e9; cache-misses 26.3M vs 25.3M; L1d misses 14.3M vs 15.7M; backend-stall counter not exposed by this VM). Repeating it shows typed's task-clock moving 858-1047 ms between runs (about +/-10%), more than the compute difference itself, and wall vs task-clock do not track run to run. CONCLUSION: on europa's 2 shared vCPUs, single-run comparisons cannot resolve a few percent; the 16-round clean square (typed at 92.9% of runfast) is the robust figure, and the counters say most of that gap is not extra work. Next measurement needs dedicated, idle cores (the node when it is idle); no code lever is indicated.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_39 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 39u32, "2026-11-04T23:20Z", "tibia", "11-04 19:20 tibia: Q8 rule vs runq, SAME RUN (replaces the cross-run 'about 1.1x' estimate): europa, strict gcc -O3 for all three, stories15M v2 Q8, 16 interleaved rounds after waiting for load 0.72; another lane's Cells run loaded part of it, so 23 of 48 rows ran above load 1.5 and only the quiet rows count (8-9 per program): strict runq.c 804 ms, typed with compiler's call-site inline rule 898 ms (1.12x runq), typed base 1166 ms (1.45x runq). The rule build's output is IDENTICAL to strict runq. So C-BORROWED-LOOP-INLINE moves typed Q8 from about 1.45x to about 1.12x of runq at strict parity.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_40 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 40u32, "2026-11-05T07:09Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:09 tibia: NODE MULTI-THREAD SQUARE #1 VOID, cause found. Typed v6 (the placed v3 plus parallel::for_index over 16-row chunks in the fp32 and Q8 matmuls; 210 omp regions in the C) built on the node (Windows, mingw gcc 15.2) with -Ofast -march=native CRASHES with 0xC0000005 after printing its text, with or without -fopenmp, and -O2 -march=native crashes too. -O0 and -Ofast without -march=native run. This is the known mingw-w64 Win64 AVX stack-alignment bug (GCC PR 54412: aligned vmovaps spills onto a stack Win64 only guarantees to 16 bytes). '-Wa,-muse-unaligned-vector-move' fixes it: v6 text is byte-identical to v3 at OMP 1 and 8 (817 chars, stories15M). v3 at the same flags never crashed; its code shape happens not to spill. Also: llama2.c's win.c no longer builds on gcc 15 (VirtualProtect PDWORD vs uint32_t*), so it needs -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types. The square's harness now refuses to time anything whose parity check fails. First timings with the workaround were taken while another Cells job ran, so they are NOT a result: v6 15M 18.2 s at t1 and 32.7 s at t8 vs v3 about 2.3 s. That is an 8x+ slowdown that contention alone cannot explain; a suspect is per-region overhead in Windows libgomp (210 regions per token on small rows). Square #2 (tibia-tf6-square-3) is queued after gl2, before cl2. Europa Linux v6 at 2 threads matched v3 earlier (no AVX bug there).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_41 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 41u32, "2026-11-05T07:27Z", "tibia", "11-05 03:27 tibia: NODE SQUARE #2, CLEAN (job tibia-tf6-square-3, 1088 s; i7-4810MQ 4C/8T Haswell, Windows, mingw gcc 15.2, every build -Ofast -march=native with '-Wa,-muse-unaligned-vector-move' for the typed builds; llama2.c run.c with win.c). Parity gate passed first: v6 at 8 threads == v3 byte for byte (15M 817 chars, 42M 749), and typed's first 200 chars == runfast's. 8 interleaved rounds x 6 arms x 2 models, all 96 rows at node load <= 60%, 256 greedy tokens, medians in ms (min-max). 15M: runfast 1941 (1884-1975), runomp8 1830, typed v3 1917 (1877-2022), v6 t1 2170, v6 t4 1792, v6 t8 1869. 42M: runfast 5130 (5001-5180), runomp8 3674 (3591-3936), typed v3 5090 (4978-5217), v6 t1 5355, v6 t4 3841, v6 t8 3808. READING: (1) single-thread, typed v3 is AT OR SLIGHTLY AHEAD OF runfast on this machine (15M 1.2% faster, 42M 0.8% faster; ranges overlap), unlike europa's 92.9% (different CPU, and europa was noisy). (2) multi-thread, v6 reaches 95.6-96.5% of llama2.c's OpenMP build on 42M (3808 at 8 threads vs 3674) and beats it on 15M at 4 threads (1792 vs 1830). (3) the price of threading at 1 thread is 13% (15M) / 5% (42M) over v3: atomic refcounts plus 210 omp regions per token. Next lever: fewer, larger parallel regions per token (fuse the q/k/v and w1/w3 matmuls into one region). The goal row 'tok/s >= runfast single-thread' is MET on the node; '>= OpenMP build' is within 4.5% on 42M.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_42 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 42u32, "2026-11-05T08:39Z", "tibia", "11-05 04:39 tibia: CHUNK SWEEP #1 timing VOID, parity green. v7 (draft TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8/v7 = v6 plus a runtime row-chunk knob TRANSFORMER_CHUNK, default 16, for both the fp32 and Q8 matmuls) matched v3 byte for byte at chunks 4/8/16/32 on 15M and 42M at 8 threads. But 69 of 72 rows ran with the node above 60% load from other work (runomp8 15M ranged 4.1-15.8 s, against 1.8 s quiet), so no timing claim. WHY THE KNOB: at a fixed 16-row chunk, 15M's dim 288 gives 18 chunks on 8 threads, three waves with the last a quarter full (about 75% efficiency), while llama2.c's static omp schedule has no tail. Rerun tibia-tf7-chunk-sweep-2 is queued after the budget run; it now waits for 3 consecutive load readings under 30% before each round.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_43 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 43u32, "2026-11-05T10:39Z", "tibia", "11-05 06:39 tibia: CHUNK SWEEP #2 (node job tibia-tf7-chunk-sweep-2; quiet-gated rounds; only the 53 of 72 rows at <= 40% load count; parity green at chunks 4/8/16/32 on both models). Medians in ms, 8 threads. 15M: runomp8 1752, v6 (chunk 16) 1780, v7 chunk 4 2052, chunk 8 1979, chunk 16 1889, chunk 32 1891. 42M: runomp8 3529, v6 3641, v7 chunk 4 3974, chunk 8 3850, chunk 16 3816, chunk 32 3785. VERDICT: the load-imbalance hypothesis is REFUTED. Smaller chunks are slower (4 worst, 8 next), so the 16-row tail wave is not what costs time. The runtime knob itself costs about 5-6% (v7 at chunk 16 is v6's partition, but reads the env var on every matmul call and carries twice the generated C, 1.08 MB vs 517 KB). v6 stays the best typed multi-thread build: 98.4% (15M) and 96.9% (42M) of llama2.c's OpenMP build on quiet rows. Draft v7 is kept in the store as the negative record and is not a candidate. Remaining gap levers: per-token region count (fuse q/k/v and w1/w3 into one parallel region) and v6's 5-13% single-thread overhead (atomic refcounts).")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_44 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 44u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:1x compiler GROOM: stays Active: tibia-tf8-sweep (node, job-1793917448497) was still running at 11-05 21:03; its medians decide TRANSFORMER-QKV-FUSION v8.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_45 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 45u32, "2026-11-06T08:23Z", "tibia", "11-06 04:24 tibia (groom, from the star exchange): added dep KIT-OMP-RUNTIME-PATH (kit). On the box, PATH loads mingw 16.2 libgomp into gcc 15.2 exes, and v6/v8 hang at OMP >= 2, so box timings of the omp arms are VOID until a runner puts the building gcc bin first (or links -static). The node is unaffected. With the node off the network since about 00:17 (kit, fiscal) and unreachable from the box at 04:19, the v8 sweep is UNKNOWN on the node, not red. Rule from kit and fiscal: read the remote half's own log before any relaunch.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_46 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 46u32, "2026-11-06T11:39Z", "tibia", "11-06 07:39 tibia: MILESTONE, the OpenMP bar is met on 42M. The typed transformer v8 (QKV fusion) runs at a median of 2433 ms vs llama2.c runomp8 2527 ms (-3.7%, 5/5 quiet rounds, parity green; TRANSFORMER-QKV-FUSION n14). The single-thread bar was met earlier (v3 about equal to runfast). Left for the DoD: (1) 15M medians under the same quiet-skip harness (only 2 indicative quiet rounds so far, -14%); (2) 110M; (3) compile time published beside run time; then \"toward llama2.mojo\". NEXT: run tf9-sweep.ps1 -Models stories15M.bin when the node is quiet.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_47 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 47u32, "2026-11-06T11:40Z", "tibia", "11-06 07:40 tibia: TypeFeatures: the transformer keeps type-level tensor shapes (sized dims checked at compile time) and gains speed through codegen levers only (fusion, parallel::for_index), with no shape erasure.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_48 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 48u32, "2026-11-06T19:51Z", "tibia", "11-06 16:0x tibia: job-1793965243889-tibia-tf9-quiet-15m (stories15M, v8 vs v3, OMP 1 and 8) finished exit 0 but is VOID: 0 of 5 quiet rounds, all 8 attempts skipped with calm 0 after ~900 s each, because the node ran kit TRAIN34, compiler-split-oracle and fiscal gates all day. Only its parity line counts: v8 at OMP 1 and 8 == v3 (817 chars). Not relaunched on the node today; the 42M verdict that closed TRANSFORMER-QKV-FUSION stands. job-1793967335894-tibia-tf-inline-ab is next in that queue and will likely VOID the same way.")
inl note_of_transformer_cpu_speed_49 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-CPU-SPEED", 49u32, "2026-11-07T15:54Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:5x tibia groom round 2 (stale Active, no note in 12 h): Planned. The OpenMP bar is met (42M v8 -3.7 pct vs runomp8); left for its DoD: 15M under the quiet-skip harness, 110M, and compile time beside run time, all needing a quiet node (the 11-06 15M sweep VOIDed on load). Next step: rerun tf9-sweep.ps1 when the node is quiet; Mojo work is TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO.")
inl transformer_gpu () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-GPU", "Transformer GPU: CUDA and WGSL kernels for matmul, attention and the elementwise ops from the same typed source; tok/s per model size on one rented L40S; gate: WGSL under 50% of CUDA on the same GPU means publish CPU + CUDA only", Feature, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "TRANSFORMER-CPU-REFERENCE,GPU-STACKED-RUNS", "transformer,gpu,wgsl,cuda,benchmarks,team:tibia,tibia", "current", "current")
