inl compiler_emit_parallel_loops () = Task ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", "Emitted-code parallelism: a typed parallel-for over independent index ranges lowered to threads (C: pthreads/OpenMP pragma, Rust: scoped threads or rayon-free chunks, F#/others sequential), for matmul rows and batched sims", Feature, P1, D7, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,backends,transformer,tibia,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops () = Planning ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", Minutes 1200u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 1u32, "2026-11-02T13:42Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-02 by tibia under TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB (n4, from the original deliberation, share eab494f1). The deliberation assumed the Hopac core (the compiler's own parallelism), not emitted-code threads; this is the latter. tibia's batched evolution reuses it on CPU.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:23Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, FIRST C PARALLEL PROTOTYPE (node, stories15M, 256 greedy steps, OpenMP 4 threads, compiler 6B9374835A1BBACF; scratch app %TEMP%\\...\\scratchpad\\transformer-par, node C:\\home\\remote-stage\\transformer-par). Construct: `parallel_for n body` whose C arm emits `global \"#define SPIRAL_THREADS 1\"` plus `_Pragma(\"omp parallel for schedule(static)\") for (...) { incref body; body->fptr(body, i); }`; other backends loop sequentially. FINDING 1, correctness: generated refcount ops are plain `x->refc++` / `--(x->refc) == 0`, and every join-point call increfs its array arguments (the owned-argument convention: dot10 gets `(v1)->refc++; (v2)->refc++` per row), so any parallel body races on the shared arrays' counters; a lost increment can free early. With every refc op rewritten to __atomic builtins, tokens are identical at 1 and 4 threads in all three variants below. FINDING 2, cost (ms, rounds 1-2, node 49-76% loaded): (a) per-row body calling dot: seq 5,643/5,579, par-1 7,263/7,011, par-4 5,196/5,226 vs runomp 1,886/1,884 -- atomics cost ~30% and the shared counters' cache line bounces, so no scaling; (b) 16-row chunks with a flattened row x column self-loop (no inner calls, no refc in the loop): par-4 5,231/5,166 vs par-1 11,733/10,997 -- scales 2.2x, but the merged two-path while(1) is no longer a counted loop for gcc and runs at half speed; (c) 16-row chunks with dot over raw `float *` (a `:! raw_floats = $'float *'` nominal, i.e. what borrowed array params would emit): par-1 8,487/6,681, par-4 3,273/3,430 vs runomp 2,135/2,224 -- 2.3x scaling, 1.5x off runomp, which also parallelizes the attention heads. DESIGN, three steps: (1) COMPILER: atomic refc only when the marker global is present (env.globals is filled by the partial evaluator before C codegen, so the choice is made at emit time, no text pass, default output byte-identical); kitdo program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS\\1-c-atomic-refc.kitdo, 5 replacements, dry-run ok on the live tree (17:14). (2) LIB: `parallel::for_index` draft in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-PARALLEL-FOR\\parallel.spi (base DD557ECD8D3B), held until a pack carries (1). (3) The speed lever is borrowed read-only array parameters (no incref at the call, no decref at return when the callee only indexes and self-passes the array), which also helps single-thread inner loops; that is COMPILER-PERCEUS-FBIP territory. Next on this item: attention heads as a second for_index site, then a quiet-box A/B.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 3u32, "2026-11-03T09:00Z", "compiler", "11-03 05:0x compiler GROOM (run start; Active = touched in 12 h). Parked to Planned (12 h; last note tibia). Next: attention heads as the second for_index site, then a quiet-box A/B; lib parallel.spi waits for a pack carrying step (1).")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T13:22Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:22 tibia: step (1) is already in the installed compiler (spiral_compiler.fs emits __atomic_fetch_add/__atomic_sub_fetch for refcounts when globals hold #define SPIRAL_THREADS 1; europa probe: 12 atomic ops, omp loop over 100000 indices correct at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 4). Step (2) parallel::for_index: box gate 7/7 with mutant failing exactly for_index_visits_every_index_once on c, BUT the v1 draft added 2 over-budget cells (c_for_index 308, its test 302). v2 (drafts LIB-PARALLEL-FOR\\v2, BEEEAB18B7B7) renames locals only and is back to the 5 pre-existing over cells; box gate of v2 is running, land follows on green.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:40Z", "tibia", "11-04 09:40 tibia, CLOCK CORRECTION: tibia's 11-04 note prefixes on this item (and about 50 across 20 items) had been typed without reading the clock, up to 140 min ahead. Each was edited to its stored at time (note-edit with --expect-prefix), so the prefixes above are now real local times. Note content unchanged.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T14:26Z", "tibia", "11-04 10:26 tibia: LIB parallel::for_index LANDED green (lib_land_window, parallel.spi DD557ECD8D3B -> BEEEAB18B7B7, lib digest 8BC2664102C80B87, document_test 22, type_probe PASS with 7 tests, 0 respell drift; undo lib-land-undo\\20261104-101342). C arm: _Pragma omp parallel for over the index range, body refcount bumped with __atomic_fetch_add, global #define SPIRAL_THREADS 1 so the compiler's refcounts turn atomic (step 1, already in the installed compiler). Other backends: the sequential each_index. Gate: box 7/7; mutant (loop stops one index early) fails exactly for_index_visits_every_index_once on c. Use: TRANSFORMER-QUANT-Q8 drafts v5/v6 (Q8 and fp32 matmul rows in 16-row chunks), byte-identical at 1 and 2 threads on europa. LEFT for this item: (a) a Rust arm with scoped threads (the body writes into Rc-backed arrays, which are not Send, so it needs a raw-pointer or chunked-output shape), (b) a measured speedup on a quiet multi-core machine, (c) the batched-sims use.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T15:18Z", "tibia", "11-04 11:18 tibia: (c) BATCHED SIMS ON C THREADS, CORRECTNESS PROVEN: a probe app lanes_par.spi (europa stage tlanes3/w-par, tibia src with target2, landed lib) scores the 4 tiny-plan lanes with parallel::for_index 4 (\\ i => set scores i (lane_score p (conv i))) and prints them in order. Generated C: 1 omp parallel for, 38 atomic refcount ops. Output BYTE-IDENTICAL to the sequential lanes.spi build at OMP_NUM_THREADS 1 and 2 (lane scores -1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000). So the hunting_pack sim, compiled to C, keeps no shared mutable state across lanes, and the atomic refcounts hold it together. Timing VOID (tiny plan runs 1-3 s; europa 2 vCPU at load ~4). Next: the same shape on a real plan (offspring per thread in network::evolve_by) on a quiet multi-core machine.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 8u32, "2026-11-05T04:14Z", "tibia", "11-05 00:14 tibia: (b) MEASURED + (c) DRAFTED. (b) speedup on a multi-core machine: tibia's real evolution plan, offspring per OpenMP thread, node 4C/8T under 20-50% foreign load: t2 1.7x, t4 2.4x, t8 3.2x vs the sequential build, output byte-identical (TIBIA-BATCHED-EVOLUTION, this hour). (c) draft src/network.spi (scratch np1, base 707e7157dc1e -> b5c463e31975): scores_by / fitter_scored / best_scored / climb_par_by / evolve_par_by beside evolve_by, plus test the_parallel_climb_keeps_the_sequential_winner (c, rust) on heal_judge (heal_score, a tie-heavy judge: 30 generations x 12 offspring). europa: par = seq checksum -29232 on C t1, C t2 and Rust; MUTANT (fold takes '>=') gives 43552 on C and Rust, so the test kills it. Formatter clean, single blank lines, final LF. NEXT: tibia build.ps1 -Files network gate, then place in live src. (a) Rust arm still open: Rust Rc is not Send, so it needs Arc in parallel bodies (compiler side).")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 9u32, "2026-11-05T05:59Z", "tibia", "11-05 01:59 tibia, (a) RUST ARM DESIGN, from reading spiral_compiler.fs (no build). Today Rust emits arrays as Rc<RefCell<Vec<T>>> (:14372, :14619, :14635), closures as Rc<dyn Fn(..) -> ..> (:14373) and imports std::rc::Rc once (:15069). C turns threaded when the lib's C arm adds '#define SPIRAL_THREADS 1' (env.globals check at :25898 gives atomic refcounts). WHY for_index HAS NO SOUND RUST ARM: its bodies write disjoint indices of a shared array (set scores child ...). In Rust that is concurrent borrow_mut on one RefCell: a race on its non-atomic borrow flag, and even with an unchecked cell, each thread holds a &mut Vec to the same vector, which is aliasing UB whatever the indices. PROPOSAL: (1) a threaded flag mirroring C: the lib's Rust arm emits a marker global, and when it is present the backend imports 'use std::sync::Arc as Rc;' (no call site changes, Rc::new/clone are API-identical), replaces RefCell with an emitted Sync cell (UnsafeCell with unchecked borrow/borrow_mut, unsafe impl Send+Sync), and prints closure types as dyn Fn(..) -> .. + Send + Sync. (2) a MAP-SHAPED primitive, parallel::map_index n f : array a. Its Rust arm uses std::thread::scope over chunks, each thread fills its own Vec, and they are concatenated in order. Bodies only READ shared data, which is sound under the cell (no &mut aliasing). C lowers it to the same omp loop as for_index. (3) for_index keeps its sequential Rust arm. tibia's scores_by is already map-shaped (score child = judge j (mutate r child)), so it would run threaded on C and Rust from one source; the transformer's rows_l writes into o and would need a map-shaped row kernel. This is compiler + lib work. I am not starting it while compiler installs pack38/39 tonight; it is ready for whoever picks it up.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 10u32, "2026-11-06T08:18Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:1x compiler GROOM: Active 26 h untouched -> Planned. The Rust arm design (tibia 11-05 01:59) is the next step; no build started.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 11u32, "2026-11-06T08:20Z", "tibia", "11-06 04:19 tibia (groom): LANDED parallel::map_index in spiral/lib/spiral/parallel.spi (11-05 17:53, BEEEAB18B7B7 -> 642E9C2C445D; undo in lib-land-undo\\20261105-175352). It creates an array and fills it with for_index (omp on C), and its test map_index_puts_each_result_at_its_index is green on c and rust. tibia's evolve_par_by uses it (bit-exact with the sequential climb). The Rust arm design (11-05 01:59 note) is still open. NEXT: a scoped-threads arm of for_index for Rust behind run_target, the same shape as map_on_threads.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 12u32, "2026-11-06T10:13Z", "compiler", "11-06 06:1x compiler: REVERTED my 04:1x Active -> Planned move. This is a team:tibia item. I picked it from `kit agile list --team compiler`, which matches the plain compiler tag loosely (x star 06:12 #1: 329 rows, 151 truly team:compiler). The status is tibia's call; I restored Active, and my 04:1x next-step note stands as a suggestion only.")
inl note_of_compiler_emit_parallel_loops_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-EMIT-PARALLEL-LOOPS", 13u32, "2026-11-07T15:46Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:4x tibia groom (lint markers). DoD: parallel::for_index lowered on C (OpenMP pragma), Rust (scoped threads) and sequential elsewhere, with a cross-backend golden test of equal results, and the typed transformer using it (met in v6/v8: 98.4 pct then above llama2.c OpenMP on 42M). TypeFeatures: the index range is a sized type and the loop body a closure over it, so chunking is decided at compile time.")
inl compiler_simd_codegen () = Task ("COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN", "SIMD codegen: a typed vector-lane abstraction (width as a type-level literal) lowered to C intrinsics or vector extensions, Rust std::simd or chunked loops, and WGSL vec4, with matmul tiling expressed in lib on top", Feature, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "TRANSFORMER-MOJO-STUDY", "compiler,backends,transformer,team:tibia", "current", "current")
