inl note_of_link_split_invariance_29 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 29u32, "2026-11-06T19:39Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:4x compiler: DoD (restated for the lint, from the title): (1) the split oracle runs on node/europa over the 5 mega samples plus lib, x, kit and Cells as corpus, (2) every legal split gives byte-equal output and equal typing verdicts to the single-unit build (0 DIFFERS), (3) REACH is reported per construct crossing the cut, (4) the oracle is wired as the regression gate for link changes. Today: (1) for samples, (2) holds on pack55d (616 cuts, 333 legal, 0 fail), (3) and the lib/x/kit/Cells corpus remain. next step: break REACH down by crossing construct from the saved results.tsv (CPU-light), then add the corpus roots.")
inl note_of_link_split_invariance_30 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 30u32, "2026-11-06T19:47Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:5x compiler: REACH BREAKDOWN of the pack55d run (saved results.tsv, 616 cuts, 333 LEGAL, 283 refused). 201 refusals are NOT split losses: all are Rust cuts of 19 samples whose SINGLE-unit Rust build fails the same way (198 'return type of main ... should be a 32-bit int', 3 'cannot find the backend Rust in the backend switch'; checked against today's single-unit --batch results: 19 of 19 error there too). Corrected REACH = 333 / (616 - 201) = 333/415 = 80.2%. The real typefeatureset loss is 82 cuts (19.8%) in 10 samples, ALL existentials crossing the boundary: 44 'Cannot dyn an existential into a runtime var', 22 'Runtime existentials cannot be destructured', 16 'Existentials are not supported at runtime' (contract_capability_session, contract_dual_interpreter_dag, contract_existential, contract_existential_job_registry, contract_generic_recursive_plan_fold, contract_indexed_free_program, contract_type_aligned_free_category, mega_brzozowski_derivatives, mega_lean_cic_bottom_up_kernel, mega_spiral_proves_spiral_relative_consistency). No GADT/forall/HKT/phantom refusal appears at all today. So the boundary work that moves REACH is existentials only (LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH's M1 vs M2 decision). next step: make the oracle skip cuts of samples whose single-unit build fails on that backend, so REACH reports 80.2% directly; then the lib/x/kit/Cells corpus.")
inl note_of_link_split_invariance_31 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 31u32, "2026-11-06T19:48Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:5x compiler: DoD: the split oracle covers the 5 mega samples plus the lib, x, kit and Cells corpus on node/europa; every legal split is byte-equal with equal typing verdicts (0 DIFFERS); REACH is reported per crossing construct with samples that fail single-unit excluded; the oracle runs as the regression gate for link changes.")
inl note_of_link_split_invariance_32 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 32u32, "2026-11-06T19:53Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:5x compiler: ORACLE FIX in the tree (apps/compiler/scripts/test.ps1, split suite, unstaged, parse 0): a sample-backend pair whose SINGLE-unit build fails is skipped with a 'skip <id> <backend>: its single-unit build fails' line instead of building ~20 refused cuts, and the summary prints REACH as a percentage plus the skipped-pair count. Verdicts cannot change (refused rows were never FAIL). Checked on the box with the installed core: ebpf_nested Rust (single-unit build fails) -> skipped, 0 cuts, exit 0; ebpf_nested Zig -> 3 cuts, 3 legal (100%), exit 0, the same 3/3 as the node run. On today's corpus this drops 201 cuts (about a third of the 3 h 43 min node run), and REACH then reads 333/415 = 80.2% directly. next step: the lib/x/kit/Cells corpus in matrix.Split.")
inl note_of_link_split_invariance_33 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 33u32, "2026-11-06T23:40Z", "compiler", "11-06 19:4x compiler: the corpus part of the DoD is largely met by child LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS (Done): module-boundary cuts of 6 real packages (x client + sim, Cells, kernel, regex, tibia), 51 cuts, 0 fail, all equal to the single package's --check verdict. lib/spiral and kit/src remain (implicit module lists; LINK-SPLIT-CORPUS-IMPLICIT, P2). Together with the sample oracle (616 cuts, 0 fail, corrected REACH 80.2% with existentials the only loss), what is left here is wiring both suites as the regression gate for link changes and the implicit-module roots.")
inl note_of_link_split_invariance_34 () = Note ("LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE", 34u32, "2026-11-09T10:59Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:0x compiler (groom, star exchange): remaining DoD item (4) \"the oracle runs as the regression gate for link changes\" now has ONE entry: scripts/test.ps1 -Suite linkgate (unstaged) runs -Suite split then -Suite split-corpus with the same -Mode/-Configuration/-TimeoutSec/-Parallel/-Filter, exits 1 if either fails, and runs before the per-cache mutex (each part takes it in turn). Plumbing proven on ganymede: an empty filter gives exit 0/0 -> 0; an invalid filter gives exit 1/1 -> 1 (child stdout routed to the host so only exit codes are counted; the first try counted output lines and reported 1 on success). Next: run linkgate on the next link-touching pack, and add it to the pack-gate recipe (pack gates run -Suite all + linked today).")
inl link_gadt_facade () = Task ("LINK-GADT-FACADE", "M2, GADTs through a link: boundary, iggy-style: the GADT and its typed facade live in a MODEL unit that every side imports and inlines at compile time (no code of its own). GADT refinement happens in each importer, and only a concrete erased codec, generated from the same GADT by reflection so encoder and decoder agree by construction, crosses as compiled code. Pilots: the Cells daemon protocol (request r, 5 replies), iggy's job/lease/slot message model (Rust and Gleam), and x's wire reads r / writes r. Tests: a round-trip property per constructor; NEGATIVE compile tests, where a wrong reply type at a call site in an importer unit must fail to type-check; split-invariance kept", Story, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS", "team:microblog,compiler,linked-units,typefeatureset,gadt,iggy,cells", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_link_gadt_facade () = Planning ("LINK-GADT-FACADE", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl link_generic_interface () = Task ("LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", "M3, generic exports through unit interfaces: <stem>.unit.json also carries the typed definition of each polymorphic/GADT-indexed export, the way Rust rlib metadata, GHC INLINABLE unfoldings and OCaml .cmx inline info work, and the importer specializes it with Spiral's partial evaluator. The compiled ABI stays monomorphic, so LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE holds at the ABI level, not the source level. Specialized copies land in the importer unit and count toward its 1000-line gate. Tests: split-invariance reach on forall/GADT/HKT cuts; an interface-hash change rebuilds exactly the importers", Story, P3, D7, 0u32, Planned, "COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,typefeatureset,gadt", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_link_generic_interface () = Planning ("LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", Minutes 720u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_link_generic_interface_1 () = Note ("LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:26Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:25 x: filed from the user's typed-boundary direction (user 11-02 to x: 'shouldn't the typed boundaries between crates/modules be as type rich as iggy's (and the mega samples' reach)? would it be hacky or a stretch to allow gadts to work through somehow? try reaching a sota model ... this can't be forgotten otherwise kit will drift forever, all projects actually').")
inl note_of_link_generic_interface_2 () = Note ("LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (groom): DoD: a unit exports a generic (forall) definition through its interface file (<stem>.unit.json carrying the typed definition), and a consumer instantiates it at its own types with output equal to the single-unit build; at least one linked sample with a generic export and two instantiations. typefeatureset plan: the exported forall carries its type variables and kinds as typed fields. next step: decide after LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH, since both change what the unit graph stores.")
inl note_of_link_generic_interface_3 () = Note ("LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", 3u32, "2026-11-10T23:38Z", "compiler", "11-10 19:5x compiler: DECIDED (its recorded next step was \"decide after LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH\", which is Done). The bench verdict (11-08 12:27Z) made M2, the GADT facade (LINK-GADT-FACADE), the default boundary mechanism, kept M1 only for open-world plugin boundaries, and named this item (M3: generic exports through <stem>.unit.json, specialized by the importer) \"an optional later upgrade that changes nothing in the source\". So M3 is NOT scheduled now. Resume trigger, measured rather than prose: a linked build where the M2 facade costs either (a) reach (a split-oracle cut that differs only because a forall/GADT export cannot cross as a facade), or (b) > 10% generated size or compile time vs the one-unit build on the same input. Until then this item waits; lowered P? -> P3 so it stops outranking work with a live gate. (Correction: the priority was P0, now P3.)")
inl link_boundary_bench () = Task ("LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH", "Pre-registered decision between boundary mechanisms M1 (existential at the boundary, today's advice), M2 (LINK-GADT-FACADE) and M3 (LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE) on the pilots plus the split-invariance corpus. METRICS: static safety (negative compile tests caught, must be 100% for any winner), reach (legal-cut share, by construct), generated lines per unit (gate 1000), cold and incremental compile time (rebuild scope after a model-unit edit), and runtime dispatch (bench with an A/A floor). RULE: M3 is the default iff its reach >= M2's, its compile time <= 1.3x M2's and the gate holds; otherwise M2 is the default; M1 stays only for open-world, runtime-loaded plugin boundaries. The verdict revises LINKED-UNITS-MONOMORPHIC-RULE's advice and TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1's concrete-signature gate", TaskKind, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE,LINK-GADT-FACADE,LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,bench,decision", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_link_boundary_bench () = Planning ("LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl typed_boundary_ratchet () = Task ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", "No repo drifts: every repo's CI (kit, x, tibia, fiscal, spiral lib and apps, html, regex, dice, alphabet, blog) runs the typefeatureset census per unit AND per link: boundary (constructs crossing each boundary, plus the model_metrics M-scores against the mega samples) against a committed baseline, and refuses any decrease. The same gate runs locally in kit standards. A new repo gets it from the org template (TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1). DoD: a red CI on a deliberate drift commit in each repo (a scratch branch, never pushed), a green run on HEAD, and the baselines tracked", Story, P0, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-STYLE-RATCHET", "team:rig,kit,typefeatureset,ratchet,all-repos,linked-units", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_typed_boundary_ratchet () = Planning ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_typed_boundary_ratchet_1 () = Note ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:26Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:25 x: filed from the user's typed-boundary direction (user 11-02 to x: 'shouldn't the typed boundaries between crates/modules be as type rich as iggy's (and the mega samples' reach)? would it be hacky or a stretch to allow gadts to work through somehow? try reaching a sota model ... this can't be forgotten otherwise kit will drift forever, all projects actually').")
inl note_of_typed_boundary_ratchet_2 () = Note ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", 2u32, "2026-11-03T08:20Z", "kit", "11-03 04:1x kit groom (user: kit keeps drifting; groom so it catches up). Related, not a duplicate: KIT-BASELINE-ONLY-SHRINKS is kit's local guard, refusing baseline rows for new code (git HEAD comparison). This item is the cross-repo CI ratchet; it can reuse that check once it lands.")
inl note_of_typed_boundary_ratchet_3 () = Note ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", 3u32, "2026-11-08T12:22Z", "microblog", "Census 11-08 (microblog, user ask: teach every lane how far its repo is from SOTA). Proxies per repo: exports, wire exports (all-primitive signature), native lines, linked units, hand host lines. spiral: 44 exports, 40 wire (38 in apps/cells/spi/main.spi, the Elixir-to-Spiral boundary), 15 linked units, Cells 11,223 .ex + 7,647 .exs. kit: 483 exports, 111 wire, 97 linked units. x: 71 exports, 35 wire, 0 linked units. html: 28 exports, 1 wire, 8 linked units (the template). tibia, fiscal, regex, alphabet, blog: single unit, 0 exports. dice 1,921 and polyglot 19,186 host lines. Playbook with the 5 rules and the full table: memory typed-boundaries-playbook. NEXT STEP: each owning lane files its own heal item from its row; this item turns the census into a CI gate (wire exports, natives and host lines only shrink).")
inl note_of_typed_boundary_ratchet_4 () = Note ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", 4u32, "2026-11-08T16:08Z", "microblog", "Left off: the 11-08 census proxies (exports, wire exports, natives, linked units, host lines) were computed by a temp scratch script in the microblog session scratchpad (boundary_census.ps1), which is not durable and cannot be a gate. This item should own a real kit verb for the census (no new .ps1 in repos) and store per-repo baselines that only shrink, with an allow-list for planned deletions (polyglot, the recovered i574n.github file).")
inl note_of_typed_boundary_ratchet_5 () = Note ("TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", 5u32, "2026-11-11T04:40Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-11: the template that carries this ratchet to new repos is now RIG-NEW-SCAFFOLD (rig new, typed-units/2).")
inl typed_link_sota () = Task ("TYPED-LINK-SOTA", "Typed links at SOTA: linking costs no typefeatureset expressiveness. Boundaries between crates/modules/units are as type-rich as one mega-sample compilation unit and as iggy's GADT message model, while the compiled ABI stays concrete. DoD: LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE reach 100% with byte-equal outputs on the 5 mega samples; LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH's verdict applied to the Cells, iggy and x pilots; TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET green in every repo's CI", Epic, P0, D7, 0u32, Planned, "LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE,LINK-GADT-FACADE,LINK-GENERIC-INTERFACE,LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH,TYPED-BOUNDARY-RATCHET", "team:compiler,compiler,kit,x,linked-units,typefeatureset,sota,all-repos", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_typed_link_sota () = Planning ("TYPED-LINK-SOTA", Minutes 1800u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_typed_link_sota_1 () = Note ("TYPED-LINK-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:26Z", "x", "2026-11-02 10:25 x: epic filed from user 11-02 to x: 'shouldn't the typed boundaries between crates/modules be as type rich as iggy's (and the mega samples' reach)? would it be hacky or a stretch to allow gadts to work through somehow? try reaching a sota model ... this can't be forgotten otherwise kit will drift forever, all projects actually'. Not hacky: GADTs cross at compile time (M2 shared inline model unit, or M3 interface-shipped generic definitions), while only concrete ABI code crosses at run time, which is what the monomorphic rule protects.")
inl note_of_typed_link_sota_2 () = Note ("TYPED-LINK-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (groom): DoD: the split oracle's REACH (legal cuts / cuts whose single-unit build works) reaches 100% on the sample corpus with 0 DIFFERS, and the split-corpus suite stays 0 fail on every real package. Measured 11-06: REACH 80.2%, and the ONLY loss is existentials crossing the boundary (82 cuts in 10 samples), so the epic's remaining work is LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH's mechanism choice plus its implementation. typefeatureset plan: boundary types carry their full Spiral type in the unit graph (typed records), never a printed string. next step: LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH.")
inl agile_epic_dep_lint () = Task ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", "kit agile check flags a dep on an unfinished Epic (an epic is a container: a child names its parent in a note, it never depends on it), and agile next explains when an item is hidden only by an epic dep", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:rig,kit,agile,backlog-hygiene", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_agile_epic_dep_lint () = Planning ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_agile_epic_dep_lint_1 () = Note ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", 1u32, "2026-11-02T14:34Z", "main", "main 11-02: found twice in one morning (the Lean/CIC agent on SPS-NOTEBOOKS, the regex agent on 23 of its own items), then swept backlog-wide: 67 open items depended on an unfinished Epic (no team tag 31, tibia 13, main 10, kit 6, fiscal 4, compiler 2, x 1). An epic depending on its children is fine; the reverse silently hides ready work from agile next. main cleared its own 10 (parent recorded in a note). List: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\i574n.github\\GROOM-1102-MAIN\\epic-deps.csv. DoD: agile check reports each epic dep with the fix; a ratchet keeps the count at the current floor and lanes drive it to 0.")
inl note_of_agile_epic_dep_lint_2 () = Note ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", 2u32, "2026-11-02T14:46Z", "main", "main 11-02 lesson for the lint: an epic dep cannot be classified mechanically here, because the parent link is recorded ONLY as the child dep (epics do not list their children, and the JSON has no note text). main cleared 19 parent-only deps (clear children by filing and name) and restored 5 that may be real prerequisites (BEND2-IN-SPIRAL, TREE-PAGES-EVERY-REPO, CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR, FSX-FROM-SPI). So the lint should separate the two meanings: a parent field (or :parent note) for membership, deps only for real ordering; then agile next can ignore membership.")
inl note_of_agile_epic_dep_lint_3 () = Note ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", 3u32, "2026-11-02T14:59Z", "main", "main 11-02 related trap from the star exchange (fiscal #9): kit agile edit <id> deps X REPLACES the dep list (same for tags), so a 'fix' can silently drop real prerequisites. agile show first, then pass the full list. The lint should warn when an edit drops a dep that is not Done.")
inl note_of_agile_epic_dep_lint_4 () = Note ("AGILE-EPIC-DEP-LINT", 4u32, "2026-11-09T06:11Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:4x local rig MEASURED on the live backlog (1963 items, 93 Epics): 31 open items depend on an Epic that is not Done, so each is hidden from agile next until a container finishes, which by this item's rule never makes sense (a child names its parent in a note or a parent link, it does not depend on it). Examples: CELLS-PORT-MOUNTS-MANIFEST and SPIRAL-COMPILER-1-0-DOD on SPIPROJ-RETIRE; LINKED-UNITS-C and MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE on UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA; BEND2-IN-SPIRAL on LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS; FSX-FROM-SPI on SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL; SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL on SPIRAL3-EDITIONS and KINO-SOTA (an epic on epics); CELLS-NEW-PROJECT on PS1-TO-SPIRAL; JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD on RIG-SELF-HEALING; EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION on SPIRAL3-EDITIONS; KIT-LIB-REUSE on KIT-GRANULAR-MIGRATION. Not rewritten by rig: deps are each owning team's; the lint names them so owners convert each dep into a parent link (agile edit <id> --parent <EPIC>) or into a dep on the real child item. Plan (TRAIN43): agile check emits one warning per such dep with the suggested parent link; agile next prints hidden-by-epic counts per team. DoD unchanged.")
