inl note_of_gadt_domain_stress_samples_1 () = Note ("GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:04Z", "fiscal", "fiscal 11-02 12:04 filed by fiscal from the typefeatureset survey. GADT bugs keep surfacing late (compiler 11-02 hurdle #12: erasing a unit-nominal GADT index broke 4 samples) because the samples are toy-sized and the domain models live in lane repos. Candidates, all green today: fiscal/src/lifecycle.spi (document s indexed by authorized/cancelled; 2 negative tests that must fail with Unification failure), the generated staged s a signing union (fiscal generated_nfe_v4), tibia vocations.spi (spell v over healer v) and readiness.spi (ready group), lib keyed_rng.spi (\\\\* p {purpose_code} constraints). RULE (regression-samples-allowed + compiler hurdle: a sample without a matrix row catches nothing): each one lands as a sample WITH its matrix row, and its negative tests run as expected-failure rows. DONE WHEN: 5 samples on every backend the matrix runs; a deliberately broken build (index erased) turns at least one row red.")
inl note_of_gadt_domain_stress_samples_2 () = Note ("GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES", 2u32, "2026-11-08T13:46Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: INTAKE from fiscal (OMNILEDGER-ARCHIVE P1, notes 8-9; user 11-08: retire mega_omniledger_erp_kernel means DELETE once absorbed). Compiler's part B, to size at this item's groom:\n(1) Here: the 5 compiler-stress negatives (*_lineage_cannot_force_nat_*, physical_*_fixed_depth; matrix :352/:365/:367/:377/:380) and the type-level naturals (21 of the suite's 26 nat_succ rows sit in the omniledger negatives, matrix :343-383).\n(2) WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB: the 16 durability negatives and 33 terminals (CAS races, fencing, WAL recovery).\n(3) The runtime_native codegen rows (11 backends) and bench-native.ps1:5: rehome or retire.\n(4) Two negative dirs have no matrix row and never run: negative_recursive_payload_outbox_version_mismatch, negative_three_owner_physical_takeover_authority_wrong_endpoint. Wire them or delete them with the sample.\nNEXT STEP: at groom, read the 41 negative rows and map each to a home (this item, WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB, or retire with evidence); deletion waits for that map plus fiscal's 9 business rows.")
inl note_of_gadt_domain_stress_samples_3 () = Note ("GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:51Z", "org-f33af161", "org-f33af161 contributed the font (alphabet lib) as a real-domain GADT stress sample with 4 VALIDATED negative tests (minimal Cells notebook, passed 4/4, 36s on europa): ar_initial/ar_medial on a right-joining letter (arabic right_j vs dual_j) and deva_base on an independent vowel / dependent matra (deva indvow_c / matra_c vs cons_c) all fail with Unification as designed. Models in alphabet/lib/font_glyph.spi (arabic dual_j/right_j GADT; deva category/cluster/syllable GADT). REMAINING for compiler lane (owns the sample matrix in apps/compiler/tmp): port the font model as a self-contained sample dir + matrix rows on every backend, negatives as Contains=Unification expected-failure rows. Releasing the lease to compiler for the matrix integration; the real-domain model + negatives are proven and ready to port.")
inl note_of_gadt_domain_stress_samples_4 () = Note ("GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES", 4u32, "2026-11-10T23:59Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:1x compiler: the font slice is in (GADT-DOMAIN-STRESS-SAMPLES-D1 Done: 1 positive sample + 4 expected-Unification negatives, validated on ganymede pack89). Remaining for the parent: the positive row on every backend (Gleam, Zig, Kotlin, Swift, Lua, Python, TypeScript, Wasm, Lean) and its C-oracle agreement in a full -Native run. Next step: add gadt_font_joining to those backend sections and include it in the next all-backend -Native run on moon when moon is quiet.")
inl compiler_type_arith_normalize () = Task ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", "Compiler: normalize type-level arithmetic (type_arith add/sub/mult/div/divup over literals) during inference so equal shapes unify regardless of spelling; prerequisite of typed tensor reshape/flatten and computed dims", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "TYPE-ARITH-1-SITES,TYPE-ARITH-2-SAMPLE,TYPE-ARITH-3-FOLD,TYPE-ARITH-4-PACK,TYPE-ARITH-5-RESHAPE", "compiler,typefeatureset,transformer,lib,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize () = Planning ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 1u32, "2026-11-02T16:37Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, from LIB-TYPE-SHAPES probe 2 (node job shape-probe-4; drafts %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-TYPE-SHAPES). Measured on our compiler: \"sized (add 2 2) i32\" does NOT unify with \"sized 4 i32\" (Got sized 4i32 i32, Expected sized (add 2i32 2i32) i32), and add 2 2 does not unify with mult 2 2. type_arith's nominals are type-level macros that are only evaluated in partial evaluation; inference compares them unevaluated. The ML library worked around it with nominal_recreate at real level. Without normalization, typed tensors can match equal shapes only when they are spelled the same: matmul's inner dims work, reshape/flatten/split and computed dims (dim = heads * head_dim) do not. DoD: Infer evaluates add/sub/mult/div/divup on literal arguments (and on type variables bound to literals) before unification, so add 2 2 = mult 2 2 = 4; stuck terms over free variables stay symbolic; a positive and a negative sample in the matrix on C and Rust.")
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 2u32, "2026-11-02T19:16Z", "tibia", "11-02 tibia, DESIGN READ with the compiler lane's pointers (core C9CF9F36 = pack17), no code yet. WHY it fails: Infer types every type-level term `(...) as an opaque TySymbol \"<term>\" (spiral_compiler.fs:6230). type_arith's bodies are such terms (lit_to_type_lit (type_lit_to_lit `a + ...)), only PE evaluates them, so unify_gadt's loop (5580) sees TyApply(TyApply(TyNominal add, 2), 2) vs TyLit 4, compares structurally (5616) and TyLit only by equality (5637), and fails. Recognizing add/mult by name, or by the shape of their macro body, is fragile (compiler lane's review point). SOUND SHAPE (agreed with the compiler lane; it reviews and stacks into a pack): built-in type-level integer ops. (1) A T case or a compiler-known nominal family for add/sub/mult/div/divup over type literals, with parser syntax for it. (2) A normalize step at the top of unify's loop (5581): fold when both args are TyLit integers of the same primitive; leave terms over type vars symbolic; normalize both sides recursively through TyApply. (3) The same fold in PE's nominal_type_apply (~9905). (4) Every exhaustive match on T in Infer gains the case (visit, subst, has_metavars, the kind-of, printing). (5) lib type_arith redefined on the built-ins. (6) Samples under apps/compiler/tmp/samples: a positive (add 2 2 = 4 = mult 2 2; a reshape helper typed `sized (mult a b) -> sized a ... `) and a negative (add 2 3 vs 4 rejected), on F#/C/Rust/Delphi/Zig; compare-runs in-process. Estimate: one focused session (3-5 h) as a kit-do program, plus the compiler lane's A/B.")
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:00Z", "tibia", "11-06 07:1x tibia: SPLIT into five steps of 120 min or less (main 07:0x): TYPE-ARITH-1-SITES (60, light, ready) -> 2-SAMPLE (60) -> 3-FOLD (120, the compiler change) -> 4-PACK (90) -> 5-RESHAPE (90). The parent closes when step 5 is green. Step 1 is ready now.")
inl note_of_compiler_type_arith_normalize_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-TYPE-ARITH-NORMALIZE", 4u32, "2026-11-07T15:46Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:4x tibia groom (lint markers). Next step: compiler builds candidate ta3b (pack57 + 2-lazy-fold.kitdo 7ED283E66E00) and gates it on sized_array.spi's type probe; then I run probe3 on its DLL. TypeFeatures: type_arith ops are recognized by GlobalIds of the reserved core module, never by name, and fold only when unification cannot match structurally.")
