inl kit_train_incremental_regen () = Task ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", "release trains regenerate only changed owners: key regen on the train's lib snapshot, not a live lib that moves mid-run", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "", "team:rig,kit,train,regen,speed", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_train_incremental_regen () = Planning ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_1 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 1u32, "2026-11-02T21:55Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 17:5x DIAGNOSIS: each release train spends ~57 min regenerating all 281 owners although few changed (TRAIN13b: 0 of 113 skipped so far after TRAIN13 recorded keys at 17:02). test-regeneration -Incremental keys each owner on compiler hash + EVERY lib/spiral file hash + its closure's src files (relative paths, so that part is fine). The keys are computed once at the START of a run but written at the END. TRAIN13's regen ran 16:05-17:02, and lib/spiral file_system.spi + convert.spi changed at 16:14 (another lane's land), so the 281 keys it stored described a lib that no longer existed. With lanes landing into lib all day, no key ever matches. FIX DESIGN: (1) read the lib from the train's own lib snapshot (.cache/release-tree/<name>-lib), never the live lib, so keys and compiles see one lib version (this also closes the notebooks-read-live-lib leak); (2) recompute the shared lib hash right before recording keys and refuse to record (log \"lib changed during regen; keys not recorded\") if it moved; (3) narrower keys (only the lib modules a closure reaches) are a later optimisation, because whole-lib keying is the conservative-correct rule. Expected effect: a train after a train with no lib change regenerates only the changed owners (minutes instead of ~1 h). Proof: two consecutive trains on an unchanged lib, the second showing \"unchanged inputs; compile skipped\" for every untouched owner and byte-identical outputs.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_2 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 2u32, "2026-11-02T21:58Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 18:0x BLOCKER for fix (1): the Spiral compiler takes no lib override (no SPIRAL_LIB or similar in apps/compiler; SPIRAL_WORKSPACE_ROOT only feeds runtime-compat). It resolves lib/spiral from its own bundle location (apps/compiler/tmp/../../../lib/spiral), so a train can't point regen at its frozen lib copy without a compiler-side lib-root option. Ask the compiler lane for one (env SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT honoured by package resolution of spiral-/core-). Until then fix (2) alone, refusing to record keys when the lib moved mid-run, only avoids recording stale keys and saves no time, so it waits for (1).")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_3 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 3u32, "2026-11-02T22:30Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 18:3x PARALLEL REGEN landed (unstaged, for the next train; TRAIN13b still runs the serial snapshot copy): test-regeneration.ps1 -Parallel <1..8> (default 1 = unchanged) schedules up to n compiler processes and never starts an owner while one of its link: units is running or pending (rows are already link-first). release-train passes -Parallel 3. PROOF: europa (2 cores) gave byte-identical outputs for 12 owners including link pairs (kit_rec_cycle/kit_standards_scan, the agile_claim_* crates): diff -r found nothing. The box (8 cores, sharing with TRAIN13b's serial regen) went from 623 s serial to 315 s parallel for the same 12 owners (2.0x). A full 281-owner regen should drop from ~57 min to ~25 min. This is independent of the incremental-key fix, which still needs a compiler lib-root option.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_4 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 4u32, "2026-11-03T01:17Z", "compiler", "11-02 21:2x compiler: compiler half DONE as a candidate. SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT=<dir>: a named non-relative package entry (spiral-, dice-) resolves to <dir>/<name> when that folder exists, else today's packageDir resolution; relative entries and |core- untouched (SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR already covers core). Program %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN\\1-spiral-lib-root.kitdo (2 replacements, SpiProj.schema resolve_packages). Node t1101: consumer of sm'::line_count exits 2 unset, 99 with a lib copy whose line_count returns 99; A/B unset vs pack20 0 cells. Candidate DLL C:\\home\\remote-stage\\pack20\\cache-b\\...\\SpiralCompiler.dll (core AF5E086014D2DD1C), handed to kit for the two-train europa A/B; it ships in pack20b.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_5 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 5u32, "2026-11-03T01:18Z", "kit", "kit 11-02 21:2x KIT SIDE WIRED (unstaged): test-regeneration.ps1 -LibRoot <dir holding spiral/> exports SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT and keys the incremental cache on that frozen lib. release-train passes the train's lib snapshot (Split-Path of -Lib, i.e. <train>-lib/lib) only when KIT_REGEN_FROZEN_LIB=1. That opt-in is deliberate: a compiler without SPIRAL_LIB_ROOT would compile against the live lib while the keys describe the frozen one, which makes a skip decision unsafe. Enable it once pack20b, compiler's candidate (host 6B30D2F521660878, core AF5E086014D2DD1C, proven on t1101), is the installed compiler. A/B PLAN on the node with that DLL: two consecutive test-regeneration runs with -LibRoot on an unchanged lib snapshot. Pass = the second run logs \"unchanged inputs; compile skipped\" for every owner with outputs byte-identical to the first; then touch one lib file and require that only its dependent owners recompile.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_6 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 6u32, "2026-11-10T22:58Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 19:0x rig (claimed): MEASURED why incremental regen saves nothing today. TRAIN43f node regen on moon: 05:50 to 06:49 (59 min), Published 0 changed owners; 376 unchanged, yet 0 compile skipped lines: every owner recompiled to byte-identical output. TRAIN43e had the same compiler core (F414863971EB) and also skipped 0. Keys (moon %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\regen-keys, 628 files, 376 rewritten 11-10 06h by 43f) are written only after a publish and hash the compiler, the WHOLE lib (every .spi under lib/spiral) and the owner closure; 43f differed from 43e by one kit file, so the likely cause is lib churn: the node mirror re-syncs the lib per train and any one lib file change invalidates all 376 owners. RUNNING the decisive check: job-1794351494216-rig-regen-ab on moon re-runs test-regeneration -Incremental (no publish, no key writes) on the untouched train25 tree with the TRAIN43f pin; if it skips ~376, the mechanism works and the fix is keying on what each owner reads from the lib (or the frozen lib per train with the key carried across trains), not the whole lib.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_7 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 7u32, "2026-11-10T23:22Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 19:2x rig: RESULT of job-1794351494216-rig-regen-ab (moon, untouched train25 tree, TRAIN43f pin, -Incremental, no publish): 249 of 376 owners skipped, 127 compiled, 23 min wall at -Parallel 2 (TRAIN43f's own regen on the same tree: 0 skipped, 59 min at -Parallel 3). So the key mechanism WORKS; two things defeat it. (1) LIB CHURN: each owner key hashes every lib file, and the train re-syncs the lib, so any one lib change between trains voids all 376 keys (that is why 43f, one kit file away from 43e with the same core, skipped 0). (2) LINKED UNITS ARE NEVER SKIPPED (test-regeneration.ps1:272 excludes $linkedUnits), and they are most of the 127 compiled here; each split into linked units (today: rig_units, rig_drafts, task_creation) grows that set. NEXT STEP (authoring): key each owner on the lib modules its closure opens (or on the frozen per-train lib with the key carried across trains), and let a linked unit skip when its own key and its consumers' keys are unchanged; then re-run this A/B and expect ~376 skips on an unchanged tree.")
inl note_of_kit_train_incremental_regen_8 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-INCREMENTAL-REGEN", 8u32, "2026-11-10T23:22Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 19:2x rig markers. DoD: on an unchanged tree a second train regen skips every owner, linked units included, with byte-identical outputs; after one lib file changes only the owners whose closure opens that module recompile; proven by the A/B on moon. TYPEFEATURES: the key is a record per owner (compiler, lib modules reached, src closure) in the regen script; when it moves into rig it becomes a typed key union (Fresh :: owner | Stale :: owner * reason) rather than joined hash text.")
