inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_35 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 35u32, "2026-11-06T13:24Z", "x", "2026-11-06 09:24 x: REVIEWED x-dumps' follow-up media-quote-draft (quote media, HTTP Range/206, plus the HARVEST-ENRICH likes/replies/lang slice; serve 6E5665BF3140, page 7F769243E3C5, lib erlang_http_server B4CBB6280AF5). Sent back 4 fixes: (1) suffix ranges 'bytes=-N' serve the first N bytes, because offset_of '' gives 0; (2) multi-range and garbage bounds serve byte 0, so they should give a full 200; (3) the read is still whole-file, so either pread the slice or drop 'streamed' from the plan; (4) list the enrich slice in plan.json. Order: the x-dumps lib land, then the base media (waiting on the Cells regression fix, x-cellsfix2c), then this follow-up.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_36 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 36u32, "2026-11-06T13:33Z", "x", "2026-11-06 09:33 x: x-dumps applied all 4 review fixes to media-quote-draft (timeline_serve 6E5665BF3140 -> 27CABB70AB1A; page 7F769243E3C5 and lib B4CBB6280AF5 unchanged): suffix ranges via suffix_bounds, multi-range and garbage give None (a full 200), range reads use file:open/pread/close on the slice only, and plan.json lists the enrich slice. The pread path needs runtime proof in the serve notebook gate. Order unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_37 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 37u32, "2026-11-06T17:44Z", "x", "2026-11-06 13:44 x: POST MEDIA LANDED on the box (unstaged): timeline_page D9A948E4FEDF -> 1FA707CA3AB2, timeline_serve 525321456A72 -> 960B1C8CE784, sim_serve 943A4542D030 -> 8C3DF631CB92 (x-dumps' drafts, byte-identical), plus 38 generated outputs via node-proof -Land (0 conflicts, 95 unchanged); undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\x-media-20261106-134025. Proof: x-media2 (job-1793981577430, node, pinned pack55d 257DB91886B97811 at start and end): notebooks sim_serve 9/9, timeline_serve 21/21, timeline_page 9/9; sim and client builds 0; e2e RUST-OK (138 requests, 37 steps + 5 cases); census SAME over 82 files. Land note: a PowerShell row-literal slip skipped timeline_page in the first apply; it was re-applied with a fail-closed hash check at 13:4x. Next: x-dumps transfers the enriched world and restarts :8787; then the media-quote-draft follow-up (Range + quote media + enrich) gates on top.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_38 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 38u32, "2026-11-07T00:01Z", "x", "2026-11-06 20:01 x: media-quote follow-up x-media3c (job-1794004185890, node, pinned AND installed pack57 8F0094967068FC85 at start and end, a stable core): builds 0, e2e RUST-OK, census SAME; notebooks sim_serve 9/9 (its 6/9 in x-media3b was the compiler churn), timeline_page 14/14, timeline_serve 21/25 with the SAME 4 Gleam failures as 3b: a_page_hides_lost_posts_but_counts_them, the_timeline_path_serves_an_html_page, the_page_ends_with_the_scroll_script, range_spec_rejects_out_of_range_and_missing. On a stable core these are the draft's. NOT landed. Back to x-dumps.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_39 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 39u32, "2026-11-07T00:16Z", "x", "2026-11-06 20:16 x: NEXT STEP for tomorrow (agreed with x-dumps): rerun the timeline_serve notebook with media-quote-draft (page D089FFEA6102, serve 27CABB70AB1A) on the node, printing each failed row's actual/expected in full (not 200-char summaries). The rows needed are a_page_hides_lost_posts_but_counts_them (the shown set vs base) and range_spec_rejects_out_of_range_and_missing (the Gleam values for 'bytes=2000-3000'/1000 and ''/1000). x-dumps then fixes and resends hashes.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_40 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 40u32, "2026-11-07T11:09Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:09 x: media-quote follow-up LANDED on the box (unstaged): timeline_page 1FA707CA3AB2 -> D089FFEA6102, timeline_serve 960B1C8CE784 -> 0CF97B67E788 (x-dumps' drafts, byte-identical; quoted-post media, HTTP Range/206 with suffix/multi/garbage handling and slice reads, likes/replies counts, lang badge, and the badmap guard on quote-less posts), plus 28 generated outputs via node-proof -Land (0 conflicts, 106 unchanged); undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\x-media-quote-20261107-070839. Proof: x-media3d (job-1794011669803, node mirror xlib2, pinned pack58b 712FA1815A484F1D start and end): notebooks sim_serve 9/9, timeline_serve 25/25, timeline_page 14/14; sim exit 0; census SAME. x-dumps' badmap diagnosis is confirmed by the 3 page tests turning green.")
inl note_of_x_timeline_frontend_41 () = Note ("X-TIMELINE-FRONTEND", 41u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "x", "2026-11-07 11:54 x groom: DoD: the joined timeline UI on :8787 renders media, quote media, counts and lang for the full 5-account world, with zero 500s over a 40-page deep walk, from a gated timeline_serve hash (29/29 + null/missing/string-media controls). x-dumps verified F1E100FC295C on 11-07 (40 pages all 200). What remains: the x CI green after the user's push (CELL-BUDGET fix is unstaged). Next: verify x CI once the user pushes.")
inl x_sim_real_profiles () = Task ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", "Battle-test x sim and xc client against the real downloaded profiles; find and heal edges synthetic data missed", Story, P1, D4, 45u32, Planned, "SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM,X-CLIENT", "team:microblog,microblog,simulator,client,stress", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_x_sim_real_profiles () = Planning ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", Minutes 240u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_1 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 1u32, "2026-11-03T07:18Z", "x-dumps", "User 2026-11-03: create tasks to battle-test x sim and the client with the downloaded profiles, finding edges we could not with synthetic data; heal everything found along the way (file child bugs the way xsim filed the KINO-* items). Real data: people/<id> timelines for juhondacivic, the user accounts, geygey_hadid.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_2 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 2u32, "2026-11-03T07:20Z", "x", "2026-11-03 03:20 x: INTAKE (filed by x-dumps; team:x and assignee added). PLAN: (1) read the real profiles in place (/data/x/people/<id>, never copied into a repo) through the client's posts.spi model, the shared typed model agreed with x-dumps (reply 02:3x); (2) derive small byte-pinned fixtures per edge found (very long threads, quotes of deleted posts, media-only posts, edits, Unicode and CJK weights, URL entities) and add them as sim worlds; (3) run xc against the sim seeded from each real timeline, and every divergence is a sim or client bug healed with a regression world, as the KINO-* bugs were. Proof: the x e2e (-Accept sim build, client build, RUST-OK) plus a per-profile replay count. Waits for x-dumps' harvest-complete note (geygey).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_3 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 3u32, "2026-11-03T14:18Z", "x", "2026-11-03 10:18 x: STARTED (x-dumps: geygey harvest complete). SHAPE SURVEY, read in place on europa /data/x/people (jq, counts only, nothing copied): 5 timelines, 125,528 / 31,596 / 1,950 / 1,736 / 1,732 entries; one uniform 20-field record (account_id, author, author_name, date, id, kind, kind_inferred, lost, media, quote, removed, reply_text, reply_to, reply_to_user, retweet_author, sources, text, time, utc, wayback_capture). Kinds: reply, retweet, tweet, quote, lost (geygey 36,198 / 35,717 / 34,431 / 15,765 / 3,417). 'removed' carries 5+ reason texts (suspended account, deleted by author, account no longer exists, owner limits viewers, archive HTTP 404), often on entries that are otherwise kept. Text length p50 43-54, p99 178-288, MAX 2,009 and 1,262: long-form posts far over 280, the first edge for the client splitter and posts.spi. NEXT: map the record onto posts.spi (XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI's agreed model), then derive byte-pinned fixtures per edge (long-form, each removed reason, quote of removed, lost, media-only, CJK/emoji weights) as sim worlds.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_4 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 4u32, "2026-11-03T14:29Z", "x", "2026-11-03 10:29 x: SURVEY 2 (weights, counted on europa, nothing copied): geygey 11,957 texts carry code points >= U+1100 (CJK, emoji, wide symbols); 321 weigh over 280 when wide chars count double, 209 even raw. juhondacivic: 3,763 wide, 141 weighted over 280, 122 raw. FIXTURE CLASSES so far: (a) long-form over 280, raw and weighted; (b) wide-char weight boundaries at 279/280/281; (c) each removal reason (posts.spi removal union, XDUMPS-TYPED-SPI n6); (d) quote of a removed post; (e) lost with archive 404; (f) media-only and quote+media. Each becomes a byte-pinned sim world derived from one real record (ids kept, text shortened only where the edge allows).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_5 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 5u32, "2026-11-03T15:02Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:02 x: FIXTURE RULE (recorded before deriving any): fixtures copy the SHAPE of a real record, never its content. The x repo is public and the timelines hold third parties' replies, quotes and suspended accounts, so each fixture keeps the edge's measurable properties (kind, flags, removal reason, quote/media combination, raw and weighted length, which character classes and how many of each) with synthetic text and synthetic ids. The real record stays on europa; the fixture carries only a 'derived from <class>' tag, no source id. The battle-test itself (replaying whole real timelines through the sim) runs on europa or the node against /data/x/people in place, and its outputs stay out of repos except aggregate counts.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_6 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 6u32, "2026-11-03T15:06Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:06 x: REPLAY WORLDS built in place on europa (/tmp/x-realshape, outside every repo; jq filter to-world.jq): each real timeline becomes the sim's world post list (id; created_at from utc minus millis, Z suffix; text, with retweets as 'RT @author: text' the way X API v2 shows them since no retweeted id survives; replied_to from reply_to; quoted from quote.id; conversation_id and edit_history = id). Sizes 125,528 / 31,596 / 1,736 / 1,732 / 1,950 posts. EDGE 1 found already: geygey has 36,198 kind=reply but only 29,844 posts with any ref, so ~6k replies have NO reply_to (kind inferred from HTML). The client must show a reply without a parent id, not misparse it. NEXT: after x-events2 builds xc + sim on the node, replay each world: xc timeline <user> --limit N --json against the sim, compare the returned ids with the world's newest N, and every crash, refusal or divergence becomes a child bug with a synthetic-shape fixture (n5 rule).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_7 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 7u32, "2026-11-03T15:09Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:09 x: EDGE 2: two accounts share the username fc1943s (ids 1637273636049498112 and 72784485, a renamed or reused handle), so a username lookup must pick one deliberately and say which. REPLAY RUN x-replay1 prepared (scratchpad launch-x-replay.ps1, queued after x-events2 on the same mirror): the 5 worlds go europa -> node by scp -3 through the box (no repo, no box copy kept); per world: sim-run serve, then xc timeline --json --limit 20 and 200 for the account, comparing returned ids with the world's newest-first order (created_at, then larger id first, as posts.spi's same-second test). It is a DISCOVERY run, not a gate: every non-zero exit, unparsed line or divergence becomes a child bug item with a synthetic-shape fixture (n5).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_8 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 8u32, "2026-11-03T15:42Z", "x", "2026-11-03 11:42 x: SHAPE TARGETS for the long-form class (counts only; len, wide, newlines, urls, mentions, hashtags, kind). Over 280 raw is almost all RETWEETS of long posts: geygey 204 retweet / 4 reply / 1 tweet, juhondacivic 49 retweet / 37 reply / 23 quote / 13 tweet, 72784485 25 tweet / 6 reply / 2 retweet. Longest: geygey 2009/5/9/0/0/1 retweet, 1712/2/17/0/0/1, 1516/12/10, 1424/16/12; juhondacivic 1262/0/22 retweet, 651/7/7/0/1; 72784485 314/1/0/1/1 tweet, 307/3/2/1/0, 303/0/2/1/1. EDGE 3: own 2010-era tweets OVER 280 raw that carry a URL, likely valid on X because a URL weighs 23 whatever its length, so the client's weights must count URLs as 23 or it flags a valid post as over. FIXTURE ORDER: decided by x-replay1, so first the shapes the client actually mishandles, then regression fixtures for those it passes. Synthetic texts reproduce the exact tuple above (e.g. a 2009-char RT with 5 wide chars, 9 newlines, 1 hashtag).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_9 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 9u32, "2026-11-03T16:32Z", "x", "2026-11-03 12:32 x: FIRST SHAPE FIXTURE (class a, long-form), synthetic per the n5 rule: splitter.spi gains long_form () with the exact shape of geygey's longest post (n8 tuple: 2,009 code points, 9 newlines, 5 wide chars; 'RT @x: ' + 9 lines of 200 'a' + 5 CJK + 188 'b') and a block expecting weight_on OnX = 2,014 (one extra per wide char, twitter-text weights in glyphs.spi). The arithmetic is cross-checked outside Spiral. It rides X-WEIGHT-URL-23's splitter draft (08779A98B826) in x-next2, so the long-form weight path and the URL fix are proven together.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_10 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 10u32, "2026-11-03T21:31Z", "x", "2026-11-03 17:30 x: FIRST SHAPE FIXTURE LANDED in x-next2 (job-1793737559206, pinned pack27 core 6F83B94FE33167CC; the installed core moved to D1854B99FD3FA940 mid-run): lib gate 23/23, sim accept 0, sim build 0, client build 0 (CELL-BUDGET, xc.rs 992), e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, census SAME over 76 files with 0 over one post. splitter.spi's long_form () block (geygey's longest-post shape, X weight 2,014) passes. EDGE 3 (URL = 23 on X) is fixed by X-WEIGHT-URL-23, Done.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_11 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 11u32, "2026-11-04T00:57Z", "x", "2026-11-03 20:57 x: x-replay1 VOID by a HARNESS bug, mine, not a client finding: the replay worlds copied the e2e template's 'include' (sim-small/stress/lost/gap.world.json, relative to apps/client/e2e), which don't resolve from the node stage, so the sim panicked on boot in included_users (file_system.read_all_text failed) and every xc call got connection refused. FIX: include = [] in the europa worlds (jq). Relaunched as x-replay2. The template world now replays too, as a built-in CONTROL (a known-good world that must read SAME). Side note for the sim: a missing include file panics the whole server at boot with no message naming the file; a candidate child item (fail with the path) once the replay results are in.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_12 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 12u32, "2026-11-04T01:46Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:46 x: x-replay2 (job-1793755253480, core A63F4C2A, 21 min). The include fix holds: the sim boots and answers all 12 calls; the template CONTROL reads SAME (5/5) at both limits. READING CORRECTED: every real world returned 5 posts in 2 requests because the client asks max_results=5 per account and doesn't page one account's timeline (fields.spi:45 per_account default 5; wire.spi:197 single URL). --limit caps the total across accounts, not one account's depth. My rule compared up to 'limit' entries, which was wrong; the right window is min(limit, per_account). Under that window 9 of 10 replays MATCH (first 5 = the expected newest 5). FIRST REAL FIDELITY FINDING: @fc1943s world 1637273636049498112 differs at entry 4 at both limits. Its newest posts tie within the second (2111907927535591923 and 2111907925237137484 both 19:51:01Z; 2111907922703720578 and 2111907920476561553 both 19:51:00Z). The sim's newest_first (sim_serve.spi timeline_body) sorts by the seconds-only stamp, so ties come out in an unspecified order, while the real API orders by snowflake id desc (ids are time-ordered below the second). Synthetic e2e worlds never tie, which is why fixtures missed it. NEXT: (1) sim: tie-break newest_first by id desc (a small sim_serve change plus a regression fixture with two same-second posts); (2) launcher: window = min(limit, per_account) and expected order = id desc; (3) rerun as x-replay3. Also seen: the replayed posts are dated after the template's now (2026-10-18T12:00Z) and the sim served them anyway, so the sim doesn't filter future posts; set now from the newest post in each world for realism.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_13 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 13u32, "2026-11-04T01:49Z", "x", "2026-11-03 21:49 x: tie-break fix DRAFTED (kit drafts x\\X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES\\sim_serve.spi, base 1C8907F6C334 -> 51565008C73E, formatted, widest line 120, cells under 272). The cause is in sim_serve's insertion sort: 'inserted' kept a post ahead while its stamp was not_before the new one, so same-second ties fell to input order. Now newer_or_same compares the stamp, then post_key as a number (id_at_least: longer id = newer, equal length = text order), which is X's snowflake-id order. PRE-REGISTERED RULE for the next run (x-replay3, with the launcher window = min(limit, per_account) and expected order = created_at desc then id desc): PASS = template control SAME AND all 10 real replays SAME AND sim accept exit 0 with unchanged fixtures (e2e worlds have no same-second ties, so accept must stay byte-identical; any fixture diff = FAIL, investigate). Then add a regression fixture with two same-second posts. Not launched: the run window ends at 22:00.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_14 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 14u32, "2026-11-04T04:08Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:08 x: SECOND FIDELITY EDGE (reported by x-dumps): two real accounts share the handle @fc1943s (72784485 and 1637273636049498112; a handle reused by a newer account). sim_serve's by_handle dict keeps whichever account is inserted last, so /2/users/by?usernames=fc1943s depends on world order. Real X returns only the current holder. PLAN: by_handle resolves a shared handle to the account whose newest post is newest; the other stays reachable by id. Usernames stay real (no suffixes). The joined world (europa /data/x/joined/joined.world.json, x-dumps) orders users oldest account first meanwhile. Fold into the sim_serve slice with the id tie-break, plus a shape-only fixture: two accounts, one handle.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_15 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 15u32, "2026-11-04T04:17Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:17 x: STAR EXCHANGE (user 11-04 via main), lessons this item taught, adopted as memories: derived fixtures inherit path and clock fields (include crashed x-replay1); read the consumer before a pass rule (xc = 5 per account, not --limit); real-shape data finds ties and handle reuse that synthetic fixtures never contain, so seed synthetic worlds with same-second posts and a reused handle on purpose (the next sim_serve slice adds both fixtures).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_16 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 16u32, "2026-11-04T04:31Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:31 x: JOINED WORLD READY (x-dumps 'xarchive world'; europa:/data/x/joined/joined.world.json, aggregate counts only: 5 users, 162,542 posts, 4,896 lost, 43,873 retweets without a target id, 304 same-second posts, one handle shared by 2 accounts; users ordered oldest account first). Shape decisions sent to x-dumps: media as objects {media_key, type from the extension, alt_text, path}, since the sim reads field media_key; no synthesized reposted refs (Repost : user_id in the typed model); the sim API must drop lost posts (X never serves them). NEXT RUN PLAN (not launched; run window closed 22:00): (1) sim_serve slice = id tie-break (draft 51565008C73E) + by_handle current holder + user_posts drops lost == true, with shape-only fixtures for a same-second pair, a reused handle and a lost post; (2) x-replay3 on the joined world. PRE-REGISTERED: per account, xc's first min(limit, per_account = 5) ids equal the expected list (created_at desc, then id desc, lost excluded); retweet and lost rows are counted, not compared; template control SAME; sim accept fixtures unchanged except the new ones.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_17 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 17u32, "2026-11-04T04:45Z", "x", "2026-11-04 00:45 x: sim_serve slice DRAFTED (kit drafts x\\X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES\\sim_serve.spi, base 1C8907F6C334 -> 6460F622CA1A, 970 lines, widest 120, cells under 272, respell-drift clean): (1) id tie-break (newer_or_same); (2) by_handle keeps the CURRENT holder: keeps_handle inserts only when the incoming account's newest post is at least as new as the holder's (equal = later wins, so today's last-wins behaviour holds for every existing fixture); (3) user_posts drops posts flagged lost == true (X never serves them). A cloud --check couldn't be used: the x-check stage rejects even a lib-only check with no error lines (pack32 dll; same core as fiscal's working stage). Not chased further; the node sim build type-checks it. RIDES x-next5 with world.spi + cells_executor.spi. PRE-REGISTERED (x-next5): sim build exit 0, client build exit 0, sim accept exit 0 with every existing fixture byte-identical (e2e worlds have no lost flags, no same-second pairs and no shared handles, so any fixture diff = FAIL and investigate); the postable census parity carried from x-next4 stays SAME. Land = land-x-next5 (3 hash guards). Then x-replay3 on the joined world.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_18 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 18u32, "2026-11-04T05:19Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:19 x: x-next5 (job-1793769260232, core A63F4C2A, 100 s) FAILED at the sim build: 12 ETY-ValueRestriction errors, all in my draft's id_at_least ('Metavars that are not part of the enclosing function's signature are not allowed'). sm'::length is generic in its numeric result, and comparing two lengths only with each other never fixed that type. (cells_executor gets away with the same call because it subtracts 4i32; sim_serve L714 annotates with ': i32'.) No fixture changed; nothing landed. FIX: := longer = sm'::length a - sm'::length b, then ? longer = 0i32 ?> not_before a b |: longer > 0i32. Same order (equal length = text order, longer id = newer), and the type is pinned by 0i32. Draft 6460F622CA1A -> DB8E9B3AFECA. Relaunched as x-next5b with the same pre-registered rule. LESSON: a generic numeric result compared only against itself needs a typed anchor (a literal or ': i32').")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_19 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 19u32, "2026-11-04T05:48Z", "x", "2026-11-04 01:48 x: x-replay3 PREPARED (scratchpad launch-x-replay3.ps1; launches after x-next5b, which carries the sim tie-break + handle + lost slice, has landed). EXPECTED computed on idle europa with jq (europa:/data/x/joined/expected.json, 1 KB): per HANDLE the holder = the account with the newest post (later in the list on a tie), and top 5 = non-lost posts sorted by created_at, then id length, then id text (19-digit ids would lose precision as jq numbers). Aggregates: fc1943s held by 1637273636049498112 of 2 accounts (1,736 posts, 79 lost, 1,112 reposts); geygey_hadid 125,528 / 3,417 / 35,493; juhondacivic 31,596 / 1,259 / 4,771; stewshka 1,732 / 87 / 1,129. The node never parses the 119 MB world in PowerShell; only the sim loads it, with SPIRAL_CELLS_MAX_MB=6144 and a 900 s boot bound. PRE-REGISTERED RULE: sim build 0, client build 0, the joined world served, and for each of the 4 handles at limits 20 and 200: xc exit 0 and its ids EQUAL the expected top min(limit,5) (lost excluded; reposts compared as the plain posts they are in this data). Any DIFFERENT = a finding to diagnose (sim vs expected), not a pass.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_20 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 20u32, "2026-11-04T06:55Z", "x", "2026-11-04 02:55 x: PERF RISK for x-replay3 (read from the code, not measured yet): sim_serve's newest_first is an INSERTION sort (fold + inserted), O(n^2), and timeline_body runs it over a user's WHOLE post list on EVERY request. geygey has 125,528 posts, so ~7.9e9 comparisons per request; x-replay2's single-account world survived it, at an unmeasured cost. If x-replay3's geygey requests time out, that's the cause, not xc. Fix: sort each user's feed once at world load (by_id holds users), or a merge sort. Lib has only array sorts (am'.sort_by), no list sort. Measure first: x-replay3 already times each handle.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_21 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 21u32, "2026-11-04T07:01Z", "x", "2026-11-04 03:01 x: EDGE SCAN of the joined world (jq on europa; aggregate counts only; the first try used jq array subtraction, O(n*m), and ran 25+ min; with an object index it took seconds): 163,307 posts; 0 malformed created_at, 0 empty text on non-lost posts, 0 posts after the world's now. 44,245 of 45,977 distinct ref targets (96%) are OUTSIDE the world (replies and quotes of non-tracked accounts): expected, since sim_serve's posts_of drops unknown ids when expanding refs. ONE duplicate post id: the same post reposted by BOTH @fc1943s accounts. In this archive a repost carries the ORIGINAL's id, while real X gives each repost its own id. The sim's by_post dict keeps one copy per id (the last with_author wins), so one account's repost vanishes from its timeline. Impact today: 1 post. As more accounts join, overlapping reposts grow. Options: the emitter keys reposts by (account, original id), e.g. the repost's own status id where the archive has it; or the sim keys by_post per author. Raised with x-dumps.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_22 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 22u32, "2026-11-04T07:06Z", "x", "2026-11-04 03:06 x: SHARED-REPOST FIX drafted (x-dumps verified the archive has NO own status id for a repost, only the original's, and we agreed not to fabricate ids). The sim keeps by_post keyed by the ORIGINAL id (X's lookup semantics: /2/tweets/:id, ref expansion, conversation parents, writes) but TIMELINES are now per author: user_posts takes each post from the user's own list, with_author u, kept while standing_in w (its id still in by_post, so deletes still hide it, and not lost). New test a_repost_shared_by_two_accounts_stays_on_both: a two-user world decoded from JSON gives author_ids [1] and [2]. In x-next8's sim_serve draft (kit drafts x\\LIB-ERLANG-RAW-TCP\\sim_serve.spi E439C08F64E4); europa --check exit 0 in the stacked context. sim_serve is 975 lines: the next sim slice must split it (1k gate).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_23 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 23u32, "2026-11-04T07:14Z", "x", "2026-11-04 03:14 x: LIB LIST SORT drafted for the quadratic newest_first (kit drafts spiral\\LIB-LIST-SORT\\list_sort.spi EC96EE545E32, registered after doc_render in lib package.spiproj, stacked on the doc move's spiproj draft). sorted_by first l is a stable merge sort: recursion depth log2 n, merges tail-recursive with an accumulator (safe for 125k-element lists on Rust too), left wins ties. Tests sorts_and_keeps_equal_keys_in_order, empty_and_single_lists_are_already_sorted (rust + gleam). Max cell 264; europa --check exit 0. lib had only array sorts (am'.sort_by). USE: sim newest_first = list_sort::sorted_by newer_post (strict), where newer_post a b = not (newer_or_same b a); and the timeline route sorts each feed ONCE at boot. Applied to the sim only after x-replay3 measures the current per-request cost (measure first).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_24 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 24u32, "2026-11-04T07:21Z", "x", "2026-11-04 03:21 x: 1K-GATE SPLIT PLAN for sim_serve (975 lines in x-next8's draft; not needed until the next sim_serve change, so not done now; doing it now would reopen x-next6's frozen drafts). The file has no section headings and its clusters interleave (auth, writes, search and users spread across 98-969). Clean cut: a new module sim_wire listed BEFORE sim_serve takes (a) the term helpers L9-29 (term alias, call, str, int, text, atom, second, truth, tag_of, pair, byte_count, plus terms_of from ~L138) and (b) the pure OAuth 1.0a signing L189-225 (pct, unescaped, hmac_sha1, pair_text, encoded_pair, signed_params, signing, oauth1_signature and the RFC example self-test), about 57 lines, giving ~918. Verification and caller classification (L227-285) stay, since they need request and api_world. sim_serve opens sim_wire, so its call sites don't change. COST: sim_publish, timeline_serve and sim_progress call sim_serve::text, so those become sim_wire::text in the same slice.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_25 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 25u32, "2026-11-04T08:24Z", "x", "2026-11-04 04:24 x: x-next5b (job-1793770169332, pinned core A63F4C2A, 2 h 40 min) FAILED its pre-registered rule, exit 8; NOTHING landed. Every stage passed except one: sim accept exit 0 with NO fixture changed (the tie-break + handle + lost slice and the date/line-width/BEAM-guard changes alter no existing output); sim build 0; client build 0; e2e RUST-OK (37 + 5, 138 sim requests); all Rust units under 1k (xc 992, digest 995); postable census SAME over 77 files. The notebook gate: feed_merge 6/6 passed (cursor offsets_of/resumed proven), but timeline_serve 3/4 FAILED (a_cursor_prints_feeds_then_offsets, a_printed_cursor_parses_back, a_bad_offset_reads_as_zero; an_empty_query_is_the_first_page passed). The print-only test fails without any query parsing, so the fault is in dotted or the Gleam rendering of the cursor text; a verbose node rerun of that file is under way. The installed core moved A63F -> D1379FEDC807 during the run. Plan: x-next5c = the same 9 files with timeline_serve fixed (the others are proven by this run's stages).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_26 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 26u32, "2026-11-04T08:26Z", "x", "2026-11-04 04:26 x: x-next5b's timeline_serve failures DON'T reproduce in isolation. The same file (6E893B50869E) with the same pinned core (A63F) in the same node mirror, run alone through run_notebook (no --gate): 4/4 PASS. In x-next5b it ran under --gate in ONE invocation together with client feed_merge.spi (a different package) and failed 3/4. So the suspect is the gate run or cross-file interference in one notebook invocation (a Cells harness concern, x's own territory), not the cursor code. Isolating now: --gate alone vs --gate with both files, on the node. If the batch alone reproduces it, the x-next5c gate runs one invocation per file and a Cells harness item gets filed with the repro.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_27 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 27u32, "2026-11-04T09:42Z", "x", "2026-11-04 05:42 x: lib list_sort LANDED (EC96EE545E32, with the doc move): the merge sort for the sim's quadratic newest_first and the timeline route's boot-time feed sort is now available in lib. Applied to the sim after x-replay3 measures the current cost.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_28 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 28u32, "2026-11-04T10:03Z", "x", "2026-11-04 06:02 x: x-dumps re-emitted the joined world (lost is now content-based: a post with text or media is never lost; 4,860 lost; recovered stewshka tweets no longer hidden). The replay expectations follow: launch-x-replay3 now RECOMPUTES europa expected.json from the live world right before copying it (if the jq fails, nothing launches), so it always compares against the world it serves. Fresh numbers: fc1943s 1,736 posts / 74 lost (was 79), stewshka 1,732 / 61 (was 87), geygey 125,528 / 3,417 and juhondacivic 31,596 / 1,259 unchanged. Rule unchanged.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_29 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 29u32, "2026-11-04T11:34Z", "x", "2026-11-04 07:34 x: x-next5c (job-1793782508291, pinned core 0758BAF4, 2 h 32 min) PASSED its pre-registered rule and LANDED (9 hash-guarded drafts + 42 regenerated outputs, 0 conflicts): sim accept 0 with NO fixture changed, sim build 0, client build 0, e2e RUST-OK 37 + 5, all Rust units under 1k, census SAME over 77, notebook gate one fresh process per file: feed_merge 6/6, timeline_serve 4/4 (x-next5b's 3/4 was the stale warm server). LANDED for this item: sim_serve.spi DB8E9B3AFECA: id tie-break (newer_or_same), by_handle keeps the current holder, user_posts drops lost posts.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_30 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 30u32, "2026-11-05T05:26Z", "x-dumps", "BATTLE-TEST (live 8787 serve on real joined world) 2026-11-05 — pagination integrity: walked 20 pages following the cursor chain, 996 posts, 0 ordering violations. Cross-page AND within-page timestamps are monotonically newest-first across the 5-account merge (each page first-date <= prev page last-date); dates descend smoothly 2026-10-18T19:51 -> 2026-07-07T02:52; no repeated cursors (no loop); next-link advances cleanly. Pages are 50 posts except a few at 48/49 = the lost-filter (visible/surviving) correctly dropping lost==true posts mid-page on real data. The merge-sort across 5 feeds holds global order across page boundaries - the class of bug synthetic fixtures miss. UI pagination core VALIDATED on real data.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_31 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 31u32, "2026-11-05T05:29Z", "x-dumps", "Battle-test continued 2026-11-05 (live 8787 serve, real joined world): (1) all 5 feeds present+correct in the cursor (72784485, juhondacivic 1450.., geygey 1096.., stewshka 1819.., fc1943s-later 1637..); page1 = 50 fc1943s (real: world now=2026-10-18 = fc1943s last activity; accounts cluster temporally as juhondacivic/geygey suspended earlier); offset 20000 = geygey (125K-post feed), renders clean. (2) MERGE CORRECTNESS: the 20-page walk had 0 date-ordering violations, which PROVES the 5-feed merge interleaves by date globally - a broken merge (drain one feed then the next) would show a date jump-up at a feed transition; none seen. (3) ROBUSTNESS: out-of-range offsets (all feeds c=999999) -> HTTP 200, 0 posts (graceful past-end, no crash); malformed/mismatched-feed cursors -> reset to page1 (resumed_cursor same-accounts guard), HTTP 200; serve stayed UP through every edge test. SIM/UI side battle-tested SOLID on real data. Remaining for this item: xc CLIENT battle-test (x-driven).")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_32 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 32u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "x", "2026-11-06 04:1x x: groom: Active -> Paused (untouched 27 h, heavy). x-dumps battle-test notes (11-05 01:2x: 20 pages, 996 posts, 0 ordering violations, all 5 feeds present) stand as the last evidence. Its deps SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM and X-CLIENT are still open. Resume: X-CLIENT unblocks, or x-dumps asks for a replay on the real 114 MB world, which also closes X-SIM-WORLD-CACHE DoD4.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_33 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 33u32, "2026-11-07T18:10Z", "x", "2026-11-07 14:10 x: RE-DECIDED (11-07 run): stays Paused at 45. Reason: dep X-CLIENT is still Blocked on the user push. x-dumps keeps battle-testing the real world on :8787 (11-07: 3 crash shapes found and fixed with null/missing/string-media fixtures, 40-page deep walk clean). Resume when X-CLIENT unblocks.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_34 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 34u32, "2026-11-07T20:04Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 16:0x x: re-decided, stays Paused. Reason: dep X-CLIENT is Blocked (98%), and this is load=heavy while node/box sit at 100% CPU with other lanes' jobs. Resume (set Planned) when X-CLIENT is Done and the x verify (pack61, crates per test) is green.")
inl note_of_x_sim_real_profiles_35 () = Note ("X-SIM-REAL-PROFILES", 35u32, "2026-11-09T18:30Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:5x microblog: Paused -> Planned; the structured wait item:X-CLIENT holds it (load=heavy, so it also needs a quiet node slot). dod: the sim and xc client run over the real downloaded profiles with every edge found healed or filed. next: after X-CLIENT is Done and a cold client verify is green (r3, queued on moon after the sim verify).")
