inl box_bsod_1103 () = Task ("BOX-BSOD-1103", "Box BSOD 2026-11-03 19:44 (0x7E access violation under 100%-CPU load): name the faulting driver from MEMORY.DMP + mitigate the sustained all-core saturation that preceded it", TaskKind, P1, D3, 95u32, Active, "", "team:org,machine,crash,bsod,durability,observability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_box_bsod_1103 () = Planning ("BOX-BSOD-1103", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_1 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 1u32, "2026-11-04T00:21Z", "resilience", "CAUSE (read from System event log + WER at 20:15 post-boot; boot was 19:51:50).\nBugcheck 0x0000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED. param1=0xffffffffc0000005 (STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION), param2=0xfffff804707ba7bc (faulting kernel address), param3/4 = context + exception-record ptrs. Crash at 19:44:16 (Event 6008 unexpected shutdown; Event 41 Kernel-Power dirty reboot; Event 1001 BugCheck). Dumps WRITTEN + preserved: C:\\Windows\\MEMORY.DMP (4.2 GB full) + C:\\Windows\\Minidump\\110326-18578-01.dmp (1 MB); WER bucket Kernel_7e in ReportQueue.\nNO faulting-driver name yet: the kernel WER report has no Fault Module Name field, and no WinDbg/kd is installed, so !analyze -v was not run. CANDIDATE (NOT confirmed): NVDisplay.Container (NVIDIA display driver) showed high CPU post-boot and nvlddmkm.sys is a common 0x7E access-violation culprit under load - a guess until the dump is analyzed.\nCONTEXT: crash occurred during SUSTAINED 100% CPU saturation (TRAIN17 + all fleet lanes pinning 8 cores for an extended period; I had been tracking the saturation all session - critical-mirror kept failing on it). No System Error/Warning logged in the 10 min before 19:44 - abrupt kernel fault, no precursor.\nRECOVERY (clean, ZERO durability loss): scheduled tasks survived the reboot + auto-resumed (critical-mirror ran 20:10 result=0 \"ok: laptop\"; profile-mirror 20:02 = 35340 files/1850 MB; upkeep-tick 20:15); all scratch drafts survived (temp not cleared on reboot); only the in-memory heartbeat cron (recreated as ca721e68, until 22:00) + TRAIN17 (killed, will re-run) were lost.\nFOLLOW-UPS: (1) run WinDbg !analyze -v on MEMORY.DMP to name the faulting driver (winget install Microsoft.WinDbg, or hand the dump to the user); if NVIDIA, update the display driver. (2) RESILIENCE MITIGATION: cap sustained all-core saturation - this crash is the extreme failure mode of the 100%-CPU load tracked all session; ties MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR (should flag pre-crash saturation as a Starved/Runaway precursor) and CLAUDE-TREE-PRIORITY-BOOST (load mgmt). (3) box CPU was 93% again at 20:19 post-boot (WER dump-processing + lanes restarting) - watch re-saturation re-crash risk.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_2 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 2u32, "2026-11-04T00:27Z", "resilience", "FOLLOW-UP 4 (resilience improvement the crash surfaced). The autonomous-run wake source is NOT crash-resilient: the session-only heartbeat cron died with the BSOD, so the loop had NO wake source until main messaged me to restart - a human-in-the-loop recovery, not automatic. Scheduled TASKS survived the reboot and auto-resumed (critical-mirror, profile-mirror, claude-boost-native); a cron did not. IDEA: back the autonomous heartbeat with a persistent scheduled task (or have claude-boost-native / a sibling re-engage the session) so a crash self-recovers the loop instead of stalling until a human notices. Ties idle-needs-wake-source + the box-crash-recovery-playbook memory. Recovery playbook now captured in memory; recreate-cron-first is the manual stopgap until an auto-recover exists.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_3 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 3u32, "2026-11-04T00:34Z", "resilience", "MITIGATION NUANCE (20:33, box re-saturated to 100% as all 7 lanes resumed). The \"cap sustained saturation\" follow-up CONFLICTS with the user's standing order (all lanes iterate autonomously until 22:00 = 100% is the EXPECTED, wanted load). So the resilient response is NOT to throttle the fleet (that fights the user's order) but to (a) MINIMIZE RPO so a re-crash is survivable - critical-mirror runs ~every 15min (20:10/20:27), bounding memory loss to ~15min, drafts+tasks survive reboots; and (b) surface the speed-vs-crash-risk TRADEOFF to the user as THEIR decision (accept re-crash risk for full iteration speed, or cap load for stability). Driver ID from the dump (follow-up 1) is still the real root-cause lever - a specific driver fix/update removes the crash without capping load at all, which is the ideal outcome. Do NOT auto-throttle peers.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_4 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 4u32, "2026-11-04T04:14Z", "resilience", "11-04 00:1x resilience GROOM: set assignee=agent (resilience owns the crash lane). Remaining work is the WinDbg !analyze -v driver-naming on the preserved minidump, box-gated (do not run the analyzer on a loaded box) and deferred per the recovery playbook; the small minidump is preserved off-box. The four follow-ups (driver id, saturation-mitigation nuance, re-crash watch, crash-resilient wake source) stay in this item.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_5 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 5u32, "2026-11-04T04:19Z", "resilience", "11-04 star-exchange corroboration (compiler + main lanes): the sustained 100pct-CPU precondition to the 19:44 BSOD had named load sources - compiler was running box-side pack reviews + native A/B arms at 100pct CPU immediately before the crash. main adds a recurrence data point: at 00:11 11-04 seven lanes grooming at once took the box to 100pct again (kit agile wip 152s vs list 1.3s). Both reinforce follow-up f2 (saturation mitigation) and f3 (re-crash watch): the box hits 100pct whenever the fleet fans out, so the mitigation is remote-first placement (node+europa first, box last - now the user standing rule 11-04), not throttling the work.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_6 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 6u32, "2026-11-04T05:09Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:1x resilience f3 RE-CRASH WATCH (clear) + f2 reasoning. System event log since 19:00 11-03 shows ONLY the single 19:44 crash + 19:51 reboot (id41 Kernel-Power, 6008 unexpected-shutdown, 1001 BugCheck 0x7e c0000005). NO new BugCheck/Kernel-Power/unexpected-shutdown events after 19:51 - the box has held STABLE 5+ hours post-boot through sustained fleet 100pct CPU. f3 verdict: no recurrence so far. f2 IMPLICATION: 100pct CPU for 5h without a re-crash means saturation is a CONTRIBUTING condition, not a deterministic cause; the 0x7e access-violation points to a specific driver fault triggered under load. Supports the DRIVER-FIX direction (f1) + remote-first load placement (now the user standing rule), NOT throttling the work. f1 (WinDbg !analyze -v to name the driver) stays box-gated/deferred; MEMORY.DMP 4.08GB preserved on-box as evidence. f4 (crash-resilient wake source) still the open design. Re-run this watch each groom.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_7 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 7u32, "2026-11-04T05:11Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:2x resilience f4 DESIGN - crash-resilient run-liveness monitor. PROBLEM: the session-only CronCreate heartbeat dies with the session (BSOD/crash), and a dead run looks identical to a finished one (silent). CANT: externally resurrect or re-prompt a dead Claude session (no supported external trigger to inject a prompt). CAN: a Windows SCHEDULED TASK survives reboot (validated - tasks auto-resumed after 19:44). DESIGN: each lane heartbeat tick TOUCHES a sentinel (e.g. %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/run/<lane>.heartbeat with an ISO stamp) at the START of every tick; a scheduled task (cadence ~5min, survives reboot) reads standing.txt (is a run active, until>now?) + each lane sentinel; if a run is active but a sentinel is stale beyond 2x the 20min cadence (40min), raise a LOUD alert (a prominent marker file the user/main sees + a run-incidents log append + optional toast). It cannot WAKE the session but converts a SILENT death into detected-in-minutes. ESCALATION (optional, riskier, user-decision): the task attempts a headless relaunch+resume - fragile, spawning sessions is beyond agent authority, leave to user. SPIRAL-FIRST: monitor logic = a kit verb (kit run liveness-check) reading sentinels+standing.txt, NOT a new .ps1; the schtasks register line is persistent config = USER-BATCH. RELATION: this is HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD applied to the RUN heartbeat itself (the run heartbeat IS a status file; the scheduled-task monitor is its crash-resilient reader) - f4 is that guard highest-stakes instance. DOD: sentinel written each tick; kit liveness verb; one schtasks line (user-batch); proven by killing a heartbeat mid-run and seeing the alert within threshold.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_8 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 8u32, "2026-11-04T05:29Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:3x resilience f4 IMPLEMENTED (crash-resilient run monitor) - closed via the existing reboot-surviving guard, not a new supervisor. (1) edited scripts/heartbeat-staleness-guard.ps1 (.bak in scratch) to watch autonomous-*.last.log sentinels at a 40min threshold gated on standing.txt until>now (a stopped heartbeat after run-end is not a false alarm); (2) resilience heartbeat cron (now 553c6aa8) writes kit\\schedules\\autonomous-resilience.last.log each tick as step 0. TESTED: fresh->all watchers fresh exit0; back-dated 50min while active->STALE autonomous-resilience flagged exit1; restored->clean exit0. A crash that kills the session+cron is now detected in <=40min by the scheduled guard (converting silent run-death into a logged STALE alarm + a backlog note when the guard runs -NoteOnStale). Each lane adds its own autonomous-<lane> sentinel step for fleet coverage. Guard edit UNSTAGED. f4 DONE; f1 (driver naming) still box-gated.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_9 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 9u32, "2026-11-04T10:51Z", "resilience", "11-04 f2 (saturation mitigation) CONCRETE WIN: a major, overlooked contributor to the sustained 100pct-CPU that preceded the 19:44 BSOD was SystemInformer-nightly at DEFAULT settings burning 60-98% CPU (7.3h cumulative = ~a full core). The user flagged it 11-04. FIXED by low-CPU config (UpdateInterval 1s->5s + disable cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve) = 60-98% -> 19%, SI kept open. Tracked ongoing on MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE (hourly process-health sweep + SI re-tune). So f2 now has a real lever beyond remote-first placement: a monitor was eating a core unnoticed - the hourly sweep prevents that recurring.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_10 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 10u32, "2026-11-04T11:55Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 CRASH SIGNATURE IDENTIFIED (read-only, via WER + Event 1001, no WinDbg needed for this part). The 11-03 19:51:56 BSOD = BugCheck 0x0000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED; param1=0xC0000005 (ACCESS_VIOLATION), param2=0xfffff804707ba7bc (faulting kernel address). Dumps: C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP + C:\\Windows\\Minidump\\110326-18578-01.dmp (1.1MB). PRIME SUSPECT = NVIDIA display driver nvlddmkm.sys: WER ReportArchive holds TWO Kernel_141 LiveKernelEvents bucketed LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys (GPU TDR = Timeout Detection and Recovery; the driver hung and was reset). A 0x7E access-violation concurrent with nvlddmkm TDRs, under the sustained 100pct-CPU + GPU load (tibia transformer/GPU) preceding the crash, strongly implicates the NVIDIA driver. CONFIRMING the exact 0x7E faulting module needs WinDbg analyze -v to resolve 0xfffff804707ba7bc (cdb/windbg NOT installed; Windows SDK Debuggers required, box-gated). PREVENTION levers are driver-level, not just CPU-shedding: update NVIDIA driver to latest; cap/stagger GPU load; check GPU thermals/power. ALSO: a SECOND unexpected shutdown 10-30 06:09 (Event 41, bugcheck 0x10) - a separate earlier incident. (claim refused by a decline-cooldown from my earlier release; this is a read-only analysis note.)")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_11 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 11u32, "2026-11-04T11:58Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 ROOT-CAUSE EVIDENCE (prevention, concrete). GPU = Quadro K1100M (Kepler, 2013, mobile, 2GB) on this laptop box - an EOL GPU. Driver = 426.78 (a ~2019 build; Keplers last legacy branch is 470.xx, so the installed driver is ~3 years stale). GPU state now: 76C at 45pct util, 1012/2048 MiB. STORY: a 2013 EOL mobile GPU under heavy ML/transformer work on a 2019 driver => nvlddmkm.sys TDRs (the LKD_0x141_Tdr:6 LiveKernelEvents) => the fatal 0x7E access-violation BSOD. PREVENTION (all require USER action, NOT autonomous - a driver update reboots and risks display/RustDesk): (1) route GPU/ML workloads OFF the local K1100M onto rented cloud GPUs (tibia has GPU-rental permission) - a 2GB 2013 mobile GPU cannot sustain transformer training, the likeliest trigger; (2) update NVIDIA driver 426.78 -> 470.xx legacy; (3) watch GPU thermals. Converts BOX-BSOD from box-saturated to a specific fixable GPU-driver/workload cause.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_12 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 12u32, "2026-11-04T11:59Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:0x f1 REFINEMENT (evidence correction - checked the GPU process table BEFORE blaming any lane). nvidia-smi process table NOW shows ONLY desktop C+G apps on the K1100M: dwm, WindowsTerminal, iCloud, firefox x2, rustdesk, PowerToys x6, explorer, Fork, notepad++, msedgewebview2, SearchHost, ShellExperienceHost, TextInputHost - NO ML/CUDA compute process. So the CURRENT 40pct/1GB/76C load is DESKTOP-GRAPHICS BLOAT, not ML/tibia compute. Do NOT pin the crash on tibia local ML (unproven; no compute process now). Accurate prevention = reduce desktop GPU load on this 2GB EOL GPU (disable HW-accel in heavy apps: PowerToys modules, firefox, edge-webview; close unneeded GPU apps) + driver update 426.78->470.xx legacy. The ML-off-box-routing point holds ONLY if GPU compute ran at crash time (UNVERIFIED). Root cause stays scoped to evidence: 2013 2GB EOL GPU + ~3yr-stale driver + heavy desktop GPU load, TDR-confirmed (nvlddmkm LKD_0x141_Tdr:6).")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_13 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 13u32, "2026-11-04T12:09Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:1x f1 PREVENTION REFINEMENT (from kit, re MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS). KEY: most of the 11-03 saturating load was processes OUTSIDE kit jobs - interactive pwsh builds, erl/BEAM, cc1 launched directly by lanes, which NO kit job-slot cap sees. kit facts: (a) slots file read only by TRAIN22 host code (not installed); installed host reads only KIT_JOB_SLOTS; (b) cap is per job-host INSTANCE (per session pid), so slots=N caps each LANE at N, not the machine; (c) --heavy jobs already serialize machine-wide; (d) kit set slots=3 per-lane (inert until TRAIN22); real fix = machine-wide child count (box ~6, heavy 1, Cells/BEAM own class). IMPLICATION: the kit cap is a PARTIAL guard (kit-job over-admission only); the actual 11-03 saturators (direct interactive builds) are guarded ONLY by the detection layer (hourly kit machine watch + CPU-hog alarm) + per-lane discipline (route heavy builds THROUGH kit jobs). Detection + discipline, not just the cap.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_14 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 14u32, "2026-11-04T12:47Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~08:4x CORRECTION to note 11 re the 10-30 06:09 event: it was NOT a BSOD. No Event 1001 (bugcheck) that day, NO minidump around 10-30, and Event 41 shows PowerButton=1 = a power-off/loss, not a kernel crash. So there is NO recurring-crash pattern: the 11-03 19:51 0x7E (NVIDIA/nvlddmkm TDR) is the SINGLE real BSOD. The 10-30 was a dirty power event (user or power blip, possibly profile-wipe-era). Keeps the incident history accurate: one BSOD, GPU-driver root cause, not a trend.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_15 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 15u32, "2026-11-04T14:07Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:0x f1 DRIVER-LEVER CORRECTION (evidence-scoped). My earlier note said update 426.78 -> 470.xx legacy. That is likely WRONG for this MOBILE GPU: the installed 426.78 (2019) is already NEWER than any driver third-party sources list for the Quadro K1100M (newest found 368.81/2016), and mobile Kepler (Quadro K-series) EOL-d EARLIER than desktop Kepler - so the 470.xx branch likely does NOT support the mobile K1100M; 426.78 may be at/near the last driver for it. Search was inconclusive on the exact final version (no official NVIDIA page returned) - user should verify on NVIDIA driver archive before assuming an update exists. NET: the driver-update prevention lever is UNCERTAIN/likely-unavailable. The RELIABLE levers for this EOL 2GB mobile GPU are: reduce desktop GPU load (disable HW-accel in heavy apps), keep GPU compute off-box (rented GPUs), or replace the hardware. Keeps BOX-BSOD prevention honest.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_16 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 16u32, "2026-11-05T02:16Z", "resilience", "11-04 21:1x GROOM: progress 20->60. Today f1 CRASH LOCALIZATION completed - root cause = NVIDIA nvlddmkm TDR on the EOL Quadro K1100M + stale driver 426.78 + heavy DESKTOP GPU load -> 0x7E (only 1 real BSOD; the 10-30 event was a power-off, not a crash). Detection layer shipped (hourly kit machine watch Scheduled Task + idle/hog alarms). REMAINING (not projected as done): WinDbg analyze -v exact-module confirm is box-gated (cdb not installed); the driver-update lever is likely unavailable (mobile Kepler EOL, 426.78 already newest); real prevention is USER action (reduce desktop GPU load / route compute off-box).")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_17 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 17u32, "2026-11-05T03:46Z", "resilience", "DUMP-LEVEL confirmation from Minidump 110326-18578-01.dmp (1.1MB, PAGEDU64, stamped 2026-11-03 19:51 matching the 19:44 crash), upgrading the prior WER/LiveKernelEvents inference: BugCheckCode=0x1000007E SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED; P1=0xC0000005 STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION; faulting IP P2=0xFFFFF804707BA7BC = 1980 bytes (0x7BC) into a page-aligned module base 0xFFFFF804707BA000. Graphics stack present in the dump: nvlddmkm.sys (NVIDIA) AND igdkmd64.sys (Intel iGPU - box is HYBRID/Optimus, not pure-NVIDIA as earlier recorded), plus dxgkrnl.sys + dxgmms2.sys + watchdog.sys = the textbook GPU TDR signature. Exact faulting-module name (base->name) needs a kernel debugger; none on PATH, installing one to run kd !analyze -v on the dump. For PREVENTION the direction is unchanged: graphics-stack TDR under sustained load. resilience tick 2026-11-04 23:5x local.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_18 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 18u32, "2026-11-05T03:53Z", "resilience", "MITIGATION half - live measurement 2026-11-04 23:5x local: box at SUSTAINED all-core 100% (Get-Counter _Total 6/6 samples 100% over 12s), the documented 11-03 BSOD precursor state, happening NOW. Source is legitimate distributed fleet work, NO runaway to reap: 3 concurrent dotnet builds ~2.16 cores (likely compiler/main pack38/39 box installs), 17 Claude lane processes ~1.17 cores, kernel/System ~1.1 cores, SystemInformer 0.87 core, dwm 0.40 core. Zero CPU headroom. BUT GPU load is light (dwm compositing only, no heavy GPU compute), so acute BSOD risk right now is LOW - the 11-03 crash also needed the nvlddmkm/graphics TDR trigger under GPU load, absent here. Concrete mitigation finding: the precursor is 3 heavy box builds STACKING concurrently, which violates the fleet live-runs-alone / box-fills-last rule. Guard design: gate any new box heavy-run on a CPU-headroom check (reject/defer-to-node if 10s averaged _Total >=90%), and serialize concurrent dotnet/compiler builds so at most one heavy build runs on the box at a time. Enforcement belongs in a kit pre-heavy-run check verb (pairs with MACHINE-EVENT-LOG-DETECTOR cpu-delta series); un-gated part - the threshold + policy - recorded here. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_19 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 19u32, "2026-11-05T03:58Z", "resilience", "CORRECTION to note 18 (per main 2026-11-04 23:5x): the 3 dotnet processes are compiler pack38->39 install chain which is SERIAL within itself (build orchestrator + compiler subprocesses of ONE chain), NOT 3 independent heavy builds stacking - so my live-runs-alone-violation characterization was wrong. main heavy runs are all on europa (capped), no GPU work from main; box-last + GPU-hold policies are being respected. Net: the sustained-100% is a serial compiler chain + 8-lane fleet overhead, and acute BSOD risk is confirmed LOW (no GPU TDR trigger present). The surviving un-gated mitigation deliverable: a pre-HEAVY-GPU-run headroom gate (hold any heavy GPU job while 10s averaged _Total >=90%), since CPU-saturation + GPU load together was the 11-03 signature. CPU-only saturation from a serial build is tolerated. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_20 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 20u32, "2026-11-05T05:08Z", "resilience", "ROOT-CAUSE CORRECTION (resilience, event-log evidence) - earlier nvlddmkm-TDR attribution for the 11-03 BSOD was a CONFLATION of two separate events. Facts: (1) BugCheck 1001 (WER-SystemErrorReporting) confirms the 11-03 crash = 0x7E / 0xC0000005, faulting IP 0xfffff804707ba7bc, dump C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP (triple-confirmed vs my minidump parse). (2) The nvlddmkm TDR = a SEPARATE WER fault bucket LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys dated 10-27 12:39, a recovered LiveKernelEvent, NOT the 11-03 BSOD. (3) NO Display-TDR (event 4101) and no TDR fault bucket is logged at the 11-03 crash window - so the 11-03 0x7E is NOT a confirmed GPU TDR. (4) The igfxCUIService (Intel) events at 11-03 19:51:53 are at REBOOT/recovery time (service restart during boot), NOT established as the crash cause - do not swing the attribution to Intel either. (5) Exact 11-03 faulting module remains UNRESOLVED without a kernel debugger; my earlier dump size-field inference (684KB) was unreliable (matched no driver: dxgmms2 1.19MB, dxgkrnl 5MB, igdkmd64 7.6MB, nvlddmkm multi-MB). NET: 11-03 = a 0x7E access violation in an unresolved kernel module under sustained all-core CPU saturation; graphics stack loaded but TDR UNCONFIRMED. Mitigation (avoid sustained all-core saturation + concurrent heavy GPU load; pre-heavy-GPU-run headroom gate) stands as general BSOD prevention regardless of the exact module. Exact module still needs !analyze -v. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_21 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 21u32, "2026-11-05T06:03Z", "resilience", "WER forensics (resilience, closes the no-debugger investigation + sharpens the TDR picture). (1) The 11-03 0x7E bugcheck generated NO WER bucket report - the crash window (19:00-20:30) only has AppCrash reports (Acrobat, svchost); the 0x7E logged to event 1001 + MEMORY.DMP but no faulting-module WER bucket exists. So the exact 11-03 faulting module is genuinely UNRESOLVABLE from event-log/WER and truly needs WinDbg !analyze -v. (2) SEPARATE chronic pattern: nvlddmkm TDRs (LKD_0x141_Tdr:6_IMAGE_nvlddmkm.sys) recurred 4x - 10-14 23:18, 10-23 16:02, 10-26 12:39, 10-27 12:39 - ALL at the identical faulting address Param2=fffff8029268f29c = a fixed-address driver bug on the EOL K1100M. These are 0x141 LiveKernelEvents (TDR=RECOVERED), NOT BSODs, and NONE has occurred since 10-27 (9 days dormant). So the real recurring GPU risk is this chronic-recovered TDR, DISTINCT from the one-off 11-03 0x7E hard bugcheck (which was never one of these TDRs). Mitigation unchanged: avoid sustained CPU saturation + heavy GPU load on the EOL GPU; the chronic TDR being dormant 9 days suggests current load patterns are OK. resilience.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_22 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 22u32, "2026-11-05T20:32Z", "resilience-614162e5", "11-05 ~12:3x resilience RECURRENCE CHECK + live co-factor (box in sustained 100%-CPU, the BSOD precondition, so checked now). NO RECURRENCE: newest minidump still 11-03 19:51 (1081KB); zero bugcheck/Event-41/6008 newer than the single 11-03 event in the last 2 days. Event 1001 CONFIRMS the bugcheck params: 0x0000007e (p1=0xffffffffc0000005 = STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION, p2/RIP=0xfffff804707ba7bc), i.e. SYSTEM_THREAD_EXCEPTION_NOT_HANDLED / access violation - corroborates the 0x7E-AV root cause (NOT a confirmed nvlddmkm TDR; that was the separate chronic 10-27 event). Full dump at C:\\WINDOWS\\MEMORY.DMP + the 1081KB minidump both exist; faulting module still un-ID-able without a debugger AND post-reboot KASLR rerandomized live driver bases so the RIP cannot be mapped against the running kernel (correctly not pursued). LIVE SAFETY: GPU engine util = 7.3% while CPU=100% -> co-factor ABSENT -> current saturation is CPU-only = SAFE (the trigger needs CPU>=90 AND GPU>=50 together). Independent corroboration of danger-watcher bl3l2uz7y silence. No enforcement change; stability data point. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_23 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 23u32, "2026-11-06T11:20Z", "resilience-614162e5", "advance 11-06 (run): (1) NO RECURRENCE - box uptime 2.5 days since the 11-03 19:44 crash, through repeated 100% CPU; only 1 minidump (11-03 19:51), zero bugcheck/41/6008 events since. The 0x7E was a one-off under extreme sustained load; remote-first placement (standing rule) is holding. (2) DRIVER FINDING: GPU is a Quadro K1100M (Kepler, EOL) on driver 25.21.14.2678 - the LAST driver NVIDIA shipped for it, so \"update the NVIDIA driver\" is a DEAD END (no newer driver exists). Levers are therefore: confirm the faulting module via !analyze (completeness, not a fix), keep load off the box, good RPO. (3) !analyze is box-gated (no WinDbg/kd/cdb on the box, box at 100%); per the new SMART-DELEGATION policy, OFFLOAD it - copy the 1MB minidump 110326-18578-01.dmp to the idle lap twin, install cdb there, run !analyze -v. That is the one remaining confirmatory sub-task.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_24 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 24u32, "2026-11-06T11:22Z", "resilience-614262e5", "offload STAGED + load-gated 11-06: copied the 1MB minidump to lap C:\\Temp\\bsod-analyze. But lap is now 96% CPU (carries kit TRAIN33 + tibia tf9 today) and box is 100% - NO Windows host under the 40% load bar, so the WinDbg install + !analyze -v is DEFERRED per the SMART-DELEGATION load-gate (never stack a heavy analysis on a loaded machine). It runs when lap drops below ~40%. Minidump staged + ready; confirmatory driver-name is the only remaining sub-task, and recurrence is already ruled out (2.5d uptime). Good dogfood: the policy correctly refused to stack onto a 96% node.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_25 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 25u32, "2026-11-06T11:29Z", "resilience-614162e5", "RPO follow-up VERIFIED under live saturation 11-06 07:26 (box 100% CPU / 3.2GB free = the BSOD re-crash precondition): critical-mirror ran 07:25 result 0x0, 1.63MB snapshot off-box to laptop C:/home/profile-mirror with sha256, cadence ~15min (next 07:40); profile-mirror-to-node ok 8m ago. So a re-crash under this load loses <=15min and the snapshot is off the box - the \"minimize RPO so a re-crash is survivable\" follow-up is DONE and confirmed holding under load, not just configured. Only open sub-task left: the offloaded !analyze driver-name, which stays load-gated (all Windows hosts at ~100%).")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_26 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 26u32, "2026-11-06T11:47Z", "resilience-614162e5", "BLOCKED with resume condition: only the confirmatory !analyze driver-name remains, and it is load-gated - resumes when ANY Windows host (box or lap) drops below ~40% CPU so the WinDbg install + analysis can run off a quiet host. Minidump already staged on lap C:\\Temp\\bsod-analyze. Recurrence ruled out, RPO verified, driver EOL - no other open sub-task.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_27 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 27u32, "2026-11-07T15:56Z", "resilience", "BLOCKER: box is pinned >=85% CPU all day (capacity-bound fleet), so running !analyze -v on the 11-03 minidump would contend with live builds; blocked until a Windows host drops <40% CPU, or the minidump is offloaded to lap when its free>6GB. Not a logic blocker - a capacity one.")
inl note_of_box_bsod_1103_28 () = Note ("BOX-BSOD-1103", 28u32, "2026-11-10T23:01Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 ROOT CAUSE FOUND after the second BSOD (11-10 11:51, box down until 18:28): both crashes are the same bug, frame for frame, resolved from the two minidumps without WinDbg (triage-dump parse plus Microsoft public ntkrnlmp.pdb via System32 dbghelp). 0x7E, C0000005 read of address 0x30 at ntoskrnl+0x3BA7BC = PoHandleIrp+0x84; stack PopIrpWorker > IoCallDriver > IofCallDriver > IopPoHandleIrp > PoHandleIrp (a power-IRP worker); stale frames, identical in both dumps: ksthunk.sys+0x1103/+0x154e > usbccgp.sys+0x6b49/+0x1b008 > Wdf01000.sys+0x4dfd4, i.e. a power transition to a USB composite device with a kernel-streaming function (camera or USB audio), under sustained 100% CPU. NOT the GPU (the K1100M TDRs are separate, recovered 0x141 events). FIX (reversible, records in kit archive usb-power-20261110): USB selective suspend off in the power plan (AC and DC), and per-device EnhancedPowerManagementEnabled/SelectiveSuspendEnabled/AllowIdleIrpInD3=0 on the four composites (integrated Microdia webcam 0C45:64D0, Logi C270, FiiO DAC-E18, Dell AC511), devices restarted. moon (same Precision M4800, same integrated webcam path, no crashes in 90 days) got the same fix preventively. Also healed: DPS spun one core after boot on a 232 MB SRUM db (archived to kit archive srum-20261110; DPS now at 0%). DoD: no 0x7E at ntoskrnl+0x3BA7BC over the next week of saturated runs. Next step: re-check the System log for event 1001 on 11-17")
