inl lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr () = Task ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", "lib seq' and list' gain a C/Pascal/Lua representation (array_base) so concat and utf8_byte_list can get portable arms", Story, P3, D4, 75u32, Paused, "", "team:compiler,compiler,lib,portable,backends", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr () = Planning ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_1 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:15Z", "compiler", "11-04 00:1x compiler groom: from PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES (closed at its DoD). seq::seq' (seq_switch': Gleam list, F# seq, Python list, Rust Vec, TS array, Zig slice) and list' (utf8_byte_list) have no C, Pascal or Lua representation, so concat and utf8_byte_list cannot get arms there. Next step: pick the representation (array_base t, as concat_list already does on C/Pascal since 10-08), add the seq_switch' C/Pascal arm, then a portable sample. No caller needs it today: the 5 seq' lib callers (crypto 181, file_system 1521, parsing 198/200, process 576) are host-only modules; portable code uses concat_list.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_2 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 2u32, "2026-11-05T17:47Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:4x compiler: SLICE 1 LANDED (unstaged): base.defaultof gains C ('(T){0}', a C99 compound literal for scalars and structs) and Pascal ('Default(T)') arms; strings, options, pairs and unions still go through its generic run cases. This was one of the two C blockers under listm'.to_array' (the other is to_array' itself, the list' representation this item is about). New test defaultof_scalars (string, i32, i64, f64, bool, option) on rust, c, pascal; the existing defaultof test stays rust + gleam, since Gleam has no scalar arms (an i64/f64/bool case there breaks gleam build). Draft round-tripped canonical + fixpoint; lib copy gate 2 passed 0 failed; lib_land_window closed GREEN: 3 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (26 typed), 0 respell drift. Live base.spi 07D68C748FD0 -> E5584788886D, lib digest 922986812B99DB81, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-133511. Next slice: a C/Pascal arm for list' (array_base t, as concat_list already uses).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_3 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 3u32, "2026-11-05T17:51Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:5x compiler: PROBE after slice 1 (installed pack51 + landed defaultof arms): reflection::typed_name_snake on C now passes the defaultof gap and stops at the NEXT one: 'backend_switch has no arm for the C backend', reached from base.spi:655-659 (base.try) < resultm.spi:32 (from_try). OPEN QUESTION before adding more C arms: both C traces enter a function's LUA arm first (listm'.to_array' at :770, resultm.from_try at :32), which suggests the C backend falls back to Lua arms in some backend_switch dispatches when no C arm exists. Find that rule in the compiler's backend_switch lowering before writing C arms for try/from_try: a C arm would change what C programs that reach those functions get today. (Typed name styles on C are already covered without this: !!!!UnionCaseName(x, style) and lib case_name_snake/kebab/lower fold at compile time.)")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_4 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 4u32, "2026-11-05T17:53Z", "compiler", "11-05 13:5x compiler: CORRECTION to the previous note, answered from the source: there is NO Lua fallback for C. lib backend.spi's backend_switch gives each backend its own arm or a 'backend_switch_has_no_<B>_arm_in_lib_spiral' marker, and the compiler's !!!!BackendSwitch (spiral_compiler.fs ~11101) evaluates EVERY arm: the current backend's for its value, the others ONLY FOR THEIR TYPE (comment: needed so compile-time hashmaps keep inference sound). Inside a non-current arm (to_array''s Lua arm, from_try's Lua arm), nested backend_switches still resolve against the CURRENT backend (C), so defaultof/try's missing C arm surfaces as the fatal marker although C never runs Lua code. Consequence: a lib function needs a C arm if it is reachable from ANY arm of a switch a C program reaches, not only the C arm. Two fixes, both open: (a) lib, give the reached helpers C arms (done for defaultof; next base.try, where the Gleam-style 'fn () |> some' is the honest C meaning, since C has no exceptions to catch); (b) compiler, make type-only arm evaluation tolerate the missing-arm marker (it is type-correct: ': t'), so only the current backend's arm can trigger it. (b) would stop this class at the source; it needs a probe of where the marker reaches the output during type-only evaluation.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_5 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 5u32, "2026-11-05T17:55Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:0x compiler: SECOND CORRECTION, mechanism only partly determined, so the 13:5x note's fix (b) is WITHDRAWN. Read further (~11127): type-only evaluation of a non-current arm runs with backend set to THAT arm's backend (backend_strings.Add backend, fresh seq), so a switch nested inside a Lua arm resolves against Lua and can only produce a Lua marker, never a C one. The fatal fires only when a 'backend_switch_has_no_C_arm' marker is in the emitted C (RustGapTrace.missing_arm_error), so the gap is on C's real path. 'Reached from' is the lexicographically smallest frame list recorded for that marker (note_missing_arm at EMacro), and its frames name listm'.spi:766-770 / resultm.spi:32. Why C's real evaluation passes through those Lua-arm line ranges is NOT determined (closure creation sites in the trace are one candidate). Established and safe: lib C arms remove the marker where C reaches it (defaultof, done and proven); the next is base.try. Not established: any compiler change; do not act on the 13:5x (b).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_6 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 6u32, "2026-11-05T18:09Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:0x compiler: SLICE 2 (base.try C arm, 2-try-c.kitdo) NOT LANDED. The arm itself is the Zig form ('fn () |> some', since C has no exceptions to catch) and round-trips canonical, and the existing catch test stays green. But the new C test fails at gcc: try's signature mentions ex_fn : exn -> option t, and the exn TYPE is a per-backend switch with no C entry, so the C output names Nil / nil / exn / BaseException / Rc / backend_switch as types (main.c:38-50 'unknown type name'). A C try needs a C representation of exn first. That is a design decision (C cannot raise; candidates: an empty struct, or an int error code), not a mechanical arm, so it waits for a decision instead of a guess. The draft stays in drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\2-try-c.kitdo, unlanded. The live lib is unchanged by this slice.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_7 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 7u32, "2026-11-05T18:19Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:1x compiler: SLICE 2 still NOT LANDED, and the blocker is now located in the COMPILER, not lib. I added a C entry to exn_switch (C : 'const char *', the text form Zig uses; C never constructs an exn since failures abort). gcc output is unchanged: main.c still declares fields of type Nil / nil / exn / BaseException / Rc (and, in the first run, 'backend_switch' as a type name). The C backend does not resolve a TYPE-level backend_switch macro (':! exn = $''backend_switch (exn_switch)''') to one entry: it emits every entry of the record. So no lib type defined through backend_switch (exn, list', and likely seq') can appear in C output until codegenC resolves type-level backend_switch for its own key, as the other codegens do. That compiler fix is the real first step of this item; the defaultof value arms (slice 1, landed) were unaffected because they are value-level. Draft 2 stays unlanded in drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\2-try-c.kitdo (now with the exn C entry). Next: find where codegenC lowers type macros (CMText 'backend_switch' + CMType record) versus how codegenRust picks its key.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_8 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 8u32, "2026-11-05T18:21Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:2x compiler: ROOT GAP FOUND IN codegenC. Every other codegen has a case 'YMacro [Text \"backend_switch \"; Type (YRecord r)]' that picks its own key (Fsharp 13083, Rust 14094/14100, Pascal 15338, Zig 16168, Lean 16849, Gleam 18852, Lua 19972, Wasm 20994, TypeScript 25160, Cpp 27512 via field_try_find \"Cpp\", Python 28511). codegenC (module CodegenC, tyv ~26264) has only the generic 'YMacro a -> concat', so a type-level backend_switch renders as ALL record entries, which is the Nil/nil/exn/BaseException/Rc/backend_switch soup gcc rejected. Program 3 drafted (drafts\\spiral\\LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR\\3-codegenc-type-backend-switch.kitdo, 1 replacement, anchored on codegenC's own 'The C backend doesn''t support stack mutable' line): the same case for key \"C\" (Map.tryPick, since field_try_find lives in CodegenCpp), with the Cpp-style error when the record has no C entry. pack52 = pack51 + 3 building on the box. Then: the try slice (2) on pack52, and a C/Pascal parity review.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_9 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 9u32, "2026-11-05T18:35Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:3x compiler: PROGRAM 3 + SLICE 2 PROVEN TOGETHER. pack52 = pack51 + program 3 (codegenC type-level backend_switch); bin core A688EE5AD426A5A0, host A1BF06EB. lib copy with slice 2 (base.try C arm + exn_switch C : 'const char *') gated through Cells with SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL = pack52 (private daemon port 47933): try_keeps_values (c), try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values (lua, cuda, rust, typescript) and defaultof_scalars (rust, c, pascal) give 'tests: passed 3 failed 0', type errors 0. On installed pack51 the same C test failed at gcc (unknown type names Nil/nil/exn/BaseException/Rc from the unresolved type switch). Order to ship: pack52 install first (C/Pascal examples parity vs pack51 running now), then slice 2 through the lib land window (lib on node needs pack52 too, since the type switch is only resolved for C with the fix).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_10 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 10u32, "2026-11-05T18:59Z", "compiler", "11-05 14:5x compiler: pack52 INSTALLED on the BOX (14:49-14:58): SpiralCompilerCore.dll A688EE5AD426A5A0, SpiralCompiler.dll A1BF06EB9AD87F00, both equal=True; undo spiral-bin\\compiler-installs\\pack52-20261105-144931. Parity vs pack51 before install: C + Pascal examples 1034 rows (972 ok + 62 expected errors), 0 compile-status / 0 residual diffs. Handed to main for the node. NEXT: land slice 2 (base.try C arm + exn_switch C entry; canonical draft in the compiler scratchpad libdef\\out\\base.spi, proven 3/3 on pack52) through lib_land_window once the node has pack52.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_11 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 11u32, "2026-11-05T19:00Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:0x compiler, SCOPE NOTE: what remains after slice 2 lands is the list' C/Pascal representation itself (list'_switch C entry, probably array_base t as Rust uses Rc<Vec<t>>, plus C arms in about 11 listm' functions: empty', cons', box, rev', fold', fold_back', to_array', item, ...). Now feasible, since pack52's codegenC resolves type-level switches, but it's a few hours with reviews for a P3 with no caller (concat_list already reaches C through concat_right_fold over native lists; utf8_byte_list and seq' concat are the only beneficiaries). Parked at that boundary for this run; the next lane picks it up from here.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_12 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 12u32, "2026-11-05T19:25Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:2x compiler: SLICE 2 LANDED (unstaged), after pack52 reached the node (15:09). base.spi: exn_switch gains C : 'const char *' (the text form Zig uses; C never builds an exn, since failures abort), base.try gains the C arm 'fn () |> some' (the Zig form: nothing to catch), and a new C test try_keeps_values. Proven before the land on pack52: try_keeps_values (c) + try_catches_failures_and_keeps_values (lua, cuda, rust, typescript) + defaultof_scalars (rust, c, pascal) 3 passed 0 failed. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 3 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (27 typed), 0 respell drift. Live base.spi E5584788886D -> 952C96278E2E, lib digest 590C666A4EE3CFB7, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-151333. Item state: compiler gap closed (pack52 codegenC type switch), defaultof + try/exn on C; the list' C representation remains (see the 15:0x scope note).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_13 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 13u32, "2026-11-05T19:55Z", "compiler", "11-05 15:5x compiler: SLICE 4 LANDED (unstaged): resultm.from_try gains the C arm 'fn () |> ok' (the Zig form) and a C test from_try_keeps_values. Lib copy gate 2 passed 0 failed (from_try_keeps_values on c + the existing catch test), type errors 0. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 7 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (8 typed), 0 respell drift. Live resultm.spi 8014CE936593 -> D8844A7D1117, lib digest 8EBE1BE440A3DE7E, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-154458.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_14 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 14u32, "2026-11-05T20:31Z", "compiler", "11-05 16:2x compiler: SLICE 5 LANDED (unstaged): sm'.of_char gains the C arm '!!!!PrimToString(input) : string' (the first draft lacked ': string' -> ETY-ValueRestriction on every target, fixed), plus a C test of_char_is_the_one_character_string_on_c. Lib copy gate 3 passed 0 failed (c + gleam + rust char tests), type errors 0. lib_land_window closed GREEN: 30 consumer signatures unchanged, apps/spiral compiles, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (the to_char_array shadow note is pre-existing), 0 respell drift. Live sm'.spi B0854BB9B5D7 -> 0A15625F655D, lib digest 43B0A30D3337560E, undo spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261105-161414. END TO END on C: the runtime styled-names probe (reflection::typed_name_snake/kebab over typed_case_name, which goes through styled_name: to_char_list |> listm::fold |> rev |> concat_list) now compiles to C (31,681 bytes), builds with gcc 0 errors and exits 20 = len light_green 11 + len deep-blue 9. The REAL gap was of_char; SPIRAL_RUST_GAP_TRACE=1 named it (the fatal's 'Reached from' named base.try/from_try, whose C arms (slices 2 and 4) are genuine but were not this program's blocker). Landed today on this item: defaultof C/Pascal, try + exn C, from_try C, of_char C, plus compiler pack52 (codegenC type-level backend_switch). Remaining: the list' C/Pascal representation (scope note 15:0x).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_15 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 15u32, "2026-11-05T20:34Z", "compiler", "11-05 16:3x compiler: CONSUMER CHECK before going further. In today's C + Pascal examples run (pack51, 1034 rows: 62 compile errors) and the all-backend run (6825 rows), the ONLY missing-arm failures are samples/backend_switch_missing_arm_negative (the deliberate negative). The other C/Pascal errors are type errors (24), parse failures (5), the main-return-type checks (2) and expected-error samples. So no program in the corpus is blocked on a missing C/Pascal lib arm any more, and the list' representation work has no current consumer. Stopping here for this run, at a real stopping point rather than a time limit: the next slice is worth doing when a C/Pascal program actually reaches list' (utf8_byte_list or seq' concat).")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_16 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 16u32, "2026-11-06T08:18Z", "compiler", "11-06 04:1x compiler GROOM: parked on purpose -> Planned. 11-05 consumer check: no corpus program is blocked on a missing C/Pascal lib arm. Resume when one is.")
inl note_of_lib_seq_list_c_pascal_repr_17 () = Note ("LIB-SEQ-LIST-C-PASCAL-REPR", 17u32, "2026-11-06T14:55Z", "compiler", "11-06 10:5x compiler: Planned -> PAUSED (re-decided; it kept showing as ready). Reason: the 11-05 consumer check found no corpus program blocked on a missing C or Pascal arm in lib seq/list (C+Pascal examples 1034 rows, all-backend 6825 rows; the only missing-arm failures were backend_switch_missing_arm samples on purpose), and the remaining work is a list' representation for C/Pascal with no demand. Resume when: any program fails with a missing C or Pascal arm in lib seq.spi/list'.spi (pack53c's fatal now names the real site), or a lane asks for a C/Pascal build that reaches list'.")
