inl kit_agile_cas_retry () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", "agile state lock stale only when its holder is gone (root cause of the fleet CAS conflicts); retry dropped, batching split to KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", TaskKind, P0, D2, 90u32, Blocked, "", "team:rig,agile,fleet-papercut", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_cas_retry () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 1u32, "2026-11-04T04:20Z", "kit", "Star exchange 11-04: x-dumps (8 retries), fiscal (5 of 13 writes), main (3 of 36 sets), compiler, tibia: every lane hit \"state/agile.spi changed since it was read; nothing written\" while grooming at the same time, and an identical rerun succeeded every time. DoD: (1) note, set and edit re-read and re-apply up to 3 times on a CAS conflict, then exit with a distinct conflict code; (2) a batched `agile set+note` (one read, one write per groom item, tibia #11); (3) a notebook test with two writers racing on a scratch copy. Not a lock: the CAS is correct, so the retry stays inside the writer. Also on list --json: print the field shapes (tasks wrapper, tags/deps one comma-joined string, notes a count) in help (x #8, compiler #10).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 2u32, "2026-11-04T04:52Z", "kit", "11-04 00:5x ROOT CAUSE FOUND, so a retry would only hide it. kit_agile_mutation's state lock (state/agile.spi.lock) treated ANY lock older than 10 s as stale and stole it, while a write to the 13 MB store takes far longer under load (lanes report >120 s). So a second writer took the lock from a live one, both computed from the same read, and the later commit hit the CAS check (\"changed since it was read\"). FIX (unstaged; type-checked on europa pack31, as-is and test bodies exit 0; an injected u64/i32 mismatch was rejected, exit 5): the 9-line host block becomes typed Spiral. The lock dir now holds `holder` = \"<pid> <start ms>\". A lock is stale only when its holder pid is dead (kit_process_alive, linked) or it's over 10 min old (pid-reuse guard). A lock with no holder line (an older kit binary) keeps the old 10 s rule. The wait budget goes 30 s -> 5 min. Host code left: two one-line primitives (create_dir, remove_dir). Notebook test a_lock_is_stale_only_when_its_holder_is_gone_or_ten_minutes_old uses a stub liveness, so no sysinfo in the cell. Next: rides TRAIN19 (TRAIN18's snapshot was 00:43). Then the in-process retry plus a distinct exit code, and batched set+note, as belt and braces.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 3u32, "2026-11-04T05:15Z", "resilience", "11-04 01:2x resilience BATTLE-TEST DATA (from tonight orphan sweep + BEAM-GUARD grooming, during active 7-lane concurrent grooming at box 100pct CPU). Ran 69 edit-tags writes (53 bare-main->team:main canonicalizations + 16 multi-lane primary-owner assignments) on the 13MB agile.spi, each wrapped in a 4-try IMMEDIATE-retry loop (no sleep/backoff) on non-ok output: 69/69 succeeded, 0 exhausted retries. Plus ~8 note/set writes same window, 0 fail. FINDINGS for the implementation: (1) immediate retry (no sleep) is sufficient - the CAS window is short and a rerun of the identical call wins, matching main n9 (3/36 sets rejected, all passed on retry) + compiler/x/tibia reports. (2) built-in retry should cap at ~4 tries and return the LAST error if exhausted (not loop forever). (3) BATCHED set/edit is the bigger win: 69 sequential single writes each re-serialize the whole 13MB file (0.7-1.7s each, worse under load, occasional 120s spill); a batch verb applying N (id,field,value) edits under ONE mkdir-lock+CAS would cut 69 rewrites to 1. (4) kit agile wip scans files (~152s under load) - keep it off the hot path. Recommend the batch API carry a list applied in one transaction. I can be the battle-test harness when it lands (re-run the 69-edit canonicalization pass).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 4u32, "2026-11-04T05:33Z", "kit", "11-04 01:4x DECISION (kit): the in-process retry and distinct exit code are DROPPED from this item. With the lock fix (n2), the lock is held across the whole route (read, compute, commit) and is no longer stolen from a live holder, so a CAS conflict can come only from an older binary still using the 10 s rule. That ends when TRAIN19 installs, since every lane runs the same %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe. A retry would only mask a regression of the lock rule. The CAS check stays as the guard. The batched set+note moves to KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH: both runners read fixed argv positions (task_notes.spi note_request; set is the kit_agile_routes host route), so a combined call needs typed arguments first (the S6 shape). DONE WHEN: TRAIN19 installs and a groom by 2+ lanes at once shows zero \"changed since it was read\". parked: waiting on TRAIN19.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_5 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 5u32, "2026-11-04T05:54Z", "kit", "11-04 02:0x: the n2 lock fix put kit_agile_mutation at 1294 generated lines (s7 node precheck; gate 1000), because the lock and lib file helpers inlined into the mutation crate. It was pulled from TRAIN19 (parked copy in drafts\\kit\\OVER-GATE-parked-1104b) and REBUILT as its own linked crate kit_agile_lock (lock.spi: the holder-pid lock, stale rule and test). It exports kit_agile_lock_taken / kit_agile_lock_released (single string arg, links); kit_agile_mutation keeps only two linked calls. europa --check exit 0 for both, plus the lock test body. Rides TRAIN20 after a node size precheck.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_6 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 6u32, "2026-11-04T07:02Z", "kit", "11-04 03:0x: node size run: kit_agile_lock 390 lines, kit_agile_mutation 932 (was 1294 with the lock inlined); europa --check exit 0. Code complete in the tree; set Blocked on TRAIN20 (notebooks + install), then a 2-lane concurrent groom confirms zero \"changed since it was read\". Blocked so the stop gate stops re-offering an item with nothing left to do before the train.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_7 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 7u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: NEW REPRO from fiscal: kit agile set failed at 04:21 with promote stage: Access is denied. (os error 5) during the groom rush, after the note for the same item had landed. The promote rename races another writer or a reader holding the file. DoD addition: promote retries a sharing violation (os error 5 and 32) with a bounded backoff inside the verb, and the typed outcome says Retried n before ok or Refused after the bound.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_8 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 8u32, "2026-11-09T05:13Z", "org3", "org3 STARRED-UNBLOCK advice (kind=PREMISE-RECHECK + HANDOFF): code-complete, Blocked-on-TRAIN20. LIVE EVIDENCE the problem is STILL ACTIVE + frequent during THIS autonomous run (11-09 00-05Z): my kit agile note writes on the ~10.3MB agile.spi hit the >120s wait REPEATEDLY tonight (several backgrounded at the 120s tool timeout) under multi-lane concurrent grooming - so the INSTALLED kit still uses the old 10s-stale-lock rule; the kit_agile_lock holder-pid fix (n2) has NOT landed via TRAIN20. This is a live fleet papercut now, not latent - prioritizing TRAIN20's install stops every lane's groom-writes stalling up to 120s in concurrent runs (I lost ~several minutes to it tonight). HANDOFF: reaffirming resilience n3's offer - I (org3) can be the battle-test harness on landing: re-run a concurrent multi-write canonicalization pass, confirm zero 'changed since it was read', measure the write-latency drop. Ping me when TRAIN20 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_cas_retry_9 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY", 9u32, "2026-11-09T05:58Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:5x rig, NEW EVIDENCE for the dropped retry decision: KIT-SCALE-M1 bench on a state copy (europa): 10 concurrent agile note writers on one item, 6 exited 2 (state/agile.spi changed since it was read; nothing written, run the call again), 4 landed, none lost. Every caller then has to retry by hand, and agents mostly do not, so writes are refused under ordinary multi-lane load. A note is a commutative append; an in-verb bounded retry (re-read, re-apply, jittered backoff, give up after N with the same exit 2) is safe for note and for set/edit of distinct fields. Premise check: this item is Blocked on TRAIN20, long installed (the box runs TRAIN41), so the block is stale; re-decide with the retry back in scope.")
