inl note_of_kit_agile_set_note_batch_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", 1u32, "2026-11-04T05:33Z", "kit", "Split from KIT-AGILE-CAS-RETRY (11-04): a groom writes the 13 MB store twice per item (set, then note). Step 1: task_notes.spi note_request_from (words : list string), pure, with the runner passing argv as a list (the x shape). Step 2: the set route (kit_agile_routes host) as typed Spiral taking the same list. Step 3: `agile set <root> <id> <pct> <status> --note <text> --as <author>` applies both edits to one read and commits once, under one lock hold.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_set_note_batch_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-SET-NOTE-BATCH", 2u32, "2026-11-09T06:16Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 03:0x local rig, NEW EVIDENCE (KIT-SCALE-M1 benches on europa, 9.5 MB state copy): every agile write re-reads and re-parses the whole state (one write 2.4 s alone, 10-35 s under 10 writers), each write is an optimistic-concurrency window (10 concurrent writers: 6 of 10 refused; with 5 caller retries 2 still gave up), and 20 concurrent writers exceeded a 1.5 GB memory scope. A note followed by a set is two such writes; rig alone did that pairing about 15 times tonight and every groom does it per item. One composite write halves the parse cost, the lock hold and the conflict window for the commonest pair. Raised P1 -> P0. Code for TRAIN43 (steps 1-3 as written above); pairs naturally with the append-only note segments that M1 points to.")
