inl machine_proc_health_tune () = Task ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", "Hourly process-health sweep + autonomous SystemInformer low-CPU config tuning to SOTA: SI stays OPEN but its settings are continuously optimized for minimal CPU; each sweep also checks top CPU consumers + dangling/orphan processes and heals them", TaskKind, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "team:org,management,machine,health,monitoring,systeminformer,bsod-prevention,user-batch", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_machine_proc_health_tune () = Planning ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_1 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:49Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:5xZ user directive: 'si needs to be always open, but we gotta monitor so its settings are always optimized for low cpu consumption, gotta iterate that autonomously until sota, opening closing etc. this manual process check and deliberation needs to happen at least hourly ... we could've prevented that bsod.' CONTEXT: SystemInformer-nightly ran DEFAULT settings (UpdateInterval 1s, EnableCycleCpuUsage/Stage2/NetworkResolve all on) = 60-98% CPU sustained, 7.3h cumulative = a full core, a major contributor to the sustained 100pct saturation that preceded the 11-03 BSOD (BOX-BSOD-1103). APPLIED now (settings.xml, .bak in scratch): UpdateInterval 3e8->1388 (1s->5s), EnableCycleCpuUsage 1->0, EnableStage2 1->0, EnableNetworkResolve+DoH ->0, EnableServiceNonPoll 0->1. Measuring the result (then iterate toward SOTA: balance low CPU vs responsiveness to catch spikes). TODO: fold the process-health+SI-CPU check into the per-heartbeat durability pass (every 20min, exceeds hourly) + a reboot-surviving scheduled task for crash-survival.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_2 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 2u32, "2026-11-04T10:51Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~10:5xZ RESULT: SI config optimization WORKED - CPU 60-98% -> 19% (box 100%->~76% from the SI core alone), SI kept OPEN per the user. Config in settings.xml: UpdateInterval 5s, cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve off, service non-poll on. 5s chosen over 10s to keep spike-detection (the BSOD-prevention purpose). STANDING MECHANISM (2 parts): (1) NOW - the per-heartbeat durability pass includes a process-health+SI-CPU check (every 20min, exceeds hourly); carried across context resets by memory systeminformer-nightly-cpu-burn. (2) DURABLE (remaining build) - a reboot-surviving SCHEDULED TASK (hourly) running the sweep + SI re-tune, so it catches saturation even outside an autonomous run / after a crash (the BSOD can happen anytime). Ongoing SOTA iteration: next try handle-snapshot off + SampleCount down at the SAME 5s interval (lower CPU, no responsiveness loss).")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_3 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:04Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:1x SI TUNING CONCLUSION (averaged measurements): interval is NOT the driver - 5s and 10s both avg ~70% of ONE core (8.7% box), so the ~0.7-core is a build/provider floor (rendering/ExtendedTools/plugins), interval-independent. Config cut SI from main's ~1.5 cores -> ~0.7 core (~0.8 core reclaimed), SI kept OPEN. FINAL config: 5s interval (best spike-detection at same CPU as 10s) + cycle-CPU/stage2/network-resolve off. To go below ~0.7 core needs disabling ExtendedTools/plugins (lose GPU/disk/net monitoring) or a stable SI build = a feature tradeoff = USER'S CALL (surfaced). METHOD LESSON: single-sample CPU is noise (saw 19% and 82% for the same config); always average (Get-Counter N samples). ALSO: heartbeat cadence 20min->10min (new cron 93cc7415) + the process-health sweep folded into the heartbeat prompt (standing, survives resets) = halves this lane's idle gap + makes the hourly check every-10min.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_4 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 4u32, "2026-11-04T11:44Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:4x DURABLE SWEEP + kit-internals findings. ROOT CAUSE of the 'why aren't we doing this already' gap: kit ALREADY has the sweep (kit machine watch [--sample-seconds n]: per-process CPU, rogue/dangling detect, memory-hog detect, kit-job tree cores, window + stop-gate state) but it was UNSCHEDULED - health.tsv last written 11-02 16:04, 2 days stale. Nothing ran it => no hourly check => the 11-03 BSOD had no early warning. FIX: created reboot-surviving Scheduled Task 'kit-machine-watch-hourly' (hourly :17, LogonType Interactive, 10y) running kit machine watch --sample-seconds 3 appended to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\hourly.log (cmd redirect in the task action, no new .ps1 - spiral-first). Remove: Unregister-ScheduledTask kit-machine-watch-hourly. TWO findings for team:kit: (a) PERSISTENCE BUG: machine watch --sample-seconds N prints the sample + 'ok unix=' but does NOT append a health.tsv row (verified tty AND non-tty); the 22 existing rows came from another invocation - native append should be fixed/documented. (b) CPU-HOG ALARM GAP: auto-'hogs' = MEMORY only (>=8192MB); a CPU burner like SI (0.6 core, top of the list) is shown but not alarmed. Recommend a CPU-hog rule (non-kit-job process sustained >0.5 core => flag) - exactly the SI pattern that fed the BSOD. The session heartbeat (10min) does agent-side deliberation meanwhile.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_5 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 5u32, "2026-11-04T11:49Z", "resilience", "11-04 ~07:5x VERIFIED: the Scheduled Task runs + persists. Fixed an earlier LastTaskResult=1 - Task Scheduler appends the action argument as ONE string and cmd strips only the OUTER quote pair, so the whole inner command must be wrapped in an extra outer quote pair (cmd /c [outer-quote] [quoted kit.exe path] machine watch --sample-seconds 3 [append-redirect] [quoted hourly.log path] [stderr-merge] [outer-quote]). After the wrap, a Start-ScheduledTask run produced hourly.log with a real sample (per-process cores + rogue/memhog counts). NextRunTime 08:17, hourly. Reboot-surviving telemetry layer is LIVE. Resilience half done. Remaining is team:kit: native health.tsv append + a CPU-hog alarm.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_6 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 6u32, "2026-11-05T04:20Z", "resilience", "SI-SOTA tuning verified holding (resilience, box at 100% load): the three CPU-expensive toggles are OFF and held from prior tuning - EnableCycleCpuUsage=0, EnableStage2=0, EnableNetworkResolve=0. SI currently 0.78 core / 10% of box, ws 191MB; this is LOAD-driven (303 procs churning at 100%) not config-driven and falls back toward the ~0.4-core quiet floor when the compiler pack chain finishes. UpdateInterval=1388ms (ambiguous - neither SI default 1000 nor an obvious tuned value; may have drifted). Raising it to ~5000ms would cut sampling ~3.5x, BUT SI reloads settings only on restart and rewrites settings.xml on exit, so applying it means bouncing SI (risks the stays-OPEN directive + losing other tuned keys) for an uncertain gain - deliberately DEFERRED on a live saturated box. Per-tick health sweeps continuing; hourly scheduled task firing (LastResult=0). resilience.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_7 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 7u32, "2026-11-05T07:21Z", "resilience", "DIAGNOSIS (resilience) - the hourly kit-machine-watch LastResult=267009 at 02:17 AND 03:17 is NOT a sweep failure. Evidence: hourly.log has NO 02:17 \"ok\" entry, then one at ~03:18 (unix 1793863119, processes=344 rogues=0 hogs=0). With MultipleInstances=IgnoreNew + ExecutionTimeLimit=PT72H + Priority=7 (below-normal), the 02:17 instance HUNG under ~178% box oversubscription (CPU-starved), took ~61min to complete, wrote ok at ~03:18, exited 267009 (slow/degraded-completion code); the 03:17 trigger was IgnoreNew-skipped because 02:17 was still running. So under extreme fleet load the detailed sweep degrades to ~60min-slow and skips a trigger, but STILL completes + logs healthy data. NOT a danger gap: the separate resilience danger-watcher (bash bg, normal priority) covers BSOD(GPU+CPU)/OOM(commit) continuously at 90s. Deliberately NOT bumping the task Priority - below-normal is correct for a monitor; raising it steals CPU from an already-oversubscribed fleet. The real signal here is the box oversubscription itself (14+ core-demand on 8), which is the user \"are we saturating our machines\" concern - fleet routing (main/x-dumps), not a monitor-config fix. resilience.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_8 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 8u32, "2026-11-05T11:49Z", "resilience", "CROSS-MACHINE CENSUS (resilience, user asked: dangling/stale procs stealing resources? why lanes idle?). FINDING: NO dangling/stale resource-theft on box, node, or europa - all heavy CPU is legitimate active work. Box 100% = tibia cl5.exe (3 cores, actively computing, 81min) + x sim server (idle) + 8-lane overhead. Node = kit job host pid15484 is CURRENT version (size 24897536) + active, supervising fiscal-identity/main-replay-array/tibia-np4-gate-1/kit-TRAIN29; fresh erl/rustc/cell builds; 0 stale builds; 9.4GB free. Europa idle (loadavg 0.35), 0 zombies, only by-design k3s/iggy/containerd. So lanes are NOT resource-starved - they run node jobs. The real idle = train-GATED work stuck because TRAIN22->29 all fail to land green on CODE gates (line-limits/notebook-reds/naming), which is kit train code, NOT a resource/process issue. Reaping nothing fixes it. One resource note: tibia cl5 holds 3 of 8 box cores (active compute); if legit heavy work it belongs off-box per box-last, if stuck tibia should reap. resilience 2026-11-05 07:4x.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_9 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 9u32, "2026-11-07T15:52Z", "resilience-614162e5", "PLANNING: estimate=60m load=light assignee=agent team=resilience deps=none. DONE DEFINITION: the hourly machine-watch process-health sweep runs green (SI/SystemInformer at below-normal, low idle CPU), classifies dead-parent orphans by family, and reaps only invalid ones; verified by a sweep log showing 0 false reaps + SI CPU <2% idle. NEXT STEP: confirm the installed machine-watch sweep tuning is at SOTA (SI low-CPU config) and close; the process-health heal work from 11-06 is landed. TYPEFEATURES: orphan verdict as a typed union (Orphan|Legit|Reaped) over the process-sample stream, no stringly state.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_10 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 10u32, "2026-11-07T15:57Z", "resilience", "DoD: done when machine-watch classifies each process as Healthy/Runaway/Starved/Parked from sampled cpu_ms+rss+priority, a recorded sample with one planted runaway and one BelowNormal-parked proc asserts exactly those fire, and the SystemInformer-equivalent thresholds are tuned so a normal build is NOT flagged (0 false positives on a captured idle+build frame).")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_11 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 11u32, "2026-11-07T18:28Z", "resilience", "POPUP HEAL (user 11-07 asked to find the lane popping cmd windows): the culprit was THIS lanes own kit-machine-watch-hourly task - created 11-04 before the conhost --headless convention, it ran a BARE cmd.exe /c under InteractiveToken and popped a visible cmd window every hour at :17 (sampler caught pid 6956 at 14:17:08). Action migrated to conhost --headless (no window), AND I hardened the battery flags (DisallowStartIfOnBatteries True->False, [[scheduled-watchdog-silently-refused]]: on battery the hourly health sweep was silently skipping). Verified functional (hourly.log: kit machine watch ok processes=363). KEY HEALTH BLIND SPOT found (BEAM incident): the sweep reads FreePhysicalMemory but NOT commit charge - a 35GB-committed runaway reads fine on free-RAM while thrashing; ADD % Committed Bytes In Use (alarm >=70%) to the sweep + monitor.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_12 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 12u32, "2026-11-07T18:35Z", "resilience", "COMMIT-CHARGE SIGNAL ADDED + LIVE-VERIFIED (11-07 14:34) - closes the BEAM-incident blind spot. node-utilization-monitor.ps1 now probes committed-memory % (1 - FreeVirtualMemory/TotalVirtualMemorySize, i.e. commit charge incl pagefile) for BOTH box (local) and lap (added as the 4th value in the node EncodedCommand probe), alarms COMMIT-HIGH at >=70%, deduped. Live reading: box 42% / lap 44% (healthy). Rationale: today the x BEAM runaway hit 35GB COMMITTED on 16GB while FreePhysicalMemory still looked ok - the old monitor could not see it; commit% would have crossed 70% and alarmed. parse OK, run OK.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_13 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 13u32, "2026-11-09T04:01Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 00:0x VERIFY: NOT closable, the evidence contradicts the 90%. (1) SystemInformer runs at priority HIGH (the DoD says below-normal) and used 9.3% of the box's 8 logical cores over a 5 s sample (~0.74 core) at 00:0x. The box was busy (not an idle frame), so the CPU number isn't the DoD's idle measurement, but the priority is a plain mismatch. (2) No machine-watch sweep log exists under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules (no *machine*watch* file), so '0 false reaps' can't be shown from there; the sweep may log elsewhere or no longer run. NOT changed by org2: SystemInformer is the user's own workstation tool, and High may be the user's deliberate choice (responsiveness during saturation); a priority or config change goes to the user. Next step: find where the hourly sweep logs (or whether its scheduled task still exists), capture an idle frame for SI CPU, and ask the user whether SystemInformer should run below-normal. Progress 90 -> 70.")
inl note_of_machine_proc_health_tune_14 () = Note ("MACHINE-PROC-HEALTH-TUNE", 14u32, "2026-11-09T04:03Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 00:1x CORRECTION + ROOT CAUSE (I found the sweep's real log): the hourly sweep is scheduled task kit-machine-watch-hourly (last run 23:17, result 0) and logs to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\machine-watch\\hourly.log, not kit\\schedules. Its recent sweeps are GREEN: 'kit machine watch ok processes=300-378 rogues=0 hogs=0' every hour, and the SystemInformer CPU column reads 0.27-0.89 (< the DoD's 2%); my 9.3% was a busy frame, not idle. THE REAL GAP: SystemInformer's PID changed 5944 -> 20616 at the 23:17 sweep (cumulative CPU 42, i.e. freshly restarted), and the new process runs at priority HIGH. The below-normal tuning was applied to the running process and does not survive a SystemInformer restart. Fix options (the user's call, since it changes their tool): SystemInformer's own persistent priority setting, or the hourly sweep re-applying BelowNormal when it finds SystemInformer above it (a self-heal like the other sweep heals). Progress 70 -> 85: everything is met except persistence of the priority. Next step: ask the user which option, or whether High is intended.")
inl kit_gate_work_list () = Task ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", "Stop gate turns idle into work: on each block it names the lane's top 3 own-team ready light items (kit agile next --team <lane> --load light,none --open) plus the shared open list, never one global auto-claim; the 90 s spin guard lets a stop through only when that list is empty or the lane did tool work since the last block", Story, P0, D3, 100u32, Done, "KIT-AGILE-ANY-USABILITY", "kit,team:rig,agile,hooks,autonomy", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_gate_work_list () = Planning ("KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl kit_hooks_one_process () = Task ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", "Tool hooks as one native kit verb (kit hook pre|post <event>), not 3 pwsh starts per Edit: kit-work-in-repo, kit-backlog-state-guard and kit-style-check run in one kit.exe call, fail open past 2 s, and each run logs its own duration", Story, P1, D3, 65u32, Planned, "", "kit,team:rig,hooks,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_hooks_one_process () = Planning ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_1 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 1u32, "2026-11-04T10:57Z", "main", "11-04 07:0x main: at 100% box CPU a bare pwsh hook start took 5.4 s (measured), so every Edit paid ~15 s across its 3 pwsh hooks and every Bash/PowerShell call paid the cmd.exe lease check. hooks-fast-and-fail-open applies. kit agile next itself took 3.4 s at the same load, so a native verb is the cheap path.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_2 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 2u32, "2026-11-04T11:02Z", "kit", "11-04 07:3x kit: merging duplicate KIT-HOOK-VERB (kit filed it at 06:58, minutes before this item) here. Its spec, kept: DoD = an Edit runs one kit process (<1 s at 100% CPU) giving the same verdicts as the current pwsh hooks on a recorded fixture of hook payloads; guards fail closed and name their verdict; non-guards fail open past 2 s. NEXT: list ~/.claude/settings.json hooks (PreToolUse x3: Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit|PowerShell|Bash, Write, Edit|Write|MultiEdit; PostToolUse x1: Edit|Write|MultiEdit; Stop; SubagentStop) with their scripts as the spec. TYPEFEATURES: hook_event union, one check union per guard, verdict result union (Allow | Deny reason | Report text).")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_3 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 3u32, "2026-11-04T11:21Z", "kit", "11-04 08:0x kit: SPEC from ~/.claude/settings.json + scripts. Per tool call today: PreToolUse (1) Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit|PowerShell|Bash: cmd.exe `if exist %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\tree-leases\\*.lease` then kit-tree-lease.ps1 (61 lines; 0 leases now, so cmd only, cheap); (2) Write: kit-work-in-repo.ps1 (18 lines: deny new files in C:\\home\\data, deny new .py under C:\\home\\git, advise on new .tsv/.rows/.csv; existing files pass); (3) Edit|Write|MultiEdit: kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 (14 lines: deny a path matching \\docs\\backlog\\state\\*.spi). PostToolUse (4) Edit|Write|MultiEdit: kit-style-check.ps1 (98 lines: baseline-only-shrinks on kit\\standards\\census.spi + lint-macros*.spi vs git HEAD, exit 2; then, for kit\\src\\**\\*.spi|spiproj only, style drift on the added text). So an Edit = 2 pwsh + 1 cmd and a Write = 3 pwsh, even though (2), (3) and (4) exit early for almost every path. INTERIM, no code (hook owner decides): give hooks (2), (3), (4) path `if` conditions in permission-rule syntax, so pwsh starts only for matching paths (state/*.spi; new files under C:\\home\\git or C:\\home\\data; kit\\src and kit\\standards). RISK: a wrong pattern silently fails OPEN for a guard, so prove each with a deliberate deny attempt (an Edit to a backlog state file must still be denied). FULL FIX (this item): `kit hook pre|post` = the 4 checks as typed rules in one process.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_4 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 4u32, "2026-11-04T12:03Z", "kit", "11-04 08:4x kit: typed MODEL started (unstaged), new crate src/kit_hook_rules (rules.spi; workspace member, lock refreshed). hook_tool union (Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit/Shell/Other), verdict union (Allow | Deny reason | Advise text), hook_policy record (repos_root, closed_roots, refused_extensions, advised_extensions), so kit names no machine path; the roots come from the policy. state_verdict mirrors kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 (direct files in docs/backlog/state/*.spi; only Edit/Write/MultiEdit). new_file_verdict mirrors kit-work-in-repo.ps1 (Write of a NEW file: a closed root is denied, a refused extension under repos_root is denied, an advised extension is advised). combined: the first Deny wins, Advise texts join. pre_verdict composes them. 3 tests. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (with bodies); box notebook queued. NEXT: tree-lease rule (lease files + live pids: a boundary read, then a pure match), the style-check port, then `kit hook pre|post` reading the stdin JSON and printing hookSpecificOutput.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_5 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 5u32, "2026-11-04T13:02Z", "kit", "11-04 09:4x kit: VERB BUILT (unstaged, routed): `kit hook pre [--repos-root d] [--closed-root d,..] [--refuse .x,..] [--advise .x,..]` reads the PreToolUse payload on stdin (new boundary package kit_os_json: value_of/value_or_null/text_at/quoted, one-expression serde_json primitives that pass the style hook), takes tool_input.file_path (or notebook_path), applies kit_hook_rules.pre_verdict (backlog-state guard + new-file policy) and prints ONE line: a deny or advise hookSpecificOutput JSON, nothing to allow; an unreadable payload allows (same as today's scripts). New crate kit_hook (+ member, lock); VerbHook in command_spec_domain + kit_cli_model + kit_cli. Tests: flags parse, a state-file edit denies, an ordinary edit prints nothing, non-JSON allows. europa pack34 --check exit 0 (hook with bodies, direct_verbs, cli model, cli main). AFTER TRAIN22 (hook owner swaps): replace kit-backlog-state-guard.ps1 + kit-work-in-repo.ps1 with one `kit hook pre --repos-root C:\\home\\git --closed-root C:\\home\\data --refuse .py --advise .tsv,.rows,.csv` entry (matcher Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit). Remaining: tree-lease rule (tokenizer, fail closed) and the PostToolUse style check.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_6 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 6u32, "2026-11-04T13:03Z", "kit", "11-04 09:5x kit DECISION: the tree-lease guard stays in pwsh for now. Its write-verb regex relies on lookbehind/lookahead (unsupported by Rust regex), so a port needs a shell tokenizer, and a subtle miss would make a fail-CLOSED guard silently fail open. It already costs only one cmd.exe `if exist` check when no lease exists (0 leases today). Port it later with a fixture corpus of real commands (every write verb, redirects, here-strings, 2>&1 / >$null exclusions) that the old and new versions must agree on.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_7 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 7u32, "2026-11-04T22:26Z", "kit", "11-04 18:5x kit: the FIXTURE CORPUS the 09:5x decision asked for is BUILT (unstaged): kit_hook_rules/lease_corpus.spi, lease_corpus () : list (string * bool), 90 REAL command lines harvested from this lane's own Bash/PowerShell calls (7570 distinct, <= 88 chars), made neutral (paths -> C:\\work\\file or /work/file, item ids -> ITEM-1, lane names -> lane, emails -> someone@example.org, per kit's no-external-names rule), and LABELED BY THE CURRENT PWSH GUARD'S OWN REGEX (case-insensitive, as -match is): 45 writes, 45 non-writes. A Rust port must agree with every row before it replaces the fail-closed guard. What the labels show about today's guard: every \"2>&1\" line counts as a write (the redirect rule (?<![<>=-])>{1,2} has no 2>&1 / >$null exclusion), and here-string prose trips it (\"move\" in a note, the > of an email address), so the guard over-blocks commands that name a leased root; that is safe (fail-closed) but is exactly what the port should fix deliberately, not by accident. europa --check ok with the count test; box notebook kit-corpus-tests.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_8 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 8u32, "2026-11-04T22:34Z", "kit", "11-04 18:4x kit: lease_corpus PROVEN on the box (kit-corpus-tests 1/1: 90 rows, 45 write labels from the pwsh guard).")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_9 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 9u32, "2026-11-04T23:20Z", "kit", "11-04 19:3x kit: the PORT'S CORE is written (unstaged): kit_hook_rules/lease_verbs.spi, a typed classifier with no regex (Rust regex has no lookaround): write_sign = LongVerb | ShortVerb | Redirect (position) | GitVerb, write_sign_of / writes. Rules, one per regex arm: long verbs by substring on the lower-cased command; short verbs only when the chars on both sides are not [A-Za-z0-9_./\\\\-] (the lookarounds); a > whose previous char is not <>=- and next is not = (equivalent to (?<![<>=-])>{1,2}(?!=) after backtracking); \"git\" + at least one blank + a write subcommand. Tests: the_classifier_agrees_with_the_pwsh_guard_on_every_corpus_row (all 90 lease_corpus rows) and one case per rule. europa --check ok with bodies; box notebook kit-lease-verbs-tests. EXTRA EVIDENCE (scratchpad Python mirror of the same logic vs the guard's regex): 0 disagreements on 20082 distinct real command lines from this lane's transcript. Known edge: word chars are ASCII where .NET \\w is Unicode, which only matters for a short verb glued to a non-ASCII letter. NEXT: wire writes into the hook binary and run it next to the pwsh guard in shadow mode (log disagreements) before switching.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_10 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 10u32, "2026-11-04T23:26Z", "kit", "11-04 19:3x kit: (1) first classifier notebook (kit-lease-verbs-tests) RED before any test ran: in the notebook runner `:< lease_corpus` (a bare sibling import) was ETY-UnboundModule, while europa's package-mode --check resolved it; the qualified `:< kit_hook_rules.lease_corpus` fails in package mode instead. kit_coordinator_table/dispatch.spi's bare `:< table` does pass in notebooks, so the cause is specific and not yet found. Not chased tonight: the corpus is now INSIDE lease_verbs.spi (one module: corpus + classifier + 3 tests; lease_corpus.spi removed from the package, its old copy kept in the session scratchpad). (2) the shadow-mode BINARY side is built (unstaged): `kit hook writes` reads a Bash/PowerShell hook payload from stdin and prints \"kit hook writes=yes sign=short:rm\" (or verb:/redirect@N/git:) or \"kit hook writes=no\"; test a_shell_payload_is_classified_like_the_lease_guard. After install, the pwsh guard can call it and log any disagreement before the switch. europa --check ok on both with bodies; box notebooks kit-lease-verbs-tests2.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_11 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 11u32, "2026-11-04T23:36Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit: classifier PROVEN on the box (kit-lease-verbs-tests2: lease_verbs 3/3, i.e. agreement with all 90 corpus rows + one case per rule). Two follow-ups: (a) the hook.spi test that went through writes_answered was RED only because notebook Rust cells have no serde_json (kit_os_json) - the binary has it as a Cargo dep; (b) node size kit-size-hook: kit_hook 1497 OVER, since kit_hook_rules is inlined (not linked) and pulled the classifier + 90-row corpus in. FIX (unstaged): NEW linked crate kit_hook_verbs (lease_verbs.spi moved there + sign_line, export kit_hook_sign_line : string -> \"writes=yes sign=...\"|\"writes=no\", test the_answer_line_names_the_sign); kit_hook links it and keeps a one-line writes_answered; the JSON-dependent hook test is gone (sign tests live in the verbs crate); kit_hook_rules back to rules only. Manifests/lock: cargo metadata --offline --locked exit 0. europa --check ok on all three with bodies. Size (kit-size-hookverbs) and notebooks (kit-hookverbs-tests) pending.")
inl note_of_kit_hooks_one_process_12 () = Note ("KIT-HOOKS-ONE-PROCESS", 12u32, "2026-11-04T23:45Z", "kit", "11-04 19:4x kit: hook split GREEN. Node size: kit_hook 1497 -> 860, kit_hook_verbs 673, kit_hook_rules 10. Box notebooks 3/3 (lease_verbs 4/4 incl. the 90-row agreement + the_answer_line_names_the_sign). Rides TRAIN27. After install: wire `kit hook writes` beside kit-tree-lease.ps1 in shadow mode (user hook config, so ask first).")
