inl kit_job_tree_linux () = Task ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", "kit jobs on Linux: tie a job's process tree to its supervisor (process group + PR_SET_PDEATHSIG / cgroup), so killing the supervisor takes the tree down, as the Windows job object does", TaskKind, P1, D4, 60u32, Active, "", "kit,team:rig,jobs,linux,remote", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_tree_linux () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 1u32, "2026-11-04T12:53Z", "kit", "11-04 09:1x kit FINDING: europa had 2 sleepers of kit's own test killing_only_the_supervisor_takes_the_whole_job_tree_down (kit_jobs/stop_cli.spi) alive for 15.5 h after their parent died (stopped by kit at 08:2x). Cause, read in kit_jobs/host.spi: child_container() and container_capped() are Windows-only (`#[cfg(not(windows))] fn container() -> u64 { 0 }`), so on Linux nothing ties the job tree to the supervisor; a SIGKILLed supervisor leaves the tree running, and memory caps are a no-op. This matters now that heavy runs go node/europa-first. DoD: that test passes on europa (no beat after the supervisor kill) and no fixture process outlives the run. NEXT: in a boundary package (kit_os_process), spawn job children with process_group(0) and pre_exec prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, SIGKILL) for the direct child; on supervisor exit or stop, killpg(job pgid); evaluate a cgroup v2 scope (systemd-run --user --scope) for grandchildren that re-parent. TYPEFEATURES: a container union (WindowsJob handle | UnixGroup pgid | NoContainer) instead of a bare u64 0.")
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 2u32, "2026-11-06T12:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 08:0x kit, evidence from kit CI run 42203030322 (ubuntu-24.04): kit kill <supervisor pid> printed stopped 0 of 1 processes, failed 1, so on Linux the kill verb does not stop even the supervisor itself, before any tree question. The stop_cli test killing_only_the_supervisor_... now runs only where a job object holds the tree (on_windows); DoD addition: Linux kill stops the pid, then the tree (process group plus PR_SET_PDEATHSIG or a cgroup), and the test drops its on_windows guard.")
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 3u32, "2026-11-09T11:19Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 08:0x local rig, CODE READ (src/rig_jobs/host.spi): logged_spawn already starts the child in its own process group on unix (command.process_group(0), line 173), and child_tree_stopped kills the whole group (kill -KILL -- -<root>, then counts survivors in /proc by parent, lines 204-210), so a supervisor-initiated stop DOES take the tree on Linux today. The remaining gap is exactly the test's case: the SUPERVISOR is SIGKILLed, so nobody kills the group. PR_SET_PDEATHSIG (pre_exec prctl) would only cover the direct child, not grandchildren in the group, and adds an unsafe pre_exec to a ratchet-limited legacy host block. DESIGN instead: the group id is the child pid (process_group(0)), already in <job>/pid; whoever first notices the supervisor stopped beating (the job host loop and job list both mark a job lost) kills that group once: a Spiral decision (lost + pid file + group still alive) calling one existing unix primitive (kill -KILL -- -<pgid>, the same as child_tree_stopped), and records reaped=N in the job dir. DoD unchanged: stop_cli killing_only_the_supervisor_takes_the_whole_job_tree_down passes on Linux with its on_windows guard removed. Next step: find the lost-detection site (rig_job_host host_step or rig_jobs list) and add the reap there in a boundary primitive.")
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 4u32, "2026-11-09T11:20Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 08:1x local rig: reap site found: src/rig_job_orphans/orphans.spi orphan_settled already handles Settled JobLost with a dead supervisor (unattended = supervisor 0 or not alive) for stop-requested and held jobs; the group reap goes in that branch. No pid-group kill primitive exists yet (rust_std_process has only spawn-and-kill helpers), so it needs one boundary primitive group_killed pgid. HAZARD that blocks writing it blind: the group id is the child pid, and a lost job seen hours later may have that pid RECYCLED as another process's group leader; kill -KILL -<pid> would then hit an unrelated group. Guard required before any reap: the group leader's start time (/proc/<pid>/stat field 22) must fall within the job's started_ms window, or its cmdline must equal the job's argv[0]; otherwise record reaped=skipped-pid-reused. Proof needs Linux (kit CI ubuntu leg or a europa build), so this lands with its stop_cli test un-guarded in the same train.")
inl note_of_kit_job_tree_linux_5 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-TREE-LINUX", 5u32, "2026-11-09T13:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:4x local rig, BUILT (unstaged, unprobed; in the post-TRAIN42e probe batch). (1) rig_os_process/process.spi: boundary primitive group_signalled signal group (kill <signal> -- <group>, output captured, false when kill is missing or refuses), tested with a malformed group only: a test must never signal a real or overflowing group. (2) rig_process_alive/alive.spi (linked, HostFree): group_reap union ReapedGroup | NoGroupLeft | SkippedPidReused | SkippedNoChildPid | SkippedWindows; reap_skip refuses Windows (the job object holds the tree), pid <= 1 (kill -- -1 signals EVERY process), and a running leader whose start time does not match the pid file write time within 2 s (reuse guard: measured on the box, the child started 5 ms before its pid file was written). A dead leader is reaped: Linux never reallocates a pid still used as a pgid, so only the job's own survivors hold it. Exported as rig_process_lost_group_reaped. (3) rig_job_orphans/orphans.spi: in the JobLost branch, when the supervisor is gone and <job>/reaped is absent, reap once and write the outcome word to <job>/reaped (runs from job state, job wait and the job summary). (4) rig_process_table/host.spi: the kill survivor count on Linux now excludes zombies (likely cause of CI 42203030322 stopped 0 of 1: a SIGKILLed supervisor stays a zombie until reaped; unverified until a Linux run). (5) stop_cli test renamed back to killing_only_the_supervisor_takes_the_whole_job_tree_down, on_windows guard REMOVED, it now waits (45 s) for state=lost so the Linux reap fires. Also fixed in passing: the kit-style hook boundary regex was ^kit_os_ (missed by the rename), now ^(kit|rig)_os_; and the rig_jobs census regression from the rename (host-block 24 -> 25: two env-twin reads split over two lines) is fixed by using rust_std_env.model.variable_twin for JOB_RELAUNCHED and JOB_WATCH (host-block now 23). Ratchet ok on all 5 packages. PROOF PLAN: moon probe (Windows path + compile) in the batch; the Linux DoD needs the kit CI ubuntu leg or a ganymede kit build after the train lands.")
