inl kit_train_watcher_retry () = Task ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", "launch-train's box-side watcher retries a lost ssh route instead of ending the train's install step", TaskKind, P0, D2, 90u32, Active, "", "kit,team:rig,train,remote", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_train_watcher_retry () = Planning ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_1 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 1u32, "2026-11-05T23:50Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:4x kit FILED from TRAIN32: the box job (job-1793906232412-kit-TRAIN32-node) ended exit 1 after 4.5 h because remote.ps1 found no route to the node (port 22 unreachable on both LAN and tailscale, sshd starved at 100 pct CPU); both ports answered a minute later and the node half kept running notebooks. A green train would have lost its install step to this blip. DoD: the box-side poll treats a no-route error as transient (retry with backoff for up to ~10 min before giving up), logs each retry, and a forced one-poll ssh failure in a test still ends with the node half's real verdict.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_2 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 2u32, "2026-11-05T23:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 19:5x kit: BUILT (unstaged) in scripts/launch-train.ps1: the node-half wait is now a loop. It breaks on the real verdict line (kit job wait ok ...); any other output (a lost route, a dropped ssh session) is logged as 'node wait lost its route (n of 10): <last line>' and retried after 60 s, up to 10 straight misses, then the existing exit=0 check decides as before. Parse 0, CRLF kept (live hash now 81841578748EB2B8). Not yet exercised: proves itself on the next train that hits a blip; a forced-failure test needs a fake remote, not done.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_3 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 3u32, "2026-11-06T08:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit: REPRO from TRAIN33. The box-side watcher (job-1793925783556) ended exit 1 at about 00:17 after 3 h 34 m with remote.ps1 no route to node on both routes (<moon-lan-addr> and <moon-ts-addr>). The node half job-1793925896101 started 20:44:56 and its result is unread. At 04:16 the node still fails ping on both, while the router and europa answer, so the node is down or off the network, not the box. DoD addition: a lost route ends the watcher in a typed LinkLost state with result Unknown, never exit 1, and the install step resumes when the route returns. Same class as TRAIN32 lost watcher; feeds KIT-NET-SIM incident replay.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_4 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 4u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: confirmed by fiscal (n7: a long node job wait hold gets reset, and an offline node makes the waiter exit 1) and tibia (n8: node unreachable at 04:19 on both routes), so three lanes hit it in two days. x (n4): a timeout on a loaded node is not red either; rerun the same inputs with a wider budget. Priority stays with the typed LinkLost and Unknown outcome; this is the most cross-lane item kit holds.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_5 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 5u32, "2026-11-06T08:26Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit groom: P1 to P0. Three lanes hit it in two days (kit TRAIN32 and TRAIN33, fiscal, tibia), and until it lands no node train result can be read once the route drops. CPU-light: a retry loop plus a typed Unknown outcome in launch-train, provable on the box with a fake route that fails twice.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_6 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 6u32, "2026-11-06T08:26Z", "x", "2026-11-06 04:2x x: CROSS-LANE (star exchange 11-06): kit n1, fiscal n7, tibia n8 and x all hit \"lost route = reported red\". Ask kit to widen this item from the train watcher to every box-side `kit job wait`: on no route, retry within a bounded window, then exit with a distinct UNKNOWN code that names the host, never 1.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_7 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 7u32, "2026-11-06T10:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:1x kit groom 2, star exchange consolidation: FIX IN THE TREE (unstaged), scripts/launch-train.ps1. ROOT CAUSE: the 10-try wait loop existed, but remote.ps1 throws on no route, and a throw from a script called with & is not captured by 2>&1, so the launcher died at the first miss. Changes: (1) the wait call is wrapped in try/catch, so a lost route counts as a miss; (2) after 10 misses it throws node half UNKNOWN with the resume command instead of falling through to a red; (3) new -ResumeNodeJob <id> with -NodeStage skips parity, sync and launch, rebuilds the before-hashes from the launch snapshot, and goes straight to fetch, copy back and box install. Parse check 0 errors. FIRST USE: at 06:09 job-1793959738326-kit-TRAIN33-resume resumed the green TRAIN33 node half (regen ok, crates 358/0, notebooks 234/234) without rerunning 3.5 h. Left: the typed LinkLost outcome in kit itself (KIT-NET-LINK) and a box test with a fake route that fails twice.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_8 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 8u32, "2026-11-06T14:20Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 10:4x kit: the retry budget now matches the outage that actually happened. The 10 one-minute misses (from this morning) would still have given up during the 6 h node sshd outage; now the box watcher retries until the node job own timeout plus slack (11 h, the node half runs under a 10 h limit and the box launcher under 12 h), backing off 1, 2, 3, 4, then 5 minutes between tries, and logs each miss with the remote.ps1 exit code (251 NoRoute, 252 NoBanner, 253 AuthRefused from KIT-REMOTE-NO-ROUTE-EXIT). After 11 h it throws node half UNKNOWN with the -ResumeNodeJob command. So TRAIN33 would have installed itself at about 06:05, when sshd came back, with no manual resume. Same change carried into the REMOTE-EXEC-S4 draft (EFA80778, still 4 hunks against live). Parse 0 on both. Left: a live proof, the next time a route drops during a train.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_9 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 9u32, "2026-11-06T14:20Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "NEXT STEP: watch the next node train; if the route drops, check that the log shows misses with exit 251/252/253 and that the install continues by itself when the route returns, then close. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: the route outcome is the closed union NoRoute | NoBanner | AuthRefused | RemoteFailed from KIT-REMOTE-NO-ROUTE-EXIT, mapped once from the exit code; the pwsh watcher only branches on it, and the kit side (kit job --on, REMOTE-EXEC-S4) carries it as a typed outcome of the dispatch union.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_10 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 10u32, "2026-11-06T22:21Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 18:2x kit: DETERMINISTIC PROOF of the retry loop (no live node drop happened during a train today): a scratch harness extracts the loop verbatim from scripts/launch-train.ps1 (lines 141-150, brace-matched, not retyped) and runs it with a fake route and recorded Start-Sleep. Case 1, two lost routes (ssh timeout, remote.ps1 route-outcome=NoRoute) then kit job wait ok: outcome ok after 3 calls, backoff sleeps 60 s and 120 s, 2 typed miss lines. Case 2, retry window spent: throws node half UNKNOWN with the -ResumeNodeJob hint after 1 call, no sleep. The live half (a real drop mid-train) stays open as the last DoD clause; REMOTE-EXEC-S4 moves this wait to kit job wait, where the same loop applies.")
inl note_of_kit_train_watcher_retry_11 () = Note ("KIT-TRAIN-WATCHER-RETRY", 11u32, "2026-11-09T02:55Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:0x rig: closed the TRAIN41 gap in scripts/launch-train.ps1 (unstaged; old copy archived as launch-train.ps1.watcher under LOCALAPPDATA kit archive r3b-env-before-20261108). Before, a failed node half threw BEFORE the hash-verified fetch of its regenerated outputs and verdicts, so TRAIN41r needed a hand fetch (scratchpad train41-finish.ps1). Now the node verdict is recorded as nodeOk, the outputs and any verdicts are fetched and copied back by hash first (copy-back still only where the live file is unchanged since the snapshot), a missing verdict file is fatal only when the node said ok, and THEN a failed half throws, naming the snapshot so failed notebooks can rerun under a new name. Parse-checked. Next step unchanged: watch TRAIN42 (the retry path and this fetch path), then close.")
inl kit_net_topology () = Task ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", "One typed fleet model: networks (home, work), one hub each, machines, links (direct, jump, reverse tunnel) and harvest windows, replacing ad hoc remote-machines.json reads", Story, P0, D3, 50u32, Active, "", "kit,remote,federation,machines,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_topology () = Planning ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). WHY: remote-machines.json and memory files hold the fleet today (box, wifi node moon, europa, SP relay, codespaces) and the work network (WORK-HUB and its LAN, 244/245 behind the SP reverse tunnel) exists only in resilience scratch notes. DoD: one Spiral model of networks, hubs, machines, links and harvest windows; kit machine list prints it; remote-machines.json is generated from it (compat) or retired; tests refuse a machine in two networks, a network with zero or two hubs, and a push plan to an outbound-only machine. MILESTONES: M1 model + tests 90 m; M2 kit reads it wherever remote-machines.json is read today 90 m. Estimate 180 m = sum of milestones. NEXT STEP: grep every reader of remote-machines.json in kit and scripts, then write the model module. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: network_id, machine_id, hub_id nominal; link GADT indexed by a direction phantom (Outbound | Inbound) with cases Direct, Jump (list hop), Reverse (relay, port), so a dispatch that needs Inbound cannot be built for an outbound-only work machine; harvest window as a typed local-time range with a timezone nominal (21:00-06:00 crosses midnight: a test); authority as a phantom-tagged hub witness. Design reference: SCION (the ETH path-aware internet architecture, not Anthropic Scion) isolation domains: each network its own trust root, hubs as the core, cross-domain traffic only through core links.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_2 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:5x kit: schema owner for remote-machines.json (resilience ask 06:4x): machine specs, live headroom and per-machine admission gates (europa: load under 4 AND MemAvailable at least cap + 600 MB inside a systemd-run MemoryMax scope; wifi node: a CPU-load gate) are fields of the typed topology; the JSON file is generated from it so scripts and resilience monitors read one shape.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_3 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 3u32, "2026-11-06T14:17Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 10:3x kit: M1 DRAFTED (unstaged), new source-only package src/kit_net_topology (topology.spi). Types: link GADT cases Direct address | Jump (hops, address) | Reverse (relay, port); machine_os; day_window (minutes of the local day); network {id; harvest}; machine {id; network; hub; link; os}; fleet_problem MachineInTwoNetworks | HubCount (network, n) | UnknownNetwork (machine, network); push_plan PushDirect | PushThroughJump | PullOnly (relay, port). Rules: fleet_problems refuses a machine in two networks, a network with zero or two hubs and an unknown network; push_plan_of makes a Reverse machine pull-only; in_window handles a window that crosses midnight (21:00-06:00 includes 23:30 and 05:59, excludes 06:00 and noon). Tests use neutral names (alpha, beta, relay.example), since kit knows no real machine: the real fleet (box, wifi node, europa, WORK-HUB behind the SP relay) is DATA, read in M2 from the fleet file that replaces remote-machines.json. Proof pending: the node is at 100 pct CPU with three probes; the notebook runs when a slot frees. Deviation from the plan: the Inbound/Outbound phantom index is not used yet (the existential packing for a mixed fleet list is the open design question), so PullOnly is a runtime verdict for now.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_4 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 4u32, "2026-11-06T14:53Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 11:0x kit: M1 PROVEN on the node (job-1793976054104-kit-probe1106h): topology.spi 3/3 tests pass (a clean two-network fleet; no hub, two hubs, one machine in two networks, unknown network; reverse link is pull-only and a 21:00-06:00 window crosses midnight; the fleet file reader: Network and Machine rows, minute parsing, and an unknown role reported as UnreadRow instead of guessed). M2 started: the fleet file row format is defined and parsed (inl network_of_x () = Network (id, HH:MM, HH:MM) and inl machine_of_x () = Machine (id, network, hub|member, direct|jump|reverse, address, hops-or-port, windows|linux)). NEXT: a kit machine fleet verb that reads the fleet file from the kit config dir, prints the machines and the fleet_problems, and generates remote-machines.json from it; then point remote.ps1 readers at the generated file.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_5 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 5u32, "2026-11-06T15:46Z", "resilience-614162e5", "WORK-NETWORK ROWS for the fleet model (resilience-614162e5, verified from relay-lab/connect.ps1 + revtunnels-up.sh, not guessed). SP relay = <pluto-addr> (root) is the reverse rendezvous for the work LAN. SUGGESTED ROWS:\ninl machine_of_des_073 () = Machine (\"work-hub\", \"work\", \"hub\", \"jump\", \"<work-hub-addr>\", \"<pluto-addr>,244@2244\", \"linux\")\ninl machine_of_244 () = Machine (\"244\", \"work\", \"member\", \"reverse\", \"<pluto-addr>\", \"2244\", \"linux\")\ninl machine_of_245 () = Machine (\"245\", \"work\", \"member\", \"jump\", \"<work-addr>\", \"244\", \"linux\")\nIMPORTANT role nuance (one-hub model does not fit cleanly - you decide how to represent): 244 is the LAN REVERSE-INGRESS ANCHOR - it runs the SP reverse tunnel (revtunnels-up.sh on 244: -R 2244:localhost:22 -R 2245:<work-addr>:22 root@SP), so from home 244 is reverse via SP:2244 and 245 is ALSO reachable reverse via SP:2245 OR jump via 244 (its configured fallback=jump Via=244). work-hub (<work-hub-addr>) is the INTERNAL hub (connect.ps1 mesh note: \"WORK-HUB reaches 244/245/232/sp/sjc direct, work-host via its 2203 tunnel\") but from HOME it is reached by chaining SP relay -> SP:2244 (244 anchor) -> LAN hop to <work-hub-addr> (the live chain: ssh -J rustdesk,ubuntu@127.0.0.1:2244 <user>@<work-hub-addr>). I see NO dedicated work-hub reverse port in this config copy - if work-hub runs its own reverse tunnel, it is not in the Downloads copy; confirm work-hub-side revtunnels if you want work-hub-direct. TWO MORE work machines in the config (not asked but present): work-host = Windows/cmd.exe, via work-hub:2203 tunnel -> work-host sshd:2222 (Machine \"work-host\" work member jump \"127.0.0.1:2203\" \"work-hub\" windows); 232 = <work-addr> Windows/PowerShell, fallback jump via work-host, DECLARED (needs ssh user+key, not live). SOURCE CAVEAT: read from the LOCAL Downloads/relay-lab/connect.ps1; the work-hub-resident copy I edited this session only ADDED home nodes (box=2246, wifi=2247) for the bidirectional mailbox - those are HOME machines, not work, so the work rows above are unchanged. Harvest window 21:00-06:00 work-local is fine. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_6 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 6u32, "2026-11-06T15:49Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 11:5x kit: fleet file now holds both networks (in %APPDATA%\\kit\\fleet.spi, data, not code): home (box hub, node via ssh alias laptop, europa) and work from resilience n5 (work-hub hub jump <work-hub-addr> via <pluto-addr> and 244@2244; 244 member reverse <pluto-addr>:2244; 245 member jump <work-addr> via 244; work-host member jump 127.0.0.1:2203 via work-hub, windows). 232 left out until it has an ssh user and key (declared, not live). MODEL GAP from resilience: the one-hub rule fits, but ingress does not: 244 is the reverse-ingress ANCHOR for the work LAN (it runs the SP reverse tunnel for itself and 245), while work-hub is the hub but has no reverse port of its own, so from home work-hub is a jump through the anchor. Next model step: an ingress role separate from hub (Anchor) and a check that every network without a direct route has exactly one anchor; the push plan for a jump through a reverse anchor stays pull-only. The kit machine fleet verb (crate kit_net_topology, linked from kit_machine_watch) is in node probe job-1793979553254-kit-probe1106l and rides TRAIN34.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_7 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 7u32, "2026-11-06T18:00Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 14:0x kit: model now has the INGRESS ANCHOR rule (AnchorCount: a network with any non-direct machine needs exactly one reverse-linked machine; home all-direct needs none; work has 244), and the fleet row parser reads fields by index from an array instead of 3- and 7-element list patterns (row_of_line was 536 generated lines). Node probe job-1793986866463: topology.spi 3/3 pass with the anchor test. SIZE: the crate with the verb is still 1603 generated lines, spread over about 70 functions (no single culprit), so the verb needs a THREE-CRATE SPLIT, not trimming: (1) kit_net_fleet_file parses fleet text into array_base of flat rows (kind, fields, minutes as primitives); (2) kit_net_topology turns flat rows into the typed model, runs the rules, and returns report lines (array_base of {stream; text}); (3) kit_machine_watch only prints them. Links cross with records-in-arrays only (memory spiral-link-boundary-shapes). Until then the package stays source-only (tests run, no crate) and the verb wiring is held in TRAIN35-HOLD-1106. NEXT: the split, after TRAIN35 is underway.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_8 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 8u32, "2026-11-06T21:51Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 17:5x kit: TRAIN35 probe sizes put kit_net_topology at 1434 generated lines (over 1000), so it was held out of TRAIN35 and SPLIT AGAIN (unstaged, in the tree after the TRAIN35 snapshot): new crate kit_net_fleet_rules (rules.spi: member {id; network; hub; reach word}, reach GADT DirectReach|JumpReach|ReverseReach, fleet_problem GADT, hub/membership/anchor rules, problem_text; export problem_lines ((networks, members)) returns array_base string). kit_net_topology keeps the typed link/window model, row reading, push plan, window and rendering, and flattens machines into members for the rules export. Test a_fleet_has_one_hub_per_network_one_anchor_when_indirect_and_each_machine_in_one_network moved to rules. Proof: node probe job-1794001491840-kit-probe1106x (notebooks, sizes, links topology -> rules, topology -> fleet_file, machine_watch -> topology).")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_9 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 9u32, "2026-11-07T00:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 20:1x kit: SIZE GATE PASSED, proven on the node (probe job-1794009130815-kit-probe1106ab, exit 0): kit_net_topology 962 generated lines (was 1434 -> 1109 -> 1059), kit_net_fleet_rules 577, kit_net_fleet_file 478, kit_machine_watch 940; notebooks fleet_file 2/2 and topology 2/2; links topology -> rules, topology -> fleet_file (4), machine_watch -> topology. The last cut came from reading the generated Rust: minute_of was 74 lines and hhmm was inlined 4 times into the network listing, so both moved into kit_net_fleet_file as exports with link shapes (clock_minutes returns a 0-or-1 array_base i32, clock_text returns string). Partition of rows is one recursive pass. Ready for the train after TRAIN36 (kit machine fleet verb live there).")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_10 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 10u32, "2026-11-07T23:34Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 19:4x kit: waits on install: TRAIN39 (built and proven on the node or pending its probe; the box still runs TRAIN33). Close or continue from the post-install check.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_11 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 11u32, "2026-11-08T12:13Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 08:1x rig, post-install check on the box with the TRAIN39 kit (kit.exe 56642297, installed 02:10). M1 is LIVE: kit machine fleet gives ok networks=2 machines=7 problems=0 from APPDATA\\kit\\fleet.spi (home: box hub, node, europa; work: work-hub hub, 244 reverse anchor, 245, work-host). M2 is open: kit and the scripts still read remote-machines.json directly. NEXT STEP: grep every reader of remote-machines.json in kit and i574n.github scripts, then generate it from the fleet file or move each reader.")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_12 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 12u32, "2026-11-08T20:46Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 17:2x rig: concrete M2 case: kit job start --on <host> runs plain kit on the remote and ignores remote-machines.json kit (europa and ganymede both need --bin <path>). M2 makes job start read the machine entry kit path (and later the fleet model).")
inl note_of_kit_net_topology_13 () = Note ("KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", 13u32, "2026-11-09T03:54Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 04:2x rig M2 DESIGN (code after TRAIN42, which is already carrying env names, prefix, delegate, heavy refusal, defaults.env, waiter backoff and Prim): no kit code reads remote-machines.json today (grep: 0 readers in src), and the M1 fleet rows (APPDATA kit fleet.spi: Machine id, network, role, link, address, hops-or-port, os) lack the two fields --on needs. Plan: (1) Machine rows gain ssh_alias and rig_path (typed: a machine_reach record { alias; binary }), filled for box, moon (alias wifi, also wifi-ts and wifi-ip), europa (vultr), ganymede, comet; (2) rig_job_dispatch machine_binary resolves fleet row first, then jobs/machines/<alias>.bin, then plain kit, and --on accepts a fleet id (node, europa) as well as an ssh alias, which closes REMOTE-EXEC-S4 gap 1 (node is a registry name, not an ssh alias); (3) kit machine fleet --emit writes remote-machines.json and the .bin files from the fleet, so remote.ps1 readers keep working while they migrate. Tonight's manual state to fold in: .bin files for ganymede, wifi, wifi-ts, wifi-ip, laptop, vultr, comet; node.bin archived. DoD unchanged; next step (1) with a fixture fleet holding one alias per machine.")
inl kit_net_sim () = Task ("KIT-NET-SIM", "Deterministic two-network simulator: seeded discrete events (owner jobs, guest jobs, harvest windows, link drops, clock skew, hub crash), incident replays and a bounded exhaustive protocol check", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY", "kit,remote,federation,simulation,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_sim () = Planning ("KIT-NET-SIM", Minutes 270u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_sim_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-SIM", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). WHY: the work network is not ours to experiment on, and the failures we keep meeting (europa thrash x3 on 11-05, the 51 GB x_compat, TRAIN32 losing its watcher to an ssh blip, node-watch frozen at BUSY for 2 days) are timing races a deterministic simulator reproduces in milliseconds. DoD: a seeded simulator of two networks with properties P1 no guest runs while an owner job is running or queued past the vacate bound; P2 no guest starts outside the window or with p90 wall past its end; P3 each op takes effect exactly once under at-least-once delivery; P4 one writer per item; P5 a stale heartbeat never reads as idle. Recorded incidents replay as fixtures. Plus a bounded exhaustive check: 2 hubs, up to 3 ops, every interleaving and partition point, all properties hold. MILESTONES: M1 event model + seeded scheduler 90 m; M2 P1-P5 + incident replays 90 m; M3 bounded exhaustive check 90 m. Estimate 270 m. Load medium: M3 enumerates, run it on the node. NEXT STEP: the event union and the seeded clock, sharing proc_event with KIT-ORPHAN-SIM. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: sim_event GADT indexed by the state it requires (a Vacate needs a Running guest); properties as typed predicates over the trace; seeds nominal u64; the checker enumerates a closed op union, so a new op case breaks the checker until it is covered.")
inl note_of_kit_net_sim_2 () = Note ("KIT-NET-SIM", 2u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: incidents to replay, from the star exchange: the node off the network at about 00:17 on 11-06 (both routes, lasting past 04:19) with TRAIN33 half finished; europa ConnectedNoBanner under an uncapped compile loop; a promote os error 5 under a burst of groom writes from 8 lanes.")
inl kit_agile_hub () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", "One agile hub process per network: holds the parsed monolith in memory, applies writes as ordered typed ops, checkpoints to agile.spi, serves list/next/show/claim; ends whole-file CAS races", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-AGILE-EVENT-LOG", "kit,agile,federation,scale,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_hub () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_hub_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). WHY (measured): at 14.86 MB with 7 lanes, list 1.3 s, wip 152 s, note 1.5-4.6 s (tibia 11-05), a batch queued over 120 s outside elapsed_ms, rename 398 s CPU and never landed (KIT-SCALE); every write rewrites the whole file and loses CAS races. The note-segment writer shipped in TRAIN7 but is off fleet-wide. Scion: one authority holds state and serves queries (status 207x faster). DeltaDB: checkpoint plus incremental changes with provenance. DoD: one hub per network holds the parsed state, applies every write as a typed op in order, checkpoints to agile.spi and segments, answers list/next/show/claim; CLI verbs use it when it answers and today path otherwise; replaying the op log on a copy reproduces agile.spi byte for byte; the STATE-CONCURRENCY-SOTA 50-writer 10-minute test passes with 0 lost updates and p99 write under 5 s (targets: write p50 under 100 ms, reads under 50 ms). MILESTONES: M0 segments on for kit alone after TRAIN33 30 m; M1 op GADT + byte-identical replay 90 m; M2 hub serving reads + bench vs file 90 m; M3 writes through the hub + 50-writer test on the node 90 m. Estimate 300 m. NEXT STEP: once TRAIN33 installs, KIT_AGILE_NOTE_SEGMENTS=1 for kit for a day (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG next step). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: agile_op GADT indexed by commutativity (Commutes: NoteAppend; Sequenced: set, edit, claim, rename) so federation can only route Sequenced ops to an authority; provenance (lane, session id, transcript offset) on every op; the hub backend is the first verdict of KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION (expected Gleam by G1/G2: long-lived, many concurrent clients, supervised); the CLI stays Rust.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_hub_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", 2u32, "2026-11-06T16:01Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 12:0x kit, storage decision with compiler (GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S): kit agile check re-attests the whole 15.7 MB agile.spi on every write because it is one module and receipts are per module; compiler traces show the check is linear in size, parse and tokenize about 3/4 of the cost, so no compiler change gets a write under 5 s. DECISION: shard the state into 64 modules keyed by FNV-1a 32 of the item ID mod 64 (state/agile_<nn>.spi), with every row of an item in its shard (Task, Planning, Note, Parent, Asked), head unions in an agile_types module, agile.spi kept as the module that lists the shards, one receipt per shard. It supersedes the month-keyed note-segment writer (never switched on). compiler measures a 64-shard split of today file (all cold, then one changed shard warm) before kit builds it; that number decides whether this hub still needs its in-memory cache. Migration must be lossless (row count and sha256 of the row set equal).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_hub_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", 3u32, "2026-11-06T16:04Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 12:1x kit, compiler measurement (first half): split of today agile.spi with the FNV-1a item key mod 64 is LOSSLESS (18,517 rows both sides, row-set sha256 5493C199C303BB0D both sides); shards min 90 KB, avg 245 KB, max 604 KB (the tail is items with long note histories). One-shard check, types + 1 shard, cold process, box at 100 pct, 3 runs: median shard agile_07 (240 KB) 4.6 / 4.8 / 6.5 s; largest agile_57 (604 KB) 6.1 / 10.1 / 14.9 s; about 1 s of each run is process start that a warm checker removes. DECISION: keep N=64 and bucket NOTES: Task, Planning, Parent and Asked stay in the item shard; a note goes to FNV-1a(item ID + : + (n / 32)) mod 64, so a note write re-checks only the shard with the item newest bucket and closed buckets never change (their receipts stay valid). Oracle for the writer: compiler scratchpad shard-state.ps1 (its row-set sha is the migration test). Pending from compiler: the cold all-64 run and post-bucketing shard sizes on the node.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_hub_4 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-HUB", 4u32, "2026-11-06T16:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 12:1x kit: note bucketing measured by compiler on the same frozen snapshot: still lossless (18,517 rows, row-set sha 5493C199C303BB0D); N=64 sizes min 107 / avg 245 / max 412 KB (was 604), the tail now plain hash variance. DECISION REVISED: N=128 with the same bucketing (projected avg about 123 KB, max about 210 KB), because agile.spi grows about 1 MB a day and N=64 would pass the 5 s cold write within weeks. FINAL LAYOUT: Task, Planning, Parent and Asked rows in shard FNV-1a(item ID) mod 128; notes in FNV-1a(item ID + : + floor(n / 32)) mod 128; unions in agile_types; agile.spi lists the shards. compiler re-runs the cold all-shard check at 128.")
inl kit_agile_federation () = Task ("KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", "Two agile monoliths, home (box hub) and work (WORK-HUB hub): per-item authority network, read replicas over iggy, notes merge as a set, sequenced ops route to the authority, typed refusal during partitions", Story, P0, D6, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-AGILE-HUB,KIT-NET-SIM,LIB-IGGY-CLIENT", "kit,agile,federation,iggy,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_agile_federation () = Planning ("KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", Minutes 330u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_agile_federation_1 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). DoD: every item has one authority network (all existing items: home); each hub keeps a read replica of the other network items fed by its op stream over iggy (topic agile.<network>); notes append anywhere and merge as a set; field edits, status, claims and renames route to the authority hub, which sequences them; during a partition foreign claims are refused with a typed reason and notes wait in an outbox; moving an item between networks is one Transfer op both hubs accept; authority never fails over on its own (no split brain, the Nomad authoritative-region lesson). FIX FIRST (a finding from the model): notes are numbered n<k> per item, so two networks appending at once both write n13. Note identity becomes (origin network, origin seq), the displayed number is assigned by the authority on accept, and today ids map 1:1 to (home, k). MILESTONES: M1 note identity + stable display numbers 90 m; M2 routing + replica + outbox inside KIT-NET-SIM 90 m; M3 two real hubs at home (box root + a second root on the node as the work stand-in) over the public iggy with a forced partition 90 m; M4 WORK-HUB hub live 60 m. Estimate 330 m. NEXT STEP: M1 on a copy of the backlog, proving every existing note keeps its displayed number. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: authority as a phantom on item handles (item<Home>, item<Work>), so a Sequenced op on a foreign item typechecks only through route_to_authority; a claim needs an authority_witness only the authority hub mints; op ids nominal (network_id, u64).")
inl note_of_kit_agile_federation_2 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", 2u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "main", "transport proven 11-06: WORK-HUB (work hub) can now read AND write the box (home hub) fleet backlog over the SP reverse tunnel with its mesh key - kit runs on the box, ssh is just transport; lease persists on disk across separate ssh calls so separate sessions/machines coordinate via the lease singleton. Still TODO: the per-item authority network (who owns which item across the two monoliths) - this only proved the link + shared-root single-writer model.")
inl note_of_kit_agile_federation_3 () = Note ("KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", 3u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: design constraint from resilience (4-hop ssh measured): cross-network operations are async writes into a mailbox (the iggy stream, or a dropped file as the fallback), never live reads across the chain. This matches the outbox in M2; reads are always served by the local hub from its replica plus the authority acks.")
inl kit_resource_admission () = Task ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", "Jobs admitted by resource need, not count: p95 peak memory, cores and p90 wall learned per job kind from kit archive evidence; admit only within free minus reserve; enforce with job objects (Windows) and systemd scopes (Linux)", Story, P0, D4, 0u32, Planned, "DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", "kit,jobs,resources,remote,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_resource_admission () = Planning ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", Minutes 270u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_1 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). Generalizes MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS (counts) to resources. WHY: the 11-05 europa rule (MemAvailable at least cap + 600 MB AND load under 4, systemd scope MemoryMax, MemorySwapMax=0) lives in a memory file and a hand-written sh wrapper; three lanes broke it on 11-05 (two thrashes, then kit itself at 17:52 with 1700M on 2 GB free). A guest on another network machine cannot be allowed that. DoD: every job kind has a need (p95 peak RSS, cores, p90 wall) estimated from the kit archive job evidence; the job host admits only when need fits free minus reserve and enforces it (Windows job object memory limit, Linux systemd scope); replays of the three 11-05 europa thrashes, x 7.7x slowdown and the 51 GB x_compat are each refused in tests. MILESTONES: M1 need model + estimator from archive rows 90 m; M2 admission + incident replay tests 90 m; M3 enforcement per OS in the job host 90 m. Estimate 270 m. NEXT STEP: count which job records in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive carry peak memory today; if none, the job host starts sampling it (M1 needs that). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: megabytes and cores as nominal units with checked arithmetic; need record with provenance Measured of sample_count | Declared | Default, and a Default need is admitted only on an idle machine; enforcement GADT per OS (Windows job object | Linux scope) selected by the machine typestate.")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_2 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 2u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: inputs from x-dumps and x. A below-normal-priority task with a fixed wall-clock cap gets killed every run on a 100 pct CPU box (task result 267014), so a wall estimate is valid only at the load it was measured under: the estimator keys p90 wall by job kind AND load band, and admission never pairs a low priority with a tight cap. Cells cells default to a 300 s budget, so the learned wall feeds the --timeout directive instead of the default.")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_3 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 3u32, "2026-11-06T10:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:1x kit groom 2, star exchange consolidation: tibia: a quiet-wait that gives up after 900 s and runs anyway wasted a node sweep (9 of 12 rounds at 52-100 pct load, wall cap hit). Admission rule: when the machine is not calm, SKIP or requeue the round, never run it loaded, and size the job timeout as rounds x (run + max wait). x: a timeout above the 40 pct load bar is VOID until rerun.")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_4 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 4u32, "2026-11-06T10:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:5x kit: merged with resilience SMART-DELEGATION: the estimator half is DELEG-JOB-PROFILE (now kit-owned, a dep here); this item keeps admission and enforcement. Evidence from resilience: 647 of 672 jobs (96 pct) ran on the saturated box, the nodes about 4 pct; compiler and fiscal ran 0 pct on the node; the wifi node is an idle 16 GB twin at 19 pct CPU.")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_5 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 5u32, "2026-11-06T11:00Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:2x kit, estimator rules agreed with resilience. (a) Admit on the real peak (DELEG-JOB-PROFILE), never the nominal cap: fiscal gates peak p95 3.2 GB and max 5 GB against a 9000 MB cap, so they never go to europa (about 2.1 GB free) and go to a 16 GB host. (b) Missing peak has two meanings, typed rather than defaulted: on Windows it is Unmeasured (jobs from before the supervisor fix), assumed at or above the lane p95; on Linux it is ByDesign (no job object), so use the declared class ceiling (light Linux under 2.5 GB) plus the MemAvailable at least cap + 600 MB gate. (c) Later: Linux can measure too. The europa wrapper already runs jobs in a systemd scope, and cgroup v2 memory.peak gives that scope peak, so the Linux supervisor can record it the same way and (b) shrinks to Unmeasured only.")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_6 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 6u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries PROC-7 (Supervision cannot be starved); HEALTH-2 (Effective capacity and worker attribution). Why: 10-24 target KIT-NEXT-FITS-NOW is Done; the remaining caps/effective-capacity/reserve asks are this open P0 (admit by learned memory/cores within free minus reserve, job objects/systemd scopes)")
inl note_of_kit_resource_admission_7 () = Note ("KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", 7u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. PROC-7 · Supervision cannot be starved · P0 · ota, erp, sbin · Open / Two cold Cargo builds saturated the machine until `eoie status` could not even be scheduled. Reserve capacity for the control path (lower child priority, per-lane job/thread budgets such as `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` and `RAYON_NUM_THREADS`), admit new heavy children only under process/CPU/memory/load caps (compiling 151 C++ units in parallel exhausted memory and reset the runtime; after a timeout, a native build should retry at a lower job cap), size heavyweight-child width separately from CPU width (2 MSBuild workers were stable where 4 timed out, while 56 Rayon threads were fine), use non-blocking wait authority, and keep a stop path that is not queued behind saturated workers. Honor the requested timeout precisely (a 24 s capture returned after 30 s). || HEALTH-2 · Effective capacity and worker attribution · P1 · node, ota, ge2e · Open / Derive worker counts from actual capacity (cgroup quota, affinity, free CPUs), record configured vs effective concurrency, include stack and worker budgets in parallel-run receipts (to tell timeout, OOM and stack overflow apart), and attribute a parallel worker's panic to its work item. Record the execution environment at lease start (visible CPUs, mounts, capabilities), since 'the same container' sometimes changed underneath a session.")
inl kit_net_harvest () = Task ("KIT-NET-HARVEST", "Idle-hours guests on the work network: admit home jobs 21:00-06:00 only when the work machine runs no owner-agent job, they fit headroom and policy and end before 06:00; vacate within 60 s when an owner job arrives", Story, P0, D5, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY,KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION,REMOTE-EXEC-S5", "kit,remote,federation,scheduling,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_harvest () = Planning ("KIT-NET-HARVEST", Minutes 270u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_harvest_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-HARVEST", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). User 11-05: lanes use work-network resources 21:00-06:00, only when its machines run no local jobs managed by their own agents. DoD: the work hub admits a home job onto a work machine iff (a) its local time is inside the harvest window; (b) its kit job host reports no running or queued local job, with a fresh heartbeat (stale = busy, fail closed: the HEARTBEAT-STALENESS-GUARD lesson); (c) the job need fits free minus reserve (KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION); (d) the slice passes KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY; (e) the job p90 wall from history ends before 06:00 (the Slurm backfill rule). When an owner job is queued the guest is vacated (stopped, requeued at home) within 60 s, and stragglers at 06:00. The machine side decides admission (HTCondor START/VACATE); the home side only requests. MILESTONES: M1 admission predicate + vacate state machine + tests 90 m; M2 P1/P2 hold over 30 seeded nights in KIT-NET-SIM 90 m; M3 one real night with the node as a work stand-in and a synthetic owner job at 02:00 90 m. Estimate 270 m. NEXT STEP: admission as a pure function over (window, owner_state, headroom, policy verdict, p90) with its refusal union. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: guest_kind closed union Restartable | Checkpointed | Pinned (no constructor makes a Pinned guest); refusal union OutsideWindow | OwnerBusy | OwnerUnknown | NoHeadroom | Crossing of reason | WouldOverrun; guest typestate Requested, Admitted, Running, then Finished or Vacated, so a Vacated guest cannot be marked Finished.")
inl kit_net_link () = Task ("KIT-NET-LINK", "Cross-network links as supervised kit records: the box/wifi-node reverse tunnel through SP and the iggy bus get health probes, restart with backoff and a drop log; the WORK-HUB key is restricted to a forced kit command by default", Story, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY,KIT-REMOTE-NO-ROUTE-EXIT", "kit,remote,federation,network,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_link () = Planning ("KIT-NET-LINK", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_link_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). NOW (resilience, 11-05 22:4x, user ask): the box runs sshd (scoop OpenSSH 10.4, key-only, auto-start) and a reverse tunnel from the box to SP (SP:2246 to the box, SP:2247 to the wifi node). It is a nohup ssh from a Git Bash shell (pid 19928) that dies with that shell or on reboot, unlike machine 244 systemd tunnel. The WORK-HUB key sits in administrators_authorized_keys, so it gets a full admin shell, not only kit agile. DoD: links are kit records (KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY) with a health probe; the job host supervises each one (restart with backoff, every drop recorded with time and cause); iggy on europa is the bus both hubs dial out to and ssh through SP is the control fallback; the WORK-HUB key is restricted to a forced kit command unless the user wants a shell (default restricted, decision record). MILESTONES: M1 link record + probe 60 m; M2 supervised restart + drop log 90 m. Estimate 150 m. NEXT STEP: get the tunnel command line from resilience (its owner today) and wrap it as a supervised kit job. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: link_state typestate Down, Connecting, Up of since, back to Down of cause; drop cause closed union; key scope union ForcedCommand of verb | Shell.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_2 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 2u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "main", "built+verified 11-06: box reverse-tunnel keeper live - SYSTEM scheduled task box-revtunnel runs a reconnect loop (AtStartup, RestartCount=999, ServerAliveInterval=15), exposes box:22->SP:2246 and wifi:22->SP:2247; survives reboot. work-hub reaches the box with its mesh key and drove kit agile list on THIS backlog end-to-end. GOTCHA: OpenSSH as SYSTEM rejects a key readable beyond SYSTEM/Admins (bad permissions) - needs a locked copy in C:\\ProgramData\\ssh. Next step for this item: replace the scheduled-task keeper with a supervised kit record (kit job --on / a kit-owned keeper) so the link is a typed record, not an OS task.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_3 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 3u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: resilience and x-dumps lessons folded into the DoD. (1) An ssh client run as SYSTEM refuses a key whose ACL is readable beyond SYSTEM and Administrators, so the link service carries its own locked key copy. (2) Never restart the sshd of a host reachable only through it; append keys and recheck Running. (3) Through a ProxyJump chain only the origin keys authenticate the last hop; a nested ssh on an intermediate uses that host keys. (4) Long chains carry small async writes reliably and time out on data-returning reads. (5) The link probe types three states: Refused (host or sshd down), ConnectedNoBanner (the remote VM is starved; cap its load) and Ok, since the fixes are opposite. (6) ED25519 TLS fails under Windows Schannel while the server is healthy, so iggy probes use rustls.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_4 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 4u32, "2026-11-06T08:25Z", "resilience", "received from kit (star exchange): a remote key that opens a FULL admin shell (mesh key -> SP -> box) turns every bug into an admin action; make it a forced-command + supervised service, not a nohup. Partly done - the box tunnel is now a supervised SYSTEM scheduled task (not a nohup). STILL TODO for this item: (a) restrict the mesh/authorized_keys entries with command=\"<tunnel-only>\" ,no-pty,no-port-forwarding-except-needed; (b) the planned kit-owned keeper record supersedes the scheduled task. Folds with the supervised-kit-record next step above.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_5 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 5u32, "2026-11-06T08:56Z", "resilience", "work-hub relay-lab registration DONE 11-06: connect.ps1 ($Nodes box=2246/wifi=2247 + ReverseTunnelPorts), mbox-core.ps1 (box,wifi are valid mailbox nodes), README.md (Nodes & access). Edits done in-place on work-hub over the box->SP->244->work-hub chain, each parse-checked with a timestamped .bak. 244/245 anchor configs left untouched (box/wifi anchor from the box side). So work-hub connect.ps1 box -Mode reverse now builds the right ssh line.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_6 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 6u32, "2026-11-06T10:14Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:1x kit groom 2, star exchange consolidation: more probe states and recovery, from main, resilience and tibia. (1) Host key verification failed means sshd ANSWERED, so classify it as reachable (known_hosts is keyed by alias, so probe through the alias). (2) Name the failed layer: ping vs port 22 vs banner. (3) Out-of-band recovery with the node admin credential works: net use to IPC$ then sc.exe start sshd over tailscale SMB (resilience); without the credential an open 445 is not a management path. (4) A laptop node needs lid action Do nothing and a persistent virtual display. (5) Multi-hop ssh: carry the key with -i through -J (no agent), clean up in an EXIT trap, bound with ConnectTimeout instead of an external timeout, or the ProxyJump helpers orphan. (6) Probe with a known-output command (ssh <alias> hostname) before trusting an empty result; nested pwsh quoting returns nothing silently (tibia).")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_7 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK", 7u32, "2026-11-06T10:14Z", "resilience", "link is LIVE and in daily use (box:22->SP:2246 via SYSTEM keeper, wifi:22->SP:2247, work-hub reaches the fleet backlog end-to-end). Remaining to meet the stated DoD \"supervised kit records\": wrap the keeper as a kit-owned record instead of a Windows scheduled task - that part is the only thing between here and Done; owner resilience+kit.")
inl kit_net_crossing_policy () = Task ("KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY", "Typed crossing policy between networks: only public-repo slices and their outputs cross; private data only encrypted with origin-network keys; secrets never; guest manifests checked before dispatch", Story, P1, D3, 30u32, Active, "", "kit,remote,federation,security,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_crossing_policy () = Planning ("KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_crossing_policy_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). WHY: work machines belong to the work network and its rules; what home sends there (and back) is decided by a typed policy, not by whichever lane dispatches. DoD: each path class is PublicRepo (the AGPL i574n repos), PrivateData (x-dumps data, profile, transcripts, memory) or Secret (keys, tokens, credentials by name); by default only PublicRepo slices and their job outputs cross; PrivateData crosses only encrypted with keys that stay on the origin network (backups); Secret never crosses; a guest slice manifest is checked before dispatch and refused naming the offending path. USER DECISION (kind=ByDefault): what the employer allows on the work machines is the user call; until the user says otherwise the default above holds and nothing private crosses. MILESTONES: M1 policy model + manifest check tests 90 m; M2 wired into dispatch refusal 60 m. Estimate 150 m. NEXT STEP: classify today trees with one census over the repos and the LOCALAPPDATA kit dirs. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: path_class closed union; verdict GADT Allowed | AllowedEncrypted of key_ref | Refused of path * reason; key_ref nominal carrying its origin network phantom, so a work key can never encrypt home data by mistake.")
inl note_of_kit_net_crossing_policy_2 () = Note ("KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY", 2u32, "2026-11-09T05:31Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:1x rig MODEL WRITTEN (live tree, unstaged): new package src/rig_net_crossing (crossing.spi, package.spiproj with spiral-, standards.spi HostFree + LibReferenced), deliberately NOT a Cargo workspace member yet, so TRAIN42's crate set is unchanged; membership plus Cargo.lock refresh land with TRAIN43. Types: path_class GADT (PublicRepo | PrivateData | Secret); class_rule record { prefix; class } supplied by the caller (kit names no repo, path or host; the rules file lives on the origin network); crossing GADT (Crosses | CrossesEncrypted | Refused of string). class_of: every matching prefix applies and the STRICTEST wins (a key under a public tree stays Secret); an unmatched path is PrivateData (safe default). crossing_of: public crosses, private only encrypted, secret never. manifest_verdict folds a slice and the first refusal names its offending path. One test block, 9 expectations (strictest rule, private default, encrypted private, named refusal, secret refused even encrypted, empty slice). PENDING: moon notebook probe (moon at 100 pct CPU now; batched with the rig_job_dispatch rerun). Next step after green: the rules reader (one rules file per network) and the dispatch hook that checks a guest slice before kit job start --on to a work-network machine. DoD per the 11-05 note; user default stands: only public-repo slices cross until the user says otherwise.")
inl kit_net_backup_share () = Task ("KIT-NET-BACKUP-SHARE", "Each network keeps an encrypted copy of the other network hardest-to-recover data (kit archive, memory, drafts, agile checkpoints); keys never leave the origin; monthly cross-network restore drill", Story, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY", "kit,backup,federation,remote,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_backup_share () = Planning ("KIT-NET-BACKUP-SHARE", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_backup_share_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-BACKUP-SHARE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). User 11-05: the networks share backups. Builds on resilience CLOUD-NODE-BACKUP (decision: the decrypt key on both private machines, monthly drill), KIT-ARCHIVE-OFFBOX-MIRROR and PROFILE-RESTORE-DRILL-PORT. DoD: each network holds an encrypted copy of the other hardest-to-recover data (kit archive, memory, unstaged drafts, agile checkpoints), pushed in the harvest window over the bus; keys never leave the origin network; a monthly drill restores from the far copy and compares hashes. MILESTONES: M1 encrypted bundle + restore test on a copy 90 m; M2 cross-network push + drill 90 m. Estimate 180 m. NEXT STEP: reuse the deterministic bundle (KIT-REMOTE-SYNC) with an encryption stage; restore-test it on the box. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: backup generation nominal (network, date); encrypted payload parameterized by key origin, so a restore needs that network key; drill result union Matched | Mismatched of list path | Unreadable.")
inl kit_net_heal_exchange () = Task ("KIT-NET-HEAL-EXCHANGE", "Heals and heavy proofs run as harvest guests and return as manifest-proven patch bundles applied unstaged on the box; each hub watches the other heartbeat and delivers typed alerts without taking authority", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-REMOTE-SYNC,KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION", "kit,heal,federation,remote,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_heal_exchange () = Planning ("KIT-NET-HEAL-EXCHANGE", Minutes 150u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_heal_exchange_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-HEAL-EXCHANGE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). User 11-05: the networks share heals. DoD: heavy heals and proofs (GRANULAR-HEAL class, lib gates, notebook batches) run as harvest guests; results come back as a typed patch bundle with a manifest proof (KIT-REMOTE-SYNC) and are applied on the box only as unstaged changes after the receiving hub gates (git stays the user own on the box); each hub watches the other heartbeat and posts a typed alert through a verified delivery path (the alarm-detects-but-never-delivers lesson), never taking over authority. MILESTONES: M1 patch bundle round trip with manifest proof 90 m; M2 cross-hub watch + delivery probe 60 m. Estimate 150 m. NEXT STEP: once KIT-REMOTE-SYNC M4 lands, a heal computed on the node returns as a bundle and applies unstaged. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: patch bundle typestate Computed, Proven of manifest, Applied, so an unproven bundle has no apply; alert delivery as a typed receipt.")
inl kit_backend_deliberation () = Task ("KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION", "kit backend deliberate <component>: BACKEND-BALANCE rules as typed predicates over measured facts decide Gleam, Rust or a split per component, write a decision record and re-run when facts change", Story, P0, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,backends,gleam,rust,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_backend_deliberation () = Planning ("KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_backend_deliberation_1 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). Makes EOIE-BACKEND-MAP and BACKEND-BALANCE decidable by kit (user 11-05: autonomous, deterministic deliberation of where each piece of code goes, Gleam or Rust). DoD: the BACKEND-BALANCE rules (R1-R3 favor Rust: per-command latency, bulk scans, native libs; G1-G5 favor Gleam: supervision, many long-lived concurrent actors, timeouts and monitors, hot reload, distribution) are typed predicates over measured facts per component (cold start vs budget, concurrency, lifetime, supervision need, peak memory; rows from BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL); kit backend deliberate <component> prints a decision record (rule, evidence rows, verdict, re-check trigger) and re-runs when a fact changes; it reproduces the manual classification in EOIE-BACKEND-MAP n1 (CLI verbs Rust; job host, windows, machine watch Gleam). First live verdicts: the agile hub and the federation coordinator. MILESTONES: M1 predicates + fact rows + reproduction test 90 m; M2 verb + records for the hub and the coordinator 90 m. Estimate 180 m. NEXT STEP: encode R1-R3 and G1-G5 from the BACKEND-BALANCE notes as a closed rule union. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: rule closed union; fact rows typed by unit (ms, MB, count); verdict GADT indexed by backend (Rust | Gleam | Split of data_plane * control_plane) carrying its evidence; re-check trigger as a typed fact delta.")
inl note_of_kit_backend_deliberation_2 () = Note ("KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION", 2u32, "2026-11-09T05:56Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:4x rig input check before code: BACKEND-METRICS-MODEL is Done (the typed metric model), but the facts this item decides over are per COMPONENT (cold start vs budget, concurrency, lifetime, supervision need, peak memory) and no such rows exist yet; what exists tonight is per JOB: rig_job_duration_ms and rig_job_peak_bytes per job (KIT-EXEC-METRICS-M1, in TRAIN42) and a seed duration profile per job stem and host (DELEG-JOB-PROFILE n8, which showed up to 15x spread with host load). Writing R1-R3 / G1-G5 predicates now would decide over invented numbers. Held on KIT-EXEC-METRICS (the parent; M2-M4 add more machines, the self-check and the dotnet hold bench). When facts arrive, slice 1 is a pure model: component_facts record, rule union R1 PerCommandLatency | R2 BulkScan | R3 NativeLib | G1 Supervision | G2 ManyActors | G3 TimeoutsMonitors | G4 HotReload | G5 Distribution, each a predicate over the facts, and a verdict union (Gleam | Rust | Split of string) with the fired rules as the decision record, tested on 3 fixture components.")
inl kit_net_link_bench () = Task ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", "Measure the cross-network path now: latency, throughput and drops over the SP reverse tunnel and the public iggy TLS bus, plus an overnight drop log; numbers become the link model of KIT-NET-SIM", TaskKind, P1, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "kit,remote,federation,bench,iggy,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_net_link_bench () = Planning ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_1 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:07Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:0x kit (user 11-05 22:5x, kit-two-hubs groom). Child of KIT-TWO-HUBS (parent named here; deps are only real ordering). CPU-light, can start tonight: the SP reverse tunnel to the box and the wifi node is up since 22:46 and the public iggy on europa answers TLS on 8090/3000 (x-dumps, 11-05). DoD: measured latency (p50/p99), throughput and drop count for box to SP tunnel ssh and box to europa iggy TLS (1k small messages, one 10 MB and one 1 GB bundle; europa transfers past 2.5 GB truncate, so cap at 1 GB), plus an overnight drop log of the SP route; the numbers become the KIT-NET-SIM link parameters and the bundle chunk size. MILESTONES: M1 one-shot bench 60 m; M2 overnight drop log 30 m. Estimate 90 m. NEXT STEP: a scratch bench driving ssh and the xarchive events.rs iggy client (rustls; box Schannel rejects the ED25519 CA, IGGY-PUBLIC-CLOUD). TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: link_sample record (link_id nominal, at, rtt_ms, bytes, outcome Ok | Dropped of cause) shared with KIT-NET-LINK.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_2 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-11-06T08:19Z", "main", "now measurable (11-06): the SP reverse-tunnel link box<->wifi<->work-hub is live. Next step: a one-shot latency+throughput probe over SP:2246 (ssh round-trip ms, scp of a 10MB blob for MB/s, count drops over 5 min) recorded as a note here; cheap, no compile. Caveat: the wifi half (2247) is down until the wifi node sshd is restarted, so bench the box half (2246) first.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_3 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-11-06T08:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 04:2x kit, star exchange consolidation: bench hygiene from tibia and x: gate each sample on exit code and output parity BEFORE timing it (a crash that dropped stdout once timed as 3x faster); record load before each round and keep only rows under 40 pct load; run any A/B inside one job before blaming a change.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_4 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-11-09T06:01Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:0x local rig, first numbers (cheap, no compile): box -> SP (pluto) full ssh connect plus true, 5 samples: 2580, 1633, 1710, 1161, 1085 ms (median 1633, min 1085), i.e. several round trips of the ~185 ms box-SP path per handshake; a persistent connection (ControlMaster, unavailable on Windows OpenSSH) or one long-lived session would cut most of it. The four reverse-tunnel listeners are UP on pluto (127.0.0.1:2244, 2245, 2246, 2247). NOT measured, on purpose: (1) throughput to pluto, because pluto is production RustDesk and a 10 MB burst could degrade a live session (never-degrade rule); measure it against a non-production endpoint instead; (2) the hop through 2246 to the work network, because the box has no work-network ssh aliases (its config holds moon, europa, pluto, ganymede only) and crossing needs credentials held for that side (KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY); that half waits for an alias the user or org defines, inside the 21:00-06:00 night window. Next step: a throughput probe box -> ganymede (non-production, same internet path class) and the overnight drop log as a 5-minute ssh true loop to pluto at low rate.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_5 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 5u32, "2026-11-09T06:02Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:0x local rig, throughput on a non-production endpoint: box -> ganymede ssh connect plus true 3048, 2701, 2085, 2389, 2554 ms (median 2.5 s; ganymede at load 9-10 tonight, so part of this is its sshd under load); scp of a 10 MiB random blob: upload 7.8 s = 1.28 MiB/s, download 12.3 s = 0.81 MiB/s, sha256 identical round trip. CONSEQUENCES for the link model: (1) per-ssh-call setup of 1.6-2.5 s dominates small remote operations (kit job list --on and every forwarded verb pay it), so batching or one long-lived session matters more than bandwidth; (2) at about 1 MiB/s, a 67 MB stripped kit binary takes about a minute and a 424 MB debug binary about 6 minutes, which is why stripping before copying mattered tonight; (3) uploads beat downloads here, so fetch-backs of big logs should be compressed. Left: the overnight drop log (a slow ssh true loop to pluto) and the work-network half (needs an alias). Rows for KIT-NET-SIM: { link = box-SP; connect_ms_median = 1633 } and { link = box-ganymede; connect_ms_median = 2554; up_mib_s = 1.28; down_mib_s = 0.81 }.")
inl note_of_kit_net_link_bench_6 () = Note ("KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", 6u32, "2026-11-09T06:31Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 04:1x local rig DROP LOG box -> SP (pluto), 15 min, one ssh connect plus true every 15 s (latency only; RustDesk untouched): 52 samples, 0 failed; connect ms median 1456, p90 3789, max 11871 (raw rows: LOCALAPPDATA kit reports link-drop-pluto-20261109.tsv). FINDING: no drops at night, but the TAIL matters more than the median: 10 pct of connects take over 3.8 s and the worst took 11.9 s, longer than kit's old hard-coded ConnectTimeout=10, so a healthy link can still fail a kit --on call on timeout alone; the per-machine .connect file (KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR item 7, in TRAIN42) should default above the observed p99 for each link, not to 10. Link-model rows for KIT-NET-SIM: { link = box-SP; connect_ms_median = 1456; connect_ms_p90 = 3789; connect_ms_max = 11871; drop_rate = 0 of 52 }. Left: the work-network half (needs an alias) and a daytime drop log for comparison.")
inl kit_two_hubs () = Task ("KIT-TWO-HUBS", "kit across two networks: home (box hub) and work (WORK-HUB hub) each keep one agile monolith; hubs federate items, jobs, backups and heals over iggy; lanes borrow idle work-network capacity 21:00-06:00 only when its own agents run nothing (user 11-05)", Epic, P0, D8, 0u32, Planned, "KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY,KIT-NET-SIM,KIT-AGILE-HUB,KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION,KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION,KIT-NET-HARVEST,KIT-NET-LINK,KIT-NET-CROSSING-POLICY,KIT-NET-BACKUP-SHARE,KIT-NET-HEAL-EXCHANGE,KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION,KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH", "kit,remote,federation,iggy,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_two_hubs () = Planning ("KIT-TWO-HUBS", Minutes 2520u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_two_hubs_1 () = Note ("KIT-TWO-HUBS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-05 23:1x kit, filed from the user (22:5x): kit leans on iggy more and more; where code goes between Gleam and Rust is deliberated autonomously; to scale we keep TWO agile monoliths, one per network, each run by a hub: home (the box) and work (WORK-HUB); lanes borrow work-network machines in idle hours (21:00-06:00) only when that network own agents run no local jobs; the networks share backups and heals. GOAL: when the user starts using the work link, the hard parts are already built and proven. SHAPE: per network one hub = one agile authority + one job-host view + one link to the bus. Items have an authority network; writes to an item are sequenced by its authority hub (single writer); notes are commutative appends accepted anywhere. Hubs exchange op streams over iggy (both dial out to the public iggy on europa; ssh through SP stays as control fallback). Work machines admit home jobs as guests under the work hub policy (window, owner idle, headroom, crossing policy, fits before 06:00) and vacate them when an owner job arrives. CRITICAL PATH: note segments on (EOIE-AGILE-EVENT-LOG), then KIT-AGILE-HUB, then KIT-AGILE-FEDERATION; KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY, then KIT-NET-SIM, then KIT-NET-HARVEST; KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION and KIT-BACKEND-DELIBERATION in parallel; KIT-NET-LINK-BENCH can start now (the SP tunnel to the box and wifi node is up since 22:46). Ready with no deps tonight: TOPOLOGY, RESOURCE-ADMISSION, CROSSING-POLICY, BACKEND-DELIBERATION, LINK-BENCH. Everything before the live WORK-HUB milestones is provable at home: the node stands in for a work machine and a second backlog root for the work hub. Intra-network execution stays EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR (S1-S12); this epic is the cross-network layer over it. Estimate 2520 m = sum of the 12 children milestone estimates (estimates-from-rules b); deps list the children so the epic closes after them.")
inl note_of_kit_two_hubs_2 () = Note ("KIT-TWO-HUBS", 2u32, "2026-11-06T03:08Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "PRIOR ART: lessons kept, not dismissed. ANTHROPIC SCION (CI-OFFLOAD n1, EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR n9): (1) server-authoritative history, so each hub is THE authority for its items and lanes ask the hub instead of each parsing 14.9 MB (today list 1.3 s, wip 152 s, note 1.5-4.6 s, rename 398 s CPU and never landed); Scion reported status 207x and pull 25x from that move. (2) Clients fetch only the files they need, so guests ship the package-graph slice their crates reach (EOIE-AFFECTED-CRATES + KIT-REMOTE-SYNC manifests), never whole mirrors (MIRROR-TRANSFER-COST: 800 MB). (3) It was built when agent merges grew 12x, so the trigger is measured: KIT-SCALE 20/50 writers is ours. ZED DELTADB (EOIE-MEMORY n1): checkpoints plus incremental changes carrying the conversation behind each one, so agile.spi stays the checkpoint, ops are the deltas, and each op carries lane, session id and transcript offset, letting a decision trace to the conversation that made it. HTCONDOR (cycle scavenging since 1988): the machine owner policy decides START, SUSPEND and VACATE on the machine side; matchmaking is two-sided (ClassAds); guests are restartable or checkpointed. So the work hub owns admission and jobs declare Restartable | Checkpointed | Pinned. SLURM BACKFILL: start a job only if its estimated duration fits the gap, so no guest starts unless its p90 wall ends before 06:00. NOMAD MULTI-REGION: one authoritative region per object, replicas elsewhere, hence per-item authority and no automatic failover. FOSSIL: content-addressed artifacts merge by set union, hence notes as a set keyed (origin network, origin seq). SCION, the ETH path-aware internet architecture (same name, different system): isolation domains with their own trust roots joined only through core links, which is our two-network shape (KIT-NET-TOPOLOGY). NOT NOVEL: idle-cycle harvesting, event logs, authority routing, set CRDTs, encrypted off-site backup. NOVEL, the combination (each piece is modest): (a) one Spiral type model compiled to Rust (data plane) and Gleam (control plane) with byte-identical codecs, so a lease or job transition that is illegal in the type is illegal on every machine and backend; (b) the owner whose idleness gates harvesting is an AI agent team whose activity is a typed job-host state, not a keyboard-idle guess, so preemption is exact; (c) the coordination state of agent teams (tasks, claims, decisions, memory) as compiler-checked records federated across networks with per-change provenance (DeltaDB keeps conversations but untyped; ai-memory keeps text); (d) placement and policy decisions made by pre-registered typed rules over measured facts (decision records v2) and re-run when facts change, so the system deliberates instead of asking.")
