inl fork_posts_blog_render () = Task ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", "The upstream fork's CI (i574n/The-Spiral-Language gh-pages) renders each post through blog's engine into a real article page (like blog's dist/articles, with its style, TOC and RSS) and links it from the site root; today scripts/post.ps1 only runs the post's tests as a Cells notebook and publish.ps1 publishes the .spi.html notebook view, so readers get a code notebook, not the post", Story, P1, D3, 90u32, Blocked, "UPSTREAM-FORK-PAGES", "upstream,fork,blog,ci,pages,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fork_posts_blog_render () = Planning ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fork_posts_blog_render_1 () = Note ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:45Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:5x (user: make the compiler upstream's CI also render the blog posts properly). Checked: UPSTREAM-FORK-PAGES (Done) only promised posts published into a tree site; POST-1-IN-FORK made the post compile against blog/engine; no item runs blog's generator in the fork. post.ps1 already builds a blog overlay per post at target/post/<name>/blog (engine, posts, app + the post, package.spiproj rewritten), so the missing step is running blog's generate (scripts/generate.ps1: BuildSpiral engine/generate.spi -> blog-generate) on that overlay and copying its output (index.html, articles/, style.css, rss.xml) into dist/posts/<name>/ before publish. DoD: the fork's next CI run serves posts/<name>/ as a blog article for both posts (upstream_consolidation, c_self_tail_loop), proven on a codespace first.")
inl note_of_fork_posts_blog_render_2 () = Note ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", 2u32, "2026-11-06T11:35Z", "main", "main 11-06 08:3x IN THE TREE (fork, unstaged), proof PENDING on a codespace. scripts/post.ps1: the overlay now also copies blog's generator/, and overwrites the overlay's app/generate.spi to render [ <post>.entry () ] (both fork posts define inl entry () : page.entry). After the notebook gate it runs BuildSpiral on the generator, then cargo build --release (CARGO_TARGET_DIR target/post/cargo, shared by the posts), runs blog-generate in the overlay, and fails if dist is empty. scripts/publish.ps1: after publish-tree, copies each target/post/<name>/blog/dist into dist/posts/<name>/article. target/ is gitignored, so no untracked debris in the fork. Both scripts parse with 0 errors. Proof job: codespace on i574n/The-Spiral-Language master + these 2 files, running workflow.ps1 then publish.ps1 and counting dist/posts/*/article files. Known gap: blog's rss.spi base_url is hard-coded to https://i574n.github.io/blog, so the fork article's feed links point at the blog site; the pages themselves are fine.")
inl note_of_fork_posts_blog_render_3 () = Note ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:38Z", "main", "DoD: the fork's CI publishes posts/<name>/article/ as a blog-engine article for both posts (upstream_consolidation, c_self_tail_loop), proven on a codespace with workflow.ps1 + publish.ps1 before the user's push, then live after it. Next step: read the codespace proof (fork-cs drive.log); if green, add an article link per post to the fork's readme.")
inl note_of_fork_posts_blog_render_4 () = Note ("FORK-POSTS-BLOG-RENDER", 4u32, "2026-11-06T12:18Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:5x PROVEN on a codespace (i574n/The-Spiral-Language master + the 2 fork scripts + blog at HEAD with engine/index.spi and engine/site.spi overlaid): workflow exit 0, publish exit 0, both posts gated (tests pass) and rendered: dist/posts/<name>/article/ holds 6 files each, with the post's own slug page (c-self-tail-loop/, upstream-consolidation/), and every link is rooted at /The-Spiral-Language/posts/<name>/article/ (style.css and home). Two bugs found by the first proof and fixed: (1) a shared CARGO_TARGET_DIR made the second post reuse the first post's generator binary (both articles showed c-self-tail-loop), now one target dir per post; (2) the blog engine emitted root-absolute links (href=\"/style.css\"), which also breaks the LIVE blog (https://i574n.github.io/style.css is 404, so i574n.github.io/blog/ renders unstyled), now site.files_at root url, with files = files_at \"/blog/\". Blocked on the user's push, in order: blog first (the engine change), then the fork (scripts/post.ps1, scripts/publish.ps1). Then check the fork's live posts/<name>/article/ and add an article link per post to the fork readme.")
inl html_readme_any_case () = Task ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", "html renders a root README whatever its case (README.md, readme.md, Readme.md): html/cli/html.spi:69 joins the literal \"README.md\", so on case-sensitive Linux CI the upstream fork's readme.md never renders on https://i574n.github.io/The-Spiral-Language/", Story, P1, D1, 95u32, Blocked, "-", "html,readme,pages,fork,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_html_readme_any_case () = Planning ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_html_readme_any_case_1 () = Note ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:45Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:5x (user: the readme didn't get rendered). Cause found: html/cli/html.spi:69 := path = dir </> \"README.md\"; the fork's file is readme.md (lowercase). On Windows the lookup is case-insensitive, so local runs render it, and only Linux CI misses it. The live fork index has the .readme CSS but 0 class=\"readme\" sections, while spiral's index has 1. DoD: pick the root file whose lowercased name is readme.md (prefer the exact README.md when both exist), with a test row for readme.md on Linux, and the fork's next CI run shows the readme below the tree.")
inl note_of_html_readme_any_case_2 () = Note ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", 2u32, "2026-11-06T11:27Z", "main", "main 11-06 08:0x DONE IN THE TREE, unstaged; blocked on a compiler regression before proof. (1) Fix: README lookup prefers the exact README.md and else takes the first name whose lowercase is readme.md (html_walk/walk.spi readme_in/readme_of). (2) cli/html.rs was already at 990 lines of 1000 under the current compiler, so the fix (+57) broke the 1000-line gate. Structural fix: a new linked unit html_walk (walk.spi + walk_api.spi + unit.spi, exports walked/readme/refusal/slurped) holds the listing, walk and README code. Now cli/html.rs = 764 lines and html_walk/walk.rs = 344, and the CLI calls all 4 exports through the link (grep html_walk:: = 7 sites). Wired into Cargo members, cli package.spiproj packages+link, cli/Cargo.toml, and build.ps1 units. Test: walk_api.spi rust test a_lowercase_readme_renders_and_a_missing_folder_is_refused (readme.md with CRLF -> (true, '# lower'); missing folder refused). (3) BLOCKED: cargo build on europa fails with E0308 at html_pages_viewer/html_pages_file, because the installed compiler (A1BF06EB9AD8, core A688EE5AD426) emits html_model's viewer union as a local US8. The same happens for UNCHANGED HEAD html.spi, so it's a compiler regression, sent to compiler with the repro. Resume = when compiler fixes or names the item: regenerate html_walk + cli, cargo build + --self-test on europa, a render of a lowercase-readme folder, then the cells gate for walk_api.spi.")
inl note_of_html_readme_any_case_3 () = Note ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", 3u32, "2026-11-06T11:38Z", "main", "main 11-06 08:4x Blocked on LINKED-UNION-EMITTED-LOCAL (compiler, P0, bisecting pack44..52 on a clean copy of html HEAD). Resume when it lands: regenerate html_walk + cli, cargo build + --self-test on europa, render a lowercase-readme folder, then the cells gate for walk_api.spi.")
inl note_of_html_readme_any_case_4 () = Note ("HTML-README-ANY-CASE", 4u32, "2026-11-06T11:51Z", "main", "main 11-06 09:0x PROVEN on Linux (europa, cargo +nightly-2026-10-18 release, exit 0): html --self-test exit 0; a folder holding only a lowercase readme.md plus sub/a.txt renders with 1 class=\"readme\" section containing its heading. CORRECTION to my 08:0x note: it was NOT a compiler regression. Compiler found a stale unit graph: the box's %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\units graphs for html_model/html_pages were from 11-03 (viewer with 4 cases), and I had rebuilt only cli, so the consumer's 6-case viewer matched no key and fell back to a local US<n>. Rebuilding all units in build.ps1 order (model, inline, table, parse, render, pages, walk, markdown, cli; 323 s on the box) gives viewer linked correctly. Generated sizes: cli 755, pages 939, parse 843, walk 344, all under 1000. Every unit .rs is regenerated by pack52 (unstaged). Blocked on the user's push of html; then html CI (both OSes) runs the cells gate including walk_api's rust test, and the fork's next CI shows the readme.")
inl kit_next_starvation () = Task ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", "kit agile next starves user-requested big items: WSJF divides by the estimate and counts only P0/P1 dependents, with no aging and no weight for an explicit user request, so a P1 480-min user ask (CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT, asked 11-01) ranked 67 of 122 and no lane picked it up in 5 days", Story, P1, D3, 70u32, Active, "", "kit,agile,next,ranking,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_next_starvation () = Planning ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", Minutes 135u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_1 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:45Z", "main", "main 11-06 06:5x diagnosis (user: weird that kit didn't bring the physics plot task up). CELLS-PLOT-EXPORT: P1 D5 480 min, wsjf=4000, rank 67/122 of team:main ready items, while 60-min P1 items score 25600. Its only dependent PHYSICS-OUT-OF-LIB is P2, which adds little to unblocks (64). Step 1 was done 11-01, but progress stayed 0% and the status Planned, so nothing marked it started. Groom rounds look only at Active and Blocked items, so a Planned user ask never surfaces. Proposal: (1) aging, where score rises with days since the user's request; (2) a typed user-request marker (date + quote) that floors the rank, e.g. into the top 10 of its team; (3) next prints items whose estimate is over 240 min with a split hint instead of burying them; (4) a groom check, Planned P0/P1 user asks untouched for 3+ days.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_2 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:57Z", "main", "main 11-06 07:0x BACKTEST (data: agile.spi notes + next for 8 teams; script in main's scratchpad starve.ps1). (A) The current formula, reverse-engineered and matching 208 items exactly: wsjf = (V(P) + 100*unblocks) * 60 / est, with V = P0 102400, P1 25600, x4 per level; difficulty and age are unused. (B) What it produced last week: 416 closes, 73% of them items created and closed within the 7 days; items over 240 min are 41% of the ready backlog but only 14% of closes (median 3.2 d to close vs 0.8 d for items of 60 min or less). P2 closed 107 against P0's 80. (C) Neglect: 113 ready P0/P1 non-Paused items idle 3+ days, 41 of them user asks; 16 sit in their team's top 10 (HOPACSOTA-P6 is compiler's #1, idle 5.3 d), so lanes also don't consult next. (D) Variants scored on the same population; percentiles, lower is better. V0 current: last week's closes median 45%, 99 of them in the top 20%; neglected user asks median 63%; big P0/P1 71%. V1 + aging (1 + min(idle,14)/7): 48% / 80 / 49% / 67%. V2 + aging + user ask x2: 50% / 84 / 32% / 61%. V3 est capped at 240: 55% / 71 / 19% / 38%. V4 est capped at 120: 60% / 55 / 28% / 29%. DECISION: adopt V2 in the score. It costs last week's winners little (top-20% count 99 -> 84) and halves the user-ask percentile. Fix big items by a REAL split, not a formula cap. V3/V4 pretend an item is small and push out the fast closes that drive throughput, while a split makes items truthfully small, so they score high under V0 anyway. Split rule: an item with est > 240 gets its DoD steps as child items of 120 min or less (deps chained, parent = rollup), and next prints 'split' for unsplit items over 240. User-ask marker: a typed field (date + quote), not a title regex (my backtest used the title regex and the user-batch tag, which is noisy). Regression guard: replay this backtest in kit's tests (fixture = this week's state), so a formula change must keep last week's closes in the top 20% at 80+ of 99 and lower the neglected-user-ask median.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_3 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 3u32, "2026-11-06T10:58Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:1x kit accepts main asks (1)-(4), in this order. (3) FIRST: a fixture of this week state plus the replay test (80+ of the 99 stay in the top 20 pct; the neglected user-ask median falls), so V2 lands against a guard. (1) V2 in agile_task_next/ranking.spi: backlog_task gains idle_days and a typed user_ask (date, quote) field; rank_key multiplies by (1 + min(idle,14)/7) and by 2 for a user ask; idle_days comes from the item last-note time already computed for list --json (KIT-AGILE-LIST-LAST-NOTE). The typed user ask is a Task schema field written by agile add/edit (--asked <date> --quote <text>), never a title regex; a one-time migration fills it from notes that quote the user, recorded in a review list rather than guessed. (4) The groom check reuses that field. (2) split: next prints split for unsplit items over 240 min; kit agile split <id> turns DoD milestones into chained child items of at most 120 min with the parent as rollup. Rides the train after TRAIN33 with --team exact, lease renewing default and peak sampling.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_4 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 4u32, "2026-11-06T12:48Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 08:5x kit: (3) REPLAY GUARD BUILT AND GREEN, (1) AGING WIRED (unstaged, rides the train after TRAIN33). FIXTURE: 649 rows derived from main 06:55 snapshot (starve.json): 316 items closed in the 7 days before it plus 333 ready open items, each with priority, minutes (estimate or the difficulty fallback), unblocks (recomputed on the pre-rename graph), idle days, user ask (main title-regex flag, fixture only) and closed. It lives inside agile_task_next/ranking.spi (a separate module hit the Cells sibling-mount trap), rows without IDs two per line, ranking.spi 760 lines. TEST a_ranking_change_keeps_last_weeks_closes_near_the_top_and_lifts_neglected_user_asks pins V0 (107 of last week closes in the top fifth, neglected user-ask median percentile 64) and requires any new formula to keep 80 pct of those closes and lower that median; V2 (aging x (1 + min(idle,14)/7), user ask x2) gives 88 and 43. Metrics are :~ join points with precomputed keys (the first version inlined folds over the constant list and the cell timed out at 300 s). PROOF node job-1793968468573-kit-replay3: ranking.spi 8/8 pass. LIVE NEXT: backlog_task gained idle_days, asked and parent; next indexes each item last-note hour once (note rows) and multiplies the main-agent key by the aging factor; subagent order unchanged; asked stays false until the typed user-ask field lands (next step: Task schema field via agile add/edit --asked <date> --quote <text>). Crate proof pending: node job-1793969211360-kit-probe1106a.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_5 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 5u32, "2026-11-06T12:48Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "DoD: (1) the replay guard in agile_task_next/ranking.spi passes (done 08:5x); (2) next ranks by V2 with aging from last-note time and a typed user ask; (3) the Task schema has a typed user-ask field (date + quote) written by agile add/edit, filled once from notes that quote the user with a review list; (4) a groom check lists Planned P0/P1 user asks untouched 3+ days; (5) next prints split on unsplit items over 240 min and kit agile split <id> makes chained children of 120 min or less; installed by a train. MILESTONES: replay guard 60 m (done); aging in live next 45 m (in the tree); typed user-ask field + migration 90 m; groom check 30 m; split verb 120 m; install 15 m = 360 m. NEXT STEP: the typed user-ask field in the Task record (agile_task_text model) and agile edit --asked. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: user_ask as a union (NotAsked | AskedOn of date * quote) in the task model, rank_formula as a closed union (WeightOverMinutes | AgedAskWeighted) already in ranking.spi, replay_row a record; no string tags for the ask.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_6 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 6u32, "2026-11-06T14:24Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 10:5x kit: (3) TYPED USER ASK BUILT (unstaged): new package agile_task_asks (Asked (id, yyyy-mm-dd, quote) rows in state/asks.spi, registered in state/package.spiproj like parents), verb kit agile edit <root> <id> --asked <yyyy-mm-dd> --quote <text> | --asked --clear routed in kit_agile_routes, quotes sanitized to one safe line; next reads the rows through the new linked crate agile_task_annotation (note-hour index, parents and asks in one pass, so agile_task_next stays small) and sets asked. Unit notebooks green on the node (asks 1/1, verb 1/1, annotation 1/1, ranking 8/8 incl. the replay guard after the i32 aging rewrite); CLI test asks_cli.spi runs in the next train. MIGRATION REVIEW LIST (not applied): 74 open items whose title (60) or a note (14) quotes the user with a date, at kit scratchpad user-asks-review.json (id, day, quote, from). Apply after the install: one agile edit --asked per reviewed row; noise to drop on review: rows whose quote is only a pointer (56 review list) and the batch comment why mine? that main pasted on many items.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_7 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 7u32, "2026-11-06T16:04Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 12:0x kit: (2) SPLIT VERB DRAFTED in the kit drafts store (KIT-NEXT-SPLIT\\agile_task_split: split.spi model + verb.spi), kept out of the tree until TRAIN34 has taken its snapshot. Model: milestones parsed from the latest note line with MILESTONES: (segments split on ;, each needs N m or Nm, parentheses allowed, (done) steps skipped, the = total and NEXT STEP tail cut); split_plan GADT SplitInto | NoMilestones | MilestoneTooBig (over 120 m, refused) | AlreadySmall (estimate 240 m or less). Verb kit agile split <root> <id>: reads the item through the installed agile show, then for each milestone runs agile add <id>-M<k> (TaskKind, parent priority, D1, the milestone minutes as estimate, deps chained to the previous child, a note with DoD and NEXT STEP) and agile edit --parent <id> through the installed binary, so every write takes the normal lock and lint path; finally a note on the parent. Tests: the milestone parser on four real note shapes from today backlog and a shown-item reader. NEXT: move into src after TRAIN34 launches, add AgileSplit to agile_subcommand_domain and its route, node notebook + size probe.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_8 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 8u32, "2026-11-06T16:39Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 12:4x kit: TRAIN34 STOPPED at 12:4x before its notebooks (node job-1793982758230 and box watcher job-1793982137092, both exit 143 on request). Reason found by reading the route chain: kit_agile_mutation gates agile edit with argument_count_is 5, which edit --parent and edit --asked --clear meet, but edit <root> <id> --asked <date> --quote <text> has 7 arguments, so it would be refused as usage before reaching kit_agile_routes, and the asks_cli notebook in the train would go red. FIX (unstaged): AgileEdit arity is agile_either_arity 5 7; two long lines in that file wrapped so the precheck stays clean. TRAIN34 relaunches after node probe job-1793982955768 (toml split, rules and precheck fixes) reports, without the oversized topology crate.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_9 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 9u32, "2026-11-06T19:50Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 15:5x kit: DoD (4) GROOM CHECK BUILT (unstaged): new linked crate kit_cadence_asks (asks.spi: ask_priority GADT UrgentAsk|HighAsk|LowerAsk; neglected_ask {id; priority; asked_on; idle_hours}). It joins the typed Asked rows (state/asks.spi via linked agile_task_asks.asks_of) with the linked cadence rows (status, tags, last note) and the Task priority cell, and lists Planned P0/P1 asks whose last touch (the later of the last note and the ask date) is 3+ days old. It is a standing part of kit agile cadence (groom rounds already read it): rows kit agile cadence neglected-ask id=X priority=P1 asked=yyyy-mm-dd idle_days=N, summary neglected_asks=N, --ask-days n (default 3), --team respected. Test planned_p0_p1_user_asks_untouched_for_days_are_listed (P1 untouched 5 d listed; P0 touched 26 h ago and P2 not; --team x drops). Node notebook queued after probe kit-probe1106t. Left: install (train after TRAIN34), then the 74-row user-ask review list is applied with agile edit --asked.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_10 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 10u32, "2026-11-06T20:44Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 16:4x kit: groom check notebook GREEN on the node: kit_cadence_asks/asks.spi 1/1 and kit_agile_cadence/cadence.spi 1/1 (probe job-1793996878160-kit-probe1106u); links kit_agile_cadence -> kit_cadence_asks and kit_cadence_asks -> agile_task_asks confirmed in generated Rust. Review list ready: 62 of 74 approved (12 dropped: a general 11-02 remark, not an ask), quotes re-extracted from titles; applied with agile edit --asked after TRAIN35 installs.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_11 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 11u32, "2026-11-06T21:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 17:5x kit: DoD (5) split verb: TRAIN35 probe sizes put agile_task_split at 1178 and kit_agile_mutation at 1003 (the new AgileSplit subcommand case), so split was held out of TRAIN35 and REWORKED (unstaged, back in the tree after the snapshot): parsing moved to a new linked crate agile_split_plan (plan.spi: milestones and the agile show reader; exports shown_fields_of -> record of primitives and shown_steps -> array_base milestone); agile_task_split keeps the split_plan GADT, the decision and the child writes. No new subcommand: the verb is agile edit <root> <id> --split milestones, routed in kit_agile_routes beside --parent and --asked (5 args, inside the existing edit arity), so kit_agile_mutation goes back under 1000. split_cli.spi updated to that form. Proof: node probe job-1794001491840-kit-probe1106x.")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_12 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 12u32, "2026-11-06T22:36Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 18:3x kit: split rework PROVEN on the node (probe job-1794001491840-kit-probe1106x): agile_split_plan/plan.spi 1/1, agile_task_split/verb.spi 1/1; sizes agile_split_plan 796, agile_task_split 818, kit_agile_routes 876 (all under 1000); links routes -> agile_task_split and agile_task_split -> agile_split_plan confirmed. Ready for the train after TRAIN36 (its CLI test split_cli.spi needs that train binary).")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_13 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 13u32, "2026-11-09T17:13Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 17:2x rig groom (the gate's split request). Checked against the DoD: (1) replay guard green, (2) aging wired, (4) groom check built, (5) split verb built and in daily use; (3) is half done: the typed ask field and agile edit --asked exist, but state/asks.spi does NOT exist on the live backlog (0 asks recorded) and the 74-row review list from 11-06 is not in the drafts store (lost with a scratchpad). MILESTONES: rebuild the user-ask review list from notes that quote the user, one row per item with date and quote 60 m; apply it with agile edit --asked after a one-item check that the installed verb writes state/asks.spi 45 m; verify the lift on agile next (a P1 user ask over 240 m ranks in its team top 10) and close 30 m")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_14 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 14u32, "2026-11-09T17:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "MILESTONES: rebuild the user-ask review list from notes that quote the user, one row per item with date and quote 60 m; apply it with agile edit --asked after a one-item check that the installed verb writes state/asks.spi 45 m; verify the lift on agile next, where a big P1 user ask now ranks in its team top ten, and close 30 m")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_15 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 15u32, "2026-11-09T17:15Z", "kit-split", "split into 3 chained children by kit agile split")
inl note_of_kit_next_starvation_16 () = Note ("KIT-NEXT-STARVATION", 16u32, "2026-11-09T19:20Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 23:5x rig, GATE FIX (hook ~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1, parse 0): the ready list now drops every id that has children in state/parents.spi (26 today), next to the waits filter and failing open like it (missing file = empty list). Cause: the gate offered CONVO-IN-SPIRAL (an epic split into M1..M5 this morning) under \"Split me\", and would offer any split parent as ready work although its work lives in the children. The same rule belongs in kit agile next itself (a parent with children is never a ready leaf); that edit is in agile_task_next, so it rides TRAIN44 with the agile family. Also today: TODO-AS-SOURCE got its first Parent row (KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS) and KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH was split into 4 children (children=4, matching).")
inl smart_delegation () = Task ("SMART-DELEGATION", "resource-aware nodes-first work delegation", Epic, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "management,delegation,kit,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_smart_delegation () = Planning ("SMART-DELEGATION", Minutes 1200u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_smart_delegation_1 () = Note ("SMART-DELEGATION", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "EVIDENCE 11-06 (672 kit job records + live specs): 647/672 jobs (96%) run on the box (i7-4810MQ 4c/8t 16GB, pinned 100% CPU / 2.9GB free); only 25 on nodes. compiler (134 compiles) and fiscal (69 gates, median 22m) run 0% on the node. The wifi node lap is a TWIN (4c/8t 16GB Windows) idle at 19% CPU / 12GB free = the big unused lever. europa is tiny (2c/3.4GB), OOMs on mem-heavy work. GOAL: make offload the DEFAULT, routed by job resource-profile + live capacity, not lane habit. Children: DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES, DELEG-JOB-PROFILE, DELEG-JOB-CLASS, DELEG-ROUTER, DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY, DELEG-BENCH-HOST. Builds on EOIE-REMOTE-EXECUTOR, MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS, EOIE-NEXT-FITS-NOW, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS.")
inl note_of_smart_delegation_2 () = Note ("SMART-DELEGATION", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:52Z", "resilience-614162e5", "PLANNING: estimate=1200m load=heavy assignee=agent team=resilience. DONE DEFINITION: a resource-aware router places each heavy job on a host that satisfies class_peak_mem + same-OS + cpu-headroom (gated on live DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES), measured by box CPU coming off 100% during offloadable runs. NEXT STEP: kit builds DELEG-ROUTER consuming the n3/n5 predicate (CPU+memory-floor) + extends peak_private_mb tracking to all heavy classes; resilience logs one measured manual-route win when a Windows host first has headroom. TYPEFEATURES: placement as a typed decision = Place(host) | Queue(reason) | NoCompatibleHost over a typed host_capacity record (os, free_mb, cpu_pct).")
inl note_of_smart_delegation_3 () = Note ("SMART-DELEGATION", 3u32, "2026-11-07T15:56Z", "resilience", "DONE DEFINITION: done when every heavy launch consults the utilization timeseries and picks node-first/box-last by measured free MEMORY (binding constraint: fiscal peaks 3-5GB), refusing to place on a host under its free-memory floor.")
inl note_of_smart_delegation_4 () = Note ("SMART-DELEGATION", 4u32, "2026-11-07T15:57Z", "resilience", "DoD: every heavy launch consults the utilization timeseries and picks node-first/box-last by measured free MEMORY (binding constraint: fiscal peaks 3-5GB), refusing any host under its free-memory floor; a recorded timeseries frame drives the placement decision in a self-checking sample.")
inl note_of_smart_delegation_5 () = Note ("SMART-DELEGATION", 5u32, "2026-11-09T17:59Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: this is an EPIC (1200m, assignee=pair) tracking children DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES/JOB-PROFILE/JOB-CLASS/ROUTER/WIFI-NODE-HEAVY/BENCH-HOST - not a light leaf pull. The children are the workable units. Re-tagged load=heavy; park on user (an epic of this size with assignee=pair needs the user's scheduling/pairing).")
inl deleg_util_timeseries () = Task ("DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES", "rolling per-machine utilization time-series", Story, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "resilience,delegation,monitor,smart-delegation,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_util_timeseries () = Planning ("DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl deleg_job_profile () = Task ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", "per job-type x machine duration and resource profile", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES", "resilience,delegation,metrics,smart-delegation,kit,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_job_profile () = Planning ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_1 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "GAP: nothing aggregates the kit job records (677 today) into a routing signal. DoD: a scheduled job computes per-(label-type x machine) median/p90 duration, timeout%, lost%, oom137%, peak private MB, writes to remote-machines.json (or a profile file) the router reads; matches the 11-06 hand analysis (box light median 5m/p90 28m, box heavy 11m/120m, node 82m).")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_2 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:5x kit: kit takes ownership. This profile IS the estimator of KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION (p95 peak memory, cores and p90 wall per job kind, now also per machine, plus OOM and timeout counts, keyed by load band per the 11-06 star exchange). One typed record, owned by kit; remote-machines.json is its export for scripts, not a second source. resilience feeds the measured numbers from the 672-job analysis.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_3 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 3u32, "2026-11-06T10:53Z", "resilience", "PROFILE DATA for the estimator (resilience, 672 kit job records, box %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\jobs, 2026-11-06T06:47). Loadable rows:\nMACHINES (spec | live): box cpu=i7-4810MQ/4c8t ram=16GB live=100%CPU/2.9GBfree os=windows ; lap cpu=4c8t ram=16GB live=19%CPU/12GBfree/14GBdisk os=windows ; europa cpu=2c ram=3.4GB live=0.20load/2.1GBfree/26GBdisk os=linux mem_ceiling~2.5GB\nOUTCOME by where [n finished timedout lost oom137]: box 647 567 13 6 0 ; node 25 18 2 0 0\nDURATION finished min [where,heavy -> n median p90]: box,light 454 5.0 28.3 ; box,heavy 113 10.7 119.8 ; node,light 18 82.6 270.5\nPER-LANE [n median_min timeout% lost% oom% node%]: kit 379 5.2 1 1 0 6 ; compiler 134 7.2 3 0 0 0 ; fiscal 69 21.7 1 0 0 0 ; main 55 3.0 2 0 0 0 ; beam 12 2.4 8 0 0 0\nHEAVIEST finished [lane where min state]: node box 1440 timed-out ; cloud node 1440 timed-out ; node box 1047 lost ; fiscal box 360 timed-out ; kit node 312.6 finished ; kit box 300.2 timed-out\nSIGNALS: box light p90 28m = keep-local threshold; compiler+fiscal are heavy-windows 0% offloaded -> lap; 0 oom in records only because nothing mem-heavy ran on europa (not proof it is safe there); lost/timeout tail = long remote runs (watcher death + 24h caps). Raw schema: each job dir exit file = code\\tend_ms\\tstate; started_ms, timeout_ms, heavy-wrapper.ps1 marks heavy; argv shows -Node/-SshHost/--on for remote.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_4 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 4u32, "2026-11-06T10:56Z", "resilience", "PEAK PRIVATE MB cut (7-day, resilience) from the 65 jobs carrying peak_private_mb (memory-tracked/--max-private-mb only; coverage is fiscal-heavy):\nOVERALL p50=1747 p95=3221 max=4966 MB\nper-lane [n p50 p95 max]: fiscal 64 1751 3221 4966 ; compiler 1 136 136 136\ntop families by p95 [n p50 p95 cap]: fiscal-restriction 2 3746 3746 9000 ; fiscal-box7-signature-list 2 3221 3221 9000 ; fiscal-cr-docs-4 2 2831 2831 9000 ; fiscal-box4-fidelity 3 2595 2619 9000 ; fiscal-box3-postland 5 2034 2198 9000 ; fiscal-quiet 2 2011 2011 9000\nROUTING RULE: fiscal gates peak p95=3.2GB/max=5GB private -> do NOT fit the 3.4GB europa (2.1GB free); route to a 16GB host (box or lap). This is why the memory reaper kills box jobs (125-lost: node-watch, kit-train5, stub-probe) when a 3GB+ gate runs on a box with 2.9GB free.\nCOVERAGE GAP: only memory-tracked jobs record peak_private_mb (65/672); kit/compiler/main heavy jobs do not, so the estimator is memory-blind for them. Fix in DELEG-JOB-CLASS: heavy/compile jobs always sample peak (supervisor records peak_private_mb at end even with no cap).")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_5 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 5u32, "2026-11-07T13:03Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump (KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM; source drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md, 2026-10-22): this item also covers dump entries FIXT-3 (Resource sampler gate). Why: per job-type x machine resource profile needs the peak RSS/thread sampler (was parked 10-24)")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_6 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 6u32, "2026-11-07T14:14Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Covered by this item. FIXT-3 · Resource sampler gate · P2 · node · Open / Peak RSS / FD / thread counts across owned children, with a statement of sampling perturbation and compact summaries.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_7 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 7u32, "2026-11-07T21:01Z", "resilience", "OBSERVED RESOURCE PROFILES (11-07, direct measurement from the util monitor + this sessions incidents - fills the instrumentation gap where kit/compiler/main record 0 peak_mem; use as seed data for the class model): (1) compiler candidate build (laptop node): dotnet x5-12 + rustc x1-4, ~100% CPU, lap COMMIT 21-44% of a 48GB limit (so ~3-6GB committed), CPU-BOUND, DURATION 1.5h+ (long); peak_mem moderate, the binding constraint is CPU+time not RAM. (2) fiscal node-full-build: 3-5GB peak (per prior notes), mem-bound. (3) x-roundtrip BEAM: PATHOLOGICAL 35GB committed when buggy (infinite Enum.zip), normally small - outlier, not a stable class. (4) xsim serve (europa k3s): beam.smp 761MB RSS steady, long-lived service. (5) europa pack61b BUILD: needs MemAvailable >=2100MB (hard gate) on a 3.4GB VM - the tightest mem class on europa. (6) mirror/critical lanes: light I/O, <100MB, seconds. (7) kit trains: 1-5h (prior notes). CLASS-MODEL IMPLICATION (reinforces my earlier note): carry BOTH peak_committed_mem AND cpu-bound-vs-mem-bound per class - the laptop build is CPU/time-bound (place by free cores+time), fiscal/europa-build are MEM-bound (place by free MemAvailable). peak_private_mb alone misses the CPU-bound long builds. team:kit owns the model; this is observed seed data.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_profile_8 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-PROFILE", 8u32, "2026-11-09T05:33Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 02:2x rig SEED PROFILE from the job stores (job list --on ganymede, moon, europa; last 300 records each; 347 finished exit 0; label stem = label without its trailing run number). Per host and stem, n / median / max seconds: moon kit-probe 11 / 220 / 3413; moon rig-probe 5 / 308 / 1195; moon compiler-budget-split-tests 3 / 7 / 2610; moon tibia-bcdataset-cells 3 / 172 / 635; ganymede tibia-zig-smoke 3 / 2427 / 2492; europa kit-linux-proof 8 / 3 / 69; europa kit-growth 5 / 149 / 275; europa kit-claim-proof 4 / 331 / 399; europa kit-probe 4 / 329 / 574; europa sota-gate 4 / 20 / 36; europa kit-census-size 4 / 56 / 97. FINDING: max over median reaches 15x on moon (probes), 2-4x on europa, about 1x on ganymede smoke; so a profile must carry load-dependent spread (the host load at start, which the job record lacks), not one duration, and a placement that ignores host load will mis-predict (tonight two moon probes VOIDed at the 300 s cell cap with moon at 100 pct CPU, while the same notebooks pass in about 5 min when quiet). Peak memory is now exported per job by KIT-EXEC-METRICS-M1 (rig_job_peak_bytes, in TRAIN42), which fills the 0 peak_mem gap resilience noted. DEP DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES is still open; this seed does not need it. Next step: record load_at_start in each job (one host sample at supervisor start) so the profile can be conditioned on load.")
inl deleg_job_class () = Task ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", "resource class per recurring job type", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "resilience,delegation,smart-delegation,kit,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_job_class () = Planning ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_deleg_job_class_1 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "DoD: every recurring job label carries a resource class (cpu-heavy|mem-heavy|io|short|bench), os=windows|linux, and a memory ceiling - set from DELEG-JOB-PROFILE and kept in the job registry; kit job start reads it to route. mem-heavy (jq big data, full-corpus body stage, ld under MemoryMax) is flagged so it never lands on the 3.4GB europa.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_class_2 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:54Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:0x kit takes ownership (resilience offered): the resource class lives in the kit job registry, as the job class union MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS n-last proposed (Plain | Heavy | Compile | Cells), widened with the resilience classes (heavy Windows, light Linux under 2.5 GB, Linux CI, short). The class is declared at job start and is the key of DELEG-JOB-PROFILE rows; DELEG-ROUTER and KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION read it. resilience keeps measuring which jobs land in which class.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_class_3 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", 3u32, "2026-11-06T10:58Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 07:1x kit, coverage gap from resilience DELEG-JOB-PROFILE n4 (only 65 of 672 jobs carried peak_private_mb, almost all fiscal): FIXED IN THE TREE (unstaged; next train proves it). kit_job_supervise/supervise.spi now samples container_peak_mb on every loop, not only when a cap is set: the supervisor always runs the child in a Windows job object (child_container in kit_jobs/host.spi), and PeakJobMemoryUsed is tracked whether or not a limit is set, so the reading was always available and was being discarded. New peak_recorded writes the file at every heartbeat, before the exit line on a normal finish, and on any halt, and skips 0 so Linux, which has no job object yet, never records a fake zero. The cap check is unchanged (cap_reached still needs cap above 0). Until it installs, the estimator treats a missing peak as unknown, assumed at or above its lane p95.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_class_4 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", 4u32, "2026-11-06T18:09Z", "resilience-614162e5", "MEASURED RESOURCE CLASSES (resilience-614162e5, from a read-only census of all 680 kit job records %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/jobs on 11-06; full data on DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY n5). Directly feeds this item - per recurring job type, duration + peak private memory: \n- FISCAL gates: duration 50m-3.8h; peak_private_mb 1.2-5.0GB (ceiling ~5GB: regen-all-restriction 4966, box10-nfe4 4214, stages3 3221, box7 2964). PLACEMENT: needs host free_mb > ~6500; fits only lap-when-free; NEVER box (1-2GB free) or europa (3.4GB total) by RAM. \n- kit TRAINs: duration median ~62m, p90 ~3.5h, max 5.2h; memory NOT instrumented (peak_private_mb absent -> records 0). \n- COMPILER packs: 1-2.5h; mem not instrumented. \n- MAIN lib sweeps: ~70m; mem not instrumented. \nKEY FINDINGS for the class model: (1) MEMORY, not CPU, is the binding placement constraint (fiscal cannot leave the box by RAM regardless of cores); the class model should carry a peak_mem field and the router gate host_free_mb > class_peak + headroom. (2) INSTRUMENTATION GAP: only fiscal jobs record peak_private_mb; kit/compiler/main record 0 - the class model cannot bound the 1-5h TRAINs (the jobs that most need placing) until peak_private_mb is tracked for ALL heavy classes. ASK: extend peak_private_mb capture beyond fiscal. This is analysis for a kit-owned item (not claiming it); use freely. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_deleg_job_class_5 () = Note ("DELEG-JOB-CLASS", 5u32, "2026-11-09T19:02Z", "tibia", "11-09 15:0x tibia (help pool): MEASURED PROFILES for tibia's recurring job types (11-09, from the job tails' elapsed_ms and the gates' own resource lines), as input for the class key. (1) tibia-bc-* (BC training + held-out eval, predict.ps1 -Module bc_*): cpu-heavy, linux, taskset 2 cores; peak RSS ~1.2 GB (dotnet compile) then ~300 MB (predict); 44-102 min (healtrace 44, engage 52, healcal 75, indep3 102). (2) tibia-verify* / libgate* / regexgate* / xsdgate* / forwards / shadow (format + --check + cell budget + one module's Cells run): compile+cells, linux, ~1.2 GB dotnet peak, 2-20 min of run (xsdgate5 3.4 min, libgate-fs 20 min; jobs that queued show 30+ min wall, so elapsed_ms includes queueing and should not be the class key). (3) tibia-jp* / Zig ReleaseFast builds: MEM-HEAVY, linux: compiler measured a 3.02 GB peak RSS under a 4.5 GB cgroup cap (pack75), 2.35 -> 1.90 GB on pack77 with outlining; must never land on a < 4 GB host. (4) tibia-rebench* / port4 (timing arms): BENCH, needs a quiet node (today's A/A on a shared 2-slot ganymede drifted 33 pct), so the class must also mean \"exclusive node\", not only CPU. (5) tibia-libland* (lib_land_window -PrivateLib on moon): windows, cells document_test, ~10-15 min, ~1-2 GB. (6) tibia-qat_arm_* (europa systemd units, not kit jobs): cpu-long single-thread, MemoryMax 300M, 6.4-13 h. GAP found: kit job list rows report elapsed since PLACEMENT (state=placed), so run time and queue time are mixed; the profile key needs a started_at.")
inl deleg_router () = Task ("DELEG-ROUTER", "kit routes jobs by profile and capacity, offload by default", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "DELEG-JOB-PROFILE,DELEG-JOB-CLASS,MACHINE-SLOT-CAPS,KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR,KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION", "resilience,delegation,kit,smart-delegation,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_router () = Planning ("DELEG-ROUTER", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Pair)
inl note_of_deleg_router_1 () = Note ("DELEG-ROUTER", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "THE core: kit job start with no explicit --on picks the machine from job class + live capacity, defaulting to OFFLOAD: heavy-windows->lap, light-linux(<2.5GB)->europa, linux-CI->codespace, short/interactive->box. Admission gates (lanes empirical rules): europa only when load<4 AND MemAvailable>=cap+600MB wrapped in systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax -p MemorySwapMax=0; lap gated on its CPU load. DoD: a week shows node-share rise from ~4% and the box no longer pinned during compiler/fiscal runs; zero policy-wrong placements in a sample.")
inl note_of_deleg_router_2 () = Note ("DELEG-ROUTER", 2u32, "2026-11-06T10:52Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-06 06:5x kit: kit takes ownership (agreed with resilience 06:4x). Scope: placement INSIDE one network; KIT-NET-HARVEST is the cross-network half and reuses this router with the work hub as the admitting side. Depends on KIT-RESOURCE-ADMISSION, which supplies the per-job-kind needs and the admission gates the router checks before picking a machine. Default stays offload: heavy Windows to the wifi node, light Linux under 2.5 GB to europa behind the memory gate, Linux CI to a codespace, short jobs on the box.")
inl deleg_wifi_node_heavy () = Task ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", "route heavy Windows jobs to the idle wifi-node twin", Story, P1, D3, 80u32, Blocked, "", "management,delegation,node,smart-delegation,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy () = Planning ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_1 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "IMMEDIATE WIN (no porting - lap is a 4c/8t 16GB Windows twin of the box, idle at 19%): route compiler compiles (134 jobs, 0% node today) and fiscal gates (69, median 22m, 0% node) to lap via kit job start --on lap. DoD: these default to lap; measured box CPU comes off 100% during their runs and wall-clock throughput rises. Can start manually before DELEG-ROUTER automates it.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_2 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 2u32, "2026-11-06T11:23Z", "resilience-614162e5", "live-load caveat 11-06 run: the \"idle lap twin\" premise is SITUATIONAL. Today lap is at 96% CPU - it carries kit TRAIN33 + tibia tf9, i.e. lap IS the compute node today. So right now there is NO idle Windows offload target (box 100%, lap 96%, europa only 2c/3.4GB). This confirms DELEG-ROUTER must gate on LIVE load from DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES, never assume lap is idle; and when all Windows hosts are saturated under full fan-out, the lever is adding a 4th Windows node or staggering heavy work in time (feeds NODE-VALUE-METRICS), not just placement. The earlier \"route compiler/fiscal to the idle lap\" win holds only when lap has headroom.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_3 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 3u32, "2026-11-06T14:49Z", "resilience-614162e5", "DATA-GROUNDED PLACEMENT PREDICATE (resilience-614162e5, from the DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES CSV this session wired in: 10 samples, 2026-11-06 07:32-10:44, 3h12m of full fan-out). MEASURED: box_cpu=100 on ALL 10 samples (min 100); lap_cpu=100 on 9/10, 87% once (min 87); worker_cpu 2-16% on the 7 samples that answered, -1 (probe timed out under the 15s bound) on 3/10; node_free 9-13GB; lap_free_mb 2253-8091 (dipped to 2.2GB at 08:15 even at 87% CPU => memory pressure tracks CPU). FINDING: across 3+ hours of heavy fan-out NEITHER Windows host ever had headroom - the \"idle lap twin\" is not a momentary exception, it is the steady state under full fan-out. So the earlier \"route compiler/fiscal to the idle lap\" win is real but applies ONLY in the (empirically rare today) windows where lap is actually idle. PREDICATE DELEG-ROUTER should consume (gates on the LATEST CSV row, never a static assumption): a Windows-heavy job -> lap IFF lap_cpu<60 AND lap_free_mb>3000; else box IFF box_cpu<85; else there is NO Windows spill target -> QUEUE/stagger in time (do not stack a 3rd heavy job onto a pinned host). europa only for Linux-portable LIGHT work when worker_cpu<50. EVIDENCE the predicate is live-load-correct: in these 10 samples lap_cpu<60 occurred 0 times and box_cpu<85 occurred 0 times => the predicate would have correctly QUEUED every heavy job this window instead of stacking, which is what actually kept both hosts pinned. CAVEAT (measurement gap, same class as a load-starved guard): the europa-load probe returns -1 under box saturation (3/10) - the router cannot gate on europa load exactly when offload matters most; fix is a node-side cron writing /proc/loadavg to a file the box reads, OR a longer probe bound, so saturation does not blind the gate. This upgrades the item from a prose caveat to a testable predicate + measured hit-rate; the automation (DELEG-ROUTER) stays team:kit. CPU-light, no run, no git.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_4 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 4u32, "2026-11-06T14:50Z", "resilience-614162e5", "PLANNING: estimate=180m load=light assignee=agent team=resilience deps=none. NEXT STEP: (resilience half) when the DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES CSV first shows a row with lap_cpu<60 AND lap_free_mb>3000, manually route one compiler or fiscal job to lap via kit job start --on lap and record the box-CPU delta + wall-clock vs a box run (proves the predicate win on live data). (kit half, team:kit) DELEG-ROUTER consumes the note-3 predicate, gating on the latest CSV row. Close when one measured manual-route win is logged AND the europa-probe blind-spot (node-side loadavg file) is fixed so the gate is not load-blind.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_5 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 5u32, "2026-11-06T15:01Z", "resilience-614162e5", "JOB-RECORD RESOURCE PROFILE (resilience-614162e5, 2026-11-06 10:5x; read-only census of all 680 kit job dirs in %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/jobs, duration=exit.end_ms-started_ms, mem=peak_private_mb where present; this is the \"time/memory per run type\" the user asked for). MEASURED DURATIONS (finished jobs): kit TRAINs median ~3708s (62m), p90 ~12686s (3.5h), longest 18758s (5.2h, TRAIN21-node); fiscal gates 3000-13791s (50m-3.8h); compiler packs 3465-9068s (1-2.5h); main lib sweeps ~4200s (70m). => heavy classes run 1-5h; never stack two on one host. MEASURED PEAK MEMORY (fiscal is the only instrumented class): fiscal jobs peak 1252-4966MB private (regen-all-restriction 4966, box10-nfe4-family 4214, stages3-cr-ubl 3221, box7-signature 2964, cr-docs 2795, delphi4 2174, quiet-sm 1988); so FISCAL CLASS CEILING ~=5GB. HARD PLACEMENT CONSTRAINT this reveals (memory, not CPU, is the binding limit): a job needs host_free_mb > peak + headroom. In the measured window box had only 1191-2291MB FREE and europa has 3.4GB TOTAL => a 3-5GB fiscal job PHYSICALLY CANNOT run on the box (OOM/swap) or on europa (ever), regardless of CPU. lap (free 2253-8091MB) is the ONLY viable fiscal host, and only when lap_free_mb>~6000. THIS IS WHY \"offload fiscal off the pinned box\" fails even when desired: RAM, not cores. So the note-3 predicate gains a MEMORY FLOOR per class: route a heavy Windows job to host H iff H_cpu<60 AND H_free_mb > class_peak_mb + 1500. For fiscal(~5GB) that means only a host with >6.5GB free - europa is excluded by RAM independent of its Linux/portability limit. MEASUREMENT GAP (feeds team:kit DELEG-JOB-PROFILE): peak_private_mb is recorded ONLY for fiscal jobs; kit/compiler/main jobs show 0 (not instrumented), so the router cannot apply the memory floor to the classes that most need placing (the 1-5h TRAINs). ASK kit: extend peak_private_mb tracking to all heavy job classes, not just fiscal. CPU-light read-only census, no run, no git.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_6 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 6u32, "2026-11-06T15:03Z", "resilience-614162e5", "PLANNING: estimate=180m load=light assignee=agent team=resilience deps=none. NEXT STEP: resilience half (predicate + memory floor + measured durations/mem) is specified and data-grounded at 55%; remaining = (team:kit) DELEG-ROUTER consumes notes 3+5 (CPU+memory-floor predicate), and extend peak_private_mb tracking to kit/compiler/main job classes; (resilience) log one measured manual-route win when the CSV first shows lap_cpu<60 AND lap_free_mb>6000. Close when a measured win is logged AND heavy-class memory is instrumented AND the router gates on live CPU+mem.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_7 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 7u32, "2026-11-06T15:07Z", "resilience-614162e5", "MONITOR SIGNAL-QUALITY REFINEMENT (resilience-614162e5, from the 11:0x monitoring verify). node-utilization-monitor.ps1 raised WASTE at 10:44: \"box 100% saturated while idle: compute(10%) - offloadable capacity sitting unused\". But the job-record census (note 5) proves the classes actually saturating the box - fiscal (3-5GB peak), kit TRAINs, compiler packs - CANNOT run on the idle host it names (europa: 3.4GB RAM total, Linux, 2c), by memory AND OS. So the WASTE alarm reports \"offloadable capacity\" that is not usable for the work in flight; it is a FALSE-POSITIVE whenever the only idle host is europa and the box load is heavy Windows jobs. REFINEMENT (future monitor edit, NOT mid-run): gate the WASTE alarm on COMPATIBLE idle capacity - only alarm \"offloadable\" if an idle host can actually host the saturating class (same OS + free_mb > class peak). With the current fleet that means WASTE should fire only when LAP is idle (the sole Windows twin), never on idle-vultr-alone for Windows load. This makes the \"is there usable offload?\" signal match the hard constraints in notes 3+5. Captured as a monitor refinement; deferred from this heartbeat (live-monitor script edit on a pinned box is a deliberate change). CPU-light observation, no run, no git.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_8 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 8u32, "2026-11-06T15:20Z", "resilience-614162e5", "MONITOR WASTE-ALARM FIX APPLIED + VERIFIED (resilience-614162e5, 11-06 11:1x; applies note 7). Edited node-utilization-monitor.ps1 (unstaged, .bak in session scratch, PS parse OK): WASTE now triggers only on USABLE offload = the lap twin (same OS) idle AND lap_free_mb>=3000; europa compute-idle no longer counts (Linux/3.4GB cannot host the heavy Windows classes that saturate the box, per note 5). New $idleUsable gates $waste; alarm text names the compatible host and states europa is excluded with its %. VERIFIED behaviorally: ran the script once (no -NoteOnWaste, so no delivery) - it emitted ONLY \"DISK-LOW node C: 11GB\" and NO WASTE, where the 10:44 scheduled line HAD fired WASTE on europa compute(10%) idle. So the false-positive (idle-vultr-counted-as-offloadable-for-Windows-load) is gone while the real DISK-LOW alarm still fires; exit 1 = alarm present, clean run, no crash. This makes the monitors \"is there usable offload?\" signal match the measured CPU+memory+OS constraints (notes 3/5). Strictly safer: removes a false alarm, removes no real alarm. Next scheduled run (with -NoteOnWaste, within 30m) will exercise the delivery path; will confirm its output at a later tick (scheduled-run verification per the silently-refused-watchdog lesson). No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_9 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 9u32, "2026-11-06T20:30Z", "resilience-614162e5", "FULL-DAY DATASET confirms: no Windows-offload headroom exists (resilience-614162e5, 11-06, from DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES 23 samples 07:32-16:16). box_cpu >=85% on 23/23 samples (100% of the day). lap: 0 samples with a usable offload window (lap_cpu<60 AND lap_free_mb>3000); min lap_cpu = 87% ALL DAY (lap carried kit TRAIN + tibia the whole time). europa avg 19% (idle) but Linux/3.4GB - cannot host the heavy Windows classes (fiscal 3-5GB, trains) per n5. CONCLUSION (now full-day, not just the morning 10 samples): the \"route heavy Windows jobs to the idle lap twin\" premise NEVER applied today - there was no idle Windows target at any sample. So the DELEG-ROUTER placement predicate (n3) is correct but had zero applicable windows; the real lever under sustained full fan-out is CAPACITY (a 4th Windows node) or TIME-STAGGERING heavy runs, not placement (placement optimizes a resource that does not exist when every Windows host is pinned). This strengthens the case for NODE-VALUE-METRICS / a 4th node over DELEG-ROUTER-alone. Side note: the 16:16 sample is all -1 (monitor node-probe timed out) because a large resilience scp was briefly saturating the link - transient, resolved. resilience-614162e5.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_10 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 10u32, "2026-11-07T17:17Z", "resilience", "PROBED 11-07 13:15 (compiler asked why packs build box-only while lap idle): laptop (lap route) has rustc 1.101.0-nightly in %USERPROFILE%\\.cargo\\bin + rustup + SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR set, 1-3% CPU, 12GB free RAM all day. Node-first compiler builds are NOT capacity-blocked. Three fixable gaps: (1) rustc/cargo NOT on the non-interactive ssh PATH -> `ssh laptop rustc --version` returns EMPTY; export it (~5min). (2) zig.exe not found under spiral tree - confirm byte-compare gate linker (MSVC vs zig cc). (3) laptop C: at 12GB free < 15GB mirror floor (DISK-LOW) - a Rust target dir competes with mirror landings; free ~5GB or retarget. NEXT STEP: export the laptop ssh PATH + verify rustc prints, then build-candidate/review-candidate need a route param + trap-cleanup ssh (no agent eval, no external timeout, no WSL bash) to run lap-side; install stays box-side (authoritative pin).")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_11 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 11u32, "2026-11-07T17:48Z", "resilience", "NODE MEMORY INCIDENT 11-07 13:4x (user asked to diagnose the wifi node): the laptop was at 84% RAM used / 2.7GB free of 16GB. Single cause: x-lane erl.exe (BEAM) pid 13876 running mix run C:/home/remote-stage/x-roundtrip/roundtrip_diff.exs - 10.4GB working set but 35.6GB COMMITTED (pagefile thrash), started 13:32, climbing. Classic BEAM blow-up: the .exs materializes the full roundtrip diff in memory instead of streaming. Alerted x to kill+stream+cap. This starved all node work incl the critical mirror floor. HEAL (systemic, mine): node jobs MUST launch under a committed-memory cap so one runaway crashes its own process, not the node - see DELEGATE-5-PREFLIGHT note.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_12 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 12u32, "2026-11-07T18:09Z", "resilience", "PLACEMENT DECISION IMPLEMENTED + LIVE-VERIFIED (11-07 14:0x). node-env.ps1 Select-OffloadTarget -JobBudgetMb <n> is the routing core: box-first when box CPU < 85% (local avoids the slow-link transfer cost), node-first (laptop twin) when box is saturated AND Test-NodePreflight -ForCompute passes for the job memory budget (memory is the binding constraint - fiscal peaks 3-5GB). Verified live: with box at 100%, Select-OffloadTarget -JobBudgetMb 4000 returned target=laptop \"laptop passed preflight\". So the FULL decision chain is in place: identity (DELEGATE-11) -> 5 preflight refusals (DELEGATE-5) -> Select-OffloadTarget. NEXT STEP: the LAUNCH integration (ship the job to the chosen target + run under the memory cap + collect results) is kit the verb owner (KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR); resilience provides the decision + refusal predicates, which are done. DoD (resilience side): a heavy job with a known mem budget is routed to the laptop when the box is saturated and the laptop can hold it, else kept/deferred with a named reason - met by Select-OffloadTarget. parse OK. No commit, no stage.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_13 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 13u32, "2026-11-07T20:17Z", "resilience", "BLOCKER: resilience part done+verified (Select-OffloadTarget routing decision: box-first when box<85%, node-first when saturated+preflight-ok, memory-aware). Remaining is OTHER-LANE: the launch integration (ship+run on the chosen target) is KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR. RESUME: when kit lands the executor verb that calls Select-OffloadTarget.")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_14 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 14u32, "2026-11-08T05:33Z", "resilience", "P0 NODE-OFFLOAD HEAL (user via main, 01:0x: box 100pct CPU + <1GB free while node idle = lanes not coordinating). ROOT CAUSE: Invoke-NodeProbeWin returned route=none INTERMITTENTLY under box saturation (100pct CPU/<1GB free makes a single-pass ssh+remote-powershell probe flaky), so Select-OffloadTarget/Test-NodePreflight saw \"no node\" and fell back to the box -> more load -> feedback loop. The node itself was healthy (3 routes answering, 10.9GB free). FIXES in node-env.ps1 (unstaged, parse-clean, all live-verified): (1) PROBE ROBUSTNESS - Invoke-NodeProbeWin now caches the last-good route (kit/schedules/node-route.cache, 10min TTL), tries it first, and retries the whole chain Attempts=2x; a transient reset under load clears on retry. Verified: ok=True route=laptop attempt=1, cache written. (2) Select-OffloadTarget is now MEMORY-AWARE (was CPU-only at 85pct): saturation = cpu>=85pct OR free<2000MB, so a <1GB-free box offloads even if CPU dipped. (3) NEW Test-BoxHeavyGuard [-FloorMb 2000] [-IsHook]: FAIL CLOSED for box heavy work (free<floor AND node up via cache -> ok=False placement=node route=laptop), FAIL OPEN for hooks and node-unreachable. Verified both branches live (ok=False \"box-starved: free 1749MB<3000MB and node(laptop) up\" / hook ok=True). (4) FOLDED into idle-with-ready-alarm.ps1: BOX-SATURATED alarm when box CPU>90pct SUSTAINED (2-tick, ~15-30min) while node CPU<50pct; delivers to main(NODE-ALWAYS-FED) on rising edge. Verified live: box=100pct node=31-45pct -> PENDING then BOX-SATURATED fired. ADOPTION REMAINING (team gates): the heavy-run paths (lib_gate.ps1, box-law-gate.ps1, launch-train.ps1, notebook batch) must CALL Test-BoxHeavyGuard before committing to the box - the predicate is landed; wiring it into each gate is the enforcement step (like LANE-ACTIVITY-BEACON, main routes per-lane).")
inl note_of_deleg_wifi_node_heavy_15 () = Note ("DELEG-WIFI-NODE-HEAVY", 15u32, "2026-11-09T07:48Z", "org3", "org3 STARRED-UNBLOCK advice (kind=PREMISE-RECHECK + ACTION, from tonight's moon verification): this item's core premise - an idle Windows TWIN to route heavy jobs to - is now RE-VERIFIED (and was briefly in doubt: I'd wrongly concluded moon=box earlier, which would have made this item moot). CONFIRMED 11-09 by MachineGuid: moon = ssh wifi = DESKTOP-6E8C14G, a SEPARATE 8-logical-CPU Windows node (guid 26fe0dc3, distinct from box=ALIAS guid 2b2c714b), identity b4c8f166, takes kit jobs + preflight-ready (Test-NodePreflight -Route wifi -ForCompute passes). So the twin is real + usable. The resilience routing predicates are landed+verified (Select-OffloadTarget memory-aware; Test-BoxHeavyGuard fail-closed; probe caching/retry). NON-BLOCKED next step (independent of KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR): each heavy-run gate (lib_gate.ps1, box-law-gate.ps1, launch-train.ps1, notebook batch) can CALL Test-BoxHeavyGuard before committing to the box TODAY - the guard REFUSES a starved box + names the node route; only the actual ship-to-node needs the executor. So gate-owners adopt the guard now; the executor is the last mile. Bonus: the drift-watch now COVERS moon (found its dotnet cache SDK rc.2, compiler healing to rc.3), so a heavy job routed there won't silently diverge once healed.")
inl deleg_bench_host () = Task ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", "benches run only on a quiet machine", Story, P2, D2, 75u32, Active, "DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES", "management,delegation,bench,smart-delegation,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_deleg_bench_host () = Planning ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_deleg_bench_host_1 () = Note ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", 1u32, "2026-11-06T10:47Z", "resilience", "tibia/x lesson: a timing on a loaded machine is noise/VOID; count only <=40% load. DoD: a bench job refuses/VOIDs on a host above 40% load and routes to the quietest eligible machine (box is 100% now so benches go to idle lap); the <=40% bar is recorded with each timing.")
inl note_of_deleg_bench_host_2 () = Note ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:56Z", "resilience", "NEXT STEP: DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES (dep) is now Done, so gate bench launches on its route==idle + box<40% signal: add a bench-eligible predicate to node-utilization-monitor output and have the bench launcher read it instead of launching blind.")
inl note_of_deleg_bench_host_3 () = Note ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", 3u32, "2026-11-07T20:28Z", "resilience", "BENCH-ELIGIBLE PREDICATE BUILT + VERIFIED (11-07; dep DELEG-UTIL-TIMESERIES is Done). node-env.ps1 Test-BenchEligible -Route <local|laptop|europa> [-MaxCpu 20] returns @{ok;reason;cpu;busy}: a host is eligible only when QUIET - CPU < MaxCpu (default 20%, strict because benches need a near-idle host for STABLE timings, not merely unsaturated) AND no active heavy/build process (rustc/dotnet/cargo/zig/erl/beam/node/swiftc). OS-aware (local CIM, Windows-node via Invoke-NodeProbeWin, Linux via loadavg+pgrep). Verified: box at 100% -> ok=False \"not-quiet CPU 100% >= 20%\". DoD: a bench only launches on a machine Test-BenchEligible approves. NEXT STEP: the bench LAUNCHER calls Test-BenchEligible first and picks the first eligible host (or defers) - that launcher integration is the remaining 30% (whoever owns the bench run; resilience provides the predicate). TYPEFEATURES: eligibility verdict @{ok;reason;cpu;busy}.")
inl note_of_deleg_bench_host_4 () = Note ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", 4u32, "2026-11-09T15:50Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 15:5x WHAT COVERS THE DoD TODAY (read from code): (1) kit job start --quiet [--quiet-hold m] [--quiet-wait m] (kit/src/rig_job_gate/gate.spi compile_hold_loop): holds a job until no COMPILE windows are open on its host, and takes a CompileHold window so none open during it; blind to non-kit load (e.g. QAT arms run outside kit). (2) lib bench_model (landed 15:04): every sample carries machine_state {busy_cores; load}, quiet p s refuses samples above plan.quiet_cores, and the A/A band VOIDs a whole table when the arms drift (tibia measured 33 pct drift on a busy ganymede, PERF-STUDY-4). (3) node-env.ps1 Test-BenchEligible (resilience, 11-07): CPU < 20 pct and no heavy process, per route; currently has NO caller. Missing: ROUTING, i.e. picking the quietest eligible host before submit. Next step: a thin bench-submit path = Test-BenchEligible over the candidate routes -> kit job start --on <first eligible> --quiet, deferring when none is eligible; then regex's bench and bench-pair use it.")
inl note_of_deleg_bench_host_5 () = Note ("DELEG-BENCH-HOST", 5u32, "2026-11-09T16:53Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: the bench-eligible predicate (Test-BenchEligible, node-env.ps1) is DONE + verified by resilience; remaining 30% = the bench LAUNCHER calling it first and picking the first eligible host. That launcher belongs to the bench-run owner = the NODE-BENCH sweep tooling (my NODE-BENCH-METRIC design already specifies the saturated sweep must gate on Test-BenchEligible / <40pct quiet host, box last). So the launcher integration lands with the NODE-BENCH sweep, which is parked on rig (the --saturated nodebench flag). Parking DELEG-BENCH-HOST on item:NODE-BENCH-METRIC; clear when the bench launcher is built.")
