inl note_of_giant_state_3_fix_top_1 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-3-FIX-TOP", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:14Z", "compiler", "11-06 compiler: split from COMPILER-GIANT-STATE-PROFILE. DoD: one kitdo program; the exponent of the fixed phase drops to 1.2 or less on the step-1 slices; --check diagnostics and format output are byte-identical before and after; all-backend parity unchanged.")
inl note_of_giant_state_3_fix_top_2 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-3-FIX-TOP", 2u32, "2026-11-06T14:32Z", "compiler", "11-06 10:4x compiler: RE-SCOPED by step 2 (no superlinear phase; the cost is a linear constant, parse + tokenize about 3/4). New DoD: cut the parse constant for state files of one-line bindings (a measured candidate, e.g. caching block parses by content hash across checks, which kit agile check re-runs on a mostly unchanged file), proven by the same q/h/full slices on a QUIET node (median of 3, load <= 40%), with --check diagnostics byte-identical. NEXT STEP: profile-speedscope -View Thread on a quiet full-size trace to name the parser function that dominates. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: any cache key is a typed record (path, content hash), not a string concat.")
inl note_of_giant_state_3_fix_top_3 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-3-FIX-TOP", 3u32, "2026-11-06T16:10Z", "compiler", "11-06 12:1x compiler: RE-DECIDED P0 -> P2. Step 2 found no superlinear phase, and the 5 s write goal is met by kit's sharding instead (GIANT-STATE-4: N=128 shards, avg 123 KB, max 259 KB, lossless; one shard of about 240 KB checked in about 4.6 s cold even on a saturated box). A parse-constant cut (parse + tokenize = about 3/4 of --check) would still speed up every compile, but nothing waits on it now. Resume when: a lane reports a parse-bound compile it needs faster, or the cold all-shard check (job-1793981352933 on the node) shows the full check too slow for kit's train attestation.")
inl note_of_giant_state_3_fix_top_4 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-3-FIX-TOP", 4u32, "2026-11-11T00:29Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:3x compiler (groom): the 11-06 resume condition was prose; now a wait row: resume when a lane reports a parse-bound compile (parse + tokenize dominating --check) that kits sharding does not already fix.")
inl giant_state_4_check_5s () = Task ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", "giant state step 4: kit agile check attests in 5 s or less at full size during a write burst (or an incremental per-binding design, measured)", TaskKind, P0, D3, 75u32, Active, "GIANT-STATE-3-FIX-TOP", "team:rig,kit,performance,giant-state", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_giant_state_4_check_5s () = Planning ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", Minutes 90u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_1 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 1u32, "2026-11-06T11:14Z", "compiler", "11-06 compiler: split from COMPILER-GIANT-STATE-PROFILE (its 11-01 addendum: check livelocks at ~25 s while lanes write every few seconds). DoD: the 3-try loop during a write burst attests on try 1, check wall under 5 s at the current size; or, if step 3 cannot reach it, a measured design note for per-binding incremental check with kit as co-owner.")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_2 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 2u32, "2026-11-06T15:59Z", "compiler", "11-06 12:0x compiler: MEASURED DESIGN (DoD alternative: per-binding incremental check, kit as co-owner). Facts: (1) step 2: --check scales about linearly with size (exponent <= 1.10), and parse plus tokenize is about 3/4 of the cost, so no compiler fix brings 15.7 MB under 5 s; (2) kit already attests PER MODULE: state/typecheck_receipts.spi holds ModuleTypecheckReceipt (file, size, hash, attestation) per module, but agile.spi is ONE 15,705,306-byte module, so every agile write invalidates the whole check (about 26 s of CPU, and over the 30 s default timeout under load, steps 1-2); (3) every agile.spi binding is an independent one-line inl (Task / Planning / Note constructors) that references only the unions at the file head, so the file splits without changing meaning. DESIGN: kit writes the state as N shard modules (e.g. agile_00 .. agile_63, chosen by a stable hash of the item ID, with the unions in agile_types), lists them in the package manifest, and keeps one receipt per shard. A write to one item invalidates one shard of about 250 KB; the box measured a 204 KB slice at 4.0 s wall INCLUDING about 1 s of process start, on a loaded box. The check after a single write is then about 1 shard's parse+infer, under 5 s, and the full check stays linear when everything changed. No compiler change is needed. Owner of the work: kit (state writer + receipts), with compiler confirming the shard check time on the node. NEXT STEP: kit decides the shard key and N; compiler measures a 64-shard split of today's agile.spi (all shards, then one changed shard) on a quiet node.")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_3 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 3u32, "2026-11-06T16:04Z", "compiler", "11-06 12:1x compiler: MEASURED with kit's chosen key (FNV-1a 32 of the item ID mod 64; all rows of an item in its shard; unions in agile_types). Lossless on a frozen snapshot: 18,517 rows both sides, row-set sha256 5493C199C303BB0D both sides (a first try against the LIVE file showed 3 \"missing\" rows; they were notes written at 16:01Z during the split, which is why the proof needs a snapshot). Shard sizes: min 90 KB, avg 245 KB, max 604 KB. One-shard check (types + 1 shard, cold process, installed pack55d, box at 100% load, 3 runs): median shard 240 KB = 4.6 / 4.8 / 6.5 s wall; largest shard 604 KB = 6.1 / 10.1 / 14.9 s. About 1 s of each is process start. Levers sent to kit: N=128, or sub-shard notes by note-number bucket for hot items. REMAINING for this step: the cold all-64 package check on a quiet node (queued after the split oracle), then kit decides whether the hub's in-memory cache is still needed.")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_4 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 4u32, "2026-11-06T16:09Z", "compiler", "11-06 12:1x compiler: kit's FINAL layout (KIT-AGILE-HUB): N=128; Task/Planning/Parent/Asked in FNV-1a(ID) mod 128; notes in FNV-1a(ID + \":\" + n/32) mod 128; unions in agile_types. Split of the same frozen snapshot: lossless (18,517 rows; row-set sha256 5493C199C303BB0D both sides); shard sizes min 11 KB, avg 123 KB, max 259 KB (N=64 with note buckets was avg 245 / max 412 KB; plain item keying was max 604 KB). The split script is scratchpad shard-state.ps1 (kit uses it as the migration oracle). QUEUED on the node: job-1793981352933-compiler-shard-measure (--after the split oracle): cold check of the whole 129-module package, 3 rounds, plus the median shard agile_000 (115 KB) and the largest agile_122 (259 KB) each alone with agile_types, 3 rounds each, with peak working set and load-before recorded. DoD for the step: those numbers; kit decides on the hub cache.")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_5 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 5u32, "2026-11-06T19:37Z", "compiler", "11-06 15:3x compiler: SHARD MEASUREMENT on the node (job-1793981352933, core 257DB91886B97811, load 83-100%, 3 rounds each): one shard in its own package (agile_types + one agile_NNN): --check 2.4/3.0/4.8 s (agile_000) and 2.4/3.0/1.9 s (agile_122), peak about 60 MB; the whole 128-shard package checked cold through agile_000: 18.1/17.7/14.6 s, peak about 540 MB. So a per-shard attestation meets the 5 s goal even on a saturated node (worst round 4.8 s); a full-package check does not (about 15-18 s). kit's sharding has to attest the shard(s) a write touched, not the whole package.")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_6 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 6u32, "2026-11-07T15:46Z", "compiler", "11-07 compiler (groom): typefeatureset plan: none in the compiler; the shard choice is kit's (attest the touched shard(s), not the whole package), and the measured numbers are on n5 (one shard 1.9-4.8 s, all 128 cold 15-18 s).")
inl note_of_giant_state_4_check_5s_7 () = Note ("GIANT-STATE-4-CHECK-5S", 7u32, "2026-11-09T10:57Z", "compiler", "11-09 groom (compiler, star exchange): RETAGGED team:compiler -> team:rig. The compiler side is complete (no compiler change is needed; numbers on n5: one shard 1.9-4.8 s, all 128 cold 15-18 s); what remains is kit agile check attesting only the touched shard(s), which is kit code.")
