inl transformer_mojo_baseline () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", "transformer: measure llama2.mojo against the typed transformer (v8) and llama2.c (run, runfast, runomp) on ONE Linux host (a 4-core codespace; Mojo needs Linux, the node is Windows and europa's 2 CPUs are too noisy), stories15M/42M/110M, 256 greedy tokens, quiet-gated interleaved rounds, single thread and 4 threads", Story, P1, D3, 55u32, Active, "", "tibia,team:tibia,transformer,typefeatureset,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_mojo_baseline () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_mojo_baseline_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia (groomed from main's relay of the user's 11-07 direction: \"can typefeatureset make magic so the transformer beats the Mojo one; otherwise what are we even doing here\"). Why first: llama2.mojo has never run on our hardware; the only numbers are its README's (Xeon 4 vCPU stories15M: c-1t 105, mojo-j1 154, c-omp4 292, mojo-j4 327 tok/s, TRANSFORMER-MOJO-STUDY), and our typed v8 is measured only against llama2.c on the Windows node (v3 = runfast single-thread; v8 beats runomp8 by 3.7 pct on 42M). DoD: one table on one host, all arms with the same tokens (parity: typed and llama2.c byte-identical; Mojo's first 200 chars recorded), medians and ranges per arm, the harness committed as a scratch script outside the repos, and the gap to Mojo stated per thread count. NEXT: create the codespace, install Mojo (pixi/modular), clone llama2.mojo and llama2.c, build the typed C with the same gcc flags.")
inl note_of_transformer_mojo_baseline_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", 2u32, "2026-11-07T22:09Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:1x tibia: STEP 1 (feasibility plus parity) DONE on europa (Linux, 2 vCPU). llama2.mojo (github tairov/llama2.mojo, commit b86ad44 of 2026-09-20, \"Update for Mojo 1.1 compatibility\") builds in 11 s with Mojo 1.2.0 (2016d0cd) plus MAX 26.7.0 from PyPI, in venv ~/venvs/mojo (1.2 GB; the plain mojo wheel lacks the `max` package that llama2.mojo imports). PARITY: `./llama2 stories15M.bin -t 0 -n 256 -j 1` prints text byte-identical to our typed v3 (node tf-v3.exe, OMP 1): 812 characters, no difference (stories15M.bin hashes the same on both hosts, size 60816028). Indicative speed only: 153.8 tok/s with one worker at europa loadavg 2.46, near the README's mojo-j1 154; not a measurement. NEXT STEP: the DoD's one-host table. europa has only 2 vCPUs, so it can give the single-thread row (where Mojo leads, 1.47x per the README) if quiet-gated with 16 interleaved rounds and an A/A pair (see memory ab-quiet-gate-per-run): llama2.mojo -j 1 vs our v8 C built on europa with the same gcc -Ofast -march=native vs llama2.c runfast. The 4-thread row still needs a 4-core Linux host (a codespace), which is not created yet.")
inl note_of_transformer_mojo_baseline_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", 3u32, "2026-11-07T22:11Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:1x tibia: PRE-REGISTERED, the single-thread row on europa (written before any timed run). Arms, all on europa (Ubuntu, gcc 15.2.0, 2 vCPU), stories15M, greedy, 256 steps, same tokenizer.bin: MOJO = llama2.mojo -j 1 (Mojo 1.2.0 + MAX 26.7.0); V8 = our typed v8 C (v8.c BDD375D03DF8, gcc -Ofast -march=native -fopenmp, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1); RUNFAST = llama2.c run.c 9C4F2D5C6AE0 built with gcc -Ofast -march=native; plus an A/A arm V8B (the same binary run again in each round). Parity first: all four print the same 812 characters, else VOID. Gate before EVERY run: /proc/stat idle at least 90 pct over three consecutive 2 s windows, up to 5 min per run, VOID after 20 failed waits. 16 rounds, arm order rotated each round. Metric: wall ms per run (the 60 MB load included in every arm). Noise band: the median |V8 - V8B| across rounds. Prediction: V8 within 10 pct of RUNFAST (the node gap was about 9 pct); MOJO within 20 pct of RUNFAST either way (the README's 1.47x lead was over plain run, not runfast). Decision: the single-thread gap is median(V8) / median(MOJO). At or below 1 + (noise band / median MOJO): the typed transformer matches Mojo single-thread on this host. Above it: the gap, in percent, is the target for TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO's next lever. No arm, flag or round count changes after a look.")
inl note_of_transformer_mojo_baseline_4 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", 4u32, "2026-11-07T22:25Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:3x tibia: SINGLE-THREAD ROW MEASURED (europa, 16 of 16 quiet-gated rounds, 0 skips, parity: all four arms print the same 809 characters; scratch tf9/mojo-sweep.sh and mojo_verdict.py; binaries mojo 497b890ea677, v8 15fc0b0cd515, runfast e6e6b8770a7a, stories15M cd590644d963). Wall ms per 256-token run, model load included: MOJO median 1048.5 (range 866-1259); V8 1108.0 (876-1659); RUNFAST 1020.5 (863-1301); A/A V8B 1040.5 (917-1203). Noise band, the median |V8 - V8B|: 126.5 ms. VERDICT by the pre-registered rule: MATCHES MOJO, with gap median(V8) / median(MOJO) = 1.057 against a threshold of 1.121. READ WITH THE CAVEAT: the A/A band is about 12 pct, so this host cannot resolve a gap below about 12 pct either way; V8 beat MOJO in 5 of 16 paired rounds. What it does show: on this 2-vCPU host the Mojo single-thread lead the README reports (1.47x) does not appear against -Ofast C. MOJO is +2.7 pct vs RUNFAST, and V8 is +8.6 pct vs RUNFAST (the node's about 9 pct gap again). Both predictions held (V8 within 10 pct of RUNFAST, MOJO within 20 pct). NEXT STEP: the 4-thread row on a 4-core Linux host. The noise lesson for it: the band must be tighter than the effect, so use more tokens per run (-n 1024, so load is a smaller share) or more rounds.")
inl note_of_transformer_mojo_baseline_5 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE", 5u32, "2026-11-08T21:30Z", "tibia", "11-08 17:3x tibia groom: the open 4-thread row needed a 4-core host; ganymede has 4 vCPU (vc2, shared Skylake). NEXT STEP: run the llama2.mojo / typed transformer / llama2.c rows at -t 4 on ganymede with an A/A arm; label the row \"vc2 shared vCPU\", not \"4 dedicated cores\".")
inl transformer_typed_simd_dot () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", "transformer, lever 1: a lib dot product over sized rows whose lane count is a type literal (default 16, as llama2.mojo's nelts) with K independent accumulators, specialized at compile time to the row length (288/512/768 are literals, so partial evaluation emits exact chunks: no remainder loop, no bounds checks), letting gcc -O3 -march=native vectorize it; first used in the transformer's matmul rows", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "tibia,team:tibia,transformer,typefeatureset,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_typed_simd_dot () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl transformer_beat_mojo () = Task ("TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO", "transformer: beat llama2.mojo on the same Linux host at 1 and 4 threads with the typed transformer, through type-level levers (the user's 11-07 question: can typefeatureset make magic here)", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE,TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT", "tibia,team:tibia,transformer,typefeatureset,spiral", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_transformer_beat_mojo () = Planning ("TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO", Minutes 960u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_transformer_beat_mojo_1 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:04Z", "tibia", "11-07 tibia: GROOMED. Gap on paper (Mojo README, Xeon 4 vCPU, stories15M): single thread Mojo 1.47x llama2.c; 4 threads 1.12x llama2.c-omp. Ours on the node: single thread = runfast, 8 threads 3.7 pct faster than runomp8 (42M). So the work is mostly single-thread. TYPED LEVERS, in order: (1) TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT: lanes as a type literal plus K accumulators, chunking fixed at compile time by literal dims (the main gap); (2) fuse w1/w3 in the FFN as QKV was fused (Mojo's batch_matmul fuses up to 3 outputs per pass over x); (3) one persistent worker pool per forward pass with a barrier between stages (Mojo's design) instead of a parallel region per matmul; (4) attention split by heads across threads; (5) static memory plan (LIB-ARENA-PLANNER: one arena, 16-byte aligned views, so vector loads can be aligned); (6) explicit vector codegen (COMPILER-SIMD-CODEGEN) only if (1)'s auto-vectorized form leaves a gap. Each lever is measured as a v-next A/B on the node and then on the Mojo host. DoD: on TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE's host, typed tok/s >= llama2.mojo at 1 and 4 threads on stories15M and 42M, token parity kept, compile time published beside run time. Honest note: if (1) to (5) leave a gap, the item records it with numbers rather than claiming parity.")
inl note_of_transformer_beat_mojo_2 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO", 2u32, "2026-11-07T15:46Z", "tibia", "11-07 11:4x tibia groom (lint markers). Next step: TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE first (one Linux codespace, Mojo vs typed v8 vs llama2.c at 1 and 4 threads); no lever work before that table exists.")
inl note_of_transformer_beat_mojo_3 () = Note ("TRANSFORMER-BEAT-MOJO", 3u32, "2026-11-07T22:25Z", "tibia", "11-07 18:3x tibia: input from TRANSFORMER-MOJO-BASELINE (note 4). On one Linux host (europa, 2 vCPU, gcc 15.2), single-threaded on stories15M, our typed v8 is within noise of llama2.mojo -j 1 (Mojo 1.2): medians 1108 vs 1049 ms, A/A band 126 ms, byte-identical text. The README's 1.47x single-thread Mojo lead was over plain run.c; against -Ofast C it is gone (Mojo +2.7 pct vs runfast). Together with TRANSFORMER-TYPED-SIMD-DOT's finding that v8's single-thread loop is memory-bandwidth-bound (about 7 GB/s on 15M and 42M alike), the room left on one thread is small; the remaining Mojo comparison that matters is multi-thread (4 workers), which needs a 4-core host.")
