inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:06Z", "fiscal", "11-07 fiscal: step 2 of the modeling ceiling. DoD: the per-family table from FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-1-TABLE is handed to kit's modeling-topology metric in the format kit agrees (one row per family x construct kind, plus the summary), published on the fiscal pages next to reports.md, and trended across runs (the previous run's TSV from the pages vs this one). TYPEFEATURES: none new. Deps: FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-1-TABLE; kit's format.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 2u32, "2026-11-07T11:12Z", "fiscal", "11-07 kit's item is KIT-MODELING-TOPOLOGY (child of KIT-REUSE-METRICS; one typed history row per project per day, never rewritten; its ceiling = the best package in each project). Asked kit for its row schema; fiscal will send both the hand-written-src density figures and the schema-expressible ceiling per generated family.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 3u32, "2026-11-07T11:12Z", "fiscal", "11-07 kit's format (typed Spiral rows in target/ci-metrics/modeling-ceiling.spi, see FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-1-TABLE note 3) means step 2 is publishing that file on the fiscal pages each run (kit keeps the one-row-per-day history and derives % and distance); fiscal's pages show the per-family % computed locally.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 4u32, "2026-11-07T15:45Z", "fiscal", "NEXT: once step 1 writes target/ci-metrics/modeling-ceiling.spi, add it to scripts/publish.ps1's published files and check it appears under gh-pages target/ci-metrics-<os>/ on the next push.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 5u32, "2026-11-07T20:21Z", "fiscal", "11-07 16:2x CORRECTION to the 11:45 NEXT: no publish.ps1 change is needed. gh-pages.yml uploads the WHOLE target/ci-metrics folder as artifact ci-metrics-<os> (lines 55-56) and the push job downloads pattern ci-metrics-* into gh-pages/target (83-84), so every file the ceiling test writes there (modeling-ceiling.spi for kit, modeling-ceiling.tsv for fiscal's reports section) lands under gh-pages/target/ci-metrics-<os>/ beside reports.md. publish.ps1's include list (publish-tree -Include *.tsv ...) only shapes the main dist tree and would additionally copy the .tsv. Trend: kit keeps the one-row-per-day history from the published .spi (FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-1-TABLE note 3), so fiscal's side is the per-family % in reports.md. NEXT: after step 1 lands and the user pushes, check gh-pages target/ci-metrics-ubuntu-24.04/modeling-ceiling.spi exists and type-checks, then tell kit its URL.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 6u32, "2026-11-09T03:55Z", "fiscal", "11-08 STEP DONE on the box (unstaged): scripts/publish.ps1 now includes 'modeling-ceiling.spi' in publish-tree's -Include list. *.tsv was already published, so modeling-ceiling.tsv already went out; the typed-rows .spi kit reads did not. modeling_ceiling.spi (landed 11-08) writes both to target/ci-metrics/ (src/modeling_ceiling.spi:230-231). Proof: the file appears under gh-pages target/ci-metrics-<os>/modeling-ceiling.spi after the user's next push (CI-gated). Then kit's KIT-MODELING-TOPOLOGY reads it, and the per-family % trend on fiscal pages (reports.spi section) is the remaining part of the DoD. NEXT STEP: the reports.spi trend section (previous run's published TSV vs this run's).")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 7u32, "2026-11-09T05:43Z", "fiscal", "11-09 TREND IMPLEMENTED (unstaged; proof pending). Pages confirmed: last run's table is published at https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/target/ci-metrics/modeling-ceiling.tsv (200, 10856 bytes; publish-tree's --others includes gitignored metrics); modeling-ceiling.spi was 404 there, confirming the publish.ps1 include from 11-08. build.ps1 (before reports, in full and merge modes): best-effort download into target/ci-metrics/modeling-ceiling.previous<suffix>.tsv (FISCAL_PAGES_URL overrides the base; any failure logs and continues). reports.spi 2B5D9EBF: a 'previous %' column per family (ceiling_previous_cell over last run's rows; no previous run shows '-'), threaded through ceiling_header/ceiling_row_cells/ceiling_table/ceiling_body/ceiling_section/coverage_sections/rendered; new tests the_ceiling_table_shows_the_previous_run + no_previous_run_shows_a_dash. Cell budget 9 = HEAD (helpers split out), formatter-stable on pack63c. NEXT STEP: Proof: gate src reports (build.ps1 -Files reports on a node; it reads the previous table from pages), then the user's next push shows the column on the pages.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 8u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 reports gate GREEN on ganymede: reports discovered 8 executed 8 passed 8 in 235 s, including the_ceiling_table_shows_the_previous_run and no_previous_run_shows_a_dash; build.ps1 read the previous modeling ceiling from the published site (https://i574n.github.io/fiscal) before the unit, so the previous column is end to end. reports.spi 2B5D9EBF and publish.ps1 35950412 (adds modeling-ceiling.spi to -Include) stay unstaged for the user.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 9u32, "2026-11-09T09:34Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 HANDOFF SENT to rig (KIT-MODELING-TOPOLOGY): the table is live at https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/target/ci-metrics/modeling-ceiling.tsv (HTTP 200, 318 rows, 45 families; columns family, construct, expressible, typed, emitted). project = fiscal, day = the CI run date (deploy commit plus the reports.md stamp); a day column is offered if rig wants it inline. The fiscal-pages trend is the reports gate's previous-run column (8/8 then 9/9 green on ganymede). Remaining: rig's reader, which is rig's item.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 10u32, "2026-11-09T09:45Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 rig asked for an explicit day column (rows must stand alone once archived/merged; its reader keys on project, day, family, construct). DONE: src/modeling_ceiling.spi 49F4D223 (unstaged): ceiling_tsv_line and ceiling_tsv take the day; the header is family, construct, expressible, typed, emitted, day (appended LAST so reports.spi's positional reader of columns 0-3 is unaffected); write_modeling_ceiling computes ceiling_day () once and passes it to both the .spi and the .tsv. Cell budget unchanged (12 before and after). Proven on ganymede: each_construct_is_typed_per_emitted_definition and writes_the_modeling_ceiling_for_every_family passed; the written tsv header ends in day, rows carry 2026-11-09, and 0 rows have a column count other than 6. Rig told. Publishes on the user's next push.")
inl note_of_fiscal_modeling_ceiling_2_export_11 () = Note ("FISCAL-MODELING-CEILING-2-EXPORT", 11u32, "2026-11-09T11:35Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Wait: when the user pushes fiscal (day column publishes) and lane rig (KIT-MODELING-TOPOLOGY reader)")
inl cells_bench_codespace () = Task ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", "Cells vs dib benchmark harness on a codespace: the same notebooks under both runners, in-job A/A, per-phase medians as typed rows", TaskKind, P1, D3, 25u32, Active, "", "team:microblog,cells,bench,codespace", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_bench_codespace () = Planning ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_1 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:07Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:07 x: user 11-07 via main: 'benchmarks on codespaces, reliable numbers, no waiting for CI'. A codespace has quiet, dedicated CPUs, unlike the node (shared, 100% loaded) and the box. Both runners install there: dotnet-interactive/dotnet-repl for dib (the node cannot install those old tool builds, per ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH n4), and Cells. DoD: (1) a driver that clones and overlays the tree (main's codespace driver pattern) and runs N=5 rounds of each notebook under each runner with an A/A pair in the same job; (2) per-phase rows (VM start, first cell, warm cell, compile, native build, output) as a typed Spiral record; (3) medians plus spread published on this item. Shared harness for ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH (main) and CELLS-SCALING-BENCH (compiler).")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_2 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 2u32, "2026-11-07T22:03Z", "x-b7595ac3", "2026-11-07 18:1x x: DoD (2) drafted, unproven: drafts\\spiral\\CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE\\bench_rows.spi 412772A1E9F3. Typed rows: bench_row { runner Dib|Cells; notebook; cells; round; arm ArmA|ArmB (the in-job A/A pair); phase VmStart|Parse|PrefixCompile|Codegen|NativeBuild|FirstCell|WarmCell|Output (the DoD phases plus compiler's CELLS-SCALING-BENCH ones); ms }. One tab line per row (lowercase case names) with a strict reader (bad phase or a non-digit count reads as None). summary_of gives median, low, high, n via list_sort::sorted_by; aa_gap is the absolute gap between the two arms' medians, the noise floor any runner-vs-runner delta must beat. 4 gleam tests. Open: (1) the codespace driver. No codespace exists on this account (gh codespace list is empty); creating one bills, so it needs main or the user to say which repo/machine type. (2) Prove this module on the node with the Cells runner.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_3 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T19:45Z", "microblog", "org3 11-08: comet (codespaces) is this item's node, go. BLOCKER: gh codespace list on the box returns [] (no codespace on this gh account), though the fleet roster lists comet-codespaces; asked org3 which account/context holds it and how lanes reach it (remote.ps1 line 21 or relay-lab). Not creating a codespace from this lane (new billing, unknown account). NEXT STEP (CPU-light, no comet needed): write the driver: clone + overlay the tree, N=5 rounds per notebook under dib and under Cells with the in-job A/A pair, rows in the bench_row tab format of drafts CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE bench_rows.spi.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_4 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 4u32, "2026-11-08T19:46Z", "microblog", "BLOCKER CLEARED (org3 11-08): comet is EPHEMERAL, a capability, not a standing host. Pattern: gh codespace create -R fc1943s/dotfiles -m basicLinux32gb (2 cores, free tier, the box's own gh account fc1943s, the one remote.ps1 line 21 uses) -> gh codespace cp the driver and inputs -> gh codespace ssh to run -> delete after the rows land. org3 offers to spin and tear it down on a ping. NEXT STEP: finish the driver (clone + overlay, N=5 per notebook under dib and Cells with the in-job A/A pair, bench_row rows), then ping org3 or create it directly, and always delete it after.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_5 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 5u32, "2026-11-08T19:49Z", "microblog", "CORRECTION (user 11-08: \"don't we have memories or tasks or rig stuff about these needing to be created on demand then deleted?\"). Yes: KIT-CODESPACE-RUN (team:rig, 11-06) specifies the on-demand verb (create with --idle-timeout 120m --retention-period 1h, tools, overlay, steps, fetch, delete verified gone, usage row), from 9 proven scratch drivers in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\i574n.github\\CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS. This item does NOT get a new driver: it copies the dice-cs4 drive.ps1 + remote.sh pair, adds the dib vs Cells N=5 A/A steps, and becomes the 10th reference driver for KIT-CODESPACE-RUN. No hand-created codespace; sweep gh codespace ssh leftovers after (memory sweep-leftover-shells). Memory codespace-on-demand-pattern records this.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_6 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T00:06Z", "microblog", "comet is ready (rig 11-08 ~20:05, kit job start --on comet, 2 slots, no compiler: bring the toolchain). NOT queued in batch 1 on purpose (~38 free core-h left this month): the dib half is not runnable yet. Prerequisites (ERL-VS-DIB-BENCH n4/n5): dotnet-interactive 1.0.712001 + dotnet-repl 0.3.259 need an older .NET runtime (the node install failed for that reason), and no Spiral .dib notebooks remain, so the dib side of the 3 notebooks (small, lib physics, x client) must be recreated. NEXT STEP (box, CPU-light): one comet driver = the proven codespace-spiral-setup.sh bootstrap + a .NET 8 runtime + the pinned old tools + recreated .dib notebooks + both halves N=5 with an in-job A/A, emitting bench_row lines (drafts bench_rows.spi); target batch 2 as ONE job.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_7 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 7u32, "2026-11-09T03:34Z", "microblog", "FINDING 11-09 (autonomous run): the dib side needs a Spiral kernel for dotnet-interactive, and the ONLY one is in polyglot's fork: polyglot/deps/dotnet-interactive/src/Microsoft.DotNet.Interactive.Spiral (Microsoft.DotNet.Interactive.Spiral.fsproj). spiral has none (only Cells' dib renderers and tests mention it). polyglot is slated for salvage-then-delete (org), so asked org to keep the kernel buildable in the salvage (kernel project + fork commit + the box's pinned tool versions dotnet-interactive 1.0.712001 / dotnet-repl 0.3.259). BLOCKER: the dib half of the bench until that salvage location exists. The Cells half and the comet bootstrap (codespace-spiral-setup.sh) do not depend on it.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_8 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 8u32, "2026-11-09T04:50Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 UNBLOCKED (org salvage reply): the kernel SOURCE is saved non-public at C:\\home\\data\\archive\\polyglot-spiral-dib-kernel (fsproj, SpiralKernel.fs, SALVAGE-NOTE.txt with the rebuild recipe). The dir alone is not buildable (ProjectReferences to the fork's Interactive and Formatting projects plus NuGet). The buildable unit is the fc1943s/polyglot fork at 053c128d (on github-fc1943s/main-i574n; fc1943s is the frozen dump, so it stays), kernel at deps/dotnet-interactive/src/Microsoft.DotNet.Interactive.Spiral. Batch-2 comet driver outline, ONE kit job on the standing comet (kit job start --on comet, stage under /home/codespace/remote, never /tmp):\n(1) bootstrap from codespace-spiral-setup.sh (spiral + The-Spiral-Language pinned to the box commit, git remote add origin before the fetch);\n(2) .NET 8 runtime beside the 11 SDK for the pinned tools dotnet-interactive 1.0.712001 and dotnet-repl 0.3.259;\n(3) shallow fetch of fc1943s/polyglot at 053c128d and dotnet build of the Spiral kernel only;\n(4) recreate the 3 .dib notebooks (small, lib physics, x client) from their .spi via Cells' dib renderer (Domain to_spi and parse_dib round-trip exist);\n(5) N=5 per side with an in-job A/A, emitting bench_row lines.\nCost guard: about 38 free core-h this month and 2 cores, so the kernel build plus 2x5x3 runs must fit one job under a step budget; abort at exit 64 if a pin is stale. Next: write the remote.sh from the dice-cs4 pair (copy, not new), then queue it.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_9 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 9u32, "2026-11-09T04:52Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 cost finding for the dib half: the salvaged kernel (SpiralKernel.fs, SpiralKernelExtensions.fs) opens Polyglot.Common and attaches to the fork's own Microsoft.DotNet.Interactive build, so step (3) is not a single kernel dll. It builds the fork's dotnet-interactive tool (Formatting + Interactive + the Spiral kernel + polyglot's Common), likely tens of minutes on comet's 2 cores, plus restore. The archive on the box holds only the kernel sources (17 files, no SALVAGE-NOTE.txt yet; asked org). Driver base chosen: the tibia-cs pair (11-08, newest, already has the exact-tree fixes: git remote add origin before the fetch, Language pinned). Its tools/tree steps carry over unchanged with fresh pins and delta tars. New steps: kernel (fork at 053c128d, dotnet build of the tool, .NET 8 runtime), dib (render the 3 notebooks), bench (N=5 per side, in-job A/A). Budget: estimate the kernel build first with a dry restore step before committing the full batch.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_10 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 10u32, "2026-11-09T05:27Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 SALVAGE CORRECTED (git-verified on the box; memory polyglot-spiral-dib-kernel-salvage updated, org told). polyglot's deps/ is gitignored, so 053c128d lacks the kernel. The kernel fsproj compiles spiral/lib/spiral/*.fsx, deleted on 10-08 in 98492a63, so the dib side has been unbuildable since then. Pin set for the comet kernel step, laid out as polyglot/deps/{dotnet-interactive,dotnet-repl,spiral,The-Spiral-Language}: polyglot ee5e2bfd, fc1943s/dotnet-interactive e65cb067, fc1943s/dotnet-repl f8a86d07, spiral 9ca0f0fe, The-Spiral-Language 3cf0b4da. The repl registers SpiralKernel (default kernel spiral) and the compiler runs in-process, so no server is needed. Run with dotnet repl --run nb.dib --exit-after-run. Consequence for the bench: the dib side runs the 10-07 compiler and lib, so the notebooks must be ones that compiled then. Use the x client and lib sources as of 9ca0f0fe or earlier, not today's (the dialect and spelling changed since). Fairness: compare Cells at today's pack63c on today's notebooks vs dib on 10-07 notebooks only as an end-to-end runner comparison, and for a same-source A/B also run Cells on the 10-07 notebooks where it still accepts them. Driver skeleton: the tibia-cs pair (tools/tree unchanged) plus a kernel step (4 fetches, dotnet-install via the fork's global.json, dotnet build deps/dotnet-repl) and a bench step.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_11 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 11u32, "2026-11-09T05:30Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 driver WRITTEN and first slice LAUNCHED: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\i574n.github\\CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS\\cells-bench-cs\\ (drive.ps1 + remote.sh, the 10th reference driver for KIT-CODESPACE-RUN). It targets the SHARED standing comet (animated-umbrella-*, basicLinux32gb), never creates or deletes a codespace, stages under /home/codespace/remote/microblog-bench (clear of rig's layout), and sweeps leftover gh/ssh after. Steps: tools (pwsh, OTP, Elixir, Gleam, skipped when present); tree (today's spiral + delta + compiler + Cells deps, for the Cells half); kernel (the corrected 4+1 pins, which org verified durable with ls-remote; old dotnet via dotnet-install --jsonfile on the fork's global.json; dotnet build of the fork's dotnet-repl); smoke (one #!spiral cell 'inl main () = 40i32 + 2', 3 timed runs of dotnet repl --run --exit-after-run). Launched tools,kernel,smoke only (box background bxv3032nh) to prove the dib path cheaply before spending core-hours on tree and the N=5 bench. Logs return to cells-bench-logs.tgz beside the driver.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_12 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 12u32, "2026-11-09T05:57Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 ~05:50Z: the shared standing comet (animated-umbrella-9rp445g4p2pjrq, Available at 05:31Z) is GONE from gh codespace list; I did not delete it. The driver's first launch failed on step parsing (pwsh -File passes the comma list as one string; fixed: split + unknown-step abort). The relaunch then hit the empty list, which slipped past a -notmatch guard on an empty array (fixed: [string] cast + IsNullOrWhiteSpace abort). Nothing ran on comet, and the box processes were swept. BLOCKED on a comet: per policy no hand-created codespace; rig owns comet (KIT-CODESPACE-RUN). Resume when rig recreates the standing comet or the kit codespace run verb lands.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_13 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 13u32, "2026-11-09T10:45Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "BLOCKER: no comet codespace exists; rig recreates the standing comet only after a successful TRAIN42 (TRAIN42, b and c failed on 11-09; d running). NEXT STEP: when gh codespace list shows animated-umbrella-*, run cells-bench-cs\\drive.ps1 -Steps tools,kernel,smoke, then tree and the N=5 bench.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_14 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 14u32, "2026-11-09T11:33Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "Wait: when gh codespace list shows animated-umbrella-* (rig recreates comet after a green TRAIN42)")
inl note_of_cells_bench_codespace_15 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", 15u32, "2026-11-10T09:38Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:4x GROOM: TRAIN42 is dead (rig re-pointed its own waits to TRAIN43e); rig recreates the comet codespace after a green train, so the wait row now names train:TRAIN43e. gh codespace list at 05:0x: none.")
inl cells_bench_parallel () = Task ("CELLS-BENCH-PARALLEL", "Measure Cells parallel execution against dib limits: many .spi files at once, CPU spread across cores, dependency-ordered scheduling, wasted (duplicated) work", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", "team:microblog,cells,bench,parallel,codespace", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_bench_parallel () = Planning ("CELLS-BENCH-PARALLEL", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_bench_parallel_1 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-PARALLEL", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:07Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:07 x: user 11-07 point 2: 'parallelization, the dependency problem, CPU spread across cores without wasted work when running many .spi files, the coordination the dib way couldn't do'. On the harness: run 1, 4, 16 and 64 .spi notebooks concurrently, with core utilization sampled each second; count duplicate compiles of the same package or crate (the wasted work); compare the dependency-aware order against naive parallel; and run dib's serial FSI chain as the baseline. Output: are we beating dib yet (a yes/no per scale), plus the top 3 fixes.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_parallel_2 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-PARALLEL", 2u32, "2026-11-09T18:28Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:4x microblog groom: dod: for 1, 4, 16 and 64 concurrent .spi notebooks, wall time, per-second core utilization and the count of duplicate compiles of the same package/crate, on the codespace harness. next: run the 4-point sweep once CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE's harness is back (comet parked until TRAIN42 is green).")
inl cells_bench_memo_layers () = Task ("CELLS-BENCH-MEMO-LAYERS", "Measure every Cells memo layer: AST/result memo, compiler single-flight, hopac session memo, crate/build reuse; why crate cells do not reuse across runs", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE", "team:microblog,cells,bench,memo,codespace", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_bench_memo_layers () = Planning ("CELLS-BENCH-MEMO-LAYERS", Minutes 120u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_bench_memo_layers_1 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-MEMO-LAYERS", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:07Z", "x", "2026-11-07 07:07 x: user 11-07 point 2: 'is cell memoization smart, including the compiler's single-flight and hopacsota memo, not only AST?'. Known gap: main measured on 11-06 that crate cells do not reuse across runs (date_time cold 736 s, warm 833 s on the box; CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS n23), so warm was slower than cold. DoD: hit/miss rates per layer for a cold run, a warm rerun, and a one-line edit rerun on the same notebooks; the reason the crate build misses (key contents, target dir per run, or invalidation); one fix item per layer that misses when it should hit.")
inl note_of_cells_bench_memo_layers_2 () = Note ("CELLS-BENCH-MEMO-LAYERS", 2u32, "2026-11-09T18:28Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:4x microblog groom: likely explanation for n1's 'warm slower than cold' (date_time 736 s cold, 833 s warm on 11-06): the Cells result memo was OFF for every notebook run from about 11-06 (nil build_receipt plus Keyword.has_key?, CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS n30), so a 'warm' run then recompiled everything. Re-measure only after that fix lands. dod: hit/miss rates per memo layer (result memo, compiler single-flight, hopac session, crate reuse) for a cold and a warm run of the same notebooks, with the memo fix in. next: rerun the date_time cold/warm pair on the CELLS-BENCH-CODESPACE harness after the fix lands.")
inl toml_format_taplo () = Task ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", "Canonical TOML formatting with taplo at 120 columns and a 4-space indent, checked by kit standards in every repo", TaskKind, P2, D2, 75u32, Active, "", "toml,formatter,standards,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_toml_format_taplo () = Planning ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_1 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:13Z", "main", "User 11-07: are there toml formatters to avoid the 120-column problem? Yes: taplo 0.10.0 (installed on the box via scoop 11-07). taplo fmt --option column_width=120 --option indent_string=\"    \" matches our manifests except for collapsing arrays that fit in 120 onto one line (html_walk link = [...]; fiscal 1 of 84). DoD: an org taplo config (.taplo.toml per repo, or one in .github referenced by kit), kit standards runs taplo fmt --check over every spiral.toml/Cargo.toml, and all repos pass. Next step: write the config, run --check across the 11 repos, apply to main-owned repos, tell the owners. TypeFeatures: none; formatter config.")
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_2 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 2u32, "2026-11-07T20:57Z", "main", "main 11-07 17:0x CONFIG + MAIN REPOS DONE (unstaged).\n.taplo.toml, the same in html, dice and tictactoe:\n- exclude .git/**, **/.git/**, **/manifest.toml (Gleam lock files), **/target/**, deps/**, **/node_modules/**\n- [formatting] column_width = 120, indent_string = 4 spaces, array_auto_collapse = false\nMeasured on 77 tracked toml files in 9 repos: taplo defaults would change 17; array_auto_collapse = false brings it to 13 (it keeps html's hand-written multi-line spiral.toml arrays); excluding Gleam manifest.toml leaves 11.\nAPPLIED to html (Cargo.toml: an over-120 members array expanded), dice (Cargo.toml, ui/Cargo.toml: trailing commas) and tictactoe (5 Cargo.toml).\nProof: taplo fmt --check exits 0 in all three; parsed-JSON identity before (HEAD) vs after: 6/6 files identical; cargo metadata OK for html and dice (tictactoe's fails at HEAD too: lib_spi/supervisor needs a generated fable_modules dir that isn't present).\nTRAP found: without the .git exclude, taplo walks .git/fork/*.toml (the Fork client's own config). Caught and killed before any write; the timestamps prove those files are untouched.\nLEFT: (1) kit standards runs taplo fmt --check (pass explicit git ls-files lists; whole-tree scans walk target/ and take minutes) -> kit; (2) the same .taplo.toml for the other repos: spiral (apps/plot, near/wallet Cargo.toml), dir-tree-html (fc1943s, user's call), kit, x, tibia, fiscal -> owners.")
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_3 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 3u32, "2026-11-07T21:12Z", "fiscal", "11-07 17:0x fiscal (asked by main): html's .taplo.toml copied verbatim into fiscal (untracked, unstaged). taplo 0.10.0: 83 of 84 tracked .toml pass fmt --check; all 84 parse (taplo get -o json, 0 errors). The one exception is generated/generated_cfdi_v4_0/spiral.toml, whose packages array is a 124-char single line written by fiscal's generator (src/generator.spi module_package). It is NOT formatted by hand: the next regen would rewrite it and trip the generated-drift check. Fix in the generator: module_package emits taplo's wrapped array (one entry per line, 4-space indent, trailing comma) past 120 columns, changing only that unit; it lands with fiscal's next generator landing (generator test + node regen gate), since generator.spi carries stacked unlanded drafts tonight. Alternative (not taken): exclude generated/** in fiscal's copy, which would fork the org config.")
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_4 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 4u32, "2026-11-07T21:29Z", "fiscal", "11-07 17:3x fiscal DRAFT for the one non-conforming file: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\fiscal\\TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO\\src\\generator.spi BD5C5A48, stacked on fiscal's LAW generator draft 9670695D (the next generator landing, so it rides that landing's regen + gates). module_package now builds typed entries (package_entries) and emits packages_line, or packages_block when the line passes 120 columns (one entry per line, 4-space indent, trailing comma, closing bracket alone). taplo 0.10.0 with the org .taplo.toml accepts the exact emitted block (fmt --check exit 0 on a probe). Only generated_cfdi_v4_0/spiral.toml crosses 120 today, so the regen changes that one unit file. Tests: the CFDI generator test now expects the wrapped complement lines; new a_long_package_list_wraps_like_taplo pins both forms (one line under 120; wrapped with the trailing comma past it). GRANULAR per-cell 0 overages, formatter applied. PENDING: type check + Cells with that landing (node).")
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_5 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 5u32, "2026-11-07T21:48Z", "main", "main 11-07 17:5x: .taplo.toml is now also in regex, blog, alphabet and i574n.github; every main-owned repo carries it. Their tracked toml (plus blog's untracked spiral.toml) already passed taplo fmt --check, with parsed-JSON identity before/after, so nothing was rewritten. spiral's 2 Cargo.toml were applied earlier (semantics identical). fiscal adopted the config (83/84 pass; the 1 failure is generated, and the fix is in its generator's module_package, folded into its next generator land). Owners x, tibia and kit (kit standards integration after TRAIN38) were told.")
inl note_of_toml_format_taplo_6 () = Note ("TOML-FORMAT-TAPLO", 6u32, "2026-11-07T21:50Z", "fiscal", "11-07 17:4x TYPE CHECK GREEN (europa, core 79a209d7eb6c5540): the fiscal generator draft BD5C5A48 (taplo-wrapped module_package) passes --check as a module and with all 27 test bodies as definitions (incl. a_long_package_list_wraps_like_taplo and the updated CFDI package test). Execution and the regen that rewrites generated_cfdi_v4_0/spiral.toml come with fiscal's next generator landing (node).")
