inl lib_host_binding_files () = Task ("LIB-HOST-BINDING-FILES", "Pilot: move one lib module's backend bindings out of the generic module into per-backend binding files, so names lose language prefixes and porting or dead-binding detection becomes mechanical", TaskKind, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,structure,layers,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_host_binding_files () = Planning ("LIB-HOST-BINDING-FILES", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_host_binding_files_1 () = Note ("LIB-HOST-BINDING-FILES", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:14Z", "main", "User 11-07: shouldn't lib be more generic instead of prepending the language's name to functions (gleam_byte_size, env_gleam_argv, rust_metadata_len...)? Is it like the rec-marker problem; should bindings move to files matching each language's path, making rewrites and absorptions mechanical and unused code detectable, without becoming a chore like Bend's laws proofs? main's judgment: yes, the prefix is a symptom of host bindings living inside generic modules. Generic names stay in the layer module (file_system.read_text) and their run_target/backend_switch arms call a binding module per backend; the file says the language, so the prefix goes. CAUTION: the user said on 11-01 to keep lib flat (no subfolders unless they pay off), so the pilot measures both shapes on file_system: flat siblings vs host/<backend>/ folders, by naming churn, census clarity and dead-binding detection, and the user's rule stands unless the pilot shows a big payoff. Includes the duplicate read_lossy host macro (html walk.spi:23 vs lib xml.read_text_replacing_invalid_utf8, also an os function in the wrong layer). DoD: the pilot table plus one landed module. Next step: inventory file_system's backend-prefixed names and host blocks. TypeFeatures: backend as the closed union the compiler already has.")
inl org_dep_chain () = Task ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", "Repo dependency chain without upward edges: spiral (base) must not reference x, tibia or other product repos, keeping today's pipeline wins", TaskKind, P2, D4, 80u32, Active, "", "ci,org,repos,team:org", "current", "current")
