inl note_of_org_dep_chain_1 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 1u32, "2026-11-07T11:14Z", "main", "User 11-07: can the repos' dependency chain be made smarter, without spiral having to reference x, tibia etc., while not losing what we achieved pipeline-wise? kit derives the graph (KIT-REPO-GRAPH flags base-to-product edges); main owns the CI design. DoD: 0 upward references from spiral/lib to product repos; each flagged edge moved to its consumer (product CI pulls base, never the reverse), or replaced by a generic hook; CI green on all repos after the user's push. Next step: take KIT-REPO-GRAPH's first edge list and classify each edge (test corpus, workflow trigger, doc link, code import).")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_2 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 2u32, "2026-11-09T15:23Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 11:3x microblog took the next step without waiting for KIT-REPO-GRAPH: an edge list by grep over spiral's tracked files (compiler tmp/samples and lockfiles excluded), matching ../, git/, home/git/, i574n/ or github.com/i574n/ followed by a product repo name (x, microblog, tibia, fiscal, dice, alphabet, regex, blog, html, kit, rig): 20 hits in 6 files. CLASSIFIED. REAL UPWARD EDGES (2): (a) TEST CORPUS: apps/cells/test/spiral/document_test.exs @dice ../../../../../dice and alphabet ../../../../../alphabet/apps, document round trips over the sibling repos (the known DocumentTest mirror gap). Move to the consumers: dice and alphabet CI run the round trip on their own files with Cells pinned, and spiral keeps a small in-repo fixture. (b) TOOLING: apps/compiler/scripts/machine-watch.ps1:3 $kit = ../../../../kit/kit.exe, a base script invoking a product binary by sibling path; it also breaks at the kit->rig folder rename (KIT-NEXT-NAME-R4). Use the installed binary on PATH (Get-Command) or an env override instead. FIXTURE STRINGS ONLY (not dependencies, 4 files): apps/zed/src/server.rs tests 307-346 ('C:/home/git/kit' candidate paths, also a personal path in a pushable repo; use a neutral root such as C:/work/repo), cells directives_test.exs 106-107 ('../dice' as a package example), mounts_name_test.exs 7 ('C:/home/git/tibia/target/zig-oracle-c'), mount_text_parity_test.exs 87/95 ('../x/y/', generic). Workflows: no spiral .github file names a product repo. dod progress: 2 real edges to move, 4 cosmetic renames. next: (b) is a one-line heal for compiler's script; (a) needs the dice/alphabet CI owners.")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_3 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 3u32, "2026-11-09T16:12Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 12:1x microblog: edge (b) is already gone in the working tree. apps/compiler/scripts/machine-watch.ps1 is deleted (unstaged ' D', another lane's cleanup; no tracked consumer names it). Once the user pushes that deletion, the base-to-product tooling edge is 0. For the record, the spiral convention for finding kit is LOCALAPPDATA\\kit\\bin\\kit.exe with a Get-Command kit fallback (cells_land_window.ps1:48, lib_land_window.ps1:20-21, run_notebook.ps1:48, install-pack.ps1:92 with KIT_BIN on Linux), never a sibling-repo path. Remaining: edge (a), the dice/alphabet test corpus in document_test.exs.")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_4 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 4u32, "2026-11-09T17:59Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:0x microblog (Cells owner), edge (a) SPIRAL SIDE DONE (unstaged): the two document_test.exs tests that read the sibling repos ('alphabet: documents is a .spi source that writes back byte for byte' and 'dice's Spiral sources are .spi files ... write back byte for byte') are tagged @tag :sibling_repos and read their roots from Spiral.Cells.TestHelpers.dice_root/alphabet_root, i.e. SPIRAL_CELLS_DICE_ROOT / SPIRAL_CELLS_ALPHABET_ROOT, falling back to the sibling path. test_helper.exs excludes :sibling_repos when either corpus is absent, so a spiral-only checkout (CI, node mirrors) reports them as excluded instead of failing (the old 'DocumentTest mirror gap'), and nothing in spiral requires a product repo to pass. CONSUMER SIDE (owners of dice/alphabet CI): run the round trip with their checkout as the root: SPIRAL_CELLS_DICE_ROOT=<dice> (or ALPHABET) mix test --only sibling_repos in deps/spiral/apps/cells. Lines 99/129 are '../dice' strings inside fixtures, not dependencies. Parse-checked; the europa per-test suite mirror carries it (no siblings there, so it exercises the exclusion).")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_5 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 5u32, "2026-11-09T18:10Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 18:2x CONSUMER SIDE, plan + one blocker. dice and alphabet are org repos, so org2 adds the round-trip step to their entry scripts (dice lib/build.ps1 already runs Cells; alphabet's build likewise), not to YAML: in deps/spiral/apps/cells, SPIRAL_CELLS_DICE_ROOT=<dice checkout> mix test <document_test path> --only dice_corpus (alphabet: SPIRAL_CELLS_ALPHABET_ROOT, --only alphabet_corpus). BLOCKER in the spiral half (Cells test helper, microblog's): test_helper.exs:10 sibling_repos? requires BOTH corpora, so in dice's CI (no alphabet checkout) dice's own test would be excluded and the step would prove nothing. Asked microblog for one tag per corpus (:dice_corpus / :alphabet_corpus), each excluded only when its own root is missing. Then: the two consumer steps + a green consumer CI after the user's push closes the item (0 upward edges: tooling edge (b) already gone, corpus edge (a) moved to its consumers).")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_6 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 6u32, "2026-11-09T18:11Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:1x microblog, per org2 (the consumer CIs have only their own repo): ONE TAG PER CORPUS. document_test.exs tags the alphabet round trip @tag :alphabet_corpus (line 321) and the dice one @tag :dice_corpus (line 526). test_helper.exs has dice_corpus? (dice_root/lib/dice.spi) and alphabet_corpus? (alphabet_root/apps/documents), and excludes each tag only when ITS root is missing. Consumer step (org2 wiring), in deps/spiral/apps/cells: SPIRAL_CELLS_DICE_ROOT=<dice> mix test test/spiral/document_test.exs --only dice_corpus, and the same with SPIRAL_CELLS_ALPHABET_ROOT and --only alphabet_corpus. Parse-checked; the europa mirror updated.")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_7 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 7u32, "2026-11-09T18:14Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 18:3x CONSUMER STEPS WRITTEN (unstaged, parse 0) after microblog split the tag (test/spiral/document_test.exs: @tag :dice_corpus line 526, :alphabet_corpus line 321; each excluded only when its own root is missing). dice/lib/build.ps1 and alphabet/apps/documents/build.ps1, right after their notebook run (skipped with -fast/-SkipNotebook like it): set SPIRAL_CELLS_DICE_ROOT / SPIRAL_CELLS_ALPHABET_ROOT to the checkout, run `mix test test/spiral/document_test.exs --only dice_corpus|alphabet_corpus` in the Cells dir (mix.bat on Windows, as cells_land_window does), print the tail, and FAIL CLOSED on a non-zero exit OR when no test ran (`N tests, 0 failures` with N >= 1 required), so a re-excluded tag cannot pass silently. Backups in drafts\\i574n.github\\READ-DIR-NAMES-ALIAS. Proof next: both commands on ganymede's proof tree with microblog's two test files synced; then CI green after the user's push closes the item.")
inl note_of_org_dep_chain_8 () = Note ("ORG-DEP-CHAIN", 8u32, "2026-11-09T20:20Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 20:2x consumer proof (ganymede job org2-swap5, exit 0): the corpus edges now live in their consumers. dice/lib/build.ps1 and alphabet/apps/documents/build.ps1 run spiral's document round trip over their own checkout (mix test test/spiral/document_test.exs --only dice_corpus | alphabet_corpus, fail closed on 0 tests): dice rc=0 1 passed, alphabet rc=0 1 passed. Same job: regex full build green, Cells gate 131/131 over 22 files (lib regex_* modules gated from regex's build, regex_xsd included). Remaining DoD item: CI green on all repos after the user's push (spiral, dice, alphabet, regex, i574n.github).")
