inl kit_standards_portability () = Task ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", "kit standards portability rules for platform-bound paths and suffixes", Story, P2, D2, 40u32, Planned, "", "kit,team:rig,standards,portability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_standards_portability () = Planning ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_1 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 1u32, "2026-11-07T13:04Z", "kit-fc1568a6", "11-07 kit groom of the old eoie dump. SOURCE: drafts/kit/KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM/agile_eoie.md (EOIE consolidated pendencies, 2026-10-22), entry CENSUS-3 (portability); parked on 10-24 by KIT-CONSOLIDATED-INTAKE, now relevant because kit jobs run on Linux workers (europa, codespaces) and kit standards has no portability rule. DoD: kit standards on a fixture package reports one finding each for a drive-letter absolute path, a POSIX-only absolute path, a literal .exe suffix and a hard-coded path separator in code, and zero findings on a clean package; config paths and test fixtures named as such are exempt. MILESTONES: four probes in kit_style_probes 60 m; rule wiring and fixture test 60 m. NEXT STEP: add the probes beside the existing census words and run them over kit to size the baseline. TYPEFEATURESET PLAN: portability_hazard union (DrivePath, PosixPath, ExeSuffix, Separator), findings as typed rows.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_2 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:15Z", "main", "INLINE TEXT of the old agile_eoie dump entries (user 11-07: keep the lessons inline so the dump file link can be lost). Source: eoie-consolidated agile_eoie.md (2026-10-22, status measured at eoie a831bad), copies at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM\\ and i574n.github\\recovered\\agile-eoie\\from-des073\\eoie-consolidated\\; verdicts from KIT-EOIE-DUMP-GROOM n3. Verdict Import (this item was created from it).")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_3 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 3u32, "2026-11-09T15:31Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 11:4x microblog, baseline SIZING (the next step; grep over kit/src/*.spi, test blocks NOT yet excluded, so these are upper bounds). DrivePath (a string starting X:\\ or X:/): 162 hits in 38 files; the largest are agile_todo_model/roots.spi 15, rig_net_crossing 10, agile_todo_cache 9, rig_hook_rules 9, rig_path_first 9, rig_tree_lease 9, rig_job_coordinator 8, rig_env_pins 7. Many look like test fixtures, so the probe must skip :# blocks. PosixPath (\"/home|usr|tmp|root|etc|var|opt/\"): 38 in 25 files, mostly the *_policy_probe/*_negative_probe mains plus rust_std_fs and rig_process boundary_probe, which are fixtures by role (exempt by name, as the DoD allows). ExeSuffix (a literal .exe\"): 78 in 37 files: cold_proof_domain/native_binary_tests 11, rig_env_pins 8, rig_job_escaped 7; the real hazards are probably the few outside tests (rig_command_line, rig_job_bin, rig_run_build, rig_os_launch). Separator (a \"\\\\\\\\\" or '\\\\' literal): 141 in 95 files, but most are the normalization itself (replace backslash with /, escape handling), not hazards. So a Separator probe needs context: flag a backslash only inside a path join (++# between path parts), not as a replace argument. next: probes in kit_style_probes with :# block skipping, then a real baseline per kind.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_4 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 4u32, "2026-11-09T19:14Z", "tibia", "11-09 15:1x tibia (help pool): FIELD CASES from one day of mixed Windows/Linux work, as fixture candidates for the four probes. (1) A platform-bound DIRECTORY API: remote.ps1:177 used [Environment]::GetFolderPath('LocalApplicationData'), which is ~/.local/share on Linux, so node status called a working compiler missing (KIT-REMOTE-STATUS-COMPILER-PROBE; fixed by rig with LOCALAPPDATA ?? XDG_CACHE_HOME ?? $HOME/.cache). This is a fifth probe kind the DoD does not list: OS-specific special-folder calls. (2) A tool-resolution trap: on moon, `tar -xzf \"C:\\...\"` resolved to GNU tar, which reads `C:` as host:path; Windows needs %SystemRoot%\\System32\\tar.exe. (3) Env-dependent absolute paths: \"$env:SystemRoot\\System32\\tar.exe\" broke under the kit job supervisor, whose environment lacks SystemRoot, so a literal is safer there. (4) CRLF vs LF bytes: a Linux mirror of the box lib differs on every file, so a digest-based proof only matches from a Windows node; worth a \"line-ending-sensitive hash\" note in the rule docs. Baseline: tibia's .ps1/.spi carry 0 drive-letter absolute paths (grep [A-Z]:\\(home|Users) outside target/deps/notebooks), so tibia is a clean-package fixture. The probes themselves are rig's.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_5 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 5u32, "2026-11-09T19:23Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 15:4x microblog (help pool), PROBE DRAFT, drafts store kit/KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY (probes.spi + measure.spi, .base = current kit files; sha CF627E9813B9/E263DB578BEB). It is a SUPERSET of the accepted KIT-NUMBER-DRIFT draft (MagicNumber), so rig applies one change. Three new drift cases, each MayNotRise: DrivePath (a string starting X:\\\\ or X:/), PosixPath (a string starting /home|usr|tmp|root|etc|var|opt/), ExeSuffix (a literal .exe closing a string). Words drive-path, posix-path, exe-suffix. Tests: the words row, plus a 5-line sample in measure.spi expecting drive 2 (both spellings) / posix 1 / exe 1. Every Spiral literal was decoded and matched mechanically (perl): all expected counts hold. Sizing over kit/src: drive 176 lines in 40 files, posix 38/25, exe 79/37 (test blocks included, as for every probe). LEFT OUT on purpose: Separator (a backslash only matters inside a path join, which a line regex can't see; it needs a parse-level probe) and tibia's SpecialFolder case (GetFolderPath lives in .ps1, which the .spi census doesn't read; it belongs in a script lint). Not compiled (no box compute while the user is present).")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_6 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 6u32, "2026-11-09T19:25Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig: microblog's n5 probe draft (drafts kit/KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY: MagicNumber + DrivePath + PosixPath + ExeSuffix, all MayNotRise, against the same .base files; literals decoded and checked against the test samples) is ACCEPTED and REPLACES the MagicNumber-only draft in TRAIN46, so one apply lands this item and KIT-NUMBER-DRIFT together. Separator and the GetFolderPath case stay out, per microblog's reasons in n5.")
inl note_of_kit_standards_portability_7 () = Note ("KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY", 7u32, "2026-11-10T05:39Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 01:4x rig: APPLIED microblog's KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY superset draft into kit (unstaged), which carries this rule: probes.spi copied as-is (its .base equalled the live file); measure.spi took ONLY the base->draft hunk via patch (fuzz 1, offset 6, placed inside the same test block), because the draft base predates rig's census line-count fix and copying the draft would have reverted it; both fixes are present. Adds SingleSlashPath (NeverRises, word single-slash-path, a quoted drive path with one backslash before a letter) plus MagicNumber, DrivePath, PosixPath, ExeSuffix (MayNotRise). A tree scan for the single-backslash pattern in kit src finds 0 hits, so NeverRises starts clean. kit standards 0 on both packages; rig_style_measure ratchet 0; rig_style_probes ratchet shows host-macro 5 -> 7, which is PRE-EXISTING (the pre-draft file gives the same 5 -> 7; the probe file's own pattern strings containing the dollar-quote text). Probe of rig_style_probes on ganymede PENDING; rig_style_measure is a linked consumer (unprovable off-train). The claim was still refused at apply time (microblog's lease not yet cleared after their release message). Rides TRAIN44. Next step: read the probes probe result, then TRAIN44's notebook verdicts for both packages.")
inl dragon_line_catalog () = Task ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", "Catalog what we know about the dragon line so far, from the recovered KMLs, W43 geodesy, the W66 protocol PDFs and the vault chat (private inputs)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 90u32, Active, "", "recovery,dragon,private,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_dragon_line_catalog () = Planning ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_dragon_line_catalog_1 () = Note ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", 1u32, "2026-11-07T13:46Z", "main", "User 11-07 (asked via the resumed disk-sweep agent): catalog what we know about the dragon line so far. INPUTS, all PRIVATE (personal coordinates and documents; never into a public repo):\n(1) %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\i574n.github\\DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG\\private\\dragon_v20260125-alpha24.kml (81,577 B, 107 placemarks: home, root, anchor, gate, orion, 817, ...), copied from work-hub Downloads\\data (it was the ONLY copy).\n(2) the same folder's W6.zip (660,059 B, only copy, from work-hub): the same KML plus the signed PDFs 1_PROTOCOLO_W66_-_CONVERGENCIA_GEODESICO-DOCUMENTAL_DO_SISTEMA_DRAGON_UNIX, 2_W5D_MAPAS_..., 3_W5G_-_LAUGH TALE_-_O_TERCEIRO_PONEGLYPH, and a Relatorio PDF for each.\n(3) W43.kml (29 placemarks: dragon1, dragon/sirius, cloverleaf/orion, 817), in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\i574n.github\\ARCHIVE-RECOVERY-1107\\private\\.\n(4) W43.py (great-circle geodesy over lon/lat), in i574n.github\\recovered\\w43\\.\n(5) C:\\home\\git\\vault\\Untitled 1.md (110 KB, untracked, 2026-02-13): a long chat about the Dragon/Unix system, the Orion line, property 822 and the W66 protocol.\nLost: Downloads\\files\\gis\\dragon1_refactor.kml (the recovered copy is garbage).\nDoD: one private catalog (a markdown doc in the private store, or a typed Spiral record set if the user prefers) listing every placemark with its source file and version, the named lines (dragon, orion, cloverleaf, sirius), the protocol documents (W66/W5D/W5G) with dates and claims, and what W43.py computes over them; plus the open questions. Next step: unzip W6.zip in its own empty folder, list its contents, and diff the placemark sets of the dragon KML vs W43.kml. TypeFeatures: none yet.")
inl note_of_dragon_line_catalog_2 () = Note ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:17Z", "main", "main 11-07 FIRST CATALOG PASS (user 11-07: put an md detailing the files we got, whether alpha24 is the most stable since it was handcrafted, whether newer ones add stuff or just bloat): i574n.github\\recovered\\dragon\\README.md, all inputs copied beside it (unstaged, never to commit). Versions: dragon1_refactor.kml (2025-12-28, 18,217 B) lost; W43.kml (doc dragon1, 2026-01-08, 29 placemarks = 6 lines + 23 points); dragon_v20260125-alpha24 (107 = 10 lines + 97 points, bundled in W6.zip 02-09, standalone copy 08-13 byte-identical, sha256 81bd9f9b). No alpha1..23 and nothing newer on either machine; the W66 protocol (02-11, 46 pages, it says the KML was drawn by hand), the vault chat (02-13), W5G (02-21) and the 08-13 copies all use alpha24, a Google Earth export, so alpha24 is the stable hand-made version. Diff vs W43.kml: 25 names kept; cloverleaf/orion, crater1, dragon->crater1 renamed into the orion and crater folders, the line ignore dropped; new anchors (places: home, root, anchor, gate, apg/heart, 808; folders orion 10, 817 6, 817b 3, 1978 4, crater 4, sm 4, bridge 8; lines dragon->sm, dragon->brain, bridge->sm, poa->paslaten); bulk: brain (27) and poa (24) are town lists that seem to sit along the dragon->brain and poa->paslaten lines, and (temp) 817 road is marked temporary. W43.py = great-circle geometry plus KMLPatcher/KMLAnalyzer that snap lines through anchors. Other inputs: W5D (4 pages, 02-18), W5G/W6G Laugh Tale (24 pages, 02-21), three signature validation reports, HPOW.md (8,674 B, nine arithmetic verification steps), ДЕНЬ_ПОБЕДЫ_W43.md (699,946 B, Russian), EC20251231-pre.md (5.1 MB Espiral Comum dump: 604 W43, 105 dragon, 37 Orion mentions). Open questions are listed in the README. Progress set by the owner.")
inl note_of_dragon_line_catalog_3 () = Note ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", 3u32, "2026-11-09T15:00Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 15:0x SLICE 1 (aggregate only here: the backlog is public; per-point rows are in the drafts store's private\\crosstrack-alpha24.csv). The file/version/tool catalog already exists as recovered/dragon/README.md (11-07 sweep); this slice answers its open question 1 (anchors vs waypoints) by geometry: each Point's cross-track distance to each of the 10 LineStrings' great circles (spherical, R 6371 km; plane from the first two vertices, because the 9 five-vertex lines are full great circles whose first and last vertices nearly coincide). RESULT for alpha24: 94 of 97 points lie within 5 km of a line, 3 within 50 km, 0 farther. By folder (on-line <=5 km / n, dominant line): orion 10/10 (dragon->cloverleaf x9), 817 6/6 (dragon->817), 1978 4/4 (dc@817), 817b 3/3 ([dc;dc@817]@817), crater 4/4 (dragon->crater x3), sm 4/4 (dragon->sm x3), bridge 6/8 (bridge->sm; 2 within 50 km), places 6/7 (1 within 50 km), brain 27/27 (dragon->brain x24), poa 24/24 (poa->paslaten x24). So brain and poa ARE waypoints picked along their lines (not independent anchors), and every named folder hangs off one line. Next step: open question 3, how W43/W5D/W5G/W66 relate and which claims each rests on, from the PDFs' text + the vault chat (private reads, aggregate note here).")
inl note_of_dragon_line_catalog_4 () = Note ("DRAGON-LINE-CATALOG", 4u32, "2026-11-09T15:02Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 15:0x SLICE 2, open question 3 (how the W-numbers relate), structure only; the PDFs carry personal identifiers, so no values here. Text extracted privately (drafts store private\\text\\, pdftotext -layout). W66 (protocol 1, 46 pp, signed) is the root document and the only one citing the alpha24 KML: Part I primes 19 and 43 as the fixed numbers, Part II personal identifiers (private, not catalogued here), Part III a Unix-time layer where W43 = an MJD timestamp written in base 43 plus a star-culmination coupling, Part IV the great-circle network (the Orion axis dragon->817, the 817 meridian), i.e. the KML's lines. So W43 is an ENCODING (base-43 time code) that names both the KML document (W43.kml = \"dragon1\") and the geodesy tool (W43.py), not a separate protocol. W5G (titled W6G inside; refs W66 x16, W43 x14, W6D x5) is an interpretive reading layered on W66 (13 parts, mapping the system's numbers onto a fiction's names). W5D (4 pp) has no W-references: an astrology/numerology reading. HPOW.md (from-des073) = 9 arithmetic checks written to verify W66's claims. Dependency: W43 (encoding, 2025-12) -> W43.kml/W43.py (2026-01) -> alpha24 KML (01-25) -> W66 (02-11) -> W5D (02-18), W5G (02-21) -> HPOW (verify). Open question 2 (the lost dragon1_refactor.kml) stays unanswerable: no copy survives. Next step: none left in scope besides user review; the catalog = README.md + notes 3-4 + the private crosstrack CSV.")
inl regex_state_space_video () = Task ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", "regex state-space video rendered in GH Actions, after 2swap Adventures in State Space: the derivative automaton growing, then minimizing, encoded to mp4 on the Pages site", Feature, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "regex,automata,video,team:org,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_regex_state_space_video () = Planning ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", Minutes 1020u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_1 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:27Z", "main", "User 11-07: for the regex video (2swap's Adventures in State Space, the Klotski state graph, youtube.com/watch?v=YGLNyHd2w10; found in the Espiral Comum chat EC20251231-pre and stroma sample2.pdf), what would you do, WGSL compiled from Spiral or Rust? Create the task to start it in the regex repo so it renders the video in GH Actions. PLAN (main): Spiral compiled to Rust first, WGSL later as a measured arm. Why Rust first: regex's engines are already Spiral to Rust, every stage stays testable in test blocks, frames can be byte-identical between runs (a determinism gate), and GH runners have no GPU, so WGSL would run on a software adapter anyway. The WGSL arm is for the one GPU-shaped step, the O(n^2) layout force pass, reusing tibia's proven wgsl-run path, and becomes the default only if it beats the Rust arm on the runners for large automata. VIDEO: the derivative automaton grows as BFS discovers states (one frame batch per derivative), nodes colored accepting / live / dead, edges by charset class; then minimization animates Nerode classes merging; a few regexes from the bench catalogue, including (a|b)*a(a|b){n} for the exponential blow-up and a date pattern; title cards from the same renderer. PIPELINE in gh-pages.yml (ubuntu-24.04 only): the Spiral binary writes raw RGB frames to stdout, ffmpeg (preinstalled on ubuntu runners) encodes H.264 yuv420p mp4, the mp4 goes on the Pages site next to the bench report and into the artifact. Children: REGEX-SSV-GRAPH, REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT, REGEX-SSV-RENDER, REGEX-SSV-WGSL. DoD: a push to main renders and publishes the video in Actions; two runs give identical frame hashes; runtime fits the job budget; README links the video. TypeFeatures: state and edge kinds as unions; frame, color and coordinate units as phantom-typed numbers.")
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_2 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:55Z", "main", "Next step: REGEX-SSV-GRAPH (typed state and merge rows checked against the DFA and minimal engines).")
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_3 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 3u32, "2026-11-10T10:48Z", "org3", "ROOT-CAUSE TRACE (org3, user 11-10 \"trace all agile usage since the regex video that prevented finishing it, heal thoroughly\"): the video was filed 11-07; its first child REGEX-SSV-GRAPH went Done the SAME day; then REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT -- whose only dep (GRAPH) was already satisfied, with NO wait and NO block -- sat READY and unstarted for 3 days, holding RENDER and WGSL behind it. Nothing in the backlog mechanics blocked it. It had no owner. MEASURED systemic cause: 288 Planned items are currently READY (deps satisfied) -- 57 P0, 146 P1, 69 P2 -- nearly all assignee=agent with dual team:org,main tags. LAYOUT is a P2 buried under 203 higher-priority ready items, so a flat priority-sorted pull never reaches it; and a finished child (GRAPH) did not pull its ready sibling forward because the backlog has no \"finish-the-chain\" signal (a ready child of a partially-Done epic that would complete a user-visible deliverable). Three amplifiers: (1) assignee=agent + dual-team = ownership diffusion; (2) flat priority buries deliverable-completing work under fresh P0/P1; (3) no epic-progress boost. HEAL (this item): bump REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT P2->P1 as the blocking step of a started user deliverable. HEAL (structural, on HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE): the ready-index must add a finish-the-chain rank boost + a ready-but-unowned-N-days escalation + skill-fit owner routing, so rig's delegator surfaces this class instead of letting it sit. Implementation owner (LAYOUT=deterministic force-directed layout Spiral->Rust, RENDER=rasterizer+ffmpeg CI) is compiler/main skill -- to confirm in lane coordination.")
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_4 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 4u32, "2026-11-10T11:01Z", "org3", "PARALLEL DELEGATION PLAN (user 11-10: \"both compiler and the three org lanes; rig supports so we parallelize even better while dogfooding/battletesting/benchmarking rig\"). The LAYOUT->RENDER/WGSL chain looks serial but the non-gated halves start NOW, so 4 lanes work at once and rig delegates:\n- compiler -> REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT (GATING): deterministic force-directed layout Spiral->Rust (seeded, fixed steps, per-frame coords hash-identical). Its coords feed the rasterizer + the WGSL parity. Start immediately; it is the long pole.\n- org lane -> REGEX-SSV-RENDER / CI-scaffold half (PARALLEL NOW): gh-pages.yml pipeline Spiral binary -> raw RGB stdout -> ffmpeg H.264 yuv420p -> mp4 in dist/+Pages+artifact, size/runtime caps, README link. Testable on DUMMY/solid frames before LAYOUT lands. The rasterizer half (anti-aliased disks/edges/labels/title cards consuming real coords) snaps in once LAYOUT is ready.\n- org lane -> determinism gate (PARALLEL NOW): two-run identical-frame-hash harness, testable on the GRAPH rows + dummy frames.\n- tibia/org -> REGEX-SSV-WGSL / harness half (PARALLEL-ish): wire tibia's proven wgsl-run path + the Rust-arm parity + timing rig; the force-pass port finalizes against LAYOUT's interface.\n- rig -> DELEGATES these to skill-fit lanes via its smart-delegation (the ready-index skill-fit routing + finish-the-chain boost on HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE note 17). This epic is rig's FIRST real delegation workload = the dogfood/benchmark: did rig route LAYOUT->compiler, parallelize the non-gated prep, and NOT assign a child whose gating dep is unfinished? REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT is now P1 (was P2) as the long pole. org3 tracks the delegation benchmark + resilience bracketing of the CI runs.")
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_5 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 5u32, "2026-11-10T11:16Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 07:2x rig, DELEGATION (per org3 plan note 4; the user's headline case of ready work sitting unowned): GRAPH is Done, so LAYOUT is ready and is the only gating long pole. ROUTED: REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT -> compiler by skill-fit (Spiral->Rust force layout), asked directly to claim it. PARALLEL NOW, sliced as unblocked light team:org items so the org lanes' gate pools offer them, one owner each by the partition: REGEX-SSV-RENDER-CI (the ffmpeg pipeline on dummy frames), REGEX-SSV-DETERMINISM (two-run identical frame hashes over GRAPH rows, with a jitter negative control), REGEX-SSV-WGSL-HARNESS (the wgsl path and CPU-vs-GPU parity on a synthetic layout). NOT assigned: REGEX-SSV-RENDER and REGEX-SSV-WGSL proper stay gated on LAYOUT and only swap real frames/layout into the prepared harnesses. Critical path is now LAYOUT alone; the other three halves overlap with it. Next step: compiler claims LAYOUT; org lanes pull the three slices.")
inl note_of_regex_state_space_video_6 () = Note ("REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO", 6u32, "2026-11-10T11:48Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 07:5x rig, delegation status: compiler took LAYOUT the same morning it was routed. Drafted and gated: ssv_layout.spi (Fruchterman-Reingold over discovery frames, deterministic across OSes by construction, digest() for REGEX-SSV-DETERMINISM, repulsion split out for the WGSL port), 5/5 rust tests on ganymede, frozen in drafts\\regex\\REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT. It waits on ORG applying the frozen draft (regex repo, copy-only). compiler also filed REGEX-SSV-GRAPH-BLOCKS (org) and REGEX-SSV-MERGE-LAYOUT (compiler, deps on it). The three non-gated slices (RENDER-CI, DETERMINISM, WGSL-HARNESS) are in the org pools. Benchmark for org3: LAYOUT, unowned for 3 days, went from routed to gated in under an hour once it had a named owner.")
inl regex_ssv_graph () = Task ("REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", "regex state-space graph export: derivative BFS order, state kinds (accepting, live, dead), charset-class edges, and the Nerode merge steps of minimization, as typed rows with tests", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "regex,automata,video,team:org,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_regex_ssv_graph () = Planning ("REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_regex_ssv_graph_1 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:27Z", "main", "Child of REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO. DoD: for each catalogue regex the graph agrees with the DFA and minimal engines (state and edge counts, accepting set); the discovery order and the merge steps are deterministic; test blocks cover a 3-state, a dead-state and an exponential (a|b)*a(a|b){n} case. Next step: read dfa.spi and minimal.spi for the state and class data already there.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_graph_2 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", 2u32, "2026-11-09T16:57Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 16:5x DRAFTED regex/src/ssv_graph.spi (unstaged; added to src/spiral.toml after table_emit; budget clean), built ON the lib modules REGEX-LIB-3 landed. Types: state_kind = Accepting | Live | DeadEnd; edge_end = ToState i32 | ToDead; state_row {index; kind}; edge_row {source; codes : code_range; target}; merge_round {round; blocks}; graph {states; edges; rounds}. States come in the eager DFA's BFS discovery order (row index); kinds from row.accepting + regex_minimal.live_states; edges = each row's classes zipped with its next targets; merge rounds REPLAY regex_minimal's Moore refinement (initial_blocks -> refine_rows until the block count is stable) recording the block count per round, since minimize only returns the final partition. Tests (rust): \"01\" -> kinds [Live; Live; Accepting], 2 live edges; both(\"01\",\"00\") -> [DeadEnd; DeadEnd] (both does not intersect sets, so the And(1,0) state is a real dead end), 1 live edge; agreement: state count == size_of (build r) and the final round == minimal_size, for fourth_from_end (minimal 16) and tri_cyclic_nonminimal (4 -> 2); tri_cyclic rounds start at 2 blocks and end at 2. Gate: ganymede job org2-check.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_graph_3 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", 3u32, "2026-11-09T17:57Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 18:0x DONE: gate 4/4 (ganymede job-1794244038842-org2-batch, after the tuple-pattern fix `| (c :: cr), (t :: tr)`: the comma binds tighter than ::) and inside the full regex build job-1794244806602-org2-swap3: Cells 122/122 over 21 files (ssv_graph.spi included), REGEX-OK. DoD met: the graph agrees with the DFA (state count == size_of) and minimal (final round == minimal_size) engines for fourth_from_end and tri_cyclic_nonminimal; discovery order deterministic (BFS row index); merge rounds deterministic (replayed Moore refinement); 3-state, dead-state (both 01 00) and exponential cases covered. Proof: those two jobs.")
inl regex_ssv_layout () = Task ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", "regex state-space layout in Spiral to Rust: deterministic force-directed positions per frame (seeded, fixed steps), stable as states are added and merged", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "REGEX-SSV-GRAPH", "regex,automata,video,team:org,main", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_regex_ssv_layout () = Planning ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_1 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 1u32, "2026-11-07T14:27Z", "main", "Child of REGEX-STATE-SPACE-VIDEO. DoD: positions are a pure function of the graph and the frame number (identical across runs and OSes within the tolerance the renderer needs); new states enter near their parent; merges interpolate; tested on the GRAPH rows. The repulsion pass is the hot loop that REGEX-SSV-WGSL may move to the GPU.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_2 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 2u32, "2026-11-07T14:55Z", "main", "Next step: after REGEX-SSV-GRAPH, a deterministic force-directed layout (fixed seed and iteration count) whose per-frame coordinates hash identically across two runs.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_3 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 3u32, "2026-11-10T11:44Z", "compiler", "11-10 08:0x compiler (routed by rig): DRAFTED + GATED, frozen at %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/drafts/regex/REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT/ (ssv_layout.spi b1836bf2, new file; spiral.toml c71488f1 over spiral.toml.base 51179c84 = adds ssv_layout after ssv_graph). Design: Fruchterman-Reingold over discovery frames. Frame f shows states 0..f; state i enters at its BFS parent (smallest earlier source with an edge to it) plus an integer-derived offset, then 30 fixed iterations settle the frame with a fixed temperature schedule; the newest state moves at full temperature, older ones at 0.2x, so the layout is stable as states arrive. Positions are a pure function of (graph, frame), warm-started frame to frame. CROSS-OS determinism by construction: only + - * / and sqrt (IEEE correctly rounded; Rust never contracts to FMA), no sin/cos/exp (platform libm), fixed op order; digest = quantized coordinates folded mod 1e9+7 for the REGEX-SSV-DETERMINISM gate. repulsion is its own function (the WGSL hot loop). GATE: ganymede, in-package Cells (regex src + lib snapshot, installed pack83 pinned): 5/5 rust tests pass on fourth_from_end rows: pure (digest of two independent builds equal), frame f has f+1 states, new state 0.987 from its parent (< 3.0), older states drift 0.643 between frames 4 and 5 (< 2.0), all positions finite. NOT DONE: merges interpolate. ssv_graphs merge_round carries only block COUNTS, not membership, so the layout cannot know which states merge; needs ssv_graph to expose per-round blocks (state -> block id), then layout interpolates members toward their block centroid. Budget: largest cell 257 < 272.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_4 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 4u32, "2026-11-10T11:45Z", "compiler", "11-10 08:1x compiler: merge interpolation split off as REGEX-SSV-MERGE-LAYOUT (compiler; depends on REGEX-SSV-GRAPH-BLOCKS, org, which adds per-round membership). This items discovery-frame layout is complete and gated; it closes when org applies the frozen draft (copy-only on the box, confirming gate). RENDER can use it now.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_5 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 5u32, "2026-11-10T12:07Z", "org3", "org3 11-10 APPLIED the frozen draft (the lane:org wait = \"org applies the frozen draft\", now answered -> cleared). Copied compiler's ganymede-gated ssv_layout.spi (124 lines, 5/5 rust tests on pack83) into regex/src/ + registered ssv_layout after ssv_graph in src/spiral.toml. Node-proof/box-copy: the proof is compiler's ganymede gate; this is the box copy, no box re-gate (ssv_graph.spi is mid-edit by org2 for REGEX-SSV-GRAPH-BLOCKS, so a box gate now would hit an incomplete package). ORTHOGONALITY confirmed by reading the draft: ssv_layout never references merge_round.members, so it is independent of org2's GRAPH-BLOCKS change and of my deferred determinism cells (both in ssv_graph.spi). RENDER (REGEX-SSV-RENDER) can consume layout(g,f) now. Note for my REGEX-SSV-DETERMINISM gate: ssv_layout already has a pure-layout digest + a determinism cell (line 100-103); my gate should layer per-frame first_diff + the jitter negative control on top of digest(layout g f), superseding the states-only proxy. Closing per compiler's stated DoD (closes when org applies). The next full regex package gate (after org2's blocks land) will confirm the combination.")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_6 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 6u32, "2026-11-10T12:39Z", "compiler", "11-10 08:4x compiler: DONE. org3 applied the frozen draft on the box (regex/src/ssv_layout.spi + spiral.toml, unstaged) and org2 confirmed it green in a combined gate with the updated ssv_graph (ssv_layout 5/5, ssv_graph 5/5). Discovery-frame half of the DoD complete; the merge half continues in REGEX-SSV-MERGE-LAYOUT (now unblocked: REGEX-SSV-GRAPH-BLOCKS Done, merge_round.members = block id per state, -1 for dead).")
inl note_of_regex_ssv_layout_7 () = Note ("REGEX-SSV-LAYOUT", 7u32, "2026-11-10T12:40Z", "compiler", "Evidence: compiler ganymede in-package gate 5/5 (pinned pack83: pure digest, f+1 states per frame, new state 0.987 from parent, drift 0.643, finite positions); org2 combined gate ssv_layout 5/5 + ssv_graph 5/5 after org3 applied it on the box.")
