inl kit_note_text_file () = Task ("KIT-NOTE-TEXT-FILE", "agile note/note-edit take --text-file so long notes with quotes survive PowerShell argv; agile edit takes --as", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,cli,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_note_text_file () = Planning ("KIT-NOTE-TEXT-FILE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_note_text_file_1 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-TEXT-FILE", 1u32, "2026-11-07T15:55Z", "main", "From the 11-07 star exchange (main + resilience hurdles). (1) Passing a note through a PowerShell double-quoted native argument that contains inner double quotes splits it into several operands. kit either rejects it (\"expects <root> <id> <text>, received 13 operands\") or, worse, accepts only the first piece: main's DIALECT-RENAME-EDITION3 n4 landed as 99 bytes of a 998-byte note, silently. Fix: `agile note ... --text-file <path>` (and for note-edit), plus a warning when the text is shorter than some prefix of argv implies, or when extra operands follow. (2) `agile edit` has no `--as`, so edits are unattributed (resilience). DoD: both verbs accept --text-file; edit accepts --as; a test feeds a note with inner quotes and newlines through pwsh and reads it back byte-identical. Next step: add --text-file to note and note-edit. TypeFeatures: the note source as a union (Inline of text | File of path), resolved once.")
inl note_of_kit_note_text_file_2 () = Note ("KIT-NOTE-TEXT-FILE", 2u32, "2026-11-09T15:26Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 12:4x local rig, BUILT for agile note (unstaged, unprobed; task_notes.spi in the post-TRAIN42e probe batch): note_request gains text_file; note_request_from accepts --text-file <path> alone or with --as <author> in either order (6 or 8 words, so the router's operand count is the same as <text> --as <author>); note_entry reads the file through note_text_of (trimmed), refuses \"--text-file <path> does not exist\", and the empty-text refusal still applies. Tests: both orders, and inline text names no file. FOUND while building: note_text_of ALREADY reads stdin when the text is \"-\", so kit agile note <root> <id> - --as <a> with the note piped in is an existing quote-proof path today (worth a line in the help). Left on this item: note-edit --text-file, agile edit --as, the usage/shape text (the router arity gate reads the shape and notes_cli asserts the usage line, so that change gets its own test), and the truncation warning (extra operands already fall to the malformed usage refusal).")
inl fiscal_manifest_drift_guard () = Task ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", "init.ps1 compares each corpus document manifest with the list its committed generated unit embeds, and fails under -Strict, so a corpus fetch never turns CI red silently", TaskKind, P1, D2, 90u32, Active, "", "team:fiscal,fiscal,ci,corpus,guard", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard () = Planning ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-07T15:56Z", "fiscal", "11-07 fiscal, from the 11-07 star exchange (lesson: a corpus fetch under deps/corpus silently changed init.ps1's manifest scan, hence two generated units, and the user's push 6b82ad5 went red on CI; fixed by skipping '<region>/pint-<region>/'). GUARD: after init.ps1 writes the document manifests, it compares each manifest with the document list embedded in the committed generated unit that reads it (ubl-<kind>-documents.txt vs generated_ubl_<kind>_2_1, hu-nav / gr-mydata / mx-cfdi vs their units; ^^-split literals joined before comparing) and prints one line per manifest: entries, embedded, added, removed. With -Strict (CI) any difference fails init with the added paths named, so a new corpus fetch shows up at init time on the box, not as generated drift after a push. DoD: the comparison runs in init.ps1 (no new .ps1), the box run prints 0 added / 0 removed for every manifest today, a planted extra UBL XML under deps/corpus makes -Strict fail naming it, and build.ps1 CI passes -Strict. TYPEFEATURES: none (pwsh glue). NEXT: add the comparison after the manifest loop (init.ps1 ~line 913).")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 2u32, "2026-11-07T17:33Z", "fiscal", "11-07 13:4x DONE IN PLACE (fiscal scripts/init.ps1 FF2E8741, unstaged; no new .ps1). After the manifest loop, init.ps1 collects every corpus path the committed generated units embed (^^-split string literals joined first) and checks each deps/corpus/*-documents.txt: a listed document no unit embeds is printed as 'init.ps1 / manifest drift / <manifest>: <n> documents no committed generated unit embeds, first <path>'. Under GitHub Actions (GITHUB_ACTIONS=true, no workflow edit needed, since fiscal's YAML is generated from the org pins) it throws, so CI fails at init with the new path named instead of at build.ps1's generated-drift step. Trap found and avoided: init's corpus scan loop reuses $root for each file's XML root element, so the guard derives generated/ from $corpusRoot, not $root (nothing else after the loop uses $root).\nPROOF (box, init.ps1's own manifest section run alone): (1) clean: 'every listed document is embedded in a committed generated unit (300 corpus paths)', exit 0, over all 15 manifests; (2) a planted copy of a UBL invoice under deps/corpus/zz-guard-plant/ with GITHUB_ACTIONS=true: throws '1 document manifests list documents the committed generated units do not embed ...', exit 1; (3) plant removed: clean again, ubl-invoice manifest back to 155 entries, 0 planted left.\nDoD: met on the box (the -Strict switch became CI detection); CI pending the user's next push.\nPENDING: the first CI run after the user's push (GitHub, fiscal gh-pages).")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 3u32, "2026-11-09T05:12Z", "fiscal", "11-09 CI RED (org2 report): fiscal CI is red on all 3 user pushes since 11-07 (6b82ad5, 11bfba3, 212e0a8; run 42551396881). Both builds fail in build.ps1 at family-gates.ps1:4: '/home/runner/work/fiscal/fiscal/generated/family-gates.tsv is missing: the generator writes it (test writes_the_family_gate_table)'. HEAD's generator has that writer, writing FISCAL_ROOT/generated/family-gates.tsv (FISCAL_ROOT is set by build.ps1), and CI's generator reports 28/28 executed, so the table is written somewhere else or removed before family-gates.ps1 runs. CI also shows generated drift: ~20 units ' M' (CI's regen differs from the committed copy, likely a compiler/formatter version difference). Ruled out: build.ps1 never resets generated/ after drift; the CI log has no memo/cache replay lines. REPRO in flight: ganymede fiscal-gany-rename runs the generator (run.ps1 -Row regen) on Linux. NEXT STEP: check fr/work-regen/fiscal/generated/family-gates.tsv after that chain; present = CI-sequence-specific, absent = generator-side bug.")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 4u32, "2026-11-09T05:15Z", "fiscal", "11-09 REPRO STEP 1: on ganymede (Linux, the same HEAD generator via the bundle overlay) the generator WROTE generated/family-gates.tsv (5610 bytes, 03:57 UTC, three minutes into the regen run) while the generator unit was still running. So the writer works on Linux. Leading hypothesis: a LATER generator test prunes generated/ entries that are not unit dirs (stale-output cleanup) and deletes the table after it was written; CI reads it only after the whole sequence. NEXT STEP: check the same path when this generator run finishes (fiscal-gany-rename); gone = find the pruning test and exempt family-gates.tsv (or write the table after the prune).")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 5u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 CI RED CHECK. (1) Ganymede repro of the generator on Linux: generated/family-gates.tsv written 03:57 UTC and still present after generator end (28/28, 4776 s), so the generator does not delete it. (2) build.ps1 exports FISCAL_ROOT = repo root (HEAD line 16), so the writer test cannot land the table under another cwd. (3) main is STILL RED: run 42551396881 on 212e0a8b failed on both windows-2025 and ubuntu-24.04; the green gh-pages runs (42564338855 on 518c4abe) are deploy commits from the push job, which runs even when builds fail, so they are not build evidence. Last green main build: f6f01e99 (11-06). NEXT: read 42551396881 failing step lines per OS (both OS failed, so not a Windows-only path issue) and compare with the local tree before the user pushes again.")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 6u32, "2026-11-09T06:36Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 CI RED ROOT CAUSE + FIX (run 42551396881 artifacts ci-metrics-ubuntu-24.04). UBUNTU: the one failing generator test IS writes_the_family_gate_table (rust, failed, 38 s): panicked at main.rs:1627:35, Result::unwrap on Os NotFound. CI clears build products (generated/ included) before the regen, and the writer test wrote generated/family-gates.tsv without creating generated/; Cells runs the 28 tests in no fixed order, so on ubuntu it ran before any sibling (generates_every_family_version, write_generated_module_with via ensure_folder) had recreated the folder. Windows and the ganymede repro happened to order it later. Every other metric writer (reports, canon_coverage, pint_terms, canon, modeling_ceiling) already calls directory_create_all first. FIX: generator.spi writes_the_family_gate_table now calls ensure_folder (fiscal_paths::under generated) first (one line; generator.spi BC710324, unstaged). PROOF on ganymede (Linux, core 2BE9257D), generated/ moved aside, only that test via run_notebook --test: control (live 60ACECF9) failed with the identical main.rs:1627:35 NotFound panic and no table; fixed (BC710324) passed in 49 s and wrote the table; tree restored to 60ACECF9 with its table. WINDOWS failed differently: generated: 46 files differ from the committed copy (the generator passed there), i.e. the committed generated/ is stale against the regen; the user's next commit must include the regenerated generated/ (fiscal never commits). Both OS failures are now explained.")
inl note_of_fiscal_manifest_drift_guard_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-MANIFEST-DRIFT-GUARD", 7u32, "2026-11-09T11:35Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Wait: when the user pushes fiscal (the CI fix: generator writes_the_family_gate_table creates generated/; then confirm main green)")
inl tfs_conversion_loss_laws () = Task ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", "typed loss direction on conversion pairs (Iso, Retraction, Lossy) with derived round-trip law tests", TaskKind, P3, D4, 50u32, Active, "-", "typefeatureset,lib,quality,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws () = Planning ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_1 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 1u32, "2026-11-07T16:24Z", "main", "main 11-07, from the user's question on the SciAm 'nothing can be fully transparent' result (Nicolis, Piazza, Esposito, arXiv 2610.12560: any medium that bends light must absorb some, via phonons). Transfer: a conversion that changes representation is lossless in at most one direction unless a law proves otherwise. Existing pieces already do this by hand: the kernel subst.spi laws (instantiate after lift = identity, a retraction, not the reverse), fiscal's canon NotCarried losses and its 'lossless only where bijective' rule, lib round-trip-first landings, KIT-USELESS-ABSTRACTION (a wrapper with refractive index 1: changes nothing, so pure cost). Novel part: make the direction a declared, checked feature instead of prose. DoD: a typed marker on a conversion pair with three cases (Iso: both round trips are laws; Retraction: one named round trip is a law; Lossy: names what is lost); the cells gate derives the round-trip law tests from the marker over the module's sample set; pilot on 3 pairs (kernel lift/instantiate, regex/xsd_regex adapter once REGEX-CORE-INTO-LIB lands, one fiscal canon pair); one A/B where a deliberately broken inverse fails the derived test. TypeFeatures: conversion_shape union (Iso, Retraction of direction, Lossy of loss list), phantom direction tag, derived law cells. Next step: write the marker as a plain union in a draft and hand-derive the kernel pair's two laws from it, comparing against subst.spi's existing tests.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_2 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 2u32, "2026-11-07T16:47Z", "main", "main 11-07 groom: linked from KIT-USELESS-ABSTRACTION (forward = identity Iso), FISCAL-CANON-MODEL (NotCarried = Lossy, bijective reverse = Iso), REGEX-CORE-INTO-LIB (dep; adapter pilot), CONV-JEPA-OBJECTIVE (decision = Lossy, calibration names the loss). First pilot needs no dep: the kernel pair in spiral/apps/kernel/src/subst.spi (instantiating_a_lifted_term_gives_it_back is the one-way law). Next step unchanged.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_3 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 3u32, "2026-11-07T23:11Z", "main", "main 11-07 19:1x FIRST SLICE LANDED, proof: green. lib/spiral/round_trip.spi (8EAA8F1F6E37, undo 20261107-185803):\n- `shape` union: Iso | Retraction of direction (ThereAndBack | BackAndThere) | Lossy of list string;\n- laws_hold checks the round trips a declared shape promises; tight refuses a declaration that is too weak (a Retraction whose other trip also holds should be Iso; a Lossy whose both trips hold is not lossy).\nTests (rust) on europa: 3/3 pass. The u8 <-> i32 widen/narrow pair is a tight Retraction ThereAndBack; declaring it Iso fails its laws; an identity pair is Iso and NOT tight as a Retraction.\nTwo traps found on the way (memory index-spiral-traps): a nested constructor pattern `| Retraction ThereAndBack =>` silently drops the definition (callers see Unbound); a `:= ^` generic recursive helper unrolls under peval (EJP0040 >1000 dynamic ifs), so recursive helpers are `:~ ^` join points.\nNext step: pilot 1 from the DoD, the kernel's lift/instantiate pair from spiral/apps/kernel/src/subst.spi declared Retraction ThereAndBack, with tight checked over its samples.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_4 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 4u32, "2026-11-07T23:15Z", "main", "main 11-07 19:2x PILOT 1 DONE, proof: green. spiral/apps/kernel/src/subst.spi gains `lifting_then_instantiating_is_a_tight_retraction`: lift t 1 / instantiate t (value ()) declared round_trip.Retraction ThereAndBack; laws_hold AND tight both hold over the kernel's own 7 samples. So the reverse trip (instantiate then lift) really fails on at least one sample, and the declaration is neither too weak nor wrong. Kernel gate on europa: 8/8 pass (the 7 existing substitution laws + the pilot); unstaged. The hand-written one-way law now has a declared, checked shape. Next: pilot 2 (the xsd_regex adapter, after REGEX-CORE-INTO-LIB) and pilot 3 (one fiscal canon pair; fiscal's call), then derive these tests from the marker instead of writing them.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_5 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 5u32, "2026-11-08T00:01Z", "fiscal", "11-07 19:5x fiscal PILOT 3 (asked by main, optional): draft %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\fiscal\\TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS-PILOT3\\src\\canon.spi 69892145 (base live canon.spi 6C66D6C63016), one new test cell the_canon_text_codecs_declare_their_round_trip_shapes using qualified round_trip:: names. SCOPE NOTE: fiscal has no executable UBL<->canon converter yet; the canon model is a term catalog plus coverage tables, so 'UBL to canon and back is lossless' is prose and nothing can be called over fixture invoices. The pilot therefore declares the two executable pairs the canon model does have: (1) EN 16931 data type <-> its name: round_trip::Iso, laws checked over all 9 types and all 9 names; (2) cardinality <-> its text (cardinality_text / cardinality_of): Retraction ThereAndBack, laws_hold AND tight, because 1..01 and 0..002 read back as 1..1 and 0..2 (decimal_i32 accepts leading zeros), so the text side does not survive while every cardinality does. PROOF (europa, core 79a209d7eb6c5540, 900M scope): --check module exit 0 and all 6 canon test bodies exit 0; the cell executed alone (compile 0, rustc 0, run exit 0). PENDING (europa): negative control with the cardinality shape declared Iso, which must fail. Follow-up when fiscal grows a UBL invoice <-> canon converter: the same cell shape over fixture invoices, expected Iso for UBL and Retraction or Lossy (BT-3 named) for NF-e/CFDI.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_6 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 6u32, "2026-11-08T01:00Z", "fiscal", "11-07 21:0x fiscal PILOT 3 NEGATIVE CONTROL (europa, core 79a209d7eb6c5540, 900M scope): the same cell with the cardinality shape declared round_trip::Iso (canon cd567866) compiles and runs to a panic, 'a cardinality survives printing and reading back' (laws_hold is false: Iso also demands text -> cardinality -> text, and 1..01 reads back as 1..1). So the positive run (Retraction ThereAndBack, laws_hold and tight both true, run exit 0) is not vacuous. Pilot 3 stands as drafted (canon 69892145), unlanded, to land with fiscal's next src landing.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_7 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 7u32, "2026-11-08T03:36Z", "fiscal", "11-07 23:3x fiscal PILOT 3 LANDED in the fiscal working tree (unstaged, nothing committed): src/canon.spi 6C66D6C63016 -> 69892145 (base unchanged at land), adding the_canon_text_codecs_declare_their_round_trip_shapes over lib round_trip.spi (live). Proof before landing: europa --check module + all 6 canon bodies exit 0, the cell executed green, and its Iso negative control failed as designed. The user's next push carries it; fiscal CI runs it with the canon tests.")
inl note_of_tfs_conversion_loss_laws_8 () = Note ("TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS", 8u32, "2026-11-08T16:05Z", "i574n", "Related steal (11-08): WITCHCRAFT-STEALS takes TypeClass's declared-laws-per-class idea for Spiral prototypes; derived round-trip laws here and declared prototype laws there should share one law-test generator.")
inl cells_livemd_explicit_target () = Task ("CELLS-LIVEMD-EXPLICIT-TARGET", "Cells: retire the last implicit default (single-cell eval!/run Builders.default() = rust) so spiral.livemd's smart cells name their ///> targets", TaskKind, P2, D3, 80u32, Active, "CELLS-NONTEST-TARGETS-EXPLICIT", "cells,spiral,team:microblog", "current", "current")
