inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_1 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T11:13Z", "management", "Built 11-08 (resilience handing to management): node-env.ps1 Get-ActiveLanes/Resolve-LaneTeam + schedules/lane-aliases.txt source-of-truth; idle-with-ready-alarm + resource-report now DERIVE rosters; lane-reconcile.ps1 detects+enforces. Scheduling pending.")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_2 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T11:16Z", "management", "lane-reconcile 2026-11-08 07:14: applied=247 learned=0 detected=0. ENFORCE applied=247")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_3 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T11:21Z", "management", "RECONCILE LIVE + SCHEDULED 11-08: lane-reconcile.ps1 applied kit->rig for 247 live items (team:rig=247; the 62 Done kept team:kit as history). Registered hourly scheduled task lane-reconcile (Priority 2, -Apply -NoteOnChange, conhost --headless pwsh, Interactive/i574n). Source of truth = schedules/lane-aliases.txt. Rosters (idle-with-ready-alarm, resource-report) now DERIVE their lane list from Get-ActiveLanes (the live CC session registry ~/.claude/sessions), so a rename never leaves them stale. SAFETY PROPERTY: auto-detect is non-destructive - a detected rename only learns the new session name as an alias of its existing team (rosters follow instantly), it never mass-retags the backlog; flipping a backlog team canonical (e.g. kit->rig) needs a one-line lane-aliases.txt edit. Memory: lane-rename-reconcile.")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_4 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 4u32, "2026-11-08T12:20Z", "management", "lane-reconcile 2026-11-08 08:20: applied=1 learned=0 detected=0. ENFORCE applied=1")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_5 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 5u32, "2026-11-09T14:46Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 14:5x VERIFY + ONE GAP. Built half healthy: scheduled task lane-reconcile Ready, hourly, conhost.exe headless, last run 10:20 result 0 (\"applied=0 learned=0 detected=0\"); lane-aliases.txt maps org=org,org2,org3,management,i574n,main,resilience,x-dumps,heal,healing; microblog=microblog,x; rig=rig,kit,eoie. GAP (the stall class): the Stop gate (~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1) resolves teams WITHOUT lane-aliases.txt: (1) line 45's registry fallback hard-codes ^org\\d*$ -> org, so any other old name (management, kit, x, main) becomes a team with 0 items; (2) teams.txt values are not canonicalized: ec368ac8=x-dumps survives, though aliases fold x-dumps into org, so that session's gate searches an empty pool. Fix (rig, gate owner; sent 14:5x): resolve both paths through lane-aliases.txt like node-env.ps1 Resolve-LaneTeam, print the source, and say \"unknown\" instead of defaulting to main. DoD then: every live session's gate team == Resolve-LaneTeam(its name).")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_6 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T14:47Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DoD: every live session's Stop-gate team equals Resolve-LaneTeam(its session name) over lane-aliases.txt, and the hourly lane-reconcile stays green. Next step: rig switches the gate's two resolution paths to lane-aliases.txt; then org2 checks each live session's gate line against Resolve-LaneTeam.")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_7 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 7u32, "2026-11-09T14:50Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "ANSWER from rig: 11-09 11:4x local rig, GATE TEAM RESOLUTION FIXED (org2's report): ~/.claude/hooks/autonomous-gate.ps1 no longer keeps its own session->team rule. Both sources (teams.txt value, else the session registry name) now pass through %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\schedules\\lane-aliases.txt, the reconciler's source of truth: a row \"team = alias,...\" maps the team itself or any alias to the left-hand canonical team (same parse: skip # comments, split on the first =, trim the comma list). The hard-coded org\\d* rewrite is gone. The gate prints \"Team for this session: <team> (from <source> '<raw>', via lane-aliases)\", or \"UNKNOWN to lane-aliases.txt: add it there\" for a name the map lacks, or \"lane-aliases.txt missing\". The no-name fallback stays explicit (main + the NOTHING message), never silently aliased. Verified against the live map: x-dumps -> org, org2 -> org, management -> org, kit -> rig, x -> microblog, rig -> rig, nobody -> UNKNOWN; parse check 0 errors. teams.txt can now shrink to explicit overrides.")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_8 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 8u32, "2026-11-09T14:52Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 15:0x DONE. rig switched the Stop gate to lane-aliases.txt (gate lines 47-60: teams.txt value or registry name -> alias rows, left side canonical; unknown prints \"UNKNOWN to lane-aliases.txt: add it there\"; the org\\d* rewrite is gone). Independent DoD check (org2, same parse over the live map): every gate input resolves to a canonical team with items: compiler->compiler, fiscal->fiscal, microblog->microblog, org/org2/org3->org, rig->rig, tibia->tibia, x-dumps->org; no UNKNOWN. Hourly lane-reconcile stays green (10:20 result 0). Proof: the gate code read at 15:0x + this mapping.")
inl note_of_lane_rename_reconcile_9 () = Note ("LANE-RENAME-RECONCILE", 9u32, "2026-11-10T10:23Z", "management", "lane-reconcile 2026-11-10 06:20: applied=1 learned=0 detected=0. ENFORCE applied=1")
inl microblog_ui_client_parity () = Task ("MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY", "Serve timeline UI reaches client functional parity + x-exact detail view and wrap-aware compose", Feature, P2, D6, 0u32, Paused, "MICROBLOG-MIGRATION", "microblog,ui,parity,typefeatureset,team:microblog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_microblog_ui_client_parity () = Planning ("MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_microblog_ui_client_parity_1 () = Note ("MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY", 1u32, "2026-11-08T11:18Z", "management-f33af161", "User vision 11-08 (technical playground, NOT user-facing - precision over polish): the serve UI should let you do from the SCREEN what today only the client xc can do - POSTING (reply/quote/repost) and the rest. (1) WRAP-AWARE COMPOSE: while typing in the post input, show exactly where each word will wrap in the timeline - align the input like a real post (same 516px column, font, metrics) so the typed wrap == the rendered wrap. (2) X DETAIL-POST VIEW: clicking a post opens X enlarged reading view where the font size and the WRAP POSITION change; add a UI TOGGLE to enable this precise-wrap mode so the timeline + the input show exactly where the detail-screen wrap happens. Find X detail-view + compose screenshots to match. (3) HEIGHT EMULATION: each post rendered height must equal X exact height, posts stacked to match the full on-screen spread pixel-for-pixel (like rewriting Doom). Depends on the pixel-perfect font/width work (46A542536225 is the first step: 516px column + exact metrics).")
inl note_of_microblog_ui_client_parity_2 () = Note ("MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY", 2u32, "2026-11-08T12:09Z", "microblog", "groom (user 11-08): Planned -> Paused with the whole migration. The new repo should start from settled modeling: finish the Cells Elixir rewrite and the typed-boundary work first. Resume when CELLS-PORT-CLIS, LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH and TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 are Done (deps on MICROBLOG-SKELETON and MICROBLOG-LIB-MODEL).")
inl microblog_cross_backend_reuse () = Task ("MICROBLOG-CROSS-BACKEND-REUSE", "SOTA business-code reuse across the Rust client and Gleam server via the Spiral dialect", Feature, P2, D7, 0u32, Paused, "MICROBLOG-MIGRATION", "microblog,architecture,reuse,typefeatureset,backends,team:microblog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_microblog_cross_backend_reuse () = Planning ("MICROBLOG-CROSS-BACKEND-REUSE", Unestimated, Load Heavy, Unassigned)
inl note_of_microblog_cross_backend_reuse_1 () = Note ("MICROBLOG-CROSS-BACKEND-REUSE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T11:19Z", "management-f33af161", "User vision 11-08: the client is Spiral->Rust, the server is Spiral->Gleam/BEAM. Reuse a LARGE amount of BUSINESS code between both backends in a SOTA way using our Spiral dialect, instead of duplicating per backend. Prime shared cores: the post/timeline domain model, the wrap+layout math (both the client compose box and the server timeline need the exact same wrap calculation - strongest driver), validation, cursor/pagination, repost/reply/quote logic. Thin per-backend adapters/bindings only at the FFI edges (erlang_* / BEAM on the server, rust FFI on the client). Rework whats needed; HEAL when things dont land (like last weeks cells ///> backend flags and the transitive-FFI traps - those are the boundary surfacing). Keeping MULTIPLE backends is DELIBERATE and valuable: it battle-tests the Spiral boundaries and the typefeatureset (type-level modeling), which a single backend would hide. Goal: one business core, proven identical on both backends by the cells gates. Ties to the wrap-aware compose in MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY (same math, two backends).")
inl note_of_microblog_cross_backend_reuse_2 () = Note ("MICROBLOG-CROSS-BACKEND-REUSE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T12:09Z", "microblog", "groom (user 11-08): Planned -> Paused with the whole migration. The new repo should start from settled modeling: finish the Cells Elixir rewrite and the typed-boundary work first. Resume when CELLS-PORT-CLIS, LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH and TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 are Done (deps on MICROBLOG-SKELETON and MICROBLOG-LIB-MODEL).")
inl spiral_grotesque_font () = Task ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", "Original proportional grotesque font built from scratch in Spiral via the typefeatureset, reusable", Feature, P3, D7, 76u32, Active, "", "spiral,font,typefeatureset,reuse,microblog,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_grotesque_font () = Planning ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 1u32, "2026-11-08T11:37Z", "management-f33af161", "User vision 11-08 (technical playground): build an ORIGINAL proportional sans-serif font FROM SCRATCH in Spiral using the typefeatureset (type-safe glyph/outline modeling), named just grotesque (or proportional), living in the SPIRAL repo (or html) as a reusable asset for the microblog UI x-faithfulness and beyond. COPYRIGHT: a typeface DESIGN is not copyrightable (only the font file is), so own-drawn outlines that match Chirps style/metrics are original + committable; Chirp is only a LOCAL measurement/visual reference, never redistributed. Approach: (1) measure a local Chirp copy (metrics, x-height, glyph widths, weights 400/500/700/800) as the faithfulness target; (2) model glyphs as type-safe bezier-outline definitions in Spiral; (3) generate a real woff2/otf via a font-assembly backend (fonttools/opentype tables). Ties to MICROBLOG-UI-CLIENT-PARITY (the wrap math needs stable font metrics) and the cross-backend reuse thesis. Big + research-y; first deliverable is the measurement + a glyph-modeling spike.")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 2u32, "2026-11-08T12:06Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler tip (binary buffers): the lib already has backend-neutral byte primitives in spiral/lib/spiral/bytes.spi (static module 'bytes'): filled, counting, repeated, utf8, copy_into (out, at, part), concat (list of arrays into one array), hex, and the LITTLE-endian le_u64 / le_word. There is a self-checking test, little_endian_words_round_trip. OpenType is BIG-endian throughout (uint16, uint32, Fixed 16.16, F2Dot14, Offset32, Tag), so add be_u16 / be_u32 / be_i16 next to le_u64, with a round-trip test written like the existing one, instead of hand-shifting in each table emitter. The table directory checksum is a sum of big-endian u32 words over each table padded to 4 bytes, so a be_word reader plus a pad_to_4 also belong in bytes.spi. Build each table with filled/copy_into into a preallocated array, then concat the tables. am'.spi's to_le_bytes / as_bytes are Rust-only backend_switch escapes; avoid them if the font code should run on every backend.")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 3u32, "2026-11-08T12:12Z", "management-f33af161", "BACKEND + MODELING decided (i574n routing 11-08): BACKEND = Spiral RUST backend (users rule: reuse Rust crates when targeting Rust). ASSEMBLER = Google fontations crates - write-fonts to build the OpenType tables, read-fonts/skrifa to read them back for round-trip tests (fontc is built on these). NOT fontTools (would be a new .py, which the user avoids) and NOT a hand-written table writer: Spiral owns the glyph model + metrics, the crate owns byte layout + checksums. Ship TTF/OTF first, woff2 after (a maintained woff2 encoder crate, or brotli + the woff2 table transform). BEZIER MODELING via typefeatureset: model on-curve/off-curve points + segment kinds; TrueType glyf is QUADRATIC and CFF is CUBIC so a GADT indexed by curve ORDER avoids mixing them; a PHANTOM unit type (font-units vs em) avoids scale bugs. Binary-buffer/struct-packing primitives: ask compiler (lib). html SVG path work is i574ns with no reusable path model to offer; tibia asked for graphics tips. This is the first concrete proof of the cross-backend-reuse thesis if the glyph model is shared.")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 4u32, "2026-11-08T12:26Z", "management-f33af161", "CHIRP METRICS measured 11-08 (design target for the grotesque font; via fontTools read of the local chirp-regular-web.woff2 - one-off analysis, the BUILD stays Rust/fontations). Regular/400: unitsPerEm=1000, xHeight=500 (0.50em), capHeight=710 (0.71em), ascent=952 descent=-241 lineGap=0, 731 glyphs. Advance widths (em): n 0.563, o 0.565, e 0.539, a 0.543, g 0.515, i 0.235, l 0.235, t 0.356, H 0.727, M 0.863, w 0.734, space 0.250, period 0.317. So the grotesque target: 1000 upm, a TALL x-height (0.5em - key to Chirps screen-first look), narrow i/l (0.235), wide M (0.863). Next spike step: a minimal write-fonts proof (2-3 glyphs at these metrics -> valid OTF -> skrifa round-trip) to validate the Rust pipeline, then model glyphs in Spiral (GADT by curve order + phantom font-unit type). Can measure medium/bold/heavy weights the same way when needed.")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 5u32, "2026-11-09T11:54Z", "org", "11-09 org: MAJOR progress, item was stale at 0%. LATIN FACE COMPLETE + DELIVERED (the Chirp-replacement core deliverable). 69 glyphs spiral-generated end-to-end (lib/font_glyph.spi outlines -> write-fonts 0.55 -> valid TTF -> skrifa round-trip): full a-z + A-Z + digits 0-9 + 7 punct, all render-verified off-box on europa, lib Cells quad-count gate green 54/54. Tables: head/hhea/maxp/post/hmtx/cmap/glyf/loca + OS/2 v4 (weight 400, USE_TYPO_METRICS, metrics 952/-241/0 from Chirp) + name (family placeholder Sans). woff2 via Google reference woff2_compress on europa (63pct smaller, 2076 vs 5592 bytes). DELIVERED to microblog at europa:/home/remote/stage/fonts/ (sans.ttf + sans.woff2 + sha256 each); run-build.sh emits both in one build. microblog integrates via @font-face alias XSans into timeline_page, rides next pluto redeploy; org holds the p99 bracketing. double-story a upgraded from single-story placeholder (17 quads, render-verified). Full build traps + API in memory spiral-grotesque-font-rust-proof. REMAINING (the other 35pct): multi-script Cyrillic/Greek (cheap, reuses Latin primitives) -> Hangul (algorithmic jamo composition) -> Arabic (cursive joining + GPOS, the marathon); optional weight axes. Per-increment work is LIGHT (author a glyph + a europa build ~40s) even though the epic is heavy; consider light child items per script for the ready-queue.")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 6u32, "2026-11-09T11:54Z", "org", "DoD: the proportional sans face is spiral-generated end-to-end and deployed to microblog as woff2 replacing Chirp, covering Latin (DONE+deployed v1), then Cyrillic, Greek, Hangul, Arabic, each with a skrifa round-trip and the lib Cells quad-count gate green. Next step: model Cyrillic reusing the Latin primitives (A=A O=O P=P C=C share outlines; the ellipse/stem/arch/arc_seg toolkit covers all of it) - add letters to lib/font_glyph.spi + the emitter glyphs list, render-verify each on europa, start with the ~10 Latin-lookalikes then the distinct forms (Б Д Ж З Л Ф Ц Ч Ш Щ Ю Я). Per-increment load is light (one build ~40s).")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 7u32, "2026-11-09T12:49Z", "org", "11-09 org: multi-script Cyrillic progressing well. Font now 88 glyphs = Latin (deployed v2) + 19 Cyrillic caps: 11 Latin-lookalike (reused outlines А В Е К М Н О Р С Т Х) + 8 distinct (Г П Ш Ф З И Ц Щ, new rect/poly4/ellipse glyphs, each render + gate verified). All pure-composition from the proven toolkit. Next step: remaining distinct Cyrillic caps (Й Э Ъ Ы Ь Ю Я Б Д Л Ж У - these are MEDIUM, need arcs/mirrors/bowls, not pure rects) then lowercase Cyrillic, best done fresh per beat. Cyrillic stays out of the delivered microblog artifact (Latin v2) until the script is complete (partial = .notdef boxes for Russian text).")
inl note_of_spiral_grotesque_font_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-GROTESQUE-FONT", 8u32, "2026-11-09T14:55Z", "org", "11-09 org: MILESTONE - the complete CAPITAL alphabet of all three scripts is done (font 133 glyphs). Latin both cases (deployed v2) + Cyrillic 32/32 caps (А-Я, all render-verified: 11 Latin-lookalike reuses + 21 distinct incl the hard Ж/Д/Й-breve/Ч/Я/У/Э) + Greek 24/24 caps (Α-Ω: 17 lookalikes + Θ Ξ Λ Δ Σ Ψ Ω distinct) + 7 Cyrillic lowercase + 1 Greek lowercase + digits + punct. All distinct glyphs composed from the proven primitive toolkit (rect/ellipse/poly4/arch/arc_seg/open-rings), each render + quad-count-gate verified. Build loop: emitter build + ASCII render per letter on whichever of ganymede/europa is free (both shared, fluctuate), batched lib cells gate. REMAINING (the user's 'can take days' multi-script arc): the BULK LOWERCASE (Cyrillic а-я + Greek α-ω, mostly distinct small-forms - the real running-text coverage), then Hangul (algorithmic jamo composition) + Arabic (cursive+GPOS). Deployed artifact stays Latin v2 until a script's lowercase completes (partial = .notdef boxes in running text).")
inl quiet_window_forecast () = Task ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", "Forecast quiet windows per machine (box, node, europa) from the job queue and waits-as-data, and schedule timing-sensitive and heavy work into them", Feature, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "delegate,scheduling,heavy,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_quiet_window_forecast () = Planning ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_1 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 1u32, "2026-11-08T12:02Z", "i574n", "USER 11-08 08:0x: 'since the wifi node have the same hardware as this one here but without the other windows interference, we could coordinate with kit perfect quiet windows, planning/forecasting when the box will be fully waiting for new work, maybe waiting for europa isnt enough because there it's weak'. Facts 11-08 12:00 node: same CPU class as the box (i7-4810MQ), but not idle: microblog sim verify (xqueue3) and rig regen probe (kit-probe1108a) running, plus SystemInformer and RustDesk using CPU at rest. europa: load 1.5, 1.6 GB available (3.4 GB total), too weak for A/B timing. Shape: every job declares expected duration, cores and memory (typed resource claim); the forecaster reads running jobs, queued jobs and waits-as-data (AGILE-WAITS-AS-DATA, TRAIN40) and prints the next window where a machine is fully free, per machine; DELEGATE-12-QUIET-SLOT and KIT-JOB-QUIET-START become consumers (an exclusive slot is booked in a forecast window instead of polled for). The node is the preferred A/B and bench host (no box desktop interference); the box is used when its forecast says it will sit waiting for work. Accuracy is measured: forecast vs actual idle start, logged per window. DoD: kit prints the next quiet window per machine with a confidence; a bench job booked into it runs with no co-scheduled job (proof from the job log); forecast error tracked over a week. Next step: inventory what every lane's heavy jobs already record (start, end, cores) to seed duration estimates.")
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_2 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 2u32, "2026-11-08T12:57Z", "i574n", "i574n 11-08 12:5x FIRST HAND-RUN (before any tooling): node queue after TRAIN40's node half (rig est. 11:30-12:30 local): slot 1 compiler Gleam build-layout bench (~40 min, single gleam build at a time, A/A copy included; user question on Gleam parallel build, feeds X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT); slot 2 i574n incremental A/B (cell workspace, ~30-45 min); then TRAIN41 (also waits for pack62 on box + node). pack62's node copy is held during a quiet slot. Rig asked to log the actual TRAIN40 node-half end vs the estimate, as the first forecast-error data point. Hand-relay is the baseline this item must beat: today it costs one message per hop.")
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_3 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 3u32, "2026-11-08T13:37Z", "i574n", "13:4x DATA POINT, booking collision by hand: fiscal queued 6-7 h of node shards (ERP-unblock landing gate) at 13:10, before seeing the queue note, and believed the node was free of TRAIN40 (it was not: stage train25 still running) while microblog restarted xqueue3. Three lanes on the node at 100%. Resolution: fiscal's regen kept, its shards dequeued behind the two quiet slots (compiler Gleam bench, i574n A/B), then fiscal. Lesson for the tool: a lane can't see other lanes' queued or running node jobs, so the forecaster needs the live job list from every lane's stages (remote-stage names), not only kit's own jobs.")
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_4 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 4u32, "2026-11-08T14:15Z", "i574n", "DATA POINT 1 (rig): TRAIN40 node half forecast at 08:2x to end 11:30-12:30; actual 10:13:47 (66/66 notebooks); error 77-137 min early. Cause: scaled from TRAIN39's full set (196 notebooks) while TRAIN40 ran only the 66 module notebooks of the 29 packages changed since TRAIN39's manifest (regen 48 min, notebooks 70 min, sharing the node with microblog's sim verify). Rule: forecast a train from changed packages x their notebooks, known at launch, about 64 s per notebook on a shared node; never from the last train's wall time. DATA POINT 2 (i574n, 10:1x): the moment TRAIN40's node half ended, rig launched kit-probe1108f on the node while fiscal's regen was still running (load 93%), so the booked quiet slot did not open. The end of one job is when every lane's queue rushes in; the forecaster must hold a booked slot (an admission lock), not just predict it.")
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_5 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 5u32, "2026-11-08T14:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 10:1x rig, FORECAST DATA POINT 1 (asked by i574n): TRAIN40 node half forecast at 08:2x to end 11:30-12:30; actual end 10:13:47; error 77-137 min early. Cause: scaled from TRAIN39 full set (196 notebooks) while TRAIN40 ran only the module notebooks of 29 changed packages (66 notebooks). Measured: regen 48 min, notebooks 70 min (about 64 s per notebook on a node shared with a sim verify). Rule for the forecaster: a train notebook count is known at launch (changed packages times their notebooks), so forecast from that count and a per-notebook rate, not from the last train wall time.")
inl note_of_quiet_window_forecast_6 () = Note ("QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST", 6u32, "2026-11-08T18:11Z", "i574n", "DATA POINT 3 (11-08 14:1x): the post-fiscal gap ran as booked with no collision: fiscal confirmed nothing queued, then compiler's monolith arm (quiet precheck passed), the node runner swap, the i574n sweep, then TRAIN41 handed to rig by message. Cost of the hand protocol for one gap: 7 messages across 4 lanes. Near miss: fiscal's shardsB had been queued with an auto-start on load < 70%; it had already run, but a still-queued load-triggered job would have entered the booked gap. A load threshold must also consult the reservation.")
inl agile_wip_sla () = Task ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", "kit agile verbs have a measured latency SLA on the live backlog (wip, list, next, show, note), benchmarked per train with a regression gate", Story, P0, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "agile,performance,sla,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_agile_wip_sla () = Planning ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_agile_wip_sla_1 () = Note ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", 1u32, "2026-11-08T12:02Z", "i574n", "USER 11-08 08:0x: 'we gotta benchmark to make the agile wip fast, bad thing to spend too much time with a buggy feature, lanes unlearn it and that causes cognitive dissonance, the healing pipeline gotta be fast with sla, rig could have that also with the typefeatureset'. Facts: agile wip hangs on the live backlog (memory: lanes switched to list --ready); agile.spi is ~9.6 MB and grows ~1 KB per edit; KIT-AGILE-SEAL-DONE is the planned compaction. A verb lanes avoided becomes a verb lanes forget, and its fix is then never trusted. Shape: a bench over the live backlog snapshot (copy, never the live file) times each read verb cold and warm; budgets are typed values in the source (a verb declares its latency budget, the typefeatureset makes a verb without one fail to compile); each train runs the bench and refuses to install on a budget breach. Initial budgets proposed: show/note under 1 s, list/next/wip under 2 s at p95 on the box. DoD: wip under budget on the live backlog; the bench runs in every train; one breach demonstrated as a refused install (negative control). Next step: time wip, list --ready, next and show on a snapshot today to get the baseline, then decide seal-first vs index-first.")
inl note_of_agile_wip_sla_2 () = Note ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", 2u32, "2026-11-09T22:31Z", "org-614162e5", "11-09 18:2x org-614162e5 (resilience cross-team help, baseline step). BASELINE MEASURED on a SNAPSHOT COPY of the live backlog (21M, state/agile.spi 10.3MB; never timed the live file - lane edits would add noise + contention). Each read verb timed with cd-into-root + dot-root (a /c/... path under MSYS_NO_PATHCONV fast-fails state/agile.spi-missing and measures only the ERROR path - caught + corrected). timeout-capped 45s so a hang records bounded, not infinite. Box under agent load (upper-ish; a quiet re-run tightens). RESULTS (s, 3-4 reps):\n- show: 2.02-2.14 STABLE (cold==warm) -> 2x the proposed <1s budget. cold==warm means this is a pure LOAD+PARSE floor (CPU parsing 10.3MB), not disk cache.\n- list --ready: 1.96-3.84, median ~2.4 -> just over <2s.\n- next --open 60: 7.24-18.77, median ~9, p-high 18.8 -> 4-9x over <2s. SEVERE + high variance.\n- wip: >45 TIMEOUT (rc124) -> confirms the wip-hangs memory; worst verb, did not finish.\nSEAL-FIRST vs INDEX-FIRST (the note-1 decision point), from the data: show's ~2s is a PARSE FLOOR present under EVERY verb (cold==warm proves it is compute-parsing the whole file, not IO). So SEAL/compaction cuts that floor for all verbs and alone likely brings show+list under budget. But next+wip add an O(whole-list) filter/sort ON TOP of the floor (7-45s), so they need an INDEX (or seal to shrink n enough) beyond compaction. ANSWER: seal-first for the broad win (drops the ~2s floor everywhere), then index/algorithmic work specifically for next+wip which seal alone will not fix. A per-verb regression budget (not one global) is warranted: show/list post-seal; next/wip need the deeper fix. Snapshot + raw timings in my scratchpad if rig wants the reps.")
inl note_of_agile_wip_sla_3 () = Note ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", 3u32, "2026-11-09T22:51Z", "org-614162e5", "11-09 18:4x org-614162e5 SEAL EXPERIMENT - corrects my note-2 seal-first lean. Ran kit agile seal on a SNAPSHOT copy (never live) + re-timed. FINDINGS: (1) seal is INCREMENTAL not full-compaction: it archived 417 items but shrank state/agile.spi only 3.4% (10,755,360 -> 10,387,089 B; 10.26 -> 9.91 MB). Most of the file (581 planned + 167 active + ~825 unsealed done) stays live. So seal does NOT materially cut the parse floor. (2) Post-seal wall-clock was WORSE not better (show 4.6-6.0s vs 2.0-2.1s pre-seal; list 5-13s vs 2-4s) - but this is CONFOUNDED by box load (the seal itself burned 32s CPU; concurrent load rose). CONCLUSION, evidence-backed: verb wall-clock on the box is DOMINATED BY BOX LOAD, not agile.spi size at this scale - show ranged 2-6s across this session purely from load while the file changed <4%. IMPLICATIONS for the SLA design, correcting note 2: (a) seal-first is NOT the clear win I reasoned - the file barely shrinks and latency is load-bound; seal is for archive-growth hygiene, not verb speed. (b) The REAL lever is an INDEX (compact dispatch over active+ready without re-parsing 10MB) - the O(whole-list) scan is the structural cost. (c) CRITICAL: the SLA benchmark itself MUST control for load or it measures box noise, not verb cost - run on a QUIET box, or measure CPU-TIME not wall-time, or on a pinned/dedicated node (nice/taskset, cf. off-train-notebook-probe). A wall-clock p95 budget on a loaded box will flap and fail the regression gate on load, not regressions. Lesson: I reasoned seal-first from cold==warm; the experiment did not support it - measure, do not reason, about perf.")
inl note_of_agile_wip_sla_4 () = Note ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", 4u32, "2026-11-09T22:58Z", "org-614162e5", "11-09 19:0x org-614162e5 PRODUCTION-IMPACT (wild evidence, strengthens P0). The autonomous-gate's own kit agile next --open 60 against the LIVE backlog MISSED its time budget and the gate served a STALE cached pool list (from 18:52, ~10+ min old) - observed in the gate text itself. This is the user's exact stated harm manifesting (11-08: lanes unlearn a slow verb, cognitive dissonance): a control-loop that depends on agile next can no longer enumerate the ready pool within budget, so it mislabels what is ready/claimed and can misdirect a lane to a stale item. Confirms the baseline (note 2: next 7-18s, wip timeout) is not academic - a real scheduler is already degraded by it. Reinforces index-first (note 3): the gate needs a FAST bounded ready-pool query (a compact index, not a 10MB scan), and until then callers should prefer list --ready (memory kit-agile-wip-hangs-use-list-ready) and tolerate a cached list. This is the negative-control the DoD wants, found in the wild.")
inl note_of_agile_wip_sla_5 () = Note ("AGILE-WIP-SLA", 5u32, "2026-11-10T11:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 07:4x rig, PARTIAL BASELINE (CONTAMINATED: the box was at 100 pct CPU, so absolute times are inflated; rig stopped the run itself, since timing on a saturated box is neither CPU-light nor clean). Snapshot of the live backlog (21 MB; state/agile.spi 11 MB, 2,176 items), installed kit, 3 runs each: agile wip 285,159 / 135,248 / 141,164 ms; agile list --ready 4,306 / 9,995 / 4,167 ms. next and show were not reached. The RATIO (wip 30-60x list --ready) is far beyond load noise, so it already answers part of the seal-first vs index-first question: wip's cost is not the parse that list --ready also pays, it is wip's own per-item work (memory kit-agile-wip-hangs-use-list-ready). A compact ready-index helps next and the gate (HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE C1), but wip needs its own algorithmic fix. Next step: rerun the full baseline (wip, list --ready, next --team, next --open 60, show, list all --json) on a quiet host or node with the same snapshot, then profile wip's per-item step.")
inl heal_pipeline_sla () = Task ("HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA", "The healing pipeline has an SLA: time from a detected fault (alarm, red gate, broken verb) to a fix landed or a typed waiver, measured per incident", Story, P1, D4, 35u32, Active, "", "healing,sla,resilience,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_heal_pipeline_sla () = Planning ("HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_heal_pipeline_sla_1 () = Note ("HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA", 1u32, "2026-11-08T12:02Z", "i574n", "USER 11-08 08:0x: 'the healing pipeline gotta be fast with sla'. management absorbed resilience's healing duties 11-08. Shape: every alarm or red gate opens an incident record (typed: detected_at, owner lane, severity); closing needs a landed fix or a waiver with an expiry; the SLA per severity is data, the beacon reports breaches. Pairs with AGILE-WIP-SLA (rig owns tool-verb latency; management owns time-to-heal). DoD: one week of incidents with measured time-to-heal; breaches surfaced in the daily digest. Next step: list the alarm sources that already exist (node-env alarms, lane-reconcile, gates) and what each records today.")
inl note_of_heal_pipeline_sla_2 () = Note ("HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA", 2u32, "2026-11-09T12:07Z", "org3", "org3 11-09 FIRST SLICE: SLA metric defined + alarm-source list + REAL baseline from this session's incidents (grounded, not hypothetical). \nKEY FINDING that shapes the SLA: the latency splits in TWO, and HEAL latency is NOT the problem - DETECTION latency is:\n- HEAL latency (detected -> fix landed): consistently FAST this session: probe ssh-hang 3 probes detected 11:54 -> all fixed ~12:00 = ~6min; europa 4 orphan waiters detected ~11:35 -> healed 11:40 = ~5min; moon rustdesk-hung detected ~11:34 -> healed 11:36 = ~2min; SLA-soak-exit detected (notification) 11:25 -> relaunched+verified 11:27 = ~2min; drift-aliasing detected+fixed same beat.\n- DETECTION latency (fault born -> detected): the BOTTLENECK: europa waiters lived 2.5-5.6 DAYS before detection; moon rustdesk hung 3 DAYS; the 3 probe hang-risks were latent ~forever (no probe self-audit existed). \nSo the heal pipeline is FAST to heal but SLOW to DETECT. The SLA must measure BOTH legs; breaches to watch = DETECTION gaps, which NMR-COVERAGE (probe reach) + NMR-ORPHAN-TRACK (continuous orphan scan) + NMR-PROBE-ROBUST (probe self-audit) directly close.\nALARM SOURCES that exist today (the next-step list): node-env drift-watch (edge note), idle-with-ready-alarm, saturation-monitor (drain/overload/comet), SLA soak (pluto stall), store-offsite-backup (verify), node-utilization-monitor, heartbeat-staleness-guard. Most record a .last.log with a write-time; few record a typed detected_at + owner + severity incident row.\nDoD: a week of incidents with BOTH legs measured (detect-latency + heal-latency) per severity; breaches surfaced in the daily digest; pairs AGILE-WIP-SLA (rig: verb latency).\nNext step: a typed incident record (detected_at, fault-born-at if knowable, owner, severity, closed_at|waiver) that the existing alarms open on fire; seed it with this session's 5 incidents above as the first week's data.")
inl note_of_heal_pipeline_sla_3 () = Note ("HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA", 3u32, "2026-11-09T15:44Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09 SLICE: typed INCIDENT ROW + both-leg SLA thresholds (the schema org3 slices point to; management owns time-to-heal).\nINCIDENT (typed model, pairs typed-boundaries-playbook): id; source (drift-watch | saturation-monitor | SLA-soak | heartbeat-staleness-guard | idle-with-ready | node-utilization | store-offsite); owner_lane; severity (S1 node-down/prod-down | S2 degraded: red gate, broken verb, resource pressure | S3 latent: orphan, drift, probe gap); born_at (best estimate of fault origin, nullable); detected_at (required); closed_at (required to close); resolution (fix | waiver); waiver_expiry (required iff waiver; past expiry REOPENS). Derived: detect_latency = detected_at - born_at; heal_latency = closed_at - detected_at.\nSLA PER SEVERITY, BOTH LEGS: S1 detect<=5m heal<=30m; S2 detect<=1h heal<=4h; S3 detect<=24h heal<=1wk.\nGROUNDED CHECK vs org3 baseline: HEAL leg already meets S1/S2 (~2-6m this session). DETECT leg blows S3 massively (europa orphans 2.5-5.6 DAYS, moon rustdesk 3 DAYS vs the 24h S3 target) -> EVERY breach this session was a DETECTION breach, confirming detection is the SLA risk.\nborn_at ESTIMATION (so detect_latency is measurable, not just a write-time): orphan=process etimes/start; drift=fingerprint-change time; disk=first df-over-threshold sample; stale-file=writer last-fresh mtime. Where born_at is unknowable, record detect_latency=UNBOUNDED and classify as an S3 detection-gap incident -> routes to NMR-COVERAGE (probe reach) + NMR-ORPHAN-TRACK + NMR-PROBE-ROBUST, which are exactly the items that close it.\nDIGEST: daily digest reports per-severity breach counts SPLIT BY LEG; a repeated UNBOUNDED from one source escalates to a coverage item.\nOWNERSHIP: schema + weekly measurement = org (manual census now, as org3 baselined); the automated incident STORE + beacon = a rig verb (AGILE-WIP-SLA sibling) when rig lands it. Not blocked on rig: org measures manually meanwhile.\nNEXT STEP: measure one week of incidents, both legs per severity (DoD). Recurring weekly; the cadence row parks it until the week of 2026-11-16 for the next census.")
