inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_1 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:12Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "DoD: R2 reads gate-cost rows once FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET writes them and flags a trend crossing 80 percent of the cap; R4 reads machine-watch samples and the job queue; the fiscal gate as the box top memory consumer 11-08 01:xx trips R4. NEXT: wait for FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET row shape; R4 can start from kit machine watch samples. TYPEFEATURES: trend and hog evidence records in the Resource family.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_2 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 2u32, "2026-11-08T13:47Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 10:4x rig: R2 input shape settled by fiscal (FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET note 3): target/ci-metrics/gate-cost<label><shard>.tsv, tab-separated with a header, columns Run, Host, Os, Core, Shard, Slots, Unit, Phase (Regen, Format, Unit, Test, Examples, Merge), Seconds, InputLines, InputBytes, Target, LoadStart, LoadEnd, FreeMbStart, Exit; a merged run joins shards into gate-cost<label>.tsv; rows start today. KIT-JOB-FAN writes its per-shard rows in the same shape. NEXT: read the merged tsv as typed rows (one record per row, Phase as a union) and flag a gate whose Seconds trend crosses 80 percent of its cap.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_3 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 3u32, "2026-11-09T04:05Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 00:2x rig: input check before modeling: fiscal's merged gate-cost rows (target/ci-metrics/gate-cost<label>.tsv, shape fixed 11-08 in FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET n3) do not exist on the box yet; fiscal target/ci-metrics holds only the 10-25..10-27 Cells marker files. Modeling against no rows would be a guess, so held on team:fiscal until the first merged tsv lands; then read it as typed rows (one record per row, Phase union Regen | Format | Unit | Test | Examples | Merge) and flag a gate whose Seconds trend crosses 80 pct of its cap.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_4 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 4u32, "2026-11-09T11:34Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 fiscal ANSWER (the item waited on team:fiscal for merged gate-cost rows): they exist and are published. Live: https://i574n.github.io/fiscal/target/ci-metrics-windows-2025/gate-cost.tsv (HTTP 200, 351 rows; columns Run, Host, Os, Core, Shard, Slots, Unit, Phase, Seconds, InputLines, InputBytes, Target, LoadStart, LoadEnd, FreeMbStart, Exit; Phase = Regen | Format | Unit | Test | Examples). The ubuntu copy publishes at target/ci-metrics-ubuntu-24.04/gate-cost.tsv on the next green-enough push: build.ps1 (fiscal, unstaged, ships with the user's next push) now keeps writing every metrics table even when a gate step fails, so red runs publish their rows too. Also new for the trend half: build.ps1 writes target/ci-metrics/growth.tsv (Unit, Before, Now, Percent, BeforeRun) flagging units that grew over 25 % and 60 s at the same Os, Core, Slots and InputBytes against the previous published rows; R2 can read that directly or recompute from gate-cost. Budget: a unit over -UnitBudgetMinutes (60) is a build problem unless waived in build.ps1 with a date and an item. Nothing else needed from fiscal; this item can proceed.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_5 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 5u32, "2026-11-09T11:58Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 08:5x local rig, FIRST LOOK at fiscal's published rows (pages windows-2025 gate-cost.tsv, fetched to scratch): 351 rows, ONE run (202611090239-runnervmm7ud1) since the pages keep only the latest, 34,042 s of unit time: Test 21,980 s (248 rows), Unit 10,471 s (100), Regen 1,154 s (generator, the single heaviest row), Format 435 s, Examples 2 s. Hogs: generated_ubl_creditnote_2_1 unit 1,146 s + its writes test 1,115 s, generated_ubl_invoice_2_1 982 s + 961 s. growth.tsv is NOT published yet (404 on both paths; it ships with the user's next fiscal push). CONSEQUENCE for R2: a trend needs history the pages do not keep, so rig must keep it: each heartbeat (or a scheduled kit job) fetches the latest gate-cost.tsv, appends its run as typed rows to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\metrics\\fiscal-gate-cost.spi (record codec, no TSV in kit), and computes per-unit Seconds trend against the cap from FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET (cap values still to read there). R4 (hog: one process holding a host while others wait) does not need fiscal and can start from machine watch samples + job queue. Next: read the caps on FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET, then the history store model. TypeFeatures: gate_phase union (Regen, Format, Unit, Test, Examples, Merge), gate_cost_row record, trend verdict (Steady | Rising pct | NearCap pct).")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_6 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 6u32, "2026-11-09T12:00Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:0x local rig, CAPS read from FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET n1: flag a unit whose seconds grew > 25 pct at the same lib and core; FAIL when the slowest shard passes 60 min (dated waiver row excepted). R2 threshold = 80 pct = 48 min per shard. The published CI windows run is unsharded (Shard=all, Slots=2): Unit+Regen+Format = 12,060 s, i.e. about 100 min of wall at P2, already past the 60 min cap if the cap applies to CI runs (it may be meant for the sharded node gate only; asked fiscal). So the first R2 reading is a question of scope, not a trend: which runs the cap governs.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_7 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 7u32, "2026-11-09T12:02Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:1x fiscal ANSWERED the scope (relayed): the budget as implemented (build.ps1 -UnitBudgetMinutes, default 60) is PER UNIT in every mode incl. CI (waivers dated in build.ps1); there is no whole-run cap in build.ps1; the CI wall cap is the workflow job timeout-minutes 420 (deploy 180). The opening note's slowest-shard wording was superseded (FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET n14-n16). So R2 scopes: (a) per-unit Seconds vs 3,600 (alarm at 80 pct = 2,880 s), (b) whole-run wall vs 420 min (alarm at 336 min). The 11-09 run passes both: slowest unit 1,146 s (32 pct), run ~100 min wall (24 pct). Model slice (unprobed, post-train probe batch): src/rig_degrade/degrade.spi (HostFree + LibReferenced, kit standards 0): gate_phase union with phase words, trend union (Steady | Rising pct | NearCap pct | OverCap pct) via trend_of previous latest cap (80 pct near, 25 pct growth = the build.ps1 growth flag), host_sample record and hog_verdict (Hog when one process holds >= 80 pct CPU for >= 30 min while jobs wait) for R4. Tests use the real 11-09 numbers. Next: the typed history store fed by the pages each heartbeat, then R4 from machine watch samples.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_8 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 8u32, "2026-11-09T23:34Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 19:3x rig, proof: the model passed its notebook gate on ganymede, off-train (rig_degrade/degrade.spi 2/2 (the D4 caps model); Cells run_notebook --gate, rust target, rig's probe layout). The first full off-train batch had shown this package half-proven (first test 'spiral not loaded'); a warm re-run passed every test, so that was a cold-start artifact of the first batch, not the package. The probe wait is answered; next step is the item's IO half per its last note.")
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d4_9 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D4", 9u32, "2026-11-09T23:36Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 19:4x rig: parked on train:TRAIN44. The model is probe-green; the recorded next step is a walk, a store fed by a heartbeat, or wiring into shared kit code (agile next / todo scan / job host), which goes into TRAIN44 with its own regen and crate-gate proof.")
inl agile_degrade_d5 () = Task ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D5", "degradation hits become incidents: typed incident rows for HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA, an unblock line in the owning team next, and a scheduled run", TaskKind, P0, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,healing,degradation,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_agile_degrade_d5 () = Planning ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D5", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_agile_degrade_d5_1 () = Note ("AGILE-DEGRADE-D5", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:12Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "DoD: each hit opens one incident row (deduplicated per item and signal) that HEAL-PIPELINE-SLA times; the hit shows in the owning team agile next through AGILE-WAITS-RESOLVE routing; a scheduled run (hourly, CPU-light) on the box. NEXT: after AGILE-WAITS-RESOLVE lands the routing. TYPEFEATURES: incident row indexed by the degradation case.")
inl rig_units_check () = Task ("RIG-UNITS-CHECK", "rig units check/sync: each unit's link list is the only edge list; packages, Cargo path deps and workspace members are derived and hand edits refused; kind-edge rule from [package.metadata.spiral] kind", Feature, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "units,typed-units,standards,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rig_units_check () = Planning ("RIG-UNITS-CHECK", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rig_units_check_1 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-CHECK", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:13Z", "i574n", "From TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 (notes 8-10; it gates MICROBLOG-MIGRATION). rig accepted 11-08 13:1x as a kit verb, landing after TRAIN41 so it ships as rig units. MEASURED drift: html writes every edge three times and the copies disagree (inline and table link 3 units but list 1 Cargo dep); kit's 602 crates do the same. SPEC: units check <root>: (1) source of truth = each unit's link list (spiral.toml link or package.spiproj link:); (2) derived: packages = core + spiral + link closure, Cargo [dependencies] path deps = link edges, workspace members = all units; refuse anything not derived; (3) kind-edge table from [package.metadata.spiral] kind (model | boundary | feature | bin): model links only lib, boundary links model, feature links model and never boundary, bin links anything; (4) entry file named after the unit (no shared unit.spi: Gleam *_ffi sidecars collide); (5) module names unique across the unit graph (linked modules open by bare name). units sync <root> rewrites the derived copies. TypeFeatures: the unit graph is typed (kind union + edge record), the kind rule is a type-checked table, not string matching. Launch precheck guard 6 becomes a call to units check. DoD: units check passes on i574n.github/examples/typed-units and on html after its derived lists are synced; one refused hand edit demonstrated (negative control); kit/rig's own crates checked (count of drifted edges reported, then synced). Next step: after TRAIN41, model the graph over the parsers kit already has (spiproj, spiral.toml, Cargo walk in the crate gate).")
inl note_of_rig_units_check_2 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-CHECK", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 12:0x rig GROOM (microblog UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA n6, compiler n2/n3). ORIGIN: this delivers the left-off promise of COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS n2 (11-01, kit/compiler agreement, hole 1: one source of truth): kit generates Cargo [dependencies] from the compiler unit graph because hand-kept deps drift. DESIGN CHANGE: the input is an ABSTRACT typed unit graph, not a parse of link: lists (LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST: the user did not ask for link: and wants its benefits without it). Until the compiler emits the graph, an adapter builds it from package.spiproj and spiral.toml; the emitters never read manifests directly. One graph feeds three emitters: Cargo.toml plus workspace members (Rust), one gleam.toml per app with units as src/<unit>.gleam (Gleam), one build.ninja per app (C, if LINKED-UNITS-C is accepted). Early-cutoff keys follow compiler's sound rule (own source plus interface hash plus the importer used set with body hashes), owned by UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH, not here. Re-sized from 480 m into 5 slices of at most 240 m (sum 750 m). Order after TRAIN41: U1, U2, U3, U5, then U4 when C is accepted. NEXT: U1. TYPEFEATURES: unit_graph record of units and edges; unit_kind union (Model, Boundary, Feature, Bin); a manifest GADT indexed by backend (CargoManifest, GleamManifest, NinjaManifest) so an emitter cannot return another backend's file.")
inl note_of_rig_units_check_3 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-CHECK", 3u32, "2026-11-09T18:08Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 14:0x microblog groom: cleared this item's wait 'train:TRAIN41'. TRAIN41 was installed 11-08 19:12 (rig, KIT-TARGET-SIZE-RULES n-last: kit.exe 93BDD93F), so the wait was answered but still hid this Planned item from rig's pool. Next step unchanged from rig's 11-08 groom note.")
inl kit_job_handoff () = Task ("KIT-JOB-HANDOFF", "jobs carry their lane; job start hands over the lane next ready light item; next and gate flag a lane with pending jobs and no light item in hand", TaskKind, P0, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,jobs,idle,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_handoff () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-HANDOFF", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_handoff_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-HANDOFF", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:20Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "Filed 11-08 by rig from microblog's report (user 11-08: Cells port should finish pseudo parallel, always advancing CPU-light items while heavy runs go; that is the whole purpose of rig). GAP: kit job records do not know which lane started them, so nothing can tell a lane that it has pending jobs and no light work in hand. DoD: (1) kit job start records the starting lane (from --as or the session lane name) in the job dir and job list shows it; (2) right after the job id, job start prints the lane's top ready light item (the same query as the stop gate, own team first) so launching a heavy run hands over the next light step; (3) agile gate and agile next --team T print a pending-jobs line when lane T has 1 or more running jobs and 0 Active light items (N pending, 0 light in hand, pull one of the listed items); CLI tests for each. Related: KIT-NEXT-FITS-NOW (Done) ranks what fits now; KIT-GATE-WORK-LIST (Done) names ready light items at the stop gate; BACKLOG-LIGHT-DEPTH (i574n) keeps 3 ready light items per team so the handover is never empty; AGILE-WAITS-RESOLVE routes cross-lane blocks. NEXT: add a lane field to the job placement and supervisor records in kit_jobs. TYPEFEATURES: job_owner record (lane, team) on the job model, and a lane_load union (Fed, Starved of pending count) computed per lane.")
inl note_of_kit_job_handoff_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-HANDOFF", 2u32, "2026-11-09T04:02Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 00:1x rig DESIGN against what exists (code in TRAIN43: TRAIN42 code scope is frozen while its regen pre-check runs). (1) The lane is half there: rig_job_create job_launched already writes <job>/author from RIG_AUTHOR or KIT_AUTHOR; missing: job start --as <lane> sets it explicitly, job list prints author=<lane>, and with --on the author must travel in the remote job line (env does not cross ssh; machine defaults.env from KIT-REMOTE-EXECUTOR item 4 is per host, not per lane, so --as is the carrier). (2) The handoff print after job start reuses agile next --team <lane team> --load light,none --open 1 (the stop gate query); the jobs package must not depend on agile, so the print lives in the CLI route that composes both (rig_cli_routes), not in rig_job_create. (3) The pending-jobs line in next and gate counts rows of job list (local plus the remote records the box keeps for --on) whose author maps to the team, and says N pending, 0 light in hand. TypeFeatures: a lane_handoff record { lane; pending : i32; next_item : option string } so the gate and next print the same typed fact. Next step: (1) --as plus the author column, with a CLI test.")
inl fiscal_build_hours () = Task ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", "fiscal's full build takes hours: measure where the time goes per phase and per unit, then cut it with compiler, rig and fiscal levers, keeping ONE entry script (no CI YAML sharding)", Epic, P0, D6, 30u32, Active, "FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET,LARGE-INPUT-SCALING,FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS,FISCAL-GATE-SCALE,KIT-CODESPACE-RUN", "fiscal,performance,bench,codespaces,typefeatureset,team:fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_build_hours () = Planning ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", Minutes 720u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:32Z", "i574n", "USER 11-08 13:3x: 'we need ways to help them fix this hours issue though, benchs to see what can be improved, compiler, rig, etc. we could use these parallel workflows to generate metrics and get info faster but that sprawl into bad standardization in no time, maybe we could be using codespaces more'. Context: CI runs one 420-min job per OS; a full generated build hit the 6 h node cap 11-08; 68 of 81 generated units are over 1000 lines (UBL Invoice 57,667). The multi-job YAML sharding was REVERTED 11-08 (user: drift). RULE: every host runs the same command, build.ps1 with -Shards/-ShardSet/-MergeShards (already in the script); scale = more hosts running it, never YAML logic. PLAN: (1) MEASURE (fiscal): build.ps1 emits typed per-phase rows (regen, spiral compile per unit with input lines and generated lines, cargo/rustc per crate, notebook tests per unit, host, core hash) for one full build on the node; rank the top 10 units by wall time. (2) COMPILER lever: LARGE-INPUT-SCALING already slices fiscal's generated units; fit compile time vs input size (linear or worse?) on the top 10, and say which phase dominates (peval, codegen, GC: memory gc-pause-dominates-compiles). (3) FISCAL lever: split generated units below 1000 generated lines (FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS, typed-units/1 template), which makes them parallel and cacheable; measure the wall change. (4) RIG lever: fan the same shards over box, node, europa AND codespaces with one verb (KIT-CODESPACE-RUN, REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER, CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS); codespaces are standard hosts, not workflows, so metrics come back fast without YAML sprawl. (5) Every lever is an A/B with the quiet-window rule (QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST). DoD: the per-phase table recorded; the full build's wall time cut by a measured factor with the same command on every host; CI still one job calling build.ps1. TypeFeatures: phase rows as a typed union (phase kind) keyed by unit and host; regressions refused by a ratchet. Next step: fiscal adds the per-phase rows to build.ps1 and runs one full node build in the next quiet window.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T13:38Z", "i574n", "compiler 11-08 13:4x: slotted after pack62/63; starts when fiscal posts the top-10 per-phase table. Prior data (LARGE-INPUT-SCALING, europa 11-07): fiscal units 0.9 -> 3.9 MB had --check (parse + infer) time exponents 0.23-0.54, SUBLINEAR, peak 1.1 GB, so the front end is ruled out as the hours cause at those sizes. Remaining suspects: peval and codegen on the big units (UBL Invoice 57,667 lines) and GC pauses; compiler fits per-phase time vs size on fiscal's 10 and runs a GC-off/server-GC A/B if GC shows. Implication for fiscal's per-phase rows: split the Spiral compile row into check vs full build (peval + codegen) so the phases separate.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 3u32, "2026-11-08T13:41Z", "fiscal", "11-08 fiscal groom of this epic (user via i574n, 13:3x). Children, one per lever: (1) MEASURE: FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET (row shape settled in its note today: per-unit and per-phase typed rows with host, core and load). (2) COMPILER: LARGE-INPUT-SCALING (compiler) on the top 10 units from (1). (3) FISCAL split: FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS (new today: generated units below 1000 lines on typed-units/1), with FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES as its closure-sharing half. (4) RIG fan-out: the same build.ps1 -Shards/-ShardSet/-MergeShards command run by more hosts (box, node, europa, codespaces as hosts, KIT-CODESPACE-RUN), never YAML. FISCAL-GATE-SCALE (fiscal's earlier epic: shard, cache reach, budget, versioned families) is nested here as the horizontal and cache lever. Already done 11-08: build.ps1 -Shards/-ShardSet/-ListOnly/-MergeShards, the CI default -Parallel cores/2 inside the one job, per-unit cache keys by lib reach (an xsd_census change now re-tests 0 units instead of 83). First full measurement: the fiscal-erp node shards, booked after TRAIN40, compiler's bench, i574n's A/B and microblog's slot. NEXT STEP: FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET's rows in build.ps1, then the top-10 table here. Estimate: rolls up from the children.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 4u32, "2026-11-08T13:46Z", "i574n", "rig 11-08 13:5x, the RIG lever sequenced: after TRAIN41 and RIG-UNITS-CHECK, (1) KIT-CODESPACE-RUN (no deps, 240 m: a codespace's lifecycle as one verb: create, pinned tools with ok/MISSING abort, overlay, marker-polled steps, fetch, always delete, core-hour rows); (2) REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER closes on top of it (95%; codespaces registered as a machine kind; also waits on LIB-GATE-LINUX-PORTABLE); (3) KIT-JOB-FAN, to be filed by rig: one verb running the SAME command over box, node, europa and N codespaces as shard k of n (shard index substituted into argv), merge on the box when all shards exit 0, one cost row per shard. Standardization lives in that one verb, never per-repo YAML. CAVEAT (measured on the 9 codespace drivers): a codespace pays boot plus the tools step, several minutes, per run, so codespace shards pay off only for shards well over ~20 min. Fiscal's hour-long shards qualify; short gates stay on box/node/europa.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 5u32, "2026-11-08T14:37Z", "i574n", "FIRST NUMBERS (fiscal, node 11-08, ERP-unblock landing regen, core 65DEE79C): generator 28/28 in 4209 s (70 min), formatter 83/83 in 666 s (11 min), 167 files written, 44 units changed (all xml_codec reachers but cfdi_tfd_1_1). Shards: all 12 on the node as two jobs (0-5, 6-11), about 3-3.5 h each at -Parallel 4, merge on the node. NOT on the box: fiscal units peak at 3-5 GB each and the box had ~3 GB free (rule: host free > peak + 1.5 GB). So the full build is ~1.3 h regen + ~6.5 h shards = ~8 h on one node; per-unit seconds per shard come with the shard logs.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 6u32, "2026-11-08T17:57Z", "i574n", "FIRST FULL RUN (fiscal, node 11-08, pinned pack61b copy, ERP-unblock landing set): regen 70 min (generator 28/28 in 4209 s) + formatter 11 min; shards 0-5 ~09:55-12:25 (green), shards 6-11 12:25-13:52 (exit 1), merge 13:52-13:54. Verdict 82 of 83 units green; generated_nfe_v4 VOID (2 of 4 tests hit the generator's size-scaled cell timeout 3,258,112 ms under -Parallel 4 contention; the other 2 passed). Total wall ~5 h end to end on one node (regen + 2 shard jobs + merge), down from the earlier 6 h cap hit. Rerun as the landing proof: the encke rental row fiscal-gate-p24 on pack62 with cell timeouts x2. Data point for the compiler lever: generated_nfe_v4 is a top-10 candidate (a single test running past 54 min under contention).")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 7u32, "2026-11-08T18:00Z", "i574n", "CORRECTION to note 6 (fiscal 11-08 14:0x): the node gate ran 100 units (42 in shards 0-5, 58 in shards 6-11): 99 green, generated_nfe_v4 VOID. '82 of 83' in note 6 was wrong; 83 is only the generated-unit count. Timings unchanged.")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 8u32, "2026-11-08T21:33Z", "fiscal", "11-08 GROOM: 15% -> 30%. Measured today: a cold 100-unit gate is 645 unit-min; the critical path is one unit (ubl_invoice_2_1 4325 s); contention costs at least 1.42x per unit at P4; per-phase cost rows now exist. Rows are at Downloads\\relay-lab\\logs\\fiscal\\20261108-node-gate.*. NEXT STEP: split the 5 UBL units (FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS first slice) and run the first mixed-host weighted gate (FISCAL-GATE-SHARD).")
inl note_of_fiscal_build_hours_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS", 9u32, "2026-11-09T04:00Z", "fiscal", "11-08 BIGGEST LEVER MEASURED: the six UBL units are 98% byte-identical (12,297 of 12,311 top-level blocks, 4.10 MB per copy), so a cold gate compiles the same model 6 times (~4.3 h of unit-time at P4). A shared model unit saves ~20.5 MB of source and ~3+ h per cold gate, and removes the 4,325 s critical-path unit. Details and next step on FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS.")
inl pins_binaryen_typed () = Task ("PINS-BINARYEN-TYPED", "Binaryen (wasm-opt) becomes a typed pin: dice's hand-added CI install step (version_133, commit 0cce1be 11-07) moves into pins.spi + the dice workflow generator, so workflows.exe reports dice identical", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "pins,ci,dice,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pins_binaryen_typed () = Planning ("PINS-BINARYEN-TYPED", Minutes 45u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pins_binaryen_typed_1 () = Note ("PINS-BINARYEN-TYPED", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:34Z", "i574n", "Found 11-08 13:4x by fiscal's render: dice/.github/workflows/gh-pages.yml:45 differs from the typed source because a 5-line Linux step installing binaryen version_133 (curl + GITHUB_PATH) was committed by hand (user commit 0cce1be 'tests', 11-07). dice needs it: contract/build.ps1:56 runs wasm-opt -Oz, and ui/index.html uses trunk's data-wasm-opt. Do NOT delete the step to match the generator (dice CI would lose wasm-opt). Fix: a Binaryen tool row with version 133 in pins.spi (the same version is also pinned elsewhere; the deps audit found version_133 current), plus a dice job step in workflows.spi that renders it; then workflows.exe reports dice identical. Spiral flake.nix:15 drift (nightly 2026-10-18) was fixed in the same pass (now 2026-11-05). DoD: workflows.exe and pins_check report every repo identical. Next step: read how pins.spi models a downloaded tool (zig or bun rows) and add binaryen the same way.")
inl note_of_pins_binaryen_typed_2 () = Note ("PINS-BINARYEN-TYPED", 2u32, "2026-11-09T11:40Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 11:4x WRITTEN (unstaged): pins.spi gains a typed release_download union (Binaryen) with download_version Binaryen = \"version_133\" (a separate type because tool is total over setup ACTIONS: action_of/yaml_key, and binaryen is a downloaded release); workflows.spi dice() passes [binaryen_install ()] as notebook_build's before-steps, built as Run { plain_run (binaryen_lines ()) ?| condition = Some \"runner.os == 'Linux'\"; guard_first = true }, which the renderer prints as \"- if: runner.os == 'Linux'\" + a nested \"run: |\" with the two lines (curl the release tarball into $RUNNER_TEMP, echo its bin dir to $GITHUB_PATH), i.e. byte-for-byte the hand-committed step at dice gh-pages.yml:45-48, placed after setup-python and before workflow.ps1 as today. Wait: job ganymede:org2-pins (box poller; runs pins.ps1 over a layout of every org repo's .github/workflows and diffs dice's regenerated YAML against the committed one; DICE IDENTICAL closes this).")
inl note_of_pins_binaryen_typed_3 () = Note ("PINS-BINARYEN-TYPED", 3u32, "2026-11-09T14:18Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 14:1x PROOF: ganymede job-1794231762019-org2-pins: == DICE IDENTICAL (dice's gh-pages.yml rendered from the typed workflows.spi with the release_download Binaryen step equals the hand-edited one byte for byte). The \"13 workflow files differ\" line is the stage holding only i574n.github + dice (other repos' files absent there), not drift. Remaining: none in scope; the edit rides the user's push.")
inl fiscal_typed_units () = Task ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", "fiscal generated units on typed-units/1: model unit per schema closure plus thin document roots, every unit under 1000 lines", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1", "team:fiscal,fiscal,generator,typed-units,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_typed_units () = Planning ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", Minutes 720u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:41Z", "fiscal", "11-08 opened by fiscal for FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS lever (3). Today 68 of 81 generated units are over 1000 lines (UBL Invoice 57,667), and one unit is one compile, so the slowest unit bounds every gate. typed-units/1 (TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1): one package per unit (model, boundary, feature, bin), link: between units, unit-graph-ordered parallel build, a 1000-line ratchet. For fiscal the generator emits each schema family as a model unit (types and codecs of the shared closure, linked under packages and link per typed-boundaries rule 1) plus thin root units per document, so the six UBL documents share one cac/cbc model unit instead of six 3.8 MB copies. DoD: the generator's output passes the 1000-line ratchet for every unit; the UBL and CT-e v4 families build as model + thin roots with byte-identical decode/encode results on their corpus; per-unit wall time from FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET rows shows the slowest unit at least 4x faster than today's UBL units. NEXT STEP: pick the UBL 2.1 family as the first slice (largest units, one shared closure) and size 3 milestones. Estimate unsized until then.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-11-09T03:59Z", "fiscal", "11-08 PAYOFF ROW (measured, per the studied 'payoff before encoding' rule). The six generated UBL 2.1 units (invoice, creditnote, debitnote, despatchadvice, receiptadvice, applicationresponse; 4.1-4.2 MB each, 25.0 MB total) each hold 12,311 top-level blocks (edition-2 heads: and/instance/nominal/inl/union/type/let), and 12,297 of them, 4.10 MB, are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all six. Each document differs by only 14 blocks. So the gate compiles the same 4.1 MB cac/cbc model 6 times: node P4 unit times 1,774-4,325 s each, ~4.3 h of unit-time for UBL alone. One shared model unit plus six thin document roots compiles the model once: ~20.5 MB of source and ~3+ h of unit-time saved per cold gate (if time scales with size), and the critical path becomes one model compile plus a thin root instead of the 4,325 s ubl_invoice. This is the biggest FISCAL-BUILD-HOURS lever measured so far. DEPENDENCY: linked units (Rust rlibs via link:, spiral AGENTS 'Suite linked') are proven in the compiler's linked suite; the template is TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1. NEXT STEP: a generator slice that emits generated_ubl_model_2_1 (the shared 12,297 blocks) plus one root per document that links it; regen + gate only the 7 UBL units on moon; measure the unit times against today's (node rows in relay-lab logs/fiscal/20261108-node-gate.*).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 3u32, "2026-11-09T04:02Z", "fiscal", "11-08 TREE-WIDE PAYOFF (measured: blocks split on edition-2 heads; a block is shared when byte-identical across units). The 83 generated units hold 67.7 MB, of which 43.1 MB (64%) are DUPLICATE copies of blocks another unit already holds. Top sharing groups, by bytes saved if each compiles once: 20.1 MB for the 6 UBL documents; 3.4 MB for CT-e/CT-e OS/CT-e simp/GTV-e v3+v4 (7 units); 2.0 MB for the same plus CT-e v2 (8); 1.4 MB for the v4 subset (4); 1.4 MB for a 16-unit Brazilian core (CT-e, GTV-e, MDF-e, NF-e events); 1.3 MB for Chile DTE + EnvioDTE; 1.2 MB for all 18 Costa Rica units; 1.0 MB for 16 certificate units. So the shared-closure model units (versioned families + typed units) cut the gate's compiled source by up to ~64%, most of it in UBL (FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS n2) and the Brazilian transport family. NEXT STEP: UBL first (biggest, simplest: one shared group of 6), then the CT-e/GTV-e closure, which matches FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES step 2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 4u32, "2026-11-09T09:00Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 FEASIBILITY FINDING (box text scan of the 6 committed UBL units; read-only): the 12,297 byte-identical blocks are NOT separable as emitted today. (1) The UBL invoice unit is 4,788 top-level groups but ONE 'let rec' group of 7,373 members holds every codec function, and its head is the document-own x_invoice_type_of_children; the 152 types sit in one 'union rec' group whose members include the document type (x_invoice_type is an 'and union' member). A recursion group cannot be split across units, so the shared members are tied to each document's own head. (2) The prototypes (to_text, of_xml, to_xml ...) are declared in each unit's header block, which also carries the per-unit --timeout, and the ~1,220 shared 'instance of_xml/to_xml <type>' blocks depend on them. (3) Each document owns 15 blocks: header, its type and field GADT (_field, _get, _fields, _field_meta), codecs (_of_children, _of_xml, _children, _attributes, _to_xml), example, of_xml/to_xml instances, and the trailing tests block. PREREQUISITE, now the NEXT STEP: xsd_emit emits recursion groups by STRONGLY CONNECTED COMPONENT (only genuinely mutually recursive types/codecs grouped, in dependency order) instead of one giant group; then the shared cac/cbc closure is a set of groups that never contain a document type, the prototypes move to the model unit's header, and each root keeps its 15 own blocks plus a link to the model. Side benefit to measure: smaller recursion groups may cut type-inference time per unit. This is xsd_emit work, so it queues after XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS-3-READ lands (one xsd_emit draft at a time).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 5u32, "2026-11-09T09:11Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 SCC CHANGE MAP (read-only, lib xsd_emit 7A6892B9 + the step-3 draft): (1) codecs: functions_group (~:3424) joins ALL function texts into ONE 'let rec' (it strips the first text's 'and let ' and concatenates the rest), via readers_text <- function_texts (readers + written + exampled walks); (2) types: group_members / emit_group_member with emit_member_head ('union rec ' first, 'and union ' after, ~:1531) builds one recursive type group. (3) The dependency graph ALREADY EXISTS: every emitted item carries references (record ~:389, filled from reference_dependency ~:686 for bases, fields and choices). A type's codecs (_of_children, _of_xml, _children, _attributes, _to_xml, _example) call exactly the codecs of the types it references, so FUNCTION SCCs = TYPE SCCs: no new tracking. PLAN: compute Tarjan SCCs over emitted.references (names -> items); emit types per SCC in reverse topological order (dependencies first): a singleton without a self reference as plain 'union' / 'nominal', a cyclic SCC as 'union rec ... and union ...'; emit codecs per the same SCC order, one 'let rec ... and let ...' per SCC (a singleton non-recursive SCC can be a plain 'let'). Expected: UBL invoice goes from 1 group of 7,373 codecs to many small groups, and the 6 documents' shared closure becomes groups that never include a document type. PROOF: regen identity of decode/encode results on the corpus (the text changes, behavior must not) + per-unit compile time before/after (smaller recursion groups may cut inference time). Queued after XSD-FIELD-DESCRIPTORS-3-READ lands (gate running on moon and europa).")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 6u32, "2026-11-10T15:39Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 11:4x Slice D1 DONE: the dependency-SCC computation (scc_order, recursive_group) is drafted, type-probed (43 typed, 0 errors) and canonical, at drafts\\spiral\\FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1\\xsd_emit.spi 05ED9255E232, stacked on the MDFE-D1 xsd_emit draft. Next step: once FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES-1-MDFE-D1 lands on the box, prove the SCC draft via lib_land_window (-PrivateLib on ganymede, executed_cells), land it, then slice D2: emit types per SCC (plain union/nominal for non-recursive singletons, union rec groups otherwise) and split functions_group the same way.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_7 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 7u32, "2026-11-10T22:48Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 18:5x BSOD RECOVERY (box down 11:51-18:28). Checked every fiscal file landed or drafted today against its recorded hash, none half-written: lib xsd_emit FEE124468E4F (MDFE-D1 land completed at 11:4x, before the crash), event_log AAC5B4F3, fiscal posting 2C5CF36A, families 33AF4D84, lifecycle CF106BDA, currency A6CDA12D, p2p DD5BDC7E, document DE4BB79E, canon AA43331F, SCC draft 05ED9255E232, SFS zip 6F59BF9A; init.ps1 parses with its SFS pin. LOST: the SCC lib proof launch (node-proof was still syncing from the box; no fiscal-scc-proof job exists on ganymede). RELAUNCHED at 18:5x: same lib tar (box digest still 219CE68B16EA64C8; tar D2A3C72EB347 byte-identical on both ends), draft 05ED9255E232, -BaseHash FEE124468E4F, -PrivateLib -Apply, 3 h timeout; predicted box post-land 7162127521827459. Next step: on its green close, control round trip on the box, then land copy-only with -ProvenLog and slice D2.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_8 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 8u32, "2026-11-11T00:04Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:4x SCC PROOF GREEN on ganymede (job-1794351103643-fiscal-scc-proof, relaunched after the BSOD, ~2.5 h on a node at load 7-10): signatures and consumers ok, document_test 26 passed, type_probe PASS (43 typed), executed_cells xsd_emit discovered 43 executed 43 failed 0, respell drift 0, 'LANDED and closed green'. Log at session scratchpad xsdproof\\scc-proof.log. Box land running now: control round trip of the unchanged live xsd_emit first, then lib_land_window -ProvenLog -Apply (base FEE124468E4F, draft 05ED9255E232). Next step: on landed, slice D2 (emit types per SCC: plain union/nominal for non-recursive singletons, union rec groups otherwise; then split functions_group the same way), and start the MDFE-D2 lib proof stacked on this land.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_9 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 9u32, "2026-11-11T00:09Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:5x SCC LANDED on the box: control round trip of the unchanged live xsd_emit canonical, then lib_land_window -ProvenLog (ganymede job-1794351103643-fiscal-scc-proof, executed_cells 43/43) -Apply under tree-write window win-1794355725378-10760; live xsd_emit.spi = 05ED9255E232 (unstaged), undo under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo. The lib now has scc_order / recursive_group over emitted.references (dependency-first SCCs by reach size), not yet wired into emission. Next step: slice D2 (emit types per SCC, then split functions_group), queued behind FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES-1-MDFE-D2 on the one-draft xsd_emit line.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_10 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS", 10u32, "2026-11-11T01:57Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:5x the xsd_emit draft line is free again (MDFE-D2 landed, live 3AF2661A013F). Next step: slice D2 (emit types per SCC: plain union/nominal for non-recursive singletons, union rec groups otherwise; then split functions_group the same way), drafted over live 3AF2661A013F.")
inl kit_job_fan () = Task ("KIT-JOB-FAN", "kit job fan: one verb runs the same command as shard k of n over box, node, europa and codespaces, then merges on the box", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kit,jobs,compute,codespaces,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_job_fan () = Planning ("KIT-JOB-FAN", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_job_fan_1 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FAN", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:46Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "Filed 11-08 by rig (user 11-08 via i574n: parallel workflows sprawl into bad standardization; maybe use codespaces more; fiscal multi-job YAML sharding was reverted as drift). One verb fans the SAME command over hosts instead of per-repo CI YAML: kit job fan <label> --hosts box,node,europa,cs:N -- <command with {shard} and {shards}> then --merge <command>. DoD: (1) each host runs shard k of n through kit job start --on (codespaces through KIT-CODESPACE-RUN as a host kind), with {shard} and {shards} substituted into the argv; (2) the merge runs on the box only when every shard exits 0, otherwise it names the failed shards and their tails; (3) one cost row per shard in the FISCAL-GATE-BUDGET gate-cost shape (Run, Host, Shard, Seconds, Exit and the rest) so AGILE-DEGRADE-D4 reads it; (4) proven on fiscal build.ps1 -Shards N -ShardSet k plus -MergeShards (FISCAL-GATE-SHARD), merged report identical to a single-host run. Codespace shards pay off only for jobs well over about 20 min (boot plus tools step). Order: after TRAIN41, RIG-UNITS-CHECK, KIT-CODESPACE-RUN and REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER. NEXT: shard argv substitution and the all-exit-0 merge on box plus node first. TYPEFEATURES: a fan_host union (Box, Node, Europa, Codespace of machine) and a shard_result GADT (ShardOk, ShardFailed of tail) so a merge cannot start from a failed shard.")
inl note_of_kit_job_fan_2 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FAN", 2u32, "2026-11-09T18:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 21:5x local rig, PLAN AND MERGE CORE BUILT (unstaged, unprobed; in the probe batch): src/rig_job_fan/fan.spi (pure, HostFree). fan_host union Box | Node alias | Codespace cores; shard_plan hosts expected_minutes argv numbers the shards 1..n over the hosts that are worth it and substitutes {shard} and {shards} into every argv word (DoD 1); worth_hosting drops a codespace for jobs under 20 min (the item's boot-plus-tools payoff threshold; today's measured setup was ~2.5 min with the tools bundle, so the threshold could drop later); merge_decision_of returns Merge only when every shard exited 0, else FailedShards naming them (DoD 2). Test: a 10-minute job skips the codespace and numbers 2 shards; shard 2 of 2 gets the substituted argv; a 45-minute job keeps the codespace; merge vs failed shards. NEXT: the IO verb over kit job start --on per host (codespaces via the proven drive/remote drivers as a host kind), the cost rows per shard (DoD 3), then the fiscal proof (DoD 4).")
inl note_of_kit_job_fan_3 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FAN", 3u32, "2026-11-09T21:23Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig, proof: the model passed its notebook gate (run_notebook --gate, rust target) on ganymede, outside the TRAIN42e train, which timed out on moon: rig shipped the box lib and today's 41 self-contained model packages to /root/rig-probe and gated each (nice, 2 pinned cores). The probe wait is answered; next step is the item's IO half per its last note.")
inl note_of_kit_job_fan_4 () = Note ("KIT-JOB-FAN", 4u32, "2026-11-09T22:08Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig: parked on train:TRAIN43. The model is probe-green; the recorded next step adds a CLI verb or wires into shared kit code (rig do / job host / job list / proxy fs / agile entry), and TRAIN43 snapshots the live tree about 19:30 local, so shared-code edits wait until it has snapshotted and go into a post-TRAIN43 train with their own regen and crate-gate proof.")
inl node_bench_metric () = Task ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", "Deterministic cross-node benchmark score: one Spiral workload run identically per node yields a comparable vector (cpu-1t, cpu-Nt, mem-bw, disk-io) stored as a node label", Story, P1, D3, 75u32, Paused, "", "infra,node,benchmark,delegation,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_bench_metric () = Planning ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_1 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 1u32, "2026-11-08T13:53Z", "management", "Filed from the user 11-08 node-naming thread. One Spiral source compiled per backend, fixed seed+iterations, score=work/walltime normalized to box=1.0. Vector: cpu-1t, cpu-Nt (captures 4c/8t vs 2c), mem-bandwidth (STREAM triad), disk-io (SSD-vs-HDD can invert the disk ranking). Payoffs: placement label for Select-OffloadTarget; perf-drift detection folded into Invoke-DriftWatch; a node x backend matrix via Spiral. ALSO run under load not just idle, to get effective-capacity + a contention-sensitivity ratio (loaded/idle) - bounded so it never tips a starved node (respect Test-BoxHeavyGuard). Owner management (node tooling) + compiler/rig for the Spiral codegen.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_2 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 2u32, "2026-11-08T14:13Z", "management", "User 11-08: idle AND saturated are CO-EQUAL metrics, not baseline+degradation - the fleet is on both states often, so report idle-score and saturated-score as first-class vector dimensions (each sub-metric measured in both states). The loaded/idle ratio is derived; both absolutes are primary. The saturated run stays bounded/low-priority (respect Test-BoxHeavyGuard) so it never tips a starved node.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_3 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 3u32, "2026-11-08T16:46Z", "management", "User 11-08: the benchmark must be a SINGLE self-contained EXECUTABLE with minimal filesystem interaction (compile -> run -> print the score -> exit; no stage dirs, no dangling files/procs/ssh), so it runs cleanly on restricted work nodes like Jupiter/work-host. Fits the Spiral->native-exe design. PERMISSION granted to execute the benchmark exe on the nodes during autonomous runs to collect the number. This is the ONE sanctioned use of Jupiter near-term (besides rare manual on-demand); heavy fan-out stays off until tooling is stable.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_4 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 4u32, "2026-11-08T16:59Z", "management", "User 11-08: PRIORITIZE finishing the benchmark. GPU/WGSL dimension: we have no GPU machines, but a WGSL (wgpu) compute probe as a sub-dimension DIFFERENTIATES the fleet and can affect the rating. Per node it yields: (1) ADAPTER CLASSIFICATION - discrete / integrated / software-fallback + backend (Vulkan/D3D12/GL/llvmpipe): box+wifi have a real (weak Kepler) Quadro K1100M, work-hub/244 integrated Intel, the VMs+cloud are software-only; (2) GPU-COMPUTE throughput for the real-GPU nodes; (3) a GPU-HEALTH signal (clean run vs TDR/crash - ties to node-wgsl-runner-flaky-crash + the box/node TDR risk). Fits single-exe/min-FS (a wgpu binary is one exe; it auto-falls-back to software where no GPU). The value is DIFFERENTIATION + a gpu-capable placement label (route tibia/shader work to box/wifi), NOT raw power (the GPUs are weak). Treat a wgsl crash as the software/unavailable classification, not a benchmark failure. Keep it an ADD-ON sub-score; the cpu/mem/disk/idle+saturated core stays primary.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_5 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 5u32, "2026-11-09T15:16Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 15:2x STATE RECONCILED (the item said 0% Planned; the work landed 11-08 per memory nodebench-metric-and-rating): kit/infra/nodebench/ holds ONE src/main.rs (306 lines, Rust, zero-dep lean profile + --features gpu wgpu profile), metrics CORES ST_MS MT_MS MEM_GBPS DWR_MBPS DRD_MBPS DSYNC_MS DIOPS GPU_CLASS[...], best-of-N ns timing, RAII-cleaned disk temp; rate.ps1 computes the log-relative 0-1000 rating centrally (blend parallel .28 / single .24 / mem .18 / disk .22 / gpu .08, renormalized over measured dims); the 11-08 fleet sweep ran (jupiter top, earth last). Single self-contained exe, minimal FS, per the user's 11-08 constraints. GAP vs the user's framing \"one Spiral source compiled per backend\": the source is Rust, not Spiral, and the idle-vs-saturated co-equal pair (user 11-08 10:13) is not yet a recorded column. DoD: (a) idle AND saturated scores recorded per node as first-class columns in scores.tsv; (b) the workload kernels (ST/MT/MEM) come from one Spiral source compiled to the native exe, the disk/GPU probes staying Rust FFI. Next step: (a) first, the cheaper one: add a --load flag pair to rate.ps1's sweep (idle run, then a bounded saturated run under Test-BoxHeavyGuard) and record both.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_6 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 6u32, "2026-11-09T16:04Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09 DESIGN for DoD (a) idle+saturated co-equal dims (user 11-08 10:13), sequenced against the single-exe/min-FS constraint (user 11-08 12:46):\n1. SATURATION METHOD (self-contained, keeps the single-exe rule): nodebench gains a --saturated mode that SPAWNS its own contention co-load (CORES-1 busy threads pinned, plus a memory-churn thread for MEM) BEFORE timing ST/MT/MEM, so effective-under-contention capacity is measured with NO external stressor, no extra files/procs/ssh. Idle mode = today. Guard: refuse --saturated (exit, classify UNAVAILABLE) if the node is already above a load threshold at start, so it never tips a starved node (Test-BoxHeavyGuard parity). This is a Rust change to kit/infra/nodebench/src/main.rs = rig/compiler lane.\n2. SCORES SCHEMA (backward-compatible): scores.tsv gains an optional 4th TAB column state=idle|sat; existing rows (no 4th col) default idle, so the current sweep still rates unchanged. A node contributes one idle row + one sat row.\n3. rate.ps1 GROUPING (org, ready once sat data exists): normalize each dimension WITHIN its own state-fleet (idle rows among idle, sat among sat) so idle-rating and sat-rating are each fleet-relative and CO-EQUAL (not baseline+degradation); derive ratio = sat/idle per node as the contention-sensitivity signal; output idle | sat | ratio columns. The log-relative normalizer + weights are unchanged, just applied per state-partition.\nSEQUENCE: (1) rig adds --saturated to nodebench [NEXT, blocks the data]; (2) org runs the idle+sat sweep in a quiet window, box last, each node self-saturating + guarded; (3) org lands the rate.ps1 per-state grouping (now testable with real sat rows). DoD (b) one-Spiral-source kernels stays a separate rig/compiler codegen track. Parking on lane:rig for step 1; clear when nodebench has --saturated.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_7 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 7u32, "2026-11-09T16:13Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 14:5x local rig, STEP (1) DONE: nodebench --saturated (kit/infra/nodebench/src/main.rs, unstaged). A CoLoad RAII guard starts cores-1 spinning cpu_work threads plus one memory-churn thread (32 MB copies, safe on the 1 GB node) BEFORE ST/MT/MEM are timed, and is dropped (stop flag + join of every thread) before the disk and GPU probes, so those stay unloaded per the design; Drop runs on every return path. The output line gains STATE=idle|sat; rate.ps1 collects only numeric KEY=VALUE pairs, so today's rating ignores it and org's per-state grouping can read it. Proof: compiled on ganymede with rustc --edition 2021 (debug, nice 19), exit 0, 4.9 MB binary; NOT run there (a saturated run would load every core under tibia's jobs); the scratch build dir was removed. Release builds keep the documented flags (rustc -C opt-level=3 -C lto=fat -C codegen-units=1). Org's step (2), the idle+sat sweep in a quiet window, is unblocked.")
inl note_of_node_bench_metric_8 () = Note ("NODE-BENCH-METRIC", 8u32, "2026-11-09T16:48Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 16:5x rig: the lane:rig wait reappeared with no new note after rig's answer in note 7 (16:13Z): --saturated built and compiled; the next step is org's idle+sat sweep. Clearing it again; if org needs something new from rig, a note naming it will route it.")
