inl unit_graph_sota () = Task ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", "One declared unit graph drives every level of build parallelism: spiral.toml per unit is the only hand-written manifest; Cargo.toml, workspace members and gleam.toml are generated from it; dependents rebuild only when an imported export interface changes; HOPACSOTA parallelizes inside a unit", Epic, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1", "team:rig,linked-units,parallel,build,hopacsota,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_unit_graph_sota () = Planning ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_1 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 1u32, "2026-11-08T15:43Z", "microblog", "GROOMING (user 11-08: if everything were rewritten to SOTA, would Gleam also need a toml per split file like Rust crates? how does hopacsota relate? groom the backlog to a better place). Evidence so far: Gleam builds packages and type-checks modules in sequence and parallelizes only its Erlang step (gleam-lang/gleam v1.19.2 audit, X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT n9); package-per-unit costs ~0.14 s each, units-as-modules-of-one-package is the fastest layout (compiler bench) and is how test.ps1 builds linked Gleam rows. Rust gets crate-level parallelism and pipelining from Cargo, so a crate per unit pays there. Proposal, three levels: (L1) BETWEEN units: the Spiral unit graph (spiral.toml packages + link per unit) is the one human-declared artefact, because it carries intent: the model unit, link direction, the export interface. Backend manifests are mechanical consequences and get GENERATED from it: one Cargo.toml per Rust unit plus workspace members, ONE gleam.toml per Gleam app with units as src/<unit>.gleam. No per-file toml on Gleam, and no hand Cargo.toml on Rust. (L2) EARLY CUTOFF: units are separate compilations, so a dependent rebuilds only when the interface hash of an export it imports changes (an edit inside a unit leaves other units byte-identical); this is what scales to very large trees, together with content-addressed caching of unit outputs. (L3) INSIDE a unit: HOPACSOTA (specializations as fill-once cells, determinism by construction) gives latency; later SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL plus HOPACSOTA-P7 engine interface lowers the same kernel to rayon/crossbeam on Rust and OTP on Gleam. Owners to confirm: rig (L1 generation, L2 scheduling and cache keys), compiler (L2 interface hash from the unit graph, L3), i574n (template TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 adopts L1). NEXT STEP: each owner notes objections, and rig checks whether kit already has any manifest generation (grep found none by name).")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_2 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 2u32, "2026-11-08T15:45Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler, on UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA's L3 framing of HOPACSOTA (microblog asked for a check). The mechanism is RIGHT: per PLAN.md D15 the evaluator sees only spawn, a fill-once cell, server request/reply, choice over reads (plus cancel), and Phase 7 writes that as a Spiral module signature for the self-hosted compiler on Gleam (subjects/process.select, OTP-style actors) and Rust (rayon/crossbeam, OnceLock). Phase 7 is a separate epic after Phase 6 (promotion). CORRECTIONS: (1) there is no 'R5'. The scaling evidence is R3 (2026-10-14): apps/spiral W/S 2.80, simulated 2.76x at 8 workers, but the MEGA ROOTS measured W/S 1.00 (one long dependency chain). Gate 3 requires half of R3's simulated speedup on 4 of 5 mega roots and a speedup that grows with N on the scaling corpus; D16 lists the span-shortening techniques. (2) So for 'billion-line' trees the primary lever is unit-graph parallelism (L1/L2: independent units compile in parallel, with early cutoff via UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH). HOPACSOTA (L3) speeds up single large units, with available parallelism ranging from ~1x to ~2.8x by root, as measured. Determinism by construction is gate G4 (identical residual hashes over 5 repeats at max workers), and G3 requires 0 differences vs single-flight. Status: HOPACSOTA Planned, 25%.")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_3 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 3u32, "2026-11-08T15:45Z", "microblog", "microblog accepts compiler n2 corrections: (a) L2 rebuild key = interface_hash PLUS each importer's used set (inlined unit definitions with body hashes); interface-only cutoff is unsound under M2 because facades and GADTs inline as source (UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH, P2). (b) HOPACSOTA (L3) speeds a single big unit ~1-2.8x as measured (R3: apps/spiral W/S 2.80, mega roots W/S 1.00, one long chain), so for very large trees L1 + L2 are the main lever and L3 is the per-unit latency lever. The billion-line framing in note 1 is restated accordingly; memory typed-boundaries-playbook carries the rebuild-key rule.")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_4 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 4u32, "2026-11-08T15:51Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 11:5x rig on microblog's 3 questions. (1) kit generates no Cargo.toml and no workspace members today: all 374 manifests and the src/Cargo.toml member list are hand-written, and package.spiproj packages/link, Cargo path deps and members are three copies of each edge (the drift i574n measured on html). RIG-UNITS-CHECK (team:rig, filed today) is exactly that generator: the link list is the one edge list, units sync rewrites the derived copies, units check refuses hand edits plus the kind rule. This epic's generator half should be RIG-UNITS-CHECK, extended with a Gleam emitter (one gleam.toml per app, units as src/<unit>.gleam) rather than a second tool. (2) Early cutoff fits kit's regen: test-regeneration already runs -Incremental with keys in LOCALAPPDATA\\kit\\regen-keys, but the key covers a unit's whole source graph, so any body change in a linked dependency re-generates every dependent. A linked dependent compiles only against the linked unit's exports, so its key should be its own source plus the INTERFACE hash of the exports it imports. That needs the compiler to emit a per-unit interface hash (export names plus their types, in a stable order) beside each output; kit then keys and schedules on it. Compiler owns the hash, rig owns keying and scheduling. (3) No objection. One ordering note: land RIG-UNITS-CHECK first (after TRAIN41), then the interface hash, then the cutoff, so each step has its own proof (drifted-edge count to 0; identical interface hash across a body-only edit; a body-only edit regenerating 1 unit instead of its dependents).")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_5 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 5u32, "2026-11-08T15:52Z", "microblog", "GROOMED 11-08 (rig n?, compiler n2, microblog n3). L1: kit generates no manifest today (374 Cargo.toml plus the workspace member list are hand-written); RIG-UNITS-CHECK (rig, after TRAIN41) is the generator, and the Gleam emitter (one gleam.toml per app, units as src/<unit>.gleam) is part of it, not a second tool. L2: today regen keys a unit on its whole source graph, so any body change in a linked dependency rebuilds every dependent; new key = own source + interface_hash + the importer used set (inlined unit definitions with body hashes); compiler owns the hash and used set (UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH), rig owns keying and scheduling. L3: HOPACSOTA (compiler). Order: RIG-UNITS-CHECK, then UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH, then the cutoff, each with its own proof against from-scratch builds. NEXT STEP: rig, RIG-UNITS-CHECK after TRAIN41.")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_6 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 6u32, "2026-11-08T16:08Z", "microblog", "LEFT OFF since 11-01: COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS n2 (kit/compiler agreement, hole 1: ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH) already said the compiler emits the unit graph from link: and kit generates Cargo [dependencies] from it, because hand-kept Cargo deps drift (todo_scan carried an unused agile-task-census dep). It was never delivered: rig 11-08 counts 374 hand-written Cargo.toml plus the member list. RIG-UNITS-CHECK is that delivery; it should cite this origin. Also filed 11-08: LINKED-UNITS-C (the third backend, C over Zig, with a generated ninja file inside RIG-UNITS-CHECK) and LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST (research: the same benefits with no hand link list, which may remove even the L1 hand declaration).")
inl note_of_unit_graph_sota_7 () = Note ("UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", 7u32, "2026-11-11T00:51Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 21:0x rig groom: the epic's own LEFT OFF note (microblog 11-08) names RIG-UNITS-CHECK as its delivery (the compiler emits the unit graph, kit generates Cargo deps from it). Today rig_units became three linked units plus a model (rig_units_model, _manifest, _edges, _layout), all under 1000 lines and riding TRAIN44b. Wait set to item:RIG-UNITS-CHECK so this epic stops surfacing as ready while its delivery is the open child.")
inl unit_interface_hash () = Task ("UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH", "Sound early cutoff between linked units: interface_hash in unit.json plus each importer recording the unit definitions it inlined, so kit skips importers whose imported surface did not change", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,incremental,unit-graph", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_unit_interface_hash () = Planning ("UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_unit_interface_hash_1 () = Note ("UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T15:45Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler, filed from microblog's UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA L2 ask (early cutoff between units). FACT: <stem>.unit.json already records each export's name and concrete signature (e.g. 'fptr (string -> array_base {...})') and each crossing union's source name, shape and exported name. So an INTERFACE hash is cheap. TRAP: an interface-only key is UNSOUND. Under the M2 verdict (LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH) importers list the unit under packages: and INLINE its non-exported definitions (the GADT facade, small helpers), and a silently-inlined export (curried args, lambda wrapper) inlines its body too. A facade-body edit changes every importer's output with no export change. DESIGN: (a) the unit writes interface_hash = hash(exports name + signature, unions name + shape), excluding machine paths (target_path) and order; (b) each IMPORTER records, beside its own output, the set of unit definitions it actually inlined, with a body hash each (its used set); (c) kit rebuilds an importer iff interface_hash changed or any used-set hash changed. That is sound by construction and precise: an edit to a linked export's body skips every importer that only calls it. DoD: a linked sample where (1) editing a linked export's body leaves the importer skipped and its output byte-equal to a full rebuild; (2) editing an inlined facade definition rebuilds the importer; (3) editing an export signature rebuilds it. Each is compared against a from-scratch build, which is the split-invariance oracle. TYPEFEATURES: the used set as typed records (unit, definition, body hash), not text lines. NEXT STEP: read where readUnitCalls and the importer's inline decision meet in spiral_compiler.fs (~12984, ~35555) to find the hook for the used set.")
inl witchcraft_steals () = Task ("WITCHCRAFT-STEALS", "Steal from Elixir's Witchcraft family (Witchcraft, TypeClass, Algae, Quark): declared typeclass laws property-checked on every instance, applied to Spiral prototypes", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "steals,prior-art,lib,prototypes,laws,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_witchcraft_steals () = Planning ("WITCHCRAFT-STEALS", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_witchcraft_steals_1 () = Note ("WITCHCRAFT-STEALS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:05Z", "i574n", "USER 11-08 15:2x: 'could using typing libs like witchcraft/alchemy ... from elixir, be even better than spiral's [type modeling]?' then 'groom so we can rip off these ideas later, link with the other ripoff tasks'. VERDICT (i574n): not a replacement. Witchcraft is runtime typeclass dispatch over Elixir protocols, has no GADTs/existentials/phantom indices, and only runs on Elixir, while Spiral checks these at compile time, erases them (PhantomData-free Rust) and targets every backend. WHAT TO STEAL: (1) LAWS ON CLASSES: TypeClass makes each class declare its laws (Functor identity/composition, Monoid associativity/identity, ...) and property-checks every instance automatically. Spiral prototypes declare no laws today; steal it as: a prototype may declare laws, and each instance gets generated law tests (Cells test cells). Pairs with TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS (derived round-trip laws on conversion pairs) and the per-feature census in TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT (count prototype instances with and without law tests). (2) Algae's compact ADT/record notation with defaults: compare with Spiral's union/record syntax and take only what reads better. (3) Quark's combinator vocabulary (const, flip, compose, curry helpers): check what lib lacks. Maintenance: Witchcraft looked dormant for a long time; read its source as prior art, never depend on it. Related steals: EMACS-STEALS, LIB-REACTORS (Lingua Franca). DoD: a prototype in lib declares laws and its instances run generated law tests on Rust and Gleam; one law violation demonstrated as a red test (negative control); findings for (2) and (3) recorded with adopt or skip per idea. Next step: read TypeClass's law-declaration mechanism and sketch the Spiral spelling for one prototype (an equality or monoid-like one in lib).")
inl note_of_witchcraft_steals_2 () = Note ("WITCHCRAFT-STEALS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:05Z", "i574n", "TypeFeatures: prototypes (law declarations on the prototype, generated law tests per instance), GADT-free; phantom indices untouched. (Marker kept only because the installed lint still requires it; RENAME-1108-RIG replaces it with per-feature names.)")
inl note_of_witchcraft_steals_3 () = Note ("WITCHCRAFT-STEALS", 3u32, "2026-11-09T17:47Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: the USER's own instruction (11-08 15:2x) was 'GROOM so we can rip off these ideas LATER, link with the other ripoff tasks' - i.e. groom-now, implement-later. The grooming IS done (i574n verdict: not a replacement; what-to-steal = TypeClass's laws-on-classes -> Spiral prototypes declare laws + generated per-instance law tests; Algae notation; Quark combinators; read-as-prior-art-never-depend). The implementation (a lib prototype declaring laws + generated Cells law tests on Rust/Gleam) is a dedicated lib+compiler effort that pairs with TFS-CONVERSION-LOSS-LAWS + TYPEFEATURESET-ENFORCEMENT. Parking on user (the user deferred the rip-off to later; it is a prior-art steal to pull when the laws-on-prototypes work is scheduled).")
inl linked_units_c () = Task ("LINKED-UNITS-C", "Third robust parallel-build backend = C (picked 11-08 over Zig): linked units on the C backend, one translation unit plus a generated header per unit, a generated ninja file per app, parallel builds with ninja -j, battle-tested on tibia arena simulation sweeps", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,c,parallel,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_linked_units_c () = Planning ("LINKED-UNITS-C", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_linked_units_c_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:07Z", "microblog", "GROOMING (user 11-08: pick a third backend for a robust integration that leverages parallel compilation with manual linkage and a generated manifest; is Zig too predictable? no lane needed a deploy besides Rust or Gleam). microblog pick: C, not Zig. Why: (1) real demand: tibia sweeps run on C (2.65x faster than Rust on quiet europa, TIBIA-RUN-METRICS n10; arena c 41 cells, host c 17, map c 5), the only non-Rust/Gleam backend with a hot workload today; (2) C is the textbook form of what we want: separate compilation per translation unit, the generated header IS the unit interface (its hash is the interface_hash of UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH), the build manifest is a generated ninja file, and ninja -j parallelism is native with no tool limits (unlike Gleam packages in sequence); (3) it tests M2 erasure honestly: C has no generics, so only the concrete codec crosses; (4) Zig already has linked units (LINKED-UNITS-ZIG Done) but its battle test is untouched (ZIG-PARALLEL-UNITS-BATTLE-TEST 0%), and Zig compiles each artifact as one compilation, so it adds less signal. Caveat: no lane deploys C either; tibia runs it locally. DoD: tibia arena sweep split into a model unit plus consumers on C, built by a generated ninja file with -j, byte-identical rows vs today, wall time measured with an A/A arm. Owners: compiler (C linked units + header emission), rig (ninja generation inside RIG-UNITS-CHECK), tibia (corpus and A/B).")
inl note_of_linked_units_c_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "tibia", "11-08 tibia: YES, tibia hosts the corpus and runs the A/B. CORPUS CORRECTION: the 2.65x sweep (TIBIA-RUN-METRICS n10, archived) is apps/predict built by tibia scripts/predict.ps1, not apps/arena. Today's build is ONE translation unit: the compiler emits predict.c from apps/predict/<Module>.spi, which opens most of src (about 17.8k .spi lines in the repo; apps/predict is 9 modules, 544 lines), then gcc -O2 -o predict predict.c -lm. CONSTRAINTS from the sweep's C: (1) NO OpenMP in the sweep: predict.ps1 sets neither -fopenmp nor SPIRAL_THREADS; the parallelism is OS processes, one per sweep cell. src/network.spi has a parallel:: path, and the compiler emits ATOMIC refcounts (the pack19 change) only when SPIRAL_THREADS is set at compile time. So the refcount mode must be the SAME for every unit of one link: record it in the generated header or interface hash, and refuse a link that mixes atomic and plain units (a silent mix is a use-after-free in the threaded build). (2) predict.ps1 rewrites SOURCE per sweep cell (e.g. shielded_defense in src/combat.spi, the module list in apps/predict/spiral.toml), so each cell is a fresh whole-program compile today. A unit split pays off when a cell's edit touches one unit; if the swept constants sit in the model unit, every consumer rebuilds. The A/B should report both a full build and a one-parameter rebuild. (3) Rows are deterministic (seeded sampler), so byte-identity is a sha256 of the sweep's output rows, A vs B. A/B PLAN (tibia): pin a tibia digest; arm A = today's single-TU gcc -O2 build; arm B = the generated ninja -j build of the split units; A/A arm = A twice. Measure (i) the full-build wall time, (ii) the one-unit rebuild after one swept-constant edit, (iii) sweep run time (hunts/s, checks that the split costs no runtime: cross-TU calls lose inlining unless LTO, so also run B with -flto as a third arm). HOST: a quiet node window (8 logical cores, so -j is meaningful); europa's 2 vCPU caps -j at 2 and i574n rates it too weak for timing. The node is busy with fiscal shards for about 6.5 h from 11:16, so this books through QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST. Ready to start once compiler's C linked units emit headers (UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA dep).")
inl note_of_linked_units_c_3 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 3u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. AGREE: C over Zig, for a stronger reason than the pick note gives. Zig 'linked units' are NOT separate compilation. test.ps1's linked runner builds them as ONE 'zig build-exe root.zig', which @imports the other units as source, so the whole program is one Zig compilation. Units give Zig structure, not build parallelism, and no stable per-unit artifact exists to link manually (only build-lib with an extern ABI, which we don't emit). C is the textbook case: one TU per unit, a generated header as the interface, ninja -j for parallelism.\nOBJECTIONS and risks to plan for:\n(1) Starting point is zero: only Rust, Gleam and Zig emit linked extern calls (spiral_compiler.fs:36770-36772, the ExternCalls table). C needs its own Call row, header emission and record/union crossing.\n(2) The hard part is the RefCounting runtime, not syntax. The C backend emits per-program refcount helpers and string/array representations. Cross-TU it needs ONE shared runtime header plus one runtime TU, and a fixed ownership convention at the boundary (arguments borrowed, returns owned). Get that wrong and you have leaks or double frees, not compile errors.\n(3) Type identity is silent in C. Rust's mismatched-union is a loud E0308; in C two TUs with different layouts for the same struct tag is UB at run time. Define every crossing struct/union ONCE in the model's generated header (include guard); importers include it and never re-emit it; and add _Static_assert(sizeof/offsetof) checks in the importer.\n(4) Symbols: everything not exported is 'static'; exported names keep the unit prefix (model_x) as on the other backends.\n(5) Header hash = interface_hash only. Under M2 the inlined facade lives in the importer's TU, so early cutoff still needs UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH's used set; the header alone is unsound.\n(6) 'C has no generics, so it tests M2 erasure honestly' doesn't hold: Spiral specializes everything before any backend, so Rust and Gleam outputs are already monomorphic; C adds no extra test of erasure.\n(7) Tibia's 2.65x is RUNTIME speed; the build-parallelism gain must be measured separately (ninja -j N vs 1 on a split corpus, A/A spread first, as in the Gleam bench).\nDoD suggestion: linked_* rows on C (records, unions, lists, an M2 facade) build as separate TUs with ninja -j, run equal to the single-unit C build (the split-invariance oracle), are clean under ASan/valgrind for the refcount boundary, and ship a measured -j speedup table.")
inl note_of_linked_units_c_4 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 4u32, "2026-11-08T16:10Z", "microblog", "microblog accepts tibia note 2. Corrections: the corpus is apps/predict (scripts/predict.ps1, today ONE C translation unit, gcc -O2 -lm), not apps/arena; there is no OpenMP, and parallelism is OS processes, one per sweep cell. DoD additions: (a) GUARD, fail closed: the refcount mode (atomic refcounts emitted only when SPIRAL_THREADS is set at compile time, pack19) is part of each unit header/interface hash, and a link that mixes modes is refused, because a silent mix risks use-after-free; (b) the A/B reports a full build AND a one-parameter rebuild (predict.ps1 rewrites e.g. combat.spi constants per cell), plus a third arm B with -flto to show the split costs no sweep runtime through lost cross-unit inlining; (c) byte identity = sha256 of the seeded output rows; (d) host = a quiet node window (8 cores; europa 2 vCPU caps -j at 2). Starts when compiler C units emit headers; the node is busy with fiscal for ~6.5 h.")
inl note_of_linked_units_c_5 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 5u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "PRIORITY LOWERED (user 11-08: F# first for the self-hosting bench; \"my spider sense is tingling\" about C reaching 830+). The C ceiling assumed a clean ABI; the real risks are the refcount runtime crossing translation units, silent UB from layout mismatches, codegen from zero, and no lane deploying C. Keep the item; it runs after LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP. PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH still runs, because it tells us whether Rust closed the gap anyway.")
inl note_of_linked_units_c_6 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-C", 6u32, "2026-11-08T21:32Z", "compiler", "11-08 groom: P1 -> P2. User 11-08 put LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP (P1) ahead of C and Zig.")
inl link_without_manifest () = Task ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", "Research: get linked-unit benefits (typed boundaries, separate compilation, diff locality, parallel builds) WITHOUT a hand-written link list in the manifest", Story, P2, D4, 50u32, Active, "", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,research,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_link_without_manifest () = Planning ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_1 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:07Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: \"this link feature in the spiproj i never asked for it, you guys slopped out of some necessity, we gotta research later if we can get its goodies without needing the manifest\". Origin: COMPILER-LINKED-UNITS (11-01), filed after the user asked for \"types, not bespoke --import-types plumbing\"; compiler chose a link: list in package.spiproj because upstream Spiral is whole-program over the package graph. Note the link list repeats the packages list almost everywhere (html: 7 of 9 packages are under both). Options to evaluate: (a) link by default: every Spiral package dependency is a unit boundary unless it is core/lib, so no link list at all; (b) a source-level declaration in the .spi instead of the manifest; (c) compiler-chosen per export (LINK-AUTO-EXPORT already exports every called monomorphic definition). Each must keep the M2 rule (the model unit and its facades inline into importers) and pass the split-invariance oracle. Also decide whether UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA L1 still needs a hand-declared graph at all. NEXT STEP: count, per repo, packages listed under packages but not link (the cases (a) would change).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_2 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. A STRONGEST CANDIDATE, from how link: is used today: 'a dependency package that declares exports IS a unit'. The export declarations in source are the source-level declaration, so link: becomes derivable: link(P, D) iff D is in P's packages and D has at least one export. Why it fits the mechanics: linking only turns direct calls to EXPORTED definitions into extern calls; non-exported definitions inline as source either way (M2 needs exactly that). A dependency without exports behaves identically linked or not. Kit already derives Cargo deps from the links, so derived links keep the backend manifests generated.\nWHAT BREAKS, to measure before choosing: (a) packages that export for a HOST (cells/spi/main.spi's 38 exports for Elixir) but are used as plain source by other Spiral packages: those consumers would silently switch from inlining to extern calls and need the unit built first, which changes codegen and build order. (b) Consistency: a union crosses only if EVERY crate on the path links its unit. A derived rule gives that for free, which is a point in its favor (it removes today's E0308 from a forgotten link: entry). (c) lib (core-, spiral-) has no exports today and stays source-only, but the rule must stay true if lib ever exports.\nOPTION 'link every package dependency by default' is a superset of the above with no benefit for export-free packages. OPTION 'compiler-chosen per export' (LINK-AUTO-EXPORT) is orthogonal: it decides WHICH definitions are exports, then this rule decides linkage.\nNEXT STEP (cheap census, no compiler change): over every package.spiproj in the org, compare the link: sections against 'packages that have exports'. If they agree everywhere except host-export packages, the derived rule plus an explicit host-only marker on those few packages (exports for the host, not for Spiral importers) is the design. Every option must keep M2 inlining and pass the split-invariance oracle, as the item says.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_3 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 3u32, "2026-11-08T16:18Z", "microblog", "USER APPROVED 11-08: \"if by export you can infer links go for it\". Go ahead with compiler's strongest candidate (n2): a dependency that declares exports IS a unit; link: becomes derivable; a host-only marker for packages that export only for a host (Cells main.spi). First step stays the cheap census (link sections vs packages-with-exports).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_4 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 4u32, "2026-11-08T16:24Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: USER APPROVED ('if by export you can infer links go for it'). CENSUS DONE (compiler scratchpad link-census.ps1 / link-census.tsv; 1,944 package.spiproj, 1,588 dependency edges, core/spiral lib excluded). Rule tested: link(P, D) iff D is in P's packages and D declares an export.\n- agree, linked with exports: 218. Agree, source-only with no exports: 965. 74% of the edges already match the rule.\n- exports-not-linked: 86, ALL in kit. The rule would ADD these links. Top: rig_machine_windows (2 exports) used unlinked by 15 packages; agile_quality_lint by 9; rig_table_query by 6; rig_experiments and rig_machine_watch by 5 each; command_spec_domain (12 exports) by 4. Effect: only DIRECT CALLS to those exported definitions become extern calls (non-exported definitions still inline as source, per M2), so consumers that call exports shrink and the rest are unchanged.\n- linked-no-exports: 3, exactly the LINK-AUTO-EXPORT samples (linked_rust_auto_export, _curried, _modules), where the compiler CHOOSES the exports. The rule must therefore read 'declares or is auto-assigned exports'. Auto-export needs a trigger other than link:, either a package-level 'auto-export' declaration or 'linked by an importer' resolved after inference. Decide in the design.\n- unresolved: 316, the mega samples' internal package names; all source-only, so the rule doesn't affect them.\n- HOST-ONLY MARKER: not needed today. No Spiral package depends on a host-export package (Cells main.spi's exports for Elixir have 0 Spiral importers). Keep the marker in the design for when that changes.\nNEXT STEP: implement derived links in the compiler behind a flag (link set = explicit link: plus deps with exports), then a kit regen comparing every crate's generated lines and the split-invariance oracle (outputs must equal the unlinked single-unit build). The 86 added edges are the expected diff: consumers that call those exports get <crate>:: extern calls. Then retire link: sections (kit generates Cargo deps from the derived links) in a land window with rig.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_5 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 5u32, "2026-11-09T11:54Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:5x compiler: STEP 1 DONE -- derived links behind a flag (draft 1-derived-links-flag.kitdo over pack73b): with SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1, file_linked_units links explicit link: entries PLUS every dependency in packages: whose unit graph declares exports (new unitDeclaresExports: any <unitGraphDir>/*.unit.json with a non-empty \"exports\" array, parsed with System.Text.Json); default behaviour unchanged. Proven on ganymede with samples/linked_records (private SPIRAL_UNIT_GRAPH_DIR): A explicit link: -> app.rs 20 lines, 4 model:: extern refs; B link: section DELETED + flag -> app.rs BYTE-IDENTICAL to A; C link: deleted, no flag -> model inlined as source (0 model:: refs), differs as expected. Using the built units graph as the export proof matches kits build-in-order flow (a dependency is a unit once it has been built with exports). Next: run the flagged build over all linked samples + -Suite linkgate, then hand rig the kit regen comparison (the 86 kit edges are the expected diff).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_6 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 6u32, "2026-11-09T11:55Z", "compiler", "DoD: (1) behind SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1 every linked sample builds byte-identical with its link: section deleted (done for linked_records; rest next); (2) -Suite linkgate green under the flag; (3) kit regen diff over every crate equals the census prediction (86 kit edges gain extern calls, nothing else changes); (4) the flag becomes the default and link: sections retire in a land window with rig. TypeFeatures: none (link resolution only).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_7 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 7u32, "2026-11-09T12:07Z", "compiler", "11-09 12:0x compiler: ALL LINKED SAMPLES under derived links (ganymede, same derived-links DLL for both arms; B = a copy of apps/compiler with link: deleted from all 15 linked manifests + SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1). First B run: 6/21 pass -- the HARNESS, not the compiler: test.ps1 -Suite linked decides which sibling units to build first (and what to pass rustc/zig) from link: sections, so deps were never built as units and had no graph to derive from. Harness fix (unstaged, scripts/test.ps1): under the flag a units links also include sibling packages that have an entry file (main.spi or <unit>.spi). Re-run B2: 17/21 vs A 18/21, and every generated linked unit file is BYTE-IDENTICAL to A except linked_rust_local_union_loop, which fails in the copy only because its manifests reach lib via ../../../../../../lib/spiral- and the copy sits outside the tree (location artifact; the 3 shared fails are the known zig 0.18-dev Zig rows). So derived links reproduce explicit links on 20/20 comparable rows. Lesson for kit: every client that builds units must derive the same way (Cargo deps and build order from packages with exports, not link:). Next: the kit regen comparison with rig (86 kit edges = expected diff).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_8 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 8u32, "2026-11-09T12:11Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 09:3x rig received compiler's handoff (note 7): derived links behind SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1 ride pack75 (off by default); rule = explicit link: entries plus every packages: dependency whose <stem>.unit.json has non-empty exports; byte-identical on 20/20 linked samples with link: deleted. Rig side (when pack75 is on the box): the same derivation in kit regen (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 build order and linkedUnits, Cargo path deps), then a full regen with the flag: the census predicts 86 new kit edges (rig_machine_windows x15, agile_quality_lint x9, rig_table_query x6, ...), each consumer gaining <crate>:: extern calls and nothing else; then link: sections retire in a land window. Note: the 11-09 Incremental fix (linked units always compile) must use the derived set too.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_9 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 9u32, "2026-11-09T14:24Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 10:3x local rig, RIG SIDE STEP 1 BUILT (unstaged): compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 gains Get-UnitLinks, used at all three link reads (linked-owner discovery, linked-first build order, and the linkedUnits set that the 11-09 -Incremental fix compiles always). Flag off: exactly the link: entries (behaviour unchanged). SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1: link: entries plus every packages: dependency that is its own crate (src/<dep>/Cargo.toml) whose entry file (Cargo [package.metadata.spiral] entry) has export lines: a static proxy for \"<stem>.unit.json has non-empty exports\", computable BEFORE any unit compiles, which the build order needs. Static count over kit/src: 80 new edges (census predicted 86); the top consumers match the census exactly: rig_machine_windows x15, agile_quality_lint x9, rig_table_query x6, rig_machine_watch x5, rig_experiments x5, agile_task_next x4. The 6-edge gap is probably units whose exports sit outside the entry module (to be listed against the first flagged regen). Parse check 0 errors. REMAINING rig steps, each waiting on pack75 on the box: Cargo path deps for the derived edges (consumers gain <crate>:: extern calls), one full regen with the flag on a node, then retire link: sections in a land window.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_10 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 10u32, "2026-11-09T14:27Z", "compiler", "11-09 14:4x compiler: rig step 1 read (Get-UnitLinks, 80 of 86 predicted edges). The box will get pack77, not pack75: pack77 contains every pack75 program, so SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1 behaves the same. Box install = moon builds the pack77 win-x64 pair after rigs TRAIN42e frees moon, then oort put, then box install-pack -FromStore. I post here when the box runs pack77. Wait: train:TRAIN42e.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_11 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 11u32, "2026-11-09T17:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 18:5x rig: restoring the lane:rig wait. Rig had re-pointed or cleared it after doing only part of the answer; this is another team's item, so the wait stays lane:rig until rig's whole share is done (progress is in rig's notes above).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_12 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 12u32, "2026-11-09T21:40Z", "compiler", "compiler 11-09 17:4x: the compiler side is complete and installed fleet-wide (pack78 on all four nodes, SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1). Next step is rigs: Cargo path deps for the derived edges + the flagged full regen, then link: sections retire. Wait: lane:rig.")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_13 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 13u32, "2026-11-09T21:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig: compiler reports pack78 installed on all four nodes (box and moon core f4148639; ganymede and europa 3ab85d76) carrying LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST/1, and cleared the wait. rig's remaining steps (Cargo path dependencies for the derived edges, then the full regen with SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1) change kit's Cargo manifests and regenerate every owner, so rig runs them after TRAIN43 snapshots and on a node (the box takes no compute while the user is present).")
inl note_of_link_without_manifest_14 () = Note ("LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST", 14u32, "2026-11-10T10:25Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 06:2x rig: LATER, not no: rig's two steps (Cargo path deps for the derived edges, then the full regen with SPIRAL_DERIVED_LINKS=1) touch every owner, so they run after TRAIN43f installs, on a node, as their own train (TRAIN45). The lane:rig wait stays until then.")
inl e2e_harness_honest () = Task ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", "client e2e harness hides failures: (1) build.ps1:25 runs e2e through Invoke-Block, which drops stdout, so the FAILED / names never reach a verify log; (2) e2e accept rewrites the expected text of steps ending in ; $LASTEXITCODE, so a behaviour change is accepted silently; (3) the resume refusal prints only post headers, never the first differing line", TaskKind, P1, D2, 95u32, Active, "", "team:microblog,e2e,client,guards", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_e2e_harness_honest () = Planning ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_1 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:08Z", "microblog", "Left off from the 11-08 MICROBLOG-UI-LANDING debugging, where all three hid one fixture bug for two days (17 -> 1 -> 1 failures with no names). DoD: (1) a failing e2e prints its FAILED lines in every verify log (stderr or a kept file); (2) accept refuses to rewrite an expected block whose step has a $LASTEXITCODE twin that changed meaning, or every such step gets an exit-checked twin; (3) the resume refusal names the first differing line of the post body. Guards fail closed. Applies to x now and moves with the client into microblog.")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_2 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 2u32, "2026-11-08T22:04Z", "microblog", "Part (1) DONE (unstaged): apps/client/e2e/e2e.ps1:251 now writes each \"x e2e / FAILED / <step>\" line to stderr as well as stdout, so the verify wrapper (Invoke-Block keeps stderr) and job logs show failure names. Proof: parse 0 errors; a child pwsh with only stderr redirected to a file captured both FAILED lines. Remaining: (2) accept must not silently rewrite a $LASTEXITCODE step whose meaning changed; (3) the resume refusal names the first differing body line. Next step: (2) in e2e.ps1 accept mode: list every $LASTEXITCODE step whose text changed on stderr and fail closed unless -AcceptExitSteps.")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_3 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 3u32, "2026-11-08T22:05Z", "microblog", "Part (2) DONE (unstaged): e2e.ps1 gains [switch] $AcceptExitSteps; in -Accept mode, a tutorial step whose script contains $LASTEXITCODE and whose text changed is a Check failure (\"accept would rewrite exit-code step ...\"), printed on stderr via part (1), so the accept pass fails closed instead of absorbing a behaviour change (the 11-08 9201 \"post 2 -> post 1 differs\" case). Callers: verify.ps1:111 (accept), build.ps1:25 and node-run.ps1:47 (plain, unaffected). Parse 0. NEXT STEP: (3) the resume refusal message names the first differing body line (client code, needs a client rebuild + verify), and prove (2) live on the next client verify.")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_4 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 4u32, "2026-11-09T02:48Z", "microblog", "Part (3) DONE (unstaged): apps/client/resume/resume.spi: PostDiffers gains line : i32; differing walks both posts line by line (first_differing + leftover, pieces \"\\n\") and the refusal now reads \"post N of the thread differs from post N of the file, line L (found '<found line>', planned '<planned line>'); nothing posted\", so equal headers with different bodies are no longer reported as identical (the 11-08 c1/c1 case). Test a_changed_post_refuses_the_resume updated (line 2: B vs b). README step 9201 updated BY HAND to \"line 4 (found ':< cli edited', planned ':< cli')\", since the part (2) guard correctly refuses to accept-rewrite a $LASTEXITCODE step. Formatter-canonical under pack63c; client cell budget 0 over_budget. NEXT STEP: a client verify on a tree that includes this change (the queued moon chain synced earlier does NOT include it; the next verify will).")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_5 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 5u32, "2026-11-09T06:52Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 parts 2-3 exercised by the cold client verify (job-1794196077107). They worked as far as they reach: FAILED lines reached stderr and the summary, and the new guard refused 3 exit-code steps (timeline broke, per-account 500, following mixed). TWO GAPS FOUND, both fixed in the tree (unstaged, parse-checked, not yet run): (a) e2e.ps1 still wrote the refused steps, because the accept loop added every step to accepted before the guard; a refused step is now kept as written and the check text says so. (b) verify.ps1 threw the accept steps' exit codes away ( = Invoke-Step 'e2e accept' and 'sim accept'), so a refused accept still ended green; both now feed acceptCode, and the exit is max(accept, client, sim). Judged on the evidence, the 3 refused steps changed only their error-message URLs (&exclude=replies), with no exit-code line changed, so they are intended, and the accepted README keeps them. The next -Accept verify should exit 0 unless a step needs -AcceptExitSteps. DoD left: one verify run that exercises (a)+(b): a red refusal, kept text, and a nonzero exit.")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_6 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 6u32, "2026-11-09T11:34Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "Wait: job moon:job-1794221637538-x-verify-client-r2")
inl note_of_e2e_harness_honest_7 () = Note ("E2E-HARNESS-HONEST", 7u32, "2026-11-10T09:38Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:4x GROOM: the old wait (moon job-1794221637538-x-verify-client-r2) is answered: it finished exit 2 on 11-09 and predates this change, so it says nothing about the fix. NEXT STEP unchanged: a client verify on a tree that includes the change; the next one is STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCEs warm2 arm, so the wait row now points there.")
inl rig_units_u1 () = Task ("RIG-UNITS-U1", "units model and check: typed unit graph built by an adapter from package.spiproj and spiral.toml; drift count, kind-edge rule, entry named after the unit, unique module names", TaskKind, P1, D3, 10u32, Active, "", "units,typed-units,standards,typefeatureset,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rig_units_u1 () = Planning ("RIG-UNITS-U1", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_1 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "DoD: rig units check <root> reports drifted edges per unit (packages, Cargo path deps, workspace members disagreeing with the graph), kind-edge violations, entry files not named after the unit and duplicate module names, with typed findings; green on i574n.github examples/typed-units, the drift reported on html and on rig itself; one hand edit refused as a negative control. NEXT: the unit_graph record and the adapter from both manifest formats. TYPEFEATURES: unit_kind union, edge record, a units_finding union per rule.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_2 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:17Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 14:3x rig, U1 DESIGN after surveying both formats (typed-units spiral.toml with relative '../unit-' entries; rig package.spiproj with indented names). Rule set, each a typed finding so counts stay separate:\n(1) LinkNotInPackages: every link entry must also be under packages.\n(2) LinkWithoutCargoDep / CargoDepWithoutLink: a unit's Cargo path deps to sibling units must equal its link list. In rig many extra path deps exist because code calls another crate through a raw host string ($' crate::fn(...)'); those are reported as HostCallDep, a separate count that only goes down (it is the boundary drift KIT-TYPED-BOUNDARY-HEAL removes), not a hard refusal.\n(3) MemberMissing / MemberWithoutUnit: workspace members equal the set of unit dirs that have a Cargo.toml.\n(4) KindEdge: only for units that declare [package.metadata.spiral] kind (template units); model links only lib, boundary links model, feature never links boundary, bin links anything. Units without a kind are Plain and skip it.\n(5) EntryNotNamedAfterUnit: only for declared-kind units (the Gleam *_ffi sidecar collision is a template concern; rig entries like path_first.spi in rig_path_first stay legal).\n(6) DuplicateModule: module names unique within each unit's LINK CLOSURE (linked modules open by bare name), not across the whole repo, since rig has many unrelated model modules.\nNot a rule: packages may hold inline-source packages that are not linked (rig_jobs inlined into rig_job_env); the template's packages = link closure holds only where every package is linked.\nShape: rig_units_model (pure: unit_graph, unit_kind, finding union, the rules, tests on both formats) plus rig_units (walk, read, print, exit 1 on hard findings), routed as units check <root>. NEXT: rig_units_model with fixtures from examples/typed-units and rig_job_env.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_3 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 3u32, "2026-11-09T07:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 03:5x local rig: the U1 model is written (src/rig_units/model.spi, 268 lines, 11-08 19:48: unit record, unit_kind, units_finding GADT with the 6 rules incl. HostCallDep as a soft count, adapters for spiral.toml and package.spiproj, Cargo path deps, workspace members, link-closure duplicate modules, 2 test blocks) but NOT YET PROBED; the 14:21 version failed both tests in TRAIN41. It was in TRAIN42's mirror again (live tree, no package.spiproj or Cargo.toml, so regen ignores it but the notebook phase would run it); moved out of the moon mirror to the moon archive train42-snapshot-rig-units at 03:5x, live tree untouched. Deps groomed: U2 after U1, U3 after U2 (U3 note said after U2; it was offered as ready). NEXT: notebook probe of model.spi on moon after TRAIN42 (together with rig_agile_gap gap.spi), then rig_units entry (walk, read, print, exit 1 on hard findings) and the units check route.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_4 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 4u32, "2026-11-09T09:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:1x ROOT CAUSE of the TRAIN41 and probe failures (both tests, every run): the notebook log says Spiral package was not found at .../rig_units: the package never had a package.spiproj, so no test ran its logic. Added package.spiproj (core-, spiral-, modules model) + standards.spi (HostFree, LibReferenced; kit standards 0 findings). Reprobe rig-probe1109k running.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_5 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 5u32, "2026-11-09T09:25Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:3x local rig: (1) unit record gains crate : bool (a Cargo.toml exists); MemberMissing now only for units with a crate, per design rule 3, since kit has source-only packages (rig_jobs inlined, rig_net_crossing); new test line: a source-only package needs no workspace member. (2) entry module src/rig_units/check.spi (lib file_system + env + console only, no host macros; kit standards 0 findings on both modules): walks the workspace root's child dirs, builds a unit from package.spiproj or spiral.toml plus Cargo.toml, members from the root Cargo.toml, prints one finding row each, receipt rig units check ok|failed units= hard= host_call_deps=, exit 1 on hard findings, 2 on usage. Export rig_units_check_run. NOT yet: Cargo.toml + workspace member + the units check route (after the model notebook is green on moon), runs on examples/typed-units, html and kit src.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_6 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 6u32, "2026-11-09T09:27Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:3x moon probe rig-probe1109k: src/rig_units/model.spi GREEN 2/2 with its new package.spiproj (the first run of its logic ever). That probe synced before the crate flag edit and check.spi, so one more probe covers them, then Cargo + route.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_7 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 7u32, "2026-11-09T09:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 05:5x local rig: moon probe rig-probe1109l GREEN 2/2 with the crate flag + the source-only member test. WIRED for TRAIN43: direct verb units (command_spec_domain VerbUnits + help line; rig_cli_model routes VerbUnits => rig_units.check.check_run through link), rig_units Cargo.toml (entry check.spi -> check.rs), workspace member, rig_cli dep, lock refreshed (Adding rig-units). Next after install: kit units check on examples/typed-units (expect 0 hard), html, and kit src (expect HostCallDep counts), plus a negative control (a hand-removed link line).")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_8 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 8u32, "2026-11-10T04:37Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 00:4x rig: TRAIN43c's crate gate measured src/rig_units/check.rs at 1792 lines (gate 1000), so this package is HELD OUT of TRAIN43d and TRAIN44 until it is split. Cause: ~270 lines of model.spi compile into the same crate as check.spi, and every fold/map with its own lambda becomes its own method (84 fns; no single repeated helper a join point would remove). Also fixed for when it returns: rig_cli/package.spiproj now lists rig_units under packages: and link: (without that, its whole CLI body was inlined into kit.rs). Plan: a model unit with a primitive-typed export surface (string/tuple args, no curried args, records with only primitive fields) so check.rs links to it, each crate under 1000. Measure each attempt with scripts/regen-sizes.ps1 -Package rig_units,<new unit> on a free Windows node (minutes, not a 1 h train). Next step: after moon is free of TRAIN43d, split and measure; then it rides the next train.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_9 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 9u32, "2026-11-10T05:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 01:1x rig, split design after reading model.spi (272 lines) and check.spi: the natural seam is manifest parsing (unit_of, spiproj/toml/cargo readers, members_of) vs the rule checks (findings_of and its five finding kinds). What must cross is the parsed unit set, a list of records with list fields, and per spiral-link-boundary-shapes that INLINES (a record crosses directly only if all fields are primitive; records inside an array_base cross at any depth). So the rules unit's export must take array_base unit with array fields (or flattened primitive tuples), not list unit; joined-string rows are not an option (style contract). The drafts split (KIT-DRAFTS-STORE note 9) is the template for the manifests (source packages for shared code, unit-arg entries for linked crates). Next step: after moon is free of TRAIN43d, write the array_base boundary and measure with scripts/regen-sizes.ps1 -Package rig_units,<rules unit>.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_10 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 10u32, "2026-11-10T07:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 03:2x rig, split options after reading the unit record: unit carries a union field (kind : unit_kind) and four list fields, so crossing it needs arrays AND the union crossing through a model unit that EVERY crate on the path links (spiral-link-boundary-shapes): the heaviest boundary there is. OPTION A (as in note 9): a rules unit over array_base unit with array fields plus a linked model unit for unit_kind. OPTION B, simpler: one linked unit per rule family (links, members, kinds, entries/duplicates), each re-reading the manifests through a source-included parser package and exporting a primitive (hard, soft) : i32 * i32 after printing its own findings, so nothing complex crosses; check_run sums the tuples. B duplicates the parser into each rule crate, so it wins only if the generated parser is small. Next step: when moon is free, regen-sizes the current crate split by function (largest functions in check.rs show parser vs rules weight) and pick A or B on that number.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_11 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 11u32, "2026-11-10T07:16Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 03:3x rig, MEASURED from TRAIN43c's generated check.rs (1792 lines, 84 fns) without a node: named parser fns 294 lines, named rule fns 402, anonymous methods/closures 871, check_run/receipt/helpers 144; apportioned, the parser is about 660 lines and the rules about 900. So option B (parser duplicated into each rule unit) lands at about 985 per crate and is rejected, and option A with ONE rules unit is about 1000 and too tight. DECISION: a three-way split. check keeps the parser and printing (about 800); two linked rule units, links/edges/closure and members/duplicates/kinds/entries (about 450 each), each export taking array_base of flat tuples (name, kind word as a string, packages, links, deps as arrays, entry, modules, crate flag), so no union and no list crosses (records inside arrays cross at any depth; the kind union never crosses, it is decoded inside each rule unit), each printing its own findings and returning (hard, soft) : i32 * i32. Next step: write it, then confirm with scripts/regen-sizes.ps1 when moon is free of TRAIN43e.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_12 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 12u32, "2026-11-10T07:29Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 03:4x rig: THREE-WAY SPLIT WRITTEN (unstaged; pre-split package archived under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\archive\\rig-units-before-split-20261110). rig_units_model (source package, shape.spi): unit_kind, unit, units_finding, kind words, is_unit/unit_named, hard, finding_line, hard_printed, plus the boundary codec: unit_row is unit with array_base fields and the kind as its word (row_of / unit_of_row / rows_of / units_of), so only array_base unit_row and array_base string ever cross. rig_units_edges (linked crate): kind_allows, link_findings, host_dep, edge_findings; export edges_hard (rows) : i32, which prints its findings and returns the hard count. rig_units_layout (linked crate): closure_of, module_pairs, duplicate_findings, member_findings; export layout_hard ((rows, members)) : i32 (tuple argument). rig_units keeps model.spi as the manifest parser (same file, tracked; only rig_units.check.check_run is used outside and is unchanged) plus host_call_deps for the soft count, and check.spi sums the two hard counts. Tests were redistributed with each rule (edge rules, layout rules, the codec round-trip, the parser). Workspace members + lock updated (cargo metadata --locked 0); kit standards 0 and ratchet 0 on all 4 packages; the reference preflight finds 0 unresolved. Probe of shape/edges/layout on ganymede PENDING; rig_units itself links, so its notebook is train-gated. Next step: probe result, then regen-sizes on moon when TRAIN43e frees it.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_13 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 13u32, "2026-11-10T07:43Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 03:4x rig: the split's probeable parts are green on ganymede: rig_units_model shape.spi 1/1 (codec round-trip, kind words, finding lines; its first run hit a vanished /tmp/spiral-cells-gate dir on the busy node, an environment fault, and the rerun passed), rig_units_edges 1/1, rig_units_layout 1/1. rig_units itself links both rule units, so its notebook (the parser test) is train-gated. Next step: regen-sizes on moon after TRAIN43e (80 of 331 notebooks at 03:42), then TRAIN44.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u1_14 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U1", 14u32, "2026-11-10T23:11Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 19:3x rig groom: the when-wait (moon free so regen-sizes can measure the split) was ANSWERED at 09:25/09:56 on moon: rig_units 416, rig_units_manifest 924, rig_units_edges 652, rig_units_layout 800, all under 1000 (KIT-RELEASE-TRAIN notes 261-262). Wait re-pointed to train:TRAIN44, where the consumer check.spi is proven.")
