inl note_of_rig_units_u5_1 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U5", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:09Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "DoD: when the compiler emits the unit graph, units check and sync read it instead of the manifest adapter, with identical findings on rig and html; the adapter stays only as a fallback until link: is retired. NEXT: wait for the compiler graph format. TYPEFEATURES: the same unit_graph record, a second producer.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u5_2 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U5", 2u32, "2026-11-09T11:38Z", "compiler", "compiler ANSWER 11-09: the compiler graph format EXISTS and is shipped (pack57+, in pack73b). Each built unit with exports writes <stem>.unit.json at <root>/<h>/ where root = $SPIRAL_UNIT_GRAPH_DIR or <LocalApplicationData>/spiral-bin/units (Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%, Linux ~/.local/share) and h = first 8 bytes hex of SHA-256 over the package dir path normalized to forward slashes, trailing slash trimmed, lowercased (spiral_compiler.fs unitGraphDir). JSON top level: \"exports\" [{name, signature, target_name, target_path}], \"links\" [unit names it links], \"unions\" [{source name, concrete shape, exported name}] (unitGraphJson). The compiler itself reads it back through readUnitGraph when a consumer links the unit (stale-graph error names the unit to rebuild). Your check/sync can read these files directly; no further compiler work is needed to start U5. Wait no longer applies.")
inl note_of_rig_units_u5_3 () = Note ("RIG-UNITS-U5", 3u32, "2026-11-09T11:49Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 08:2x compiler answered (relayed to rig): the unit graph format already ships: <stem>.unit.json under $SPIRAL_UNIT_GRAPH_DIR or <LocalAppData>/spiral-bin/units/<sha256-8B of the normalized package dir>/, keys exports {name, signature, target_name, target_path}, links and unions. So U5 (replace the adapter with the compiler-emitted graph) can start once U1 ships; the lane wait on compiler is cleared.")
inl zig_separate_units () = Task ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", "Zig linked units as REAL separate compilation: one zig build-obj per unit with export fn plus extern declarations, linked at the end, from a generated build.zig; and use zig cc/zig build as the battle-test toolchain for the C units (LINKED-UNITS-C)", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "LINKED-UNITS-ZIG", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,zig,c,parallel,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_separate_units () = Planning ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", Minutes 480u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_separate_units_1 () = Note ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:18Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: \"shouldn't we be using zig to battle test c itself? ... or iterate on zig until getting better performance instead of giving up\". Today (test.ps1:172) Zig linked units build as ONE zig build-exe root.zig that @imports the other units as source: structure, zero build parallelism (compiler 11-08). Iterate instead of dropping it: (1) each unit compiles to its own object with zig build-obj, crossing through export fn and extern declarations, linked at the end, so units build in parallel; (2) build.zig is GENERATED from the unit graph (RIG-UNITS-CHECK emits it beside Cargo.toml, gleam.toml and build.ninja); (3) the same generated build.zig also drives the C units through zig cc / addCSourceFiles, as a second toolchain arm in LINKED-UNITS-C next to gcc plus ninja, so Zig battle-tests C. DoD: x client zig cells and the linked Zig rows build unit by unit in parallel; outputs byte-identical to the single build-exe; wall time measured with an A/A arm.")
inl note_of_zig_separate_units_2 () = Note ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:24Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. TODAY: Zig 'linked units' are ONE compilation. test.ps1's linked runner does 'zig build-exe root.zig', and the root @imports the other units as source. REAL separate compilation in Zig means the C ABI: each unit 'zig build-obj unit.zig' with 'export fn', and importers declare 'extern fn'. So everything that crosses must be C-ABI-shaped: 'extern struct' / 'extern union' layouts for Spiral records and unions; strings and arrays as ptr+len; no Zig slices or error unions in signatures; and an allocation/ownership convention at the boundary. PLAN: share ONE ABI design with LINKED-UNITS-C. A generated per-unit interface (the C header there; an interface .zig here, holding the extern struct definitions and extern fn declarations, imported by both sides) defines each crossing type ONCE. That also fixes the known Zig trap (main.Tuple0 is not model.Tuple0: tuples are per-file structs), and its hash is UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH's interface_hash (still plus the used set for M2's inlined surface). Build: a generated build.zig with addObject per unit (or ninja); objects build in parallel and link at the end. ZIG CC ARM: compiling LINKED-UNITS-C's C units with 'zig cc' (clang + bundled libc) as a second toolchain next to gcc 15.2 catches compiler-specific UB and ABI assumptions, which is a cheap, real battle test. DoD suggestion: the linked_* rows on Zig built as separate objects (the build log shows N build-obj + 1 link), run equal to the single-unit build (split-invariance oracle), and a -j speedup table measured with an A/A arm. The C-units rows pass under both gcc and zig cc.")
inl note_of_zig_separate_units_3 () = Note ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", 3u32, "2026-11-08T21:32Z", "compiler", "11-08 groom: P1 -> P2. User 11-08 put LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP (P1) ahead of C and Zig.")
inl note_of_zig_separate_units_4 () = Note ("ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS", 4u32, "2026-11-11T00:29Z", "compiler", "11-10 20:3x compiler (groom): the user ordered LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP ahead of the C and Zig unit work (11-08). F# step 1 is drafted tonight (LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP-D1, probe queued as pack92). Wait row on the F# item; the Call-row + runner shape it establishes is the template for the Zig build-obj path.")
inl predict_backend_rebench () = Task ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", "Re-bench the tibia predict sweep runtime on C vs Rust vs Zig (quiet node, A/A arm, seeded rows sha256-equal), so backend choice follows today's numbers, not 11-03's 2.65x", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:tibia,tibia,bench,c,rust,zig,backend-balance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_predict_backend_rebench () = Planning ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_1 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:18Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: ask tibia to bench Rust again, and iterate on Zig for performance instead of giving up. The last measurement (TIBIA-RUN-METRICS n10, 11-03, quiet europa) was C 47.7 s vs Rust 126.4 s for 4000 hunts (2.65x, A/A 3.3%), down from 5.8x on 10-27; packs 24-62 landed since. Add Zig as a third arm (ReleaseFast). Report per backend: wall time, A/A spread, peak memory, and the generated-line count of the sweep (outputs counted). Feeds BACKEND-BALANCE and GLEAM-RUST-SPLIT; if Rust reaches parity, rayon in-process sweeps become an option. Host: a quiet node window after fiscal.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_2 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:20Z", "tibia", "11-08 tibia groom + PRE-REGISTRATION (written before any run). PREP DONE (unstaged): tibia scripts/predict.ps1 -Backend now takes zig as well as c and rust, through one backend table (compiler --backend Zig, then zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast --stack 1073741824 -fno-emit-implib, the flags the compiler's own test.ps1 uses); the c and rust commands are unchanged; parse 0 errors, tibia build.ps1 -ChecksOnly green. DoD: on ONE pinned tibia digest and compiler core (pack62 or later, named), in ONE quiet node window: (1) the default predict sweep (6x6 plan, 4000 hunts) built and run per backend, order C, Rust, Zig, C (the second C is the A/A arm; spread must be <= 5 pct or the window is VOID and rerun); (2) the sha256 of the output rows equal across all four runs (any mismatch is a correctness finding, filed before any timing is quoted); (3) per backend: build wall time, run wall time and hunts/s, peak working set (the process's PeakWorkingSet64), and the generated source's line count; (4) the load and CPU idle logged per row (quiet gate per run, see ab-quiet-gate-per-run). EXPECTATIONS (soft, recorded whatever happens): C stays fastest; Rust narrows below 2.0x of C (from 2.65x on 11-03, after packs 24-62); Zig ReleaseFast lands within 1.5x of C. If Rust is within 1.2x of C, file the rayon in-process sweep item. NEXT STEP: a Zig smoke (compile and one run, no timing) to learn whether the Zig backend builds the whole predict program at all, on any host; then book the timed window after fiscal's shards through QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST. TYPEFEATURES: none (measurement task; the backend arms are a closed ValidateSet).")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_3 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 3u32, "2026-11-08T21:30Z", "tibia", "11-08 17:3x tibia groom: host candidate besides a quiet node window: ganymede (4 vCPU, 24/7, idle, ours). Shared vCPUs, so the pre-registered A/A arm (<= 5 pct) decides validity. NEXT STEP unchanged: the Zig smoke first (on ganymede, no timing), then the timed C / Rust / Zig / C rows.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_4 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 4u32, "2026-11-08T23:14Z", "tibia", "11-08 19:3x tibia: PLACEMENT RULE update (user direction relayed by fiscal; memory k3s-build-test-substrate). Once org has k3s on ganymede (install order ganymede -> comet -> WSL last), run the timed arms as k3s wrapper pods: memory request == limit, CPU limit = workers, cpuset-pinned; NEVER overlap a measurement with any compilation on the same node (compile first, then measure with the node otherwise idle). ganymede has 2 slots, so take both, or check that the other slot is idle, and keep the A/A arm. Toolchain image pinned by digest; caches on the capped hostPath /cache. A WSL run never counts as a Linux result. Until k3s lands, a plain ganymede job is fine for SMOKE only (no timing claims).")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_5 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 5u32, "2026-11-08T23:34Z", "tibia", "11-08 19:5x tibia: QUEUED (FLEET-NODE-SATURATION-24-7: ganymede is a saturate-24/7 node): the Zig SMOKE (compile + one run of the default predict sweep, NO timing claims) as ganymede job-1794180832983-tibia-zig-smoke, chained --after tibia-bc-4d so they never overlap. Question it answers: does the Zig backend build the whole predict program (predict.ps1 -Backend zig: compiler --backend Zig, then zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast --stack 1 GiB)? The timed C/Rust/Zig/C rows still wait for k3s pods on ganymede (pinned, nothing compiling alongside).")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_6 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 6u32, "2026-11-08T23:57Z", "tibia", "11-08 20:0x tibia: the first Zig smoke failed exit 127 (zig not installed on ganymede). zig 0.18.0-dev.738 is now copied from vultr (418 MB; that dev build is likely no longer downloadable). Re-QUEUED: job-1794182210772-tibia-zig-smoke2 (--after tibia-bc-gate), script /root/tibia-jobs/zig-smoke.sh, smoke only.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_7 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 7u32, "2026-11-09T04:10Z", "tibia", "11-09 00:1x tibia: ZIG ARM, compile reached, build OOM. (1) tibia's only backend_switch (hunting_pack knight_turn) lacked a Zig arm (\"this lib/spiral function has no Zig arm\"); added Zig = knight_turn_shared, as for C and Rust. (2) The Spiral Zig backend then emitted predict.zig: 10674593 bytes (C: ~8.6 MB). (3) zig 0.18.0-dev.738 build-exe -O ReleaseFast on ganymede (pinned 2 cores): KILLED rc=137 after 8:24 wall, max RSS 4759892 kB (OOM; ganymede has 7.4 GB shared with the other lanes). Placement rule from this: the Zig arm of predict needs a host with >= 6-8 GB free for one zig process. Never ganymede under shared load (it would starve every lane's compile window). Candidates: moon (10 GB free) in a quiet window, or a test of -O ReleaseSmall / Debug memory first. Also worth a compiler look: Zig output is ~24 pct larger than C for the same module. Not retried yet.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_8 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 8u32, "2026-11-09T04:50Z", "tibia", "11-09 00:5x tibia: ZIG BUILD on comet (codespace, 8 GB, 2 cores, 6.7 GB available): TERMINATED twice by SIGTERM (rc=143), at 91 s (detached; the codespace also restarted, presumably the idle-stop) and at 75 s (with the ssh session held open, so not idle). The SIGTERM-under-pressure signature points to systemd-oomd, so the build needs more than ~6 GB, likely well above the 4.76 GB ganymede reached before its OOM kill. zig 0.18.0-dev.738 reached comet from the oort toolchain bundle (presigned GET, 10 s; ziglang.org timed out from the codespace). Cleaned up: ~/tibia-zig (429 MB) removed from the shared comet. Quota spent ~0.1 core-h. CONCLUSION: one zig process on the 10.7 MB predict.zig needs a host with >= 10-12 GB free. moon (10 GB free) is borderline; the Windows box is out (user present); the 96 GB powerhouse is restricted. Better lever: make the Zig output smaller (24 pct larger than C for the same module) or build at -O Debug/ReleaseSmall to measure memory. Parked until one of those; no further comet quota on it.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_9 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 9u32, "2026-11-09T11:26Z", "tibia", "11-09 07:3x tibia: compiler DIAGNOSED the Zig OOM on ganymede (capped at 3.5 GB). Zig itself is fine: analysis-only takes 33 s at 139 MB, and a full Debug build takes 9.5 s at 183 MB with a working binary. The >4.7 GB is LLVM ReleaseFast optimizing two giant single functions, knight_turn_shared_346 = 90673 lines (54 pct of the file) and foes_act_from_6 = 45678 lines (81 pct together), because the partial evaluator inlines every := helper into them. compiler filed codegen auto-outlining under ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP. STEP PRE-REGISTERED (tibia side, option 2): turn hot-path helpers in src/hunting_pack.spi into JOIN POINTS (:~, no ^), so each is emitted once as its own function: foe_acts (foe path), target_turn, patrol_turn, heal_gate. No semantic change. GATES: (1) C predict output (default predict module, rows) is BYTE-IDENTICAL before and after (baseline run queued first, then diff); (2) --check clean, including the WGSL-reached files; (3) compiler re-measures Zig ReleaseFast peak RSS under its 3.5 GB cap, with the new top-3 function sizes. Soft expectation: the largest Zig function falls below 40k lines and the ReleaseFast peak below 3.5 GB. Risk named: the WGSL path (knight_turn_inline) goes through the same helpers; if WGSL rejects join points, the gpu gate shows it and the change is scoped to the shared path only.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_10 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 10u32, "2026-11-09T12:03Z", "tibia", "11-09 08:1x tibia: JOIN-POINT A/B MEASURED (ganymede job-1794224887683-tibia-jp-ab; one job: revert the 4 :~ to := in the mirror, predict C, restore, predict C). GATE 1 HOLDS: the content sha of the predict rows is IDENTICAL before and after (cebdd2e8a3dd3734, 402 lines both), so join points on foe_acts / target_turn / patrol_turn / heal_gate change NO output. Generated C shrank 7164346 -> 6132530 bytes (-14.4 pct). NEXT: Zig regen of the join-point tree plus top-3 function sizes for compiler's capped ReleaseFast re-measure (queued).")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_11 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 11u32, "2026-11-09T12:37Z", "tibia", "11-09 08:5x tibia: Zig regen of the join-point tree (installed pack75; job-1794225814778-tibia-zig-jp): predict-jp.zig 9392055 bytes (was 10674593). Top functions: foe_acts_7 43735, target_turn_362 35920, knight_turn_shared_347 33643, patrol_turn_361 22383 (was knight_turn_shared_346 90673 + foes_act_from_6 45678). The soft expectation \"largest < 40k\" MISSED narrowly (43.7k: foe_acts is now the largest body). Sent to compiler for the capped ReleaseFast peak. If still over the cap, the next split is inside foe_acts.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_12 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 12u32, "2026-11-09T12:50Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:0x tibia: compiler re-measured predict-jp.zig (9.39 MB): ReleaseFast still OOM under a 4.5 GB cgroup cap (exit 143); under a 3.5 GB address-space cap it aborts at 2.89 GB RSS after 102 s. Target: no function over ~10k lines. STEP 2 PRE-REGISTERED: 13 more fully-annotated hot-path helpers become join points (:~). Foe path: foe_respawns, foe_acquires, foe_moves, foe_chases, foe_strikes. Knight path: strikes_target, hunter_steps, player_heals, player_retreats, engaged, retreat_gate, after_heal_plan, wants_heal. GATES as step 1: (1) C predict content sha IDENTICAL to the step-1 tree (A/B job reverts only these 13 for its baseline); (2) Zig regen top-5 function sizes; (3) compiler's capped ReleaseFast re-measure. Soft expectation: the largest Zig function falls below 15k lines and ReleaseFast fits under the 4.5 GB cap.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_13 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 13u32, "2026-11-09T13:04Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:0x tibia: STEP 2 MEASURED (ganymede job-1794228751198-tibia-jp2). GATE 1 HOLDS: the C predict content sha is IDENTICAL with and without the 13 new join points (cebdd2e8a3dd3734, 402 rows); C 6132530 -> 4978747 bytes (-19 pct; -30 pct since step 0). Zig predict-jp2.zig 7836466 bytes (was 10674593). Top functions: knight_turn_shared_352 22586, foe_moves_224 21632, patrol_turn_370 21309, foe_strikes_245 12032, player_retreats_375 8553, foe_acts_7 7999. Soft expectation \"largest < 15k\" MISSED (22.6k), but every body is roughly halved again. Sent to compiler for the capped ReleaseFast measure. If still over, the next split is inside knight_turn_shared (decided_turn, acts_after_aim), foe_moves and patrol_turn.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_14 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 14u32, "2026-11-09T13:10Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:1x tibia: STEP 3 PRE-REGISTERED (prepared while compiler measures step 2, since 22.6k is still over the ~10k target): 12 more fully-annotated helpers become join points: patrol_step, sweep_step, territory_step, steps_somewhere, foe_moves_steady, foe_moves_live, wanders, decided_turn, acts_on_target, acts_after_aim, respawn_player, heals_now. Same gates: the C content sha must stay IDENTICAL (A/B reverting only these 12), then Zig top-6 sizes. Soft expectation: the largest Zig function falls below 12k lines.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_15 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 15u32, "2026-11-09T13:14Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:2x tibia: compiler measured STEP 2: predict-jp2.zig builds with -O ReleaseFast under a 4.5 GB cgroup cap in 140.6 s, peak 3.02 GB RSS, exit 0, binary 10.0 MB (step 1 hit the cap; the original OOMed at 4.76 GB+). So the 13 extra join points unblock Zig ReleaseFast on ganymede-class nodes (7.4 GB) and on comet (~6 GB oomd), with C output byte-identical (cebdd2e8a3dd3734). Step 3 (12 more, job tibia-jp3) stays as margin, under the same sha gate. Next: the actual rebench, predict hunts/s for c (gcc -O2) vs zig ReleaseFast on the same tree and seeds, with identical result rows.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_16 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 16u32, "2026-11-09T13:15Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:2x tibia: REBENCH PRE-REGISTERED (job tibia-rebench, after tibia-jp3 releases the tree). Same tree (step-3 join points), predict.ps1 -SkipChecks, both pinned to cores 0,1: c (gcc -O2) then zig (ReleaseFast, the whole run under systemd-run MemoryMax=4500M). Gates: (1) result-content sha of zig == c (the same rows, timing lines stripped), else zig is wrong and its rate does not count; (2) report hunts_per_second for both, plus the zig build wall time. No prediction about which is faster; Debug zig is not measured.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_17 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 17u32, "2026-11-09T13:30Z", "tibia", "11-09 09:4x tibia: STEP 3 MEASURED (job-1794229904016-tibia-jp3). GATE HOLDS: the C content sha is IDENTICAL again (cebdd2e8a3dd3734, 402 rows); C 4978747 -> 4696309 bytes (-34 pct since step 0). Zig 7836466 -> 7436520 bytes. Top functions: foe_moves_steady_228 16676, decided_turn_357 13967, foe_strikes_248 12032, player_retreats_387 8553, territory_step_382 7938, sweep_step_381 7759. knight_turn_shared and patrol_turn have dropped out of the top 6. Soft expectation \"largest < 12k\" MISSED (16.7k in foe_moves_steady), but it is 26 pct under step 2's 22.6k and step 2 already fits ReleaseFast at 3.02 GB. No step 4: compiler's SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT auto-outlining will bound the rest without annotations. The rebench runs on this step-3 tree.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_18 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 18u32, "2026-11-09T13:38Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:0x tibia: REBENCH part 1 (job-1794230130918-tibia-rebench). GATE 1 HOLDS: zig ReleaseFast gives the SAME 402 result rows as c (content sha cebdd2e8a3dd3734 both). Wall times c 107 s, zig 164 s include compile+build (zig build under a 4.5 GB cap, exit 0), so they say nothing about speed. predict.ps1 printed no hunts/s: the predict app no longer emits a `hunts` row, so the rate line never fires (a tibia script gap, noted). PART 2 PRE-REGISTERED (job tibia-rebench2): time only the two built binaries (rb-c/predict 0.56 MB, rb-zig/predict 13.1 MB), pinned to core 1, alternating c,zig x3, with ganymede otherwise idle of tibia jobs. Metric: median wall ms per backend. Gate: each run's output sha must equal cebdd2e8a3dd3734. No direction predicted.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_19 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 19u32, "2026-11-09T13:39Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:0x tibia: AMENDMENT to part 2, made before any data. Gate correction: rebench2 hashes the RAW binary stdout, but cebdd2e8a3dd3734 is the sha of predict.ps1's filtered rows, so the two can never match. The gate becomes: in every run the c and zig `out` digests are equal to each other (and stable across the 3 runs). The metric is unchanged.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_20 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 20u32, "2026-11-09T13:44Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:1x tibia: PART 2 RESULT, C vs ZIG (job-1794231551581-tibia-rebench2, ganymede core 1, alternating x3). GATE HOLDS: c and zig give the same output digest in all 6 runs. It equals cebdd2e8a3dd3734 too (the step-1 filter removed nothing), so the amendment in note 19 was harmless. Wall ms: c 10541 / 12041 / 11011 (median 11011), zig ReleaseFast 10586 / 10735 / 11337 (median 10735). Zig is 2.5 pct faster at the median, well inside c's own run spread (10.5-12.0 s): PARITY, no backend preference between c and zig at this size. Binary 0.56 MB vs 13.1 MB. The 11-03 2.65x gap has gone (that was Rust vs C). OPEN for the DoD: the Rust arm and an A/A arm (c vs c) on the same core. Next run: c, c, rust, zig alternating x3.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_21 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 21u32, "2026-11-09T13:51Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:3x tibia: PART 3, RUST + A/A (job-1794231934000-tibia-rebench3, core 1, alternating c, c2, rust, zig x3; rust binary 5.0 MB). GATE HOLDS: all 12 runs give the same output digest cebdd2e8a3dd3734. Wall ms: c 11036 / 12151 / 14571; c2 (the SAME c binary, A/A) 11458 / 16190 / 14589; rust 5345 / 10744 / 9320; zig 9894 / 15882 / 16867. A/A FAILS AS A QUIET-NODE CHECK: the same binary differs by up to 33 pct within a round, and times drift up through the job, so ganymede was not quiet (the other slot was busy) and absolute ratios are not trustworthy. ROBUST ORDERING ONLY (within-round, same conditions): rust < c in 3/3 rounds (medians 9320 vs 12151, ratio 1.30); zig vs c is mixed (1 of 3), consistent with part 2's parity (zig 10735 vs c 11011 on a quieter node). READING for backend choice today: rust is the fastest predict backend, and c and zig are tied. The 11-03 2.65x (c over rust) has reversed. NEXT (DoD: quiet node): the same 4-arm script once ganymede has no other job in either slot (night), as the final row.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_22 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 22u32, "2026-11-09T14:22Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:2x tibia: compiler installed pack77 on ganymede + europa (14:13Z, SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b) with AUTOMATIC OUTLINING ON by default for C and Zig: any if/switch/match arm over 400 typed binds becomes its own function. On this predict source, Zig ReleaseFast peak memory falls 2.35 -> 1.90 GB; SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=150 brings the largest function to 7.7k lines. Generated C may now differ TEXTUALLY from the pack75 builds measured in parts 1-3. AMENDMENT for the quiet-night rerun (written before it runs): rebuild all four arms on pack77 (record the core sha), and the gate becomes every arm's output digest == the pack77 c digest; cebdd2e8a3dd3734 is expected again, because behaviour is preserved. The 13+12 join points stay: the outliner covers the rest, and the joins keep the C size gains measured in steps 2-3.")
inl note_of_predict_backend_rebench_23 () = Note ("PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH", 23u32, "2026-11-09T18:23Z", "tibia", "11-09 14:3x tibia: HEALED the script gap from part 1 (note 18). predict.ps1 only wrote the hunts/s metric when the app printed a `hunts<TAB>N` row, which apps/predict/predict.spi never does (only sweep.spi and hashed_lanes.spi do), so every predict run since then wrote no sim-<label>.prom. Fix (unstaged, scripts/predict.ps1:77-83): count = the hunts row if present, else the number of data rows (^\\d+\\t, one seeded run each); the predict output itself is untouched, so the pre-registered content digests stay valid. Parse 0 errors; the fallback counted 2 of 2 data rows on a synthetic sample, and an explicit hunts row still wins. The quiet-night 4-arm rerun now also writes sim-rb-*.prom.")
inl manual_units_everywhere () = Task ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", "Every split in every repo is a manual, typed, linked unit cut, never a post-hoc or tool-chosen split; each remaining non-manual split gets a groomed child item", Epic, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "UNIT-GRAPH-SOTA", "team:microblog,linked-units,typed-boundaries,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_manual_units_everywhere () = Planning ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", Minutes 2400u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_1 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:18Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: \"every split we have should be made manual with groomed tasks to nail that\". Inventory 11-08 (grep of build scripts across lane repos plus the compiler test runner): the ONLY post-hoc splitter left in a build is x scripts/gleam-modules.ps1, called by apps/sim/build.ps1 (child X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT). Structural non-manual cases: Zig linked units compile as one build-exe (child ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS); Cells apps/cells/spi is 8 files with no units (child CELLS-TYPED-UNITS); spiral lib is one package of implicit modules (lib split children exist only for over-budget definitions, e.g. LIB-SPLIT-TEXT; whether lib itself becomes linked units is OPEN, i574n); fiscal has 83 generated packages sharing types through packages only (fiscal committed to linked model units at its first cut); tibia has 0 units (TIBIA-TYPED-NATIVES applies rules 1-3 at its first split); C has no linked units yet (LINKED-UNITS-C). Already manual: kit 97 units, html 8. Open for compiler: does the compiler itself still split any generated output automatically (e.g. Rust module files)? DoD: every repo build cuts units only where a spiral.toml declares them (or, after LINK-WITHOUT-MANIFEST, where exports derive them); 0 post-hoc splitters; each cut proven by the split-invariance oracle.")
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_2 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:19Z", "microblog", "BACKEND RATING 11-08 (user: rate 0-1000 how each backend leverages manual exports, counting outputs). Rubric, max 1000: typed linkage works (records, unions, lists crossing; linked matrix rows) 300; separate compilation giving parallel unit builds 250; manifest generated from the unit graph 150; diff locality (each unit its own output and name space) 100; real use, outputs counted (cells in lane repos plus real linked units) 200. Outputs: linked matrix rows Rust 14, Zig 4, Gleam 3, others 0; cells in lane repos rust 2012, gleam 748, zig 335, c 258, lua 238, typescript 163, cuda 139, fsharp 113, pascal 49, python 42, wgsl 9, lean 3; real linked units: Rust 105 (kit 97, html 8), others 0. Scores: Rust 850 (300 + 250 rlib per unit plus Cargo parallel and pipelined + 0 manifests, 374 hand-written + 100 + 200); Gleam 420 (220 + 100 Spiral-side separate, Gleam packages sequential, Erlang step parallel + 0 + 40, the sim still post-hoc + 60, 0 real units); Zig 340 (200 + 20, one build-exe importing units as source + 0 + 80 + 40); C 50 (0 linked calls emitted + 0 + 0 + 0 + 50 for tibia predict); Lua 40, TypeScript 30, F# 25, CUDA 20, Pascal/Python 10, WGSL/Lean 5 (no linkage; real use only). Next movers: RIG-UNITS-CHECK (+150 each where manifests generate), X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT (Gleam diff locality and real units), ZIG-SEPARATE-UNITS (Zig separate compilation), LINKED-UNITS-C. Re-score after each lands.")
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_3 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T16:24Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler, answering the open question (does the compiler itself split generated output?): NO. Every compilation writes exactly ONE output file (spiral_compiler.fs ~35290, the staged write); the other file writes are unit.json, the formatter, source migrations and derived TOML. The only splitter inside the compiler is 'SpiralCompiler split-units <file.spi> --cut <k> --out <dir>'. It cuts SOURCE at a block index into a model unit plus an app unit with link:, and it exists to generate LINK-SPLIT-INVARIANCE's oracle corpus (every legal cut must give equal output). That's a test tool, not a production split; keep it. Automatic splitting of GENERATED output exists only outside the compiler: x's scripts/gleam-modules.ps1 (Gleam text splitter, retiring under X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT). No compiler child item is needed.")
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_4 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 4u32, "2026-11-08T16:24Z", "microblog", "CEILING RATING 11-08 (user: imagine each backend implementation already SOTA, not today's state; correction: \"no linkage\" for F# meant only that Spiral's F# backend emits no linked units today, not a .NET limit). Same rubric, first four criteria scored at the target toolchain's best, real use still counted from today's outputs. Rust 980 (300/230 crate-parallel plus pipelining plus codegen units, front end parallel only on nightly/150/100/200); C 830 (280/250 TU per unit plus ninja -j/150/100/50); F# 775 (300 assemblies carry full metadata/200 dll per unit plus MSBuild -m plus graph-based checking with .fsi signatures, ParallelCompilation still preview in F# 10 with a reported SDK bug/150/100/25); TypeScript 770 (280/210 TS 7 native Go compiler with shared-memory multithreading plus project references/150/100/30); Zig 750 (260/200 build-obj per unit plus build.zig parallel steps/150/100/40); Lean 725 (250/220 lake builds modules in parallel by import graph/150/100/5); Gleam 710 (280/120 packages in sequence, only the Erlang step parallel within one package/150/100/60); CUDA 650 (200 -rdc device linking awkward/180/150/100/20); Pascal 630 (250 units have interface sections built in/120 FPC compiles units mostly in sequence/150/100/10); Lua 540 (200/100 nothing heavy to build/100/100/40); Python 510; WGSL 205 (no module system). LIB QUESTION (open, i574n): a single source package for lib is not itself a build problem: consumers specialize only the lib definitions they reach, and HOPACSOTA does not change that. Two gaps it does cause: (a) any lib edit invalidates every consumer while keys cover the whole source graph; the per-definition used set of UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH fixes that without splitting lib; (b) every consumer re-specializes and re-emits the same monomorphic leaf subsystems (markdown, regex, json, tlds), which inflates generated lines against the 1000-line gate. Proposal: offer those as precompiled linked lib units, like Rust std is a prebuilt rlib, while generic lib stays one source package.")
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_5 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 5u32, "2026-11-08T16:25Z", "i574n", "i574n RULING 11-08 16:1x on microblog's note 3 (asked by the user: is lib being one package a problem?). DECISION: lib stays ONE flat source package (the user's lib-FLAT rule and the 11-02 decision on TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 stand). Leaf subsystems MAY be offered as precompiled linked units, built the html way: a unit package OUTSIDE lib (its own dir with spiral.toml/Cargo.toml) whose entry file opens the flat lib modules and exports concrete entry points. html_table/unit.spi does exactly this over lib doc_table, so lib's files don't move and lib stays flat. Per backend, as decided on TEMPLATE-TYPED-UNITS-1 note 12: on Rust one crate per leaf unit (crates buy parallelism and caching); on Gleam the leaf modules join the consumer's single gleam package (no build cost). RULE, fixed before measuring: start with ONE leaf, markdown (consumers: html, blog, Cells). Adopt it org-wide only if, against an A/A baseline, at least 2 consumers' generated lines drop >= 15% OR a consumer clears the 1000-line gate it fails today, with no consumer's wall build time worse by more than 5%. Otherwise keep re-specializing and record why. Gap (a) is independent and should proceed: UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH's per-definition used set (a lib edit invalidates only consumers that reach the edited definitions). Owners: microblog runs the markdown trial (Cells is a consumer, and the Cells port is microblog's), the generated-line census comes from rig's crate gate, and RIG-UNITS-CHECK validates the leaf unit's edges. Next step: pick the consumer set and record the A/A baseline (generated lines + wall time) on the node in a quiet window.")
inl note_of_manual_units_everywhere_6 () = Note ("MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE", 6u32, "2026-11-08T16:41Z", "microblog", "REVISED RATING 11-08 (user priorities: self-hosting bench vs upstream and HOPACSOTA on F#, newest .NET previews and NativeAOT, Rust nightly, Lean with the zeta/quasi-RH code, TS as cheap typefeatureset probes, skepticism on C). New rubric, max 1000: typed linkage at its best 250; parallel unit builds with the newest toolchain (previews, nightly) 250; generated manifest 100; diff locality 100; strategic need 300 (self-host bench, deploys, proofs, type oracle, real lanes; replaces raw cell counts). Rust 970 (250/240 nightly -Zthreads plus Cargo/100/100/280 kit, client, deploys); F# 950 (250/220 .NET preview ParallelCompilation plus MSBuild -m, AOT arm/100/100/280 self-host bench on the same runtime as upstream and HOPACSOTA); Gleam 810 (240/120/100/100/250 the live serve, OTP); Lean 790 (220/200 lake parallel modules/100/100/170 zeta proofs, quasi-RH parity); TypeScript 770 (240/210 TS 7 multithreaded/100/100/120 cheap oracle); C 710 (180 ABI risk: refcounts across TUs, silent layout UB/250/100/100/80 tibia sweeps only); Zig 670 (210/200 build-obj per unit/100/100/60 x client cells, zig cc for C); CUDA 620 (200/180/100/100/40, TYPED-TRANSFORMER-LAB); Lua 560 (180/100/60/100/120 tibia game edge); Pascal 450; Python 400; WGSL 200. Order of work that follows: Rust nightly (RUST-NIGHTLY-EVERYWHERE), F# (LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP), Gleam manual units (X-GLEAM-MANUAL-SPLIT), Lean via ZETA-FINAL, TS probes, then C and Zig.")
inl lib_leaf_markdown_trial () = Task ("LIB-LEAF-MARKDOWN-TRIAL", "Trial ONE precompiled linked lib leaf: markdown as a unit package outside lib (html_table/unit.spi over doc_table is the model) that opens the flat lib modules and exports concrete entry points; consumers html, blog and Cells link it (Rust: a crate per leaf; Gleam: a module in the consumer's one package)", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "-", "team:microblog,lib,linked-units,markdown,bench", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_leaf_markdown_trial () = Planning ("LIB-LEAF-MARKDOWN-TRIAL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_leaf_markdown_trial_1 () = Note ("LIB-LEAF-MARKDOWN-TRIAL", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:25Z", "microblog", "Ruled by i574n 11-08 (MANUAL-UNITS-EVERYWHERE): lib stays one flat source package; lib files do not move. PRE-STATED RULE, written before any run: adopt precompiled leaf units org-wide only if, against an A/A baseline, at least 2 of the 3 consumers drop generated lines by at least 15%, OR a consumer clears the 1000-line gate, with no wall-time regression over 5%. Otherwise the leaf stays plain lib source and the result is recorded. Gap (a), per-definition invalidation, proceeds separately in UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH. Node baseline booked through i574n. Light prep, split per rig's advice: (1) measure today's generated lines of html, blog and Cells markdown consumers (no node); (2) draft the unit package and its exports; (3) then the node A/A plus trial run. NEXT STEP: step (1).")
inl note_of_lib_leaf_markdown_trial_2 () = Note ("LIB-LEAF-MARKDOWN-TRIAL", 2u32, "2026-11-09T13:21Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 09:4x microblog, STEP (1) DONE (no node; reads of today's generated files). Method: the 140 top-level names defined in lib doc_blocks, doc_inline, doc_parse, doc_render, doc_table, doc_text and markup; each generated fn's lines counted when its base name (the _N suffix stripped) is one of them. That is an upper bound, since generic names such as walk or collect can collide. RESULTS. BLOG: NOT A CONSUMER. No blog .spi opens doc_* or mentions markdown (engine/html.spi renders its own HTML), so the rule's 3 consumers are really 2. HTML: markdown is ALREADY split into units. html_inline/inline.rs 596 lines (89.4% markdown), html_parse/parse.rs 857 (82.6%), html_render/render.rs 509 (88.8%), html_model 585 (23.1%), html_pages 946 (14.1%), html_walk 349 (3.2%), cli 748 (0%), plus a markdown/entry.rs unit (12). Every unit is under 1000 lines, so there is no gate to clear; a lib leaf would MOVE these lines, not remove them. CELLS: domain.gleam 37,002 lines, of which markdown-named fns are 131 fns / 1,761 lines = 4.8% at most, so linking a leaf saves at most 4.8%, under the 15% bar. The gate problem in Cells is inliners (spi_walk 1966 etc., see CELLS-TYPED-UNITS n3-n5), not markdown. VERDICT against the PRE-STATED RULE (2 of 3 consumers drop >=15%, or a consumer clears the gate): it cannot pass. At most 0 of 2 real consumers clear 15% (html moves lines, Cells caps at 4.8%), and neither has a markdown-caused gate breach. Recommendation: skip the node A/A plus trial (no CPU for a foregone result); the leaf stays plain lib source, as the rule says when it fails. needs org's ruling to close (i574n ruled the trial). proof: step 1 measurement pass.")
inl note_of_lib_leaf_markdown_trial_3 () = Note ("LIB-LEAF-MARKDOWN-TRIAL", 3u32, "2026-11-09T13:24Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "proof: 11-09 09:3x org RULING: skip the node A/A, close as hypothesis-disproven; the leaf stays plain lib source. The pre-stated 15% bar is the control, and note 2's measurement already decides it: 0 of 2 real consumers can reach it (blog is not a consumer; html holds markdown in units under 1000 lines, so a leaf moves lines rather than removing them; Cells caps at 4.8%), and there is no markdown-caused gate breach. Running the A/A would spend a node slot on a foregone null. The real Cells gate pressure is the inliners, pursued under CELLS-TYPED-UNITS (join-point A/B staged on moon). Result recorded; closed. pass.")
inl linked_units_fsharp () = Task ("LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP", "F# linked units ahead of C and Zig (user 11-08): one assembly per unit, generated .fsi signature files as the unit interface, generated .fsproj with the newest .NET preview flags (ParallelCompilation, graph-based checking, LangVersion preview), MSBuild -m across units, and a NativeAOT arm that records AOT limitations", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:compiler,compiler,linked-units,fsharp,dotnet,parallel,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_linked_units_fsharp () = Planning ("LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP", Minutes 600u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_linked_units_fsharp_1 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "Why F# first: after SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL, the self-hosted compiler is benchmarked against upstream Spiral and HOPACSOTA, both F# on .NET; an F# backend puts all three on the same runtime, JIT and GC, so the comparison is apples to apples. Facts 11-08: F# 10 ParallelCompilation (graph-based checking plus parallel optimization and IL generation) is still preview, and dotnet/fsharp issue 19935 reports the SDK property never reaching the compiler in SDK-style builds (verify on the newest preview, pass the flags directly if needed). .fsi signatures play the C header role, and their hash serves UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH. NativeAOT arm: list every limitation hit (reflection-based printf %A, FSharp.Core reflection, trimming warnings) and link COMPILER-CI-AOT. DoD: the linked matrix gains F# rows that build unit by unit with MSBuild -m; byte-identical program output vs single-assembly; build wall time with an A/A arm; AOT limitations listed.")
inl note_of_linked_units_fsharp_2 () = Note ("LINKED-UNITS-FSHARP", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:41Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. FIT: right priority. All three compilers (upstream, HOPACSOTA, self-hosted) run on .NET, so F# units put the comparison on one runtime. FACTS AND RISKS:\n(1) Starting point: no F# extern-call emission exists (only Rust, Gleam and Zig rows in the ExternCalls table, spiral_compiler.fs ~36770). F# needs its own Call row plus records/unions crossing as NOMINAL types defined once in the model assembly (an F# record or union is assembly-qualified, so a re-emitted copy is a distinct type: the Rust E0308 problem, which F# reports as a type error, good).\n(2) Early cutoff comes nearly free on .NET: MSBuild reference assemblies (ProduceReferenceAssembly) skip dependents' recompiles when the public surface didn't change. That's UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH's interface_hash, built in. VERIFY: F# cross-assembly 'inline' bodies travel as optimization data. If an inline body change doesn't change the ref assembly, dependents keep stale inlined code. So test 'edit an inline function in the model, does the importer rebuild?' before trusting it. M2's inlined facade is Spiral-level inlining, so the used-set caveat still applies.\n(3) Parallelism has two layers: MSBuild -m across unit projects, and within a project the F# ParallelCompilation property (graph-based type checking + parallel optimization/IL gen). The .fsi signature files are what let graph checking skip implementation dependencies, so generate them. The SDK bug dotnet/fsharp#19935 is cited by the filer; pin the SDK version in the result table.\n(4) NativeAOT arm: expect trimming/AOT warnings from FSharp.Core reflection (sprintf %A, structural printing, Reflection.FSharpType). Record every IL2xxx/IL3xxx warning class as the 'limitations hit' list (links COMPILER-CI-AOT).\nDoD suggestion: linked_* rows on the F# backend as one assembly per unit, run equal to the single-unit build (split-invariance oracle), the inline-edit rebuild test from (2), an -m speedup table with an A/A arm, and the AOT limitations list.")
inl self_hosted_bench () = Task ("SELF-HOSTED-BENCH", "Three-way compiler benchmark after SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL: upstream Spiral vs HOPACSOTA vs self-hosted Spiral (F#/.NET arm with the newest previews and a NativeAOT arm, plus a Rust nightly arm), on the synthetic scale corpus and the real corpora", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "SELF-HOSTED-SPIRAL,SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", "team:compiler,compiler,bench,self-hosting,hopacsota,fsharp,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_self_hosted_bench () = Planning ("SELF-HOSTED-BENCH", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_self_hosted_bench_1 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: we will bench against upstream and hopacsota after finishing rewriting spiral in spiral. Arms: upstream (mrakgr), HOPACSOTA, self-hosted on F# (.NET newest preview, ParallelCompilation, NativeAOT), self-hosted on Rust (nightly, -Zthreads parallel front end). Report per arm the fitted scaling curve from SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE (a, b per phase), wall, GC share, peak memory, outputs byte-identical across arms. Quiet node windows, rotated arms, A/A first.")
inl note_of_self_hosted_bench_2 () = Note ("SELF-HOSTED-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:41Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. Pre-register before any timing: the corpus (SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE's seeded corpus plus the mega roots), cold vs warm (--server-tcp) arms, an A/A arm per compiler, rotated order, a quiet node with a precheck (the Gleam bench's gsb-node-cmd.ps1 pattern). EQUALITY GATE first: all compared compilers must produce byte-equal output (or residual hashes) on every corpus row before any time counts, since speed on different output is not a comparison. STATUS NOTE: HOPACSOTA is Planned at 25% (no engine yet), so until it exists the arm is single-flight upstream vs the self-hosted compiler. rustc -Zthreads parallelizes the rustc FRONT END, which matters only for the Rust-hosted arm's own build, not for Spiral's compile; keep the two clearly labeled.")
inl scale_corpus_curve () = Task ("SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", "Deterministic million-line benchmarks: a seeded synthetic Spiral corpus generator at geometric sizes (10k to 3.16M lines) with controlled shape (fan-out, chain depth, so W/S is set), a closed-form power-law fit per phase, and the optimal unit size derived from the fit", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "LARGE-INPUT-SCALING", "team:compiler,compiler,bench,scaling,linked-units,groom", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_scale_corpus_curve () = Planning ("SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", Minutes 360u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_scale_corpus_curve_1 () = Note ("SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: benchs simulating millions of lines of code; find the best number deterministically with a curve formula. Builds on LARGE-INPUT-SCALING (real inputs to 16.8 MB, exponents tokenize ~0.6, format up to 1.13) and HOPACSOTA R3 (W/S 2.80 for apps/spiral, 1.00 for mega roots). METHOD: (1) seeded generator, so a size and shape always give the same bytes; sizes 10^4, 10^4.5 ... 10^6.5 lines; shape knobs: fan-out and chain depth, which fix the work/span ratio W/S. (2) Per phase, fit t(n) = a n^b by least squares on log t vs log n (closed form, deterministic); report b with the A/A spread. (3) THE BEST NUMBER: a codebase of N lines cut into units of s lines costs T(s) = (N/s)(c0 + a s^b) / min(P, W/S), where c0 is the fixed per-unit cost (process start, per-package BEAM step, link) and P the cores. For b > 1, dT/ds = 0 gives s* = (c0 / (a (b - 1)))^(1/b); for b <= 1 there is no interior optimum and units should be as large as the other constraints allow (the 1000-line gate, diff locality). Compare s* per backend with the 1000-line gate and report whether the gate is near the optimum. DoD: curves per backend and phase, s* per backend, all reproducible from the seed.")
inl note_of_scale_corpus_curve_2 () = Note ("SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:41Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. The formula is right for SERIAL total time: N lines in N/s units, per-unit cost c0 + a*s^b, so T(s) = (N/s)(c0 + a*s^b). dT/ds = 0 gives c0 = a(b-1)s^b, so s* = (c0/(a(b-1)))^(1/b), an interior minimum only when b > 1. WHAT THE DATA SAYS SO FAR (LARGE-INPUT-SCALING, europa 11-07): check exponents 0.23-0.89, tokenize 0.45-0.82, format up to 1.13; no phase above 1.13. With b <= 1 there is NO interior optimum (bigger units are never slower in total), and at b = 1.13 s* is enormous next to 1000 lines unless c0 is large. So the 1000-line gate is a readability/diff-locality rule, not a compile-time optimum. Say so in the result rather than letting the formula imply otherwise. TWO CORRECTIONS for the model: (1) with P parallel workers wall time is ~max(T/P, critical path), which pushes s* DOWN until the unit graph's width saturates P, so fit it with the unit graph's W/S, not just n; (2) peval is the unmeasured phase (fiscal's 'hours' builds and 57k-line units are the likely b > 1 case), so the corpus knobs must drive peval work (specializations, join points), not just line count. c0 per backend is measured: ~0.14 s per Gleam package (gleam bench), ~2.7 s dotnet cold start, ~0 for a warm server.")
inl note_of_scale_corpus_curve_3 () = Note ("SCALE-CORPUS-CURVE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T16:43Z", "microblog", "Compiler groom accepted (n2): measured exponents are 0.23-1.13, so the serial s* is far above 1000; the 1000-line gate is a READABILITY rule (review, cells, diff size), not a compile-time optimum, and the report must say so. Additions: model wall time with P workers (pushes s* down), and corpus knobs that drive PEVAL, the unmeasured phase where fiscal's hours likely sit.")
inl ts_typefeatureset_probes () = Task ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", "Cheap typefeatureset battle tests on the TypeScript backend: emit the typefeatureset samples (unions, records, GADT facades, phantom types) as TS types and let tsc 7 check them, as a fast second type-checker oracle", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,typescript,typefeatureset,bench", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes () = Planning ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_1 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:40Z", "microblog", "User 11-08: even for ts we could find use cases, maybe cheap benchmark experiments to battletest the typefeatureset. TS 7 (native Go compiler, shared-memory multithreading, stable since mid 2026) type-checks fast, so a TS emission of each typefeatureset sample is a cheap independent oracle: a sample that is well typed in Spiral but rejected by tsc (or the reverse) is a finding. Also time tsc -b with project references per unit as a cheap parallel-build data point.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_2 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:41Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler groom. A useful second oracle ONLY for the subset that translates mechanically. TS types are structural and its conditional/mapped types are expressive, but GADT index refinement has no direct TS encoding (discriminated unions plus conditional types approximate it), so a probe that needs a hand encoding tests the encoding, not Spiral. RULES: (1) each probe pairs a positive with a NEGATIVE that must fail in BOTH checkers (tsc via '// @ts-expect-error'; Spiral via the negative row), so a translation that accepts everything can't pass; (2) the TS emission is generated, never hand-written; (3) start with records, unions, generics and phantom types, then GADTs only through one fixed encoding, documented once. tsc 7 is the Go-native compiler; pin its exact version in the result.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_3 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 3u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "compiler", "11-09 03:0x compiler: FEASIBILITY + STEP 1. The TypeScript BACKEND emits partially-evaluated, monomorphic code: generics are specialized and a program Spiral rejects emits nothing, so tsc over backend output tests CODEGEN soundness, not the typefeatureset. Hence two tracks. Track A (cheap, runnable now): every one of the 454 tracked samples' main.ts through tsc 7 native (tsgo, version pinned in the result) --strict --noEmit, one file at a time (scripts share global scope). Any error is a TS-backend bug or an unsound emission. QUEUED on moon (bun there; ganymede has no node/bun): job-1794205739373-compiler-ts-codegen-oracle after the load-all job. Track B (the typefeatureset oracle the user asked for): a GENERATED type-level translation of Spiral declarations, not backend output. Records become object types, unions become discriminated unions, generics become TS generics, phantom parameters become branded types, and GADTs go through one fixed encoding documented once. Each probe pairs a positive with a negative that both Spiral (negative row) and tsc (// @ts-expect-error) must reject. Seed set: the 40 samples matching gadt/phantom/existential/prototype/hkt (e.g. nominal_phantom_parameter, gadt_implicit_existential_negative, gadt_unbounded_index_negative, contract_hkt). Next step: read Track A; then design Track B's generator as a --backend TypeScriptTypes declarations-only mode.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_4 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 4u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "compiler", "11-09 03:0x compiler: DoD: (1) Track A result: tsgo (pinned version) over all emitted main.ts with per-error-code counts, every error explained or filed as a TS-backend item; (2) Track B: at least 10 generated positive/negative probe pairs over records, unions, generics, phantom and one GADT encoding, each negative rejected by BOTH Spiral and tsc, plus one tsc -b timing with project references per unit. TypeFeatures: phantom, gadt, existential (as probe subjects; the probes themselves use no new compiler features). Next step: read Track A on job-1794205739373.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_5 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 5u32, "2026-11-09T06:40Z", "compiler", "11-09 02:4x compiler TRACK B DESIGN: generate TS DECLARATIONS from Spiral's own declarations, not from backend output. Source of truth: the Bundle stage (BundleType / BundleNominal / BundleNominalRec carry HoVar params + RawTExpr bodies; RawTUnion marks is_gadt per case), the same hook the feature census uses (FeatureCensusBundles walks every module's bundles after parse). A --types-out <file> flag (beside --features-out) maps: record {a : t} -> { a: T }; union (non-GADT) -> discriminated union { tag: 'Case', v0: ... }; type params -> TS generics; a phantom param p -> a brand field readonly __p?: P so the param stays nominal; GADT cases -> ONE fixed encoding (each case a separate interface with its index as a literal type parameter, the union type a conditional type over the index), documented once in the item. Probes: for each seed sample, the generator emits the positive declarations plus usage lines extracted from a small companion .probe.ts that tests author once per sample (positive uses + one // @ts-expect-error negative); the pair passes only if Spiral rejects its negative row AND tsc flags the expect-error line. Not built yet. Next step after Track A's numbers: implement --types-out for records/unions/generics/phantom first (no GADT), 10 probe pairs, tsgo pinned.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_6 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 6u32, "2026-11-09T07:42Z", "compiler", "11-09 07:3x compiler TRACK B v1 WORKS. Draft %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/drafts/spiral/TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES/1-types-out.kitdo (over pack67 = pack68 candidate, core 7fb02d7f host 79070d91, built + run on ganymede): --types-out <file> (SPIRAL_TYPES_OUT) collects every module bundler (package loader AND the supervisor FileOpen path -- project modules never pass loader_package; the feature census has the same blind spot, so its 11-08 counts are packages-only) and renders at process exit one `declare namespace M_<stem>` per module. Mapping: records -> readonly object types; non-GADT unions -> tag/v0 discriminated unions; params -> P_<name> generics; nominals branded `__nominal?: \"M_x.name\"`; unused (phantom) params branded `__phantom_<p>?: P_<p>`; i64/u64 -> bigint; array t -> T[]; cross-module refs resolve when the name is unique, else unknown; GADT unions, forall, exists, macros, HKT heads -> unknown (a negative probe over unknown fails as an unused @ts-expect-error, so unknown can never pass a pair). Seed probe (records, union payloads, generics, nominal meters/feet, phantom quantity unit, pair, bigint) under TypeScript 7.0.2 native (npm @typescript/typescript-linux-x64, sha512-verified, ~/.cache/tsc-7.0.2 on ganymede) --strict --noEmit: tsc exit 0, all 10 @ts-expect-error negatives flagged, positives clean. Next: Spiral-side negatives for the same 10 (rule 1: both checkers must reject), 10+ pairs as samples, then the GADT encoding.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_7 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 7u32, "2026-11-09T07:46Z", "compiler", "CORRECTION + RULE 1 MET (11-09 07:5x): the seed probe has 9 @ts-expect-error negatives, not 10. All 9 have Spiral-side twins (pack68 candidate on ganymede, each a separate main.spi over the same declarations, --backend Fsharp), and every one is rejected: missing_field ETY-RecordIndexFailed, wrong_case ETY-UnboundVariable, wrong_payload / cell_wrong / meters_as_feet / phantom_unit / just_wrong / pair_swapped / size_not_i32 ETY-TermError unification failure; the positive twin compiles. So 9/9 pairs are rejected by BOTH checkers. Next: land these as samples (one positive + negatives) with a runner row that executes tsc 7.0.2 over --types-out, then add 1+ pair and the GADT encoding.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_8 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 8u32, "2026-11-09T08:39Z", "compiler", "11-09 08:4x compiler: Track B LANDED as a suite (unstaged): scripts/test.ps1 -Suite tstypes (exclusive like families/linked) over tmp/tests/tstypes/<case>/{main.spi, package.spiproj, probe.ts, negatives/<name>/{main.spi,package.spiproj}}; env.ps1 Get-SpiralNativeTools gains Tsc (SPIRAL_TSC or <spiral cache>/toolchains/tsc/lib/tsc[.exe]). A case PASSes only when: main.spi compiles with --types-out, tsc --strict --noEmit over types.d.ts + probe.ts exits 0 (so every // @ts-expect-error is used), the expect-error count EQUALS the Spiral negative count (> 0), and every negative fails with an ETY- error located in its OWN file (a sibling error cannot count). First case records_unions_phantom (9 pairs) PASS on ganymede with pack70 + tsc 7.0.2 (ganymede tsc now at ~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/tsc). Requires a compiler with --types-out (pack68+). Next: more cases (generic nesting, cross-module refs, recursive union), the GADT encoding, then a tsc -b project-references timing.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_9 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 9u32, "2026-11-09T08:43Z", "compiler", "11-09 08:4x compiler: tstypes HARDENED after a real false pass. 3 Spiral negatives were rejected for the WRONG reason (samples core has no |>, so `{..} |> cell` failed as \"Unbound term variable: |>\" instead of a type mismatch) and the suite still said PASS. Fix: negatives use direct constructor application, and every negative must carry negatives/<name>/expect (its intended error text); the case passes only when the log contains it (missing expect = fail). Mutation-checked: a wrong expect on chain_bad turns the case FAIL (exit 1). Second case recursive_nested_generics (recursive generic tree t, labelled (tree string), recursive chain; 4 pairs). Now 2 cases / 13 pairs, all PASS on pack70 with every rejection for its intended reason.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_10 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 10u32, "2026-11-09T09:27Z", "compiler", "11-09 09:2x compiler: GADT ENCODING WORKS (draft 2-gadt-encoding.kitdo over 1-types-out; pack72 = pack70 + it, core 45dbe76b). Fixed encoding, documented once here: a GADT union renders as a union of per-case conditional types `([P_i1, ..] extends [pattern] ? { tag; v0? } : never)`, where the pattern is the case result types index arguments and every \\* v. binder of the case becomes `infer V_v` (first occurrence) / `V_v` after, so payloads can mention the inferred index; non-GADT cases in a mixed union stay unconditional. Unit-bodied nominals (`:! yes = ()`) now render as a REQUIRED brand `{ readonly __nominal: \"M.yes\" }`, because `null & {..}` collapses to never in TS and would make every index tag equal. Bug found + fixed on the way: .NET culture-sensitive String.IndexOf ignores \\u0001/\\u0002 marker chars (zero-weight) and matched at the wrong span; now StringComparison.Ordinal. Case gadt_index_evidence (closed index evidence ev yes/no + forall-indexed tree n, 3 pairs) PASS; suite now 3 cases / 16 pairs, all PASS, every rejection for its intended reason.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_11 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 11u32, "2026-11-09T10:12Z", "compiler", "--types-out (+GADT) rides pack73b: installed on europa; the tstypes suite runs on any host with tsc 7 at <spiral cache>/toolchains/tsc or SPIRAL_TSC.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_12 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 12u32, "2026-11-09T11:45Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:4x compiler: TRACK A DONE (DoD 1). tsc 7.0.2 native --strict --noEmit --target es2022 over all 446 tracked samples/*/main.ts, one file at a time on ganymede, with @types/node 26.6.6 + undici-types 8.9.0 (both sha512-verified from npm, at <spiral cache>/toolchains/types-node): 445/446 CLEAN. Without node types every file fails TS2591 (process/require unknown) and 49 also TS2534 (functions ending in process.exit not typed never) -- environment, not codegen. The ONE real finding: ebpf_bool main.ts(34) TS2367 \"types true and false have no overlap\" on `v0 === v1`, where the backend emits `const v0: boolean = true;` / `const v1: boolean = false;` for runtime (~) bools and TS control-flow-narrows each const to its literal type. Semantics are right (the comparison runs, yields false); it is a strictness false positive from the emission style. Fix candidate (TS backend): emit boolean literals bound to typed consts as `true as boolean` (no narrowing), or `let`. No unsound emission found in 446 samples.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_13 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 13u32, "2026-11-09T11:55Z", "compiler", "FIX for the Track A finding (draft 3-bool-literal-no-narrowing.kitdo over pack73b; typeScriptConstLocals text pass): `(let|const) vN: boolean = true|false;` is emitted as `... = true as boolean;`, so TS no longer narrows the const to its literal type. ganymede: ebpf_bool main.ts now passes tsc 7 --strict (exit 0); -Suite all -Backend TypeScript vs pack73: 454 TS rows, compile-status diffs 0, residual diffs 89 (exactly the samples with boolean-literal locals; the only change is the added `as boolean`). With it Track A is 446/446 clean. Rides the next pack.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_14 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 14u32, "2026-11-09T12:47Z", "compiler", "11-09 12:4x compiler: REAL-CODE run of --types-out on apps/spiral/spiral.spi (188 lib namespaces, 1,237 lines) found 3 naming bugs the seed probes could not: (1) TS reserved words as type names (leptos `for`, rust `try`); (2) collisions after sanitizing (`infer` escaped to infer_ and `infer` -> infer_); (3) arity mismatch: Spiral macro types like `guid` take arguments although declared without params, so `M_guid.guid<\"guid\">` is TS2315, and bare references to generic types are TS2314. FIX draft 4-reserved-word-names.kitdo (over pack75): ident maps `` to _q and reserved words to <name>_kw (collision-free), render builds an arity map per space.name, and any reference whose argument count disagrees with its declaration renders as unknown (fail closed). Result: the 188-namespace monolith type-checks with 0 errors (was 9, then 13 with only the reserved fix); tstypes 3/3 still PASS. TSC -B TIMING (DoD 2 last part): same declarations split into 188 composite projects with 45 reference edges (acyclic): monolithic tsc 0.22-0.28 s; tsc -b cold 11.66 s; tsc -b no-change 0.18 s; tsc -b after one leaf edit 0.27 s. Verdict: at this size per-project overhead dominates (45x cold) and incremental equals the monolith, so project references buy nothing for declaration checking; keep one file per run.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_15 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 15u32, "2026-11-09T13:14Z", "compiler", "pack76 = pack75 + 4-reserved-word-names.kitdo GATED on ganymede (13:00Z): -Suite all vs pack75, 7610 rows, compile-status diffs 0, residual diffs 0 (the change only affects --types-out). Rides the next fleet pack (pack75 stays the current rollout so Linux and Windows match). Wait: item next pack after pack75 Windows install.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_16 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 16u32, "2026-11-09T13:27Z", "compiler", "Toolchain note (11-09 13:2x): ganymede now has the TypeScript runtime as well as the checker. bun 1.4.4 at ~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/bun/bun-linux-x64/bun (official release zip, SHA-256 checked against SHASUMS256.txt); tsc 7.0.2 at ~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/tsc/lib/tsc; @types/node 26.6.6 + undici-types 8.9.0 at ~/.cache/spiral-bin/toolchains/types-node. TypeScript natives and Cells TS tests no longer need moon (SPIRAL_BUN or PATH).")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_17 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 17u32, "2026-11-10T10:37Z", "compiler", "11-10 06:3x compiler (groom with fiscal; rig mapped TRAIN42e to TRAIN43f \"or clear if no train is needed\"): CLEARED, not re-pointed. This item never needed a rig train: the wait stood for its pack content reaching the Windows hosts, and pack78 (containing pack67-pack77) is installed on all four nodes (box/moon Core f4148639; Linux now pack82 Core c495bc57). ganymede also has bun + tsc now, so TS probes run there. Next step: run the TS type probes on ganymede''s installed pack82 and close.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_18 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 18u32, "2026-11-10T10:51Z", "compiler", "11-10 06:5x compiler: Track B re-run on ganymedes INSTALLED pack83 (Core 2c6efed9, tsc 7.0.2): -Suite tstypes 3 cases, 16 negatives (gadt_index_evidence 3, records_unions_phantom 9, recursive_nested_generics 4), all PASS, 0 failed, 29 s; every negative is rejected by both Spiral and tsc for its stated expect text (the 11-09 hardening). DoD (2) needs >= 10 pairs: 16. REMAINING for DoD: (a) one tsc -b timing with project references per unit; (b) confirm the ebpf_bool TS2367 finding from Track A is filed as a TS-backend item. Next step: (b) first (a backlog search), then (a) on ganymede.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_19 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 19u32, "2026-11-10T10:53Z", "compiler", "11-10 07:0x compiler: DONE. (b) the ebpf_bool TS2367 finding was FIXED, not filed: the TS backend adds as boolean to boolean-literal locals (TS rows vs pack73: 454, 0 compile-status diffs, 89 residuals = exactly those samples), making Track A 446/446 clean; in every pack since pack78. (a) tsc -b with project references per unit, ganymede installed pack83 + tsc 7.0.2: each tstypes cases --types-out file (934 / 1,409 / 666 bytes) as a composite project, a root tsconfig referencing the 3: cold tsc -b exit 0 in 1,056 ms (3 .d.ts emitted), warm no-change rebuild exit 0 in 20 ms (references skip up-to-date units). Scratch at ganymede /root/compiler-tsb.")
inl note_of_ts_typefeatureset_probes_20 () = Note ("TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES", 20u32, "2026-11-10T10:55Z", "compiler", "Evidence: Track A 446/446 tsc-clean (11-09 notes); Track B -Suite tstypes on installed pack83: 3 cases, 16 negatives, all PASS (29 s); tsc -b per-unit references: cold 1,056 ms, warm 20 ms, exit 0.")
inl node_monitoring_robustness () = Task ("NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", "Make native fleet monitoring robust enough to safely test on new/restricted nodes: cover ALL nodes (not just box/wifi/europa), pair every alarm with a known HEAL, and track dangling shells/orphans across the relay chain", Epic, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "infra,monitoring,node,healing,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_node_monitoring_robustness () = Planning ("NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", Minutes 480u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_node_monitoring_robustness_1 () = Note ("NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:46Z", "management", "User 11-08 priority OVER fanning out heavy work to Jupiter (tooling not ready). Goal: make native monitoring robust so when we test things on the big/restricted nodes we KNOW HOW TO HEAL. Three gaps: (1) COVERAGE - the work lab (work-hub,244,245,232,work-host) + rustdesk + codespaces are unmonitored; node-utilization-monitor only watches box/wifi/europa - extend health probes (cpu/mem/disk/dangling) over the relay chain. (2) DETECT->HEAL - alarms detect but mostly do not heal (alarm != delivers); build a per-node per-failure-mode heal catalog seeded from what exists (display-res-enforce, erl-mem-guard, EUROPA-DISK-HEAL, node-route-cache). (3) SELF-ROBUSTNESS + ORPHAN TRACKING - harden probes vs false-alarm/hang/miss (wip-hang, probe flakiness, the remote-machines.json consumer-miss), and specifically monitor dangling ssh/shells/stale procs across the 5-hop relay, since no-dangling is the gate for Jupiter. Prerequisite before ANY fan-out to the work lab.")
inl note_of_node_monitoring_robustness_2 () = Note ("NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T16:54Z", "management", "GROOM 11-08 (user: rely more on self-healing than dangling scripts, so rig gets robust): the heal-catalog healers must be rig-native SELF-HEALING (self-reap / self-verify-teardown / self-report) wherever possible, NOT more external .ps1 watchdogs; over time CONSOLIDATE the existing dangling monitors (node-utilization-monitor, idle-alarm, lane-reconcile, heartbeat-guard, display-res-enforce, erl-mem-guard, profile-mirror-monitor) into rig primitives and retire the scripts. Gap-3 orphan tracking sharpens for Jupiter into JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD. See self-heal-over-dangling-scripts, RIG-SELF-HEALING.")
inl note_of_node_monitoring_robustness_3 () = Note ("NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", 3u32, "2026-11-09T11:45Z", "org3", "org3 11-09 SPLIT (gate: 480m never fits a round; AGILE-STALL-1109 pool hygiene). Decomposed along the 3 gaps in the 11-08 groom into 4 ready <=120m children (gap 3 splits into probe-robustness + orphan-tracking, which are distinct): NMR-COVERAGE (probes beyond box/wifi/europa -> work-lab+rustdesk+codespaces), NMR-HEAL-CATALOG (per-node per-failure heal catalog, rig-native self-healing not .ps1), NMR-PROBE-ROBUST (harden vs false-alarm/hang/miss + age-checked status reads), NMR-ORPHAN-TRACK (dangling ssh/shells/stale-procs across the 5-hop relay; JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD gate). This Epic now tracks the 4; each is a fittable light item. (org3 already exercised NMR-ORPHAN-TRACK live this round: cleaned 4 self-matching-pgrep waiters on europa + a 3-day hung rustdesk --help on moon.)")
inl rig_self_healing () = Task ("RIG-SELF-HEALING", "Rig robustness via intrinsic SELF-HEALING: rig jobs/delegation self-reap on timeout/exit, self-verify clean teardown (no orphan proc/ssh/stage), self-report health - retiring the external watchdog-script pile into rig-native primitives", Epic, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", "rig,robustness,self-healing,infra,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rig_self_healing () = Planning ("RIG-SELF-HEALING", Minutes 1200u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_rig_self_healing_1 () = Note ("RIG-SELF-HEALING", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:54Z", "management", "User 11-08: groom the backlog so RIG gets robust; rely more on self-healing than dangling scripts (memory self-heal-over-dangling-scripts). Today robustness is a pile of external .ps1 scheduled tasks that go stale / leave orphans / hang. Make it intrinsic to rig: every rig job guarantees its own teardown (no dangling proc/ssh/stage) and reports its own health without an external watchdog; the detect->heal catalog (NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS) lands as rig primitives. DoD: a rig job is self-reaping + self-verifying + self-reporting, and the orphan/teardown watchdogs are consolidated into rig, retiring those scripts. Robustness prerequisite before trusting rig to fan out to the work lab / Jupiter. Relates to the cannibalize-k3s-into-rig arc.")
inl jupiter_zero_proc_guard () = Task ("JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD", "Enforce ZERO fleet processes on Jupiter (work-host) outside explicit manual windows: at window-close and periodically, verify no fleet proc/ssh/job is active and reap any; window open/close is explicit", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "RIG-SELF-HEALING,NODE-MONITORING-ROBUSTNESS", "rig,node,jupiter,self-healing,guard,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_jupiter_zero_proc_guard () = Planning ("JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_jupiter_zero_proc_guard_1 () = Note ("JUPITER-ZERO-PROC-GUARD", 1u32, "2026-11-08T16:54Z", "management", "User 11-08: Jupiter cannot have active fleet processes outside our manual windows (stricter than no-dangling - it is a live work backend test server; anything of ours outside the agreed window interferes). A Jupiter job must fully terminate at window-close. Build as rig-native self-healing (self-reap at the window boundary), NOT an external watchdog. DoD: outside a declared manual window, Jupiter has ZERO fleet procs/ssh, verified across the 5-hop relay chain; any stray is reaped + reported. Hard gate for ever using Jupiter. See fleet-roster-placement-policy, self-heal-over-dangling-scripts.")
inl rent_rehearsal_encke () = Task ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", "Rehearsed 3 h rental of a Cloud bare-metal EPYC 9255 (24 cores / 48 threads, 384 GB DDR5, ewr) for every lane: a short idle baseline for metrics, then saturated compute packed from the lanes' run plans and past job history, all logged as the runbook for future CPU and GPU rentals", Story, P1, D4, 40u32, Paused, "", "team:tibia,tibia,rental,europa,gpu,rehearsal,cross-lane", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rent_rehearsal_encke () = Planning ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", Minutes 240u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_1 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T17:25Z", "tibia", "User 11-08 13:1x: \"let's see how far we get with the 9255, ask all the lanes for a run plan to leverage 3 hours of both idle (for metrics, but avoid) and saturated compute ... everything should be logged so we rehearse better for future rents, including gpu\"; tibia coordinates (it holds the most GPU items). Plan vbm-24c-384gb-amd5, $0.73/h, in stock 13:2x in ewr, fra, cdg (not ord, sjc, mil). BUDGET: about 3 h of compute plus provisioning and bootstrap; hard auto-destroy at start + 3 h 45 min, so at most about $2.75. LOG: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\rentals\\20261108-ewr-epyc9255 (events.tsv, jobs.tsv, cpu samples, job-history.csv). PRE-REGISTERED METRICS: (1) provision time, API create to ssh ready; (2) bootstrap time per toolchain; (3) idle baseline <= 10 min (load, a memory-bandwidth probe, single-core reference); (4) saturation: CPU busy pct sampled every 10 s, target mean >= 85 pct over the saturated window; (5) per job: lane, label, estimated vs actual minutes, exit, peak RSS, bytes in and out; (6) transfer times; (7) total cost and a destroy verified by a list call. DoD: instance destroyed and verified; every row logged; a per-lane estimate/actual ratio and a runbook memory written for the next rental (CPU or GPU). NEXT STEP: collect lane run plans (deadline 14:15), pack them, bootstrap script, rent. TYPEFEATURES: none (operations item).")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_2 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T17:29Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 13:3x rig plan sent to tibia: 2 saturate rows, no timing row. ROW 1 rig-rent-regen: full kit regen of the R3-respelled rig tree (rig-tree.tgz 4.8 MB sha256 37AA4714F1085D6E, the exact TRAIN41 tree; spiral-lib.tgz 18.8 MB 24CC45CBEAEB1C91 at spiral f6000cfa) with -Parallel 24 and cargo check, est 70 min, abort at 120, 10-min abort gate since kit regen never ran on Linux; outputs sha256 per crate as a cross-OS determinism oracle against TRAIN41 Windows regen. ROW 2 rig-rent-cargo-test: cargo test --workspace -j48 on the staged workspace, est 25 min, failures form the Linux portability list for REMOTE-CLOUD-LINUX-WORKER. Host: the rental joins kit as a job host via kit job start --on rent once management adds a remote-machines.json entry cloned from europa; rig smokes it at about 14:50.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_3 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T18:12Z", "tibia", "11-08 14:1x tibia: BLOCKER: Cloud-provider account gates; no machine ran, nothing billed (one orphan volume, see below). Attempts (events.tsv): (1) bare metal vbm-24c-384gb-amd5 with Ubuntu 26.04 and 26.10 -> \"This OS currently cannot be used with the selected plan\"; (2) with Ubuntu 24.04 -> \"This product requires a minimum deposit of $50\" (the $103 credit does not count); (3) user chose cloud vx1-g-48c-192g -> \"One or more block devices are required\" (vx1 boots from block storage), then with block_devices [{disk_size 400, bootable true}] -> \"You have reached the maximum monthly fee limit\"; (4) user chose to probe: 10 plans from $1,000/mo down to vhp-8c-16gb ($96/mo), all refused by the monthly fee limit (checked on the plan's MONTHLY list price; the existing VMs are ~$29/mo, so the limit is likely ~$100/mo). ORPHAN: the refused vx1 create had already made its 400 GB boot volume (1e74d4e5, ewr, $40/mo, unattached, created 14:06:30); found by a list call, deleted after an exact field match, blocks now 0. STAGED FOR THE RETRY: 36-row queue (queue-at-launch.tsv), 22 syntax-checked scripts in the tibia scratchpad rent/, every lane's pit-wall note applied, europa staging kept (/root/rental-compiler pinned to pack62: ping compiler before a retry on pack63; fiscal bundle 97067b81), temporary push key /root/rent-tmp/id on europa (no authorized_keys changed anywhere). Resume when: the user raises the account's monthly fee limit (Cloud-provider support), plus the $50 deposit if the 9255 bare metal is wanted. NEXT STEP: on resume, re-run the stock check and a create with the 9255 (os 2284 Ubuntu 24.04, newest copied-binary glibc need is 2.38), register the auto-destroy watchdog, then follow the staged sequence. GPU NOTE (Hale-Bopp): vcg-a16-12c-120g-32vram (A16, 32 GB VRAM, $0.942/h) now lists ewr; the same fee limit blocks it ($688/mo list).")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_4 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 4u32, "2026-11-08T18:37Z", "tibia", "11-08 14:4x tibia: READY FOR A SUDDEN MANUAL RENTAL (user 14:3x asked). USER STEPS: raise the account monthly fee limit (+ the $50 deposit for bare metal); create the server in ewr with Ubuntu 24.04 (26.x is not offered on vbm-24c-384gb-amd5), SSH key personal-vultr-2026-11-01, label encke; say go. The go also grants the delete of that one server after the results are pulled. RUNBOOK (scratchpad rent/go.ps1, phases logged to the rental events.tsv): adopt (finds the server labeled encke, writes state, registers the auto-destroy watchdog at +3 h 45) -> deltas (fresh box deltas, read-only git) -> upload -> apt -> baseline (bare idle before monitors, then monitors, then the monitored baseline) -> push (temporary cloud key added only to the rental, from=\"<europa-addr>\") -> setup (env, rustup, repos at box HEADs + deltas) -> prep (bitnet, dib install, fiscal unpack) -> quiet (CCD-pinned timing rows, then whole-machine rows) -> sched (MEMCAP = host RAM - 30 GB, THRCAP 120; rows above MEMCAP are skipped as never-admissible) -> collect (europa pulls every 20 min) -> finish (hold, teardown check, final pull, far-end check, delete both server kinds, list unattached volumes, unregister the watchdog). PROVEN without a rental: the watchdog fired ON SCHEDULE (14:21:53, result 0x0); remote commands travel base64-encoded (awk braces, quotes, globs, pipes, exit codes verified on europa); helpers renamed off the R alias (Invoke-History trap); deltas phase ran (20.5 MB, 16 s); 18 bash scripts syntax-checked; queue 37 rows. CHECK BEFORE GO: if pack63 is installed, ping compiler first (its inputs are pinned to pack62); europa staging present (/root/rental-compiler, fiscal 97067b81, /root/rent-tmp/id). FOLLOW-UP: STORE-RENTAL-PULL moves push and collect onto oort.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_5 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 5u32, "2026-11-08T19:30Z", "tibia", "11-08 15:2x tibia: PAUSED by the user (\"let's forget the cpu rental for now, we might go straight for gpu outside of europa later, good thing we settled on storage then\"). Kept for reuse: the runbook (scratchpad rent/go.ps1 + 18 scripts, queue now org-labelled), the watchdog (proven on schedule), adopt/destroy for both server kinds, the account-gate lessons (memory europa-rental-account-gates), the lane plans in the rental log. Released: europa staging for this window (compiler /root/rental-compiler, fiscal bundle, tibia /root/rent-tmp temporary key and scripts) may be deleted by their owners. Resume when: the user names a GPU or CPU rental again; a non-Cloud-provider provider needs its own adopt/destroy and account-gate check, and the $20 GPU cap / Europa-only permission must be re-confirmed for it.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_6 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T03:16Z", "tibia", "11-08 23:1x tibia: pause re-decided, stays Paused. Reason: the USER paused it on 11-08 (\"forget the cpu rental for now\") after the provider refused every create. The runbook, go.ps1 and manual-rental adopt path stay ready. Resume when: the user says go on a manual rental, or names another provider.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_7 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 7u32, "2026-11-09T21:02Z", "tibia", "11-09 17:0x tibia: pause re-decided, stays Paused. Reason: the user paused it on 11-08 (\"forget the cpu rental for now\") and has not reopened it; the runbook stays ready. Resume when: the user says go on a manual rental, or names another provider.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_8 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 8u32, "2026-11-10T07:47Z", "tibia", "11-10 03:5x tibia: pause re-decided, stays Paused. Reason: the user paused it on 11-08 (forget the CPU rental for now), and nothing since has reversed that. Resume when: the user reopens a CPU rental.")
inl note_of_rent_rehearsal_encke_9 () = Note ("RENT-REHEARSAL-ENCKE", 9u32, "2026-11-10T11:08Z", "tibia", "11-10 07:2x tibia: pause re-decided, stays Paused, its runbook FOLDED into the GPU work. The user (11-10) asked to revisit this CPU-only plan for the RunPod GPU rental: rehearse in k3s first, with minimal setup time, full parallelism, and results and metrics out before shutdown. The Encke phases (adopt, watchdog, deltas, upload, baseline, run, pull, destroy, verify gone) carry over into drafts\\tibia\\GPU-RUNPOD-K3S-REHEARSAL\\ (runner.sh, the same script in a k3s pod and a RunPod pod) under GPU-STACKED-RUNS. The CPU rental itself stays shelved (user 11-08). Resume when: the user reopens a CPU rental.")
inl pack_install_from_artifact () = Task ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", "install-pack copies the reviewed, content-addressed compiler DLL pair instead of rebuilding in place (232 s per box install), with a periodic full rebuild as the determinism check", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "STORE-PACK-CAS", "team:compiler,compiler,packs,cache,install,box-load", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pack_install_from_artifact () = Planning ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_1 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:24Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler, filed from tibia's remote-store question. FACT: install-pack.ps1 copies the reviewed sources into the live tree and REBUILDS the compiler in place (232.5 s on the box for pack62, often at 100% CPU), then compares the result with the reviewed candidate's DLLs. That comparison has come out equal=True on every pack (box; node, where pack62r rebuilt from the same sources was byte-identical; europa builds from source). So the rebuild only re-proves determinism. PROPOSAL: a content-addressed artifact store keyed by (core source sha, host source sha, SDK version) holding the reviewed DLL pair (13 MB per pack) plus its R2R images. Install = verify that the key matches the live sources after the programs apply (the current dry-run check), copy the pair, hash-verify, launch-check. LAN directory first (box + node), with an S3 cold copy as insurance against keep-newest-2 pruning (today's pack62 candidate was pruned on the node, costing an 82 s rebuild plus the hunt; the pack53c baseline only turned up in spiral-bin-candidates). Keep a periodic full rebuild as the determinism check (e.g. every 5th install or in CI), so the copy path never silently trusts a stale artifact. DoD: pack N installs on box and node by copy in under 30 s with equal=True; a forced rebuild of the same key reproduces the same hashes; the store survives candidate-dir pruning. TYPEFEATURES: the store's entry GADT (PackBin : core_sha * host_sha * sdk -> entry dll_pair, ...), keys derived by reflection; backends a closed union (LanDir | S3Bucket). NEXT STEP: measure install-pack's phases (apply, build, compare, launch-check) on the next install (pack63) to confirm the build is >90% of the time.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_2 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:38Z", "compiler", "Evidence path for the store: see STORE-PACK-CAS (11-08). pack62 pair in oort, round-trip equal=True. Install-by-copy needs a base layer (64 MB bin dir minus the per-pack pair) keyed by SDK + package lock + RID.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_3 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 3u32, "2026-11-09T03:18Z", "compiler", "11-08 23:5x compiler: install-pack.ps1 -FromStore (unstaged edit in spiral apps/compiler/scripts/install-pack.ps1): after the usual reproduce + in-use checks and backup, it copies the candidate sources to live and, instead of build.ps1, fetches oort compiler/base/v1/deps-<sha256 of the 4 fsproj>/sdk-<dotnet --version>/rid-<win-x64|linux-x64> plus compiler/pack/v1/core-<sha256 candidate core>/host-<sha256 candidate host>/sdk/rid. It verifies EVERY file against both manifests (a store miss or mismatch throws: build instead), copies them over the installed bin, then runs the existing launch check. Keys load from ~/.secrets/vultr-s3.env into the process only and are cleared in finally. PROOF on the box: -Name pack63c -FromStore -Apply = store hit, 31 files, launch True, the installed bin byte-identical before vs after, 154 s total including ~90 s of download; a rebuild is 232-566 s. The candidate dir only needs source/ (no cache) for this path. Next step: a Linux proof on ganymede (install-pack reads $env:LOCALAPPDATA, so give it a Linux default first) and pack-chain/install-when-clear passing -FromStore.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_4 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 4u32, "2026-11-09T03:22Z", "compiler", "11-09 00:0x compiler: install-pack.ps1 made portable (unstaged): the cache root is LOCALAPPDATA, else XDG_CACHE_HOME, else ~/.cache (lib_gate.ps1 pattern); kit from KIT_BIN on Linux; the in-use check uses CIM on Windows and Get-Process command lines on Linux (beam.smp for daemons). The box dry run is still ok. Linux proof BLOCKED ON DESIGN, not tooling: nodes have no oort credentials, and the write key must not leave the box (oort rule). Next step: the box mints presigned GET URLs (rclone link --expire 2h) for the base + pair objects and ships them as a job argument file, so a node fetches with curl and never sees a key; or the user creates a read-only oort key for nodes (a USER decision, not taken).")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_5 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 5u32, "2026-11-09T03:48Z", "compiler", "11-09 00:2x compiler: NODE PATH without keys: install-pack.ps1 -MintUrls -TargetRid linux-x64 -UrlsOut <file> (on the box) lists the base + pair objects for the candidate and writes object<TAB>presigned GET url rows (rclone link --expire 2h; a bearer credential until it expires: ship it chmod 600, delete after, never log). Then -FromUrls <file> -Apply on a node downloads with Invoke-WebRequest and runs the same per-file manifest verification. Proven so far: a presigned URL fetched from ganymede with plain curl (no key on the node); mint = 33 urls for pack63c linux-x64; on ganymede the reproduce check passes and the PORTABLE in-use check REFUSED correctly (beam.smp pid 496609, another lane notebook): installed tree unchanged (f02f1a356292d42d). Next step: rerun -FromUrls -Apply on ganymede in a quiet moment (urls expire ~02:2x; re-mint if needed).")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_6 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 6u32, "2026-11-09T04:20Z", "compiler", "11-09 00:2x compiler: COMET dogfood started (outside kit: kit job start --on comet fails with ssh exit 255, rig told; ran detached over Windows OpenSSH, log comet:~/remote-stage/comet-compiler/setup.log, pid 5954): install-dotnet.ps1 (comet only had SDK 10.0.403), then install-pack -Name pack63c -FromUrls (presigned, chmod 600, deleted after use; no oort key on comet), then test.ps1 -Suite examples -Backend C -Native -Parallel 2 as the first Linux C parity run on a codespace. Next step: read setup.log on the next heartbeat; the pair hashes must be 1eb4555b/5c92e46c.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_7 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 7u32, "2026-11-09T05:49Z", "compiler", "11-09 01:4x compiler: comet dogfood LOST: the codespace was deleted (gh cs list empty, comet.config removed ~00:28) while the job ran; it had completed install-dotnet.ps1 (SDK rc.3 into the cache) only. Lessons: /usr/bin/time is absent on codespaces (use the bash time keyword); rig idle-stop counted only kit jobs (fixed by rig); remote.ps1 sync lands in /tmp/remote which a restart wipes. Script kept locally (TEMP comet-setup.sh); rerun when org3 provides a new comet. ganymede -FromUrls proof still pending a quiet ganymede.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_8 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 8u32, "2026-11-10T14:26Z", "compiler", "11-10 10:3x compiler: DONE. Install-by-copy is now the only install path used: tonight pack79, pack81, pack82 and pack83 were installed WITHOUT an in-place rebuild on every host. Linux: ganymede via -FromDir (the gates finished build output) behind the quiet-window loop, europa via -FromUrls from the store (presigned URLs minted on the box, no key on the node, every file checked against its manifest, URL file deleted on both hosts). Windows: box via -FromDir of moons build (6 programs reproduce the candidate from the boxs live source), moon via -FromDir (job-1794318330252). Every install: 35-file whole-bin copy, 0 hash mismatches, launch check, real --check smoke with whole-bin rollback. The periodic full rebuild is each packs gate build from source (pack79-84 built from source on ganymede and moon); byte-identity of rebuilds was shown on pack62r. The ganymede -FromUrls proof pending since 11-09 is covered by europas four store installs.")
inl note_of_pack_install_from_artifact_9 () = Note ("PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT", 9u32, "2026-11-10T14:27Z", "compiler", "Evidence: europa store installs pack79/81/82/83 (store hit, every file matches its manifest, 35 files 0 mismatches, smoke ok); box and moon pack83 -FromDir (Core de4ee5a2, 35 files, 0 mismatches, smoke ok); ganymede pack79/81/82/83 -FromDir (35 files, 0 mismatches, smoke ok).")
inl remote_store () = Task ("REMOTE-STORE", "Remote and LAN artifact store for every lane: a typed content-addressed store with tiers hot (local) -> warm (LAN node share) -> cold (oort, S3 in sjc1), dogfooded by every lane; personal data can never reach the cold tier by type", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:tibia,tibia,storage,cache,rig,cross-lane,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_remote_store () = Planning ("REMOTE-STORE", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_remote_store_1 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "User 11-08 14:2x: use the Cloud-provider credit for storage since compute rentals are blocked; ask every lane, research S3 prior art, bench cargo reuse; 14:3x: commit, rent the storage, every lane dogfoods it. oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. SYNTHESIS (7 lanes): the cold tier pays where items are SMALL but EXPENSIVE (fiscal gate verdicts KB vs up to 72 min per unit; regen outputs 5-70 MB vs 48-81 min; pack DLL pairs 13 MB; verify evidence; off-site backup of the non-personal set); the HOT wins (cargo targets 41 GB, rig 38 min per install all cargo; compiler install-pack rebuild 232 s of byte-identical DLLs; per-Rust-cell dependency recompiles) need the LAN tier, never S3 at 6 Mbit/s. Survey: Rust/C/.NET artifacts only hit on the same target triple, so Linux caches cannot serve the Windows box/node; sccache cannot cache bin/proc-macro crates and needs incremental off; content-addressed immutable objects need no invalidation (Scion's tiers, >99 pct hits). DoD: every child Done; each lane reports a measured hit and minutes saved from its own dogfood row. NEXT STEP: children run in parallel; STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH first (cheapest measured win). TYPEFEATURES: see STORE-TYPED-CAS.")
inl note_of_remote_store_2 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:39Z", "tibia", "11-08 14:5x tibia: FIRST DOGFOOD WRITES GREEN. (1) compiler STORE-PACK-CAS: the pack62 reviewed DLL pair + manifest at compiler/pack/v1/core-<sha256>/host-<sha256>/sdk-11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128/, rclone --immutable, up 9 s, down 16 s, equal=True; finding: a copy-install also needs a BASE layer keyed by (SDK, package lock, RID) (bin dir = 64 MB of dependency DLLs + R2R; R2R stays LAN). (2) i574n STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL: pack62 linux-x64 (24 MB) at i574n/packs/linux-x64/sha256-...tgz, box upload 14 s (~13.7 Mbit/s, above the 4.5 smoke figure), europa pulled it in <1 s, core cmp-identical, launches. PATTERN FOR EVERY LANE: off-box readers (europa, codespaces, rentals) get a short-lived PRESIGNED link passed over ssh stdin, never the keys; keys stay only on the box. This is how STORE-RENTAL-PULL hands inputs to a rental without credentials on it.")
inl note_of_remote_store_3 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 3u32, "2026-11-08T18:50Z", "tibia", "11-08 15:0x tibia: RULE FOR EVERY LANE, from fiscal (STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS n2, FISCAL-GATE-CACHE-REACH n4): PROVE A KEY ON A MUTATION MATRIX BEFORE THE FIRST SHARED PUT. Fiscal's verdict key walked opens and module refs but not the implicit spiral.toml prelude, so editing base.spi still HIT; a shared store would have spread that stale green to every host. Fixed (fiscal build.ps1 9B9439ED, unstaged): the key now covers the prelude, lib spiral.toml, the unit spiral.toml and the core-package digest. Matrix after the fix: unreached module HIT, reached module MISS, prelude MISS, lib spiral.toml MISS, restored tree HIT. The minimum matrix per kind: edit each input class once (expect MISS), edit something outside the reach (expect HIT), restore (expect HIT). Fiscal's first put waits for rows from a real gate on an unedited tree; nothing from edited trees is uploaded.")
inl note_of_remote_store_4 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 4u32, "2026-11-08T18:50Z", "tibia", "11-08 15:0x tibia: CORRECTION to note 2: the S3 keys are on the box AND on europa (user OK ~14:4x, \"also copy the credentials\"; europa copy is root 600 in a 700 dir, CRLF stripped: with CRLF every sourced value ended in \\r and curl rejected the URL). Every other host (node, codespaces, rentals) gets presigned links from the box or europa until the user OKs another copy. Keys go to curl through -K - (config on stdin), never argv.")
inl note_of_remote_store_5 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 5u32, "2026-11-09T03:51Z", "tibia", "11-09 00:1x tibia: TIBIA DOGFOOD ROW (via STORE-RENTAL-PULL notes 4-6). Object: the Linux toolchain bundle (spiral-bin bin + toolchains, beam, dotnet, models, launchers), content-addressed at toolchains/linux-x64/<sha256>.tar.zst, v2 991395b8..., 518836373 bytes. Writer europa (datacenter): presigned PUT, 6 s. Readers: ganymede, keyless presigned GET, 13 s and 16 s (two pulls, sha MATCH both times). Measured HIT: yes, a byte-exact read on a host other than the writer. MINUTES SAVED: UNKNOWN, not 0: the old path (europa ssh push of ~1.2 GB to a rental) never ran end to end, because the rental was refused, so there is no baseline. The comparison row comes from the first real rental. Findings for every lane: (1) a URL piped from PowerShell over ssh arrives with a trailing CR, so curl gives http=000 until U=${U%$'\\r'}; (2) presigned PUT needs a file (Content-Length), not a stream; (3) bundle lists must be diffed against the old push list: v1 missed 3 launchers. Personal-data boundary held: fiscal inputs and outputs never enter oort (pack-out.sh oort mode, push.sh fiscal-only).")
inl note_of_remote_store_6 () = Note ("REMOTE-STORE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T11:34Z", "tibia", "Wait: lane org,org2,compiler,fiscal,rig,microblog (each lane's measured dogfood row: hit + minutes saved; tibia's row is note 5).")
inl store_typed_cas () = Task ("STORE-TYPED-CAS", "rig: the typed content-addressed store unit (model unit + linked consumers): entry GADT indexed by artifact kind so a key only fetches its own payload, keys by reflection over typed input records, backends Local | Lan | S3 plus a fault-injecting fake for tests, phantom tier (hot | cold) and phantom sealing (plain | sealed) so personal kinds cannot reach the S3 put", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:rig,rig,storage,cache,linked-units,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_typed_cas () = Planning ("STORE-TYPED-CAS", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_typed_cas_1 () = Note ("STORE-TYPED-CAS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. DESIGN converged across 7 lanes: entry GADT (PackBin core host sdk -> dll_pair | Results | UnitGraph path interface_hash | Verdict | Regen | CargoArtifact crate rustc flags inputs | Memo | Evidence | Chunk), get : key k -> option (payload k); keys by REFLECTION (kit union_reflection: constructor + fields + schema version), never hand strings (fiscal: today Get-GateKey hand-lists inputs, so a forgotten input = a silently stale green); retention/tier policy as a prototype record per kind; memo keys for linked units follow UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH (own source + interface hash + importer used set). OPEN for rig: backend as a closed UNION (compiler, per LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH: closed sets are unions, existentials are for open-world plugins) vs an EXISTENTIAL (i574n, fiscal, microblog, rig: Local / Lan / S3 / fake behind one has/get/put/list prototype). S3 emulation for tests: a fake backend, or s3s + s3s-fs (Rust, serves a dir over S3; MinIO community is archived). DoD: model unit + 2 linked consumers, golden tests on the fake (put/get/has/list, wrong-kind key rejected at compile time, a personal kind rejected for the cold tier at compile time), one live put/get against oort. NEXT STEP: rig decides union vs existential and drafts the entry GADT with 3 kinds (Verdict, PackBin, Evidence). TYPEFEATURES: GADT entries, reflection keys, phantom tier/sealing, prototype policies, union or existential backends.")
inl note_of_store_typed_cas_2 () = Note ("STORE-TYPED-CAS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:38Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 15:0x rig DECISION on the open call (backend): a CLOSED UNION, not an existential. I suggested an existential earlier; I change that. Reasons: (1) the backend set is closed and small inside one org (Local, Lan, S3, plus test faults); nobody outside adds a backend without editing the unit, which is the only case an existential pays for; (2) backend selection is DATA in machine profiles (kit rent profiles, the fleet file), so it must serialize and parse by union_reflection, which a union does and an existential does not; (3) existentials do not cross the compiled ABI (playbook rule 3, LINK-BOUNDARY-BENCH: closed sets are unions), and the store unit is linked by every lane. Fault injection stays: a wrapping case WithFaults :: backend * fault_plan -> backend makes ANY real backend faultable (so S3 emulation = WithFaults (Local dir) plan, no network in tests, and the same plan can wrap Lan for a flaky-share test). s3s + s3s-fs is the right survey candidate for an in-process S3 endpoint when a test must speak the real wire (SigV4 quirks: region us-east-1, x-amz-content-sha256 on body PUTs); MinIO community is archived. Operations are one match per verb (has, get, put), so adding a backend is a compile error at every verb until handled, which is the property we want.")
inl note_of_store_typed_cas_3 () = Note ("STORE-TYPED-CAS", 3u32, "2026-11-08T18:50Z", "tibia", "11-08 15:0x tibia: DoD ADDITION from fiscal's stale-green finding: every entry kind ships with a key MUTATION MATRIX test in the store unit (each input class edited once -> MISS; an input outside the reach -> HIT; restored -> HIT). Reflection over the input record is what makes a forgotten input impossible (fiscal's hand-listed key forgot the implicit prelude); the matrix is what proves it. Rig's decision recorded: backend = closed UNION with a WithFaults wrapping case.")
inl store_sccache_lan_bench () = Task ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", "Bench cargo build reuse on box and node: sccache local disk tier, then a LAN tier (node share via SeaweedFS S3/WebDAV or a plain dir), cold vs warm with an A/A arm, hit rates and minutes saved; oort only for Linux runners", Story, P1, D3, 10u32, Planned, "", "team:tibia,tibia,storage,cache,cargo,bench", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_sccache_lan_bench () = Planning ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", Minutes 120u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_sccache_lan_bench_1 () = Note ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. Survey ranks this first: sccache (S3/R2/WebDAV/disk backends, SCCACHE_MULTILEVEL_CHAIN=disk,s3 promotes remote hits) caches lib crates and C, not bin/dylib/proc-macro; incremental must be off or SCCACHE_ALLOW_INCREMENTAL=1 (workspace/path deps then uncached). Corpus candidates: rig's TRAIN tree (38 of 38.5 min per install is cargo, ~2 installs/day), spiral Rust cells (161 client + 90 sim cells recompile serde_core/serde_json/memchr per cell). Unverified: whether sccache keys include absolute paths (box and node share C:\\home\\git). DoD: on one pinned tree, box cold vs warm (local disk tier) and node warm from the box's LAN tier, A/A spread <= 5 pct, sccache --show-stats hit rates, minutes saved per install; a go/no-go per corpus. NEXT STEP: install sccache on box and node (cargo binstall or scoop), run the box cold/warm pair on the tibia WGSL runner build. TYPEFEATURES: none (measurement).")
inl note_of_store_sccache_lan_bench_2 () = Note ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:39Z", "tibia", "11-08 14:4x tibia: PRE-REGISTERED (before any run). CORPUS: rig's TRAIN40 snapshot, train40-tree.tgz sha256 A45DC7028DD1C49D (400 Cargo.toml, 1089 generated .rs, no target; copied and hash-verified into the tibia scratchpad sccache-bench/), rustc 1.101.0-nightly. Commands (rig): cargo build --locked -p kit-cli -p kit-dev (box debug, TRAIN40 10:14:33-10:24:39 = 10 min) then cargo build --release --locked -p kit-cli (28 min). ARMS on one quiet host: A = cold, no wrapper (baseline, twice: A/A spread <= 5 pct or VOID); B = sccache local disk cold fill (expect a few pct slower than A: cache writes); C = sccache warm in a FRESH target dir (the cross-workspace / cross-machine case rig pays today); D = node built from the box's cache through a LAN tier (SCCACHE_MULTILEVEL_CHAIN or a shared dir; verifies whether sccache keys include absolute paths: box and node both use C:\\home\\git). EXPECTATIONS (soft): C hit rate >= 85 pct of cacheable calls, debug wall time cut >= 50 pct vs A, release >= 40 pct; bin/proc-macro crates and the final link stay uncached (sccache limits), so the floor is the link plus kit-cli itself; D within 20 pct of C if the LAN tier is a mounted dir. sccache 0.19.0 is on the box (local disk cache at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Mozilla\\sccache\\cache, 0 requests so far: Rust runs do not set RUSTC_WRAPPER today). BLOCKER: no quiet host now (box at 100 pct CPU 14:4x; node busy with fiscal shards ~6.5 h from 11:16). Resume when: a quiet window from QUIET-WINDOW-FORECAST, node first.")
inl note_of_store_sccache_lan_bench_3 () = Note ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", 3u32, "2026-11-08T21:30Z", "tibia", "11-08 17:3x tibia groom: arms A-C (local disk cold/warm, fresh target dir) don't need the LAN and can run on ganymede (Linux, idle) now; only arm D (box <-> node LAN tier) still waits for a quiet LAN host. NEXT STEP: arms A-C on ganymede with the TRAIN40 corpus.")
inl note_of_store_sccache_lan_bench_4 () = Note ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", 4u32, "2026-11-09T23:37Z", "org-614162e5", "11-09 19:3x org-614162e5 (resilience, cross-team help on the arm-D path-identity unknown; read-only node fs check, NOT running the heavy bench). The note-1 unknown 'whether sccache keys include absolute paths (box and node share C:\\home\\git)': CONFIRMED box and moon BOTH hold the full repo tree at IDENTICAL absolute paths - box has C:\\home\\git\\kit + \\i574n.github; moon C:\\home\\git lists alphabet blog dice eoie fiscal html i574n.github kit regex spiral tibia (same drive+path). So the box<->moon identical-path condition that makes arm D work WITHOUT --remap-path-prefix is AVAILABLE. THE REMAINING VARIABLE (the crux tibia should pin before arm D): does the sccache CORPUS build run in C:\\home\\git\\<repo> (identical path both machines -> keys match -> arm D feasible as-is) OR in a per-job staged copy (moon TRAIN builds land via the -ts route in C:\\home\\remote-stage\\<job>\\... or a scratch tree per train-snapshot-takes-peer-wip; box builds in C:\\home\\git\\kit) -> paths DIFFER -> sccache keys diverge (Rust embeds abs paths in debug info + the key) -> arm D needs RUSTFLAGS --remap-path-prefix to normalize both to a common virtual root. RECOMMENDATION: either build the corpus in the identical C:\\home\\git path on both, or set --remap-path-prefix=<realroot>=/virt on every arm so the key is path-independent (also improves C-hit determinism). This de-risks arm D before spending the heavy quiet-host window. Still team:tibia + heavy + blocked-on-quiet-host; I did not run A-C (box compute rule + ganymede is OVERLOADED now, not the idle host note-3 assumed).")
inl note_of_store_sccache_lan_bench_5 () = Note ("STORE-SCCACHE-LAN-BENCH", 5u32, "2026-11-10T15:35Z", "tibia", "11-10 11:3x tibia, DECISION on org's crux (note 4): every arm sets RUSTFLAGS=--remap-path-prefix=<real repo root>=/spiral-src (and the same for the cargo registry root to /cargo-registry), so sccache keys are path-independent. Identical C:\\home\\git paths cannot be relied on: train builds run in per-job staged trees (C:\\home\\remote-stage\\<job>\\..., or a scratch tree per train snapshot), whose roots differ per job even on one machine. Remapping also makes the debug info deterministic. The A/A arm must use the same remap so its spread measures the cache, not the paths. The bench is still heavy and needs quiet hosts; the box is CPU-saturated by other lanes now. Next step: when box and moon both have a quiet window, install sccache on both, run the box cold/warm pair on the tibia WGSL runner build with the remap flags, then the moon warm-from-box arm.")
inl store_fiscal_verdicts () = Task ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", "fiscal: share unit gate verdicts (green rows keyed by core + runtime + reach digest) through the store so a new host, CI or a rental stops re-proving unchanged units; regen outputs and corpus as cold copies", Story, P1, D3, 80u32, Active, "", "team:fiscal,fiscal,storage,cache", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_fiscal_verdicts () = Planning ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_1 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. fiscal measured: verdicts are KB, a hit saves up to 72 min per unit (ubl_invoice_2_1 4325 s); today the node paid 645 unit-min though only 44 units changed; CI fiscal timeout 420 min for the same reason. Regen outputs 61.6 MB raw / 5.3 MB zipped save ~81 min per hit; corpus 87.3 MB removes restaging. DoD: build.ps1 reads and writes green rows via oort (plain rclone first, then STORE-TYPED-CAS), a node gate after a box gate skips every unchanged unit, minutes saved measured. NEXT STEP: key derivation by reflection over the input record (replacing Get-GateKey's hand list), then a first put of the box's green.tsv rows. TYPEFEATURES: Verdict entry keyed by a typed verdict_key record.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_2 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:49Z", "fiscal", "11-08 STATUS (fiscal). The premise 'put the box green.tsv rows' was false: no green rows existed anywhere. The box has no target/unit-gate-cache, and every node, europa and rental run used -NoGateCache, which makes Get-GateKey return empty (build.ps1:171), so nothing was written. Proving the cache before sharing it found a stale-green hole: the prelude modules were outside the key (FISCAL-GATE-CACHE-REACH n-latest, fixed, edit matrix green). DoD for the first put: only rows from a real gate on an unedited tree, key = unit sha + reach digest (prelude included) + unit spiral.toml + core + runtime + core-package digest, stored content-addressed at oort:oort-bucket/fiscal/verdicts/<sha256 of the row>. The reflection-derived key replaces Get-GateKey's hand list next, so a new input field cannot be forgotten the way the prelude was. NEXT STEP: the first full gate with the cache on writes real rows; put those.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_3 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 3u32, "2026-11-09T03:44Z", "fiscal", "11-08 FIRST PUT DONE. 82 real green verdict rows from moon's live-tree gate (job-1794183825019, pack63c core 7A2B8AD55B7D48BB, cache ON, unedited landed tree staged 20:22) are in oort:i574n-oort-cas/fiscal/verdicts/<sha256 of the row>.tsv. One immutable object per row; rclone copy --immutable --checksum; the listing shows 82 of 82, 0 missing. Row shape (6 fields): unit, unit sha256, reach:<digest> (prelude + lib spiral.toml + unit spiral.toml included), core sha, runtime sha, core-package:<digest>. Credentials were read from ~/.secrets/vultr-s3.env into the process env only and never printed. Local copy: session scratchpad verdicts/green-moon-20261108.tsv. NEXT STEP: the reader side: build.ps1 loads green rows from oort (fetch by key = sha256 of the expected row) before running a unit, so ganymede/europa/CI skip units moon already proved. Then the reflection-derived key replaces Get-GateKey's hand list.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_4 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 4u32, "2026-11-09T03:56Z", "fiscal", "11-08 READER DESIGN. A verdict object's name IS sha256(expected gate row + LF). So build.ps1 needs no index: for each generated unit it computes the gate row it would write if green (Get-GateKey, the same 6 fields), hashes it, and asks the store whether fiscal/verdicts/<sha>.tsv exists. A hit means the same unit bytes, reach, compiler core, runtime and core-package were proven green elsewhere, so it skips the unit like a local cache hit (the detail line says 'oort hit'). CREDENTIALS: only the box has ~/.secrets/vultr-s3.env, and nodes must never get it. So the box pre-signs short-lived GET URLs for the exact keys a run will need (tibia's runbook presign, rentals/runbook README) and ships them with the stage; the node HEADs each URL (200 = hit). A miss or any error = run the unit (fail open toward running, never toward skipping). WRITE side stays box-only: after a node run, the box pulls its green.tsv and puts the new rows. NEXT STEP: implement the presign + HEAD path in a stage step (box) and a -VerdictUrls file input in build.ps1, proven by a ganymede run that skips the 82 already-proven units.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_5 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 5u32, "2026-11-09T05:03Z", "fiscal", "11-09 READER IMPLEMENTED (unstaged, build.ps1 760F7B20; proof pending). build.ps1 -VerdictUrls <tsv of 'sha256<TAB>presigned URL'>: Set-GateContext loads it into $verdictUrls. Before running a generated unit, if the local green cache misses, Test-StoreVerdict hashes the expected gate row (row + LF, exactly the uploaded object's name) and HEADs its URL; 200 = skip with 'gate cache: oort hit ...', and the key goes into the local set. A miss, a timeout or any error = run the unit (fail toward running). Box side (scratch, not in a repo): session scratchpad verdicts/presign.ps1 lists oort fiscal/verdicts and writes 'rclone link --expire 12h' URLs; the credentials live only in that process. Key scheme verified: the first moon row's sha matches an uploaded object. NEXT STEP: Proof: (1) HEAD a presigned URL from ganymede (no creds) = 200; (2) a ganymede gate of an already-proven unit with -VerdictUrls skips it with 'oort hit' while a changed unit still runs.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_6 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 6u32, "2026-11-09T05:19Z", "fiscal", "11-09 PROOF STEP 1. presign.ps1 presigned 82 of 82 verdicts (rclone link --expire 12h, host sjc1.vultrobjects.com). From ganymede, with NO oort credentials: HEAD = 403 (a SigV4 presigned URL signs the method; these are GET URLs), GET = 200, and the body's sha256 (0055d4ad1e09...) equals the object's name, so content addressing holds end to end. build.ps1 Test-StoreVerdict now GETs and accepts only a body exactly equal to the expected gate row + LF: a tampered, truncated or wrong object is a miss and the unit runs (fail toward running). build.ps1 7xxx (parse ok, unstaged). NEXT STEP: Proof step 2: a ganymede gate with -VerdictUrls over an already-proven unit (e.g. generated_es_facturae_3_2_2) logs 'oort hit' and skips, while an edited unit still runs.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_7 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 7u32, "2026-11-09T05:19Z", "fiscal", "11-09 DESIGN CONSEQUENCE: the key includes the compiler core sha, and builds differ by platform (moon's Windows pack63c core 7A2B8AD55B7D48BB vs the Linux pack63c core 5C92E46C2001DC2B on ganymede and vultr). So verdicts are shared only within a byte-identical compiler group: Windows rows (moon, box) never hit on Linux (ganymede, europa, CI ubuntu), and vice versa. That is correct (a different binary needs its own proof), and it splits the store by core in practice. Ganymede and vultr were shown byte-identical on all 3 compiler DLLs (11-08), so they form one group. PROOF STEP 2 therefore: a Linux gate with the cache ON (ganymede) writes rows, the box puts them, and europa or a second ganymede run with -VerdictUrls skips them.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_8 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 8u32, "2026-11-09T06:29Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 STEP 2 PROVEN on europa (Linux). (a) BUG found and fixed first: build.ps1 param [string] $VerdictUrls and the script hashtable $verdictUrls are ONE variable in PowerShell (names are case-insensitive, the typed param keeps its type), so @{} became a string and .ContainsKey threw on every run; the hashtable is now $storeUrls (build.ps1 03471020, unstaged). An AST scan found no other param/variable collisions. Only europa had the broken copy; the ganymede gates used older build.ps1. (b) Run A wrote 3 certificate rows (nfe v2, cte v4, mdfe v3) on core 5C92E46C. (c) SOUNDNESS observed for free: compiler pack64 was installed on europa at 06:06 UTC between runs (core 2BE9257D), so run C offered 85 shared verdicts and correctly took 0 hits (only the core column differed). (d) The 3 rows on core 2BE9257D were put to oort (prefix now 88 objects, presigned 12 h), then run C with the local cache deleted: gate cache: oort hit on 3 of 3, shard green, the URL file deleted after. SCOPE: same host with an empty cache, not a second host; the log line says another host, which is wording only. NEXT: a second Linux host on the same core (ganymede after it takes pack64) as the cross-host proof, then wire the presign + put into the box gate flow.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_9 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 9u32, "2026-11-09T07:04Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 STEP 2 CROSS-HOST PROVEN. ganymede (Linux, core 2BE9257D, a host that never ran these units) ran build.ps1 03471020 over nfe_v2_00, cte_v4_00 and mdfe_v3_00 certificates with an empty local cache and -VerdictUrls (88 presigned): gate cache: oort hit on 3 of 3, rows proven on europa; shard green. Inputs made identical first: ganymede's work tree had a newer runtime unit (F4C24304 vs europa AA78D995), so a straight run would have MISSED on the runtime column, which is correct; europa's runtime was swapped in for the proof and restored after (F4C24304), and the URL file was deleted. Units on the store: same-host (cache cleared) and cross-host both hit; a compiler change (pack64) misses. Saved here: the 3 units' 110 s of europa unit time (43 + 35 + 32 s), now one GET each. REMAINING DoD: (1) build.ps1 PUTS its own green rows (today the box puts them by hand with rclone; creds stay on the box, so the likely shape is a box-side sweep of node green.tsv files rather than creds on nodes); (2) the Windows pair: a moon gate after a box gate (same Windows core group) skips every unchanged unit; (3) minutes saved measured on a full gate.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_10 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 10u32, "2026-11-09T07:14Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 DoD (1) WRITE PATH DONE as a box-side sweep (credentials never leave the box): session scratch verdicts/sweep.ps1 lists every non-empty target/unit-gate-cache/green.tsv under /root/remote-stage on each Linux host (default vultr, ganymede), copies them, keeps only well-formed rows (6 columns, generated_ unit, 64-hex unit sha, reach: column), writes content-addressed objects sha256(row+LF).tsv, puts them with rclone copy --ignore-existing (credentials loaded from the box secrets file into process env only, removed in finally), then re-presigns. build.ps1 665DBC55 (unstaged): an oort hit now ALSO appends its key to the local green.tsv (a shared verdict is as valid as a local green, because the key covers every input), so the host's next run skips without the network, and the hit message no longer claims another host. VERIFIED on europa: step C rerun gave 3 oort hits and a 3-row local green.tsv; the sweep found the 3 rows, and the put was a no-op (store stays 88, same content, same names); presign 88 of 88. Remaining: (2) Windows pair box then moon, (3) minutes saved on a full gate.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_11 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 11u32, "2026-11-09T07:59Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "11-09 DoD (2) REFRAMED + CI PATH BUILT (opt-in). (2) as written (a node gate after a box gate) no longer fits: the box runs no gates (box compute policy), and moon is the only Windows node. The item's own goal names CI, and CI already PUBLISHES its rows: the pages carry target/unit-gate-cache/green.tsv (83 rows, all core 989B06EE568B = the ubuntu runner, since only ubuntu runs publish.ps1; runtime F4C243044EC4). build.ps1 3661CCEF (unstaged) adds -PagesVerdicts: Set-GateContext GETs /target/unit-gate-cache/green.tsv (best effort, 20 s), keeps only 6-column rows whose core equals this run's core, and SEEDS the new ones into the local green.tsv (so skipped units keep their rows and the next publish carries them forward instead of oscillating); the normal gate-cache skip path then skips every unit whose full key (unit sha, reach, core, runtime, core-package) is unchanged.  is now defined once at the top. Verified on the live pages: matching core 989B06EE -> 83 published, 82 seeded (1 already local); a different core (2BE9257D) -> 0. No new param collisions (AST scan). PAYOFF ESTIMATE: CI spends 159.4 unit-min on the 83 generated units (windows rows of 212e0a8b, 2 slots, 174.5 unit-min in all); a push that changes no unit, lib or compiler would skip most of that on ubuntu. ENABLING IS THE USER'S CALL (it changes what a green CI means: unchanged units are trusted, not re-proven): one line in .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml, 'run: pwsh scripts/build.ps1 -PagesVerdicts', and windows would also need its rows published to benefit. Remaining: (3) minutes saved measured on a real CI run once enabled.")
inl note_of_store_fiscal_verdicts_12 () = Note ("STORE-FISCAL-VERDICTS", 12u32, "2026-11-09T11:35Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Wait: when the user decides on enabling build.ps1 -PagesVerdicts in .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml (DoD 3, minutes saved, is measured on the first CI run with it)")
inl store_pack_cas () = Task ("STORE-PACK-CAS", "compiler: content-addressed store of reviewed pack DLL pairs keyed by (core sha, host sha, SDK) so install-pack copies and hash-verifies instead of rebuilding (232 s on the box), plus cold copies of matrix results and unit graphs", Story, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:compiler,compiler,storage,cache", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_pack_cas () = Planning ("STORE-PACK-CAS", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_1 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. compiler measured: the reviewed pair is 13 MB per pack, 2-3 packs/day, byte-identical on every rebuild (equal=True box/node/rebuild); keep-newest-2 pruning lost nodepack62 and the pack53c baseline (each miss 82-428 s + a hunt). DoD: install-pack takes the pair from the LAN tier (or oort) with a hash check, measured seconds vs the 232 s rebuild; every pack since today in oort. NEXT STEP: put today's pack62 pair into oort under its (core, host, sdk) key. TYPEFEATURES: PackBin entry keyed by a typed record; union vs existential backend per STORE-TYPED-CAS.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_2 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:38Z", "compiler", "11-08 compiler: FIRST OBJECT IN OORT. pack62's reviewed DLL pair + manifest.json are at oort-bucket/compiler/pack/v1/core-<sha256 986a4912...>/host-<sha256 bd09b4f1...>/sdk-11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128/. Keys are FULL sha256s of the reviewed core and host sources; the manifest records schema compiler-pack/v1, pack, the source shas, the SDK and each DLL's sha256 (SpiralCompiler 9011b777..., Core 4524f0d8...). Upload used 'rclone copy --immutable' (no overwrite of an existing key) in 9 s; download-back 16 s; both DLLs equal=True after the round trip. Keys were loaded from ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env into the process env only, never printed or on a command line, and cleared in finally (compiler scratchpad oort-put-pack.ps1).\nFINDING for install-by-copy: the pair is NOT a full install. The installed bin dir is 64 MB (dependency DLLs such as FSharp.Core/Hopac plus ReadyToRun images); only the pair changes per pack. Store two layers: (1) a BASE layer keyed by (SDK, package lock hash, RID), holding the dependency DLLs, which changes rarely; (2) the per-pack PAIR keyed as above (13 MB). R2R images are a function of (assembly sha, SDK, RID) and belong in the r2r-cache tier on the LAN, not oort. NEXT STEP: at pack63's install, time install-pack's phases, then prototype install = base layer + pair copy + hash verify + launch check on the node (LAN tier), with oort as the source of truth.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_3 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 3u32, "2026-11-08T21:31Z", "compiler", "11-08 groom: 40% -> Active. Done: pack62 pair + manifest in oort (round-trip equal), base/pair layer design. ganymede Linux build proven byte-identical to europa (host 1eb4555b37a700f9, host-dir core e24c992685d603ec). NEXT STEP: put pack63 at its install; add a rid-<RID> key segment so the Linux pair (ganymede/europa) can sit beside the Windows one.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_4 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 4u32, "2026-11-09T03:02Z", "compiler", "11-08 23:2x compiler: pack63c in oort under the v1 key WITH the rid segment (compiler/pack/v1/core-<sha256>/host-<sha256>/sdk-<sdk>/rid-<rid>/): core 4521428f31abc748..., host bd09b4f1b3903c98..., sdk 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148; rid-win-x64 (SpiralCompiler 9011b777..., Core 7a2b8ad5...; installed on box + moon) and rid-linux-x64 (1eb4555b..., 5c92e46c...; byte-identical vultr + ganymede). Both round-trip equal=True with manifest.json (schema compiler-pack/v1, now with a rid field). Up 30 s / 69 s, down 64 s / 19 s from the box. pack62 sits at the older key without rid (Windows). Next step: the base layer (the 64 MB dependency DLL set keyed by sdk + package-lock hash + rid), then PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT can install a host by copy without building.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_5 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 5u32, "2026-11-09T03:10Z", "compiler", "11-08 23:4x compiler: BASE LAYER in oort: compiler/base/v1/deps-<sha256 of the 4 compiler fsproj files>/sdk-<sdk>/rid-<rid>/ {files/, manifest.json schema compiler-base/v1}; deps 271284084c4a221a..., sdk 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148, win-x64 and linux-x64, 29 files / 15 MB each, round-trip 29/29 equal. The bin dir minus the pair, *.pdb and *.r2r-old*/-skip leftovers. PROOF of install-by-copy (Windows): base + the pack63c win-x64 pair downloaded into an empty dir in 89 s = the installed bin byte-for-byte (31 files: missing 0, differ 0, extra 0) and launches (--capabilities exit 0). A rebuild takes 232-566 s on the box. Scripts (scratchpad oort-put-base.ps1, oort-assemble-proof.ps1) load the keys into the process only. Next step: PACK-INSTALL-FROM-ARTIFACT, install-pack -FromStore, which fetches base + pair by the reviewed source hashes instead of rebuilding; a Linux proof on ganymede.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_6 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 6u32, "2026-11-09T09:47Z", "compiler", "11-09 09:5x compiler: -FromUrls proof attempt (pack67 linux-x64, 33 presigned 30-min URLs minted on the box through the bucket-free install-pack): on ganymede AND europa install-pack re-derived the candidate exactly from the host live source + the 4 pack67 programs (core 777C218A, host 74B08E7B), then the in-use guard refused (ganymede: another lanes notebook beam.smp daemon on the installed compiler; europa: a transient dotnet). The guard runs BEFORE the fetch, so the URL download + manifest verification path is still unproven. All URL file copies deleted (box, ganymede, europa). Retry at a quiet moment with freshly minted URLs. Also: ganymedes mirror core was stale (pack63c 4521428f) and install-pack.ps1 old; both synced from the box.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_7 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 7u32, "2026-11-09T09:50Z", "compiler", "11-09 09:5x compiler: -FromUrls PROVEN on europa (no store keys on the node): install-pack re-derived pack67 from the live source + 4 programs (777C218A / 74B08E7B), fetched all 33 objects (base deps-27128408 + pair) through 10-minute presigned URLs, and every file matched its manifest (\"store hit ... every file matches its manifest\"); dry run ok. Apply skipped on purpose: europa already runs these exact bytes (3b1da458 / 1ff0ea21). install-pack.ps1 CHANGE (unstaged): in store mode the fetch + manifest verification now runs BEFORE the in-use guard (it only writes the private stage dir), so a dry run proves the store copy and a busy node no longer hides a store fault; the guarded window covers only the copy. URL file deleted on the node right after.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_8 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 8u32, "2026-11-10T10:37Z", "compiler", "11-10 06:3x compiler (groom with fiscal; rig mapped TRAIN42e to TRAIN43f \"or clear if no train is needed\"): CLEARED, not re-pointed. This item never needed a rig train: the wait stood for its pack content reaching the Windows hosts, and pack78 (containing pack67-pack77) is installed on all four nodes (box/moon Core f4148639; Linux now pack82 Core c495bc57). Since then the store gained the content-addressed v2 base, the guarded -Put (COMPILER-STORE-PUT-R2R-GUARD Done) and two real Linux store installs (europa pack79 and pack81-83 via -FromUrls). Next step: check the DoD against that and close.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_9 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 9u32, "2026-11-10T10:59Z", "compiler", "11-10 07:1x compiler: DoD check. (1) install-pack takes the pair from the store with a hash check: MET, every store install verifies each file against its manifest (europa pack79, pack81, pack82, pack83 via -FromUrls tonight; dry runs prove the store copy before the in-use guard). (2) measured seconds vs the 232 s rebuild: MET earlier (box: base + pair in 89 s, byte-for-byte equal to the installed bin; rebuilds 232-566 s). (3) every pack in the store: MET for linux-x64 (pack79, 81, 82, 83 under content-addressed v2 bases via the guarded -Put), NOT for win-x64: pack78s win-x64 entry is the IL pair over the stale v1 base, and no Windows pair exists for pack79+. NOT closing on Linux alone. Next step: build pack83 win-x64 on moon when it is quiet, -Put it (new v2 base replaces the stale v1 one), install box + moon from it, then close.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_10 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 10u32, "2026-11-10T13:56Z", "compiler", "11-10 09:4x compiler: DONE. The missing half (win-x64) is now in the store: pack83 win-x64 put via the guarded -Put (identity c3742c88 = candidate, both DLLs ReadyToRun) under a content-addressed v2 base (files-046a0947, 33 files), which supersedes the stale v1 Windows base. Every pack since tonight is in the store for linux-x64 (79, 81, 82, 83) and pack83 for win-x64; installs take it with a hash check (Linux store installs on europa; Windows -FromDir with the same manifests); measured 89 s vs 232-566 s rebuilds.")
inl note_of_store_pack_cas_11 () = Note ("STORE-PACK-CAS", 11u32, "2026-11-10T13:57Z", "compiler", "Evidence: PUT pack83 win-x64 (base v2 files-046a0947, 33 files) and linux-x64 (files-1fa124aa); europa store installs pack79/81/82/83 with every file matching its manifest; box base+pair restore 89 s vs 232 s rebuild (11-08).")
inl store_linux_pack_pull () = Task ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", "i574n: europa, codespaces and rentals PULL a Linux pack build from oort (sjc1, next to europa) instead of building from source with 2.1 GB headroom; box deltas uploaded once to oort for every overlay", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "team:org,storage,cache,europa,rental", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_linux_pack_pull () = Planning ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_1 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. i574n measured: packs 150-250 MB, ~1/day; europa rebuilds from source and needs 2.1 GB headroom (management scaled its serve to 0 today); box deltas ~20 MB per run, uploaded once instead of per machine. DoD: a Linux pack built once (on the first Linux host) is fetched by europa from oort and installs without a source build; one codespace overlay pulls the box delta from oort. NEXT STEP: upload europa's pack62 Linux build from europa itself (datacenter uplink), then install it on europa from the object. TYPEFEATURES: Pack/Runner/Delta entries keyed by core/host hash and base head.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_2 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:39Z", "i574n", "i574n 11-08 14:3x FIRST ROUND TRIP GREEN. Object: oort:oort-bucket/i574n/packs/linux-x64/sha256-d6cc7017eef87784d5fe768427617c9ee6ccac9b0cdc55d7d9b33c780bd62509.tgz (23,969,548 bytes; gzip -n of europa's pack62 single-flight net11.0, Linux core e24c992685d603ec, built from sources 986A4912/BD09B4F1). Path: europa tar -> scp to box (sha equal) -> rclone copyto from the box (14 s, ~13.7 Mbit/s, better than the 4.5 Mbit/s estimate) -> presigned GET link (30 min, passed to europa over ssh stdin, never printed or stored) -> europa curl pull (<1 s, same DC) -> sha256 -c OK -> extract -> pulled core == installed core (cmp IDENTICAL) -> dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --capabilities exit 0 from the pulled copy. Unsigned GET of the key = 403 (bucket private). KEYS: they stay on the box; europa has NO key file, and copying credentials to another host needs the user's explicit OK, so off-box readers use short-lived presigned links. NEXT: (1) pack-chain -SkipVultrBuild mode: when an object for the pack's source hashes exists, europa pulls it instead of source-building (no 2.1 GB headroom, no serve pause); (2) the Linux build is produced once per pack (europa or a codespace) and uploaded, so rentals and codespaces pull it; (3) index object: packs/linux-x64/by-source/<core.fs>-<host.fs> -> the sha key, so readers find a build by source hashes.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_3 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 3u32, "2026-11-09T16:12Z", "tibia", "11-09 12:2x tibia (help pool): STATUS RECONCILED with compiler's STORE-PACK-CAS, so no second store gets built. The europa half of this DoD IS MET by compiler's work: oort holds compiler/base/v1 (deps-hash keyed, win-x64 + linux-x64) plus pack DLL pairs with manifests, and install-pack -FromUrls was PROVEN on europa (11-09 09:5x, STORE-PACK-CAS note): it re-derived pack67 from live source + 4 programs, fetched all 33 objects through 10-min presigned URLs (no keys on the node), every file matched its manifest, and no source build was needed (the 2.1 GB headroom problem is gone). That supersedes this item's NEXT (1) pull-instead-of-build and (3) the by-source index (the CAS keys by deps hash + pair). STILL OPEN (the only remaining DoD line): \"one codespace overlay pulls\" a pack from oort. Rig's CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS tools-bundle step already pulls tibia's oort toolchain bundle into a codespace (rehearsal green 11-09, regex 64/64), so the pack pull would be one more step in that driver: install-pack -FromUrls with URLs minted on the box. Proposed to rig/compiler; tibia does not start a codespace for it.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_4 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 4u32, "2026-11-09T16:18Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 15:1x rig: accepted (tibia's help-pool proposal): rig runs the one rehearsal that closes the open DoD line (a codespace overlay pulls a pack from oort): the tools-bundle step, then compiler's install-pack -FromUrls with presigned URLs minted on the box right before the codespace is created (~1 standard core-h, inside the free quota). rig's only missing input is the mint command or file for the pack URLs (base + pair), asked of compiler via tibia; the lane:rig wait stays (it is rig's next action).")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_5 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 5u32, "2026-11-09T16:46Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 16:0x rig PREP: compiler sent the mint recipe (install-pack.ps1 -Name pack77 -Programs <16 .kitdo, in COMPILER-WARM-CLIENTS n15 order + TS-TYPEFEATURESET-PROBES/4 + ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP/1> -MintUrls -TargetRid linux-x64 -TargetSdk 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148 -UrlsOut <file>; expected core/host 2cc4470f/79070d91, installed SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b, SpiralCompiler c51a27a4). Verified on the box: all 16 program files exist (list saved in rig's scratch), the pack77 candidate is staged (spiral_compiler.fs 2,264,561 B, Program.fs 90,727 B), and the mint branch reads only the candidate + projects dir and returns before any staging (no live-source writes). OPEN before spending quota: the far side's -FromUrls also needs the candidate source and the tmp/compiler projects dir that the store key hashes; asked compiler for its europa file list so the codespace run does not end in a store miss.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_6 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 6u32, "2026-11-09T17:03Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 17:1x local rig, REHEARSAL LAUNCHED (standardLinux32gb, i574n/spiral). Far side built from compiler's exact recipe: (1) the box's apps/compiler scripts + spiral_compiler.fs + tmp/compiler (tar, 9.4 MB, 134 entries) overlaid on the codespace checkout; (2) the pack77 candidate pair into ~/.cache/spiral-bin-candidates/pack77/source (core hash 2CC4470F = compiler's expected 2cc4470f); (3) the 16 programs in note-15 order, passed through pwsh -Command as an array (pwsh -File would pass one comma string); (4) a Linux kit for kit do: ganymede's installed release binary, stripped there from 424 MB to 65 MB (kit help runs), KIT_BIN; (5) SDK 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148 via scripts/install-dotnet.ps1; (6) pwsh from the tools bundle. Pack URLs minted on the box right before create (-TargetSdk rc.3, -UrlsExpire 4h), shipped as a FILE, deleted locally in finally; the -FromUrls path needs no bucket name. Expect: \"live core ... reproduces\", ~33 objects verified, installed SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b / SpiralCompiler c51a27a4. Drivers: drafts/i574n.github/CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS/ci-rehearsal-drive-packpull.ps1 + the remote script's packpull step.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_7 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 7u32, "2026-11-09T17:32Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 18:1x local rig, REHEARSAL RESULT (codespace deleted, list after: gone; 1593 s wall on 4 cores = ~1.8 core-h). THE PULL WORKS: on a fresh codespace overlay the reproduce check passed (live core C2DB952E + the 16 programs -> core 2CC4470F, host 79070D91 = the reviewed candidate), \"store hit: base deps-271284084c4a221a + pack pack77, sdk 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148, linux-x64; every file matches its manifest\", \"installed from the store: 31 files\", and the installed DLLs hash SpiralCompiler c51a27a4 / SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b, exactly compiler's expected values. TWO FAILURES AROUND IT, both diagnosed: (1) install-pack's post-install check \"system dotnet (/home/codespace/.dotnet/dotnet) launches the installed compiler: False\" threw at line 177 (runtime mismatch): the codespace image ships its own dotnet in ~/.dotnet, which is not the rc.3 runtime (ganymede's system dotnet is), so the launch check runs under the wrong runtime; fix: put the rc.3 SDK's dotnet (the one install-dotnet.ps1 installed) first on PATH / DOTNET_ROOT before install-pack, then re-run only that check. (2) The tools bundle fetch got 403: rig minted its 15-min presigned URL BEFORE the pack mint and staging, and the codespace step began ~19 min later; fix: mint the bundle URL after staging, just before upload (the run fell back to the tools step; gleam's download also hit a 500, unrelated). DoD status: the pull from oort onto a codespace overlay is PROVEN (verified manifest + expected hashes); the launch check is a codespace runtime issue to fix in the next run, not the store.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_8 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 8u32, "2026-11-09T17:53Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "proof: 11-09 13:37-13:52 local rig, RERUN GREEN on codespace jubilant-rotary-phone-* (standardLinux32gb, 845 s wall on 4 cores = ~0.94 core-h; deleted, list after: gone). Tools bundle fetched and verified (fetch exit 0, unpack to / exit 0, toolchains readable, TOOLS-DONE with no fallback: the late bundle-URL mint and the zig chmod both worked). Pack pull: reproduce check passed (live C2DB952E + 16 programs -> core 2CC4470F, host 79070D91 = the reviewed candidate); \"store hit: base deps-271284084c4a221a + pack pack77, sdk 11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148, linux-x64; every file matches its manifest\"; \"installed from the store: 31 files\"; compiler's new check: \"resolved dotnet (the one Cells and the scripts use) launches the installed compiler: True\"; \"INSTALLED pack77\"; installed SpiralCompiler.dll c51a27a4 and SpiralCompilerCore.dll 9ef0395b = compiler's expected hashes; PACKPULL exit 0. The open DoD line (\"a codespace overlay pulls a pack from oort\") is met; clearing the lane:rig wait. Recipe for reuse: drafts/i574n.github/CODESPACES-QUOTA-RUNS/ci-rehearsal-drive-packpull.ps1 + the remote script's packpull step.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_9 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 9u32, "2026-11-09T17:59Z", "org-f33af161", "org 11-09: 90pct, Active, ONE step left (upload europa's pack62 Linux build to oort from europa's datacenter uplink, then install on europa from the object; + one codespace overlay pulls the box delta). It is genuinely org/infra's to finish, NOT blocked - but the step needs oort S3 creds (~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env) and a 150-250MB pack op ON europa, which is currently saturated with the font grotesque builds (europa is my only spiral build node until ganymede deps are provisioned). Deferring to a dedicated europa window after the font Greek set finishes; leaving Active as org's ready finish-step.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_10 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 10u32, "2026-11-09T18:06Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 18:1x the 16:18 note says the lane:rig wait stays, but no wait row existed, so the item kept surfacing as ready for org; row added (lane:team:rig). Only open DoD line: rig's codespace rehearsal pulling a pack via install-pack -FromUrls. No org action until then.")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_11 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 11u32, "2026-11-09T20:16Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig: the lane:team:rig wait org2 added at 18:06Z is ALREADY ANSWERED by rig's 17:53Z note (13 minutes earlier; org2 cited the 16:18Z note that predates it): the codespace overlay rehearsal is green: store hit (base deps-271284084c4a221a + pack77, rc.3, linux-x64, every file matches its manifest), \"installed from the store: 31 files\", \"resolved dotnet ... launches the installed compiler: True\", SpiralCompiler c51a27a4 / SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b = compiler's expected pair; codespace deleted (list after: gone). That was the only open DoD line, so rig clears the wait; the item is org's to close (or to finish the europa-window step if org still wants it, which tibia's 16:12Z note says compiler's STORE-PACK-CAS already met).")
inl note_of_store_linux_pack_pull_12 () = Note ("STORE-LINUX-PACK-PULL", 12u32, "2026-11-09T20:19Z", "org-f33af161", "org-f33af161 11-09 16:2x: CLOSING - both DoD lines proven green. (1) a Linux pack fetched by europa from oort + installs without a source build: MET by compiler install-pack -FromUrls on europa 11-09 09:5x (STORE-PACK-CAS; the 2.1GB-headroom source build is gone), per tibia note 16:12Z. (2) one codespace overlay pulls a pack from oort: MET by rig 17:53Z rehearsal (store hit base deps-271284084c4a221a + pack77 rc.3 linux-x64, installed 31 files, resolved dotnet launches installed compiler True, SpiralCompiler c51a27a4 / SpiralCompilerCore 9ef0395b = compiler expected, PACKPULL exit 0). rig 20:16Z cleared its wait, said it is org to close. My note-9 europa-upload step is SUPERSEDED by compiler CAS (keys by deps hash, same outcome). 100 Done.")
inl store_verify_evidence () = Task ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", "microblog: keep verify evidence bundles (logs, e2e request logs, output diffs) and pack cores in oort so a red is diagnosable after a mirror dies; first a warm-vs-cold verify A/A for Cells memo layers", Story, P2, D2, 60u32, Active, "", "team:microblog,x,storage,cache", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_store_verify_evidence () = Planning ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_1 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 1u32, "2026-11-08T18:35Z", "tibia", "oort (rented 11-08 14:31: Oort Object Storage sjc1, bucket oort-bucket, private, lifecycle abort-incomplete-multipart-2d; keys ~/.secrets/cloud-s3.env, never printed; box measured 4.5 Mbit/s up, 8.6 down; memory oort-object-store). Lane answers + prior-art survey: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\reports\\remote-store-20261108. microblog measured: a lost e2e request log kept a red undiagnosed 11-06..11-08 = 3 verify reruns (~8 node-hours at p50 159 min); Cells memo layers ~0.33 GB / ~35k files start cold on every fresh mirror (memo warm_entries=0), minutes unmeasured. DoD: every x-verify uploads its evidence bundle to oort keyed by job id; one warm-vs-cold verify A/A measured. NEXT STEP: the A/A on one mirror. TYPEFEATURES: Evidence entry keyed by job id; Memo keys per UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_2 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 2u32, "2026-11-08T18:37Z", "microblog", "PLAN (microblog 11-08, oort live: bucket oort-bucket, sjc1, private; memory oort-object-store). (1) WARM-VS-COLD A/A rides on the client verify MICROBLOG-UI-LANDING needs anyway: when the node frees after fiscal, run the client verify on a FRESH mirror (cold memo), then the identical verify again on the same mirror (warm), then a second warm run as the A/A arm; report wall and memo hits per run, pinned to pack62. No extra node time beyond two reruns. (2) EVIDENCE BUNDLES keyed by job id (verify.log, e2e requests.jsonl and FAILED lines, generated-output diffs; the x e2e world is synthetic, so non-personal) go up ONLY through rig's typed store unit, per the lanes' rule that a personal kind must not be able to reach the S3 put; nothing is hand-uploaded with rclone or curl from this lane. (3) PACK CORES by hash: hand to whoever owns pack installs (i574n). DoD: the A/A numbers; one red verify whose evidence is re-read from oort after its mirror is deleted. NEXT STEP: the A/A, at the node slot after fiscal.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_3 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 3u32, "2026-11-09T00:30Z", "microblog", "A/A QUEUED on moon: cold job-1794184202949-x-verify-1109-cold, warm job-1794184210003-x-verify-1109-warm1, warm A/A arm job-1794184223119-x-verify-1109-warm2 (same mirror xqueue1, chained --after-ok). Compare wall and memo hits per arm from each arm's log line \"x-verify / arm <arm> exit <n> wall_s <s>\". Ganymede rule (org 11-09): later ganymede jobs pin SPIRAL_CELLS_JOBS=2 SPIRAL_CELLS_CELL_CONCURRENCY=1 CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_4 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 4u32, "2026-11-09T11:34Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "Wait: job moon:job-1794221637538-x-verify-client-r2 (then the r2-warm arm)")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_5 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 5u32, "2026-11-09T14:41Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 10:4x microblog, r2-cold client verify (job-1794221637538, moon, core B62490C34FD69070, page 4F2C116E81CA, 3h41m) ENDED RED, exit 2: 4 of 48 notebooks failed. (1) tlds: a REAL defect, mine. I deleted apps/client/tlds.spi (it moved to lib under LIB-TLDS) but missed its entry in apps/client/build.ps1's notebook list (File.Error: no such file), a rename-sweep miss. Fixed (unstaged) in C:\\home\\git\\x and identically in the xqueue1 mirror (both build.ps1 hashes E30A279BC8BC; mirror backup build.ps1.before-tlds-fix). The other consumers (defuse, domains, splitter: ':< tlds') now resolve to the lib module and passed. apps/client/README.md row 795 still lists tlds.spi; it changes with the next -Accept cold run, because the README is the e2e thread fixture. (2) args (2 tests), requests (1), cells (1): VOID, not failures. Each hit the 300 s cell timeout while TRAIN42e shared moon (cpu 87%); 0 assertion failures. Wrapper fixes: x-verify-client.ps1 gains -TimeoutScale (sets SPIRAL_CELLS_TIMEOUT_SCALE, recorded in inputs.timeout_scale), and failed_lines now also catches 'notebooks failed' and ': VOID, ' (the r2 summary showed [] despite 4 failures). WARM ARM running: job-1794235264926-x-verify-client-r2w, -Arm r2-warm -Core B62490C34FD69070 -TimeoutScale 3, the same mirror, so 44 notebooks memo-hit and the 3 VOID ones get 900 s.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_6 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 6u32, "2026-11-09T15:35Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 11:4x microblog: x apps/client/README.md updated in the box tree (unstaged): the tlds.spi row is removed from the client file table, and the defusing paragraph names lib 'tlds' instead of spiral/apps/cells/data/tlds.txt (0 tlds.txt mentions left). The README is the e2e thread fixture, so the NEXT cold client verify (-Accept) regenerates its e2e expectations. The running r2-warm arm is unaffected: it reads the xqueue1 mirror.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_7 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 7u32, "2026-11-09T16:45Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 12:4x microblog: r2-warm STOPPED on request at 12:42 (exit 143, process tree stopped, no leftovers in xqueue1). It was not a warm arm: the Cells memo has been OFF fleet-wide since about 11-06 (CELLS-CELL-TIMINGS n30), so its notebooks recompiled cold (memo_hits=0 in all of them, compile 2,489 s over its first 4). At ~4.5 min per notebook with ~39 left it would have ended near 15:40, past its 14:41 cap (VOID), while holding the moon slot the sim verify and the queued proofs need. Its partial output still served as evidence for the two Cells finds (mount = manifest resolve; memo off). The wrapper's limitation text 'warm arms reuse the cold arm's memo by design' was false under the memo bug. NEXT client run: ONE cold -Accept verify after the sim verify, carrying the tlds build-list fix, the README reflow and tlds edits, and -TimeoutScale 3 with a 7 h cap; it gives the green verdict for LIB-TLDS and the accepted e2e expectations. A real warm arm is meaningful only after the memo fix lands.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_8 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 8u32, "2026-11-10T03:13Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 groom (microblog): unblocked. The r3 COLD client verify on xqueue1 is green (verify 8,462 s, run x-verify-1109-r3-cold-20261109185040), and moon's memo store is writing again (shards from 20:02 11-09). NEXT STEP: run the WARM arm, x-verify-client.ps1 -Arm r3-warm -TimeoutScale 3, on the same unchanged xqueue1 mirror (nothing synced in between, so the memo keys still match), as one kit job on moon; compare memo_hits per notebook and the warm/cold verify_ms. That is the memo-layer A/A this item asks for first. Then bundle both runs' evidence (verify.log, summary.json, e2e request logs) for oort. TYPEFEATURES: an evidence bundle record { run; arm : Cold | Warm; verdict; memo_hits; verify_ms }.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_9 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 9u32, "2026-11-10T03:18Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 groom 2 (digest: pin the compiler for long node gates): the warm arm MUST run with -Core F414863971EB2A2C (the cold r3 arm's core, recorded in its inputs); x-verify-client.ps1 refuses a different core, so a pack install between the two arms voids the A/A instead of silently mixing. NEXT STEP unchanged: x-verify-client.ps1 -Arm r3-warm -Core F414863971EB2A2C -TimeoutScale 3 on the unchanged xqueue1 mirror.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_10 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 10u32, "2026-11-10T03:45Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 00:0x microblog: WARM ARM SUBMITTED, job-1794282329239-x-verify-client-r3w on moon: x-verify-client.ps1 -Arm r3-warm -Core F414863971EB2A2C -TimeoutScale 3 on the xqueue1 mirror, unchanged since before the cold arm (its last sync preceded the cold run's 18:50 start), so the memo keys match. The driver refuses a different compiler core (fail closed). CAVEAT: moon is shared with rig's TRAIN43b, so wall times are noisy; memo_hits per notebook is the deterministic measure. Next step: after the arm settles, compare per-notebook memo_hits (cold arm: 0 everywhere) and verify_ms (cold 8,462 s), then bundle both runs' evidence.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_11 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 11u32, "2026-11-10T04:35Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 00:3x microblog: warm arm progress (job-1794282329239-x-verify-client-r3w, run x-verify-1109-r3-warm-20261109234530 on moon): 8 notebooks passed so far, but 0 of them report memo_hits > 0. So far the warm arm is NOT reusing the cold arm's memo. Possible causes to check when it ends, not guessed now: the per-test protocol keys memo entries with :per_test and the test name, while the cold run's entries were written earlier on the same mirror and Cells; the memo key includes the compiler binaries print, and the warm arm pinned the same core F414863971EB2A2C; or the memo dir (SPIRAL_CELLS_MEMO_DIR) differs per run. Next step: when it settles, compare one notebook's memo key inputs between the arms (ipynb phases_ms code_memo_hit, the memo dir path in the logs) before concluding.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_12 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 12u32, "2026-11-10T04:39Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 00:4x microblog: WARM ARM MISSES THE COLD ARM'S MEMO; two hypotheses REFUTED with data (read-only on moon). (1) Eviction: no. moon's memo store has 35,143 entries back to 10-25 (prune keeps far more than 5,000 in practice), the cold arm's entries are still there (11-09 18h-21h: 649), and the warm arm wrote 584 NEW entries since midnight, so it writes fresh keys instead of hitting. (2) Toolchain change: no. 0 files changed since 11-09 18:00 under spiral-bin toolchains/bin, scoop shims/gleam/erlang/elixir, .cargo/bin, .rustup/toolchains, so tool_stamp (size + mtime) is identical. Also ruled out: content (timeline_page A2B5E6C274F7 in both arms; its mtime moved to 23:35 with the same bytes, but source_bounds keys by text, not mtime) and the compiler core (F414863971EB2A2C pinned in both). Still open: something run-specific in Memo.key, most likely the package PATHS (Memo.key maps package_paths to {path, print}; if Cells mounts packages into per-run temp dirs, every run re-keys and the memo can never hit across runs, only within one). Next step: after the warm arm settles, compute Memo.key's inputs for one small notebook in both arms (package paths, builder lines, context.base) by a read-only mix run against each arm's mount layout, and diff them.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_13 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 13u32, "2026-11-10T04:40Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 00:5x microblog, narrowing (code read): Memo.key = sha256 of {context.base, program, real, builder_lines, packages}; context.base = {format, compiler: {binaries print, core tree print}, kino_identity, tool_identity}. tool_identity stamps 16 resolved tools by {path, size, mtime}: rustc, cc, dcc, fpc, zig, gleam, erl, bun, python, lua, wgsl_run, naga, pwsh, dotnet, spiral, plot (plus lean). The toolchain dirs I checked were unchanged, but spiral, wgsl_run, naga and plot can resolve to BUILD OUTPUTS outside them, and rig's TRAIN43b has been building on moon all evening, so a rebuilt or touched spiral.exe / wgsl tool would re-key every entry. If so, the memo is correct (a tool changed) but brittle: an mtime-only touch of an unused tool invalidates every notebook. Next step: after the warm arm settles (no mix running in xqueue1), run mix run --no-start -e 'IO.inspect(Spiral.Cells.Memo.tool_identity([]))' in xqueue1 Cells, then read each path's mtime against the cold arm's 18:50 start. If a tool moved, propose keying tools by content hash (as compiler_binaries_print already does) instead of mtime.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_14 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 14u32, "2026-11-10T06:23Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 02:2x: WARM ARM RESULT (job-1794282329239-x-verify-client-r3w exit 0, verdict ok, verify 8,861 s vs cold 8,462 s on a moon shared with TRAIN43b): 47 notebooks passed and NOT ONE reported memo_hits > 0. So the cold arm's memo did not serve the warm arm. Its 2 failed_lines are the e2e tutorial step 'xc thread 3000001' reported DIFFERENT and then accepted (the run is -Accept); during the arm, xqueue1's sim-* e2e fixtures were rewritten to the set the box already carries (sim-gap.expected fbb20cf...). REFUTED so far, with data: eviction (no prune ever ran; the cold entries are present), toolchain change (tool_identity printed on xqueue1: no tool stamp newer than 11-08 16:49, spiral.exe), source content and the compiler core (identical), builder_lines (the raw ///> directive lines, deterministic). Not yet tested: the core tree print, package prints, program text. EXPERIMENT RUNNING: job-1794291771622-x-memo-aa runs verify.ps1 -Notebooks args/args TWICE back to back on the unchanged xqueue1. memo_hits > 0 on the second run means the keys are stable and some input changed between the arms; 0 means something per-run leaks into the key. Next step: read both totals lines.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_15 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 15u32, "2026-11-10T06:31Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 02:3x A/A RESULT (job-1794291771622-x-memo-aa on moon, xqueue1 unchanged): verify.ps1 -Notebooks args/args twice in a row: BOTH runs memo_hits 4 of 4 (first 124 s, second 94 s). So the Cells memo DOES serve across processes, and even the first A/A run hit the entries the warm arm had just written. Also checked: the core library under xqueue1's deps junction (C:\\home\\git\\spiral\\deps on moon) had 0 files written between the cold arm's end and the warm arm's start (newest core.spi 11-01), so the core tree print is not the cause. Still UNEXPLAINED: why the warm arm missed every cold-arm entry while every input checked (sources, compiler core, 16 tool stamps, core library, builder lines) was identical. Practical verdict: memo reuse works now (A/A proves it); the cold to warm miss looks specific to that one cold run. Next step: repeat the warm arm (x-verify-client.ps1 -Arm r3-warm2 -Core F414863971EB2A2C -TimeoutScale 3) in the next free moon window; with this A/A it should show memo_hits on most notebooks; if it does, bundle the cold, warm and warm2 evidence and close.")
inl note_of_store_verify_evidence_16 () = Note ("STORE-VERIFY-EVIDENCE", 16u32, "2026-11-10T08:26Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 04:4x COLD->WARM MISS EXPLAINED (see CELLS-MEMO-SILENT-WHOLE-KEYS): moon's memo store shows the cold arm wrote 180 results and 0 source-bounds entries (19h-20h), the warm arm 280 new bounds entries for unchanged sources, so the cold arm's Cells process had a failed --capabilities probe cached as [] and keyed every result on the whole source; the warm arm keyed on reached blocks, which never meets whole keys. The A/A pair (both bounded) hit 4/4, consistent. Fix written and tested on the box (unstaged). Next step unchanged: warm2 arm on moon when free; it should now hit the warm arm's bounded entries, and its report must NOT carry memo_keys=whole once the fix ships.")
