inl note_of_rig_reformat_cell_splits_1 () = Note ("RIG-REFORMAT-CELL-SPLITS", 1u32, "2026-11-09T02:06Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 21:4x filed by rig. USER DECISION 11-08 20:3x (compiler asked the user directly, kit 28 named in the counts): fix the pack63c reformat cell-budget overruns by SPLITTING definitions, keeping budget and layout. kit has no budget gate, so this is a baselines-only-shrink cleanup, not a TRAIN42 blocker. Scope = the definitions compiler measured as newly over (before = its snapshot under the compiler session scratchpad fleet/snap/kit, after = live): rig_delegate/model.spi 0->5; patch_effect_domain/model.spi 4->6; bundle_retention_domain/retention_plan.spi 3->5; +1 each in rig_ci_parity/parity, rig_jobs_doctor/check, rig_experiment_verb_args/verbs, parallel_workstream_contract/model, rig_job_after_model/model, rig_source_topology_core/model, bundle_manifest_contract/model, toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/policy, bundle_lifecycle_domain/model, toolchain_command_domain/bridge, agile_task_asks/asks, cold_rebuild_contract/bindings, agile_cadence/model, agile_task_inspection/task_inspection, patch_fallback_domain/model, agile_todo_model/model, rig_do_file_steps/steps, agile_todo_scan/todo_scan, rig_experiment_stats/experiment_stats. NOT in scope: the ~3800 legacy over-budget definitions (a kit budget ratchet is a separate question). Method: per-file before/after mix spiral.cell_budget --check on a node to name the exact definitions, then hoist the chain tail into a helper, box edits only. DoD: the before/after check on a node shows 0 definitions newly over; notebooks of the touched packages green; ships in TRAIN43. Next step: after TRAIN42 installs, run the per-definition delta. TypeFeatures: none new, splits only.")
inl note_of_rig_reformat_cell_splits_2 () = Note ("RIG-REFORMAT-CELL-SPLITS", 2u32, "2026-11-09T02:07Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-08 21:5x rig: the BEFORE snapshot moved by compiler to a durable box path: LOCALAPPDATA spiral-bin scratch fleet-reformat-20261108 (snap/kit = 1,774 pre-reformat kit .spi; snapshot-src.tar = the 11-repo snapshot; corpus.tsv and changed.tsv = file lists). compiler keeps it until this item is Done; tell compiler at close so it can be archived. Next step unchanged: per-definition delta on a node after TRAIN42 installs.")
inl note_of_rig_reformat_cell_splits_3 () = Note ("RIG-REFORMAT-CELL-SPLITS", 3u32, "2026-11-09T15:50Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 11:5x microblog did the NEXT STEP on the box, CPU-free, without waiting for TRAIN42: the per-definition before/after delta with the exact awk budget oracle (memory cell-budget-awk-oracle; block = text up to blank line(s) followed by an unindented line, trailing whitespace trimmed, weight = characters with newlines as 1, limit 273/272/271 by cell index; it treats autolinks as plain text, so +-23 where a domain-like token sits). BEFORE = compiler's snap/kit, AFTER = live kit/src, over the 22 named files: 184 -> 214 over-budget blocks; 31 are newly over (matched by file and head line). The list (file:line weight>limit): rig_delegate/model.spi:58 1740>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:86 276>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:98 277>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:108 282>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:136 759>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:149 660>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:159 679>272; rig_delegate/model.spi:170 981>272; patch_effect_domain/model.spi:100 280>272; patch_effect_domain/model.spi:120 281>272; bundle_retention_domain/retention_plan.spi:41 280>273; bundle_retention_domain/retention_plan.spi:63 289>273; rig_ci_parity/parity.spi:12 277>273; rig_jobs_doctor/check.spi:65 280>273; rig_experiment_verb_args/verbs.spi:69 281>272; parallel_workstream_contract/model.spi:132 288>272; rig_job_after_model/model.spi:150 275>272; rig_source_topology_core/model.spi:108 288>272; bundle_manifest_contract/model.spi:45 276>273; toolchain_runtime_policy_domain/policy.spi:1 275>273; bundle_lifecycle_domain/model.spi:89 284>272; toolchain_command_domain/bridge.spi:20 285>273; agile_task_asks/asks.spi:18 277>273; cold_rebuild_contract/bindings.spi:14 277>273; agile_cadence/model.spi:76 278>272; agile_task_inspection/task_inspection.spi:88 280>272; patch_fallback_domain/model.spi:91 281>272; agile_todo_model/model.spi:126 275>272; rig_do_file_steps/steps.spi:131 275>272; agile_todo_scan/todo_scan.spi:62 283>273; rig_experiment_stats/experiment_stats.spi:90 278>272. Most are 2 to 17 over (a one-helper hoist each). The outliers are rig_delegate/model.spi: options_of 1740 at :58 and 4 test blocks (759/660/679/981, at :136 :149 :159 :170), which look like code added after the snapshot rather than reformat growth. Files: drafts\\kit\\RIG-REFORMAT-CELL-SPLITS\\newly-over.tsv and oracle.awk (rerun: gawk -f oracle.awk <file> from kit/src). next: hoist per row, then rerun the oracle (0 newly over) before the node check.")
inl lib_layers_3_base_split () = Task ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", "lib: move the multi-backend primitive bindings (base, sm', date_time, am', convert, ...) to the new Prim layer in layers.spi once TRAIN42 ships Prim, so the layer ratchet measures real defects instead of a taxonomy gap", TaskKind, P2, D4, 10u32, Blocked, "", "team:org,lib,layers,standards,granular", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lib_layers_3_base_split () = Planning ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", Minutes 240u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_1 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-11-09T02:49Z", "org2", "Child of LIB-LAYERS (see its 11-08 22:5x analysis). DoD: base's macros live in a Host binding module; the pure functions move to named Std modules (no new 'base/common/util' drawer); every consumer is updated (lib + all repos; rename sweep); kit standards --layers shows violations drop by about base's share with the baseline shrunk; lib_land_window gates green (signatures, consumers, document_test, type_probe, respell_drift). Next step: a module map of base (each function: pure or macro-bound, plus its consumers) as a draft under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT. TypeFeatures: Layer stays a closed union; the Host binding module is typed by backend arm.")
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_2 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-11-09T02:50Z", "org2", "org2 11-08 22:5x MODULE MAP v0 at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT\\base-map.tsv (kind, name, macro count, lines per top-level definition). base.spi has 916 lines and 91 top-level blocks: 59 definitions, 3 type aliases, 29 test blocks; 215 macro lines. Non-test: 44 macro-bound, 18 macro-free. Macro-bound top: try_unit 30, try_finally 26, from_pair 14, let' 12, (=.) 11, (<>.) 11, ^ 10, exn_switch 9, use 9, capture 9, pair_switch 8, new_pair 7, choice2_switch 6, null 6, exn 4. Macro-free: export, eval, flip, do, join_body, join_body_unit, the dotted arithmetic (+. -. *. /.), <>.., ++, pair, ||>, rust_quietly, rust_panic_text, try_unit_fsharp_python, try_unit'. FINDING: no file opens base with :<; it is in scope through the package prelude, so a split must keep every name resolvable from the prelude (re-export from the new modules, or put the new modules in the prelude), or every consumer in every repo breaks. That's the main risk; the consumer gate in lib_land_window must cover the other repos. NEXT STEP: decide the target modules (proposal: base_host for the 44 macro-bound with Host layer; keep the 18 macro-free in Std as base_ops or fold them into existing named modules), then check whether the prelude can hold base_host without a cycle (Core must not import Host per the rank rule, so check what Core modules use from base first).")
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_3 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-11-09T02:54Z", "org2", "org2 11-08 22:5x CYCLE CHECK: none of the 28 Core-layer modules (per layers.spi) uses any macro-bound base name (word match over the 44 names; operators =. and <>. checked separately: 0 hits). So moving the 44 macro-bound definitions to a Host-layer base_host module creates no Core -> Host edge; the rank rule holds for Core. Evidence lists: core-uses-bound.txt (empty) and bound-names.txt beside base-map.tsv. Still to check before drafting: (1) Std modules that use the macro-bound names (expected: many; Std -> Host is allowed by rank? the import rule from LIB-LAYERS-2 decides); (2) how the prelude exposes base (package-level packages list vs an implicit open) so the split keeps names resolvable for other repos. NEXT STEP: read the Layer rank rule in layers.spi / kit standards to confirm Std -> Host is permitted, then draft base_host.spi + the slimmed base.spi in the drafts dir.")
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_4 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-11-09T03:48Z", "org2", "org2 11-08 23:5x Blocker: the layer model, not the code. kit ranks Std (2) below Host (4), so Std may not import Host (UpwardImport), and macros are allowed only above Std. Splitting base's macros into a Host module would turn every Std caller into an UpwardImport, which relocates violations instead of shrinking them. Waits on a model decision (rig / user): a layer for multi-backend primitive bindings (proposal 'Prim', between Core and Std, macros allowed only when every backend has an arm). Resume when that decision lands; the module map (base-map.tsv) and the Core-safety check stay valid for it.")
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_5 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-11-09T03:52Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 04:1x rig DECISION on the layer model (asked by org2): ADOPT a Prim layer. Multi-backend primitive bindings (base, sm', date_time, am', convert, threading, async ...) are one portable API with a backend_switch arm per backend; they fit no layer today (Std forbids macros, Host takes one backend, Std may not open Host), so the 2,242 macros-in-Std violations measured a taxonomy gap, not defects. Model change in rig_standards_layers_model (live tree, unstaged, rides TRAIN42): lib_layer gains Prim, ranked Core 1 < Prim 2 < Std 3 < Os 4 < Host 5 < Ecosystem 6, so Std opening Prim is a downward import; macros_allowed Prim; tests: Std opens Prim, Prim cannot open Std, Prim opens Core, layer_named Prim, macros in Prim only. SLICE 2 (org2's condition, not built yet): a Prim macro counts as a violation unless it sits in a backend_switch that has an arm for every backend, so the metric then reports real defects (single-backend macros in portable modules, missing arms). Until slice 2, Prim macros are allowed unconditionally; that looseness is recorded here on purpose. The lib layer table rows (LayerRow module, Prim) are org's lib edit after TRAIN42 installs. Unblocks: this item, once the table rows move.")
inl note_of_lib_layers_3_base_split_6 () = Note ("LIB-LAYERS-3-BASE-SPLIT", 6u32, "2026-11-09T03:56Z", "org2", "org2 11-09 00:0x RESUME CONDITION set (the model decision has landed in the tree): rig adopted the taxonomy fix; rig_standards_layers_model gains Prim, ranked Core 1 < Prim 2 < Std 3 < Os 4 < Host 5 < Ecosystem 6, so Std -> Prim imports downward and Prim allows macros (tests: rank, Prim -> Std refused, Prim -> Core allowed, the macro rule). It rides TRAIN42. Slice 2 (rig): a Prim macro counts as a violation unless its backend_switch has an arm for every backend; until then Prim macros pass. This item's scope changes accordingly: NO code split of base. Instead, move the multi-backend primitive modules' rows in spiral/lib/spiral/layers.spi to Prim (base, sm', sm'_real, date_time, am', convert, threading, async, resultm, listm', optionm', seq, ... chosen by 'own macros are all-backend switches'), landed through lib_land_window; the expected result is the Std violation count dropping by those modules' macros, for the right reason. Resume when TRAIN42 is installed on the box.")
inl cells_port_per_test_protocol () = Task ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", "Cells uses pack63c's per-test compile protocol (handoff from compiler, CELLS-MODULE-SCOPE-TESTS): send the original file + test name (CLI --test <name>, daemon compile<TAB>backend<TAB>input<TAB>output<TAB>test<TAB>name) instead of assembling prelude + cell programs with a --package header", Story, P1, D4, 90u32, Active, "", "team:microblog,cells,cells-in-spiral,compiler,dialect", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol () = Planning ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_1 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 1u32, "2026-11-09T02:49Z", "microblog", "Handoff 11-09 from compiler: pack63c is installed on box/moon/vultr/ganymede; a test compiles inside its own file's module and package (nearest manifest) with the file's top-level opens, so it needs no ///- --package . and no static prelude; only the selected test parses; builds without --test stay byte-identical; a static or missing test returns \"Cannot find test <name> in file <file>\". rig adds NoPackageDot after the switch. TOUCH POINTS: compiler_client.ex:668 builds compile<TAB>backend<TAB>spi<TAB>rs<TAB>timeout<TAB>mtime<TAB>pid; compiler_daemon.ex:822 forwards a 4-field compile to the server; notebook.ex assembles programs (static prelude filter :601, @static_prelude :688, canon_flags --package :1217); notebook_cli.ex:77 writes the --package header; memo.ex:527 keys on --package flags. RULES: the label is CELLS-PORT-* so the .ex ratchet allows the edit, but the move should SHRINK Elixir (program assembly and prelude handling go away); memo keys must still change when the test's file or package changes (UNIT-INTERFACE-HASH used-set idea). DoD: Cells compiles every test through --test with the same verdicts as today on the full Cells suite and an x verify, and .ex lines go down. NEXT STEP: design the request shape (extend the daemon line with test fields, keeping the old shape for non-test builds) and the memo key, box-side.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_2 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 2u32, "2026-11-09T02:51Z", "microblog", "DESIGN fixed by compiler 11-09 (Program.fs:1183-1193): (1) the Cells client->daemon line is Cells' own: append <TAB>test<TAB><name> to the existing 7 fields. (2) The daemon->compiler line must be exactly 3, 4 or 6 fields: compile<TAB><backend><TAB><input><TAB><output><TAB>test<TAB><name>; field 5 is the literal test; any other count = invalid request; so compiler_daemon.ex:822 sends 6 fields when there is a test name, else the 4 it sends today. (3) The reply is identical for test and non-test builds: spiral-session<TAB>ok<TAB><pid><TAB><bytes><TAB><binding><TAB><revisionMode><TAB><backend> or spiral-session<TAB>error<TAB><pid><TAB><message>. (4) A static or missing test = error \"Cannot find test <name> in file <file>\". (5) The output holds the test promoted to the entry term: run it like a main build. (6) A warm server reopens the file when the selection changes (no restart). (7) Memo key: + test name, - --package. NEXT STEP: implement (1)+(2)+(7), then remove notebook.ex's prelude/--package assembly, with the .ex count going down.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_3 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 3u32, "2026-11-09T03:10Z", "microblog", "SLICE 1 DONE (unstaged, behaviour-neutral: no caller sets :test yet): compiler_client.ex request_line appends <TAB>test<TAB><name> when req.test is set; compiler_daemon.ex parse_request reads test from the trailing pair and no longer mistakes the literal test for a pin; session forwards the 6-field compile<TAB>backend<TAB>input<TAB>output<TAB>test<TAB>name when present, else the 4 fields as before. Diff +10/-3 in 2 files (CELLS-PORT label allows it; slice 2 removes the larger program assembly). mix compile exit 0. TRAP RECORDED: do NOT run mix format on Cells files: Cells' Elixir is not mix-format clean (long lines), and a format pass rewrote both whole files (+244/-60), which the .ex ratchet refuses; restored from HEAD plus only these edits. NEXT STEP: slice 2: notebook.ex sends the original file + test name (req.test) instead of assembling prelude + cell with a --package header, and memo.ex keys on the test name (drop --package); then the Cells land window with a full-suite proof on a node.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_4 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 4u32, "2026-11-09T03:13Z", "microblog", "SLICE 2 DESIGN (box read 11-09): the call chain is notebook.ex:971 step.program |> Spiral.Cells.run(opts) -> cells.ex:321 writes prepared.code to a temp spi -> compile/10 (cells.ex:541) does PROGRAM SURGERY: detects a test-block main via \"spiral_cells_test ()\", appends \"0i32\", compiles with a 2-strategy retry (:550, :559 show_value), and splices the inl spiral_cells_value wrapper (:736) -> compile/11 (:566) -> compile_default (:741) -> shared_compile (:763, the daemon) or one_shot_compile (process). PLAN: (a) notebook.ex at :971 adds test_source: <notebook path> and test_name: <from cell_source \":# <name> =\" header> to opts for TEST cells only; (b) compile/10 gets a per-test branch BEFORE the surgery: when opts has both, compile test_source with test: name through compile_default (shared_compile puts test into the CompilerClient request; one_shot_compile adds --test <name>), with show_value false and no 0i32 append, since the compiler promotes the test to the entry term; (c) the memo key stays on the assembled program text (derived from the same source, so it changes exactly when the test or its file does) plus the test name; (d) the program assembly (static prelude, --package canon flags, notebook_cli header) is deleted only after (b) is proven, which is where the .ex count drops. PROOF needed: the full Cells suite (mix test --max-cases 4 --timeout 900000 on a node, 3 h) plus one x verify, verdict-identical to today. Risk: verdict drift where the surgery mattered (show_value cells, test-block mains).")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_5 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 5u32, "2026-11-09T04:36Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 slice 2 landed in the live spiral tree (unstaged, compiles), opt-in via SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST=1, so the default requests and memo keys are byte-identical. notebook dispatch_spiral passes :test_source (the cell's .spi path) next to :test_name. cells.ex shared_compile wraps the request in per_test_request: when the flag is on and the step is a named test of a .spi with no _real sibling, spi becomes the source file and the request carries test, which compiler_client already sends as the 6-field line (compile, backend, input, output, test, name; pack63c). memo.ex key adds {:per_test, test_name} to the base only when the flag is on. The one-shot (process) compiler path is unchanged (falls back to the generated program). Proof queued: job-1794198981519-cells-per-test-ab on moon after the x verify. A/B notebook --gate on mount_text.spi, manifest.spi (Gleam tests) and x resume.spi (Rust tests), flag off vs on, same tree (mirror cellspt1 at bf5cc30a + live edits, digest BCB3B8A8), compare exit and test counts per file. A green A/B there opens the full Cells-suite run with the flag on; flipping the default comes after that.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_6 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 6u32, "2026-11-09T05:19Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 acceptance test added (compiler's report): lib/spiral/async.spi's 3 Rust tests have been red in spiral CI since 11-07. They call new_future_move, async_of_future and run_synchronously, which are defined above them in the same file, but the file's ':# async static' prelude makes each cell prelude + body only, hence ETY-UnboundVariable. Under the per-test protocol they compile inside async.spi's module and should pass. The queued A/B (job-1794198981519, script updated on moon before start) now runs async.spi too, and its verdict excludes async.spi from the off/on equality and records acceptance (expected: control red, candidate green). If that holds, flipping the per-test default also turns spiral CI green on those 3.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_7 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 7u32, "2026-11-09T08:44Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 A/B PROVEN (moon, job-1794212718688-microblog-head2, run per-test-ab-20261109042521; mirrors cellspt1 spiral + xqueue1 x, pack63c). Flag off -> on: async.spi 0/3 -> 3/3 (compiler's acceptance: the 3 Rust tests red in spiral CI since 11-07 now pass in their module scope). manifest.spi 10/10 and x resume.spi 4/4 are identical in both arms. mount_text.spi is 0/3 in both arms (ETY-UnboundVariable at 4:13, pre-existing and independent of the flag; reported to compiler). The opt-in path therefore fixes the target case with no collateral change on these files. Next: a full Cells suite with the flag on (node), then flip the default (drop the env gate), which also turns spiral CI's async.spi green. (The first head run's red was my wrapper's missing --path, so it's void.)")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_8 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 8u32, "2026-11-09T08:50Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 ACCEPTANCE CASE ADDED (compiler): apps/cells/spi/mount_text.spi BEFORE compiler's 11-09 fix had no '///- --package .' header and no opens-only static block. The flag-ON arm still failed it (ETY-UnboundVariable at 4:13, reported as main.spi:4:13, i.e. the assembled cell program), so the per-test request was NOT used for its ///> gleam tests. Under per-test, a header-less file must still resolve its package (NoPackageDot is the point). Suspect: per_test_request's conditions (test_name, test_source, real) or shared_compiler? are false for that step; the error shape shows the compile went through the generated program. Repro: the pre-fix header-less mount_text.spi (git show HEAD:apps/cells/spi/mount_text.spi). Before flipping the default: probe which condition fails (log per_test_request's decision per step) and add this file to the A/B set.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_9 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 9u32, "2026-11-09T09:12Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 more NoPackageDot acceptance cases (compiler fixed them in the tree by adding headers; the HEAD versions are the header-less repros): apps/cells/spi/run_result.spi (no ///- line), source_blocks.spi and verdicts.spi (no header, no opens-only static block), plus mount_text.spi. Under the per-test flag, all four HEAD originals should compile in their module scope without the header. Compiler also found 2 real defects once they compiled: source_blocks expected b5/l5 for a 6-byte literal (now b6), and run_result's ///> zig tests reach the Gleam-only text module (retargeted to gleam).")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_10 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 10u32, "2026-11-09T09:59Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 CORRECTION to notes 8-9 (my error; I had read only the control log). Per-test WAS applied to the header-less mount_text.spi in the flag-ON arm. Its error was 'File mount_text has a type error ... mount_text.spi:115:65 ETY-UnboundVariable': _assert_eq in a test body, compiled inside module mount_text. The control arm's error was the different 4:13 / main.spi one from prelude+body assembly. The cause is the file's missing ':< testing' opens, not a per-test gap. compiler confirmed with --test on ganymede: in the NoPackageDot target shape (opens-only static block kept, only the ///- line dropped) all 3 tests compile. So there is NO per-test gap for header-less files. The right acceptance case keeps the ':# <name> static = / :< testing' block and drops only '///- --package .'. Notes 8-9's 'per_test_request skips header-less files' is withdrawn. The SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST_TRACE probe stays in as a cheap diagnostic. Next unchanged: full Cells suite with the flag on, then flip the default.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_11 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 11u32, "2026-11-09T10:45Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "TYPEFEATURES: the per-test request is an extra field on the compile request map (test name and source path); a typed request record replaces the map when Cells moves to rig (CELLS-TYPED-UNITS). NEXT STEP: a full Cells suite with SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST=1 on a node, then flip the default.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_12 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 12u32, "2026-11-09T13:32Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 09:4x microblog: placement agreed with org2. Europa if it drains, racing moon after the sim verify; first to start wins, never a double run. STAGED on europa: /home/remote/cellspt-eu/spiral = the box's spiral working tree (git ls-files tar, 10.2 MB, carrying the unstaged Cells per-test changes; 91 MB unpacked), runner /home/remote/stage/per-test-suite.sh: SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST=1, a private MIX_BUILD_PATH, deps.get, compile, then mix test --max-cases 2 --timeout 900000, streamed line by line to the run log with failure/summary lines echoed for kit job tail. Box poller (scratchpad pertest-poller.sh, background): every 5 min it checks that label x-cells-pertest is not already in job list, that tibia's qat_arm processes are gone ([q]at_arm pattern, so it never matches its own shell) and that loadavg-1 < 4. Then it submits ONCE: kit job start --on vultr x-cells-pertest 21600000 systemd-run --scope -p MemoryMax=1536M nice -n 10 bash per-test-suite.sh. It gives up at 20:30. Teardown after: /home/remote/cellspt-eu, the stage run dir after the log is read, procs, ssh. Read against the known mirror gaps (LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3, JSON parity floor); green means flip the default.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_13 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 13u32, "2026-11-09T14:24Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 10:2x microblog, CI census (read-only gh): this item is now on the critical path of TWO red public CIs. (1) i574n/spiral gh-pages red 11-08 21:34 and 11-09 02:22. The only failing notebook is lib/spiral/async.spi, 0/3 (run_synchronously_drives_a_future_to_its_value, sleep_then_ignore_completes, catch_rust_turns_a_panic_into_an_error; FatalError at async.spi:394:5, main.spi:6:5, ETY-Unbound...), lib totals 500 passed / 3 failed. That is exactly this item's acceptance case: the per-test A/B (11-09) turned async 0/3 into 3/3. So spiral CI turns green when the per-test protocol is ON by default and pushed. (2) i574n/dice gh-pages red 11-09 02:31: 2 'fails' tests (a_raw_number_is_not_a_bound, a_source_without_draw_face_cannot_roll, zig) whose headers the 11-08 formatter respelled to 'fails ETY - TermError ...'. Cells HEAD does not parse the spaced code; the fix is the unstaged notebook.ex expectation regex in the box tree ((\\S+(?:\\s+-\\s+\\S+)*) normalized to ETY-TermError). Both fixes are in the box's unstaged Cells; they need the europa full suite green, then the default flip, then the user's push.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_14 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 14u32, "2026-11-09T17:54Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 13:5x microblog: europa timing per tibia: qat_arm_d ends ~16:45 local (16x16000 steps; the 8000-step arm took 6.4 h), then tibia tears down /root/tibia-qat. The box poller (until 20:30) then submits x-cells-pertest, so the ~6 h suite (2 cores, --max-cases 2) ends around 22:45, AFTER this run's 21:00 end. FOR THE NEXT SESSION: read /home/remote/stage/per-test-suite-<stamp>/mix-test.log on europa (vultr alias) plus 'kit job list' label x-cells-pertest; compare failures against the known mirror gaps (LispTest x2, DocumentTest x3, JSON parity floor) and CELLS-SUITE-FLAKY-UNDER-LOAD's list; green -> flip Directives.per_test_compile? to default-on (System.get_env(\"SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST\") != \"0\"). Then tear down /home/remote/cellspt-eu and the stage run dir. The mirror also carries: memo nil-opt fix, Manifest.cached, explicit-target refusal, cell_budget wiring + reference fix, text.spi remodel, compiler_bounds hardening.")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_15 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 15u32, "2026-11-09T19:35Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 15:3x microblog: EUROPA SUITE, started earlier than planned (tibia's QAT ended 15:2x; the poller submitted at 15:26). Two false starts, both MY setup bugs, not Cells reds: (1) x-cells-pertest exit 3 in 10 s: compile failed, 'Package .../deps/The-Spiral-Language/VS Code Plugin/core is not loaded'. The cellspt-eu mirror had no deps/ (my staging tar excluded it); fixed the europa convention way, deps -> /home/remote/git/spiral/deps symlink, like every other mirror there. (2) x-cells-pertest2 exit 2 in 104 s, 60 failures, 46 of them 'Spiral.Cells.Result.json_safe/1 is undefined': a MIXED snapshot. I had synced notebook.ex alone at 13:00, and it already carried another lane's 12:06 json_safe call, while result.ex (which defines json_safe) stayed old. Fix: whole-tree hash diff box vs mirror. Cells: 9 files behind (result.ex, compiler_daemon.ex, 3 tests, 4 spi scripts), synced, apps/cells lib+test+spi now IDENTICAL (156 files). Lib/spiral: 9 changed + 6 box-only (regex_*, bench_model) synced, stale physics.spi moved to cellspt-eu/stale; lib sources IDENTICAL (179). Expect 2 env reds regardless: the Lisp tests need target/build/lisp/spl2spi (apps/lisp/build.ps1), absent on this mirror. NOW RUNNING: job-1794252900792-x-cells-pertest3 (europa, 6 h cap, MemoryMax 1536M, nice 10), watcher armed. TEARDOWN NOTE: cellspt-eu/spiral/deps is a SYMLINK to the shared deps; unlink it first (rm the link, never rm -r through it).")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_16 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 16u32, "2026-11-09T19:51Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-09 15:5x microblog: EUROPA SUITE GREEN (known mirror gaps only) -> PER-TEST FLIPPED DEFAULT-ON (unstaged): Directives.per_test_compile? is now System.get_env(\"SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST\") != \"0\" (opt out with =0). No test pinned the old default; the memo key already carries :per_test, so entries re-key once instead of mixing modes. EVIDENCE, same host A/B (europa, SPIRAL_CELLS_PER_TEST=1, --max-cases 2): HEAD bf5cc30a control 381/395 (14 red); this tree, run 3, 387/401 (14 red); after 3 heals, run 5 job-1794253712022-x-cells-pertest5: 395/398, 1 skipped, 6 excluded, 3 red = the known mirror gaps only (LispTest x2: target/build/lisp/spl2spi not built there; DocumentTest export: spiral CLI not built there). HEALS FOUND BY THE SUITE: (a) Memo.compiler_binaries_print opened files by their LOWERCASED names, so on case-sensitive Linux every stamp was :missing and a compiler core change never re-keyed the memo on any Linux node (3 memo tests red on HEAD too); fixed: key and dedupe by the lowercased name, open by the real basename (Windows keys unchanged). (b) mount_text_parity_test's old-Elixir oracle used OS-dependent Path.basename/dirname (backslash is a separator only on Windows); made separator-neutral (3 Linux-only reds, also red on HEAD). (c) cells_test sibling-mount cell (line 375) had no ///> rust line: the one site my explicit-target sweep missed (2 reds, mine). (d) 3 sibling-repo corpus parity tests (json value, manifest path, manifest declares) tagged :sibling_repos, excluded when the parent dir holds no git checkouts (test_helper sibling_repos?), like :dice_corpus. Their size floors still fail closed where the corpus exists. NEXT: the spiral CI async 0/3 should go green once this is pushed (user's push).")
inl note_of_cells_port_per_test_protocol_17 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-PER-TEST-PROTOCOL", 17u32, "2026-11-10T09:39Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:4x GROOM: no code step left; the spiral CI async 0/3 turns green only after the user pushes spiral. Structured wait row: user:push-spiral.")
inl rig_omp_path_first_slowdown () = Task ("RIG-OMP-PATH-FIRST-SLOWDOWN", "kit path-first loads gcc 15.2 libgomp, about 4x slower than mingw 16.2 libgomp on OpenMP exes now that the 16.2 hang is gone", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "rig,openmp,box,perf,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rig_omp_path_first_slowdown () = Planning ("RIG-OMP-PATH-FIRST-SLOWDOWN", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rig_omp_path_first_slowdown_1 () = Note ("RIG-OMP-PATH-FIRST-SLOWDOWN", 1u32, "2026-11-09T04:36Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 00:5x rig, measured on the box with the transformer (Spiral C backend, 213 omp regions, gcc 15.2.0 -O2 -fopenmp, stories260K, OMP_NUM_THREADS=4, same exe each run): libgomp from mingw 16.2 first on PATH (interactive) 12.8 s; libgomp from gcc 15.2 first (kit path-first) 61.5 s direct and 47.2 s via kit job start; gcc 15.2 libgomp at BelowNormal 78.4 s. So the slowdown is the 15.2 runtime, not kit jobs or priority. Path-first was added (KIT-OMP-RUNTIME-PATH) because 16.2 libgomp HUNG 15.2-built closure-in-omp exes on 11-05; that hang no longer reproduces (closed today with evidence). Now path-first costs about 4x on OpenMP exes. DECISION NEEDED (rig can make it, it is rig's path-first file): drop the gcc path-first row so exes load the newest libgomp, OR keep it and build OMP exes with the matching 16.2 toolchain. Evidence first: rerun both arms 3x each with an A/A control, balanced order (memory ab-quiet-gate-per-run), before changing APPDATA kit path_first.spi. DoD: a measured choice recorded, path_first.spi changed or kept with the numbers. Next step: the 3x3 balanced runs on a quiet box.")
