inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_1 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 1u32, "2026-11-09T10:56Z", "compiler", "rig 11-09 star exchange: TRAIN42c's crate gate failed because lib sm'.trim on the Rust backend emits two scan helpers + a 95-line string_slice (~170 lines) where rust_std_string trim was one host call; rig restored the host call in kit. Relates to RUST-STRING-WRAPPER-INLINE (Paused). DoD: Rust codegen for trim/substring of an Rc<str> lowers to str::trim()/char-boundary slicing with identical results on the portable sm' tests (rust + c + gleam agree), and agile_task_creation-sized crates shrink by >= 150 lines. TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_2 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 2u32, "2026-11-09T11:05Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:1x compiler: done as a LIB arm, no compiler change needed. substring already lowers to slicing on Rust; the bloat was trim = trim_with [] -> the portable trim_start/trim_end loops. lib sm.trim now has a Rust run_target arm: Rc::<str>::from(input.trim_matches(|c| code point is 32/9/13/10)), i.e. exactly libs trim_chars set (space, tab, CR, LF), not str::trims Unicode White_Space, so results stay identical on every input (those ASCII bytes never occur inside a multibyte sequence). Code points instead of char literals because single quotes clash with $.. macro text. Other backends keep trim_with []. Tests: the trim test gains ///> rust; new trim_strips_space_tab_cr_lf_and_keeps_inner_text (rust + gleam). Measured on ganymede: a one-call trim programs Rust output is 98 -> 17 lines. Cells gate of sm.spi on a private lib copy running.")
inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_3 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 3u32, "2026-11-09T11:05Z", "compiler", "Next step: read the sm.spi Cells gate on ganymede (~/remote-stage/trimgate.log); if the trim tests pass on rust + gleam + zig and nothing else changed vs the known sm control (replace_agrees_on_c Pascal mojibake is pre-existing), land the lib edit (unstaged) and tell rig the Rust trim is back to near-host size.")
inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_4 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 4u32, "2026-11-09T11:39Z", "compiler", "GATED 11-09 11:36Z: lib sm.spi with the Rust trim arm + the two trim tests, Cells gate on ganymede (in-mirror swap with an EXIT trap; mirror restored, hash ba8b6988 verified): 84 executed, 84 passed, 0 failed, failed_targets {}. Rust one-call trim program 98 -> 17 lines. The lib edit is unstaged in spiral lib/spiral/sm.spi for landing. Told rig.")
inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_5 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 5u32, "2026-11-09T14:06Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 10:1x local rig, ANSWER to the lane:rig wait. Your Rust trim arm (lib sm'.spi, unstaged, Cells 84/84) is present in the box tree the trains snapshot. Rig's switch of agile_task_creation from rust_std_string.model.trim to sm'.trim (4 call sites, lines 67, 68, 99, 153) is BLOCKED BY SIZE, not by the lowering: task_creation.rs is 992 of 1000 lines in TRAIN42e's regen (moon), and lib trim is an inline := that expands per call site (~17 lines each), so even one site risks the gate that already stopped TRAIN42 and TRAIN42c. Filed RIG-TASK-CREATION-SPLIT (model/IO split first, then the switch). Elsewhere in kit the remaining rust_std_string uses are module opens over concat5/trim_end_matches, not trim calls; rig_experiments was healed today by moving experiment_plan to sm'.concat_list (std-string-use back to its baseline 6). Nothing else of yours waits on rig here: clearing the wait.")
inl note_of_rust_std_string_lowering_6 () = Note ("RUST-STD-STRING-LOWERING", 6u32, "2026-11-09T14:21Z", "compiler", "11-09 14:2x compiler: per-call-site cost MEASURED (ganymede, installed pack77): lowered sm.trim -> Rust program with 1 call 17 lines, 4 calls 23 lines = ~2 lines per extra call site (17 is one-time overhead); the old lib trim was ~40 lines per call (98 -> 218). So rigs estimate of ~17 per site (from the one-call total) overstated it ~8x; task_creations 4 sites would add ~4-8 lines vs the host trim. Told rig; ordering vs RIG-TASK-CREATION-SPLIT is rigs call. Compiler side complete.")
