inl zig_output_size_blowup () = Task ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", "Zig backend output is ~24% larger than C for tibia predict (10.7 MB) and its build needs > 6 GB (OOM on ganymede 4.76 GB and comet)", TaskKind, P2, D5, 70u32, Active, "", "team:compiler,compiler,zig,codegen,size,memory", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_output_size_blowup () = Planning ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_1 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 1u32, "2026-11-09T10:56Z", "compiler", "tibia 11-09 star exchange: predict.zig 10.7 MB, 24% larger than the C output for the same program; zig build OOM-killed on ganymede (4.76 GB peak before kill) and on comet (systemd-oomd SIGTERM rc 143 past ~6 GB). Next: diff a mid-size sample's Zig vs C by construct (struct copies, union payload, defer chains) to find the inflating shapes; measure zig build peak RSS per shape. DoD: a named shape fixed with Zig output within 10% of C on predict and its build under 4 GB. TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_2 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 2u32, "2026-11-09T11:21Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:2x compiler, first measurement (zero CPU: tracked sample outputs): over 227 samples with C > 2 KB the Zig/C byte ratio averages 1.56 (small samples dominated by preamble); over the 11 samples with C > 20 KB it is 1.22 (1.21-1.48), consistent with tibia predict 1.24. Worst large: typecheck_long_let_body 1.48 (73 KB vs 49 KB), and its 1600 dominant lines show the cause: Zig `const vN: i32 = vM +% @as(i32, 1);` vs C `int32_t vN = vM + 1l;`. With a typed operand on the other side, both the `: i32` annotation and the `@as(i32, ..)` around the literal are redundant in Zig (comptime_int coerces; the const infers its type), ~40% of each such line. Candidate fix: emit bare literals in binary ops whose other operand is a typed variable, and drop the const annotation when the RHS type is already determined. Caveat to measure before claiming the memory win: Zig build RSS is driven by semantic analysis of huge functions more than by text size, so the next step is to time zig build peak RSS on typecheck_long_let_body before and after a hand-edited lean version.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_3 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 3u32, "2026-11-09T11:22Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:2x compiler MEASURED (ganymede, zig build-exe -O ReleaseSafe, /usr/bin/time): typecheck_long_let_body original main.zig 73,004 B vs a lean rewrite of its 1600 `const vN: T = vM +% @as(T, k);` lines to `const vN = vM +% k;` 49,004 B (-33%): build 26.71 s / peak 336 MB vs 25.47 s / 336 MB, both binaries exit 236. So the literal/annotation noise is a TEXT-size issue only; it does not drive zig build time or memory. The >6 GB on tibia predict must come from another shape (huge single functions, deep inline chains, comptime work, union switch fan-out). Next step: get tibias predict.zig (10.7 MB) and bisect its peak RSS by function (zig build with -fno-emit-bin per extracted function group), then fix the dominant shape; the literal cleanup stays a cheap optional size win.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_4 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 4u32, "2026-11-09T11:25Z", "compiler", "11-09 11:3x compiler: ROOT CAUSE for tibia predict.zig (ganymede, all runs capped with ulimit -v 3.5 GB): Zig semantic analysis only (-fno-emit-bin) 32.9 s / 139 MB; full Debug build 9.5 s / 183 MB (binary 34.7 MB, ok). The >4.7 GB (OOM on ganymede, oomd SIGTERM on comet) is LLVM optimizing (-O ReleaseFast) two giant single functions: knight_turn_shared_346 = 90,673 lines (54% of 168,303) and foes_act_from_6 = 45,678 lines; the top 10 of 501 functions hold 90% of the lines. The partial evaluator inlines every callee into them. Text noise (redundant @as / annotations) is unrelated (measured, note 3). Fix directions: (a) user-side: join points (non-inlined helpers) inside those two hot paths; (b) compiler-side, the real item: automatic outlining in codegen when a function body passes a size threshold (cut at statement boundaries into join-point helpers), which bounds per-function optimizer cost on every LLVM/GCC backend. DoD rewritten: predict ReleaseFast peak < 2 GB with no user annotation; text-size cleanup optional.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_5 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 5u32, "2026-11-09T11:28Z", "compiler", "Design constraint measured (zero CPU, tracked sample C outputs): the largest function body in any compiler sample is 1,605 C lines (typecheck_long_let_body); the next is 398. An automatic outlining threshold of ~8,000 lines per emitted function would therefore never fire on the existing corpus (gate = residual-identical everywhere) while cutting tibia predicts 90,673- and 45,678-line bodies into bounded chunks. Next step: find in the codegen where a join points body is emitted (shared by C/Zig/Rust) and split an over-threshold body at top-level statement boundaries into helper functions that take the live variables as parameters.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_6 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 6u32, "2026-11-09T12:25Z", "compiler", "11-09 12:3x compiler: SHAPE of the giant body (knight_turn_shared_346, 90,673 lines, indent-depth profile): 27,548 lines (30%) are flat depth-1 statements; the other ~63k sit under ONE top-level control structure, nested 7-39 levels deep (mode around depth 20) -- a decision tree from inlined pattern matches. So chopping flat statement runs alone would bound only 30%. Design (revised): outline at BRANCH level -- any if/switch arm (or loop body) whose emitted size passes the threshold becomes a separate join-point function taking its live-in variables and returning the arms value; apply bottom-up so each outlined piece is itself bounded; flat runs chop as a second rule. Doing it on the typed IR (before backend codegen) gives C, Zig and Rust the same bound. Threshold ~8,000 lines never fires on the current corpus (largest sample body 1,605). Next: find where the partial evaluator emits match-tree branches (TyIf/TyIntSwitch/TyUnionUnbox arms) to place the size check.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_7 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 7u32, "2026-11-09T12:30Z", "compiler", "11-09 12:3x compiler IMPLEMENTATION PLAN (read from the IR, spiral_compiler.fs ~9265-9310 + PartEvalResult ~9913): branches live in TypedOp as TyIf(cond, tr, fl), TyIntSwitch(v, arms, default), TyUnionUnbox(vs, union, Map case -> (data, TypedBind[]), default) and TyWhile; methods are JoinPointKey JPMethod((name, E), ConsedNode<RData[] * Ty[]>) registered in PartEvalResult.join_point_method (body TypedBind[] option, return Ty option), called by TyJoinPoint(key, TyV[] args), and every backend emits methods by iterating that table. Pass: after peval, before codegen, walk each method body bottom-up with a size estimate (bind count weighted by nested arms); an arm over the threshold is replaced by TyLocalReturnOp(TyJoinPoint(newKey, freeVars arm)) with newKey = JPMethod((synthetic name, a fresh unique E), hashcons(RData of the arms free TyVs, their Tys)) registered with body = arm (its TyLocalReturnData becomes the method return) and return type = the arms result Ty. Risks to test first: free-variable tags must equal the methods parameter tags as each backend derives them from the key; arms that mutate outer layouts (TyLayoutMutableSet) or contain TyFailwith keep semantics through the call; closures captured inside arms. Gate: threshold 8,000 = residual-identical corpus; then tibia predict ReleaseFast peak < 2 GB. Not started: needs a dedicated focused pass (several hours, peval-level). tibia is unblocked meanwhile (Debug builds 183 MB, manual join points).")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_8 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 8u32, "2026-11-09T12:49Z", "compiler", "11-09 12:5x compiler: tibia added join points (foe_acts, target_turn, patrol_turn, heal_gate as :~ in hunting_pack): C output byte-identical, 7.16 -> 6.13 MB; Zig 10.67 -> 9.39 MB; largest function 90,673 -> 43,735 lines (foe_acts_7; then target_turn 35,920, knight_turn_shared 33,643, patrol_turn 22,383). ReleaseFast re-measure on ganymede: under ulimit -v 3.5 GB it aborted (signal 6) at 2.89 GB RSS after 102 s; under a cgroup cap (systemd-run MemoryMax=4500M, swap 0) it was terminated at the cap (exit 143). So still > 4.5 GB: LLVM ReleaseFast cost tracks several 20-44k-line bodies, not only the single largest. Measurement lesson: ulimit -v caps ADDRESS SPACE and aborts LLVM early; use a cgroup MemoryMax scope to cap resident memory safely on a shared node. Next (tibia): split to <= ~10k lines per function; (compiler) branch-level auto-outlining per note 6.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_9 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 9u32, "2026-11-09T12:53Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:0x compiler DESIGN KEY (read from codegen): every backend emits a method lazily via codegenMethodBody env backend jp_body key = env.join_point_method.[jp_body].[key] and names its PARAMETERS from rdata_free_vars(key args) = the (tag, ty) of each ReV leaf. peval keys store positional indices (data_to_rdata: ReV(hc (call_args.Count-1, ty))) and rename the body to match; an outlined arm instead gets a key whose ReV leaves carry the free variables ORIGINAL tags, so the generated parameters are exactly the v<tag> names the unchanged arm body already uses: NO IR renaming pass needed, and the arms own locals keep their tags (same counter space as the enclosing method, so no clash). Registration: add (backend ConsedNode of the enclosing method, a synthetic unique E) -> (Dictionary key -> (Some arm body, Some arm result Ty, Some \"<method>_arm<n>\"), HashConsTable) to env.join_point_method; the call site is TyJoinPoint(JPMethod(jp_body, key), freeVarTyVs) wrapped as the arms new single statement (TyLocalReturnOp for tail arms, TyLet for value arms). Remaining unknowns to check in code: how the arm result Ty is read off its final TyLocalReturnData/Op, whether any backend assumes 0..n-1 parameter tags (stack-slot sizing), and the hopacsota PartEvalSeams view of join_point_method (pass runs after peval, single-threaded).")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_10 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 10u32, "2026-11-09T13:13Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:1x compiler: (1) tibia round 2 (13 more :~ helpers; C byte-identical; Zig 7.84 MB; largest fn 22,586) BUILDS with -O ReleaseFast on ganymede under a 4.5 GB cgroup cap: 140.6 s, peak 3.02 GB, exit 0, binary 10.0 MB (was OOM > 4.76 GB). tibia is unblocked. (2) COMPILER AUTO-OUTLINING implemented as draft 1-outline-large-arms.kitdo (opt-in SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=<binds>, backends C + Zig; default off = byte-identical): after peval, every method body is walked bottom-up; an if / int-switch / union-unbox arm over the limit (and not calling its own method, not containing TyBackend, with a typeable result) becomes a synthetic join-point method. Two bugs found and fixed on the way: free vars must be order-sensitive (a loop variable used then re-assigned inside the arm stays free), and the body must be RENAMED to pevals positional convention (C names method parameters v0..vn-1, not by key tags; free vars -> 0..n-1, arm-internal tags shifted by +n). Smoke on ganymede with SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=5: native_string_utf8_normalization_bounded_source (13 arms) and native_cube_paced_stream_direct (21 arms) give the same exit codes on C and Zig and the same C stdout as unoutlined. Next: stress suite (-Backend C,Zig -Native with a tiny limit vs none), then predict.zig original with SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT under the cap.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_11 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 11u32, "2026-11-09T13:29Z", "compiler", "Decision 11-09 13:3x: the literal-cast text cleanup is DROPPED for now. The Zig backend prints every integer literal as @as(T, k) through one literal printer (spiral_compiler.fs ~16215-16232); omitting it safely needs operand context in every binary-op case (a bare literal is only fine when the other operand is a typed runtime value), and the measurement (note 3) showed it saves text only (-33%), with no build time or memory gain. The memory fix is outlining (join points now; SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT pass under test).")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_12 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 12u32, "2026-11-09T13:37Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:4x compiler: AUTO-OUTLINING STRESS-PROVEN (ganymede, same outlining DLL both arms = pack76 + 1-outline-large-arms.kitdo): -Suite all -Backend C,Zig -Native, control (no limit) vs stress (SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=20, forcing outlining on every sample with branchy bodies): 1,685 rows, compile-status diffs 0, native diffs 0, EXIT diffs 0, STDOUT diffs 0; generated code changed in 118 rows (where arms were outlined); both arms native 1233 ran, oracle DISAGREE 0. So the pass preserves behaviour on C and Zig. Next: (a) tibia predict source with SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT ~ 2,000 binds and no manual join points -> ReleaseFast peak under the cgroup cap; (b) make it a pack candidate (default off, so the default suite is residual-identical by construction); (c) then consider a default threshold that never fires on the corpus (largest sample body 1,605 C lines).")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_13 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 13u32, "2026-11-09T13:46Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:5x compiler: AUTO-OUTLINING ON REAL CODE (tibia predict, current source with tibias join points, Zig, ganymede, ReleaseFast under a 4.5 GB cgroup): SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT is in binds, not lines -- 2000 moved 16 arms and left the largest function at 16,676 lines; 400 moved 104 arms (largest 9,386 lines); 150 moved 299 (largest 7,743). Build: unoutlined 109.3 s / peak 2.35 GB; limit 400 106.3 s / peak 1.90 GB (-19% memory, slightly faster). So outlining stacks on top of manual join points and brings predict under 2 GB. Calibration for a default: bind limits must be tuned in emitted-size units (a corpus where the largest sample body is 1,605 C lines); next = check limit 400 against the corpus (how many sample arms it moves) before proposing it as default; meanwhile it ships opt-in.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_14 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 14u32, "2026-11-09T13:52Z", "compiler", "11-09 13:5x compiler: SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=400 NEVER FIRES ON THE CORPUS: -Suite all -Backend C,Zig compile-only with limit 400 vs the no-limit control: 1,685 rows, compile-status diffs 0, generated residual changed 0 (the native/exit \"diffs\" in that comparison are only because this arm skipped -Native). Yet on tibia predict it moves 104 arms and cuts the ReleaseFast peak 2.35 -> 1.90 GB. So 400 binds is a corpus-safe DEFAULT candidate for C + Zig (residual-identical everywhere today, bounds tibia-scale bodies). Next: a pack with the pass + default 400 for C/Zig (env 0 disables), gated by -Suite all (expect 0 residual diffs) + the forced-outlining stress already green, then tell tibia. Wait: train:TRAIN42e (moon) before the next pack ships.")
inl note_of_zig_output_size_blowup_15 () = Note ("ZIG-OUTPUT-SIZE-BLOWUP", 15u32, "2026-11-09T13:54Z", "compiler", "pack77 candidate = pack76 + 1-outline-large-arms.kitdo with DEFAULT 400 binds for C + Zig (SPIRAL_OUTLINE_LIMIT=0 disables, any other value overrides; core 2cc4470f). Gate launched on ganymede 13:54Z (~/remote-stage/pack77/p77.log, suite vs pack76; the printed label says pack75 but the baseline file is pack76s suite): expect compile-status 0 / residual 0, since 400 never fires on the corpus. Wait: job ganymede:pack77-gate.")
inl kit_wip_todo_markers_m1 () = Task ("KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS-M1", "KIT-WIP-TODO-MARKERS milestone 1: todos box timing A/B against agile list on the live registry with the installed path-first scan", TaskKind, P1, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "kit,todo,wip,agile,wipe-redo,team:rig", "current", "current")
