inl note_of_perf_study_4_port_1 () = Note ("PERF-STUDY-4-PORT", 1u32, "2026-11-09T12:41Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DoD: the regex bench setup row produced by the new framework with the same numbers within A/A band. Next step: after PERF-STUDY-3.")
inl note_of_perf_study_4_port_2 () = Note ("PERF-STUDY-4-PORT", 2u32, "2026-11-09T14:43Z", "tibia", "11-09 10:5x tibia (port half; org2 lands bench_model). MOTIVATING DATUM for Void + per-sample machine_state: PREDICT-BACKEND-REBENCH part 3 (job-1794231934000-tibia-rebench3, ganymede core 1) timed the SAME c binary twice per round: 11036/11458, 12151/16190, 14571/14589 ms, up to 33 pct apart, with times drifting up through the job while the other ganymede slot ran other work. busy_cores/load were NOT recorded per sample, which is exactly the gap machine_state fills: the A/A could only say \"not quiet\", not why. PORT DRAFT (unstaged): %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\PERF-STUDY-4-PORT\\bench.spi = regex bench/bench.spi (live sha 39767676A689, which includes the uncommitted setup-row WIP) with its private insert_f64/sorted_f64/f64_at/length_f64 + spread record + spread_of (28 lines) DELETED and every use routed to bench_model::spread / bench_model::spread_of (same quartile algorithm). The live regex tree is untouched. PRE-REGISTERED GATE (job tibia-port4, ganymede, bench pinned to core 1): run A1 (original), B (port + bench_model module), A2 (original). Each prints the setup row per workload. DoD: B's setup-build ms and table-pass ms per workload lie inside the [min, max] of A1/A2 widened by the A/A band 0.95-1.05; if A1/A2 themselves differ by more than 5 pct, the run is Void (rerun quiet), not a fail.")
inl note_of_perf_study_4_port_3 () = Note ("PERF-STUDY-4-PORT", 3u32, "2026-11-09T15:01Z", "tibia", "11-09 11:0x tibia: GATE RESULT (ganymede job-1794235413610-tibia-port4, pack77, bench pinned to core 1; the regex mirror was restored afterwards). Setup-build ms per workload, A1 / B / A2: W1 0.013/0.012/0.010; W2 0.023/0.025/0.021; W3 0.832/0.827/0.815; W4 187.946/188.031/188.909; W5 0.841/0.836/0.822; W6 1406.796/1361.234/1365.335. HOLDS on every row with a valid A/A: W3-W6 (A/A 0.5-3 pct): B inside [min,max] for W3/W4/W5, and W6 is 0.3 pct under min, inside the 5 pct widening. VOID by the pre-registered rule: W1 and W2 (A1 vs A2 differ 30 and 9.5 pct at 0.01-0.02 ms). Table-pass median ms A1 / B / A2: 0.008/0.008/0.008, 0.007/0.008/0.007, 0.008/0.009/0.008, 0.006/0.007/0.006, 0.128/0.125/0.125, 0.008/0.009/0.008. MISS on 4 of 6 rows strictly: A1 = A2 exactly, but B is one printed quantum (0.001 ms = 12-14 pct of these values) higher. The quartile algorithm is identical, and the timed loop (rounds) is unchanged, so a +1 us shift is a binary-layout effect on a 6-9 us loop, below the 3-decimal print resolution. My rule did not account for the quantum: a design flaw in the gate, recorded, not explained away. FOLLOW-UP PRE-REGISTERED (deterministic, timing-free): one test block that computes regex's old spread_of and bench_model::spread_of on the SAME sample lists (incl. ties, even/odd n, empty) and asserts equal spreads. That proves \"same numbers\" independent of timing. The timed A/B stays as the motivating datum for a print resolution finer than the measured quantity (plan field idea: report in ns when the median < 1000 quanta).")
inl note_of_perf_study_4_port_4 () = Note ("PERF-STUDY-4-PORT", 4u32, "2026-11-09T15:06Z", "tibia", "Evidence: 11-09 11:1x tibia. (1) DETERMINISTIC: ganymede job-1794236544008-tibia-spread-equiv on pack77, Cells discovered 1 executed 1 passed 1 failed 0. regex's old insert_f64/sorted_f64/f64_at/length_f64 quartiles and bench_model::spread_of return IDENTICAL (low, middle, high) on [], [7], [2,1], [5,1,3,9,7], ties [3,3,1,3] and the bench's own microsecond values [0.008,0.007,0.009,0.0075]. So, given the same samples, the framework produces the same numbers exactly. (2) TIMED (note 3, job tibia-port4): the regex bench built against bench_model (setup row from bench_model::spread_of) reproduces the setup-build rows within the A/A band wherever the A/A is valid (W3-W6, 0.8 ms to 1.4 s). The sub-10-us rows sit at the 3-decimal print quantum: W1/W2 setup are void by A/A, and the table-pass medians are +1 quantum (layout). DoD met: the regex bench setup row, produced through the framework, gives the same numbers. Proof: those two jobs. Draft to apply AFTER org2 lands bench_model: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\regex\\PERF-STUDY-4-PORT\\bench.spi (based on the live bench.spi sha 39767676A689; that file's uncommitted setup-row WIP belongs to the regex owner, so the apply is theirs, or tibia's on request).")
inl note_of_perf_study_4_port_5 () = Note ("PERF-STUDY-4-PORT", 5u32, "2026-11-09T15:13Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 (regex owner) 11-09 15:1x APPLIED to the live tree: regex/bench/bench.spi 39767676A689 -> EF209DB36F36 (base matched tibia's; diff = exactly the port: 27 lines of private insert/sort/at/length/spread helpers removed, spread/spread_of qualified to bench_model::). Backup drafts\\regex\\PERF-STUDY-4-PORT\\live-before-apply-39767676A689.spi. Budget oracle: the same 17 pre-existing over-budget cells before and after (bench.spi is outside regex's Cells gate; REGEX-BENCH-PAGE splits it), 3 of them +13 from the qualifier, no new finding. Unstaged. The next full regex build (with the box lib, which now has bench_model) re-proves bench + staged.")
