inl note_of_kit_idiomatic_core_m3_1 () = Note ("KIT-IDIOMATIC-CORE-M3", 1u32, "2026-11-09T16:25Z", "kit-split", "split from KIT-IDIOMATIC-CORE by kit agile split (milestone 3). DoD: the staged_write family with its receipts, proven in the parent item notes. NEXT STEP: read the parent latest note for this milestone.")
inl note_of_kit_idiomatic_core_m3_2 () = Note ("KIT-IDIOMATIC-CORE-M3", 2u32, "2026-11-09T21:53Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig, DONE (unstaged; the M2 shape, beside read.spi in rig_do_algebra): src/rig_do_algebra/write.spi, the staged_write family. TYPEFEATURES: write_statement receipt GADT: Edit (path, old, new) -> edited { path; replaced }, Write (path, text, write_mode CreateOnly | ReplaceOnly) -> written { path; bytes; created }, Copy (from, into) -> copied; staged r = Staged (receipt, next tree) | Refused reason, so stage files statement returns exactly the receipt its statement names or a refusal, over the same in-memory tree_file list as the read family; tree_after returns the unchanged tree on a refusal, which is what makes a multi-statement program all-or-nothing. Refusals: an edit whose old text matches 0 or 2+ times (exactly 1 required), CreateOnly on an existing file, ReplaceOnly on a missing one, a copy from a missing file. proof: notebook gate green on ganymede (write 1/1, read 1/1 alongside): one replacement staged, a created file after it, an ambiguous edit refusing with the tree unchanged, create never overwriting, a copy. Two Spiral traps hit on the way: exists is a keyword like open, and a :: GADT case using a type parameter in its payload needs its own \\* r. binder.")
