inl note_of_mega_prototypes_from_repos_5 () = Note ("MEGA-PROTOTYPES-FROM-REPOS", 5u32, "2026-11-09T17:22Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 17:2x DONE. Census (edition 2 prototype/instance + edition 3 :?/:+, .spi under lib, regex, fiscal/src, tibia/src, x/apps, html, dice, alphabet, kit/src): instances per prototype, top: signal_get 8, purpose_code 8, then 14 xsd_emit prototypes at 6 each. BEST USES for the modeling guide: (1) FORM-INDEXED WALKER, lib xsd_emit.spi:2186-2212 (record_texts, element_part, choice_part, group_part, attribute_step, element_witness, choice_witness, ...; instances per form at 2314/2321 reading, 2418/2426 staged_reading, 2532/2539 writing, 2633/2640 staged_writing, ...): one generic schema walk written once against prototypes, each emit form an instance set. Beats plain functions: without it the walker is copied 6x or carries a runtime form tag switched at every node; with it the form is a type and dispatch is resolved at compile time (zero-cost, and a new form = one instance block). (2) OPEN PURPOSE ENUM, lib keyed_rng.spi:18 `:? purpose_code p : p -> u32` with roll_key p (instances keyed_rng.spi:36 derived, :48 sample_purpose): callers plug their own purpose types into the RNG key without lib knowing them; a closed lib union could not be extended by callers. (3) HOST ABSTRACTION, lib leptos/leptos.spi:134 signal_get (+ signal_get_untracked, signal_set, signal_update): one call-site syntax over several leptos signal kinds. Mega samples have 0-4 prototype instances each, so (1) is the guide's prototype example. Proof: the census + the cited lines.")
