inl planning_of_realworld_3_auth () = Planning ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_1 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 1u32, "2026-11-09T18:16Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DoD: register/login issue a token; protected routes refuse a missing or bad token with the spec's 401 shape; tests. Next step: pick lib crypto primitives for hash + HMAC. TypeFeatures: authenticated vs anonymous request as a phantom state. Parent: SPIRAL-REALWORLD (split by org2 11-09: 2400 m never fits a round).")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_2 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 2u32, "2026-11-09T20:44Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-09 rig (help pool), THE RECORDED NEXT STEP (pick lib crypto primitives for hash + HMAC), from lib/spiral/crypto.spi: PRESENT sha256 (rust/py/zig variants), sha1, hmac_sha1_base64 (key, text), ed25519_verify (verify only, no signing), hex helpers. MISSING: HMAC-SHA256, a constant-time compare, and ANY password KDF (no pbkdf2/scrypt/argon2). PICKS: (a) tokens = HMAC over the user id with a server secret; hmac_sha1_base64 is usable now (HMAC-SHA1 stays a sound MAC, its security does not rest on SHA-1 collision resistance), HMAC-SHA256 when the lib gains it; the compare must be constant-time (a lib gap: add constant_time_equal next to hmac). (b) passwords: plain or salted sha256 is NOT acceptable (fast hashes are brute-forceable); the lib needs a KDF first, recommended argon2id behind the Rust host boundary the way rust_sha1_digest reaches a crate, or PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with a high iteration count if it must stay pure; that is a lib item before register/login can be done. DRAFT of the typed boundary (not applied; yours): drafts\\spiral\\REALWORLD-3-AUTH\\auth.spi. TypeFeatures as planned: request s indexed by anonymous | authenticated (Authenticated user_id only built by authenticate), so a protected handler taking request authenticated cannot receive an unchecked request; the crypto arrives as an auth_crypto capability record { mac; same }, so the real primitives plug in without touching the logic; Unauthorized reasons missing / malformed / bad token and the spec's 401 body {\"errors\":{\"body\":[reason]}}. Its test uses a TOY mac (a test double, never for real use). UNCOMPILED (box: no compute while the user is present).")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_3 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 3u32, "2026-11-09T21:05Z", "tibia", "11-09 17:1x tibia (help pool): GATING rig's uncompiled auth.spi on ganymede (job tibia-auth, a scratch copy in my spiral mirror's apps/realworld; the module is added to spiral.toml there only). The awk oracle finds 2 cells over 272: authenticate (724) and the single test (594). A budget-split v2 is ready in the same drafts folder (auth.v2.spi, max cell 263): authenticate = token_of (scheme) + checked (split_token + verified); the test becomes four (admits its user; another secret is a bad token; wrong scheme missing + no mac malformed; the 401 body). It folds in whatever type errors the first gate finds.")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_4 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 4u32, "2026-11-09T21:16Z", "tibia", "11-09 17:2x tibia: rig's token boundary is GREEN as auth.v2.spi (59A7CF7FB315, in the same drafts folder; the formatter's layout adopted after a token-identical check). Gate on ganymede (job tibia-round2, a scratch copy in apps/realworld with auth added to spiral.toml): package --check clean, cell budget 0 findings (v1 had 724 and 594), Cells 4/4 passed: a token admits the user it was issued for; a token signed with another secret is a bad token; a wrong scheme is missing and a token without a mac is malformed; a refusal carries the spec's 401 body. Phantom state: request anonymous / request authenticated, so a protected handler cannot take an unchecked request. NOT YET THE DoD: register/login need a password KDF, and the lib has none (rig's note 2: no pbkdf2/scrypt/argon2, no HMAC-SHA256, no constant-time compare). Its tests use a TOY mac (a test double). NEXT, org's decision (spiral is org's): apply auth.v2.spi as apps/realworld/auth.spi + the spiral.toml line, and open the lib item for the KDF + constant_time_equal (argon2id behind the Rust host boundary, the way rust_sha1_digest reaches a crate, is rig's recommendation).")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_5 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 5u32, "2026-11-09T21:21Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-09 21:2xZ APPLIED tibia's green draft (rig token boundary) as spiral/apps/realworld/auth.spi, registered after realworld_model in apps/realworld/spiral.toml (unstaged); the budget oracle on the box copy shows 0 findings. Proof cited: tibia's ganymede gate, package --check clean, Cells 4/4. Design: request s indexed by anonymous/authenticated, crypto injected as auth_crypto { mac; same } (toy_crypto only for tests). LEFT for DoD: register/login password hashing -> opened LIB-CRYPTO-AUTH-PRIMITIVES (HMAC-SHA256, KDF, constant-time compare) for team lib.")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_6 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 6u32, "2026-11-09T23:17Z", "tibia", "11-09 19:2x tibia: the lib dependency is DONE (LIB-CRYPTO-AUTH-PRIMITIVES), so the toy MAC can go. drafts\\spiral\\REALWORLD-3-AUTH\\auth.v3.spi (24401358BF75; base = the applied apps/realworld/auth.spi 59A7CF7FB315) adds real_crypto () = { mac = crypto::hmac_sha256_hex; same = crypto::constant_time_equal } and two rust tests (sha2 hmac subtle): an HMAC-SHA256 token admits the user it was issued for; a token under another secret is a bad token. toy_crypto stays as the test double for the pure tests. Gate on ganymede (job tibia-rw-crypto5, the landed lib): auth 6/6 executed. LEFT for the DoD: register/login over pbkdf2_sha256_hex (salted password hash at registration, constant_time_equal at login) issuing a token; that is REALWORLD-3's next slice.")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_7 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 7u32, "2026-11-09T23:22Z", "tibia", "11-09 19:3x tibia: DoD REACHED in draft, ready for org2 to apply: drafts\\spiral\\REALWORLD-3-AUTH\\auth.v4.spi (DCC5DF449277; supersedes v3; base = apps/realworld/auth.spi 59A7CF7FB315). Adds REGISTER and LOGIN on top of v3's real_crypto. register s u password salt rounds stores a self-describing hash 'pbkdf2-sha256:<rounds>:<salt>:<hex>' (lib pbkdf2_sha256_hex) in user.password_hash through realworld_model::create_user; the salt is supplied by the caller (random salt generation is the IO side's). login s secret email password looks the user up by email, re-derives with the stored rounds + salt, compares with crypto::constant_time_equal, and returns LoggedIn <Authorization header> (an HMAC-SHA256 token for that user id) or LoginRefused 'email or password is invalid'. The SAME message is used for an unknown email and a wrong password, so login cannot enumerate users. Tests (10, all executed): v3's 6 + a registered user logs in and the token authenticates as user 1; a wrong password is refused; an unknown email gets the same refusal; the stored hash names its scheme and never holds the password. Gate on ganymede (job tibia-auth4, the landed lib crypto, spiral CLI linked for the crate targets): auth 10/10, 0 unsupported. Note for production: the tests use 2 PBKDF2 rounds for speed; the server should pass a high count (OWASP's current guidance for PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 is 600000).")
inl note_of_realworld_3_auth_8 () = Note ("REALWORLD-3-AUTH", 8u32, "2026-11-09T23:23Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DONE 11-09 23:2xZ. Applied tibia's auth.v4.spi over spiral/apps/realworld/auth.spi (base 59A7CF7FB315 matched; unstaged). The DoD is met on the landed lib crypto (LIB-CRYPTO-AUTH-PRIMITIVES Done: crypto.spi hmac_sha256_hex, pbkdf2_sha256_hex, constant_time_equal): register stores a self-describing pbkdf2-sha256:<rounds>:<salt>:<hex> hash (caller-supplied salt); login re-derives and compares in constant time, then issues an HMAC-SHA256 token; an unknown email and a wrong password get the same refusal; real_crypto replaces toy_crypto outside tests. Proof: tibia's ganymede gate 10/10 executed against the landed crypto; budget oracle 0 on the box copy (org2's mirror lacks the spiral CLI for the ///> rust -d crate tests, so those report unsupported there). Note: tests use 2 PBKDF2 rounds; production callers must pass a high count.")
inl realworld_4_http () = Task ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", "Conduit HTTP server: bind the typed routes to an HTTP listener (reuse x's sim_serve-style dispatch) with JSON in and out", TaskKind, P2, D3, 95u32, Active, "REALWORLD-3-AUTH", "team:org,spiral,app", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_realworld_4_http () = Planning ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_1 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 1u32, "2026-11-09T18:16Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DoD: the server answers every route with spec-shaped JSON on a local port; a smoke test hits 3 routes. Next step: reuse the existing Spiral HTTP serve pattern (x apps/sim sim_serve.spi typed dispatch). TypeFeatures: the GADT route dispatch from REALWORLD-1. Parent: SPIRAL-REALWORLD (split by org2 11-09: 2400 m never fits a round).")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_2 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 2u32, "2026-11-09T23:39Z", "tibia", "11-09 19:4x tibia (help pool): SLICE 1 GREEN = the PURE dispatcher the HTTP listener will call: drafts\\spiral\\REALWORLD-4-HTTP\\server.spi (F96C7BBC7440), a new apps/realworld module that opens auth (needs REALWORLD-3's auth.v4). dispatch keys store request returns { store; reply = { status; body } } from an http_request record { method; path; authorization; body }; route_of maps POST /api/users/login, POST /api/users, GET /api/user to a route union (anything else is 404). Bodies decode with lib json::decode straight into the API's login_body / registration_body (Conduit's user envelope); replies are spec-shaped user objects (email, token, username, bio, image) and the errors.body list. Statuses: register 201, login 200, current user 200, bad or missing token 401, wrong password 401, unknown route 404. Tests 5/5 executed on ganymede (job tibia-server6; auth.v4 overlaid; spiral CLI linked for the crate targets). TRAP met: a literal empty header made the partial evaluator fold sm'::substring 6 (0-6) on an empty string in a branch the runtime never takes; the test binds the header as a dynamic value (:= ~empty), and token_in is now total. auth's token_of has the same shape (a concern for static empty headers only). LEFT for the DoD: the listener (bind a port, read a request, write the reply) reusing x's sim_serve erlang_tcp / erlang_http_server pattern on the Gleam target, the remaining routes, and the 3-route smoke test over a real port.")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_3 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 3u32, "2026-11-09T23:59Z", "tibia", "11-09 20:0x tibia: REALWORLD-3-AUTH is Done (auth.v4 applied by org2), so slice 1 is unblocked: drafts\\spiral\\REALWORLD-4-HTTP\\server.spi applies as apps/realworld/server.spi with a server line after auth in apps/realworld/spiral.toml (5/5 on ganymede against v4). NEXT: the listener slice (sim_serve pattern) and the remaining routes.")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_4 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 4u32, "2026-11-10T00:07Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 00:0xZ slice 1 APPLIED: tibia's server.spi -> spiral/apps/realworld/server.spi, registered after auth in apps/realworld/spiral.toml (unstaged). One edit on apply: the test the_current_user_route_needs_a_valid_token renamed to the_user_route_needs_a_valid_token, because org2's budget oracle had that cell at 274 > 272; now 0 findings. Proof: tibia's ganymede gate 5/5; org2's mirror gate rc=0 with 0 type errors (its 5 ///> rust -d crate tests report unsupported there: no spiral CLI on that mirror).")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_5 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 5u32, "2026-11-10T10:10Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 10:1xZ clearing the lane:org2 wait: slice 1 was applied 11-10 00:07Z (note above) and is still on the box: spiral/apps/realworld/server.spi 1d110daa30c9 (tibia's F96C7BBC7440 plus the budget rename of one test), server registered after auth in apps/realworld/spiral.toml (unstaged). Next step is tibia's listener slice")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_6 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 6u32, "2026-11-10T12:12Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 claimed 11-10 12:1xZ for the listener slice. Finding: the lib's only HTTP server is the BEAM one (erlang_tcp + erlang_http_server, what x's sim_serve uses), and realworld's auth crypto has Gleam implementations for every primitive it uses (crypto hmac_sha256_hex, pbkdf2_sha256_hex, constant_time_equal all have Gleam arms), so the listener can run on the BEAM exactly like sim_serve: accept, read until erlang_http_server complete, map the request to server's http_request, dispatch threading the store, reply json_response. Probe first: server.spi's 5 tests with a ///> gleam target added are gating in a scratch app copy (ganymede org2-rwg). Next step: if the dispatcher is green on gleam, draft apps/realworld/serve.spi (listener + one in-process round-trip test), else record what blocks gleam")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_7 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 7u32, "2026-11-10T12:39Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 12:3xZ: probe org2-rwg GREEN: server.spi's 5 tests pass with a ///> gleam target added (no failed targets), so the realworld stack runs on the BEAM. The listener module (drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/serve.spi: request_from maps erlang_http_server's request to http_request, answer_raw is the pure raw-text handler threading the store, the sim_serve-style accept loop, serve refusing to start without REALWORLD_SECRET / REALWORLD_SALT) went RED on its first gate (org2-rws, EJP0040) and exposed a real model bug: realworld_model's store lookups (listm'::try_find / filter / map / contains / collect) unroll without bound once the store is run-time data, which every real request makes it; the earlier tests only ever had compile-time stores. Fix drafted: realworld_model.spi with three fold-based helpers (find_by, keep_where, values_of over core listm::fold, which auth's user_with already uses safely) replacing every listm' use, order preserved. Gating model + server + serve together as org2-rws2")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_8 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 8u32, "2026-11-10T12:50Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 correction to my previous note: the EJP0040 trace verifies only listm'::try_find unrolling over the run-time store; listm'::filter over run-time header lines worked in the same test (lib erlang_http_server header_value). realworld_model's filter/map/contains/collect were moved to the fold helpers as a precaution, not on evidence")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_9 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 9u32, "2026-11-10T13:26Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 listener slice GREEN and on the box + mirror (unstaged), proof: ganymede org2-rws3 (pinned, scratch app): realworld_model 17/17, server 5/5 (now executing: every test carries ///> gleam beside ///> rust -d, so the BEAM target runs where the rust crate target has no spiral CLI), serve 1/1 on gleam. apps/realworld: realworld_model.spi 837c8bd94b41 (fold-based find_by / keep_where / values_of replace every listm' use, so store lookups survive run-time data), server.spi acb268b87b6c (+5 ///> gleam), serve.spi 4eb5e5e02cc9 (new: request_from, answer_raw, the sim_serve-style accept loop, serve refusing to start without REALWORLD_SECRET / REALWORLD_SALT; test raw_http_requests_round_trip_through_the_dispatcher: register 201, current user with the token 200, unknown route 404, all from raw HTTP text built at run time), spiral.toml 5466f1e99961 (serve registered after server). Next step: a socket smoke run (start serve on a port in a BEAM job, curl register / login / user) and the remaining Conduit routes (profiles, articles, comments, tags) as typed routes in server.spi")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_10 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 10u32, "2026-11-10T13:54Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 routes slice 2 drafted (kit drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/s2/server.spi, base = box server.spi acb268b87b6c): GET /api/tags ({\"tags\":[...]} from realworld_model.get_tags) and GET /api/profiles/:username ({\"profile\":{username,bio,image,following}}, following true only for a valid token whose user follows the target, 404 for an unknown name). route_of now hands reads to read_route and dispatch to reads, to keep cells small. Also fixes a budget miss of mine: the landed server.spi's +///> gleam lines pushed the_user_route_needs_a_valid_token over 272 (now 0 findings in the whole file). 3 new tests, all ///> rust -d + ///> gleam. Gating as org2-rwr (server + serve, scratch app = box realworld + draft)")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_11 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 11u32, "2026-11-10T14:06Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 correction to note 9: the box hashes were swapped by sort order (serve.spi sorts before server.spi). Correct: apps/realworld/serve.spi acb268b87b6c, server.spi 4eb5e5e02cc9. Slice 2 first gate org2-rwr was red on my own sed rename (made -> m also hit the register test); rust -d tests now execute on ganymede, and both profile tests failed only on the rust target, likely the same broken crate; rerun org2-rwr2 with the rename fixed")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_12 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 12u32, "2026-11-10T14:45Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 14:4xZ: slice 3 (articles read) gate org2-rwa (scratch app = box realworld + s3 server.spi + auth fix): auth 4/4 executed (6 rust-only crate tests unsupported on the mirror), serve 1/1, server 10/11 with both profile tests now green on rust and gleam. FOUND AND FIXED a real auth bug: auth.token_of sliced \"Token \" off headers shorter than 6 bytes (the starts_with guard does not fold on the rust target, so the dead branch's substring is evaluated on the empty literal; at run time an empty Authorization header would crash). It now checks sm'::length header >= 6 first (drafts s2/auth.spi). The last red, the_article_list_counts_its_articles on rust, was the same mechanism in my own route code (profile_name's fixed 14-byte slice evaluated on /api/articles); both slug slices now go through a length-guarded after helper. Rerun org2-rwa2 queued")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_13 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 13u32, "2026-11-10T14:59Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slices 2+3 GREEN and on the box + mirror (unstaged), proof: ganymede org2-rwa2 (pinned, scratch app): server 11/11 and serve 1/1 on rust -d and gleam, auth 4/4 (6 rust-only crate tests unsupported on the mirror). apps/realworld/server.spi e367ac72ff53: GET /api/tags, GET /api/profiles/:username (following for the token holder, 404 unknown), GET /api/articles/:slug and GET /api/articles (Conduit article objects with author profile, favorited, favoritesCount; articlesCount), slugs read through a length-guarded after helper. apps/realworld/auth.spi b44d5648f21a: token_of checks length >= 6 before slicing (real bug: an empty Authorization header sliced out of range; compiler filed PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR for the rust-target fold). Slice 4 (POST /api/articles: token required, Conduit tagList decoded via an article_input record, slug from the title, 201 with the article) is gating as org2-rwc")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_14 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 14u32, "2026-11-10T15:15Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 15:1xZ: slice 4 (POST /api/articles) gates found two things: (1) a record update applied to a call ({ as_jake path with ... }) silently drops the definition (Unbound jake_posts); binding first fixes it; (2) decoding Conduit's tagList as list string hits EJP0040 in lib json_real.spir:108, because list is a recursive union and the decoder's generic union path unrolls on run-time bodies; tagList now decodes as array_base string (the decoder's run-time-safe array case) and list_of converts it for the model. Slice 4 is folded into slice 5 (social writes: POST/DELETE /api/profiles/:u/follow and /api/articles/:slug/favorite, token required, answered through profile_route / article_route as the caller), gating as org2-rwd2")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_15 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 15u32, "2026-11-10T15:29Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slices 4+5 GREEN and on the box + mirror (unstaged), proof: ganymede org2-rwd2 (pinned, scratch app = box realworld + draft): server 15/15 and serve 1/1 on rust -d and gleam, 0 unsupported. apps/realworld/server.spi 29978bfef00e adds POST /api/articles (token required, 401 otherwise; Conduit body decoded with tagList as array_base string then list_of into the model; slug from the lowercased title with spaces as dashes; 201 with the article) and POST/DELETE /api/profiles/:username/follow and /api/articles/:slug/favorite (token required; the store is toggled if the target exists, then answered through profile_route / article_route as the caller, so unknown targets stay 404). Routes now covered: users (register, login, current), tags, profiles (get, follow, unfollow), articles (list, get, create, favorite, unfavorite). Next step: the remaining Conduit routes (update and delete article, comments add/list/delete, feed, list filters by tag/author/favorited, update user) and a socket smoke run on the BEAM")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_16 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 16u32, "2026-11-11T00:28Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slices 6+7 GREEN and on the box + mirror (unstaged), proof: ganymede org2-rwf2 (pinned, scratch app = box realworld + draft): server 22/22 and serve 1/1 on rust -d and gleam, 0 unsupported. apps/realworld/server.spi a9be1643fcde adds: comments (POST /api/articles/:slug/comments, GET .../comments; comment JSON with id, dates, body, author profile), DELETE /api/articles/:slug (author only: 200, others 403, unknown 404, anonymous 401), GET /api/articles/feed (articles by followed authors; anonymous 401), PUT /api/user (email, username, bio, image patched from optional fields; others kept). The route enum groups ToSocial / ToArticleOp ops, and dispatch is chained layers (reads > social_reads > account_reads > comment_reads > listed_reads) to keep cells under budget. First run red on definition order (between used before it was defined), fixed. Routes now covered: users (register, login, current, update), tags, profiles (get, follow, unfollow), articles (list, feed, get, create, delete, favorite, unfavorite), comments (add, list). Remaining for the item: list filters (?tag, ?author, ?favorited, limit/offset), PUT /api/articles/:slug, DELETE comment, then the BEAM socket smoke run")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_17 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 17u32, "2026-11-11T00:34Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 taking the BEAM socket smoke run per org2 handoff (org2 keeps route authoring). Plan: draft serve_main (Gleam entry calling serve 8080) built like x/apps/sim sim_serve, run on ganymede with per-run REALWORLD_SECRET/SALT from /dev/urandom (env only, never a file/tree), curl register 201 / login 200 / current-user 200 / nowhere 404 + empty-auth 401 over a real port. Raw results to follow.")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_18 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 18u32, "2026-11-11T00:36Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slice 8 drafted (kit drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/s8/server.spi, base a9be1643fcde, budget 0): PUT /api/articles/:slug (author only, 403 otherwise; optional title/description/body patched, the slug regenerated from the new title, the article kept at its id and list position), DELETE /api/articles/:slug/comments/:id (comment author only), and tagList back to list string now that JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS landed (list_of removed). Tests: the_author_edits_an_article_and_its_slug_follows_the_title, the_author_deletes_a_comment. Gate org2-rw8 chained on ganymede. The BEAM socket smoke run is now org3 (offered 11-10 20:3x; needs a gleam main around serve.spi serve, built like x sim_serve, with per-run secrets in the job env only). Left after slice 8: list filters (?tag/?author/?favorited/limit/offset), which need a query field on http_request")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_19 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 19u32, "2026-11-11T00:39Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 smoke-run progress: drafted the entry serve_main.spi (kit drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/serve_main.spi) modeled on x simframes: main()=serve_exports();0 with export \"serve\" ((\\ x => serve::serve 8080i32):string->i32) so erl -eval halt(serve_main:serve(<<>>)) starts the accept loop. Needs a spiral.toml modules line 'serve_main' after serve on landing. BLOCKER on the actual run: box user-idle=0min (user present) so the proven x pwsh serve workflow (BuildSpiral+gleam-modules.ps1+scoop erl) can't run on the box per the no-box-compute-while-present rule; routing to ganymede per org2. Asked org2 for the ganymede standalone-serve build incantation. Pending that, then: per-run REALWORLD_SECRET/SALT from /dev/urandom in job env, curl register 201/login 200/current-user 200/nowhere 404 + empty-auth 401.")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_20 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 20u32, "2026-11-11T00:52Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 smoke-run STAGED, ready-to-fire, blocked on a ganymede slot. Got microblog's full recipe (BuildSpiral spiral/lib/spiral/lib.ps1 -> multi-part gleam; gleam.toml target=erlang deps=stdlib/erlang/gary/otp/envoy = x frames minus xsim_split; realworld needs NO per-app .erl since its lib FFI is @external like sim_serve; start: erl -noshell -pa */ebin -eval halt(serve_main:serve(<<>>)) in build/dev/erlang, curl :8080). Built the complete node job: kit drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/rw-smoke-ganymede.sh (stage sibling scratch -> gleam pkg -> BuildSpiral -> gleam build -> serve+curl 201/200/200/404 + empty-auth-401 liveness -> teardown; per-run secrets from /dev/urandom env-only). LAUNCH BLOCKED: trivial probe job held 'pending/waiting for a slot' on ganymede 116s then stopped (node contended, remote jobs not waking). Fires on a free ganymede slot (or box when user leaves - user present now, 0 idle). Recipe saved to memory standalone-spiral-beam-server-build.")
inl note_of_realworld_4_http_21 () = Note ("REALWORLD-4-HTTP", 21u32, "2026-11-11T01:01Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 BEAM socket smoke run GREEN — all 5 checks over a real TCP port on ganymede (job rw-smoke2, exit 0). Built the realworld app as a standalone BEAM server (serve_main entry + gleam.toml erlang target, deps stdlib/erlang/gary/otp/envoy; gleam build clean 7.95s), served on :8080. RAW: R1 POST /api/users -> 201 {user with token 1.629843...}; R2 POST /api/users/login -> 200; R3 GET /api/user Token -> 200; R4 GET /api/nowhere -> 404 {errors.body:[no such route]}; R5 GET /api/user EMPTY auth -> 401 {errors.body:[missing token]} and server ALIVE after (org2's token_of out-of-range-slice fix holds over a REAL socket, not just the in-process test). Secrets per-run from /dev/urandom, env-only. Teardown killed own pid only; scratch removed. This clears the DoD's socket-run part (the in-process dispatcher was already 22/22). Recipe + ready job: memory standalone-spiral-beam-server-build + kit drafts spiral/REALWORLD-4-HTTP/rw-smoke-ganymede.sh. Remaining for the item is org2's route authoring (list filters, PUT article, DELETE comment).")
