inl planning_of_spiral_ui_8_keyed_list () = Planning ("SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_keyed_list_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", 1u32, "2026-11-10T04:43Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "DoD: a view case Each (signal m (list r)) * (r -> key) * (r -> view m k) whose mount emits one subtree per row and whose refresh, after the list signal changes, emits Remove/Create/Append ops only for rows whose key appeared or vanished (kept rows untouched), with dom_op Remove applied by ui_dom_ts; rust+typescript gates show adding one row patches one subtree and removing one emits one Remove. Found by org2 11-10 porting dice's transaction table (SPIRAL-UI-7): dice builds tbody rows from a reactive vec (vec_to_view), which the landed stack cannot express. Next step: add the Each case to view m k and a keyed binding kind in ui_dom, in the SPIRAL-UI probe package. TypeFeatures: the key type is a parameter; rows stay typed. Proof: Cells gate rust+typescript (SPIRAL_BUN) green.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_keyed_list_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", 2u32, "2026-11-10T05:11Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slice 1 drafted at drafts/spiral/SPIRAL-UI-8 (ui_view 478c8df14ff4, ui_dom 481b610e327b, built on the UI-6C tags): view case Each packs the row type existentially (read, key, row); render emits one row per item; mount keeps (key, root, end) per row plus a sync closure over parts m; refresh emits Remove for vanished keys, mounts only new keys, drops bindings inside removed rows; dom_op gains Remove. Limits: new rows append (moves = slice 2), nested keyed lists inside rows untracked, ui_dom_ts ignores Remove. Next step: after the ganymede proof job org2-ui8 is green, org2 lands copy-only on the box and takes slice 2 (ts Remove + moves).")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_keyed_list_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", 3u32, "2026-11-10T05:46Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 slice 1 LANDED on the box copy-only from the ganymede proof (job org2-ui8: close green, lib digest A649EC008697E040; executed cells ui_view 5/5, ui_dom 5/5, ui_dom_ts 4/4, discovered = executed, 0 unsupported): lib/spiral/ui_view.spi 481b610e327b (Each + each + render), ui_dom.spi 478c8df14ff4 (Remove op, keyed rows, refresh sync). Correction to note 2: those two hashes were swapped there. Slice 2 (ui_dom_ts applies Remove; fake DOM test that a dropped key detaches its row and a new key appends) proving as job org2-ui8b. Next step: after org2-ui8b is green, org2 lands ui_dom_ts on the box and closes this item; moves are SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_keyed_list_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", 4u32, "2026-11-10T06:25Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 DONE: slice 2 (ui_dom_ts applies Remove) and the moves follow-up landed on the box copy-only from the rebased ganymede proof org2-ui8r (box lib exactly, incl. compiler's rust_testing change; close green, digest 0B91517ACADD5744; executed cells ui_dom 6/6, ui_dom_ts 6/6, 0 unsupported): lib/spiral/ui_dom.spi bee757f9f044, ui_dom_ts.spi aeaee461da41. Keyed lists now render, mount, remove, insert mid-list and reorder by moving the same nodes, in rust and typescript. The first box attempt refused on the digest guard because the box lib had moved after the proof; the guard worked as intended.")
inl cells_memo_windows_glob () = Task ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", "Cells memo on Windows: prune never runs and the compiler-core stamp is missing (backslash glob patterns)", TaskKind, P1, D2, 85u32, Active, "", "cells,memo,windows,bug,team:microblog", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_memo_windows_glob () = Planning ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_memo_windows_glob_1 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", 1u32, "2026-11-10T04:45Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 01:0x microblog, MEASURED on the box (plain elixir, read-only): Path.wildcard treats backslashes in a PATTERN as escapes, and Cells builds its Windows dirs as 'C:\\Users\\...\\AppData\\Local/spiral-bin/...'. (1) Memo.prune globs context.dir/*/*.etf: the backslash pattern matches 0 entries, the forward-slash form 3,865, so on Windows pruning has NEVER deleted anything (moon's store: 35,143 entries back to 10-25; the 5,000 cap is dead code there). (2) Memo.compiler_binaries_print globs dir/SpiralCompiler*.dll: [] with backslashes vs [SpiralCompiler.dll, SpiralCompilerCore.dll] forward, so on Windows the print never includes the CORE, and a compiler change that touches only SpiralCompilerCore.dll keeps serving stale memo results on every Windows host (the Windows twin of 11-09's Linux lowercase bug). DoD: both globs normalize the dir to forward slashes first; a Windows-only test shows a backslash dir finds its entries; the Cells suite green on a Windows node. Next step: patch memo.ex (one String.replace per glob) plus the test, then gate on moon.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_windows_glob_2 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", 2u32, "2026-11-10T04:47Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 01:1x microblog: PATCHED (box tree, unstaged): memo.ex gains glob(dir, pattern), which normalizes backslashes to / before Path.wildcard (the convention compiler_daemon.ex:743/803 and targets.ex:361/450 already follow; Memo was the one that missed it), used by prune (*/*.etf) and compiler_binaries_print (SpiralCompiler*.dll). New memo_test case: on Windows a backslash-spelled tmp dir must still find its entry; on Linux the same test runs unspelled. The other Path.wildcard sites in Cells lib were checked: compiler_daemon 1095, new_project 30 and server 14 glob compile-time or cwd paths, which Elixir spells with /. EXPECTED EFFECTS on Windows hosts: the next store after deploy prunes the store to the newest 5,000 (moon drops ~30k entries once), and a core-only compiler change re-keys the memo. Next step: after the warm arm on moon settles (it uses xqueue1's Cells), sync xqueue1 spiral from the box and run mix test test/spiral/cells/memo_test.exs there as a kit job on moon (Windows), then the full Cells suite in the next Windows window.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_windows_glob_3 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", 3u32, "2026-11-10T04:50Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 01:2x microblog AUDIT for the same mixed-separator trap elsewhere in Cells lib (read-only): every Path.wildcard site (8) and every Path.relative_to / path-prefix site (3). Safe: compiler_daemon 743/803 and targets 361/450 already normalize; compiler_daemon 1095, new_project 30 and server 14 glob compile-time or cwd paths (Elixir spells them with /); memo.ex 358 relative_to runs on a Path.expand-ed dir (Path.expand normalizes to / on Windows) with files derived from it; mounts.ex 147 relative_to uses a path and root built by the same Path.join from one base. So Memo's two globs were the only unnormalized ones; no further fix needed. Next step unchanged: the Windows memo_test on moon after the warm arm settles.")
inl note_of_cells_memo_windows_glob_4 () = Note ("CELLS-MEMO-WINDOWS-GLOB", 4u32, "2026-11-10T06:19Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 02:2x PROOF ON WINDOWS (the box, as a local kit job at Normal priority while the box sat at 59%; job-1794291257399-x-memo-windows-test exit 0): mix test test/spiral/cells/memo_test.exs with the patched memo.ex (E40869FB6130) and memo_test (F29A6087CC30): Result 30 passed, 0 failed, 167 s. That includes the new backslash-dir glob test and the compiler-core re-key tests, which exercise compiler_binaries_print with a Windows-spelled dll path and cannot pass while the core DLL is missing from the print. Next step: the full Cells suite in the next Windows window (moon after TRAIN43b, or the box when idle) to close the DoD, then the change reaches the fleet when the user pushes.")
inl spiral_ui_8_moves () = Task ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", "Keyed list moves: reorder kept rows with an InsertBefore dom op instead of remounting, and track keyed lists nested inside rows", Story, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST", "spiral,ui,lib,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_spiral_ui_8_moves () = Planning ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 1u32, "2026-11-10T05:25Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10: UI-8 slice 1 appends new rows and keeps kept rows in place, so a reorder of existing keys does not move them, and a keyed list inside a row is not synced (its sync is dropped with the local mounted state). DoD: refresh emits the minimal moves for a reordered key list, nested lists patch, rust and typescript cells green. Next step: after SPIRAL-UI-8-KEYED-LIST lands, org2 drafts InsertBefore in ui_dom and ui_dom_ts.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 2u32, "2026-11-10T05:52Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 05:5xZ drafted at drafts/spiral/SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES (ui_dom bee757f9f044 over the landed 478c8df14ff4; ui_dom_ts a573823077b5 over the UI-8 slice-2 draft). Finding: slice 1 was wrong for a key inserted mid-list too, not only reorders (old a,b to new a,c,b mounted c at the end: DOM a,b,c). Fix with no new op, since appendChild of an attached node moves it: prefix-stable sync keeps the longest prefix where the new keys match the surviving old order, then re-appends every later row in new order (kept rows emit Append parent root, new rows mount). Cells: ops for the mid insert (mount c, then Append 2 6 for b) and the reorder c,a,b (three Appends) in rust and typescript; a fake-DOM cell (fake appendChild now detaches from the old parent like the real DOM) that the same row nodes end in order c,a,b. Nested keyed lists still open. Next step: after org2-ui8b lands slice 2 on the mirror, org2 proves this draft there.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 3u32, "2026-11-10T06:17Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 moves PROVEN on the mirror (job org2-ui8m: lib close green, digest 6CC6E24360AB73CE; executed cells ui_dom 6/6 and ui_dom_ts 6/6, 0 unsupported), after slice 2 (org2-ui8b2, ui_dom_ts 5/5). Box land path: the slice-2 box land refused on the digest guard because compiler changed lib/spiral/rust_testing.spi on the box after that proof, so job org2-ui8r re-proves slice 2 plus moves as one draft against the box lib exactly (box rust_testing, ui_dom 478C8DF14FF4, ui_dom_ts 6B73B0A9A0CC copied to the mirror first). Next step: after org2-ui8r is green, org2 copy-lands ui_dom and ui_dom_ts on the box with that proof and closes UI-8 and this item.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 4u32, "2026-11-10T06:25Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2: prefix-stable moves LANDED on the box with UI-8 (ui_dom bee757f9f044, ui_dom_ts aeaee461da41; cells 6/6 each). Left for this item: keyed lists nested inside a row (the row mount drops its local lists, so an inner list is not synced on refresh). Next step: org2 drafts nested-list sync by keeping each row's own keyed lists in its row entry.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 5u32, "2026-11-10T06:25Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 TYPEFEATURES: the row entry stays a typed key * root * end triple; nested sync keeps each row's keyed lists in the row's own record so a refresh reaches them through the parent row's sync closure, with no untyped node lookups. DoD: a keyed list inside a keyed row re-syncs on refresh (rust and typescript cells, executed equal to discovered). Next step: org2 drafts the nested sync on the landed ui_dom.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 6u32, "2026-11-10T06:37Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 nested-list design PROBED green (ganymede job org2-rsp, pinned compiler, 1 cell executed): a recursive union | ^ syncer m = Syncer : i32 * (m -> list i32 -> list i32 * list (syncer m)) holds a closure whose result includes its own children, and a join point (:~ ^) walks the runtime list of them. So keyed m becomes a recursive union whose sync returns rows, parts and the nested keyed lists the newly mounted rows created; refresh syncs the parent then the returned children, with no recursive record. Next step: org2 drafts that in ui_dom over bee757f9f044.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 7u32, "2026-11-10T06:45Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 nested keyed lists DRAFTED at drafts/spiral/SPIRAL-UI-8-NESTED (over the UI-9 drafts): keyed m is now a recursive union Keyed : parent * rows * sync, and sync returns rows, parts, the keyed lists born inside newly mounted rows, and the removed row ranges. mount_each registers the parent then its born children in mount order; refresh walks them parent first, skips any keyed list whose parent node lies in a removed range, and appends newly born lists. ui_view adds each_by (an Each over any graph reader, for inner lists read from the row's data). Cell: groups a:[1,2] b:[3] then a:[1,3] patches only group a's inner list (Remove 5, mount span 10 under node 2), and dropping group a emits only Remove 2 with its inner list skipped. Proof chained after the UI-9 rerun as job org2-ui8n (pinned compiler, SPIRAL_CELLS_SERVER=0). Next step: after org2-ui8n is green, org2 lands UI-9 and this together on the box.")
inl note_of_spiral_ui_8_moves_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-UI-8-MOVES", 8u32, "2026-11-10T07:24Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 DONE, proof: nested keyed lists landed on the box copy-only from job org2-ui8n (lib digest 0A2B34AC9BBAF04B; all cells executed in the isolated rerun org2-ui9ts2: ui_dom 8/8 including the nested cell, ui_dom_ts 7/7): lib/spiral/ui_view.spi 39f60b8c8dca (each_by), ui_dom.spi 3d80218a3f50 (recursive Keyed union; refresh syncs parents first, skips lists under removed rows, appends lists born in new rows). With the earlier prefix-stable moves, keyed lists now insert, remove, reorder and nest.")
inl rig_census_spi_path_escapes () = Task ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", "Style census flags single-backslash Windows paths in .spi string literals (an escape like backslash-r silently becomes a control character)", Story, P2, D2, 50u32, Planned, "", "kit,census,spiral,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_rig_census_spi_path_escapes () = Planning ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_rig_census_spi_path_escapes_1 () = Note ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", 1u32, "2026-11-10T05:25Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 11-10 evidence: a crates.spi test literal C:\\home\\git\\regex\\Cargo.toml turned its backslash-r into a carriage return, so repo_crates returned None and the gate went red for code that was correct (ganymede job org2-pins3). DoD: rig_style_census reports a string literal holding a drive-letter path with a single backslash before a letter, with a fixture pair. Next step: rig adds the rule to rig_style_census.")
inl note_of_rig_census_spi_path_escapes_2 () = Note ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", 2u32, "2026-11-10T05:34Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "11-10 01:3x microblog (help pool): DRAFTED inside the portability probe superset rig already took for TRAIN46 (drafts store kit/KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY, sha C8A1E79851F4/018208085D85, .base unchanged): new drift case SingleSlashPath, direction NeverRises (a bug class, not style), word single-slash-path, pattern: a quote, a drive letter, a colon, exactly ONE backslash, then a letter, i.e. a string literal whose backslash Spiral reads as an escape (the crates.spi backslash-r that became a carriage return). A correctly escaped path has a second backslash after the colon, so it does not match. Fixture pair in the measure.spi sample: the single-backslash C:-home literal counts 1; the escaped D: literal and the C:/work/x literal count 0. Checked mechanically (literals decoded with perl): SingleSlashPath 1, DrivePath still 2, PosixPath 1, ExeSuffix 1, MagicNumber 2. 0 lines over 120. Next step: after TRAIN46 applies the superset (rig), read the first census baseline for single-slash-path, which should be 0 or a short list of real bugs.")
inl note_of_rig_census_spi_path_escapes_3 () = Note ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", 3u32, "2026-11-10T05:35Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "TYPEFEATURES: the hazard is one more case of the existing probe side union (SingleSlashPath :: probe drift) with its own direction (NeverRises), so the census row stays a typed probe_row; no string flag. Sizing: 0 single-backslash drive-path literals in kit/src today (grep -P over every .spi), so the baseline starts at 0 and NeverRises makes any new one a red. Next step: after TRAIN46 applies the superset (rig), confirm the census reports single-slash-path 0 for every kit package.")
inl note_of_rig_census_spi_path_escapes_4 () = Note ("RIG-CENSUS-SPI-PATH-ESCAPES", 4u32, "2026-11-10T05:39Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 01:4x rig: APPLIED microblog's KIT-STANDARDS-PORTABILITY superset draft into kit (unstaged), which carries this rule: probes.spi copied as-is (its .base equalled the live file); measure.spi took ONLY the base->draft hunk via patch (fuzz 1, offset 6, placed inside the same test block), because the draft base predates rig's census line-count fix and copying the draft would have reverted it; both fixes are present. Adds SingleSlashPath (NeverRises, word single-slash-path, a quoted drive path with one backslash before a letter) plus MagicNumber, DrivePath, PosixPath, ExeSuffix (MayNotRise). A tree scan for the single-backslash pattern in kit src finds 0 hits, so NeverRises starts clean. kit standards 0 on both packages; rig_style_measure ratchet 0; rig_style_probes ratchet shows host-macro 5 -> 7, which is PRE-EXISTING (the pre-draft file gives the same 5 -> 7; the probe file's own pattern strings containing the dollar-quote text). Probe of rig_style_probes on ganymede PENDING; rig_style_measure is a linked consumer (unprovable off-train). The claim was still refused at apply time (microblog's lease not yet cleared after their release message). Rides TRAIN44. Next step: read the probes probe result, then TRAIN44's notebook verdicts for both packages.")
