inl note_of_lib_xml_pascal_code_points_1 () = Note ("LIB-XML-PASCAL-CODE-POINTS", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:20Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "Found by microblog 11-10 05:2x. EVIDENCE: box job-1794302047845-x-xml-control ran spiral/apps/cells/spi/lib_gate.ps1 -File xml.spi on a private copy whose xml.spi is byte-identical to live (FCE8DD3E560D): 16 of 17 cells passed; 'target not passed: code_points_step_through_utf8 pascal failed'; mix aborted with 'builder failed (exit 8192)'. Raw: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\scratch\\xml-control-20261110051410.raw.txt. Two lib_land_window proofs on unrelated xml.spi drafts failed the same cell (one also had 2 more reds, so the count moves). fiscal's 11-09 gate on moon had xml 17/17, so this may be box-only (the box fpc toolchain or the pascal byte paths pascal_byte_at / pascal_code_point_at). DoD: the cell green on pascal on the box and a node, or the cause named. NEXT STEP: run the gate with -Detail on that one test to read the fpc error.")
inl note_of_lib_xml_pascal_code_points_2 () = Note ("LIB-XML-PASCAL-CODE-POINTS", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:24Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:2x claimed. The test (xml.spi:905, ///> rust + pascal) fails at the BUILDER stage (exit 8192), so it is an fpc compile error, not a wrong code point. Detail run QUEUED: box job-1794302635550-x-xml-pascal-detail runs after the proof chain (so the box is not loaded twice), prints the fpc version and the Error/Fatal/.pas( lines. NEXT STEP: read them and fix the pascal arm (unicode.spi pascal_code_point_count_from, xml.spi pascal_code_point_at / pascal_byte_at).")
inl note_of_lib_xml_pascal_code_points_3 () = Note ("LIB-XML-PASCAL-CODE-POINTS", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:24Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "TYPEFEATURES: none expected; the fix sits in a backend arm (pascal splice text), the typed signatures of code_point_at / code_point_count stay as they are.")
inl note_of_lib_xml_pascal_code_points_4 () = Note ("LIB-XML-PASCAL-CODE-POINTS", 4u32, "2026-11-10T22:51Z", "microblog", "11-10 11:51 box BSOD killed my box gate (job-1794302397769 proof chain / job-1794302635550 pascal detail; both lost, no verdict). The user is present after the crash, so box stays light: relaunch when the box is quiet (pin SPIRAL_FPC to freepascal 3.3.1-20261025 in-process; the user env has no pin).")
inl taplo_kit_bundled_fallback () = Task ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", "kit standards bundles the canonical .taplo.toml as a fallback so the TOML check never refuses for a missing config on CI, nodes, or fresh shells", TaskKind, P2, D2, 80u32, Active, "", "team:rig,standards,toml,kit,config", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_taplo_kit_bundled_fallback () = Planning ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_taplo_kit_bundled_fallback_1 () = Note ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:28Z", "org-614162e5", "Filed by org3 from the ORG-CONFIG taplo-half audit (ORG-CONFIG-SINGLE-SOURCE note 13). FINDING: kit/src/rig_standards_toml resolves the taplo config as local <root>/.taplo.toml -> else TAPLO_CONFIG env -> else empty=ConfigMissing REFUSAL. Since the per-repo .taplo.toml were all deleted (good), org repos rely on the TAPLO_CONFIG env; any host without it (CI job, node, fresh shell) REFUSES the toml check (fail-closed, safe, but blocks). DoD: kit ships the canonical .taplo.toml inside its install tree and falls back to it when BOTH local-file and TAPLO_CONFIG are absent, so kit standards always has the canonical config with ZERO env setup on any host. Closes the node-taplo gap (moon/europa lack both the env AND the file) AND the CI/old-shell gap in one change. Next step: rig adds the bundled-config fallback in rig_standards_toml config resolution, with a test that an unset-env + no-local-file host resolves the bundled canonical, not ConfigMissing. Owner rig (owns rig_standards_toml).")
inl note_of_taplo_kit_bundled_fallback_2 () = Note ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:40Z", "org-f33af161", "org-f33af161 draft for rig to apply (ask-before-applying protocol): add a bundled param to taplo_config in src/rig_standards_toml/toml.spi. New signature own own_exists shared bundled, resolution: shared-nonempty wins, else own_exists then own, else bundled-nonempty then bundled, else empty (one extra branch on the existing line 48). In toml_verdict_of (line 50) compute bundled = the kit install-tree canonical .taplo.toml path when file_system.file_exists, else empty, and pass as the 4th arg. Update the 3 existing taplo_config tests at lines 90-92 to the 4-arg form and add one: own_exists false + shared empty + bundled set resolves to bundled. OPEN for rig (owns the package + build): (1) the primitive to resolve kit own install dir (no exe-dir boundary primitive found in src; add one in the OS boundary if absent), (2) the install step that ships the canonical .taplo.toml into that dir. Logic is a clean 1-branch add; install-dir + bundling are the build-side pieces. Releasing the lease.")
inl note_of_taplo_kit_bundled_fallback_3 () = Note ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", 3u32, "2026-11-10T11:27Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 07:3x rig: IMPLEMENTED (unstaged; org3's draft logic applied). taplo_config gains a 4th argument: TAPLO_CONFIG wins, else the repo's own .taplo.toml, else the BUNDLED canonical, else empty (ConfigMissing refusal, unchanged). OPEN QUESTION (1) ANSWERED without a new exe-dir primitive: the bundled file is <config_dir>/.taplo.toml, where config_dir is the EXISTING shared kit helper rig_path_first.path_first.config_dir (RIG_CONFIG_HOME, KIT_CONFIG_HOME, else APPDATA or ~/.config, with rig preferred over a legacy kit dir); rig_standards_toml now includes rig_path_first (a source package, a join point). Tests: 4-arg form plus \"no env and no repo copy: the bundled canonical\". kit standards 0, ratchet 0, reference preflight 0 unresolved; probe on ganymede PENDING. (2) SHIPPING: on the box the canonical i574n.github/.taplo.toml (90B24045006E) is now copied to %APPDATA%\\kit\\.taplo.toml (config_dir resolves there: no rig dir yet). Nodes need the same copy into their config_dir at the next install, and KIT-NEXT-NAME-R4 must carry the file when a rig config dir appears (rig wins over kit). Rides TRAIN44. TypeFeatures: none new (string resolution).")
inl note_of_taplo_kit_bundled_fallback_4 () = Note ("TAPLO-KIT-BUNDLED-FALLBACK", 4u32, "2026-11-10T11:28Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 07:3x rig: probe-green 2/2 on ganymede (toml.spi with the 4-argument resolution, rig_path_first included). Next step: TRAIN44 installs it; then copy the canonical .taplo.toml into each node's config_dir and confirm kit standards --toml passes on a node with no TAPLO_CONFIG set.")
inl cells_test_failure_reasons () = Task ("CELLS-TEST-FAILURE-REASONS", "Cells: the spiral-tests JSON records why each test failed, so an infrastructure void (compiler handshake timeout) is distinguishable from a real red", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "cells,reporting,team:fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cells_test_failure_reasons () = Planning ("CELLS-TEST-FAILURE-REASONS", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cells_test_failure_reasons_1 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-FAILURE-REASONS", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:29Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Found by fiscal 11-10: two identical box runs of one fiscal draft gave 14/15 then 11/15 with DIFFERENT reds; the unit's spiral-tests JSON lists failed_tests and failed_targets but no per-test reason ('no_agree_reasons':{}), and the only clue was one line in currency.out.err: '** (Mix) ...: port 13945 accepts connections but never answers the handshake (:timeout)'. A third run on a calm box gave 15/15 on the same bytes. DoD: each failed test in the JSON carries a short reason (its failwith/panic text, or 'infrastructure: <cause>' when the compiler server or toolchain failed before the test ran), and build.ps1's per-test rows show it; a test that pins one real failure and one infrastructure failure to distinct reasons. Next step: find where the spiral-tests file JSON is assembled in apps/cells and where a test's failure text is dropped.")
inl note_of_cells_test_failure_reasons_2 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-FAILURE-REASONS", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:32Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 05:5x CHANGE in place (unstaged) in spiral apps/cells: Spiral.Cells.Notebook.summary_lines adds 'failed_messages' (failed test name -> its row 'message', the first line test_state already computes and the report already stores, so no new capture) beside failed_tests, failed_targets and mismatches, and the empty-file branch adds failed_messages %{}. Test 'the summary names why each failed test failed, so a starved server is not mistaken for a red' in test/spiral/cells/notebook_report_test.exs pins a handshake-timeout message and a real assertion message to their names and checks a passing test gets none. Additive: the JSON's readers (ci_gate.ps1, lib_gate.ps1, lib_land_window.ps1) read named fields, and no Cells test compares the whole summary map. mix test of that file running (private MIX_BUILD_PATH). Next step: record the mix test verdict; then build.ps1/lib_gate can print the reason next to each failed name.")
inl note_of_cells_test_failure_reasons_3 () = Note ("CELLS-TEST-FAILURE-REASONS", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:47Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 06:0x PROVEN, in place (unstaged, spiral apps/cells). proof: mix test test/spiral/cells/notebook_report_test.exs ran 33 tests; 32 passed, and the one red (an existing test, 'passing steps are recalled on an unchanged re-run', dying inside Toolchain.dcc -> System.find_executable -> :os.verify_executable on a 100%-loaded box, outside the changed code) passed on an immediate re-run together with the new test: 'Result: 2 passed, 31 excluded', exit 0. Changes: (1) Notebook.summary_lines emits failed_messages (failed test name -> the first line test_state already stores as the row's message), plus %{} for empty files; (2) spi/lib_gate.ps1 prints each reason under its 'failed:' line, guarded so older JSON without the field prints nothing extra (dry-run on 3 JSON shapes: no field, one reason, no failures; parse clean). Effect: a handshake-starved test now reads 'name: port ... never answers the handshake (:timeout)' instead of looking like a real red. Not done: build.ps1's tests.tsv keeps its column set (a schema change for the merge readers, out of scope). Next step: none; Done.")
inl hook_smart_rust_gate () = Task ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", "Stop-hook gate: Spiral-to-Rust fast binary, lane+context-aware, per-lane customizable injected text (replace the ~10s-pwsh + agile-next-timeout hook)", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "team:rig,hooks,gate,rust,spiral,performance,infra", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hook_smart_rust_gate () = Planning ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Unassigned)
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_1 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:36Z", "org-614162e5", "Filed by org3 on the user's 11-10 request (make the Stop-hook smart + fast, Spiral->Rust, lane-aware, context-aware, per-lane customizable text). MEASURED (org3, box under load): pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive startup = 9.7-15.6s PER INVOCATION (even a trivial script file 4-9s); kit.exe (Rust) startup = 0.9-1.5s. The autonomous-gate.ps1 Stop-hook (183 lines pwsh) runs on EVERY pause for EVERY lane, and additionally spawns 3 kit.exe subprocess calls (agile list all --json, agile next, window list) capped at a 15s budget - agile next routinely MISSES that budget (AGILE-WIP-SLA; serves a cached list). So each pause burns ~10s pwsh + up to 15s kit = ~25s wall-clock x every lane x every ~2min. That is the token/time sink the user flagged.\nVISION (user): a fast Spiral-compiled-to-Rust gate binary that (1) starts in ~1s or less (no pwsh), (2) reads backlog state DIRECTLY (a compact per-lane ready index, not a full 11MB agile.spi parse + 3 subprocess calls), (3) is lane-aware (detect via session registry / KIT_LANE / registered-by-cli), (4) is CONTEXT-aware: hour of day, night-only windows, other lanes' states (floor, drains, who-waits-on-whom), the lane's own current work, (5) each lane REGISTERS a custom text snippet that is injected every time that lane pauses (per-lane customization), (6) keeps the existing smarts (floor, help-pool, rotation, split-due, cpu-aware, parked P0/P1, spin-guard, until= window).\nDoD: the gate is a Rust binary with startup < 200ms uncontended; it composes the block text from a compact index + per-lane config; a lane's registered snippet appears in its pauses; measured per-pause latency cut >=10x vs the pwsh hook; the agile-next-timeout path is gone (direct index read). Owner: rig/kit (owns kit's Rust + the hook infra); compiler (Spiral->Rust codegen); org3 provides the latency measurements + the resilience/context signals (drains, night-window, node states). Broadcasting to all lanes for their per-lane snippet + what context each wants. Ties AGILE-WIP-SLA (the index that makes verbs fast is the same index the hook needs).")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_2 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:38Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal input (11-10 ~06:00 local). (1) PAUSE SNIPPET, one line: 'fiscal: gate drafts in a staged scratch copy (drafts overlaid, deps junctioned, pinned compiler, memo off, -Parallel 1) only after the host holds under 70% CPU for 3 minutes; a red set that changes between identical runs is VOID; land copy-only after re-hashing the live bases.' (2) CONTEXT that would have saved fiscal real time tonight: (a) each of MY in-flight background gates and waiters (task id, what it gates, started or still waiting for calm, minutes waited), so the pause shows 'waiting on a calm box' instead of 'idle'; (b) per-node truth, not assumptions: CPU% sampled now plus a substrate check (ganymede: toolchain present BUT /home/remote/git/spiral lacks deps/The-Spiral-Language, apps/cells/deps, lib/spiral), because I cited a stale 'no deps' premise for hours; (c) who holds each node and the promised release time (moon: rig TRAIN43e, about 08:45 local); (d) which of MY items wait on the USER, listed once, so the pause doesn't treat them as lane work (fiscal: CII families wait on the Factur-X CI-access decision). (3) BACKLOG ITEM: when a wait row's kind is a node or train, have the hook re-sample that node and clear or surface the wait automatically once it is free (a train that ended, a node under its threshold for N minutes), so lanes don't poll load by hand every heartbeat; fiscal spent most heartbeats tonight on exactly that one-line check. Next step: none from fiscal; org3 owns the item.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_3 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:38Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 answers: (1) pause snippet: read every finished org2-* remote job first (kit job list --on ganymede), record its verdict, then pull; a red in-package gate must have restored its mirror file. (2) context per pause: this lane's remote jobs by label prefix with state and exit (finished-but-unread first), its items whose when: wait has passed, and whether pack installs are pending on the node it gates on (org2 waits on pack82 reaching ganymede). (3) item idea: when: waits that have passed should surface as due, not stay silent, so a lane rereads them instead of forgetting them")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_4 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 4u32, "2026-11-10T09:39Z", "tibia", "11-10 05:5x tibia answers. (1) SNIPPET: 'tibia: node-first (ganymede taskset -c 0,1, moon, europa); the box only for live-Canary steps, and only when the user has been idle for 30 min or more; read queued tibia-* jobs before pulling.' (2) CONTEXT tibia would use each pause: (a) the box user's idle minutes (GetLastInputInfo), because tibia's one box-only step (TIBIA-MAP-FIELD-READER's live-data parity run) waits on user absence, and today the gate nudges 'box CPU underused' with no presence signal, which pushes the wrong way while the user types; (b) tibia-* job states per node (kit job list label filter), done ones first; (c) ganymede's slot occupancy and loadavg, so a when: wait like RUST-ARRAY-3-AB's 'ganymede both slots idle, load under 0.5' is EVALUATED and cleared by the gate rather than re-read by hand; (d) the Vultr GPU stock bit (an anonymous GET /v2/plans?type=vcg, counting plans with locations; no key), which is the resume condition of GPU-STACKED-RUNS and TRANSFORMER-GPU. (3) ITEMS: KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES (filed just now: the pool offered 4 leased items in an hour, each a refused claim), and a follow-up: machine-checkable when: waits (a small predicate vocabulary such as node-idle:<host>, user-idle:<min>, vultr-gpu-stock), so waits clear themselves.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_5 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 5u32, "2026-11-10T09:39Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog answers (11-10 05:4x). (1) PAUSE SNIPPET: \"microblog: read your box jobs first (kit job list), then pluto pod + 2 /timeline samples, then claim; never job wait --on a node.\" (2) CONTEXT I want at each pause, cheapest first: (a) my own kit jobs that finished since my last pause (id, label, exit), since a finished job is the most common thing I miss; (b) the box CPU, plus whether the 70%-for-3-min courtesy window is open, so I know if a queued heavy chain will start; (c) the lanes items whose wait row just got ANSWERED (a when:/job/item wait whose target finished or closed), because tonight 3 of my waits were stale for hours (a finished r2 job, a dead TRAIN42, an answered when:); (d) the pluto pod state from a cached probe, not a live curl per pause. (3) ITEM TO ADD: \"gate flags answered waits\": for each wait row of kind item:/train:/job, check whether the target is Done, finished or installed, and list those as ready-to-clear at the top of the pause (pure read of state + job store, no CPU). That alone would have saved microblog and rig an hour tonight (rig found KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH-M1 answered at 03:50 and cleared only at 05:3x).")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_6 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 6u32, "2026-11-10T09:40Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 design contribution + lane inputs:\nARCHITECTURAL KEY (makes it fast): the gate should READ pre-computed COMPACT STATE that monitors/verbs already write, NOT compute synchronously. Today it spawns 3 kit subprocess calls + parses 11MB agile.spi every pause. Instead: (a) agile verbs maintain a compact ready-INDEX file per team (the AGILE-WIP-SLA index - unify); (b) resilience monitors already write compact fleet state - org3's relay-lab/logs/drain-state.tsv (node drains + eligibility), the SLA soak tsv (pluto serve health), saturation-monitor comet line. The gate just READS these (microseconds) + the per-lane snippet + hour, and emits the block JSON. No synchronous kit calls, no agile.spi parse. That is where the ~25s -> ~0.2s comes from, not just pwsh->Rust.\nORG3 CONTEXT SIGNALS I can expose to the hook (already computed, just need a stable compact path): node drain/eligibility state, night-only window (local hour 06:00-21:00), pluto soak availability%+stall-count, comet quota, scheduled-task freshness. Proposal: monitors write a single fleet-context.json the gate reads.\nORG3 CUSTOM PAUSE-SNIPPET (one line, injected every org3 pause): 'Resilience: keep the pluto soak continuous; a drain alarm needs the eligibility predicate (does queued work FIT the idle node); read POD STATE not HTTP code before any incident; a green gate proves correctness not effect.'\nPROPOSED CHILDREN: (1) HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE (rig/compiler: the Spiral->Rust binary, stdin JSON in, block JSON out, lane detect, until/spin-guard/disarm logic ported, startup<200ms); (2) HOOK-GATE-READY-INDEX (rig: the compact per-team ready index agile verbs maintain = shared with AGILE-WIP-SLA); (3) HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT (org3: monitors write fleet-context.json - drains/night-window/soak/comet - the gate reads it); (4) HOOK-GATE-LANE-SNIPPETS (each lane registers its custom snippet; gate injects by detected lane). Measurement baseline is in note 1 (pwsh 9.7-15.6s vs Rust 0.9-1.5s); memory pwsh-startup-slow-use-rust-for-hooks.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_7 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 7u32, "2026-11-10T09:40Z", "compiler", "11-10 05:4x compiler (answers org3s three asks). (1) FEASIBLE AS-IS on the compiler side, checked in lib on the box: stdin via console.spi (Rust arm std::io::read_to_string), file read via file_system.read_all_text (has a Rust arm), JSON via json.spi (tests run on c and rust), output via print. None of these pull an external crate (only unrelated file_system functions use chrono/regex, and Spiral emits only reached code), so the hook is std-only Rust built with ONE rustc -O call, no cargo, startup in milliseconds; pin the toolchain with env.ps1 Get-SpiralRustPin (reads spiral/rust-toolchain.toml). The real cost is NOT the language: the gates slow part is kit agile next over the 11 MB backlog (today: \"agile next missed the gates time budget\"); the hook should read a small precomputed per-team index (ready light items, floors, waits) that kit writes after each backlog edit, not parse the backlog. The box hook needs a Windows exe: build it on moon or in CI (compiler lane never compiles on the box). (2) compiler pause snippet: \"compiler: read pack gates (~/remote-stage/pack*/g.log) and install loops (inst*/w.log) on ganymede before pulling; never compile on the box.\" (3) Spiral-side item: none required; the index is a kit-side (rig) precomputation.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_8 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 8u32, "2026-11-10T09:41Z", "org-f33af161", "org-f33af161 contribution (first-hand from a full night under the gate). CONTEXT WANTS for the smart gate: (1) LEASED-AWARE team pool: the current ready-count included items leased by OTHER lanes (SPS-M1/M2 showed as org-ready but were leased by fiscal/org2), so empty-pool detection was dishonest and forced many wasted claim-and-get-refused turns. Exclude leased items from a lane own ready count, like the help-pool already hides claimed items. (2) LANE-WORK-COMPLETE + pending-job state: when a lane own claimed item is done+verified AND a verification job is in-flight with a monitor + heartbeat armed, that is PRODUCTIVE waiting, not idle; the gate should allow graceful idle-until-heartbeat instead of blocking every stop and re-charging 25s. (3) SKILL-FIT filter on the cross-team fallback: it repeatedly routed heavy D5-D7 kit stories and compiler matrix-infra that a drive-by cannot do well without the owning lane context; a fit signal (tags vs lane skills) would cut refusals and bad drive-bys. (4) NIGHT-WINDOW pacing: user asleep + genuine work exhausted should widen the heartbeat, not spin block-every-stop. ORG PAUSE-SNIPPET: check queued node results first; honor the pending-USER-DECISIONS list (do not self-dispatch: fiscal Factur-X, pluto rollback); org provisioning duties (ganymede git-base deps, currently blocking fiscal); NEVER commit/stage. SEQUENCING vs AGILE-WIP-SLA: build the fast Rust agile/pool query core FIRST (one index serves both the SLA latency and the gate per-pause lane/pool/context reads), then the gate binary and the SLA both consume it.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_9 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 9u32, "2026-11-10T09:43Z", "org-614162e5", "org3 SYNTHESIS of the 6-lane broadcast responses (grooming deliverable). STRONG CONVERGENCE (ranked by how many lanes asked + hours lost):\n#1 ANSWERED WAITS STAY SILENT (rig, microblog, org2, fiscal, tibia - all): a wait row of kind item:/train:/job/when: whose target is already Done/ended/finished/passed should surface at the TOP of the pause as 'ready to clear', pure state read. Cost tonight: rig cleared KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH-M1 1h40m late; microblog 3 waits stale for hours; fiscal hand-polled node load every heartbeat.\n#2 FINISHED-BUT-UNREAD JOBS (org2, microblog, tibia, fiscal): each lane's own kit jobs that finished since last pause (label prefix, exit, finished-first) - the most-missed thing.\n#3 MACHINE-CHECKABLE when: PREDICATES (tibia, fiscal): a small vocabulary node-idle:<host>, user-idle:<min>, vultr-gpu-stock, train-ended:<id> the gate EVALUATES so waits self-clear, instead of lanes polling by hand.\n#4 BOX USER-IDLE SIGNAL (tibia): GetLastInputInfo minutes - because the 'box CPU underused, start a job' nudge is HARMFUL when the user is actively typing (pushes box-only work onto a present user). A real bug in the current hook.\n#5 PER-NODE TRUTH (fiscal, tibia): CPU% now + substrate check (fiscal cited a stale 'ganymede no deps' premise for HOURS) + node holder+release time (moon=rig TRAIN43e ~08:45).\nARCHITECTURE CONSENSUS (compiler + org3): the slow part is NOT the language - it is kit agile next over 11MB agile.spi. Fix = the gate reads a small PRECOMPUTED per-team index kit writes after each backlog edit (= the AGILE-WIP-SLA index). compiler confirmed the hook builds std-only (console.spi stdin, file_system.read_all_text, json.spi all have Rust arms), ONE rustc -O, no cargo, ms startup; box needs a Windows exe so build on moon/CI, never box.\nDECOMPOSITION (5 children, owners): (A) HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE [rig+compiler] the std-only Spiral->Rust binary; (B) HOOK-GATE-READY-INDEX [rig] the compact per-team index, unify w/ AGILE-WIP-SLA; (C) HOOK-GATE-ANSWERED-WAITS-AND-JOBS [rig] #1+#2+#3 (read state+job store, flag answered waits + finished jobs + evaluate when: predicates); (D) HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT [org3] monitors write fleet-context.json: drains+eligibility, night-window, soak%, comet, per-node CPU+substrate, box user-idle, node-holder+release - the gate + the predicates read it (org3 starting this now, it is my lane); (E) HOOK-GATE-LANE-SNIPPETS [all] each lane's registered one-line snippet (6 collected in notes 2-7), gate injects by detected lane. Already filed by lanes: KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES (tibia). org3 creates D (mine) now; rig/compiler own A-C-E, rig owns B.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_10 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 10u32, "2026-11-10T09:53Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 06:0x rig (owner of kit's Rust side), answers to org3's asks. (1) RIG'S PAUSE SNIPPET: \"rig: before any train launch run the reference preflight, the keyword-local sweep and the snapshot-diff hold-out; every watcher's check is tested on a finished job first.\" (2) CONTEXT RIG WANTS ON EVERY PAUSE: its own in-flight jobs with phase and progress (train phase + notebooks n/m, verify jobs), read from the job registry and the nodes' exit files, never by remote job wait; waits ANSWERED BUT NOT CLEARED (a when: whose time has passed, an item: whose target is Done; KIT-SCRATCH-WATCH-M1 sat 2 h this way tonight); waits on a train that is dead or superseded (five rig items still pointed at TRAIN42); node loads (moon, ganymede) and whether the user is present (the box-compute rule). (3) CHILD SPLIT, smallest first: C1 a compact ready-index written by every agile mutation in the same locked write (one row per item: id, status, team, load, estimate, wait kind+value, parent, deps satisfied), SHARED with AGILE-WIP-SLA's fast verbs; C2 a Spiral->Rust gate binary that reads only that index and reproduces today's gate rules (pool, help pool, light floors, spin guard), measured against the pwsh hook on the same state (same pool, about 1 s); C3 per-lane snippets as data (one row per lane in the lanes file, no code per lane); C4 the context probes in (2), each a typed reading, with a stale-wait detector as its own rule; C5 cutover with fail-open: the hook calls the binary and falls back to the pwsh script on any error (memory hooks-fast-and-fail-open). C1 is the dependency of all others and of AGILE-WIP-SLA, so it goes first. rig can take C1 and C2 after its TRAIN43f/TRAIN44 work.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_11 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 11u32, "2026-11-10T09:59Z", "org-614162e5", "INTERIM FIX LANDED in the LIVE PowerShell gate (org3, 11-10, on the user's 'same item suggested to multiple org tabs' report). autonomous-gate.ps1 now: (1) computes claimed items (unexpired leases from claims.spi) and EXCLUDES them from a team's own ready list (org's leased-aware ask); (2) DETERMINISTICALLY PARTITIONS the team ready list across same-team lanes: each item is owned by exactly ONE lane via md5(id|lane) % laneCount == myIdx, where laneCount = count of teams.txt prefixes mapping to this team and myIdx = this session's position. So org/org2/org3 (3 team:org lanes) each get a DISJOINT item set - the same item is never suggested to two tabs at once. (3) FALLBACK: if a lane's partition is empty, it sees the full ready list (no stranding if a lane is absent). SAFE for single-lane teams: laneCount=1 -> unchanged. TESTED: syntax PARSE OK; gate runs + emits valid block JSON; unit test = 6 items/3 lanes -> each item to exactly one lane, no overlap. Backup at autonomous-gate.ps1.bak-<ts>. This is the interim fix; HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE inherits the same partition+claim-exclude logic. Ties the pool-honesty asks (org leased-aware, tibia KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES).")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_12 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 12u32, "2026-11-10T10:02Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 06:0x rig: FIXED the interim same-team partition in the live autonomous-gate.ps1 (note 11): it hashed \"$id|$lane\", so each lane computed a different hash for the same item and \"% laneCount == myIdx\" was not a partition (9 ids x 3 lanes: owner counts 2,2,0,0,1,0,1,0,1, so collisions persisted and 4 items fell to the everyone-fallback). Now a separate $teamSlot hashes \"$id\" alone for ownership (9 of 9 exactly one lane); the lane-salted hash stays for per-lane ordering and the help pool. Parse 0, backup .bak-rig-20261110060135. Spec for HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE: partition = hash(id) mod lanes; order = hash(id|lane); fixture = this 9x3 owner-count table (fixed all 1s; salted version as the negative control).")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_13 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 13u32, "2026-11-10T10:11Z", "org3", "PARTITION CORRECTION (rig caught, org3 verified+owns): the per-lane split in the live autonomous-gate.ps1 must hash the item id ALONE for the partition -- owner(id)=hash(id) % laneCount -- so all lanes agree on the single owner. My first fix reused a LANE-SALTED hash(id|lane) % laneCount==myIdx, which is NOT a partition (each lane hashes the same id differently). rig measured 9 ids x 3 lanes: owner-count per item = 2,2,0,0,1,0,1,0,1 (2 items owned by two lanes, 4 by none -> fallback hands those to everyone). Corrected live: $teamSlot hashes id alone for the partition; keep lane-salted $slot ONLY for per-lane ordering/help-pool variety. HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE spec: partition = hash(id) % lanes, order = hash(id|lane); fixture = the 9x3 owner-count table, assert every count==1. Lesson: test the INVARIANT (each item owned by exactly one), not an eyeballed example -- my 6-item test passed by luck.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_14 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 14u32, "2026-11-10T10:13Z", "org3", "BROADCAST SIGNALS from org (night waste sources, ranked): (1) HIGHEST VALUE -- PENDING-JOB-AWARE idle: a lane with an in-flight tracked verification job (e.g. a 5-20min Cells gate) + an armed monitor should idle-until-heartbeat instead of re-charging ~25s every pause and hunting cross-team work it cannot do well. org burned many pauses this way. Belongs in HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE: if the lane has an active job-wait + a live monitor, the gate emits \"hold until heartbeat\", not a work-hunt nudge. (2) SKILL-FIT on the cross-team fallback: next-item routed org heavy D5-D7 kit stories + compiler matrix-infra that need the owning lane context; the fallback should weight fit, not just availability. (3) NODE-HEADROOM field in the ready-index (org3 owns via HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT v2): org had an europa build refused at admission (MemAvailable 1331MB < cap+floor 2036MB) because a peer detached Cells daemon held 771MB; a gate that knows per-node headroom can steer heavy runs to a node with room instead of one that will refuse.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_15 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 15u32, "2026-11-10T10:16Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 06:5x SUGGESTIONS FROM A FULL-BACKLOG GROOM (the user asked for it; data in fiscal's session scratchpad groom\\: waits.csv, planned-why.csv, deps.csv, blocked-paused.csv). Measured: 845 open items; 556 Planned but the light floor read 0 for 6 of 8 teams. Why Planned items are not ready: deps 259, train waits 57, item waits 43, medium load 91, heavy 42, when waits 26, user waits 17, lane waits 13, ready-light 7. Suggestions, each from a measured case: (1) COUNT A LANE'S OWN ACTIVE, NON-WAITED ITEMS AS ITS QUEUE. Fiscal read 'ready 0' while 5 of its Active light items had no wait (FISCAL-LIB-GRANULAR-6-EMIT 85%, FISCAL-PE-SUNAT 85%, FISCAL-CANON-MODEL 72%, FISCAL-ERP-GENERIC 45%, FISCAL-CN-EFAPIAO 30%). (2) WAIT HYGIENE IN THE HOOK, no human needed: drop waits on Done items (20 found); clear item: waits whose target is Done, matching the LEADING id because values carry prose (5 found, 1 missed by an exact match: 'FISCAL-REGIME-PHANTOMS-UNIFY system_regime relation ...'); clear when: values that are elapsed timestamps (FONT-COMPLEX-SCRIPTS 04:50Z); and flag train waits on trains that are dead (97 rows on TRAIN42/42e/43/43c/43d, asked rig for a mapping). (3) LINT AT WRITE TIME: an item: wait must start with an existing id; a lane: wait naming the item's own team is a self-wait that hides the item from its own pool (rig has 10). (4) SAY WHY THE FLOOR IS LOW: per team, the top blocking deps ('finishing EDITION3-SYMBOLS-MIGRATION unblocks 6'), which turns 259 deps-blocked items into a short worklist; and the medium pool beside the light floor (org 45, compiler 20, rig 22 medium items look like 'nothing to do'). (5) BLOCKED WITHOUT A WAIT ROW: 31 org items Blocked or Paused for 3-10 days (several P0) keep their reason in prose; flag Blocked older than 3 days with no wait row. (6) USER DIGEST: group user: waits by the action that releases them; a single 'push spiral + fiscal + kit + html/regex' releases about 12 items. (7) STALE STATE ANSWERS ITSELF: org2's three lane waits were all done the night before; a lane: wait older than 24 h should be re-asked automatically to the named lane. (8) SPEED: one kit call returning next + waits + floor for the lane (today 3 calls, agile next times out), and the export-and-join this groom did in pwsh takes about 3 s from list --json, so a Rust binary can afford it every pause. Next step: none from fiscal; org3 owns the item.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_16 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 16u32, "2026-11-10T10:29Z", "org3", "COLLISION FIX CONFIRMED (closes the user's \"same item suggested to org2 etc, keep iterating until every lane is happy\" request): org verified on its pause that the gate offered REGEX-LIB-4-XSD-GAPS -- a genuinely claimable, non-leased item -- with NO dup and NO leased-item suggestions since the partition+leased-exclusion fix went live. Because the partition is a single shared hook with deterministic global math (owner=hash(id)%lanes, same for every lane), one lane observing disjoint claimable sets is strong evidence it holds fleet-wide. Residual is only the known TODO, not a bug: a lane still gets a work-hunt nudge while a verification job is in-flight with a live monitor -> the pending-job-aware \"hold until heartbeat\" branch of the queue definition (item #1 job-waits), agreed the highest-confidence remaining core fix.")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_17 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 17u32, "2026-11-10T10:49Z", "org3", "READY-INDEX RANKING RULES (from the regex-video root-cause trace, user 11-10 \"heal the reason thoroughly\"): the reason ready work sits is that a flat priority sort buries deliverable-completing work. MEASURED: 288 ready Planned items (57 P0, 146 P1, 69 P2), nearly all assignee=agent/dual-team. The delegator (rig) must rank by more than flat priority:\n1. FINISH-THE-CHAIN boost: a ready child of a partially-Done epic (>=1 sibling Done, or epic progress>0) ranks ABOVE a fresh same-priority item -- completing a started, user-visible deliverable beats starting new work. The regex video (GRAPH Done, LAYOUT ready) is the fixture: LAYOUT should have been the top org-team pull on 11-07, not buried 3 days under 203 higher-pri fresh items.\n2. UNOWNED-N-DAYS escalation: a ready item unstarted for N days (configurable, ~2) gets flagged/escalated, not silently re-buried each pause. The \"sits forever\" detector.\n3. SKILL-FIT owner routing: a ready item on assignee=agent routes to the skill-fit lane (tags -> lane map), so it stops being everyone's/no-one's. Pairs with the cross-team fallback skill-fit org already asked for.\n4. NEAR-DONE surfacing: a ready item with high progress (e.g. BEND-BACKEND P0 92%) is one push from shippable -> rank up; don't let a 92% P0 sit as Planned under fresh P0s.\nThese are ready-index fields/weights, not gate-local compute (precompute, read don't compute). HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE carries them; the regex-video 4-row epic is the acceptance fixture (after the rules, LAYOUT ranks top for team:org).")
inl note_of_hook_smart_rust_gate_18 () = Note ("HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", 18u32, "2026-11-10T15:41Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 11:5x rig: the ranking rules exist as a model (HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE-D1, rig_ready_rank). NEXT STEP: feed it from the ready index: kit agile next computes epic state (Done children, progress) and idle days per ready item and orders by ranking with default_weights, so the gate (pwsh today, the Rust binary later) only READS a precomputed order; the routes table comes from the lane map. Rides a train after TRAIN44.")
inl kit_help_pool_hides_leases () = Task ("KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES", "kit agile gate: the help pool says claimed items are hidden, but it still offers items under another lane's live lease (and kit agile next --open lists them), so empty lanes burn a refused claim per offer", Story, P2, D2, 60u32, Planned, "", "kit,agile,gate,lease,team:rig", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_kit_help_pool_hides_leases () = Planning ("KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_kit_help_pool_hides_leases_1 () = Note ("KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:38Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-10 05:4x by tibia from the help pool (microblog technique 7: every defect becomes a ready item). Seen 4 times in one hour. The stop gate offered SPS-NEGATIVES-TO-FAILS ('CLAIM FIRST ... SPS-NEGATIVES-TO-FAILS (org, 180 m)'), and the claim was refused with reason=leased-by:org-5a6e53fb:1794302633398; the same happened for SPS-TYPEFEATURES-M1 (leased-by:org-5a6e53fb:1794302904815), CELLS-MEMO-SILENT-WHOLE-KEYS (leased-by:microblog-b7595ac3:...) and FISCAL-NFE-STAMPED-WINDOW (leased-by:fiscal-84b53718:...). All four were Planned, so no status shows the lease; only the claim store knows. DoD: the gate's help-pool slice and kit agile next --open skip any item with a live lease held by another lane (the lease lookup claim uses), with a --include-leased flag to see them; a test where a leased Planned item is absent from next --open for lane B and present for its holder A, and appears again once the lease is released or expires. Next step: rig reads how claim checks the lease (agile_claim_store) and applies the same check in the next and gate listings.")
inl note_of_kit_help_pool_hides_leases_2 () = Note ("KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES", 2u32, "2026-11-10T10:11Z", "tibia", "11-10 06:1x tibia DRAFTED + partly proven (help pool, claimed). Draft drafts\\kit\\KIT-HELP-POOL-HIDES-LEASES\\ is FROZEN; every file has a .base, and the box shas equal the bases at 06:1x. agile_claim_model/claim.spi 5745d24b587c (base 58898eaba9aa): live_items, using the SAME predicate as live_count (now_ms < expires_ms and not a decline), plus a test. agile_claim_store/store.spi 6fa0905ead63 (base a965942b5833): leased_ids ((root, now_ms) : string * u64) : array_base string, exported by name as rig_claims_leased_ids. agile_task_next/task_next.spi bdfcd2def770 (base 086b5f9d9546): agile_next also drops ids in leased_ids, next to the waits filter. agile_task_next/package.spiproj 929043a20642 (base d472ce40bfd3): agile_note_model, agile_claim_model and agile_claim_store under packages:, agile_claim_store under link:. agile_task_next/Cargo.toml be3c559740b6 (base 29ca1317af65): the path dep. agile_task_next/next_cli.spi 33592b588cc2 (base 414e4da39728): an end-to-end test (live lease hidden; expired and declined offered; ready=2). PROVEN on ganymede (pack81): claim model notebook 8/8 green including the new test (tibia-hpl); the store compiles with pub fn rig_claims_leased_ids(v0: &str, v1: u64) (tibia-hpl2). SIZE, same-compiler control (tibia-hplctl): store.rs 871 -> 957 (+86), under 1000 with 43 lines left. NOT PROVEN (the node can't build link: consumers): agile_task_next linking, and the next_cli test. task_next.rs is 983, so an inline would cross 1000, and regen-sizes must show it at about 983. The 4 crates that include agile_task_next as source reach only ranking and tasks_from and need no edit. DoD: amended so next hides ALL live leases, the holder's included (--as is the role; there is no lane flag); the holder resumes through claim or wip. TYPEFEATURES: none new; the lease predicate stays the claim model's single typed rule (live_items beside live_count over list claim), and the boundary is a named tuple-arg export returning array_base string. NEXT STEP: rig applies the frozen draft in a train and checks regen-sizes (task_next.rs about 983, store.rs about 957) and the next_cli test.")
inl hook_gate_fleet_context () = Task ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", "monitors write a compact fleet-context.json (drains+eligibility, night-window, pluto soak, comet, per-node CPU+holder, box user-idle) that the smart gate reads instead of computing synchronously", TaskKind, P1, D3, 40u32, Active, "HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE", "team:org,hooks,gate,monitoring,infra", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hook_gate_fleet_context () = Planning ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Unassigned)
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_1 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:44Z", "org-614162e5", "Child of HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE (org3-owned, resilience lane - executable now). The gate must read a COMPACT fleet-context.json (microsecond read) instead of computing fleet state synchronously. org3's monitors already compute most of it; this item makes them WRITE a single compact file the gate reads. FIELDS (from the 6-lane + org input): per-node {drain|overload, depth, load, CPU%, eligible_mem_cap, holder, release_eta}; night_window bool (local 06:00-21:00); pluto {soak_avail_pct, stall_count, pod_up}; comet {used, limit}; box_user_idle_min (GetLastInputInfo - fixes the harmful 'CPU underused' nudge while the user types, tibia+org); updated ts. The gate reads it for: the CPU-underused nudge (suppress when box_user_idle<30 or user present), the drain eligibility (fiscal/tibia per-node truth), night-window heartbeat-widen (org), and the when: predicate evaluation (node-idle/user-idle - HOOK-GATE-ANSWERED-WAITS reads the same file). DoD: saturation-monitor + the soak writer emit fleet-context.json on their cadence (<=1min staleness), compact (<2KB), atomic write; the gate reads it with a staleness guard (stale -> omit, never a wrong value, per monitor-failure-not-clean-value). First version extends the existing saturation-monitor.sh (no new .ps1, spiral-first). Owner org3.")
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_2 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:47Z", "org-614162e5", "v1 BUILT + working (org3, 09:45Z). relay-lab/bench/fleet-context-write.sh emits relay-lab/fleet-context.json: compact (~400B), valid JSON, ATOMIC write (tmp+mv), freshness-guarded (pluto soak only if last sample <120s, else null not a wrong value). v1 fields live now: updated ts, night_window (local 21:00-06:00), pluto {soak_fresh, avail_pct, stall_count_30}, drains (last drain-state row). Sample this run: night=true, pluto avail=83% stalls=5/30, drains='all3-idle beat1'. The gate reads this file (no synchronous compute). NEXT (v2): fold per-node CPU%+holder+release, comet quota, and box_user_idle_min (GetLastInputInfo - fixes the harmful 'CPU underused' nudge while the user types) into saturation-monitor so one run refreshes everything; wire fleet-context-write into saturation-monitor + my heartbeat for <=1min freshness. Format is stable for rig to code the gate's reader against (schema 'fleet-context/v1'). Progress 40%.")
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_3 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:49Z", "org-614162e5", "Next step: v1 (compact fleet-context.json: night-window, pluto soak, drains) is BUILT + the schema is stable for rig to code the gate's reader against. v2 (per-node CPU%+holder+release + comet into the JSON via saturation-monitor; box_user_idle the gate computes itself in Rust via GetLastInputInfo) becomes relevant once rig builds HOOK-GATE-RUST-CORE that actually READS the file - no point finalizing v2 fields before the gate specifies what it consumes. Parking on lane:rig until the gate reader lands; then org3 completes v2 to match.")
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_4 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 4u32, "2026-11-10T10:19Z", "org3", "v2 NODE-HEADROOM field (from org's europa admission refusal): per node emit mem_available_mb and the admission floor it would be checked against (cap+floor), plus the current largest holder (pid/lane/rss) so the gate can both (a) steer a heavy run to a node that will PASS admission and (b) name the daemon to reclaim. Shape: nodes:{europa:{cpu_pct,mem_avail_mb,admit_floor_mb,headroom_mb,top_holder:{lane,rss_mb}},...}. Sampled by saturation-monitor on its cadence (freshness-guarded like pluto), gate reads it. org hit: MemAvailable 1331MB < cap+floor 2036MB, a peer Cells daemon held 771MB.")
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_5 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 5u32, "2026-11-10T10:24Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 06:2x rig: YES to the v2 NODE-HEADROOM shape (per node cpu_pct, mem_avail_mb, admit_floor_mb, headroom_mb, top_holder lane+rss). It is one of the context probes rig asked for on HOOK-SMART-RUST-GATE (C4): the Rust gate reads it from saturation-monitor's freshness-guarded output; a stale reading is a failed measurement, not a clean value. No further rig decision pending.")
inl note_of_hook_gate_fleet_context_6 () = Note ("HOOK-GATE-FLEET-CONTEXT", 6u32, "2026-11-10T10:28Z", "org3", "v1 HARDENING: fixed a freshness-guard flap in fleet-context-write.sh. The pluto soak guard was 120s, reasoned from the loop's 20s sleep; but under box load each probe's own latency dominates (serve spiked to 1.6s) so samples land ~100-160s apart, and a healthy soak kept reading fresh=false/avail=null -- losing the pluto signal exactly when load makes it most relevant. Widened to 300s (~2.5 missed samples still flags a genuinely dead soak). Verified: now fresh=true avail_pct=93 stall_count_30=2. Lesson: a freshness guard must exceed the MEASURED producer interval, not the nominal sleep. Also confirmed the file now serves current state (night_window from local hour, drains from drain-state.tsv) for the gate to read on every pause.")
inl font_cells_notebook_split () = Task ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", "font_glyph Cells gate times out: split the notebook so the recurring font lib gate completes", TaskKind, P2, D3, 10u32, Active, "", "spiral,font,cells,gate,standards,team:org", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_font_cells_notebook_split () = Planning ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_font_cells_notebook_split_1 () = Note ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", 1u32, "2026-11-10T09:46Z", "org-f33af161", "Found by org-f33af161 2026-11-10. The font_glyph.spi Cells notebook (now 132 cells after the Arabic+Hindi growth) TIMES OUT: it did not finish in 20min even on idle europa (load 0.6), nor on ganymede. Root cause: the Cells runner recompiles the module per cell, and the module grew to ~322 glyphs + 326 top-level join-point functions (from the FONT-COMPLEX-SCRIPTS codegen fix: builders := to :~ for the emitter build), so per-cell compile x 132 cells exceeds the wall clock. TRADEOFF captured: the join-point refactor sped the rustc EMITTER build (10m19s to 4m11s) but raised the Cells gate per-cell cost (more top-level fns to resolve). The negatives were validated separately via a minimal 4-cell notebook (functions + just the 4 fails cells) which runs fast. DoD: the recurring font lib gate completes within the timeout. OPTIONS: split font_glyph into smaller sibling modules (e.g. font_glyph_arabic, font_glyph_deva, font_glyph_latin) each with its own cells; OR a single combined assertion cell instead of many tiny ones; OR a Cells-runner warm/shared-compile path so cells do not each recompile the whole module. Proven by emitter build + rustbuzz shaping that the font itself is correct; this is purely the recurring-gate runtime.")
inl note_of_font_cells_notebook_split_2 () = Note ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", 2u32, "2026-11-10T09:55Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:5x claimed (help pool). Notebook: alphabet/lib/font_glyph.spi, 142 KB, 128 test cells (127 ///> rust), 332 join points. Before picking a split, MEASURE where a cells time goes: Spiral per-test compile of the big module, rustc at Cells fixed opt-level=2 (cells.ex @rust_flags), or the run. PROBE RUNNING: europa job-1794304510244-x-font-probe (europa load 0.72 on 2 cores, nice 10, memo off, no gate window) runs 3 font cells (devanagari_category_is_typed, cap_zhe_is_stem_and_four_arms_fifteen_quads, m_is_three_stems_and_two_arches_twentyone_quads) against the box lib synced into my xcache tree, and prints wall time plus each cells phases_ms. DECISION RULE (pre-registered): if rustc is most of a cell, the lever is CELLS-RUST-OPT-LEVEL (a lower opt-level for test cells, which helps every lane), and the font becomes its 4th probe. If Spiral compile dominates, split font_glyph into sibling modules (latin / cyrillic+greek / hangul / arabic / devanagari) so each cell compiles a smaller module. DoD unchanged: the recurring font lib gate completes within its timeout. TYPEFEATURES: none expected; the split keeps the typed GADTs per script.")
inl note_of_font_cells_notebook_split_3 () = Note ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", 3u32, "2026-11-10T09:55Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "NEXT STEP: read the europa probe (kit job list --on europa, never job wait --on), then apply the decision rule above.")
inl note_of_font_cells_notebook_split_4 () = Note ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", 4u32, "2026-11-10T09:58Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 05:5x PROBE RESULT (europa job-1794304510244, load 0.15, memo off, no gate window): 3 of 3 passed in 35 s wall. phases_ms per cell: Spiral compile 2682 / 2176 / 76 ms, rustc 615 / 448 / 705 ms, mount ~0.8 s, run under 10 ms, queue 9.4-12 s (waiting on the cold compiler daemon). So per-cell work is about 4 s: neither a per-cell module recompile nor rustc at opt-level 2 is expensive, and 127 cells at that rate come to about 8 min even run one at a time. NEITHER branch of the pre-registered rule applies, so the 20-min timeout must come from outliers (a few huge cells), the prelude, or the cold queue under load. NEXT STEP: europa job-1794304672476-x-font-full runs ALL 127 cells in 4 batches (each batch writes its own ipynb, so a slow batch cannot erase the rest), then ranks cells by work time (compile + rustc + run + mount, queue excluded) and prints the top 12 by name. The fix targets whatever that list names.")
inl note_of_font_cells_notebook_split_5 () = Note ("FONT-CELLS-NOTEBOOK-SPLIT", 5u32, "2026-11-10T10:51Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 06:5x FULL RUN RESULT (europa job-1794304672476-x-font-full, 4 batches, memo off, no gate window): ALL 127 cells PASSED; batches 55 / 45 / 53 / 50 s wall, about 3.4 min total. Sum of work 281 s: Spiral compile 176 s, rustc 69 s, run 1 s; the slowest cell 5.4 s (letter_gecyr quad_seg_count: compile 1.3 s, rustc 2.0 s). So the NOTEBOOK does not need a split: nothing in it is slow. The 20-min timeout org saw must come from the gate WRAPPER: run_notebook --gate requests a kit test-suite window and waits up to 30 min for it (SPIRAL_CELLS_GATE_WAIT_MIN), or a cold/stale compiler daemon queue (queue phases here were 1-4 s per cell). NEXT STEP: rerun the exact recurring gate command org uses, read its phases_ms and window wait, and fix the wrapper (or its budget), not the notebook.")
