inl note_of_warm_client_lib_land_window_1 () = Note ("WARM-CLIENT-LIB-LAND-WINDOW", 1u32, "2026-11-10T11:01Z", "compiler", "Child of COMPILER-WARM-CLIENTS. Pattern proven by test.ps1 -Suite linked (11-09, now default): one resident --server-tcp compiler per (core hash, root), jobs/results TSV, retired on core-hash change; ganymede A/B on 21 rows: cold 131 s vs warm 85 s (1.54x), 0 compile/native/exit/stdout/residual diffs. DoD: this client reuses one warm process, before/after wall times on the same inputs, byte-identical outputs. TypeFeatures: none (gate script). Next step: the owner measures one lib_land_window consumer gate cold, then routes its compiles through a --server-tcp session.")
inl note_of_warm_client_lib_land_window_2 () = Note ("WARM-CLIENT-LIB-LAND-WINDOW", 2u32, "2026-11-10T12:02Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 08:0x local (slice-on-demand check): NOT sliced, because the authoring half already exists: lib_land_window.ps1 lines 291-299 route the consumer compiles through ONE compiler process (--batch jobs/results TSV in mix-builds\\lib-land-consumers) when SPIRAL_LAND_WARM=1; the default stays per-consumer. What remains is a RUN: measure one consumer gate cold (SPIRAL_LAND_WARM unset) and warm (=1) on the same lib state, compare wall time, and check the generated consumer .rs outputs are byte-identical; if warm is faster and identical, flip the default to warm (one line). NEXT STEP: run both arms back to back in the next quiet box or free node window (a consumers-only land of a no-op draft keeps the rest of the window out of the timing).")
