inl note_of_gpu_multi_node_pipeline_1 () = Note ("GPU-MULTI-NODE-PIPELINE", 1u32, "2026-11-10T11:45Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-10 07:4x from the user (verbatim): 'plan later that if the experiments pay off with the $15 we might test multi gpu cluster pipelines later to battletest/dogfood rig's coordination capabilities'. Gate: GPU-STACKED-RUNS must first show single-GPU runs pay off (a stacked tibia/transformer queue at measured high GPU utilization, results analyzable afterwards). DoD: 2+ rented GPU pods driven as kit job hosts through rig's coordination (kit job start --on <pod>, chains, waits), one pipeline whose stages cross pods, all results out with sha checks, every pod stopped or terminated and verified gone, with cost and the setup/teardown overhead per pod recorded against the single-pod baseline (node 12's first rental: 9 min 25 s billed for 20 s of work). Every rig coordination defect found becomes a ready rig item. TypeFeatures: a pod lifecycle union (Creating, Running, Stopped, Terminated) and a stage record, never string states. Next step: after GPU-STACKED-RUNS records a paid-off stacked run, draft the 2-pod plan with rig.")
inl tibia_lanes_cuda () = Task ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", "tibia's predict lanes on the H200 through the Spiral Cpp/CUDA backend (CudaHost/CudaDevice), since the datacenter GPU exposes no Vulkan for the WGSL lanes", Story, P1, D4, 80u32, Active, "", "tibia,gpu,cuda,runpod,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_lanes_cuda () = Planning ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 1u32, "2026-11-10T11:56Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-10 07:5x by tibia. Why: node 12 (RunPod H200) refuses Vulkan instance creation, so apps/predict/lanes_gpu.spi (WGSL) can't run there; the fork compiler already has CUDA targets in its Cpp backend (spiral_compiler.fs 28057-28058, 28478-28479). Plan: (1) compile the lanes kernel with the Cpp backend's CudaDevice target on ganymede inside an nvidia/cuda devel image (nvcc needs no GPU to compile), rehearsed as a k3s pod; (2) check lanes 0-3 equal the C lanes on the CPU host side first; (3) one rental: the CUDA probe, then the lanes kernel at increasing lane counts, GPU utilization sampled, results and sha out, pod stopped. DoD: lanes 0-3 equal C on the H200, a measured lanes/s at saturation, and the cost recorded. TypeFeatures: the backend target as the existing union (CudaHost | CudaDevice), lane counts as typed sizes. Next step: compile lanes_gpu's kernel through --backend Cpp with the CUDA target on ganymede and read what the CudaDevice path rejects.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 2u32, "2026-11-10T12:02Z", "tibia", "11-10 08:0x tibia STEP 1 (ganymede job tibia-cudaprobe, pack81, --backend 'Cpp + Cuda', the backend name from spiral_compiler.fs 37639): lanes, lanes_gpu and gpu_plan all COMPILE (rc 0). Each emits <e>.cpp (host; lanes 23,905 lines, lanes_gpu 23,944), <e>.hpp, <e>.corelib.hpp and a 1-line <e>.cu, with 0 __global__ and 0 __device__: no device code, because the lanes code never enters the CUDA kernel construct, and lanes_gpu's GPU path is the WGSL-only run_target branch ($'spiral_lane'; '_ => 0u32' elsewhere). So the backend is usable and the host side builds, but a CUDA kernel needs the device-launch construct the ML library used with 'Cpp + Cuda'. Next step: find that construct (the kernel closure and grid/block launch) in the fork's samples or the ML library and draft a lanes kernel entry with it.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_3 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 3u32, "2026-11-10T12:05Z", "tibia", "11-10 08:0x tibia: THE KERNEL CONSTRUCT IS FOUND, and our compiler supports it. In mrakgr's ML library (C:\\home\\git\\Spiral-s-ML-Library, old dialect), corecuda/base.spi:69-71 call_backend enters device code with join_backend CudaDevice (f () : ()), and base.spi:120 on, run_cuda_device_from_cuda_host, emits the launch: cudaFuncSetAttribute (MaxDynamicSharedMemorySize, NonPortableClusterSizeAllowed) and a cudaLaunchAttribute with cluster dims, then the launch over blocks_per_grid x threads_per_block. Our fork compiler keeps the construct: spiral_compiler.fs tokens 1093 ('join_backend', new spelling '~~>' at 1281), parser case_join_point_backend 3349, and the codegen comment at 29081 ('Kernels (join_backend CudaDevice) go through CodegenCpp.codegen''). On H100/H200, base.spi:33-35 notes cluster-launch limits (132 SMs, but clusters of 16 allow 112 blocks), so use blocks_per_cluster 1 first. Next step: draft apps/predict/cuda_lanes.spi in today's dialect: a minimal port of call_backend + run_cuda_device_from_cuda_host (cluster 1, no dynamic shared memory) and a trivial kernel (each thread writes its global index into a device array, host copies it back and checks it), compiled with --backend 'Cpp + Cuda' on ganymede; nvcc it in an nvidia/cuda devel k3s pod (no GPU needed to compile); then run it on node 12 at the next rental, before porting the lanes body.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_4 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 4u32, "2026-11-10T12:15Z", "tibia", "11-10 08:1x tibia STEP 2: A REAL CUDA KERNEL from today's compiler. Draft drafts\\tibia\\TIBIA-LANES-CUDA\\apps\\cuda_probe\\cuda_probe.spi (current dialect, about 30 lines): main enters '~~> CudaHost device_sum ()'; device_sum cudaMallocManaged's 64 int32, calls launch, sums on the host and prints OK when the sum is 2016; launch enters '~~> CudaDevice kernel_body p' and emits cudaLaunchKernelEx with a 2x32 grid. Generated with pack81 --backend 'Cpp + Cuda' (job tibia-cudaprobe2): cuda_probe.cu holds a __device__ kernel_body_2(int32_t * v0) that writes threadIdx.x + blockIdx.x * blockDim.x, an extern C __global__ cuda_device_entry0(int32_t * v0), and launch_1 with auto kernel = cuda_device_entry0 then cudaLaunchKernelEx(&v1, kernel, v0); cuda_probe.cpp main calls cuda_host_entry0. So the ML library's call_backend pattern works in today's dialect with no lib port. Trap fixed: a backslash-n inside a single-quoted macro reaches C as a literal newline inside a string (the macro unescapes once), so the newline is now putchar(10). In flight: job tibia-cuda-nvcc compiles and links it with nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 -arch=sm_90 in an nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-devel-ubuntu22.04 k3s pod on ganymede (no GPU there, so only compile and link are proven). Next step: after the nvcc rehearsal links, run the binary on node 12 at the next rental (expect sum 2016 OK), then port the lanes body into the kernel.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_5 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 5u32, "2026-11-10T12:59Z", "tibia", "11-10 08:5x tibia STEP 3 (nvcc rehearsal GREEN, rental attempt FAILED to start). Rehearsal: the k3s pod tibia-cuda-nvcc4 (nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-devel-ubuntu22.04 on ganymede, image cached after a 111 s first pull) compiled and linked the probe with nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 -arch=sm_90 in 2 s (binary ed794eb5fe2d, 997 KB; only #20012 warnings on defaulted ctors in corelib.hpp). Negative control without a GPU: it prints 'alloc CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version' and exits 3, so the error checks work (the unchecked first build segfaulted). Two macro traps fixed: a backslash-n becomes a literal newline (use putchar(10)), and a bang inside a macro is ALWAYS an interpolation (so != broke the tokenizer; use 'if (!alloc)'). RENTAL: (a) podResume of the stopped node-12 pod fsouc1ubw5nyrp FAILED: 'not enough free GPUs on the host machine', since a stopped pod is pinned to its host; (b) a fresh H100 SXM secure pod (bb9vu31vnslrgo, .99/h) stayed RUNNING with no container runtime for 19 min (the runpod/pytorch 2.4.0 cuda12.4.1 image never started on that host); terminated at 12:56:24Z, cost about .26, no run. Balance now 12.90 (two rentals today about .10). LESSONS: weeks-on-and-off reuse needs a NETWORK VOLUME (datacenter-scoped, attachable from any free GPU there) instead of a stopped pod; the readiness deadline should be about 6 min, not 19 (abort and pick another host); and the probe needs no PyTorch (nvcc links cudart statically, so only the driver and sshd are needed), so use the smallest RunPod base image. Next step: retry the probe on a fresh pod with a small RunPod base image and a 6 min readiness abort, expect 'cuda_probe sum 2016 ... OK', then port the lanes body.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_6 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 6u32, "2026-11-10T13:08Z", "tibia", "11-10 09:0x tibia STEP 4: THE CUDA ROUTE IS PROVEN ON A DATACENTER GPU. A fresh RunPod H100 SXM secure pod (tx6o60jwgx18f5, .99/h, runpod/pytorch 2.4.0 cuda12.4.1 image, ready in 22 s on this host) ran the k3s-rehearsed binary ed794eb5fe2d (built from the Spiral 'Cpp + Cuda' output by nvcc -arch=sm_90 in a k3s pod on ganymede): 'cuda_probe alloc no error', 'launch no error', 'sync no error', 'cuda_probe sum 2016 expect 2016 OK', rc 0, on driver 595.91.07. The whole rental (create, wait, upload, run, terminate) was ONE scripted unit: billed 95 s, about .11 (the first rental took 9 min 25 s for 20 s of work). A detached 10 min watchdog was armed and disarmed; billing back to .006/h (the stopped node-12 volume). Balance 12.73 (today's rentals total about .27 of the ). proof: the probe's own self-check printed OK on the H100. So a Spiral kernel written with '~~> CudaDevice' runs on the rented GPU with no Vulkan and no lib port. Next step: port the lanes body (apps/predict lanes, today WGSL via run_target) into a CudaDevice kernel, one lane per thread, check lanes 0-3 against the C lanes in a k3s rehearsal (host side on ganymede), then a stacked rental measuring lanes/s.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_7 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 7u32, "2026-11-10T13:39Z", "tibia", "11-10 09:3x tibia STEP 5: the lanes kernel BLOCKS on the compiler. cuda_lanes.spi (lane_score per thread, 1-D grid of 64, managed out array, host printing lane rows in the C lanes format) fails partial eval: 'Cannot find the backend CudaDevice in the backend switch op.' Lib backend_switch records (backend.spi run_target_args' and others) have Cpp/CppHost keys and no CudaDevice/CudaHost. Adding CudaHost/CudaDevice arms to run_target_args' alone (drafts\\spiral\\TIBIA-LANES-CUDA\\backend.spi, overlaid only on tibia's ganymede lib copy, restored after) did NOT clear it, so more switches are on the lane path. The compiler-level fallback is filed as COMPILER-CUDA-SWITCH-FALLBACK (team:compiler, patch drafted). C REFERENCE captured (spiral --backend C lanes.spi + gcc, tiny plan): lane 0 -1000121800, lane 1 -1000138000, lane 2 -1000600000, lane 3 -1000600000; the CUDA lanes must print exactly these. Next step: after COMPILER-CUDA-SWITCH-FALLBACK is in a pack on ganymede, rerun the lanes gate (drafts\\tibia\\TIBIA-LANES-CUDA + scratch lanesgate), fix what nvcc rejects in the device code, then rent for the lanes 0-3 parity check.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_8 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 8u32, "2026-11-10T15:07Z", "tibia", "11-10 11:1x tibia: the switch fallbacks WORK (pack85 + lib program 2 got past am'.spi:11). Next stop, structural: 'Array length is not supported in the Cuda C++ backend as they are bare pointers.' Filed COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH (team:compiler). Until then the CUDA route is proven only for array-free kernels (cuda_probe sum 2016 OK on the H100). Next step: after COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH lands in a pack, rerun the lanes gate (pack pin + lib program 2), fix what nvcc rejects, then one scripted rental for the lanes 0-3 parity check.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_9 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 9u32, "2026-11-10T22:48Z", "tibia", "11-10 18:5x tibia (after the box BSOD at 11:51, down until 18:28): pack88 (COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH, fat SpiralArray {ptr, len}) + lib program 2 COMPILED THE WHOLE LANE CODE: ganymede job tibia-cuda-lanes5 (finished 11:5x, survived the box crash), spiral --backend 'Cpp + Cuda' rc 0, cuda_lanes.cu 4,752,661 bytes + cuda_lanes.hpp 262,589 + corelib.hpp + a 105-byte cpp. Its nvcc step found nothing only because my gate copied from the source dir while this compile wrote to lanesout/; fixed in gate v6 (tibia-cuda-lanes6, nvcc -arch=sm_90 on the existing output, in the nvidia/cuda k3s pod). Recovery checks: RunPod spend 0.006/h (only the stopped volume), no pod running, no stray watchdog; the applied map-split files (17) still equal their drafts. Next step: after tibia-cuda-lanes6 links, run the scripted one-shot rental (rental-oneshot.ps1 pattern) on an H100 and compare lanes 0-3 with the C reference (-1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000).")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_10 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 10u32, "2026-11-10T22:53Z", "tibia", "11-10 19:0x tibia: gate v7 (pack88, my uint32_t macro fixed to unsigned int): the generated CUDA has exactly 2 nvcc errors left, both run-time-sized stack arrays (VLAs) from the fat-array lowering; reported on COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH note 2. The rental script is ready (scratchpad rental-lanes.ps1: uploads cuda_lanes.bin, timeout 300, prints the lane rows plus start/end). Next step: after compiler's next pack lowers run-time-sized arrays, rerun gate v7 with that pack pinned, then run the rental and compare lanes 0-3 with the C reference.")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_11 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 11u32, "2026-11-11T00:30Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:3x tibia: FIRST GPU RUN OF THE REAL LANES. pack89's generated CUDA BUILT on a RunPod H100 (nvcc 12.4 -O1, -arch=sm_90, about 8 min single-threaded, binary 8.6 MB sha 63203f7b16a6; ganymede OOMed at 3 GiB) and RAN: alloc ok, launch ok, then sync 'an illegal memory access was encountered', lanes all 0. compute-sanitizer: 'Stack overflow at loop_5(int, SpiralArray<int>, ...)' called from lane_body_2, on every thread. ptxas had warned that cuda_device_entry0's stack size cannot be statically determined: the recursive loops with alloca'd run-time arrays (pack89) exceed CUDA's default per-thread stack (about 1 KiB). FIX in tibia's host code (cuda_lanes.spi): cudaDeviceSetLimit(cudaLimitStackSize, 131072) before the launch (128 KiB; 256 KiB would reserve about 69 GB for all resident threads on an H100). The same line is inserted into the pack89 output for the rerun. Rental lessons: the first build-and-run attempt lost its output because an 8-minute silent nvcc dropped the ssh connection (no keepalive); now ServerAliveInterval=15. That rental cost .24 (37 min incl. the hang); balance .37. Rerun in flight. Next step: after the rerun, compare lanes 0-3 with the C reference (-1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000).")
inl note_of_tibia_lanes_cuda_12 () = Note ("TIBIA-LANES-CUDA", 12u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:5x tibia: three rentals tonight (build + run on the H100, about .7 each plus .24 for one that hung): lanes build, launch, then overflow the stack in loop_5, unbounded because pack89 allocas run-time arrays inside recursive loops. Not fixable by any stack limit (cap below 1 MiB). GPU rentals for this item are PAUSED until compiler changes the in-loop storage. Spend today: .05 of the  balance; balance .95, volume-only billing. Scripts ready to rerun unchanged: drafts\\tibia\\TIBIA-LANES-CUDA\\rental-build-run.ps1 + remote-build-run.sh + gen-pack89.tgz (regenerate from the new pack). Next step: after compiler's next pack, regenerate cuda_lanes.cu on ganymede and rent once for the lanes 0-3 parity check.")
