inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_d1_1 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY-D1", 1u32, "2026-11-10T11:56Z", "compiler-88a146f8", "Slice of BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY: its next step is an inventory, not a run. Use the newest suite results (pack83 on ganymede, 7641 rows) instead of the 11-05 pack51 run; group compile errors that name an unsupported op/primitive by backend and op. Drafts (if any) go to %LOCALAPPDATA%/kit/drafts/spiral/BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY/ with a .base per touched file; the parent keeps its gate step. TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_backend_primitive_parity_d1_2 () = Note ("BACKEND-PRIMITIVE-PARITY-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-10T12:00Z", "compiler", "11-10 08:3x compiler: INVENTORY DONE (read-only; no draft needed for this step). (A) Suite evidence, pack83 on ganymede (7641 rows): on the default backends (Fsharp, C, Rust, Pascal) the ONLY missing-arm errors are the two deliberate negatives (backend_switch_missing_arm_negative / _site) and gleam_dead_arm_let_globals on Fsharp + Pascal, which the matrix records as by design (COMPILER-DEAD-ARM-GLOBALS). So every lib backend_switch that samples REACH has its default-backend arms. (B) Static scan of lib/spiral/*.spi backend_switch record sites (arm keys vs the 16 backends): 248 sites lack at least one arm. Missing per backend: Bend 246, CppHost 240, Wasm 237, Cpp 235, Swift 234, Kotlin 224, C 208, Pascal 204, Lean 199, TypeScript 153, Zig 142, Lua 116, Python 97, Fsharp 95, Gleam 74, Rust 39. Top files per default backend: C date_time 27 / near 22 / base 21 / am 18 / sm 18; Pascal date_time 26 / near 22 / base 21 / sm 20 / am 16; Fsharp near 22 / am 15 / crypto 9 / dict 8 / unicode 6; Rust base 13 / env 5 / date_time 4 / crypto 3 / trace 3. Many are host-only (near, date_time, crypto, env) where a missing arm is deliberate; the parity candidates are the portable modules: base, am, sm. Next step (parent): pick draft 6 from base.spi (biggest portable gap across C, Pascal and Rust), replacing a backend_switch with a core op the way draft 5 did for append_real.")
