inl note_of_cells_port_compiler_client_d1_1 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT-D1", 1u32, "2026-11-10T14:16Z", "microblog-b7595ac3", "microblog 11-10 14:1xZ found in the migration audit: spi/daemon.spi (177 lines: compile_ask, compile_reply, status/hello/pool replies, request r GADT, fields/tabbed codec) and spi/client.spi (125 lines: a typed transact over erlang_tcp) have NO domain export and no consumer; compiler_client.ex (850 lines) hand-builds the same tab lines. DoD: domain exports for encode/decode green on the Gleam build; compiler_client.ex uses them for every request kind; a parity test shows the old hand-built lines and the Spiral-encoded lines are byte-equal for a table of requests, and that decoding the recorded replies gives the same structs; compiler_daemon_test + warm_notebook_test unchanged. TYPEFEATURES: the request r GADT stays inside Spiral; the wire is the existing tab line. NEXT STEP: after CELLS-PORT-MEMO-D2/D3 land, read compiler_client.ex request builders and draft the exports.")
inl note_of_cells_port_compiler_client_d1_2 () = Note ("CELLS-PORT-COMPILER-CLIENT-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:15Z", "microblog", "landed (unstaged): daemon.spi status_row + gleam test, main.spi export status_reply, domain.ex passthrough status_reply: 1, compiler_client.ex live_daemons reads CompilerClient.status_fields (Spiral decode; empty pid = nil row), test compiler_status_parity_test.exs (old hand parse as oracle, 10 reply shapes). ganymede proof2: 35 passed rc=0 (parity + compiler_daemon_status + compiler_daemon tests). Base-checked all 4 files before copy.")
inl lua_bench_strings_timeout () = Task ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", "Lua: bench_strings times out (> 900 s, 1 round) while every other backend finishes; find the superlinear step", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,lua,performance,benchmark,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout () = Planning ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout_1 () = Note ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", 1u32, "2026-11-10T14:45Z", "compiler", "From BACKEND-BENCH-CI-2 11-10 (moon, pack83): bench_strings ran on 12 backends; Python and TypeScript finish it at 1 round, Lua times out at 900 s. Lua codegen emits StringIndex and StringSlice as string.sub (linear in the slice, no utf8 walk), so the cost is elsewhere: candidates are u32 Mult/BitwiseXor lowering on Lua (masking helpers), the code char map (string EQ), or per-slice allocation. TypeFeatures: none. Next step: on moon, time bench_strings on Lua at text lengths 512 / 1024 / 2048 to get the growth exponent, then profile the hot op.")
inl note_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout_2 () = Note ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:24Z", "compiler", "11-10 11:2x compiler: cause found, not a hang. (1) test.ps1 caps a Lua run at 60 s (scripts/test.ps1 Invoke-Native for Lua, line ~1118); the workload is the designed n^2/2 (moon Lua 5.1: 252 chars 0.3 s, 504 1.1 s, 1008 4.34 s, so exponent 2.0) and took 77.1 s at 4095. (2) The hot op: Lua 5.1/5.4 have no bit32/bit, so every u32 xor/and/or fell back to spiral_bit_combine, a 32-step loop with a closure call per bit (7.3 us/op). Fix (drafts\\spiral\\LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT\\1-lua-bit-byte-tables.kitdo): lazily built 256x256 byte tables per op, 4 lookups per op: 1.12 us/op, 120 ms one-time build, identical to the bitwise fallback over 200k pseudo-random pairs and to a Python reference over 1000 mixed bxor/band/bor steps. Full workload on moon Lua 5.1: old 77.1 s, new 22.3 s (under the 60 s cap). pack87 = pack86 + this (core f0ac9715), gating on ganymede vs pack86. Next step: read ~/remote-stage/pack87/g.log at \"p87 done\", then install and rerun bench_strings Lua on moon.")
inl note_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout_3 () = Note ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", 3u32, "2026-11-10T15:29Z", "compiler", "DoD: (1) bench_strings on Lua finishes inside test.ps1s 60 s Lua run cap on moon (Lua 5.1) and agrees with the other backends (exit 241); (2) the new bit fallback matches the old one bit for bit (200k-pair check plus a Python reference); (3) suite vs the previous pack has 0 diffs; (4) installed. TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout_4 () = Note ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", 4u32, "2026-11-10T22:57Z", "compiler", "11-10 18:5x compiler: runtime proof on moon (Lua 5.1; no Lua on ganymede, so ganymede proves codegen only: pack87 suite 7640 rows 0 diffs, residual changed 29 = the Lua preamble text): test.ps1 -Suite all -Backend Lua -Native with installed pack83 vs a moon pack87 build: 459 rows, all ran under both, exactly 1 diff: samples/bench_strings timeout -> exit 241 in 33.2 s (cap 60 s); other bench rows: float 7.2 s, sieve 5.0 s, tree 7.2 s, overhead 46 ms. DoD 1-3 met; 4 (installed) rides with pack88/89: store put, ganymede installing.")
inl note_of_lua_bench_strings_timeout_5 () = Note ("LUA-BENCH-STRINGS-TIMEOUT", 5u32, "2026-11-10T23:34Z", "compiler", "Evidence: DONE. pack89 (contains pack87s Lua bit byte tables) INSTALLED on all four machines 11-10 evening: box 19:18 local (smoke --check ok), ganymede 23:15Z, europa 23:19Z, moon (kit job job-1794352960944-compiler-moon-inst89b, exit 0). Runtime proof (moon, Lua 5.1): 459 Lua rows run under pack83 and pack87; the only diff is samples/bench_strings timeout -> exit 241 in 33.2 s (cap 60 s). Bit fallback identical to the old one over 200k pairs and to a Python reference over 1000 mixed steps. Suite (ganymede) 7640 rows 0 diffs. DoD 1-4 met.")
inl fiscal_typed_units_d1 () = Task ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1", "draft: xsd_emit dependency SCCs over emitted.references (reach sets, mutual reach = one component, dependency-first order by reach size, recursive_group for self loops), tested on a literal graph and on run-time (dyn) data; emission unchanged until D2 wires it into group_members/functions_group", TaskKind, P2, D1, 100u32, Done, "", "team:fiscal", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_fiscal_typed_units_d1 () = Planning ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_d1_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1", 1u32, "2026-11-10T14:48Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Slice of FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS: drafts go to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1\\ with a .base per touched file; the parent keeps its gate step. Stacks on the FISCAL-VERSIONED-FAMILIES-1-MDFE-D1 xsd_emit draft (FEE124468E4F, in proof): the .base is that draft, so it rebases only if that proof goes red. Next step: write the draft.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_d1_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-10T14:56Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 11:5x DRAFT written: drafts\\spiral\\FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1\\xsd_emit.spi 992F00EFD7C9 over .base FEE124468E4F (the in-proof MDFE-D1 draft). Appended, emission unchanged: aliases reaches/groups/sized; edges_of and reach (worklist over emitted.references, names outside the unit such as builtins are harmless); reach_table (one reach set per item); mutual (each reaches the other) -> members_of / add_component / components (folded with core listm::fold, item order kept); scc_order sorts the components by reach-set size with a stable insertion (slot): if group C uses group D then reach(C) strictly contains reach(D), so smaller-first is dependency-first without a separate topological pass; recursive_group = more than one member or a self reference (singletons without one become plain union/nominal in D2). Tests: dependency_groups_come_dependencies_first (graph a<->b, c->a, d->d, e, f->c,string gives [d][e][a;b][c][f]), a_cycle_or_a_self_reference_is_recursive, a_group_without_a_cycle_is_plain, and dependency_groups_hold_on_run_time_items (dyn input, guards the compile-time-literal trap). Every new cell under 271 by the oracle (239 -> 239). Cost is O(n * e * n) with list lookups, fine for the ~150 types per unit; a map-based Tarjan is the upgrade if a unit grows. Next step: canonical round trip (running), then a type probe on a node once the MDFE-D1 proof frees the xsd_emit base.")
inl note_of_fiscal_typed_units_d1_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1", 3u32, "2026-11-10T15:35Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 11:4x DRAFT DONE. ganymede job-1794324356175-fiscal-scc-check, against the pre-C/Pascal lib tar with the proven MDFE-D1 xsd_emit (FEE124468E4F) as base: type-probe xsd_emit.spi PASS, tests typed 43 (39 existing + the 4 new SCC tests), errors 0, control ok; round trip fixpoint true, canonical only after adding the final newline, so the draft is now the canonical bytes 05ED9255E232. Budget-neutral by the oracle (239 -> 239). Draft: drafts\\spiral\\FISCAL-TYPED-UNITS-D1\\xsd_emit.spi, .base = the MDFE-D1 draft. Next step: none for this slice; the parent proves (executed_cells) and lands it after MDFE-D1 lands, then D2 wires scc_order into group_members/functions_group.")
inl tibia_terrain_spi_stale () = Task ("TIBIA-TERRAIN-SPI-STALE", "tibia src/terrain.spi is stale against the server map: 143 of 308 generated terrain definitions (arena_spot_bits_*) differ from the map tool's current output, with the ORIGINAL unsplit map too", Story, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "tibia,map,terrain,team:tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_terrain_spi_stale () = Planning ("TIBIA-TERRAIN-SPI-STALE", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_tibia_terrain_spi_stale_1 () = Note ("TIBIA-TERRAIN-SPI-STALE", 1u32, "2026-11-10T14:55Z", "tibia", "Found 11-10 by tibia while proving TIBIA-MAP-FIELD-READER parity on ganymede: the map output (identical for the split and the original, sha 4cb20a8caec8) formatted and respelled with the current compiler gives 308 definitions, 165 found verbatim in src/terrain.spi and 143 not (the arena_spot_bits_* tables). Either the Canary map changed since terrain.spi was last regenerated, or the formatter's layout of these tables changed. DoD: classify the 143 (content vs layout), then regenerate terrain.spi with apps/map/run.ps1 -Update on the box (or a node with the inputs) and confirm the arena tests that read terrain still pass. Next step: diff one missing definition's generated text against its terrain.spi version to tell content from layout.")
inl note_of_tibia_terrain_spi_stale_2 () = Note ("TIBIA-TERRAIN-SPI-STALE", 2u32, "2026-11-10T14:59Z", "tibia", "11-10 11:0x tibia CLASSIFIED: NO CONTENT CHANGE. Of the 308 generated definitions (formatted + respelled with the current compiler), 165 are verbatim in HEAD src/terrain.spi, 135 differ only in whitespace layout (e.g. '|? row <= 2 ?> X |: Y' now split across lines), and the 8 table roots (arena_spot_bits, arena_road_bits, arena_sight_bits ...) differ only in syntax: the generator now emits an else-if chain ('|? word = 1 ?>') where terrain.spi has an else with a nested if ('|: ? word = 1 ?>'), which is semantically identical. So the map data has not changed; regenerating terrain.spi is cosmetic, but needed so run.ps1's terrain check passes. HOLD: the working tree's src/terrain.spi carries another lane's unstaged rename (step_away_like_the_live_probe -> step_away_like_the_live_gauge, line 1751); run.ps1 -Update only rewrites the arena_* definitions by name, but regenerating now could race that lane's next write. Next step: after that rename lands (its file shows no unstaged diff), run apps/map/run.ps1 -Update (box when its CPU calms, or a node with the inputs) and check git diff shows only arena_* layout changes.")
inl pe_dead_branch_fold_error () = Task ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", "Partial evaluation: a constant-fold error inside a branch whose condition is not static fails the build (should be a runtime path)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,partial-evaluation,rust,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error () = Planning ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error_1 () = Note ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", 1u32, "2026-11-10T14:57Z", "compiler", "From org2 11-10 (spiral/apps/realworld, Rust target only; the same tests pass on Gleam). Shape: ? sm'::starts_with \"Token \" header ?> Some (sm'::substring 6i32 (sm'::length header - 6i32) header) |: None with a literal \"\" header: starts_with does not fold, so both branches are partially evaluated, and the dead branch's substring constant-folds on the literal and raises TracedError \"UTF-8 string of byte length 0's slice from 6 to -1 is invalid\". Second hit: a 14-byte slice of \"/api/articles\" behind a starts_with \"/api/profiles/\" guard. Logs: ganymede /home/remote/stage/org2-rwr2/gate-server.log and /home/remote/stage/org2-rwa/gate-server.log. Workaround in use: guard on length first (length >= 6 && starts_with folds). Two fixes to weigh: (1) make starts_with/ends_with on literals fold (then the branch is static); (2) a fold that raises inside a dynamic branch emits the op as a runtime call (or a runtime failwith) instead of a compile error. TypeFeatures: none. Next step: reproduce with a 5-line sample on Rust, find why starts_with on two literals does not fold, and decide (1) vs (2).")
inl note_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error_2 () = Note ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:04Z", "compiler", "11-10 11:0x compiler: root cause is in partial evaluation, not lib. EOp StringIndex/StringSlice fold when every argument is a literal, and an out-of-range literal index raised a type error at fold time, even inside a branch whose condition is dynamic. Fix (program 1-invalid-literal-fold-is-runtime.kitdo, drafts\\spiral\\PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR\\): an invalid literal index or slice (out of range, or a slice that cuts a codepoint) is no longer folded; it emits the runtime op, so it fails only if that branch runs, the way a dynamic argument would. pack86 = pack85 + this, core bd0d596e. Gate queued on ganymede behind pack85: probe deadfold (dynamic flag guarding StringSlice(\"\",6,-1) and StringIndex(\"ab\",9)) on Rust + Fsharp, pack85 vs pack86, plus the suite vs pack85. Next step: read ~/remote-stage/pack86/g.log at \"p86 done\".")
inl note_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error_3 () = Note ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", 3u32, "2026-11-10T15:23Z", "compiler", "11-10 11:2x compiler: pack86 GATED on ganymede (core bd0d596e = pack85 + 1-invalid-literal-fold-is-runtime.kitdo). Probe deadfold (runtime flag guarding StringSlice(\"\",6,-1) and StringIndex(\"ab\",9)): pack85 rc=5 \"UTF-8 string of byte length 0s slice from 6 to -1 is invalid\" on Rust and Fsharp; pack86 rc=0 on both, and the Rust output has string_slice(.., 6, -1) in the dead branch (its runtime check aborts only if reached). Suite 7640 rows, 0 compile/native/exit/stdout diffs vs pack85. Install rides with pack87 (pack86 + Lua bit tables), gating now. Next step: install pack87 (or pack86 if pack87 is red) on ganymede, europa, moon; then org2 can drop the length-first guards.")
inl note_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error_4 () = Note ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", 4u32, "2026-11-10T15:29Z", "compiler", "DoD: (1) a literal StringSlice/StringIndex whose indices are out of range (or cut a codepoint) inside a branch with a runtime condition compiles on Rust and F# and emits the runtime op (probe deadfold: pack85 fails, fix passes); (2) suite vs the previous pack has 0 compile/native/exit/stdout diffs; (3) the fix is installed on ganymede, europa and moon; (4) org2 is told the length-first guards are optional. TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_pe_dead_branch_fold_error_5 () = Note ("PE-DEAD-BRANCH-FOLD-ERROR", 5u32, "2026-11-10T23:35Z", "compiler", "Evidence: DONE. pack89 (contains pack86s 1-invalid-literal-fold-is-runtime) INSTALLED on box (19:18 local, smoke ok), ganymede 23:15Z, europa 23:19Z and moon (job-1794352960944, exit 0). Probe deadfold: pack85 fails Rust and F# with \"UTF-8 string of byte length 0s slice from 6 to -1 is invalid\", pack86 compiles both and emits the runtime string_slice in the dead branch; suites pack86-89 all 0 diffs. org2 told the length-first guards are optional now. DoD 1-4 met.")
