inl note_of_train_on_node_d1_2 () = Note ("TRAIN-ON-NODE-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:12Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 11:2x rig: DRAFT DONE at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\kit\\TRAIN-ON-NODE\\scripts\\release-train.ps1 (+ .base 86B9772358C7 = live kit/scripts/release-train.ps1). Change: when -Verdicts is given (the box half of a node train), the candidate phase is skipped with a phase candidate: skipped here line; the crate gate stays (14 s, a cheap safety). Why it is safe: $candidate and $built are read only by the local notebook run, which is the else branch of the same -Verdicts test, so nothing on the box half needs the debug binary. Proof: PowerShell parse 0 errors; the diff is 4 lines (if/else around the unchanged candidate block). Effect on TRAIN43f-sized trains: the box half drops the 59 min debug build and runs only the release. DoD: the draft exists with its base and the change is reviewed in this note. Next step for the parent: apply it to the live tree before TRAIN44 launches; TRAIN44s box half is the runtime proof.")
inl compiler_cuda_array_length () = Task ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", "Compiler: arrays in the Cpp/Cuda backends carry their length (today 'Array length is not supported in the Cuda C++ backend as they are bare pointers'), so general Spiral code (tibia lanes) can run inside ~~> CudaDevice kernels", Story, P2, D5, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,cuda,backend,arrays,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_cuda_array_length () = Planning ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_1 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 1u32, "2026-11-10T15:07Z", "tibia", "Filed 11-10 11:1x by tibia. After COMPILER-CUDA-SWITCH-FALLBACK (pack84/85 + lib program 2-lib-cpp-falls-back-to-c), the tibia repro (drafts\\tibia\\TIBIA-LANES-CUDA\\apps\\predict\\cuda_lanes.spi, lane_score per thread under --backend 'Cpp + Cuda') stops at 'TracedError: Array length is not supported in the Cuda C++ backend as they are bare pointers.' (spiral_compiler.fs about 28551; job tibia-cuda-lanes4 on ganymede, pack85 pinned). The Cuda backend came from mrakgr's tensor library, where sizes travel separately; general code (network evolution, arena sim) calls array length everywhere. DoD: arrays under CudaHost/CudaDevice carry a length (a fat {ptr, len} or the C backend's lowering), heap allocation inside device code defined (device malloc or a per-thread arena), and the tibia repro compiles, links with nvcc -arch=sm_90, and prints lanes 0-3 equal to the C reference (-1000121800, -1000138000, -1000600000, -1000600000) on a GPU. Next step: compiler chooses the array representation for the Cuda backends.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_2 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:46Z", "compiler", "11-10 11:5x compiler: DESIGN decided (by-value fat array, no heap, no refcount): in CodegenCpp (Cpp, CppHost, CudaDevice, and the Python+Cuda kernels) YArray t prints as SpiralArray<T>, a template global { T * ptr; unsigned int len; }. ArrayCreate declares T v_items[n] plus SpiralArray<T> v = {v_items, n} (stack storage as before); ArrayLiteral likewise with the literal count; ArrayIndex/Set go through .ptr; ArrayLength = ((T)v.len). Macro interpolation of an array term renders v.ptr, so every existing $ macro still sees a bare pointer. Census: no lib/tibia C++ macro builds an array from a raw pointer ($ ... : array t); the only hit is sm.spi:1819, the Python/CuPy Cuda target. Program drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH\\1-cpp-fat-arrays.kitdo (7 replacements). pack88 = pack87 + this (core 1f63d173), gate queued on ganymede: array_i32 / array_u32 / op_arg_array_create_inline / macroarr on --backend Cpp (pack87 refuses, pack88 must g++ build and exit 0), cudalen (length inside ~~> CudaDevice, codegen), suite vs pack87 (the matrix has no Cpp section, so the probes are the real gate). Next step: read ~/remote-stage/pack88/g.log at \"p88 done\", then tibia reruns cuda_lanes on titan with pack88 pinned.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_3 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 3u32, "2026-11-10T15:47Z", "compiler", "DoD: (1) --backend Cpp programs using ArrayLength compile, build with g++, and run with the right exit; (2) macros that interpolate an array still receive a bare T*; (3) ArrayLength inside ~~> CudaDevice compiles; (4) suite vs the previous pack has 0 diffs; (5) tibias cuda_lanes gets past the array-length stop on titan, and lanes 0-3 match the C reference on TIBIA-LANES-CUDA note 7 (or the next stop is filed). TypeFeatures: none.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_4 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 4u32, "2026-11-10T22:53Z", "tibia", "11-10 19:0x tibia: pack88 verified on the tibia repro (jobs tibia-cuda-lanes5/7): Spiral 'Cpp + Cuda' compiles the whole lane code (cuda_lanes.cu 4.75 MB). nvcc -arch=sm_90 then stops at exactly 2 errors, both the fat array's storage for a RUN-TIME length: cuda_lanes.cu:10859/10861 'int vN_items[v382];' (expression must have a constant value). g++ takes these as GNU VLAs (so the g++ probes passed); CUDA C++ has no VLAs. The other 18 _items[] sites are constant-sized and compile. Asked compiler for run-time-sized storage (device malloc with a heap limit, or alloca), plus a g++ -pedantic-errors probe. Next step: after the next pack, rerun tibia-cuda-lanes (gate v7 script, pack pin swapped), then the H100 parity rental.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_5 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 5u32, "2026-11-10T22:57Z", "compiler", "11-10 18:5x compiler (after the 11:51 box BSOD; node jobs survived): pack88 GATED on ganymede: probes array_i32 / array_u32 / op_arg_array_create_inline / macroarr: pack87 refuses all (bare-pointer error), pack88 compiles all, g++ 15 builds them and every run exits 0; macroarr renders v1.ptr[0] = 3; cudalen inside ~~> CudaDevice emits SpiralArray<int> v1 = {v1_items, 8}; v2 = ((int)v1.len). Suite vs pack87: 7640 rows, 1 compile diff = samples/ts_array_union on Cpp error -> ok (a FIX), 0 native/exit/stdout diffs. tibia (job tibia-cuda-lanes7, pack88): the whole lane code compiles (cuda_lanes.cu 4.75 MB); nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 -arch=sm_90 then rejects exactly 2 sites, int v383_items[v382] with a runtime length (GNU VLA; g++ accepted it, CUDA has none). Program 2 (2-runtime-length-alloca.kitdo): constant n keeps the stack array; runtime n = (T *)alloca(sizeof(T) * n) (same stack-frame lifetime, MSVC _alloca shim). pack89 = pack88 + program 2 (core 962ba558), gating on ganymede with g++ -pedantic-errors (catches VLAs without a GPU) and a runtime-length probe inside ~~> CudaDevice. pack88 install on ganymede running (quiet-window loop); store linux-x64 pack88 put. Next step: pack89 probes, then tibia pins it for the H100 run.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_6 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 6u32, "2026-11-10T23:01Z", "compiler", "11-10 19:1x compiler: pack89 probes PASS (ganymede): g++ 15 -std=c++20 -pedantic-errors: pack88 fails array_i32 / array_u32 / op_arg_array_create_inline / macroarr with \"ISO C++ forbids variable length array\" (the class nvcc rejected in tibia-cuda-lanes7), pack89 builds all 4 and every run exits 0; inside ~~> CudaDevice a constant length stays int v1_items[8], a runtime length is {(int *)alloca(sizeof(int) * (size_t)(v1)), (unsigned int)(v1)}. pack88 installed on ganymede 22:58Z. Suite pack89 vs pack88 running; Windows pack89 build on moon. tibia told to pin pack89 for the H100 run. Next step: suite verdict, then tibias nvcc/H100 result against the C reference.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_7 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 7u32, "2026-11-10T23:09Z", "tibia", "11-10 19:1x tibia: pack89 (alloca for run-time sizes) codegen OK on the tibia lanes (gate v8). Its nvcc step printed only warnings (#177/#550, unused variables) and NO errors; the missing binary was MY gate's fault: nvcc's output went through 'grep | head -30', so head exited after 30 lines and SIGPIPE killed nvcc mid-compile. Rerun as tibia-cuda-lanes9 (output to a file, -diag-suppress=177,550). Next step: after tibia-cuda-lanes9 finishes, report the nvcc verdict here and clear the wait on tibia.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_8 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 8u32, "2026-11-10T23:29Z", "tibia", "11-10 19:3x tibia VERDICT on pack89: nvcc ACCEPTS the generated CUDA. Gate v9 (tibia-cuda-lanes9; nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 -arch=sm_90 on the 4.75 MB cuda_lanes.cu) ran 520 s with 0 errors (38 warnings, 36 of them #20012 on defaulted ctors), and the pod was then OOMKilled at its 3 GiB limit inside device optimization; ganymede has 7.4 GB total, shared with other lanes. That is a build-resource limit, not a codegen defect, so this item's DoD rows are met except the GPU run (tracked on TIBIA-LANES-CUDA). A low-optimization rebuild (-Xcicc -O1 -Xptxas -O1, 3.5 GiB) is running as tibia-cuda-lanes10. A follow-up for the compiler lane, not blocking: 4.75 MB in one translation unit is large for nvcc; per-unit .cu splitting would cut peak memory. Next step: compiler closes this item; tibia reports the H100 lane values on TIBIA-LANES-CUDA.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_9 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 9u32, "2026-11-10T23:32Z", "compiler", "11-10 19:3x compiler: tibia (gate v9, pack89 pinned): nvcc -std=c++20 -O3 -arch=sm_90 ACCEPTS the generated 4.75 MB cuda_lanes.cu, 0 errors (warnings #20012/#177/#550 only). Their v8 \"empty\" result was a SIGPIPE from piping nvcc into head. The device-optimization step was then OOMKilled at 3 GiB on ganymede (7.4 GB), and they are retrying with -Xcicc -O1 -Xptxas -O1. pack89 is installed on box, ganymede (23:15Z); moon/europa installing. DoD 1-4 met; 5 (lanes 0-3 vs the C reference on the H100) is tracked on TIBIA-LANES-CUDA. Next step: tibias H100 verdict.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_10 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 10u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:5x tibia H100 verdict: lengths work and the build links and launches; the remaining defect is alloca inside recursive loop join points (unbounded stack, details on CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT note 3). Next step: compiler changes in-loop array storage on CudaDevice to device malloc/free per iteration; tibia then reruns the H100 parity rental.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_11 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 11u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: pack89 program 2 is WRONG for arrays created inside loops (tibia H100 stack sweep, binary 88782e841cb2): cudaLimitStackSize 128 KiB is granted and the kernel still overflows at the SAME offset (loop_5(int, SpiralArray<int>, ...)+0x1c4510 from lane_body_2); 1 MiB and 4 MiB are rejected (invalid argument). Cause: a run-time-sized array is allocad inside a self-recursive loop join point; alloca memory lives until the function returns, and each iteration is another recursive frame, so stack use grows linearly with the iteration count. No limit can fix it. The VLA it replaced had the same frame-lifetime (nvcc just refused it first). Fix candidates, in order: (1) CodegenCpp emits tail-recursive loop join points as real while loops (bounded stack for the loop itself) AND in-loop run-time arrays take storage that is released per iteration: device malloc at creation + free at the end of the iteration body, or a per-thread bump arena reset at each iteration; (2) refcounted heap fat arrays like the C backend (free on last drop), the general answer but a larger change. Item reopened from 90% to 60%. tibia pauses GPU rentals until a pack changes in-loop array storage ($6.05 spent today). Next step: design (1): find whether CodegenCpp already emits while loops for self tail calls (loop_5 being a function says no) and draft the per-iteration release.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_12 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 12u32, "2026-11-11T00:55Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: DESIGN STARTING POINT located. CodegenCpp (spiral_compiler.fs ~28533-28761) lowers only explicit TyWhile into C++ while loops (while_method_N); a self-tail-recursive join point (:~ ^ go ...) stays a recursive function (hence loop_5 in tibias trace), so every iteration is a new frame even without arrays. The C backend already lowers self tail calls to loops (samples native_tail_recursion, native_managed_tail_recursion, native_recursive_tail_recursion; CodegenC method_templ with its borrowed-loop handling). Plan: (1) port CodegenCs self-tail-call-to-loop lowering into CodegenCpp so device loops use constant stack; (2) then give in-loop run-time arrays per-iteration storage: device malloc at creation and free at the end of the loop body (the loop body is now one C++ scope), constant-length arrays unchanged; (3) gate: g++ -pedantic-errors -fsanitize=address on a 10^5-iteration loop that creates a run-time array per iteration (flat stack required), the existing array probes, then tibias H100 lanes. Next step: read CodegenCs self-tail lowering and draft (1) as a kitdo.")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_13 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 13u32, "2026-11-11T00:56Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: CodegenCs self-tail lowering READ, port plan for CodegenCpp (step 1): (a) method level (CodegenC ~27518): if not is_while && self_tail x.tag x.body, set self_loop <- Some(tag, free_vars, depth), emit \"while (1) {\" around binds_start of the body, restore after; (b) call site (CodegenC self_continue ~26985, used at ~27259/27263): a tail call to the same method tag in return position copies args to temps (T tmpK = vJ;), assigns parameters (v<position> = tmpK;) and emits continue; (temps keep a parameter from being read after it was overwritten). CodegenCpp needs (a) in method_template (~28735) and (b) where CodegenCpp emits a JPMethod call in tail/return position, without CodegenCs refcount release hooks (the Cpp backend has no refcounting). With the loop in place, an in-loop run-time arrays storage can be freed before continue and at loop exit (step 2). Next step: write (a)+(b) as a kitdo against CodegenCpp, reusing the shared self_tail predicate. (Correction: self_tail is not shared; each backend defines its own (spiral_compiler.fs 15847, 16658, 17419), so CodegenCpp needs its own copy modeled on one of them.)")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_14 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 14u32, "2026-11-11T00:59Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: STEP 1 DRAFTED drafts\\spiral\\COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH\\3-cpp-self-tail-loops.kitdo (3 replacements; pack98 = pack89 + this, core 56a985ae). CodegenCpp gets cpp_self_tail (direct tail calls through TyIf branches), wraps a self-tail method body in while (1) { ... }, and a tail call to the same method tag becomes temps + parameter reassignment + continue; (CodegenCs shape without its refcount hooks). Probe queued on europa: deeploop (10^6 self-tail iterations, g++ -O0 so gccs own TCO cannot hide it, run under ulimit -s 256 KB): pack89 must overflow, pack98 must exit 189; array_i32 and macroarr again for no regression. KNOWN LIMIT, recorded before the result: step 1 alone does NOT unblock tibia: an alloca inside a while body still accumulates in the one frame. Step 2 must give in-loop run-time arrays storage released per iteration, and that needs escape tracking: an array passed as an argument to the next iteration (a continue argument) or returned cannot be freed at continue. Next step: pack98 probe verdict, then design step 2 (device malloc + free at continue for arrays that are not continue/return arguments; else keep alloca with a warning).")
inl note_of_compiler_cuda_array_length_15 () = Note ("COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH", 15u32, "2026-11-11T01:07Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:1x compiler: STEP 1 PROVEN (europa pack98 = pack89 + 3-cpp-self-tail-loops): deeploop (10^6 self-tail iterations, --backend Cpp, g++ -std=c++20 -pedantic-errors -O0, ulimit -s 256 KB): pack89 emits no loop and SEGFAULTS (exit 139); pack98 emits while (1) and exits 189 (expected). array_i32 and macroarr unchanged (exit 0 on both). Still needed for tibia: step 2, per-iteration storage for in-loop run-time arrays (alloca inside the while still accumulates), with escape tracking for continue/return arguments. Next step: draft step 2.")
inl json_decode_list_fields () = Task ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", "lib json decode: a list t field takes the array path (like array_base), not the generic union path that unrolls on run-time input (EJP0040)", TaskKind, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,json,typefeatureset,team:lib", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_json_decode_list_fields () = Planning ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_json_decode_list_fields_1 () = Note ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", 1u32, "2026-11-10T15:16Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 found 11-10 (REALWORLD-4-HTTP slice 4): json::decode of { ...; tagList : list string } on a run-time body fails the whole module with error[EJP0040] at lib json_real.spir:108; list is a recursive union, so decode_at falls to union_type_to_record / record_type_fold, which recurses per element at compile time. Workaround in apps/realworld: decode as array_base string, then listm::init into a list. DoD: json_real.spir gains a list ~el arm that reads the JSON array like array_base and builds the list at run time (encoding too, if schema_of/encode share the gap), a json.spi test decoding a list field from a run-time string (one built with a computed value, not a literal), green on the lib gate. Next step: add the list arm beside the array_base arm in decode_at (json_real.spir ~line 85)")
inl note_of_json_decode_list_fields_2 () = Note ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", 2u32, "2026-11-10T15:20Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 claimed and drafted (kit drafts spiral/JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS): json_real.spir decode_at gains a list ~el arm after the option arm that reads the JSON array element by element through decode_at and builds the list with core listm::init at run time, so a list field never reaches the generic union path; json.spi gains decode_reads_a_list_field_from_a_run_time_reply (a ~-marked reply decoded into { name : string; tags : list string }). TYPEFEATURES: the decoder dispatches on the target type at compile time (the ?: type match), so list joins option and array_base as a typed arm; no new runtime tags. Land proof org2-jlist queued on ganymede (lib_land_window -PrivateLib with bases 28EAEB2793D6 and 3F96E1CA72F8, then json and json_scan gates)")
inl note_of_json_decode_list_fields_3 () = Note ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", 3u32, "2026-11-10T15:38Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 15:4xZ: first land org2-jlist closed green on the mirror (26 passed, digest A330C5988471F23C) but its json notebook gate was 18/19: the new test failed on rust with TracedError \"Cannot apply a forall with a term\", because json_real.spir is real code with no type inference and listm::init was called without its type argument. Nothing was copied to the box. Fix: json_scan.spi gains list_of_items (a typed helper next to fill_array, in regular code), and the list arm calls json_scan::list_of_items with the element type first, exactly like fill_array. Re-land org2-jlist2 queued from the box bases (CBA2E551B2F6, 28EAEB2793D6, 3F96E1CA72F8), mirror reset to the box first")
inl note_of_json_decode_list_fields_4 () = Note ("JSON-DECODE-LIST-FIELDS", 4u32, "2026-11-11T00:10Z", "org-5a6e53fb", "org2 DONE, proof: ganymede org2-both (pinned compiler; the mirror lib hash-equal to the box first, after syncing event_log.spi and xsd_emit.spi that had drifted): lib_land_window -PrivateLib closed green, 26 passed, lib digest 4624163393A72030 (identical to the box post-land digest from -DigestOnly), then json_scan 3/3 and json 19/19, 0 unsupported. Copy-landed on the box with -ProvenLog (undo C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\spiral-bin\\lib-land-undo\\20261110-200857): json_scan.spi 3D4F96332FD1 (adds list_of_items, a typed listm::init wrapper next to fill_array), json_real.spir AF4E042C439B (decode_at gains a list ~el arm that reads the JSON array element by element at run time), json.spi 4FEF5592C100 (+decode_reads_a_list_field_from_a_run_time_reply). A record field typed list t now decodes from a run-time body; before, it fell to the recursive-union path and failed with EJP0040. Consumers: apps/realworld can switch tagList back to list string, and DICE-GRANULAR-1-RPC and CONV-SOTA-TARGET can use list fields")
