inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_1 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 1u32, "2026-11-10T23:31Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "Slice of FISCAL-PE-SUNAT (which waits on the user for a CI Java leg): the file-name rule is SUNAT-only and is what SUNAT rule 1034 checks; drafts in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\fiscal\\FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME\\ with a .base. Next step: write the draft.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_2 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 2u32, "2026-11-10T23:34Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 20:5x DRAFT drafts\\fiscal\\FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME\\sunat.spi 75DB6234 over live sunat.spi (.base kept). Ported the scratch runner's rule into typed Spiral: submitted_type reads the root (Invoice -> catalog 01 type via document_type_of over cbc:InvoiceTypeCode; CreditNote -> NotaCredito 07; DebitNote -> NotaDebito 08) as an option document_type, so an unknown type yields no name instead of a bad one; issuer_ruc follows AccountingSupplierParty/Party/PartyIdentification/ID; submitted_file_name = RUC-TT-ID.xml through the existing document_type_code; file_name_of / entry_file_name read a corpus document. Tests read the 58 files at run time (no compile-time-literal trap): the_first_homologation_invoice_is_named_as_sfs_names_it (factura1Con3Items -> 12345678912-01-FF11-1.xml, the name the 58/58 run used), every_homologation_case_gets_a_submitted_name (58), no_two_homologation_cases_share_a_submitted_name. Budget 20 -> 20. Peru as a regime witness was considered and REJECTED (recorded on FISCAL-ERP-REGIME-INDEX): its documents are UBL 2.1 roots already owned by EuropeUbl. Next step: moon gate (job label fiscal-moon-sunat-name: build.ps1 -Files sunat on a PE corpus subset), then land into fiscal src.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_3 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 3u32, "2026-11-10T23:35Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 20:5x moon gate did not start: node-proof failed at its job-script scp to moon's stage (the moon entry is new tonight; org2 told). Inputs stay staged (moon home: pe-corpus.tgz with the 58 homologation documents and the three UBL manifests, 19,946 bytes; sunat.draft.spi). Next step: run the same gate on europa (one job at a time) once fiscal-regex-adapter-proof exits: ship pe-corpus.tgz and the draft, build.ps1 -Files sunat with a pinned compiler.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_4 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 4u32, "2026-11-10T23:40Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:0x CORRECTION (org2's diagnosis): the moon failure was NOT the new registry entry (its stage path matches node's and C:\\home\\remote-stage exists). moon was CPU-starved by other lanes' builds, so sshd sent no banner and reset connections; node-proof's New-Item step failed silently and the scp then failed. Plan unchanged. Next step: the gate on europa once fiscal-regex-adapter-proof exits; moon only after it answers again.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_5 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 5u32, "2026-11-11T00:00Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:3x GATE 1 on europa (job-1794354616949-fiscal-europa-sunat-name, overlay 75DB6234, 58 PE cases): sunat discovered 12 executed 12 passed 11 FAILED 1 = no_two_homologation_cases_share_a_submitted_name. Not a port bug: the Saxon run's own names (saxon-results.csv) show the same thing, 58 cases but only 43 distinct submitted names, because the Group2 Exonerado and Inafecto scenarios reuse the FF12 series and numbers and Group4's three credit notes all carry FF14-1. The homologation groups are separate scenarios, not one submission stream, so uniqueness was a false invariant. Replaced it with the true, pinned one: the_homologation_groups_reuse_their_series (distinct_count = 43), which still catches any change to the naming rule; the factura1Con3Items exact-name test and the 58-named test passed. Draft now 76539094 (budget 20 -> 20). Next step: re-gate on europa (label fiscal-europa-sunat-name2), then land into fiscal src/sunat.spi.")
inl note_of_fiscal_pe_sunat_file_name_6 () = Note ("FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME", 6u32, "2026-11-11T00:05Z", "fiscal-84b53718", "fiscal 11-10 21:4x GREEN AND LANDED. europa job-1794355375910-fiscal-europa-sunat-name2 (overlay 76539094, 58 PE cases, pinned compiler, memo off): sunat discovered 12 executed 12 passed 12 failed 0. Copied into fiscal src/sunat.spi (unstaged; was 330CB163); undo in C:\\Users\\i574n\\AppData\\Local\\kit\\drafts\\fiscal\\FISCAL-PE-SUNAT-FILE-NAME\\undo-20261110-200559. SUNAT's submitted file name (RUC-TT-SERIE-NUMERO.xml, the name rule 1034 checks) is now typed Spiral: submitted_type as a catalog-01 document_type, issuer_ruc, submitted_file_name, entry_file_name over a corpus document; it reproduces the names SFS's own engine used for the 58/58 run, including the pinned corpus fact (58 cases, 43 distinct names). Next step: none; Done (the scratch runner can now take its names from this code once fiscal has a SUNAT CI leg).")
inl cuda_per_unit_translation () = Task ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", "CUDA output: one .cu per linked unit instead of one translation unit, so nvcc's device optimization fits in node memory", TaskKind, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,cuda,linking,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cuda_per_unit_translation () = Planning ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_1 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 1u32, "2026-11-10T23:32Z", "compiler", "From tibia 11-10 (gate v9, pack89): the generated cuda_lanes.cu is a single 4.75 MB translation unit. nvcc accepts it (0 errors) after 520 s, then device optimization was OOMKilled at 3 GiB on ganymede (7.4 GB); tibia retries with -Xcicc -O1 -Xptxas -O1. Emitting one .cu per linked unit (with relocatable device code, nvcc -rdc=true and a device link step) would bound peak memory per TU and allow parallel nvcc. TypeFeatures: none. Next step: measure nvcc peak RSS vs TU size on two cut points of cuda_lanes.cu, to see whether splitting helps before designing the emitter change.")
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_2 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 2u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:5x tibia: no nvcc RSS yet: /usr/bin/time is absent in the runpod/pytorch image (the guarded wrapper skipped it). Known so far: -O3 device optimization OOMed at 3 GiB on ganymede after 520 s; -O1/-O1/-O1 builds take 552-563 s single-threaded on an H100 pod (224 host CPUs idle). Splitting into units would at least let nvcc compile in parallel. Next step: measure peak RSS with a polling sampler (ps) on the next pod build, which happens after the in-loop array storage changes.")
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_3 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 3u32, "2026-11-11T01:07Z", "tibia", "11-10 21:1x tibia: the measurement is in flight without a GPU (nvcc needs none): job tibia-nvcc-rss on ganymede, a k3s nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-devel pod at 3.5 GiB, compiling pack89's cuda_lanes.cu (4.75 MB) at -O1/-Xcicc -O1/-Xptxas -O1, with a 2 s sampler summing VmRSS over nvcc, cudafe++, cicc, ptxas, gcc and cc1plus, reporting the peak total and the top process. Either a peak figure, or OOMKilled at 3.5 GiB (a lower bound), plus which stage dominates; that tells whether splitting by unit helps. Next step: after tibia-nvcc-rss finishes, report the peak and its stage here.")
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_4 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 4u32, "2026-11-11T01:23Z", "tibia", "11-10 21:2x tibia MEASURED (job tibia-nvcc-rss, ganymede k3s nvidia/cuda:12.4.1-devel pod, 3.5 GiB, pack89 cuda_lanes.cu = 4,752,373 bytes / 73,136 lines, nvcc -O1 -Xcicc -O1 -Xptxas -O1, a 2 s VmRSS sampler over the nvcc subprocesses): cicc (front end and NVVM) stays SMALL, 197 MiB at 21 s and at most 592 MiB by 82 s. ptxas (PTX to SASS) is the memory hog: 1,814 MiB at 144 s, then 3,002-3,163 MiB by 157-167 s, and OOMKilled at 3.5 GiB (the pod was killed at 171 s). So peak memory is ptxas on ONE huge device function: everything is inlined into cuda_device_entry0 (lane_body and the whole evolution and arena sim). Splitting the .cu by unit helps ONLY if cross-unit calls stay real calls: -rdc=true separate compilation prevents cross-TU inlining, so ptxas sees several smaller functions. The same effect inside one TU: mark the large device functions __noinline__ (the fork already maps the noinline name prefix to __noinline__ on Cpp/CUDA, spiral_compiler.fs 28642), or have codegen emit __noinline__ for join points above a size threshold on CudaDevice. Expect ptxas memory and time to drop roughly with the largest function's size. Recommendation: first try __noinline__ on the per-lane top-level functions (cheap, no build-system change), then -rdc per unit if still needed. The wait on tibia is answered.")
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_5 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 5u32, "2026-11-11T01:24Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:2x compiler: RE-SCOPED by tibias measurement (note 4: VmRSS sampler on nvcc for pack89s 4.75 MB cuda_lanes.cu: cicc < 600 MiB; ptxas 1.8 -> 3.2 GiB in 20 s, OOM at 3.5 GiB on ONE giant inlined kernel function). Per-unit .cu only helps with -rdc, so the first lever is __noinline__ on large device functions. DRAFTED drafts\\spiral\\CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION\\1-cuda-noinline-large-device-methods.kitdo (2 replacements): CodegenCpp counts a methods binds recursively (through if, union match, int switch, do, while) and marks a CudaDevice method __noinline__ above SPIRAL_CUDA_NOINLINE_BINDS (default 150). pack100 = pack98 (self-tail loops) + this (core 7962cfa5), queued on europa: build, cudalenrt codegen with the default and with the limit forced to 1 (every device method must then carry __noinline__). The real verdict is tibias ptxas RSS on cuda_lanes with pack100 after the in-loop array step lands. Next step: read pack100/g.log at \"p100 done\".")
inl note_of_cuda_per_unit_translation_6 () = Note ("CUDA-PER-UNIT-TRANSLATION", 6u32, "2026-11-11T01:35Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:3x compiler: pack100 PROBE PASS (europa; pack98 + 1-cuda-noinline-large-device-methods, build exit 0): cudalenrt under \"Cpp + Cuda\" emits 1 device method; with the default limit (150 binds) it stays inlinable (0 __noinline__), with SPIRAL_CUDA_NOINLINE_BINDS=1 it is emitted as __device__ __noinline__ (1 of 1). So the switch and the size measure work. The real verdict is ptxas peak RSS on tibias cuda_lanes.cu with pack100 (expected well under the 3.5 GiB OOM if the giant kernel splits into callees), measured with tibias VmRSS sampler; that needs the in-loop array step of COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH first, since tibia paused rentals until then.")
