inl note_of_workspace_primitives_lib_d1_2 () = Note ("WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-11T00:27Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 21:1x rig: PROBE RED before any test ran (ganymede 20:24, load 3.29): ETY-UnboundModule at glob.spi:5:8, the `:< testing` line of the static block. testing.spi IS in the probe lib (160 files) and list_sort.spi opens testing the same way, so the module itself is fine. The lib's spiral.toml has NO modules list (derived modules), so the hypothesis is order: an unregistered new file placed before testing in the derived order (glob < testing alphabetically) cannot open it; list_sort works because its place is known. NEXT STEP: read how the compiler derives lib module order (withDerivedModules in the host) and either register glob after testing the way the lib lane registers modules (memory register-module-only-after-green: register only once green, so probe it as a package of its own first, with packages: |core- spiral- and `:< testing` replaced by the kit test style), then rerun. The draft is unchanged at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB\\lib\\spiral\\glob.spi; the probe removed its copy from the shared probe lib.")
inl note_of_workspace_primitives_lib_d1_3 () = Note ("WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB-D1", 3u32, "2026-11-11T01:27Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 21:3x rig: proof: the glob draft probed as a STANDALONE package depending on the lib (packages: |core- spiral-) on moon (job-1794359839464-rig-probe-glob): exec=3 failed=0 passed=3 (star within a segment, double-star across segments, question marks, both slash styles, and a run-time path). So the 20:24 red inside the lib tree was module ORDER (an unregistered file placed before testing in the derived order), not the code. DONE as a draft (%LOCALAPPDATA%\\kit\\drafts\\spiral\\WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB\\lib\\spiral\\glob.spi, lib style). Remaining for the parent: register glob in lib/spiral after testing (memory register-module-only-after-green: it is green now) through the lib landing path (lib_land_window with a node proof), then kit ports call it.")
