inl note_of_kit_spiral_tests_d1_1 () = Note ("KIT-SPIRAL-TESTS-D1", 1u32, "2026-11-11T00:13Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "Slice of KIT-SPIRAL-TESTS (note 24 port plan). Edits rust_std_fs (the OS boundary package) in its own primitive shape (boolean checks over the Rust result, like read_regular_text_equals); base archived first; the .rs test 1 is removed only after the Spiral test is green, test 2 (invalid filenames) stays Rust.")
inl note_of_kit_spiral_tests_d1_2 () = Note ("KIT-SPIRAL-TESTS-D1", 2u32, "2026-11-11T00:14Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 20:4x rig: FIRST ATTEMPT REFUSED by the edit-time ratchet and reverted (rooted_effects.spi equals its archived base again). Two boolean checks in the package's existing shape (list_limited_equals, list_limited_refused) raised rust_std_fs host-macro 31 -> 34 and host-statement/host-block 9 -> 11, and the GRANULAR-HEAL lint refuses macros longer than one call (the casts belong in Spiral). The ratchet only shrinks, so the port must RESHAPE the boundary, not add to it. ROUTE: replace the existing list_len macro (map_or + closure + try_from, the same bad shape) with ONE granular call, rig_rust_std_fs::rooted_list_names_limited(Path::new(path), limit) returning a typed result, the usize limit made in Spiral through convert, and derive list_len from it in Spiral (a large limit); net host macros unchanged or lower. Prerequisite: rust_std_fs lists only |core- under packages:, so it needs spiral- (the lib) for result and vec types; AGENTS.md wants that anyway. Then the Spiral test block (z, a and the u-umlaut name; limits 0/2/3; sorted order) and the removal of directory_names.rs test 1. NEXT STEP: that boundary reshape, probed on ganymede (Linux; rooted listing has a Linux backend).")
inl note_of_kit_spiral_tests_d1_3 () = Note ("KIT-SPIRAL-TESTS-D1", 3u32, "2026-11-11T00:15Z", "rig-fc1568a6", "11-10 rig marker. DoD: directory_names.rs test 1 lives as a Spiral test block in rust_std_fs, green on the ganymede Cells gate, the .rs keeps only test 2, and the edit-time ratchet shows rust_std_fs host-macro at or below 31.")
