inl cuda_device_stack_limit () = Task ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", "CUDA: a ~~> CudaDevice body with recursion or alloca'd arrays gets a host-side cudaDeviceSetLimit(cudaLimitStackSize) or a compile-time warning, instead of a silent ~1 KiB per-thread stack", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "compiler,cuda,codegen,team:compiler", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cuda_device_stack_limit () = Planning ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", Minutes 120u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cuda_device_stack_limit_1 () = Note ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", 1u32, "2026-11-11T00:34Z", "compiler", "From tibia 11-10 (H100, pack89): the lane code builds (nvcc -O1 -arch=sm_90, ~8 min, 8.6 MB) and launches, then fails 'illegal memory access'; compute-sanitizer: 'Stack overflow at loop_5(int, SpiralArray<int>, ...)' from lane_body_2 on every thread, and ptxas warned cuda_device_entry0's stack size cannot be statically determined. Cause: recursive device loops plus alloca'd run-time arrays (COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH program 2) against CUDA's default ~1 KiB per-thread stack. tibia raises cudaLimitStackSize to 128 KiB in its host macro. Proposal: when codegen emits device code with recursion or alloca, (a) warn at compile time naming the kernel, and/or (b) emit the cudaDeviceSetLimit call in the generated host prelude with a size from a package setting. TypeFeatures: none. Next step: decide (a) vs (b) after tibia's rerun reports the stack size that worked.")
inl note_of_cuda_device_stack_limit_2 () = Note ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", 2u32, "2026-11-11T00:42Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:4x tibia: the 128 KiB rerun (cudaDeviceSetLimit returned no error; binary f200783c6c45, nvcc 552 s) STILL fails with 'illegal memory access', so 128 KiB is not enough or the fault has moved. A diagnostic rental is running now: one build, stack sizes 128 KiB / 1 MiB / 4 MiB taken from an environment variable (no rebuild per size, and cudaDeviceGetLimit reports the granted value), then compute-sanitizer at 1 MiB to tell overflow from another fault. That gives the size that works, or shows that no size does. Next step: after the diagnostic rental, report the size (or the new fault) here; then pick (a) or (b).")
inl note_of_cuda_device_stack_limit_3 () = Note ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", 3u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "tibia", "11-10 20:5x tibia SWEEP RESULT (H100, one build 88782e841cb2): stack 128 KiB granted -> still 'illegal memory access'; 1 MiB and 4 MiB REJECTED (cudaDeviceSetLimit 'invalid argument', limit stays 1 KiB), so the per-thread stack cap lies between 128 KiB and 1 MiB. compute-sanitizer at every limit: 'Stack overflow at loop_5(int, SpiralArray<int>, int,int,int,int)+0x1c4510' from lane_body_2, the same offset. Diagnosis: an alloca'd run-time-sized array inside a self-recursive loop join point grows the stack per iteration (alloca lives until the function returns), so it is unbounded; no stack limit fixes it. Fix belongs to codegen: arrays created inside loop join points on CudaDevice need device malloc/free per iteration (or a per-thread arena), not alloca. Sent to compiler. Answers: a stack size alone does NOT work; (b) a host-prelude limit is insufficient, so warn (a) AND change the storage.")
inl note_of_cuda_device_stack_limit_4 () = Note ("CUDA-DEVICE-STACK-LIMIT", 4u32, "2026-11-11T00:54Z", "compiler", "11-10 21:0x compiler: tibias sweep shows a stack limit is not the fix here: 128 KiB granted still overflows at the same offset, and 1 MiB+ is rejected. The root cause is per-iteration alloca in recursive loops (see COMPILER-CUDA-ARRAY-LENGTH note); this item stays for the remaining case (deep but bounded recursion), proposal (a), a compile-time warning naming the kernel, unchanged.")
